\par }{\fs20\lang1024\langfe1024\noproof {\shp{\*\shpinst\shpleft1440\shptop2070\shpright10800\shpbottom2070\shpfhdr1\shpbxpage\shpbxignore\shpbypage\shpbyignore\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt0\shpz2\shplockanchor\shplid2049{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn fFlipH}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn fFlipV}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn shapePath}{\sv 4}}{\sp{\sn fFillOK}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn fFilled}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn fArrowheadsOK}{\sv 1}}{\sp{\sn posrelh}{\sv 1}}{\sp{\sn posrelv}{\sv 1}}{\sp{\sn fLayoutInCell}{\sv 1}}{\sp{\sn fLayoutInCell}{\sv 1}}}{\shprslt{\*\do\dobxpage\dobypage\dodhgt8194\dpline\dpptx0\dppty0\dpptx9920\dppty0\dpx1190\dpy2070\dpxsize9920\dpysize0\dplinew15\dplinecor0\dplinecog0\dplinecob0}}}}{\f0\fs16  \par }}{\footerf \pard\plain \s22\ql \b\f354\fs18  Page {\field{\*\fldinst PAGE}{\fldrslt 1}}{     }\'a9 2026 \uc2 Factiva, Inc.  All rights reserved.\par }{\footer \pard\plain \s22\ql \b\f354\fs18  Page {\field{\*\fldinst PAGE}{\fldrslt 1}} of {\field{\*\fldinst NUMPAGES}{\fldrslt 1}}{     }\'a9 2026 \uc2 Factiva, Inc.  All rights reserved.\par }\pard\plain \ql \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\fs20 {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 King Charles III\uc2  Promises Loyalty, Respect, And Love; Border States Sending Migrants To Washington; Washington Denied National Guard Assistance; White House Sidesteps Questions On Mississippi Visit; Biden Exhibits Softer Tone Against Republicans; Queen Elizabeth II Of The United Kingdom Passes Away; Former \uc2 U.N\uc2 . Ambassador Nikki Haley Interviewed On Political Effect Of Abortion Issue On 2022 Midterms And Possible Future Presidential Run; Ohio Senate Candidates Democrat \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2  And Republican J.D. Vance Trade Criticisms; New Polling Shows George Republican Senate Candidate \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  With Slight Lead Over Democratic Incumbent \uc2 Raphael Warnock\b0\par\par\uc2 Bret Baier, Greg Palkot, Peter Doocy, Shannon Bream, Jacqui  Heinrich, Martha MacCallum \par \uc2 8,148 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 September 2022\par \uc2 Fox News: Special Report with Brett Baier\par \uc2 FNSPBB\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 GREG GUTFELD, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST (on camera): She should turn him down kneel. Dump them. She should dump them? I don't care if it's their kid.\par \par \uc2 JESSICA TARLOV, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR (on camera): They have a baby --\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Move on. Move on.\par \par \uc2 SEAN DUFFY, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR: Move on.\par \par \uc2 JEANINE PIRRO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST (on camera): Or, you know what, you should have a net under it. All right.\par \par \uc2 Anyway, that's it for us. We'll see you back here on Monday. Have a great weekend. Thanks for joining us. And the news is next.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 DANA PERINO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST (on camera): Bye.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Good night.\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL ANCHOR (on camera): "BREAKING TONIGHT", the new King of England promises he will serve with loyalty, respect, and love for the remaining time God grants him.\par \par \uc2 Welcome to Washington. I'm Bret Baier.\par \par \uc2 King Charles III\uc2  made his first address to the British people today, one day after his mother, Queen Elizabeth II died at the age of 96.\par \par \uc2 Senior Foreign Affairs correspondent Greg Palkot is in London tonight with the latest. Good evening. Greg.\par \par \uc2 GREG PALKOT, FOX NEWS CHANNEL SENIOR FOREIGN AFFAIRS CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Bret, it was a remarkable day, a day that basically, London and the world look back at the reign of Queen Elizabeth II, and looked ahead to the reign of \uc2 King Charles III\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 We saw cannons being blasted 96 times to mark the 96 years of Queen Elizabeth's life. We heard bells being rung, in basically, in cathedrals and small chapels.\par \par \uc2 We saw the House of Commons, usually the scene of really raucous political activity. Well, that was still and memorializing and thinking of the queen.\par \par \uc2 In Buckingham Palace, we saw something else, Bret. We saw a sign of things to come. We saw \uc2 King Charles\uc2  --\par \par \uc2 PALKOT (voice-over): \uc2 King Charles III\uc2 , approaching the royal residents for the very first time as King, along with Queen Consort Camilla.\par \par \uc2 And he shook hands and met the crowd, and in a human touch, much like his mother did, much like the reason why she was beloved.\par \par \uc2 He also spoke to the nation for the first time on T.V. And I believe we have some a quote from him right now. Let's take a listen to that.\par \par \uc2 And perhaps, we don't. OK.\par \par \uc2 One more time, Charles continues his journey tomorrow at 10:00 a.m. The Accession Council, that's what it's called, will officially proclaim him king. And the remembrances of the queen continue as well.\par \par \uc2 I believe we have the quote from \uc2 King Charles III\uc2 . Let's listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KING CHARLES III\uc2 : To my darling mama, as you begin your last great journey to join my dear late papa, I want simply to say this. Thank you. Thank you, for your love and devotion to our family and to the family of nations you have served so diligently all these years.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PALKOT (on camera): A personal touch from \uc2 King Charles III\uc2 , reaching out to the country, reaching out to the world. Listen to him for the first time as King speak on television to the folks.\par \par \uc2 He's got a long road ahead, and we have a long road here too, 10 days until a very grand state funeral for the queen. Back to you, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So much pomp and circumstance, Greg. Talk about the people who've already come out and what lies ahead over these 10 days.\par \par \uc2 PALKOT: Now, it's astounding. We have seen 1000s and 1000s in front of Buckingham Palace. The minute that word came that in fact there would be the queen had passed. People were out and they saw the notice being put out in front of Buckingham Palace yesterday and today.\par \par \uc2 Yes, they grouped around the king. The king who shook hands, one woman kissed him, that's on the cheek. That's not really called for too much in royal protocol. But certainly, there's an outpouring. There's a belief in him, succeeding this beloved queen and trying to live up to that beloved queen.\par \par \uc2 Yes, we have 10 days. We have the formal proclamation tomorrow. We have the queen's body remember still up in Scotland. There will be ceremonies there. And then, it will be brought down here, and she will be on public display - - public viewing that is for three days here, before, again, that state funeral with folks from all around the world, including, we understand, the United States. Back to you.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes, President Biden already saying he is going. Greg Palkot, live in London. Greg, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in now, the British ambassador to the U.S. Dame Karen Pierce, here in studio. Madam Ambassador, thank you for being here.\par \par \uc2 DAME KAREN PIERCE, BRITISH AMBASSADOR TO THE UNITED STATES: Thank you for having me.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You know, we've just talked about the outpouring of support there in your homeland. I want you to just touch on the outpouring of support you've seen here. You had the president, obviously, signing the condolence book at your embassy.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 PIERCE: That's right.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And you have a memorial out front of the British Embassy that is growing by the hour, really.\par \par \uc2 PIERCE: That's exactly right, Bret. We have lots of Americans, some Brits, but many Americans coming to cue at the embassy, on Massachusetts Avenue in Washington, opposite the vice president's house. Leaving flowers around the big flag that's in the ground, paying tribute.\par \par \uc2 We had a lovely card from a little girl in Maryland, wrote to the royal family, she was very sorry, they'd lost their mother, their grandmother, their great grandma. So, it goes from the human to the statesman.\par \par \uc2 Like, we've had messages, from institutions, from Congress, from a whole host of people, right the way across the administration.\par \par \uc2 But it is -- it is so good to see ordinary American people give up their day, to come and pay their respects to the queen. We thank them very much for that.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes. You know, it touches a lot of people here, obviously, it really hits home, in your home country, talk about the significance of the queen, and now this transition to \uc2 King Charles\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 PIERCE: One of the things about the queen is that she embodies the nation. And at the same time, she embodies the human touch, the personal touch -- both the human and the heroic.\par \par \uc2 And I think when you meet the queen, when you meet the king, those two things come together in your mind, and you're never quite sure, which is uppermost.\par \par \uc2 I think, although the queen, if you like sits at the top of the British hierarchy, the monarchy is woven into every fabric of British life. Whatever it is, the monarch is there. At times of national grief, at times of national commemoration, and the queen has played that role amazingly.\par \par \uc2 Every world event since 1952, she has been associated with one way or another, whether it was to uplift and encourage, whether it was to console. And most people in the U.K. and I guess here have only known a post Second World War world, in which the queen has been the queen.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: It's truly amazing. From Prime Minister Winston Churchill, to Prime Minister \uc2 Liz Truss\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 PIERCE: That's right.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: 70 years. And like Queen Elizabeth II. \uc2 King Charles\uc2  making this solemn pledge, I want to play that sound bite.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KING CHARLES III\uc2 : I, too, now, solemnly pledged myself, throughout the remaining time, God grants me, to uphold the constitutional principles at the heart of our nation. And wherever you may live in the United Kingdom, or in the Realms and territories across the world, and whatever may be your background or beliefs, I shall endeavor to serve you with loyalty, respect, and love, as I have throughout my life.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You know, as \uc2 Prince Charles\uc2  of Wales, he was politically active. Climate change, obviously very important to him.\par \par \uc2 As king, does he fall into a different category? Does he become less politically active, much like Queen Elizabeth II? Do we know?\par \par \uc2 PIERCE: I think you saw in that speech that he just gave that statesman like transition from prince and heir to sovereign. And I think we're going to see more of that. But of course, as you say, King Charles has a very deep interest in issues like climate, also in training young people.\par \par \uc2 I don't think that's going to go away. I think he will give very wise counsel to the prime minister. She will have an audience with him almost every week, if not weekly.\par \par \uc2 I did want to just pick up on one thing, in the exit you showed, there is a famous broadcast from the queen when she was Princess Elizabeth, saying to the Commonwealth peoples, I dedicate my life to your service.\par \par \uc2 And I think what you saw there was \uc2 King Charles\uc2 , picking up that very well- known theme in the Commonwealth and re-pledging it. And I think that's a very good start.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Do we know when King Charles will come here? It's usually, one of the first trips, I guess.\par \par \uc2 PIERCE: He is a very good friend of America, been here several times as Prince of Wales. The Queen herself, was a great fan of America, very much believed in the things we could do together. And I'm sure he'll want to continue that as soon as he can.\par \par \uc2 He will not be crowned king for a while that that's our system. But we will hope that we might be able to see him here before too long.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And last thing as this ceremony and continues and the funeral and then, obviously, the coronation, the U.K. is going through a tough time on a number of different fronts. And all of that is happening as this is happening.\par \par \uc2 PIERCE: Very unusual to have a new monarch and a new prime minister in the same week. I can't think of any other time in history when that might have been the case. But I think it's very good and a sign of continuity, if you like, that one of the queen's last official acts was to be able to receive the new prime minister.\par \par \uc2 And I think in what you've just heard, \uc2 King Charles\uc2  say about his duty and his pledge to the Commonwealth and the British people, I think that is designed to send a message of stability, also one of optimism, I think as ever, the fundamentals of the British economy and British society as sound.\par \par \uc2 Dame Karen Pierce, British ambassador to the U.S. We really appreciate your coming in.\par \par \uc2 PIERCE: Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 BAIER (voice-over): Wall Street snapped a thre-week losing streak today. The Dow was up 377. The Dow -- the S&P 500 gained 61. The NASDAQ jumped 250. For the week, the Dow rose 2-2/3 percentage points. The S&P 500 was up 3-2/3. The NASDAQ gained more than four.\par \par \uc2 BAIER (on camera): President Biden says he will, as you heard, attend the queen's funeral whenever it is held. The president visit Ohio today for the groundbreaking of new Intel computer chip facility.\par \par \uc2 His visit comes as voters there, considering the nominees in what is expected to be a very close race for an open Senate seat. The President has been hitting now familiar talking points on the campaign trail, but there is one issue he is not talking about.\par \par \uc2 White House correspondent Peter Doocy is here to tell us what that is. Good evening, Peter.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: Good evening, Bret.\par \par \uc2 The big advantage of the bully pulpit is you can talk about whatever you want.\par \par \uc2 President Biden chooses not to talk much about the border crisis and that is becoming a big problem for lawmakers in one of America's bluest cities.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: The survival of our planet is literally on the ballot.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (voice-over): Climate change, gun control, abortion access, all headline President Biden's platform. Immigration, not so much. He is insisted that's under control.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Unlawful migration is not acceptable, and we'll secure our borders.\par \par \uc2 It's not happening. And now, officials in cities far from the border are overwhelmed, including Washington, D.C.\par \par \uc2 BRIANNE NADEAU, MEMBER, COUNCIL OF THE DISTRICT OF COLUMBIA: In many ways, the governors of Texas and Arizona have turned us into a border town.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: For some Democrats, the problem is with governors, busing migrants to their cities.\par \par \uc2 NADEAU: We don't know how long this will take. To resolve, we don't know how long they will continue busing.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: For other Democrats, the problem is within administration that won't authorize National Guard assistance led by President Biden.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: So, is he partly to blame here then?\par \par \uc2 MURIEL BOWSER, MAYOR OF WASHINGTON, D.C.: Is the federal government?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: The president?\par \par \uc2 BOWSER: Yes. The president, the White House, and anybody else who was involved in the National Guard not being granted for the district's humanitarian response?\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: The president's priority lately is defining his opposition.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Republicans awaken a powerful force in this nation. Women. W, o -- w -- no I'm not joking.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: Biden's name isn't on any midterm ballots, but he insists that when it comes to items like lowering drug prices, he has got a lot left in the tank. I'm committed to coming back and getting it done, dammit.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (on camera): Something he won't commit to, at least, not yet, a visit to Jackson, Mississippi, where residents today are still turning on faucets and watching as opaque brown water flows out.\par \par \uc2 The press secretary has defended the president's approach. So far, saying they've already dispatched the heads of FEMA and the \uc2 EPA\uc2  to Jackson and that President Biden has phoned the Jackson mayor, he is a Democrat.\par \par \uc2 A few days ago, \uc2 Karine Jean-Pierre\uc2  said a call to Mississippi's governor. \uc2 Tate Reeves\uc2  is not necessary, he is a Republican. Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: First of all, that is disgusting. We watching that water and the fact that it's not more of an outrage that a major city cannot get water. Is there any pushback?\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: There are a lot of questions, but the answer remains the same, just that President Biden's officials are taking care of this.\par \par \uc2 Remember, though, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  was the vice president, when Flint had a similar water crisis. There was an enormous federal response and national attention on that. That was in Michigan.\par \par \uc2 Now that it's in Mississippi, we are not seeing the same thing. And it's not like there isn't time in the day, because we know that today after an official event in Ohio, President Biden went straight to Delaware, where he will remain until Sunday morning. Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We're going to stay on this in the story, and we're keep asking those questions. Peter Doocy, live in the North Lawn. Peter, thank you.\par \par \uc2 A federal judge in Florida has dismissed former President Trump's lawsuit against \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  and former top \uc2 FBI\uc2  officials. The judge rejecting Mr. Trump's claims the defendants and others acted in concert to concoct the Russia investigation that shadowed much of his administration.\par \par \uc2 The judge saying the lawsuit contained glaring structural deficiencies and that many of the characterizations of events are implausible. The Trump team says it will appeal the dismissal.\par \par \uc2 Meantime, in her first one on one interview in quite some time, Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  is denouncing the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  as an activist body.\par \par \uc2 The vice president telling NBC News, justices took away the constitutional right to abortion.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I think this is an activist court.\par \par \uc2 We had an established right for almost half a century, and this court took that constitutional right away. And we are suffering as a nation because of it.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Obviously, justices in the majority say abortion was never a constitutional right. Vice President Harris says, she has great concerns about the integrity of the Court overall going forward.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now, Fox News chief legal correspondent and the new anchor of Fox News Sunday, Shannon Bream. Good evening, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 Before we get to your big inaugural show this weekend, you have an update about the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  and the investigation into who leaked that draft opinion of the Dobbs case.\par \par \uc2 SHANNON BREAM, FOX NEWS CHANNEL ANCHOR (on camera): We know along with our fantastic Supreme Court producer, Bill Mears, we had been pushing and asking the court and any other source we can get to about details on this investigation. And we can even get a no comment at this point.\par \par \uc2 So, to hear from Justice Gorsuch, speaking at a judicial conference last night, here is what he says.\par \par \uc2 BREAM (voice-over): He says, "The chief justice appointed an internal committee to oversee the investigation. That committee has been busy, and we're looking forward to their report, I hope, soon.\par \par \uc2 So, that's really the only on the record comment we have gotten. He said, he was very worried about the pressure that was placed on justices, trying to sway votes and those kinds of things. He said, doesn't matter what sides doing it, it's very dangerous.\par \par \uc2 By the way, the chief justice is going to speak tonight, 8:00 at that same conference. And so, we'll be watching to see if he has got anything to say.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So, maybe there was a little prep to say maybe you should be address this.\par \par \uc2 BREAM (on camera): Maybe.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, let's talk about the big show. You have Fox News Sunday, the inaugural run there. Who do you have?\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, it's a busy week, as you know, so, of course, we're going to cover everything regarding the queen and her legacy. We've got the U.S. ambassador to the U.K., \uc2 Jane Hartley\uc2  is going to join us.\par \par \uc2 Martha, is of course -- MacCallum, they are on the ground for that part of the coverage. We've got Senators from both sides of the aisle. We've got Jon Tester, and we've got Tim Scott to talk about pressing issues, the MAGA rhetoric of funding the government. All kinds of other foreign policy issues as well.\par \par \uc2 And we've got Andy Pettitte, he is going to be with us because, of course, we're recognizing 9/11 all throughout the show. He was there as a \uc2 Yankees\uc2 ' pitcher during the time that the President Bush, at the time, 43, was there to throw out that first pitch at the World Series, just weeks after 9/11.\par \par \uc2 So, we want to talk about how sports can bring us together as Americans and where we are now.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Feel pretty good?\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Feel great. I'm taking notes from you. And so, we'll be ready. I'll call you up tomorrow just to pick your brain a little bit more.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You're going to do awesome. Congratulations, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I'll be watching.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: See you on Sunday.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right. Up next, Republicans try to hold on to a key seat as they attempt to regain control of the Senate in the November midterms. We have the report from Columbus, Ohio.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Investigators in Las Vegas say DNA found at the scene of murder of - - murder of reporter Jeff German is linked to Democratic politician Robert Telles. Former Clark County public administrator made his first court appearance yesterday, and was not granted bail.\par \par \uc2 Police say Telles attacked German as he came out of his home, stabbing him seven times. They say Telles blamed German for ruining his marriage and political career. Telles took to \uc2 Twitter\uc2  last spring to denounce German after reporting about bullying and favoritism at his office, and an inappropriate relationship with a female subordinate.\par \par \uc2 President Biden eased off criticism over Republicans a bit today in Ohio, even though it is the side of a very hotly contested Senate race in November.\par \par \uc2 White House correspondent Jacqui Heinrich takes a look tonight in Columbus.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: The industrial Midwest is back.\par \par \uc2 JACQUI HEINRICH, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (voice-over): Touting a legislative win ahead of the midterms, and Intel's $20 billion investment for a new semiconductor plant, no ultra-MAGA semi-fascist talk from President Biden in the state Trump won in 2016 and 2020. Instead, kind words for the state's outgoing Republican senator.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I want to thank Rob Portman for being the gentleman and decent man that he is and for showing that Democrats and Republicans can work together to get big things done for our country.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: In the crowd, GOP lawmakers who supported the CHIPS and Science Act, leery, Biden might continue down his road of late attacking their party. But even Democrats are keeping their distance from Biden.\par \par \uc2 Congresswoman \uc2 Marcy Kaptur\uc2  didn't show up.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's letting Ohio solar manufacturers be undercut by China. But \uc2 Marcy Kaptur\uc2  is fighting back, working with Republican Rob Portman.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Congressman, we are proposing to make --\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Congressman \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2 , who skipped Biden's last trip to the state did attend, after saying he's campaigning as an Independent.\par \par \uc2 What did you mean?\par \par \uc2 REP. TIM RYAN (D-OH): Really just that, you know, Independent minded, I think we're raised here in Ohio to think for ourselves.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: And you dodged a question on whether the president should run in 24?\par \par \uc2 RYAN: Well, I think, generally, you know, a new generation of leadership is needed. He said from the very beginning, he was going to be a bridge to the next generation of leaders.\par \par \uc2 J.D. VANCE (R), SENATORIAL CANDIDATE, OHIO: J.D. Vance.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Ryan's opponent, Trump-backed candidate J.D. Vance says Ryan's in a pickle.\par \par \uc2 VANCE: He has to pretend that he doesn't support \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  100 percent of the time. But he also can't alienate the far left because he needs them to fund his T.V. commercials.\par \par \uc2 They votes with him every single vote. And so now, he is saying that he -- that he -- on the day that he's appearing with \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , he is saying that he thinks that somebody else needs to run for president.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Vance, on the other hand, is welcoming help from the former president despite his legal battles.\par \par \uc2 VANCE: Very often what happens is the media spins a tale about the president that ends up being totally false. And that's why my guess about what's going to happen here --\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (on camera): In this victory lap, Biden stayed on message, saying CHIPS Act funding will make the us more competitive with China, bolster national security, revive American manufacturing, and even bring down inflation.\par \par \uc2 The president now heads to Delaware for the weekend. Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Jacqui Heinrich in Columbus. Jacqui, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Up next, Martha MacCallum live from London on the events following the death of Queen Elizabeth II.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Hearing the latest with the passing of Queen Elizabeth II. Let's bring in my colleague Martha MacCallum, host of "THE STORY" here on FOX News Channel, my co-anchor for election coverage. Martha is at Buckingham Palace tonight. Good evening, Martha. Thanks for staying up with us. I just want to get your sense of being on the ground there and what you are seeing, and what you are feeling, and the road ahead here.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA MACCALLUM, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: I think today was a big day. We saw formerly \uc2 Prince Charles\uc2 , now \uc2 King Charles III\uc2  arrive at Buckingham Palace for the very first time in his 73 years as king. And the royal standard was flying over the palace for him because he was in residence. And that had to have been a big sort of eye-opening moment for him, that after all of the years of being the king in waiting, Charles is now finally front and center, and he gets to shape this period.\par \par \uc2 I do think no matter how you look at it, Bret, Queen Elizabeth is such an icon, and Prince William has so much future ahead of him with his wife. They are now the duke and duchess and the prince and princess of Wales, huge titles that were once held by \uc2 Prince Charles\uc2 , and Diana, we should mention. So I see him as sort of a transitional figure, and that he wants to sort of fill that with the most substance and stability and continuing the monarchy as he possibly can. I think he has matured over all of these years. I think there was a time when he was sort of champing at the bit and speaking out politically in ways that usually royal highnesses do not. But he signaled today that he's going to temper that, I think, a little bit. I think that is part of what he was getting at in that speech.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: A lot of plaudits around the world for that speech. And we've played some soundbites about that and about the solemn duty that he sees ahead of him. There was one little section that raised some eyebrows, and I know it caught the attention of some royal watchers, and that's when he referred to Harry and Meghan. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KING CHARLES III\uc2 , UNITED KINGDOM: I want also to express my love for Harry and Meghan as they continue to build their lives overseas.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Obviously this comes after the laying out of all of the titles for William and Catherine.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. And I think every word in a speech like this has significance, Bret. And the fact that you're exactly right, he laid out that the Duke and Duchess of Cambridge and now Cornwall, which are Charles and Camilla's former titles, is now being elevated to the Prince of Wales, Princess of Wales, which was Diana's title. And he really put significance into those titles.\par \par \uc2 Then the very next sentence, Harry and Meghan, we love them, and we wish them well in their life overseas. He did not say the Duke and Duchess of Sussex. He didn't use their titles. I think there's no mistaking the fact that the family has moved on in terms of the future of the monarchy from this couple. And every time this couple has sort of opened their mouths and stepped forward, it has turned out to be something that's hurtful to the family.\par \par \uc2 And remember, Harry has a book coming out. And I think there's a lot of trepidation ahead of that book, which is going to be probably out this winter. I think there's a reluctance to sort of reembrace ahead of that, because he has never come out to talk without saying something that he hopes or anticipates is going to get a lot of attention.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: It's fascinating, and we'll be watching. Martha, we look forward to watching your coverage from London. Thanks for staying up for us.\par \par \uc2 Up next, we talk with former U.S. ambassador to the United Nations Nikki Haley about a number of subjects.\par \par \uc2 First, Beyond our Borders tonight. Secretary of State \uc2 Antony Blinken\uc2  says Ukraine's armed forces have made significant early gains in their counteroffensive against Russian troops in southern and eastern Ukraine. Blinken in \uc2 NATO\uc2  headquarters after a quick stop in Kyiv says fighting appears set to drag on for months. Russia is acknowledging what it's calling a substantial victory for Ukraine near the country's second largest country of Kharkiv.\par \par \uc2 North Korea says it has the right to use preemptive nuclear strikes to protect itself. State media reporting leader Kim Jong-un made a new law official today. It says the country's nuclear status is irreversible and prohibits talks on denuclearization.\par \par \uc2 And this is live look at Tbilisi, Georgia. One of the stories there tonight, archaeologists in Georgia say they have found a 1.8-million-year- old tooth belonging to an early species of human. They say the discovery cements the region as the home of one of the earliest prehistoric human settlements in Europe and possibly anywhere outside of Africa.\par \par \uc2 Just some of the other stories Beyond our Borders tonight. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , (D) PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: If they had their way, it won't matter where you live in America. Women won't have a right to choose anywhere.\par \par \uc2 Republicans have awakened a powerful force in this nation -- women.\par \par \uc2 Republicans don't have a clue about the power of women. Not a clue. Let me tell you something. They are about to find out.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: President Biden rallying the troops at a DNC event.\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in former U.S. ambassador to the United Nations, former South Carolina Governor Nikki Haley. Thanks for being here.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY, FORMER U.S. AMBASSADOR TO \uc2 U.N\uc2 .: Thanks, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: The president are hitting this hard, as are Democrats around the country. Is this issue on abortion, after the Dobbs case overturning Roe v Wade, a big, big problem for Republicans?\par \par \uc2 HALEY: We're traveling around the country. We have been for over a year now. And what I can tell you is the top issues that we are hearing about, number one, hands down, the economy. It's costing people more money at the gas pump, and that's how they get to work. It's costing them more money at the grocery store. That's how they feed their family.\par \par \uc2 The second issue is crime and the border. People want their kids to be able to ride their bikes down the street and not worry. They are worrying about what is happening at the border. And we had enough fentanyl to come across the border in July that would kill every single American. The third thing we are hearing about is education. Parents are panicked. They don't know what their kids are being taught in school. And they also know that we had a problem with education pre-COVID. It's even worse post-COVID. And then they realize that we've got foreign policy threats around the world that make them wonder if we're safe here at home.\par \par \uc2 And so those are the top issues. Abortion ends up coming fifth. And what I hear on -- in the road. But I think at the same time, we look at the fact that it's a very, very important issue, and one that unelected justices didn't need to decide. And the fact that every state is getting to decide that and the people closest to it are being able to have their voices heard, that's a good thing for America.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: here's the other talking point. It is about MAGA. It is about extremism. The president is hitting it every stop. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , (D) PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: The extreme set of MAGA Republicans has chosen to go backwards, full of anger, violence, hate, and division. And that's what their game is.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Your reaction?\par \par \uc2 HALEY: The only anger, violence, and hatred that I'm hearing is from \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . I mean, his speech the other day, he looked like he was in the depths of hell, sitting there talking about half the country is bad just because we want life to go back to normal, just because we want our dollars to count, just because we want to know our kids are safe, just because we want to know that we are not glorifying prisoners but we are actually defending police.\par \par \uc2 It's really the leftists that have gone so divided. And I'm telling you, across the country we were campaigning for Dr. Oz yesterday. We were here for \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  and \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2  here in Georgia today. We're headed to Tennessee tomorrow. Across the board, people don't have the luxury of politics. They are feeling the pain in their wallets. They're feeling n their kid's schools, and they are feeling it on the streets.\par \par \uc2 And I'm telling you that what Biden is doing is only separating us more. It's not bringing us together. And people want to love our country again. And they only want people to serve that are going to love our country. And that's why I think that we're going to have a strong November.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So you're bouncing around. You have a feeling about things. If you look at the polls, let's say for the generic congressional races, the real clear politics average has shifted now with Democrats at 44.8 percent. And you have people like Speaker Pelosi saying they are going to hold the House. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 NANCY PELOSI\uc2 , (D-CA) HOUSE SPEAKER: Let me remove all doubt in anyone's mind. Democrats intend to hold the House. In fact, we intend to grow our numbers. People vote about their kitchen table and what it means to them.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: But hearing you, you see and hear something different on the scale.\par \par \uc2 HALEY: She can dream all she wants, Bret. I'm telling you, we were with Zach Nunn in Iowa. He's a fantastic pickup sheet we're going to see. We're going to Jennifer-Ruth Green and campaign for her in Indiana. She is going to be a great pickup. We've got great candidates across the board. I think we are going to win the House comfortably.\par \par \uc2 In terms of the Senate, it's only one seat, but that's easier said than done. But I'm telling you our candidates have really stepped up. And we have got great candidates. But we all need to show up. We need to show up and vote. We need to show up and contribute. And that's why I hope everybody listening will go to StandforAmericaPAC.com and help us get these candidates elected. We've already endorsed over 50 candidates, raised them over $4.5 million that has gone directly into their campaign accounts. And when they reach Congress, I think we are going to start to see things get better.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Usually people who bounce around the country like this, supporting different candidates, run for future office. When do you make a decision whether you are going to run for president? And whether former President Trump decides or not, does that effect you?\par \par \uc2 HALEY: Well, I think that we are focused on governors' races. We've got 36 governors' races that are important. So we are helping governor candidates, House candidates, and Senate candidates win in November. If we don't win in 22, there won't be a 24. And then I don't think I have to make a decision until after that.\par \par \uc2 And look, I have said for a long time, if there is a place for me and I put my hat in, I've never lost a race. I'm not going to start now. We will put 1,000 percent in, and we'll finish it. But right now, all eyes are on November, and I think that's where we need to stay focused. And this is not the time to be, for our candidates to be arrogant. We need to be humble, we need to be disciplined, and we need to be focused on what solutions we have that can lift up all Americans.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And that decision will come whether the former president gets in or not?\par \par \uc2 HALEY: Well, I think we'll read the tea leaves after that. And I have always said if there is a place for me, we'll go. But we'll see what's going to happen.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Last thing. You mentioned foreign policy and concern. We talk a lot about terrorism, but we haven't really covered it a lot. Ayman al Zawahiri killed in Afghanistan. You are worried about terrorism worldwide.\par \par \uc2 HALEY: I'm very worried about terrorism. We are coming across the anniversary of 9/11. And that's when America woke up and realized that evil truly does exist. My concern is right now evil is at our front door. Iranians know that the easiest way to get into America is through the southern border, and we are not doing anything to make sure that we're fully protecting that border. We saw 50 plus terrorists already cross that we knew of.\par \par \uc2 You've got all these people that came in from Afghanistan, and now we are finding out they weren't vetted. We're watching Iran continue to say death to America, but Biden is falling all over himself to give them a sweetheart deal, which is lunacy. We are watching China go and continue to try to take as much data as they can and really try and push their weight around so that they can take better control. We are watching the fact that there is a war in Ukraine, and we wouldn't have a war in Ukraine with Russia had we not seen that devastating debacle in Afghanistan.\par \par \uc2 So it's a time that we have to stop being naive and we have to start understanding that when we are strong, the world is more safe. When we are weak and distracted, then our enemies come out.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Former ambassador to the United Nations, former South Carolina Governor Nikki Haley, thanks for the time.\par \par \uc2 HALEY: Thanks, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Up next, the panel on the midterms and winners and losers.\par \par \uc2 First, here is what some of our FOX affiliates around the country are covering tonight. FOX 5 in Atlanta as two suburban sheriff's deputies are killed in the line of duty. The Cobb County sheriff's office says they were shot while serving a warrant on a Marietta home last night. Two people were taken into custody after nearly four-hour standoff there.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at Springfield, Illinois, from FOX Illinois. One of the big stories there tonight, a corn maze dedicated to James Bond features four of the actors who played British spy in films. High tech corn planter trackers outfitted with GPS and special equipment made the maize. It was not aliens.\par \par \uc2 That's tonight's live look outside the beltway from SPECIAL REPORT. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , (D) PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Democrats offer a vision for a better America. It's within the reach, our reach.\par \par \uc2 REP. TIM RYAN, (D-OH) OHIO SENATE CANDIDATE: I'm going to be that guy who is able to go into those rooms and say no to the Democrats if I think they are wrong.\par \par \uc2 J.D. VANCE, (R) OHIO SENATE CANDIDATE: We have \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2  who pretends to be a moderate when he is Ohio, and then we have a crazy liberal in Washington, D.C.\par \par \uc2 RYAN: The guy is a fraud. He is just -- he was 1,000 percent against Trump and for China. And now he is against China and 1,000 percent for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 VANCE: He is not quite sure what he wants to do with \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , whether he wants to bear hug him or keep him at arm's length.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: J.D. Vance and \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2  running for Senate in Ohio. The president there today. This as the mood around the country still on the economy is still underwater for the Democrats. Americans in a Gallup poll, experience of financial hardship due to high prices, you can see severe, moderate, severe, and where these numbers stack now. And then a Marist poll, top issue when you think about voting in this November's election. Inflation still tops it, but abortion is making a climb in that, as we talked about earlier.\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in our panel, Mollie Hemingway, editor in chief of "The Federalist," Jason Riley, "Wall Street Journal" columnist and senior fellow at the Manhattan Institute, and Marie Harf, former State Department Spokesperson under President \uc2 Obama\uc2 . Marie, we start with you because you are from Ohio. That race is going to be fascinating to watch.\par \par \uc2 MARIE HARF, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: It is. Ohio politics have trended pretty much to the right in these past few election cycles. But \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2  is the best candidate Democrats could have running for the Senate. And