e is going to be to demoralize Arizonans before this vote. I'm telling everyone, don't' believe anything the media is saying. This is going to be a hot race to watch. Blake, I really appreciate you coming, good to see you in the studio.\par \par \uc2 MASTERS: You know, thank you.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: A world transgender health organization is about to grant hospitals nationwide the ability to lower the age for chest and genital surgeries to minors under the age of 18. My goodness, what are we doing? Cat Cattinson a woman who de-transitioned after living as a transgender man for 15 years, she's going to respond in moments. Stay there.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Back in June, an organization called the World Professional Association for Transgender Health circulated a draft of something known as the standards of care. Now, when they make the decision in 10 days, they are expected to lower their age recommendations for breast removal to 15 years old and genital surgery, including womb and testicular removal to 17 years. Recently de-transitioned woman named Jalisa Vine took to her \uc2 YouTube\uc2  to document how devastating her own surgery was when she was a teen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JALISA VINE, DE-TRANSITIONED WOMAN: How do I love myself again? How do I feel connected to myself again? I took an entire life that should have been mine. That was my birthright. I just should have waited. I should have waited and let myself heal. I can't have kids.\par \par \uc2 I don't want to use the word "indoctrinated," but I was young and I was sad and I was impressionable, and I thought this would fix everything.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: I cried when I watched that.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is Cat Cattinson, a singer who de-transitioned after living as a transgender man for about 15 years. Also joining me is Charlie Kirk, founder of Turning Point USA, author of "The College Scam." Can, now, as someone who sought and received that care at the age being considered now, what warning would you give to teens and their parents?\par \par \uc2 CAT CATTINSON, DE-TRANSITIONED WOMAN: I would say these surgeries are irreversible. They're dangerous. And a child -- if I can de-transition as an adult, there's no way a child should be making this decision. And absolutely, the very last thing we should be doing is lowering the age at which these body mutilating procedures can be performed.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: That Jalisa video that a friend of mine sent me was so -- I was so upset by it. I don't know her, but it was so upsetting. Charlie, I know you watched that. Your reaction. It was just -- you just want to cry for her.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE KIRK, TURNING POINT USA FOUNDER: Totally. And more broadly, you can judge a society based on how we treat our children. These guidelines, it might seem a little wonky or out there. This is very important stuff for your audience to understand. These guidelines are then used as reference points for the largest children's hospitals across the country to them be able to medically mutilate children. And this is happening in cities where those same children would not be able to vote, get a tattoo. They're not able to buy liquor, and yet they are able to go into a surgical procedure at age 15 or 16 and have irreversible damage.\par \par \uc2 This is something that adults across the country regardless of political party, we need to listen to the people that have actually suffered under this and draw the line. We don't have time to get into this right now, but there is so much profit here that the pharmaceutical companies stand to make from this. This is not about care. This is not about compassion. It's about profit off of the backs of our children. It's disgusting and it's reprehensible.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Cat, here's what Dr. Marci Bowers, the president-elect of this World Professional Association for Transgender Health. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DR. MARCI BOWERS, WPATH PRESIDENT-ELECT: This is exactly the role of puberty blockers, which are introduced prior to the onset of puberty so that gender feelings can be explored, but not in a permanent way. And this is scientific, this is tracked, and in my experience, it's extremely rare to see regret.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Cat, she calls it very rare to see regret. I'm not sure what she is referencing in the way of research, but your response?\par \par \uc2 CATTINSON: So the truth is while there is still a lack of evidence into the long-term outcomes of transitioning, but recent studies show de- transition rates going higher and higher. From some studies that came out this year, it's coming out between seven and 30 percent, which is quite a bit higher than some older studies that examined surgical regret.\par \par \uc2 And one thing I want to add is that this whole method of puberty blockers to cross sex hormones to surgery, this is not based on solid, scientific evidence. It's actually based on only one study, a Dutch study, that had just 70 participants. And actually, one person in the study tragically passed away from the complications from the genital surgery. So that's a 1.4 percent mortality rate, and yet that is the study that the WPATH is using to base these guidelines for currently treating children with gender dysphoria on.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Now, Cat, how are you doing personally after going through this? My heart goes out to all of you. How are you doing?\par \par \uc2 CATTINSON: Thank you so much. It's been the most difficult thing I've ever gone through my life. Professionally as a singer, I'd say in addition to the health side effects I experienced, the way it affected my career as a singer has been the hardest thing to deal with. My singing and speaking voice will never be the same after my time on testosterone. And it's actually painful to use my voice for more than a limited time now because of the vocal damage that I obtained from testosterone.\par \par \uc2 And so there's a growing number of de-transitioners like myself, and I've heard so many of these stories. And I just hope that by spreading awareness, we can prevent more of these tragedies from happening.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Thank you. And people say people are courageous for doing this and that. Your speaking out tonight is very courageous.\par \par \uc2 Charlie, about 20 seconds here. The Biden administration is obviously on one side of this issue. Your response?\par \par \uc2 KIRK: And not only are they one-sided. They will censor you, they will smear you, and they will slander you if you dare say what that beautiful testimony was. Parents, step up. It doesn't matter what they call you. This is the fight right now for decency and biological reality.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: How about for children just to be children for a while? Let them go through growing up and learning and changing. We all were kids once, and then you grow up and you change. Cat and Charlie, thank you.\par \par \uc2 The White House gives big pharma a big boost. What's that all about? The Last Bite, it's around, I'm told, and it will explain. Stay there.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: The White House appealed to the divine today in pushing for yet another COVID booster.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It's time to go get an updated COVID-19 shot. The good news is you can get both your flu shot and COVID shot at the same time. It's actually a good idea. I really believe this why God gave us two arms - - one for the flu shot, and the other one for the COVID shot.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: I guess it's the Biden administration's definition of a call to arms.\par \par \uc2 That's it for us tonight. "GUTFELD!" and the gang, they're next. Have fun.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document INGANG0020220907ei9600001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Biden Attacks "MAGA Republicans" Over Policies; White House Clarifies Biden Attacks On MAGA Republicans; Jean-Pierre Defends Stolen Election Tweets; Top Issues Among Registered Voters; Surge Of Female Voters; Memphis Police Find Eliza Fletcher Remains; \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  And Mehmet Oz Spar Ahead Of Midterms In Pennsylvania Senate Race; New York Mayor Sends Delegation To Texas Concerning Texas Governor's Shipments Of Illegal Immigrants To New York; Talks Over Nuclear Deal Between Iran And West Possibly Faltering; Hillary Clinton Criticizes President Trump For Not Accepting Results Of 2020 Presidential Election; Conservatives Criticize President Biden For Speech Denouncing MAGA Republicans As Threat To American Democracy\b0\par\par\uc2 Bret Baier, Peter Doocy, Mark Meredith, Aishah Hasnie, Garrett  Tenney, Jennifer Griffin, Jessica Tarlov, Ben Domenech \par \uc2 8,138 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 September 2022\par \uc2 Fox News: Special Report with Brett Baier\par \uc2 FNSPBB\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 HAROLD FORD JR., FOX NEWS CHANNEL POLITICAL CONTRIBUTOR (on camera): I'm a huge Rafa Nadal fan, had been for a long time.\par \par \uc2 DANA PERINO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST (on camera): Yes.\par \par \uc2 FORD\uc2 : But he lost a great American friend. And Tiafoe, who is -- who is doing his thing. Coco Gauff is doing her thing. The Americans are still active at the tournament.\par \par \uc2 Serena is out, but this changing of the guard could be good (INAUDIBLE), for the U.S. So, congrats.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 GREG GUTFELD, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST (on camera): This looks like -- a looks a lot like Pickleball. Is this like Pickleball?\par \par \uc2 FORD: It's kind of.\par \par \uc2 Judge, do you have a statement on that (INAUDIBLE)?\par \par \uc2 JEANINE PIRRO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST (on camera): No.\par \par \uc2 FORD: No. A little bit. A little bit. A little bit.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: No.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: But it's there's -- Judge, good to see that they're still playing tennis. That people are still playing it.\par \par \uc2 OK. Oh, wait. SPECIAL REPORT is up. Hey, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL ANCHOR (on camera): I am not a sleepy bear. I'm ready to go.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: You know, you're not.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: OK. Thanks, Greg.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: You're a special bear.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Good evening. Welcome to Washington. I'm Bret Baier. A top Republican says the Biden administration Treasury Department is covering for \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 A tragic end to the search for an abducted jogger in Tennessee. And what is holding up a revival of the Iran nuclear deal?\par \par \uc2 But "BREAKING TONIGHT", now that Labor Day has come and gone, the fall campaign season is upon us and fast.\par \par \uc2 President Biden is already in attack mode. He met with his Cabinet today, where he made critical remarks about Republican economic philosophy, after spending the holiday on the campaign trail in Wisconsin and Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 We will talk live with the Republican nominee for Senate from Pennsylvania, Dr. Mehmet Oz a little bit later.\par \par \uc2 First up tonight, White House correspondent Peter Doocy, live from the North Lawn. Good evening, Peter.\par \par \uc2 PETER DOOCY, FOX NEWS CHANNEL WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Good evening, Bret. We noticed something while out on the campaign trail with the President Biden this weekend.\par \par \uc2 When he's surrounded by midterm voters, he spends a lot of time talking about MAGA Republicans. But back here at the White House, surrounded by the Cabinet, he is trying to stick to official business.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I'm not a big fan of trickledown economics. I find is that the trickle stops fairly quickly coming down.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (voice-over): He's also not a big fan of so called MAGA Republicans.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: The extreme MAGA Republican in Congress have chosen to go backwards, full of anger, violence, hate, and division.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: He is making a point about their policies too.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: The Trumpees. These MAGA Republicans in Congress are coming for your social security as well.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: President is referring to a proposal by Senator \uc2 Rick Scott\uc2 , to address wasteful government spending by voting on every spending program every five years.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 RICK SCOTT\uc2  (R-FL): Of course, he is going to lie about it. What I believe is we got to do everything we can to preserve Medicare, preserve social security. Make sure these are lasting programs.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: White House officials want to make clear MAGA Republican doesn't cover all Trump voters.\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: He was -- has been very clear that he is talking about officeholders.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: Particularly the ones that don't accept the results of the 2020 election.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Democracy cannot survive when one side believes there are only two outcomes to an election. Either they win, or they were cheated.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: But, White House Press Secretary \uc2 Karine Jean-Pierre\uc2  has tweeted about elections, she claimed, were stolen by Republicans from \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  and \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (on camera): You tweeted Trump's stolen election, you tweeted \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2  stole an election. Is denying election results is extreme now?\par \par \uc2 JEAN-PIERRE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: Why wasn't it then?\par \par \uc2 JEAN-PIERRE: So, let's be really clear that, that comparison that you made is just ridiculous. I have been --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: (INAUDIBLE).\par \par \uc2 JEAN-PIERRE: I have been -- well, you're asking me -- you're asking me a question, let me answer it.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: Yes. And you said it was ridiculous.\par \par \uc2 JEAN-PIERRE: I was -- I was talking specifically at that time of what was happening with voting rights, and what this in danger of voting rights.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: However, two years after the Georgia gubernatorial election, she was still saying that election was stolen. And while the press secretary did say in 2018 and 2019 that Trump won the Electoral College, the only difference between her claims in 2016 that Trump lost and recent Republican claims in 2020 that Trump won claims the White House says are threatening democracy is party I.D. Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: More on this with the panel. Peter Doocy, live in the North Lawn. Peter, thank you.\par \par \uc2 There are accusations tonight from a top House Republican that the Treasury Department is providing cover for the Biden family. The allegations involve the department's refusal to turn over information regarding suspicious banking behavior tied to \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Correspondent Mark Meredith has the latest tonight.\par \par \uc2 MARK MEREDITH, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (voice-over): Republican Congressman James Comer is furious with the Treasury Department after it denied his request to review any suspicious activity reports it's collected on President Biden's son Hunter.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. JAMES COMER (R-KY): This is another cover up in the Biden administration to block Republican oversight and Republican investigations of Hunter Biden shady business dealings.\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH: The reports filed by financial institutions notify the government when they suspect someone is committing fraud or money laundering.\par \par \uc2 The Treasury Department says Congress can see the reports under certain conditions. But Republicans who are not in the majority lacked the power to force Treasury's compliance.\par \par \uc2 The department also argues, if those reports are released and then leaked, it could "Undermine potential or ongoing investigations or proceedings."\par \par \uc2 Republicans or hopeful voters will be outraged by what they say is a double standard, compared to how Democrats instigated the Trump administration.\par \par \uc2 REP. TOM EMMER (R-MN): \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  is still out there, being able to peddle influence all around the world, and they do nothing to him. Americans are off about that. They're going to show up in November, and Democrats are going to answer for it.\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH: The White House insists it's not involved in the ongoing criminal probe into the first son. But critics including Iowa Senator \uc2 Chuck Grassley\uc2 , say federal investigators are moving too slowly and have yet to provide the American people with answers.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 CHUCK GRASSLEY\uc2  (R-IA): Nothing seems to be moving. Are they an enforcement agency or aren't they? Are they using political bias or not? That's why total transparency is important.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH (on camera): Senator Grassley, along with Senator Ron Johnson of Wisconsin are vowing to investigate social media companies like \uc2 Facebook\uc2 , and how they handled stories involving \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  back during the 2020 campaign.\par \par \uc2 But this really all depends, Bret on what happens in the midterms and whether or not Republicans gain the subpoena power they say they need. Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Mark, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Well, in this SPECIAL REPORT "MIDTERM WATCH", we have 63 days, little more than two months to go until the 2022 midterm elections. Actually, only three days until voters cast their first general election ballots. Early voting starts in North Carolina on September 9th.\par \par \uc2 Now, today is Massachusetts primary, next week, Delaware. New Hampshire, Rhode Island voters head to the polls in the wrap up of primaries. But let's take a look at some of the top issues facing voters this election season.\par \par \uc2 As we look at the polls, most polls have inflation or the economy listed as number one. 27 percent of registered voters in Quinnipiac's latest poll say that's the most urgent issue facing the country.\par \par \uc2 Let's see how that can play in today's Republican matchup for Massachusetts' governor. Businessman Chris Doughty says he can help the state's economy grow.\par \par \uc2 He spoke to reporters as he voted today. He says he's a job creator. He faces former State Representative \uc2 Geoff Diehl\uc2 . Diehl has president -- former President Trump's endorsement, but does lag in fundraising.\par \par \uc2 The winner goes on to face Democrat Attorney General \uc2 Maura Healey\uc2  in November.\par \par \uc2 Now, the top searched political issues on the Internet are these: Education, inflation, taxes, crime, and abortion, in this order.\par \par \uc2 New poll show that the issue of abortion is gaining prominence. 10 percent saying that it is the most important. Many more women are registering to vote across the country. The Dobbs case in the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  overturning Roe v. Wade could affect a number of races as Democrats are now running specific ads on abortion.\par \par \uc2 Let's take a look at one of those -- one of those races is the Georgia Senate race. Republican \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  has been gaining ground in that race in recent weeks. Incumbent senator, Democrat, \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  holds just a one point lead in the RealClearPolitics Average of polls.\par \par \uc2 And Georgia, as a state, has been leaning more purple than its red state neighbors in recent elections.\par \par \uc2 According to our own Fox News poll, it says the independents who really could sway this race, 19 percent in Georgia say abortion is their top issue.\par \par \uc2 Gun violence and emigration place tied for third with nine percent. But immigration policy is important too, especially for Border States. We're watching one of those in the Arizona Senate race, where the RealClearPolitics Average shows incumbent Democrat Mark Kelly with a six point lead over Republican challenger \uc2 Blake Masters\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In their latest poll out there, Arizona Independents ranked border security the second most important issue behind inflation. But again, abortion is gaining precedent around the country.\par \par \uc2 Congressional correspondent Aishah Hasnie shows us how that subject is affecting many contests.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JAKI, VOTER, DENVER, COLORADO: Joe O'Dea opposes the law protecting abortion access in Colorado.\par \par \uc2 AISHAH HASNIE, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (voice-over): In Colorado, Democrats have Republican Senate candidate Joe O'Dea on the defense over abortion rights.\par \par \uc2 JOE O'DEA (R), SENATORIAL CANDIDATE, COLORADO: It paints me as an absolute pro-life, and that's not where I'm at.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: The left has made the Dobbs decision, which returned the abortion issue to the states the focal point of their campaigns across the country.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 SEAN PATRICK MALONEY\uc2  (D-NY): Ripping away our constitutional freedom, our reproductive freedom has awakened millions of voters.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: A \uc2 New York Times\uc2  analysis of voter registration in 10 states shows the \uc2 SCOTUS\uc2  ruling galvanized female voters. The biggest surge happened in Kansas, where more than 70 percent of new voters in the month after the decision were women. It's the same state where voters rejected a measure to strip abortion rights.\par \par \uc2 Now, Republican Senate candidates in battleground states like O'Dea are leaning on their wives to soften their image among female voters. While others are softening their positions.\par \par \uc2 In Arizona, Republican Senate candidate \uc2 Blake Masters\uc2 , whose campaign web site once described him as 100 percent pro-life, now says he does not support third trimester abortions.\par \par \uc2 EMMER: I trust our candidates to know their districts, and know how they're going to appeal to their voters.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: \uc2 Republican National Committee\uc2  chair, \uc2 Ronna McDaniel\uc2  told the A.P., the GOP needs to narrow its message on the issue. Offering this: "The past four Republican presidents since Roe believe in the exception, and that is where I think a lot of the American people are," she said. Adding, "But they also believe in limitations, and Democrats have shown no inclination to have any limitation."\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HASNIE (on camera): And former \uc2 RNC\uc2  spokesman Doug Heye, also echoing that message tonight. But he also says, Bret, that Republicans should really just stick to those pocketbook issues about high inflation, the economy.\par \par \uc2 Because according to a recent Pew survey, about 77 percent of registered voters still believe the economy is the number one issue this November. Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Aishah Hasnie, live on the Hill. Aishah, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Biden administration officials are suggesting Americans may need annual coronavirus vaccine booster shots.\par \par \uc2 Dr. \uc2 Anthony Fauci\uc2  says it would be similar to the annual flu shot. New booster shots from \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  and \uc2 Moderna\uc2  are expected to be widely available this week.\par \par \uc2 BAIER (voice-over): Stocks were off today. The Dow lost 173. The S&P 500 fell 16. The NASDAQ was off 86, its longest losing streak since 2016.\par \par \uc2 Up next, we will talk live with Pennsylvania Republican Senate nominee Mehmet Oz about his race in the November midterms.\par \par \uc2 And still ahead, the latest stumbling block toward an agreement to revive Iran's nuclear deal with the West.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Electronic cigarette maker \uc2 Juul Labs\uc2  will pay nearly $440 million to settle a two-year investigation by 33 states into the marketing of its high nicotine vaping products.\par \par \uc2 The company still faces nine separate lawsuits from other states, along with hundreds of personal actions brought on behalf of those saying they became addicted to its products.\par \par \uc2 Now, a horrible story the body of a 34-year-old Memphis school teacher abducted during a run Friday has been found. Prosecutors are now charging a 38-year-old man with the murder of Eliza Fletcher.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent Charles Watson is in Memphis tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHARLES WATSON, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): Memphis police have confirmed that the human remains found in the South Memphis neighborhood Monday are those of missing teacher and mother of two Eliza Fletcher.\par \par \uc2 Police have Cleotha Abston-Henderson, the man suspected in her kidnapping and death with first degree murder and first degree murder and perpetration of kidnapping. That in addition to especially aggravated kidnapping and evidence tampering charges.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: While the outcome of this investigation is not what we hoped for, we are nonetheless please to remove this dangerous predator off the streets of Memphis.\par \par \uc2 WATSON: His arrest came less than a day after police said they found a GMC Terrain with Abston-Henderson inside that was allegedly used in Fletcher's abduction last week while she went for a run on the \uc2 University of Memphis\uc2  campus.\par \par \uc2 According to an affidavit, Cleotha Abston-Henderson's cell phone puts him in the area around the same time. Security footage shows a male jump out of a black GMC Terrain, run aggressively toward the victim and then forced Fletcher into the passenger side of the vehicle.\par \par \uc2 Police say Abston-Henderson left behind a pair of sandals, investigators later linked it to him through DNA.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Typically, we have very little evidence to go on. But in this particular case, there's a lot of pieces of evidence.\par \par \uc2 WATSON: Fletcher's body was located in an abandoned home less than a mile away from the apartment complex where witnesses including Abston- Henderson's brother allegedly saw him cleaning the floors of his SUV.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: She's just a beautiful person and it's just awful to see the evil that's in the world.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WATSON (on camera): We're hearing from the Fletcher family, they released a statement saying in part we are devastated and heartbroken by this senseless loss. Eliza was such a joy to so many, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Charles Watson, thank you.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. PAT TOOMEY (R-PA): I'm here to issue a warning to Pennsylvania voters. You can't do the job of a U.S. Senator sitting at home firing off snarky tweets. It's a demanding job. It requires extensive time on the road, long days and nights. It involves lots of communication.\par \par \uc2 Based on what I've seen from the videos of his very few public appearances, I have my doubts about whether \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  is up to the job.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Retiring Pennsylvania Senator \uc2 Pat Toomey\uc2  speaking today alongside Pennsylvania Republican Senate nominee Mehmet Oz, who is trying to pressure his Democratic opponent Lieutenant Governor \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  into agreeing to a series of debates. So far, Fetterman is refusing. Dr. Mehmet Oz joins us tonight. Thanks for being here.\par \par \uc2 MEHMET OZ (R), SENATORIAL CANDIDATE OF PENNSYLVANIA: Good afternoon, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You know, the \uc2 Pittsburgh Post-Gazette\uc2  was even more pointed than Senator Toomey in an editorial today calling for Fetterman to debate you. In your opinion, is Lieutenant Governor Fetterman medically fit to serve as a U.S. senator?\par \par \uc2 OZ: I don't know if he's medically fit, although I'm a doctor. And I probably should have an opinion on it. But the fact is that nobody knows because no one of us have had access to his medical records.\par \par \uc2 And frankly, that's his decision, what records he wants to provide. But I think he's insulting the voters of Pennsylvania by dodging the debate. He's dodging their questions. He's dodging questions from the press.\par \par \uc2 His campaign says he's doing fine. And he said that as well. And yet, he doesn't debate. I can only assume because he wants to avoid answering for some of the far left radical comments that he has made.\par \par \uc2 But if he is sick, right, then he's been lying to us as well. So either way, this is really not about health. It's about honor and integrity and honesty.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You know, while the Post-Gazette wrote today that there are legitimate health concerns about his health, it accused you and your campaign of "pressing the issue in an adolescent manner." Fetterman recently said this about the whole campaign issue.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOHN FETTERMAN\uc2  (D), SENATORIAL CANDIDATE OF PENNSYLVANIA: As a doctor, you are going around making fun of somebody that had a stroke. And when you look around and realize that there's Pennsylvanians all across the state that have serious health crises in their own life, and I don't think anybody would want a doctor in their lives making fun of them.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I understand what you just said. But do you regret the way that your campaign has dealt with this issue?\par \par \uc2 OZ: I love \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  to raise his concerns about my campaign. I'll do the same back to him when we're on the debate stage. But he knows that I've been from day one empathetic to his condition as a physician who has dealt with stroke and heart failure, I know how challenging it can be to bounce back.\par \par \uc2 But the reality is, you haven't expressed yourself in any capacity about what these debates are going to look like. I mean, share with me what I can do to help you debate, what concessions need to be made.\par \par \uc2 I'd love to consider them, to talk them through. My goal and yours, \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  should be to educate the people of Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 But it undermines democracy, Bret, when someone's campaigning for the Senate, which is, you know, the most, you know -- of all the bodies in the world, one that deprives itself on deliberation and be able to articulate arguments back and forth. And he's unable to move forward and debating me and he doesn't even want to answer questions from the press or from the voters. And democracy is built on us asking difficult questions of each other to really figure out where the truth lies.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I want to move on --\par \par \uc2 OZ: I've been compassionate through this process.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes, I want to move on to the issues. But just to hit this, your critics say it wasn't appropriate to call out a man who just suffered a stroke for your campaign to question his eating habits. I'm just clarifying you standby with the campaign's statements?\par \par \uc2 OZ: Yes, and I would love to talk about the issues, which is what the goal has been all along. And I've been trying to emphasize that \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  wants to release one-third of all prisoners, he believes in legalizing -- decriminalizing all drugs. He's for sanctuary cities with an open border.\par \par \uc2 These run afoul of the values of most Pennsylvanians. We're suffering from the highest murder rates ever in Philadelphia, which is the area that I'm from. We've got sad fentanyl deaths that are higher than they've ever been as well, because you've got an open border. And of course, cartels are profiting from human trafficking. And then now they're bringing drugs across the border.\par \par \uc2 These are all realities of the positions that \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  has tweeted about and taking strong positions on. Answer the public about what you would do. Do you regret having taken the stances? What would you do differently if you're a United States Senator?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So, you do call him a radical oftentimes on the campaign trail, you essentially link him to \uc2 Bernie Sanders\uc2  or \uc2 Alexandria Ocasio-Cortez\uc2 . And yet, if you look at the recent polls, a RealClear Average of polls, any recent poll, you're trailing by anywhere between six and 10 points. Why is that?\par \par \uc2 OZ: Well, the polls that I've seen recently are dramatic closure of that gap. And I think much of the reason for that is the Republican Party is recovering from a fructus (PH) debate.\par \par \uc2 But it doesn't frankly matter what public polls say over the summer, we're now under the final stretch, two months to go. I'm very clear on what I stand for, what I would do if I was a senator representing this beautiful commonwealth.\par \par \uc2 We don't know what \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  would do as a senator. He has not been out campaigning, but I've done 180 events. 180, he's done, you know, less than half a dozen. He's spoken for 20 minutes. He's taken no questions.\par \par \uc2 What kind of a campaign doesn't show up for its people? And this is what \uc2 Pat Toomey\uc2  was arguing today. If you're not going to show up on the campaign trail, why would we think you'd show up to actually fight for us in the Senate, and you have to be able to take some positions and defend them. But he's not doing that. So, it begins to undermine his credibility with the voters in Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We should point out, we asked Lieutenant Governor Fetterman to come on SPECIAL REPORT. We haven't received an answer. We tried numerous times. He was campaigning with President Biden yesterday in Pennsylvania among the attacks he leveled, here's just a clip.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 FETTERMAN: How many homes does Dr. Oz have? Nine, 10, 11? But I guarantee you he doesn't have a steel mill right across from one of his mansions. You know, but you're going to have one senator that lives across the street from a steel mill. Because let's tell us something -- let's say something, in the -- in the Mind (PH) Valley, we make steel. We make steel. In New Jersey -- in New Jersey, Dr. Oz makes crudite.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So, your homes, New Jersey, crudite, they have been the attack lines, common attack lines, your response.\par \par \uc2 OZ: How does it improve the lives of Pennsylvanians who are suffering with the highest murder rates in Philadelphia, high fentanyl death rates across much of rural Pennsylvania suffering from an economy that's not working for them? He speaks of steel mills, \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  says fracking is a stain on Pennsylvania and he wants to regulate fracking.\par \par \uc2 In fact, he said he wanted a moratorium on. He signed a document to that effect. How is that going to help the steel mills in Pennsylvania?\par \par \uc2 This is why everyone is in a panic about \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  coming into office. It will be a catastrophe for the commonwealth of Pennsylvania and I think for the nation as well. He's way out of bounds, far left than so many of his positions.\par \par \uc2 And just notice as he was giving his little speech there, that's all he talks about. It's personal attacks, but no real foundation to explain why he would be able to help us through our problems.\par \par \uc2 I've been very upfront about what I would do to fix the problems. And I've asked and pointed questions on his policies, his ideology, what he thinks is going on in the world and how we would fix it.\par \par \uc2 He doesn't seem able to argue at that level. And it's very concerning if you want to be a U.S. senator, because it's very different to tweet out snarky comments, as \uc2 Pat Toomey\uc2  said, than it is to actually serve in the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2 . It's a higher bar.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: But be honest, I mean, looking back at that video from the grocery store, do you wish you went to the meat aisle and picked up a steak or called it a vegetable platter?\par \par \uc2 OZ: You know, I'm proud of many of the things that I do. Campaigns are about border skirmishes continually. You work your tail off, you try to serve the voters, and you try to help them on issues that pertain to them. And the issues that I'm proudly talking about Bret have to do with the issues, the kitchen table issues that all of Pennsylvania is struggling with. That's why this race is so important. The whole nation, everyone is having the same issues.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Here's an issue they're hitting hard and that's abortion. Abortion is legal in Pennsylvania, should it be?\par \par \uc2 OZ: As a senator, I definitely do not want the federal government involved in state decisions. I have found as a position that local health care works best. I trust local democracy and the ability of voters to impact on people in Harrisburg, much more than they can control what's going on in Washington. I'm pro-life. I have three exceptions, life of the mother, for sure, and rape and incest. And I think my values align with a lot of the values in Pennsylvania. But as a U.S. Senator, I will leave those decisions to the states.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Asked a couple of people to tweet in asking for questions. Jan tweeted in, do you believe the 2020 election is stolen? The second part of this I think is me -- to me. But you won't ask a hard question like that. So, here's the first question. Do you believe the 2020 election is stolen?\par \par \uc2 OZ: I've been asked that question many times. As a doctor, I'm very precise with the words I use. There's lots more information (AUDIO GAP) gathering some I think would improve the process in general. And you did ask the question. So, contrary to what people think.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: But you believe that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is the legitimate president?\par \par \uc2 OZ: I do believe Biden is the president. He's in the White House. Now, I think most people can tell he's the president. If you look at the inflation rate at a 40-year high and, you know, fairly lackluster ability to get the economy back on its feet again, and now potentially putting us in recession. It's pretty clear that he's president.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: OK. Are you a MAGA Republican?\par \par \uc2 OZ: I support what President Trump has argued while I was in the White House that we can actually make America a great company if we put our country first, if we're tough on trade. If we do the kinds of things that were done during that administration.\par \par \uc2 I think a lot of Americans that they look objectively at how the country was doing while President Trump was in office would have to argue and could convincingly argue that it was in better shape than we are now.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, couple more quick things. A lot of people asking why you want to be a senator. Jon tweets, do you plan on donating your Senate salary if you win?\par \par \uc2 OZ: I've already been on record that I would donate my extended salary if I win, it would be a great honor to give that back to the country.\par \par \uc2 Believe me, running for Senate is you do it because you are passionate about making this country better. And my father was an immigrant to this country, grew up on a (INAUDIBLE). And we treasured the American dream. And I am the American dream.\par \par \uc2 You know, I grew up in humble means and have done very well. And I'm very proud that America made that possible.\par \par \uc2 And that was the deal my father and I and my whole family believed was the case, if you work hard, you serve your fellow American and you will do great and America will do great.\par \par \uc2 And I do have concerns that some members of our leadership don't believe that quite as vehemently especially in the far left part of the Democratic Party with \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  lives. And I want to push back on that. I do believe the American dream is alive and I think we should nurture it.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, last one very quickly. Will you support \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  for leader if you're elected?\par \par \uc2 OZ: I look forward to being in the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2  and having to make that decision for the next leader of the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2 . It will be a Republican.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: But you can't make it tonight?\par \par \uc2 OZ: Not going to make it tonight.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Dr. Mehmet oz, we appreciate the time.\par \par \uc2 OZ: Thanks, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Up next, New York's mayor sends a delegates to Texas following the frustration over the governor's shipments of illegal immigrants to his city.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Breaking tonight, a FOX News exclusive. New York's mayor has sent a delegation to Texas following frustration over the Texas governor's shipments of illegal immigrants to New York. Correspondent Garrett Tenney is in Eagle Pass with this exclusive tonight. Good evening, Garrett.\par \par \uc2 GARRETT TENNEY, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Bret, good evening to you. This visit is significant because it is the first time officials from a sanctuary city have come to the border to see this crisis firsthand. New York City Mayor Eric Adams' office is calling it a fact-finding mission to hear directly from folks on the southern border.\par \par \uc2 Our cameras were the only ones here as the delegation met with Border Patrol officials on the banks of the Rio Grande River where hundreds of migrants cross on a daily basis. A source familiar with the trip tells us the delegation also got a very small taste of the overwhelming numbers of people flooding into the state during a tour of a recently built CBP processing facility in Eagle Pass that was packed with migrants waiting to be processed. Members of the delegation wouldn't speak to us, but Mayor Adams' press secretary tells FOX News New York City will continue to welcome asylum seekers with open arms.\par \par \uc2 Adams has been fiercely critical of Texas Governor Greg Abbott for busing more than 2,100 migrants to the big apple in the last month, and tonight, Abbott's office is firing right back, saying "Governor Abbott's invitation is still open for Mayor Adams to come visit our southern border and see firsthand the devastating impacts of the Biden-made crisis on border towns smaller than a New York City borough."\par \par \uc2 It's important to note, though, that while Mayor Adams himself didn't come to the border, if Texas hadn't bused migrants up north this delegation likely never would have come here to be able to see this crisis for themselves, which is what Texas officials have been wanting Washington to do all along. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Garrett Tenney live along the border. Garrett, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Up next, we'll tell you about the latest hold up in striking a deal to revive Iran's nuclear agreement with the west.\par \par \uc2 First, here is what some of our FOX affiliates around the country are covering tonight. FOX 11 in Los Angeles with the first day of a new outdoor watering ban in portions of the county. A 36-mile pipeline delivering water from the Colorado River shut down for emergency repairs. That ban is now in effect for 15 days.\par \par \uc2 Also, this evening, consumers are being advised to be ready for potential rotating power outages in California tonight amid the hottest weather of the historic heatwave.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at New York. One of the big stories there from FOX 5, our affiliate, the start of Broadway week. Many shows are offering two- for-one tickets through September 25th. Broadway week is a twice-yearly event that has been going on since 2011. And after COVID, it's good to be back on Broadway.\par \par \uc2 That's tonight's live look outside the Beltway from SPECIAL REPORT. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: The \uc2 U.N\uc2 . atomic watchdog agency is urging Russia and Ukraine to establish a safety and security protection zone around Europe's largest nuclear plant in Ukraine. Fears persist that ongoing fighting could trigger a catastrophe in that region. Shelling continued today around the facility a day after it was again knocked off Ukraine's electrical grid.\par \par \uc2 The political pendulum seems to be swinging against the long rumored settlement to revive the Iran nuclear deal with the west. The \uc2 European Union\uc2 's top diplomat says he is less confident now than a day earlier about closing the deal. National security correspondent Jennifer Griffin tells us where things stand right now.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER GRIFFIN, FOX NEWS NATIONAL SECURITY CORRESPONDENT: The U.S. flew two nuclear capable B-52 bombers over the Middle East as tensions with Iran remain high and negotiations over Iran's nuclear program falter.\par \par \uc2 VEDANT PATEL, STATE DEPARTMENT PRINCIPAL DEPUTY SPOKESMAN: Iran's response did not put us in a position to close the deal.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: The bombers, at one point escorted by Israeli F-16 jet fighters, took off from England as the top \uc2 U.S. Air Force\uc2  general in the Middle East warned threats to the U.S. and our partners will not go unanswered. The head of Israeli intelligence, or \uc2 Mossad\uc2 , made a whirlwind trip to Washington, D.C., to try to halt the deal as Israel's prime minister visited the Nevatim Airbase to receive a security briefing.\par \par \uc2 YAIR LAPID, ISRAELI PRIME MINISTER (through translator): It is still too early to know if we succeeded in stopping the nuclear agreement.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: The \uc2 European Union\uc2 's top diplomat expressed pessimism about the deal.\par \par \uc2 JOSEP BORRELL\uc2 , E.U. FOREIGN POLICY CHIEF: I'm sorry to say that I am less confident today than 28 hours before.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: Adding to the tension, just days after U.S. military helicopters intercepted Iran's Revolutionary Guard Corps towing a stolen U.S. sail drone, Iran's navy seized two American drones taking unclassified photos in the Red Sea Friday. As a U.S. warship approached the Iranians tossed the U.S. drones overboard.\par \par \uc2 BRIG. GEN. PATRICK RYDER, PENTAGON PRESS SECRETARY: I can confirm that the cameras were missing. These are unclassified, unsensitive, off the shelf technology.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: The Fars News Agency published an interview with a known mouthpiece of Iran's supreme leader who criticized the nuclear talks as a western plot and recommended the regime quit the treaty.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN (on camera): The biggest sticking point to renewing the deal, Iran wants an end to an \uc2 IAEA\uc2  investigation into the uranium traces found at three undeclared nuclear sites. The U.S. thus far has balked, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Jen, back to Ukraine. The U.S. intelligence agency says Russia is really having to resort to some unlikely sources for weapons in the war with Ukraine there.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: That's right, Bret. Today, the Pentagon's new press secretary confirmed newly declassified intelligence suggests Russia has turned to North Korea to supply it with rockets and millions of shells, a sign that sanctions and export controls are hitting Russian supply lines in Ukraine finally. Russia turned to another axis of evil member to source drones for Ukraine recently. The Pentagon says the first batch of Iranian made drones are having mechanical problems.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: It's like the Legion of Doom or something. All right, Jen, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the panel on election politics, the push towards the November midterms, and my interview with Pennsylvania Senate nominee Mehmet Oz.\par \par \uc2 First, Beyond our Borders tonight. Russian President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  attends war games involving troops from China and other nations. It is considered a show of force amid Moscow's tensions with the west. The weeklong exercise in Russia's far east is intended to showcase growing defense ties between Russia and China.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at London. One of the big stories there, probably the biggest story there tonight, \uc2 Liz Truss\uc2  takes over as British prime minister amid an energy crisis in the U.K. Media reports say she plans to cap energy bills at a cost to taxpayers of as much as $116 billion. Truss is also tasked with curbing soaring prices, easing labor unrest, and fixing the health case the system. There seen with the queen.\par \par \uc2 Just some of the other stories Beyond our Borders tonight. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HILLARY CLINTON\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: Was I happy when I beat \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  by nearly 3 million votes but lost the Electoral College? No, I was not happy. Did I even for a nanosecond think I'm going to claim victory and try to get the Democrats to refuse to certify the election? No.\par \par \uc2 I believe he knows he's an illegitimate president. He knows. He knows that there were a bunch of different reasons why the election turned out the way it did.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: OK, \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  on CBS Evening News and then Hillary Clinton September 29th, 2019, looking back. This as "The Wall Street Journal" puts the race this way, "\uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  holds a Trump rally. His strategy is to out- Trump Trump bi polarizing the electorate around the former president because he thinks a majority will come his way. His democracy line is a political gambit. He has to swear most Republicans as would be fascists to make swing voters believe none of them can be trusted with power."\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in our panel, "\uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " columnist Marc Thiessen, "THE FIVE" co-host Jessica Tarlov, and Ben Domenech, editor at large of "The Spectator," host of "The Ben Domenech Podcast" on FOX News Radio. Jessica, we'll start to you. Welcome to SPECIAL REPORT.\par \par \uc2 JESSICA TARLOV, CO-HOST, "THE FIVE": Thank you.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Usually we see you the hour before.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: But what do you make ever "The Wall Street Journal" write up there and the president's clear strategy here that on different campaign rallies he's definitely going down the MAGA, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  road?\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: He is definitely being very clear and focused on who the enemy is in all of this, and it is \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and the people who continue to spout his election lies, who try to thwart democracy. And I thought that he was very clear about that in his speech on Thursday evening. He even led by saying I'm not talking about the conservative party in general. I'm talking about a very specific subset here.\par \par \uc2 And this is what Democrats have been wanting President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  to do, to go on offense. It has long been a problem within our party, something that we have talked about, that we feel that we are always the ones that, when they go low, we go high, as Michelle Obama said it. And at this particular moment, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  knows that independent voters are up for grabs, and that's how we are going to win the midterms, and that talking about the clear and present danger that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and the people who believe in the same things as he believes in pose is the way to win the election.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Well, there was an exchange between our own Peter Doocy and the White House press secretary today about election denial in the past. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , (D) PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Can't be a democracy when you support violence when you don't like the outcome of an election.\par \par \uc2 PETER DOOCY, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: So, if we are all in agreement that it is incorrect to say the 2020 election was stolen, what about 2016 election?\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE DEPUTY PRESS SECRETARY: Look, I'm not going to go back to where we were or what happened in 2016. We're going to focus on the here and now. We are going to focus on what is happening today.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: You tweeted Trump stole and election. You tweeted \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2  stole an election. If denying election results is extreme now, why wasn't it then?\par \par \uc2 JEAN-PIERRE: So let's be really clear, that comparison that you made is just ridiculous.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Ben, your take?\par \par \uc2 BEN DOMENECH, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Well, \uc2 Karine Jean-Pierre\uc2  doesn't have a leg to stand on on this, and it's astounding that you didn't play it, but her response saying that she anticipated this question for quite some time. The fact that she would not have an answer for it is very telling.\par \par \uc2 Look, I think that Jessica and her party may very well believe that it is to their advantage to describe millions of Americans as being the enemies of the American government. But I do not believe that that is actually a path to victory. In fact, I believe it is a course that is only being undertaken because the record of this president is so weak, it is so terrible, it is so unquestionably bad when it comes to economic issues, the border, when it documents foreign policy and every other real significant manner, that they are engaged in a project right now that I think is actually the opposite of everything that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  ran on.\par \par \uc2 Everything that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  ran on was coming together, bringing back normalcy to American governments, normalcy to the presidency. And, in fact, as he has governed, we've seen he has done the exact opposite in ways that have rifted this country apart and will continue to wreck our ability to work together around partisan lines and across all of the different divides that we have here as a nation.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Real Clear Politics average of the president's approval is still under water, Marc, at 42.1 percent, disprove 54.8. But interestingly, the generic congressional ballot is now tied, and Republicans and Democrats essentially just tied. This is the beginning of the push to the midterms. This is when everybody starts paying attention. This is when the ads happen. This is when the get out the vote starts to happen. This is game time.\par \par \uc2 MARC THIESSEN, COLUMNIST, "WASHINGTON POST": It is indeed. And I second everything that brother Ben just said. And I would add one other thing, which is the hypocrisy of the Democrats' approach right now. If \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  thinks that MAGA Republicans are a threat of the very foundations of our republic, why is the Democratic Party he leads spending tens of millions of dollars across the country trying to get MAGA Republicans elected, nominated in GOP primaries? They did it -- they are doing it right now in New Hampshire. They did it in Colorado. They did in Michigan. They are doing it in California. They are doing it across the country. They are spending tens of millions of dollars trying to make sure that the most extreme people, the people denied election results, who called \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  a traitor, are the ones who are the nominees because they think they are going to be easier to beat. If these people are such a threat to the country, why are they essentially giving them tens of millions of dollars in campaign contributions? It's total hypocrisy.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Jessica, I'll have you respond to that. But what did you make of the Mehmet Oz interview and his answers?\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: So, first the strategy that the DCCC and DNC has employed, I'm not supportive of it. I don't think that it's useful. I think it sends a bad message, and also something could go really wrong, and you could end up with \uc2 Doug Mastriano\uc2  as the governor of Pennsylvania, for instance. So not supportive.\par \par \uc2 In terms of the Dr. Oz interview, I thought that he dodged your questions pretty effectively, because you certainly went at him with issues that are important to voters. Everyone is talking about what is going on with \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2 's health at this particular moment, and you gave him a nice out, which he chose not to take in terms of taking that back and the crudites mess. But I think that \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  should be able to hold on there. I'm interested in what is going on in Georgia where I do see that gap really closing.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We'll see if he comes on, Fetterman, or does a debate or a town hall. Panel, thanks so much.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Thanks, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, it is time for "Tuesday Tweets." First from Sabrina, "Are you and others going to have town halls for candidates before the elections?" The answer to that is yes. We are working on a number of them around the country in negotiations with a whole bunch of folks, maybe even a couple of debates. We'll see.\par \par \uc2 New we have Jack Bowers who asks, "What happened to the Durham investigation? Are we getting close to the statute of limitations for any possible crimes that occurred in early Trump administration?" There is a trial next month. That's the next step. It is still going on. No sense that it is stopping any time soon. We'll cover everything, but so far no word.\par \par \uc2 Up next, Kiki Goodwin wants to know, "Can you work in more past clips of \uc2 Charles Krauthammer\uc2  on some of today's topics? He has such wisdom." The answer is yes, we always do. And you will be surprised how many times it all fits in. He could cut through the noise day to day.\par \par \uc2 And lastly, Nicholas asks, "Do you think the 2024 general election debates are going to happen?" I actually do. I think they will. We'll see.\par \par \uc2 Tomorrow on SPECIAL REPORT, our common ground segment features Democratic Senator \uc2 Amy Klobuchar\uc2  of Minnesota, Republican Senator Rob Portman of Ohio on set.\par \par \uc2 Thanks for inviting us into your home tonight. That's it for the SPECIAL REPORT, fair, balanced, and still unafraid. Here is Jesse.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FNSPBB0020220907ei9600001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Return to Office?; \uc2 Liz Truss\uc2  Becomes New British Prime Minister; Special Master Granted in Mar-a-Lago Case; Midterm Money and Messaging\b0\par\par\uc2 Abby Hornacek, Charlie Gasparino, Gerry Baker, William La  Jeunesse, Karl Rove, Andy McCarthy, David Spunt, Brian Brenberg, Peter  Doocy, Aishah Hasnie, Neil Cavuto \par \uc2 8,568 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 September 2022\par \uc2 Fox News: Your World w/ Neil Cavuto\par \uc2 CAVU\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NEIL CAVUTO, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Nine weeks out, and the push is on, as Democrats and Republicans battle for votes.\par \par \uc2 Today, a look at the money and the messaging with Peter Doocy at the White House, where President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is touting more spending, Aishah Hasnie on Capitol Hill, where both sides are just scrambling, GOP pollster Lee Carter on the bread-and-butter issues that are resonating, and our Professor Brian Brenberg on four key days between now and November that could change everything.\par \par \uc2 Welcome, everybody. Hope you had a good summer weekend, the last blast, and now the blasts forward to Election Day nine weeks away.\par \par \uc2 Let's go to Peter Doocy at the White House -- Peter.\par \par \uc2 PETER DOOCY, FOX NEWS WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Neil, good afternoon.\par \par \uc2 We spent some time over the long weekend with President Biden out on the campaign trail as he tried to make the case that he thinks Republicans like \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  more than they like entitlement programs.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: The biggest contrast from what MAGA Republicans, the extreme right, the Trumpees, they want to go to Congress, these MAGA Republicans in Congress are coming for your Social Security as well.\par \par \uc2 Now, you -- by the way, as I said, you might think I'm making this -- some of this stuff up, it's so outrageous.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: He's referring there to a plan by Republican Senator \uc2 Rick Scott\uc2  to make Congress vote to reauthorize every spending program every five years in an attempt to cut down government waste.\par \par \uc2 So, now Scott is challenging the premise of the president's claims there.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 RICK SCOTT\uc2  (R-FL): Of course, he's going to lie about it. What I believe is we got to do everything we can to preserve Medicare, preserve Social Security, make sure these are lasting programs.\par \par \uc2 That's why I believe Congress every year ought to tell the American public what they're going to do to make sure these programs survive. But the Democrats, they won't have any conversation.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: For the last five days, the president's focus has been on MAGA Republicans, but, today, with the whole Cabinet gathered together at the White House, he tried to swing things back towards the economy.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: And so this economy that works for the country from the bottom up and the middle out, as I have said from the beginning. I'm not a big fan of trickle-down economics.\par \par \uc2 I find the trickle stops fairly quickly coming down.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: And one way to tell that we are for sure in the homestretch of the midterm campaign, White House officials are doing something that they usually do when they're trying to get their base really, really excited.\par \par \uc2 They're going to host an event here tomorrow with Barack Obama -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Bring out the big guns, right?\par \par \uc2 All right, Peter, thank you very much for that, Peter Doocy.\par \par \uc2 Want to go to Aishah Hasnie right now on Capitol Hill, where Republicans are trying to get back on message about inflation, the economy. Sometimes, they have veered off course. How's that going?\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 AISHAH HASNIE, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hey there. Good afternoon to you, Neil.\par \par \uc2 That's right. So Republicans primarily are still focused on those pocketbook issues, sky-high inflation, and, of course, the economy, high gas prices. And according to former \uc2 RNC\uc2  spokesman Doug Heye, he tells me that Republicans should stick to those pocketbook issues, because a new Pew poll out Neil show 77 percent of registered voters think the economy is still the top voting issue this fall.\par \par \uc2 Now, it's clear though, abortion is energizing Democratic voters, especially women, Neil. A \uc2 New York Times\uc2  analysis of voter registration in 10. States shows that \uc2 SCOTUS\uc2  ruling, it really galvanized female voters, the biggest surge happening in Kansas, where more than 70 percent of the new voters there in the month after the Dobbs decision were women.\par \par \uc2 Here's DCCC Chair \uc2 Sean Patrick Maloney\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 SEAN PATRICK MALONEY\uc2  (D-NY): Ripping away our constitutional freedom, our reproductive freedom has awakened millions of voters. And we see that in the astonishing comeback in the special elections in New York and elsewhere.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: The GOP, though, has not had a firm position abortion as of late, so now we're starting to see Republican Senate candidates in battleground states really lean on their wives to soften their image, especially among female voters, while House Republicans are offering a different strategy altogether.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. JIM JORDAN (R-OH): We will lean into it. We're the pro-life party. We're going to protect the sanctity of human life. This was a huge win for the precious nature of human life, and I don't think we should shy away from it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: Now, \uc2 Republican National Committee\uc2  Chair \uc2 Ronna McDaniel\uc2  told the AP the GOP still needs to narrow its message on abortion, offering that the past four Republican presidents believe in exceptions, but also limitation, accusing Democrats of not having limitations on abortion at all -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Thank you very much, Aishah Hasnie on Capitol Hill.\par \par \uc2 So, if you think that Democrats have a problem getting the word about inflation out and trying to say they're on top of it, Republicans aren't getting high marks on it either. So it's sort of like a jump ball.\par \par \uc2 Lee Carter following all of that.\par \par \uc2 Lee, what does it mean when the electorate just seems to have a pox on both parties for the time being?\par \par \uc2 LEE CARTER, REPUBLICAN POLLSTER: Well, I think the problem is, most Americans don't feel like the government is out to fight for them. I think they're about -- everybody feels like the government is fighting against each other, and nothing's getting accomplished.\par \par \uc2 So you have got this huge group of people that are feeling like both parties have let them down. The Republicans' messaging has really primarily been about how bad things are with inflation. And the problem there is most people already know. What they want to hear is a solution how they're going to make it better.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the Democrats are trying to say that they're making it -- things are going to be OK, except that people don't feel that it's OK. So they feel like they're completely out of touch. What the Democrats do have is, they have a message on abortion. And I think it's really important that the Republicans take this seriously, because 65 percent of women are saying that abortion is the number one issue to them.\par \par \uc2 And that's a really big deal, because it was suburban women who really put \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  in the White House. And that's what could happen in the midterms. And I think what the Republicans need to understand is that this is not about women fighting for abortion. This is about women fighting for freedom.\par \par \uc2 Most women say that this is about them feeling like they have equal rights, protections and freedoms. In many ways, this reminds me of the mask argument, where the Democrats are saying this is all about masks and people not taking care of each other and all of this, when really what a lot of Republicans are saying, this is about fundamental rights that I should make the own -- my own decisions for myself.\par \par \uc2 That's what's happening among women right now. And the Republicans, I think, are missing the mark on how to really bring women in, saying, we're going to be the party of freedom.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes, I get all of that, Lee, but women also are familiar with these wallet and pocketbook issues. They see it head on, and they're aware that, while gasoline prices have come down, almost everything else has not.\par \par \uc2 They see it everyday at the grocery store. They see it every day they go out. And I'm wondering how Republicans play that and how they counter that.\par \par \uc2 CARTER: Well, I think there's no doubt about it that the number one issue facing Americans is the economy. Nine out of 10 Americans right now feel worse today than they did just three months ago.\par \par \uc2 And some falling gas prices isn't going to change that. You're absolutely correct. But I think what Republicans need to do in this moment is not just talk about how bad it is. Talk about how good it could be if they were to take over, because I think the Republicans are really at risk of not winning the Senate.\par \par \uc2 So, if I were to advise the Republicans right now, I would say, look, you need to come up with a three- or a five-point plan on what it is that you're going to do immediately should you win the midterms that is going to make the economy better, that's going to put relief back in the pockets of American people, because all of the policies that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has put forward and has been -- has claimed that they're going to make inflation better, two-thirds of Americans say, I don't think they're going to help me.\par \par \uc2 If the Republicans are the party that are going to help us get financial relief, they're going to be the party that wins the midterms.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Fascinating.\par \par \uc2 Lee, thank you very much good. Catching up with you.\par \par \uc2 CARTER: Thanks, Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Lee Carter following that, as only she can.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, an alert here on the markets. We are starting a fourth week on the downside for the major averages, the Dow swinging a little bit more than 173 points. I think, by now, you know the drill, fear that the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  keeps hiking interest rates, in another couple of weeks, when they meet again, another three-quarters-of-a-point.\par \par \uc2 They don't see it ending. We got a separate report today that showed a little bit of strength in the services sector, which would normally be greeted as the good news it is. But now to a market that's worried about still higher interest rates, it was deemed anything but, that we're going to continue to see those rate hikes and we're going to continue to see frustration about all of that.\par \par \uc2 Brian Brenberg follows all of this very, very closely, the King's College professor in New York City, FOX News contributor, darn good instructor on all things numbers.\par \par \uc2 So, Brian, first off, on the markets, I know there are a lot of key things you're watching, including four key pieces of economic data that you're keeping an eye on.\par \par \uc2 BRIAN BRENBERG, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But the markets are a backdrop of concern, I would imagine, to either party, because we're melting away here. Many people argue that the run-up we had was just a bear market rally, and we're back to just the bear market.\par \par \uc2 I don't know where you stand on it, but that is not a great backdrop. What do you think?\par \par \uc2 BRENBERG: Well, it's not a great backdrop, and especially if you're the party in power, because what it says is, investors are nervous about the Fed raising rates. Why? That slows the economy down.\par \par \uc2 I mean, the whole story right now, Neil, is, how high and how long does inflation stay high, and then how bad does the recession potentially get? And right now, on both of those fronts, there's a lot of uncertainty. I'm watching prices. I'm watching all these inflation reports that are coming up, but the truth is, the prices that matter are already out there. They're already on the store shelves.\par \par \uc2 They're in the rent check. Those prices aren't going to get better come November. So that story is done. That die is cast. I think the Biden administration knows that, which is why they're trying to change the story. They're trying to change the conversation.\par \par \uc2 It's not a coincidence we have seen all these -- all this heated rhetoric, semi-fascism coming up, just a few weeks before the election.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 BRENBERG: That's a move you make when you feel desperate about the bread- and-butter issue. And, right now, the bread-and-butter issue is still inflation. I agree with Lee on that.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 When you start using the fascist term, or even say half-fascist, as if that sort of takes the sting out of it, you have lost the argument.\par \par \uc2 We were just showing there, Professor, the fact that wages have been going up at a better than 5 percent clip, which is great, right, but of course, inflation running up north of an 8.6 percent clip. So the fact of the matter is, we're not keeping pace with that.\par \par \uc2 Americans know that. They see it every day at the grocery store.\par \par \uc2 BRENBERG: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yet it's an issue that stands only to get more problematic, the more data we get. You hearken to some inflation reports that are due out, but, before Election Day, we're going to several of them, a couple of more reads on the overall job picture in the United States, and, in the middle of that, the Fed hiking rates a lot and endlessly, it seems.\par \par \uc2 How does that play out?\par \par \uc2 BRENBERG: Yes, I mean, think about that as a voter.\par \par \uc2 So here you are. You know your wages aren't keeping up with prices. That's not going to change anytime soon. Prices really aren't moderating, especially the ones people care about. And, lookit, gas prices have come down, yes. They're still really, really high.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BRENBERG: So that's the preexisting problems.\par \par \uc2 Now you're looking at interest rate hikes, Neil. I mean, you think about borrowing to buy something, and you're going to see potentially two big rate hikes from the Fed before the election, that really starts to get people nervous, because now it's not just expensive to go grocery shopping, but it's getting more expensive for those big-ticket items that you might have deferred on.\par \par \uc2 That is not a good backdrop for Democrats. And I just don't see them being able to turn that tide in the next two months, which means they got to do what they are doing. They got to find something else to talk about. I just don't think Americans have the luxury of doing that. Frankly, they have got to pay for this stuff day in and day out. And so I think that does really matter when you get to the ballot box.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You know, another thing too, if you think about it, Professor -- and I don't know how your students react, but they obviously are aware of what's going on, and they see the numbers. They see it in the cost themselves to try to pay for things or get more from their parents trying to pay for things, including their own education.\par \par \uc2 I'm just wondering how they react to all of this. They're too young to remember. They weren't even born around the last inflationary cycle that I can remember quite well in the '70s. So they missed all of that, as well as leisure suits.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So, the fact that matter is, they did benefit there.\par \par \uc2 But I'm just wondering how they are responding to this, when you bring it up in class, when they bring it up with you?\par \par \uc2 BRENBERG: Well, it hits them hard.\par \par \uc2 I mean, college students don't have a lot of money. My students are living in New York City. So they get the double whammy of higher prices and New York prices.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Good point.\par \par \uc2 BRENBERG: But what they -- what they want is some honesty. What they want is somebody to explain what's actually happening.\par \par \uc2 They don't want to hear slogans. They don't want to keep hearing from the bottom up and the middle out. That's not happening in this country. I'm sorry, but prosperity is not happening from the bottom up and the middle out under \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . And he keeps saying that.\par \par \uc2 And what they would really like -- and this gets to Lee's point -- somebody to say, you know what, here's honestly what's happening with inflation, and here's why it's a problem. And here's why, by the way, it hits those just starting their careers the hardest.\par \par \uc2 If we get a recession, that's going to hit young workers the hardest. They want honesty. They're not getting that from the current administration. I agree with Lee. The GOP needs to be out there talking much more about what they want to do to fix this and create opportunity.\par \par \uc2 I understand it starts with rolling back Biden's policies. But there's more to the story than that. They can win young voters. Everybody thinks young voters are drawn to the student debt handout. I actually don't think so. I think what they want is a real story about prosperity. They don't get that right now from the left.\par \par \uc2 And I think that's the opportunity from Republicans, if they can grasp it and get on message.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes, because everyone goes to the store, right? Everyone goes out and looks at the price of things.\par \par \uc2 BRENBERG: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: And when someone tells them it's no big deal or gas prices have come down and they can all relax, I mean, I think that gets them a little angry, Professor.\par \par \uc2 BRENBERG: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So we will see what happens.\par \par \uc2 Great seeing you again, my friend. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 BRENBERG: You bet, sir.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Brian Brenberg.\par \par \uc2 All right, in the meantime here, a possible distraction for Republicans all along has been the fixation on all things \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , oftentimes for good reason, what was really behind the \uc2 FBI\uc2  raid of his home about a month ago. Now talk of a special master that's been approved to look through all those documents. For Republicans, the feeling seems to be anything that can give this time to not be a front-burner issue.\par \par \uc2 That side of the issue -- after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, I think they call this hitting the ground running.\par \par \uc2 No sooner was she taking office as Britain's fourth Conservative Party leader, third female prime minister,\uc2 Liz Truss\uc2  is now saying that she is going to visit Ukrainian President Zelenskyy in Ukraine, and soon. That is just coming from her office now.\par \par \uc2 She's hit the ground running, I should say, because a number of other initiatives, including how to address the rising energy crisis in her country, where so many Brits are refusing to pay their utility bills, and looking for some government help. She plans to have a policy, she says, on that to address that, and very, very soon. We will keep you posted.\par \par \uc2 We're also keeping you posted on the fallout from a big victory for former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . He is going to get his special master to review all those documents the \uc2 FBI\uc2  took from his home almost a month ago.\par \par \uc2 David Spunt has more on that at the Justice Department -- David.\par \par \uc2 DAVID SPUNT, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hi, Neil.\par \par \uc2 We are waiting for names proposed by the government and by Trump's legal team to find out who this special master will be. This person will have to go through thousands of pages of classified documents at the highest levels of classification in \uc2 U.S. government\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In plain English, Neil, appointing this special master will no doubt slowed down the investigation that's being led in this building, the Department of Justice. And, as you mentioned, this is a legal victory, even if a temporary legal victory, for the former president and his legal team.\par \par \uc2 This was issued yesterday by Judge Aileen Cannon. She's with a -- she's a district judge in Southern Florida actually appointed by then-President Trump in 2020. She heard arguments last week in person from Trump attorneys and federal prosecutors.\par \par \uc2 And after a nearly two-hour court session and a long weekend to think about it, she made this decision yesterday, writing: "A special master shall be appointed to review the seized property, manage assertions of privilege, and make recommendations thereon and evaluate claims for return of property."\par \par \uc2 She also goes on to say: "The government is temporarily enjoined from further review and use of any of the materials seized from Mar-a-Lago on August 8 for criminal investigative purposes pending resolution of the special master's review process as determined by this court," meaning DOJ cannot continue the investigation into \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . Right now, it's on pause.\par \par \uc2 But the investigation under way by Director of National Intelligence Avril Haines to determine the classification levels of the documents will not stop. It goes on. DOJ is also working alongside Haines and can continue the classification assessment. But, in the same breath, the actual investigation into \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  slows down.\par \par \uc2 If the Department of Justice appeals Neil, a three-judge panel with the U.S. Circuit Court of Appeals in Atlanta would hear the case. Of the 11 active judges on the circuit court, six were appointed by then-President Trump.\par \par \uc2 This is a live look in Atlanta right now at the circuit court. We are waiting to see if the Department of Justice appeals. A DOJ spokesperson tells FOX News the government is looking over the judge's order and will consider the next steps.\par \par \uc2 But legal experts from across the spectrum Neil expect DOJ to appeal this ruling, because the stakes are so high here -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Thank you, David, for that, David Spunt.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: Sure.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: To Andy McCarthy on all of this, the former assistant U.S. attorney.\par \par \uc2 Andy, where is this going?\par \par \uc2 ANDY MCCARTHY, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: I think, as David just said, Neil, it's going to the 11th Circuit. I can't see them not appealing.\par \par \uc2 The other thing I think we should probably at least mention is that there's always this talk around this time a year before a big federal election of the 60-day rule, which is not really a rule, so much as a -- sort of a guidance the Justice Department follows.\par \par \uc2 But they try not to take public steps in cases that may have an impact on the elections, because they don't like the perception of the Justice Department or law enforcement processes influencing the outcome of elections.\par \par \uc2 So I would have expected them to try to go dark anyhow for the next two months. We may now actually see more activity just because of these court proceedings. We will obviously be read in on those.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But, in the meantime, they can't pursue this criminal investigation until all of this is resolved, one way or the other way, whether they lose in an appeals court and then the meter is running on getting this special master to go through things.\par \par \uc2 I mean, if you're \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and his legal team, what does that mean?\par \par \uc2 MCCARTHY: Well, I think that's a little bit overstated, because a court has no authority to tell the government what it can investigate.\par \par \uc2 What the court has authority to do is tell them that they can't use documents that have been collected by the government through the court's processes. Here, it was a search warrant that was the authority for the government to use these -- to use these documents.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I see.\par \par \uc2 MCCARTHY: But that doesn't mean that other aspects of the investigation can't be pursued.\par \par \uc2 Remember, they had a pretty vigorous investigation before the search. So, I think not a lot is going to happen. And let's face it. This is not the most complicated investigation of all time. But I wouldn't expect a lot to happen in the next two months, in any event.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, so all -- if it comes back, Andy, to he had government documents, he shouldn't have had government documents, that's one thing.\par \par \uc2 But, of course, this escalated and the obstruction issue came up. And, as you were reminding me when you misrepresented that, lied about that, and it turns out you did have these documents, and then when your legal team ultimately gave up some documents and said that was it, there are no more documents, when, in fact, there were documents, so there's a pattern of lies here that, intentional or not, have given the government ample ground for an obstruction case, haven't they?\par \par \uc2 MCCARTHY: Yes, it -- Neil, I think, in broader lens, it's kind of the same decision that they had to make when they decided to escalate to a search warrant, right?\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 MCCARTHY: They had for a while, I think, the legal and factual grounds to go seek a search warrant, but they were also quite aware that ratcheting it up like that would have a big impact on the public and the divisions in the country.\par \par \uc2 Charging former President Trump would have, by dimensions, I think, even more of an impact. So I think we're at the point in time where the Justice Department may think that they have a case they can bring. The question is it, is it prudent under the circumstances to bring it, given the conditions of the country, given what people would say about the fact that \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  wasn't charged...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right. Right.\par \par \uc2 MCCARTHY: ... and what it says about our two-tiered justice system? That's a big deal.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes, because -- I was just going to raise that issue, and other cases, Sandy Berger with documents he hid in his pants, and all the -- we can all remember various cases here. So there would be an inconsistency to going after the former president in this case.\par \par \uc2 And I'm just wondering. The president will always go back to the argument, I was the president, and these documents were within my purview and right to have, whether he declassified them or not. There's a growing argument there whether that was the case or would have changed anything. He had them when he shouldn't have had them.\par \par \uc2 But the sheer volume of them doesn't make a difference, either, the fact that there were thousands of them?\par \par \uc2 MCCARTHY: Yes, Neil, I think that goes to where I believe President Trump is not helping himself with his public statements.\par \par \uc2 I think, if you want a resolution of this without a prosecution, the best thing he could do right now is stop talking about it. And I think, unfortunately, he sees it -- in terms of the possibility of making a 2024 run, he sees that this is a positive in terms of his political campaign.\par \par \uc2 So he keeps harping on it. But I think, by harping on it, he makes it more likely that he gets charged.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: We will watch closely.\par \par \uc2 Andy, thank you again. Good seeing you, Andy McCarthy following all of that.\par \par \uc2 MCCARTHY: Thanks, Neil.\par \par \uc2 Karl Rove is just worried about one thing. Forget about the particulars, whether it's legal or not, whether he had the documents or not, the fact that this is what Republicans end up talking about and defending, when they should be focusing on the economy.\par \par \uc2 The other side of that story that you're not hearing that you should be hearing -- next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, after Labor Day, it's the traditional back-to-work push, but, this time, really back to work, like in the offices work.\par \par \uc2 Why big investment banks are telling their workers, we're not asking anymore -- after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, a special master, whether it will be appealed, what happens if it's appealed.\par \par \uc2 To Karl Rove, the legalities of this don't matter, so much as the distraction that he says it could mean for Republicans focused on this issue, at the expense of all others that are winnable issues for them, if they stay on the economy and stay on inflation and stay on the issues that ultimately decide elections.\par \par \uc2 Karl Rove with us right now.\par \par \uc2 Karl, good to see you.\par \par \uc2 So you really come back that, despite whatever your views on this and arguing that a president can't be taking documents out of the White House, and all of that, down to the distraction it's getting to be for Republicans defending him or either commenting on it, and not on inflation that's running out of control, the big economic pocketbook issues, right?\par \par \uc2 KARL ROVE, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Yes.\par \par \uc2 Look -- yes, exactly. Look, since the search on August 8, what have we been talking about? We're now just shy of a month of talking about this issue. And if you're a Democrat, you love it, because the Republicans are forced to talk about Trump taking classified documents to Mar-a-Lago, instead of inflation, economy, the crisis at the border or crime.\par \par \uc2 So, if you're a Democrat, you want them to be talking about this. If you're a Republican, you really want to be talking about these things. And so, ironically enough, the decision for a special master, as you heard from both David Spunt in his excellent reporting, and then that very smart guy named Andrew McCarthy, as you have heard, this is going to slow the whole process up.\par \par \uc2 So you would -- not a lot is going to happen in the next couple of months. There may be appeals and so forth. But in terms of new information, and there's not going to be a lot. And Andy McCarthy said it was to the president -- the former president's advantage not to be talking about this issue.\par \par \uc2 To Republicans, it's also an advantage not to be talking about this particular issue, and instead talking about the things that people are talking about around the kitchen table, namely, how do they keep up with the rising prices? And how good is the economy going to be? And what's going to be done about the border and the crime in their neighborhoods and communities?\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You know, Karl, I don't have to remind you what the former president has written about you for saying what you have on this issue, and particularly the fact that you can't take government documents and bring them home.\par \par \uc2 But does that bother you at a certain level, that what you're getting back to is this notion we have got to get off this obsession, one way or the other, and speak about the economy? I mean, you would -- you would think and some people would say, well, maybe Karl's right about that, and that it's in the president, the former president's interest as well to make him less of an issue and more about the Republican Party's chances.\par \par \uc2 ROVE: Yes, look, I understand the angst that he feels, how strongly he feels about this.\par \par \uc2 But this is a situation that's largely of his own creation. He should not have taken those documents. But now that we're in this kerfuffle over what -- what have they gotten back and how are they handled, it is better for him not to be talking about it.\par \par \uc2 When he went to Pennsylvania this weekend, he spent over 80 minutes talking about this situation, and less than half that advancing the cause of the two candidates he was there to advance and the issues that will either determine whether they get elected or not.\par \par \uc2 People are not going to vote Republican in order to affirm President Trump's view of what those documents were, namely, his property. They will vote Republican if they have a conviction that Republicans are going to do something about the inflation that's consuming our country, do something about strengthening our economy without spending a lot more money, taking it from taxpayers and turning it over to the government, and if they do something about border security and crime.\par \par \uc2 That's what they care about. And if we're going to motivate them to vote Republican in the fall, then Republican candidates need to have the airspace to talk about him, and the former president plays a big role in giving them that airspace.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But he's digging in deeper, right? He criticized \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  again for supporting Alaska Senator Murkowski in her battle to keep her job.\par \par \uc2 And you always think of the possibility, should \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  run again and win, he's got to work with \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 . How would that go?\par \par \uc2 ROVE: Well, that's a long way off.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: It is.\par \par \uc2 ROVE: So let's focus on the here and now. And you're right.\par \par \uc2 Right now, it doesn't do any good for him to be critical of \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 . And it doesn't do -- it doesn't do his party any good to spend most of his time talking about himself, rather than talking about the issues that are consuming the country.\par \par \uc2 He did a lot of good things when it came to the border and the economy and energy and so forth. He ought to build on that by focusing on those issues and talking about the next chapter.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So when you hear you're a RINO -- I have heard worse -- but Republican in name only and all, on any level, does that bother you?\par \par \uc2 ROVE: Not really.\par \par \uc2 I mean, look, I don't need to prove to myself that I'm a true Republican. I don't need somebody else to hurt my feelings by saying I'm a RINO and having me feel sorry about myself. I have -- I have spent my adult life building and advancing the Republican Party.\par \par \uc2 I was for \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2  in 2004, not \uc2 John Kerry\uc2 , like some people were. I have been a loyal Republican, and I don't need anybody else to tell me that.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Karl Rove, very good seeing you. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 ROVE: Neil, thank you for having me.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right.\par \par \uc2 We have a lot more coming up, including the push right now to incentivize people not to own a car. I don't know, how does 1,000 bucks sound to you?\par \par \uc2 After this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, you ever get the feeling that California is not too keen on traditional gas-powered vehicles? They want them all gone, at least sales of them, by 2035.\par \par \uc2 Never mind there's been problems with the grid there and whether it can sustain all these E.V.s that they're now asking to charge up gingerly, but a separate push right now to get folks off the road by paying them to get off the road.\par \par \uc2 It gets a little more involved. Our expert on getting a little bit more involved and explaining it in English is William La Jeunesse joining us now from Los Angeles.\par \par \uc2 What's going on here, William?\par \par \uc2 WILLIAM LA JEUNESSE, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Well, Neil, several things.\par \par \uc2 Number one, there are a lot of incentives, up to $15,000 to buy an electric car in California, when you add the state and the federal. But you don't have to live in California to live under our rules. In fact, 17 states already do. And now many have to confront this debate about getting rid of the gas-powered vehicle.\par \par \uc2 Why? Well, about 15 years ago, California sued and won the right under the Clean Air Act to have stricter tailpipe emissions than the federal government. So, now these 17 states are at a crossroads. They have to confront the question, will they join the electric car mandate or not?\par \par \uc2 Some will. Colorado and Pennsylvania likely will not, along with Minnesota and Virginia.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DEREE DICKENS, CALIFORNIA ELECTRIC CAR OWNER: The power grid is already taxed, and then you're going to put greater demand, significantly greater demand, on it by having all electric cars, is -- I don't see that as a recipe for success, personally, at least not in that time span.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LA JEUNESSE: So, right now, nationally, about 6 percent of new car sales are electric. California, it's 16 percent.\par \par \uc2 But under new California rules, that's going to go to 35 percent of all electric -- sales be electric cars in four years, and they will continue to ramp up. So the question is, is it doable or prudent, given that electric car prices are high and the shortage of charging stations, not to mention actually having the electricity to keep them running?\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GOV. GAVIN NEWSOM (D-CA): Try not to use too much electricity in those key hours. And the key hours are between 4:00 p.m. and 9:00 p.m. in the evening. Try to reduce your consumption to the extent possible.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LA JEUNESSE: So, right now, Neil, we're in day six of this flex alert, basically trying to struggle to keep the lights on.\par \par \uc2 Basically, the legalese on this is that these 17 states either have to choose to be part of the federal program or the California program. There's -- the car manufacturers say, we can't meet 50 different standards in 50 different states.\par \par \uc2 So they're going to have to confront this question. And in those states where you have a mix of Republicans and Democrats, it's going to be open to debate -- back to you.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: And a lot of time. All right, we will see what happens.\par \par \uc2 William, thank you, my friend.\par \par \uc2 William La Jeunesse following all of that.\par \par \uc2 Well, \uc2 Liz Truss\uc2  is now the new prime minister of Great Britain, and she's already completed a phone call with the president of the United States, after speaking earlier with President Zelenskyy over in Ukraine. She says she's eager to go to Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 What came of the conversation with the president of this country? We will tell you -- after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, here we go again.\par \par \uc2 For the third time in the last six years, as my next guest says, Britain has a new prime minister, the fourth Conservative prime minister that has veered increasingly away from its Conservative roots. Now, will \uc2 Liz Truss\uc2  be any different?\par \par \uc2 Gerry Baker joins us right now, The Wall Street Journal editor at large and read -- and wrote on this beautifully, very eloquently, if even a little snarkily, in The Wall Street Journal.\par \par \uc2 It's always good to see you, Gerry. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 You raise a very good point about the trend among Conservative leaders, that sort of the party eats its own, whatever is going on there. But you said that: "Bowing to the gods of ever-expanding public sector and welfare state, they have raised the tax take of the national income to its highest level in 70 years."\par \par \uc2 You mentioned other examples, but this has been happening under Conservative governments. Now, she was not always a Maggie Thatcher, right?\par \par \uc2 GERRY BAKER, HOST, "WALL STREET JOURNAL AT LARGE": Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Quite the opposite. So, what do we have here?\par \par \uc2 BAKER: Yes, thanks for having me again, Neil. It's always a pleasure to be on.\par \par \uc2 Yes, the British, my ex-compatriots, seem to change prime ministers as frequently as some people change shirts. This is, as you say, the third time in six years they have changed it, making it the fourth prime minister in six years. That's the kind of thing that British used to laugh at other countries about. They used to have jokes that I had a great summer internship. I was prime minister of Italy for a few months.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 BAKER: That's basically what's going on in Britain these days.\par \par \uc2 And as I say in the column this week, Neil, the problem -- the problem has been particularly that we have had this succession of Conservative prime ministers. Conservatives have been in power in Britain since 2010. So, for 12 -- more than 12 years now, Conservatives have been in power.\par \par \uc2 But they become progressively less Conservative in the way they actually govern. So, as you mentioned very kindly, in my column, I said, yes, the tax take of the country, the proportion of income taken in tax is its highest in 70 years. That's happened under Conservative governments.\par \par \uc2 The sort of woke revolution that is sweeping this country, I mean, at least we can say, to be fair, you have got a Democratic president and we a -- we have got a Democratic present a Democratic Congress and lots and lots of Democratic-controlled states and cities around the country who are kind of pushing this woke agenda on culture and crime and policing and all this kind of stuff.\par \par \uc2 It's been done in Britain under a Conservative government. We have a party that doesn't really seem to be in -- pursuing Conservative values. So, \uc2 Liz Truss\uc2  comes along. She wins this internal election. I have to say, it's a very strange way really to pick a prime minister. She was elected by Conservative members -- the members of the Conservative Party.\par \par \uc2 It's not like a primary, where...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 BAKER: ... millions of people vote. It was 140,000 people voted.\par \par \uc2 So she wins this quite narrowly over her rival. Now, again, the promising news is, she is -- she's campaigned as a Conservative. She's been a Conservative for some time. You're absolutely right. She was once a Liberal Democrat who actually despised \uc2 Margaret Thatcher\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But that was -- we can forgive people youthful indiscretions. That was 20, 30 years ago, when she was a...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Well, \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2  was a Democrat, right?\par \par \uc2 BAKER: She is now -- absolutely, very famously, yes, what was -- yes, exactly. That's right.\par \par \uc2 So, I mean, so she -- she has -- like a lot of people, she's matured, become a Conservative, seen the lights, and is promising to do all kinds of things. The particular problem, of course, now, though, Neil, she faces is, Britain faces a massive economic crisis, the energy crisis that all of Europe is facing as a result of the war in Ukraine in particular.\par \par \uc2 It's got huge fiscal problems, partly because they have been spending so much money for so long, they're in huge amounts of debt. They have got the same inflation problem that the rest of the world has. And they have got very, very, very poor long-term economic performance in the form of productivity.\par \par \uc2 So she's got a lot on her hands. Their immediate challenge is going to be to spend a lot of money to help Brits through this terrible winter of energy prices, explode the deficit even more than they have already. And then she's going to promise to sort of remake the country in the mold of \uc2 Margaret Thatcher\uc2 . It's going to be tough.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes. And the tax cut thing that she's still pushing, and she remains loyal to \uc2 Boris Johnson\uc2 . She hasn't abandoned him. So we will see how all that balances out.\par \par \uc2 Gerry, great column, great job all the time.\par \par \uc2 And catch his show, "The Wall Street Journal at Large." He puts it all in perspective and just has a unique way of presenting it.\par \par \uc2 All right, in the meantime, you're back at work. Now, you working from home or are you working in the office? Because there's a real push to get you in the office. You in? You out?\par \par \uc2 After this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Well, to hear a lot of New Yorkers tell it, this is not something to think about, particularly in the investment arena, where some of the biggest banks on the planet now are telling workers, we would really like you to get back to the office ASAP. We're not ordering you too.\par \par \uc2 We just think would be a very good idea.\par \par \uc2 Charlie Gasparino has been following that.\par \par \uc2 But Charlie made a swipe at younger workers, millennial workers, who he says are too cozy in their slippers and pajamas to ever leave. So we thought we'd check in with Abby Hornacek, who is certainly not lazy.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: She's fully dressed right now, not in pajamas. I can't speak for the fuzzy slippers.\par \par \uc2 So, Abby, before I go to Charlie, I want you to respond to his mean, nasty, awful comments about your entire generation.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 ABBY HORNACEK, "PARK'D" HOST: How dare you, Charlie? I'm so offended.\par \par \uc2 Look, I think you invited...\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE GASPARINO, FOX NEWS SENIOR CORRESPONDENT: It's all true.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 HORNACEK: I think you invited me on to give a millennial perspective. But the joke's on you. I'm actually an old soul.\par \par \uc2 So I do see the value in younger workers returning to work. And here's why, because we have seen all this research done about how the productivity levels have gone up in some situations with people's jobs because they are working from home, they're working longer hours. They're cutting commuting costs.\par \par \uc2 But I think that is applying to people who have at least experienced working from an office once in their life before the pandemic, working that 9:00 to 5:00 and those five days a week. So I think it's extremely valuable for this younger generation who's just graduated from college, they're used to a culture where they can sleep in, maybe sneak in the back of an auditorium, not be seen, wear their pajamas all day.\par \par \uc2 And then they're carrying that into a virtual world. So they need to return to work,Well, experience what it's like to be in an office, learn those professional skills before they move to a virtual situation.\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: I don't buy any...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Charlie, you know, I knew her position.\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: Right.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: And I wanted to illustrate that to show you cast an entire generation with the same dismal brush.\par \par \uc2 Try to dig yourself out of this.\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: Well, I'm -- listen, it's not me. It's \uc2 Jamie Dimon\uc2 . It's \uc2 Larry Fink\uc2 . It's David Solomon. It's every major Wall Street firm saying, get your rear ends back to work.\par \par \uc2 By the way, all those studies...\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes, but you were isolating young people like Abby who don't share that view.\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: Well, who are the ones that are rebelling?\par \par \uc2 HORNACEK: Thank you, Neil.\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: It's not me. It's the young people. It's the people that have been brainwashed by woke college professors.\par \par \uc2 By the way, I don't believe any study that says working from home is more productive. You need someone screaming at you sometimes to make you more productive.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Abby agrees with you. And she is a young person.\par \par \uc2 HORNACEK: I agree.\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: I'm saying, there are no studies. She said there are studies.\par \par \uc2 HORNACEK: There are studies.\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: I mean, these are like the -- these are like sort of fugazi studies that are designed...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Abby, does he sound a crotchety old man to you who is insensitive?\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: I'm just telling you, Neil, you need -- you see, this is why I will never be out of work.\par \par \uc2 HORNACEK: I think Charlie needs a vacation.\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: You know why I will never be out of work? Because...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Because you have pictures of people in compromising positions?\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: Well, that.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: And I got people like Amy trying to take my job.\par \par \uc2 Oh, OK. OK.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Abby, I don't know how you can accept that.\par \par \uc2 But, Abby, let me ask you something. There are a lot of young people -- all people kind of liked working from home for a while. Those days are gone. Do you think that it should apply to everybody, everybody get back to the office?\par \par \uc2 HORNACEK: You know, I do.\par \par \uc2 I talked to a lot of friends ahead of this because I have witnessed some of my friends do -- I won't name names.\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: Of course you have.\par \par \uc2 HORNACEK: But some of them get lazy.\par \par \uc2 But I have talked to people who have been pro-hybrid situation, because I have a very different job. You have a very different job than people who work in that office setting. Like, we get -- we have a fun job, right?\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But do we need Charlie physically here with us, Abby? Be honest.\par \par \uc2 HORNACEK: I mean, I personally believe that part of productivity is the interpersonal relationships that spur ideas.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: See that?\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: Yes, but none of the millennial, Gen Z'ers believe in that. They're just lazy.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But, Charlie, see that?\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: They're so lazy.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Now, here, she could have gone back to you with a nasty comment. And she chose not to. She rose above that.\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: Listen, they're so lazy. Well, you admit it. Everybody knows it.\par \par \uc2 You know what? The funny thing is, I write this comment in The New York Post. I thought I would get brushback. No, they are agreeing with me, everybody, even the Gen Z'ers. Yes, we know our generation is a bunch of lazy hacks. I mean, it goes down the line.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Oh, look at the time, guys. Look at the time. We're out of time.\par \par \uc2 HORNACEK: Oh.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But thank you both very, very much. You have to play-at-home version of "Your World," but terrific on both sides here.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But, see, I knew where Abby was coming from. I just knew.\par \par \uc2 Here's "The Five."\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CAVU000020220906ei960002t\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Interview with DCCC chair \uc2 Sean Patrick Maloney\uc2  (D-NY); Interview with NRCC chair \uc2 Tom Emmer\uc2  (R-MN)\b0\par\par\uc2 Mike Emanuel, Lucas Tomlinson, Marie Harf, Howard Kurtz \par \uc2 8,921 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 September 2022\par \uc2 Fox News: Fox News Sunday\par \uc2 FOXN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 MIKE EMANUEL, FOX NEWS HOST: I'm Mike Emanuel.\par \par \uc2 President Biden takes aggressive aim at Republicans as it travels to political swing states to try and salvage his party's control of Congress.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: There's no question that the Republican Party today is dominated, driven, and intimidated by \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and the MAGA Republicans. And that is a threat to this country.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL (voice-over): The president's new midterm message delivered from Independence Hall, the birthplace of American democracy, and in his home state, battleground Pennsylvania, the site of a high-profile Senate showdown.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT: Two months from now, the people of Pennsylvania are going to fire the radical left Democrats.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: We'll discuss the prospects for November as Democrats see momentum shift in a favor and Republicans try to shift the subject from Mar-a-Lago.\par \par \uc2 REP. KEVIN MCCARTHY (R-CA): I want an economy that's stronger. I want the nation that's safe. I want a -- I want a country that's built upon freedom.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: We're joined by both parties' House campaign chiefs, NRCC chair \uc2 Tom Emmer\uc2 , and DCCC chair \uc2 Sean Patrick Maloney\uc2 . Emmer and Maloney, only on "FOX News Sunday."\par \par \uc2 And our Sunday panel on the stunning lack of confirmed debates just two months from Election Day.\par \par \uc2 Then --\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: How are you?\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: School districts across the country face learning loss and teacher shortages, we'll ask \uc2 National Governors Association\uc2  chair \uc2 Phil Murphy\uc2  about his trip to Washington to sort out solutions.\par \par \uc2 Plus, one week until Shannon Bream takes the big chair.\par \par \uc2 HOWARD KURTZ, FOX NEWS HOST, MEDIA BUZZ: They're all going to try to stick to their talking points. How do you tackle?\par \par \uc2 SHANNON BREAM, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: That's the trick I think in Washington.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: All right now on "FOX News Sunday."\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: And hello again from FOX News in Washington.\par \par \uc2 Labor Day weekend traditionally marks the kickoff of the fall campaign season ahead of midterm elections. Republicans are focused on inflation, crime, and the border, as they aim to win back the majority in November, while Democrats are heading into the final weeks with renewed momentum. And President Biden heads to critical swing states revived by improving poll numbers.\par \par \uc2 In the moment, we'll speak with New York Congressman \uc2 Sean Patrick Maloney\uc2 , who chairs the Democratic Congressional Campaign Committee, which works to elect Democrats to the House. And we'll get reaction from Minnesota Congressman \uc2 Tom Emmer\uc2 , chair of the National Republican Congressional Committee, the House GOP campaign arm.\par \par \uc2 But first let's turn to Lucas Tomlinson live at the White House with a look at dueling visits by the president and his predecessor to battleground Pennsylvania -- Lucas.\par \par \uc2 LUCAS TOMLINSON, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Mike, the president gave a speech Thursday night that the White House insists wasn't political. Many pundits disagree.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: MAGA Republicans look at America and see carnage and darkness and despair.\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON (voice-over): President Biden in Philadelphia delivering what the White House dubbed a speech on the continued battle for the soul of the nation. Biden did not mention his Inflation Reduction Act or student debt relief.\par \par \uc2 Also absent, Pennsylvania's Democratic nominees for \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2  and governor.\par \par \uc2 The commander-in-chief spoke while flanked by two U.S. marines, something he promised not to do while running for office.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I promise you as president, I will never put you in the middle of politics or personal vendettas. I'll never use the military as a prop.\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON: Biden made two stops in battleground Pennsylvania last week and plans another on Labor Day. He will visit Milwaukee and Pittsburgh Monday. Later this week, Biden speaks of the DNC summer meeting outside the nation's capital. And then heads to Ohio.\par \par \uc2 Joining him there, Congressman \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2 , who is running in a close Senate race against JD Vance. Ryan skipped Biden's previous trip to the state.\par \par \uc2 Former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  also traveled to Pennsylvania near Biden's hometown Saturday night.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : The shameful raid and break-in of my home, Mar-a-Lago, was a travesty of justice.\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON: Trump spoke as the Justice Department continues its investigation into how he kept classified documents at his Mar-a-Lago estate. Many Republicans have called the \uc2 FBI\uc2  search unprecedented. The former president's attorney general responded.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAM BARR\uc2 , FORMER U.S. ATTORNEY GENERAL: People say this was unprecedented. But it's also unprecedented for a president to take all this classified information and put them in a country club.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON: Bill Barr also said naming a special master for the Mar-a-Lago documents is a, quote, waste of time -- Mike.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Lucas Tomlinson reporting from the White House -- Lucas, many thanks.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now is Congressman \uc2 Sean Patrick Maloney\uc2 , who leads the House committee working to elect Democrats.\par \par \uc2 Congressman, welcome to "FOX News Sunday".\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 SEAN PATRICK MALONEY\uc2  (D-NY): Good to be with you.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: So, are Democrats going to hold a majority in the House?\par \par \uc2 MALONEY: Yes, we're going to hold the majority in the House because we have a plan to fix the problems our country is facing. The other side has only a ploy to win back power for themselves and ripping away our constitutional freedom, our reproductive freedom, has awakened millions of voters and we see that in the astonishing come back in a special elections in New York and elsewhere. We're on a roll and we're going to win this election.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Even with inflation, gasoline prices, a crisis at the border, and crime in American cities?\par \par \uc2 MALONEY: Look, those are all serious problems and we are working those problems. Gas prices are down more than $1.20 a gallon in just about 70 days. That's because the president increased supplies, went after price gouging, and released the Strategic Petroleum Reserves.\par \par \uc2 So again, we are working the problem. Inflation will come down with those gas prices but it is still too high. That's right.\par \par \uc2 But the other side has no plan, of course, to reduce inflation. They have a plan to win power for themselves. And, of course, we're the ones that are lowering prescription drug costs by finally taking on big drug companies, capping seniors' out-of-pocket costs.\par \par \uc2 The Republican plan, to expire Social Security and Medicare every five years. No, there's a real choice in this election and we're going to win that. We're going to win that choice.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: The president Thursday night said MAGA Republicans are destroying American democracy. He tried to draw a line between MAGA Republicans and mainstream GOP, but he offended a lot of folks in the process.\par \par \uc2 Here's the president in an old tweet during the campaign saying, quote: We need a president who chooses unity over division.\par \par \uc2 What happened to unifying?\par \par \uc2 MALONEY: Well, there's nothing the president said on Thursday that has not been said by leading conservatives and Republicans. I mean, what the president said is exactly what people like Judge Luttig said. It's exactly what people like Vice President \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  and \uc2 John Bolton\uc2  have said. If you listen to comments from previous presidential nominees like \uc2 Mitt Romney\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In other words, this is not Republicans versus Democrats. This is mainstream Republicans and Democrats versus MAGA extremists. This election is about mainstream versus MAGA, and I'm proud that we have a president that knows right from wrong.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: There was a lot of discomfort about the president delivering a partially political speech in front of those marines in dress uniform. Democrats railed against President Trump when he made political remarks in nonpartisan settings.\par \par \uc2 How is this different?\par \par \uc2 MALONEY: Well, I wouldn't miss the larger point, and, of course, those critics didn't complain when \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  literally staged the fourth night of the Republican political convention on the White House lawn. I mean, my goodness -- or use our military to clear peaceful protesters in Lafayette Park.\par \par \uc2 So -- but I wouldn't miss the larger part in the speech. The point of the speech is that mainstream Republicans and Democrats agree on things like it's wrong to attack United States capital, to spread a pernicious lie about the election being stolen, to steal our nation's secrets and keep them down there at the motel Mar-a-Lago.\par \par \uc2 I mean, Republicans and the Democrats are in large part in agreement. It's the MAGA movement that is extreme, that has ripped away reproductive freedom, that justifies the attack on the Capitol, that plays footsie with white supremacists and QAnon conspiracy theorists. That's what the president was talking about.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Let me follow-up, though. The president attacked MAGA forces. That isn't political?\par \par \uc2 MALONEY: What I'm telling you is that the president has a duty to defend American democracy, and he is in agreement with leading conservative voices, the ones I just mentioned, that the attack on the Capitol was wrong, that it's wrong to spread a lie about the results of an election.\par \par \uc2 I mean, Bill Barr, for goodness sake, knows right from wrong here. So this isn't just President Biden. This is mainstream versus MAGA. And we have a duty to defend American democracy.\par \par \uc2 Look, we just lost 50 years of reproductive freedom because the runaway MAGA Supreme Court tore up Roe v. Wade. And every candidate -- your next guest is running for office -- supports tearing up Roe v. Wade. We're going to defend Roe v. Wade and reproductive freedom that all of us used to take for granted in America.\par \par \uc2 So that's the movement that will take away your political freedom. It just took away your reproductive freedom, and the president is sounding the alarm.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Okay. So since Roe v. Wade has been overturned and it's becoming a state issue, should Democrats running for office tell voters whether they support any restrictions on abortion?\par \par \uc2 MALONEY: What Democrats support broadly is restoring the status quo, Roe v. Wade. We're for Roe v. Wade. The other side is going to ban abortion in all 50 states. That's your choice.\par \par \uc2 I will leave it to individual candidates to state their individual positions on any of the nuance, but I'm telling you right now, if you care about your reproductive freedom, if you think politicians shouldn't be making these decisions, that women and their doctors and their families should make these decisions, that's what Democrats support.\par \par \uc2 If you care at all about marriage equality, if you care about your reproductive choices like in vitro fertilization, or if you care about birth control, for goodness sake, these are the privacy rights we used to have in the Constitution that the MAGA -- MAGA Supreme Court just tore up and threw away. That's the choice this fall.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Do you support any restrictions on abortion, any at all?\par \par \uc2 MALONEY: Look, there were always restrictions on abortion embedded in the original Roe v. Wade decision. Anyone who's read it knows that it meant there was a balancing test, it was a compromise between viability, early on in pregnancy, and later in pregnancy, and that was the common sense of that framework.\par \par \uc2 What the MAGA crowd has done is rip away that decision from women and their doctors, take it out of the Constitution, and now, every state in the country can ban abortion. And if they get control of Congress, they will ban it in all 50 states.\par \par \uc2 But the voters have another idea, and if you -- and if you disagree with me, well, I'd like you to meet Congressman-elect Pat Ryan in Upstate New York.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Given the president's harsh comments on MAGA Republicans, how do Democrats justify running ads supporting Trump-backed candidates?\par \par \uc2 Let's play a clip.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 AD ANNOUNCER: The Gibbs-Trump agenda is too conservative for west Michigan. DCCC is responsible for the content of this advertising.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: You've said this is not putting party over country and you've said you're giving voters a choice in November. But the truth is, Republican voters had a choice in the primaries and you meddled with that.\par \par \uc2 How do you justify that?\par \par \uc2 MALONEY: Well, we just said what is true, which is John Gibbs is an anti- choice extremist who is too conservative for western Michigan. So we have a great candidate there named Hillary Scholten who is going to protect your reproductive freedom. She's going to beat him, and don't take my word for it, "The Cook Political Report" just moved that race to be much more likely one that Democrats will win.\par \par \uc2 So your next guest can explain what he lost a safe incumbent for about $400,000. We call that winning a seat, and protecting a woman's reproductive freedom by electing Hillary Scholten.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: There are Democrat groups trying something similar in New Hampshire.\par \par \uc2 Here's what Democratic Congressman \uc2 Stephanie Murphy\uc2  said about that strategy earlier this summer. Quote: Many of us are facing death threats over our efforts to tell the truth about Jan. 6. To have people boosting candidates telling the very kinds of lies that caused Jan. 6 and continues to put our democracy in danger is just mind-blowing.\par \par \uc2 What do you say to your colleague?\par \par \uc2 MALONEY: What I say is that your next guest (AUDIO GAP) results of the election with 139 other House Republicans, and I'll let those folks explain that. The fact is that the danger to our democracy didn't start with the Michigan primary. We're chockfull of people in the Congress who denied the election, who played common cause with people who attacked the Capitol.\par \par \uc2 My goodness, what we are talking about here, and "The Cook Political Report" agrees, is that we've got a much better chance to elect a strong, pro-choice member of Congress from that district in Michigan. That's doing our jobs.\par \par \uc2 And again, I'll let \uc2 Tom Emmer\uc2  explain why he lost a safe seat.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: The president's approval rating is up a bit in recent weeks. Should Democrats running for office welcome the president to their districts to campaign with them?\par \par \uc2 MALONEY: Well, you're seeing that in places like Pennsylvania and Ohio, so don't take my word for it.\par \par \uc2 And you better believe, we're going to stand with the president and say, we passed in a bipartisan way, better roads, better bridges, better health care for veterans that MAGA Republicans tried to obstruct, by the way, that we're going to negotiate with prescription drug companies, lowering those costs and capping seniors out of pocket costs. We've passed common sense gun legislation also in a bipartisan fashion -- better background checks, mental health and school safety resources.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Right.\par \par \uc2 MALONEY: That's the kind of agenda that we're stand -- we're standing in support of and you see it working in the polls. The president is on the way back and so are House Democrats.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: But typically, after a major bill is passed, such as the Inflation Reduction Act, you see candidates rushing to presidential events all across the country, eager to tout the accomplishment. We didn't see that this summer.\par \par \uc2 MALONEY: Oh, you're going to see it this week. There's going to be a celebration on the White House lawn. You're talking about negotiating cheaper progression drug costs? My (AUDIO GAP) support that.\par \par \uc2 How about a cap on out-of-pocket expenses for seniors, closing the donut hole? That's in that legislation.\par \par \uc2 You're going to see finally on minimum tax on the biggest corporations that have been getting away with murder paying zero. That's in this legislation. And we're going to combat climate change, which is important to our future, and lead the world with green energy -- green energy jobs.\par \par \uc2 You better believe Democrats are going to celebrate that. So are the voters. And that's why we've -- we've been winning special elections from Alaska to New York.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Congressman Maloney, thank you. Great to talk with you.\par \par \uc2 Up next, we will get reaction from the other side of the aisle. NRCC Chair \uc2 Tom Emmer\uc2  joins me live, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Political junkies saw a lot of headlines this week about Republican concerns about their plan to retake control of the House and Senate in midterms. Is it all overblown?\par \par \uc2 Joining us here in Washington, Congressman \uc2 Tom Emmer\uc2  of Minnesota, who leads the committee working to elect Republicans to the House.\par \par \uc2 Congressman, welcome to "FOX News Sunday."\par \par \uc2 REP. TOM EMMER (R-MN): Great to be with you, Mike.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: So, you heard your counterpart, Congressman Maloney. I want to give you a chance to respond.\par \par \uc2 EMMER: Well, this is a -- this election, Mike, can be summed up with one word: security. It's about Americans' economic security. It's about Americans' physical security. You didn't hear any of that being talked about.\par \par \uc2 On the economic front,, because of failed Democrat policies, Americans are struggling. They have to make difficult choices every single day about groceries and gas.\par \par \uc2 And on the security side, the physical security, Democrats pro-criminal policies have put -- have literally made our cities war zones. If you look at for instance the congressman's own state, New York City, they are experiencing a crime epidemic because of cashless bail, which he supports. You look at my city of Minneapolis. Minneapolis has already had 6,000 assaults were reported since the beginning of the year.\par \par \uc2 And look, it's about the southwestern border being run over by cartels who are terrorizing American citizens, about every community in this country having to deal with the fentanyl crisis and parents wondering if their child is going to be the one that dies next.\par \par \uc2 This is a security election. It's about groceries and gas. It's about being safe and secure in our homes and communities. Democrats have caused this problem, and they're going to pay for it at the ballot box.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Let's talk for the battle of control of the House. House Republican Leader Kevin McCarthy initially talked about maybe winning as many 60 seats. But that prediction has been quietly scaled back among some Republicans.\par \par \uc2 This week, Quinnipiac found more Americans approve of how congressional Democrats are handling their jobs than how congressional Republicans are handling their jobs.\par \par \uc2 Also this week, a "Wall Street Journal" poll has Democrats up three points over Republicans on the generic ballot. Republicans are up by five points in March. And the poll has independents favoring Democrats 38-35. In March, Republicans were up 12 points with independents.\par \par \uc2 Why are you losing momentum right now?\par \par \uc2 EMMER: I don't think we have. Certainly, I think, some people needed to understand that a year ago was not going to be today. As you know, I talked about getting past Labor Day weekend when everybody starts to engage, and I like where we're at, Mike.\par \par \uc2 Right now, we are almost seven points better on the generic ballot than we were just two years ago. In fact, on Election Day in 2020, Democrats on the generic were a +6.5, and I will remind her that we not only held ever Republican incumbent for the first time since 1994, but we picked up 15 seats in an environment where it was productive we were going to lose 15.\par \par \uc2 The other thing you should look at is my counterpart announced a huge media buy last week. He is spending millions of dollars defending Democrat incumbents who are in seats that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  won by double digits.\par \par \uc2 Look, money talks, and money is painting pessimistic future for Democrats at the midterms.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Congressman, "\uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " reports, quote, at least nine Republican candidates have scrubbed or amended references to President Trump or abortion from their online profiles in recent months.\par \par \uc2 Has the NRCC recommended to candidates to back off their strong antiabortion views?\par \par \uc2 EMMER: Well, I'm not going to repeat the way "\uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " reports things. I trust our candidates to know their districts and know how to appeal to their voters, the voters who are going to turn out in November and elect them to the next Congress.\par \par \uc2 That being said, if Democrats want to make abortion the main issue when every poll we have seen says that the economy and the cost of living is the number one issue, good luck to them trying to defend their extreme position. Every one of them voted for what I call the Chinese genocide bill, which would allow abortion up to moments before a child takes its first breath.\par \par \uc2 I think our candidates know how to message that and they'll be just fine in the midterms.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Let's talk abortion. This week's "Wall Street Journal" poll says more than half of voters are more likely to head to the polls because Roe v. Wade was overturned. Can you say definitively that this will not cost Republican seats?\par \par \uc2 EMMER: I think it's helped to narrow the enthusiasm gap but if you look at the enthusiasm gap, every measure up there, Republicans still lead Democrats in enthusiasm to vote in this next election.\par \par \uc2 Plus, the number one issue in every single poll, I'll say it again, is the economy, and rising cost of living. Everything is more expensive because of failed Democrat policies.\par \par \uc2 And, Mike, the one question ever voters going to have to ask when they go to vote in November, am I better off today than I was two years ago? And it is a resounding no. Eight out of 10 Americans believe this country is in the wrong track and that is why they're going to vote for a change and elect a new Republican majority in the House.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Does former President Trump being in the news hurt your candidates because then you aren't hammering inflation, gasoline prices, the border crisis, and, of course, crime in major American cities?\par \par \uc2 EMMER: Look, the American intelligence agency and this it administration has been after \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  since day one. In fact the intelligence agency has been after him since he started running for office.\par \par \uc2 And now, the White House has prioritized going after anyone who disagrees with them. In fact, the White House press secretary last week said that anybody who disagrees with the Democrats and the White House, with the Biden administration, is an extremist.\par \par \uc2 The raid on the former president's home is not only unprecedented, it's outrageous. And by the way, this double standard is why so many Americans are pissed off about this, Republicans, Democrats, independents, because while they go after \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and suggest that he's de something which they've been doing for years, I would say that they lost their credibility six years ago with the Russian collusion hoax.\par \par \uc2 And while they're doing all this, \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  is still out there being able to peddle influence all around the world, and they do nothing to him. Americans are pissed about that. They're going to show up in November and Democrats are going to answer for it.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Have you or your team at the NRCC recommended to any Republicans that they distance themselves from former President Trump?\par \par \uc2 EMMER: Absolutely not. The president has been a fantastic ally of ours, especially when it comes to fund-raising. And our candidates, again, what we tell them is you know your districts, you know how to run to the people that are going to be voting for you in November.\par \par \uc2 And I am convinced that when people vote, they are going to ask the question, am I better off today than I was two years ago? And they're going to say overwhelming, no.\par \par \uc2 Prices are out of control, my security is in jeopardy, it's time for a change. And they're going to vote in a new Republican majority.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Republicans have been highly critical of President Biden's student debt giveaway. Some have suggested it's even unconstitutional.\par \par \uc2 If you win back the majority, should Republicans in the House sue or take some other action to block the president?\par \par \uc2 EMMER: Well, I'll let the future Speaker of the House Kevin McCarthy tell you what the plan will be on that, but when it comes to this unilateral move by the president, it's not only illegal, it's unfair, and it's wrong. If you think about it, every American who either chose to go into a trade or go to work, or went to college and paid off their debt like a responsible American is now going to pay $2,000 for someone else's debt.\par \par \uc2 So, blue-collar American and responsible Americans are going to pay $2,000 for somebody else's college debt. They're not happy about it.\par \par \uc2 Bu the way, look at a poll in New Hampshire in the past week because I know there's been a lot of mixed messaging about this. Two-thirds of independents in this poll are upset by the president's action. I think that's going to be consistent across this country and Democrats are going to answer for that in November as well.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: You made reference to Leader McCarthy. Are you convinced he's the next speaker, and do you expect any challenges from your colleagues?\par \par \uc2 EMMER: I am convinced that Kevin McCarthy will -- we'll not only be in the majority come the next Congress, but Kevin McCarthy will be our speaker.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: What about the possibility you are seeking if you guys win the majority -- you are seeking to be the House majority whip, do you expect any challenges there?\par \par \uc2 EMMER: You've got to win election first, Mike. I'm concentrating on the next 65 days. Once we get done with the election, once we perform and win a new majority in the House, then we can talk about the future.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Okay. On the Mar-a-Lago search, some have said this response from Senator \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  crossed the line. Let's play it.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 LINDSEY GRAHAM\uc2  (R-SC): I'll say this: if there's a prosecution of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  for mishandling classified information after the Clinton debacle which you presided over and did a hell of a good job, there'll be riots in the streets.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: He later softened that comment and condemned violence. But is that rhetoric over the line?\par \par \uc2 EMMER: I'm not going to get into other people's words, that would be like me talk about \uc2 Maxine Waters\uc2  telling Americans to confront Republicans in public places, and I think that's wrong.\par \par \uc2 So on this one, I would say he's just echoing what I told you just a couple minutes earlier, going after \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  repeatedly with nothing, trying to impeach him based on nothing more than emotion, and now continuing this charade and invading his home when \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  is allowed to skate and not have any consequences for his behavior -- Mike, this is what the American people see, this double standard.\par \par \uc2 And I'm going to say it again -- they're pissed off, and they're going to show up in November. And I'm confident that they will vote in a new Republican majority in the \uc2 U.S. House of Representatives\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Congressman \uc2 Tom Emmer\uc2 , thanks so much for your time.\par \par \uc2 EMMER: Thank you, Mike.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Up next, there was major outcry this week from parents as we saw new data on how COVID school closures impacted student learning loss. We will ask Governor \uc2 Phil Murphy\uc2  about the fallout and we'll talk a little bit about his potential 2024 plans, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: It is one of the biggest questions of the pandemic era, were schools closed too long? We're starting to see the data just as kids head back to school again. And as governors are pressed to take stock of how they handled school closures, all of this as we see a shortage of teachers around the country. New Jersey's Democratic governor, \uc2 Phil Murphy\uc2 , just met with the White House about how to keep more teachers on the job. I sat down with him this weekend to discuss.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Joining us now, Governor \uc2 Phil Murphy\uc2  of the great state of New Jersey.\par \par \uc2 Governor, welcome back to "FOX News Sunday."\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 PHIL MURPHY\uc2  (D), NEW JERSEY: Good to be on, Mike. Thanks for having me.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Governor, you have praised President Biden's executive action on student loan debt. But what would you say to the millions of New Jerseyans who paid their bills, made sacrifices, even made more affordable education choices who are not getting any money back?\par \par \uc2 MURPHY: Yes, by the way, I'm one of those guys. I paid my student debts off into my mid or late 30s. I would say this, Mike. It's not either/or. I think it's probably a combination and/both.\par \par \uc2 This program gives relief to over a million New Jerseyans. I like the fact that the Pell Grant grantees get double the amount because finding some way to means test this, I think, is important. That's what we do a lot of in New Jersey.\par \par \uc2 But on the margin when you do the cost-benefit analysis, I think this is a really smart and good move.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: What can be done in your view to address the big-picture issue, the underlying issue, the soaring cost of higher education in this country?\par \par \uc2 For example, is it time for an institution like Princeton to tap into its $37 billion-plus endowment to help deserving students?\par \par \uc2 MURPHY: Yes, I mean, you've got a lot of programs out there. Some of them are done by us. Some of them are done by the institutions themselves.\par \par \uc2 We have something called the Community College Opportunity Grant that if you're -- it's a last dollar program. And if you qualify, again, means- tested by income, you go to community college free of charge.\par \par \uc2 We now have something called the Garden State Guarantee that could tack that onto years three and four if you're getting a bachelor's degree.\par \par \uc2 I'm sure institutions themselves can take steps. You see a lot of needs- blind admission programs increasingly.\par \par \uc2 But listen, we need to continue to make higher education more accessible and more affordable, because the premise of your question is right on the money, it has gotten too out of control and too out of reach.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: OK, let's talk COVID. You've promised a postmortem review of COVID since April 2020. When you were asked about COVID last week you said, quote, "We have just under 1,000 people still in the hospital. It is clearly something that we're living with, which is a good thing. But we're also clearly not out of the woods."\par \par \uc2 Does that mean if there's still COVID in New Jersey there's no review?\par \par \uc2 MURPHY: No, it does not, Mike. I think -- I don't have news to break today. But I think we're -- within a matter of weeks of coming up with a comprehensive program. I think I was asked that question in the context of long-term care.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: OK.\par \par \uc2 MURPHY: We've already taken, back in June of '20, a deep dive with an independent firm. But we're going to do the entire COVID response, including long-term care.\par \par \uc2 I want to make sure it's effective, not just in teaching us what went right, what went wrong, but also that it can be a tool for future governors, future administrations.\par \par \uc2 I hope, frankly, it's a trigger for Congress to take a similar step to look at the national response. But no, COVID, sadly is still with us. It will continue to be with us.\par \par \uc2 But we can't wait until the last case to do that review, and we won't. We'll be doing something, starting something soon.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: On school closures, national test results show a real drop in learning. Fourth graders' scores falling by the biggest margin in three decades.\par \par \uc2 In New Jersey, in-person learning wasn't restored until last September, about a year-and-a-half into the COVID pandemic. Question, were schools closed for too long?\par \par \uc2 MURPHY: Listen, I think that's going to be one of those things that folks debate forever and for always. We knew learning loss -- we know it's real. We know the mental health stress on everybody, kids in particular, educators, families.\par \par \uc2 I would say this, particularly early on, but frankly when the variants -- the Delta variant and others kept coming back, when we had so little knowledge about this pandemic and about this virus, we had no other choice, sadly. But we will be laser-focused on closing those gaps which we know exist.\par \par \uc2 I've just become the chair of the \uc2 National Governors Association\uc2 . My Chairman's Initiative is focused on the mental health of our students for this very reason.\par \par \uc2 So it's real, we know it. It's up to us now to close those gaps.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Like many places, New Jersey is dealing with a shortage of teachers. You were just down here in Washington in your role with the \uc2 National Governors Association\uc2  meeting with the education secretary and the first lady.\par \par \uc2 What concrete steps are you bringing to the table to fix the teaching shortage?\par \par \uc2 MURPHY: Yes, it was a -- very good discussions. New Jersey is short, I think the entire country is short. Some of the things that we've done, I think flexibility, Mike, has to be the name of the game.\par \par \uc2 For instance, we've done something which allows retirees to come back in without upsetting their pension reality. We've made it easier for substitute -- qualified substitute teachers to come in.\par \par \uc2 We're trying to get that common ground of making certifications more streamlined and easier without giving up the quality, the high quality. We are blessed with the number one public education system in America. That begins with educators. We don't want to mess that up.\par \par \uc2 We had a great discussion with the first lady and others on apprenticeship programs. I want to see us do more of that in New Jersey. We've got a grant program for diversity and extending diversity among our educators, but I'd love to see a broad-based apprenticeship program like exists in places like Tennessee.\par \par \uc2 So there's -- we're doing stuff, but there is still more work to be done.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Here we are at Labor Day weekend. We're heading into campaign season. The president in recent weeks has blasted what he calls the MAGA philosophy, saying it's like, quote, "semi-fascism."\par \par \uc2 Does that make you uncomfortable, sir?\par \par \uc2 MURPHY: You know, listen, I'm the former U.S. ambassador to Germany. So I, sadly, have seen the history of the 20th Century up close and personal.\par \par \uc2 Listen, I think the president has had a heck of a run here. I saw him the other day and told him that. And whether it's Congress getting stuff passed that he has signed, his own leadership, sadly, for the wrong reasons of what the Supreme Court did with reproductive freedoms, there's no question about it, the Democrats have momentum.\par \par \uc2 And I would say, whether you're a Democrat or a Republican in this case, as Americans we have to call out extreme behavior; we have to call out behavior that undermines institutions; that we need to trust, not undermine -- like our Justice Department, \uc2 FBI\uc2 ; that needs to respect results that we see in elections.\par \par \uc2 So I think that's more of an American reality than it is one party or the other. I think reasonable people in this country, and thank God there are hundreds of millions of them, would come to the same conclusion.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Let's talk 2024. The folks at NJ.com note your skill at being a proud progressive and yet threading the needle, marching with Black Lives Matters protesters and yet never saying defund the police.\par \par \uc2 You want New Jersey to run off 100 percent clean energy by 2050, but you don't talk about the Green New Deal. What about Murphy for president in 2024?\par \par \uc2 MURPHY: Well, I appreciate that, Mike. I've said this publicly and I said it to the president privately. He says he's running. I take him on his word. Assuming he does run, he'll have no bigger backer than yours truly. And I think that's really the base case right now.\par \par \uc2 And in the meantime, I'm going to have my nose pressed against the Jersey glass. And hopefully, whether it's through the \uc2 NGA\uc2  or the \uc2 DGA\uc2 , pick up some good playbooks from other states and other places in the nation, and try to give a Jersey flavor to them and, again, keep moving forward.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: More from the folks at NJ.com, quote, "Maybe you've heard him put more of a national spin on his speeches, declaring the American dream is 'alive and well' in New Jersey. Maybe you've seen him in TV ads touting his record, even though he constitutionally can't run for a third straight term in the Garden State. Or you may have noticed he just made his fourth trip to Israel -- a must-visit for presidential wannabes."\par \par \uc2 That sounds like potentially laying the groundwork, sir.\par \par \uc2 MURPHY: I appreciate that, Mike. By the way, it was my eighth trip, fourth as governor. It's a place that I relish going to.\par \par \uc2 Listen, I think we're based -- we're focused on the kitchen table morning, noon, and night. And I think part of that is we want New Jersey to be affordable, number one. We want it to be the state of opportunity.\par \par \uc2 And I'm proud to say that the American dream, that's our goal. That's the reality, that the American dream is alive and well in New Jersey. And that's what -- that's literally what we're focused on morning, noon, and night. And we'll stay that way.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: OK. So we'll see what the president ultimately decides about 2024. Have you met with donors to talk about 2024? And what if your friend, Senator \uc2 Cory Booker\uc2 , decides to jump in? I mean, I'm from New Jersey, but how much is too Jersey on the national stage?\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 MURPHY: Never mind somebody, perhaps, from the other side of the aisle who could run from Jersey.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Right.\par \par \uc2 MURPHY: And Cory is a -- Cory is a dear friend and a great leader. I have met with no donors about my personal ambitions in that respect.\par \par \uc2 We do have a couple of political vehicles that I'm thankful are well-funded that focus on, frankly, mostly getting the word out in terms of what we've done here, drawing a line.\par \par \uc2 We inherited a state that in many respects was broken. And so, we've gotten ourselves back up on feet. I don't ever want to see us go back.\par \par \uc2 So a lot of the focus of our political operations, if you will, is to get that megaphone out there and say, this is now accepted behavior in New Jersey, no matter who the governor is.\par \par \uc2 And that's what we'll continue to focus mostly on.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Governor \uc2 Phil Murphy\uc2  of the great state of New Jersey, thanks for your time. Enjoy the holiday weekend, sir.\par \par \uc2 MURPHY: Thanks for having me, Mike.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Up next, what former Attorney General Bill Barr says about the \uc2 FBI\uc2  search of his former boss's Mar-a-Lago home. We will bring in our Sunday group on the \uc2 FBI\uc2  raid next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. KEVIN MCCARTHY (R-CA), MINORITY LEADER: When the president speaks tonight at Independence Hall, the first lines out of his mouth should be to apologize for slandering tens of millions of Americans as fascists.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Not every Republican, not even a majority of Republicans are MAGA Republicans. Not every Republican embraces their extreme ideology. I know, because I've been able to work with these mainstream Republicans.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: House Republican Leader Kevin McCarthy saying the president insulted millions of Trump voters and President Biden saying he's only blasting the extremists. And it's time now for our Sunday group: USA Today White House correspondent Francesca Chambers; former State Department spokesperson and FOX News contributor Marie Harf; former Chief of Staff for Vice President Mike Pence Marc Short; and host of "Media Buzz," Howie Kurtz.\par \par \uc2 Welcome to all of you. So, Howie, the president gave a feisty speech in prime time, the broadcast networks did not take it. Who did he reach?\par \par \uc2 HOWARD KURTZ, HOST, "MEDIA BUZZ": Well, he reached his base. But, you know, this was billed as an official White House to vent, non-political. Even after the speech White House officials insisting it was non-political. And it was blatantly partisan, attacking Trump by name and I lost track of how many times he attacked MAGA Republicans. And at the same time though, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is out there talking about, for example, blasting Biden now as the enemy of the state, which is interesting. Enemy of the people, we usually -- journalists have that distinction.\par \par \uc2 Also talking about he should be reinstated, there should be a new election. He's thinking of pardoning January 6th people convicted in the Capitol riot. So Trump keeps making it about himself and 2020. Biden would love to make it about Trump. And even some of Trump's advisers and former advisers tell me this is not helping the GOP because the GOP wants to move forward and not relitigate the 2020 election.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Marie, the president went after MAGA Republicans. He had the marines standing behind him. Democrats were not happy when President Trump was political non-partisan settings. Why is it different now?\par \par \uc2 MARIE HARF, FOX POLITICAL CONTRIBUTOR: Well, I do not think that this should be viewed as a partisan speech. \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  went out of his way to say this isn't all Republicans, this isn't about Democrats versus Republicans, this is about a former president and his supporters saying, as Howie just mentioned, he will pardon people that attacked our Capitol, just blocks from where we are sitting today, Mike, who he said that his political opponents were enemies of the state. He has used this kind of language for years. And we have seen the repercussions. He has encouraged political violence. He has undercut the legitimacy of our elections.\par \par \uc2 And so this isn't about Democrats versus Republicans. President Biden has a duty to stand up on those stages, to use his platform to say American democracy is worth defending. A lot of Republicans agree with him. And that this election coming up isn't about parties, it's about our democracy and those principles. And a lot of people I think on both sides of the aisle are happy that President Biden is finally willing to step up and say very clearly that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and his supporters are a threat to our democracy, period, full stop. And that's what's at stake here.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Francesca, we had former Attorney General Bill Barr come out on Friday saying he's not sure that a special master is needed for going through what the \uc2 FBI\uc2  took from the Mar-a-Lago search. Here is more from the former attorney general on President Trump declassifying documents. Let's listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BILL BARR, FORMER ATTORNEY GENERAL: I frankly am skeptical of this claim that I declassified everything, you know? Because frankly, I think it's highly improbable. And second, if, in fact, he sort of stood over scores of boxes not really knowing what was in them and said, I hereby declassify everything in here, that would be such an abuse that -- and show such recklessness that it's almost worse than taking the documents.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: So what's the political impact of the former president's former ally saying this?\par \par \uc2 FRANCESCA CHAMBERS, WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT, \uc2 USA TODAY\uc2 : Well, in terms of the political impact in the midterm elections of the Mar-a-Lago search, you could expect that that would be negligible compared to issues like the economy, inflation, or abortion rights. But certainly, and we heard this from a former president last night, it is something that would be a central focus of any 2024 presidential campaign. And he again suggested last night that he could run again.\par \par \uc2 Now whether or not even then this is an issue that would propel most Americans to vote for the former president of United States, we saw him focus on those kinds of grievance political issues in 2020, and that just wasn't enough for him to be able to beat \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: All right. So, Marc, there was a big dustup this week between Dr. Oz and \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2 , the Democrat in the Senate race there in Pennsylvania, about whether they will debate each other. There's plenty of spin out there from each side. The reality is so far there are zero confirmed debates in any of these big Senate races. What's the political impact for voters?\par \par \uc2 MARC SHORT, FORMER MIKE PENCE CHIEF OF STAFF: Mike, I think that this will resolve itself. I think often there's a lot of this jockeying and they will end up having debates. I think usually it's an untenable position not to. And i think it's important for Pennsylvanian voters to know whether Fetterman is up to this task physically as well. And so I think there will be plenty debates in the process moving forward.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Howie, Minority Leader \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  has complained about a candidate problem, candidate quality issue in some of the Senate races. Senator \uc2 Rick Scott\uc2 , who is leading the Senate Republican campaign committee, firing back very publicly saying, quote, "When you complain and lament that we have bad candidates, what you're really saying is that you have contempt for the voters who chose them. Much of Washington's chattering class disrespects and secretly or not so secretly loathes of Republican voters." Have you ever seen this kind of spat?\par \par \uc2 KURTZ: I've seen jabs, I've never seen this kind of open tribal warfare between two Republican leaders. I mean, \uc2 Rick Scott\uc2  wrote this blistering piece in the Washington Examiner essentially while not naming McConnell but everybody knew he was talking about, essentially saying that this was treasonous, McConnell should shut up. It's really quite a spectacle.\par \par \uc2 If I could just circle back for one minute to the DoJ investigation of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , and that is everybody in the press now is saying, well, he's going to be indicted, he's going to go to jail, you know, lock him up. I think he may well not be indicted unless -- just unclassified documents unless \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  has something else showing overt actions to harm national security. So I think it's very difficult for him to bring charges just on the classified documents given leniency in the past towards some prominent Democrats.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Interesting. OK, so back to McConnell v. Scott. Is it helpful when Republicans are bickering with each other, Marc?\par \par \uc2 SHORT: Well, Mike, I don't think it is but I'm not sure that a lot of Americans can concentrate on that. I think that in 65 days we are going to have a decisive victory if we are talking about crime in \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's America, if we're talking about a crisis at the border, 200,000 illegals crossing every month, if we're talking about inflation, if we're talking about the reality that we are going into a deeper recession and will be by November. If instead we are about -- taking AOC talking points about defunding law enforcement or pardoning criminals, then that's going to get us off message.\par \par \uc2 If we are talking about \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , which I think he did with that terrible speech the other night that certainly was political, then I think certainly in my mind, remembers the notion of Hillary Clinton calling millions and millions of Trump-Pence supporters "deplorables." That in essence is what he's done again by calling us semi-fascists, then I think that's going to rally a lot of Republicans in November.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Do think he will be forced to apologize for the "semi-fascist" comment?\par \par \uc2 SHORT: Whether he apologizes or not, I think it's a rallying cry and I think that Republicans are going to have a decisive victory in 65 days if we're talking but \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . And what his speech did is it put him right back in the center of the debate to make it a referendum on \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Francesca, FOX News Digital reported this week that there's Republican frustration on how little money former President Trump is sharing of the money that he's raising, the millions and millions of dollars he's raising with other Republicans. One GOP strategist telling FOX Republicans will remember who helped and who did not.\par \par \uc2 Is there a risk of Mr. Trump burning more goodwill in the Republican Party?\par \par \uc2 CHAMBERS: Well, he is certainly expending political capital, as we saw him do in Pennsylvania yesterday evening. But when it comes to advertising, any -- there's no amount of money that you can spend to essentially introduce candidates to voters in the amount of time that they have between now and Election Day who are politically inexperienced. And that's an issue that Republicans are running into this cycle when they are competing against Democrats who have a record, who have run statewide. In Pennsylvania, for instance, the Democrats on the ballot this year outperformed President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  in 2020.\par \par \uc2 And so sometimes those records can work against Democrats for sure, but at the same time, that is something that Democrats see as working in their favor this election cycle.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Marie, there's a broader midterm theme here, Republicans want to make it about President Biden, Democrats want to make it about former President Trump. Is there any issue overlap here or are the parties in their separate lanes?\par \par \uc2 HARF: Well, I think increasingly Democrats want to make it about what President Biden and Democrats in Congress have gotten done. You heard \uc2 Sean Patrick Maloney\uc2  say that earlier. The infrastructure deal, burn pits and health care for veterans, for example, negotiating prescription drug prices. And so I think Democrats want to say, look, this is what we've done when we've been in charge.\par \par \uc2 And in all the states, whether it's \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  in Georgia or Dr. Oz in Pennsylvania or J.D. Vance in Ohio, these are extreme candidates. And for Republicans who have spent so much time calling my party socialist, communist, anti-American, anti-Christian, to suddenly be clutching their pearls because \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  says democracy is worth defending? That's a message Democrats are willing to take to every state, especially when it's extremist candidates on the other side in all of these states. Democrats feel good today, Mike.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: All right, panel, thanks very much.\par \par \uc2 KURTZ: We (ph) feel good too.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: See you next Sunday.\par \par \uc2 Up next, an important note for next week.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Before we go, you may have heard our colleague, a tremendous journalist, Shannon Bream will take over this chair permanently starting this coming Sunday, September 11th.\par \par \uc2 Shannon sat down with Howie Kurtz for a "Media Buzz" interview on her life, her career, and how she approaches newsmaker interviews.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KURTZ: They're all going to try to stick to their talking points. How do you tackle that?\par \par \uc2 SHANNON BREAM, INCOMING "FOX NEWS SUNDAY" HOST: That's the trick I think in Washington. I love getting some personal time with people to just kind of talk to them about their family or their interests. And you kind of disarm them a little bit in that you find out what really motivates them and kind of what lights their fire.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: You can see the full interview on "Media Buzz" today on FOX News Channel. And you will see Shannon right here next Sunday morning. Congratulations to my dear friend.\par \par \uc2 That's it for today. I'm Mike Emanuel. Have a great week and we will see you next "FOX News Sunday."\par \par \uc2 Document FOXN000020220904ei9400001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Time To Focus On Issues That Actually Matter; Companies Reduce Paid Maternity Leave, Expand Access To Abortions And Egg Freezing; It Is Time To Encourage A Strong Nuclear Family\b0\par\par\uc2 Tucker Carlson, Bill Melugin \par \uc2 7,483 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 September 2022\par \uc2 Fox News Channel: Tucker Carlson Tonight\par \uc2 TCARL\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 PETE HEGSETH, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST, "FOX AND FRIENDS" WEEKEND: So, I don't think I have those problems, but that means my team is usually not good.\par \par \uc2 WILL CAIN, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST, "FOX AND FRIENDS" WEEKEND: And if the punishment is a big tattoo, you have no problem with that.\par \par \uc2 HEGSETH: I would love that. So that's my kind of punishment.\par \par \uc2 All right, everybody, that's all for tonight.\par \par \uc2 We'll see you guys tomorrow morning. Rachel, Will and I right here in this studio, 6:00 AM. Don't miss it.\par \par \uc2 Thanks for being with us.\par \par \uc2 Tucker is up next.\par \par \uc2 TUCKER CARLSON\uc2 , FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST, "TUCKER CARLSON TONIGHT": Well, good evening and welcome to a Special Edition, an analytical edition of TUCKER CARLSON TONIGHT.\par \par \uc2 So, everybody is focused on this fall's midterms for good reason, but maybe bigger elections have already happened, more meaningful elections. These were local races that you probably haven't heard about because nobody pays attention.\par \par \uc2 Here is what they were.\par \par \uc2 Last week, the Republican Party flipped five majority-left School Districts in big counties in Florida. That would be Miami-Dade, Sarasota, Duval, Martin, and Clay Counties.\par \par \uc2 Miami-Dade alone is one of the biggest School Districts in the entire country. It's got more than 300,000 students in it. So, this change has major implications for the education and the future of millions of young people, and it is not just happening in Florida.\par \par \uc2 A Texas School Board that flipped to the Republican Party earlier this year is already working to change things. They just voted to ban race hatred and gender cult ideology in schools.\par \par \uc2 So, there may be a lesson here. Let's say you're a Republican running for Congress in November. You might be wondering, "How did these Republicans win at the local level?"\par \par \uc2 It wasn't just because it's a red wave year, because actually it's not really a red wave year so far, despite what you may have heard. In other races so far, special elections, Republicans have been underperforming, amazingly.\par \par \uc2 For example, in a special election last week in New York's 19th Congressional District, Democrat Pat Ryan beat Republican \uc2 Marc Molinaro\uc2 . Now in a red wave year, you would have thought the opposite would happen. This was a very tight district. This district went for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  by only a point and a half in the last election. That's not exactly a huge margin for the Democrats, but Democrats won anyway, despite the fact \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's approval numbers have dropped dramatically since then.\par \par \uc2 And it's not just in that district. Democratic candidates across the country have been outperforming \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's 2020 numbers in at least three other House special elections, elections in Nebraska, Minnesota, and in the 23rd District of New York.\par \par \uc2 So, why is the question. How could this be happening?\par \par \uc2 Well, the Democratic Party has a really simple explanation: Abortion. Everything is about abortion.\par \par \uc2 Democratic voters are really mad about Roe v. Wade being repealed and they're voting in bigger numbers, but there isn't actually any evidence at all to support that that's true. Instead, there is evidence that the Republican Party may be at fault by alienating its own voters and not getting them to the polls.\par \par \uc2 Ryan Girdusky has done a lot of work on this. He runs the 1776 Project PAC. He supported those Florida School Board candidates that won last week.\par \par \uc2 Girdusky recently wrote on his \uc2 Substack\uc2  that Republicans are underperforming this year because turnout in rural counties is only about 30 percent -- 30 percent of what it was in 2018, the midterm that year. Cities and suburbs, meanwhile, which are Democratic strongholds, had doubled that level of turnout.\par \par \uc2 So, why are Republicans winning in School Board races in Florida, but losing in big races in rural areas that they should be winning? Well, the answer might lie with the candidates and what they're talking about in those districts.\par \par \uc2 For example, in February, \uc2 Marc Molinaro\uc2 , the guy who just lost in the race he should have won in New York, told the County Office Building to illuminate the building in red and blue. Now, those are not the county colors, they are Ukraine's colors. Right? As if his voters care. That's the message that Molinaro, as executive director of Duchess County, New York, sent to voters: "Poughkeepsie stands with Ukraine." Right.\par \par \uc2 Everyone is sympathetic to Ukraine, but is that really the main issue? No, it's a joke.\par \par \uc2 The same candidate also sent fundraising e-mails to voters throughout upstate New York calling for more aggression against Russia. "Biden is weak on Putin," Molinaro wrote. Right.\par \par \uc2 So, does sounding like \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  actually work? Our borders are racist, but Ukraine's borders are sacrosanct. It doesn't work. Here's what he said.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 LINDSEY GRAHAM\uc2  (R-SC): The question is, "Can we do more and should we do more?" The answer is yes to both.\par \par \uc2 As to my friends in The Pentagon, you told all of us that this war would last three or four days, that Kyiv would fall, that, you know, that the country would be overwhelmed by the Russian military. They were wrong.\par \par \uc2 So, quite frankly, I trust President Zelenskyy's judgment about what would help him in real time more than anybody else on the planet right now.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Okay, so just to be clear to Republican candidates, that was \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2 . Your job is to make fun of \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  and to disavow \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2 . Your job is not to emulate \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  and steal his talking points, and if you do emulate \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  and steal those talking points, you will lose and the losing candidates did just that.\par \par \uc2 So, compare what they were saying to what, for example, Joe Kent was saying in Washington State when he won his primary there as a challenger. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE KENT (R), WASHINGTON STATE CONGRESSIONAL CANDIDATE: If any of these representatives feel so passionately about how we have to go and save the Ukrainians and provide enough lethal aid and continue to push this escalation, they need to go back to their districts and tell the American people, tell their constituents what they're asking them to do and what the ramifications potentially could be.\par \par \uc2 Why do foreign nations always come first? Why do we secure their borders and not our own?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Well, exactly. It's nothing against the Ukrainians. Again, everyone feels sorry for the Ukrainians, but Americans have a right to feel sad about what's happening in their country and to expect their politicians to work on fixing it.\par \par \uc2 So, if more Republicans in November say what you just heard Joe Kent say, they will win. If they talk about the issues that people actually care about in this country, issues that are relevant to their lives, they will win. That's how they won those School Board seats in Florida.\par \par \uc2 They weren't talking about Ukraine. They were talking about the issues that mattered to the voters in Florida: Family, safety, happiness, the right of parents to decide what their kids learn in school. That message wins.\par \par \uc2 Obviously, that's why Glenn Youngkin became Governor of Virginia.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GOV. GLENN YOUNGKIN (R-VA): We're going to embrace our parents, not ignore them. We're going to press forward with a curriculum that includes listening to parents' input, a curriculum that allows our children to run as fast as they can, teaching them how to think, enabling their dreams to soar.\par \par \uc2 Friends, we are going to reestablish excellence in our schools.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: So, that was Glenn Youngkin's message. "You're a parent. Schools exist for your children and they're your children. They don't belong to the teachers' union and we're going to listen to you."\par \par \uc2 Now, \uc2 Terry McAuliffe\uc2  was running against Glenn Youngkin. He had had the advantage of a lot of money. He'd been Governor of the State before. Here was his message and we're quoting, "I don't think parents should be telling schools what they can teach."\par \par \uc2 Oh, it turns out most people disagree and he was destroyed. His political career hit the brick wall it had long deserved.\par \par \uc2 So, it turns out the Republicans who focus on the things that matter -- families, protecting families -- will win. Along with the 1776 project, \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  was one of the biggest supporters of those School Board candidates last week.\par \par \uc2 This year, DeSantis signed a law banning teachers' unions members from talking about sex with your kindergartners because it's creepy and should be illegal. DeSantis also fired a prosecutor who refused to enforce the law.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GOV. RON DESANTIS (R-FL): The prosecutor, State Attorney for this judicial circuit, Andrew Warren, has put himself publicly above the law.\par \par \uc2 In June of 2021, he signed a letter saying that he would not enforce any prohibitions on sex change operations for minors and then most recently, after the Dobbs decision was rendered by the \uc2 US Supreme Court\uc2 , he signed a letter saying he would not enforce any laws relating to protecting the right to life in the State of Florida. And mind you, we have had prohibition on third trimester abortions for a long time.\par \par \uc2 When you make yourself above the law, you have violated your duty, you have neglected your duty, and you are displaying a lack of competence to be able to perform those duties and so today, we are suspending State Attorney Andrew Warren effective immediately.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: So, enforce the law. That's a winning message, too. It's not just about one corrupt prosecutor in the State of Florida, it is about ending the lawlessness that makes it impossible for Americans to live in their own cities.\par \par \uc2 Blake Masters\uc2  caught on to that message. That's how he won his primary in Arizona.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BLAKE MASTERS (R), ARIZONA SENATE CANDIDATE: We can never amnesty any illegal alien. Period. If your first interaction with our country is to break its immigration laws, you will never get amnesty. You will never become a United States citizen. That is a line in the sand. We will never cross it.\par \par \uc2 We have to stop incentivizing illegal immigration. If you're not deporting people, you don't have border security.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Well, that's not hard. What's the point of having a country if your own government ignores the laws that your Representatives passed? How is that democracy? No, it's chaos and lawlessness and ultimately, it's autocracy.\par \par \uc2 So, national Republicans are not that dumb. They could have figured that out, but they're saying just the opposite. It's almost like they want to lose. They're calling for gun confiscation -- gun confiscation -- guns from law-abiding citizens, which would make you as reliant as possible on police forces that are underfunded.\par \par \uc2 How underfunded? Have you checked the response times in your city recently?\par \par \uc2 In the \uc2 City of New Orleans\uc2 , according to researcher Jeff Asher, the police response time is now over two hours, long enough for you to get killed. The city is talking about canceling Mardi Gras because they have no cops.\par \par \uc2 In Seattle, the City Council just reported, "average and median response time is up in nearly every precinct when compared with the last four years of data." Okay.\par \par \uc2 In Austin, Texas, the Police Chief recently admitted that response times to the city's most serious emergencies are "dramatically slower."\par \par \uc2 So, that's a huge problem and it puts the onus of self-protection on the individual, on the family. You have to protect your own family. You don't have a choice at this point. It's always been your right. Now, it's your obligation. Are Republicans recognizing this?\par \par \uc2 Well, here is \uc2 John Cornyn\uc2 , who represents Texas calling for gun confiscation so that you can't protect yourself when the cops don't show up.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 JOHN CORNYN\uc2  (R-TX): I don't want us to pass a bill for the purpose of checking a box. I want to make sure we actually do something useful, something that is capable of becoming a law, something that will have the potential to save lives.\par \par \uc2 I'm happy to report, as a result of the hard work of a number of senators in this chamber, that we've made some serious progress.\par \par \uc2 So soon, very soon, not soon enough for me, but very soon, we will see the text of bipartisan legislation that will help keep our children and our communities safer.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: It won't save a single child and he knows that. What it will do is take firearms away from the law-abiding while they remain in the hands of the law-breaking. It will make the country more dangerous.\par \par \uc2 That's a Republican United States senator, an incumbent who unfortunately is not up for election this year, but God-willing, will be punished for what he did.\par \par \uc2 But the point is, the Republican Party in Washington seems to have missed all of this.\par \par \uc2 Look at the elections taking place already this year, and that's the clearest possible sign to the people who run the Republican Party to stop talking about Ukraine, stop taking people's guns away. Start talking about things that they care about: The economy, education, family, safety, justice, following the law, our border.\par \par \uc2 Those are the things that matter, and if you don't acknowledge that, you're going to lose.\par \par \uc2 Jason Whitlock is the host of "Fearless." He joins us tonight.\par \par \uc2 Jason Whitlock, thanks so much for coming on.\par \par \uc2 I guess, this is a species of every other story we talk about. There is a huge disconnect between the people who make the decisions, and the people who have to live under them, but the Republican Party almost seems like it wants to lose.\par \par \uc2 JASON WHITLOCK, HOST, "FEARLESS": Tucker, I'm in agreement with you.\par \par \uc2 Look, the issue is very clear to me what everybody should be leaning into. What world are we leaving our kids? That's what will motivate people to go to the polls.\par \par \uc2 Are we properly educating our kids? Are we allowing people with gender dysphoria to groom our kids into their gender dysphoria? Are we providing a safe environment for our kids and adults to live freely and have the opportunity that you and I had growing up as young people?\par \par \uc2 Are we protecting our borders so that the kids that have a right and are American citizens have the best opportunity to achieve their American Dream? That's what motivates people to vote.\par \par \uc2 We, I think, well, not we, the Republican Party making a mistake. I think they're leaning too heavily into, well, we can just discredit \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is not the problem. The Democratic Party is the problem. It's a cult that is trying to destroy America, trying to remove all of its biblical values, trying to groom our kids and trying to fundamentally rewrite, throw out this Constitution that serve me, serve you, serve them.\par \par \uc2 They've sold us out, and people need to stand -- what world are we leaving these kids? And if we look, we're not doing a great job. We should be embarrassed.\par \par \uc2 We're leaving them a world that is unlike anything we've ever seen here in America.\par \par \uc2 I don't have kids, Tucker. My motivation, though, is my childhood was so phenomenal and I was poor. I was poor, but my childhood was so phenomenal, I want to leave that world to other kids and we're not.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Yes, that is absolutely true and I think you make a wise point about Biden. No one really supports Biden. Everyone knows Biden doesn't even really exist in a meaningful sense, but if you're hoping you're going to get elected because he is incompetent, that's not how it works.\par \par \uc2 WHITLOCK: The Democratic Party has been so aggressive with trying to smear the Republican Party. Every Republican is a racist.\par \par \uc2 Why aren't we defining the Democratic Party as a satanic cult? Because it is.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: I am with you a hundred percent. I am. I think that's true.\par \par \uc2 Jason Whitlock, great to see you. Thank you for that.\par \par \uc2 Kind of sums it up.\par \par \uc2 WHITLOCK: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: So here is a question we should all be thinking about deeply. I know, we're not allowed to question the last election, but a lot of people don't think it was on the level.\par \par \uc2 So, what does happen when huge groups of voters start to believe their votes don't matter? So, I went Iowa recently and gave a speech, thinking about that very issue, that's straight ahead.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: We went to went to Iowa this summer and gave a speech at the Family Leadership Summit out there to make a simple point, really, the most meaningful points are pretty simple and here is what we said: The Republican Party needs to start paying attention to what its voters actually care about, and if they don't, things are likely to get volatile.\par \par \uc2 Here's part of it.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: What happens when people think their votes don't matter? Well, I mean, we saw it happen. A January 6th.\par \par \uc2 They get moved to action, they get so frustrated. No one will listen to me, there is nothing I can do about it, I'm showing up. I'm going to go to their doorstep. And over time, it gets worse than that.\par \par \uc2 So if you want to keep your political system stable, you have to pay attention to what people actually want, and you have to pay attention to their deepest concerns, not their peripheral ideological concerns.\par \par \uc2 Are we doing enough for trans swimmers? Can we prevent, you know, Putin from taking Crimea? What are we doing about climate change?\par \par \uc2 I mean, you know, you can make the case of all of important things, I guess, but they are not more important than your actual concerns.\par \par \uc2 And too often Republican voters -- and this is the rule on the left, it should not be the rule in the Republican Party, however -- they allow their politicians to say, "No, no, I know, you want that. Let me tell you what you really want. You want --"\par \par \uc2 It's like, no, actually, let me tell you what I really want since you're my representative.\par \par \uc2 [APPLAUSE]\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: So -- and that means you represent. So the way I judge it, and when I'm putting the show together in the morning and at night, banging out the script, I try to pay attention to the markers, the real markers of societal health, and those are the basics.\par \par \uc2 The farther you get away from the basics, the more misled you are, the farther from the target you are. So what are the basics in your life?\par \par \uc2 Well, if you're a parent, there's one basic, how are the children doing? You're as happy as your unhappiest child. Of course. How are your children doing? How are they doing physically, emotionally, and spiritually? Are they thriving? Do they live in a country that promises them the ability to thrive going forward? Will you die knowing your descendants will be as happy and secure as you have been?\par \par \uc2 Is there something that matters more than that? Is there a more important question than that? No, there's not. And anybody who says there is, again, they are so deluded that person should not have power or that person is lying to you. Period.\par \par \uc2 So that shouldn't be the single minded focus of any political party, particularly the Republican Party because there is no option.\par \par \uc2 The other party is -- and you see this happen in politics, though, never more so than now -- the parties, of course, pivot against each other, "Oh, you're for that, I'm for the other thing."\par \par \uc2 It's childish, but it's real. So, all of a sudden have a political party that is making a case against having children, making a case for devoting your life to some soulless multinational corporation.\par \par \uc2 I'm not against all businesses. I've always been for free enterprise. But when I hear people say, you know, abortion is the most important thing, the most important rights that we have, I ask myself, "What are you really saying?"\par \par \uc2 I mean, honestly, think about it for a second. And some -- and I am a pro- lifer, just to be completely clear, a fervent one. But what are they really saying?\par \par \uc2 So conservatives, you know, I think very often begin with the debate over whether this is a life and whether abortion is ending a life. Well, it is not really a debate, everybody knows that it is. Everybody knows, everybody knows. Even people screaming, "I'm proud of my abortion."\par \par \uc2 If you really felt good about it, you probably wouldn't be so brutal and loud, would you?\par \par \uc2 I'm really happy about my marriage, I am not putting on a sign and yelling to people, "I am happy with my marriage." It suggests maybe I'm not quite as happy as I claim to be.\par \par \uc2 "I'm proud of my abortion." Yes. No, you're not. You're sad about it, because it's inherently sad.\par \par \uc2 You know, it is not really a debate. We can debate what limit should be put on it, but we need to take three steps back and ask ourselves, "What is this exactly? What are they really saying?" What are they promising to the American population when they promote this?\par \par \uc2 And what they're, of course saying and what Citibank and \uc2 Nike\uc2 , \uc2 Dick's Sporting Goods\uc2  and all these huge companies that are affirmatively promoting abortion by paying their female employees to have abortions, they are suddenly admitting it is female employees, which is, you know take your victories as you get them.\par \par \uc2 What are they really saying? They're really saying, it is more important to serve us than to have a family. You will be happier as you rise within our company than you would be if you had your own children.\par \par \uc2 Now, I never heard any Republican pushback against this, but I can't imagine a more grotesque and obvious lie. Children are the most enduring source of joy -- this is not an ideological point. It is true.\par \par \uc2 [APPLAUSE]\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Even when they go wrong, as they do -- and by the way, it's never the parents' fault. Father of four, a little offensive -- no, my kids are great, but they do go wrong, actually. And sometimes tragedies occur with your own children, and there's nothing more heart wrenching, but it doesn't detract from the unchangeable fact that children are the main source of joy and meaning in the human life. Period. Period.\par \par \uc2 So anyone who tells you, "No, what you really want, what you really want, Miss 28-year-old Citibank employee, is to move up to Assistant Vice President in charge of international bonds. Really? Really?\par \par \uc2 Is someone from the HR department going to hold my hand in hospice 40 years from now? Is the company going to love me unconditionally? No.\par \par \uc2 It's a lie. It's a lie. And in telling that lie, they are stripping young Americans of the promise of the only thing that matters, which is having a family.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: I hate to admit this because it is so shameful, but this country has the highest rate of single parenthood in the world. That doesn't bother the people who run the country. In fact, some of the biggest corporations in America have just taken a major step to make that problem even worse, we'll tell you what they're doing next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BILL MELUGIN, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: Welcome to "FOX News Live," I'm Bill Melugin here in Los Angeles.\par \par \uc2 Iran's Navy briefly seized two American drones in the Red Sea before letting them go as US warships approached. Iranian state television airing video showing sailors examining the two \uc2 Saildrone\uc2  explorers before then tossing them overboard.\par \par \uc2 According to the \uc2 US Navy\uc2 , that drone were unarmed and they were taking unclassified photos of the area. This is now the second such incident in recent days.\par \par \uc2 Plus, a fast moving wildfire forcing thousands of people from their homes in three northern California towns. Fire officials in the town of Weed say the fire that began earlier today at a lumber mill has already destroyed several homes and there are injuries.\par \par \uc2 There are also several wildfires burning here in Southern California as a heatwave pushes temperatures above triple digits.\par \par \uc2 I'm Bill Melugin. Now, we'll send you right back to TUCKER CARLSON TONIGHT.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Welcome back to a Special Edition of TUCKER CARLSON TONIGHT.\par \par \uc2 Some of the biggest companies in the United States are now making it harder for their employees, for Americans to raise kids.\par \par \uc2 According to data from researchers at the \uc2 Society for Human Resource Management\uc2 , the percentage of companies offering paid maternity leave beyond the legal minimum requirements dropped to 35 percent this year. That's down from 53 percent just two years ago.\par \par \uc2 "The Wall Street Journal" reports that big companies like \uc2 Disney\uc2  are the ones primarily making the cuts. Are you surprised? And at the same time, you should know these companies are offering to pay their employees thousands of dollars if they only get that abortion. Okay.\par \par \uc2 So companies are encouraging abortion. Why is that?\par \par \uc2 Rebeccah Heinrichs is with the \uc2 Hudson Institute\uc2 . She's also the mother of five. She joins us tonight.\par \par \uc2 Rebeccah, thanks so much for coming on.\par \par \uc2 So, why would \uc2 Disney\uc2  or any other big publicly held company want its female employees have abortions?\par \par \uc2 REBECCAH HEINRICHS, \uc2 HUDSON INSTITUTE\uc2 : Well, it's better for the business, it's better for the business and there is a political and cultural demand that they are perceiving. Not only did companies like \uc2 Disney\uc2 , \uc2 Amazon\uc2 , and \uc2 Kroger\uc2  come out and say that they're going to now offer abortion coverage in the healthcare that they offer, they are also going to pay for travel for employees who want abortions who are in red states who have more restrictive, aka more humane, abortion regimes post Roe, they're going to now pay for their travel to leave that State to access an abortion.\par \par \uc2 So, they can find all of this money in the couch cushions post Roe, but they can't seem to find enough money to make sure that they have flexible care, more maternity, paid maternity for new mothers who are having new babies or think about having babies or more time at home with their new children who they have now through adoption, Tucker.\par \par \uc2 So again, it's good for their bottom line for people to have abortions, but also that they think that there's this demand signal from political leaders and from the culture that they're willing to go to the press and advertise that they're now offering this abortion services.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: It's just so openly evil, because what they're saying to their employees is, we don't care about you at all. We don't want you to have a personal life or something that might enrich you, much less children to take care of you in old age. We only care about your labor. And so whatever we can do to keep you atomized and alone, we will pay for.\par \par \uc2 I mean, is anyone saying anything about that?\par \par \uc2 HEINRICHS: Well, I mean, obviously, it's inhumane. It's barbaric. I often think, you know, I study geopolitics, and not just how the United States can continue to compete with authoritarian countries, but also what makes the United States great and worth defending.\par \par \uc2 I mean, what is the American Dream? Is the American Dream taking a woman