estroy the American society in an intentional way, and remove the father as the figure and put in the State as the authority figure.\par \par \uc2 And, you know, I have to go back in this whole situation, Tucker, and one of the basic premises of this country which is a self-governing Republic in which individuals were expected to govern themselves, which would lead to more freedom.\par \par \uc2 And part and parcel of that self-governance of the individual is a strong father figure, leading the family unit, and inculcating the idea of self- government. But if you remove those guardrails, those barriers of self- governing, you're actually actively encouraging chaos, and one of the basic human desires is for peace in order.\par \par \uc2 But if individuals and families can't or won't govern themselves, you're asking the State to insert itself into that chaos to provide some semblance of order, and with more State comes more authoritarianism.\par \par \uc2 And if we really want to get a society that wants to solve some of these problems, you'll look at the fatherless situation where, without a father, it's four times more likely to live in poverty, more drug use, more alcohol use. Seventy percent of those in juvenile detention centers come from fatherless homes. Ninety percent of school shooters come from fatherless homes.\par \par \uc2 If we want to actually fix this problem and have more freedom and prosperity, we have to have a society and a government that actually respects human life and actually respects the primacy of traditional marriage between a man and woman and has policies that actually encourage and strengthen the traditional family unit.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: So, we don't need to destroy the patriarchy, you mean? Ned Ryun - -\par \par \uc2 RYUN: Absolutely not.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Yes, absolutely not. Great to see you. Thank you so much for that.\par \par \uc2 RYUN: Thanks, Tucker.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: So, one thing you never hear leaders talk about ever is the one thing that really matters, which is beauty and most beauty comes from nature because God created nature and that's the measure of all beauty.\par \par \uc2 So in Iowa, we kind of pointed that out, because it's one of the most important things. We'll show you a clip straight ahead.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: One thing you almost never hear anybody in authority talking about is the one thing that every person needs in order to be happy, the one thing that uplifts the human spirit more reliably than any other, which is beauty. And most beauty comes from nature.\par \par \uc2 So you know if an idea is bad, it produces ugly things and with that in mind, you should be concerned that American architecture post-war is some of the ugliest in the world. The leaders seem to go out of their way to uglify things rather than beautify.\par \par \uc2 But again, nobody ever says anything about this, missing the most obvious thing of all -- kind of strange. We spoke at length about this we went to Iowa.\par \par \uc2 Here is part of it.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Can your children grow up in a country pretty much like the one you grew up in? With some improvements, things change. It's not a matter of being frozen in amber, it always has to be 1985, though, personally, I think that was a pretty good year.\par \par \uc2 [LAUGHTER]\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: But can you give that to your descendants? And do you have descendants?\par \par \uc2 And by the way, if life expectancy falls, if fertility rates fall, if testosterone levels fall, sperm counts levels fall -- all of which have by margins that are unprecedented in American history, and nobody notices, and there is no candidate who says, "Wait a second." People aren't having kids like -- find a clearer measure of societal health.\par \par \uc2 If you believe in the future, if you feel confident in your economy, and your society, you have pups. Of course. They should start there.\par \par \uc2 Here is the second thing they should do, and no one will ever do this, but I think this every day, they should care about beauty.\par \par \uc2 I don't think I've ever heard a politician mention beauty and I think about it constantly. I think about it on a couple levels.\par \par \uc2 First, I think about it on a political level. So, you -- and I'm hardly a Bible scholar, but of course, how do you judge a tree?\par \par \uc2 CROWD: By its fruits.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: By its fruits. Right. Don't judge it by, you know what the arborist told you it was. You wait until it produces something and then you know what it is. Oh, those are pears. They told me it was an apple tree. Well, I guess, they guessed wrong.\par \par \uc2 [LAUGHTER]\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: So the way that I judge ideas is by their fruit, and not just their ideological fruit, their physical, tangible, observable fruit, the things they make.\par \par \uc2 So, everything that we see around us is the product of ideas, of ideologies, that's where everything starts.\par \par \uc2 In the beginning, there was the word. It starts with a word, always and here is how we know if the ideology is a good one.\par \par \uc2 Noble ideologies produce beautiful results. They produce beauty. Poisonous ideologies produce ugliness, super simple.\par \par \uc2 There is a reason the architecture in Bulgaria, in 1975, was hideous, because Soviet 1975 was controlled by the Soviet Union.\par \par \uc2 Soviet architecture was horrifying. So, is architecture under Mao. They knocked down everything worth having, built during the preceding millennia, and they replaced it with concrete boxes. Why did they do that? Because those boxes, those buildings, that architecture, that design, send a very clear message, which is, you are worth nothing. There is no beauty for you.\par \par \uc2 Now, why is beauty so important? And why did tyrants always destroy it?\par \par \uc2 Well, they destroy it because beauty reminds you that the most important things are eternal, they're unchanging. Beauty is recognizable across cultures, across centuries. You can go to a place, a culture completely unfamiliar with, and if you see something beautiful, you recognize it immediately. Why? Because beauty has balance, and symmetry, and grace, you know it instantly.\par \par \uc2 A beautiful face in this culture is very similar to a beautiful face in that culture -- beautiful building, same thing. It has balance. Why? Why does beauty -- because it's derived from nature. It is derived from God's creation.\par \par \uc2 So, the closer we are to God's creation, the straighter the path, and the better the path.\par \par \uc2 So I look -- I look at the country because I've traveled, I've been in all 50 states. I've been -- as I said, I've been everywhere because I did it for a living for so long, and I noticed that virtually everything built after 1945, less attractive, less pleasing, less human-centered than everything built before.\par \par \uc2 I don't know what town you all live in, but think about your own town. The prettiest buildings, there were built in 1920. That's just true.\par \par \uc2 Bridge, front porches, steel roof -- the closer you get to our current moment, the more everything looks like a Dollar Store.\par \par \uc2 [LAUGHTER]\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: And the Republican Party has somehow found itself in this position where they feel like they have to defend the aesthetics of the Dollar Store and the economics of it. "Oh, it's the Dollar Store. That's free enterprise." No, it's not. It's an atrocity that diminishes people, that destroys God's creation, that oppresses us with its ugliness. That is true.\par \par \uc2 [APPLAUSE]\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: And no one wants to say it because like, I don't know, some libertarian think tank was paid to tell you that the Dollar Store is attractive. I don't think it is, actually. I don't think it is.\par \par \uc2 Their model, the people in charge have this kind of -- if I were to pick sort of their idea of beauty, it would be like a Dollar Store next to multifamily low-income housing, spray painted with unintelligible slogans. Right? That's not beauty.\par \par \uc2 Their idea of beauty is like a female impersonator screaming at you. No. Beauty is nature.\par \par \uc2 So you can tell me whatever you want about your commitment to the environment, but if you're destroying it with wind turbines, I'm sorry, I know a lot of people don't make a lot of money from wind turbines, it's just true. As an outdoorsman, like really? You're not for nature.\par \par \uc2 The Republican Party should be for nature, and not just in its aesthetics, not just in its design, but in its human relationships.\par \par \uc2 People are born wanting certain things, I have four dogs. So, I see this on display, and they all sleep on the bed.\par \par \uc2 [LAUGHTER]\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: With four dogs, four children, like the one thing I get a lot of is watching genetics at work.\par \par \uc2 [LAUGHTER]\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: And I know that people and dogs behave most of the time out of impulses they were born with that they may not even fully understand, but that do not change. Period. And that is true of all people. Okay? All people.\par \par \uc2 So politicians who don't take that into account are not serving you at all. So if -- you know, if I were advising a politician, I would say the first thing I would do, like "What is America?" Well, America is a physical place. No, it's not an idea. Anyone who says America is an idea, please. It is not an idea, it is a place, I live there.\par \par \uc2 [LAUGHTER]\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: I don't live in an idea. I don't live theoretically. I got a bed and there is like a ground underneath me.\par \par \uc2 [LAUGHTER]\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: There's like soil and trees.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: It feels like about every six months, a secret committee in the basement of some building somewhere comes up with a new fad that you're required to follow, absolutely required -- BLM, the vax, Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 One thing you know about every one of these fads is that Republican leaders fall for all of them. Why? We answer that question straight ahead.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: You may have noticed that nobody has political conversations in this country, much less debates. Everything is about the fad of the moment and they change every few months.\par \par \uc2 First it was BLM because George Floyd was murdered. Huh? Shut up, he was. Then the COVID vaccine, which you must take or else you're against science, don't ask questions. And then Ukraine whose borders are sacrosanct while yours are racist.\par \par \uc2 And so all conversations about whether you're with the latest fad or you're not, are you in the in group or the outgroup? A lot of the stuff originates in \uc2 Twitter\uc2  bio somehow, and then, for reasons that have never been clearer, the leaders of the Republican Party which should be pushing back against this insanity, go along with it.\par \par \uc2 So why is this happening? Who is convincing Republican leaders they need to go along? We asked that question.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: We have swarms of bees in circulation, okay, it's the Democratic Party and their main instrument of control, which is the American news media. And you see them at work on a cyclical basis, every six months, there will be a new mandatory fad that you're required to buy into.\par \par \uc2 And if you're wondering what it is, look at people's \uc2 Twitter\uc2  bios. You know, whether it's he/him or you know, vaxxed and boosted or Ukrainian flags, it will take whatever the new required belief is that emerges out of nowhere, usually on the basis of zero actual evidence, it can't be defended. And because it can't be defended, it's imposed.\par \par \uc2 And if you want to know whether there is a real argument in support of what they're telling you to believe, ask yourself, are they asking me or telling me? And if they're telling you, you have the answer. No. There is no real argument that could bring you to that position rationally.\par \par \uc2 In other words, if they bothered to treat you as an adult and decide to convince you as you're supposed to do in a democracy, you'd never get there. So, they tell you, you must believe that. And so the way I think about it is, which are the politicians who fall for it?\par \par \uc2 Let me give you the example before Ukraine, and by the way, I'm not -- the way they frame this prevents rational debate. So, it's like if you have questions, or if you have a more measured view of it, then you're automatically foursquare on the other side, which I'm not I'm hardly for Putin's invasion of Ukraine. Like what? That's insane. Of course, I am an American.\par \par \uc2 So, my level of interest in the internal politics, even the borders of other countries is by definition limited, because I'm a nationalist, as I think all Americans ought to be. It's one of the reasons I admire Israel so much.\par \par \uc2 Israel thinks -- the people who run Israel think about their job, which is to secure the future of the country they run. And I admire them. I wish we had leaders like that, we don't.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: We could go on forever, as you may have guessed, but we're out of time for tonight, sadly.\par \par \uc2 We'll be back every night at 8:00 PM on the show that is the sworn enemy of lying, pomposity, smugness, and groupthink.\par \par \uc2 Have the best night with the ones you love.\par \par \uc2 We'll see you soon.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document TCARL00020220903ei9200001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  Slams "MAGA Republicans" In Philadelphia Speech; Detailed List Revealing Mar-A-Lago Seized Items; Lawsuit Accuses Leader Of BLM Stealing From Donors; Airports Brace For Busy Labor Day Weekend; Law Encourages Farmers To Use Climate Friendly Methods; Animal Impact On Climate Change; Law Incentivizes Wind And Solar Investments; Biden Administration Rejects Potential Legal Challenges; Chinese And Russian Military Participate In Joint Military Games; China, Russia, And Iran Possibly Forming Alliance To Circumvent U.S. Sanctions; President Biden's Speech Stating MAGA Republicans Threat To U.S. Democracy Draws Criticism; Reports Shows 315,000 Jobs Created In U.S. Economy In August\b0\par\par\uc2 Mike Emanuel, Peter Doocy, William La Jeunesse, Garrett Tenney,  Mark Meredith, Bret Baier, Alexandria Hoff, Jennifer Griffin, Howard Kurtz \par \uc2 7,649 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 September 2022\par \uc2 Fox News: Special Report with Brett Baier\par \uc2 FNSPBB\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 KAYLEIGH MCENANY\uc2 , FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: One of my favorite people, Julie Banderas has a new book out. She is not woke and neither is Fiona's Fantastical Fort. Get Julie Banderas's book.\par \par \uc2 JESSICA TARLOV, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR: I'm sorry, Joey. That's it from us. Have an amazing weekend. Happy Labor Day. All the good things. We'll see you back here on Monday for our back to school --\par \par \uc2 MIKE EMANUEL, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CHIEF CONGRESSIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Good evening. Welcome to Washington. I'm Mike Emanuel in for Bret Baier.\par \par \uc2 New information on some of the materials seized by federal agents during the raid on former President Trump's Florida home.\par \par \uc2 We take a look at the impact of President Biden's Inflation Reduction Act on American farmers.\par \par \uc2 Some leaders of the \uc2 Black Lives Matter movement\uc2  are accused by former colleagues of stealing more than $10 million in donations.\par \par \uc2 But first, breaking tonight, reaction is emotional and politically charged, the President Biden's speech condemning what he calls MAGA Republicans and saying they are a threat to democracy.\par \par \uc2 The president made the comments against an eerie blood red background, complete with Marines on guard at Philadelphia's Independence Hall.\par \par \uc2 Critics say it is the hyper divisive president and his progressive agenda that are the real threats to the country. Now, the president seems to be backing off a bit.\par \par \uc2 White House Correspondent Peter Doocy takes a look at the address and the fallout. Good evening, Peter.\par \par \uc2 PETER DOOCY, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Good evening, Mike. The president's argument is basically that he thinks the United States can have a democracy or MAGA Republicans but not both.\par \par \uc2 So, I asked him today whether or not that applies to all 74 million Americans who backed Trump in 2020. And his answer was very revealing.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: This is not unusual, right? To have a -- to have a speech like this.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (voice over): That speech, a warning about so-called MAGA Republicans.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: They promote authoritarian leaders and they fanned the flames of political violence that are a threat to our personal rights.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: But the president tells Fox his criticism is limited.\par \par \uc2 Mr. President, do you consider all Trump supporters to be a threat to the country?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Come on, look, guys. You keep trying to make that case, I don't consider any Trump supporters to be a threat to the country.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: That's what it sounded like in Philly.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: The Republican Party today is dominated, driven and intimidated by \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and the MAGA Republicans. And that is a threat to this country.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: The President clarifies he's got nothing against voters who went MAGA before the January 6 Capitol riot.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: When people voted for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and support him now, they weren't voting for protecting the Capitol. They weren't voting for overruling an election. They were going for the philosophy he put forward.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: President Biden says there are mainstream Republicans.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I know because I've been able to work with these mainstream Republicans.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: But they're not his concern.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: MAGA Republicans are just destroying American democracy.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: The thing is, Democrats have recently spent more than $46 million nationwide to help MAGA beat mainstream in competitive primaries.\par \par \uc2 But \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2 's aligned PAC is playing in New Hampshire's Senate primary hoping to boost Republican candidate Don Bolduc who does not accept the results of the 2020 election by attacking his Republican opponent, Chuck Morse.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 's Washington establishment is going all in for Chuck Morse.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: The head of the Democratic Congressional Campaign Committee explained their thinking last week.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 SEAN PATRICK MALONEY\uc2  (D-NY): It's not a tactic we widely use. But wanting a weaker opponent is of course a time honored strategy.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: If that democratic strategy backfires on them, then the very candidates President Biden warns could tear the country apart could win.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: We're a big complicated country.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (on camera): Officials here basically said the point of that speech was to let Americans know their neighbors might be somebody the president considers an extremist but it's unclear what White House officials want people to do with that information because the president is not putting forward any new policies to protect democracy, Mike.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Peter Doocy starting us off live on the North Lawn. Peter, many thanks.\par \par \uc2 The economy gained 315,000 jobs in August. Today's report from the government also showed the unemployment rate rose to 3.7 percent. Up from a half century low of 3-1/2 percent.\par \par \uc2 The Dow lost 338 today, the S&P 500 fell 43, NASDAQ was off 154.\par \par \uc2 For the week, the Dow gave back three percentage points. The S&P 500 lost 3-1/3, NASDAQ dropped 4-1/5.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Internal Revenue Service\uc2  accidentally posted confidential information involving about 120,000 people before removing the data from its website. That's according to the Wall Street Journal.\par \par \uc2 Involved people with individual retirement accounts who earn certain types of business income with those retirement plans, the government is blaming a human coding error.\par \par \uc2 We are still waiting for a federal judge to rule on former President Trump's request for a third party arbitrator to review documents taken last month from his Florida home.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, we are learning new details about some of those documents. Correspondent Mark Meredith has details for us. Good evening, Mark.\par \par \uc2 MARK MEREDITH, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Mike, good evening to you. The government released a detailed property inventory. Basically, a rundown of everything collected when the \uc2 FBI\uc2  searched Mar-a- Lago, and the documents show agents collected thousands of pieces of evidence way more than we first suspect it.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH (voice over): Newly released records show when \uc2 FBI\uc2  agents searched former President Trump's Florida home, they found a lot more than just the classified materials they were originally searching for.\par \par \uc2 Among the items recovered spread across 33 boxes more than 10,000 government documents without classification markings, some mixed in with photographs, magazines, gifts, books and clothing.\par \par \uc2 The former president and his allies claim the search which a federal Magistrate Judge signed off on was overly broad and a potential violation of executive privilege.\par \par \uc2 Today, Trump's former Attorney General Bill Barr told Fox he believes the government went to great lengths to avoid conflict with Trump.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAM BARR\uc2 , FORMER U.S. ATTORNEY GENERAL: People say this was unprecedented. Well, it's also unprecedented for a president to take all this classified information and put them in a country club.\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH: Lawyers for the former president have asked a federal judge appointed to the bench by Trump in 2020 to select a special master or third party to independently review the items seized. Even though federal prosecutors say they've already finished reviewing the documents.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN TURLEY, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR: They told the judge that the appointment of a special master would endanger national security. And the judge pushed back on that for good reason. I mean, the special masters are appointed all the time.\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH: On Capitol Hill, top Republicans continue to defend Trump while criticizing the Justice Department's actions.\par \par \uc2 In a letter today, House Minority Leader Kevin McCarthy demanded both the Attorney General and \uc2 FBI\uc2  Director to testify about their criminal investigation into the 45th President.\par \par \uc2 McCarthy writing a cornerstone of our democracy is the equal application of the law. Unfortunately, our country has seen rampant politicization of the DOJ and the \uc2 FBI\uc2  during the past year and a half.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH (on camera): Justice Department officials say they have received McCarthy's letter but no news yet on any potential hearings. We're going to be hearing a lot from the former president though this weekend. He's got a political rally tomorrow night in Pennsylvania. And Mike, I bet a lot of money he's going to be speaking at length not only about the investigation and the search, but the Justice Department as a whole.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: No doubt about it. Mark Meredith, thanks a lot.\par \par \uc2 Breaking tonight, a major financial scandal for the \uc2 Black Lives Matter movement\uc2 . Some of its leaders are being accused of stealing millions of dollars in donations.\par \par \uc2 National Correspondent William La Jeunesse is in Los Angeles with details. Hello, William.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAM LA JEUNESSE, FOX NEWS CHANNEL NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Well, Mike, this accusation that the BLM National Foundation has been misusing donor money is now out in the open and it's not coming from the media, but prominent members within BLM who accused the parent organization the BLM Global Network Foundation and board member Shalomyah Bowers of enriching himself on donations.\par \par \uc2 So, according to the lawsuit, this is a case of a rogue administrator, a middleman turned usurper, who is hired to collect donations and account for expenditures of the BLM Movement.\par \par \uc2 However, after siphoning more than $10 million in fees from donors, Mr. Bowers decided he could not let go of his personal piggy bank.\par \par \uc2 So, it's about a year ago that Bowers and two others took control of BLM after the group failed to file financial reports and tried to conceal some expenses. While the lawsuit claims the new executive team "began changing passwords of shared social media accounts, hired expensive, high powered lawyers and media consultants to bully and harass the organizers, fraudulently raise money from unsuspecting donors, and that Bowers made some $2 million in less than eight months."\par \par \uc2 So, previously, BLM had called such accusations "right-wing misinformation".\par \par \uc2 Well, this lawsuit, however, comes from black grassroots members, after the death of George Floyd, BLM raised about $90 million from corporations and mostly online donors, while the founder claim that to be white guilt money.\par \par \uc2 BLM's response to this lawsuit on Thursday? Well, we face yet again another round of struggle for control of one organization, this time by people who say they love black people and center abolitionist values, but whose actions are furthest from our principles.\par \par \uc2 So Mike, several state agencies are investigating BLM and with this lawsuit, the public may someday find out where all that money was spent. Back to you.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: We'll follow it. William La Jeunesse live in L.A. William, thanks very much.\par \par \uc2 The Labor Day weekend marks the traditional end of the summer travel season and it's been a tough time for passengers who have had to cope with tens of thousands of flight delays and cancellations.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent Garrett Tenney is at Chicago's O'Hare airport tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GARRETT TENNEY, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): This Labor Day weekend will be one of the busiest travel periods in recent years. More than 12.7 million Americans are expected to fly between Thursday and Monday according to Hopper, a travel booking site. That would make it the busiest Labor Day weekend since 2018.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: People have spent two years of pandemic restrictions and lockdowns and they're just ready to get out there.\par \par \uc2 TENNEY: The increased demand for travel is pushing prices up. AAA says the average lowest airfare today costs 30 percent more than it did in 2020. A night in a mid-range hotel is 53 percent higher and rental cars are up almost 32 percent. None of that is keeping folks at home though. And neither are the flight delays and cancellations that have rocked the industry this summer.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I think air travel is finally getting back to normal.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: The last time I flew was in May, and there were no problems. So, this is my first time and I would say it's as bad as they're saying.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I'm tired. I'm hungry. I want a deep dish pizza if I'm stuck here.\par \par \uc2 TENNEY: Starting this holiday weekend, passengers whose flights are delayed or canceled have a new tool at their disposal. The Transportation Department's airline customer service dashboard breaks down what U.S. airlines have agreed to do for travelers when flight disruptions happen that are within their control.\par \par \uc2 But some travel experts say don't get your hopes up.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The airlines can basically weasel their way out of providing anything if they want to, short of something that can be directly attributed to their own actions. And that is going to be a little tough.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TENNEY (on camera): If you're still looking to take a trip, \uc2 Hopper\uc2 's analysts say over the next couple of months, flight prices will be dropping from their summer highs before shooting right back up for the holidays.\par \par \uc2 In Chicago, I'm Garrett Tenney, Fox News.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Up next, Bret joins us as our series on the global food crisis continues with a look at how the President's Inflation Reduction Act affects American farmers.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: The \uc2 Veterans Affairs Department\uc2  is taking steps to provide abortions and access to abortion counselling to pregnant veterans and V.A. beneficiaries. The department submitted an interim final rule to the Federal Register last night. These services will be authorized immediately after the rule is published. And the rule will be available for public comment for 30 days thereafter.\par \par \uc2 Once the rule is published, V.A. will immediately prepare to provide the services in as many locations as possible.\par \par \uc2 President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  says his administration has offered Mississippi every single thing available to address the water crisis that has gripped the state -- the state's capital of Jackson.\par \par \uc2 He said it's now up to Republican Governor \uc2 Tate Reeves\uc2  to act. The president adds he has no plans to visit the region at this point.\par \par \uc2 The Biden administration is announcing $1 billion in federal grants for clean energy, farming, and other sectors. More than $65 million will go to improve farm production in California.\par \par \uc2 Another nearly 63 million will help West Virginia shift to solar and find new use for abandoned mines. The new funding comes after President Biden signed the Inflation Reduction Act.\par \par \uc2 My colleague Bret Baier takes a closer look at how climate incentives in that law impact farmers and other efforts to make farming more environmentally friendly.\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL ANCHOR (on camera): Thanks, Mike. In tonight's SPECIAL REPORT "SPOTLIGHT ON THE GLOBAL FOOD CRISIS", how new farming technology and traditional methods impact the environment.\par \par \uc2 President Biden recently signed into law what is being marketed as the largest investment in clean and climate energy solutions.\par \par \uc2 We look at what that means for farmers and the land where our food is grown.\par \par \uc2 BAIER (voice-over): It was named the Inflation Reduction Act, but it includes nearly $20 billion to encourage farmers to go green.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BAIER (on camera): What do you think of all these initiatives in that -- in that bill?\par \par \uc2 GREG WALDEN\uc2 , FORMER REPRESENTATIVE OF OREGON: Well, it's a pretty big and expensive grab bag, certainly.\par \par \uc2 BAIER (voice-over): Most of the funding is allocated to several programs that encourage farmers to engage in practices to reduce emissions and make land more sustainable. The programs are voluntary, and incentive based.\par \par \uc2 BAIER (on camera): Can you explain some of these efforts farmers can opt into?\par \par \uc2 SEN. KIRSTEN GILLIBRAND (D-NY): So, it's basically saying, how do we create jobs in America that are around green energy and around agriculture? That all can have an impact on arresting global climate change.\par \par \uc2 BAIER (voice-over): The new law prioritizes proposals that utilize diet and feed management to reduce methane emissions from ruminants or animals.\par \par \uc2 But some experts say cows are not adding to the climate change issue.\par \par \uc2 BAIER (on camera): There is hearings about methane from the cattle.\par \par \uc2 ROGER CRYAN CHIEF ECONOMIST, \uc2 AMERICAN FARM BUREAU FEDERATION\uc2 : We are in a continuous cycle with bio generated methane. It's been going on for a long time. When Christopher Columbus arrived in the new world, there were 60 million buffalo, 60 million bison ranging in the North American prairies. It is not something new, it does not adding to the -- to the problem, it is just part of a cycle.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: How much of this deals with that?\par \par \uc2 GILLIBRAND: Not much. A lot of that is conservation program so that you keep your farmed, so that you do your agriculture in a way that helps the air in the water.\par \par \uc2 BAIER (voice-over): The law dedicates $1.4 billion to ensure some farmland does not turn into roads, cities, or other developments. But for wind and solar projects, companies receive a 10 percent tax credit.\par \par \uc2 GILLIBRAND: These are just a value added proposition. So, if they put a wind turbine on their land, they can make money, while they're growing their crops, while they're producing dairy or whatever their agricultural production is.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: But farmland is still decreasing at a rate of 2,000 acres per day, as renewable energy expands.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: To reach net zero by 2050, we're going to need to mobilize trillions of dollars in financing, tarnish both private and public sector resources.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: To reach President Biden's goal, the U.S. would also need 250 million acres in land, dedicated to wind energy. That's more acreage than Missouri, Iowa, Illinois, Wisconsin, Indiana, Ohio, and Kentucky combined.\par \par \uc2 An additional 17 million acres will be needed for solar power. That's larger than West Virginia.\par \par \uc2 CLAY JACKSON, MADISON COUNTY FARM BUREAU MEMBER, SENTERFITT FARMS: Huge tracts of good agricultural land that are going into solar power. We want to preserve agriculture.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: The agriculture department estimates there is enough uninhabited land to meet the Biden agenda.\par \par \uc2 BAIER (on camera): There's just a balance of realism versus what's practical.\par \par \uc2 GILLIBRAND: Right. Well, I think we should try. I just think this is all feasible. And we are the -- most brilliant engineers on the globe. And so, we are smart enough and capable enough to do both.\par \par \uc2 BAIER (voice-over): Despite an increase in renewable energy, many Americans are still concerned the U.S. is not doing enough. In a recent Fox News poll, more than half think climate change is a major threat to our country's stability.\par \par \uc2 BAIER (on camera): There's a lot of concern about the climate overall, left and right about the future what it looks like, especially when it comes to our production of food. Do you think it's fair to say that the best way forward is market based?\par \par \uc2 WALDEN: I think consumers have an enormous power that moves things quicker than any public policy does.\par \par \uc2 BAIER (voice-over): Every day, consumers are investing in green alternatives without government incentives.\par \par \uc2 ABBY CRAIG PRIOR, CHIEF COMMERCIAL OFFICER, BRIGHTFARMS: The indoor farming segment is growing really driven by consumer demand for fresher, better tasting local products that are grown and as clean of a way as possible.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BAIER (on camera): Whether consumer driven incentivized or regulated, finding the best approach to improve our environment has become a contentious debate, obviously, up on Capitol Hill among lawmakers.\par \par \uc2 We'll continue to follow how the incentives from the Inflation Reduction Act impact climate change, farmers, and the food we eat. Mike, back to you.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Thank you, Bret.\par \par \uc2 Up next, is President Biden's plan to bail out billions of dollars in student loan debt, even legal? We'll take a look.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL (on camera): There are increasing questions tonight about whether President Biden's plan to forgive billions of dollars in student loan debt is legal. Correspondent Alexandria Hoff takes a look from the White House.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Somebody is going to file a lawsuit.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDRIA HOFF, FOX NEWS CHANNEL WASHINGTON CORRESPONDENT (on camera): In a private meeting, the attorney's general from several Republican states explored legal avenues to challenge President Biden's student loan forgiveness plan.\par \par \uc2 KEN PAXTON\uc2 , ATTORNEY GENERAL OF TEXAS (voice-over): I think he's made a promise to try to induce people to vote for him knowing full well that he doesn't have that authority that Congress has that authority. So, we are definitely looking at a strategy.\par \par \uc2 HOFF: According to the Biden administration, the authority is there, is under what's known as the HEROES Act, the 2003 law, enacted in the wake of the September 11 attacks to provide student debt relief to members of the military.\par \par \uc2 The administration says it gives the education secretary the ability to grant loan relief during a national emergency like the COVID 19 pandemic.\par \par \uc2 A DNC senior adviser says the HEROES Act has already been invoked by the executive branch to delay loan repayments.\par \par \uc2 CEDRIC RICHMOND, SENIOR ADVISER, DEMOCRATIC NATIONAL COMMITTEE: Congress acted. And if you look at the legislation that allowed President Trump and President Biden to delay loan repayment, it's the same legislation.\par \par \uc2 HOFF: In a statement, \uc2 John Malcolm\uc2 , vice president of the Institute for Constitutional Government at \uc2 the Heritage Foundation\uc2 , a conservative think tank, told Fox News that the HEROES Act doesn't speak to canceling student loans outright.\par \par \uc2 "There are several public interest law firms in our network that are considering challenging this giveaway by Biden to his political base, and are looking for clients with legal standing and the courage to sue."\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOFF (on camera): If such legal action moves forward, lower courts may eye recent decisions from the Supreme Court that have limited the Biden administration's authority. Notably, West Virginia v. \uc2 EPA\uc2 , where the court invoked the major questions doctrine, it requires congressional authorization for actions of major significance. Mike.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Turning to international news, a man tried to kill Argentina's politically powerful vice president last night outside her home, but the handgun misfired. Officials say the attacker of \uc2 Cristina Fernandez\uc2  was quickly overpowered by her security officers. President \uc2 Alberto Fernandez\uc2 , who is not related to the vice president, says the pistol did not discharge when the man tried to fire it.\par \par \uc2 President Biden will host Pacific Island leaders in Washington later this month amid growing worries about China's activity in the region. The White House says it has invited Micronesia, the Marshall Islands, Fiji, and several nations to take part in the summit.\par \par \uc2 The Biden administration is announcing defense sales to Taiwan worth more than $1 billion that includes harpoon, air-to-sea missiles, sidewinder, air-to-air missiles, and a logistic support package for Taiwan's surveillance radar program which provides air defense warnings.\par \par \uc2 U.S. officials are keeping a close eye on joint military drills involving Russia and China. Meanwhile, Russia, China, and Iran are teaming up to form a new axis of evil of those countries facing sanctions by the U.S. National security correspondent Jennifer Griffin has tonight's report.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER GRIFFIN, FOX NEWS NATIONAL SECURITY CORRESPONDENT: Fifty-thousand troops, 5,000 pieces of military equipment, 140 aircraft, and 60 warships are taking part in the Vostok 2022 war games, a weeklong exercise that began Thursday in eastern Russia and the Sea of Japan. China and Russia, the second and third largest militaries in the world, have joined forces to fire a shot across the bow at the west, the head of Russia's pacific fleet explained.\par \par \uc2 REAR ADMIRAL VALERY KAZAKOV, RUSSIAN PACIFIC FLEET: The practical interaction between the Russian Navy and the Navy of the China's People's Liberation Army has reached a new level in terms of quality.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: The head of the Russian military, General \uc2 Valery Gerasimov\uc2 , is personally overseeing the drills. The new alliance between Russia and China is far from perfect.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT DALY, KISSINGER INSTITUTE DIRECTOR: Russia doesn't really want to be in the long term, sort of a poor younger brother in a partnership with China. And China doesn't want to have the albatross of all of Putin's adventurism hung around its neck in every instance. But when they can have low cost or no cost unity, we'll see unity.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: Until now, Beijing has stopped short of sending weapons to Moscow, which is running low on smart bombs as the invasion of Ukraine grinds on. For weapons replenishment, Russia has turned to Iran in a move that's raised eyebrows at the Pentagon. Iran recently delivered a shipment of drones to Russia for use in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 For its part, China state media has been offering Russia support in its own way -- more favorable coverage of Russia's invasion in the wake of several congressional visits to Taiwan.\par \par \uc2 ANDREW WEISS\uc2 , \uc2 CARNEGIE ENDOWMENT FOR INTERNATIONAL PEACE\uc2 : The Russians are getting symbolic value of having the Chinese run interference for them, try to embrace some of Russia's talking points. But where it really matters in terms of military direct support for Russia's war, they are badly disappointed.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: Nonetheless, Russia, China, and Iran are increasingly finding common ground. More examples of this new axis -- trade between Russia and China is up 30 percent since March, and China and Iran just entered a 25- year economic and security deal to get around U.S. sanctions. Mike?\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Jen Griffin here in Washington. Jen, thanks very much.\par \par \uc2 The Pentagon says the \uc2 U.S. Navy\uc2  intercepted an Iranian warship that had captured two American drones in the Red Sea yesterday. A U.S. official says an American helicopter crew spotted the Iranians trying to hide the drones on deck with large tarps. The U.S. insists the machines were in international waters when they were taken. The Iranian crew released the drones this morning. Assessment is being done to see if the machines sustained any damage other than scratches and scrapes. Earlier this week Iran tried to capture another sea drone. A U.S. warship stopped the Iranian vessel as it was towing the drone and took possession of the machine.\par \par \uc2 President Biden is asking Congress to provide another $13.7 billion in assistance for Ukraine. The request is part of a larger $47 billion emergency spending package. The president wants to pay for COVID-19 response, the ongoing monkeypox outbreak, and recent natural disasters in Kentucky and other states. The proposal comes as a senior Russian diplomatic is sternly warning Washington against supplying long range weapons to Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 Up next, how the media are handling the president's comments about millions of American voters being a threat to democracy.\par \par \uc2 First, Beyond our Borders tonight. An explosion tears through a crowded mosque in western Afghanistan, killing at least 18 people, including a prominent cleric close to the \uc2 Taliban\uc2 . At least 23 people were hurt. The bomb went off during Friday noon prayers when mosques are full of worshipers.\par \par \uc2 Sri Lanka's former president returns to the country seven weeks after fleeing when protesters stormed his home and office. \uc2 Gotabaya Rajapaksa\uc2  had been under siege over the country's economic crisis. He officially resigned from office after arriving in Singapore.\par \par \uc2 A drug sniffing dog leads police at Milan's airport to nearly 30 pounds of cocaine. The drugs were stuffed into the upholstery of a motorized wheelchair. Its owner was arrested.\par \par \uc2 Just some of the other stories Beyond our Borders tonight. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Last night's speech by President Biden about are threats to democracy is just the latest divisive message from a president who promised to unite the country. This evening we look at how the media are covering this. Here is FOX News media analyst and host of FOX's MEDIA BUZZ, Howard Kurtz.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOWARD KURTZ, FOX NEWS HOST: President Biden took office vowing to a unifier.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , (D) PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: We can join forces, stop the shouting, and lower the temperature.\par \par \uc2 KURTZ: But in last night's Philadelphia speech billed as an official, nonpolitical White House event, the president again slammed his opponents.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: The Republican Party today is dominated, driven, and intimidated by \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and the MAGA Republicans, and that is a threat to this country.\par \par \uc2 KURTZ: Many commentators embraced this harsh rhetoric because they agree with it.\par \par \uc2 ALEX WAGNER, MSNBC HOST: As President Biden laid out tonight the ongoing threat to democracy is still one of the fundamental challenges of our time.\par \par \uc2 DON LEMON, CNN HOST, "DON LEMON TONIGHT": It's called politics. Of course, he is going to use everything in his arsenal to give a speech to try to do what he says is a battle for the soul of democracy.\par \par \uc2 KURTZ: But there was pushback on the right.\par \par \uc2 TAMMY BRUCE, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: One of the most divisive and disgusting speeches ever delivered by a sitting president. \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  angrily suggested that 74 million Americans are enemies of the state.\par \par \uc2 KURTZ: A few journalists took issue with the White House insistence that the event wasn't partisan.\par \par \uc2 KAITLAN COLLINS, CNN CHIEF WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: It did have a political bent to it. He was making clear that he thinks that these Republicans should not be elected in to office.\par \par \uc2 KURTZ: And when CNN Anchor Brianna Keilar said Biden shouldn't have had two Marines behind him because it politicizes the military, a White House spokesman tweeted back, "Except it's not political, in the same way CNN isn't political but has made the same warnings."\par \par \uc2 Trump, of course, is no stranger to insults. He said on Truth Social that the angry Biden speech threatened America with the possible use of military force -- there was no such reference -- and questioned the president's mental health, saying he must be insane.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 KURTZ: President Biden is, of course, entitled to shift into campaign mode and rip the MAGA movement and its leader. But while he billed the speech as a presidential address -- the broadcast networks declined to carry the speech -- it sure sounded like what he would have delivered at a Democratic rally. Mike?\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Howie Kurtz, thanks very much.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the panel with a reaction to President Biden's speech, midterms election, the economy and jobs, plus Winners and Losers.\par \par \uc2 First, here is what some of our FOX affiliates around the country are covering tonight. FOX Richmond as a group of parents and teachers in Virginia sues Harrisonburg City public schools to temporarily block a pronoun policy that prevents teachers from misgendering a student. It also keeps them from disclosing to a parent if a child picks pronouns that adhere to a different sex than the one they were born with.\par \par \uc2 FOX 11 in Los Angeles as a federal judge throws out an indictment charging a dive boat captain with manslaughter in the deaths of 34 people in a 2019 fire aboard a vessel anchored off the southern California coast. The judge says the indictment failed to specify that the captain acted with gross negligence, a required element to prove the crime of seaman's manslaughter.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at Cape Canaveral. One of the big stories there tonight from FOX 35, \uc2 NASA\uc2  officials say the countdown to launch the Artemis 1 moon mission has resumed. The mission management team has given a go for a Saturday launch attempt of the space launch system, rocket, and Orion spacecraft. It comes after technical issues scrubbed Monday's attempt.\par \par \uc2 That's tonight's live look outside the beltway from SPECIAL REPORT. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , (D) PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I pledge to be a president who seeks not to divide but unify.\par \par \uc2 Every disagreement doesn't have to be a cause for total war.\par \par \uc2 The MAGA Republicans don't just threaten our personal rights and economic security. They are a threat to our very democracy.\par \par \uc2 The Republican Party today is dominated, driven, and intimidated by \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and the MAGA Republicans. And that is a threat to this country.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: That last clip from last night's speech in Philadelphia with the eerie red lighting and two Marines on duty for what was a pretty political speech at the White House. Here was the response to questions about the Marines being there and the nature of the speech.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE DEPUTY PRESS SECRETARY: We see that as leadership, and we see that as presidential. To your question about the Marines, look, the president gave an important speech, a critical speech at an inflection point. And our democracy, our values, our values that are -- our values that our men and women who protect us every day and fight for it every day believe in as well.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: With that, let's bring in our panel, \uc2 Trey Gowdy\uc2 , former Congressman from South Carolina, Leslie Marshall, Democratic strategist, and syndicated radio host \uc2 Hugh Hewitt\uc2 . Panel, welcome.\par \par \uc2 HUGH HEWITT, SYNDICATED RADIO HOST: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 LESLIE MARSHALL, DEMOCRATIC STRATEGIST: Hi, Mike.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Trey, lead us off. So thoughts about the speech last night, what about the Marines being there and the White House insisting it was an official speech and not a campaign speech?\par \par \uc2 TREY GOWDY\uc2 , FORMER SOUTH CAROLINA REPRESENTATIVE: Mike, I think threats to our democracy abound, and January 6th is a pretty obvious example. There are plenty of examples on the other side of the aisle. And if you want to be a real leader at a time when this country needs a unifier, then you would point out -- many of us consider progressive prosecutors who can undo a legislative act by themselves to be a threat to democracy. Many of us consider presidents who sign or issue executive orders that they know violate the Constitution to be a threat to democracy.\par \par \uc2 The other thing that caught my attention is the president's definition of a good Republican is one that agrees with him. Over half of the country doesn't agree with him on most of his policies. So how can that be the definition of a good Republican that you could work and agree with \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Leslie, here we are. It is Labor Day weekend, typically the launch of campaign season. The president gave the speech, and he's taken some heat for it. Your thoughts?\par \par \uc2 LESLIE MARSHALL, DEMOCRATIC STRATEGIST: Well, first, Trey, your first line we agreed on, and that scares me. We have been agreeing way too much lately. Second, love a man in uniform. Bring out more Marines, please, and to make our nation further proud. Third, every time a politician president, Democrat or Republican, opens their mouth, it is political in nature to some degree.\par \par \uc2 I've said before I think it's too divisive and not uniting say all MAGA Republicans, calling people semi-fascist, harkening back to the basket of deplorables with \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  during the time during her campaign. But I do agree, that first line that Trey said, that democracy is under attack. And I do further agree in that statement that he started with January 6th was an example of that, that we saw people willing to overthrow our government and to do violence to do it. And further, polls show that voters, especially in states like Michigan, are putting this high, equal, and sometimes topping the economy as the midterms approach.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Hugh, the broadcast nets did not take it live last night, clearly thinking it was more political than official. Your thoughts?\par \par \uc2 HEWITT: No, Mike, it wasn't a pro-democracy. It was a pro-Democratic Party incumbent speech. They're clearly in troubling. I think it will be remembered for the stabling, maybe the worst optics of a presidential speech that I can recall. It will also be remembered for what he didn't talk about. He didn't talk about his student loan big announcement just a week ago. It's played so poorly with Americans across the board that he is now down to kind of cliches about \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and his supporters and extremism. But really using Independence Hall, that black and red lighting, the Marines, it did not go over well. And I think his 38 percent Reuters poll approval is going to be even lower next week. I think the Democrats are from a world of hurt. They know it. He threw a Hail Mary last night, and it wobbled.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Let's pivot to the economy. First Friday of the month means it's jobs report Friday. Here is how some of the analysts broke it down.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: It's 315,000 coming in for nonfarm jobs. That is a slight beat overestimate.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: The jobless rate actually went up a little bit.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The labor force participation rate, 62.4. If there is one area that I'm always happy to see the numbers expand on, that would be it.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , (D) PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Bottom line is jobs are up, wages are up. People are back to work.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Trey, what's the state of the American economy?\par \par \uc2 GOWDY: Jobs are up. Credit for that. Other markers are not good. Inflation, gas prices are coming down, but they were really, really high. So look at the stock market. Who would have thought that putting money in the stock market was a riskier proposition than taking out a $10,000 student loan to study anthropology? Who would have thought that?\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: All right, Leslie, your take on the economy?\par \par \uc2 MARSHALL: I'm not an economist, but the economists that I have spoken to have said when you look at this report, especially with the increase in wages, there is more optimism that the feds won't have to be as harsh raising -- raising the inflation -- I almost said the inflation rate, sorry -- interest rates, excuse me, three quarters of a percent. Now they are looking at either one quarter or a half. So that certainly good for the Feds not to be as aggressive with that increase, and that to me shows that economists are pretty happy with this. Certainly, they could be happier. But it's not doomsday, this report at all.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: All right, Hugh, your turn.\par \par \uc2 HEWITT: The Dow was up early on jobs report, good news. By the end of the day it had tanked, third week in a row it's down. People are looking for it are not optimistic. The direction of the country is poor and the prognostication for the economy is worse.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: All right, we asked our esteemed panelists for midterm races that they are watching as we head into the Labor Day weekend. Trey, why don't you lead us off.\par \par \uc2 GOWDY: Florida Senate race, Val Demings versus \uc2 Marco Rubio\uc2 , and I'm happy to tell you why, but you may not want me to tell you why. You may want me to defer and let Hugh and Leslie go.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Leslie, your turn, midterm race to watch?\par \par \uc2 MARSHALL: Georgia Senate race, we're looking at the incumbent who is Reverend Raphael Warnock against football legend \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 . And interestingly enough, Mr. Walker is trying to paint Mr. Warnock as a liar and brings up some lies of his own. Not sure that's the best campaign strategy. But I think it's interesting, and a very close race to watch at this point in time.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: So two hot Senate races. Hugh, your turn?\par \par \uc2 HEWITT: Mine is also in the Senate. Dr. Oz was on my radio show this week and really knocked it out of the park. The morning after Stephanie Ruhle over at MSNBC did a softball pre-taped interview \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2 . Prayers for \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2 's recovery, but he is clearly struggling. I don't think it's a seven-point race. I think it's very close right now, and we did have a couple polls showing it 4 points. Dr. Oz is rallying his base, bringing back the McCormick voters. I'm very confident about that, and I don't think Mr. Fetterman is going to be able to campaign. And he is so far left, if he does he will lose voters anyway.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: A lot of questions about whether or not there will be any debates in that race. We'll see where it goes. Trey, do you want to button things up since you teased us?\par \par \uc2 GOWDY: Well, Marco is a mainstream Republican, so he is going to spend a lot of time having to explain what his fellow senator from Florida said about Social Security. And Val Demings is not a defund the police hippy. So it's kind of an unusual race, and I will be watching it.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Time now for Winners and Losers. Trey, why don't you lead us off?\par \par \uc2 GOWDY: My winner, marriage counselors because college football starts this weekend.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 GOWDY: My loser is the devil, because now everyone in the world knows where he lives because Biden used his house as the backdrop for his speech last night.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Leslie, your turn, winner and loser of the week?\par \par \uc2 MARSHALL: My winner is Mary Peltola. She flipped a red seat blue in the state of Alaska. She beat \uc2 Sarah Palin\uc2  who had bigger name recognition, former governor, and was V.P. on the McCain presidential ticket. She also was the youngest, the first woman, and the first native Alaskan. That is my winner.\par \par \uc2 My loser, Thomas Webster, a former \uc2 NYPD\uc2  police officer who has just been sentenced to 10 years for assaulting a Capitol Hill police officer.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: A lot of people looking at that Alaska race trying to figure out if it means something for November or whether Alaska is just a different place. OK, Hugh, your turn, winner and loser?\par \par \uc2 HEWITT: Well, the winner tomorrow will be Ohio State, of course. But let's talk about Herschel in Georgia. He is up in one poll and Joe O'Dea in Colorado up in another. We've got a couple of Democrats on the run who are incumbents. A couple of Republicans no one thought could win are going to win.\par \par \uc2 The loser is central Europe. Russia today announced the continuation of its ban of gas exports from their big Russian oil companies. Putin plays hardball. Originally it was going to be two days. Now it's extended indefinitely. It's going to be a long, cold, and expensive dark winter in Germany because of Putin's hardball.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Panelists, thanks. We've got to run.\par \par \uc2 It's Friday. Time for "Notable Quotables."\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Is tonight a political speech?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: No it's not a political speech.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: The Republican Party today is dominated, driven, and intimidated by \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and the MAGA Republicans. And that is a threat to this country.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The first lines out of his mouth should be to apologize for slandering tens of millions of Americans as fascists.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Launch Director Charlie Blackwell-Thompson has called a scrub.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It's unprecedented to have a raid like this on a former president's residence.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: We want to shake sure we are allowing the Justice Department to do their work.\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , (D) VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Ninety percent of the people who are going to benefit from student loan forgiveness make under $75,000 a year.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: There's a lot of people out there making 30, 40 grand a year that didn't go to college, and they need help as well.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Governor Abbott's racist and xenophobic practices of expulsion have only amplified the challenges many of these migrants have experienced.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It's very interesting how angry she appears that a few of these people have to be sent her way.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The water is not going to clear up in Washington until we get the pigs out of the creek.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Join me this weekend for FOX NEWS SUNDAY. My exclusive guests include House Campaign Chairman Congressman \uc2 Sean Patrick Maloney\uc2  and \uc2 Tom Emmer\uc2 . Plus New Jersey Governor \uc2 Phil Murphy\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Thank you for watching SPECIAL REPORT. I'm Mike Emanuel in Washington. "JESSE WATTERS PRIMETIME" with guest host Pete Hegseth starts right now. Pete?\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FNSPBB0020220903ei9200001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Biden's Policies Are The Real Threat To The "Soul Of The Nation"; Biden Engages In Revisionist History; Biden Essentially Declares MAGA As Enemy Of The State; The Media Praises Biden's Divisive Speech; When Will Herschel And Warnock Debate?; GA Senate Race Tightens As Walker Picks Up Steam; Is Tim Ryan Ashamed To Be A Democrat?; Pennsylvania Republican Senate Candidate Dr. Mehmet Oz Interviewed On Attempts To Debate Democratic Opponent \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  ; Pennsylvania Democratic Senate Candidate \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  's Position On Drug Legalization Examined; Judge Doesn't Rule Out Possible Special Master To Review Documents Seized By \uc2 FBI\uc2  In Raid Of Former President Trump Residence At Mar-a-Lago\b0\par\par\uc2 Tammy Bruce, Raymond Arroyo, Mollie Hemingway \par \uc2 7,732 \uc2 words\par \uc2 1 September 2022\par \uc2 Fox News: The Ingraham Angle\par \uc2 INGANG\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 TAMMY BRUCE, FOX NEWS HOST: In the meantime, you stay right here because your favorite Raymond Arroyo is in tonight sitting there filling in for Laura Ingraham. The combo, of course, is amazing. But tonight, Raymond is all yours.\par \par \uc2 RAYMOND ARROYO, FOX NEWS HOST: I'm all solo. Thank you so much, Tammy. Incredible insight. And we are going to take it from there and continue to look at this bizarre speech from President Biden.\par \par \uc2 I'm Raymond Arroyo. This is a special edition of THE INGRAHAM ANGLE from New York City tonight. You heard the President's opening salvo and the campaign season that his party will embrace as they move into the campaign pitch. Essentially, Biden is casting himself as the savior of the nation's soul.\par \par \uc2 With all due respect, if we needed a pope, we'd hold a conclave. But Biden wax pseudo spiritual throughout his sermon address.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  , PRESIDENT, UNITED STATES OF AMERICA: The darkness of Charlottesville, of COVID, of gun violence, of insurrection, we can see the light. Light is now visible. Light that will guide us forward.\par \par \uc2 I ran for President because I believe we're in a battle for the soul of this nation.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: The President claims that this is a battle for the soul of the nation. But you can't save the soul by killing the body. And that's what by any objective measure, Biden's policies have done.\par \par \uc2 Crime is skyrocketing all over the country. Inflation is at a 40-year high. The border is wide open. And this administration has encouraged migrants to risk their lives to enter the country illegally. All that does is strengthen the cartels and human traffickers.\par \par \uc2 The Nation's Report Card dropped today, showing that nine-year old students are sinking in math and reading, wiping out decades of progress. Our children are more at risk from drugs, violence and dumb (ph) curriculars than ever before. All of that can be traced to Biden's policies. How is any of that helping America's soul?\par \par \uc2 The pastor in chief conveniently ignored those facts sticking to his old time religion.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: There's no question that the Republican party today is dominated, driven and intimidated by \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and the MAGA Republicans. And that is a threat to this country.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: But beyond all the spiritual gobbledygook, Biden is really engaged in a partisan smear and smash operation. This is about framing himself as the white knight and Republicans as dragons. It's cynical. It's sad, and it's a total repudiation of his earlier pledges of unity and civility.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: My whole soul is in this, bringing America together, uniting our people, uniting our nation. We can see each other, not as adversaries but as neighbors.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: But why look at those who disagree with his neighbors when extreme terrorist fits them so well. Tonight, the president declared war on a whole group of citizens.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: So I want to say this plain and simple. There is no place for political violence in America. Period. I will not stand by and watch. I will not. The will of the American people be overturned by wild conspiracy theories and baseless evidence reclaims and fraud. I will not stand by and watch elections in this country stolen by people who simply refuse to accept that they lost.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: OK, ok. But if the qualifications for being an enemy of the state is questioning elections, or engaging in violence, I know who did it better.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 STACEY ABRAMS (D) FORMER GA GUBERNATORIAL CANDIDATE: Because concession means to acknowledge an action is right, true or proper. I cannot concede this.\par \par \uc2 (CROWD SHOUTING IN UNISON)\par \par \uc2 HILLARY CLINTON, 67TH UNITED STATES SECRETARY OF STATE: You can run the best campaign. You can even become the nominee and you can have the election stolen from you.\par \par \uc2 (CROWD SHOUTING IN UNISON)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: He's an illegitimate president in my mind.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Folks, look, I absolutely agree.\par \par \uc2 (CROWD SHOUTING IN UNISON)\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: No matter where you sit on the political spectrum today, any fair- minded person can see. Mob violence and election sour grapes are hardly confined to one party. And if we do a day by day political violence comparison, January 6 was one horrific day of violence, just an afternoon.\par \par \uc2 The BLM and Antifa riots that burned and destroyed cities, causing $1 to $2 billion worth of damage and injuring more than 2000 officers. That went on for months. But this is not just about the president branding, his political opponents, his violent extremist terrorists. Like those overzealous Puritans of old, he seeks to purge the unclean from the land.\par \par \uc2 By labeling a group of Americans as extremists, the government is free to investigate and prosecute them at a later date. This is an American tragedy and a failure of leadership. Rather than win over his opponents, the decision has been made to demonize and simply remove them. And if the President is so determined to save the soul of the nation, it's hard to see how placing abortion at the center of your redemption plan works.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: MAGA forces are determined to take this country backwards. Backwards to an America where there is no right to choose, no right to privacy, no right to contraception.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Now, Biden could credibly play the slightly confused grandfather with a garrulous Irishman spinning Blarney, but to try to take on the role of the spiritual reformer of the nation while advocating abortion with no restrictions when your own child is an embarrassing degenerate spectacle of drug use and debauch. The role might be beyond his reach, and a challenge beyond his powers.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is Congressman Jim Jordan, Ranking Member of the House Judiciary Committee; Mollie Hemingway, editor-in-chief of The Federalist and Fox News contributor; and Monica Crowley, former assistant secretary of the Treasury and host of the Monica Crowley podcast. Thank you all for being here.\par \par \uc2 Congressman, I'll start with you. The president said MAGA forces are determined to take this country backward. Yes. How should Republicans respond? And has the soul of the nation now been saved that the speech is over?\par \par \uc2 REP. JIM JORDAN (R-OH): No, I actually think, Raymond, he came across as a sad, angry, kind of bitter old man here. Kind of looked like the lighting and all, sort of like Darth Vader, it seemed to me, and understand in the past four days, he has called half the country fascist and extremist. And the country really wants the real problems out there.\par \par \uc2 They want them addressed, like you pointed out in your statement and your monologue. We went from a secured border to no border. We went from safe streets to record crime. We went from stable prices to a 41-year high inflation rate. And we went from $2 gas to $5 gas all in 18 months.\par \par \uc2 I think the nation would kind of like those addressed versus being called extremists and fascists and have the government now target your freedoms and liberties, like we have seen from the Biden administration Justice Department.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Now, Monica, you worked in the Trump White House. So you know optics. If you were going to like in your political opponents to Nazis, maybe employing a backdrop that looks like it could have come out of 1940s Germany, and then this opening was not the way to go.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: My fellow Americans, please if you have a seat, take it. I speak to you tonight from Sacred Ground in America.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Monica, can you remember an example of a prime time address by a president so laced with ugly and divisive political rhetoric, and a backdrop that looked like that?\par \par \uc2 MONICA CROWLEY, "THE MONICA CROWLEY PODCAST" HOST: Well, first of all - yes, the imagery there was almost satanic with that blood red lighting and the two Marines behind him. It was just insane.\par \par \uc2 Look, this was a garbage speech by a garbage president. And the fact he gave this disgusting, dangerous speech at Independence Hall is a sacrilege. He deliberately used two phrases.\par \par \uc2 He talked about extremism because of the association with terrorists and terrorism. And he also spoke about MAGA forces, as if 75 million Americans are part of some subterfuge guerrilla group.\par \par \uc2 But he deliberately used that kind of language, because he is making no secret of his intention of turning half the country into enemies of the state. And the purpose of that is so that they can leverage the power of the state against us.\par \par \uc2 This is an incredibly dangerous moment for the country. Every dictator throughout history has employed this kind of language with that kind of backdrop. And for most Americans, I think they're really starting to see what is actually happening here, which is that the most dangerous threat we face is that our own government has now been fully weaponized against us to the point where not only are the most fearsome agencies of the of the country turned against us from the DOJ, \uc2 FBI\uc2  and \uc2 IRS\uc2  . But also the President of the United States who is supposed to effectively represent us all, and instead has turned to all of his forces and fire upon half the country.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Mollie, then Biden went on to claim that it is the right who is engaged in grievance and culture wars. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: We're going to make the 21st century another American century, because the world needs this too. That's where we need to focus our energy. Not in the past, not on divisive culture wars, not on the politics of grievance. But on a future we can build together.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Mollie, who started these culture wars over school curricula and abortion? And how is returning abortion to the States destroying democracy?\par \par \uc2 MOLLIE HEMINGWAY, EDITOR-IN-CHIEF, THE FEDERALIST: Well, I keep thinking you making all these allusions to the summer of violence that we saw in 2020, where cities were destroyed with arson, where dozens of people were killed, where thousands of cops were injured, where so much destruction happened.\par \par \uc2 And President Trump gave a speech at Mount Rushmore where he did condemn political violence, and also praised our country, and spoke about the higher good that we all should aspire to. And the corporate media called that speech dark and divisive. This speech is easily the most disgraceful speech from a president in recent decades.\par \par \uc2 It is horrifying how he is issuing a call to war against every single American who didn't vote for him, or doesn't support him. And that's a lot of people, because his approval ratings are horrible. And part of the reason why is because as you know, Democrat policies are very corrosive to the body politic. The culture war where they are attacking people for recognizing biological reality, or for lying about what the Dobbs decision does, which enables people to have a say in whether to protect women and children from what we've experienced under the abortion regime previously.\par \par \uc2 This is just - it's a really dark and dangerous time. And it's at a time when violence has been committed recently against churches, Republican offices, maternal care centers, Supreme Court Justices who have been nearly assassinated. He didn't condemn any of that. And it's very dangerous.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: No, selective outrage. Congressman Jordan, here's what Biden thinks about 74 million Americans. Listen, I want your reaction.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: MAGA Republicans do not respect the Constitution. They do not believe in the rule of law. They do not recognize the will of the people. They refuse to accept the results of a free election.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Now, Congressman, he constantly tried to invoke President Trump and MAGA Republicans. But whether President Trump is in the picture or not, one gets the feeling they would be branded in the same way with the same language.\par \par \uc2 JORDAN: Right. No. And Monica, Mollie are both right. They are trying to weaponize the government against half the population against any Republican, any conservative, anyone who voted for President Trump.\par \par \uc2 And we see that, it's so bad that we have had 14 - I've talked about this, 14 whistleblowers come to our office as \uc2 FBI\uc2  agents, come to us as whistleblowers, talking about this whole narrative to treat everyone and label everyone as an extremist who simply opposes the radical policies of the left and of the Democrat Party.\par \par \uc2 As Mollie said, this is a dangerous and frightening time. The country, though, sees it. I've been out across the country helping candidates. The people I talk to, they see it for what it is. They're very nervous. And I think they're going to show up in a big way on November 8, and say enough of this. We got to put this in check and put Republicans back in control.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Monica, a new Quinnipiac poll shows that a majority of Democrats and Republicans both agree. The nation's democracy is in danger of collapse. 67 percent of Americans agree with that; 69 percent of both Republicans and Democrats also agree. Monica, isn't that actually proof that Biden is dividing the country? Because obviously, they think democracy is falling for different reasons, Republicans and Democrats. And it's not about unity.\par \par \uc2 CROWLEY: Yes, exactly right. When you see the split along those lines, and you see the numbers almost identical for Republicans and Democrats and even independents, it tells you that they're viewing the divisions in the country as extremely problematic for the future of America but for very different reasons.\par \par \uc2 This speech tonight was like an orgy of projection. Everything that President Biden accused \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and conservatives and the American First movement of they themselves are guilty of. Every single thing that he accused our side of doing, he and the Democrats are guilty of.\par \par \uc2 And I think most Americans now see that. And I do think this is a precarious moment, because we're at a real tipping point here. And I think a lot of the people he accused of being enemies of the state today really feel like this is the hill to die on.\par \par \uc2 The other side is at war with the Constitution, free market economics, economic freedom, individual liberty, and we're out here trying to preserve all of those things for the children and grandchildren of this country, and the other side is literally at war. And any Republican who is running for office needs to understand that and campaign on that for the future.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Mollie, I want to get to you before I run out of time. I want to get your reaction to the media's response to this speech. Listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: There was those moments of the American dream, right, where he reminded the country of what does actually make America great. And what we can do from here, right. There was hope.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Justice as Lincoln gave the House Divided speech. Here is the time when President Biden has chosen.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Mollie, your take on this, apparently, a group of historians met with Biden and convinced him that he was like FDR and Lincoln. FDR before the war, and Lincoln during the war. Your take on this trying to cast Biden as a wartime president.\par \par \uc2 HEMINGWAY: I think, it's very difficult to defend anything in this speech, and anyone who does it is having to work really hard to do it. You do not unite the country by going to war against half the country. You don't unite the country by weaponizing the Department of Justice to take out any and all political opponents.\par \par \uc2 I mean, it is just sheer gaslighting, what we're going through with the amount of executive authority that is being held by \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and how he's abusing that, whether it's through the loan scheme that he's using to buy votes, his weaponization of the Department of Justice, or any of the other policy issues that he's going through.\par \par \uc2 And it's to - it's just - you wonder how much more can happen and that corporate media will defend. But apparently, we see tonight that they're willing to defend and excuse anything.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Mollie, I sympathize with you. I think everybody's at a loss for words. How do you even put this fiction in context, because it's so at odds with the reality before us.\par \par \uc2 Panel, I thank you all for being here tonight on this important night.\par \par \uc2 Tonight's speech revealed that the Democrats have no midterm message given their record. Now, they'll stake their entire campaign around the divisive idea that Republicans are a threat to democracy.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, we have three GOP Senate candidates waiting to respond. J.D. Vance, \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  , Mehmet Oz will rebut by in moments.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Too much of what's happening in our country today is not normal. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and the MAGA Republicans representing extremism that threatens the very foundations of our republic.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Think about what the next step from there is. That really threatens the foundation of our republic.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is J.D. Vance, Ohio's Republican candidate for \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2  . J.D., thank you for being with us. 74 million Americans apparently are extremists now. How is that a winning strategy, campaign strategy for Biden and the Democrats?\par \par \uc2 J.D. VANCE, OHIO SENATE CANDIDATE: Well, I don't think it's a winning strategy at all, because it ignores the fact that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has effectively been in a position of leadership for 30 years in this country. And what has happened?\par \par \uc2 In 30 years, we basically haven't won a war. We have had rising suicide rates. We have rising mortality rates such that the life expectancy of the country has come down. We've lost a long-term battle against the Chinese and \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is actually taking us in the wrong direction and fighting us back.\par \par \uc2 And given all that, you would expect him maybe to take some responsibility for the fact that the country is not doing well. And instead, he's decided that the enemy is not him, not the people who've made bad decisions, but the patriotic Americans who are frustrated enough about the country that they love, to work to change it.\par \par \uc2 And I just can't imagine anything more divisive and more dangerous coming from a president who if he said anything during his campaign, it's that you would unite the country. This is not uniting at all. He's declaring war effectively on half of the country.\par \par \uc2 Not just \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  , I understand he doesn't like \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  , but on \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  's voters. And I can't imagine a president ever doing that in American history.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: J.D., this reaction to Biden's speech from CNN, well, I was doubled over when I heard it.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 EVA MCKEND, CNN NATIONAL POLITICS REPORTER: He almost seemed to sort of be reclaiming patriotism. Often you hear Republicans argue that they are the pro-America party, right? The Democrats are not