 \par \uc2 VANCE: Well, I think he probably feels even worse, but maybe he should stop voting 100 percent with \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  , and actually fight back. That's exactly what we're trying to do, which is why we've got to send these Democrats, especially in the Senate races back in come November.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: And J.D., you're neck and neck with Ryan. So we'll keep our eye on that race. Thank you for being here.\par \par \uc2 And now to Georgia, where another Democrat is distancing himself from \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  . When asked this week, if he wanted Biden to come campaign for him, incumbent senator \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  said, Frankly, I'm not focused on who I'm campaigning with, but who I'm campaigning for.\par \par \uc2 Well, the people he says he's campaigning for seem to be supporting my next guest. Republican candidate for Senate, \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  , who leads Warnock by two points in a brand new Emerson poll.\par \par \uc2 Herschel, thank you for being here. If Biden and his messaging about the soul of America is so popular, why are candidates like Warnock running for them? How do Georgians receive this, do you think?\par \par \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2  (R) GEORGIA SENATE CANDIDATE: Well, isn't it convenient that Senator Warnock, who's cut from the same cloth, who continue to want to divide this country same as \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  . And they believe that this country is such a racist, bad people that we have in this country, whereas I believe that we're not a racist, bad people. We got good people here.\par \par \uc2 And we have our problems. We can solve those problems together. So he is trying to distance himself right now, when he voted with 96 percent of the time, which means we're going to have an open border, which means you're going to put men and women sports.\par \par \uc2 Senator Warnock voted to put men and women sports. He voted for higher taxes. So that's what Senator Warnock really is right now. So he is trying to distance himself now, because he just want to get your vote so he is trying to entice you to give him a vote.\par \par \uc2 But if you vote for him again, we're going to get the same old thing. That's why the people of Georgia are looking for someone new for - like \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  . Now, I encourage you guys to go to Teamherschel.com. It's time for us to take the people seat back and put \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  and let's see.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Herschel, Senator Warnock called you out on the campaign trail this week. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. RAPHAEL WARNOCK (D-GA): My opponent said that he would debate me, anywhere, anytime. And then I accepted three debates. And I'm still waiting on him to agree to debate me.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Herschel, is that true? As he accepted three debates and why haven't you accepted any?\par \par \uc2 WALKER: Well, I have accepted a bay in his hometown and that's what I want everyone to know. Senator Warnock is a liar. He's lied on me and you see that. They put almost $15 million against me in a race that is virtually tied on my hip. I want\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: You're ahead by 2 points.\par \par \uc2 WALKER: --to support me because right now, Senator Warnock do not want to debate me, not asked for a debate in (inaudible) in front of an audience so everyone can see the contrast that he voted. And now think about this, he voted to put men and women sports. He voted for woman to kill a baby. He voted for higher taxes. He don't want to--\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: So you are committing tonight to a debate with Warnock.\par \par \uc2 WALKER: Senator Warnock on October the 14th in Savannah, Georgia, I'm committed to a debate and I told Senator Warnock, I want to tweet today. I said, wake up, get ready, put your shoes on and meet me in Savannah on October the 14th for a debate that'd be televised statewide, and he's still not decided he going to do it yet.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: OK. Well, Warnock. It's in your court. We want to see this debate, Herschel. \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  , thank you for being here.\par \par \uc2 Democrat Senate candidate \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  is still refusing to debate ahead of the midterm elections, citing his own health as the reason. Well, last night, he did his first TV interview since suffering a stroke days before the Pennsylvania primary. His challenger, Dr. Mehmet Oz is here to react next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Welcome back to THE INGRAHAM ANGLE. I'm Raymond Arroyo.\par \par \uc2 We told you last night how \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  is running away from a debate with my next guest, Dr. Oz, ahead of November's Senate election in Pennsylvania. This after claiming earlier this year that candidate need to debate. Well, now he's blaming his health after a stroke he suffered days before the primary. When asked how Pennsylvanians could be assured that he would be able to represent them if he won, he reacted by trashing Dr. Oz.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LT. GOV. \uc2 JOHN FETTERMAN\uc2  , (D) PENNSYLVANIA SENATE CANDIDATE: They're trying to not focus on the condition of their campaign right now. And when they want to get into a serious conversation and really talk about having a debate, I'd be happy to engage in that. But right now, the fact that they have chosen to have a deeply unserious campaign to just ridicule somebody that is just recovering from a stroke.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: I'll bet that makes much more sense in closed captioning.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now as Dr. Mehmet oz, Pennsylvania's GOP candidate for \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2  . Dr. Oz, your reaction to Fetterman's comments? Were you not in serious conversation with the Fetterman campaign about the debate?\par \par \uc2 DR. MEHMET OZ, (R) PENNSYLVANIA SENATE CANDIDATE: Of course. His response is tragic. I'm a doctor. I understand completely the challenges of recovering from a stroke. I've offered to help figure out a way for \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  to participate in a debate so people can hear him defend far left, radical positions.\par \par \uc2 I've also offered the possibility that the real issue is he doesn't want to actually come on a debate stage. He didn't want to do it in his primary. He was sort of forced to, didn't do very well. He has now declined two of the five debates hosted by major media organizations that I've accepted.\par \par \uc2 By the way, he has not accepted any questions on his campaign stops which he has only done three of, and I have done 180. He went to the Hamptons for a fundraiser, went on MSNBC, as you just showed, refused to answer a basic yes or no question about a debate. I think he's avoiding responsibility for his radical statements, and he is out of touch with mainstream Pennsylvania values.\par \par \uc2 By the way, he's getting a ton of money pouring in from the coasts, from people who don't know how dangerous he is. If you're worried about that, go to DoctorOz.com and support my campaign. We are going to win back in November, but we've got to have the ability to define who he is.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Dr. Oz, he has tried to label you as a carpetbagger. If you listen to these ads, your practically Frankie Valli. You're a jersey boy forever and ever if you listen to those ads. How do you escape that label?\par \par \uc2 OZ: People in Pennsylvania don't care where you're from. They care what you stand for. By the way, I grew up just south of Philadelphia. I went to medical school in Philly. I've got plenty of roots here.\par \par \uc2 The issue for \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  is he doesn't want to talk about the real challenges that Pennsylvanians face. I'll give you an example. He wants to release one-third of all prisoners, no life sentences, legalize all drugs. What's he thinking? Homicides have increased 60 percent here in Philadelphia since he took office. People in Pennsylvania want solutions. They want someone who's going to fix the problems that they are struggling with at their kitchen table. I'm the person to do that. \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  doesn't want to talk about those topics.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Dr. Oz, as you just mentioned, Fetterman says it's high time to legalize marijuana. Listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LT. GOV. \uc2 JOHN FETTERMAN\uc2  , (D) PENNSYLVANIA SENATE CANDIDATE: The war on drugs needs to stop and we need to legalize weed across this country. Then you add in the revenue, then you add in the jobs for our farmers, for our veterans, and just the freedom of it. This idea that we have allowed a plant to be illegal and criminalized in this country is absurd.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Now, Fetterman is marching in a Labor Day parade with \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and asking him to legalize marijuana before he arrives. Now, Fetterman is also launching a large-scale pardoning project for people with nonviolent marijuana criminal convictions in Pennsylvania. Dr. Oz, first, what's your reaction to this legalization and this pardoning project? And why is the only marching with \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  , do you think?\par \par \uc2 OZ: Well, I didn't join \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  for either of the two other events he had in Pennsylvania. My issue is that he wants to legalize all drugs. He even supports heroin injection sites. And we have the largest open-air drug market in the country here in Philadelphia. It's destroying the city. Fetterman is out of touch with what's going on in Pennsylvania. He's completely missing the target. Pennsylvanians need help in the things that they're struggling with right now. That includes cost-of-living, crime, and the open border with fentanyl that's hurting our young ones.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Dr. Oz, I do need to get you on the record here. Just two years ago, you said that weed was an underused tool and that it was safer than alcohol. What changed?\par \par \uc2 OZ: Oh, no, I'm supportive of medical marijuana. I think with the doctors' involvement, especially for seniors who have end-of-life issues, suffering from pain, I think it's a safer solution than, for example, narcotics in many cases. But it has to be studied, and we have to understand it, and therefore physicians should be involved. I don't want to give marijuana to every young person in America who is struggling with getting through their day. We already have a problem with getting folks mobilized to enter the workforce. I think that's a slippery slope.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Doctor, I need to talk to you also about the polls. Polls are tightening here. Fetterman is up two, four in one of the most recent polls I saw. That is a big plummet for him. Why do you think these polls are tightening so? Does it have something to do with his condition? And as a medical professional, is that health that Fetterman finds himself into date disqualifying?\par \par \uc2 OZ: Raymond, I don't know about his health because he hasn't told the true story, the transparent story that's require around his health. But people in Pennsylvania are getting frustrated that he refuses to engage me in talking about the real challenges we face, the topics that I know everyone is struggling with in their homes as they watch this show right now. And he needs to be held accountable for that. That's why the polls are close. Our campaign is pumping well. We have gotten all the right topics.\par \par \uc2 I'm more interested in making the lives of Pennsylvanians better every single day, to fix the problems. That's what doctors do. DoctorOz.com if you want to help me.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Dr. Oz, after that report showing how school-aged children fell behind because of the COVID lockdowns, the White House is trying to rewrite history about school closures. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2  , WHITE HOUSE DEPUTY PRESS SECRETARY: We were in a place where, again, schools were not open. It shows you how mismanaged the pandemic was and how the impact of that mismanagement had on kids' progress and academic well-being.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Dr. Oz, this White House and the CDC worked with the teachers unions to keep schools close. Your reaction to that?\par \par \uc2 OZ: Raymond, the White House weaponized science. You mix politics and science, politics and medicine, you get politics. That's all that's left. They literally pushed us to close schools, to offer mandates. Here in Philadelphia, we have mask mandates again for children in school, and preschoolers have to wear them all year round. There is no medical defense for that. And yet they are willing to do it because the teachers unions desire it.\par \par \uc2 I don't understand the benefit, and more importantly, I appreciate the damage that's done by putting a mask on a child and not letting them see the world and see the mouths of people talking to them, because they don't learn as well.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: No, it's an outrage, and you see the horrible scoring in that nation's report card today. Dr. Mehmet Oz, I thank you for being here.\par \par \uc2 While the judge hearing former President Trump's special master request didn't rule outright today, she completely eviscerated the Biden DOJ in court. Our legal eagles are here next to respond. You need to hear this, and a special announcement. Don't move.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Breaking tonight, we are awaiting a federal judge's ruling on Trump's request for a special master to review the documents seized during that Mar-a-Lago raid. Judge Aileen Cannon said she will issue a written ruling in due course. At times she seemed skeptical of the government's argument, asking what is the harm of appointing a special master, so much so that even the media had to admit that they likely will be disappointed with her eventual ruling.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 EVAN PEREZ, CNN SENIOR JUSTICE CORRESPONDENT: She seems inclined to grant former President Trump's request for a special master, a third party, to review some of the documents that the Justice Department has in its hands.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: The judge had indicated that she was inclined to approve the request.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I still wouldn't be surprised if she went ahead and did it only because she clearly views this as an unprecedented situation.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Joining me now is David Schoen, former lead counsel for President Trump's second impeachment, and Harmeet Dhillon, civil rights attorney and founder of the Dhillon Law Group. Thank you both for being here.\par \par \uc2 David, it seems like the judge is leaning toward appointing this special master, somebody to review these documents. But the DOJ is still dead set against it. If they really think they have such a strong case, why fight something like this? And does a special master even matter at this late date?\par \par \uc2 DAVID SCHOEN, TRUMP'S DEFENSE LAWYER DURING SECOND IMPEACHMENT TRIAL: Very good questions. You're absolutely right. It should be a no-brainer to appoint a special master in this case. I wish the Trump team had moved for it earlier because now there is an argument about mootness because the DOJ says they've already reviewed the documents.\par \par \uc2 The courts and Justice Department even have recognized the problem with using a taint or filter team like they have done so far, even though that's been standard practice, because they are still part of the same Justice Department. In fact, after the fourth circuit criticized this, they put in a special matters unit to deal with this.\par \par \uc2 But let me tell you something, why I believe it's political completely, the political weaponization of this process. It should be a no-brainer. If you care about the confidence of the American public in an important process like this, you put in a special master, you agree with it, then you move forward.\par \par \uc2 But what hasn't been commented on yet is on the 30th of August an amicus brief was filed by a group of so-called former government employees arguing against a special master. Who was behind the brief? Norm Eisen. Norm Eisen was in the Obama administration. He has been the Trump hater par excellence for the past few years. He wrote a treatise that Trump should be impeached and convicted and sentenced, on and on and on. It's a political process, and that's a real telltale sign of it there.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Harmeet, they seized 33 items of evidence, most of them boxes from Mar-a-Lago, and of those boxes 100 documents were marked classified. Where does executive privilege stand given the fact that the judge dismissed DOJ claims that the president had not because the documents weren't his? That was their argument.\par \par \uc2 HARMEET DHILLON, CEO, CENTER FOR AMERICAN LIBERTY: Look, this is obviously a situation where there is a dispute between the two sides as to whether President Trump had the right to possess those documents, whether executive privilege applied, whether the documents had been declassified, whether or not they were declassified, whether he had a right to have them under the Presidential Records Act, and the timing of turning over these documents to the National Archives, which under statute, can take a significant amount of time.\par \par \uc2 And so instead of dealing with that dispute head-on, the \uc2 United States Department of Justice\uc2  and \uc2 FBI\uc2  chose to manufacture a dispute, I think mislead the original magistrate judge about the nature of that dispute, and then go in and simply just run roughshod over not just the executive privilege argument but also attorney-client privilege. I saw no mention of work product privilege. President Trump is in a number of different lawsuits. I represent in some civil lawsuit. And there are a number of different privileges that can and should apply here.\par \par \uc2 And so like David said, it is a no-brainer that in a circumstance like this, and in my opinion, in all circumstances, it is inappropriate for the \uc2 United States Department of Justice\uc2  employees to rifle through these papers without regard for privilege, without regard for the president's claims and rights. If they have good cause to have these materials, then they should be willing to wait and have a neutral arbiter decide that, and then have that litigated in front of a federal judge. So the fact that they did it this way really demonstrates their desperation, and the political motives underlying this entire rate.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: David, the Department of Justice is citing in their response probable cause for obstruction of justice. Did the Trump team misrepresent what was in these files? And might they be in some jeopardy here? You'll remember on May 11, there was the grand jury subpoena. They said, what do you have in the files? The Trump people said we reviewed the files. There's nothing here. The \uc2 FBI\uc2  crashes in and finds it. Might this have all, A, been a set up, and, B, is the Trump legal team in any trouble?\par \par \uc2 SCHOEN: I believe there is a great deal more to this issue than has come out so far. I can say this. Evan Corcoran, one of the lawyers, was hired to be my local counsel in another case. Fine lawyer, fine person, as honest as the day is long. I don't believe for a second that he would've been a part of any misrepresentation to the government.\par \par \uc2 And I'm going to say this, as I've said before, I do not believe, and I know him very well now, that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  would have in any way intentionally, knowingly, or willfully had control documents that he didn't believe he was entitled to have control over or did anything wrong. I firmly believe that, frankly. So there's a lot more to this story to come out.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Harmeet, where do you think this goes from here? Let's say the judge appoints this special master to review materials. What makes us think DOJ hasn't already passed all of this along to the investigators in the other cases?\par \par \uc2 DHILLON: Great question. So it looks to me after this two-hour spirited hearing that the judge is likely to order a special master. She's already indicated that she's going to unseal the detailed inventory of the materials.\par \par \uc2 Now, what happens next, as the judge also indicated today in the hearing according to press that was there, that she is not going to stand in the way of the intelligence officials doing their damage assessment. So that's kind of a compromise with the government position. And to the people who are arguing that this is moot because it is too late, I disagree with that. I think that a new review can absolutely remedy the situation.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: OK, David, Harmeet, thank you for being here. We'll check in with you in the days ahead.\par \par \uc2 Up next, a big announcement about something new happening on THE INGRAHAM ANGLE, and I have a big announcement. Stay right there. You don't want to miss this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Earlier this week we went on the road to Detroit where I spent time with actor Mark Wahlberg and a few others for a big charity event. They gave me some golf tips, since I don't golf. Here's a sneak preview of the proceedings.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Tell me what this foundation means to you. I know it started really as a call from your local priest.\par \par \uc2 MARK WAHLBERG, ACTOR: Yes. Listen, we just want to give back to kids growing up in similar situations. There's always been a focus on inner city kids.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Your father was the golf coach at Fordham.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN DILLON, ACTOR: Yes, he was. Yes.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: What did he teach you?\par \par \uc2 DILLON: He taught me mostly about the mental aspect. Then he'd help me with my swing.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: I would only do this for you. What's the tip.\par \par \uc2 WAHLBERG: The tip?\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 WAHLBERG: Pick up tennis.\par \par \uc2 What's the one or two things I need to be mindful of?\par \par \uc2 SUGAR RAY LEONARD, FORMER BOXER: Hit the ball.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Don't do that! Don't do that!\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: OK, you'll see much more of my interview with Mark Wahlberg tomorrow night 10:00 p.m. eastern. I also talked to actor Kevin Dillon, boxing greats Evander Holyfield, Sugar Ray Leonard . You will not want to miss this and my wonderful golf game.\par \par \uc2 Make sure you preorder my book, "The Wise Men Who Found Christmas." It gets released October 11th and will make a wonderful Christmas gift for the children in your life.\par \par \uc2 That's it for us tonight. I'm Raymond Arroyo in for Laura Ingraham. I'll be back here this morning night. Thanks for watching this special edition of THE INGRAHAM ANGLE. "GUTFELD!" is next.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document INGANG0020220902ei9100001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Interview with Former White House Senior Advisor \uc2 Cedric Richmond\uc2 ; Interview with Former Commander of U.S. Central Command, Retired General Kenneth Frank McKenzie, Biden Attacks MAGA Republicans; Democrats Critical of Student Loan Handout; DOJ' Unseals Redacted Affidavit; Dr. Keith Knutson is Interviewed about a Cancer Vaccine; A Look at the Ukrainian Freedom Orchestra\b0\par\par\uc2 Jennifer Griffin, Lucas Tomlinson, Juan Williams, Karl Rove \par \uc2 8,601 \uc2 words\par \uc2 28 August 2022\par \uc2 Fox News: Fox News Sunday\par \uc2 FOXN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 JENNIFER GRIFFIN, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: I'm Jennifer Griffin.\par \par \uc2 The White House gives students a big break, gives Ukraine another round of aid, and goes all in against MAGA.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: The extreme MAGA Republicans have made their choice to go backwards, full of anger, violence, hate, and division.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN (voice-over): President Biden coming out swinging at Republicans after a string of victories leads to renewed optimism for Democrats in November -- while his plan to give tens of thousands of dollars to student loan borrowers faces legal and political hurdles.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: It's about opportunity. It's about giving people a fair shot.\par \par \uc2 SEN. MITCH MCCONNELL (R-KY): The people who benefit from it will love it. The question is, is it fair to everyone else?\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: We'll ask former White House senior advisor \uc2 Cedric Richmond\uc2 , who now advises the DNC, about this election year gamble.\par \par \uc2 Then, the U.S. announces the single largest share of weapons to be sent to Kyiv since the war in Ukraine began.\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: This announcement is part of our efforts to ensure Ukraine's military can continue to defend its people.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: We will discuss the U.S. response there months after Russia invaded, and the state of Afghanistan one year since the last American forces left with retired General \uc2 Frank McKenzie\uc2 , who oversaw the controversial withdrawal as CentCom commander.\par \par \uc2 And a judge orders the release of a redacted version of the affidavit used to search former President Trump's Mar-a-Lago estate. We'll ask our Sunday panel whether there was ever a national security risk.\par \par \uc2 Then, researchers may be getting closer to a vaccine for cancer. We'll sit down with one of the scientists leading the charge, Dr. Keith Knutson of the \uc2 Mayo Clinic\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Plus -- making music, not war. Ukrainian musicians, including recent refugees, fight for their country far from the front lines, and send a message to Putin.\par \par \uc2 All right now on "FOX News Sunday".\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN (on camera): And hello again from FOX News in Washington.\par \par \uc2 President Biden returned to the campaign trail this week trying to ride a wave of positivity into midterms just ten weeks from now. But the White House plan to wipe out billions of dollars in student loans is getting major pushback from Republicans and even some Democrats, who question its fairness, and the president himself is facing questions over his harsh new comments about some members of the Republican Party.\par \par \uc2 In a moment, we will talk with a former top aide to the president who now advises the DNC, former Congressman \uc2 Cedric Richmond\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But first, let's turn to Lucas Tomlinson at the White House -- Lucas.\par \par \uc2 LUCAS TOMLINSON, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Jen, President Biden kicking off his campaign outside the nation's capital where he took aim at former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and his supporters.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAAPE)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I respect conservative Republicans. I don't respect these MAGA Republicans.\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON (voice-over): The president comparing Republicans who embrace \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's make America great again philosophy to, quote, semi-fascism.\par \par \uc2 JEAN-PIERRE: When it comes to the extreme ultra wing of Republicans, they are attacking democracy. He called it what it is, that's what it is.\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON: What the president also did for some student loans forgiveness, one of progressive pipe dream, how much will it cost? A matter of debate.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: It's not going to cause inflation, number one. Number two, it will generate economic growth.\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON: Many Republicans think the move is illegal.\par \par \uc2 SEN. TOM COTTON (R-AR): \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has used the vast powers of the administrative state to reward his political supporters in higher education.\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON: After the announcement, President Biden asked about the search of Trump's Florida home.\par \par \uc2 PETER DOOCY, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Mr. President, how much advance notice did you have of the \uc2 FBI\uc2 's plans to search Mar-a-Lago?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I didn't have any advance notice. No, zero. Not one single bit. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON: The former president responding, does anybody really believe this?\par \par \uc2 A partially redacted version of the probable cause affidavit was released Friday. A federal judge will hear arguments next month on Trump's request for a third-party to review the material. Fox has learned the director of national intelligence will conduct a damage assessment about the classified documents which, quote, could endanger human sources.\par \par \uc2 This as inflation remains near 40-year highs. The chairman of the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  warning he will continue raising interest rates.\par \par \uc2 JEROME POWELL\uc2 , FEDERAL RESERVE CHAIRMAN: The failure to restore price stability would mean far greater pain.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON (on camera): The stock market plummeted following Jerome Powell's remarks. The Dow dropping a thousand points to end the week -- Jen.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: Lucas Tomlinson reporting from the White House -- Lucas, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now, one of the president's closest advisors, former Congressman \uc2 Cedric Richmond\uc2  of the \uc2 Democratic National Committee\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Congressman, welcome back to "FOX News Sunday".\par \par \uc2 CEDRIC RICHMOND, DNC SENIOR ADVISER: Thank you for having me.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: Gas prices are down, the president passed his climate and health care bill and he has kept a promise to forgive some student debt.\par \par \uc2 Thursday night, he made the case for Democrats in a fiery DNC speech. He told fund-raisers, quote: What we're seeing now is either the beginning or the death knell of an extreme MAG philosophy. It's not just Trump, it's the entire philosophy that underpins the -- I'm going to say something -- it's likes semi-fascism.\par \par \uc2 What did he mean?\par \par \uc2 RICHMOND: Well, I think he's very clear about it, that he's drawn a comparison to a party that wants to unite us, a party that's worried about families and what they're dealing with, and the party that's still litigating the 2020 election, the party that's taking away reproductive rights for women, a party that is attacking families and law enforcement and questioning the \uc2 FBI\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 As opposed to a party that really is about lifting up America to fulfill its best promise, and that's that anyone can live and be the best that they can be -- and we want to help people do that. And I think the president is riding that wave, bringing down costs of prescription drugs and all of those things.\par \par \uc2 And I think the best thing that he can do and what's appropriate is to draw a contrast between what the two parties stand for.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: Here he is in November of 2020 after he won.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: It's time to put away the harsh rhetoric, lower the temperature, see each other again, listen to each other again. And to make progress, we have to stop treating our opponents as our enemies.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: How does that square with calling his opponent semi-fascists? People compare this to \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  saying deplorables on the campaign trail.\par \par \uc2 RICHMOND: Well, people who said that would be dead wrong. The president works across the aisle all the time and gets criticized for it. We passed the infrastructure bill with bipartisan support. In fact, presidents for decades have talked about passing the infrastructure structure bill. But he did it with Republican support.\par \par \uc2 He just passed a bill to help our veterans with bipartisan support. He passed a CHIPS bill so that we could build semiconductors in this country and secure American independence and leap towards the future with bipartisan support.\par \par \uc2 So he works with Republicans and Democrats all the time, but here's the difference between the last president and this one: this president focuses on people and their families, not himself.\par \par \uc2 So we will work with Republicans, we'll work with Democrats, and I think he's shown that over and over again. And that's what he was saying in that November clip that you just showed, and that's what he's doing every day.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: Let's talk about student loans. The president announced his plan to forgive $10,000 of student loans for borrowers earning less than $125,000 per year and $20,000 for Pell grant borrowers. But take a look at the average tuition numbers in recent years, $9,400 for public universities, $37,000 for private universities.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile the average Ivy League tuition is $78,000 a year, even though the average Ivy League endowment is $22 billion.\par \par \uc2 Critics say these tools will simply raise tuitions higher.\par \par \uc2 RICHMOND: And we're going to continue to fight that and the president has been very clear about the rising cost of college education and the fact that we need to bring it under control, and he's working on that.\par \par \uc2 But let me be clear about all of those criticisms. We have a bunch of things in this country -- this country is not a zero-sum game where somebody has to fail for others to succeed. And so, we have historic tax credits, we have business tax credits to help those who own businesses, and not everyone in America owns a business. But we help business owners because we want to promote it.\par \par \uc2 So, what the president is saying for those people who fight and search and go gain an education, that we're going to help you because education is the best way out of poverty. And you shouldn't be saddled with that debt because home ownership is the best way to pass wealth on from generations.\par \par \uc2 So this is about helping the working class people. Ninety percent of this benefit will go to people who earn less than $75,000 a year. So those plumbers and electricians and police officers and teachers, this will help their children reach that American dream that they want.\par \par \uc2 And remember, everything doesn't benefit everybody, but it's about the common good and this president is focused on working families and empowering people to reach their full potential and I think that this does exactly what he's trying to do.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: But is the president's move even legal?\par \par \uc2 Here are the president and Speaker Pelosi just last year.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I'm prepared to write off the $10,000 debt, but not $50,000.\par \par \uc2 ANDERSON COOPER, CNN HOST: Mr. President, let me ask you --\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Because I don't think I have the authority to do it by the sign of a pen.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 NANCY PELOSI\uc2  (D-CA), SPEAKER OF THE HOUSE: People think that the president of the United States has the power for debt forgiveness. He does not. He can postpone, he can delay, but he does not have that power.\par \par \uc2 That would -- that has to be an act of Congress.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: What changed?\par \par \uc2 RICHMOND: Well, Congress acted. And if you look at the legislation that allowed President Trump and President Biden to delay loan repayment, it's the same legislation that allows him to forgive $10,000 worth of debt and $20,000 worth of debt to those who are on Pell grants, which is the hardest and lowest income people that are going to college. So it's the same thing.\par \par \uc2 And, look, I call it how I see it. It's the same thing that allowed President Trump to do it that allows President Biden to do it.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: Legal experts, they expect court challenges. You know conservative lawyers are already looking for the right person or group with standing to sue.\par \par \uc2 Will this even hold up in court?\par \par \uc2 RICHMOND: Yes, it will hold up in court, just like the 2020 election, which the president won with over 7 million votes.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: Why is the president doing this right before the midterms? Isn't this just an election gimmick?\par \par \uc2 RICHMOND: It's not. What it's doing is empowering people to get an education, lift themselves out of poverty, free up the high cost of the debt that they owe to colleges so that they can invest in American dreams, buy a home, invest in their children, be better community members. This is about lifting up America.\par \par \uc2 And the president said from day one that he would lift up America from the bottom up and the middle out, and the country voted for him to do that.\par \par \uc2 So, this is not a surprise, and we said it on the campaign trail that he would do this, and he's fulfilling a campaign commitment that he made to invest in America's working families and this is what this does.\par \par \uc2 And, look, I just don't understand the criticism that we're getting from the right. We have business deductions everywhere and all day long. We just gave in 2017, $2 trillion to the top 1 percent in businesses, 99 percent of the country did not benefit from the 2017 $2 trillion tax cut. People all across the country are going to benefit from this debt relief.\par \par \uc2 And so, look, we're not picking winners and losers. America is a country about the common good, and what we're doing here is making sure that we invest in all of America. And right now, we are investing in working families.\par \par \uc2 So those viewers out there that are busting their behinds to make ends meet, we're investing in you and we're not going to leave any family behind, whether it's rural or urban.\par \par \uc2 That's what this president is going to do. He campaigned on it and he's living up to his campaign commitments.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: Congressman, thank you for joining us. We hope you'll come back.\par \par \uc2 RICHMOND: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: Up next, Americans watched in horror a year ago as the chaotic departure of U.S. forces in Afghanistan unfolded. We will speak with retired General \uc2 Frank McKenzie\uc2 , a former head of CentCom, who was ordered to withdraw all U.S. troops, about what could have been done differently.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: Tuesday marks one year since the war in Afghanistan ended -- 20 years, $1 trillion, 2,456 American service members killed in action, including 13 killed by a suicide bomber outside the airport gate in Kabul almost exactly a year ago.\par \par \uc2 It happened in the final days of the chaotic withdrawal of U.S. forces from the country. U.S. forces were sent back and evacuated more than 124,000 people in 17 days, the largest evacuation of civilians in U.S. history.\par \par \uc2 On Friday, Cheryl Rex, a Gold Star mother whose son Dylan died in the attack, told Martha MacCallum the message she would like to send to the White House.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHERYL REX, MOTHER OF MARINE KILLED IN KABUL AIRPORT BOMBING: If I could speak to the administration right now, I think better planning is what they needed to do and they did not do that. They basically failed our children.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: We are also marking one year since Kabul fell to the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  as Afghanistan's elected government collapsed during the pull out of the American military.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now is the former commander of U.S. Central Command, retired General Kenneth Frank McKenzie, who was ordered to execute the withdrawal.\par \par \uc2 General McKenzie, welcome to "FOX News Sunday".\par \par \uc2 GEN. \uc2 FRANK MCKENZIE\uc2  (RET), FORMER CENTCOM COMMANDER: Jennifer, thank you. It's good to be with you today.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: General McKenzie, this was the president on July 8th, a month before Kabul fell to the \uc2 Taliban\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: The \uc2 Taliban\uc2  is not the North Vietnamese army. They're not -- they're not remotely comparable in terms of capability. There's going to be no circumstance where you see people lifted off the roof of an embassy of the United States from Afghanistan. It is not at all comparable.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: And yet the scenes of Afghans clinging to the underbelly of U.S. aircraft were remarkably similar to Saigon.\par \par \uc2 What could you have done differently from a planning perspective?\par \par \uc2 MCKENZIE: Well, Jennifer, what happened in August was not preordained. It was not set in the stars. We made a series of decisions that took us to that point in August.\par \par \uc2 The basics edition was the decision to withdraw completely from Afghanistan. There were alternatives to that, and those are alternatives that were not taken -- principally that the possibility of maintaining a small hard platform of about 2,500 U.S. and a significant \uc2 NATO\uc2  presence to continue to support at the ministerial level and the regional level to the Afghans. That would have given us the capability to remain in the country, to continue to pursue our counterterrorism objectives, and we believe avoid the collapse of the Afghan government. We did not choose to do that.\par \par \uc2 Subsequently, after we made the decision to go to zero, the decision to try to maintain an embassy platform until far too late contributed also to what happened in August.\par \par \uc2 Those are I think two of the big decisions that led us to the events that you just described in August.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: So, in your opinion, was the withdrawal a mistake?\par \par \uc2 MCKENZIE: I advised against withdrawing, my recommendation and my opinion, and it remains so today was. We had the opportunity to remain in the country with a small force.\par \par \uc2 I realize the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  could very well have chosen to attack us, but I do not believe based on intelligence I was reading at the time that we would have -- that we would have been forced to add more forces in order to maintain a 2,500 force level in Afghanistan. We have coupled that force level with an aggressive diplomatic campaign against the \uc2 Taliban\uc2 , probably more aggressive than the Doha agreement and those negotiations.\par \par \uc2 So, it would have had to of been a whole government effort, but it remains my position that we had the opportunity to stay, keep the Afghanistan -- the government of Afghanistan running.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: If you knew that Kabul was likely to fall if the U.S. pulled out all troops, should you and the other generals have resigned to try and stop it?\par \par \uc2 MCKENZIE: Well, we believe that Kabul would fall if we pull out our troops. It was just a question of when Kabul would fall and we have been saying that really since the fall of the year perform. That had been a consistent position of Central Command, our subordinates in Afghanistan, that if we leave, they're going to collapse. It's just a question of when they're going to collapse.\par \par \uc2 And, you know, we thought it might be a question of weeks, months, but as we got into the summer and the government of Afghanistan proved unable to marshal its will and its forces to defend their country, you saw an acceleration of the timeline and -- but it was not -- this was not a particular surprise to us.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: General McKenzie, we heard over and over that no one predicted Kabul would fall so quickly. But you issued this warning on August 8th that, quote, in the last formal intelligence assessment I sent up on the 8th of August, I said it is my judgment that Kabul is going to fall. I did not think it was going to fall that weekend, I thought it might last a little bit longer, 30 days or so.\par \par \uc2 General, 30 days would have been September 7th, before the 28th anniversary of 9/11 when the president wanted all troops out.\par \par \uc2 Did the president know Kabul was going to fall that quickly?\par \par \uc2 MCKENZIE: Look, I'm confident that my assessments went up the chain of command. I'm sure the president saw them. The president of United States has to make decisions based on a variety of factors.\par \par \uc2 My input was certainly one of those factors and I appreciate the opportunity to have had that input, but the president is going to have to make decisions based on a much broader range of considerations.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: Here is President Biden justifying his decision.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Look, let's put this thing in perspective here. What interest do we have in Afghanistan at this point with al Qaeda gone?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: How did you feel when you heard the president say that, you knew it wasn't true?\par \par \uc2 MCKENZIE: Well, our position has always been that al Qaeda is there because the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  host them. That's why we went to Afghanistan in the first place, and certainly, al Qaeda has been rocked back on their heels in recent years.\par \par \uc2 But al Qaeda is still present in Afghanistan and also ISIS is still present in Afghanistan, and both of those entities have a long-term aspirational goal to attack us in our homeland. And given the breathing room to reestablish themselves and reassert their strength, we believe they will -- they will do that.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: Here's what you told \uc2 VOA\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MCKENZIE: If we leave, eventually, al Qaeda and ISIS in particular are going to go into open space in Afghanistan and the threat the United States is going to rise.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: Do you think troops will have to be sent back to Afghanistan?\par \par \uc2 MCKENZIE: You know, that's a -- that's a difficult question. I know this: it is in the best long-term interest of the United States to not allow these centers of violent extremism to grow and expand in Afghanistan. And I believe under the current \uc2 Taliban\uc2  regime, that's probably what's going to happen.\par \par \uc2 The last time I was looking at intelligence, that was a position we had. I follow it like everybody else does now, in the newspaper and other sources. But I see nothing to change that opinion that the threat is growing in Afghanistan and it's merely a matter of time.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: To what extent is former President Trump and his decision to negotiate with the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  and his repeated calls to his national security team to pull all U.S. troops out to blame for how the war ended?\par \par \uc2 MCKENZIE: Jennifer, the president of United States owns the final responsibly for these actions. I believe we had two presidents of United States that wanted to exit Afghanistan and they might not have had anything else in common, but they shared that common view. So you had a continuity of objectives across two administrations that really allowed the events to occur to occur in the manner that they did.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: And the agreement, a mistake?\par \par \uc2 MCKENZIE: The Doha agreement had the potential to be a useful approach as long as we apply the principle of conditionality. And by that, I mean, Jennifer, that the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  had to live up to their half of the agreement. They did not.\par \par \uc2 It was evident pretty early on that they were not doing that, but we never -- we never effectively held them to task.\par \par \uc2 The other unintended effect of the Doha agreement was to keep the Afghan government out of the negotiating process and I believe that had a profound deflationary effect on morale both in the government of Afghanistan and their forces in the field.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: The suicide bomber at Abbey Gate was released from the Bagram Airbase prison after you left. You gave the order to leave Bagram.\par \par \uc2 Why did you not close the prison or secure the \uc2 Taliban\uc2 , al Qaeda and ISIS prisoners there before you left?\par \par \uc2 MCKENZIE: We had no capability to do that. We don't -- first of all, we were not guarding those prisoners. They were being guarded by the Afghans. We had not guarded them for some time.\par \par \uc2 When we had to go down to effectively zero, but actually 650 forces designed to protect the U.S. embassy in Kabul, we no longer have the capability to hold Bagram and to oversee and assist those Afghans who were sitting on top of the prison structure. That was an effective going -- of not accepting the argument to stay at 2,500.\par \par \uc2 At 2,500, we would have been able to maintain Bagram, and that was a principle -- that was one of the things that was attractive about staying at 2,500. We would have been able to maintain oversight, not direct oversight, but support for those Afghans that were sitting on those prisons. All of that went away when we left.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: That brings me to Syria and the al-Hawl refugee camp filled with the families of ISIS fighters, their children. What concerns you about that? And do you think U.S. troops should still be in Syria?\par \par \uc2 MCKENZIE: So, al-Hawl I think is a problem that exists in two dimensions. You got a largely defenseless population there, principally women, principally children, all very young. The threat of some immediate event to them, be it cholera, be it coronavirus, be it a terrorist attack and killing large numbers of people is very real.\par \par \uc2 At the same time and along the deeper dimension, the radicalization of children that's occurring in that camp is going to be a gift -- we are going to give ourselves five or ten years on the road as these ISIS radicalized fighters reappear. We have to find a solution for radicalization and reintegration into society for these children, and it has to be a solution bounded in the region, and that's going to be very important.\par \par \uc2 So the decision to stay or go in Syria is ultimately a political decision. We went in there for a very specific purpose, to go after ISIS. That fight continues, not directly with us but rather through our Kurdish partners in the region. So that's a reason to stay.\par \par \uc2 But ultimately, we're going to have to balance that and determine is it worth the risk to our forces to keep them there. I would just note in the ending that if we were to pull out, it is unclear to me would first of all help the Kurds maintain the al-Hawl camp, and second, who would sit on top of the prison structure in Syria, in eastern Syria, which holds many hardened ISIS fighters.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: And, lastly, to what extent did the U.S. pullout from Afghanistan encourage Putin to invade Ukraine?\par \par \uc2 MCKENZIE: That's a great question, and probably not an expert on that. I would say that everybody saw it and drew their own conclusions about U.S. resolve. I would also say that many times in the past, people have drawn conclusions about U.S. resolve and they've been wrong.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: Lastly, General McKenzie, if the U.S. signs the Iran nuclear deal, as it looks like they're going to and sanctions relief is provided, what impact will that have on the Iranian Revolutionary Guard Corps?\par \par \uc2 MCKENZIE: I think it will give them added funding to further -- to further support their destabilizing and malign activities across the region.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: Thank you, General McKenzie. Thank you for joining us this morning.\par \par \uc2 MCKENZIE: Thank you, Jennifer. Always good to see you.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: A year later, there remain tens of thousands of Afghans who were brought to the United States during the evacuation who still need their legal status settled so they can stay in the U.S. One who doesn't, this is baby Jila Razoli (ph). She's an American-born in the U.S. on August 16th just one year and one day after the fall of Kabul.\par \par \uc2 Her father is Afghan interpreter Zabiullah Johnny Razoli (ph) who served five years with the 82nd Airborne. Last year, there were cheers when Johnny and his wife Farzana (ph) and their three young daughters finally arrived on American soil.\par \par \uc2 They had help from Senators \uc2 Thom Tillis\uc2  and Chris Coons and the wife of Army Sergeant Michael Verardo, Sara Verardo (ph). Johnny was Mike's translator in Afghanistan.\par \par \uc2 Verardo was injured in 2010. In two separate IED attacks, he lost limbs and has been through more than 100 surgeries.\par \par \uc2 Now, the children of both families play on the same soccer team and even go trick-or-treating together. They represent the very best of America.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the president sees a boost in his approval ratings, but did his return to the campaign trail throw a wrench into his string of wins? We'll bring in our Sunday panel, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: The MAGA Republicans don't just threaten our personal rights and economic security, they're a threat to our very democracy. They refused to accept the will of the people. They embrace -- embrace political violence. They don't believe in democracy.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: President Biden making sure former President Trump is front and center in his first appearance on the campaign trail leading up to the November midterms.\par \par \uc2 And it's time now for our Sunday panel.\par \par \uc2 "Axios" senior politics reporter Josh Kraushaar, former Bush White House advisor Karl Rove, Fox News political analyst Juan Williams, and "Wall Street Journal" White House reporter Catherine Lucey.\par \par \uc2 Welcome, panel.\par \par \uc2 JUAN WILLIAMS, FOX NEWS POLITICAL ANALYST (ph): Good to be with you, Jennifer.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: Juan, the president blasted the MAGA movement. He referred to the philosophy as, quote, semi-fascism. How similar is this to \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2 's deplorable comments?\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Jen, I would say, you know, what the president said was clearly after January 6, 2021. \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  spoke before January 6, 2021. After January 6th, you had the reality that there were a group of people, Trump supporter's, who attacked the \uc2 Congress of the United States\uc2  to stop the certification of a legitimate, fair election. You had people in the crowd who wanted to threaten, if not hang, Vice President Pence. You had a situation where there were Trump officials who were pressuring state officials to manufacture votes and send phony electors to the \uc2 Congress of the United States\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 I think if people behave that way, then they can be -- they can expect to be called semi-fascist, fascist, because it's antidemocratic action. You're acting against the constitution of these United States. And I think, you know, without being dramatic, that's how democracy dies. They were trying to undermine, to subvert this Constitution. And I think we also - we didn't play it, but I think it's important to differ - to say that President Biden differentiated tween that group of people and conservative Republicans. He says he's worked with conservative Republicans on a bipartisan basis on Capitol Hill on many bills.\par \par \uc2 This week we had an NBC poll that said now the idea that this democracy is under attack is a number one concern of American voters.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: Karl, let's talk about voter energy. When we were on the panel together last December, the Mississippi abortion case was before the Supreme Court. Back then you said the jury was still out on how voters would respond if Roe fell. It did, and now we have primary results.\par \par \uc2 Is this going to cost Republicans in the midterms?\par \par \uc2 KARL ROVE, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: I think on the margins, yes. If candidates don't look like they understand that the American people hold two conflicted opinions. Two-thirds of the American people did not want Roe v. Wade overturned and two-thirds of the American people don't want abortions in the second and third trimesters. So, if you have candidates -- Republican candidates who say no abortions, no exceptions, than they're going to be in trouble with the voters. But most Republicans are smart enough to say, you know what, let's leave it to the states and - and let's try and find common ground.\par \par \uc2 I have to say one thing, though, about - about the previous -- Joe - \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , President Biden, was attacking Republicans generally. He was not talking but the people who assaulted the Capitol that sits to the south of us here. He was attacking the entire party.\par \par \uc2 And you saw that in \uc2 Cedric Richmond\uc2 's comments, who said, well, we're really sort of big. We're willing to work with the semi-fascist Republicans on - on issues of common ground. This was - this was not in keeping with what the president promised us.\par \par \uc2 My whole soul is in it, he said, in his inaugural, bringing America together, uniting our people, uniting our nation. That was after January 6th. We must see each other not as adversaries but as neighbors. We should treat each other with dignity and respect. He was not treating his political opponents with dignity and respect.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: But, Karl, he clearly -- clearly was seeking to -\par \par \uc2 ROVE: No, he did not. No, he did not.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: He said clearly, I'm not talking about conservative Republicans.\par \par \uc2 ROVE: No. No, he didn't - he didn't say that. He - those words never passed his lips.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: He said that. And - he - they did. And what - and also he clearly acted at people who weren't neighborly and attacked the Congress.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: OK. All right. I need to move over - OK.\par \par \uc2 Josh, Senate Republicans canceled ad spending in several key states, like Arizona. What's behind this?\par \par \uc2 JOSH KRAUSHAAR, SENIOR POLITICS REPORTER, "AXIOS": Well, look, you heard Biden's confidence. I mean the fact that he is now going after Republican suggests a certain confidence that he think some of these Republican candidates are too far to the right. It's not about the Republican Party overall, it's about these individual races where Republicans nominated a whole bunch of Trump-endorsed candidates.\par \par \uc2 In Arizona, \uc2 Blake Masters\uc2 , he's the nominee. Trump-endorsed. Mitch -- he attacked \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  throughout much of the primary campaign. He's trailing in the polls.\par \par \uc2 There are a lot of races like that where Republicans are going to have to figure out where to spend the money because some of these candidates are just a little bit too far to the right of the swing states that they're running in.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: Catherine, is student loan debt cancellation just a play to get young voters and minorities to come out in November?\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE LUCEY, "THE WALL STREET JOURNAL": Well, Jennifer, you know that the president campaigned on this. This was something that he had been promising to do something on for a long time. And the White House really saw that they - that there was a group of people who were looking for this relief. They tried to target it, you know, to a certain group of borrowers, obviously, with the income limits. So, they see this as a - a policy decision that was important to make.\par \par \uc2 I think, in some ways, the thing to think about more with November is less - is, you know, certainly this could motivate a certain group of voters, but there's a lot of candidates, there's a lot of Democrats who aren't huge fans of this decision. And we've heard --\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: Tim Ryan.\par \par \uc2 LUCEY: Exactly, I was going to - Tim Ryan, exactly, is someone who has been critical of this. And so there are Democrats who think that this could be a problem for them in November. So you have that dynamic playing out.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: Karl, let's talk of at the \uc2 FBI\uc2  search of Mar-a-Lago.\par \par \uc2 We now have portions of the affidavit unsealed. From a national security perspective, there were classified materials found. What case does former President Trump have? Is he in trouble?\par \par \uc2 ROVE: Well, I think he's in trouble with public opinion. We do know now that in January, the 15 boxes that were overturned had 184 classified documents, 700 pages of material. We know that the documents that were seized by the \uc2 FBI\uc2  in the search of Mar-a-Lago in -- on August 8th, that there were 11 packets of classified material. We don't know how many documents make up those 11 packets. We don't know how many pages.\par \par \uc2 But now, apparently, all of the classified documents and all of the presidential records are out of the hands of Mar-a-Lago and back in the hands in the National Archives.\par \par \uc2 My hope is that the president has -- the former president has no exposure on this. It would tear our country apart if he were now to be indicted, particularly after \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  was not indicted over - over the - the use of a - of a private email server to share classified information.\par \par \uc2 But he -- those documents should not have left the White House. They should not have gone to Mar-a-Lago. The Presidential Record Act of 1978 forbids it. And the president himself, former President Trump said, they could have had them back anytime they wanted and that long ago, all they had to do was ask. Well, apparently, they were asking as -- no later --\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: For a year. For a year.\par \par \uc2 ROVE: No later than May - no later than May, and probably earlier. We have a letter now as part of the affidavit that says in - on - in -- on May 26th, I believe it is, they sent a formal letter. But it's clear from that letter that they were - they had been asking for these documents to be returned previously.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: I think it's clear the Department of Justice was justified in this step. And, boy, if \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  had done this, I think a lot of people's hair would be on fire.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: Very quickly, Josh, Catherine, legal jeopardy for the president?\par \par \uc2 KRAUSHAAR: Legal jeopardy, yes, but I think the politics are a lot more mixed because it's clear he mishandled classified information. Is it incompetence or is it malice, that's the political question?\par \par \uc2 LUCEY: And I think we just - there's a lot of questions we don't have answers to, right? The affidavit didn't give us a lot of cluse about where this is going. We know that -\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: I think it suggested they are looking at obstruction of justice, which is up to 20 years in prison.\par \par \uc2 LUCEY: Yes. Yes, and the Espionage Act. We just -- I think we have to see how this plays out.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Thanks, panel. See you next Sunday.\par \par \uc2 Up next, a bit of good news this week. I want to introduce you to a researcher whose team is making great strides towards a cancer vaccine.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: Thirteen years ago I was diagnosed with a stage three triple negative breast cancer shortly after my son Luke was born. On Mother's Day in 2011, FOX NEWS SUNDAY documented our families journey.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: We lost a little bit of innocence this year, but what we gained as a family in terms of them understanding that life isn't always fair and that if you're strong and you set your mind to something, you can get through it. I think we gained a lot more than we lost.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: Many members of our audience have shared with me their own cancer stories. Each year in the United States more than 264,000 women a year are diagnosed with breast cancer, about 42,000 women in the U.S. die each year from the disease. Ten to 15 percent will be diagnosed like me with triple negative, an aggressive form that until now has had no drug that can halt a deadly recurrence.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Keith Knutson and his team of researchers at the \uc2 Mayo Clinic\uc2  in Florida, with an initial $13 million grant from the Defense Department, have made enormous progress on a possible vaccine. It not only might prevent a recurrence of triple negative breast cancer, but also may eventually be given to women to prevent all breast cancers, using the body's own immune system.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Keith Knutson joins me now.\par \par \uc2 Doctor, welcome to FOX NEWS SUNDAY.\par \par \uc2 KEITH KNUTSON, PH.D., MAYO CLINIC CANCER RESEARCHER: Thank you. It's great to be here.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: Are we one step closer to a cure? Why is this such a significant breakthrough moment in cancer research and treatment?\par \par \uc2 KNUTSON: Well, you know, cancer is really a - you know, just a multitude of different diseases. And even within breast cancer, there are lots of different subtypes of breast cancer. And so it's very important to try to understand what you're going to target and how you're going to target it and then go through the steps that you can show you can target it and it can be safely administered to people. And it's - it's a challenge and it takes several years to do this. And you really need to stick with it. And, you know, that's the important steps that we go through as we, you know, of course, research in developing cancer vaccines.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: How do these cancer vaccines work?\par \par \uc2 KNUTSON: Well, not too unlike an infectious disease vaccine. So, what's all on our mind right now, of course, is Covid vaccines. And it's not really two different than that. What we want to do is we want to identify what is foreign and then use that foreign substance, and typically that's a protein, and administer that along with a -- an immune activator called an adgavent (ph) in some way or another stimulate the immune system so that it forms either antibodies or t cells, or both. And then that gives you the power to fight off the disease should it occur, you know, later, or tomorrow, or, you know, at some other point in your life.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: Tell me about your two most significant trials for breast cancer.\par \par \uc2 KNUTSON: We have a number of different clinical trials that are ongoing. And there's three ways to think about how we approach the development of vaccines. The first way is to use vaccines to shrink a tumor. The second way is to use vaccines to prevent a recurrence in individuals that have been previously treated for cancers. And the third way is to use vaccines to prevent the cancers right from the get-go. That's called primary prevention.\par \par \uc2 So, the trials that are ongoing right now in our group, and several other groups across the country, are to use vaccines to prevent occurrence. So, you had spoken about the triple negative breast cancer vaccine that we have. That's very advanced for us. It's in phase two clinical trials. The next step is phase three clinical trials. And the phase two clinical trials was just recently fully accrued. So, we have 290 patients. Several of those patients have been treated with placebo. Two-thirds of them have been treated with the vaccine. These individuals don't have cancer right now. The idea is, can we prevent the cancer from coming back?\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: And, finally, what are the women in your studies telling about receiving these vaccines, how it makes them feel right now?\par \par \uc2 KNUTSON: Right. So, the -- unlike an infectious disease vaccine where, you know, you can - you can rapidly assess in the population whether there's an infection, we need to wait. So, despite the fact that, you know, we have accrued the 290 patients on study, now really the challenge begins to trying to understand who's going to relapse and who's going to be protected from disease. And oftentimes that takes years to evaluate.\par \par \uc2 So, we've got, ,you know, probably got another two or three more years where we'll be evaluating these patients to see if a vaccine has an activity.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: But can you see one day where we can actually have a cure for cancer? And will these vaccines play a role?\par \par \uc2 KNUTSON: Absolutely. Absolutely. So, in recent times, there's been a tremendous amount of effort from not just our laboratory but other laboratories around the country to using the knowledge that we now have with respect to the immune system and what we can target in cancer to develop vaccines that can prevent cancers from happening in the first place.\par \par \uc2 So, our team is partnering with the \uc2 National Breast Cancer Coalition\uc2  and the \uc2 National Cancer Institute\uc2  to develop a primary prevention vaccine for breast cancer. We're not just talking about triple negative breast cancer, we're talking about all of the breast cancers, the 260,000 cases per year that was initially brought up as the number of cases in the U.S. that we have every year with breast cancer. And preventing that, or at least rapidly reducing the incidence so that we, you know, don't have to deal with the advanced stages of the disease.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: Well, Dr. Knutson, from my family and from all women in the United States, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 KNUTSON: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: Up next, Ukrainian refugees use their musical talents to send a message to \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 . I'll introduce you to a few of them as they fight back with instruments of peace.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KERI-LYNN WILSON, UKRAINIAN FREEDOM ORCHESTRA CONDUCTOR: Must find a way to - to keep on this mission until Ukraine is free and independent.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: She's conducted orchestras around the world and now she's leading a new ensemble. It's called the Ukrainian Freedom Orchestra, made up of dozens of Ukrainian refugees who are part of a new international orchestra that just finished touring Europe in the United States. I sat down with a few of the musicians at the Kennedy Center to discuss what they hope to achieve as the war in Ukraine enters its sixth month.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN (voice over): They fled Ukraine in the hours and days after Russia's invasion, carrying with them a few dollars, their children, and their instruments. Their bows now serve as their weapons as \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 's army tries to expunge Ukraine from the map of Europe.\par \par \uc2 KERI-LYNN WILSON, UKRAINIAN FREEDOM ORCHESTRA CONDUCTOR: I consider my musicians soldiers of music who are free and independent Ukraine. Putin keeps trying to say there is no culture. We are fighting on the cultural front and our weapons are our instruments.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: Keri-Lynn Wilson assembled 75 Ukrainian refugees who had never played together for an international 12-city tour that ended at the Kennedy Center in the nation's capital.\par \par \uc2 WILSON: It came together when I was horrified by the invasion in February. This was very personal for me because I still have family who are in Ukraine. And I thought, I could somehow bring these refugees together and - and create an orchestra, to give them a voice back, because Putin has silenced them.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: You compare your musicians to foot soldiers. People have described you as defiant in artistic resistance.\par \par \uc2 WILSON: We're armed with our emotions. We're driven by the fact that we want to prove, not only to Putin, but to the world, for the future, not only of Ukraine, as a free and independent country, but for the future of democracy.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: Among her soldiers, Olga Sheliskova (ph), the director of Kyiv's Mozart orchestra who fled Ukraine with her 16-year-old daughter and cat.\par \par \uc2 OLGA SHELISKOVA (ph): It was dangerous. We can see the rockets on the - on the sky. And the sound of explode.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN (on camera): Why is it important for you to be in this orchestra right now?\par \par \uc2 SHELISKOVA: We have to remind about the work.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: Do you feel like a soldier?\par \par \uc2 SHELISKOVA: I'm a musician.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: What is your message to \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 SHELISKOVA: We are exist. Ukraine exist. Ukraine is independent country.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN (voice over): Bassist Nazari Stets (ph) received special permission from President Zelenskyy to leave Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN (on camera): How do you feel right now about the idea of going back to Ukraine?\par \par \uc2 NAZARI STETS (ph): It's the best thing I can do. I'm a professional musician. I think I'm a little bit useless in army. So this is my - my front. I can play Ukrainian music.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: This orchestra has been compared to soldiers.\par \par \uc2 STETS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: How are you a soldier?\par \par \uc2 STETS: We can tell the truth to the whole world, I am a soldier, because I'm telling the truth and I'm not scared of that.\par \par \uc2 WILSON: Those who have chosen to stay in Ukraine, what courage, what bravery, and what determination to stay in their country to fight for its independence.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN (voice over): The pieces she chose to play during the tour all have deep symbolism.\par \par \uc2 WILSON: I wanted to feature Ukrainian composers.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: Like Vallantine Silvestrof (ph), whose seventh symphony is dedicated to his wife who died suddenly.\par \par \uc2 WILSON: We dedicated to the soldiers and the innocent victims of this war. Then I chose Beethoven's Vildelio (ph). It's called (SPEAKING IN FOREIGN LANGUAGE), which means "monster."\par \par \uc2 It is about fighting against the tyrant.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: Since the invasion on February 24th, the Kennedy Center has bathed itself in the colors of Ukraine's flag.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: It was our way of saying we are watching. We know. We care. We are supporting you.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: Ukraine's ambassador thanked the American people.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: In this time of tyranny, dictatorship, and total false propaganda, everyone in our global orchestra should feel like an essential instrument making an important and influential sound. God bless America. (SPEAKING IN FOREIGN LANGUAGE).\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: Each night the orchestra ends playing Ukraine's national anthem.\par \par \uc2 WILSON: There's something very cathartic about playing it. And there's never a dry tear in the audience. And we're crying as we play it in - in our hearts.\par \par \uc2 SHELISKOVA: We are proud of our country. Proud of our soldier, real soldier, brave men who fight.\par \par \uc2 STETS: Really touching all the time. I see the whole audience with Ukrainian flags. This is really emotional moment. It's about a bright future.\par \par \uc2 SHELISKOVA: Your support very important for us, for Ukrainian people. We feel that we are not alone.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: A message we could all use this Sunday.\par \par \uc2 Thank you for joining us. I'm Jennifer Griffin.\par \par \uc2 Before we go, you may have heard our colleague, a tremendous journalist, Shannon Bream, will take over this chair permanently next month. You will see her here beginning September 11th. Until then, you can find Shannon weeknights on Fox News Channel anchoring "FOX NEWS @ NIGHT."\par \par \uc2 Have a great week, and we'll see you next FOX NEWS SUNDAY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FOXN000020220828ei8s00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 What The \uc2 FBI\uc2  Did Was An Actual Attack On Democracy; Highly Transparent \uc2 FBI\uc2  Targets Political Rival; More From Tucker's Interview With Andrew Tate; Most Censored Man, Andrew Tate Moves To Rumble After All Other Platforms Shut Him Down; Washington Post Exploits Pro-Lifer's 