c2 Joe Biden\uc2  was asked how is that possible? And of course he doesn't know, he doesn't know anything including what date it is. So, he responded by mocking the question.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REPORTER: President Trump said he declassified all these documents. Could he just declassify them all?\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Well, I just want you to know, I declassified everything in the world, I'm President. I can do it. Come on. Declassified everything?\par \par \uc2 I'm not going to comment because I don't know the details. I don't even want to know. I'll let the Justice Department take care of it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Yes, I don't know the details. It wasn't coordinated with the White House. Actually, it was coordinated with the White House. It was. What liars.\par \par \uc2 So, what is in this affidavit? This heavily redacted affidavit released today? Well, Harmeet Dhillon is the perfect person we go to for questions like that. She is, among other things, the Chairwoman of the Republican National Lawyers Association, and she joins us tonight.\par \par \uc2 Harmeet, thanks a lot for coming on. So, what have we learned from this document?\par \par \uc2 HARMEET DHILLON, FOUNDER, CENTER FOR AMERICAN LIBERTY: Well, it is heavily redacted. I'd say about 50 percent of it is redacted, but what we've learned is pretty consistent with what \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  came out and said, namely, that this is a dispute about documents.\par \par \uc2 And I think what's really striking when you read the unredacted portions of the affidavit is that this is indeed a dispute. There are two sides of the dispute, and yet, there are some major chunks left out in what the DOJ told this Judge, this Magistrate Judge. For example, there is no discussion in here about what you just said, namely, the power of the sitting President of the United States to declassify documents, which I think is very clear.\par \par \uc2 There is no discussion about the Presidential Records Act, which gives the former President a significant chunk of time to go through these records that he possessed and went through, and then determine which should be made public up to 12 years after he leaves office.\par \par \uc2 And so, this is a very one-sided presentation of facts, but it isn't completely silent on details. I think the DOJ maliciously left the name, for example of some of President Trump's allies, specifically Kash Patel who is specifically called out and named as somebody who has commented on the President's declassification practices during the time he was in office.\par \par \uc2 This, of course, puts a target on Kash's back and has been the subject of much glee and hand rubbing on the other networks.\par \par \uc2 And so I think this is a great example, if you're a lover of Civil Rights and the Constitution, of how the \uc2 United States Department of Justice\uc2  can be used to weaponize and target political enemies.\par \par \uc2 It is an abuse of that process to do that, but that's exactly what we're seeing in this affidavit.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Really quick, in one sentence, we were told at the outset by Joe Scarborough, among many others, that there were dangerous nuclear secrets in this trove of documents. Have you seen any indication that that's true.\par \par \uc2 DHILLON: Tucker, the people who run the DOJ, they fetishize these markings and all this bureaucratic stuff they put out there. They have to. It justifies their existence. No, there is no evidence of that in the unredacted affidavit, Tucker.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Thank you for that. Harmeet Dhillon, great to see you tonight.\par \par \uc2 So earlier this week, a man called Andrew Tate was basically erased from the internet by all the Big Tech companies and some of the financial companies, too and we wondered why. Who is this guy?\par \par \uc2 So, we sat down and we talked to him. What don't they want you to hear from Andrew Tate? We played you a bunch of it last night. But there is one part we haven't played about self-respect, and why that's so important and we thought it's worth showing it to you and we will when we come back in just a minute.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Whenever you see huge multinational companies coordinating to hurt a single individual, you get a little taste of the future, maybe your future.\par \par \uc2 So, we watched carefully earlier this week, when in a matter of hours, virtually every social media company on the planet decided to shut down a guy called Andrew Tate. We are not experts on Andrew Tate, we are too old, but we wanted to hear what they don't want you to hear.\par \par \uc2 So, we sat down and had a long interview with him, which is on FOX Nation. We played part of it yesterday. But there was one line in that interview that we wanted to bring you because it was just interesting. And it made us think we've been thinking about it ever since.\par \par \uc2 Here it is, Andrew Tate.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: You said people don't have enough respect for themselves to analyze the ways they were lied to. What does that -- that is an interesting sentence. What does that mean?\par \par \uc2 ANDREW TATE, FORMER PROFESSIONAL KICKBOXER: It means that if I was fooled, if I was one of the people on social media, who was genuinely purporting the idea that people should be locked in their homes nearly indefinitely because of a common cold; if I was fooled like that, I would feel deep shame and I would really take some time and look in the mirror and analyze who managed to trick me to such a degree.\par \par \uc2 How my mind got so poisoned, how I got played to such a level, and I would feel deep shame because I would understand that my words had repercussions and I have been purporting a worldview or a narrative which has been genuinely destructive and I would sit there and I would be upset and angry and sad and I think I can't believe they've managed to do that to me and I can't believe that bought into it and did it to other people.\par \par \uc2 But unfortunately, there are people out here, who a year ago, were running around screaming to other people in the street to wear a mask, who are now not wearing a mask themselves, despite the fact that COVID still exists and they don't have enough self-respect or enough respect for the truth to even feel sorry or shameful for what they used to do.\par \par \uc2 It's incredible to me. Don't you have any ounce of honor? Like, look what you've done to the world? You were screaming in people's faces. Now, you're out here without a mask on yourself.\par \par \uc2 It's truly amazing, the cognitive dissonance that people experience and it's the same now. There are people watching videos of me saying I'm a misogynist and no matter how many times I prove them otherwise, because "The Daily Mail" said the word "misogynist," it just simply must be true, and these people are so deeply stuck inside of the matrix that it is difficult to even -- what can we do as rational adults?\par \par \uc2 You must struggle with this yourself, Tucker. How many times have you tried to tell the truth to people? It's crazy. It's really -- it's a crazy world we're in.\par \par \uc2 I don't know how I even got here. Now, I am on your show for being a misogynist. It's mind bending.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: So, I spoke to a young man I know who says that he first became aware of you a couple of years ago, and people in his social circle became aware of you for the same reason, which is because you are one of the only people on the internet raising questions about the effects of SSRIs on young people.\par \par \uc2 Tell us your view of that. And what why that topic?\par \par \uc2 TATE: Yes. Throughout the years, I've been on the internet and I've had a whole bunch of opinions, some controversial, some otherwise. And I've always been a person who spoke my mind.\par \par \uc2 I say some things that people heavily disagree with. I'm anti- antidepressants, I don't believe in antidepressant drugs. I don't believe in trying to take pills to alter your mood because you feel anxious or unhappy or depressive.\par \par \uc2 I was anti a bunch of things when it comes to COVID and the vaccine and such. I have been anti a bunch of these things and I've just been talking my personal view and my personal experiences and I was never really looking to go to war with any of these large tech giants on any serious level. I was just like everybody else on the internet saying what he truly believed.\par \par \uc2 I truly believe we live in a world now where information warfare is the true warfare. And narrative control is to true narrative control -- sorry, narrative control is the true weapon that the people in charge of the world need to create and protect.\par \par \uc2 By making sure they can control what people see and how people think, they can genuinely affect reality in real time. And anybody who is a danger to that narrative on any level, for them, the risk isn't worth the reward and it is better just to nuke that person absolutely.\par \par \uc2 I don't know how I truly ended up in that category, because like I said, I'm just inspiring men to go to the gym and train hard and have standards for themselves and speak up for themselves, the forgotten young men of the world.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Whatever you think of Andrew Tate or you thought of Andrew Tate, that is true. A lot of that is true.\par \par \uc2 If you've been fooled by whatever story they told you on the internet or on television, whether it was about BLM, or COVID, or Ukraine, it's okay, all of us are fooled from time to time. But have higher standards for yourself. Admit it. Feel the shame. The decent people feel when they have been fooled and when they fooled other people and stop doing it.\par \par \uc2 Anyway, that whole interview is available on "\uc2 Tucker Carlson\uc2  Today." It is on FOX Nation.\par \par \uc2 By the way, Tate told us there is one platform still willing to host him and that's Rumble. We thought it would be interesting to talk to the CEO of rumble, Chris Pavlovski, who joins us now.\par \par \uc2 Chris, thanks so much for coming on.\par \par \uc2 So, everybody cancels this guy because he is criminal of some kind, an uncharged criminal, but you charge in and sign him up. Why did you do that?\par \par \uc2 CHRIS PAVLOVSKI, CEO, RUMBLE: Well, you know, when you take a look like right now, if you go to \uc2 Google\uc2 , and you search Andrew Tate video, you're going to get all the platforms that banned him showing content from him. You're not even going to get the content from him on what he is saying.\par \par \uc2 This goes to that narrative control that he was saying, it is very correct. It is completely stacked against him. You can't see what he says, you have no idea what he is talking about, because you're only getting what they want you to get. And the only place he is right now to hear him is on Rumble and you can't find it when you search for Andrew Tate video and that's a huge problem.\par \par \uc2 And what we're standing up for at Rumble is allowing people to say whatever they want, whether we disagree or agree with what they're saying. And I think that's a fundamental right that we have to give to everybody in America that is like what our democracy stands on.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Well, that is what the country was founded on, of course, was freedom of conscience and freedom of speech.\par \par \uc2 Really quick, they told you -- they told everybody that this guy was just completely off limits. He is a criminal and anyone who would watch this as also a criminal. Were you intimidated at all when you publicly came out and said, I'm signing him up?\par \par \uc2 PAVLOVSKI: No, I'm not going to get bullied like that. If you see what's happening right now on the internet, this is transcending politics. A lot of Rumble's growth came from politics, but now we're transcending politics.\par \par \uc2 You have people like the Nelk Boys, Stevewilldoit getting cancelled. You have people like Jake Paul, Adin Ross screaming from the top of the hill for free speech.\par \par \uc2 People are really waking up to what is going on right now, and we're just happy to be in the middle of this. I don't know how it's going to shake out in six months right now, but Andrew Tate showed up onto our platform yesterday and he did a live stream today and he had a hundred thousand people watching him concurrently.\par \par \uc2 So this is a major moment, I think right now in the tech world, and how it shakes out in the next six months is going to be very telling, but we have major influencers out there right now, finally waking up to this and it's now transcending politics.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: You can't have a free society without free speech. It's really that simple. So, I appreciate what you're doing. Chris Pavlovski, thank you.\par \par \uc2 PAVLOVSKI: Thanks for having me.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: So "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " was just caught lying again. That's a daily occurrence. But in this case, with a pro-life activist, really one of the most brazen examples of manipulation of the truth by the media we have ever seen.\par \par \uc2 We thought we would talk to that person smeared by "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " and we will, straight ahead.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: When Elaine Riddick was just 14 years old, the State of North Carolina's Eugenics Board forcibly sterilized her. The State accused her of being "feeble minded and promiscuous." She had just been raped. Horrible story.\par \par \uc2 So, just days after she gave birth to her child, the State prevented her from having children ever again. They sterilized her.\par \par \uc2 Since that episode, Riddick has become a pro-life activist, but you'd have no idea of that from reading "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 ."\par \par \uc2 "The Post" just published a piece inverting the truth. It was entitled "She survived a forced sterilization, now she fears more could occur post-Roe." Their article goes on to equate forced sterilization with bans on abortion and it paints Riddick as a fervent supporter of Roe v. Wade. Is she? We thought it might be worth inserting facts into the story. So, we are speaking directly to Elaine Riddick now. She is Executive Director of the Rebecca Project for Justice.\par \par \uc2 Elaine Riddick, thanks so much for taking the time to come on the show.\par \par \uc2 So, I'll just be totally direct with you. Are you, as "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " says a supporter of abortion and of Roe v. Wade?\par \par \uc2 ELAINE RIDDICK, EXECUTIVE DIRECTOR, REBECCA PROJECT FOR JUSTICE: I am a pro-lifer and I am against abortions. I have been all my life.\par \par \uc2 I have been fighting eugenics and abortion and Planned Parenthood since I was 19 years of age, 1971. I am 68 years of age now and I have never in my life been for abortion.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: How could "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " put you with a picture? We have it on our screen, I don't know if you can see it, put you in a story as a pro-abortion activist when you run an organization that opposes abortion. How could that have happened, do you know?\par \par \uc2 RIDDICK: Well, the only thing I can say is that they were trying to win some pointers. You know, I don't know how they did it, but it was a lie. And again, I have never been, you know for abortion.\par \par \uc2 I'm the person that have always been educating Black women about having abortions and going into Planned Parenthood and having them lynching their wombs and their bodies.\par \par \uc2 I am the one that have been educating Black women about killing their babies and having their babies lynched in their wombs. So, I don't know how a "\uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " could have written that article because it was false. It's not true.\par \par \uc2 I am a pro-lifer. I will always be a pro-lifer. I would never ever stand for killing of innocent children, innocent babies. I would never ever do that. That's against my policies because number one, when they sterilized me the same day when that I had my son, they lynched my womb, and I had to live with that all my life, so why would I be on their side?\par \par \uc2 They killed all -- aborted every single baby that I could have had, do it at one two. I just had a baby and one that I had by cesarean birth and while they had opened, they had gutted me open like I was a hog and while they had me gutted open, they sterilized me at the same time.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: It's horrifying and that was the Eugenics Board which of course was created with help with Planned Parenthood, which "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " supports.\par \par \uc2 Have you asked "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " to correct this lie that they printed about you?\par \par \uc2 RIDDICK: Well, I did ask them to retract the lie that they printed about me, they did retract some of it, and I also wrote them a letter and asked them to print the letter that I had written and they have not printed that letter yet.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: It's really an amazing story. I appreciate your coming on tonight. Thank you very much, Elaine Riddick.\par \par \uc2 RIDDICK: You're very welcome, and thank you for having me.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: So Congresswoman \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2  has been one of the most direct and vocal Members of Congress against sterilizing children, chemically castrating them and the trans cult more broadly.\par \par \uc2 And for that, twice in two days, someone has tried to kill her, truly, calling the police saying there is an active shooter in her home, so officers would show up with guns drawn and very potentially shoot her to death.\par \par \uc2 A sitting Member of Congress, \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2  joins us after the break.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Just after midnight this Wednesday morning, several police officers arrived, guns drawn at the home of Congressman \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2  in Georgia. Now, they arrived because someone had called the police and said falsely that a man had been shot several times in a bathtub in Marjorie Taylor Green's house.\par \par \uc2 Then again on Thursday mornings, it was 3:00 AM, police received another report about "a male possibly shooting his family members and then himself" at Marjorie Green's house.\par \par \uc2 Now, the point of all of this, of course, these lies was to get the police to show up and shoot \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2 . So, this is an attempt to kill a sitting Member of Congress for political reasons.\par \par \uc2 Everyone else seems to be ignoring the story. We think it's a story. \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2 , as we said, is a Member of Congress from the State of Georgia. She joins us tonight.\par \par \uc2 Congresswoman, thanks so much for coming on. Who did this? Do you have any idea?\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 MARJORIE TAYLOR GREENE\uc2  (R-GA): We don't know, but it is under investigation and there are multiple layers of Federal law enforcement working with our local Rome Police.\par \par \uc2 Tucker, I have to tell you, I am so grateful for our local police because they handled this in such a professional way, but we already know what can happen in Washington, especially at the Federal level, where we're seeing the Department of Justice and the \uc2 FBI\uc2  there being used as political weaponry against political enemies.\par \par \uc2 And whoever is doing this to me seems to be a direct result of the legislation that I introduced last week, the Protect Children's Innocence Act because they called in and confessed to the 9-1-1 call center saying that they were a trans kids activist and they are angry at me for trying to stop these awful surgeries, hysterectomies, double mastectomies, castration of children.\par \par \uc2 And you know, this should not be happening, but they're so angry at me for trying to protect kids that they're willing to have me killed and definitely working hard to do that.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: So, here is someone who is attempting to murder a sitting Member of Congress, a Federal official as you are for political reasons. If there is a reason we have a Justice Department, it is to respond to things like that.\par \par \uc2 Tell us, has the Justice Department jumped on this with all four paws to find out who did it?\par \par \uc2 GREENE: They are working on investigating it. The person that did this used some sort of computer system. So, they didn't call directly from their phone, they called in using some sort of system like a text to speech type of program, where they called in and you can't hear their voice, so we don't know what their phone number is.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Right.\par \par \uc2 GREENE: But it's all under investigation and they need to find this person because you know they won't stop. This is politics today. It is now political war.\par \par \uc2 But I have to tell you, Tucker, I am actually really angry at what they've done. I'm more resolved than ever, and I'm not going to back down.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: I believe you there. A rhetorical question, what's it like to be woken up at three in the morning with police officers with their guns drawn?\par \par \uc2 GREENE: It's terrifying. It's terrifying. Sound asleep, you hear a doorbell, knock at the door, seeing flashlights, open the front door to guns drawn, and you're shocked. You don't know what's happening.\par \par \uc2 And then the police are having the same situation. They don't know if they're responding to a horrible incident where someone has been killed or possibly could be killed.\par \par \uc2 But they're there to do their job, and they quickly figured out that I was swatted and it's