bit more than we thought we might get given the history of how these things come out. There's a look at them spread across the wall.\par \par \uc2 The former president called this release today a "total public relations subterfuge." The DOJ says that premature disclosure of more of this information could have a negative impact on their continuing investigation. And here is President Biden asked about this investigation which he's been really careful to keep at an arm's length at least publicly moments ago.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: President Trump said that he declassified all these documents. Could he have just declassified them all?\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Well, I just want to know I declassified everything in the world. I'm president. I can do it. Come on, I'm not going to comment. I don't know the detail. I don't even want to know. Let's the Justice Department take care.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: OK, so that's just short time ago from the president. Bret Baier is standing by, but first to David Spunt, our reporter covering all of this live at the Justice Department. Good afternoon. Good to see you again, David. What's the latest here?\par \par \uc2 DAVID SPUNT, FOX NEWS JUSTICE CORRESPONDENT: Good to see you, Martha. Interesting document. As you mentioned, 38 pages. Here's page 30 and 31, which you can see and you can consume some information. Then there's page 15 and 16. So, it just depends on which page you look at.\par \par \uc2 But there is some new information that we have in this affidavit as far as the amount of different pieces of documents that were taken specifically from Mar-a-Lago. And this was approved by the Trump team back in January of 2022 earlier this year, the Trump team voluntarily gave over 15 boxes that had levels of classification I want to read to you right now. And this is a direct quote from the affidavit.\par \par \uc2 A preliminary triage of the documents with classification markings revealed the following approximate numbers: 184 unique documents bearing classification markings, including 67 marked as confidential, 92 documents marked as secret, and 25 documents marked as top secret."\par \par \uc2 There's another part of this on page 30 that's when -- the government put in a motion to seal this affidavit so the public could not see it. It says premature disclosure of the content of this affidavit and related documents may have a significant negative impact on the continuing investigation and may severely jeopardize its effectiveness by allowing criminal parties an opportunity to flee, destroy evidence stored electronically and otherwise, change patterns of behavior and notify criminal confederate. So, without essentially calling the former president of the United States a criminal, the Department of Justice uses the word twice in that sentence. And that's the reason that they wanted to specifically seal.\par \par \uc2 One more point, Martha, on page -- let me look at it right here. Page number four of this affidavit, it mentions Sensitive Compartmented Information, SCI, one of the highest levels of classified information, and it's mentioned toward the beginning of this because it lays out that we will be seeing some of this information with the SCI later on in the affidavit. We don't see it later on in the affidavit, meaning perhaps it's behind some of those black lines. Martha?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: David, thank you very much. With that, we bring in Brett Tolman, former U.S. attorney, now executive director of Right on Crime. Brett, great to have you with us.\par \par \uc2 BRETT TOLMAN, FORMER U.S. ATTORNEY: Hi, Martha!\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Your sort of overall headline take given what we have and have not learned here in this document today.\par \par \uc2 TOLMAN: Martha, this -- the affidavit reads more like it's attempting to convince the reader that legally the DOJ is on good footing and that there are reasons for them to want more of the documentation, but it does not read like a typical criminal affidavit. It outlines the back and forth with the lawyers, it outlines some of the law, but it fails to give you know some key aspects of the law that might go in favor of the former president.\par \par \uc2 So, it's a very curious document what we have in front of us. I'm not really impressed that what we are able to read justifies what the DOJ has done in this case.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Interesting. So, you're saying you don't think that this is a coming criminal prosecution in this case just based on what you can read so far in this document. Andy McCarthy just said the same thing moments ago. Let's play that.\par \par \uc2 ANDY MCCARTHY, FOX NEWS LEGAL ANALYST: I'm increasingly convinced, John, that the Justice Department doesn't have any intention of prosecuting President Trump for classified information violations or record retention, unless he talks himself into being charged by the kinds of stuff that they're saying now.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: What do you think about that, Brett?\par \par \uc2 TOLMAN: The document doesn't outline, Martha, anything that is what we consider mens rea or criminal intent. It does not outline any facts that suggests that there is criminal intent in this case. It outlines a back and forth, it outlines disputes over documents. It also, you know, interestingly, it reveals some things that the \uc2 FBI\uc2 , you know, reviewed many of the documents and did so without a tank team. So, they were not concerned too much about privileged documents, when they triage those first, you know, 15 boxes or so.\par \par \uc2 There's just all kinds of issues with the affidavit if you're -- if you're trying to present a case that justified not only the urgency with which they've argued, the way that they carried out the search warrant, but you read this document, Martha, and what you are impressed with is the fact that there's some legal issues here that neither side seems to -- seems to be prevailing on. And that suggests more of a civil dispute than criminal intent and a criminal case.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: And what about the part that David just highlighted on the screen where it says that criminal parties could destroy some of this evidence or possibly flee? Does that concern you? And also, just worth pointing out, that this came after a springtime grand jury subpoena which earlier, Andy McCarthy said, would signify a criminal investigation underway here? So, those two things seem to not match up?\par \par \uc2 TOLMAN: Yes, they don't match up. You also don't know who that criminal target is. You know, frankly, Martha, when I read this, I wondered if President Trump is in fact the criminal target. Because you don't see anything that outlines any factual allegations that he was involved in transporting, removing, destroying, or doing anything with the documents. You don't see any of that.\par \par \uc2 Now, of course, there's pages that are redacted. Will it be in there? Perhaps. But if you really wanted to make a justification for a document that is going to be ultimately revealed in its entirety, if you wanted to make a justification for this search warrant, you would definitely want to be able to outline criminal intent in the affidavit.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, this is the case, Brett. Does this point to a heavy handed overreach here? The former president called it PR subterfuge by the \uc2 FBI\uc2  and DOJ -- and just, you know, obvious -- putting the obvious out, there's a lot of redacted information in here. I mean, there could be something under there that we're not aware of, that you're not aware of that might change our opinion here. But based on what you know now, is the President right on this, the former president?\par \par \uc2 TOLMAN: Yes, I would not want this out if I'm DOJ, but it's not for the reasons that I'm really concerned about protecting, you know, against destruction of evidence or harm to potential witnesses. It's more that it screams a civil dispute between a former president who's entitled to have documents versus, you know, National Archives that wants more documents.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, that does raise the possibility that has been talked about a lot since the raid on August 8 of potential overreach here that could be embarrassing, potentially. I'm just pointing out again, there's a lot we don't know but the potential for that appears to be there. Brett, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 TOLMAN: Thanks, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Good to see you as always.\par \par \uc2 So joining me now once again, Bret Baier, anchor and executive editor of "SPECIAL REPORT" and Fox News Chief Political Anchor. Bret, good to have you with us today.\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Hey, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You know, I just want to -- do we have the President Biden soundbite when he was asked about this earlier and keeping a distance on this? Because given what Brett is saying, what Brett Tolman is saying, this is -- you know, it becomes a real political football, right? So, if this was a raid that is overreach, it puts out into the public bloodstream a lot of sort of half dirt here, right? You know, obstruction of justice, espionage act, all of these things that could potentially be used politically if they don't move forward and do anything criminal here, right?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes, and listen, we knew that the President had waved the previous president's executive privilege. And it was very bizarre when it came out publicly that he was doing that. But it was public at the time. And then it was confirmed in that NARA letter that we saw this week about that. And that's what generated this move forward to get these documents.\par \par \uc2 So, the White House overall at 30,000 feet knew something was happening. The President insists that he did not know the timing and exactly when that raid was going to happen at Mar-a-Lago. I think that there's more to ask about the White House knowledge of all of this.\par \par \uc2 But I will say, Martha, there's a couple other things as I'm reading through the 38 pages that are interesting. One is, and this will not sit well with the former president, in the legal memo justifying the warrant is this line that reveals that the \uc2 FBI\uc2  and the Justice Department spoke to a "significant number of civilian witnesses about the former President's actions."\par \par \uc2 Significant number, and that is probably not going to sit well with the foreign president. There are some who speculated maybe these are secret service agents who felt compelled to come forward after they saw this information. It seems like there's a number of people here. And part of the redaction in these 38 pages is to protect the identity of those witnesses who have testified in front of a grand jury.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, it's great point. And you know, it's protecting these civilian witnesses who aren't named in here, obviously, they may be the difference between the June 3rd visit where these boxes were taken out, and then the need that was compelled in between what may have -- they've been told about where these other things might be.\par \par \uc2 And they say in this -- in this 38 page document that they had reason to believe that some of these things had, you know, sort of filtered out to other parts of the premises. And that was why they needed to go back and search the pine room and other areas in the residence because some of these documents they felt had been, you know, perhaps hidden or moved for another reason to other parts here.\par \par \uc2 You know, it's just interesting to look at this in light of what the White House knew because we've talked a lot about the fact that this is such a unique situation. You have a president who served for a term who may run again, and this President is, you know, oversees the Justice Department in the executive branch.\par \par \uc2 Here's \uc2 Karine Jean-Pierre\uc2  talking about what they didn't know about the Mar-a-Lago probe appearing on another network last night. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: When it comes to the investigation, the search that we saw recently that you all have reported on, we have been very clear on this. The President was not briefed. No one at the White House was briefed.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, it strikes me listening to that, Bret, that the word she's now using is briefed.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Not aware.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Right.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: But we weren't briefed. Now, briefed could have a larger connotation. You know, that's a sit down, shown file, shown documents. It doesn't say they weren't aware.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: That's right. And someone in that position chooses their words carefully. So, we'll continue to look into that. I think you make a good point about where they're looking for documents at Mar-a-Lago. We still haven't seen in any of these 38 pages why they have to go into the former First Lady's closet to get documents or information. There's nothing in here that specifically says that.\par \par \uc2 There is some information about where those boxes were held in the basement and concerned about the security and the possibility that it would be access to foreign nationals specifically. And there's no reference to what the Trump people say, is the effort to put another lock on that door in the basement or where these documents were being held in the -- in the floor below.\par \par \uc2 I think, you know, again, it's painful to say, and we keep on coming to it, but this opens more questions than it answers. And the redacted part would tell us a lot. It's not going to get unredacted. And now, you know, there's an ask for a special master to look at all of these documents, but it seems like the \uc2 FBI\uc2  has already been through all of this.\par \par \uc2 And so, now is the time where the DOJ will come to a conclusion whether it's moving forward with an indictment or what the next step is. It's got to happen pretty fast because this limbo time where we're looking at redacted documents is not going to sit well.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. And that's why, you know, if they have gone over their skis here, and they decide that they're not going to indict, then what you've got is all of these stories the leaks that come with it, all of it sort of hanging out there in the atmosphere and in the bloodstream to continue to be picked and pulled apart and other stories to leak out about things that were in those redactions that aren't going to have any sort of judicial process that goes further on them.\par \par \uc2 And I think there'll be a lot of discussion about that if we move towards 2024 with a similar scenario of these -- of these potential candidates, Bret. But thank you, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Sure.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Great to see you. We'll see you tonight at 6:00.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You bet.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Bret Baier.\par \par \uc2 All right, so for months, President Biden has taken this swipe at members of the Republican Party. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I have no illusions about this Republican Party, the MAGA party.\par \par \uc2 The ultra-MAGA Republicans.\par \par \uc2 Ultra-MAGA Republicans, who seem to control the Republican Party now.\par \par \uc2 Look, this is not your father's Republican Party. This is the MAGA gang. This the MAGA crowd.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, now he's calling some Republicans semi-fascist, as his poll numbers start to bounce back a little bit. So, is this a new tactic that he feels is working?\par \par \uc2 Former senior Trump adviser Kellyanne Conway joins me next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I know speaking of unity, because sound is sound like a foolish fantasy --\par \par \uc2 We can see each other not as adversaries, but as neighbors. We can treat each other with dignity and respect. We can join forces, stop the shouting and lower the temperature. For without unity, there is no peace, only bitterness and fury.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, the president end on a platform of unifying the country. That was the inaugural address. It never really launched that program. And now, the president's attacks on many Americans are getting even sharper.\par \par \uc2 Last night, he was at a DNC campaign event in Maryland, saying this, "What we're seeing now is either the beginning or the death now of an extreme MAGA philosophy. It's not just Trump, it's the entire philosophy that underpins the -- I'm going to say something. It's like semi-fascism."\par \par \uc2 President Biden was asked about those comments moments ago, and here is what he said.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: What did you mean by semi-fascism, sir?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Mr. President, in December, you will ---\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: You know what I mean.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You know what I mean. Let's bring in Kellyanne Conway, former senior counsellor to President Trump and author of "Here is the deal."\par \par \uc2 This narrative of fascism and Republicans are trying to take away your freedoms. We're hearing it from the press secretary, the president. You know, it's clearly a coordinated narrative that they feel is going to work for them, Kellyanne.\par \par \uc2 KELLYANNE CONWAY, FORMER SENIOR COUNSELOR TO PRESIDENT TRUMP: It's not going to work for them. Martha, for a couple of reasons. First, plain and simple. You don't attract voters by first, insulting them.\par \par \uc2 I don't understand, but I'm pretty happy about the strategy that Biden and \uc2 Charlie Crist\uc2  and \uc2 Andrew Cuomo\uc2  have all had.\par \par \uc2 Biden is insulting 74 million Trump-Pence voters from two short years ago. \uc2 Andrew Cuomo\uc2  was governor famously said, if you're a pro-lifer, you don't belong in New York. And \uc2 Charlie Crist\uc2 , just yesterday, the day before, Martha, told all those \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  voters, I don't even want you to vote for me.\par \par \uc2 So, alienating voters is no way to get their attention and affection. Number two, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , the President of the States was just asked to explain what he meant by semi-fascism. His press secretary was asked then on CNN last night. Neither one of them gave a definition. They're either ignorant, or arrogant, or some awful combination of the two.\par \par \uc2 But you can't just call people names and have nothing to back it up. Look, it really struck me last night to watch President Biden in a suburb of Washington, D.C. This is how they're going to deploy him for the next 75 days. It's not like he's out there campaigning with candidates very few if any want him to be by their sides.\par \par \uc2 And the vice president today, I read again, what I read most days, most weekends, "The vice president has nothing on our public schedule.\par \par \uc2 So, if you don't have anything substantive to peddle to the public, you're going to call them names. And it's a -- it's a bad strategy. It's terrible for unify -- unifier.\par \par \uc2 Martha, a quick personal flourish story. You showed \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  on his inauguration day, January 20, 2021. Four years earlier to the day, I sat an exclusive lunch in the U.S. Capitol following the inauguration of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  as president. He acknowledged to the room to great applause and had them stand up.\par \par \uc2 He said I'm very honored, very, very honored, quote, unquote, that President \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2  and Secretary \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  are here with us today. Thank you so much for coming. These are two great people.\par \par \uc2 I really have nothing else to say about them, President Trump said, because I respect them so much.\par \par \uc2 He knew that \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  had committed crimes, was accused of a credible wrongdoing after he beat her, and let it go. He wanted to unify the country that way. He said in his address, and then, he acknowledged none other than Bill and \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  on inauguration day.\par \par \uc2 Look at what \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is doing. It really is just corrosive to our body politic and to our democracy. We are a divided country. We're in a divided Senate, divided politics. It doesn't help when the United States calls people, semi-fascists. And then, he in his press secretary seems to not even know what it means.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, his numbers have improved, and the Gallup poll is at 44 percent right now.\par \par \uc2 And, you know, it builds on a number of comments. It builds on the former President \uc2 Obama\uc2  saying, you know, it's not surprising, "they get bitter, they cling to their guns and religion."\par \par \uc2 And we remember \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2 , calling half of Trump supporters, she puts them into what she called the basket of deplorable. So, you know, and because the abortion issue appears to be gaining some traction in some of these early primaries and elections, like New York 19, Kellyanne.\par \par \uc2 It feels like they're leaning hard into this argument that they're trying to take away your freedoms. And, you know, these are sort of like, you know, barbaric individuals in this country who don't know what they're talking about.\par \par \uc2 It'll be a tough sell, because as I say, 74 million record number voting for an incumbent President supported Trump and Pence less than two years ago, Martha. So, it's very difficult for people to see each other that way.\par \par \uc2 But look, on abortion, if the Republican -- if the Democratic Party wants to be the party of calling people names and abortion. Let's do it. Have at it, because yes, it's going to motivate many of the pro-choice voters. But I think the pro-life voters are also animated and electrified to come out because they're grateful that their voices have been heard and that people are putting a stop to eighth and ninth-month abortions, fetal pain abortion, taxpayer funded abortion, and abortion for sex selection reasons, which is disgusting and speaking out barbaric.\par \par \uc2 But look, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's numbers -- poll numbers had nowhere to go but up. This was going to happen. And what's really fascinating to me is I think he still has a big problem in his own party.\par \par \uc2 He calls ultra-MAGA as a slight -- Martha, Do you know how many hats and T- shirts and bumper stickers have been -- have been made by people proud to be called ultra-MAGA?\par \par \uc2 Let's review. MAGA stands for Make America Great Again. And so many Americans right now are saying, make America great again, again. How can we get back to energy independence to an economy that worked to one percent inflation, not nine percent inflation?\par \par \uc2 To not having 2 million people pouring over the border, to fentanyl not being the number one killer. To Putin not being in Ukraine. The list goes on and on, Martha. We all know that.\par \par \uc2 And so sure, I've always thought that his numbers would "rebound".\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CONWAY: The big question for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  now is, will it be followed by a number of progressives being happy with his agenda, number one? Number two, how many Democratic candidates are going to ask him to campaign with them?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Kellyanne, always great to see you. Thank you very much, Kellyanne Conway.\par \par \uc2 CONWAY: Thank you, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You too. Have a great weekend.\par \par \uc2 So, coming up right here, Georgia Senate candidate \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 , backed by President Trump in his Senate race in Georgia, a race that is closely watched for the Senate future and also for the White House, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: The Labor Day is right around the corner. Midterm is about 10 weeks away right now. And one of the very closely watched races is the race for the Georgia Senate seat. And it's being viewed in some ways as a test of former President Trump's influence over his party, the GOP. And as a potential proving ground, perhaps for Senator \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2 , who some Democrats strategists believe if you can win in Georgia, that he might be in that pool of people that would be considered in a presidential race.\par \par \uc2 There's a look, this is a fairly tight race in this poll. It's about four points apart right now. And there's a long way to go here in election time clocks, Fox views this race is a toss-up, as you see those numbers there. The challenger to the sitting senator, Warnock is Republican \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 , and he joins me now. We have invited Senator Warnock to give him equal time. We have not heard back to set a date for that. But that invitation is open as well. Mr. Walker, welcome, always good to have you here. Thank you very much for joining us today.\par \par \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2  (R), GEORGIA SENATE CANDIDATE: Hey, thank you for having me on. And call me Herschel.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right, Herschel. So, you know, I want to ask you about the comment that we just played from President Biden, where he said that MAGA followers belong to this scary arm of the Republican Party, and that he sees them as semi fascist. What's your response to that?\par \par \uc2 WALKER: Well, my response is the same. As I've been saying, with Senator Warnock, they continue to bring decisive. They want to divide. They want to divide the people, even though they say they're trying to bring people together. But you can't bring people together by calling them names. And that seemed to be what \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and Senator Warnock continue to do is to call people names. You see what they called me on MSNBC. And he didn't check them on that.\par \par \uc2 So one of the things I think that people need to realize that those people are not what they say that they are, Senator Warnock. He's a wolf in sheep's clothing. And I think right now it's time for him to be exposed.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, you know, one of the things that has been talked about in this race is whether or not you are a strong enough candidate for this race. And this is from Senator \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 , and a comment he made the other day. And I want to give you a chance to respond to this. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MITCH MCCONNELL (R-KY), SENATE MINORITY LEADER: There's probably a greater likelihood the House flips in the Senate. Senate races are just different. They're statewide. Candidate quality has a lot to do with the outcome.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: What was your personal reaction to that comment, Herschel?\par \par \uc2 WALKER: Well, my reaction to that is I didn't hear my name in that comment, and that the most of the thing that people need to realize is I didn't get in this race because of Washington, D.C. I got in this race because of people of Georgia. I saw that my family was hurting. And I saw that Senator \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  was part of that. He was bringing things that people Georgia do not like. They don't want high gas prices. They don't want higher groceries. They're sick and tired of the crime on the street, that they need someone in Washington that's going to put a change to it. And that's \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 So I decided to run. I'm an outsider. I'm not a politician. I don't look like one. I don't talk like one. I don't act like one. Because I'm not beholden to anyone. And I think that's what the people see. That's a reason to rise right now. It virtually tied because they see me get out. And I'm working for the people. He seems to be working for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, you have a huge following in Georgia, where you're from, and people followed your football career so closely there and embrace you in a lot of ways. One of the things that from your past, during a really difficult time in your life, suggestions, abuse allegations from your former wife, and from a few other women out there, so you had to have known that you were going to see the kind of ads that are out there right now. Do you wish that you hadn't run given this or what's your reaction? And what do you say to people who watch these ads and have a question about you now?\par \par \uc2 WALKER: Well, I don't think you have a question about me. One thing you can see that I'm very transparent. I've been very transparent. People know that I've been knocked down many times, but I've gotten up. My ex-wife and I, we're the best of friends with a house and my wife. We have done a lot of things together. And I did that help people because then you can be walking around wounded mentally.\par \par \uc2 But one of the things I want you to know is as long as you seek help, there's people out there that can help you. But people want to demonize that I'm going to be a champion for mental health. I will always be a champion for mental health and that's why I did it. But people see where I'm at today, where I'm at today as I'm here to help people. I'm here to help Georgians because Georgia needs someone to go to Washington to represent them honestly, not to be someone that say this and do something different.\par \par \uc2 Senator Warnock went to Washington forgot about the people of Georgia. Well, I'm for the Georgia people, and I'm going to fight for the Georgia people as he fight for the elite in Washington and the fight for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. When we covered the Georgia runoff race, the President was there with Kelly Loeffler and \uc2 David Perdue\uc2  supporting both of them. That didn't work out. The GOP lost the control of the Senate. Obviously, you're in one of the most important races for your party in this country. How are you going to close that gap?\par \par \uc2 WALKER: Well, I'm going to pull that out as getting around and listening to what people got to say. You know, I have invited Senator Warnock to a debate which he's hiding behind the media, he's hiding behind his \uc2 Twitter\uc2  account, because he don't want people to see the contrast between himself and myself. I believe in lower taxes. He believes in higher taxes. He believes in putting men and women sports, well, I don't.\par \par \uc2 Right now he believes in people paying someone else's debt. Well, I don't. Well, the people need to see this contrast between the two of us. And he need to quit hiding and come out and let the people see that there are differences between Senator Warnock and \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, we would love to see that debate. And we'd be happy to host that debate for the two of you. And as I said, we've got an open invitation for him to come here and tell his side as well. \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 , thank you very much. Good to have you with us today, sir. We'll be following your race closely.\par \par \uc2 WALKER: Hey, thank you. And I want to encourage everyone to go to teamherschel.com, or whatever you can do to help to put me in that seat to represent Georgia and represent America because I'm going to fight for us all and bring us together.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right, \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 . Thank you very much, sir. Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 All right, so that's going to be interesting, right, watching that Georgia race? What's not so great right now is watching the Dow. It's going to close in about 24 minutes. And that will be probably a relief for a lot of investors. We'll see if it comes off of these, I think this is the lowest point of the day, down 923 points right now, two and three quarter percentage points. Jerome Powell said that he will continue to forcefully fight inflation in that Jackson Hole meeting. Everybody listens to every word he says to get a sense of where interest rates are headed. And he sure made it sound like they were going to go up more than they are already.\par \par \uc2 Jackie DeAngelis watching all of this for \uc2 Fox Business Network\uc2  and for all of us, Jackie, great to have you with us today.\par \par \uc2 JACKIE DEANGELIS, FOX BUSINESS CORRESPONDENT: Good to be with you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Ouch. Wow.\par \par \uc2 DEANGELIS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: That's a tough day.\par \par \uc2 DEANGELIS: Well, we're bouncing around, session lows here, as you mentioned, market is going to close at 4 o'clock. And I think investors are looking to put this week to bed. We had a rough day on Monday as well. And basically, Jerome Powell's comments in Jackson Hole, Wyoming where his first opportunity to respond to the Inflation Reduction Act, which was really skinny builds back better, right, another $739 billion of spending, then we had the announcement of the forgiveness of student loans that we're anticipating is going to cost anywhere from 300 to $500 billion.\par \par \uc2 And so he's had an opportunity to say inside, well, these numbers are not baked into the inflation cake, and I thought things were going to be OK. Investors thought he doesn't have to be as aggressive as he was planning on being. Now he's saying, we have to use those tools. And we have to use them forcefully, which means we have to be more aggressive than Wall Street wants to see him. When he raises rates, cost of debt, you know, and borrowing goes up for these companies. They're paying more out on interest payments. And that slows what they're able to do.\par \par \uc2 The concern, of course, is that maybe they start laying more people off. We've seen a string of layoffs from big tech companies for example.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Did he really tie those things together in his comments. Did he say because I'm now saddled with the Inflation Reduction Act and student loans, you're probably going to get some more interest rate hikes?\par \par \uc2 DEANGELIS: He didn't say it that way. But the market is certainly reading into his commentary to say he's taking a different approach than he was taking before where he was looking at a little bit of a softer landing and what has changed since then.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, well, he's got to be wondering why all of that just got dumped on his plate. He's in a very difficult environment to fight inflation. And now you've got billions more in stimulus that are coming down the pipeline, close to trillion actually. Thank you, Jackie. Always good to see you.\par \par \uc2 DEANGELIS: Yes, good to see you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: We'll see what happens. Keep an eye on the Dow, down 908 points right now. About 20 minutes left in the trading day on a Friday afternoon.\par \par \uc2 So exactly one year ago in the final dark days of our chaotic exit from Afghanistan, scores of civilians, and these 13 U.S. service members whose pictures we are on our wall here on the left hand side were killed in a horrific murderous suicide bombing that happened at Kabul's airport. Those Americans were trying to help desperate Afghans flee \uc2 Taliban\uc2  rule under extraordinarily difficult circumstances, enormous crowds, very tough security around that area. Now one of those who was lost was Marine Corps Lance Corporal Dylan Merola. He was 20 years old. His family says that he always dreamed of joining the military that he was excited to do what he was doing.\par \par \uc2 His mother Cheryl joins us in just a moment. But first, a look back at that tragic day with national security correspondent Jennifer Griffin. Hi, Jennifer.\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER GRIFFIN, FOX NEWS NATIONAL SECURITY CORRESPONDENT: Hi, Martha. Well, the scenes that Abbey Gate exactly a year ago were apocalyptic. The U.S. Marines did their best to handle the crowd. U.S. commanders knew the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  and ISIS would like to place a suicide bomber on board, one of the C-17 evacuation flights. Then came the explosion.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TECH SGT BENJAMIN GIBSON, \uc2 U.S. AIR FORCE\uc2 : It was hectic, it was. In the air on the ground everywhere until we find out what was going on. There were vehicles passing on coming back towards us. Bring it back, just bodies.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: Air Force Tech Sergeant Benjamin Gibson was serving as an air traffic controller for the 621st Contingency Response Squadron. He spent most of those chaotic days in a tent by the side of the Kabul airport runway guiding aircraft as they landed and took off.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GIBSON: When the attack happened, I was sitting there with the rest of my team and the other Marines as you hear a large explosion. From where we were sitting, we were sitting right next to that runway. You see and hear that explosion and you get a shake, right, like a small earthquake, and everybody just stares.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: On August 26th, Tech Sergeant Gibson arranged 35 medical evacuations to Landstuhl in Germany.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GIBSON: A Marine screamed out at the top of his lung, get your kid on. As long as he screamed, in my head, I'm like, all right, I have to defend myself. At the same time I had to continue my services in their tracks. It actually came to a point where we had to hold the landing area because we don't know where this threat is coming from.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: Marine Sergeant Tyler Vargas Andrews age 24 was one of the marine standing guard overlooking Abbey Gate. He described the chaos in an interview with "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 ." Vargas Andrew has lost his leg and became a double amputee from the blast. He's spent much of the past year recovering at Walter Reed.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SGT. TYLER VARGAS-ANDREWS: I just see a flash and boom like this massive wave of pressure just hit me. My right ear is just like super high pitched ringing, like -- and my left ear just muffled when I hear people -- screaming and I'm just like, what the -- like, I knew what happened right away. I was like, holy -- like I got blown up as I just got hit by a -- bomb.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: We remember him and the 13 Americans who were killed at Abbey Gate exactly a year ago today, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: We certainly do. Jennifer, thank you very much for that report. Jennifer Griffin at the Pentagon.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is Cheryl Rex, mother of Marine Lance Corporal Dylan Merola, a gold-star mother, we thank you for your -- the sacrifice of your family. And we thank you for being with us today. Cheryl, I know this must be a very, very hard day for you after a very long year. What's going through your mind today and as you listen to Jennifer's report there?\par \par \uc2 CHERYL REX, MOTHER OF LANCE CPL DYLAN MEROLA: I would really like to have the investigation report. Because the stories that are coming out at the moment, and I would like them to publicly released from the DoD, an investigation report of the truth of what happened in Afghanistan.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: There is one report of a Marine who says that they gave a picture of someone that they were concerned about, intelligence was concerned about and that they saw someone they thought might be that person and ask for permission to fire and that that was not given to them. That's all we have on that it's just one person's account. It may or may not be accurate. But it -- what is it like, you know, to hear that there were those concerns and to listen to the voices that you just listened to in Jennifer's report.\par \par \uc2 REX: To know that they had, you know, potentially had eyes on the suicide bomber is really heartbreaking. really heartbreaking that they could have saved not just a 13 family member children, but also the 160,000 Afghans or 160 Afghan women and children and men that they killed that day.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You know, talk to me a little bit about Dylan, Cheryl. He was 20 years old. And you say that he was a very happy young man who really wanted to serve his country.\par \par \uc2 REX: Yes, Dylan was so excited to serve his country. He was proud every moment, every step of the way. The glow on his face, the moment that he came out of boot camp was just amazing and priceless.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: And you have another son now who has joined the Marines, right Cheryl?\par \par \uc2 REX: I do. My son is 24 years old and he joined the Marine Corps.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You know, I know gold star mother is something, you know, that you're obviously the nation is proud of you for this sacrifice. It's not a title that anybody ever wants. Tell me about what this year has been like for your family. And what you would say about that mission, if you had a moment to talk to the leadership in this country.\par \par \uc2 REX: This past year has been extremely difficult on my family and the families of the 13. Not only them, but also all the children that were out there, Marines, their families, everybody that day, struggled and are still struggling with the events that happened on that day. If I could speak to the administration right now, I think better planning is what they needed to do. And they did not do that when you basically failed or children.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: When you spoke with him, did you have any idea, do you think he had any idea that