nificance.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: He made abortion rights a central focus of his campaign. This was a big test for Democrats, who are hoping that this becomes a driving issue and mobilizes voters in November.\par \par \uc2 LAURA BARRON-LOPEZ, CNN POLITICAL ANALYST: They're seeing that abortion is a good and effective, for them, argument against the attacks on the economy.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Supreme Court nuking abortion rights has shifted the midterm momentum to the Democrats.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Well, it's either a lie or wishful thinking, because the truth is the outcome here turned on a single county. And guess who was executive for that county? Yes, the county executive was Pat Ryan.\par \par \uc2 In fact, Democrats have been pointing to the four special elections that swung their way since the Dobbs decision as proof positive that abortion will lead them to the electoral promise land. But as Dave Wasserman have cooked political notes, those four districts have something in common.\par \par \uc2 "Dems' successes in these specials have been fueled by high engagement in college-heavy enclaves like Lincoln, Nebraska; Rochester, Minnesota; Ulster County in New York; and Ithaca, New York, pockets not all that representative of the larger fall electorate."\par \par \uc2 Not to mention that in poll after poll, voters have signaled that their number one concern is the economy. And well, wait, where does abortion fall? According to Pew, it's the eighth most important priority behind things like education, safety, and Supreme Court appointments.\par \par \uc2 But you got to hand it to the Democrats, I think, because as Republicans and leadership, they gripe about the quality of our candidates. Democrats, no matter how bad things look, no matter how dismal they are politically, they just put out a happy face.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VAN JONES, FORMER SPECI