 News contributor and opinion editor for "The Washington Times" and Monica Crowley, host of the fantastic "Monica Crowley Podcast." And I was just on Molly's podcast, she was on mine. It's like a thing. Great. Charlie, are we making too big of a deal of this Mayor Pete, New Hampshire foray?\par \par \uc2 CHARLES HURT, OPINION EDITOR, "THE WASHINGTON TIMES": No, I don't think so at all. I think it's pretty extraordinary. Clearly the knives are out for President Biden. I think even within his own administration, they don't think that he's likely to make it through either this term or through another term. And they think that his political goose is cooked. And so of course, they are all jockeying and trying to shimmy up the greasy poll to get into a position to replace him when and if he doesn't wind up being the nominee of 2024, which it's truly up for grabs at this moment.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Monica, he has a little preview of his message to the middle class, very relatable. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PETE BUTTIGIEG, TRANSPORTATION SECRETARY: We cannot afford to allow climate change to hit its worst, most catastrophic effects. And we cannot afford to go any longer with our kids breathing in the pollution coming in from old-fashioned vehicles now that we know there is a better technology.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Old-fashioned, we're all old-fashioned, Monica, with our cars. By the way, electric car average price $66,000, which is in more the luxury level, not the moderate level.\par \par \uc2 MONICA CROWLEY, FORMER ASSISTANT SECRETARY OF TREASURY: I'm not sure how many votes he is going to score with the middle-class, the working-class, and the poor, Laura, with the message that, gee, if energy prices are really crushing you and your family, just go get an E.V., an electric vehicle. And also while you're at it, install solar panels, too, which will cost you a fortune. This is going to be his message.\par \par \uc2 I don't know where Mayor Pete thinks he's going in this Democratic race to replace \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  should Biden not run again, because he's like number 10 on the Democrat totem pole for potential candidates, right. He's going to have to walk over \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , Mrs. Clinton, \uc2 Michelle Obama\uc2 , \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2 . So maybe he's trying to lay the groundwork here, but he only gets the plaudits of the ruling class and the media because he checks a particular box, and that's it.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: He had a big fundraiser in the D.C. area. That's his base, like Bethesda, Maryland, right, Charlie. A new poll, by the way, today --\par \par \uc2 HURT: He's a figment of the media imagination.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Exactly, the beltway swamp. So today in Pennsylvania, a new poll shows that Trump would easily beat Scranton Joe in a 2024 matchup. Now, Charlie, it's one poll. It's five points, though, after Trump has been through the ringer here with January 6th and now the raid and stuff. But is this why the Democrats are scrambling to find a replacement for the memory care patient we have as a president?\par \par \uc2 HURT: Yes, step back and think about it. If \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is as bad as they say he is, the Democrats should be thrilled with the idea of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  trying to run for president because they're going to beat him so badly. The truth of the matter is they are terrified of him, they want to destroy him because they don't want him to run because they're afraid that he'll win. And I believe that he would win if he does run again.\par \par \uc2 But want's really terrifying for them is that it's not just \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . It is the Trump issues, and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has remade the Republican Party, and whoever the nominee is in 2024 is going to be running on Trump issues. And those Trump issues are a huge winner, and that's what terrifies Democrats.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Monica, very quickly, \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2 , she thinks she could be a spoiler here. She's raising a lot of money to attack Trump and DeSantis. On a scale of one to 10, how much of an effect will \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  and her burgeoning war chest have here, one to 10?\par \par \uc2 CROWLEY: Negative 10. Laura, negative 10.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: All right, so not so much.\par \par \uc2 CROWLEY: Look, if we thought \uc2 Pete Buttigieg\uc2  wasn't going anywhere, \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  is really not going nowhere. She has zero consistency.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Charlie, Monica, great to see you both tonight.\par \par \uc2 Up next, my end of summer message. Stay there.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: For the past few weeks on THE ANGEL, we've given you many reasons why we believe that our populist movement is the best and, frankly, the only chance to hold of the regime's efforts to weaken America. The road ahead is going to be full of challenges, big ones. But consider this -- we are fighting for the things that we love, our country, our churches, our homes, our children, the entire American way of life. We're never going to let the other side destroy this.\par \par \uc2 So enjoy these last days of summer. Spend time with your friends, your family. Take a road trip. Tell your kids about the great country we have. And in September, the next great phase of our populist movement will begin.\par \par \uc2 That's it for us tonight. I'll see you after Labor Day. Good night.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document INGANG0020220826ei8p00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Biden Forgiving Up $20K In Student Loan Debt; Political Fallout of Biden' Student Debt Relief; Awaiting Judge's Decision On Mar-a-Lago Document; One-On-One With \uc2 Jared Kushner\uc2 ; Uvalde School District Fires Police Chief; Potential Impact Of Roe Reversal On Midterms; Sen. Scott On Potential 2024 WH Run; Fed Judge Orders Redacted Affidavit Release By Fri\b0\par\par\uc2 Martha MacCallum, Marc Thiessen, Madison Alworth, Garrett Tenney,  David Spunt, Andy McCarthy \par \uc2 10,258 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 August 2022\par \uc2 Fox News Channel: The Story with Martha MacCallum\par \uc2 HUNDD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 SANDRA SMITH, FOX NEWS HOST: I'm Sandra Smith.\par \par \uc2 JOHN ROBERTS\uc2 , FOX NEWS HOST: And I'm John Roberts. The only sad thing about tomorrow is it's the second last Friday of the summer season.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Oh, don't say it. Enjoy.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: Enjoy every, every moment.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: I'm John Roberts, Martha MacCallum is standing by, she has got THE STORY which starts right now, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA MACCALLUM, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Indeed, it does. John and Sandra, thank you very much. Good afternoon, everybody. I'm Martha MacCallum. Senator Tim Scott and \uc2 Jared Kushner\uc2 , both join me today to talk politics and the future of the country, ahead on THE STORY.\par \par \uc2 Right now, we await President Biden's departure from the White House, we're going to see what and if anything, he says on his way out, he's going to speak at a \uc2 Democratic National Committee\uc2  rally, as an internal battle in his party is underway. Moderate Democrats balk at the cancellation of student debt. They fear the reported half trillion in new spending could hurt them in the midterms that hurt the country as it may Wallop, another inflation balloon on our economy. The White House continues to struggle with this question.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: How much does this cost?\par \par \uc2 MIGUEL CARDONA\uc2 , SECRETARY OF EDUCATION: You know, the (INAUDIBLE) are still coming out, depending on how many people take advantage of it.\par \par \uc2 SUSAN RICE, DOMESTIC POLICY COUNCIL DIRECTOR: That remains to be determined. And it will be a function of what percentage of eligible borrowers actually take up this opportunity.\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: We have to get a better sense, right, of what we're talking about as far as cost.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. That's where that is. Let's bring in Marc Thiessen, AEI scholar and Fox News Contributor. Marc, great to have you with us this afternoon. What's your take on this as it settles in? And the numbers up to a half trillion in new spending by some estimates with this new bill to relieve people of their burdensome student debt?\par \par \uc2 MARC THIESSEN, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Yes, it really depends how much it's going to cost and how many people pick up the $10,000 on the sidewalk? Yes, right. Anybody's not going to take them up on this offer? Give me a break. I mean, look, it's good -- the Penn Wharton model says it very clearly, it's going to cost 300, $300 billion, at least on the low end of the estimate. And this, if you wanted proof that the Democratic Party is no longer the party of the working class, has become the party of the elites. This is this is the evidence right here.\par \par \uc2 The Penn Wharton model says that between 69 and 73 percent of the debt being forgiven is going to be from people in the top 60 percent of the income distribution. You're going to, you're going to -- half of all student debt is graduate school debt. It's law school it's a, it's a, it's medical school, it's a business school, so you're going to have cops, and, and, and construction workers paying for the debts of future lawyers, and doctors, and business moguls, who have huge earning potential that is regressive. And it's not it's not good public policy, but it's also not good social policy. It's a, it's a complete abandonment of the working class.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, this is an editorial from the Washington Examiner, Marc. And it says this, "Nothing separates people more today than the line between those who haven't have not gone to college. By giving away as much as $40,000 to families privileged with a college education, Biden is benefiting the most fortunate among us at the expense of those least fortunate, making existing inequalities even worse." And of course, we heard the absolute opposite of that from the press secretary at the White House who said this is super targeted to these people who need help.\par \par \uc2 THIESSEN: Yes, so they are taking $40,000 from families who either didn't go to college or whose kids are not going to college and giving it to couples making $249,000. That's what this is. You're going to have hospital janitors paying for the medical school debt of doctors. You're going to have, you're going to have teachers paying for the, the, the debt of their well to do parents. You're going to have firefighters paying for the, the graduate school debt of the rich families whose houses they save. It's, it's regressive.\par \par \uc2 And the worst part of it is actually, there's so many bad parts, it's hard to list the worst part. But for me, the worst part is that the legal justification for this is they're using the Heroes Act, which was a law passed after 9/11 to make it possible to forgive or, or lessen the debt burden on National Guard troops who are going to be sent, sent to fight terrorists. And they're using that to pay for the college debt of well to do kids. I'm sorry, kids who take out loans that they can't afford to repay are not heroes, and they should not be getting their loans forgiven under the Heroes Act.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: That's a very good way to put it. The Wall Street Journal says that it makes chumps of Congress and every American who repaid loans or didn't go to college. And you've touched on something here, that is sort of structural to the way that I think the administration sees the future of the American economy, which is that we're going to pay for more and more things for people or find more ways to redistribute the income away from certain groups and towards other groups.\par \par \uc2 And there's going to be a real push, I think, to challenge whether or not the President actually has the right to do this, and it's under the auspices have a have a pandemic, which the CDC says we don't have to take, you know, modifications or restrictions for anymore.\par \par \uc2 THIESSEN: But this is, but that's -- you're absolutely right, the left traditionally, at least they under one paper, they say they want to redistribute from the rich and give it to the poor. They're sort of this is like reverse Robin Hood. This is redistributed from the poor and the working class and giving it to the rich. You know, it's the opposite. So, I -- this makes no sense from, from me from a social justice standpoint. If you're the elected Democrat who believes in social justice, how is taking money away from, from, from working class people and giving it to the wealthy elites? I guess those are the people who vote for Democrats these days.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 THIESSEN: That's why they're doing it.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You know, obviously, it's going to rock some of these elections out there and make it a challenge. This is Alison Spanberger, she's running against William -- Prince William County Board Supervisor, Yesli Vega, Lee Vega, in really one of the most competitive races is the Virginia Seventh District. Here's what she said about this today. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. ABIGAIL SPANBERGER (D-VA): This is a step that is a band-aid step, a point in time step impacts millions of Americans. But there are families across the country that are dropping their kids off at school, at universities this week. We aren't yet contending with the root issue, which is the affordability crisis.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You know, one of the big stories this week, Marc, has been the tightening between Republicans and Democrats as we look towards the midterms. What do you think the impact of this is on races like Spanberger's?\par \par \uc2 THIESSEN: Well, first of all, we got to stop talking about Mar-a-Lago and talk about this. That's the first thing, because you know, the Democrats want to talk about Trump all the time. We need to be talking about the disasters that they're unleashing. And when she talks about millions of Americans dropping their kids off at school, there are millions of American parents who did the right thing, who sacrificed family vacations, who made sacrifices, in order to pay for their kids' college to make sure that they didn't start their lives with massive debt.\par \par \uc2 There are millions of American kids who made responsible choices, who decided to go to community college or state college instead of going to Harvard and Yale and some of these more expensive schools, so that they could start their lives out without debt. And now, what we're finding out is that those people with responsible Americans who did the right thing are going to pay for the irresponsible choices of people who took on more debt than they can afford. It's ridiculous.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I think most people feel like if you take out a loan, you are doing that with the understanding that you have to pay it back. That, that's the way we were all raised.\par \par \uc2 THIESSEN: Amen.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: And that's the way it works. It is difficult sometimes, but you have to make sacrifices. And I think, you know, sometimes sacrifices are not as popular as they used to be. Marc, thank you very much. Good to see you as always, my friend.\par \par \uc2 THIESSEN: Take care, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, let's check in with Fox Business Correspondent Madison Alworth getting some, getting some on the ground reaction to this from taxpayers in one of my favorite places: Hoboken, New Jersey. Hi, Madison.\par \par \uc2 MADISON ALWORTH, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hi, Martha. You know, we've heard a mix from people here on the ground. I will say, many of those who are in favor of these payouts, they hold student loans themselves. Others are frustrated to be bailing out borrowers, especially if they've already paid back their student loans.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Having gone to college and just gotten done finishing paying my daughter's tuition and bills. I'd like to be reimbursed.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: People who are in older generations were like yes, of course, I was able to pay off my "debt." It was a little bit of money after college and it's nothing compared to what you have to take on now.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Who's going to pay for that? I don't see how it helps inflation at all. It's just more debt that goes away.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ALWORTH: The White House calls it forgiveness, but it's actually a bailout to borrowers, financed by all taxpayers. It's estimated that this program will cost the average taxpayer over $2,000. You know, consistently, Martha, the people most upset are those who have worked hard and often made significant sacrifices, like you mentioned, to pay off their loans. We're talking about people like Candice Jackson, who paid $114,000 off in student loans by working hard as a nurse. She spoke to Fox Business today. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CANDICE JACKSON, PAID OFF STUDENT LOAN AS NURSE: I worked 96 hours a week most of the time, working three jobs as a nurse and a nurse practitioner. You're recognizing that this system is broken. And instead of trying to fix the system, they're trying to put you know, sounds like band-aids over gunshot wounds.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ALWORTH: Those wounds, the skyrocketing cost of college. You know, Martha, more thought people whether they agree with this or not all do seem to think that the cost of college has just become too much. But clearly, this $10,000 payout does nothing to address the cost of tuition in this country. Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, it might be better to go at it that way figure out a way to bring down the cost of college across the country which has been going up at a pace that far exceeds inflation. Madison, thank you very much. Madison Alworth joining us from Hoboken today.\par \par \uc2 And we have two big exclusive interviews this hour. I will speak with Senator Tim Scott and also former Senior Trump adviser \uc2 Jared Kushner\uc2 , as we wait to get more information on what exactly was in the affidavit that made the judge say, "Yes, you need to go raid Mar-a-Lago." That is next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, breaking just a short time ago, the Department of Justice had until noon today to let a Florida judge know what they would be OK with revealing about the reasons for raiding Mar-a-Lago, the Florida home of Former President Trump. Now, we're waiting for the judge's response. He's going to look at it and say, OK, this is your suggestion of what should be redacted. I agree with that. I don't agree with that. I'm going to put it out. I'm not going to put it out. So, we'll see what we get.\par \par \uc2 Former Senior Adviser to President Trump, \uc2 Jared Kushner\uc2 , joins me in just a moment. He's the author of the new book Breaking History. But first, let's go to David Spunt at the Justice Department for the latest on this story. Hi, David!\par \par \uc2 DAVID SPUNT, FOX NEWS JUSTICE CORRESPONDENT: Hi, Martha! Those redactions now in the hands of Judge Bruce Reinhart in southern Florida. He has a serious decision to make, as you mentioned, what he may be weighing. And he said publicly in court and in a court filing that there are various pros and cons that he has to look at here when weighing if he should release a redacted version or an unredacted version to the masses.\par \par \uc2 Reinhardt in court last week issued an order Monday also weighed both sides of the issue. He said that, releasing the affidavit and disclosing the affiant, that is the person who swears to the affidavit, could quote impede the ongoing investigation through obstruction of justice and witness intimidation or retaliation. He says this factor weighs in favor of sealing. In the same document, he switched it up a little bit Martha and said unsealing the affidavit would promote public understanding of historically significant events. This factor weighs in favor of disclosure.\par \par \uc2 Two weeks ago today, Attorney General \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  here at the Justice Department said he was OK with releasing the search warrant. But that search warrant had limited information. Of course, we want to see the affidavit. That's the document that includes the probable cause and will explain why the judge gave the green light for federal agents to descend on Mar-a-Lago earlier this month.\par \par \uc2 The Affidavit has detailed information including what crime or crimes prosecutors are eyeing, Martha. The former president is pushed for the release of the unredacted affidavit in full for the public to see. Now, another federal judge, Martha, tomorrow, Aileen Cannon gave the Trump team a deadline to resubmit the former President's request for a special master. That's a neutral third party arbiter to take a look at how DOJ is handling evidence.\par \par \uc2 The problem some people say that the former president's team, legal team, waited too late because the Department of Justice, the feds, they've had two and a half weeks to look through this evidence. Martha?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: OK, David, thank you very much, David Spunt.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now in studio, \uc2 Jared Kushner\uc2 , former senior adviser to former President Trump and the author of the new book Breaking History. It's a look at the successes of the Trump White House through his eyes from the inside and his perspective behind the peace deals in the Middle East, Abraham Accords, criminal justice reform, Operation Warp Speed. Jared, welcome and congratulations on the book.\par \par \uc2 JARED KUSHNER\uc2 , FORMER WHITE HOUSE SENIOR ADVISER: Thank you for having me.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Good to have you with us today.\par \par \uc2 KUSHNER\uc2 : Great to be with you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Thanks for -- thanks for coming in. I just want to get your reaction to this latest, you know, that -- on the affidavit, because one of the glaring things -- you know, we cover this story all the time, and I know you follow it closely as well, but the question is, you know, what is it?\par \par \uc2 So we heard, oh, it might be nuclear documents. Then that story sort of died and nobody picked it up. Nobody confirmed that that's what it was. Do you know what's -- what it is that they're so concerned about these documents?\par \par \uc2 KUSHNER\uc2 : Yes. So, first of all, I learned through my time in politics that half measures often don't work. And so I think what the judge do is release all the information. I think that's in the public's interest. This way people can know whether there's a serious allegation or this is more manufactured. I read a lot of my book about the Russia investigation that we live through where a lot of this was arbitrated through leaks to the press.\par \par \uc2 So, we had a situation I write about where during the transition, I met with then-incoming National Security Adviser General Flynn with the Russian ambassador to start working on getting their perspective on what was happening with ISIS and Syria and with the Iran nuclear deal, and all the different problems we had there.\par \par \uc2 And then what happens is that there's a leak from the Intel Community to the \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  saying that we requested a secure back with the Russians. And then the CNN takes it and runs with it. And it becomes craziness for at least a couple of days with people accusing us of treason and whatnot.\par \par \uc2 So, I think that right now, you have to be very careful about believing what you read through leaks to the press. And I think that transparency was something that's serious where they read the home of their leading political opponent is a very serious thing.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Absolutely. It's a very serious thing. I think that Americans on both sides of the political aisle have some real questions about what they did. There's a section in the book where you talk about the Zelenskyy transcript and you argued, let's get it out there, let's just put it out there.\par \par \uc2 KUSHNER\uc2 : Yes.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, are you arguing right now to your former boss and father in law, you know, put out what these documents are, put out what they're about in these in these presidential documents, tell everybody what's in there?\par \par \uc2 KUSHNER\uc2 : Yes, well, I don't know whether he knows what's in the boxes or not. Again, these were boxes that were in storage. If you know -- anyone knows \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  knows he keeps a ton of stuff. They're always in boxes all over the place. And, you know, who knows if they were things that he did intentionally or unintentionally. There was reports I've read that the GSA pack the boxes.\par \par \uc2 So, like I said, I think that right now what we want to do is what's in the affidavit. I think that's the transparency we should get to. But I do think President Trump was probably one of the most transparent presidents that we've had in a very long time. I mean, he sat for interviews all the time, he was always asking -- answering questions from the press. You knew what he thought of at 12:00 at night when he would tweet. You know what he thought at 6:00 in the morning when he would tweet. And so, I know he definitely is often on the side of transparency.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Have you asked him, you know, if he knows what's in there or if he went through them or if he intentionally kept something back when the \uc2 FBI\uc2  -- when the lawyer said that they had already taken out everything that was classified?\par \par \uc2 KUSHNER\uc2 : I haven't asked him that but I have been with him since then and he hasn't seemed too concerned about it.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: OK. You know, the flip side of this is that his polls are -- he's rising in the polls and his support within the Republican Party is rising. So, was this raid politically advantageous to -- if he becomes a future presidential candidate? Do you think it was a good thing?\par \par \uc2 KUSHNER\uc2 : I think what the raid did -- first of all, it was not a good thing. It was not good for our country. I think it's very important that the \uc2 FBI\uc2  and the DOJ do their jobs to try to earn back the public trust. We had a very, very tough period where they spied on the campaign -- again, I write about that in my book -- and then all the different special counsels all the investigations. It really politicized the Justice Department in a way.\par \par \uc2 And, you know, I have a long history with this seeing politicized prosecutors go after my family. That was why I was on the side for criminal justice reform. I actually partnered with a lot of people on the left in order to pass it. And I write in the book about how we were able to get a bipartisan agreement done on criminal justice reform in a very divided time by working through Congress.\par \par \uc2 But I do think that what we need to have now is a lot more transparency from the Justice Department. And I think they should be trying to work to get people to have faith in what they're doing.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, since you bring it up, and I mentioned this, you write movingly about your father's experience and that he was arrested and he ultimately went to jail for two years. Is that a concern in your -- in your family that you've married into right now about your father in law?\par \par \uc2 KUSHNER\uc2 : Well, first of all, I've been watching them try to get him now for six years. You know, again, he had no legal problems. He was respected as a great businessman before he announced he was running for president. And then once he started and started getting traction, they started, you know, accusing us of colluding with Russia, they started investigating he campaign, once they spent two years promising the world that Trump was going to have problems because of that.\par \par \uc2 And it turned out that, you know, we -- I mean, myself, I did 16 hours of testimony with the House, Congress, special counsel, and it turned out that we did exactly what we said. He ran an amazing campaign, he connected with the voters. I write about the upstart campaign a lot in the book.\par \par \uc2 And then they when they did the impeachment on the Ukraine, trying to investigate corruption in Ukraine, which also, you know, ended up becoming politically advantageous. So, what I've seen time and time again, is I find that he drives his enemy so crazy that they end up making mistakes in over pursuing him. And I've just seen that they've been trying to get him in every which way.\par \par \uc2 But it's nothing new. Again, this is probably like maybe like, you know, try six or seven that they've had. But you know, I detail a lot of the first tries in the book. And again, it's the same people doing the same thing in the same ways, leaking the same salacious things, and then later get disproven to the same sources. And it's something that I think is more concerning for our democracy than anything else.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, you know, you talk about the book and the decision to write the book. And it really goes through a lot of the major things that I mentioned in the intro that you worked on during the course of the campaign. Is this book, also a sign that you and the family are sort of coming back, that you're ready for another run at the presidency? Because both you and your wife Ivanka have been, you know, sort of quiet over the past couple of years. Is this like, OK, we're back, we're back on the scene, and we're ready to go?\par \par \uc2 KUSHNER\uc2 : Oh, no, don't read anything into it other than the fact that the experience I had in the White House was very, very unique. And again, I think that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was an outsider president. He was a businessman. He wasn't a career politician. I was in the business world in the private sector. We both choose -- chose to give that up. I followed him into it. And then I think the book shows what it's like to be an outsider in Washington and how if you're results driven, you can get things done.\par \par \uc2 And so, when I left government, I really -- I didn't do a lot of communicating, I wasn't talking to the press. I just achieved the six-peace deal in the Middle East which was pretty historic. Everyone doubted. They were laughing when I was put on the file for three years. I was getting endless, you know, criticism from the media and from all the experts saying that I would never achieve anything.\par \par \uc2 And then we got a peace deal done, which was the biggest peace deal in over 40 years in the Middle East which changed the full trajectory of the world. And then we got the second one, the third one. And several people were encouraging me saying you don't want this to be lost to history. I personally through my time in government, learned so much from books by trying to put myself into the shoes of people before me who dealt with complicated situations.\par \par \uc2 And I really wrote this book so that I can put this out for whoever wants to know what it was like to be living in the Trump administration. There's two major currents that I try to cover. One is the onslaught of all the investigations which we see with Russia, the impeachment, the media attacks. But the other current which I think is much less covered is how President Trump got so many things done, right?\par \par \uc2 Results do matter, facts do matter. When President Trump was in office, there were no wars. We're getting along with Russia. I write a lot about his interactions with \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 . I write about his interaction with President Xi. We make trade deals with China, with Mexico. The economy was booming. The wealth gap in the country was shrinking. Inflation was low, gas prices were low.\par \par \uc2 And these weren't accidents. You know, governance matters, policy matters. President Trump I was able to execute all these things in a great way. And I want people to know how that got done in a very hostile environment.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: The one thing -- you know, I'm on that list. President Biden has spoken positively about the Abraham Accords, and that he hopes to build on them. So, when you -- there's so much has changed though in the world since you left office. I mean, there's a war now in Europe, the exit from Afghanistan. When you look around -- you had an opportunity to meet President Xi. You went to North Korea. What concerns you the most? What do you want to sort of put the, you know, hair on fire moment on and bring people's attention to about what's going on right now in the world?\par \par \uc2 KUSHNER\uc2 : I think that President Trump broke the mold and brought people into Washington who never would have gotten into Washington. They brought common sense. And his success was not a rebuke of the Democrats. It was a rebuke of the Democrats and the Republicans. It was a rebuke of the career political class.\par \par \uc2 What's happened under President Biden, he's been in Washington for over 50 years is he brought back a lot of the "experts" who got us into problems, who allowed China to take advantage of us in the first place, who allowed the Middle East to turn into the mess that it was when we inherited it. And I think what they've done is they're running back now to Iran to make the same deal. They showed weakness with the way that they exited with Afghanistan, which is probably one of the most incompetent things that, you know, I ever watched.\par \par \uc2 Again, if President Trump was in office, there was absolutely no way that would have happened. I saw him speak to the \uc2 Taliban\uc2 . We had parameters and they feared him, and the same thing with Russia. And again, I think that, you know, if you notice they took Crimea in 2014 under President \uc2 Obama\uc2 . Under President Trump, we had no problems with Russia.\par \par \uc2 I even writing this book about a deal we did with Russia and Saudi Arabia where I detail the discussions between President Trump and \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  and President Trump and \uc2 King Salman\uc2  in order to get the \uc2 OPEC\uc2  plus oil deal done in the midst of COVID which saved 11 million jobs and in our energy industry here in America, which again, you know, President Trump had us energy independent. They've now rolled back by getting rid of the Keystone pipeline, doing all these asinine policy things.\par \par \uc2 And so, I think that what you need is you need strength. You need strength in foreign policy, and you need to have purposefulness and understand what America's role in the world. We shouldn't be lecturing everyone. We should be focusing on what our interests are as a country, figuring out how to keep us safe, prevent wars, and make trade deals that will help America's economy prosper.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Let me ask you two very quick questions, and if you can give me a quick answer, I'd appreciate it. Because we asked viewers what they wanted to ask you, and one of them was, would Jared work in the White House with President Trump if he were to serve another term?\par \par \uc2 KUSHNER\uc2 : So, I think I go through -- first of all, my answer is I'm really loving my life right now. I get to -- get to know my kids. I cook them breakfast every morning. I love being in the private sector. I really believe that people are supposed to go into Washington and then leave Washington. I don't think you should be a career politician.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, no, you would not go back.\par \par \uc2 KUSHNER\uc2 : It's not my preference. But again, it's also something where if you're called to serve your country, it's a very, very hard thing not to do.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, maybe.\par \par \uc2 KUSHNER\uc2 : But a strong preference not to.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right. And just very quickly, you said yesterday that the President, you know, doesn't need some of the people who were there first - - in the first round, that he's got a lot of very qualified people with him who I think could help him. Who do you -- who are you referring to there? Who are the people close to him now that you think are very qualified and would be advising him in a second term?\par \par \uc2 KUSHNER\uc2 : So, I think the best place to look is there's an organization that started after he left called America First Policy Institute. It was co- founded by \uc2 Brooke Rollins\uc2 , \uc2 Larry Kudlow\uc2 , Linda McMahon, three of the total superstars in the administration. I worked quietly to get them together. Now, it's about 150 people. And what they've been doing is creating what I would say is the bench for the Republican Party, but the Trump Republican party where they take the policies.\par \par \uc2 When we got to Washington, it was it was a strategy free environment. You know, there was no -- everyone was saying headlines, you know, let's repeal ObamaCare, but they had no plan to replace it. So, what they're doing now is they're working on very tactical policy and recruiting great people.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, we've heard about the cabinet in waiting, right?\par \par \uc2 KUSHNER\uc2 : No, it's just a lot of people who are high competency, low ego, who really believe the policies of President Trump.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, we like Larry a lot. Larry is around here a lot. So, it's great to see you, Jared. We're running out of time. It's so good to have you. And congratulations on the book, Breaking History, very good read, obviously written by Jared himself. And sometimes that doesn't happen.\par \par \uc2 More on THE STORY. Coming up next, Senator Tim Scott joins me. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Three months after the elementary school massacre in Uvalde, Texas, the school district has now fired the police chief. We have all seen the excruciating surveillance video which shows officers on the scene standing in the hallways for more than an hour, while the gunman completed his murderous rampage there of 19 children and two teachers. His lawyer claims that Chief Pete Arredondo was quote, not in charge on that scene. Here's how some of the victim's relatives have reacted to his firing this morning.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JESSE RIZO, UVALDE VICTIM'S UNCLE: I am frustrated that it took this long. I think that this should have been handled a long time ago.\par \par \uc2 BARBARA MILLER, UVALDE VICTIM'S AUNT: Accountability, you know, stand up, own it. And you'll see what it is. So let's go on and move away. So someone else can do his job.\par \par \uc2 VINCENT SALAZAR, UVALDE VICTIM'S GRANDFATHER: It's a step forward. But it's a tiny step. It took three months to get here.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Correspondent Garrett Tenney following this story for us from Chicago. Hi, Garrett.\par \par \uc2 GARRETT TENNEY, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Yes, Martha. One thing we heard over and over again last night from those families is that Pete Arredondo's is just one small step in their push for justice and accountability. And it's clear, there's still a lot of frustration in the community, with everyone from the school board to police to lawmakers over the lack of information and transparency they've gotten going back to the day of the shooting, and then through all of the investigations that have taken place since. Those frustrations were evident during the brief public comment period at the Uvalde School Board's meeting last night.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Our babies are dead. Our teachers are dead. Our parents are dead.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: You don't care about these families. If it was one your children hands will be rolling right now, but because it's now you don't care.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I have messages for Pete Arredondo and other law enforcement over there that date, turn in your badge and stepped down. You don't deserve wear one.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TENNEY: After spending an hour behind closed doors, the board voted unanimously to fire Pete Arredondo for his much criticized handling of the response to the shooting and Robb Elementary School. Arredondo did not attend the meeting but his attorney released a 17-page statement just beforehand, suggesting the now former chief is the victim of a racist attempt to make him the fall guy and firing him won't make a real difference in helping the community to heal. Writing in part, so naturally, those affected lash out and seek more retribution by identifying a new target to focus their grief on, with the belief that it will help them stop hurting. Unfortunately, it won't. Two wrongs don't make a right. Retribution will not bring anyone back.\par \par \uc2 Martha this may not be the last we hear from Pete Arredondo. In that letter, his attorney hinted at a potential lawsuit over his firing and asked the board to immediately reinstate him with all back pay and benefits. Martha?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right, thank you very much Garrett Tenney in Chicago.\par \par \uc2 So the latest salvo from the Biden White House at Republicans as they accused them of taking away America's freedoms, reaction from Senator Tim Scott next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Welcome back to the story, who can forget this memorable exchange during the debate over abortion rights when Treasury Secretary \uc2 Janet Yellen\uc2  said that aborting a child was an economic choice. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANET YELLEN\uc2 , TREASURY SECRETARY: What we're talking about is whether or not women will have the ability to regulate their reproductive situation in ways that will enable them to plan lives that are fulfilling and satisfying for them. It means the children will grow up in poverty and do worse themselves.\par \par \uc2 SEN. TIM SCOTT (R-SC): I'll just simply say that as a guy raised by a black woman in abject poverty, I'm thankful to be here as United States Senator.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So that was before the roe decision from the Supreme Court, which is now described by access as having quote fueled a stunning turnaround for Democrats ahead of the midterms. And with that we bring in South Carolina Republican Senator Tim Scott, his new book, "America, a Redemption Story: Choosing Hope and Creating Unity." There's a look at the cover of his new book. Senator Scott, always good to have you with us. Thank you for being here, sir.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: Thank you, ma'am. It's good to be back with you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So you know, I guess the question is politically, did Republicans underestimate the impact of this Roe v. Wade decision, \uc2 SCOTUS\uc2  decision?\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: I don't think we did. And frankly, I'm so thankful that my mama chose life. As we listened to that clip, one of the most powerful forces on Earth is a mother who makes a powerful solid decision to bring life into this world, and to have the Secretary of the Treasury suggest that poor women and particularly black poor women should not make that choice so that they increase the labor hood -- labor force participation rate is just utterly irresponsible and incredible. I do not believe that this will have a negative impact on us at the polls, to be honest with you.\par \par \uc2 But ultimately, that decision about life is not a left or right. It's a wrong or right decision. And I'm thankful that for my mother, she made the choice that brought me into this world. And so many millions of other kids are having the same experience enjoying a fruit filled life, even for those of us starting in poverty. And it's one of the reasons why I talk about the painful, miserable story of my grandparents growing up in Jim Crow south in my book, because I want people to know that there's life. There's reasons to be optimistic, there's reasons to remain hopeful, to be resilient, to be tenacious. And to know that in America, all things are possible.\par \par \uc2 I weave those stories together, because there's so many headwinds, so many negative stories really being populating all the screens around the country. Thank God for your show, that it takes a positive approach to the issues that are so important to Americans.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, you know, I just think that that exchange is very powerful. And I think that a lot of people don't look at, you know, they look at some of the burdens of parenting as something that they are willing to take on. And that, you know, raising a poor child is as important and valuable a life as raising a child in the middle class or any other child. But it may be that Republicans have not articulated this in a way that people understand because I think when you look around the country, and people talk about this issue, they talk about banning abortion, or you know, not -- they don't talk about the fact that in some states like New Jersey, where I'm from, abortion is available up until nine months, up until the very end of a pregnancy. But the White House is they see an opening here. This is \uc2 Karine Jean-Pierre\uc2  earlier today. Let's play that.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: Well, we're asking me about what the President is going to lean into. This is something that he will talk about, which is how Republican legislators are working to rollback the freedoms Americans have, you know, really dependent on for nearly half a century. And it's more and more clear that it is against the will of the majority of Americans.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So how do you answer that? How do you combat that in the electoral environment if it becomes an obstacle for your party?\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: Well, I hope we maintain common sense and keep our focus on the American people and not on November as relates to this very important issue. Think about the words of governor, former governor Northam, a pediatrician, Virginia's governor, who said at the time or at least alluded, that perhaps infanticide was a new approach to this conversation around protecting and or eliminating life. We err on the side of protecting it. Those comments are about eliminating it. Even in the third trimester, you think about the fact that there will be states throughout this country who will allow for late term abortions. And frankly, in the world, there are only seven countries that allow for abortion after the 15th week, and that includes America in those seven countries. This is far more than just a political issue. This is an issue about the future of this nation and about humans flourishing because they had the chance to live.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: And I think it's important to point out that the Supreme Court did not ban abortion, the Supreme Court made a decision to turn it back to the States and to allow the voters in those states like they just did in Kansas, where they upheld the laws that are in existence where regarding abortion, so those people in Kansas had an opportunity to vote. And we saw this, you know, special election 19 in New York, where Pat Ryan won, and he was running basically on preserving choice in New York.\par \par \uc2 I want to ask you put one more screen up here and change the subject for you on my last question here, if I may, Senator. This is this is a "\uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " analysis by Aaron Blake, and this is the top 10 Republican presidential candidates for 2024 ranked as he sees them based on a number of factors. And he has you in the number four spot after \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 , \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 , and then Tim Scott. Is that a number that you're pleased to see? And is this something that we can expect to see more of about you in the future?\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: Well, I certainly hope that I'm in the first position as it relates to my reelection. The most important election I have today is being the Senator for the great people in my wonderful state of South Carolina. I am very thankful to continue to serve in this capacity. And I certainly hope that the voters of my state will give me one more shot to continue to serve them in the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2 , and we'll worry about other polls. The other only other poll that I'm really concerned about is why my gamecocks are not in the top 25 in the latest college rankings. We've got to work on that a little bit.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right, well, everybody has something they've got to work on. Thank you very much, Senator Scott. I hope you'll come back soon. We always enjoy talking to you. Good to see you again, sir.\par \par \uc2 All right, folks, we do have some breaking news. And it is out of the Department of Justice and the judge in Florida brand new information on his decision Judge Reinhart's decision about the affidavit surrounding the armed raid of the former President Trump's home in Florida. David Spunt has this breaking news for us. David, what have you got?\par \par \uc2 DAVID SPUNT, FOX NEWS JUSTICE CORRESPONDENT: Well, Martha, I tell you one thing, Judge Bruce Reinhart is quick. He's wasting no time. The deadline was just not even four hours ago. And already he just put out this order, right now a two-page order announcing that the redactions proposed by the Department of Justice must be put in the public docket, which means those redactions will be released to the public at some point on or before noon tomorrow. So it could happen at any time between now and noon tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 I want to read some of what Judge Reinhart said in this order. He wrote, I find that the government has met its burden of showing a compelling reason, good cause to seal portions of the affidavit because disclosure would reveal the identities of witnesses, law enforcement agents and uncharged parties, the investigation strategy, direction, scope, sources, and methods. Again, Martha honor before noon, Eastern Time on Friday, August 26th, the government shall file in the public docket a version of the affidavit containing the redactions.\par \par \uc2 That means whatever the government said, they want redacted, or whatever DOJ said it wants redacted. That's what we're going to see. So meaning it's unlikely we're going to see a lot. I don't want to speak out of turn here or speak early, because we haven't seen the document yet. But the attorney general who works in this building, the Department of Justice where I am now, didn't want much of this released at all, actually any of this release, because he was concerned about the ongoing investigation and laying out a roadmap.\par \par \uc2 So in the end, Judge Reinhart is siding with the Department of Justice here, and while this will be released, before noon tomorrow, it's unlikely it's going to give us the information that we have been craving for weeks to find out the probable cause in the alleged crimes the Department of Justice is eyeing, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, and that is the question I was asking \uc2 Jared Kushner\uc2  about as well. You know, what is it, what is so hot, and so, you know, so classified, so important to national security, that it merited this raid at the House, that they couldn't get these documents that there was no other way to get them in their hands, what is the substance of it? Because numbers really don't matter, you know, 15 boxes, 300 documents, what matters is what it is that compelled the action that took place. And that's what we don't know.\par \par \uc2 So what could happen now? So now it's in the hands of the Department of Justice, the judge says, I want it released by noon, can they say? No, we think there's no compelling reason we're going to go to a higher court on this. What could happen?\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: Absolutely, they can. They can appeal it, which would further delay the process. I'm looking at my inbox right now just to see if there's a change, no update yet from the Department of Justice. But DOJ could absolutely say we don't want any of this released. We want to appeal this decision. And that would further delay the process. So while over the past five minutes, we're thinking OK, we're going to see this here in the next you know, 12 hours so or more than 12 hours it's possible this could be delayed even more if DOJ comes in and steps in and says we don't want anything released. But as of now, Judge Reinhart moved very quickly here. He said he looked through this entire thing, and believes that the government's redactions are appropriate. And I would suspect, there are a lot of redactions from the government, we may just see the front page, which has the name of the former president and the address of Mar-a-Lago.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, we're going to bring in Andy McCarthy in just a minute, David. But I have you for another minute here. So, you know, when you, that's the question, we all have this experience before, right, where these documents come out. And then there's so much in them that is blacked out, I think of a lot of the releases that happened during the Russia investigation. That was ongoing for several years. So it could be that there's, you know, a name here, a location there. But it seems that this would this judge, look at the way they did this and say that, you know, you have so much redacted here that there's absolutely no public value in releasing this the way that you have it go back and work on it again, he didn't say that.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: No, he didn't, which makes me think that he's OK with sending us, you know, eight pages or 10 pages, however long it is with big black chunks in it, that that don't really have much information in it. Because, you know, last week in court, which was actually a week ago today, he kind of hinted that he was in favor of releasing as much as possible, given the great public interest, given the historical significance and the unprecedented nature of this, then over the weekend, he thought about it and came back and then in a court filing on Monday, while he said yes, it is great public interest. He said yes, there are concerns about exposing identities of witnesses, which is a concern among the federal law enforcement community and also the federal law enforcement community extremely concerned about releasing the names of these agents that were inside Mar-a-Lago, after all, we've seen an uptick in threats against the federal law enforcement community, no question.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So you say that they can absolutely appeal this, they can go back to a higher court and say that they think there's a compelling reason or offer a compelling reason why all of this needs to stand or seal at this point. But the thing is that there you talk about the compelling public interest in this story, and the judges recognition that this is such an extraordinarily unique situation we have never ever seen in the history of this country. A raid by the \uc2 FBI\uc2 , which falls under the Department of Justice under the executive branch of government under the, you know, of which the White House is at the top. A former president and potentially future candidate for the presidency of the United States, raiding his home, authorized by the administration of a president who may be the person running against him. So this presents a very compelling public interest to say, you know, why was this necessary, we need to have some more clarity and transfer and transparency on this.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: And, you know, Martha, Judge Reinhart said last week in the courtroom that, as you just mentioned a couple of minutes ago, that if you send out a document with a bunch of redactions, it's almost pointless. He didn't use the words pointless, but it causes more questions, it raises more questions than it provides answers. But that appears to be what he's doing right now in this order, saying that I find the government has met its burden of showing a compelling reason, good cause to seal portions of the affidavit. And I believe we're talking about big portions here, because in the officials that I've spoken to here at the Justice Department, they do not want this affidavit out, which is typical, by the way, other administrations would do the same thing going back decades. It's just an investigative, you know, a way an investigative way that things have gone that they don't want this to go out. They don't want the public sphere to be able to see this. But the judge wants to release some of this, even if it doesn't have much information.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. David, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: You're welcome.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Breaking news in this story from the Department of Justice, David Spunt giving us that report that the judge is authorizing the release of this redacted affidavit in the form that it's in from the Department of Justice as they submitted it to him. David, thank you for that.\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in former federal prosecutor and Fox News contributor, Andy McCarthy. Andy, great to have you with us at the end of the hour here. What's your reaction to this news?\par \par \uc2 ANDY MCCARTHY, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: I'm not surprised that there'll be some redactions, Martha, because the judge I think, maybe he regrets having done this, but he kind of dug in on the idea that at least some information should go out. And they're thinking about this, you know, this big debate we've been having about whether there should be a special master or not, the Justice Department had to have told the magistrate when they sought the warrant, for example, that they had a procedure in place if they got privileged documents how they were going to review them and make sure that the prosecution team didn't see any you know privilege information that's the kind of thing you could easily turn over and say that you turn something over without saying a word about the investigation and the probable cause, and the witnesses and all that stuff. So I think he wants to be able to hold his head up high that, you know, he made them redact or unredact stuff, but not hurt the investigation.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, I mean, that, you know, his role in this has been under some scrutiny as well, because he's signed off on something that, as I said, is completely unprecedented. So the Department of Justice comes to him to this judge magistrate in this case, and he gives them two weeks, right, to go in there. And the thing that we are still waiting for is some kind of clarity on what compelled that. And obviously, there's a great public need to know the answer to that question, because I think on both sides of the aisle there is a discomfort, Andy, with this kind of intrusion into the former president's home. So how much does that public need to know how important is it in this case, at this stage of the game?\par \par \uc2 MCCARTHY: I think it's less important in a criminal case than it would be, for example, in a FISA situation, where that's classified, and you never expect to see this stuff. In a criminal investigation, when you write a warrant like this, you know that it's eventually going to become public. Because usually what happens if you go to the point of having probable cause to crimes got committed, there's usually charges. And when that happens, the underlying parts of these affidavits are turned over in discovery because they become grist for motions to suppress evidence at trial.\par \par \uc2 So this information is eventually going to become public. And I think even if there aren't charges filed, it'll become public. It's not like a national security case. This is a question of when we get to see this information, not if. And frankly, Martha, I think there's an awful lot of information that's come out in the last two weeks, I think we have a pretty good idea of what must be in the affidavit. We don't know, witness names. But I mean, if you go through all the information that we've learned, and I, you know, did a little timeline for myself today, you can see why they thought they had probable cause to believe there was classified information there, that there may have been records, retention violations. There's a lot of information that we know.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. You know, and it can kind of go back and forth on how compelling the need was to go in the way they did. Was there an opportunity to say, look, if you don't open the door, and let us in and show us what you've got? We're going to have to, you know, go another route, of course, then you lose the element of surprise, which apparently they wanted to retain in this case.\par \par \uc2 You also wrote about the back and forth between the lawyers and at the Archives at the NRA. And the fact that they basically got an assurance from Trump attorneys, according to the filing, that there wasn't anything left, that they got everything that was classified, and they signed off on that, the Trump attorneys, according to what we're learning. And said, nope, there's nothing else is classified. Then after the raid, they found more things that were classified. And you say, you know, that was a big -- that was kind of a dumb move on their part, right?\par \par \uc2 MCCARTHY: Well, it's not only a dumb move, Martha, I think it goes to the point that you were just making, which is one of the things we want to know, the thing we most want to know is why this, why now, why a search warrant as opposed to some other lesser intrusive means of getting this information. And the statement that you just referred to came in the context of they gave the Trump people a grand jury subpoena to turn over all of the remaining classified information in June.\par \par \uc2 And then they gave them another grand jury subpoena for surveillance video around June 22nd, I think it was. You know, I think they were trying to -- that they have built a record where they can at least say to you, you know, look, we sat down with them. We had conversations with them. We told them a million times that we needed to stuff back. Then we went to criminal investigation, we started to issue grand jury subpoenas. And still we didn't get the information back. We saw things in the video we didn't like and we started to conclude that the only way we were ever going to get this information, some of which was highly classified was to go in and take it.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. Just to --\par \par \uc2 MCCARTHY: I think that's going to be their story.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Just a few seconds here but of course, the Trump side and the President has been on Truth Social a lot today says, you know, Presidential Records Act, I'm allowed to keep this stuff quick thought, Andy.\par \par \uc2 MCCARTHY: Yes, it's not his. They get to keep it. He gets to see it. They get to keep it.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right. Andy McCarthy, breaking news, thank you very much, we'll stay on top of it.\par \par \uc2 MCCARTHY: Thanks Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Thank you, and that is the story of Thursday, August 25th already almost the end of summer. The story goes on. Thank you very much for joining us for the show today. I look forward to seeing you all back here tomorrow have a great afternoon. Stay tuned though because your world is straight ahead.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document HUNDD00020220826ei8p00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Judge Open Path For Redacted Trump Affidavit Release; Grand Jury Subpoenas White House Documents; \uc2 Rochelle Walensky\uc2 : We Made Some Pretty Public Mistakes; \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  Paints Mehmet Oz As Out Of Touch After Viral Video; Oz Trolls Fetterman For Campaign Trail Absence; New York Sucker Punch Caught On Video; Women In Afghanistan Have Fewer Rights Including Rights To Education Currently Under \uc2 Taliban\uc2 ; Large Numbers Of Migrants Continue Crossing U.S. Southern Border; Large Amounts Of Fentanyl Continue To Be Smuggling Into U.S. Through Southern Border\b0\par\par\uc2 Bret Baier, Mark Meredith, Jonathan Turley, Peter Doocy, Bryan  Llenas, Nate Foy, Jennifer Griffin, Bill Melugin \par \uc2 7,832 \uc2 words\par \uc2 18 August 2022\par \uc2 Fox News: Special Report with Brett Baier\par \uc2 FNSPBB\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 JESSICA TARLOV, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR: I've never seen that before.\par \par \uc2 JOEY JONES, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR: Me neither.\par \par \uc2 KATIE PAVLICH FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR: You've never seen that ever before?\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: The word just drops.\par \par \uc2 PAVLICH: Did you know the rules on baseball too in addition to duck hunting?\par \par \uc2 JONES: I know as of a minute ago.\par \par \uc2 PAVLICH: OK. All right. Well, that is it for us, SPECIAL REPORT is up next. Hi, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Thank you, Katie. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 PAVLICH: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Good evening. Welcome to Washington. I'm Bret Baier.\par \par \uc2 Breaking tonight, a federal judge is ordering the Justice Department to prepare for the release of some of the information used to obtain the search warrant of the Trump Florida compound Mar-a-Lago.\par \par \uc2 Lawyers for several news media outlets argued today the material should be unsealed. Attorneys for the Justice Department said that would hurt their investigation. The judge has told the government to give him a list of proposed redactions by next week.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent Mark Meredith is outside the courthouse in West Palm Beach, Florida tonight. Good evening, Mark.\par \par \uc2 MARK MEREDITH, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Bret, good evening to you.\par \par \uc2 As you mentioned, Judge Bruce Reinhart is giving the Justice Department one week to decide which items in the affidavit against the former president should remain sealed and which items can be released.\par \par \uc2 The Justice Department is likely to ask for a lot of different redactions here. They say that this case is still very much in the works and they're also worried that information released could put some witnesses who are cooperating with the government at risk.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH (voice over): Citing intense interest in the case against former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . Today, lawyers from multiple media outlets argued for the government to unseal a criminal affidavit and additional materials outlining where the federal investigation stands. And what led up to last week's unprecedented search at Mar-a-Lago, Trump's Florida State.\par \par \uc2 DEANNA SHULLMAN, MEDIA GROUP ATTORNEY: That is the public interest, we are entitled to monitor the affairs of our government at all levels. And that is the interest in this essence that we were asserting today.\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH: But federal prosecutors told Judge Bruce Reinhart full disclosure of such materials could severely impact the government's case, as it investigates how classified and top secret documents ended up at Mar-a-Lago long after Trump left office.\par \par \uc2 The Justice Department's Jay Bratt telling the court today "The government is very concerned about the safety of witnesses in this case."\par \par \uc2 Late today, the government unsealed additional materials tied to the original search warrant on August 5th, the government pushed for secrecy telling the judge "The United States admits that there is good cause because the integrity of the ongoing investigation might be compromised and evidence might be destroyed."\par \par \uc2 Lawyers for the former president did not speak at today's hearing, but his spokesperson is sounding off.\par \par \uc2 In a statement late today, Trump's team says "Magistrate Judge Reinhardt rejected the DOJ cynical attempt to hide the whole affidavit from Americans. No redactions should be necessary and the whole affidavit should be released."\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH (on camera): While the redaction requests are due next Thursday, the Justice Department will also have the option to appeal if it disagrees with what the judge ultimately decides.\par \par \uc2 Meaning this could go on for quite some time. And as they mentioned in court today, this investigation is still very much in the early stages.\par \par \uc2 We are looking to see if the former president may have any more to say, also whether or not he's going to release any of the security footage from Mar-a-Lago which would show federal agents at his property earlier this month.\par \par \uc2 Federal sources say they are worried it's possible that that video could incite further threats against federal agents, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Mark Meredith outside the courthouse. Mark, thanks.\par \par \uc2 A top executive at former President Trump's family business is pleading guilty to evading taxes. The deal for Allen Weisselberg could result in his testimony against the company in court.\par \par \uc2 Weisselberg was accused of dodging taxes on lavish fringe benefits he received including a lease payment for luxury car, rent for a Manhattan apartment and private school tuition for his grandchildren.\par \par \uc2 He was sentenced to five months incarceration and five years probation. He must testify when the Trump organization goes on trial in October on related charges.\par \par \uc2 Meantime, \uc2 The New York Times\uc2  is reporting a grand jury has subpoenaed Trump White House documents stored at the National Archives and provided to House lawmakers investigating last year's capital riot.\par \par \uc2 The Times is reporting the request comes from prosecutors conducting their own probe into the January 6th event.\par \par \uc2 Let's get some analysis on all of this now. We welcome Jonathan Turley, professor at \uc2 George Washington University Law School\uc2  and former \uc2 FBI\uc2  Special Agent Maureen O'Connell.\par \par \uc2 All right, Jonathan, what we saw today essentially was a punt, a little bit, but something is going to come out. The issue is redacted documents from the government often can be painful for media organizations.\par \par \uc2 We have a couple of examples of previous redacted documents and basically, it's all black ink on the pages. Is that what we're looking at here?\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN TURLEY, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR: Well, I if they're true to form, this could come back these 10 pages or so like legal haiku. I mean, they reduced everything. I've been in a case where they basically blacked out everything but the caption and I was defense counsel.\par \par \uc2 So, the question is going to be whether the judge is willing to go go toe to toe with the Department of Justice and say, look, this seems to be excessive, it often is. I've been in a lot of these cases. And they often go way overboard. The problem is that unlike the cases I'm involved in, there won't be adversarial counsel in that meeting, this is going to go to the judge, the judge is going to have to look at that affidavit and have the temerity, have the courage to say, I think you could do more.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes. Maureen, the DOJ filing on this specific motion has this, this is to keep it sealed. The United States admits that there is good cause for the search because the integrity of the ongoing investigation might be compromised, and the evidence might be destroyed.\par \par \uc2 That raised some eyebrows today in -- that there was worry that the evidence would be destroyed. They have already talked about witnesses that they are concerned about revealing in these documents if they were unsealed, what do you see in these filings?\par \par \uc2 MAUREEN O'CONNELL, FORMER FBI SPECIAL AGENT: Well, if they say that they were so concerned about documents being destroyed, then that that really doesn't hold much water for me, because we've heard that there were discussions about whether or not they were going to execute this warrant. And my sources tell me they were spirited discussions for a couple of weeks.\par \par \uc2 So, if they were really concerned, and I've been concerned many times in my career when writing warrants about the destruction of evidence, and also, when we're hitting houses or executing warrants. When we're concerned about, there are exigent circumstances. And we know for a fact that something is most probably going to be destroyed.\par \par \uc2 But here, I don't really see that based on the timeline that we've discovered in the last couple -- in the last week.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And Jonathan, that is what we keep on coming back to, is the timeline, if they're in discussions, and there's a subpoena in June, and then it's urgent for them to get these documents, and something happens in there. And yet, they wait till August 5th to file and then they wait again for the search, a raid.\par \par \uc2 TURLEY: Right. I mean, the Wall Street Journal said that the attorney general sat on the application for weeks. And yet, you have the Justice Department, at least according to media, leaking these stories that they were out for nuclear secrets, the time is of the essence. And it's a series of delays.\par \par \uc2 That's reason a lot of this affidavit probably can be released, because what we're really interested in, as you notice, that period between the June meeting and the raid. What happened? Did they actually make a second request to the Trump team? Were they told to go pound sand? What happened during that period?\par \par \uc2 Because when you read what we saw today, it sounds like a no knock warrant. It sounds like people are going to flush contraband down the toilet.\par \par \uc2 Does the affidavit talk about the destruction allegation, and put some meat on these allegations that we're reading in the press?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: It's interesting to point out that while the media companies are arguing for the total unseal in court, they've made filings to that case, the Trump attorneys have not made a filing. They didn't have to. They didn't -- we weren't requested to but they did not make a filing to that extent. The former president has been out publicly saying he wants it all out there. Do you see anything there?\par \par \uc2 O'CONNELL: Well, sometimes I think it's just a real good idea to sit back and let the chips fall where they may. But when I look at the totality of the situation here, I really think when we talk about spirited conversations going on for a couple of weeks and DOJ, it seems to me the DOJ is the heavy in this situation that they were trying to push the bureau to do this warrant, they were trying to push the bureau to really, really hit this situation hard.\par \par \uc2 And my guess would be that the bureau was the one going against the DOJ in this particular situation. Because when you consider which entity has more to lose, the \uc2 FBI\uc2  clearly has much more to lose from this situation.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: That's fascinating from an inside \uc2 FBI\uc2  perspective. And very quickly, Jonathan. There are other investigations going on here. The January 6th investigation not just on Capitol Hill, but with the DOJ. And there are other things happening that we don't see.\par \par \uc2 TURLEY: Oh, yes, there's a lot of torpedoes in the water for the former president and any one of them can hit. And we still don't know what evidence outside of what was being sought and might have been found.\par \par \uc2 The Justice Department refused a request for a special master to sort this stuff out to protect privileged and non-relevant information, that struck many of us as being rather odd.\par \par \uc2 So, we'll have to see what they gathered and more importantly, who they will give it to.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: OK. Jonathan, Maureen, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 TURLEY: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 O'CONNELL: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: The Biden ministration is still not commenting tonight on the story we told you Wednesday about a big shake up at the \uc2 Centers for Disease Control and Prevention\uc2 , the CDC.\par \par \uc2 However, the head of the agency admits that agency made significant mistakes in dealing with the coronavirus pandemic that contradicts the president's long standing contention that the CDC and the government were providing the best possible information and treatments for the worldwide health emergency as it was happening.\par \par \uc2 White House Correspondent Peter Doocy has tonight's report with the president traveling Wilmington, Delaware. Good evening, Peter.\par \par \uc2 PETER DOOCY, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Good evening, Bret, a major change in the way this administration approaches COVID, they're about to stop buying COVID vaccines.\par \par \uc2 Soon, if you want one, you're going to have to go to your doctor. So, a president that long boasted about making it easier to get shots in arms is done doing that. And there's more.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: The CDC's COVID apology tour rolls on.\par \par \uc2 ROCHELLE WALENSKY\uc2 , DIRECTOR, CDC: We made some pretty public mistakes and we need to own them.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: Here's what that looks like. The CDC says we must take our concrete lessons learned from COVID-19 to improve how we deliver our science, guidance and programs to the American people. But there's some doubt about whether or not the agency learned the right lessons.\par \par \uc2 SEN. RAND PAUL (R-KY): If they want to reorganize, is humility. I think they need to step back and get back into the advice game, not the mandate game.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: Walensky was candid about COVID's difficulties for months.\par \par \uc2 WALENSKY: You know, this is hard.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: Now, a CDC in transition must confront a new challenge.\par \par \uc2 WALENSKY: There have been over 13,500 cases of monkeypox identified across 49 states, as well as Washington DC and Puerto Rico.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: And the CDC may have been caught flat footed.\par \par \uc2 DR. \uc2 ROBERT REDFIELD\uc2 , FORMER DIRECTOR, CENTERS FOR DISEASE CONTROL AND PREVENTION: I think with monkeypox, there's no doubt that the agency unfortunately was reactive, not proactive.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  reports about what happened when the government realized it didn't plan properly. "They would stretch the nation's limited supply of the only \uc2 FDA\uc2  approved vaccine for monkeypox by splitting doses to cover five times as many people and admission after repeated reassurances by top government officials that the United States did not have enough shots for every at risk American after all."\par \par \uc2 DR. \uc2 ANTHONY FAUCI\uc2 , DIRECTOR, \uc2 NATIONAL INSTITUTE OF ALLERGY AND INFECTIOUS DISEASES\uc2 : Please don't blame the CDC for this. This is not a CDC bundled response.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: But a shortage of vaccines was not on the horizon when President Biden was asked about the smallpox vaccine that also works for monkeypox just three months ago.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Do you think that the U.S. has enough smallpox vaccine stockpiled?\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I think we do have enough to deal with the likelihood of the problem.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (on camera): Well, there's a new problem, the only company in the world right now making monkeypox vaccines is \uc2 Bavarian Nordic\uc2  and they are in Denmark, but \uc2 HHS\uc2  just struck a deal to bring some production stateside to a facility in Michigan. They're also now ordering 1.8 million new doses, that is a signal that this administration thinks monkeypox could soon be a much bigger problem, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Peter Doocy in Wilmington Delaware. Peter, thank you. Stocks were up slightly today, the Dow gained 19, the S&P 500 finished ahead 10, the NASDAQ rose 27.\par \par \uc2 In tonight's midterm spotlight, we are releasing new Fox polls that indicate Democrats are leading in two big Senate races while President Biden and former President Trump are close in favorability.\par \par \uc2 In Wisconsin, Mandela Barnes the Democrat there leads incumbent Republican Senator \uc2 Ron Johnson\uc2  by four percentage points among registered voters. And President Biden is two points up on former President Trump in favorability.\par \par \uc2 Arizona voters favor incumbent Democrat Mark Kelly by eight points over challenger \uc2 Blake Masters\uc2 . And they like the former president over the current president in Arizona.\par \par \uc2 It's long been said a picture is worth a thousand words. Well, tonight in the Pennsylvania Senate race, a video seems to be worth a half million dollars. Democrat \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  says his campaign has raised that much in just 24 hours after the release of images it contends prove Republican candidate Mehmet Oz is out of touch with average voters. He pushes back.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent Bryan Llenas shows us tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BRYAN LLENAS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): That's Republican Pennsylvania Senate candidate Dr. Mehmet Oz walking through a grocery store in April, complaining about the inflationary costs of vegetables.\par \par \uc2 MEHMET OZ, SENATORIAL NOMINEE OF PENNSYLVANIA: That's asparagus, that's $4.00. That's $20.00 for crudites and this doesn't include the tequila.\par \par \uc2 LLENAS: It's now a viral video, thanks to a merciless social media campaign from his Democratic challenger Lieutenant Governor \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The campaign says it raised over $500,000 in 24 hours thanks to the crudite crisis.\par \par \uc2 LT. GOV. \uc2 JOHN FETTERMAN\uc2  (D-PA): We call this a veggie tray.\par \par \uc2 LLENAS: Fetterman released new stickers reading Wegners let them eat crudite, pointing out Dr. Oz's mispronunciation of the supermarket chain Wegners. It's just the latest attack painting Dr. Oz as an out of touch wealthy celebrity from New Jersey.\par \par \uc2 Here Fetterman plays 10 homes on a Monopoly board space. Dr. Oz admits he owns 10 properties, but says he legitimately owns two homes.\par \par \uc2 According to Fox News polls, Dr. Oz is trailing Fetterman by double digits and is seen unfavorably by 55 percent of Pennsylvania's registered voters.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Oz insists there's plenty of time left and is attacking Fetterman for being absent from the campaign trail.\par \par \uc2 DR. MEHMET OZ (R), SENATORIAL CANDIDATE, PENNSYLVANIA: He has taken a page out of \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's playbook. You know, he is staying at home, means that the media and his advertising do his dirty work.\par \par \uc2 LLENAS: Fetterman suffered a stroke three months ago. He held his first rally last week and is working with a speech therapist.\par \par \uc2 LT. GOV. \uc2 JOHN FETTERMAN\uc2  (D), SENATORIAL CANDIDATE, PENNSYLVANIA: You can count on us to eliminate the filibuster.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LLENAS (on camera): Dr. Oz is not the only Trump-backed Senate candidate struggling, so to our candidates, \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  in Georgia and \uc2 Blake Masters\uc2  in Arizona, both are polling low. Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We'll follow them all. Bryan, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Up next, disturbing video out of New York of an unprovoked attack.\par \par \uc2 BAIER (voice-over): Look at that. And why the perpetrator who was charged with attempted murder is back on the streets tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ERIC ADAMS (D), MAYOR OF NEW YORK CITY: The people of this city, they want to make sure people who are part of the catch, release, repeat system don't continue to hurt innocent New Yorkers --\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Kentucky Supreme Court is keeping the state's near total abortion ban in effect. The high court there says the state's prohibition will remain in place while it reviews arguments by abortion clinics challenging the state's law. It was the latest legal setback for the two remaining abortion clinics in Kentucky, both in Louisville.\par \par \uc2 Abortion rights have been reinstated and then revoked again by judges in Kentucky since the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  overturned Roe v. Wade in June.\par \par \uc2 Breaking today, three men, including a mafia Hitman are being charged in the killing of notorious Boston crime boss James Whitey Bulger, in a West Virginia prison.\par \par \uc2 Those charges against them come four years after Bulger was beaten to death just hours after being transferred from a facility in Florida. The incident raised questions about why the known snitch was placed in the general population instead of more protective housing. Bulger was serving a life sentence for 11 murders and other crimes.\par \par \uc2 Breaking tonight, a New York City sex offender arrested on suspicion of attempted murder is back on the streets tonight. He was allowed to walk free after his charges were downgraded to a misdemeanor. And the video of this attack, you see there, is disturbing.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent Nate Foy has details for us tonight from New York. Good evening. Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE FOY, FOX NEWS CHANNEL NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Good evening, Bret. 55-year-old Van Phu Bui is free tonight after his charges of attempted murder were reduced to third degree assault.\par \par \uc2 Now, keep in mind this man has been out on parole since 2019 after he was convicted of sexually abusing a 17-year-old back in 1994. Now, take a look at this video of the disturbing attack.\par \par \uc2 FOY (voice-over): This was in the Bronx last Friday. Police say he snuck up behind this man outside a restaurant and sucker-punched him in the face. Interestingly enough while wearing gloves.\par \par \uc2 There were no words exchanged before this. Police say it was unprovoked. The victim you can see smashed his head on the concrete and was rushed to the hospital. He was knocked unconscious.\par \par \uc2 Police say Bui then went back into the restaurant before eventually leaving. He's a registered level three sex offender, which is the most serious level there is.\par \par \uc2 Now, the Bronx District Attorney's Office confirms with Fox News. He's charged with third degree assault, which is not a serious enough crime to require bail in New York.\par \par \uc2 This comes as crime is surging in the Big \uc2 Apple\uc2 . Felony assault is up 19 percent from this time last year. Overall crime is up 36 percent. You see shootings here in public, innocent people being victimized.\par \par \uc2 And ironically enough, Bret, earlier today, in New York City Mayor Eric Adams was again criticizing the state's 2019 bail reform laws. That time and time again, allow repeat offenders and violent criminals like this back on the streets.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ADAMS: We passed a lot of laws for people who commit crimes. But I just want to see what at a list of laws we pass that deals with a New Yorker who was the victim of a crime.\par \par \uc2 The people who are the victims of robberies, we seem to have forgotten them.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 FOY (on camera): And Bret, this is a case that many will point to, because the victim here, a 52-year-old man is in a coma 