deal to resume Ukraine's grain exports, the volatile situation at the Russian occupied nuclear power plant, meantime, and the efforts to help end the war.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, we continue our series looking at the first anniversary of the U.S. withdrawal from Afghanistan with a focus on women's rights under the \uc2 Taliban\uc2 . Here is a short version -- there are not many of them. This evening, national security correspondent Jennifer Griffin explains how access to higher education, employment, and travel opportunities is now restricted.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER GRIFFIN, FOX NEWS NATIONAL SECURITY CORRESPONDENT: Twenty-four- year-old Fahima Noori fled Afghanistan for Charlotte, North Carolina, where she is studying to be a nurse. She has known how brutal the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  are since she was a baby.\par \par \uc2 FAHIMA NOORI, AFGHAN REFUGEE: The \uc2 Taliban\uc2 , they bombed our house, and I was just in the crib sleeping, and suddenly the rocket hits our house and everything caught fire. That's why I got the scars on my face.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: An aid group brought her to the U.S. and now she is on a student visa. If she returns to Afghanistan, she won't be able to go to school because the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  have banned girls from being educated above the sixth grade, reneging on a promise to educate girls. Fahima has five sisters who were left behind.\par \par \uc2 NOORI: One of my sisters, she graduated from the university right before \uc2 Taliban\uc2 . She is at home. One of my sisters she graduated high school. She is just at home. And I called them and talked to my sisters. They always cry.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: Sonia Yousafzai and her 12-year-old daughter Asanat fought their way on to Kabul Airport last August, making it on to one of the last flights the same day that 13 Americans were killed by a suicide bomber at Abbey Gate.\par \par \uc2 SONIA YOUSAFZAI, AFGHAN EVACUEE (through translator): They announced that no