h the help of Border Patrol as the migrant migrants were headed for the Del Rio sector of the U.S. border. That sector is home to Eagle Pass, Texas, where FOX News witnessed another day of mass illegal crossings. This, part of a huge single group of 300 migrants that crossed illegally during the early morning hours, almost all of them single adults.\par \par \uc2 On the other side of town, more of the same. This single group of 150 also crossing illegally into Eagle Pass. In a 24-hour span from Wednesday to Thursday, there were more than 2,000 illegal crossings here in the Del Rio sector. FOX News cameras witnessed migrants using a rope to cross the river, some carrying infants with them. Three bodies pulled from the river on Eagle Pass on Wednesday alone.\par \par \uc2 REP. TONY GONZALES, (R-TX): Bottom line is President Biden does not care about anyone along the border. Certainly, he doesn't care about the Hispanic community. This administration has a Hispanic community. I don't expect to see him.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN (on camera): And Bret, a disturbing arrest by Border Patrol in Yuma, Arizona, agents announcing today they arrested a Mexican national with a prior conviction for the rape of a child age three, or younger. We will send it back to you.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Bill Melugin along the border. Bill, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the panel discusses today's hearing about whether to unseal that material, that affidavit used to obtain the search warrant for the Trump compound in Florida.\par \par \uc2 First, Beyond our Borders tonight. The death toll from Wednesday's bombing at a mosque in Kabul now rises to at least 21. Police say at least 33 others were wounded. There has been no immediate claim of responsibility there.\par \par \uc2 Thousands of train workers in Britain stage a new round of strikes paralyzing rail service across that country. It's the latest move in escalating dispute over pay and working conditions, exacerbated by a deepening cost of living crisis.\par \par \uc2 And the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  announces a new trade talks that -- new trade talks with Taiwan as the island nation holds a military drill with missiles and canons, simulating a response to a Chinese missile attack. China meantime warns again it will take action if necessary to safeguard its sovereignty. It's the latest exchange between the two since tensions escalated earlier this month when House Speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  led a delegation to Taiwan.\par \par \uc2 Just some of the other stories Beyond our Borders tonight. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DEANNA SHULLMAN, MEDIA GROUP ATTORNEY: He understands that the public is going to likely be entitled to some parts of this warrant application and its affidavit. It is not the government's job to tell the public what is meaningful in terms of the release of its own information.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It may be a while before we see anything, if we ever see anything city. So I wouldn't hold my breath waiting for next week.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We are in unchartered grounds here, because this is an unprecedented sort of situation. The public interest obviously is very high. The political ramifications are very high.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: OK, the judge down in Florida, the magistrate, Reinhart, taking a middle course here, deciding that he is inclined to unseal some part of the affidavit but wants to hear from the government lawyers in a week about the possible redactions and their concerns.\par \par \uc2 Meantime, a Trump spokesperson says "President Trump has made clear his view that the American people should be permitted to see the unredacted affidavit related to the raid and break-in of his home. No redaction should be necessary, and the whole affidavit should be released." The government prosecutor, Jay Bratt, says, "This is not