ved materials or the seized materials returned to the president, and his has often said these were not things that they should have seized. Hugh and I went to the same law school, pretty good law school in the Midwest. I probably would have done it if I were advising him.\par \par \uc2 Two things I learned today, Bret. One was we got confirmation that there was a confidential informant, maybe even more based on what the lawyer for the media organizations suggested and I kind of read into what she was saying. And I could wrong, and if I am wrong, I look to be corrected.\par \par \uc2 And finally, the judge, this judge knows as much as any American but for the prosecutors at the Justice Department because he knows what is in that affidavit. He has had the argument before him. And he believes that some of this affidavit should be released. We said a few days ago, and you have been saying for days now Bret, this pace of this investigation picked up. It has been forced by some of the things that have happened, whether the Justice Department wanted it to happen or not, and they are going to have to adjust to it. And this judge, apparently, is going to release this affidavit and give us a lot more transparency. And I, for one, like Hugh and others, I welcome that.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We'll see how much transparency. It could be totally redacted. We put up the redactions before of some documents that we've asked for under a FOIA request.\par \par \uc2 Marc, there is this time issue, and eventually the Justice Department is going to have to come to a conclusion whether they are going to charge the former president or not. Meanwhile, we are running the traps onto this affidavit and that could get appealed. Your thoughts on all of this?\par \par \uc2 MARC THIESSEN, COLUMNIST, "WASHINGTON POST": Well, first of all, I'm open to the possibility that this was a legitimate search and that it was right to happen, and mishandling classified information is a serious issue.\par \par \uc2 But the problem is that the Justice Department and the \uc2 FBI\uc2  have no credibility for all the reasons that Hugh listed. If you are going to take the unprecedented step of raiding the home of a former president, you have to deal with unprecedented transparency. And if this does get to the point where there's a prosecution, the legitimacy of the prosecution, opinions are hardened. People are coming to conclusions in the absence of objective information.\par \par \uc2 And it's not just the politicization, the politicized seven-year investigation of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  by the \uc2 FBI\uc2  and the Justice Department that is called into question. It's the fact that the \uc2 FBI\uc2  whistleblowers have come forward and said that inside the \uc2 FBI\uc2 , senior officials have had a scheme to suppress the Hunter Biden investigation. You have \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2 , who weaponized the \uc2 FBI\uc2  to go after parents. So they have politicized themselves.\par \par \uc2 And if they want the American people to trust them in something as serious as this, they need to do this with more transparency than they would even be comfortable with.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes. You've got Senator \uc2 Chuck Grassley\uc2  and others writing letters to the \uc2 FBI\uc2  about that concern about bias in previously cases. We will follow this, all elements of it. Up next, the Trump effect on the midterms, plus the new polls we just brought you on this show, Wisconsin and Arizona. We'll talk politics next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 GOV. RON DESANTIS, (R) FLORIDA: The left is even more militant than they have ever been. They are playing for keeps.\par \par \uc2 That is not an agenda that is speaking to the average American family who is worried about the price of gas and food and all these other stuff. They are not sitting on the kitchen table talking to each other, saying oh, man, I just wish we could abolish the Electoral College.\par \par \uc2 RON KLAIN, WHITE HOUSE CHIEF OF STAFF: What will have an impact on Americans of both political parties, of all parties, is seeing one group in Washington, one political party in Washington fight to bring inflation down, and another give speeches about it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Setting up the argument heading into the midterms there. The new polls we showed you earlier, Wisconsin Senate, there you see Mandela Barnes, the Democrat, up four, margin of error plus three. In Arizona, the incumbent Democrat Mark Kelly up eight, again, plus or minus three. A lot of people say -- we're back with the panel -- that really these races don't come into focus for Americans until after Labor Day when everybody gets back from summer and starts focusing. Marc, do you buy that, or are these people, a number of them Republicans, in trouble?\par \par \uc2 THIESSEN: I don't buy that. And of course, the races will tighten. They always do. But these -- I'm very worried about how some of these Trump handpicked candidates are doing in these races. You mentioned that \uc2 Blake Masters\uc2  is losing to Mark Kelly in your poll by eight points. The FiveThirtyEight average he is behind by 10 points. Dr. Oz is behind by 11 points. \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  is losing by three. And J.D. Vance is losing to Tim Ryan in the FiveThirtyEight poll. That is a seat that Rob Portman won by 20 points.\par \par \uc2 So if \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is the least popular president in the history of presidential polling, we have the worst inflation in 40 years, highest gas prices on record, worst crimewave since the 1990s, worst border crisis in American history. If this isn't a red wave to put all red waves away and somehow the Democrats managed to keep the Senate, that would be a huge defeat for the Republican Party.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Hugh, you're already hearing the Senate minority leader. He said it here on SPECIAL REPORT. He said it again today, saying that, well, it's possible that we don't win the Senate back. And it's candidate choices is what he called it today, \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  did.\par \par \uc2 HEWITT: Leader McConnell is rightly setting expectations low. But, Bret, you and I talked about it. I do not believe polling is anywhere near accurate. I went back and looked at the \uc2 Ron Johnson\uc2 , Russ Feingold race from 2016. In August of 2016, Johnson was down by 11 and 12 points in Monmouth and Mercap (ph). In September he was down by double digits in two more polls. The last three polls taken the week before the election for \uc2 Ron Johnson\uc2  had him down one, two, and five. He won by 3.4.\par \par \uc2 I remind people Wisconsin the week before the election, ABC and "\uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " said \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  was going to win by 17 points. And \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  won by 0.6 points. Wisconsin cannot be polled. Arizona just had their primary. So I just don't buy the polls. And I also think people like Herschel, who I talked to today, they are becoming better candidates by the week. The leader is setting expectations low, but I still think he is looking to become the majority leader again.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Fair enough. And I get your comments about polls and how to poll. And I think we have a major problem in the country about polls, and we're going to try to bounce around to these states and talk to actual people to make sure we get a sense of the pulse on the ground.\par \par \uc2 I want to turn to the Pennsylvania race. \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2 , lieutenant governor in the Senate race, Harold, out campaigning for the first time just a few days ago. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LT. GOV. \uc2 JOHN FETTERMAN\uc2 , (D) PENNSYLVANIA SENATE CANDIDATE: Who would ever think that I would be the normal one in the race here. I gave away the lieutenant governor governor in Pennsylvania, the only lieutenant governor in the history to do that.\par \par \uc2 Eliminate the filibuster, and let's get some