o not only subpoena, but to impeach any and every official who was involved in wrongdoing, both current and past and to ensure that we finally uncover the truth about who was involved in each and every one of the scandals and misinformation campaigns I just walked through.\par \par \uc2 Because as you said, the damage an investigation, a false investigation can do is beyond imagination and to live under the clouds for years of these fake investigations is not merely an attack on \uc2 Donald J. Trump\uc2 , but an attack on every single American who participated in democracy to make him President.\par \par \uc2 CAIN: Stephen, I want to ask you this as we end our conversation, I can't help but note that this is a day as well, when Congress has passed a bill to expand the \uc2 IRS\uc2  enforcement agency by 87,000 new agents.\par \par \uc2 You know, I think the burden of proof for the DOJ on this allegation, this investigation into \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is going to be more than the burden of proof in a court of law.\par \par \uc2 The American people have lost trust in institutions -- media, science, medical, \uc2 FBI\uc2 , Intelligence Agencies -- what do you think, on the backend of this, Stephen will be the attitude of the American people towards institutions that have been granted inherited credibility for decades?\par \par \uc2 MILLER: Well, look, people looked at \uc2 Steve Bannon\uc2  and \uc2 Peter Navarro\uc2  facing jail and \uc2 Eric Holder\uc2  walking free, and they see that there is a double justice system; one for people that support the regime and one that are opposed to it.\par \par \uc2 You talk about the 87,000 IRS agents. Yes, it's a violent attack on the economic welfare of our middle class, but it is also an ability to police speech in America, because what does the \uc2 IRS\uc2  do? They decide who's a nonprofit, they decide tax deductibility. They decide who is a political organization, who is a charity, who can be involved in our elections, who can't be. This is an enormous power that is now being given to the \uc2 IRS\uc2  to further put a bureaucracy that is lifetime appointed and unelected in charge of our political system.\par \par \uc2 It's very frightening, I will tell you.\par \par \uc2 CAIN: Yes, and it just highlights this is about more than a former President. This is about the American people.\par \par \uc2 Stephen Miller\uc2 , you always put into perspective well. I appreciate you being with us on TUCKER CARLSON TONIGHT.\par \par \uc2 MILLER: Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 CAIN: All right, let's turn now to \uc2 Matt Whitaker\uc2 . He is the acting or was the acting Attorney General under \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , and he joins us now.\par \par \uc2 Matt, I'm glad to have you with us.\par \par \uc2 MATT WHITAKER\uc2 , FORMER ACTING ATTORNEY GENERAL: Hi, Will.\par \par \uc2 CAIN: Let's go into the -- what would it need, Matt, walk me through the process of accomplishing a search warrant, for the \uc2 FBI\uc2  to execute a raid on a former President? Who would have to approve? And what type of burden would they have to shoulder on an evidentiary basis to send \uc2 FBI\uc2  agents into Mar-a-Lago?\par \par \uc2 WHITAKER: Well, it's a burden, but it's not insurmountable and one of the first things they need to do is have an alleged crime and then second, they need to swear a fact pattern that supports the probable cause to issue that subpoena.\par \par \uc2 Ultimately, it's going to go in front of a Magistrate Judge who is going to sign off on it after it is sworn out by an officer of the law. But you know, let's take two steps back, to open a criminal investigation at all, you have to have predication.\par \par \uc2 And so you jump through all those hurdles at the Department of Justice, and you end up then executing a search warrant. But you know, these types of issues like a search warrant, arresting someone, and all those types of law enforcement activities, obviously, put a -- cast a pail or cast a shadow on that individual that is now subject to those law enforcement actions.\par \par \uc2 And in this case, I just think this is an outrageous expansion of the \uc2 FBI\uc2 's -- really, I don't know how else to say it -- attack on \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  for the last six years.\par \par \uc2 CAIN: So Matt, I want to take you up on your invitation to back it up a couple of steps. So in order to launch the investigation, who would have to give the greenlight? Who would have to ask for the search warrant? Before it gets to that Magistrate Judge, I'm going to ask you, and I'll ask you ahead of time and you can help answer this -- I would assume, require the Director of the \uc2 FBI\uc2 , perhaps the Attorney General \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2 , would it, in your estimation go as high as to the White House and the President of the United States, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 WHITAKER: Yes, I would be surprised if this Department of Justice allowed \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  to be involved, that would obviously be a line that shouldn't be crossed in a criminal investigation. But yes, I would expect that \uc2 Chris Wray\uc2  and \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  both signed off on the criminal investigation of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . And that, obviously, is a big step.\par \par \uc2 And you know, both of them, you know, fearing for their future probably have some measure of conflict in the situation. But a typical investigation, an agent can go to a supervisor, you know, tell him what he has and make sure that there are reasonable investigative steps that can be taken based on an initial predication of that case, obviously, in concert with a prosecutor like a US Attorney's Office.\par \par \uc2 But you know, this type of case where you're going after a former President of the United States and possibly future President of the United States, would involve the Attorney General and the \uc2 FBI\uc2  Director and senior officials at the highest levels.\par \par \uc2 CAIN: So I'm going to ask you to speculate here, Matt. You can decline if you would like, but I'm going to ask you to speculate. You know, we've listened to \uc2 Chris Wray\uc2  over the last several weeks or so and in the last week, at the very least, testified before Congress, about the resources of the \uc2 FBI\uc2  and where he could not dedicate resources.\par \par \uc2 We heard him asked about the Hunter Biden investigation, the \uc2 Gretchen Whitmer\uc2  investigation, against violence against pro-life advocates and/or pro-abortion advocates and pro-life sinners. He talked about essentially, Matt, the limited resources of the \uc2 FBI\uc2  and therefore, prosecutorial discretion in where they choose to put their resources.\par \par \uc2 So I'm curious, the \uc2 FBI\uc2  has chosen to devote resources to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . Why, in your estimation? What is this about? Is this about supposed classified documents? Is this about January 6? Why is this at the top of their list of priorities?\par \par \uc2 WHITAKER: Well, Will, that's a great question. And obviously, what you prioritize, you put resources towards and if you had many \uc2 FBI\uc2  agents at Mar-a-Lago today that took away from other things that those agents could be working on, including violent crime in our major cities, but you're right, the Department of Justice and the \uc2 FBI\uc2  appear to be focusing a lot of resources on January 6th, and on \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and the previous administration. And it's hard not to believe that these are politically motivated.\par \par \uc2 I have spent seven and a half years at the Department of Justice. It's an institution that should be trusted, but they have eroded that trust to a point now, where I think they, to your point earlier, they have a burden to demonstrate that this investigation is not completely politically motivated, after \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  walked off scot-free for the last seven years.\par \par \uc2 CAIN: You know, Matt, and I don't know how they satisfy that burden. You know, at a minimum, Matt, it would require some form of a guilty verdict in some court of law. But what we've seen over the past several years is the power of allegation, a simple investigation, and without some, at a minimum, a guilty verdict. But I would say the burden is probably more heavy than that, because the American people have to look at the inconsistencies.\par \par \uc2 You know what we are both talking about, right? Hunter, you know, pro- abortion violence at pro-life centers, whatever -- limited resources -- and we cannot help but see, because of these hypocrisies partisan application of law enforcement.\par \par \uc2 WHITAKER: Yes, you're absolutely right. And I think this is where, you know, the American people need to ask very difficult questions. Congress needs to ask very difficult questions, because at the end of the day, if they're really planning on bringing \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  to Washington, DC, in front of a Washington, DC jury and a Washington, DC judge, you know, this Republic maybe not strong enough to bear that burden, ultimately.\par \par \uc2 But we will see, you know, the \uc2 FBI\uc2  is into an unprecedented area. The Department of Justice under Democrats' leadership, I think is going into very dangerous field right now.\par \par \uc2 CAIN: Matt, what happens next? What happens next? Will we hear from the DOJ and the \uc2 FBI\uc2  on why this raid occurred?\par \par \uc2 WHITAKER: Yes. I would expect that we're not going to hear much from the Department of Justice. I can't imagine that \uc2 FBI\uc2  is used just to enforce the Archives' authority to have the presidential records. They certainly didn't seem to care much about \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2 's records at the time.\par \par \uc2 And so I just think, you know, it's going to be a very quiet uncomfortable period for some time. And you know, I think everybody needs to be ready for what happens next.\par \par \uc2 CAIN: And one last question, Matt. You were acting Attorney General, you have an expansive view of American history. In your estimation, we heard from \uc2 Stephen Miller\uc2  a little bit earlier that in his estimation, he can't find another example of something like this in American history, can you?\par \par \uc2 WHITAKER: I can't, and I think this is unfortunately -- we've crossed the Rubicon to some extent where previous administrations are now investigated, and previous Presidents are investigated by the next administration. This is something from Banana Republics, not the United States of America.\par \par \uc2 CAIN: \uc2 Matt Whitaker\uc2 , that's exactly what we've begun to hear, Banana Republic. Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 WHITAKER: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 CAIN: You've heard it mentioned several times, the hypocrisy, the -- perhaps not hypocrisy, the partisan consistency on where resources are deployed is too obvious to ignore.\par \par \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's son, \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  lied on a Federal gun form. He also is on tape smoking crack, and hooking up with prostitutes. In addition, the \uc2 FBI\uc2  has been in possession of \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 's laptop for more than two years and that laptop includes evidence of tax fraud. It also contains evidence of the Biden's family's overseas influence peddling operation in Ukraine, in China, potential corruption at the highest levels of the American government that you would think, would at least warrant, I don't know, a look under the covers by law enforcement.\par \par \uc2 So why hasn't \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  been raided by the \uc2 FBI\uc2 ? Gerry Callahan is a radio host in Massachusetts, and he joins us now. Gerry, great to see you. Maybe I shouldn't be asking you to explain the hypocrisy. Maybe it's obvious, it is just partisan consistency.\par \par \uc2 GERRY CALLAHAN, RADIO HOST IN MASSACHUSETTS: It is obvious, Will. It couldn't be more obvious. You don't have you know, speculation when it comes to Hunter. You have his