e next many, many, several steps.\par \par \uc2 This is a former President, this is not something that has happened before. So clearly, they feel like they have the strength of whatever they've alleged, and whatever they've ascertained so far in this investigation to move forward.\par \par \uc2 Now, we've been considering this in the context of the January 6 investigations and hearings. But what we're hearing tonight, the indications seem to be this is not about that, that this is about presidential records and the maintaining of them after someone has left office. So it can be a different thing.\par \par \uc2 If you're talking about indicting a President, a former President for something that happened while he was President, that's kind of the conversation we've been having if this is connected to January 6th, but tonight, it sounds like a different conversation. Like they may be talking about pursuing something against the President for something they believe that happened after his time in office and what he did with records after he left Washington and was no longer the President of the United States.\par \par \uc2 So those are two kind of different tracks there, but you've got to think because of all of those political calculations, they wouldn't move forward unless they had some confidence level that they have at least the basic makings for what you would pursue in this kind of case because otherwise, it does get into the optics of where we've seen all of these other cases where it seems like a hammer is being used instead of a flyswatter in some of these cases, only they inside the DOJ and \uc2 FBI\uc2  know tonight.\par \par \uc2 CAIN: Yes. If this were all there were to it; that would be a hard image for the \uc2 FBI\uc2  to overcome. You would have to think they calculated a next step. So this is not the end of their story.\par \par \uc2 Shannon Bream, thank you so much for being with us tonight.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: See you, Will.\par \par \uc2 CAIN: It i