ce or suspicion that the \uc2 FBI\uc2  is just the secret police of the Democratic Party.\par \par \uc2 It's a terrible thing for America, and I think it's going to backfire terribly on the Democrats because it's just going to rally support behind \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  because no matter what people think of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , they know that this is an outrage to be raiding a former President's home. He's not just the former President either, he is the likely future candidate for President in 2024.\par \par \uc2 So this is so political and disastrous, and when \uc2 Christopher Wray\uc2 , the \uc2 FBI\uc2  Director, appeared just on Thursday with a really shabby disingenuous performance, where he didn't answer any of the questions that the senators needed to ask, crucial questions about our national security and about the bearing of the Hunter Biden laptop story investigation, and about lying about it as people --\par \par \uc2 Two people inside the \uc2 FBI\uc2  said that it was Russian disinformation, when the DNI and Bill Barr and the \uc2 FBI\uc2  Director himself came out in October 2020 to say that the laptop was not Russian disinformation, that yes, the \uc2 FBI\uc2  had it in their custody. They had to do that because Adam Schiff, again, was lying and saying that the laptop was Russian disinformation, just as he lied about the first impeachment of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , he said he didn't know anything about Ukrainian whistleblower, just as he lied about the Russia collusion hoax.\par \par \uc2 This is the Chairman of the Intelligence Committee with oversight of the \uc2 FBI\uc2 . It is really quite frightening.\par \par \uc2 CAIN: How do you explain it, Miranda? How do you explain the devotion of resources to this, to what we're watching unfold today and tonight, and the total lack of a devotion of resources and that really doesn't do it justice because the \uc2 FBI\uc2  didn't just declined to investigate \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 . They spun it as disinformation. So how do you explain that disparity?\par \par \uc2 DEVINE: Well, it just shows the politicization of the \uc2 FBI\uc2 , and under a completely weak Director, \uc2 Christopher Wray\uc2 , who was, you know, one of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's worst personnel choices. The \uc2 FBI\uc2  is completely out of control. It's a number of different field offices that are their own fiefdoms. God knows what they're up to, some of these, particularly the Washington, DC office which seems to have carriage for this current raid.\par \par \uc2 A hundred \uc2 FBI\uc2  agents raiding the home of the former President, potential future presidential candidate. It is incredible as you've had people say, it is unprecedented. And it's really frightening because the \uc2 FBI\uc2  is supposed to be doing something about crime and terrorism. Yet, their biggest ever investigation has been into the Capitol riot on January 6th, which was bad, but in the context of things that are wrong in this country, it is really not the worst thing to be looking at.\par \par \uc2 And yet it seems that \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2 , Adam Schiff, \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2 , sadly, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  seem to be running the \uc2 FBI\uc2  and its priorities.\par \par \uc2 CAIN: All right. Well, Miranda, you characterized it as potentially a phishing expedition. Shannon Bream said they have to have their next steps in mind. Whatever it is, I think something is very clear, the burden of proof is going to be incredibly high on the American institutions because the electorate, a large percentage of the electorate are looking at this with a great amount of jaundiced skepticism.\par \par \uc2 Miranda, great to talk to you this evening.\par \par \uc2 DEVINE: You too, Will.\par \par \uc2 CAIN: All right, you're looking there on your screens at new pictures of former President Trump leaving Trump Tower in New York City tonight, there on your screen just a moment ago as the sun has set in New York City.\par \par \uc2 We turn now to Joe Kent for reaction. He is a former Army Special Forces and he is running for Congress in the State of Washington.\par \par \uc2 Joe, it's great to have you with us tonight. You're five? I believe -- we are -- you are six, basically, from before \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's election to tonight, we have seen investigation after investigation and allegation after allegation. Do you think tonight what we're looking at now will be any different than the outcome of the past six years? Or will it simply be another illustration of the power of an allegation?\par \par \uc2 JOE KENT (R), CONGRESSIONAL CANDIDATE FOR WASHINGTON STATE: Well, it has to be different than what we've seen before because we've seen the complete and total weaponization of our national security state.\par \par \uc2 You mentioned how this all began with the Russiagate sham hoax and we saw the national security state at the highest levels weaponized against President Trump and his campaign and throughout his administration, and now, with the narrative coming from January 6th, and make no mistake, this is where the narrative really, really was fortified to turn these potent tools against not just President Trump, but many of his top advisers, people who weren't even on the ground January 6th, and then folks that were put away, thrown into essentially political prisons without any kind of due process.\par \par \uc2 And now the national security state continues to be on the hunt against President Trump or even all the way down to, you know, parents that show up to School Board meetings. So we have to realize that we're at war.\par \par \uc2 When we take back the house in 2023, bringing the national security state to heel must be our top priority. Any Republican who is not ready for that fight is unfit for duty.\par \par \uc2 CAIN: You know, Joe, I've mentioned this several times tonight, but I imagine if you're sitting at home watching, this sense of -- and we heard earlier on the program, it's not simply corruption. It's just what we're watching unfold in America isn't limited to, as you mentioned, the national security state, it's not limited to law enforcement.\par \par \uc2 I think, very aptly, Joe pointed out School Board meetings, which of course, the DOJ classified those parents as domestic terrorists. But beyond that, censorship within big technology. It's hard, I would imagine for an American citizen who doesn't subscribe to the current thing, whatever that political philosophy may be driving it, but whatever is the current thing, to think there is anything even resembling a fair playing field in America?\par \par \uc2 KENT: No, not at all. You know, I served for a while in the military, I was in Special Forces. I served in lots of third world countries that were really ran as police and security states, and I think that's the reason why a lot of this looks very familiar to me.\par \par \uc2 But if you're an American, you've enjoyed how great this country is where we have these freedoms. This is hard to recognize, but people need to recognize exactly what this is. It's the totality of this regime and I call it a regime because it's the weaponization of the national security state, their partners in Big Tech, like you mentioned, and it's really being totally weaponized against anyone who doesn't fully comply.\par \par \uc2 I mean, President Trump said for years that it wasn't him that they were after, it was just us. He happened to be in the way. Well, even now that he is really kind of out of the way, he is potentially rising back up again. They're going after him, but it's a total full frontal assault. They're going after every single one of us.\par \par \uc2 So we really have to start electing Republicans who know what time it is, because our one hope of saving this Republic is going to be fought out on the floor of the Oversight Committee. The and we have to not just bring these people to heel, but we have to start bringing them to full account and I mean criminal charges, too, not just you know sound clips for how we're really going to take them on to turn into fundraising asks. We really have to start going after these people and taking away their power.\par \par \uc2 CAIN: One last question for you, Joe. You're running for office. So, let's just cut to the heart of the matter. This is politics. What do you think this does to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's candidacy were he to run in 2024?\par \par \uc2 KENT: Well, I think we all know that President Trump is more than likely going to run and they're going to keep coming at him. So, what we have to do is we have to get unified. The Republicans, the conservatives, the America First Movement, we have to see what the other side is doing.\par \par \uc2 The other side is 100 percent in lockstep. They are unified. We have to get the exact same way. We just fought a really hard primary throughout the entire country. Some of them are still going on it. I understand that, but we have to come together if we're going to save this Republic, we have to work in unison.\par \par \uc2 Trump saved us from a lot back in 2016, but even Trump couldn't bring us through all of this. However, together, we can take back our Republic and we have to get unified.\par \par \uc2 CAIN: All right, Joe, I also understand that you are currently leading your race for Congress. So best of luck and thank you tonight for jumping on TUCKER CARLSON TONIGHT.\par \par \uc2 KENT: Absolutely. Thank you for having me.\par \par \uc2 CAIN: All right, there are reports tonight that the raid on \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's Mar-a-Lago residence was approved at the highest levels of the DOJ, at the very least. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is calling this raid lawless and unprecedented. The question is, who had to sign off on this?\par \par \uc2 Jonathan Turley is a law professor. He joins us to break down the legal implications.\par \par \uc2 Jonathan, I'm really happy to have you on the program. How high did this have to be approved?\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN TURLEY, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR: I think that Attorney General \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  almost certainly signed off on this. It is hard to imagine a decision of this magnitude would be made without his knowledge and approval. He hasn't recused himself, and so that assumption, I think is well founded.\par \par \uc2 The news accounts indicate that this is not about January 6th, but about the Presidential Records Act. And the National Archivist under that Act can ask the Attorney General to intervene when someone is not complying, including a former President by turning over material. We know the material was taken previously, two months ago.\par \par \uc2 Now, putting all of that together, we're still speculating as to what the Federal Judge signed off here as probable cause of a crime. There are provisions like Section 2071, which allows for three years in prison for people who knowingly conceal information they're not allowed to retain that would include classified information.\par \par \uc2 But the problem with the PRA or the Presidential Records Act, being the basis of this type of action, is that it has not been traditionally a criminal matter. Certainly, it has not been that way for former Presidents.\par \par \uc2 Indeed, you had people like Sandy Berger, a former national security adviser, who was found to have taken classified documents out, hidden in his socks and he was not prosecuted. You also have records cases like \uc2 James Comey\uc2 , who was accused of taking \uc2 FBI\uc2  material when he left the \uc2 FBI\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The history has been not to prosecute those cases, which is going to become more relevant as we go forward.\par \par \uc2 CAIN: Wow. That is fascinating and important information. So what you've highlighted for us here is the willingness of the DOJ to go after, I assume "classified material" in the past, Jonathan, and then choosing again, once again, to see an inconsistent application here when it comes to President Trump.\par \par \uc2 One legal question before we go, Jonathan, would it not -- let's assume this is about those, as you pointed out, we're speculating. Let's assume it's about those documents, and they are -- I'm assuming classified has a lot to do with this, that they were not supposed to be removed because of their classification.\par \par \uc2 Were \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  President of United States, when he left the White House and you're assuming when he left, he still had the power of the presidency and took those documents? Doesn't he also have the power to declassify documents, Jonathan?\par \par \uc2 TURLEY: He does. I testified on that in Congress on this very issue about President Trump when he was still President. He had the ultimate declassification authority that ended when his presidency ended and they could be arguing that if you did not turn over these documents, he knowingly retained documents past their privilege.\par \par \uc2 CAIN: That is very, very key. That's excellent information tonight from Jonathan Turley. Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 TURLEY: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 CAIN: All right, the raid on Mar-a-Lago isn't the first time \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has used the DOJ to target his political opponents. Jeff Clark was a former Assistant Attorney General in the Trump administration. Earlier this year, armed Federal agents arrested Jeff Clark in his pajamas in a pre-dawn raid and Jeff Clark joins us now.\par \par \uc2 Jeff, as you see everything unfold and of course, your recent experience. What's your reaction?\par \par \uc2 JEFF CLARK, FORMER ASSISTANT ATTORNEY GENERAL: Well, Will, thanks to you to be here, but I'm outraged for President Trump and more importantly, I'm outraged for the presidency. This is an unprecedented step that just shows that the department is being weaponized, that it is out of control, that the \uc2 FBI\uc2  is out of control.\par \par \uc2 I'd heard earlier remarks by \uc2 Steve Bannon\uc2