upposed to be writing.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Things are going pretty south, are they not, in Ukraine right now? Glenn, that's a problem for all these people who thought the Russian army is going to collapse at any moment.\par \par \uc2 GREENWALD: You know, so often, Laura, if you go back and look at every war, the media makes statements that gets everyone riled up in favor of the war. And over time, usually pretty quickly it turns out that the media narrative is false.\par \par \uc2 I can't tell you how many times I read in major outlets from very authoritative sources that the Russian military was days away from collapsing, that the Ukrainians were going to vanquish them. And clearly, that's not going to happen. It's become a bottomless pit of money, a bottomless pit of weapons. There's weapons flowing all through Ukraine that nobody can account for, who knows where they're going.\par \par \uc2 And so now you start to see the Biden White House using Tom Friedman's columns to raise doubts about Zelenskyy, who just a month or two ago, you weren't allowed to even question, or you'd get accused of being a Kremlin agent. Now saying, that the Biden White House really doesn't trust him. That there's a lot of problems in the relationship. Clearly something has gone very wrong in this proxy war, and you start to see the signs of that now.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Glenn, great to see you. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 And speaking of China, I want to take you back to something we covered on this show now was three months ago.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Over the past two years, Chinese nationals have began buying up tens of thousands of acres of farmland in the state of Oklahoma. While they've been doing so in a number of states, Oklahoma has become their go- to state for these purchases.\par \par \uc2 Given the economic and the National Security ramifications here, Congress should get involved.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: 