 \par \uc2 DR. SCOTT JENSEN, (R) MINNESOTA GUBERNATORIAL CANDIDATE: We had news journalists around here saying, where is Governor Walz, at the time, and they're still saying it. But today, Laura, all across Minnesota people are saying, where was Governor Walz? Where is Governor Walz? They're wondering when the nightmare is going to be over.\par \par \uc2 Governor Walz took no initiative to remove the autonomous zones, to rebuild the third precinct. He just doesn't seem to get it. It wasn't just a building. It's a spirit of lawfulness. Unfortunately, for Governor Walz, he was responsible for the epicenter of the riots in 2020. And there are cities across the nation that burned because of the inability to act. He froze because he wasn't sure what his base would tell him.\par \par \uc2 He needed to know whether or not it was safe, and he does this over and over again, whether you're talking about putting the National Guard on the streets, letting the third precinct burn, telling the state patrol to stand-down and let Christopher Columbus statue get torn down two, three weeks later. And even the last month in Minneapolis there was clear evidence that Minneapolis is still a very troubled city. Governor Walz did nothing. He froze.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Dr. Jensen, independent journalists Rebecca Brannon caught what happened when a resident asked Governor Walz about the third precinct. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Why would you let a police station burn to the ground and not send troops in there to stop?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Governor, we've got some folks waiting for us in the car. We should head there.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: You can't answer my question? You can't answer that.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Dr. Jensen, that's one of his constituents. Is it any way to treat the people who actually make the state go, lived here