ight law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document INGANG0020220804ei8300001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 China Prepares Show Of Force After Nancy Pelosi Taiwan Trip; \uc2 Jackie Walorski\uc2  And...\b0\par\par\uc2 Bret Baier, Peter Doocy, David Spunt, Alicia Acuna, Chad Pergram,  Lydia Hu, Kelly O'Grady, Claudia Cowan \par \uc2 7,956 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 August 2022\par \uc2 Fox News: Special Report with Brett Baier\par \uc2 FNSPBB\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 China Prepares Show Of Force After Nancy Pelosi Taiwan Trip; \uc2 Jackie Walorski\uc2  And Two Aides Killed In A Tragic Crash; Senators Hold Hearing On Virology Studies "Gain Of Function" Research; Arizona Gubernatorial Race Trump-Backed Candidate Claims Victory In GOP Primary, Sen.\par \par \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  (R-KY), Is Being Interviewed About The Manchin-Schumer Reconciliation Bill, NATO Expansion, The Southern Border, And The Upcoming Midterms.; Republican Efforts To Win Control Of Congress This Fall; Democrats Prepare For Showdown Over Spending Bill; Abortion Ruling Impact On Midterm Elections; Biden Administration Still Addressing Ongoing Nationwide Baby Formula Shortage; Landlords Criticize Some Cities Voting To Extend Eviction Moratoriums Initiated As COVID Era Measure; House Speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2 's Husband Paul Pelosi Pleads Not Guilty To Charges Related To Car Accident; Senate Minority Leader \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  Says Democrats May Retain Control Of Senate After Midterm Elections; Kansas Voters Reject Measure Allowing State Legislators To Outlaw Abortion\par \par \uc2 KATIE PAVLICH FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR: And just a nice, good story but you can't miss your hair appointment.\par \par \uc2 HAROLD FORD JR., FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR: I know someone else who does this.\par \par \uc2 GREG GUTFELD, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: She could have used a driverless car, right? Old people need -- old people can driverless car, you can drive wherever you want to go. I know -- I know Bret Baier is thinking about getting a driverless car.\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Sure, why not? Or you could drive me, Greg.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: It would be an honor.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Thanks, guys. Good evening and welcome to Washington. I'm Bret Baier. We are moments away from my exclusive interview in studio with Senate Minority Leader \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  about a host of issues.\par \par \uc2 Then, House Speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2 's husband Paul Pelosi, pleads not guilty to DUI charges as we learned some damaging information from a newly released police report.\par \par \uc2 And could help be on the way to ease the months long baby formula shortage? What took so long and will it be enough?\par \par \uc2 But first, breaking tonight, House Speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  says the U.S. will not abandon its commitment to Taiwan after meeting with Taiwanese leaders over the past day, the Beijing's rebuttal in China causing a lot of concern as China prepares for a large scale show of force in the Taiwan Strait to make clear that its claims are non-negotiable over the island it regards as a renegade province.\par \par \uc2 The live fire military drills are expected to be the most provocative action towards Taiwan in a quarter century.\par \par \uc2 White House Correspondent Peter Doocy joins us now from the North Lawn. Good evening, Peter.\par \par \uc2 PETER DOOCY, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Good evening, Bret. On the ground and in the water near Taiwan, a lot is changing. But here at the White House, the president's position towards China is not changing. And tonight, he remains in COVID isolation as Speaker Pelosi jets around Asia.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 NANCY PELOSI\uc2  (D-CA): We commend Taiwan for being one of the freest societies in the world.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (voice over): That's not how China sees things though. Neither is this:\par \par \uc2 PELOSI: Today the world faces a choice between democracy and autocracy.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: The Communist Party of China is readying a response effectively encircling Taiwan and warning ships and airplanes to steer clear as they prepare for large scale military exercises, foreign ministers of the G7 write: We are concerned by recent and announced threatening actions by the People's Republic of China PRC, particularly live fire exercises and economic coercion, which risk unnecessary escalation.\par \par \uc2 President Biden didn't directly mentioned any of this. But he did talk to his national security advisors about a "free and open Indo-Pacific".\par \par \uc2 SEN. BILL HAGERTY (R-TN): I was ambassador to Japan, we use the term Kabuki theater. I think that's what's going on here. You're going to see a lot of theater. But I don't expect this to turn into anything that's kinetic.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: So, Republicans aren't sure the Chinese will back up this kind of tough talk from a Chinese ambassador. "The U.S. must pay the price for its own mistake. And we mean what we say."\par \par \uc2 White House officials won't say if they consider that a serious threat.\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , DEPUTY PRESS SECRETARY: The United States will not seek and does not want a crisis. We are prepared to manage what Beijing chooses to do.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: White House officials also won't weigh in on whether or not Speaker Pelosi's trip was worth it, something they haven't done before during or after her visit.\par \par \uc2 REP. KEVIN MCCARTHY (R-CA): We've got a real weakness in this White House with the White House wouldn't even backup the speaker for going to Taiwan.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: when they go, he doesn't dictate if they go. Now he can say if he thinks it was good or not.\par \par \uc2 JEAN-PIERRE: I mean, that's not how it works. That is not how it works here.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (on camera): Well, we're going to learn a lot more about how it works here as we wait to see if U.S. military assets in the region move as requested by the Chinese.\par \par \uc2 It hasn't even been a week since President Biden told President Xi on the phone that he opposes any efforts to undermine the peace and stability in the Straits of Taiwan. And yet here we are, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Peter Doocy live in the North Lawn. Peter, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Also breaking tonight, sad news, Republican representatives \uc2 Jackie Walorski\uc2  was killed today after being involved in a car accident in her Northern Indiana district. We're told two of her aides were also killed in that accident.\par \par \uc2 Congressional Correspondent Chad Pergram joins us now live from Capitol Hill, horrible news. Good evening, Chad.\par \par \uc2 CHAD PERGRAM, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONGRESSIONAL CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Grim news, Bret. The House is out this week. That's why Walorski is back in her district.\par \par \uc2 The Elkhart, Indiana Sheriff's Office says Walorski was traveling in an SUV going southbound, a northbound car went left of center and crashed head on into the SUV.\par \par \uc2 Four people killed in all. Walorski and two aides. Her district director Zach Potts and Emma Thomson, her communications director. The driver of the other car Edith Schmucker also died.\par \par \uc2 GOP Indiana Senator \uc2 Todd Young\uc2  says he is trying to process Walorski's death. He just talked to her on the phone a few days ago.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 TODD YOUNG\uc2  (R-IN): This is going to be a really big gap to fill in our public life but more personally, Jackie was a person of deep faith and strong conviction and just incredibly smart person.\par \par \uc2 But what I really remembered about her, she had an amazing sense of humor, high energy. She knew how to light up a room. She'd go into a room and immediately people would be happier.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: House GOP whip Steve Scalise says Jackie and her staffers died serving her constituents. House Speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  ordered flags at the Capitol lowered to half-staff in honor of Walorski.\par \par \uc2 Pelosi says Walorski was admired by colleagues on both sides for her personal kindness. Walorski was a native of South Bend.\par \par \uc2 Former Mayor and Transportation Secretary \uc2 Pete Buttigieg\uc2  tweeted he was shocked and saddened at the news. Walorski was first elected in 2012. She was involved with tax policy and veterans issues.\par \par \uc2 Jackie Walorski\uc2  was 58 years old, Bret.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Chad Pergram live on the Hill. Chad, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Controversial gain of function research taking center stage at a Senate subcommittee hearing today at issue whether the research method was being practiced at the Wuhan lab in China where COVID-19 may have originated.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent David Spunt has the latest from Capitol Hill. Good evening, David.\par \par \uc2 DAVID SPUNT, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Good evening to you, Bret. We are 2-1/2 years into this pandemic and today was the first official hearing focusing solely on gain of function and how it relates to the origins of COVID 19.\par \par \uc2 Though it's been used in recent years, gain of function Bret in the medical community is still considered somewhat controversial. Researchers genetically alter an organism to make its effects more enhanced, or more potent.\par \par \uc2 Today, medical doctors announced a firm belief this type of research was done at the Wuhan Institute of Virology. And the consensus today while this was a lab leak, it likely was an accident.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I am concerned by efforts to continue to focus on gain of function, because it is so ill defined.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT (voice over): Senator \uc2 Rand Paul\uc2  from Kentucky who you had on last night on SPECIAL REPORT called for today's hearing for well over a year, Bret. He believes the Chinese Communist Party and other health officials in that country are just not being truthful about the origins of COVID-19.\par \par \uc2 SEN. RAND PAUL (R-KY): Do we need to be having hearings on this? Should we have follow up hearings? Should we have legislation? And if a million people died, there's a chance this came from a lab. I think without question, we should. Both sides of the aisle should be looking at this.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: Despite Senator Paul's claims, Dr. \uc2 Anthony Fauci\uc2  and other top officials at the \uc2 National Institutes of Health\uc2  have denied multiple times that they specifically funded any grants related to gain of function and coronaviruses that would affect humans.\par \par \uc2 Fauci was not at the hearing today, but spoke on this broadcast just a couple of weeks ago.\par \par \uc2 DR. \uc2 ANTHONY FAUCI\uc2 , DIRECTOR, \uc2 NATIONAL INSTITUTE OF ALLERGY AND INFECTIOUS DISEASES\uc2 : Upon relooking at this carefully now, we think it's more likely that it was a natural evolution.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Do you still believe that? There are more and more organizations that point in to a lab leak.\par \par \uc2 FAUCI: Bret, more people saying it doesn't mean there's more evidence of it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SPUNT (on camera): And if Republicans take the Senate come January, Bret, they're already promising to hold more hearings on gain of function and the origins of COVID-19. Bret, back to you.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: David Spunt live on the Hill. David, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Fox News has confirmed former White House Counsel \uc2 Pat Cipollone\uc2  received a subpoena from a federal grand jury as the Justice Department looks into potential crimes related to the January 6th Capitol riot.\par \par \uc2 During a previous appearance before the House January 6 committee, Cipollone agreed that there was no evidence of election fraud sufficient to undermine the outcome of the 2020 election.\par \par \uc2 Meantime, the Justice Department is filing a lawsuit against \uc2 Peter Navarro\uc2 , claiming the former adviser to President Trump used an unofficial e-mail account while working in the White House and wrongfully retained presidential records.\par \par \uc2 The legal action comes just weeks after Navarro was indicted on criminal charges after refusing to cooperate with that congressional investigation into January 6th attack on Capitol Hill.\par \par \uc2 President Biden signed an executive order today laying groundwork for Medicaid to help women seeking abortions to travel between states to obtain access to the procedure.\par \par \uc2 Details are still being ironed out because it's illegal to use federal funding to pay for abortions unless the woman's life is in danger or the pregnancy is a result of rape or incest. The White House Press Secretary had strong words on the issue and the Supreme Court's Dobbs decision today. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JEAN-PIERRE: The Supreme Court made this extreme decision to take away a constitutional right. It was an unconstitutional action by them.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: That raised eyebrows. Yesterday in Kansas, voters overwhelmingly rejected the constitutional amendment to the state constitution that would have given state lawmakers the chance to either further restrict or ban abortions in the state.\par \par \uc2 A midterm watch, there was no shortage of fireworks in yesterday's primaries. In the Missouri Senate race, \uc2 Eric Schmitt\uc2  clinched the Republican nomination a large margin over his opponent former governor \uc2 Eric Greitens\uc2 , following President Trump's -- former President Trump's dual Eric endorsement there. Schmitt will face Democratic nominee Trudy Busch Valentine in November.\par \par \uc2 In Michigan, Trump endorsed candidate Dixon -- Tudor Dixon secured the Republican gubernatorial nomination setting up a matchup with incumbent Democrat \uc2 Gretchen Whitmer\uc2  seeking a second term in Michigan's newly redrawn third congressional district.\par \par \uc2 Trump endorsed candidate John Gibbs defeated moderate Republican incumbent Peter Meijer.\par \par \uc2 In Washington state, two representatives who voted to impeach former President Trump, \uc2 Dan Newhouse\uc2  and \uc2 Jaime Herrera Beutler\uc2  appear to be surviving challenges as that count goes on from Trump backed candidates.\par \par \uc2 In Arizona, former President Trump played a major factor in the Senate and gubernatorial races, one of which is yet to be decided officially.\par \par \uc2 Senior Correspondent Alicia Acuna has the latest tonight from Phoenix. Good evening, Alicia.\par \par \uc2 ALICIA ACUNA, FOX NEWS CHANNEL SENIOR CORRESPONDENT: Good evening, Bret. Yes, too close to call still in that governor's GOP primary race, but that hasn't stopped the candidate who is endorsed by former President Trump from doing a very Trump thing, declaring victory before all the votes are counted.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KARI LAKE (R), GUBERNATORIAL CANDIDATE OF ARIZONA: We are so proud of the victory we have and we are going to lead this state to its brightest days ahead. I want to reach out to the people who I ran against, they gave us -- they gave it a good fight.\par \par \uc2 ACUNA: Reporters challenged Kari Lake's acceptance like speech. She claims the number of ballots outstanding will land in her favor. Here's where things stand. Kari Lake leads Karrin Taylor Robson by a little more than 11,000 votes. Up and down the GOP ballot. Trump backed or Trump aligned candidates in Arizona won their primaries. Republican strategist Stan Barnes says it represents something bigger than the former president.\par \par \uc2 STAN BARNES, POLITICAL CONSULTANT: Whatever he started, for good or for ill is going to be the thing that moves American politics for the next few election cycles, including this one in Arizona.\par \par \uc2 ACUNA: Former Vice President \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  endorsed Karrin Taylor Robson. Her team tells us they are still waiting for additional results.\par \par \uc2 As folks went to bed last night, things appear to be in her favor. She had a nine point lead thanks to the early voter ballot counts. Lake strategy was to push folks to vote in person on Election Day when those numbers started coming in, the tide turns.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ACUNA (on camera): And Bret, there are still about 150 to 260,000 outstanding ballots here in Maricopa County. It's the most populous jurisdiction in the state, in a little less than four hours, we're expecting some more results, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Numbers in Arizona. Alicia Acuna live in Phoenix. Alicia, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Stocks surged today in the technology led rally. The Dow was up 416, the S&P 500 gained 64, the NASDAQ jumped 319.\par \par \uc2 Up next, Senate Minority Leader \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  joins us here in studio for an exclusive interview on a host of topics.\par \par \uc2 And later, is there an end in sight to the baby formula shortage? We have new information to that.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Joining me now in studio, Senate Minority Leader \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 . Senator, thanks for being here.\par \par \uc2 SEN. MITCH MCCONNELL (R-KY): Glad to be here.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Welcome back.\par \par \uc2 I want to talk first about the reconciliation bill, and where you think things stand. Have you talked to Senator \uc2 Kyrsten Sinema\uc2  from Arizona? She is obviously a linchpin here. Democrats call this the Inflation Reduction Act. Senator Schumer needs every Democratic vote. Where do -- where do things stand right now?\par \par \uc2 MCCONNELL: Well, first, I think all of us were somewhat shocked by Senator Manchin's reversal of positions he's taken as recently as last week against raising taxes. This raises taxes, it increases the burden of taxation on lower income people, significantly.\par \par \uc2 The calling on Inflation Reduction Bill is rather laughable. Independent analysis indicates it actually increases inflation in the next two years and may have an impact over 10 years.\par \par \uc2 And their policies from last year have already produced 40-year high inflation. So, it's a terrible package. It appears as if they're all in line, but one. It will be up to Senator Sinema from Arizona, who's quite independent and determine what the final contours of the bill are.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I asked people to tweet in if they had questions, and Joe tweeted, how did Senator McConnell get played on the reconciliation bill?\par \par \uc2 MCCONNELL: Well --\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Did you have a deal that the passage of the CHIPS Bill would not lead to a bill that this was kind of sprung on you?\par \par \uc2 MCCONNELL: No, reconciliation is something done by one party only. There's nothing we could have done to prevent the Democrats from doing a bill that only they will vote for.\par \par \uc2 So, it's not a question to be played here. What's the storyline here is that Senator Manchin had agreed to something that he had said publicly and privately over the last two weeks, he would never agree to.\par \par \uc2 We're not involved in reconciliation bill. It won't be a single Republican vote for it. There's nothing we could do to deter it other than to criticize it publicly. And that's what we're doing right here.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So, Democrats now are using your repeated sighting of Democrat experts to tout this new bill. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MCCONNELL: Yes. I may just quote \uc2 Larry Summers\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Larry Summers\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Larry Summers\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Don't take it from me. Listen to \uc2 Larry Summers\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 LARRY SUMMERS\uc2 , FORMER UNITED STATES TREASURY SECRETARY: This bill is fighting inflation, and it's got a whole set of collateral benefits as well. But it's fair to call it the Inflation Reduction Act.\par \par \uc2 MCCONNELL: Ask \uc2 Larry Summers\uc2 , ask \uc2 Jason Furman\uc2 ,\par \par \uc2 JASON FURMAN\uc2 , FORMER DIRECTOR, NATIONAL ECONOMIC COUNCIL OF THE UNITED STATES: I think it is quite important for things like climate change, protecting families from premium increases, lowering the cost of prescription drugs. And it does all of that while making progress on inflation.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So, Summers and Furman are for this bill. Who is right here?\par \par \uc2 MCCONNELL: Well, I was -- the quotes they use me or Summers' observations about what they did last year. The $2 trillion, they don't (INAUDIBLE) on the economy, they're both Furman and Summers predicted, would produce rapid, rampant inflation, and it did.\par \par \uc2 What they've done this year is kind of fall in line. And certainly, wanted to get back on the White House Christmas card list. But people who are not active Democrats, Independent observers have said that has no impact on inflation at all, over the long term, and actually increases it slightly in the short term.\par \par \uc2 So, permanent. Summers were certainly helpful as active Democrats and describing last year's bill. But this year, they are sort of falling in line because I think they want to get back into good grace of the White House.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So, what do you say to the House GOP members, specifically? Because this is where it's coming from a lot. Its criticism of you and your leadership on these bipartisan deals, infrastructure, guns, most recently, the CHIPS bill.\par \par \uc2 MCCONNELL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Semiconductors, and they say, you're giving the president of an opposition party major victories that they're now using to prop up him with low approvals.\par \par \uc2 MCCONNELL: Yes. Well, I guess they've forgotten a Supreme Court that I've helped usher into. Three new Supreme Court justices, the 2017 tax bill.\par \par \uc2 And let me answer the criticism. I think just because you have closely divided government doesn't mean you do nothing. The past two administrations tried to achieve bipartisan infrastructure didn't get it done. We needed to rescue the post office, just because there's a Democrat in the White House, I don't think means Republicans should do nothing that's good for the country, in the meantime.\par \par \uc2 On the big issues, we are totally opposed to what this administration tried to do. But on things like school safety, mental health, infrastructure, postal reform, why would we not want to make progress for the country, no matter who's in the White House?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Just moments ago, the Senate passed the \uc2 NATO\uc2  expansion that adds signs off on adding Sweden and Finland.\par \par \uc2 You said, if any senator is looking for a defense to vote, no, I wish them good luck. It's a slam dunk for national security. That's what you said. What do you -- what do you say to an American military family that says sending U.S. troops to defend Sweden or Finland territorial integrity, if they're intact, is not a slam dunk for them?\par \par \uc2 MCCONNELL: Well, look, I would -- I would say, the \uc2 NATO\uc2  alliance is the most successful military alliance in world history. The way to prevent Americans from having to actively involve in combat is to prevent it in the first place. And that's what \uc2 NATO\uc2  is about. It won the Cold War without firing a single shot.\par \par \uc2 Putin has succeeded not only in actually expanding \uc2 NATO\uc2  and making it even more effective as a deterrent.\par \par \uc2 Remember, Reagan said peace through strength. And that's what \uc2 NATO\uc2  is all about. And that's why Finland and Sweden add a lot to \uc2 NATO\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 BAIER: There are Republican campaigns now here in the U.S. around the country focusing on the record setting illegal immigration on the border. To you, right now, what is the bigger national security threat?\par \par \uc2 Russia in Ukraine and what's happening there or the situation on our southern border?\par \par \uc2 MCCONNELL: I don't really get to pick. I think they're both important. I mean, we both need to secure the border, which the previous administration did an excellent job of. And we need to beat the Russians in Ukraine. It's important to do that. The prime minister of Japan concerned about President Xi. And China said you want to send Xi a message? Defeat Putin in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Here is another \uc2 Twitter\uc2  question from Nate, what will send McConnell commit to doing his first 90 days should he become senate majority leader again that he thinks will have a chance of receiving bipartisan support?\par \par \uc2 MCCONNELL: Well, we'll be looking for things that we can do for the country, no matter who's in the White House. But I think you can say to us, if there's a Republican House and Senate next year, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  will finally become the moderate he promised the American people he would be when he ran for president because he would have no choice.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: How do you see the election landscape now? There are some campaigns J.D. Vance in Ohio, \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  in Georgia, Mehmet Oz in Pennsylvania. They're trailing Democrat opponents, some of them pretty significantly in key states that you ticked off the last time we talked, being really important to you.\par \par \uc2 MCCONNELL: Yes. I think it's going to be a very tight. We have a 50-50 Senate now. We have a 50-50 nation. And I think when the Senate race smoke clears, we're likely to have a very, very close Senate still, with either us up slightly, or the Democrats up slightly.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Do you think abortion plays a bigger issue, especially after this Kansas vote last night? POLITICO writes, "Row Jolts the Midterms: The heavily Republican state rejected an anti-abortion constitutional amendment, it marked a political earthquake with the potential to reshape the entire midterm campaign." You buy them?\par \par \uc2 MCCONNELL: Well, I think what the Supreme Court has done is said people who are elected by the American people are going to deal with this highly sensitive issue. And it will be playing out all year. And I don't think we really know until the end of the year, what kind of an impact putting this issue back into the hands of those of us who are elected as opposed to nine unelected judges will have on the country. We're in the process of finding that out.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Do you think Kansas tells you something about that?\par \par \uc2 MCCONNELL: It tells us that there were a lot of people interested in the issue in Kansas, there is no question about that.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Senator, you're losing some of your most trusted lieutenants: Rob Portman, \uc2 Pat Toomey\uc2 , \uc2 Richard Burr\uc2 . You could be looking, as I mentioned, J.D. Vance, Mehmet Oz, \uc2 Ted Budd\uc2 . You have Trump loyal Republicans like \uc2 Eric Schmitt\uc2 , who just won a Missouri. \uc2 Blake Masters\uc2  in Arizona.\par \par \uc2 And some of those candidates have refused to say on the trail that they back you as leader. Is that the new face of a Trump -- of five Republican Party? And are you the guy to lead that in the Senate?\par \par \uc2 MCCONNELL: Yes. Well, look at it this way. I've been elected eight times out of opposition. If I haven't a vital role in this job, and there's always an election every two years for leader, if anybody wants to challenge me, how about it?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: If you don't win the majority, are you still going to be leader?\par \par \uc2 MCCONNELL: I am.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Last thing, is there one area where you look back at the Republican Party of old 80s, 90s, even early 2000, and you say these new guys got this right, and we got it wrong, whether it's trade or China, or the border?\par \par \uc2 Is there something that you look back and say it's different now?\par \par \uc2 MCCONNELL: Well, that's a really interesting question. I think -- I think there is a large part of the country that would like to see no matter what kind of government they elect, some progress. And we've tried to demonstrate that in the last year and a half with some of the issues you and I just talked about.\par \par \uc2 But they also don't like what this administration doing. The president has a horrible approval rating. And they want somebody to stand up to that. And we have on the big issues, whether it's spending, taxes, inflation, crime, open borders, there are total differences.\par \par \uc2 And so, that's why we have elections. And this fall, the American people need to decide, are they happy with what this all Democratic government is giving them? Or do they want to make adjustments?\par \par \uc2 Knowing full well, that Democratic presidents are going to be there for two more years, which is why I said, flip the Congress and \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  will become a moderate, he has no choice.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is a \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  you've known for decades. Is he the same \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 ? And do you think he's running in '24?\par \par \uc2 MCCONNELL: Look, I like Joe. As far as I can tell, he's the same Biden I worked with all those years. I have no idea whether he's running in '24. I'm focused on 22.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Does it help you in '22 if he is?\par \par \uc2 MCCONNELL: I don't think he's going to announce before then. But we're going to have a referendum on his performance and the performance of his Democratic House and Democratic Senate this November. The American people can decide how happy they are with that.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Senator McConnell, always welcome back on SPECIAL REPORT. We appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 MCCONNELL: Thank you, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Up next, a landlord revolt. Why Los Angeles property owners say their homes are being stolen because of Democratic policies there.\par \par \uc2 But before we head to break, legendary Dodgers broadcaster Vin Scully has died.\par \par \uc2 The Hall of Fame announcer passed away last night at his home in Los Angeles. Scully, the longest tenured broadcaster with a single team in pro- sports history. He's with the Dodgers for 67 years. Going back to when the team played in Brooklyn.\par \par \uc2 Vin Scully was 94.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Conspiracy theorist Alex Jones says he now understands it was irresponsible of him to declare the Sandy Hook Elementary School massacre, the shooting there, a hoax, and that he now believes it did actually happen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Do you understand now that it was absolutely irresponsible of you to do that?\par \par \uc2 ALEX JONES, HOST, "INFOWARS": It was, especially since I've met the parents, and it's a 100 percent real.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: The testimony comes a day after the parents of a six-year-old boy who was killed in the 2012 attack testified about the suffering, death threats, and harassment they've endured because of what Jones trumpeted on his media platforms. The "InfoWars" host is taking part in the first of three trials in which juries will decide how much Jones must pay for defaming families.\par \par \uc2 We have new data showing the extent of the ongoing national baby formula shortage. Today the Biden administration announced an additional fly formula mission. But will it be soon enough? And what took so long? FOX Business correspondent Lydia Hu in Nutley, New Jersey, with the latest good evening.\par \par \uc2 LYDIA HU, FOX BUSINESS CORRESPONDENT: Good evening there, Bret. Today, White House Press Secretary \uc2 Karine Jean-Pierre\uc2  acknowledged that the shortage on baby formula is still very much ongoing.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE DEPUTY PRESS SECRETARY: We have done the work and will continue to do the work to make sure that families have safe baby formula so that they can feed their kids. This continues to be a priority for us. This has not gone away.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HU: New data out today shows that all formula types are about 18 percent out of stock. Powdered formula is nearly 28 percent out of stock across the country. Typically, outage rates are between five and seven percent. Some states suffering outages more than others. Alaska with more than 42 percent of supplies out of stock tops the list, followed by Kansas, Utah, Colorado, and Minnesota. And families across the country still struggle.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JILLIAN ARROYO, PARENT: It's been extremely stressful. I'm on a super limited diet to keep our daughter healthy. And we have no end in sight, no idea when we're going to have access to her formula again.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HU: It was back in late April the nation's parents were starting to feel the effects of the months-long closure of \uc2 Abbott Nutrition\uc2 's baby formula manufacturing plant in Sturgis, Michigan, which started in February. The plant reopened in July after severe weather prompted a second closure. Production has resumed on specialty formula EleCare, but is not yet restarted for widely used Similac.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the administration has invoked the Defense Production Act to ramp up domestic manufacturing and has also imported more than 64 million bottles worth of formula. But that is equivalent the amount American consumers purchase in one week, analysts say.\par \par \uc2 Now the \uc2 FDA\uc2  Commissioner Dr. \uc2 Robert Califf\uc2  said it could be another two months before we see supply and demand for baby formula level off. But at one point, Bret, he was predicting the outages and the shortages would be over by the end of July. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Amazing. We'll follow it. Lydia, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Landlords around the country are pushing back against COVID era eviction protections for tenants. Property owners say an eviction moratorium extension could push them into bankruptcy or foreclosure. FOX Business correspondent Kelly O'Grady has details tonight from Los Angeles with this story is really hitting a flash point. Good evening, Kelly.\par \par \uc2 KELLY O'GRADY, FOX BUSINESS CORRESPONDENT: Good evening, Bret. The city council here just voted to extend that eviction moratorium for an additional year. It's a bit of a comic edit situation because rent prices are skyrocketing. But those protections were for COVID, and the city has returned to near normal operations. Many of these landlords are small mom- and-pop owners who fear they will never recoup that money. State fund applications have closed, so rent that does not get paid here simply piles up with little recourse. Owners also worried some tenants see this as another year of free rent.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 FRED SUTTON, CALIFORNIA APARTMENT ASSOCIATION: Those owners will eventually lose those properties or have to sell those properties. If you have someone that's abusing the system, it's not right. And the county and city of Los Angeles at this point are protecting and enabling abuse of the system.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O'GRADY: This is causing some landlords to say that their homes are being stolen by Democratic policies, a claim supported by the one council member to expose that extension. He says, quote, "Policies we put in place to protect people are now affecting rental owners that have not been able to run their businesses as usual, properties they have saved all their lives as in come to support themselves."\par \par \uc2 Last summer, the Supreme Court struck down the CDC moratorium on evictions, but the White House argues it has, quote, a duty to families everywhere. At yesterday's summit on eviction prevention they doubled down on making the COVID predictions permanent.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GENE SPERLING, SENIOR WHITE HOUSE ADVISER: On eviction prevention, our goal must be to not return to pre-pandemic normal, to not return to policies where evictions are too often a first resort, not a last resort.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O'GRADY: And as the White House weighs things like student loan forgiveness, critics are saying, is rent next? And what happens to those small business landlords as these protections continue? Back to you, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Good questions. Kelly, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Up next, while House Speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  was in Taiwan, her husband had a court date. We'll take you there.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: The first grain shipped to leave Ukraine under a Black Sea wartime deal pass inspection in Istanbul and is heading to Lebanon. Ukraine says 17 other vessels at its ports are loaded up with grain waiting for permission to leave. No word yet on when they could depart.\par \par \uc2 World food prices, as we've been saying, have been soaring as the war has brought exports from Ukraine, a major global grain supplier.\par \par \uc2 As House Speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  travels in Asia, husband Paul was in court and is pleading not guilty to misdemeanor driving under the influence charges related to a May car crash in northern California. But court documents reveal stunning new details as new questions arise over whether he should face stiffer charges. Senior correspondent Claudia Cowan joins us now from Napa. Good evening, Claudia.\par \par \uc2 CLAUDIA COWAN, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Good evening to you, Bret. Paul Pelosi did not appear in person. His lawyer entered not guilty pleas and then left in a hurry, dodging questions about her client's behavior that night. According to documents, Pelosi's breath reeked, and he flunked a sobriety test after he allegedly drove his \uc2 Porsche\uc2  into the path of an SUV, injuring that driver and totaling both cars. Highway Patrol officers noted Pelosi's eyes were red and watery. He was unsteady on his feet, and his speech was slurred.\par \par \uc2 When they asked for his I.D., the 82-year-old handed over his license along with an 11-99 Foundation membership card. That's a charitable organization that supports police and their families. If he was hoping that would get him special treatment, it didn't work. They arrested Pelosi and locked him up for a few hours at the \uc2 Napa County\uc2  jail. Here's his mugshot. And he was charged with two misdemeanor counts of drunk driving causing injury. His blood alcohol level was just above the legal limit, but it was taken two hours after the crash, time for Pelosi to sober up. But a licensed phlebotomist was needed to come to the station late on a Saturday night. Legal analysts say that may account for the delay.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Napa County\uc2  district attorney says if the 48-year-old SUV driver had broken bones or suffered more serious injuries, the charges here would've been felonies. \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2 , who was in Rhode Island at the time had no comment on her husband's arrest, which was filmed by Highway Patrol officers. Police release dashcam video all the time, but despite repeated requests from FOX News, the video of Pelosi's arrest is still under wraps.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ALLISON HALEY, NAPA COUNTY, CALIFORNIA, DISTRICT ATTORNEY: Dashcam video is evidence in this case, and I'm ethically prohibited from releasing that. I don't release these items in any case. I won't release them in this case before I'm ready to do so.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 COWAN: Everyone is back here on August 23rd. The two sides may reach a settlement or set a date for an injury to trial. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Claudia Cowan in Napa. Claudia, thank you. Something tells me Jesse Watters will have more on this story coming up.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the panel with reaction to my interview with Senator \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  and a look back at last night's primary results and what they mean.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 MITCH MCCONNELL\uc2 , (R-KY) SENATE MINORITY LEADER: I think it's going to be very tight. We have a 50/50 Senate now. We have a 50/50 nation. And I think when this Senate race smoke clears, we're likely to have a very, very close Senate still with either of us up slightly or the Democrats up slightly.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: There you have it, Senator McConnell talking about the landscape, not really painting it like a big red wave anymore.\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in our panel, "\uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " columnist Marc Thiessen, Juan Williams is a FOX News analyst, and Josh Kraushaar, "Axios" senior political correspondent. Is that what you took from that Josh, kind of looking at the landscape in some of these races that maybe they're underwater on where they didn't think they were going to be?\par \par \uc2 JOSH KRAUSHAAR, "NATIONAL JOURNAL": The big headline is that McConnell is leaving the option that Democrats might actually hold their Senate majority. There is a really lowering of expectations, specifically on the Senate side, because \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 , a whole lot of Republican officials are worried about the caliber of these Senate candidates, Dr. Oz in Pennsylvania, \uc2 Blake Masters\uc2  last night winning the nomination in Arizona, \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  in Georgia. These are battleground states that Republicans should win given the political environment is so favorable. But boy, if you lose these winnable races, all of a sudden, the majority looks a lot more precarious.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I asked Senator McConnell about the changing face of the Republicans in the Senate, and looking at these candidates, they are not the Rob Portmans of old. They are Trumpian. They are Trump loyalist, a lot of them. And does that change the way that Republicans Senate operates?\par \par \uc2 JUAN WILLIAMS, FOX NEWS POLITICAL ANALYST: It certainly will. I think that's why you saw Senator McConnell make a big deal out getting \uc2 John Thune\uc2 , who is one of his leadership team, to run again. The fear there is that you get people like \uc2 Rick Scott\uc2 , the senator from Florida, to move into leadership if the leadership around him erodes. And the leadership around him are people in that model that you just referred to, Bret, people who are more of the establishment Republican mold.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: The Roy Blunts.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Thoughts?\par \par \uc2 MARC THIESSEN, COLUMNIST, "WASHINGTON POST": It's feeling a lot like 2010 again. The win is at the Republicans back, and they're picking candidates that could -- we haven't picked a witch yet to run.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 THIESSEN: But you mentioned Rob Portman. J.D. Vance is leading \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2  by three points in Ohio. Rob Portman won that seat by 20 points six years ago. That's a big difference.\par \par \uc2 And this is also, it's a real risk for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , because a lot of these candidates are people that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  pushed, and people listen to him. And if Republicans lose the Senate second time because of Trump selected candidates, that's not going to be good for him going into 2024.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So I also mentioned that the GOP House criticism is that McConnell is bending over backwards on these bar bipartisan deals and leaving them in the lurch as they are running for reelection, giving President Biden these wins in their words. I'm talking about the House GOP members. Here's how he answered some of them.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 MITCH MCCONNELL\uc2 , (R-KY) SENATE MINORITY LEADER: On the big issues, we are totally opposed to what this administration is trying to do. But on things like school safety, mental health, infrastructure, postal reform, why would we not want to make progress for the country no matter who is in the White House?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Josh, what about that whole exchange there?\par \par \uc2 KRAUSHAAR: \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  has always believed that when something is politically popular, when there's an opportunity to score some points by going bipartisan, it's a good thing to do. I think there are worries right now about divisions within the Republican Party. There's a lot of grumblings about McConnell's leadership, the semiconductor bill passing and now the reconciliation bill going through on a partisan basis. But I think Republicans need to show that they can get things done if they want take back the Senate majority, I think McConnell understands the politics of the situation well.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Juan, he insists he wasn't played. He says this is a Democrat deal with this reconciliation. It's their applecart to deal with if they have got all the votes to do it. What about that exchange and how they went down?\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: To me, what is striking here is that Senator McConnell is someone who famously said I'm here to prevent \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  from having a second term. And I think in the White House the Biden team sees him as antagonistic to their agenda. It's just that the Biden team has now really racked up a substantial number of wins. I don't think it's helping them in their approval ratings. It doesn't seem to be getting through as a message, a narrative to the American people that Biden's first two years have been fairly successful as a matter of a scorecard of legislative achievements.\par \par \uc2 And I think much of that has to do with McConnell. And I think McConnell has been a fierce fighter. Now that's my perspective. I understand the perspective that you represent in that question, Bret, which is that some of the House members say we want to stop everything. But I think that, again, this goes back to the first question you asked me. McConnell wants to prevent a Republican party that is a constructive party. I do think he wants to blow everything up. Yes, he doesn't like Obama, Biden, he doesn't like the Democrats, all that. But he wants the moderate Republican voter out there to feel comfortable with him and with his leadership.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes, and that's why I wanted to ask it that way from that perspective. But when you ask him specifically what he's going to get done on a bipartisan way if he is the leader in the first 90 days, it's tough to pin him down on the legislative priorities.\par \par \uc2 THIESSEN: Sure, it is. But keep in mind that McConnell is playing 3-D chess when a lot of people are playing 2-D chess here. So for example, Biden, the first thing he does, passes a $1.9 trillion COVID bill, social spending disguised as COVID relief, sets off the worst inflation in 40 years. He wanted to follow that up with a $3 trillion Build Back Better, and he called it infrastructure. He was trying to pass all the social spending disguised as infrastructure.\par \par \uc2 What McConnell and the Republicans did is they took all the infrastructure that people really liked and put it in a separate bipartisan bill which left the Democrats with nothing but a big, unpopular social spending bill. If they hadn't done that, they would've had a $3 trillion monstrosity, possibly, to follow this.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I want to go back to last night. This is the "Cook Political Report," saying "Election results raise more questions than answers. The biggest story of the August second election didn't involve any candidates but could have significant implications for the upcoming November elections. In Kansas, the resounding rejection of a constitutional amendment that would have allowed the Kansas legislature to further restrict abortion access suggests the motivating power of the abortion issue for this fall." Is that fair to say that that is going to have repercussions, Josh, in other places, or are we undercounting the abortion issue as motivating?\par \par \uc2 KRAUSHAAR: I think the big story last night in Kansas was the turnout, Democrats have been disillusioned, dispirited, upset about the Biden White House. They showed up on abortion. So I don't think this is going to dramatically change the midterm environment, but it certainly might help the Democratic base show up in some of these swing elections.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I've got 10 seconds, Marc.\par \par \uc2 THIESSEN: I think that most people, this is probably the best time that Dobbs could have happened, because with inflation and everything else going on, there a lot of things that people are more upset about than abortion right now.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And that question on that ballot was quite something. OK, guys, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Finally tonight, a SPECIAL REPORT salute. Police in St. Pete Beach, Florida, responded to an unusual call for help. A sergeant and two deputies dispatched to a hotel found sea turtle hatchlings all over the place, including in the pool, the bathroom, even the storm drain. Eventually the officers scooped up those turtles and guided them safely to the sea. Never done.\par \par \uc2 Tomorrow on SPECIAL REPORT, \uc2 FBI\uc2  Director \uc2 Chris Wray\uc2  testifies before the \uc2 Senate Judiciary Committee\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Thanks for inviting us into your home tonight. That's it for this SPECIAL REPORT, fair, balanced, and unafraid. "JESSE WATTERS PRIMETIME" is next. And I teased that you're going to have more on this Pelosi story. I assume that I'm right.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FNSPBB0020220804ei8300001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Primary Night In 5 States; Manchin Tax Bill Will Double The Size Of The IRS; Pelosi Visits Taiwan As China Issues More Threats\b0\par\par\uc2 Sean Hannity, Bret Baier, Ari Fleischer, Lara Trump \par \uc2 8,165 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 August 2022\par \uc2 Fox News: Hannity\par \uc2 SHAN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 SEAN HANNITY\uc2 , FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Tucker, I know the difference between Kid and Chris Rock, just in case any of your audience thought otherwise.\par \par \uc2 TUCKER CARLSON\uc2 , FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Which do you prefer?\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: You're getting ready -- I'm like, oh, Tucker, hi, one of those moments.\par \par \uc2 Anyway, thank you, Tucker.\par \par \uc2 And welcome to HANNITY.\par \par \uc2 All right. Brace yourself and prepare for an audit because West Virginia Democratic Senator Joe Manchin is now attempting to double the size of our \uc2 IRS\uc2 , more than double actually. More on Manchin's hard left turn. That's coming up tonight.\par \par \uc2 Also, Georgia Senate candidate \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  will join us. He will respond to nothing but vile, racist attacks against him, guess what? From the woke media mob at NBC.\par \par \uc2 Later, we have a full report on China's petulant saber-rattling during Speaker Pelosi's trip to our ally, Taiwan.\par \par \uc2 And back here at home, we are learning shocking details about Speaker Pelosi's husband, Paul Pelosi, his recent arrest. It wasn't just drinking. Apparently, drugs were involved. We have the details coming up.\par \par \uc2 But, first, breaking right now, it is 9:00 on the East Coast and 6:00 on the West Coast. That means more polls are closed in Arizona, Michigan, Kansas, and Missouri, and voting still underway in the state of Washington.\par \par \uc2 Here with at the very latest at the big board himself is our own Bret Baier.\par \par \uc2 Bret, a lot of big primaries tonight.\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Yeah, five of them. You got Arizona, Kansas, Michigan, Missouri, and Washington, Sean.\par \par \uc2 A lot of action happening around the country. Let's start in the governor's races. Michigan, that Republican primary to take on \uc2 Gretchen Whitmer\uc2 , the Democrat incumbent who's looking for a second term. As you go in here, those polls have just fully closed and you're not going to see a lot here coming in fast. You'll get the early mail-in vote.\par \par \uc2 But Tudor Dixon who is former President Trump's endorsement, he endorsed her Friday, is in the early lead and she has a couple of counties here that look really good for her versus her competition. For example, down here in Oakland County, she is really racking up the votes and this is a place where Rinke should be doing well, known very well around Detroit. So this is kind of a harbinger maybe of what the state is starting to look like, but it's again early in all of these races as the polls are just closed.\par \par \uc2 A big Senate contest tonight, Missouri, and this is just getting numbers in again, super early, but remember that the former president endorsed Eric in this race. He endorsed Eric, but there are two Erics, \uc2 Eric Schmitt\uc2  and \uc2 Eric Greitens\uc2 . Vicki Hartzler is not named Eric. She's also in the race.\par \par \uc2 But looking at some of these numbers in the different counties, a lot of experts who know Missouri really well say \uc2 Eric Schmitt\uc2  is poised to have a good night tonight. We will follow these numbers as they come in.\par \par \uc2 As you mentioned there are just a lot of interesting races especially in Arizona on the governor's side and the Senate side, again trying to take on the incumbent Mark Kelly in Arizona those numbers even though the polls are going to close probably not going to get them until about 11:00 p.m. Eastern Time. We always want to make sure that we have all the numbers from Arizona, Sean.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: All right. Bret Baier, at the big board tonight, thank you. We will check back in momentarily.\par \par \uc2 We'll keep you updated as results continue to pour in tonight.\par \par \uc2 But, first, tonight, Democrats are once again poised to spend a massive huge monumental amount of your money and as of now, one senator stands in their way. The last Democrat with a backbone, is it going to be \uc2 Kyrsten Sinema\uc2 ? Time will tell.\par \par \uc2 Now, if she does ultimately cave, the Schumer-Manchin $500 billion green new deal energy 