ave coordinated with the White House, the Pentagon, they should have had a common front. And they said, we're going to go, what are you going to do about that? I have no problem with that.\par \par \uc2 But this waffling and then arguing and yes, you should, yes, you -- all it did was radiate weakness.\par \par \uc2 And the question is, why does China think that they have such a prerogative to tell us that the third ranking person in the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  in a sense can't and cannot go places? And the answer is in 18 months, we've destroyed U.S. deterrence. There was the humiliation in Afghanistan. You had the chairman of the joint chiefs calling up his Chinese counterpart, basically to imply that his president was unstable and he would I guess trust the Chinese PLA leader more than he would his own command.\par \par \uc2 And then you had the hesitation with Ukraine where we offered to fly out Zelenskyy and you mentioned all of the energy problems.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Let me ask you this --\par \par \uc2 HASON: \uc2 U.S. Army\uc2  has only made -- China's only met 40 percent of its recruitment. Forty percent of its recruitment is turning into a social justice institution. The Chinese know that better than we do and they're -- they've surmised, they thought these people talk loud and they carry a twig and we're going to take advantage of it.\par \par \uc2 It's time we call them -- you know, we're going to have to build up our defenses, especially missile defense, and we're going to have to be symmetric with China. If they want 38 -- if we have 2,500 students in China, why do they have 380,000 in the United States? Why --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: There's o rationale.\par \par \uc2 HANSON: -- in the United States anywhere you want.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Let me ask you an exit question.\par \par \uc2 HANSON: There's no symmetry. We got to insist on symmetry. We don't want to be -- we don't want to be pugnacious. We just say, we want to get along with you but we have to treat each other equitably and you're not doing that and we're going to insist that you do it.\par \par \uc2 But before we do that, Sean, we've got to get our house economically and militarily in order. We've got to get deterrence back. We've got to beef up the military. We've got to get a new -- we've got to get a new defense secretary. To be candid, we need a new joint chiefs, we need a whole new attitude in the military that is for deterrence and battlefield efficacy rather than these extraneous issues that radiate to the Chinese that we don't -- we're not serious about defending our allies in our own interests.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: I only have about 30 seconds. Do you believe as I do that all these hostile regimes had a genuine fear of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and they don't have any fear of \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 HANSON: Well, the record says that they went into Ukraine and they went into Crimea and they went into Abkhazia and they went in again into Ukraine and they didn't do anything from 2017 to '20 because they thought \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was unpredictable. And in high stakes strategic poker, you -- unpredictability with a strong defense, it creates deterrence. And that's what -- that's why they didn't they weren't adventurous.\par \par \uc2 And now, they feel that Biden because he's weak and the military has eroded, that he's going to make that up with this braggadocio talk and they don't -- they don't -- they're not impressed. And that's tragic --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Victor Davis Hanson --\par \par \uc2 HANSON: -- has restored deterrence.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: By the way, he's got a great course on CS Lewis at seanforhillsdale.com. It's an advertiser on my radio show. The course is phenomenal, if you like CS Lewis like I do.\par \par \uc2 All right. Straight ahead tonight, Texas Governor Greg Abbott has invited New York, D.C. -- I'm sorry, New York Mayor \uc2 Eric Adams\uc2 , D.C. Mayor Muriel Bowser to witness the crisis firsthand at the border after complaints about migrants flooding their cities. Why won't they accept the invitation of Governor Abbott? He'll join us next.\par \par \uc2 And an update on tonight's key primary races, straight ahead.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: All right. It is primary night in multiple states, including high-profile races in Arizona. Tonight, results are pouring in.\par \par \uc2 Here with the very latest -- I don't know if we call it, the Hemmer board, the big board, the Hemmer big board, the Baer big board, the HANNITY big board, I don't know what we call this thing anymore. I guess tonight, it's the Baer big board.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: The billboard, I'm just borrowing. It's the Bret board tonight, but we've got some numbers coming in.\par \par \uc2 And first of all, Sean, I want to update you on that Michigan governor's primary, remember, Tudor Dixon was former President Trump's endorsement there, she is leading big time, and if you look at the state, it looks like this is her night. She will likely win this primary. It hasn't been called yet, but you look at some place like Ottawa county, almost 70 percent of the vote there. She is leading by 10 points and there are counties like this where you're getting a lot of vote and this could be something that gets called pretty quickly.\par \par \uc2 Another race where we're seeing kind of a standout right now is in Missouri, that primary. Remember, the two Erics were endorsed by former President Trump, \uc2 Eric Greitens\uc2  and \uc2 Eric Schmitt\uc2 . This looks like an \uc2 Eric Schmitt\uc2  runaway right now.\par \par \uc2 Look at all that yellow on the board. You look at some place like Buchanan County out west, in Missouri, and there's -- you know, 27-point lead, about 85 percent of the vote in, in that county, it looks like \uc2 Eric Schmitt\uc2  could be the winner there.\par \par \uc2 Let's take a look at a couple of other things. There are some interesting House races around the five states where there are primaries tonight. And one of the interesting dynamics is that there are three incumbent Republicans who voted for President Trump's impeachment who are up facing stiff primary challenges.\par \par \uc2 Two in Washington, Jamie Herrera Beutler versus Joe Kent, Dan Newhouse, the incumbent, the endorsement for former President Trump is Lauren Culp there. And then in Michigan, you have Peter Meijer who's the incumbent congressman facing John Gibbs also endorsed by former President Trump.\par \par \uc2 I think we can get some numbers on that Michigan race if we check in here - - yes, again, it's early. They're eight percent in, but you have -- you know, roughly votes separating the two with Gibbs an early lead.\par \par \uc2 One more thing I want to just show you real quick here and that is -- this is not a race that is a congressman, senator, governor. This is a ballot measure in the state of Kansas and has to do with abortion, whether the state should do away with abortion rights and the constitution of the state.\par \par \uc2 Right now, it is no. No gets 63 percent of the vote and yes is at this point 37 percent. You've got about 30 percent of the vote in. That's a big vote about abortion. They could have repercussions other places but Kansas as you know pretty red, starting to get numbers Arizona's really early, we're probably not going to get numbers from there until about 11:00 p.m.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Let me ask you, on that yes vote, wouldn't that mean that the state legislature then, it would be empowered to make the law or restrictions or whatever law they wanted on that level if in fact it was yes?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: The way this ballot measure is written is should the state do away with abortion rights. That's in the constitution in Kansas. And the no is no, they should not. Now, all of this in the wake of the Roe v. Wade ruling is interesting because each of these battles is going to be fought in each one of these states.\par \par \uc2 The state legislature could do whatever it wants, but it put it to a ballot measure in the state of Kansas right now, and it's still pretty early, only 30 percent in. But the no, don't amend the Constitution to take out abortion rights in the Kansas constitution is leading 63-37.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: All right. Bret Baier at the big board tonight. Bret, thank you.\par \par \uc2 All right. Now with Washington, D.C., New York City getting just a small little glimpse into the Biden border crisis, Texas Governor Greg Abbott, he's now invited Mayor Muriel Bowser and Mayor \uc2 Eric Adams\uc2  from New York, down to south Texas, to get a firsthand look at the worsening humanitarian disaster that he's been dealing with that's been unfolding because of Biden's open borders catastrophe.\par \par \uc2 Now, Abbott wrote in a letter, quote, as governor, I invite you to visit our border region to see firsthand the dire situation that only grows more urgent with each passing day and to meet with local officials who like yourselves realize this matter -- this matter deserves immediate federal action. Unfortunately, so far, the mayor seems -- both of them seem less than enthused.\par \par \uc2 A spokesperson for Mayor Adams responding, quote, instead of a photo-op at the border, we hope Governor Abbott will focus his energy and resources on providing support and resources to asylum seekers in Texas as we have been hard at work doing in New York City.\par \par \uc2 Okay, the only problem is they're dealing with the problem on a level a thousand times bigger.\par \par \uc2 Here to explain more, Texas Governor Greg Abbott.\par \par \uc2 This, you know, it's funny. When Muriel Bowser said she wanted the National Guard called up because you rightly said, okay, you -- if \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  wants illegal immigrants and he's going to aid and abet in the law breaking, he can take care of the people that he bring -- allows into the country illegally. And so, you're sending them to Washington, D.C.\par \par \uc2 I want every red state governor to follow your lead and when planes drop off illegal immigrants, put them in buses and send them to 1600 Pennsylvania Avenue. I think that that -- Joe's problem, he caused it, he aided it, why not handle it? I think everyone should do it.\par \par \uc2 GOV. GREG ABBOTT (R), TEXAS: Exactly right, and believe me, we have more buses ahead of their way as we speak right now. But, Sean, this just shows the hypocrisy of these liberal leaders up in the Northeast who think -- well, that border cross is created by \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , that's fine as long as it's Texas that has to deal with it.\par \par \uc2 But as soon as they have to deal with the real consequences of Biden's border caused crisis, they're up in arms calling for the National Guard as you point out, dealing with just a tiny fraction of what we have to deal with every single day. We're going to keep sending those buses up there until they fully understand.\par \par \uc2 And most importantly, Sean, until the Biden administration does its job to enforce the laws concerning the border.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Right. So you got their attention and I hope that everybody follows suit because honestly, you've been dealing with this in massive record numbers every single month. And it's -- it literally has overwhelmed your state and at this point, if \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  continues not to enforce the laws of the land, I don't think you really have any choice because I don't think the state of Texas ought to bear the brunt of his bad policies and I think I -- I think that was the right move.\par \par \uc2 Now, are you getting more and more buses? Are you going to send more and more people?\par \par \uc2 ABBOTT: So, because of the increased influx of people coming into our state and because what the border patrol is doing, they're just processing the paperwork of these people coming here illegally and then dumping off these immigrants into our small local communities, we're helping out these small local communities by sending even more buses to these communities and in putting these new migrants on the buses and sending them to Washington, D.C., and we're looking at other cities to send them to.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Well, New York sounds like a good one. I don't -- I try and stay as far away from the city. I get in the car, I go in a building, I get back in the car and I go home. That's it.\par \par \uc2 Governor, great job as always and we're going to follow this really closely. Also your reelection is pivotal, I know you're not going to have a hard race but with that said, I never take any election for granted and you've done a great job for the people of Texas. Governor, thank you.\par \par \uc2 All right. Coming up a guest on MSDNC makes a vile hateful remark against \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 . We're going to play the tape. Herschel will respond. That's next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: All right. \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  continues to rise in the polls in Georgia, that's happening every day, and that is prompting rage from the media mob including this pretty disgusting smear from this commentator on MSDNC, Elie Mystal. He's made a lot of incendiary remarks in the past. Take a look.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ELIE MYSTAL, THE NATION: Now, you ask why are Republicans backing this man who's so clearly unintelligent, who so clearly doesn't have independent thoughts? But that's actually the reason. Walker's going to do what he's told and that's what Republicans like. That's what Republicans want from their Negroes, to do what they're told.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Here with reaction is Georgia Senate candidate \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 You know, I noticed the race has gotten vicious. I noticed the media in Georgia has turned on you completely. They're in the tank for \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2 . The polls show it's still very close, and I'm predicting that you're going to be rising more and more every day because the campaign's just getting geared up.\par \par \uc2 What's your reaction to this idiot on NBC?\par \par \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2  (R), GEORGIA SENATE CANDIDATE: Well, you know, I think MSNBC and this gentleman here, and I said gentlemen, they're upset because \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  and the left crazies have spent over $25 million against me and the race is still even. So, right now, they're a little upset because they don't realize that I'm going to win this race, so they're trying to do whatever they can to call me names, to get me off track.\par \par \uc2 But this country is not a bad place. This is a great country. They want you to believe that this is a racist place. That's why I'm encouraging everyone to go to teamrehearsal.com and let's get people like this, \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2 , and his left crazy out of office and put people in that want to bring people together because this is called the United States of America.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: You know, look, question that the people of Georgia will face in this race are very simple. If you're happy with record high inflation, record high gas prices, open borders, if you're happy with the pull out in Afghanistan, the war in Europe, if you're happy that our president is getting lectured and threatened by the president of China, then vote for \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  because he is a reliable Schumer, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  vote. That's what the people of Georgia need to decide. Or do they want a different path which you're offering?\par \par \uc2 Now, will you debate \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 WARNOCK: Well, I'm going to open this up on your show right now. I have accepted a debate in Savannah, Georgia, in his backyard, that we can debate October the 14th in front of a crowd that it'll be his people because we're in his backyard and I -- if this debate is going to be about the people, it's not about some political party. It's not about the press.\par \par \uc2 But the people need to see the differences between Senator Warnock and \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 . And what I mean by that, they're going to see that, as you just said, Georgia don't want high gas prices. Well, he voted against the pipeline. Georgia want to protect our men and women in blue. Well, he called them names, he called them thugs.\par \par \uc2 Georgia don't want men and women sports where he voted to put men in women's sports, where it is time that people see the difference.\par \par \uc2 So in October the 14th, I want Senator Warnock to be ready because I've accepted a debate then now he can quit talking and show the people that he's going to stand behind his words and show up for the debate.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: What is his percentage of the vote? How many -- what percentage of the time is he voting with Schumer and Biden? Do you know?\par \par \uc2 WALKER: Well, this is what's going to be interesting. Right now, he's trying to walk away from Biden but he's voted with him 96 percent of the time. So you can't walk away from that.\par \par \uc2 You cannot walk away that you voted with him 96 percent of the time and now you come around trying to pretend you're trying to help Georgia. All you ever done, you voted against Georgia, you voted with \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  96 percent of the time. Now, you got to stand behind that.\par \par \uc2 And I even invited him to do a discussion, a Lincoln-Douglas discussion on why he said he's voted with Biden for 96 percent of the time. He said no there as well. So, it is time for \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  to get back into Georgia, quit being with the Washington elite in Washington and prove what he believes.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Yeah. All right. We're going to be watching. Herschel, I hope you debate him a lot of times.\par \par \uc2 WALKER: Tell everybody, go to TeamHerschel.com.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: All right, TeamHerschel.com, you got it.\par \par \uc2 WALKER: Tell them to go to TeamHerschel.com, let's get him out of this seat.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: All right. We appreciate you being with us, \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Now we have a lot coming up in the program. Apparently, we have a call -- if they put it up, apparently breaking. Tudor Dixon won the Michigan primary which we had predicted a little earlier in the program. So that result just came in. That was President Trump's endorsed candidate.\par \par \uc2 Straight ahead, more HANNITY coming up.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: All right. Unfortunately, that's all the time we have left this evening. Thank you for making this show possible.\par \par \uc2 Let not your heart be troubled. She is back after a long weekend. Laura Ingraham, how was your weekend?\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document SHAN000020220803ei8200001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 China Military Surrounds Taiwan For Exercises; Xi Is Weakened Which Could Spell Danger; The Last Week Has Exposed Our Untenable Position; Trump Endorsements Tested In Key Primary Races; \uc2 Eric Schmitt\uc2  Wins GOP Nomination For \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2 ; Minneapolis PD's 3rd Precinct Is Still Abandoned; Radical Leadership Leaves Minneapolis In Shambles; Retired Minneapolis Police Officer And Minneapolis Business Owners Discuss Effects Of Violence On Minneapolis Community; Tudor Dixon Wins Republican Gubernatorial Primary In Michigan And Will Face Democratic Incumbent \uc2 Gretchen Whitmer\uc2 ; Some Democratic Primary Candidates Endorsed By \uc2 Bernie Sanders\uc2  Losing Primaries\b0\par\par\uc2 Laura Ingraham, Bret Baier \par \uc2 8,432 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 August 2022\par \uc2 Fox News: The Ingraham Angle\par \uc2 INGANG\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 SEAN HANNITY\uc2 , FOX NEWS HOST: All right. Unfortunately, that's all the time we have left this evening. Thank you for making this show possible. Let not your heart be troubled. She is back after a long weekend. Laura Ingraham, how was your weekend?\par \par \uc2 LAURA INGRAHAM, FOX NEWS HOST: It was nice. I actually enjoyed a couple of days off, Hannity. It was a lot of fun.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Awesome.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Boy, we have a big news night tonight, don't we?\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Big news night tonight. We got elections going on. I know you got it covered wall to wall and I'll be watching every minute.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: All right. Thanks so much, Sean.\par \par \uc2 I am Laura Ingraham. This is THE INGRAHAM ANGLE on a supercharged Tuesday night. It's 10 a.m. in the morning in Taiwan and Speaker Pelosi just met with the Taiwanese president as China levels, more threats and conducts military exercises nearby.\par \par \uc2 And here stateside, a number of critical Republican primaries have the attention of Washington and Bedminster. It looks like we already have winners, if you can believe it or not in Missouri and in Michigan. And the polls have just closed in Arizona.\par \par \uc2 Now this hour is going to be a high wire act as we bring you all of these breaking stories. But we begin tonight with threats and truth. That is the focus of tonight's 'Angle'.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 QIN GANG, CHINESE AMBASSADOR TO U.S.: Five years ago, Speaker Gingrich visited Taiwan. It was completely wrong. The U.S. side should draw lessons from it instead of making repeated mistakes.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: It's now, well, let me just tell you one thing. It's 10 a.m. in Taiwan, and to her credit, \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  didn't back down when China essentially threatened to shoot her plane out of the sky.\par \par \uc2 Now she's been on the ground for, what, about 12 hours or so landing here just after 10:30 a.m. Eastern today. We're going to bring you news about the specifics of her meetings at any moment.\par \par \uc2 Now, remember, trip details have been largely kept under wraps due to the security concerns. But by going ahead with this trip, \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  is defying the White House and ignoring the threats made by Chinese Communist Party leadership.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GANG: China's sovereignty cannot be infringed and the Chinese people cannot be humiliated. And the reunification of China cannot be stopped. The PLA will not stay idle.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Now, think about all those American companies, like \uc2 Nike\uc2  and all the hedge funders like Larry Fink, the billions they make in China. It's a country that considers a brief visit by \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  with a longtime ally, like Taiwan, a humiliation. And then it uses it as a pretext to activate its military.\par \par \uc2 Now, Speaker Pelosi has demonstrated that supporting the CCP is inconsistent with modern day liberalism. And when that happened, most in the establishment, they didn't get angry with China. They got angry with Pelosi.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RICHARD HAASS\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE COUNCIL ON FOREIGN RELATIONS: There was no immediate reason why the speaker had to go to Taiwan, because there's no change in the fundamentals of U.S. policy.\par \par \uc2 THOMAS FRIEDMAN, NEW YORK TIMES COLUMNIST: Right now is the time to have good relations with China, open dialogue and not doing anything to provoke them. I wouldn't be poking the bear right now.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Don't poke the bear. Well, I was heartened though to see that \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  and some of the more establishment-type Republicans stood up to the PRC.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. MITCH MCCONNELL (R-KY): I think it's important that the speaker did go to Taiwan. I don't think the Chinese get to tell members of Congress where they can go.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Now, this moment has forced the establishment to acknowledge some very unpleasant truths about China that they've tried to keep hidden for years. President Xi is an evil dictator who doesn't want to just crush Taiwan just as he crushed Hong Kong. But ultimately, he wants to dominate us as well.\par \par \uc2 Yet the Biden administration, they still refuse to grasp or respond to this threat. Now, this just isn't the opinion of the 'Angle'. Listen to Obama's former ambassador to China.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MAX BAUCUS, FORMER U.S. AMBASSADOR TO CHINA: Poor \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , he looks weak because he either told her not to go. And looks weaker than Chinese guys, always weak because he told her not to go and she went anyway. And that - it just look a little bit weak to the Chinese.\par \par \uc2 Chinese understand strength better than do people in any other country and they could smell weakness 100 miles away.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: And weakness is what they're smelling here. For the week leading up to Pelosi landing in Taiwan, the Pentagon and the White House were panicking and waving her off.\par \par \uc2 The fact is, they all know we aren't ready for a conflict with China. They just didn't want us to know that. But now we do despite our $800 billion per year in military spending. We're just not prepared for President Xi's challenge.\par \par \uc2 Now, thanks to China's admission into the \uc2 World Trade Organization\uc2  21 years ago and our idiotic trade policy pre Trump, China now has a larger standing army than we have. Marinate on that for a second.\par \par \uc2 So bottom line, all the establishments coddling of the CCP has empowered them and weakened us. And now, we find ourselves more vulnerable militarily and economically. And all these supply chain issues we're dealing with expose how stupid and how reckless our establishment was.\par \par \uc2 But now is not the time to cry over all the spilled milk. If you think we're too dependent on China for key supplies, then it's time for us to build new supply chains. If you think we need an army that hasn't been hollowed out by wokeness then it's time to reform the military.\par \par \uc2 If you think that the Chinese are prepared to sacrifice for their country, and yet we Americans are not prepared to do so for our own, let's start about how - think about how we can start teaching young people to love America instead of hating her. But don't keep pretending that we can give in to President Xi's demand over and over again without those demands getting worse.\par \par \uc2 Today, he's telling us who can visit Taiwan. Tomorrow, he may be telling us what we can say about China even at home.\par \par \uc2 Now this has already happened in the film industry, hasn't it? The future of America belongs to the political movement that best recognizes the danger of President Xi, and figures out the best strategy to keep us all living from China's vise grip.\par \par \uc2 And by the way, don't for a moment fall in with any of the cynics who say, Oh, we can't untangle from China at this point. We're too enmeshed with China. They're wrong. We have a system that delivers the highest standard of living in the world to over 320 million people of all different backgrounds and ethnicities.\par \par \uc2 They have a giant prison camp. We're talking about, I don't know, Winnie the Pooh can get you sent away for years. Yes, we've been sloppy. And yes, we've made mistakes. But instead of whining about how hopeless our position is, the time has come for all of us to live in the truth about what we're facing.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHRISTOPHER WRAY, FBI DIRECTOR: There is just no country that presents a broader threat to our ideas, our innovation, and our economic security than China.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Finally, that guy's right about something. And, of course, Xi makes mistakes as well. The more he issues threats, the better America is going to look. If you live in Korea, or Japan, or India, or Australia, or New Zealand, you should be thinking, Oh, oh, we could be next.\par \par \uc2 Unless that is, the Americans are strong enough to hold them off. So this isn't a day for hand ringing. It's not even a day for recriminations against all those politicians. You know who you are, by the way, from both parties who bollocks the whole China situation.\par \par \uc2 Today is a day to come to terms with the fact that President Xi and the CCP are the single most dangerous threats to us and the world. And that it's time to change course before it's too late. And that's the 'Angle'.\par \par \uc2 All right. Joining me now is Gordon Chang, Gatestone Institute senior fellow, and author of "The Great U.S.-China Tech War". Gordon, within the last hour or so, we've learned that Pelosi visited parliament and did meet with the Taiwanese president. We haven't seen a response from China yet. But will they ramp up the threats, and will those threats turn into real action?\par \par \uc2 GORDON CHANG, "GREAT U.S.-CHINA TECH WAR" AUTHOR: China has to do something, Laura, because they made this a test of wills. And it's important for Speaker Pelosi to go and actually be photographed. That's the most important thing.\par \par \uc2 The New York Times\uc2  say, Oh, this is just symbolic. Well, for people in a insecure, totalitarian state, symbolism is everything. And what Speaker Pelosi said to people in Taiwan and people in the region, people in the world, people in America, was that we will not be intimidated.\par \par \uc2 This is important, because if she didn't go, we would have been emboldening the worst elements in the Chinese political system by showing that intimidation against America works. This is a great day for America.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Now, the Biden administration, Gordon, it keeps distancing itself from Pelosi. Here was John Kirby today.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: When the Speaker of the House says, we stand with Taiwan, America stands with Taiwan. How can the Chinese construe that as anything else but that you're supporting independence?\par \par \uc2 JOHN KIRBY, PENTAGON PRESS SECRETARY: I'll let the speaker speak for herself. We do not support Taiwan independence and nothing has changed.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Now, Gordon, what does that response mean for folks out there watching who don't understand the nuances of this relationship?\par \par \uc2 CHANG: Yes. That response is basically Communist Party talking points. It's Kirby actually saying what Beijing wants him to say.\par \par \uc2 And Biden has made this problem worse. He started doing that on July 20, when he said to reporters, Oh, the Pentagon doesn't think that the speaker's trip is a good idea. Because what he was saying to Beijing was, Look, our administration is divided, come in and intimidate us some more.\par \par \uc2 And that is, in fact, what happened, Laura. Because the already dire sounding threats became even more dire after President Biden's comments. This became a real test of wills, because Biden wouldn't support the Speaker of the House.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Now, Gordon, some of my good friends disagree with my analysis, your analysis of the situation. I think this is an invaluable, important moment for the United States, because the establishment can't hide anymore. They can't kind of paper this over. They're literally threatening in not so many words, to shoot her plane out of the sky.\par \par \uc2 And they're like, well, let's do more business with China. Let's drop the tariffs on China. Let's enrich China. What the heck is that strategy gotten us over the last 20 years?\par \par \uc2 CHANG: It's gotten us into a very dangerous situation where there are no good options. On Thursday, when \uc2 Xi Jinping\uc2  threatened Biden, basically threatened the life of the president by saying, that those who would play with fire will perish. Biden should have hung up the phone. Instead, we get language from the White House readout about the necessity of deepening communication to work with China in areas where we agree.\par \par \uc2 China looks at that and says, We own the Oval Office. So this is a time where the United States needs to stand together. And we need a president who is willing to defend the United States instead of just propagating Communist Party talking points.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Gordon, we really appreciate you tonight, especially of all nights. Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 Now, tonight is arguably the biggest primary night to-date here in the United States as cue races, especially on the GOP side will be decided in five states.\par \par \uc2 Fox's own Chief Political anchor Bret Baier has some details on some of these big calls that have just been made. Bret.\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS HOST: Yes. Hey, Laura. Yes, big night. Five states. One of them just getting your call. And that is the Senate race in the state of Missouri.\par \par \uc2 Eric Schmitt\uc2  will win that Republican primary. This is the seat, remember, of retiring, \uc2 Roy Blunt\uc2 , a Republican. This state leans Republican. But this was a massive win for Schmidt.\par \par \uc2 You remember, the former President Trump had that endorsement that really perked everybody's ears? He said, I endorse Eric. Well, there are two Erics. There was Eric Wright and \uc2 Eric Schmitt\uc2 . \uc2 Eric Schmitt\uc2  took that endorsement.\par \par \uc2 And look at all this yellow throughout the state. If you look at just dip into a couple of these counties, St. Louis County. Look at this lead, 58 percent to his nearest challenger, Vicky Hartzler. If you just go up here, St. Charles County, again 56 percent. And then Lincoln County, 48 percent.\par \par \uc2 In some of these counties, there is a complete blowout. So this was predicted to be very close at one point. This is not close at all for this. So it's a big win for \uc2 Eric Schmitt\uc2 . On the Democratic side, it is a bit of more of a race. Bush Valentine versus Kuns. It's - we don't know yet, 56 percent in. But, again, this Senate seat leans towards the Republicans heading in to this race.\par \par \uc2 If we look at another call tonight, and this is the governor's race, the primary Republican primary in Michigan. Again, another run away Tudor Dixon, who were the former President endorsed on Friday. She really has dominated this race all across the state of Michigan. She will face incumbent governor \uc2 Gretchen Whitmer\uc2  seeking a second term on the Democratic side.\par \par \uc2 I know you've interviewed her, Laura. A lot of people think that she's impressive to the point where Democrats spent a lot of money trying to attack her, even in this Republican primary, and it really wasn't close.\par \par \uc2 If we take a look, another storyline tonight across the five states are the House races. You have three of them that are incumbents who voted for foreign President Trump's impeachment. They are in tight races, especially the numbers we're getting so far. This is very tight and Michigan three.\par \par \uc2 And then as we go, we talked about the Michigan governor's race. But we're not going to have numbers from Arizona till about 11:15 Eastern Time is what we're being told. We're being very careful with the numbers in Arizona.\par \par \uc2 And for Governor, you have the battle between Kari Lake and Karrin Taylor Robson, the former president versus the former vice president as far as endorsements. And then \uc2 Blake Masters\uc2  is the endorsed candidate for former president Trump. For Senate, this would obviously face the Democratic incumbent Mark Kelly.\par \par \uc2 We look to get some numbers probably in the next hour. And that should be an interesting race to watch tonight. Laura.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Bret, thank you. Kind of the establishment versus the America First types, we're seeing that dynamic play out. But that Arizona race is fascinating. Really interesting people on both sides. Bret, thank you.\par \par \uc2 And by the way, we're hoping one of the winners Tudor Dixon, the GOP primary winner in Michigan is going to join us a little bit later in the hour tonight. So stay tuned for that. But up next, I'm coming to you outside of the Minneapolis area where two years after the \uc2 George Floyd\uc2  riots, all is not well.\par \par \uc2 Now, I conducted interviews with the police, business owners, other folks to get a sense of how we got here and where things are headed. Those exclusive packages, in moments.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Now tonight, more financial questions swirling around the purchases by a major \uc2 Black Lives Matter\uc2  organizer.\par \par \uc2 Now, remember, it was just a few months ago that BLM tried to dig its way out of another scandal involving the purchase of a $6 million home by claiming it was done for legitimate business purpose.\par \par \uc2 But according to the Washington Examiner, "Three months have passed since the National Black Lives Matter group announced the launch of an art fellowship program used to justify its secret purchase of a $6 million LA mansion. But the charity hasn't even begun to accept applications for the program. \uc2 Black Lives Matter Global Network Foundation\uc2  announced the fellowship program just days before the New York Magazine revealed in early April that the charity had secretly purchased the property with donor cash in October 2020."\par \par \uc2 And now, a new report. Another organizer used donations to purchase a pet. It's only $40,000. Now, where the money was spent and how it was spent by the loosely organized \uc2 Black Lives Matter\uc2  outfit. Look at these examinations, audits are already underway. But we always knew this was a scam through their pro BLM donations and testimonials of politicians, celebrities, CEOs.\par \par \uc2 Remember they all sought to immunize themselves from future charges of racism. That's why they opened up their wallets. They groveled and they frankly embarrassed themselves.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: You have the brilliance of \uc2 Black Lives Matter\uc2  and that movement and what it has done to force change.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: We should be celebrating \uc2 Black Lives Matter\uc2 . Because the continuity of evil in our country has led us to this moment.\par \par \uc2 SEN. MITT ROMNEY (R-UT): We need a voice against racism. We need many voices against racism and against brutality. We need to stand up and say that \uc2 Black Lives Matter\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Thanks, Mitt.\par \par \uc2 Now, police reform became the catchphrase. But what the left really demanded was to abolish the police altogether.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. ILHAN OMAR (D-MN): Not only do we need to disinvest from police, but we need to completely dismantle the Minneapolis Police Department.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Tonight we're here just outside of Minneapolis, a few miles from Omar's district where everything blew up after the death of \uc2 George Floyd\uc2 . Now, it's just 26 months ago.\par \par \uc2 Now what happened in the city sparked nationwide unrest. We all remember it, in the summer of love 2020. Shortly after the riots here ended though, we came to assess the damage that this one's fairly calm community was enduring. It was overwhelming what I saw. I couldn't believe any of it frankly. And yet the suffering hasn't stopped.\par \par \uc2 The number of Minneapolis homicides in the first half of 2022 is 39 percent higher than they were during the first half of 2019. Even the college towns, they're not safe. At the \uc2 University of Minnesota\uc2 , there were about 2100 criminal incidents reported across the university area neighborhoods, that is up 40 percent from the prior academic year.\par \par \uc2 So two years after our trip to this city, we decided to return to see how things have changed or not since all this upheaval. We were shocked by what we saw and what we heard. In terms of public safety and commercial activity, the aftermath here is not pretty.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (CROWD SHOUTING)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Unbelievable. The third precinct here that fails to keep this entire, about 14 square mile area safe, burned, and now abandoned. And there is no clear place where the third precinct operates from.\par \par \uc2 That was 10 weeks after the \uc2 George Floyd\uc2  riots. And as you can see, not a lot has changed. The third precinct is still abandoned. There's barbed wire up, there's fencing. It's all fenced off. The remnants of it being torched that night, it's a monument to anarchy. It's a shock that this is allowed to stand.\par \par \uc2 What does it feel like being here?\par \par \uc2 ANDREW SCHROEDER, SERGEANT, MINNEAPOLIS POLICE DEPARTMENT: Honestly, it's kind of weird. It brings back a lot of memories, emotions, kind of raw stuff that the whole department the whole city went through.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Sergeant Andrew Schroeder is in his eighth year with the Minneapolis Police Department, and Lieutenant Jeff Waite has been here for 28 years. They both worked through the riots back when the department had nearly 900 sworn officers. Now, two years later, they're down to about 550.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: What are the biggest challenges you're facing as a police officer here, two-plus years after the Floyd killing?\par \par \uc2 SCHROEDER: I think one of the biggest challenges is just working with the deficit of officers, working on rebuilding the trust with the community, working on getting more cops to help us and working with what limited resources we have now.\par \par \uc2 JEFF WAITE, LIEUTENANT, MINNEAPOLIS POLICE DEPARTMENT: It makes it tough for us to actually just do our jobs without being harassed and cameras in our face when we're just trying to work. I mean, we're not trying to take away anybody's rights. We're just trying to do our job. And you constantly have people questioning, how we do things, and telling us that we're not doing things right. And it just - it's nonstop.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Race relations have been challenging, to say the least, in the aftermath of some of these police-involved killings, after Chauvin and Floyd. How are race relations among police officers in what is a very diverse police force?\par \par \uc2 WAITE: I don't think that race really means anything to us. I mean, we treat each other as fellow humans and as fellow officers and the race of my fellow officer means nothing, including the race of the people that we're dealing with out here.\par \par \uc2 SCHROEDER: I treat everyone I deal with just like I would want my mother or father to be treated. That answer is simple. And I have had favor in my career and that I have great relationships with especially our diverse community in North Minneapolis.\par \par \uc2 I have people that call me personally instead of calling 911. I have people that would rather speak with me than other officers. And that's been a blessing for me, especially to kind of work to bridge the gap in this difficult time.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Joining me now is retired Minneapolis Police Officer Scott Gerlicher; and Minneapolis business owner, Lonnie McQuirter of 36 Lynn refuel station.\par \par \uc2 Let's start with you, Scott. I mean, I met you about a year ago. And I think you were just getting ready to retire. You just had retired. My breath was taken away. Standing there, the precinct is still a visible monument to anarchy. Your reaction just to that fact. Let alone the 350 police officers your former police department is now short.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT GERLICHER, RETIRED MINNEAPOLIS POLICE OFFICER: Yes. It's really a sad state of affairs and really, I think, exemplifies some really poor city leadership. You have that vacant building sitting there. You have a precinct, a temporary precinct located in another area of the city, not even in the neighborhood that it serves.\par \par \uc2 We're dealing with not only what the officers pointed out, where every call they go on, they're being antagonized by people out on the street. But we're also dealing with a very liberal activist city council that not only doesn't support the police for the most part, but also works against the police department almost at every turn.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Lonnie, as the business owner, your perspective on how, given everything we've heard about the spike in crime, I could see what was happening with my own eyes. How do you survive here?\par \par \uc2 LONNIE MCQUIRTER, MINNEAPOLIS GAS STATION OWNER: So great question, Laura. It's been a real challenge of late to attract staff and to have that look on the face that really gives a lack of confidence with our customers coming in the store when we have so much - so many headwinds coming our way lately in the cities.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Well, Mayor Frey was just - he's just reelected, right? I mean, he's here for a duration. Is he doing everything he can in your view, Scott, given the deterioration we've seen across the board?\par \par \uc2 GERLICHER: I mean, I do believe the mayor is trying. But it's a long road ahead. And frankly, he's in a difficult position also with the City Council that we have. You have a vast majority of those city council members, as I said, that don't want a police department. And are doing everything in their power--\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: No police at all?\par \par \uc2 GERLICHER: No police whatsoever. They've made it very clear. And they're doing whatever they can to try to destroy the MPD, while the police department is trying to build itself up. It's tough when you're down 30 percent, over 30 percent of officers. Yes. And you're trying to build things back up, yet on the street through your city leadership, you're not getting the support that you need. Unless something changes and the residents of Minneapolis make some changes in the future, I don't know where the city is going.\par \par \uc2 There have been three shootings just in the last four hours in Minneapolis. One of which was a homicide at Fifth and Nicollet. People who go into the twins game had to walk around the body that was laying on the platform of the light rail station, right downtown Minneapolis.\par \par \uc2 The violent crime seems to have set in downtown Minneapolis, and is frankly getting to be out of control. And the officers just don't have enough staff to do that. They're hamstrung by what they--\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: And they're still vilified, first trying to do this. Now, Lonnie, you've actually offered to pay for your employees' concealed carry permit classes after the riots, and another Minneapolis gas station owner did the same. We spoke to one of his employees, Manny, who took him up on that offer. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MANNY REYES, PENN MINNOCO, GAS STATION EMPLOYEE: Through the years, last 10 years it started getting bad. But then after 2020, the \uc2 George Floyd\uc2  incident, that's when I figured I could get up here. My brother, my sister, my niece, and my nephew all got permits through Rick.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Wow, after 2020, after the riots.\par \par \uc2 REYES: Yes.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Lonnie, how much of this feeling of unease is due to the depleted presence of law enforcement because of the political and activist pressure that was brought to bear here?\par \par \uc2 LONNIE MCQUIRTER, MINNEAPOLIS GAS STATION OWNER: I'd say a good bit of it. And I can't say enough to my staff and to those frontline employees to really have situational awareness about when they're in their workplaces and just to be aware, because this is not the same city it was a few years ago.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: What do you say to the activists who were pushing for all this reform? A lot of them have left. They've moved on to the next target.\par \par \uc2 MCQUIRTER: Yes, so they've been able to have the luxury of working from home or living outside the cities. And I'd kindly invite them to go take a look at any of the frontline workers that are working inside the cities, whether you're in L.A. or you're in New York City in the Bronx, bodega. We really see what's going on here. And it's kind of the elephant in the room that no one wants to really put themselves in the forefront to describe about the crime that's been going on in the cities lately.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Scott, it's so invaluable to have an actual small business owner, and you with your decades of experience in law enforcement, because you both share a similar perspective on this. This is not -- what I keep telling people, this is not political. This is common sense. Without safety, you cannot maintain order on the streets. And then businesses don't want to come in, and then it's a death spiral. Saw it in Chicago, seen tonight Detroit, seen it in Baltimore. I'm worried this is the next spiral from which it's going to be hard to pull ourselves out of.\par \par \uc2 GERLICHER: This is where we're at in Minneapolis right now. You're absolutely right. I just talked to some residents today who, a couple weeks ago, some folks took over their house in a home invasion, burnt it down. Three houses were burnt down. I mentioned the shootings today. There's no fear of consequences --\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: None.\par \par \uc2 GERLICHER: -- by these violent offenders out here. We have a very weak county attorney. We have a very weak judicial system that would much rather favor diversion programs for violent offenders that are doing carjackings other than putting people in jail where they belong for these violent offenses. There's a time and place for prevention, but we're dealing with thugs out there that are victimizing people each and every day.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: And business owners, minority business owners trying to make it.\par \par \uc2 GERLICHER: There's hundreds of businesses just like Lonnie here trying to make ends meet.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Governor Walz, where are you? This is my question. I mean, this is time -- again, this is not political. There needs to be change, in this country, but here, we're seeing -- this is a microcosm of what's happening all over this country in urban America. Governor Walz where are you? We've got an election coming up. We'll see what happens.\par \par \uc2 Lonnie and Scott, we're going to keep coming here because this story is not going away, but thank you both. Thank you both for your perspective tonight\par \par \uc2 Now, we're going to show you more from our visit to Minneapolis tomorrow night and detail the toll that this has taken on other businesses, many of whom have not recovered. Now this is two years later, as we've said.\par \par \uc2 But up next, an update on the primary results still coming in at this hour. Mollie Hemingway, Stephen Miller, Monica Crowley all here for reaction. Plus, we're still hoping to hear from the GOP victor in Michigan's gubernatorial primary. Her name is Tudor Dixon. Get used to hearing it. You're going to hear a lot more of her in the coming months.\par \par \uc2 Stay with us. This is a big breaking news night. We're delighted you're with us.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: FOX's chief political anchor Bret Baier is back with us with some updates on this big primary night. Bret, what's the latest?\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Well, we're anxiously awaiting all those numbers from Arizona, Washington a little bit later, probably 11:15-ish eastern time.\par \par \uc2 But I want to update you on one of these House races that we talked about before. Remember there are three incumbents up in a primary, Republican incumbents who voted for impeachment. One of them is Peter Meijer, and this race is really closed. Former President Trump endorsed the opponent Gibbs. The current spread now is about 500 votes. This is Ottawa County in Michigan, this is Kent County, and we have seen this going back and forth, but it is really, really tight. We're starting to get above 50 percent, so that is one to watch.\par \par \uc2 If we go back just to look at a couple Democratic races that are really interesting, this is in Michigan, congressional district 11. Levin was endorsed by \uc2 Bernie Sanders\uc2  and the Squad, taking a shellacking in this race so far. The more moderate Stevens in the Democratic Party, about 70 percent in, that looks like it's going that way.\par \par \uc2 I want to take you to the Senate, and we called that race in Missouri for \uc2 Eric Schmitt\uc2 , overwhelmingly winning in the Republican primary. But on the Democratic side, again, a progressive candidate in Kunce, endorsed by \uc2 Bernie Sanders\uc2  and other progressives, really not doing well in the big picture here as you see this ticking forward. He could close the gap with Busch Valentine here, but we've got about 75 percent in. So that will be an interesting race. Again, that state, Missouri, leans red.\par \par \uc2 We are waiting for a lot of these numbers out west in Arizona and in Washington, but there is a big ballot measure in the state of Kansas. Now, this is how it's written. A yes vote on the measure would remove the state constitution -- from the state constitution the right to an abortion and hand the issue to the state legislature. A no vote on this measure would make no changes, keeping abortion rights enshrined in Kansas's state constitution. Right now, no is at 63 percent, and yes is at 37. And we've got about 85 percent in. Sorry for my drawing there. But it looks like no is going to win.\par \par \uc2 What does that mean? It means that Kansas will have those abortion rights enshrined in the state constitution, but it also means something bigger, broader for other states. As you know, this will be a battle as we head into some more of these primaries and as we head to the general election in November. Laura?\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: All right, Bret, thank you. We're watching this and can't wait for those Arizona calls. That will happen, I think you're going to be anchoring that right around 11:15 so we'll be watching that and check back with you if anything else changes before that.\par \par \uc2 Here now is Mollie Hemingway, editor in chief at "The Federalist," and FOX News contributor Stephen Miller, founder of America First Legal and former senior adviser to President Trump, and Monica Crowley, former Treasury assistant secretary, host of "The Monica Crowley Podcast." All right, Mollie, your big take away thus far tonight?\par \par \uc2 MOLLIE HEMINGWAY, EDITOR IN CHIEF, "THE FEDERALIST": Well, it's hard because we're still waiting on so much. That Kansas referendum is very interesting. I do think that pro-lifers should understand that so much money was spent by hardcore abortion supporters to make sure that that amendment failed. It also was a pretty -- there's a lot that was packaged there, and I think that usually prolife initiatives do much better when they're incremental. So it's a reminder also that as Roe v Wade was overturned and abortion law and policy is returned to the states where people get to weigh in, there's a lot of work to be done for people who want to protect women and children from the scourge of abortion, or people who want to make sure that it is enshrined in law. So there's a lot more to be done there, and we'll see a lot more in the years to come.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Stephen, we're watching \uc2 Eric Schmitt\uc2  do his victory speech in Missouri. President Trump was not going to endorse anyone. He sort of endorsed Erics, which was kind of cute. But your thoughts tonight on that victory.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN MILLER\uc2 , FORMER WHITE HOUSE SENIOR ADVISER TO TRUMP: Well, look, I know \uc2 Eric Schmitt\uc2  well. He's an incredible attorney general for the state. He's close with President Trump. President Trump is close with him. He's led the way on a lot of the biggest losses against Biden administration, and my organization has worked with him on some of those as well, too. So I think it's a really great night in Missouri for President Trump, for \uc2 Eric Schmitt\uc2 , and for the whole America First movement. I think he's going to be a real rising star in the party.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: And Monica, some of these big progressives in Missouri got clobbered. You saw those results on the Democrat side. Some \uc2 Bernie Sanders\uc2  characters were not going anywhere. Have people kind of had it now with the progressive left given what's happening in our cities, what we're documenting here in Minneapolis?\par \par \uc2 MONICA CROWLEY, FORMER ASSISTANT SECRETARY OF TREASURY: Yes, I think a lot of the American people have seen the throughout with these socialists who in many cases declare themselves to be open socialists. And I think a lot of the American people have now taken full measure of what they actually stand for and what they're delivering for their communities and for their states, which is just absolute catastrophe in every direction, whether it's economic privation, whether it's cities in collapse, whether it's skyrocketing inflation, whether it's a wide-open border and the embrace of all of the problems that come with a wide-open border.\par \par \uc2 I think a lot of folks understand that those progressive candidates really mean what they say when they talk about the fundamental transformation of the nation. They mean to strip away individual liberty and economic freedom and replace is with a neo-Marxist collectivist kind of model. And the American people, whether they're Democrats, independents, or Republicans, seem to be rejecting that across the board.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: All right, panel, stay with us, because we have a FOX News alert.\par \par \uc2 Now, as we mentioned just moments ago, businesswoman Tudor Dixon was declared the winner in Michigan's Republican gubernatorial primary, and that means she's going to take on Governor Gretchen "Witless" Whitmer, the queen of lockdowns, in November. And we're pleased now to be joined by Tudor in her first post victory interview. Tudor, congratulations, so excited for you tonight. Your message to Governor Whitmer, who's watching I'm sure.\par \par \uc2 TUDOR DIXON, (R) MICHIGAN GUBERNATORIAL NOMINEE: Thank you, Laura, for having me. My message to Governor Whitmer is families won tonight. Families came out and voted, and families want a family friendly Michigan, and we're going to deliver it. And we're going to hold her accountable to her terrible record here in the state of Michigan. We're not going to let the people forget. And trust me, as I've gone around this state, they haven't forgotten.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Now, she put out a statement tonight following your victory, and in it she said, "Tudor Dixon has made clear that she will drag Michigan backwards in reversing progress, rebuilding Michigan's infrastructure, and sew distrust our democracy, dangerous for Michigan women and families," blah blah blah. Sounds like it was written by \uc2 Ilhan Omar\uc2 . Tudor, your response?\par \par \uc2 DIXON: I'd ask families, are you better off now than you were four years ago? Does she really think there's any way to drag Michigan any further down than she has? Does she really think it's possible for us to be in a worse condition? Because right now we're at the bottom. We're at the bottom in education. We have a problem with cities. We need to come back for safe cities. We need to have businesses, have a government that comes around them, that protects them, that wants them to succeed. And this is not what they have seen for the last four years, but this is what we are going to deliver to the state of Michigan. So my message to \uc2 Gretchen Whitmer\uc2  is watch out. Your record stands strong, and it's bad. And the people of Michigan want to get rid of it. The only place we're going is up, and we're going to bring Michigan back.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Now, Tudor you spoke to President Trump, I know, just a short while ago. Come on, give us a little, a few little details on what he said.\par \par \uc2 DIXON: He's pretty excited about this race. He said, look, this has become a national story. You're going to get this woman out of there. You can go up against her. I always knew that you were the one that could go up against her. And, you know what? There's something pretty special about walking off stage with a bunch of people that have supported you and came here just to see me tonight. And then walking backstage and talking to President Trump and having him say you're going to do this. This is an amazing night. I'm grateful to all the people here in Michigan, I'm grateful for you to have me on tonight just that I can talk to everybody and say on national television, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Tudor Dixon, this is going to be one of those gubernatorial races that the nation is watching. And, by the way, this caused quite the commotion during your remarks just a few moments ago.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DIXON: Conservative businesswoman and mother, and a far-left, Bernie parroting, career politician. And now we have the opportunity to truly hold Governor Whitmer accountable for the pain she's inflicting on each and every one of us during the past four years.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Tudor, how are you going to drive that message home to voters across Michigan? Women are birthing parents and men are birthing parents, there's no mothers anymore in the eyes of the left. And we know Whitmer doesn't really believe in women and men anymore. She believes in changing all definitions.\par \par \uc2 DIXON: Well, yes, she's stumbled on what a woman is. She's called us menstruating people. Gross. Please never call me a menstruating person. We've been called birthing people. You know what, moms are mad. That's one job you can't take away from us. Whether we adopted our children, whether we birthed our children, we are mothers. Mothers are going to stand up for what is right. They want their name back, but they also want their kids treated right.\par \par \uc2 So we are going to go out there, like I said, with this family friendly message, and that's resonating, and it's going to. We're going to go across the entire state of Michigan, talk to everyone we can. We're going to come on your show, we're going to be out there. This is a national race now. When she ran for vice president, it became a national race. So thank you, Gretchen. We will meet you on national T.V.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: All right, Tudor, thank you so much. Congratulations.\par \par \uc2 And back with me now is our panel, Mollie Hemingway, Stephen Miller, Monica Crowley. Mollie, this really is a bellwether, I think, in many ways. Michigan is a battleground state. It's that old rustbelt. Stephen and I have talked about it over the years with immigration and jobs and outsourcing. And this is going to be a battle royale between Dixon and Whitmer.\par \par \uc2 HEMINGWAY: It's an important race for so many reasons. Whitmer really made a name for herself by embracing every kind of lockdown, school lockdown, COVID restriction, really did a lot of damage to the state for that reason.\par \par \uc2 It's also interesting because the FBI and Department of Justice have meddled quite a bit in Michigan. They got involved before the last election with their entrapment plot, kidnapping plot. They've been involved in trying to meddle in Republican politics. That's a big national issue is the two standards of justice that we have seen with the Department of Justice that's completely politicized, going after parents. And it will be interesting. Michigan is a state that has tended to be very Democrat but this is a very strong candidate, so I think a lot of people are excited to see what she can do in this very red wave year.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Stephen, we have a new slogan here on THE INGRAHAM ANGLE. It's called no state left behind. We don't think any state of our 50 states should be just cast off by any party, but the Republican Party -- no. California, Oregon, Washington state, Connecticut, Massachusetts, Vermont, Maine, Michigan, Detroit, Chicago. None of these places persist have to be governed the way they've been governed. And each of them, in my view, has to be part of an overall 50 state strategy for the conservative movement. Time to take them all back.\par \par \uc2 MILLER: Amen to that. You saw this recently on the congressional side with Myra Flores winning in an overwhelmingly Democrat district, and an 84 percent Latino district in the south of Texas, a Mexican American congresswoman, winning an extraordinary victory. Because woke policies, the idea of birthing people and menstruating people, the idea that you're going to put Critical Race Theory and segregate our children by skin color, the idea you're going to let violent criminals out of jail over and over again to reoffend and reoffend and assault our people, open borders, no limits whatsoever on immigration, energy prices through the roof, inflation through the roof, and basic anti-American Marxism being pushed at every level of society, this doesn't sell anywhere.\par \par \uc2 And I would say to Republicans, this is very important -- if you don't crown these issues, you're going to leave so many seats on the table in these midterms. The polls have been tightening, and it's because Republicans aren't hitting these issues enough. Talk about them every single day.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Yes, never underestimate the GOP's ability to screw things up. Monica, I want you to listen to some of the coverage on Mess-NBC about tonight's primaries. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: We often make the mistake of talking about disinformation and attacks on our democracy as though they exist separately from racism.\par \par \uc2 EDDIE GLAUDE, PRINCETON UNIVERSITY PROFESSOR: Absolutely. We need to understand that there are folks who believe that this country must remain a white nation in the vein of old Europe and are going to defend it at all costs. The demographic shifts represent an existential threat.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Monica, to Stephen's point just a moment ago, I think this turns off voters. Pocketbook, parental issues, pocketbooks and parents, those are the two things that are going to drive the turnout in November.\par \par \uc2 CROWLEY: Yes, and I think, Laura, that most of the American people are just exhausted and completely over this kind of identity politics and this woke politicking. I think they've had enough of it. I think they've put up with it for a while, but they've seen the end result of all of this, and they're just done with it. It's just boring and predictable and destructive on top of it. So I think it's being roundly rejected by the American people.\par \par \uc2 And to your point, Laura, about leaving no state or community behind, I think that is absolutely essential, because when you're talking about deep blue areas like California or New York or Illinois, it is an uphill battle for conservatives or populists to score particularly statewide. But in a lot of these states we've seen over the years that those citizens will put up with a lot until they don't. And I think we have now reached that moment, that tipping point where you've got people across the board absent, the most progressive kinds of radical voters, but I think there are so many that are just ready to take their country back with basic, commonsense policies. And to Stephen's point --\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: They want to follow a leader.\par \par \uc2 CROWLEY: -- every Republican needs to make --\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: They want to follow leaders.\par \par \uc2 CROWLEY: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: All right, guys, we've got to roll. Mollie, Stephen, Monica, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 I have some final thoughts on this chaotic night. It was a big night. And Minneapolis, what we're seeing here again, in moments.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: All right, we promised you a big night. I think we delivered, don't you think? We're still waiting on those results out of Arizona and Washington tonight. As I just said, this has to be a 50-state effort. No state, no city, no town in America should be left behind, not to this disastrous leadership from the Democrat Party.\par \par \uc2 Now, that's it for us tonight. Remember here in Minneapolis, it's America now and forever. "GUTFELD!" and all the breaking news, next.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document INGANG0020220803ei8200001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Pelosi Taiwan Trip Defies Chinese Military Threats; Concerns Over \uc2 Taliban\uc2  Relationship With Al-Qaeda; New Concerns Over \uc2 Taliban\uc2  Presence In Afghanistan; Death Toll Climbs As Hundreds Remain Missing In Kentucky Flooding; \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  Says Only Bernie Sanders Would Love \uc2 Joe Manchin\uc2 's Deal; Sen. \uc2 Rand Paul\uc2  (R-KY) Interviewed On Senate Spending Bill, House Speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2 's Trip To Taiwan, And Senate Hearing Pertaining To Possible Origins Of Coronavirus In Lab In Wuhan, China; Gubernatorial And Midterm Primary Elections Taking Place In Arizona, Kansas, Michigan, Missouri, And Washington; U.S. Drone Strike Kills Al-Qaeda Leader Ayman Al- Zawahiri In Kabul, Afghanistan\b0\par\par\uc2 Bret Baier, Jennifer Griffin, Peter Doocy, Jack Keane, Jeff Paul,  Chad Pergram, Alicia Acuna, Kimberley Strassel, Trey Gowdy, Harold Ford  Jr. \par \uc2 7,887 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 August 2022\par \uc2 Fox News: Special Report with Brett Baier\par \uc2 FNSPBB\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 JESSICA TARLOV, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR (on camera): Thank you. That's a good word. I like the Deshaun Watson call out there too (PH).\par \par \uc2 Anyway, a dog had a very happy ending after being adopted by one of the police officers who help rescue him from a hot car. The American Eskimo pup named Snow had been trapped for as many as 24 hours on a scorching June day. Four New York City police officer was able to get to him. We have to go. He is alive and adopted.\par \par \uc2 JESSE WATTERS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST (on camera): We have to go. She said, and that Bret is up next.\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL ANCHOR (on camera): All right, spa lizards, Gavin's goats and dog happy endings. Got it. Thanks.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Save it.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Good evening. Welcome to Washington. I'm Bret Baier. "BREAKING TONIGHT", we're following two major stories.\par \par \uc2 The Biden administration gets a national security victory, the killing of al-Qaeda leader Ayman al-Zawahiri tainted by mounting evidence that after America's withdrawal from Afghanistan, that country has once again become a staging ground for Islamic terror groups.\par \par \uc2 We'll have a live report and commentary in moments. But first, House Speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  arrives in Taiwan, despite China's threats of retaliatory measures. Soon after Pelosi's a rival there, China announced a series of military operations and drills for defying their warnings.\par \par \uc2 National security correspondent Jennifer Griffin has details tonight from the Pentagon. Good evening, Jen.\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER GRIFFIN, FOX NEWS CHANNEL NATIONAL SECURITY CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Good evening, Bret.\par \par \uc2 The Pentagon on edge tonight, watching China's reaction. \uc2 Taiwan's Defense Ministry\uc2  said 21 Chinese war planes entered its air defense identification zone as tensions between the two global powers enter a new phase.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Pelosi!\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN (voice-over): Supporters and critics gathered as House Speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  arrived in Taiwan with five other members of Congress just before midnight. She is the highest ranking U.S. official to visit the island democracy and first House Speaker to do so since Newt Gingrich, 25 years ago.\par \par \uc2 Taiwan's tallest building welcome Speaker Pelosi with a simple message. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 China's foreign ministry\uc2  again warning that the U.S. is playing with fire and might get burnt.\par \par \uc2 REAR ADM. JOHN KIRBY (RET.), PENTAGON PRESS SECRETARY: We will not engage in sabre rattling. We will continue to operate in the seas and the skies of the Western Pacific as we have done for decades. We will continue to support Taiwan.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: The Chinese military announced live fire exercises in six areas around Taiwan from August 4th to 7th, after Pelosi and her delegation leave. Chinese state media says a series of military operations will begin tonight.\par \par \uc2 Taiwan's Defense Ministry\uc2  said the exercises threatened Taiwan's ports. The U.S. military deployed four warships east of Taiwan, an aircraft carrier, the USS \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2  left a port visit in Singapore last week.\par \par \uc2 USS Antietam, a guided missile cruiser; USS Higgins, a Navy destroyer; and USS Tripoli, an amphibious assault ship are also nearby.\par \par \uc2 This is not the first time Pelosi has challenged China. In 1991, she unfurled a banner in Tiananmen Square, which said to those who died for democracy in China. Chinese guards tried to stop her.\par \par \uc2 In an op-ed published in \uc2 The Washington Post\uc2  just after she landed, Pelosi wrote, "We cannot stand by as the Chinese Communist Party proceeds to threaten Taiwan and democracy itself.\par \par \uc2 Indeed, we take this trip at a time when the world faces a choice between autocracy and democracy.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: General \uc2 Mark Milley\uc2 , the chairman of the Joint Chiefs has not spoken and does not plan to speak to his Chinese counterpart about Pelosi's unannounced visit. Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Jennifer Griffin, live at the Pentagon. Jennifer, thank you.\par \par \uc2 The killing of terrorist leader, Ayman al-Zawahiri is raising new concerns tonight over the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  its relationship with al Qaeda in Afghanistan. President Biden had previously said al-Qaeda was gone.\par \par \uc2 But new details surrounding Zawahiri's killing indicate that is far from the case. In fact, Zawahiri was being sheltered in an upscale neighborhood in the capital city. White House correspondent Peter Doocy joins us live from the North Lawn with the latest. Good evening, Peter.\par \par \uc2 PETER DOOCY, FOX NEWS CHANNEL WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Good evening, Bret. President Biden remains in quarantine with rebound COVID tonight but Biden officials are telling us that the reason this was a CIA drone strike and not a military raid was because of the president's desire to minimize civilian casualties and also the location of the target.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: unmanned aerial vehicles are a tool, very powerful tool.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (voice-over): Inside the White House Situation Room, July 1st, President Biden was shown a model of a Kabul safehouse, affiliated with the \uc2 Taliban\uc2 , where spy chiefs believed they'd spotted the leader of al-Qaeda.\par \par \uc2 Soon after, he authorized the drone strike that would happen 30 days later. Applauded even by Trump national security officials.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Look, it was a righteous kill.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: But there is an issue.\par \par \uc2 SEN. MITCH MCCONNELL (R-KY): Al-Qaeda is back as a result of the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  being back in power.\par \par \uc2 White House officials didn't give \uc2 Taliban\uc2  leaders a heads up about this weekend's hit because they were harboring Zawahiri. That's leading to fresh concern that Afghanistan is once again a safe haven for terrorists plotting against American interests.\par \par \uc2 REP. MICHAEL WALTZ (R-FL): The \uc2 U.N\uc2 . is saying 10 to 15,000 foreign fighters are gravitating back. Remember, it only took 19 to plot and plan and hit 9/11.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: The White House claims it is keeping tabs on terrorists everywhere.\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: I don't think that al Qaeda can look at what happened over the last 48 hours and feel like Afghanistan is going to be much of a safe haven for them.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: President Trump negotiated a deal with the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  in 2020; Stop attacking Americans and the U.S. would begin withdrawing. Another part of the deal, the so called, Doha agreement was for the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  to forbid terrorists from operating in Afghanistan, a deal that has been broken.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (on camera): You're saying that you've always known there was a small number of al Qaeda in Afghanistan. President Biden said, what interests do we have in Afghanistan at this point, with al-Qaeda gone?\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: In a major way, al-Qaeda was not playing at -- let me -- let me -- let me finish. They weren't playing a major role in operations, or resourcing or planning in Afghanistan.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: Now, they won't say how many members of al-Qaeda they think remain in Afghanistan. And they're also not telling us what President Biden plans to do to punish the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  for letting terrorists operate in Afghanistan.\par \par \uc2 But John Kirby did tell me, they're not going to wait for a spectacular terrorist attack in the U.S. to respond. And they think that this weekend's strike against Zawahiri is proof of that. Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Peter Doocy live in the North Lawn. Peter, thank you. Joining me now, our Fox News, senior strategic analyst, retired General Jack Keane.\par \par \uc2 General, thanks for being here.\par \par \uc2 GEN. JACK KEANE (RET.), FOX NEWS CHANNEL SENIOR STRATEGIC ANALYST (on camera): Yes, great to be here.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: 21 years, four presidents. He was on your hit list at one time. Ayman al-Zawahiri, how big a deal is this?\par \par \uc2 KEANE: Oh, it's very big deal. I mean, two Democratic presidents, two Republican presidents, and that kind of perseverance and determination. And the fact that our Intel and kind of counterterrorism professionals stayed on this target, past it, the people who succeeded him while others retired, never gave up. Yes, it's a big deal.\par \par \uc2 But what we have to know what it's not, it is not a vindication of the president's withdrawal decision from Afghanistan. And that's the reality of it. I mean, the American people can see what's happening here. We turn the country over to our adversaries, the \uc2 Taliban\uc2 . They are in control, they are harboring in an influential neighborhood with \uc2 Taliban\uc2  leaders live.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: In fact, let me put up that map. This is the Sherpur area in Kabul, which is really just down a couple blocks from where the U.S. Embassy was.\par \par \uc2 KEANE: Yes, exactly. And many of the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  leaders live in that neighborhood. And certainly, the Haqqani leadership knew full well who was there. They're protecting him, and guess what? They're also protecting the fighters that are out in the countryside.\par \par \uc2 And I believe that, yes, we can conduct Over-the-horizon attacks and raids. We proven that in Afghanistan with the \uc2 Osama bin Laden\uc2  and other targets there. We prove it in, in western Syria against ISIS and also against al- Qaeda. We've proven it in Yemen, and we proven it in here.\par \par \uc2 But here is what we can't do when we don't have any boots on the ground is developed targets of the organization itself.\par \par \uc2 This is one person. There is hundreds of fighters at a minimum in Afghanistan that belong to al-Qaeda. Some estimate there in the 1000s, we heard the congressman say that.\par \par \uc2 There were 1000s of ISIS-K fighters who aspired to kill Americans that are in Afghanistan, getting those targets, so we can destroy the organization is the challenge, not being able to pull the trigger. It's developing the targets.\par \par \uc2 And we don't have boots on the ground. We had 300 Afghan security forces, Bret. They were our human ear -- eyes and ears. We had three CIA bases and seven military bases.\par \par \uc2 And now, we're trying to convince ourselves that we can develop all of those targets against the organization? Not going to happen. We -- we've been -- it's almost a year since we left.\par \par \uc2 We haven't developed one target against ISIS-K, and they have 1000s.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Right.\par \par \uc2 KEANE: We've had one target against al-Qaeda, and it's a significant one, and a strategic one to be sure. But we need to destroy the organization.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Let me turn topics to the other major foreign policy story. House Speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  in Taiwan, landing in Taipei. This is Michael Pillsbury, expert in China. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL PILLSBURY, SENIOR FELLOW AND DIRECTOR FOR CHINESE STRATEGY, \uc2 HUDSON INSTITUTE\uc2 : Five U.S. presidents have said things like we acknowledge China's claim or we don't challenge it. But we've never come out and said Taiwan belongs to China. That's --\par \par \uc2 Whether that she gets into Taiwan airspace and lands without harassment from the Chinese is one thing. Whether she leaves peacefully tomorrow or they don't harass her airplane, that's another thing.\par \par \uc2 So, there's a very sensitive 24 hours ahead of us, Sandra.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Obviously, she did land. She is there. It was a rock star welcome. Your thoughts on this move and kind of reaction to it?\par \par \uc2 KEANE: Yes. Well, I think it was the right thing to do. Certainly, the prominence of who the Speaker is in \uc2 United States government\uc2 . But I think the Biden administration bungled it, certainly leaking it was harmful.\par \par \uc2 But then, the president getting involved and saying well the military doesn't favor it. That spun up the Chinese. It was then that the big campaign and all the bravado and the bluster about threatening us started. Why? Because they thought that they could get the Speaker to back down, and get the president to tell us so.\par \par \uc2 But where are we here? I mean, we self-center, we self-deter, and self- censor ourselves for years here. In the language we use, you see John Kirby, not wanting to say that Taiwan is an independent country.\par \par \uc2 It's a sovereign nation, is a democracy, of course it is. But he doesn't want to use the language.\par \par \uc2 We don't set -- we have war plans to defend Taiwan, if China attacks them. It's still a president's decision. We don't send the secretary of state there, any of the assistant secretaries. We don't send the secretary of defense, the chairman of Joint Chiefs, any of there was -- we don't even send the commander of the Indo-Pacific Command to work and synchronize with Taiwan the plans and practice those plans. We self-deter and self-censor ourselves.\par \par \uc2 China isn't telling us to do that. We do that ourselves. We got to stop it.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: It's fascinating. And what would happen if they did attack is a really big question for any administration. General, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 KEANE: Yes, great talking to you.\par \par \uc2 BAIER (voice-over): Stocks fell today on inflation fears and the aforementioned geopolitical tensions. The Dow lost 402. The S&P 500 drop 27. The NASDAQ was down 20.\par \par \uc2 BAIER (on camera): Russian President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 's rumored girlfriend is being hit by a new round of U.S. sanctions targeting Russian elites.\par \par \uc2 The treasury department announcing it has frozen the visa of \uc2 Alina Kabaeva\uc2 , a former Olympic gymnast.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  says Kabaeva is also the head of a Russian national media company that promotes Russia's invasion of Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 Up next, we'll go live to Kentucky where hundreds are still missing following those deadly flash floods, and a new threat is taking in a different form there. We'll take you there.\par \par \uc2 BAIER (voice-over): First, "BEYOND OUR BORDERS" tonight. British prosecutors are charging a man with intending to injure or alarm Queen Elizabeth II. Charges under the country's Treason Act stem from a Christmas Day incident when the suspect was arrested at Windsor Castle, allegedly being caught with a crossbow on palace grounds.\par \par \uc2 Chilean authorities are investigating a mysterious sinkhole that appeared over the weekend in a mining area in the north part of the country. Massive as you see here. More than 80 feet in diameter, more than 650 feet deep.\par \par \uc2 And iguanas have naturally reproduced in the Galapagos, Santiago Island for the first time in almost two centuries. Reproducing. The return of the species to the island means they'll once again play a role in the ecosystem there by creating ponds (PH), removing soil, dispersing seeds, and even providing food for the animals.\par \par \uc2 Congratulations on the reproduction. Just some of the other stories "BEYOND OUR BORDERS" tonight. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: An 80-year-old Southern California man is recovering from a heart attack tonight stemming from a shooting incident inside his business. Dramatic surveillance video shows the liquor store owner using his shotgun to shoot a would-be robber who was armed with a rifle.\par \par \uc2 Multiple suspects took off in a dark colored SUV almost leaving a person behind. You can see the shooting right here. Four suspects were later found at a hospital nearby, including one who was suffering from a gunshot wound consistent with that shotgun blast.\par \par \uc2 The Justice Department is challenging Idaho's restrictive abortion law, arguing it conflicts with a federal law requiring doctors to provide pregnant women medically necessary treatment that could include abortion.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent David Spunt asked Attorney General \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  about that decision today.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DAVID SPUNT, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): What's the point of the Supreme Court if DOJ is going to go around and do these kinds of things? Will there be other states like this?\par \par \uc2 MERRICK GARLAND\uc2 , U.S. ATTORNEY GENERAL: This is not in any way going around the Supreme Court. The Supreme Court said that each state can make its own decisions with respect to abortion, but so too can the federal government.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: The announcement is the first major action by the DOJ challenging a state trigger law since the Supreme Court overturned Roe v Wade in June.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 NFL\uc2  is suspending \uc2 Miami Dolphins\uc2  owner \uc2 Stephen Ross\uc2  in making the team forfeit two draft picks following a lengthy investigation into tampering allegations. The probe found the Dolphins had impermissible communications with then \uc2 New England Patriots\uc2  quarterback Tom Brady and then \uc2 New Orleans Saints\uc2  head coach Sean Payton. \uc2 NFL\uc2  Commissioner Roger Goodell calls the findings unprecedented.\par \par \uc2 The death toll from the devastating flooding in Kentucky rising to at least 37 people today, hundreds more are still missing amid wide range search and rescue efforts.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent Jeff Paul is in Kentucky with the latest tonight. Good evening, Jeff.\par \par \uc2 JEFF PAUL, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Yes, Bret and there hasn't been as much rain as previously forecasted. And that's coming as a huge relief for the folks here in eastern Kentucky.\par \par \uc2 But as those floodwaters start to recede, and those water levels go down, we're really starting to get a better grasp of the level of devastation.\par \par \uc2 Over here to this side, you can see what that strong current did to this truck. Another car to this side over here, you can see destroyed from all of that water rushing through this region and beyond that, this was a row of homes now reduced to just bricks and memories of what used to be.\par \par \uc2 JOE ENGLE, SHERIFF, PERRY COUNTY, KENTUCKY: And you see their lives scattered on a creek bank, photographs laying in the mud. Their bodies are underneath rubble, laying on creeks, people that was laying down at sleep. And you know, woke up in the -- you know, didn't wake up, washed away.\par \par \uc2 PAUL: Now, with hundreds still unaccounted for rescue crews are working around the clock by land and air to get those stranded to safety as helicopter rescue shows just how isolated some folks are, whose only way out is being airlifted.\par \par \uc2 Many more are cut off from the outside world without cell signal power and in many cases, water. The recovery efforts intensify. Officials feared the death toll will likely go up from where it currently stands at 37 killed.\par \par \uc2 But as hard as things are right now, locals here say Eastern Kentucky is resilient.\par \par \uc2 JAMES ESTEP, FLOOD VICTIM: I'm going to work right now. When I come back home in the morning, I might not get (INAUDIBLE) get back to my house. I might actually (INAUDIBLE) But you know, that's life. We just got to move on and we are too.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PAUL (on camera): And this community is going to have to be tough over the next couple of days. Temperatures are going to get up and it's going to be very hot. Beyond that, a lot of the ground out here very loose from all of that rain. The fear of falling power poles and trees is very relevant, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Paul live in flooded Kentucky. Jeff, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Up next, Democratic senators trying to get all 50 members on board with their new spending legislation. We'll discuss that and more with Kentucky Republican Senator \uc2 Rand Paul\uc2 . We'll also talk about the flooding.\par \par \uc2 First, here's what some of our Fox affiliates around the country are covering tonight. WSVN in Miami where federal authorities are targeting U.S. companies taking part in the shark fin trade. Recent indictments shows some U.S. businesses are taking advantage of legal loopholes.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at Petco Park in San Diego from our affiliate Fox 5. One of the big stories there tonight, the \uc2 San Diego Padres\uc2  win The Juan Soto Sweepstakes ahead of Major League Baseball's trade deadline. It's tough for Washington National fans. Washington National sending the superstar outfielder and first baseman Josh Bell to the \uc2 Padres\uc2 . In return, the Nats are receiving six players in a deal still being finalized.\par \par \uc2 That's tonight's live look outside the Beltway from SPECIAL REPORT, we'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: President Biden is naming FEMA's Robert Fenton as the White House National monkeypox response coordinator. Fenton helped lead FEMA's mass vaccination effort for COVID-19 when Biden first took office.\par \par \uc2 There are more than 5,800 confirmed monkeypox cases -- monkeypox cases in the U.S., California has joined New York State in declaring a state of emergency to combat the outbreak.\par \par \uc2 The White House meantime is shutting down criticism that Democrats spending bill upcoming would actually increase taxes on middle class Americans, something nonpartisan experts say will happen if it's passed.\par \par \uc2 It comes as Democratic Senator \uc2 Joe Manchin\uc2  tries to get his colleague \uc2 Kyrsten Sinema\uc2  on board with that legislation.\par \par \uc2 Congressional Correspondent Chad Pergram shows us where things stand tonight on Capitol Hill.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHAD PERGRAM, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONGRESSIONAL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): The bill comes down to two things, does it slice inflation and does it raise taxes?\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 JOE MANCHIN\uc2  (D-WV): They keep talking about taxes. This is not a tax bill and it's not a spending bill.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: \uc2 Joe Manchin\uc2  is on the defense.\par \par \uc2 HARRIS FAULKNER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: I'm saying Americans, $400,000 and below now are going to be taxed.\par \par \uc2 MANCHIN: That's a lie. That is a pure outright lie.\par \par \uc2 FAULKNER: So, their taxes are not going to go up?\par \par \uc2 MANCHIN: Not at all.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: Republicans claim the bill veers far to the left.\par \par \uc2 SEN. MITCH MCCONNELL (R-KY): I would say to our friend \uc2 Joe Manchin\uc2 , he made a terrible deal, it's astonishing. This is an agreement only Bernie Sanders would love.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: Democrats accused Republicans of desperation.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 CHUCK SCHUMER\uc2  (D-NY): Republicans will say anything to smear our proposal.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: Democrats tout analysis from the Committee for Responsible Budget. It says the bill will modestly reduce inflation. It's unclear if Democrats have the votes to pass the bill. Manchin talked on bended knee on the Senate day as to Democrat \uc2 Kyrsten Sinema\uc2 . She's non-committal.\par \par \uc2 MANCHIN: She'll make a decision based on the facts we're exchanging. She makes a good decision based on facts.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: Democrats need all 50 of their members plus vice president Harris to pass the bill. But while this is about the math, this is also about the health. All 50 Democrats have only been COVID free on three short occasions since late March.\par \par \uc2 SCHUMER: We are not talking about a plan b. We are going to stay healthy.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM (on camera): To Senate may not vote until the weekend on the bill, but it will vote tonight on a bill to provide aid to vets exposed to toxic burn pits. The GOP delayed the bill last week. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Chad, and that is expected to pass, right?\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: Probably here in about 45 minutes to an hour.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Chad Pergram live on Capitol Hill. Chad, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now, Kentucky Republican Senator \uc2 Rand Paul\uc2 . Senator, welcome back to SPECIAL REPORT.\par \par \uc2 I wanted to ask you about the bill and about what's happening on Capitol Hill, but first get your reaction to the flooding in your home state of Kentucky.\par \par \uc2 SEN. RAND PAUL, (R-KY): We are devastated for our friends in many families who I know personally up in eastern Kentucky. Nearly 40 people have perished in the flooding. And the flooding comes so rapidly when it comes down the side of a mountain into these narrow gorges and hollers in between the mountains, that water just rose so fast that children were swept away. But our hearts go out, and we are trying to do everything possible. We've got all our staff out there trying to help coordinate a help get aid to people.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, let's turn to legislation. You've been a spending hawk, trying to keep the government from spending more money it doesn't have, in your words. Now you have this act that the Democrats are calling the Inflation Reduction Act. What is your take on it and where it stands tonight?\par \par \uc2 PAUL: I think most importantly when you're in a recession it's a bad idea to raise taxes. \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  even believed that as far back as 2008. At one time \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  believed that. But raising taxes is a really bad idea when things are tenuous. It in all likelihood could tip us over into a full-blown recession if we're not already there, so I think it's a bad idea.\par \par \uc2 There's going to be $400 billion in taxes. People debate over who is going to pay the taxes, but most importantly the money will be taken from the productive economy and put into the nonproductive economy, which is government. So it's not a good idea no matter who pays the taxes. But there are estimates that say as much is $16 billion in either direct or indirect taxes will be on people making less than $200,000 a year. We know for certain that people who use natural gas to heat their house, that their taxes will go up, and that involves a lot of people from making $20,000 a year all the way to millionaires.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You deal with colleagues across the aisle. You've been up on the Senate a while. Are you surprised Senator Manchin made this deal? And where do you think Senator Sinema is on this?\par \par \uc2 PAUL: I guess I'm not surprised by almost anything up here, and I think that they will have 49 solid votes and we'll see which way Senator Sinema goes on this. She has in the past said that she is concerned about taxing the economy, particularly as we are in the midst of a recession, so we'll see what happens. But I think it is a bad idea.\par \par \uc2 And ultimately when you tax a corporation, they pass that through in price increases. So the immediate effect of this will be prices going up. I wouldn't technically call it inflation because inflation is a creation of the money supply that dilutes the value of the dollar, but it will add to the price of goods, so we have inflationary pressure plus tax pressure. It all will be forcing the price of goods higher.\par \par \uc2 We also have trouble in the natural gas with reduced supply, with the war, and with inflation. Gas and oil have gone up tremendously. Things that we make from natural like fertilizer have gone up dramatically. If you have an extra tax on natural gas your food prices go up, and when food prices go up the poorest among us are the most her because they are the least able to abide higher prices at the grocery store.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Let me take you overseas, House Speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  in Taipei tonight. What do you think of that trip and China's reaction to it?\par \par \uc2 PAUL: I think that relations between us, China, and Taiwan are very tricky. And I think people should be very careful not only about what they say about these things but what they do, whether it's trips or treaties. We have a treaty coming up in the Senate Foreign Relations Committee that changes the treaty for the first time in 40 years, and people on Capitol Hill like to beat their chest and show how much they love freedom versus communism. Nobody up here hates socialism and communism more than me, but I'm a little more hesitant to ratchet up rhetoric just to ratchet up rhetoric when we don't know what the response will be.\par \par \uc2 So I would hate to see military action between China and Taiwan. I think that what's happened in Ukraine should show China that the economic impacts of what will happen to you will be significant. I hope that's a deterrent. But us changing the treaty with Taiwan just to show that we're beating our chest and we're tough and we're pointing our finger at China, I'm afraid sometimes that that kind of reaction and having the speaker over there could actually backfire.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You've called for hearings on the origin of COVID. You say you would like to pursue that. Obviously, if Republicans take control, that's much more likely. I had Dr. \uc2 Anthony Fauci\uc2  on. I asked him and pressed him a couple of different ways about this. Take a listen to this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Do you dispute the accuracy of any of these emails that came out from the Oversight Committee showing top virologists warning you that the evidence pointed to gain-of-function research and a lab leak?\par \par \uc2 DR. \uc2 ANTHONY FAUCI\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE CHIEF MEDICAL ADVISER: Upon relooking at this carefully now, we think it's more likely that it was a natural evolution.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And you still believe that? There are more and more organizations that point right to a lab leak.\par \par \uc2 FAUCI: Bret, more people saying it doesn't mean there's more evidence of it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You've had some battles up on Capitol Hill with Dr. Fauci. Your thoughts on all of that?\par \par \uc2 PAUL: Before we even get to whether the virus came from a lab, we have to explore, were they doing gain-of-function research? Were they taking viruses, mixing them with unknown viruses, and creating more lethal viruses, or viruses that were more infectious or transmissible? I say without question they were doing this. \uc2 Anthony Fauci\uc2  says they weren't. Tomorrow we will have the first gain-of-function hearing on Capitol Hill, the first exploration of this topic in two years. A million Americans died, and we have not had one single hearing. Tomorrow, we have the first hearing, and I will three scientists, very esteemed scientists, scientists with hundreds of papers, peer-reviewed papers, editors of journals -- this is an elite group of scientists that will be there tomorrow -- I'm going to ask them, was it gain-of-function research that was going on in Wuhan?\par \par \uc2 That doesn't prove it came from a lab, but it proves that dangerous research was happening there and that it could have come from the lab. And my question is, even if we don't know for certain, let's say it's 50/50, that's still a big probability that it came from a lab. At that point, wouldn't we want to make sure we're controlling -- we have restrictions on exporting nuclear technology. Should we have restrictions on exporting DNA technology to communist China? I think yes.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Senator \uc2 Rand Paul\uc2 , appreciate your time. We'll cover that hearing. Thanks.\par \par \uc2 PAUL: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Up next, our midterm watch. Former President Trump's political sway being put to the test in a number of states.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Welcome back. It's primary day. This is SPECIAL REPORT midterm watch, and we have five states in primaries today on August 2nd. They are Arizona, Kansas, Michigan, Missouri, and Washington. And we are today 98 days from election 2022. Hard to believe, but we are there.\par \par \uc2 Closing in on it, some interesting races tonight, House races up. Three Republicans actually in the House who voted to impeach former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , they are up for contested primaries. In Washington state, Republican \uc2 Jaime Herrera Beutler\uc2  faces off against Joe Kent. Kent is endorsed by the former president, has made a big campaign about calling Beutler a RINO, America Last RINO, he says.\par \par \uc2 Dan Newhouse\uc2  is the incumbent. He faces a couple of challengers, significant primary challengers, although he has a big lead in fundraising. Loren Culp is the Trump-backed candidate in this race.\par \par \uc2 And in Michigan, incumbent Peter Meijer faces off against Trump-backed challenger John Gibbs. Interestingly in this race, Representative Meijer has been talking about the Democrats spending nearly half-a-million dollars to boost Gibbs with ads, a move we have seen in a number of different states from Democrats backing Trump candidates.\par \par \uc2 Now let's go to the governors' races, and one that we're looking at very closely, an anticipated race in Michigan. And this is really a toss-up. Former media personality Tudor Dixon received the former president's endorsement on Friday. She goes up against challengers Kevin Rinke, Garrett Soldano and Ryan Kelley. Kelley, by the way, faces charges for his role in the January 6th Capitol riot in this race.\par \par \uc2 The winner of this primary in Michigan will take on the incumbent Democratic governor \uc2 Gretchen Whitmer\uc2 . She is seeking a second term after getting national attention for controversial policies, really, during the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 And we are going to go into Arizona, and there are a number of close races there. Two basic races for governor and Senate. The Republican bid for governor features dueling endorsements. The former president going for Kari Lake, the former vice president going for Karrin Taylor Robson. And several candidates are trying to unseat Democrat incumbent Senator Mark Kelly on the Senate side. Alicia Acuna is following it all from Phoenix tonight. Good evening, Alicia.\par \par \uc2 ALICIA ACUNA, FOX NEWS SENIOR CORRESPONDENT: Good evening, Bret. This may be 2022, but in Arizona there is a whole lot of 2020 going around.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER U.S. PRESIDENT: \uc2 Blake Masters\uc2  is an incredible person, a very smart guy, and an America first fighter.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ACUNA: Despite being new to politics, \uc2 Blake Masters\uc2  has surged in the GOP race for the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2  nomination. Part of his message, he believes former President Trump won in 2020, as does businessman Jim Lamon who is self-funded and put money towards the so called Stop the Steal movement in Arizona when some in the GOP accused elections officials of helping \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  win here.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JIM LAMON, (R) ARIZONA SENATE CANDIDATE: What we need to do to make sure Arizona is a safe, secure election.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ACUNA: State Attorney General \uc2 Mark Brnovich\uc2  also vying for the GOP Senate nod, says he is still paying politically for defending Arizona's election process and certifying the result.\par \par \uc2 To the governor's race, where Karrin Taylor Robson, endorsed by former Vice President Pence, is who Democrats would consider the biggest threat come November. But Trump backed Kari Lake has gained popularly on the former president's messaging.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KARI LAKE, (R) ARIZONA GUBERNATORIAL CANDIDATE: The only way they can win is if they load up the voter rolls with dead people, people who have moved, and imaginary friends.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ACUNA: Arizona Secretary of State, Democrat \uc2 Katie Hobbs\uc2  is running for his party's gubernatorial nomination. Hobbs calls Lake's strategy dangerous.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KATIE HOBBS\uc2 , ARIZONA GUBERNATORIAL CANDIDATE: And this is part of an ongoing attack on our democracy that we have seen play out since the 2020 election with threats to election officials, attacks on the freedom to vote.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ACUNA: Now, Bret, polls close here in Arizona at 7:00 p.m. local time, 10:00 p.m. your time. Results will be in about an hour, a little after. And we'll have those for you.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Alicia Acuna live in Phoenix. Alicia, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Breaking tonight, just minutes ago, the State Department has issued a new worldwide caution update for U.S. citizens following the killing of Ayman al-Zawahiri. The department just moments ago saying current information suggests terrorist organizations continue to plan terrorist attacks against U.S. interests in multiple regions across the globe.\par \par \uc2 Up next the panel on Speaker Pelosi's trip to Taiwan and the politics of the U.S. killing of one of the world's most wanted terrorists.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 MIKE GALLAGHER\uc2 , (R-WI): I do generally believe that this is a rare area of bipartisan agreement. We can move forward and learn the right lessons from our failures in Ukraine and Afghanistan and apply those lessons in the Indo-Pacific before it's too late.\par \par \uc2 JOHN KIRBY, NATIONAL SECURITY COUNCIL COORDINATOR FOR STRATEGIC COMMUNICATIONS: There's no reason for China to escalate the tensions because nothing has changed about America's foreign policy when it comes to the One China policy and the support for Taiwan.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Maybe this whole idea of 50 years of a One China policy is wrong and we need to revisit it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Speaker Pelosi landing in Taipei in Taiwan amid China bluster about that trip as it continues. In "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " the speaker wrote, "Why I'm leading a congressional delegation to Taiwan. By traveling to Taiwan, we honor our commitment to democracy, reaffirming that the freedoms of Taiwan on all democracies must be respected."\par \par \uc2 We'll start there with our panel, Kimberley Strassel, a member of the editorial board at "The Wall Street Journal," \uc2 Trey Gowdy\uc2 , former Congressman from South Carolina, and Harold Ford Jr., former Tennessee Congressman, co-host of THE FIVE. Kimberley, thoughts?\par \par \uc2 KIMBERLEY STRASSEL, "WALL STREET JOURNAL": I think what this is really revealing, Bret, is that we are just going to have to maybe rethink this whole strategic ambiguity, which is what has been behind the Taiwan-China- America policy for so long. We for decades have said, OK, well, China will pretend as though they are just waiting for peaceful reunification. We will abide by China's one policy and not then be ambiguous about what we're going to say about Taiwan's defenses.\par \par \uc2 But China has been saber rattling so much, and the president stepped in it some when he rattled all that a couple of months ago and suggested we would come to Taiwan's aid. But the degree to which China is being aggressive, we may have to rethink that and actually be more forceful about what we are going to do to protect Taiwan.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Trey, the speaker is meeting with a semiconductor company there in Taiwan. She is also going to meet with leaders there. And what she says will make a difference, obviously, on the world stage. It's a public trip that, in the past, these haven't been public.\par \par \uc2 TREY GOWDY\uc2 , FORMER SOUTH CAROLINA REPRESENTATIVE: You know, Bret, there are 100 reasons for the Speaker of the House to meet with Taiwanese officials, but we don't owe China a single reason. We have been so accommodating of Chinese sensitivities, and sometimes the country is just too sensitive. I don't think anyone thinks World War III will begin because a perhaps soon to be former Speaker of the House visits Taiwan. So look, China just needs to get over it.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Harold?\par \par \uc2 HAROLD FORD JR., FORMER TENNESSEE REPRESENTATIVE: So first, thanks for having me. I think both sets of comments need to be combined. And as you think about it, we need to as a nation think about the next 10 to 20 to 30 years and think about the development of a serious and coherent strategy in this region of the world. That obviously involves our thinking around trade and investment, national security and human rights.\par \par \uc2 I don't think we've had that kind of serious calibration or recalibration, at least it has not occurred in the public forum like it should, and that is what I interpreted Kim saying in large part. We have to do it there and we have to do it here. The way we invest in our national security here has to take into effect, into account in a very real way the kind of challenge we face in that region. We ought to reinvest in ways to build out supply chain infrastructures here so we can be less reliant.\par \par \uc2 My only challenge with this trip is I hope it's not a one-off trip. This has to be part, I hope it's part of a larger strategic and smart framework to help us counter China where we have to and collaborate where we should.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Meantime, the killing of Ayman al-Zawahiri in Afghanistan, here's Congressman Mike Waltz on that.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. MIKE WALTZ, (R-FL): I think this should be celebrated. I also think, though, it leads to a lot of questions. Number one, what was the leader of Al Qaeda doing in Kabul? Why was he someone who has been in hiding for decades? Now, why did he feel so comfortable to really be out in the open?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: "Politico" writes it this way, "Even in success, why Biden's victory lap will be short. The al-Zawahiri news signals a real achievement but also raises a worrisome question. If the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  have opened their country to a key figure in the attacks of 9/11, what does it tell us about who else is being welcomed to Afghanistan?" Trey?\par \par \uc2 GOWDY: Yes, I don't get a chance to agree with "Politico" much, but they are right. It's a victory for Biden. Keep in mind, Bret, conventional wisdom, he opposed the strike on \uc2 Usama bin Laden\uc2 , so this is good for him. It is bad in that Afghanistan is a prison for women and girls but a safe haven for terrorists less than a year since we withdrew. That is not good politically and will continue to rear its head.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: John Kirby was asked this safe haven question this morning specifically. Here's what he said.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOHN KIRBY, NATIONAL SECURITY COUNCIL COORDINATOR FOR STRATEGIC COMMUNICATIONS: I don't think that Al Qaeda can look at what happened over the last 48 hours and feel like Afghanistan is going to be much of a safe haven for them. We have proven that over the horizon counterterrorism capability works.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Kimberley?\par \par \uc2 STRASSEL: We have proven it in this particular case. But again, how many more people are there, how many are we missing? It seems beyond credulity to think that this guy was walking around as brazenly as he was without the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  knowing it and tolerating it if not encouraging it. How many more are there? And it really highlights how much harder it, in fact, has become for us to no longer have ears on the ground, and that hasty withdrawal has just raised that challenge even higher.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Harold?\par \par \uc2 FORD: I think politicians, all of them, should relent a little bit in taking victory laps on any matter. I'd agree there. Where I disagree is we were right to leave Afghanistan. They did it in a way that I would not have done it. But I have to think, if you are someone like this guy that was just killed, and you want to kill Americans and kill women and girls there in Afghanistan, you probably are going to pause after what happened to this guy in the last 24 to 36 hours.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Trey, the State Department just releases this warning for U.S. citizens, and we haven't had a warning like this in a while. Terrorism has not been front and center with the American mindset as we've been further and further away from 9/11. But it's always there.\par \par \uc2 GOWDY: It is, and they are always plotting to do harm to us. And I am glad that there is no statute of limitations when you kill Americans. It's been 20 plus years, and we've carried out the sentence. And I congratulate the president for doing it.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Kimberley, last word.\par \par \uc2 STRASSEL: Yes. No, look, this was definitely a victory. We should celebrate that. But we've got to be very aware that these guys aren't going to stop.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, panel, thanks so much.\par \par \uc2 OK, it is Tuesday. You know what that means. Time for Tuesday tweets.\par \par \uc2 Matt Sweetwood asks, "How do we know for sure that we actually got al- Zawahiri? "We do not have DNA confirmation. We are not going to get that confirmation. Quite frankly, based on multiple sources and methods, we've gathered information and we don't need it," John Kirby told CNN."\par \par \uc2 OK, so intel sources told us they had a visuals, photographic identification before the strike which was a positive I.D. We were told there were no doubts it was him. High confidence was the intel assessment. They didn't need the DNA. My guess is they used facial recognition, at least that's the sense. They also had all the chatter from the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  and people on the ground in terrorist circles, so that's the deal.\par \par \uc2 Next, we have redted72 asked "Is the Taiwan trip \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  legacy building?" Maybe. It could be that she fears a minority, and this is part of that. But she has principles that she talked about in "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " op-ed. We'll see.\par \par \uc2 Up next, Chris Hoskins wants to know, "Would you want to moderate a presidential debate in 2024?" Sure, yes.\par \par \uc2 And lastly, Burt Vanderlaan asked "Does a truly moderate or centrist candidate have any chance in either political party these days? Our Primary contests here in Michigan seem to be just the battle of the extremes." I think that's a great question, because if you look at the country ideologically according to polls, we're pretty much in the middle, maybe even center right on a lot of issues overall. And so you would think a centrist candidate in certain states would do well. We haven't built the system that way. We'll see if it changes after the 2022 election.\par \par \uc2 Tomorrow on SPECIAL REPORT an exclusive interview with the Senate Minority Leader \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Thanks for inviting us into your home tonight. That's it for this SPECIAL REPORT, fair, balanced, and still unafraid. Here's Jesse.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FNSPBB0020220803ei8200001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 WH Sending Kamala To Red States To Push Abortion; Dem Dilemma: What Do We Do About Kamala?; Biden Shows Cowardice On Pelosi's Taiwan Trip; Military Establishment Doesn't Want To Upset China; Exposing Biden's Radical Judiciary; The Biden Admin Refutes Impending Recession Fears; \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2  Holds Confirmation Hearing for Biden Administration Judicial Nominee; President Biden Recovering From COVID-19; Staffers Of Democratic House Members Arrested For Occupying Democratic Senator \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2 's Office To Encourage Climate Legislation; Two Families Get In Fight At Disney World; Entire Police Force In Kenly, North Carolina, Quits Due To New Town Manager\b0\par\par\uc2 Laura Ingraham, Ben Domenech, Mike Pompeo \par \uc2 7,930 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 July 2022\par \uc2 Fox News: The Ingraham Angle\par \uc2 INGANG\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 SEAN HANNITY\uc2 , FOX NEWS HOST: Please set your DVR, so you never miss an episode of Hannity. For news anytime, anyplace, anywhere, Foxnews.com, Hannity.com.\par \par \uc2 And in the meantime, let not your heart be troubled. I can hear Laura in the back - I can hear already.\par \par \uc2 LAURA INGRAHAM, FOX NEWS HOST: You can hear me. I'm not speaking. You're hearing MP. I'm not even saying anything and I'm getting blamed.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: That wasn't you. I thought it was you. Anyway, happy Monday. How are you?\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Happy Monday. I like the blue on blue combo. That's very fetching. I like that.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Thank you. I'll lend you my tie anytime you want.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: You've now hit your limit of compliments for the week. No more for you. All right, Hannity.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: I don't deserve any compliments. I'm fine with that.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Well, it looks great. We'll pick up where you left off.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Have a great show. Oss.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: And awesome show as always. All right. I'm Laura Ingraham. This is THE INGRAHAM ANGLE from a very busy Washington tonight.\par \par \uc2 How do you solve a problem like Kamala? That's the focus of tonight's 'Angle'.\par \par \uc2 All right. Biden's COVID diagnosis as mild as it may be is still a triggering event for the DC establishment. What if something happens to the President and \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  has to step in?\par \par \uc2 Well, this scenario is a terrifying prospect for anyone who's followed her performance over the past few years. Now, in an administration of punch lines, she's in a class by herself.\par \par \uc2 Last Sunday during a podcast interview where she managed to make the Democrats Holy Grail of abortion sound evermore callous? If that's possible. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Listen, women are getting pregnant every day in America. And this is a real issue and we need to act with a sense of haste about what is at play, what is at stake.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: There's a baby at play. Just to clarify.\par \par \uc2 Now, at least she didn't do the trademark hyena cackle at the end of that. But she was saving all of that for today in MIT in Indianapolis, where she was nervously avoiding any opportunity for Q&A with the press.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: So in terms of the format, we'll let the press in. Representative will say a few words, introduce me, I'll say a few words. And then we will bid the press goodbye. And we will get into the nitty gritty of it all.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Why is that funny? What wasn't funny was her infamous exchange with the press in Poland last March. She was sent there to Eastern Europe, I assumed, to reassure our allies over Ukraine. But the only one reassured by her performance was Vladimir Putin.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Is the United States willing to make a specific allocation for Ukrainian refugees?\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: A friend in need is a friend.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: This is just another in a long line of cringe worthy utterances from our VP. Even when they use paid child actors to push some NASA initiative, the kids couldn't help save her bad acting.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: I just love the idea of exploring the unknown. And then there's other things that we just haven't figured out or discovered yet, to think about so much that's out there that we still have to learn. Like, I'd love that.\par \par \uc2 I can literally see the craters on the moon with your own eyes, with your own eyes. I'm telling you, it is going to be unbelievable.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Those kids should have been paid double for not laughing there. They obviously couldn't risk just putting her with regular kids in an informal setting because lord knows what she'd say.\par \par \uc2 This is how she defended teaching kindergarteners about sex and gender identity in public schools.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: We're going to stand up against a law that says, Don't say gay, basically restricting kindergarten through third grade teachers in Florida to be able to love openly and teach what they believe is important for people to understand.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Well, well, well, that teachers should be able to love openly? And the fact that I thought reading, spelling, arithmetic. That was what five, six and seven-year olds were supposed to be learning. I don't know. I'm totally out of it.\par \par \uc2 Now, for the Democrats having a woman as utterly incompetent as Harris as vice president is fraught with political peril. And of course for the country, it's a national security nightmare.\par \par \uc2 Now, if Harris has to take over as commander-in-chief, marinate in that for a moment, China is going to take Taiwan in about 30 minutes. Now, the most important security issue facing America on the home front, of course, is our open border. And the White House put her in charge of it.\par \par \uc2 And in June 2021, a friendly reporter asked her a simple question about the issue.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Do you have any plans to visit the border?\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: I'm here in Guatemala today. At some point, we are going to the border. We've been to the border. So this whole thing about the border, we've been to the border. We've been to the border.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: You haven't been to the border.\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: And I haven't been to Europe. I mean, I know--\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: If any one answer effectively ended her political career, I would say, that should be it. Thus, they work hard to limit her to carefully choreographed events.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: Every American, no matter their income, should be able to afford high-speed internet. Right now, the cost of internet is fully covered for millions of Americans. Call 877-384-2575, or if you have access, go to Getinternet.gov.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Come on. This isn't fair. This whole monologue is not fair. She got the number right. OK. But Joe would have struggled with that.\par \par \uc2 There are, though, I should say, a few lonely fans of Harris out there. Historian Jeffrey Frank in a \uc2 New York Times\uc2  column contends that more people would appreciate her many talents if only the White House gave her more to do.\par \par \uc2 There's little evidence that the Biden White House feels much of the urgency felt by General Eisenhower to enhance the role and preparedness of the person who might inherit the presidency at any moment. A historian wrote that comparing Kamala to \uc2 Richard Nixon\uc2 . Yes. Just like (ph) Iken Nixon, if Nixon was actually known to sound like a total bubblehead.\par \par \uc2 Kamala's lips move, and words come out, and she manages to say absolutely nothing.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: When we discuss this issue and when we contemplate what it means, understand that it could have a profound impact on just about everyone in our country, who has any association, or interest or concern about these various issues.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: You see what I mean? Now, the White House, they're not shrinking Kamala's portfolio because they don't like her personally. They are doing it because she's terrible. She's relegated to safe appearances or interviews with those groundbreaking journalists that glamour the skim and something called refinery 29. That was on her portfolio today.\par \par \uc2 So also, ask yourself this, if Democrat insiders thought vice president Harris was the future of the party. Why would she be burning staff at the current rate she's burning it? On social media, they call this the Kexodus. Here's the list and counting.\par \par \uc2 Now, these aren't minor figures. These people are her senior staff.\par \par \uc2 Now, National Review put it plainly. "If you're a \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  fan who feels like she's being snubbed, ignored, shunted aside, or underutilized, it's not the GOP or Fox News. This is all on Biden, or at least Biden's senior staff."\par \par \uc2 Look, they don't like her. They don't respect her. They know she's a drag on an already dragged down president. Now, of course, we all know why Biden selected her in the first place though.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT, UNITED STATES OF AMERICA: And I commit that I will, in fact, appoint a - I'll pick a woman to be vice president. There are a number of women who are qualified to be President tomorrow. I would pick a woman to be my vice president.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: It was more important for Biden to achieve a first, the first female woman of color as vice president, than to actually pick someone for his or her experience and intellect.\par \par \uc2 Now the Democrats and the country are in a serious bind. So how do you solve a problem like Kamala? Well, there's no easy answer. But I can assure you that the senior Democrats in this town are spending a lot of time racking their brains about what to do.\par \par \uc2 As for Biden himself, having Kamala a heartbeat away from the presidency, may have actually have been an ingenious move. Governor DeSantis has a theory.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GOV. RON DESANTIS (R-FL): I thought Biden picking her at first was like the worst decision ever, because she's not great. But she's like the best impeachment insurance in 25th amendment insurance anyone could have. Because as bad as Biden is, even though he can barely read the teleprompter, and as much as people disapprove of him, nobody wants Harris.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: And that's the 'Angle'.\par \par \uc2 All right. Joining me now is Stephen Miller, former senior adviser to President Trump, founder of America First Legal; and also Ben Domenech, editor-at-large at The Spectator and Fox News contributor.\par \par \uc2 Stephen, do you think that they were trying to hobble her early on when they gave her the border assignment?\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN MILLER, "AMERICA FIRST LEGAL" FOUNDER: I think they knew early on what we've all figured out, which is that she is horrendously incompetent, totally useless, has the speaking skills at best of a first grader if you're being generous, and has absolutely no ability to do anything useful whatsoever.\par \par \uc2 And since they knew, very sick, but they knew that the border was going to be in a never ending state of crisis. They figure they might as well lay that on her. Even though, of course, we know that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and his whole administration have the entire board disaster on their hands in conscience.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Now, Ben, in Politico today, they say that, "Harris plots her next move, more aggressive, volley in the abortion rights arena". I mean, is that going to catapult her into, I guess, being as close to \uc2 Richard Nixon\uc2 , an intellect and historical--\par \par \uc2 BEN DOMENECH, THE SPECTATOR EDITOR AT LARGE: Look, Laura, what you don't appreciate about this moment is the importance of the passage of time, passage of time in the century of understanding of the vice presidency.\par \par \uc2 Look, you can assign the vice presidency to being a warm bucket of spit. But as you look at the various vice presidents who have held this role over the years, many of them have been very important in terms of assisting the President that they have worked with, including \uc2 Richard Nixon\uc2 , including his role for \uc2 Dwight Eisenhower\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But when it comes to Kamala, she is not just unimportant to this administration, she is the grab bag for everything that they don't want to deal with, including the border, including all of these other things that are supposed to be on her portfolio. But that she's not going to deal with, no one has faith with her - in her as being a potential replacement for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And as it becomes clear that he is someone who is completely incapable of fulfilling the role of the presidency. And that 2024 is just looming on the horizon, you're going to see more and more Democrats start to question her, start to try to figure out how can we possibly replace her in terms of the next person to step up?\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: I think, that's right. And, look, people are always talking about, Well, is it going to be Trump, or some other person, DeSantis. And there's a fight between the establishment and the populace. And yes, that's true.\par \par \uc2 So the Republicans have a challenge to try to figure this all out. But they have a real - they have - Biden can't run, nobody wants him, and nobody wants Kamala. So guess, who's next? Well, here's our Secretary of Transportation diving into the hot button issue of, guess, is this transportation? Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PETE BUTTIGIEG, SECRETARY OF TRANSPORTATION: Our marriage deserves to be treated equally. And I don't know why this would be hard. If they don't want to spend a lot of time on this, they can vote yes and move on. And that would be really reassuring for a lot of families around America, including mine.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: I mean, you can't turn the dial without seeing him on every show. Like weekends, he's seven days a week on television. So if they could put Kamala out there, they would Stephen, we have airline issues. I mean, not really in this portfolio, but we have baby food shortages still.\par \par \uc2 DOMENECH: Supply chain.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Yes, supply chain. But we have the big thing going on at the ports in California. And this guy is about, Well, let's talk about me. This is unbelievable.\par \par \uc2 MILLER: You've got the supply chain crisis. We have the airline crisis, we have the infrastructure crisis. And all they can do is put Pete out on TV to either, A, talk about his own marriage; or B, talk about how roads in America are racist and our bridges are bigots.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: But is he their plan C?\par \par \uc2 MILLER: He is plan F, G, H. I mean, who knows how far down the list he is. Their team, their roster is so incompetent. Look, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  would move through all of them. The reality is, is that whether it's Biden, or Harris, or Buttigieg, or Newsom, look at the catastrophe of California, crime, drugs, homelessness. Pritzker, look at Chicago.\par \par \uc2 The Democrat Party is in a complete state of crisis, because they don't have one viable national leader.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: But, Ben, they are never going to admit that liberalism doesn't work, right? They're not going to do that. So they're going to keep finding a slicker messenger. That's why Newsom is kind of rising to the top of the heap, because it's kind of slick.\par \par \uc2 DOMENECH: You just have to automatically raise somebody up if their teeth are that shiny. I mean, it's one of these things where you look at Pete, and it's like--\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: You can do your hair like him. You're just not doing.\par \par \uc2 DOMENECH: Yes, I probably should. But here's the thing. When you look at news, when you look at Pete, what you hear from him is so much leaning into this kind of culture war animosity that says, Republicans are bigots.\par \par \uc2 Well, you know, guess what, when \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  ran for president, he was someone saying that marriage was between a man and a woman.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: You're right.\par \par \uc2 DOMENECH: It was \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  who was coming along and being the first President to say, Actually, you know, I'm ok with this. So maybe you should recycle this kind of bigotry, this kind of approach to culture war dynamics through reality in order to understand that actually we really more care about whether you are delivering on what your job is for Americans as opposed to engaging in this kind of hot button culture war issue in order to try to raise your hierarchy within this ridiculous circumstance of replacement for Kamala.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: They're grinding up Biden, they're grinding up Kamala. They're going to grind through \uc2 Pete Buttigieg\uc2 . I think they're going to keep going until they find someone is the better messenger for liberalism.\par \par \uc2 Stephen and Ben, so good to see you in person.\par \par \uc2 MILLER: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Both of you.\par \par \uc2 And \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2 's planned trip to Taiwan is a rare moment of truth for the speaker. The looming battle against China and the single greatest struggle for human rights worldwide. The acquiescence to a murderous regime is a stain on the legacies of Bush, Clinton, Obama and Biden.\par \par \uc2 Pelosi could actually set herself apart from all of them. So why should she listen to those morons at the Pentagon who don't want her to go.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Mr. President, do you think it's a good idea for Speaker Pelosi to travel to Taiwan this summer?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Well, I think that the Military thinks it's not a good idea right now.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Really? They thought the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  was going to back down too, right?\par \par \uc2 So my next guest even volunteered to go with \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  to Taiwan, so if she's serious about bipartisanship, she should take him up on his offer. Joining me now, \uc2 Mike Pompeo\uc2 , former Secretary of State, Fox News contributor.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Secretary, have you heard from the speaker since you made your offer?\par \par \uc2 MIKE POMPEO\uc2 , FORMER SECRETARY OF STATE: Laura, thanks for having me on. I have not heard from her. It was a little bit tongue in cheek. And then I'd prefer to travel with lots of other people.\par \par \uc2 But having said that, this is about America. If the Speaker of the House now allows some second rate Chinese foreign diplomat, who says, boy, we might do something that's really hard on America. If you're speaker of the House, the third person, the third highest ranking federal official says, No, you can't come here. We're going to do something we acquiesce to that threat. We're in for a long decade here in the United States of America.\par \par \uc2 President Biden needs to get serious. He needs to defend the things that matter to the American people from California to New York to Florida to Washington to allow the Chinese to just issue a statement and change the direction, change the actions that the United States will take, because we are fearful of them. We are in for a long struggle, and it will not end well with that kind of failed leadership.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: So what message does it send if we allow China to dictate the travel of the woman who's third in line for the presidency? What message does that send to them?\par \par \uc2 POMPEO: It tells every one of our allies in the region, the Japanese, the South Koreans, the Australians, it says, Don't hang with the Americans. They're fearful. And it tells our adversaries, you mentioned Afghanistan, certainly the same thing happened in Ukraine as well.\par \par \uc2 We said, a minor incursion would be ok, when you're not strong. This is a tough, nasty world out there, Laura. When you're not strong, especially again, what is the greatest threat to the American people today, the Chinese Communist Party, when you demonstrate weakness, they are going to walk through the fire, and they're going to make life really difficult for us.\par \par \uc2 It doesn't need to be that way. We're the most powerful nation in the world. We have an economy that is unrivaled, We can beat them, we can win this, we can continue to have another 250 good years for America. But if a speaker of the House is just going to travel to a sovereign nation, the nation of Taiwan, and th