\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice-over): The White House is framing a return to the Iran nuclear deal as a way to bolster global oil supply.\par \par \uc2 Deputy Secretary of State \uc2 Wendy Sherman\uc2  , saying Iran should take the deal because, "They would get sanctions relief. They would improve their economy and sell their oil again and the world needs their oil, so they could get a good price for it."\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (on camera): Her language makes it seem like, maybe, you know, they're not so bad if they bring down gas prices, which has been hurting the president and polls obviously.\par \par \uc2 REAR ADM. JOHN KIRBY (RET.), PENTAGON PRESS SECRETARY: Well, look, I mean, there's obviously a tangential potential when you tangential benefit. But again, Jacqui, that's not the purpose here. The purpose is to try and prevent them from having a nuclear weapons capability.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: President Biden did not participate in a meeting with oil and gas CEOs at the White House last month, but fist bumped with the Saudi Crown Prince as part of a bid to get more oil from \uc2 OPEC\uc2  plus states, returning home without a commitment.\par \par \uc2 And yet, it's the media in the administration's crosshairs. Communications director \uc2 Kate Bedingfield\uc2  telling staff in a memo, Biden's not getting enough credit for gas prices falling over the last month. Writing, "Despite the data, you wouldn't know gas prices are coming down from watching the evening news or reading the paper. The media is doing its own version of rockets and feathers, covering the spike, but not the drop."\par \par \uc2 Credit the president apparently deserves when prices go down but not up.\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER GRANHOLM\uc2  , UNITED STATES ENERGY SECRETARY: To be really clear, obviously, the president does not control the price of gasoline. No president does.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Can't do much right now. Russia is responsible.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Team Biden not admitting the positive trend on gas prices makes for a tough time to declare a climate emergency. The White House delaying executive orders, but not saying why.\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2  , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: We still have it on the table and we'll -- I don't have a circle -- a date circle in the calendar.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (on camera): Tough times for the Biden White House, not the main reason he shouldn't run again, says President Clinton's former labor secretary. \uc2 Robert Reich\uc2 , who also served in the \uc2 Obama\uc2  transition, adding his voice to growing calls that Biden is simply too old. Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Jacqui Heinrich live on the North Lawn. Jacqui, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Testimony has begun in the criminal contempt trial of former Trump advisor \uc2 Steve Bannon\uc2  . Tied to his initial refusal to testify in front of the January 6th Capitol riot committee.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent David Spunt has the latest tonight from the Justice Department. Good evening, David.\par \par \uc2 DAVID SPUNT, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Bret, good evening to you. After multiple delays, this trial is finally underway. Wrapping up for the day, just about an hour or so ago, federal prosecutors painted a picture of \uc2 Steve Bannon\uc2  as someone who blatantly ignored the requests for documents and a deposition by the January 6th committee.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT (voice-over): Bannon and his team left the courthouse. You see in there not long ago, after his attorneys asked to delay the trial by a month after the judge allowed new government evidence the judge again rejected the request to delay the trial.\par \par \uc2 Prosecutors began telling the jury that Bannon chose to show his contempt and decided he was above the law. Bannon is charged with two counts of criminal contempt of Congress. Each charge could land him from 30 days to a year behind bars.\par \par \uc2 The last time, Bret, someone was successfully prosecuted for this. 1974, Nixon aide G. Gordon Liddy. That's how rare it is for the Justice Department to actually prosecute this charge and win in court.\par \par \uc2 Late this afternoon, Bannon said he wanted the January 6 committee members personally, to come to that courtroom.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 STEVE BANNON\uc2  , FORMER WHITE HOUSE CHIEF STRATEGIST: We have a constitutional crisis in this nation right now, and they're charged me with a crime. Have the guts and the courage. The guts and the courage to show up here.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: Bannon is arguing he did not cooperate with the committee because he didn't have to. Citing executive privilege for his conversations with then- President Trump.\par \par \uc2 Federal prosecutors say, given the fact that Bannon left the White House in 2017, executive privilege is just not applicable in this case. Both sides are at odds over the extent of executive privilege for both former government employees and former presidents. The issue could wind up eventually at some point at the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT (on camera): Bannon's trial is expected to wrap up at the end of the week. Prosecutors say their case will take about a day. This all happens just a couple of days before the primetime hearing of the next January 6 committee hearing.\par \par \uc2 Bannon now, Bret, says that he's willing to testify in front of the committee in public, on camera. The committee says too little too late. They'll talk to him, they just want to do it behind the scenes because they are concerned that Bannon will grandstand in front of cameras. Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: David Spunt at the Justice Department. David, thanks.\par \par \uc2 As mentioned at the top, the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2  will vote shortly on the first step toward a scaled down version of a semiconductor chips bill. Senators and members of the House have already approved different versions of the measure.\par \par \uc2 Congressional Correspondent Chad Pergram has an update tonight on where things stand at this hour. Good evening, Chad.\par \par \uc2 CHAD PERGRAM, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONGRESSIONAL CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Bret, good evening.\par \par \uc2 The Biden administration is desperate for a win. That's why it's pushing a scaled down $52 billion-dollar package to bolster the production of semiconductors in the U.S.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 CHUCK SCHUMER\uc2  (D-NY): I urge my colleagues, if you want to fight inflation, if you want to create more good paying jobs here at home, America bring American jobs home.\par \par \uc2 If you want to keep America strong and competitive in the 21st century, support the CHIPS Act today.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM (voice-over): Democrats are trimming broader versions of the bill to just chips, because the House and Senate face an impasse trying to reach a compromise. But House Democrats insist on their plan, which includes taxes and trade oversight.\par \par \uc2 REP. RICHARD NEAL (D-MA): But I think that the House product was the superior product. And I think as best we can adhere to it. We should.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: Commerce Secretary \uc2 Gina Raimondo\uc2  is appealing to lawmakers to accept a slimmer bill based on national security concerns. There is anxiety that Chinese chips could be used to spy and interfere with American military technology.\par \par \uc2 GINA RAIMONDO\uc2  , UNITED STATES SECRETARY OF COMMERCE: You're not going to get everything right away. We should do a big bill over time. But don't let the perfect be the enemy of the good, because the only winner in that scenario is the People's Republic of China.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: Still, Republicans worry about spending $52 billion exclusively for chips.\par \par \uc2 REP. STEVE SCALISE (R-LA): If you look at where the biggest problem with our debt and deficits are right now is that so much new spending is moving into the mandatory side, which is, in essence, autopilot spending it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM (on camera): The Senate holds a test vote tonight to start debate on the chips bill. And the House has just voted to reaffirm same sex marriage. The vote there, 267 to 157. 47 Republicans voted yes. Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Chad Pergram live on the Hill. Chad, thank you.\par \par \uc2 BAIER (voice-over): Several Democratic members of Congress arrested today during a protest near the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  . \uc2 Capitol Police\uc2  had warned that they would arrest those who did not listen to the warnings to stop blocking traffic there.\par \par \uc2 Among those arrested, \uc2 Ilhan Omar\uc2  of Minnesota, \uc2 Alma Adams\uc2  of North Carolina, and New York's \uc2 Alexandria Ocasio-Cortez\uc2  , who appeared to be handcuffed but was not.\par \par \uc2 No lawmakers were handcuffed they were just escorted away. Police say 17 lawmakers were among the people taken into custody.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now, Massachusetts Democratic Congressman \uc2 Seth Moulton\uc2  and Utah Republican Chris Stewart. Gentlemen, thank you for being here.\par \par \uc2 BAIER (on camera): I want to talk to you about the bill that you're working on together. And on suicide prevention and how important that is. But I do want to ask you first about this semiconductor chips bill and where both of you see it from your different points of -- on both sides of the aisle of how this is coming down.\par \par \uc2 First, to Congressman Moulton.\par \par \uc2 REP. SETH MOULTON (D-MA): Sure. Well, competing against China should be a bipartisan priority. And it largely has been. I mean, the Senate passed a $250 billion version of this bill. I don't know why we're down to just a $50 billion bill.\par \par \uc2 I mean, obviously McConnell has said he's opposed to it. But the $50 billion is a step in the right direction. But it's sort of like saying, OK, Harvard's at the head of the pack, like we'll just fund Harvard.\par \par \uc2 But, what -- are we leaving everybody else out? We want the entire nation to be competitive against China. That's what the larger bill is about. I certainly hope we can pass that in a bipartisan way.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Congressman Stewart, the House Minority Whip Steve Scalise said that adding $30 plus or 50 plus billion in new mandatory spending is a problem for some Republicans because it's mandatory spending, he says that's driving our debt. Where do you stand?\par \par \uc2 REP. CHRIS STEWART (R-UT): Well, I agree with Steve, and I'm concerned about that. But I'm, I'm proud to stand here with my friend Seth and say, look, the bipartisan manner, we understand the existential threat that China is it is the generational threat that our we and our children are going to have to encounter part of that as the economic threat. And a big part of that is chip and chip production and moving that manufacturing back to the U.S.\par \par \uc2 We agree on that. I actually liked this bill, there's a lot in this bill that I think is not only a great idea, I think it's absolutely essential. There are some concerns, the debt is obviously one of them. I think having -- not having guardrails that are written into the language that, you know, actually confines how the money can be spent. I think there's some ways that bill could be improved.\par \par \uc2 Hopefully, we'll be able to do that, if not here in the House when the two bills come together in conference. But again, I think there's a lot of support for this idea that America should lead, as Mr. Moulton just said.\par \par \uc2 You know, America should lead on these technical technological issues.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Congressman Moulton, there's some critics who say that this effort today on the House debate on Respect for Marriage Act is kind of spinning wheels. In that, the Supreme Court didn't deal with that issue. And how do you say to your constituents that this is really important for the House to pass this bill?\par \par \uc2 MOULTON: Well, I don't think I have to say it, because Justice Thomas said that this is something that will be under review. And in a very bipartisan way, we pass this bill today, saying this is where the House of Representative stay -- stands. And I think that's the important for the American people to know.\par \par \uc2 I know that back home in Massachusetts, a lot of people care about having a vote of seeing a vote on this issue.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, Congressman Stewart, I want to ask you about this suicide prevention effort and the 988. And how it essentially transforms or how you envision a transforming health for mental health. Yes.\par \par \uc2 STEWART: Yes, this is just such an important bill. I mean, this will save 1,000s of lives. We know that. I mean, there is so many people who are desperate. If you're worried about your spouse, or one of your children, or a roommate, whoever it might be, if you're in the middle of a mental health crisis, there are 1-800 numbers all over the country.\par \par \uc2 The problem is, though some of them are clever, you still don't remember them, and they're different from city to city and state to state. 988, 3 digits. 988. That's all anyone has to know. And they'll immediately be put in contact with someone who can help them.\par \par \uc2 And again, if you're in the middle of this crisis, if you've given up hope, if you really feel life is no longer worth living, or I'll never feel better, I'll never feel happy again. We're telling you, there is hope. We can help you. 988. That's a simple solution. We're so pleased to work together on this.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes, you know, the Wall Street Journal Congressman Moulton says, "One in six calls to national suicide prevention lifeline end without reaching a counsellor: Network about 200 call centers that answers calls to the line is already under some strain. 1.5 million of 9.2 million calls between 2016 and 2021 were abandoned by callers or disconnected before a counsellor could respond, and it goes on. That's one of the reasons that you all are pushing this 988 number.\par \par \uc2 MOULTON: That's right. I mean, we heard a lot of this criticism, people saying that they weren't ready for this bill. The call centers wouldn't be ready. We've actually increased federal funding 18-fold since last year just to meet this demand. And the whole point as Chris said, is that there are more people out there who need help that are getting help.\par \par \uc2 And we know that having this three digit number available, 988, that everyone can remember means more Americans will get help in that desperate moment. And one of the amazing statistics about with all the horrible numbers around suicides across America, is that an extraordinary number of people who attempt suicide, but don't complete it, never try again. They regret it.\par \par \uc2 So, it just shows if you can get help in that moment, or if a loved one can get connected to someone they can talk to. We're going to save a lot of lives.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Sir --\par \par \uc2 MOULTON: And it's no mistake that there are two veterans working on this. We understand this as vets very well.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes, quickly. First of all, this -- what's the process here? What -- what's the timeline?\par \par \uc2 STEWART: Well, it went live recently Bret. I mean, just a few days ago, and so, it's now rolled out nationally.\par \par \uc2 A few states have a little bit of a bandwidth problem but those calls are now being forced to other states. It's live and we're -- and indications we have is the role has been very successful.\par \par \uc2 Very, very briefly, if I could, a family I know in Utah, a beautiful young teenage daughter. Her last call was to a help center that did not answer of the phone. She had to leave a message, and then, she shortly after committed suicide. That is the tragedy we're trying to avoid here.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes, a lot of people don't understand that there are things happening on a bipartisan level on a number of different fronts. We'd like to highlight those here on SPECIAL REPORT.\par \par \uc2 Congressman, thank you very much for the time.\par \par \uc2 REP. SETH MOULTON (D-MA): Thank you, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Wall Street had its best day in more than three weeks with positive corporate earnings reports. The Dow surging 754 today, the S&P 500 gained 106, the NASDAQ jumped 353.\par \par \uc2 Tesla\uc2  CEO \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  has lost his fight to delay \uc2 Twitter\uc2  's lawsuit against him over his withdrawal from a deal to buy the social media site. A Delaware judge has set an October trial. He says delay threatens irreparable harm to the company.\par \par \uc2 Twitter\uc2  is trying to force Musk to make good on his April promise to buy the company for $44 billion. Musk says \uc2 Twitter\uc2  has failed to provide adequate information about the number of fake accounts.\par \par \uc2 Up next, Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  appears to compare abortion rights to slavery. A lot of people weighing in on that, we'll check the fallout.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: The CDC is reporting fentanyl related overdose deaths are up 44 percent for black people, 39 percent for American Indian and Alaska Native people. The statistics are from the year 2020. Deaths increased for whites by 24 percent. And that is a historically high figure.\par \par \uc2 Charges have been dropped against a New York City bodega worker arrested for killing a man who attacked him. The Manhattan District Attorney's office endured harsh criticism for charging Jose Alba with murder. The 61- year-old clerk was seen on video stabbing his 35-year-old attacker at a convenience store July 1st.\par \par \uc2 Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  is taking criticism tonight for what some