that kind of language has been part of his playbook, like when he said this in 2012 about \uc2 Mitt Romney\uc2  and Republicans.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Unchain Wall Street. They're going to put you all back in chains.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (on camera): After President Biden won in 2020, he said it was time to put away the harsh rhetoric and lower the temperature. More than a year and a half later, his spokesperson says comparing pro-life laws to slavery is OK, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Peter Doocy in the White House Briefing Room. Peter, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Up next, it's primary day in Maryland. We'll tell you why it's shaping up as a battle between the current governor there and former President Trump.\par \par \uc2 First, here's what some of our Fox affiliates around the country are covering tonight. Fox 5 in Las Vegas as a transformer at the Hoover Dam catches fire, leading to an explosion and plumes of smoke there.\par \par \uc2 Boulder City Fire Department says this morning's blaze was extinguished when the crews arrived. No injuries reported, there was no risk to the power grid.\par \par \uc2 Fox 5 in New York as a powerful storm system triggers flash flooding in some communities. The system brought strong gusty winds, heavy downpours. National Weather Service issued several weather alerts for New York, New Jersey and Connecticut.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at Pensacola, Florida. One of the big stories there tonight, the U.S. Navy's Blue Angels names the first woman as the demonstration pilot for the 2023 airshow season.\par \par \uc2 Lieutenant Amanda Lee of Mounds View, Minnesota is the first woman to join the iconic flight squadron as a pilot. Lee and the other pilots will begin a five month training program later this year.\par \par \uc2 That's tonight's live look outside the Beltway from SPECIAL REPORT. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Breaking tonight much of Europe is experiencing record temperatures as a brutal heatwave rolls through the continent. Britain shattered its all-time record with a reading of 104.4 degrees, Fahrenheit, of course, at \uc2 Heathrow Airport\uc2  . London's mayor says the temperatures are causing a huge surge in the number of fires in the city. One grass fire in Winnington required more than 100 firefighters to come to that scene.\par \par \uc2 Ferocious wildfires continue in southwestern France. More than 2,000 firefighters have been deployed there. Tens of thousands of people have been evacuated. Nearly 750 health related deaths have already been reported in Spain and neighboring Portugal. More than 30 forest fires around Spain have forced thousands of evacuations.\par \par \uc2 Many parts of this country also dealing with record heat. Robert Ray of FOX Weather has specifics from Round Rock, Texas which can't be cool today. Good evening, Robert.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT RAY, FOX WEATHER: Good evening, Bret. I found a little shade here in the lone star state. And I will say over 100 million people across the U.S. are experiencing excessive heat warnings. Not a good situation, 60 million of those people, Bret, are in the lower Mississippi Valley, the southern plains, and here in Texas. Dallas, the big D, expected to be about 110 soon. Oklahoma City actually did hit 110 degrees. It's the first time since August of 2011. And the state capital of Austin about 110. San Antonio, El Paso, even over to Shreveport, Louisiana, Bret.\par \par \uc2 And you see that big orange ball. That is the sun, and indeed it's been wreaking havoc here for weeks. Dallas today seeing temps around 110 and the same all over the state of Texas. That 10-day forecast is unreal. It shows no relief. Temps will continue to range in the triple digits with lows just in the 80s at night, Bret. And dangerous conditions as people are trying to stay cool in ponds, lakes, rivers, fountains, and even the air 