d our Borders tonight, China says it will take resolute and strong measures should House Speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  proceed with reported plans to visit Taiwan. "The \uc2 Financial Times\uc2  " reports Pelosi is due to visit the self-governing island China claims as its own territory in August. She would be the highest ranking American lawmaker to visit the close U.S. ally since then speaker \uc2 Newt Gingrich\uc2  traveled there 25 years ago.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at Venice from Earth TV. One of the big stories there in Italy tonight, the 98-year-old Italian man has become the country's oldest graduate again. Giuseppe Paterno has added a masters in history and philosophy from the \uc2 University of Palermo\uc2  to an initial degree in the same subjects he earned there two years ago. He has no plans to rest and wants to write a novel using his trusty typewriter, he says. Congratulations.\par \par \uc2 Just some of the other stories Beyond our Borders tonight. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GINA RAIMONDO\uc2  , COMMERCE SECRETARY: You're not going to get everything right away. We should do a big bill over time. But don't let the perfect be the enemy of the good, because the only winner in that scenario is the People's Republic of China.\par \par \uc2 REP. HAKEEM JEFFRIES, (D-NY) HOUSE DEMOCRATIC CAUCUS CHAIR: If it substantially moves the ball forward for the American people, I expect that it will have support here in the House of Representatives.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I think we've got to look at these bills and make sure we get a return. We have got to get our fiscal house in order.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: This is a chips bill that deals with the shortage of semiconductor chips in the U.S., competition with China, other countries, and the importance of that. Obviously, it also comes after this story that we brought you earlier, \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  , "Speaker Pelosi's husband buys millions in chip stocks right before vote on massive chip subsidy. \uc2 Paul Pelosi\uc2  purchased 20,000 shares of \uc2 Nvidia\uc2  , one of the world's latest semiconductor companies on June 17th according to the speaker's disclosure report released Thursday. Obviously, Speaker Pelosi would be aware of the timing of this legislation over in the Senate," according to Representative Norman.\par \par \uc2 What about this bill and other things? Let's bring in our panel, \uc2 Josh Kraushaar\uc2 , "Axios" senior political correspondent, syndicated radio host \uc2 Hugh Hewitt\uc2  , and former White House press secretary \uc2 Ari Fleischer\uc2  . Ari, let's talk big picture. First of all, the importance of doing something like this. There are skeptics up on Capitol Hill, but it seems like it's gaining bipartisan momentum.\par \par \uc2 ARI FLEISCHER\uc2  , FORMER WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: Yes. Any time you put the label "anti-China" on it, it's a great way to pick up support from both parties. And we should be anti-China. But I would prefer us to be anti- China by getting China to back down on this intellectual property theft and other actions that China takes that makes them a rogue nation that steals from the west.\par \par \uc2 I'm not sure that providing corporate subsidies, which is really what this is, is the solution. I always worried that Washington solution is to spend billions and just give it to corporations because it's the chosen industry of the week or the month or the year. It has gotten us into trouble before. It can be wasteful, and then the markets correct, and we've already spent the billions. So I understand the impetus, but I'm not sure about the substance.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Fair enough. Hugh, thoughts about that? And the proponents, obviously, of this bill say something is needed to jumpstart this industry to bring it back to the U.S.\par \par \uc2 HUGH HEWITT, SYNDICATED RADIO HOST: Rare is the day, Bret, that I disagree with Ari, but today is one of those days. We need four things in the United States -- clean water, clean air, good food, and semiconductor chips. We used to make 37 percent of the chips in the world. That was as recently as 1990. We're down to 12 percent. The $54 billion in chips plus, and there might be another $24 billion, the $54 billion is subsidy, the $24 billion is tax credit, they go to manufacturing plants like the new one announced in Ohio. There's a new one in Arizona. Both of those are from intel. We need to onshore semiconductor production. We need it for national security.\par \par \uc2 So unlike a lot of the infrastructure spend last year, this $54 billion makes a lot of sense to me. As \uc2 Rick Scott\uc2  said in the clip leading up, we've got to see a return on the investment. This one will see fabrication plants in places like Ohio because of \uc2 Mike DeWine\uc2  's efforts.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Josh, a lot of people don't follow the intricacies of what's in bills, including this one, what's good, what's bad. They leave it to lawmakers to make those cases. But they do follow something like \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  's husband making a big stock buy. Now she is going, reportedly, to Taiwan. Don't know if she is going to be talking about semiconductors, but that story kind of bleeds through in the middle of all of this.\par \par \uc2 JOSH KRAUSHAAR\uc2 , "NATIONAL JOURNAL": Yes. Any time you have any perceived conflict of interest, whether it's stock trading or investments, it's going to raise some important questions. But I think the bigger picture, Bret, is that we're actually seeing a little bit of a flurry of bipartisan activity in Congress. If this passes the Senate it looks like it will get a significant number of Republican senators joining with Democrats in passing this legislation. That will be the second bill in two months, when you include the gun control and mental health funding bill that passed through Congress last month.\par \par \uc2 So even though like the big items that Biden is looking to get, trying to push the climate change legislation, a lot more government spending, that has hit a road block because it doesn't have public support. Some of these small bills, like Hugh said, increases the competitiveness of the semiconductor industry, helping out intel, these are things that have wide political support. And it looks like they are getting bipartisan backing in Congress.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We'll follow it. It might be a small bill that's just on strictly the chips, or it might include some other things. We'll see about the vote tonight.\par \par \uc2 Ari, the fallout from the president's trip overseas, "Wall Street Journal," "Biden Saudi Arabia's visit was worse than an embarrassment. The trip was worse than a missed opportunity. It damaged U.S. national security interest in the Middle East by highlighting the world that neither Saudi Arabia nor other Gulf states trust the U.S. enough to make any sacrifices to renew badly frayed relations. In a speech to Arab leaders, the president proclaimed we will not walk away and leave a vacuum to be filled by China, Russia, or Iran. That fell on doubting ears from a man they watched walk away from Afghanistan." Thoughts?\par \par \uc2 FLEISCHER: Yes, and the ostensible purpose of it was to get the Saudis to pump more oil. And that doesn't seem to be happening. You would have thought if that was happening it would be heralded. The Biden administration would proudly announce it. And as for advancing peace through the Middle East, I think that really is now beyond the United States. It's going to be up to Israel and Saudi to deal with each other, and they seem to be content to have those quiet conversations. They will figure it out at their own pace.\par \par \uc2 So I don't know what was accomplished other than the fist-bump, which I have got to say, if you are going to go, go and shake the man's hand. If you are not going to go