ctive suicide because of climate.\par \par \uc2 And I think the elites are so frenzied, they all go to the same cocktail parties. They all talk to each other. They all come to an agreement that they're going to say this nonsense, and going to reinforce each other. But the truth is, it's false.\par \par \uc2 Just as by the way, the same elites were lying to us about COVID as we now grudgingly slowly are learning. So I think the average person will realize, your elites are lying to you.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Now, Newt, there was a moment at the White House briefing today where the Press Secretary was asked about the President's relative absence of the last three days from the public scene. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: What exactly has he been doing yesterday and today?\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE DEPUTY PRESS SECRETARY: So he's been in meetings. He's been meeting with his senior staff, he's been meeting with a staff. He's just been very busy dealing with the issues of the American people and meeting with his staff and senior staff, the last two days.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Newt, lots of meetings, even the left is turning on him.\par \par \uc2 GINGRICH: Look, I don't know whether I prefer \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  publicly incomprehensible and suffering clearly a cognitive decline, or \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  hiding while his staff protects him as he suffers cognitive decline.\par \par \uc2 But certainly, if you watch him on the trip to the Middle East, it was a total embarrassment for the United States and for those of us who believe in freedom. And I just think you have to start with the idea that this guy is being manipulated by his staff, whatever they're about to do with the environment will be an act of desperation.\par \par \uc2 But given that they've given \uc2 John Kerry\uc2  a major role, you just have to assume it'll be crazy, because Kerry is crazy. These are people who believe in a fantasy world that does not exist, could not possibly be defended in a serious public debate.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Newt, great to see you, as always, tonight. Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 And so if Biden were to go forward with these extraordinary plans to circumvent Congress on these climate moves, how quickly would it be smacked down in court?\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is Jonathan Turley, law professor at \uc2 George Washington University\uc2 , Fox News contributor. Professor, the question came up yesterday and a friend of mine who clerked with me said, that's not going to last long. But is it that clear cut? And could the concern about the court be the reason for the hesitation by the administration?\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN TURLEY, GWU LAW PROFESSOR: Well, it is troubling to hear some of the rhetoric in the last 48 hours. You had a U.S. Senator say that the chains are off President Biden now. Why? Because Congress didn't yield to his demands.\par \par \uc2 You have others that just today moved to court packing bill forward. The message seems to be that we support the system so long as it yields to our demands. I mean, you look at the climate change issue. The administration tried to go at this in a unilateral way with the \uc2 EPA\uc2  case, which was just handed down by the Supreme Court. They lost by a considerable measure.\par \par \uc2 The Supreme Court found that the Biden ministration exceeded its constitutional authority. They then went to Congress and sought a climate change and other measures there that was blocked by not just Joe Manchin, but about half of the Senate supported his position.\par \par \uc2 So what's the answer? Now, while they're going back to where they began and said, Well, the President should go at it alone. And in a (inaudible) democracy, it's often as important how you do something is what you do. What they're arguing for is for basically a single-person government. They're asking for President to yield this authority alone. And that is a very dangerous thing. It's convenient. But it's a remarkably short sighted viewpoint.\par \par \uc2 And when you have these members of Congress calling for their own circumvention, their own planned obsolescence, it's something that James Madison would never have imagined. He believed that you could have ambition, fighting ambition. But here the ambition is to become a non- entity in our three branch system.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Right. Well, they don't want to take any of these tough votes and then be held accountable for the votes. I think that's part of it. But the same time, which people I think, have forgotten.\par \par \uc2 And I know you and I've talked about this before, but this idea of an emergency being declared, we were very concerned during COVID on the state level when governors were declaring emergencies to circumvent the legislature. We still have the COVID emergency on a federal level in effect today.\par \par \uc2 And apparently, this is just going to go on forever. There's going to be no end to the COVID emergency, and then they may try to lay this new emergency on top of the COVID emergency. What's next? The racial emergency? I can see that coming.\par \par \uc2 TURLEY: Right. And the danger is really how these things are being implemented. I agree with the President on many environmental issues. But it's very important for me to see the system work. And that means taking these matters to the representatives of the people.\par \par \uc2 It's not enough to say, Well, they didn't give me what I wanted. The system is designed to force these divisive issues into Congress, and to forge a compromise. If you can't, then you've got to forge a new majority in Congress. We have midterm elections coming up.\par \par \uc2 The only thing you're not supposed to do, is to give the concentration of power in the hands of one individual. That's what this entire system was designed to avoid. So this emergency approach is just a fancy way of saying, one person rule to have the President do what they couldn't get done in Congress and couldn't get through the courts.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: And, professor, to think of the fact that they used to say that Trump was acting as the dictator. Remember that, Trump was a - now who is the dictator, or want to be a dictator? Great to see you as always Professor Turley. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Now, this hysteria pulling us backward doesn't just relate to climate, but also back to COVID. A California doctor just uncovered video showing that LA's pending move to implement masking. It's based on complete lies. The video and that doc are here in moments.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: California Governor \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2  fancies himself as a genuine 2024 contender. He's a leader of the - what he claims is the state that values freedom. Now, the reality is that he's fostered this environment when where the power, hungry and corrupt wield immeasurable power.\par \par \uc2 And on no issue has that been more apparent than COVID in California. The hysteria about the next wave on the horizon, it's allowed those in charge to make devastating decisions for their entire population.\par \par \uc2 Now, first, I want you to watch this exchange with the board president of the \uc2 San Diego Unified School District\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Students and parents who don't want to wear a mask indoors in school, are there any other options for them?\par \par \uc2 SHARON WHITEHURST-PAYNE, SAN DIEGO UNIFIED BOARD PRESIDENT: They can opt not to return to the regular school, but to go to the school where they don't have to go to school at all other than via Zoom.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: What if they were already enrolled in the summer school and now they get this mask mandate and they're not comfortable with wearing a mask?\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: They really should wear the mask.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: But if they're not comfortable, what should they do?\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: They should just let make it known that they don't feel comfortable and at that point, just not return.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: But the rot in California goes deeper than what you just heard from that woman.\par \par \uc2 Medical officials, including LA's public health director \uc2 Barbara Ferrer\uc2  had been lying to the public about the severity of the COVID cases currently in hospitals. Now watch Ferrer last week juxtapose with smoking gun video from the doctors at USC's Medical Center.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BARBARA FERRER\uc2 , DIRECTOR, LOS ANGELES COUNTY DEPARTMENT OF PUBLIC HEALTH: We're also experiencing continued increases in the number of COVID positive hospitalized patients over the past week. Among those hospitalized who are infected with COVID, about 42 percent are hospitalized with COVID-related illness. Sadly, we're starting to see increases in deaths for the first time since the winter surge.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Only 10 percent of our COVID positive admissions are admitted due to COVID. Virtually, none of them go to the ICU. And when they do go to the ICU, it is not for pneumonia. They are not intubated. They are not these horrible 100 percent of file. We haven't seen one of those since February. It's been months.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: That is devastating. Here now is the man who uncovered that video, Dr. Houman Hemmati, board-certified MD and research scientist based in California.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Hemmati, I'd heard this from friends in Boston, the same narrative that the people who came into hospitals would test positive incidentally, when they were there for something else. That was called a COVID case, even though it wasn't. Those officials lying should never serve in public again.\par \par \uc2 HOUMAN HEMMATI, PHD RESEARCH SCIENTIST: Absolutely. I mean,