n a good note, get your USA made Freedom Matters gear at LauraIngraham.com. All proceeds for the month of July will be donated to the Woodson Center. Get the "Hannity" golf hat. This is awesome. Get the towel for your kids at the beach, awesome. All USA made. It says "Matter," but it says "Freedom Matters." I can't hold the whole thing up, it will all fall over. But you get the drill. Go to LauraIngraham.com, it's fun stuff.\par \par \uc2 Up next, why are American companies allowed to consult for the Pentagon while helping Chinese entities at the same time? Senator \uc2 Josh Hawley\uc2  wants to put an end to this. He's here to tell us next who's standing in the way. Stay there.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ALEX SAWAYA, SENIOR PARTNER, MCKINSEY AND COMPANY: China's leaders have experienced extraordinary growth of the past several years. During this time McKinsey has partnered with many of these players to unlock the power of A.I. and advanced analytics to drive innovation to transform operations and create entirely new business models.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN WEI WANG, SENIOR PARTNER, MCKINSEY AND COMPANY: Black China enables us to tap into global talent and cutting-edge technologies.\par \par \uc2 TIFFANY KWOK, PARTNER, \uc2 MCKINSEY AND COMPANY\uc2 : Combining leading minds with leading technology, this creates a new sources of competitive advantage which we call hybrid intelligence.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: That creepy video was just one of dozens you can find on consulting giant McKinsey's \uc2 Twitter\uc2  and web pages. Despite the bad look, the bad music, U.S. companies comingling with Chinese entities is nothing new, but should it be allowed if these same companies consult with our own Pentagon?\par \par \uc2 Our next guest is trying to put a stop to this, introducing legislation that would end this compromised practice of double dipping. Joining us now is Missouri Senator \uc2 Josh Hawley\uc2 , member of the Homeland Security Committee. Senator, my first question is, how is this not already barred by U.S. law given the fact that we don't get that type of reciprocity when dealing with sensitive matters that China has access to?\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 JOSH HAWLEY\uc2 , (R-MO): Yes, that is certainly the case. Laura, listen, it should absolutely be illegal, and this bill would make it so. Here's the real thing. We've got these corporations, lots of them, who want to take a buck from Uncle Sam, who want to get contracts from Uncle Sam, but they are happy to sell out our technology, our jobs, our workers, and they have been doing it for years. And it's time to put a stop to it. The least we can do is say if you can get a contract with the Defense Department of the United States, you shouldn't be advising the Chinese government. And that's what this legislation would do.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Senator, also President Biden has been dragging his feet, although we know which way he's going to go, in making this decision on whether to lift Trump's tariffs on China. Here's how the White House actually responded to questions yesterday.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE DEPUTY PRESS SECRETARY: The way that it was handled by the last administration was, was -- it was bungled. If you, if you -- if I'm going to be really honest here, it was not done in an effective way.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Senator, bungled? Trump took China head-on really smartly. They did that long report, \uc2 Bob Lighthizer\uc2 , the USTR, probably the most talented one we've ever had in our history, this was done meticulously. He didn't cower to the Chinese like Biden is obviously beginning to do.\par \par \uc2 HAWLEY: I think that the tariffs on China were some of the best things that President Trump did. And we all know what's going to happen here. \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is going to cave. He is going to roll over for the Chinese Communist Party. He's going to let them have everything back that they wanted. All I can say if you like what you're getting from Biden, if you want to send more jobs overseas to China, if you want to have to buy our energy from China, if you want to be dependent on China, then \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is your guy because that is his entire policy.\par \par \uc2 Btu for those of us who want jobs in America, good paying jobs, manufacturing jobs in this country, those tariffs are important. And I think we shouldn't roll them back, Laura. We ought to be strengthening them. That ought to be our policy.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: \uc2 Bob Lighthizer\uc2  actually wrote an op-ed in "The Wall Street Journal" today that was brilliant talking about how lifting the tariffs -- everyone knows it's not going to have any real effect on inflation. It's only going to make Chinese products stronger in the United States and hurt U.S. manufacturing at a time where I thought Biden was supposed to be improving U.S. manufacturing. That's, I guess, out the window totally.\par \par \uc2 HAWLEY: Here's the deal, the Chinese government and the Chinese economy is dependent on slave labor. Let's just tell the truth. It is slave labor that powers much of the Chinese economy. And what effectively is happening is the Biden administration wants U.S. workers to have to compete against slave labor, which will end up strengthening the slave labor. It will fund them. It will impoverish our workers. It will send our industry overseas. This is what the liberals, and unfortunately, many Republicans had been pushing for decades now.\par \par \uc2 We know what the results are. The results are jobs leaving this country. The results are hollowed out industry. We need to reverse all of that, and that's why it is important to say that we are going to hold China accountable, that we are going to protect and nurture our own industry in this country, and we are going to protect American workers. That ought to be our policy.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Senator Hawley, there's so much I want to get to tonight, but unfortunately, I guess we are out of time. Thank you so much for joining us, as always.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MIKE POMPEO\uc2 , FORMER U.S. SECRETARY OF STATE: If my kid was on TikTok, they would have his Social Security number, they'd know what his face looked like, they'd know who his friends were, they'd know all the people in his contact list or who he reached out to and had different chats. That's dangerous to the individual, but cumulatively they're using this data in ways that will come back to haunt the United States of America for years and years and years.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Former secretary of state and former CIA head \uc2 Mike Pompeo\uc2  offering what should be the final word on TikTok, but it still enjoys immense protection in Democratic circles. Last June \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  reversed a decision that was smartly made by President Trump to ban the Chinese owned app in the U.S. That turned out to be a devastating decision for American security as last month we learned that engineers from TikTok's parent company called ByteDance had actually accessed information on the apps U.S. users from inside China.\par \par \uc2 That has led my next guest to send a letter to the company CEO demanding answers. Here now is ranking member of the House Oversight Committee Congressman James Comer. Congressman, we have been on this TikTok thing since Pompeo was in there and the State Department. Is there any reason why we should trust the folks at ByteDance? Don't you hate that name? But is there any reason we should actually trust them?\par \par \uc2 REP. JAMES COMER, (R-KY) HOUSE OVERSIGHT RANKING MEMBER: No. In fact, they may have given false testimony to Congress. Their employees at TikTok testified to Congress. We now have learned that was false testimony about their security provisions and how they handled Americans' data.\par \par \uc2 So what we know is TikTok has access, has granted Communist China access to our browser histories and our locations. So when you're talking about browser histories, that's one thing. That is obviously a way for China to have a marketing edge over American companies. But when you're talking about location, that is a national security threat. And that's we, the Republicans, on the Energy and Commerce Committee as well as the House Oversight Committee are demanding answers from TikTok, and obviously the Democrats are sitting back, and they continue to give China a free pass.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Meanwhile, the DNC is going all in, Congressman, on TikTok. According to "Politico" they took the dive into the platform four months ago, and officials are so far happy with the results. But while the committee is pushing out its party's leader to millions upon millions of young users who congregate on TikTok, Biden hasn't necessarily been the party's most of viral messenger. Unsurprising considering Biden is coming across like a grandpa.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (MUSIC)\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: OK, Congressman, it's funny, but it's so stupid and annoying. But should the White House be promoting the use of this app?\par \par \uc2 COMER: No. No, not at all. And I think they have already received enough information from Republicans to know that TikTok is a problem. And when Pompeo was talking about things like facial recognition, who knows exactly what TikTok is doing? And all of her kids are using TikTok, that's the most popular app among teenagers now. And it's just another example of China getting their foot in the door in America. And we have been saying for months that China is a national security threat, especially through big tech.\par \par \uc2 And what is frustrating for Republicans in Congress, we have asked for hearings so that there could be transparency among what big tech is doing with respect to China. We have evidence now that TikTok through their parent company ByteDance is sharing American data, privacy concerns, with the Chinese government.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Congressman, they collect contact lists according to the piece and the Australian-U.S. Cybersecurity firm analysis. They access your calendars, they scan your hard drives including external ones to geolocate you, contact lists. This is what they are collecting. So parents, don't let your kid download that on your phone. Congressman, great to see you.\par \par \uc2 The shocking, amazing, irrepressible stupidity of \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , it was on full display at the \uc2 NAACP\uc2  conference. The highlights and Amala Ekpunobi in moments. Stay there.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  is making waves ahead of 2024 but unfortunately for her each appearance she makes does more and more to expose her immense vapidity. Take her speech the \uc2 NAACP\uc2  yesterday.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , (D) VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: It's important to note that to support a woman's ability, not her government, but her, to make that decision does not require anyone to abandon their faith or their beliefs.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Joining me now is Amala Ekpunobi, former leftwing activist and now PragerU personality, for tonight's newest segment, "Kamala with Amala." You have your own graphic. That's funny. Amala, first of all, I never understand her hand gestures. I don't know where she was pointing. But now supporting abortion for her doesn't require abandoning faith. I guess she is now a theologian, because she has already handled that border so well.\par \par \uc2 AMALA EKPUNOBI, PRAGERU PERSONALITY: First of all, Laura, I want to say, I want to publicly distance myself from this woman. I know our names are very similar, but we bear no relation and no other similarities. And everything that she said during this \uc2 NAACP\uc2  statement was just ridiculous. To say that you are not abandoning your faith by supporting this is an asinine statement to make. I'm pretty sure, I'm not even a religious person, but the Bible states thou shalt not kill. I think that's a direct correlation to the abortion discussion that we're having right now in this country. For her to say that is just as stupid as she normally is on camera.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: There was something else, Amala, that she said that was perhaps even more offensive.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , (D) VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: The \uc2 United States Supreme Court\uc2  took a constitutional right that had been recognized from the people of America, from the women of America. We know \uc2 NAACP\uc2  that that our country has a history of claiming ownership over human bodies.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Amala, she draws a direct line between abortion -- and, obviously, the pro-life community wants to save all babies regardless of skin color -- and slavery. Your reaction to that?\par \par \uc2 EKPUNOBI: It's such a ridiculous statement to make. And I do want to point out that this is what happens when you allow the government to do affirmative action hiring. She should never have been the vice president of this country, let alone in government. But we hired her because she's black and she's female, and that's where we're at right now. And that's why we're getting these statements.\par \par \uc2 I feel like every time I'm coming on this show I have to tell the left, guys, this is not the time to make a slavery comparison. And again, I'm saying, guys, this is not the time to make a slavery comparison. What the Supreme Court did is they simply said, hey, this is not in our hands. It's not backed by the Constitution. Let's democratically hand this down to the states so that your elected representatives get to make the decision on this, meaning you get your voice back on this issue.\par \par \uc2 And to go in front of somebody like the \uc2 NAACP\uc2  and say that this is a slavery issue and make that comparison is just an insult to anybody who has been affected by that. And it's an insult to true American history.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Yes, Amala, especially when Margaret Sanger, the great eugenicist, who, frankly, wanted to get rid of as many undesirable people as possible with the abortion procedure, does she not know about that? It's very, very disturbing. But I promise we will use that graphic very judiciously in the future, Amala. But it's great to see you, as always.\par \par \uc2 EKPUNOBI: Please.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: You take care.\par \par \uc2 What major fact did the White House get wrong this week? Tonight's edition of "Reading with KJP" explains, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: In tonight's edition of "Reading with KJP," we take a look at how well she knows the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE DEPUTY PRESS SECRETARY: As we know from the Dobbs decision, one of the things that we saw from Judge Thompson (ph) is that they are looking to go further.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: It's Justice Thomas. Small matter.\par \par \uc2 That's it for us tonight. "GUTFELD!" next.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document INGANG0020220720ei7j00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 D.A. Drops Murder Charge Against NYC Bodega Worker; "Law And Order" Worker Shot Dead On NYC Set; AOC Among 34 Arrested At SCOTUS Abortion Rally; Rep. \uc2 Mike Gallagher\uc2  (R-WI) Is Being Interviewed About Giving Credit To President Biden To What The White House Claimed The President Has Made A Difference In Bringing The Gas Prices Down And Relying On American Energy; White House: Biden Actions Helped Bring Down Gas Prices; Poll: Biden Approval Dropping Among Democrats; Border Patrol K9 Sniffs Out 250 Pounds Of Fentanyl; Pulitzer Rejects Trump Call To Revoke 2018 Awards; Long Lines At Food Banks As Inflation Spikes\b0\par\par\uc2 John Roberts, Gillian Turner, Shannon Bream, Alexis McAdams, Peter  Doocy, Marc Thiessen, Joe Concha, William La Jeunesse, Howard Kurtz, Lydia  Hu \par \uc2 8,239 \uc2 words\par \uc2 19 July 2022\par \uc2 Fox News Channel: The Story with Martha MacCallum\par \uc2 HUNDD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 JOHN ROBERTS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Wow, when you undermine the roadway like that, anything can happen. Great to spend the afternoon with you.\par \par \uc2 GILLIAN TURNER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Thanks for having me. I had a good time too.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: Anytime. Thanks for joining us on this Tuesday. We'll see you again tomorrow. I'm John Roberts.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: I'm Gillian Turner and THE STORY starts right now. Shannon Bream is in for Martha.\par \par \uc2 SHANNON BREAM, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Thank you John and Gillian. Good afternoon, everyone. I am Shannon Bream in for Martha MacCallum. Right now on THE STORY: case dismissed. The New York City bodega worker charged with murder for stabbing a man who attacked him is in the clear. The Manhattan District Attorney dropping the charge after the New York Post got a hold of surveillance video, you've all seen it, shows Jose Alba was defending himself. Frank Garcia has been fighting to clear Alba's name. He joins us in just a minute.\par \par \uc2 First to correspondent Alexis McAdams reporting live from New York City. Hey, Alexis.\par \par \uc2 ALEXIS MCADAMS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: Hi, Shannon, well, everyone across the country and here in New York City was waiting to hear what was going to happen with this case and we got it today. The Manhattan D.A.'s office says after they reviewed the surveillance video and interviewed witnesses and those people who called 9-1-1 that day, they will not be presenting this case to a grand jury. The mayor of New York says he supports the D.A.s decision.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ERIC ADAMS, NEW YORK CITY MAYOR: I believe after the day's review. The D.A., in my opinion, made the right decision.\par \par \uc2 MCADAMS: Now, earlier this month, Jose Alba, who you see here on your right was charged with second degree murder after police say he killed a man inside of a bodega in New York City. So, Alba was in jail when this shocking surveillance video was released. Here you can see 35-year-old, Austin Simon, there, run right behind the counter of that bodega and attacked the 61-year-old store clerk. Watch there as Simon shoves Alba right into the wall and grabs him by the collar.\par \par \uc2 Moments later, Alba grabs a knife and stabbed Simon three times to death. According to court documents. This all started after Simon's girlfriend tried to buy her daughter a snack with her EBT card that was declined. In the motion filed today, the Manhattan DA 's office says, "If Alba reasonably believed that Simon was committing or attempting to commit a burglary of an occupied building, then Alba was permitted to use deadly physical force upon Simon, if Alba reasonably believed it to be necessary to prevent or terminate commission of that burglary."\par \par \uc2 The Bodega Workers Union, we've been checking with them, and they've been having a lot of rally Shannon to try and get these charges dropped, saying the clerk was acting in self defense launching that case into the national spotlight. The Bodega Association president tells Fox News after several sleepless nights Alba's happy with the decision.\par \par \uc2 FRANCISCO MARTE, BODEGAS AND SMALL BUSINESS ASSOC: We are very sorry because the live loss is alive. You know, we have really sorry but we have to understand the fact the way how it happened. He came to attack --\par \par \uc2 MCADAMS: And you can see that attack there on camera. Also, Simon's girlfriend stabbed Alba during that fight. You could see some of those injuries there in his arm. That girlfriend has not been charged. Shannon.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right, Alexis, thank you very much. My next guest spoke to D.A. Alvin Bragg about Alba's case, Frank Garcia is Chairman of the National Association of Latino State Chambers of Commerce. Frank, great to have you with us, your reaction to all these charges being dropped.\par \par \uc2 FRANK GARCIA, CHAIRMAN, NATIONAL ASSOCIATION OF LATINO: Thank you. Very happy, very relief. It does open up Pandora's box for our membership because we feel that this is not Florida. The laws really don't protect our members. And it showed us, if we didn't come together, all the different Latino leadership group and the Republican City Council Caucus, we would have had a member in jail for 30 years that didn't deserve to be in jail.\par \par \uc2 We were ready to go to court tomorrow. And I have members calling it, "is a cancelled?" But we're very happy and relief that he's free and, and we look forward to being with him on Thursday where we're going to have a rally like a victory rally for, for him on Thursday in City Hall.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Frank, I know that you think this is just one case that kind of highlights and shines a spotlight on the bigger problem, the wider problem of other stores, of other outlets, of other bodegas, where crime has become a real problem and your members are very worried about this. Not just this one incident, but incident after incident after incident.\par \par \uc2 GARCIA: Yes, this is something that's happening every day. I want to thank Fox News. And, and Tucker for being our voice in the community. I never thought I would say that as a Latino leader, but I am because your voice showing a bodega owner out of Brooklyn where her son had the same issue that they tried to take a, beer out. And he, this -- he would have been the second case. So, we're very, very concerned we feel that this is going to be a Pandora's box.\par \par \uc2 It's going to open a lot of issues if the Democrats really don't sit down with us. The (INAUDIBLE), the, the -- our congressman hasn't said anything, (INAUDIBLE), none of our politicians that are Latino, stay quiet. But now they want to call my counterparts to say oh, he's out, where were you when we were fighting on this? So, that's really the issue now, it's me and my counterparts really looking back and meeting with the D.A., we have a meeting on Friday with him to really come up with how do we go from now?\par \par \uc2 How do we make sure this happens -- again, I'm very concerned that this will happen again, and aren't, now, we know that we can't count on some of the elected officials. And hopefully, that the mayor will learn from this and sit down with us and not wait until this blows up again, to sit down with us. His people have not sat down with us, the Commission of Small Business has but he has not.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes, and I know that you're worried because you don't want this to be a political issue. This is not a partisan issue. I mean, crime is something that affects everybody across the board. Let me play something from the mayor there in New York, what he said about the current situation on the streets.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ADAMS: Taking too long to sentence dangerous people, while they're on our streets, they continue to commit crimes. We continue to have this catch, release, repeat mindset. We got to get that under control.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, so Frank, you're going to meet with the D.A. again this week? What do you want leaders in New York to do there about crime?\par \par \uc2 GARCIA: We want to be able to sit down with the leadership. I've asked for a meeting with the Deputy Mayor Springer of economic development, she refuses to meet with us. So, this is someone the mayor has put in to work with us, to make it easier, someone that's even Filipino that's supposed to be opening up to our community. It's not. So, you know, I thank the commissioner of small business on cam for meeting with us. But we need more.\par \par \uc2 We need the governor and the mayor really to have a plan with us. I think that we put a plan to district attorney to do a anti-crime task force and invite the governor and the mayor to be part of it, where we really could be able to have cameras at the bodegas. If it wasn't for the camera, a lot of our bodegas can't afford to have cameras. That's a big issue. So, we're very concerned what happened to those bodegas that don't have a camera or they're broken and this happens again? Right now, because of COVID, a lot of our businesses are just surviving. So, you know, it has to be a plan.\par \par \uc2 We're asking corporate America, \uc2 Coca Cola\uc2  and the big corporation to step up and help us fund this because if our members aren't, you know, this happened again, then we can't be able to service them. So, there's a lot of concerns right now. To mem, this really, I'm going to lose sleep even more now, because I'm very concerned, the future of, of Latino businesses in New York and nationally, and the White House has to have a plan. I don't see the President having a plan of crime nationally. This is a national issue. This is not just New York.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes, and that video, as you referenced, made probably all the difference in this case. Frank Garcia, we wish you all the best. Always, you know, heartbreaking to lose life. We are glad that Jose Alba was not also a tragedy in this situation. Thank you, sir.\par \par \uc2 GARCIA: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Also in New York City, a "Law and Order: Organized Crime" crew member reportedly shot and killed as he was saving a parking spot on the show's set in Brooklyn. This happened about 5:00 a.m. Police say the 31- year-old man was sitting in a car when the suspect opened the door and shot him in the face and neck. That shooter still on the loose. The New York Post reports the victim was married and had three children. Republican Congressman \uc2 Mike Gallagher\uc2 , Joe Concha, and Marc Thiessen on another batch of bad polling for the president as the White House takes ownership over gas prices but only when they're going down.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Aren't you having it both ways Jared because when the gas prices go up, it's got nothing to do with the President. When we see some decline, you want him to get the credit?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Breaking moments ago, Congresswoman \uc2 Alexandria Ocasio-Cortez\uc2  among 16 lawmakers arrested at an abortion rights demonstration outside the Supreme Court. AOC's office tweeting this video. \uc2 Capitol Police\uc2  say they arrested a total of 34 people on charges including obstructing and crowding.\par \par \uc2 And we expect new reaction from the White House this hour after accusations of a double standard on gas prices, blaming \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  when they went up, and then taking credits as they've dropped. The national average for regular down more than 50 cents. Good news for all of us from the all-time high of $5.00 a gallon we hit last month. The White House says that's partly because of what it calls historic actions by President Biden to respond to what he's called the "Putin price hike."\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Putin's war is already hurting American families at the gas pump.\par \par \uc2 Putin's price hike in gasoline. The consequence of Putin price hike. We've never seen anything like Putin's tax on both food and gas. Putin's price hike is hitting America hard. I'm doing everything in my power to blunt Putin's gas price hike.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Congressman \uc2 Mike Gallagher\uc2  is standing by, but first, we go to White House correspondent Peter Doocy from the North Lawn. Hey, Peter.\par \par \uc2 PETER DOOCY, FOX NEWS CHANNEL WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: And Shannon, what we've been hearing from officials here at the White House is that when gas prices go up, it's completely out of the President's hands. It's mostly \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 's fault. But when the prices start to come down, it is entirely in the President's hands and his policies deserve a lot of a lot of credit, which is why there are now these questions about a possible double standard.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JARED BERNSTEIN, WHITE HOUSE ECONOMIC ADVISER: There's no both way thinking here at all. I very much disagree with that frame. And I think what's happening here is a president who is working tirelessly to address the largest constraint, probably the toughest constraint facing American households right now.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: But President Biden came back from the Middle East without firm commitments to get golf partners to drill more even though that was on his to do list for that trip. White House officials are saying that's OK, because the President is releasing oil from the strategic petroleum reserves and urging gas stations to lower prices.\par \par \uc2 And now, there's this memo from the comms director \uc2 Kate Bedingfield\uc2 . She writes, "President Biden's top priority is tackling inflation and reducing prices, and his plan is working." And the press secretary added this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: We have seen gas prices go down in the past 34 straight days.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: Aren't they still $2 a gallon, higher than when you guys took office, though?\par \par \uc2 JEAN-PIERRE: First of all, we have to look at the -- of how we got there, right? We think about the war that Russia has taken on in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: And it has been exactly one year since President Biden said about inflation generally. Our experts believe and the data shows that most of the price increases we've seen are expected to be temporary. But those experts and that data that he was referencing have been proven to be wrong. Shannon?\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes, Peter. I think you said, "No serious economists thought that there would be any ongoing inflation." 5.4 percent then, 9.1 percent now.\par \par \uc2 Peter, noisy day there at the White House. Thanks for working us in.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: Thanks, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right, let's bring in Republican Congressman \uc2 Mike Gallagher\uc2  of Wisconsin, a U.S. Marine veteran who serves on the Armed Services Committee.\par \par \uc2 Congressman, great to have you with us. So, let's start here. The White House says the president has made a difference in bringing the prices down because he opened up the Strategic Petroleum Reserve and because he's worked with allies to up their production. Do you give him any credit?\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 MIKE GALLAGHER\uc2  (R-WI): Well, the Strategic Petroleum Reserve is a short term sugar-high at best. It's also a serious question as to whether this was the intended purpose.\par \par \uc2 He has not gotten any major agreement with our allies, because our allies in the Middle East, in particular, still disagree with this administration over their fundamental Iran policy, which is an existential threat to both Israel and Saudi Arabia.\par \par \uc2 As for the bragging over this temporary decrease, gas prices are still $450. I mean, $4.50, on average. That's $2 higher than when the Biden administration took office.\par \par \uc2 This is nothing to brag about, you're reminded of the tweet that they released a year ago about how the average cost for a barbecue on Fourth of July went down. It shows how out of touch they are with the concerns of working class Americans.\par \par \uc2 Because, they governed for \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , because they live on \uc2 Twitter\uc2 . They don't understand the pain that their policies are causing.\par \par \uc2 Finally, I would say, in some ways the pain is the point. The point is to make oil and gas consumption so painful that everyone has to make this transition to \uc2 John Kerry\uc2 's COP26, fantasyland.\par \par \uc2 This, it's time to change course and learn from the failure of these policies.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, our new Fox News polling shows that when it comes to assigning blame as to who's responsible for what's going on with gas prices, at the top of the list is President Biden and his policy.\par \par \uc2 So, clearly, they think that he is responsible for what they're living through right now. But you make something of no. There -- this administration has talked about getting rid of the fossil fuel energy. The president has said that clearly and directly many times during campaigning to people that he would make sure that they went away. And now, he's gone to our domestic producers here in the U.S. to say, you guys, first of all, need to stop price gouging. And you also need to start doing more.\par \par \uc2 How does this relationship work at least to the betterment of the American people? Is it possible?\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Well, I just think it illustrates the absurdity of their policy. Instead of going to Texas, to Pennsylvania, and Oklahoma, and unleashing the true innovation of American energy production, which is far cleaner than our adversaries, he's going all around the world, begging Venezuela for hydrocarbons, trying to get Iranian crude on the international market.\par \par \uc2 What's more, they're actually doing the same when it comes to solar. So, they're allowing China to dominate the supply chain for solar panels subcomponents, the poly silicone.\par \par \uc2 Well, ask yourself, how does China make these so cheaply? Because they use slave labor and coal power. China is responsible for the biggest humanitarian and environmental abuses in the world, and this administration's policies are actually increasing Chinese malfeasance.\par \par \uc2 It's completely absurd. We need to be relying on American energy ascendance and unleash, the true power of American energy. It's both better for the environment, and will help working class Americans with their skyrocketing energy bills.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, just very quickly. That's going to be in complete conflict with what the progressive wing and the president's party is asking him to do. Which side did they listen to if these polls continue to crater?\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Well, we now have over a year's worth of evidence that he is solely interested in listening to the \uc2 Bernie Sanders\uc2  wing of the party. This is \uc2 Bernie Sanders\uc2 , Green New Deal agenda being inactive right now.\par \par \uc2 Compare it to his Democratic predecessor, President \uc2 Obama\uc2 . Sure, \uc2 Obama\uc2  had environmental snafu, \uc2 Solyndra\uc2  stands out. But \uc2 Obama\uc2  was also willing to sign a deal on crude oil export ban.\par \par \uc2 The -- he's of radical departure even from the policies of the \uc2 Obama\uc2  administration so the Sander's agenda is extended.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: That seems like this is going to be --\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Very different administration in that respect. Congressman, thank you for your time. Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: New polling shows just one in four Americans approve of how the president is handling inflation. And his support among Democrats is on a steady decline. Our panel breaks all the numbers down after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: President Biden's approval rating hitting another new low, just 38 percent in a new CNN poll.\par \par \uc2 62 percent of Americans say they disapprove of the job he's doing and his numbers are dropping among some crucial groups as we get closer to the Midterms. Let's bring in our panel.\par \par \uc2 Marc Thiessen, former chief speechwriter for President \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2 , now an \uc2 American Enterprise Institute\uc2  scholar. Joe Concha, The Hill media and politics columnist, and a Scott Bolden, former D.C. Democratic Party chair and former chair of the \uc2 National Bar Association\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Panel, great to have all of you with us.\par \par \uc2 MARC THIESSEN, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR (on camera): Hey, Shannon. Good to be with you.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, so I want to talk about a couple of these CNN numbers. This one poll talks about sort of Democrats are losing their excitement about President Biden as well.\par \par \uc2 It says, "With Midterm Elections approaching, the poll finds no indication that Biden's standing with the public is improving, and among some critical constituencies. It is worsening among Democrats, for example, Biden's approval numbers have softened by 13 points since the spring.\par \par \uc2 Marc, not a good outlook for the White House going to the midterms.\par \par \uc2 THIESSEN: Not at all. And I mean, the only good news I could find in this, is that 38 percent approval is better than the 33 percent approval he had in the New York Times Siena poll last week. But that's still pretty bad.\par \par \uc2 I mean, and the problem is, is that approval, it's not just with people in general, it's with Democrats. As you pointed out, his approval among Democrats, it was at 17 -- 71 percent in the spring, and now it's dropped 13 points.\par \par \uc2 71 percent is really bad for a president with his own party. And you've had other polling came out that shows Sienna, Times -- Siena poll, 64 percent of Democrats don't want him to run.\par \par \uc2 Another poll for the previous week showed that only 30 percent of Democrats would vote for him in a Democratic primary. So, he is losing support among his base, and among the swing voters who both of whom he needs to hold on to Congress and to win the presidency back.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes, CNN has another look at other critical groups in this polling as well. It says, "Among people of color, 45 percent now approve of Biden's overall performance, down from 54 percent in the spring. That includes a six-point dip among Black adults, and a nine-point decline among Hispanic adults."\par \par \uc2 And Scott, we have Fox News polling out, they came out Sunday that shows similar drops with key constituencies with women, with people under 50. I mean, critical groups he needs to do well. How does he win them back? Can he?\par \par \uc2 SCOTT BOLDEN, FORMER CHAIRMAN, DEMOCRATIC PARTY, DISTRICT OF COLUMBIA: Well, those are the groups that put him in the White House, think about that. And my colleague was right. There's just nothing good in this poll. I feel like I'm coming to defend numbers that are indefensible.\par \par \uc2 But I will say this: That the Democratic Party and \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , certainly owe a lot to them being in office, and they've got to woo those voters back. They can't afford that 71 percent, or those loss of six or nine percent.\par \par \uc2 If he delivers on voting rights, which he hasn't been able to do, if he delivers on the George Floyd Act, which he hasn't been able to do. Sure, we have a black, female Supreme Court justice, but it's simply not enough when he's facing trade winds or headwinds regarding inflation.\par \par \uc2 Those aren't black or white issues, Republican democratic issues. Those are American issues. And we're all feeling it, whether you have a means or not a means.\par \par \uc2 And so, he's got to get his messaging back on pack and on track. And he's got to tell his story. My Democratic friends say he's doing everything he can, but he's not getting credit for it. But that's not America's fault. That's Biden's fault.\par \par \uc2 He has got to do better messaging, he's got to bring inflation down. He's got to try to do to reduce the deficit. He's got to reduce these oil prices. And he's got to figure out why we have four percent unemployment, but we don't have companies that have enough employees to deliver goods and services on a timely basis.\par \par \uc2 It's a frustrating time to be an American right now, primarily due to the economy, and he's going to get the blame for it. He'll get credit for it when he does well, but he's getting the blame for it right now. And he can only pull himself up.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes. And Joe, we know that, that first Midterm Election for any president is historically bad. And then, with the economy, the way that it is, and all of the numbers of the polls and the dissatisfaction and laying the blame at the president's feet, it's going to be tough for him.\par \par \uc2 He now also has Democrats who are openly saying don't run in places like the \uc2 New York Times\uc2  and other big non-conservative media outlets.\par \par \uc2 Here is former Secretary Robert Reich saying this. He says, I'm 76. He said \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is too old to run again. He says, Biden's Secret Service detail can worry about his wallet, and he's got a teleprompter for wayward nouns. But I'm sure he's experiencing some diminishment in the memory department."\par \par \uc2 But Joe, listen, the message continues to be from the White House. He is running again.\par \par \uc2 JOE CONCHA, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR (on camera): Yes, and it comes from the president as well that he is running again. But there is this question now of the effort the president is making, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 When you look at Mr. Biden's public schedule, more and more, it seems like it's shrinking, particularly when he's back here in the United States. Here is the president's schedule yesterday as an example: 10:00 a.m., the president and vice president received the president's daily brief. 3:00 p.m., press briefing by White House Press Secretary \uc2 Karine Jean-Pierre\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Here it is today. 9:30 a.m., the president and vice president received the president's daily brief, 3:00 p.m. press briefing by \uc2 Karine Jean-Pierre\uc2 . Now, could there be lots of meetings going on behind the scenes, because this is the public schedule? Perhaps, but more and more, the American people are getting the feeling the porch light is on and nobody is home.\par \par \uc2 And you look at that CNN poll, seven and 10 voters believe this president has not paid enough attention to the nation's most pressing issues. And when you look at that \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  survey that came out, it's staggering. 93 percent of small businesses fear a recession is coming. 89 percent say they are being tangibly impacted by inflation.\par \par \uc2 The fear is real, Shannon, because the pain is very real. And the president just doesn't seem to be out there enough making his case, as we see again today, where is the president?\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes, and when people have to fill up their tanks and buy groceries and pay rent, all that reality will matter very much as to their perceptions of their reality, and what they're thinking heading into the midterms about who's worse possible.\par \par \uc2 Panel, thank you very much. Have a great day.\par \par \uc2 CONCHA: Thank you, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 THIESSEN: Bye-bye.\par \par \uc2 BOLDEN: Take care, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 THIESSEN: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right. Later this hour, we talked to the head of a food bank in President Biden's home state of Delaware demand there for assistance is way up, largely because of record high inflation hitting everyone. And days after California's largest ever fentanyl bust, border patrol agents uncover another massive stash being smuggled in to poison our country.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: There has been another massive fentanyl bust in California. This time, a border patrol canine sniffing out 250 pounds of the potent synthetic opioid during a vehicle stop, the estimated street value, almost $4 million. Senior national correspondent William La Jeunesse reporting live from LA. Hello, William.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAM LA JEUNESSE, FOX NEWS SENIOR NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Shannon, don't you just love looking at these police dogs with their drugs? They're like a kid with a trophy, they're so proud. Turns out that Customs and Border Patrol employ 1,500 dogs, so in the last few days they have found narcotics in cars in Tucson, they found heroin and El Centro, they have found meth in San Clemente, pot in a Lucky Charms box in Louisville, and outside San Diego that fentanyl hidden in his spare tire and gas tank.\par \par \uc2 Year-to-date, pot seizures are down but CBP is seizing more fentanyl, coke and meth than ever before. Most of it comes to the ports of entry where their dogs screen thousands of cars a day, right? They're faster, more effective than any machine. They weave through the cars looking for the smell of duct tape, fresh paint, fiberglass glue. And the dogs are responsible for 60% of the drug seizures by the border patrol. They are trained from puppies to find conceal people narcotics, and like any good student, their trainer is proud to hear of a major bust.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PJ DOWLING, CANINE INSTRUCTOR: It's a big reward, especially when they contact you with emails and some pictures that, yes, here's Kirp. He got 50 pounds of cocaine today, you know, it really pays it forward. And we have to live vicariously through their efforts.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: So we put our heart and soul, and all our effort into getting these guys raised up.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LA JEUNESSE: So, this is the CBP Puppy School in Virginia where the agency selects dogs based on the drive, the friendliness and their ability to indiscriminate sense. Those who pass go into 12 weeks of field training, before going into the field to find drugs and save lives. So the traffickers will often try to conceal their drugs in a vacuum wrapped bags or hidden compartments in the gas tank, a load of avocados or coffee. And still, Shannon, the dogs can pick it. Back to you.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Listen, they're impressive but they're also super cute. William, thank you for the story.\par \par \uc2 LA JEUNESSE: Sure.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Let's bring in Brandon Judd, now president of the National Border Patrol Council. Brandon, are you surprised at all by the amount and the quantity of what we're seeing in these introductions?\par \par \uc2 BRANDON JUDD, PRESIDENT, NATIONAL BORDER PATROL COUNCIL: I'm not at all. But let me first start, when I was a canine handler for five years, my dog was not super cute, just so that you know. But those dogs are absolutely amazing. This particular seizure most likely came through the port of entry. When you look at the way it was packed, the way it was concealed, this was most likely a vehicle that came through the port of entry. There's an ongoing investigation to find out where exactly it came through, how it came through, and why it was able to get through.\par \par \uc2 But when you look at that, the scary part to me right now is last year when the ports of entry were basically closed down for non-essential traffic, we still had the most overdose deaths in the history of the United States. There was more product on the street via the police officers than in any year in the history that we've ever seen. And now the ports of entry are back open. So now the cartels have two means to get their products across. They can come between the ports of entry by flooding the border patrol resources with illegal immigrants, and now they can also bring it through the ports of entry as long as there's a big backlog of vehicles.\par \par \uc2 And so, it's very -- it's a very dangerous situation that we're looking at right now, and if this administration doesn't actually do something to get this under control, we're going to continue to see a lot more deaths here in the United States.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, this is one of the ideas that the Florida attorney general, \uc2 Ashley Moody\uc2 , has for this administration. She wants them to classify fentanyl as a weapon of mass destruction. Here's what she said on THE STORY.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ASHLEY MOODY, FLORIDA ATTORNEY GENERAL: This is a drug that even the DEA had said, this is the deadliest drug we have ever seen. We need more than the normal approach that we have with our agencies right now. Let's take some drastic steps. Let's get everyone all hands on deck. And also let's start holding Mexico accountable to do their part.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: What about this idea of having classified as a WMD? How does that affect the impact the availability of resources or ability to shut this down or handle it differently with other countries?\par \par \uc2 JUDD: Once we do that, then it allows us to go in and start looking at the bank accounts of the criminal cartels. It allows us to -- it allows us to take the gloves off essentially, to go in and to start peeling away all of the layers that these cartels hide behind. And if we were to do that, we would be a lot more effective.\par \par \uc2 But again, it takes this White House to actually take border security seriously. It's going to take this administration to say we can't have any more deaths in this country. And if they were to do that we could be a lot more successful. We have to be able to go after the cartels. We have to be able to go after their profits. But it starts by driving illegal immigration to lower levels. And if we do that, then it frees up resources to then go after the profits of the cartels.\par \par \uc2 If we can do that, we can get turnover in the cartels. And if there's turnover in the cartels, then we can be a lot more successful.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: We need to every potential advantage. Brandon Judd, thank you to you. The man and women are out there on the border every day at night doing this work, we thank you.\par \par \uc2 JUDD: Always good to speak with you. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: We now have a statement from NBC Universal Television on the breaking story we brought you at the top of the hour. A 31-year-old crew member for "Law and Order: Organized Crime" shots and killed as he was sitting in a parked car on the show set in Brooklyn.\par \par \uc2 Statement reads, "We were terribly saddened and shocked to hear that one of our crew members was the victim of a crime early this morning and has died as a result. We are working with local law enforcement as they continue to investigate. Our hearts go out to his family and friends, and we ask that you respect their privacy during this time."\par \par \uc2 Former President Trump demanding accountability from the Pulitzer Board for awarding news outlets that peddled the Russia collusion hoax. The verdict after this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT OF UNITED STATES: So they created a fake story. And I didn't take it seriously because I knew there was nothing to it. So being a believer in our country I said, well, nothing's going to come of this story because it's fake. And two and a half years later, I was right.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: The Pulitzer Prize board will not rescind 2018 awards to The Washington Post and New York Times for their reporting on collusion between the Trump campaign and Russia during the 2016 presidential election, despite a month long campaign from former President Trump to do just that. Trump now says the honorable thing for the outlets to do is to hand back the prizes willingly, telling Fox News Digital, "The only way the \uc2 New York Times\uc2  and the \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  should get a possible Pulitzer Prize would be in a new category, disinformation for helping to perpetrate a false story created and peddled by crooked \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2 , the DNC and certain lowlife Democrats."\par \par \uc2 Well, media buzz host Howard Kurtz joins us now. Quite a mouthful there. Howie, I don't know that we ever expected the Pulitzer folks were ever going to seriously consider this, but they say they actually did carry out a couple of investigations.\par \par \uc2 HOWARD KURTZ, MEDIABUZZ HOST: Yes. Well, let me start with the overall picture, which is what the news business did with endlessly hyping and promoting this Trump investigation. It was endless. It was constant. It was deafening. You couldn't escape it. It was absolute media malpractice, breathlessly exaggerating every little tiny detail, for example, constantly predicting that Trump was about to be indicted or echoes of that today, by the way, you know. And then it became, he's going to go to jail. And even now, just you watch.\par \par \uc2 That was unconscionable. It just was really bad. And Trump then branded Russia gate as a witch hunt. And, of course, played this card of saying the papers should have to return those prices. But the challenge facing the Pulitzer Board was very different challenge.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes. And this is what the board says. They have this process these two investigations, it says the separate reviews converged in their conclusions that no passages or headlines contentions or assertions, in any of the winning submissions were discredited by facts that emerged subsequent to the conferral of the prize is, a fancy way of saying we're not taking it back.\par \par \uc2 KURTZ: Right. But the board, the only way the board would rescind those prizes would be if they could find factually inaccurate statements, but they took Trump seriously. They ordered the separate probe for each paper, and didn't find anything that was factually wrong.\par \par \uc2 Now, keep in mind that \uc2 Robert Mueller\uc2 , as special counsel during that investigation, indicted 34 people and three companies. He found that Russia had absolutely tried to influence the campaign for Trump's behalf, even though no finding of collusion with the former president's campaign. And then the Mueller probe also found that the Russia folks through various intermediaries hacked into and then leaked confidential DNC documents in an effort to damage Hillary's campaign, all that had to be covered.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: It did. As you said, though, there was some of what we were told by the media, by other people who gave quotes to the media as if there are these bombshells coming. Trust me, I've seen it. That was paddled for a long time, much of what the President had pushed back against never panned out to be anything valid. I don't know that President Trump's supporters make their decisions about anything based on what the Pulitzer committee does. But it looks like this is one where they're just going to have to agree to disagree.\par \par \uc2 KURTZ: Well, look, I don't think Trump really ever expect it. It's more of a kind of a finger in the eye thing that the prizes will be returned, and it got him some headlines. But that's a separate question from the absolute anti-Trump orgy of speculation and hype, and he's going to be indicted, and he's going to go to jail, that mark the larger media coverage of this whole thing. It was a huge embarrassment for the media. It will go down in the history books, Russia, Russia, Russia.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Quickly, do you think that we'll hear anything from the \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  or New York Post, or excuse me, \uc2 New York Times\uc2  saying we're vindicated?\par \par \uc2 KURTZ: I don't know if we will or not, you know, in today's social media age, they probably have to say something. And, you know, or they might be perceived as just sticking it to Trump once again. But really it echoes of what's happening now with January 6.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes, OK. Howie, we know you were on it? If something develops, you'll let us know. Always great to see you, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 KURTZ: Thanks, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right. In President Biden's home state of Delaware, demand at some food banks has doubled this summer as sky high costs burden Americans across the country. That's THE STORY You'll see here only with us, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: We're seeing long lines at food banks again with inflation at a 40 year high. Some Americans say they're asking for help for the first time in their lives. We're going to talk to the head of a food bank in President Biden's home state of Delaware where demand has doubled in the past year, but first to Fox Business correspondent Lydia Hu live in a food pantry in New York City. Hello, Lydia.\par \par \uc2 LYDIA HU, FOX BUSINESS CORRESPONDENT: Hi there, Shannon. You can see behind me that the workers at this food pantry, they're just starting to distribute hot meals that they've made just for this evening. On the menu tonight, beef Stroganoff and tuna fish. They expect to hand out around a thousand hot meals this evening alone.\par \par \uc2 Now, this follows their earlier distribution of around 200 bags of fresh produce and meat to a line of people that waited down the blocks, some of them for hours. The Food Bank of New York City is seeing a 93% increase in the number of visits now compared to before the pandemic, and many of the visitors are new to food pantries, even recently since inflation has surged. We talked to one woman who started coming here only eight months ago.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HU: You didn't have the food pantry, what would you do for your food needs? Where would you get food from?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I basically would just try and live off of a lot of soups. You know, one pot meals, things like that, pastas.\par \par \uc2 HU: And you can see here, Shannon, on this map that the need is evident across the country. A number of examples here like Phoenix, Arizona, where a food bank says distribution in a recent week in June is up 78%, over the same week last year, Los Angeles up 36%, an inflation not just worsening the need but also straining the food supply. \uc2 Feeding America\uc2  since food banks are purchasing nearly as much food as they did last year, but they're now paying about 40% more for those purchases. And it comes as donations are also down by about 50%.\par \par \uc2 Here in New York, the Food Bank of New York City says they have enough food right now to meet the community's need. But they are reevaluating their budget since inflation is at a record high. They're considering some options like introducing more canned meats, canned chicken and tuna because they last longer than frozen options. It's just one example of how they're trying to stretch the dollar a bit further, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Lydia Hu in New York, thank you so much, Lydia. I want to bring in Cathy Kanefsky now, president and CEO of the Food Bank of Delaware. And, Cathy, I cannot imagine the last couple of years, the demands that have been on you and your staff and your community. Tell us what you're seeing.\par \par \uc2 CATHY KANEFSKY, PRESIDENT/CEO, FOOD BANK OF DELAWARE: We are absolutely seeing I just was watching with interest, your last piece and talking about other food banks throughout the country, we're seeing the same thing. We're seeing people get in line as they were, you know, a year ago. The numbers are -- they're not dropping, if anything, they're going up a tad right now. And I say all the time, you know, people don't know what tomorrow is going to bring and we're seeing that in the people that show up for the very first time at our door, or in a line, or ordering from our options to do home delivery online.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes. One of our Fox News polls, when we ask people has inflation caused you a financial hardship over the last six months, 75% of our respondents said, yes, they've had to make tough decisions. It's actually impacted their budget, their household, their checkbook, you talked about, as Lydia did too, about people coming in for the first time. And we know it's been a rough couple of years. But it sounds like you're saying -- you're seeing more first timers, you're seeing the numbers moving in the wrong direction.\par \par \uc2 KANEFSKY: We are and we're asking people, you know, when they come to us, what brings you to us, you know, sometimes especially those that are coming for the first time. Now we're hearing that people have to decide whether they're going to put food on their table or gas in their car, and the challenges now are a little bit different than they were a year ago when it was more about COVID and recovery from the pandemic. But we knew it was going to be a long haul. We knew people weren't going to, you know, get back to normal, whatever normal is overnight. And I would say that, you know, I can speak for my team here in Delaware.\par \par \uc2 We are incredibly grateful. We have not seen a dip in the generosity of our neighbors Delawareans pulled together when they need to. And I would say that is more true now today than it ever was.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: I am so glad to hear that because that's concerning in Lydia's report. I mean, we know that you guys maximize everything that you can bring in the door to get it back out the door to help people. So to know that folks are still stepping up there. America is a very generous country. And we've seen that over the last couple of years on people helping neighbors. But what is the reality you think moving forward? How much demand can you handle?\par \par \uc2 KANEFSKY: You know, I was thinking that you might ask me this question and this answer might sound pie in the sky, but that's who I am. And that's how I live. We will not find the day that we don't meet the demand that's out there. That's our job. That's a job at a food ban, that's what we get up in the morning and come to work to do. We figure out ways.\par \par \uc2 Today we were talking about, you know, we do mass distributions, like you see across the country where lines of cars come in. We're doing three this week, as a matter of fact, and we're not as I said before, we're not seeing less people come to us. But what we are thinking about is, you know, they're coming and sitting in a car and idling, you know, while they wait. So they're using more gas to come to us to get to get food. And as I mentioned before, we have a growing home delivery program where we take food to them.\par \par \uc2 And so, those kinds of things are what we're starting to have to sit down and say how can we be more effective? How can we be more efficient?\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Cathy, God bless you, and all those who are working with you and doing these efforts across the country and to those who donate as well. We hope these numbers will go down but we know you'll always be there thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 KANEFSKY: Thank you very much, yes. And thanks to all that donate and support.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: That's THE STORY for Tuesday, July 19th. It goes on.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document HUNDD00020220720ei7j00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Senator Elizabeth Warren Makes Comments Criticizing Pregnancy Crisis Centers in Massachusetts; President Biden Visits Middle East; \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  Campaign Pays "Jersey Shore" Star Snooki to Make Video about Republican Opponent for Pennsylvania Senate Mehmet Oz; Man Saves Family from Burning House.\b0\par\par\uc2 Sean Duffy \par \uc2 7,671 \uc2 words\par \uc2 15 July 2022\par \uc2 Fox News: The Ingraham Angle\par \uc2 INGANG\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 SEAN HANNITY\uc2 , FOX NEWS HOST: News anytime, foxnews.com, hannity.com.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, this Friday night let not your heart be troubled. Laura Ingraham, THE INGRAHAM ANGLE is next. We hope you have a great weekend.\par \par \uc2 LAURA INGRAHAM, FOX NEWS HOST: I'm Sean Duffy in for Laura Ingraham. And this is a special edition of THE INGRAHAM ANGLE.\par \par \uc2 SEAN DUFFY, FOX NEWS HOST: All right. First, this is Biden's America. People are getting poorer and growing more desperate by the day. This one headline from the Associated Press sums it all up.\par \par \uc2 Long lines are back at U.S. food banks as inflation hits high. And it gets worse, a Louisiana food bank CEO warns that right now there's enough food. But that might not be the case in the future, citing high fuel costs, making it more expensive to collect and distribute the food. Americans who need help right now are in a perilous position.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KAREN MARTIN, FLORIDA SINGLE MOM: I'm not making ends meet. I'm not making it. I'm setting my savings. I get paid tomorrow. And already my whole paycheck is spoken for. And it's the first time in my life I've had to apply for food stamps, because I don't know how we're going to continue eating groceries.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I can't afford gas sometimes, so we eat our baguettes. That's where it goes now. And it shouldn't be like that in America.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DUFFY: No, it shouldn't. But this is Biden's America.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT, UNITED STATES OF AMERICA: This is the United States of America for God's sake. We can't deal with that?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DUFFY: Guess not.\par \par \uc2 Now, at the time Biden made those comments, we were all about a year into the Pandemic. So many people were out of work. Businesses had closed. And yes, there were long lines at food banks, but it was getting better.\par \par \uc2 The unemployment rate was slowly going down. The economy was rebounding. President Trump handed Biden a country that was getting back on its feet. But that all changed. And this desperation felt on the part of many Americans came back, all due to the policies that the Biden administration has put into place.\par \par \uc2 So knowing that, Americans are truly in trouble. How is the White House and its dem allies responding to all this?\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: We got a long way to go because of inflation, because of the, I call it, the Putin tax increase. Republicans do nothing to obstruct our efforts to lower your gas taxes.\par \par \uc2 REP. NANCY PELOSI (D-CA): Inflation springs from higher costs; higher costs spring from shorter supply; shorter supply is there because of COVID preventing certain products from coming into our country.\par \par \uc2 HEATHER BOUSHEY\uc2 , MEMBER OF WHITE HOUSE COUNCIL OF ECONOMIC ADVISERS: It is because in large part right now because of this energy crisis.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DUFFY: So is this because of Putin, COVID, the supply chain, I'm having trouble keeping track. These excuses keep piling up, but the solutions are never anywhere to be found.\par \par \uc2 Perhaps the most heroic effort we've seen as of late came from Congresswoman \uc2 Veronica Escobar\uc2 , who was discussing Biden's balancing act of killing oil and gas exploration in the U.S. with begging a murderous dictator for some of his oil.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. VERONICA ESCOBAR (D-TX): The President is juggling some historic challenges right now. He inherited a country that was grappling with COVID. We now are seeing global inflation.\par \par \uc2 Decades ago, we should have acted far more quickly to transition away from fossil fuels. Because we haven't made that transition, we're reliant on gas. No one should envy the position that President Biden is in right now.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DUFFY: Joining me now is Tennessee Senator \uc2 Marsha Blackburn\uc2 . Marsha, good to see you.\par \par \uc2 I got to tell you what. So listening to Congresswoman Escobar's explanation of why we're feeling this pain right now is that we didn't actually transition to green energy early enough as opposed to we're not drilling more in America, producing more American energy. What say you?\par \par \uc2 SEN. MARSHA BLACKBURN (R-TN): I say that now they're trying to make it the people's fault, because we didn't fall in line with that Green New Deal and that socialist agenda and do this decades ago. And if we had, then we wouldn't be having this problem now.\par \par \uc2 It is just amazing to me. They cannot ever say that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  said, he was going to end the oil and gas industry. And on day one, he said about taking the steps to make that promise come true.\par \par \uc2 With the Keystone with taking ANWR offline with your offshore drilling being ended, stopping fracking. The list goes on and on. He's had 42 regulations since he took office that are directed directly at making it more difficult for the oil and gas industry to drill, to explore, to produce, to refine here in the United States.\par \par \uc2 DUFFY: Senator, he did run on that, right? He indicated listen, I'm going to attack American oil and gas and energy. He did say that and some people might give him kudos for sticking to his promise.\par \par \uc2 But you and I both served in the House together. I mean, at one point when your policies don't help people, but actually hurt people, are kicking more people into poverty, you would think a leader would pivot and go, You know what, no, no, this actually isn't working. I want to make sure my people are ok, that they can afford groceries. They can put gas in their car to go to work, so they can make a living for their family.\par \par \uc2 BLACKBURN: That is right. And I've been in five Tennessee counties today. And, Sean, it is amazing. Every one of them are worried about their county budgets. They hope that the budgets hold to the end of the year.\par \par \uc2 One of the counties was telling me about how the price of energy and inflation affects everything, all these county budgets, they needed some eight-inch pipe for a water line, they had been paying $4 a foot for that pipe. You know what it is right now? $14.50 a foot.\par \par \uc2 So all of these products that come from derivatives of oil and gas, you're seeing the price hikes there too. Inflation at 9.1 percent, your groceries going through the roof, your price at the pump through the roof. People just cannot afford this.\par \par \uc2 And you know what, Sean, the food shortages, the fear this is causing with people, the way they're anticipating food shortages, because in rural America, they know that the farmers are not planting as much this year to go into that supply chain for our food that you're going to see on the shelves next year. And the reason they're not planning, the cost of fertilizer, the cost of diesel, they cannot afford to get the crop in the ground.\par \par \uc2 DUFFY: Which is now going to drive up the cost of food. This is a vicious cycle, Senator. I know you see it firsthand in Tennessee. I see it firsthand in Wisconsin. And you know what, we're grateful we have people like you fighting for some common sense policies in the Senate.\par \par \uc2 So thank you for joining me tonight too. I always appreciate your common sense.\par \par \uc2 BLACKBURN: Good. Good to be with you.\par \par \uc2 DUFFY: All right.\par \par \uc2 All right. We now know how badly things are going for this country. It's obvious. But just how bad is it going for Biden? It seems almost weekly at this point that we're seeing headlines saying, Biden's approval hits new low point of his presidency. How low can this guy go? I'll hit the polling out at Five Thirty Eight. Sum it up.\par \par \uc2 "Biden is dancing with a bleak bit of history. His approval rating of 39 percent is now the worst of any elected president at this point in his presidency since the end of World War II. In other words, Biden is arguably in worse shape than any other elected president heading into his first midterm elections, including his four most recent predecessors who, like Biden, were operating in an increasingly polarized political climate."\par \par \uc2 DUFFY: For RealClearPolitics they have an even bleaker outlook. Biden's average job approval rating is 38.4 percent with a spread of negative 17.7 percent. Wow.\par \par \uc2 All right. Joining us now is Tom Bevan, president of RealClearPolitics; and Joe Concha, Fox News contributor and media columnist at The Hill. He's also the author of the book, "Come On, Man!" Great title, Joe.\par \par \uc2 All right, Tom. Let's talk about these numbers. 39 percent, we've never seen numbers this low. Is this a progression that could go even lower than 39 percent, or is this kind of a rock bottom \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 ? No matter how bad it's going to get, he can stick at 39 percent.\par \par \uc2 TOM BEVAN, CO-FOUNDER & PRESIDENT, REALCLEARPOLITICS: I think he's pretty near his floor just because the only thing holding him up right now is support among Democrats. I mean, he's way underwater among young voters, for instance, Hispanic voters, independent voters.\par \par \uc2 I mean, you go across the border, the only two groups that he has majority support among right now are self-identified Democrats and African-American voters. That's it. So I suspect he's pretty near his floor.\par \par \uc2 And really, the bad news for Biden and for the Democrats is, when you look at his job approval on the economy which is the number one issue by far in this election, it's even lower. It's at 35 percent in our average. So he's getting very low marks across the board from the public on the number one issue that is on the minds of voters heading into the election.\par \par \uc2 DUFFY: And, Joe, I spent a lot of time in politics as a congressman for almost 10 years. And politicians care about their egos. They care about their legacy, right? And to think that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has pulled numbers this low, and is not agreeing to pivot and do things differently to help the American people out. I don't know about you, but this doesn't make any sense to me.\par \par \uc2 JOE CONCHA, THE HILL MEDIA COLUMNIST: I think when you get to a certain age, Sean, you're stubborn in your ways. And \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  simply isn't going to do that pivot, that famous pivot that \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2  did.\par \par \uc2 After 1994, he lost 54 seats in the House. And he actually said, all right, you know what, I got to work with Newt Gingrich, the new House speaker and actually passed this thing called a balanced budget sometime. And I know it's a foreign concept now, but they did. They passed welfare reform. And \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2  won going away in 1996 against \uc2 Bob Dole\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Look, just ask the last first-term Democratic President \uc2 Jimmy Carter\uc2 , what skyrocketing inflation does to a presidency. It slaughters it. And when anyone asks the President, Sean, well, what should we do to lower inflation? He says, the answer is for the government to spend trillions more in order to lower inflation. It's like greatly increasing one's calorie intake in an effort to lose lots of weight.\par \par \uc2 I tried this on vacation at the Jersey Shore recently, double cheeseburgers, tuna tacos, copious amounts of Milwaukee's best. Trust me, it doesn't work. And history shows that the last two Democratic presidents who struggled in the first term lost 54 and 63 seats in the House, respectively. And they were polling higher than Biden, Sean.\par \par \uc2 What do you think this is going to look like in November considering where things are in this country right now? It's a red tsunami that's coming. No question.\par \par \uc2 DUFFY: Joe, and I do that at the beach. Eat those burgers and that ice cream, I start to get like a second and third chin, and my kids point that out to me. So I know what you're saying.\par \par \uc2 But, Tom, the kexodus is still ongoing. The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  reporting today that Kamala's longest serving aide is leaving next month. And that's not all. Politico is reporting, her director of speech writing is leaving after just four months.\par \par \uc2 Tom, Politico calls this a continuing staff makeover. But this isn't a makeover. Is it? What is it?\par \par \uc2 BEVAN: Well, it's - and Biden lost his longtime aide Kate Beddington. She's leaving as well. We've seen a lot of turnover in this White House. And I think that's, on \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 ' side, I think it's because she's very difficult to work for.\par \par \uc2 I mean, that's, I think, well-known, well-established. And she doesn't treat her people well. And I think that's why a lot of them are leaving. I think it's also a product of the fact that this administration is struggling.\par \par \uc2 I mean, Biden should actually be firing more people. He should probably should be shaking up people across the board in his administration in an effort to get some new energy, some new ideas, and make some sort of pivot, because the course that they're on is leading to disaster.\par \par \uc2 Everybody knows that, including all the Democrats in these swing districts that are running for re-election there. They are nervous and scared to death. Because the administration keeps trying to reassure them. Inflation's peaked now. It's going to be on its way down. Gas prices are on its way down. They don't believe them. And I don't think the American public are going to believe that either.\par \par \uc2 DUFFY: You know, staffers love power. They want to be by the most powerful member possible. You're in a poll position when you're with the President, or the Vice President. And that they're leaving these positions of power that just underscores how bad it is.\par \par \uc2 Joe, I want your take on this. Give me some projection, look into your crystal ball. How many House seats are Republicans going to pick up this November? And will Republicans win the Senate based on these ultra low numbers of \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 CONCHA: If \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  lost 63 seats in 2010, and he was pulling higher than \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , then I have to believe it's around about that number, right, at least 50 or 60. As far as the Senate, that's more of a toss up right now, right?\par \par \uc2 Because - but in the end, though, I think when inflation overwhelms everything, and gas prices overwhelm everything. And crime, by the way, is a big issue as well. And we see a border that's anything but secure, education going sideways, and all this tumult abroad. If you're a Republican Senate candidate, just point out those things and make it a referendum on the current president.\par \par \uc2 I don't see how they lose the Senate there either. If those Senate Democrats - Republicans, excuse me, stick to that message and make it a choice between what we have now and what things could be, Sean.\par \par \uc2 DUFFY: Policies couldn't be so easy. This is like, I don't support \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , I don't support these policies. Vote for me. It's pretty easy, and they're probably going to win. You're right.\par \par \uc2 Tom and Joe, thanks for joining me. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 BEVAN: Thanks, Sean.\par \par \uc2 CONCHA: Good to see you. Thanks, Sean.\par \par \uc2 DUFFY: All right. You too.\par \par \uc2 All right. The great political realignment is real and it's happening as we speak. But when the media is forced to talk about it, they aren't quite sure how to frame it. That's why you see headlines like this.\par \par \uc2 "GOP sees chance to steal Hispanic voters from Democrats". Unsurprisingly, that tweet angered a lot of people.\par \par \uc2 And coming on the heels of Jill Biden's breakfast taco remark, voters are frankly sick of the pandering from Democrats and the media. Like according to The Hill, "Hispanic Democrats in Congress outnumber Hispanic Republicans 4 to 1. But right now we're seeing a new generation of Republican candidates all across the country. The National Republican Congressional Committee says, it has recruited a total of 102 Hispanic candidates this cycle."\par \par \uc2 Two of them are here with me tonight. Joining me now is Virginia congressional candidate Yesli Vega; and Texas congressional candidate, Cassy Garcia. Ladies, thanks for joining me.\par \par \uc2 Yesli, I want to go to you first. So is this alignment real, which I think it is. And if Hispanics are moving from Democrat to Republican, why?\par \par \uc2 YESLI VEGA (R) VIRGINIA CONGRESSIONAL CANDIDATE: It absolutely is real. Look, this past November, we proudly delivered 54 percent of the Hispanic vote to Governor Youngkin.\par \par \uc2 This is a movement and there's nothing the Liberal Democrat party can do to stop this movement. While progressive activists were pushing to cut police budgets across the nation, guess who has felt the skyrocketing crimes in our community, communities of color? We felt the impacts of it. We see the failed leadership coming from the Democrat Party.\par \par \uc2 And we've had enough. We are rising to the occasion. And we're going to send a loud, resounding message this November that the Democrat Party is not entitled to our vote that they don't represent our values, and that it is time now to restore our blessed Republic.\par \par \uc2 DUFFY: You know, Cassy, I have a podcast with my wife. It's called From the Kitchen Table. She is a Latina, I have 12 Little Latinos at home. The comment by \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  in the taco comment was incredibly offensive. We talk about it on our podcast From the Kitchen Table.\par \par \uc2 But do you have to talk about tacos and Hispanics when you can't talk about abortion, inflation, faith? You can't talk about school choice. You can't talk about the issues that really matter to the community. So you're talking about tacos? Is that is that the deal?\par \par \uc2 CASSY GARCIA (R) TEXAS CONGRESSIONAL CANDIDATE: Yes. Sean, thank you for having me on tonight. Democrats want to control how we speak. We don't talk about pronouns, like all Americans are worried about rising food prices and crime in our communities.\par \par \uc2 The Democrats are so out of touch. They think Hispanics would vote for them if they talk to us about tacos and promote open borders. But those are not the values of South Texas. We believe in the American Dream, God, strong borders, and opportunities for families.\par \par \uc2 I'm proud of my heritage. My family and the people in our community came to this great country to work hard, not to be dependent on the government. So every day, I meet people who tell me they voted for Democrats for decades. But that is no longer their abuelos' Democratic Party. It's become too radical, elitist, and out of touch of our values.\par \par \uc2 DUFFY: Yes. And to the point, I want to talk about this a little bit later tonight. But the attack on the Catholic Church, on all churches. People of faith is very real. And Democrats don't seem to give any pushback, any condemnation.\par \par \uc2 And speaking broadly, the Hispanic community is the community of faith. Does that come into play, Yesli, as well as to why Democrats are losing the Hispanic vote?\par \par \uc2 VEGA: It absolutely is, because we're seeing a double standard. I know that Cassy (inaudible) has also felt the impacts of the bias that we receive. Not just from the media, but from a Democrat party that claims to support women and minorities. But the moment that they realize that you're a conservative woman, they no longer want to support you.\par \par \uc2 And so we received pushback like never before. They're calling us fake Latinos. But listen, we're going to block out the noise, because we know what the task ahead means. And it means that we're going to win. We're going to take back the House.\par \par \uc2 Look, we continue to champion life, liberty and the pursuit of happiness, while radical Democrats are more interested in forcing our sons to be daughters and our daughters to be sons. We are focused on the issues and we're going to send a message this November that our vote is going to have to be earned.\par \par \uc2 DUFFY: Cassy, quickly, if you're talking to Kevin McCarthy, if you're talking to the Republican Committee, what advice do you give them if they want to win Latinos? If they want to win the Hispanic vote, what do they have to do?\par \par \uc2 GARCIA: So talking to - working with leadership right now, they're supporting our races here in South Texas with (inaudible) with Monica De La Cruz in 15, my race and Yesli Vega over in Virginia.\par \par \uc2 The Biden policies are hurting the families of South Texas right now. The Biden border crisis, we talked about baby formula shortage and the energy crisis in our district, and we are going to see a red wave come this November. The Democrat Party has left the Hispanic community. And so we're going to see more Hispanics, more Democrat Hispanics vote Republican come this November.\par \par \uc2 We're going to see a red tsunami take over like never before and we're going to make history and we're going to flip three seats in the Rio Grande Valley and Virginia. And well, Yesli Vega is also going to win as well. And so we're going to make history. We believe in the American Dream.\par \par \uc2 DUFFY: Ladies, I got to tell you what. I live with a Latina, don't mess with Latinas because you will get the fire and brimstone coming at you. Good luck in your races. And thanks for joining me tonight. I appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 GARCIA: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 VEGA: We're just getting started. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 DUFFY: Yes, you are.\par \par \uc2 All right. Did another bombshell from the January 6 committee and their media accomplices fizzle out? Yes, you bet, it did.\par \par \uc2 Matt Whitaker\uc2  is here to expose it all. That's coming up next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 DUFFY: It looks at the January 6 committee and their media pals got another bombshell detail about the Secret Service. Wrong. You might remember how the media reacted to this claim a couple of weeks ago.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CASSIDY HUTCHINSON, FORMER \uc2 MARK MEADOWS\uc2  AIDE: The President reached up towards the front of the vehicle to grab at the steering wheel. Mr. Engel grabbed his arm, said, Sir, you need to take your hand off the steering wheel. We're going back to the West Wing. We're not going to the Capitol. Mr. Trump then used his free hand to lunge towards Bobby Engel.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DUFFY: Within hours, the Secret Service was pushing back. All the players who were there denied what she said. And they all wanted to testify. We have yet to hear from them. And I guess, I have to wonder why?\par \par \uc2 And check out these headlines from yesterday. "Secret Service deleted January 6 text messages after oversight officials requested them."\par \par \uc2 Now, those headlines make it seem like it was done maliciously, which was obviously their intent. But if you read, you'll see that the DHS inspector general's report that it was done as part of a device replacement program, which according to the Secret Service was a pre-planned three-month system- wide migration.\par \par \uc2 And then 12 paragraphs down in that CNN article, you get this bit of information. "The letter does not say whether the DHS watchdog believes these text messages were erased intentionally or for nefarious reasons."\par \par \uc2 All right. Seems like the less shocking explanation. It's probably the right one.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is \uc2 Matt Whitaker\uc2 , former acting U.S. Attorney General. Good to see you, Matt. Matt, I want to read from the Secret Service statement. They said, "We notified DHS officials of the Inspector General of the loss of certain phone data, but confirmed to the OIG that none of the tax it was seeking had been lost in the migration".\par \par \uc2 Matt, it seems like the effort here is completely not the truth, but completely just there to smear the agency.\par \par \uc2 MATT WHITAKER\uc2 , FORMER ACTING ATTORNEY GENERAL: Right. And this committee is not happy with the response from the Secret Service about Cassidy Hutchinson's testimony.\par \par \uc2 You remember they pushed back, saying that, We are - there are people willing to go on the record to dispute her allegations. And obviously, they're not happy about that. So now they are going to war against the Secret Service in trying to embarrass them.\par \par \uc2 I mean, all these stories to your point, Sean, were worded to make it sound very nefarious, that somehow that these were intentionally deleted, that these were intentionally kept away from Congress. And that's not what happened. And so it's just it's so insincere how the media is covering this. But again, they are just the mouthpieces to amplify the committee's message.\par \par \uc2 DUFFY: It goes to the point when \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  put on only members of Congress that she approved. She didn't allow Republicans, Kevin McCarthy to put his members on the committee. You get a skewed committee that will put out press releases like this, that mislead the public, that would never happen if Jim Jordan was on the committee.\par \par \uc2 And I think this is the case in point of why you want a bipartisan representation on these panels.\par \par \uc2 WHITAKER: Yes. There's no doubt that this is not a fair and balanced committee. They are not seeking the truth. There is no cross examination of witnesses. There is no - none of the typical ways of questioning where both sides can get in their important facts that are then can be considered. This is a show trial that its only intention is to disparage what used to be just the Trump administration, but now they're disparaging law enforcement as well.\par \par \uc2 DUFFY: I think, you bring up a really important point. I was a prosecutor as well in Wisconsin for almost 10 years. And we have trials in courtrooms where both sides get to present their evidence. There's rules that apply to the evidence that comes in and juries make decisions. We don't have show trials to your point in Congress, where you only have one side presenting evidence and excluding the other side. You don't convict people in the court of public opinion. You do that in courts, right?\par \par \uc2 WHITAKER: Yes, Sean. And I know like you, I would watch some of these hearings, and just find myself objecting to hearsay, most of the day turned, because there was so many times where especially their star witnesses would say, Oh, I heard so and so, or I overheard, or this person told me that this other person had said. None of that would ever be admissible.\par \par \uc2 Those people would have to testify for themselves, and their - and be in their own voice. But instead, this is being told through essentially, almost at an interpretive kabuki theater. And it's a show trial, and no one should take this serious, because they have stripped away all of the American traditions of getting to the truth that protect our justice system. But this Congress plays by different rules as you, I'm sure, experienced when you were in it.\par \par \uc2 DUFFY: Matt, you know what, we want the truth. We want all of it to come out, the good, the bad, the ugly. But you do that with both sides participating, hopefully, Kevin McCarthy, if he's the next speaker will actually facilitate that. So we get all the evidence from January 6.\par \par \uc2 Listen, Matt, I appreciate you joining me and giving me your insight today. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 WHITAKER: All right. Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 DUFFY: All right. Now, let's turn to something that actually deserves committees and hearings.\par \par \uc2 The ongoing attacks on Catholic churches and pregnancy centers across the country, rocks, graffiti, red paint, even Molotov cocktails. But where is Biden in all of this. He's our country's second Catholic president. Instead of addressing each of these attacks, he is fist-bumping with the guy who ordered the murder of a "\uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " columnist Jamal Khashoggi.\par \par \uc2 And then there's Elizabeth Warren, who said this about crisis pregnancy centers.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. ELIZABETH WARREN, (D-MA): In Massachusetts right now, those crisis pregnancy centers that are there to fool people who are looking for pregnancy termination help outnumber true abortion clinics by three to one. We need to shut them down here in Massachusetts and we need to shut them down all around the country. They should not be able to torture a pregnant person like that.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DUFFY: Straight up here, stupid and evil is that comment. Joining me is Andrea Picotti-Bayer, director of the strategy for the Institute of Human Ecology at the \uc2 Catholic University of America\uc2 . Good to see you, Andrea. OK, you wrote a brand new piece on FOXNews.com today where you questioned what would happen if these were mosques being attacked. Explain.\par \par \uc2 ANDREA PICOTTI-BAYER, THE \uc2 CATHOLIC UNIVERSITY OF AMERICA\uc2 : It's very important, Sean, that we understand that at the core of this is a threat to religious freedom in America. And we all now, after the terrible terroristic attacks of 9/11, there was a surge of anti-Arab American hate. And the federal government put together a task force, worked with the Arab American community to make sure that their places of worship were safe. That same kind of coming together, working with community religious leaders, especially from the top of our federal government, should be the response to this spate of horrible vandalism against Catholic churches and, as you mentioned, pro-life pregnancy centers.\par \par \uc2 DUFFY: The pro-life pregnancy centers I think truly matter because they are giving moms who want some help. They give diapers. They give baby clothes. The give strollers. They are there to help women who want to make the choice of life. And here you have Elizabeth Warren pushing back, which in essence says I'm not about life. I'm actually an advocate for abortion. What say you?\par \par \uc2 PICOTTI-BAYER: What's really terrible, after those horrible statements by a government official, two days later there were two pregnancy centers in Senator Warren's home state that were vandalized. Political officials need to be careful with their words. There are a lot of people that are unhinged in the pro-abortion movement, and our very vulnerable pregnant women are being served by places that accompany them, that give them that kind of loving care, and they are now targets. The women are targets themselves, and the staff and the volunteers as well.\par \par \uc2 DUFFY: It is outrageous. And you know what, where is the DOJ? The DOJ should be investigating these crimes against churches, pro-life pregnancy centers. It is outrageous that they are absolutely sitting on the sidelines and going after parents who go protest at a school board meeting, CRT. Andrea, listen, thanks for your great work, we appreciate it. Thanks for joining me.\par \par \uc2 PICOTTI-BAYER: Thanks so much, Sean, for having me on.\par \par \uc2 DUFFY: Biden's visit to the Middle East creates its own Desert Storm. And Democrats are ready to throw their elder leaders out of the party. Raymond Arroyo, FOX News contributor has all the detail in Friday Follies, and he even has a special guest star. That's coming up next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DUFFY: It's Friday, and that means it's time for Friday Follies.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 DUFFY: For that we turn to FOX News contributor, the great, the awesome, the smart, the sexy Raymond Arroyo.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 DUFFY: How's that intro, Raymond?\par \par \uc2 RAYMOND ARROYO, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: I think you're overselling me there, Sean, but OK.\par \par \uc2 DUFFY: Not much, not much. Maybe a little bit.\par \par \uc2 All right, Ray, Biden went to the West Bank in Saudi Arabia today and it didn't go so well.\par \par \uc2 RAYMOND ARROYO, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Sean, at times Biden was so unintelligible, I wasn't sure if he was speaking in the Middle East or in middle earth. An elvish translation might have made more sense.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , (D) PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: And the United States has suffered loss as well, including the killing of Shireen Abu Akleh. And I had a terrible headache here -- excuse me, a terrible headache. And ah --- sorry. Swallowed wrong. I have a terrible headache years ago, and I did a very stupid thing.\par \par \uc2 When I came to office I reserved a policy -- I reversed the policy of my predecessor to respond to the pandemic, including donating more than 1 million doses of COVID-19 to the West Bank and Gaza.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: I'm sure that the Palestinians were overjoyed, Sean, to learn that he sent over a million doses of COVID to their people. Just what they needed. The good news is the president has committed $100 million to six Palestinian hospitals. Let's hope they have memory care units and an acid reflux clinic. That cough looks a little rough for him.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 DUFFY: It does. Raymond, I wonder if Jill Biden or the Easter Bunny didn't tell Joe that COVID was over.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: No. Well, I think the Easter Bunny got lost along the way as well. So what are you going to do? Poor Joe. People have worked for him for 30 years, Sean. They claim there has been no change in \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . What are they seeing? Do they have T.V.s?\par \par \uc2 DUFFY: They don't think we have \uc2 YouTube\uc2 , I know. Exactly. But Ray, performances like this on the world stage are why Democrats are looking for someone a little bit younger to lead their party.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Well, you saw that "\uc2 New York Times\uc2 " poll where 94 percent of Democrats under the age of 30 want a candidate other than Biden to run in 2024. So Sean, if you're \uc2 Joy Reid\uc2  and you want to discuss the discontent with the nearly 80-year-old president running, who would you invite on? Why, an 81-year-old retired Democrat, Senator Barbara Boxer.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BARBARA BOXER, (D) FORMER SENATOR: It doesn't matter how old you are. You could be dangerous at 80 and you could be dangerous at 40. It has nothing to do with age. We could all be, they could all be in their 30s or their 40s, they still couldn't do it. And I think that the panelist we have on here is so right. It's about the issue.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: She couldn't remember the name of her co-panelist while making the argument that it doesn't matter. They are all dangerous, Sean.\par \par \uc2 DUFFY: Raymond, I look at that point, I go, you know what, I actually agree with you, because I look at AOC, what mid-thirties, and \uc2 Bernie Sanders\uc2 , 80. They're both really dangerous. It's not their age. It's their ideas that are killing America.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: And more than the issues, it's really about how that individual ages. And the truth is \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is aging far more rapidly than other men his age, even \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2  in the past, and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 DUFFY: And \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  for sure. All right, Ray, tell us about the Democrat Senate candidate \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2 's celebrity troll of his opponent Dr. Oz.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Yes, Fetterman had a stroke a few weeks back and he's not been on the campaign trail. So he's turning to Cameo for help. That is that service where you pay celebrities to send anniversary or birthday greetings to friends. So Fetterman paid Snooki of "Jersey Shore" to drag Dr. Oz for having lived in New Jersey.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SNOOKI: Hey Mehmet, this is Nicole Snooki. And I'm from "Jersey Shore." I've heard that you moved from New Jersey to Pennsylvania to look for a new job. Don't worry, because you'll be back home in Jersey soon. This is only temporary. So good look. You got this, and Jersey loves you.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Sean, Snooki was probably not in on the joke, nor do I think she thought she was reporting a political ad since the Fetterman campaign posted that on social media. But you know people pay for these celebrity cameo endorsements all the time, Sean.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JUDGE JEANINE PIRRO, FOX NEWS HOST: I'm Judge Jeanine Pirro, and Raymond Arroyo is my favorite part of Friday Follies, and all of prime time. Who the hell is Raymond Arrow?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: I know I should've paid full price for the Judge Jeanine cameo. Next time, Judge.\par \par \uc2 By the way, it's been reported that Fetterman's campaign paid Snooki a grand total of $390 for that cameo, Sean. That's embarrassing. And Dr. Oz actually went to school at the \uc2 University of Pennsylvania\uc2 , had two children in Pennsylvania, and he's registered to vote there, so it's a low, cheap blow no matter what.\par \par \uc2 DUFFY: Hold on, Raymond. Raymond, I did reality T.V. back in the day. I'm not getting paid to do political ads. Snooki is getting paid almost $400. I'm missing out here.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: You are. I'm going to tap into your cameo and you can help endorse me next time. We'll do it next week.\par \par \uc2 DUFFY: Next week. And you paid Judge Jeanine. That's totally wrong, Raymond. Raymond, great to see you.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Have a good weekend.\par \par \uc2 DUFFY: Friday Follies. You, too.\par \par \uc2 The dramatic rescue of five kids stuck in a burning home caught on tape, and the hero who saved them is here exclusively with us. That's coming up next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 DUFFY: Tonight, we'll finish the week off with an incredible story out of Indiana. A 25-year-old man is now being hailed as a hero after rushing into a burning house to save five people, including children. FOX's Jeff Paul is in our west coast newsroom with all the details. Jeff, what do you have?\par \par \uc2 JEFF PAUL, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Yes, Sean, the hero, as you mentioned, in this situation was driving home from work just after midnight in Lafayette, Indiana, when he noticed flames coming from a house. So the man, Nick Bostic, slammed on his brakes, pulled into the driveway, and ran to the back of the house. He was yelling for anyone inside.\par \par \uc2 Now, initially, Bostic rescue around four people getting them to safety. But then he was told a child was still missing. So Bostic ran back into the house, this time met by even more intense flames and smoke. Somehow, he managed to find the last child inside, held on to her tight, then jumped out of a window from the second story.\par \par \uc2 What happened next as police showed up and it was captured on an officer's body camera.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (SCREAMING)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We need a medic.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Come with me, man, come with me. Come with me. We've got to get away from the house. Hey, we've got to get away from the house, OK?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PAUL: Incredibly, all five family members were not hurt thanks to Bostic's selfless actions. Bostic's arm was cut pretty bad from breaking the window of that second story to help the little girl escape. But listen to him as officers are trying to tend to his injuries. His focus clearly was not on himself.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 NICHOLAS BOSTIC, SAVED FIVE KIDS FROM HOUSE FIRE: Is the baby OK? Please tell me that baby is OK?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Yes, we're good.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Yes, she's OK.\par \par \uc2 BOSTIC: A hundred precent?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: She's OK.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: You did good, dude.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PAUL: All he cared about was making sure that last child he rescued was OK. Bostic spent some time in the hospital for severe smoke inhalation but was later released. Local police there in Indiana say Bostic's actions clearly saved all those lives. Sean?\par \par \uc2 DUFFY: Jeff, thank you.\par \par \uc2 And that hero you just heard about, Nick Bostic, joins me exclusively right now. Nick, great to see you. So, as I understand this story, you come to the house, right. It's on fire. You don't know if people are inside or outside. You run in. And you find out that the family was sleeping when this fire was burning?\par \par \uc2 NICHOLAS BOSTIC, SAVED FIVE KIDS FROM HOUSE FIRE: Yes, I'm assuming the family was asleep. It took a few moments before people came flying out of the top stairs door. But yes, I went in through the back door, was hollering if anybody was in there. And I made it three steps up the staircase and then outpoured for the older -- for the children other than the six-year-old. So I turned around in front of them and let them out the back door. And I asked them who else was left in there, if anybody, and they said the two-year-old, or, no, the six-year-old was in there. And so I went back in there to search for them.\par \par \uc2 DUFFY: And Nick, tell me about that, because this is when the smoke is all over inside the hours, the flames are starting to shoot up. Tell me about going back in for the six-year-old.\par \par \uc2 BOSTIC: It was dark. It was dark. It was real silent. It was like a silent killer, for sure, I would call it. When I went back inside and upstairs and got finished looking upstairs, I started to go to the stairs and go back to down to look at the first floor. When I got up there, smoke was already filled up halfway up the staircase. And it wasn't just like light smoke, medium smoke, heavy smoke. It was real heavy, like real heavy smoke.\par \par \uc2 I made my way down there. And I heard the infant, or the six-year-old, I heard her crying. And I heard it. I doubled over in my shirt, and I went down there with my eyes closed, and my hand reached out. And I use my ears to guide me to her voice. And I picked her up, and I ran to the stairs. Or, I ran to the back door, but I couldn't find it. I felt like I was getting more lost the further I'd go to try and find it. It should have just been straight, a straight shot to the staircase when you went in from the backdoor, but it wasn't like that for some reason, I guess, that night, you know. So I went back upstairs where I had seen light.\par \par \uc2 DUFFY: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BOSTIC: And I tripped at the last staircase and fell, got back up, made it to the window in that bedroom in the side of the house. I went to ripping blinds off. The string got caught around Kilani's (ph) leg, or ankle. And I just thought of all what I was doing and made sure that was completely unwrapped from around here, otherwise that was going to be my choice in saving her life, and mine and hers in jeopardy. So -\par \par \uc2 DUFFY: I've got to go in about 15 seconds. Tell me about what injuries did you have.\par \par \uc2 BOSTIC: I've got a real deep laceration on my forearm, blisters, a numerous amount of blisters. I'm recovering great, though. No broken bones. I'm ready to go. I'm ready, buddy.\par \par \uc2 DUFFY: Listen, Nick, I've got to run. Thank you for your bravery and masculinity.\par \par \uc2 We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 DUFFY: That's it for us tonight. I am Sean Duffy in for Laura Ingraham. Make sure you check out my podcast I have with my wife Rachel. It's called "From the Kitchen Table." And we put out two episodes per week. This week's episode is tacos and taquitos. Rachel and I discuss \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's dumb comments at a Latinx conference. And \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  tweeting encouraging people to have more babies. We have nine.\par \par \uc2 Thanks for watching. Greg Gutfeld takes it from here.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document INGANG0020220716ei7f00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Wholesale Prices Surge To New 40-Year High; Senators Spar Over Abortion Rights Legislation; President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  Addresses Iran And Russia During Israel Remarks; Israeli Prime Minister Urges President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  To Pressure Iran; Report Finds No Evidence To Back Trump Fraud Claims; Authors Say Fraud Claims Will Deter Voters; Shark Attacks Increase At New York Beaches; French Government Bestowed Legion Of Honor Medals On Four American Veterans Of World War II; Food Shortages And Inflation Causing More People To Turn To Prep Foods For Stockpiling; Year Over Year Inflation Rate In U.S. At 9.1 Percent; Possible Political Effects Of Inflation In U.S. Economy On Midterm Elections Examined\b0\par\par\uc2 Bill Hemmer, Aishah Hasnie, Jacqui Heinrich, Rich Edson, Gen. Jack  Keane, Bryan Llenas, Mike Emanuel, Bret Baier, Alexis McAdams, Jennifer  Griffin \par \uc2 7,700 \uc2 words\par \uc2 14 July 2022\par \uc2 Fox News: Special Report with Brett Baier\par \uc2 FNSPBB\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 JESSE WATTERS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: I mean, I'm not going to make over simplifications and stereotypes about the Irish. It's not what I do.\par \par \uc2 GREG GUTFELD, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Why not?\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Well, I'm Irish, so I can. Also, watch my show tonight "JESSE WATTERS PRIMETIME" AOC's January 6 Conspiracy Theory, don't miss that. SPECIAL REPORT --\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Is she going to be on?\par \par \uc2 GERALDO RIVERA, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT-AT-LARGE: They're best friends.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Tonight is with Bill, take it away.\par \par \uc2 BILL HEMMER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Jesse, thank you. "PRIMETIME" see you in an hour. Thank you, Jesse.\par \par \uc2 And good evening I'm Bill Hemmer in tonight for Bret Baier, once again live from our world headquarters in New York City and welcome to our program.\par \par \uc2 Breaking tonight, the grim economic picture worsens. A second hot inflation report in a row. Here's the latest. Wholesale prices rising 11.3 percent in June compared with a year ago. Weekly jobless claims spiked, reaching an eight-month high. Those numbers reinforcing the fear of a recession as the Fed tries to get inflation under control.\par \par \uc2 Today, Treasury Secretary \uc2 Janet Yellen\uc2  saying that bringing the prices down is the top priority for this administration. But the plan forward to ease the economic play -- pain rather, is not clear.\par \par \uc2 Rich Edson at the Labor Department begins our hour tonight. A lot to get to now, Rich. Good evening to you there. \uc2 Hello\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 RICH EDSON, FOX NEWS CHANNEL SENIOR NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Good evening, Bill. More government data showing the same worrying trend, higher prices throughout the economy at levels not seen in four decades.\par \par \uc2 Today's Labor Department report is assigning blame largely to one sector of the economy, as Democrats and Republicans are assigning their own blame.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 EDSON (voice over): For the second straight day, the Labor Department released a report showing significant inflation throughout the economy. The producer price index measures the costs of goods and services at the wholesale level before they reach consumers. Those prices were 11.3 percent higher in June than they were 12 months before, the fastest increase since 1981.\par \par \uc2 JANET YELLEN\uc2 , U.S. SECRETARY OF THE TREASURY: We're taking our own steps that we believe will be supportive in the short term to get inflation down.\par \par \uc2 EDSON: The Labor Department says nearly 90 percent of this annual increase is from inflation in oil, natural gas and other types of energy. This report follows Wednesday's CPI data showing inflation for consumers was 9.1 percent higher last month than in June of last year.\par \par \uc2 CECILIA ROUSE, CHAIRWOMAN, COUNCIL OF ECONOMIC ADVISERS: This inflation has its roots in the pandemic, which snarled supply chains, yes. Just like other countries around the world, and now we have Russia's war against Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 EDSON: Republicans say the White House is deflecting responsibility that Democrats are at fault for pushing a transition away from oil and trillions and stimulus.\par \par \uc2 SEN. MITCH MCCONNELL (R-KY): One of the first and most painful consequences of the Biden administration's failed policies has been the soaring cost of energy. Remember on their party's watch, the cost of heating a home, rose by double digit percentages last winter.\par \par \uc2 EDSON: The White House says its Build Back Better proposal will lower costs for consumers. Republicans say its mix of tax credits, tax increases and spending on education, childcare, housing and energy will boost inflation and slow the economy.\par \par \uc2 Democratic leaders need to secure Senator Joe Manchin's support to pass their bill. He's tried to restrain their plants.\par \par \uc2 SEN. JOE MANCHIN (D-WV): Everyone should be very cautious of what we're doing and how we're approaching things now and looking at anything that could inflame the inflationary high numbers that we have.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 EDSON (on camera): The \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  is now in a balancing act, trying to raise interest rates to curb some of these price increases without too much damage to the broader economy.\par \par \uc2 Hiring at least has been strong though, a new government report today. Another one says that last week, applications for new unemployment benefits ticked up to levels not seen since last November.\par \par \uc2 Bill, back to you.\par \par \uc2 HEMMER: Thank you, Rich, nice to see you. And after all that, stocks were mixed today, the Dow fell 143, the S&P 500 dropped 11 points and the NASDAQ finished up four points.\par \par \uc2 As lawmakers on the Hill play the blame game over the poor economic picture, there are several Democrats shifting their focus to abortion. But some of the legislation they're pushing target situations which have yet to happen.\par \par \uc2 Aishah Hasnie has that story on the Hill tonight with more.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 AISHAH HASNIE, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONGRESSIONAL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): Inside the Capitol, Democrats are pushing bill after bill on abortion rights.\par \par \uc2 SEN. CATHERINE CORTEZ MASTO (D-NV): States cannot and must not prosecute women who travel across state lines for critical reproductive care.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: But Republicans say they're panicking over scenarios that don't exist.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 JAMES LANKFORD\uc2  (R-OK): No state has banned interstate travel for adult women seeking to obtain an abortion. This seems to be just trying to inflame to raise the what ifs.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: The back and forth playing out as both parties seek to energize voters and win control of Congress this fall. As left focuses on abortion, Republicans double down on inflation.\par \par \uc2 And as it turns out, it appears to be working. \uc2 The New York Times\uc2  said, "Republicans appear to be making new inroads among non-white and working class voters, perhaps especially Hispanic voters who remain more concerned about the economy and inflation than abortion rights and guns".\par \par \uc2 SEN. MITCH MCCONNELL (R-KY): So, I think the fall election is going to be a big fight over the future of the country, and people in the end will have to decide whether they're happy with this all democratic government.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: But that's not dissuading Democrats, when asked about inflation, Representative \uc2 Sean Patrick Maloney\uc2 , who's in charge of the midterm strategy for House Democrats pivoted and once again, warned about what he thinks would happen if Republicans get their way.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 SEAN PATRICK MALONEY\uc2  (D-NY): The Republicans are going to ban abortion in all 50 states if they get control of this Congress. And that's the choice.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HASNIE (on camera): And Bill, despite all this talk about abortion, Democrats admit that they still don't have enough votes to codify Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 In fact, House Speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  earlier today said that she is unwilling to compromise with even sympathetic Republicans in the Senate to come together and put together a package that might have a chance in the Senate, she says, abortion is her kitchen table topic, Bill.\par \par \uc2 HEMMER: Something to watch going forward. Aishah Hasnie, good to see you tonight on Capitol Hill.\par \par \uc2 An update now on the story of a 10-year-old girl who was raped by an illegal immigrant and then, she crossed state lines for abortion services. Authorities are working to determine how Gerson Fuentes entered the country in the first place. The suspect had admitted to raping the child on two different occasions leading to her pregnancy. The Indianapolis Star is reporting the girl missed the Ohio deadline to get an abortion by three days.\par \par \uc2 Twitter\uc2  has suspended a far left group heavily criticized for their calls to intimidate Supreme Court justices. The Ruth Sent Us group is also responsible for publishing several conservative justices' home addresses.\par \par \uc2 For weeks, the social media company has been criticized for allowing the group to continue online. \uc2 Twitter\uc2  has not responded to Fox Digital's request for comment thus far this evening.\par \par \uc2 Overseas now, Ukrainian officials say that Russian missiles struck a city in the central part of Ukraine killing at least 21 people. More than 100 others were wounded as three different missiles strike in office building and residential buildings in the town of Vinnytsia. Ukrainian President Zelenskyy calling the attack an open act of terrorism against civilians.\par \par \uc2 Further east in Moscow, American basketball star Brittney Griner appeared in a Russian courtroom today after pleading guilty last week to drug possession charges, she faces up to 10 years in prison.\par \par \uc2 President Biden meanwhile took aim at Russia and Iran today during remarks in Israel. He also signaled patience was wearing thin for Iran to rejoin the nuclear deal.\par \par \uc2 This now prompting threats from Iran's president and all this coming ahead of Biden's plan meeting tomorrow in Saudi Arabia with that country's crown prince, just days before Russian President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  paid a visit to Tehran.\par \par \uc2 With all that going on, Jacqui Heinrich reports tonight once again from Jerusalem.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JACQUI HEINRICH, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): President Biden and Israeli Prime Minister \uc2 Yair Lapid\uc2  put pen to paper Thursday in Jerusalem promising the U.S. will use all elements of its national power to ensure Iran never gets a nuclear weapon. But the allies are divided on how to make that happen.\par \par \uc2 YAIR LAPID, ISRAELI PRIME MINISTER: Words will not stop that Mr. President, diplomacy will not stop them. The only way to stop them is to put a credible military threat on the table.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I continue to believe that diplomacy is the best way to achieve this outcome.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: The distance in those prepared remarks shows Biden failed to convince Israel returning to the Iran nuclear deal will make them safer, the White House pressing forward.\par \par \uc2 JOHN KIRBY, PRESS SECRETARY, PENTAGON: For all intents and purposes, there is a deal done. It's on the table. Now, the onus is on Iran to decide whether they want to comply with this deal or not.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Biden not imposing a hard deadline.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: We're not going to wait forever.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: While the U.S. irons out a regional defense alliance in the background.\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: It's not a formal structure at this point. So, it's difficult for me to give you a list of nations and individual capabilities. But there's significant air defense capabilities here in the region.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Tomorrow, Biden heads to Saudi Arabia after refusing to say if he'll bring up Jamal Khashoggi's murder to the man the CIA says ordered it, the Saudi Crown Prince.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I always bring up human rights. I always bring up human rights.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: The late journalist widow says she was promised Biden would at the White House this week.\par \par \uc2 HANAH ELATR KHASHOGGI, JAMAL KHASHOGGI WIDOW: It will come up and that's why they invited me.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Team Biden pressed on that disconnect.\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: Some of those conversations are not going to always be easy, but they're still important to have. The fact is that