ght Nazis and saved France from being overrun and occupied.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: He was part of the resistance support troops who succeeded in slowing down and even stopping the German advance.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: David Bailey of Bluefield, West Virginia, first landed in Normandy and then fought at the Battle of the Bulge.\par \par \uc2 CPL. J. DAVID BAILEY (RET), FRENCH LEGION OF HONOR RECIPIENT: I'm the oldest veteran of the Battle of the Bulge that happened in 1944. And I'm 100 years old.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: Ernest Marvel of Frankfurt, Delaware, helped liberate the Dachau concentration camp.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: After heavy fighting, his unit pushed the Nazis out of France.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: Marvel recalled passing his brother on the street in Germany, saluting him, not recognizing him in his uniform since they had both been deployed two years. They both made it home to their family dairy farm. Marvel traveled to Europe by boat.\par \par \uc2 PFC ERNEST MARVEL (RET), FRENCH LEGION OF HONOR RECIPIENT: We went by the Statue of Liberty, and all in our minds was we might never see it again. But we made it back, a lot of us.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: Leslie Simmler of Pennsylvania joined Marvel and Bailey as they received their medals at the French embassy in Washington, D.C.\par \par \uc2 PFC LESLIE SIMMLER (RET), FRENCH LEGION OF HONOR RECIPIENT: I don't want them to make any more veterans. No more wars.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: Benjamin Portaro of West Virginia came ashore in France a few months after D-Day and served under General George S. Patton. He could not make the journey to receive his Legion of Honor Medal first awarded by Napoleon.\par \par \uc2 PHILIPPE ETIENNE\uc2 , FRENCH AMBASSADOR TO U.S.: It was meant to express the gratitude of the nation for the courage of our soldiers.\par \par \uc2 WENDY SHERMAN\uc2 , DEPUTY SECRETARY OF STATE: The Legion of Honor is France's highest honor. And so for them to bestow such an honor on these three gentlemen speaks to the depth and the historical commitment between our two countries to fight for freedom.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: A reminder that war is still raging in Europe, a replica of lady liberty, a gift to the American people on July 4th, 238 years ago, adorned in the Ukraine flag in front of the French ambassador's residence.\par \par \uc2 In Washington, Jennifer Griffin, FOX News.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 HEMMER: And well-earned. Jennifer, thank you for that.\par \par \uc2 In a moment we continue our series on the global food crisis. My colleague Bret Baier on why more people are turning to prep foods to stockpile for future use.\par \par \uc2 First, though, Beyond our Borders tonight. First in Sri Lanka, the president emailed his resignation. After months of protests over the nation's economic collapse, Sri Lankan lawmakers have agreed to elect a new president on July 20th.\par \par \uc2 From Italy, the president has rejected premier \uc2 Mario Draghi\uc2 's offer to step down. President \uc2 Sergio Mattarella\uc2  asked the prime minister to see if could still find a majority in parliament that's willing to support him. Draghi announced his intent to resign after a key ally refused to support a government bill.\par \par \uc2 From Scotland, the world's oldest golf course hosts the world's oldest gold tournament. The Open Championship began today at St. Andrews, and after round one in the American Cameron Young is leading, finishing eight under par. Tiger Woods trails by 14 strokes after a disappointing six over par in round one. Round 2 begins tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 Just some of the other stories Beyond our Borders tonight. And we will be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HEMMER: Tonight, my colleague Bret Baier takes a look at the increasing trend of prepared foods. We examine how rising prices and shortages around the world could create a demand for products that consumers can save and eat at a later date.\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Thanks, Bill.\par \par \uc2 In the first two parts of our series we told you about impact of inflation on fresh foods. In part three we focus on prepped foods. And no surprise, prices there are on the rise there. Consumers are spending 20 percent more at good at grocery stores, and part of that is blamed on stockpiling, a habit that gained popularity during COVID lockdowns. Tonight, we look at how that trend has continued and risen, and how prepared food companies are benefiting from the increased demand, and if their supply is keeping up.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 ROGER CYRAN, \uc2 AMERICAN FARM BUREAU FEDERATION\uc2 : One of the lessons of the disruptions of COVID was that we ideally would move a little bit away from just in time to just in case. But there's a big difference between maintaining a few weeks of supply chain inventory and stocking up for the apocalypse.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Consumers spent more $2 billion on preserving and storing foods over the last year. That is up 36 percent from two years ago.\par \par \uc2 ERIC CHRISTIANSON, NUTRIENT SURVIVAL: We believe that it's not about being afraid. It's about taking action. It's about not panicking but being prepared.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Nutrient Survival is a company that focuses not only on helping people to prepare for a time of crisis when it comes to food, but also trying to get nutrient enriched food for their daily lives. Their chefs take real nutrients in food, freeze dry those so consumers can save it for another day, many days, months, years. CEO Eric Christianson says the rising prices for groceries and product shortages inspired him to take action and create more long-lasting food.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTIANSON: These are real contributors that are having to make difficult choices about what they're going to do with our future. So we are doing our best to hold the line and hold our prices so that we can do what's right for America.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Pantry staples have risen in price exponentially. Snack foods and cereals are up 13 percent. Rice and canned fruits are up more than 12 percent. Peanut butter and canned vegetables are close behind. And now customers are turning to companies like Nutrient Survival for food that lasts.\par \par \uc2 Are you ramping up production, from what you see?\par \par \uc2 CHRISTIANSON: We can't keep up. Demand has just completely outstripped our ability from an operational standpoint, so we are aggressively investing. And that's another thing that's a concern I think not only for me but for other companies, the amount of time it takes to get equipment built, installed, operational, especially with higher interest rates now that's going to put a squeeze on capital expenditure. So that's a concern for me. Fortunately, our projects have been underway for a period of time and I believe that we are well poised to meet demand.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: A limited amount of baby formula across the U.S. fueled fear among new parents and sparked questions as to whether a food shortage could happen on a wider scale here.\par \par \uc2 People shook their head and said, how can that happen in the U.S.? Is it possible five years from now we say how could this happen in the U.S.\par \par \uc2 CRYAN: Well, the baby formula situation is related to how specific some of these products are. And when you get product that's so specific that it's only really being produced in one or two plants, then if one plant goes down, it becomes very disruptive.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: But Christianson says shortages and rising prices are just the beginning. And that's why so many people now are turning to prepped foods.\par \par \uc2 For a long time it was looked at, the prepper group was preparing for a nuclear holocaust, the zombie apocalypse, whatever it was going to be. But now, to your point, a lot of more people are looking at the situation in the world and saying I need to have access to food. Is that fair?\par \par \uc2 CHRISTIANSON: That's exactly it. It's not about being afraid. It's about being smart. This can take the form of going to \uc2 Costco\uc2  and buying a couple cases of chicken noodle soup or a couple jars of peanut butter to have on hand, or it can go to buying from companies like my Nutrient Survival.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 BAIER (on camera): We'll continue to follow this story as prices rise here in the U.S. and other countries grapple with the issue abroad. This issue is not going away. Bill, we'll send it back to you.\par \par \uc2 HEMMER: Indeed, you're right, Bret. Indeed, you're right, Bret. Thank you for that.\par \par \uc2 And breaking tonight, a spokesperson for the Secret Service says a report that the Department of Homeland erased any text messages from the Secret Service are false. The statement comes after a Homeland Security watchdog told "Politico" that texts sent during and around the time of the January 6th Capitol attack were deleted after the watchdog had asked for those to be saved.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the panel on Biden's economy, his Middle East trip, and a changing Republican Party.\par \par \uc2 First, though, here's what some of our FOX affiliates around the country are covering tonight. FOX 29, San Antonio, police are searching for a woman who disappeared earlier this month. Home surveillance footage from July 5th shows Christina Powell leaving for work, but that's apparently the last time anyone saw the 39-year-old mother of two. Police say the father of one of Powell's children is cooperating.\par \par \uc2 WFXR, Roanoke, Virginia, there is some good news now, saying that everyone has been accounted for after severe flooding in the region. Officials initially reported 44 people missing, but in the end there were no fatalities, and that is great to hear.\par \par \uc2 Here's a live look at Denver, one of the big stories there tonight, FOX 31, officials announce a new edition for the Denver Zoo. Check this guy out, a male Asian small-clawed otter born July 1st to parent Pintar and Bu. Zookeepers say the pup is strong and healthy. Well done.\par \par \uc2 That's tonight look outside the beltway from SPECIAL REPORT. And we will be right back with the panel.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE DEPUTY PRESS SECRETARY: When you look at inflation, when you look at where we are economically, and we are in a strong -- we are stronger economically than we have been in history.\par \par \uc2 JANET YELLEN\uc2 , TREASURY SECRETARY: We are taking our own steps that we believe will be supportive in the short term to get inflation down.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 MITCH MCCONNELL\uc2 , (R-KY) SENATE MINORITY LEADER: Inflation is uniquely bad here because our all-Democratic government has made uniquely bad choices.\par \par \uc2 LARRY SUMMERS\uc2 , FORMER TREASURY SECRETARY: It's not going to be easy to bring inflation back to the kind of levels that we have become accustomed to in recent decades. We have let the genie out of the bottle.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HEMMER: It's a hard to shake, and it is issue number one clearly for 330 million Americans. Let's bring in the panel right now, Harold Ford Jr., former Tennessee Congressman, co-host of THE FIVE -- Harold, good evening to you -- Mollie Hemingway, editor in chief of "The Federalist," and Josh Kraushaar, "Axios" senior political correspondent.\par \par \uc2 Good evening to all of you. Josh, I want to show our viewers a graphic we first used last night. This tracks back to January of 21, far left part of your screen, inflation was at 1.4 percent. You come to June last month, you're at 9.1. So this is inflation in American. It could be Biden's economy. And the question tonight, Josh, is where is the plan, or is there one?\par \par \uc2 JOSH KRAUSHAAR, "NATIONAL JOURNAL": Inflation is the number one issue, and voters are very pessimistic about the state of the economy. I'm reminded of the famous James Carville quote, it's the economy, stupid. That is what this election is going to be all about. And I am so struck that you heard \uc2 Cecilia Rouse\uc2  in the clip saying that the economy is strong. That's not what the American public is feeling. Only 13 percent of Americans in a recent poll that came out this week thought that the economy was heading in the right direction. And Democrats are saying that same pessimistic thing.\par \par \uc2 So I think the first thing the White House needs to do is restore some credibility when talking about the economy. They've got to understand that people are feeling the pinch. They're having trouble paying for groceries and gas, and they can't keep saying the economy is great when clearly that's not how most Americans are feeling right now.\par \par \uc2 HEMMER: Harold, is there a plan or is there a prayer?\par \par \uc2 HAROLD FORD JR., FORMER TENNESSEE REPRESENTATIVE: First off, thanks for having me on. Josh is right. Democrats, we over the years have gotten more comfortable with representing and presenting the beliefs of the elites than everyday Americans. I grew up in a Democratic Party when my dad was in Congress. Uncles and aunts were in politics in Memphis, where everyday people believed in us, believed in our party and believed in the things we wanted to do for them.\par \par \uc2 Josh has polled among many -- his analysis among many illustrates and reinforces that position. If we don't realize that the progressive and AOC who is identified with them, that that position is not the position shared by most of our party members, and for that matter, most of the country, we're going to lose, and in fact, we probably should lose. I think what the president is doing, I know we'll get to it here shortly, and in the Middle East is an example of trying to get to where people are. We need lower energy prices, which means we should start producing here and even trying to persuade those around the globe to produce more as well.\par \par \uc2 HEMMER: OK, Mollie, how do they get out of this one, do you think?\par \par \uc2 MOLLIE HEMINGWAY, EDITOR IN CHIEF, "THE FEDERALIST": I think the problem is that this is not just a \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  problem. This is a problem shared by every voting member of the Democrat Party. The policies that made inflation so bad includes some spending packages that were supported by 100 percent of the senators and members of Congress who are Democrat. So it's not really a Biden economy so much as a Democrat economy, an almost a third term of the modern Democrat Party ushered in by \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The political issue is it's really a contrast with the Trump economy. Pre- COVID you had real wage growth. You had real wage gains for middle class families and for working class families. And that does speak to why those people are moving from the Republican Party as moving away from the Democrat Party as the Democrats get more focused on pleasing their wealthy interests.\par \par \uc2 HEMMER: Let's take that a step further, because Josh, you have a piece today, very interesting in "Axios." It's called the great realignment, and here's a quote. "The big picture, Republicans are becoming more working class and a little more multi-racial. Democrats are becoming more elite and a little white." You wrote it. Go ahead, explain it, Josh.\par \par \uc2 KRAUSHAAR: Yes, well, the Democrats have become the party of the college educated voter, and they've surrounded themselves in a little bit of an ideological bubble where they don't hear and respond to the concerns of a lot of everyday Americans.\par \par \uc2 And one of the more notable nuggets from that item from a "\uc2 New York Times\uc2 " poll this week showed that Hispanic voters, which used to overwhelmingly support Democrat candidates as recently as 2018, the polling shows Republicans and Democrats are now tied among that critical voting bloc. And just in 2018 Democrats held almost a 50-point advantage with Hispanics. So there is this concern among a lot of Democratic officials that they're only speaking to the elites, they're only speaking to sort of this upscale class, and they're not listening to working class voters of all backgrounds, of all races, and it's becoming a big political problem.\par \par \uc2 HEMMER: Interesting. Harold, I've got minute left, go ahead.\par \par \uc2 FORD: Democrats, pass the Chips Act. It will help Ohio and other big states bring new investments and in fact manufacture things here. Two, stop talking about defunding the police. Not only fund the police but defund those D.A.'s that are not doing a great job for us. Teach kids math, teach kids science, teach kids English, teach kids literature. And finally, stand up for women. Pass choices about freedom in this country. Expose those who don't want a rape or incest exception. You do those four things. We might have a chance of getting back with everyday Americans before November.\par \par \uc2 HEMMER: It's a big list. Thirty seconds left, Mollie, go ahead.\par \par \uc2 HEMINGWAY: That's actually another one of the big problems for Democrats is that they are too extreme on social issues. During the Clinton era, the mantra related to abortion was safe, legal, and rare. Now it's abortion on demand for any reason at all through all nine months of pregnancy. If you're trying to woo back Hispanic voters who are largely Roman Catholic and evangelical, that is no way to go about it, not to mention some of the extremism in how they talk about trans issues and other important things.\par \par \uc2 FORD: We might even win Mollie over. We might even win Mollie over if we do these things.\par \par \uc2 HEMMER: Thank you, Harold. Keep trying. Mollie, thank you. Josh, good piece. Thanks for being here.\par \par \uc2 Finally, tonight, a special day. Dignitaries --\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SALEM ABDULLAH AL-SABAH, KUWAITI AMBASSADOR TO U.S.: -- today like Kuwait and America with this memorial have come full circle.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HEMMER: The dignitaries honored guests and Gulf War veterans kicked off the groundbreaking ceremony for the new Desert Storm Memorial in Washington D.C. You can see the design commemorating the significance of those served in Operations Desert Shield and Desert Storm which helped liberate Kuwait from its Iraqi invaders in 1991, expected to open by Veterans Day in 2024. Check it out, coming soon. Cool stuff.\par \par \uc2 See you tomorrow in AMERICA'S NEWSROOM. Until then, I'm Bill Hemmer live in New York tonight. Make it a great night. In the meantime, Jesse Watters gets going right now, the old P.T., the old "PRIMETIME". Jesse, to you.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FNSPBB0020220715ei7e00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Biden Has U.S. Drowning In Humiliation; A Economy In Tatters Humiliates Us All; Newsom Setting His Sights On The White House?; Vaccines Don't Stop Spread Yet Mandates Remain; Dems' Ghoulish Views On Life On Full Display; Left Celebrates Abortions As Self-Love; Congressional Democrats Position On Access To Abortion Examined; President Biden Visits Middle East; Emmy Nominees Announces; Some Men In U.S. Getting Vasectomies In Wake Of Supreme Court Overturning Roe Versus Wade; Pennsylvania Democratic Senate Candidate \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  Facing Health Problems; Interview With Michigan Republican Gubernatorial Candidate Tudor Dixon\b0\par\par\uc2 Laura Ingraham, Trace Gallagher, Mollie Hemingway \par \uc2 7,595 \uc2 words\par \uc2 13 July 2022\par \uc2 Fox News: The Ingraham Angle\par \uc2 INGANG\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 SEAN HANNITY\uc2 , FOX NEWS HOST: We hope you set your DVR, so you never ever, ever miss an episode of "HANNITY". For news anytime, foxnews.com, hannity.com.\par \par \uc2 And in the meantime, let not your hearts be troubled. Stay tuned. Laura Ingraham, THE INGRAHAM ANGLE is here to take it away. Laura, how are you?\par \par \uc2 LAURA INGRAHAM, FOX NEWS HOST: Do you golf? Are you a golfer?\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: I used to. Used to be. I don't have time.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: So you don't know golf at all?\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: No, I golf occasionally, but not as good as I used to be.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Wait, so you're worse at golf than you were, let's say, 10 years ago? Is that the Krav Maga? Is that your Krav Maga messing up your golf? I knew that would happen.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: That's when I started training five days a week mixed martial arts.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: OK. Well, my gift to you then is going to be moot, but I'm going to send it anyway. But I have a gift for you that is just going in the--\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: A golf club that if you saw me in person, you'd hit me with?\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: You're getting warm. Now you're getting warm.\par \par \uc2 All right. We'll pick it up where you left off. I'm not going to give away the