not the case. They have a policy problem, not necessarily a message problem.\par \par \uc2 HANSON: They do. That's a good point. That's a good point, Laura. If Biden had inherited everyone of Trump's policies and left it unchanged and plagiarized like is his wont and said that it was his, we would be talking what a vigorous 79-year-old he is right now. They'd say this is spectacular. Look at the low inflation, affordable gas, the crime rate is still low. This is great. And the border is secure where the wall will be proceeding. And Biden is 79 and he did all this.\par \par \uc2 So it's not even of age. That's a force multiplying effect, but you're right, it's the agenda. And he's managed in a year-and-a-half to basically destroyed the country to the extent that a president can in such a short time.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: The left is now understanding that the working class has abandoned the Democrat Party, or the Democrats have abandoned the working class. Victor, in 20 seconds, can the Democrats in a short period of time when them back?\par \par \uc2 HANSON: No, we talked about that with the Virginia elections. Remember, Laura, we said, you asked -- you said they weren't going to change. They were going to double down. And they did, and they will continue to do that.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Victor, great to see you, as always, tonight.\par \par \uc2 And a big announcement about the month of July. Stay there.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: All right, that's it for us today. So don't forget, get your USA made Freedom Matters gear at LauraIngraham.com because all proceeds for the month of July will be donated to the Woodson Center. Of course, it was founded by civil rights icon and friend of THE ANGLE Bob Woodson back in 1981 to serve people in low-income neighborhoods.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BOB WOODSON, FOUNDER, THE WOODSON CENTER: Too many politicians look down their noses at communities. So what we're doing at the Woodson Center is recognizing that poor blacks are being exploited by race. The civil rights movement has been hijacked by the radical left, and they are using it to the detriment of black people. We are giving low-income blacks an opportunity to speak for themselves.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Really good stuff. Don't forget, LauraIngraham.com, get all your gear. This is really cool, it's a sun shirt and such a great color. I got a lot of comments on this particular shirt in Wyoming last week. So enjoy it, protect your skin from the ravages of the sun.\par \par \uc2 That's it for us. Remember, it's America now and forever. Greg Gutfeld is next.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document INGANG0020220712ei7b00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Sen. Dick Durbin (D-IL) is Interviewed about Democrats and the Filibuster; June Jobs Report; Biden Defends Saudi Talks; Death of \uc2 Shinzo Abe\uc2 ; \uc2 Boris Johnson\uc2  Resigns; Governors Make Headlines Sparking '24 Talks; Republicans Struggling in Midterms; Interview with Transportation Secretary \uc2 Pete Buttigieg\uc2 ; Interview with Sen. \uc2 John Barrasso\uc2  (R-WY)\b0\par\par\uc2 Mike Emanuel, Ben Domenech, Juan Williams, Mara Liasson, Kevin  Corke \par \uc2 9,069 \uc2 words\par \uc2 10 July 2022\par \uc2 Fox News: Fox News Sunday\par \uc2 FOXN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 MIKE EMANUEL, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: I'm Mike Emanuel.\par \par \uc2 President Biden takes executive action to protect access to abortion as women march on Washington urging the White House to do more.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: It's my hope and strong belief that women will, in fact, turn out in record numbers to reclaim the rights that were taken from them by the court.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL (voice-over): Calls for the president to declare public health emergency over abortion rights -- and vowing a summer rage in response to the Supreme Court's action. We'll have a live report.\par \par \uc2 Plus, economic silver lining. A rosy jobs report, gas prices coming off record highs, but the summer travel season marred by delays at the airport due to pandemic staff cuts and pilot shortages. We'll ask Transportation Secretary \uc2 Pete Buttigieg\uc2  about the waves of disruption and new spending to improve airports across the country.\par \par \uc2 And Senate Democrats make a last push for a party line package to revive the president's stalled domestic agenda. We'll bring in leaders from both sides of the aisle, Republican Conference Chair \uc2 John Barrasso\uc2 , and Senate Majority Whip Dick Durbin to discuss the fate of the massive spending bill ahead of midterms.\par \par \uc2 Plus, shock and sadness after the assassination of an American ally in one of the world's safest countries. And another key ally resigns after a stream of personal and policy scandals.\par \par \uc2 BORIS JOHNSON\uc2 , BRITISH PRIME MINISTER: In politics, no one is remotely indispensable.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: We'll ask our Sunday panel about any impact back here in the U.S.\par \par \uc2 All, right now, on "FOX News Sunday".\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL (on camera): And, hello, again, from FOX News in Washington.\par \par \uc2 President Biden bows to pressure from progressives, taking executive action on abortion, as Women's March protesters returned to D.C., and the White House celebrates new job numbers despite growing recession fears and those high gasoline prices hitting Americans hard during summer travel.\par \par \uc2 In a moment, Transportation Secretary \uc2 Pete Buttigieg\uc2  will join us live to discuss the major challenges we've seen this summer.\par \par \uc2 But, first, let's turn to Kevin Corke live at the White House with the administration's new action following the Supreme Court's reversal of Roe - - Kevin.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN CORKE, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Mike, a surprisingly strong jobs report and an executive action on abortion access really seemed to buoy the president with his base this week, and poll numbers show he needs it.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: The fastest way to restore woe -- Roe is to pass a national law codifying Roe, which I will sign immediately upon its passage on my desk.\par \par \uc2 CORKE (voice-over): President Biden this week unveiling executive actions and to push back against state legislatures and other lawmakers helping to further restrict abortion access.\par \par \uc2 This as thousands of pro-choice advocates protested in D.C., many urging the president to declare a national public health emergency as nearly two dozen states moved to ban the procedure.\par \par \uc2 Protests themselves have been the subject of intense scrutiny this week in Washington, following reports that an online group targeted Supreme Court Justice Brett Cavanaugh at a Washington area statehouse, forcing him to slip out the back door. The restaurant said the justice and other patrons were, quote, "unduly harassed".\par \par \uc2 The White House was less definitive.\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: We condemn intimidation, we condemn any violence. Peaceful protest -- people should be allowed to be -- to be able to do that that.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER: In a restaurant?\par \par \uc2 JEAN-PIERRE: If it's outside of a restaurant, if it's peaceful, for sure.\par \par \uc2 CORKER: All of this against the backdrop of the president's low poll numbers and American's complaints about high gas prices, economic uncertainty, even flight delays scuttling their summer plans.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 CORKER (on camera): Meanwhile, the president as you know, Mike, is preparing for a trip to Saudi Arabia and that trip comes amid heavy criticism of the kingdom's human rights record. And with that in mind, he actually penned an op-ed overnight in "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 ". In it, the president said he was looking to reorient, not rupture long-standing relations with an important ally in the region -- Mike.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Kevin Corke reporting from the White House -- Kevin, many thanks.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now, Transportation Secretary \uc2 Pete Buttigieg\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Mr. Secretary, welcome back to "FOX News Sunday".\par \par \uc2 PETE BUTTIGIEG, TRANSPORTATION SECRETARY: Thank you. Good morning.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Let's start with travel hassles during the busy summer travel season. We've had weeks of cancellations and delays amid a pilot shortage. This comes as there are complaints from passengers they're not being treated well by the commercial airlines.\par \par \uc2 And after American taxpayers gave the commercial aviation industry $54 billion during the pandemic, can you understand why some taxpayers feel like they aren't getting their money's worth from the airline industry?\par \par \uc2 BUTTIGIEG: That's right. A lot of passengers have experienced frustration, including me and millions of other Americans. Now, we've seen some improvement over the course of the summer, but still not an acceptable level in terms of performance, cancellation, and delays.\par \par \uc2 So, here's what we're doing about: we'll collaborate with airlines when they're ready to take steps that are positive and proactive, whether that's improvement in pay that are helping with hiring or flexibility in customer service.\par \par \uc2 We're also going to enforce passenger and consumer rights. And this is not new for us, as matter of fact, it was last year that I announced the stiffest fines ever imposed under our consumer protection program for airlines that were failing to provide refunds to passengers after they got stuck with cancellations. As a matter of fact, we have just concluded other 10 investigations on airlines on these issues, and have launched another 10 or so that we're going to pursue to make sure that the consumers and passengers are protected.\par \par \uc2 At the same time, I do want to emphasize again, we've seen movement in the right direction. You know, I gathered airline leaders after the Memorial Day travel weekend. We saw a really high level of cancellations and delays there.\par \par \uc2 The July 4th travel we can we just came through which had some of the busiest travel days of the year, definitely fewer cancellations and delays. The numbers I just saw from last few days, they're around 2, 2-1/2 percent, still higher than they should be. It's never going to be zero.\par \par \uc2 But we really want to see them push toward 1 percent or so with realistic scheduling, responsible customer service, and all the things airlines need to do to properly service the tickets that they are selling to passengers.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: U.S. commercial pilots must retire at age 65 by law. NBC did a study finding, quote, by 2029, not a single baby boomer will be able to legally fly commercial aircraft. As they leave, they take not just a substantial part of the labor force with them, but also decades of expertise and experience in the air.\par \par \uc2 I'm told Senator \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  is talking with colleagues about potentially raising that retirement age for commercial pilots to 67. Would you support that?\par \par \uc2 BUTTIGIEG: Look, these retirement ages are there for a reason, and the reason is safety. I'm not going to be on board with anything that could compromise safety.\par \par \uc2 Now, what's clearly the case is we need to cultivate, train, and support a new generation of qualified pilots. As a matter of fact, just last week, I was out with some programs that are helping to cultivate that interest in students and support them as they get their hours and get those qualifications.\par \par \uc2 The answer is not to keep the baby boomer generation in the cockpit indefinitely. The answer is to make sure that we have as many in as good pilots ready to take their place, to have a stronger pipeline. We're backing that up with \uc2 FAA\uc2  programs that support high school and college curriculums to get into aviation.\par \par \uc2 And, of course, ultimately, it will be for the airlines and those employers to hire and retain excellent talent.\par \par \uc2 And, you know, this is an issue we're seeing across the aviation sector, across the transportation sector at large -- the importance of having competitive pay, great job quality, so that we can bring in and keep the people that are going to be needed to power America's transportation sector.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: The White House had a major victory last year passing a massive infrastructure package. Supply chain issues and inflation are driving up at the price tag. You've said that basically the funding rolls out over five years. We're seven months into that process.\par \par \uc2 What are you and your team doing to try to move things along?\par \par \uc2 BUTTIGIEG: Well, we have been announcing a new program every few days pursuant to that infrastructure law.\par \par \uc2 Right now, for example, we're in the middle of rolling out up to a billion dollars in airport improvements. And these are going out to airports across the country. Orlando is going to see about $50 million to help expand gates and allow them to have more capacity and accommodate more passengers. Chattanooga is going to see improvements that help passengers move more smoothly through the airport.\par \par \uc2 And we're not forgetting smaller, more rural facilities that might not be household names, but could mean a huge amount economically to those regions. The Chamberlain, South Dakota, for example, their general aviation terminal right now is a mobile home. We're going to fund them to have the kind of terminal they really need in order for flight operations to grow and to succeed.\par \par \uc2 So, we're proud of what we're doing. Whether it's this airport terminal program going to 85 airports around the country that we're working on that this week, or initiatives to eliminate railroad crossings that can be both a headache and a safety hazard in communities across the U.S., or, of course, so much funding going to fixing roads, bridges, and other facilities that people need in order to get where they need to go safely and conveniently.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Quick follow-up, how quickly people see improvements in those airports?\par \par \uc2 BUTTIGIEG: So, the funding is flowing now, and obviously depends on the project a little bit. You know, constructing a whole new gate or a terminal takes longer than some of the more modest improvements. It really varies from airport to airport.\par \par \uc2 And this is an important thing to point out: we're not creating all of the designs in Washington. We know that it's local communities and individual airports or facilities that are going to know what their needs are. We're trying to trying to provide more of the funding.\par \par \uc2 But again, those dollars are going to be moving quickly. And I'll tell you, even before you see the improvements finished, you're going to see a lot of construction jobs associated with putting them in.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Will we see any projects that resemble some of the historic infrastructure investment years ago such as when the nation built the interstate highway system?\par \par \uc2 BUTTIGIEG: Sure. We're funding a lot of improvements for roads and bridges, too, but we're doing it in a better way I would say than what has been on the past. You know, there are a lot of lessons from choice of the `50s, `60s, and `70s. A lot of communities can point to where highway that was put in the period where they didn't pay attention to the community or listen to the community. It really cut up and separated a lot of our towns.\par \par \uc2 And so, we're also funding ways to reconnect those communities when a rail line or a highway overpass is doing as much harm as good. We're going to do it better this time.\par \par \uc2 The 2020s I think is going to be remembered as a period where we set up infrastructure that we're going to be proud of for an entire century. And I'm thrilled to be part of the process of working with states, working with cities, working with communities, working with airports and transit authorities, to get that work done.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Let's go to a red hot issue in Washington. Your husband tweeted after Justice \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2  left a Washington restaurant due to protesters. The tweet reads: It sounds like he just wanted some privacy to make his own dining decisions.\par \par \uc2 Is that appropriate, sir?\par \par \uc2 BUTTIGIEG: Look, when public officials go into public life, we should expect two things. One, that you should always be free from violence, harassment, and intimidation. And two, you're never going to be free from criticism or peaceful protest, people exercising their First Amendment rights.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: OK.\par \par \uc2 BUTTIGIEG: And that's what happened in this case.\par \par \uc2 Remember, the justice never even came into contact with these protesters, reportedly didn't see or hear them. And these protesters are upset because a right, an important right that the majority of Americans support was taken away.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 BUTTITGIEG: Not only the right to choose, by the way, but this justice was part of the process of stripping away the right to privacy.\par \par \uc2 As long as I've been alive, settled case law in the United States has been that the Constitution protected the right to privacy. And that has now been thrown out the window by justices, including Justice Kavanaugh who I recall swore up and down in front of God and everyone including \uc2 United States Congress\uc2  that they were going to leave settled case law alone.\par \par \uc2 So, yes, people are upset. They're going to exercise their First Amendment rights.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: OK.\par \par \uc2 BUTTIGIEG: And as long as that's peaceful, that's protected.\par \par \uc2 Compare that, for example, to the reality that as a country right now, we're reckoning with the fact that a mob summoned by the former president - -\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: All right. Let me follow-up, Mr. Secretary.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 BUTTIGIEG: -- attacked the United States Capitol, for the purpose of overthrowing the election and very nearly succeeded in the preventing the peaceful transfer of power.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: But --\par \par \uc2 BUTTIGIEG: I think common sense can tell the difference.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: But as a high-profile public figure, sir, are you comfortable with protesters protesting when you and your husband go to dinner at a restaurant?\par \par \uc2 BUTTIGIEG: Protesting peacefully outside in a public space -- sure. Look, I can't even tell you the number of spaces, venues, and scenarios where I've been protested.\par \par \uc2 And the bottom line is this: any public figure should always, always be free from violence, intimidation, and harassment, but should never be free from criticism or people exercising their First Amendment rights.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: All right. Let me ask you a campaign question, sir. Do you expect President Biden to run for reelection in 2024? And if not, will you run?\par \par \uc2 BUTTIGIEG: Yes, I do expect him to run. I'll support him.\par \par \uc2 And let me tell you why he's going to have a lot to be proud of and I'm going to choose my words carefully because I'm appearing as an official and this is not a campaign related appearance.\par \par \uc2 But I'm proud to be part of this administration that has done such work to bring America back from the brink of a recession, to create record job growth, to build achievements that would not have been thought possible just a couple of years ago, like the bipartisan infrastructure law that I have the honor of working on.\par \par \uc2 You know, people scoffed at the idea that you could get anything done on a bipartisan basis in Washington. And yet, the president delivered it, and that's after years and years, multiple presidents, multiple congresses coming and going, promising infrastructure bills without any results. This president, this administration, got it done and I'm proud to be part of that.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Mr. Secretary, thank you and thanks for making time this morning.\par \par \uc2 BUTTIGIEG: My pleasure, thank you.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Up next, the impact of the Supreme Court ruling on abortion and how it could change the trajectory and outcome of November's midterm elections. We will speak with two Senate leaders, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Right now, Senate Democrats are in negotiations to bring back parts of the president's Build Back Better agenda, which fell apart last year. To get it done, they're trying to find agreement without the help of Republicans on prescription drug pricing, climate spending and tax increases on the wealthy.\par \par \uc2 But Senate Republicans are pushing back and threatening to pull support from another major bill in the process. In a moment, we will speak live with the Senate majority whip, Democrat Dick Durbin of Illinois.\par \par \uc2 But, first, from Wyoming, Senate Republican Conference chair, \uc2 John Barrasso\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Senator Barrasso, welcome back to "FOX News Sunday".\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 JOHN BARRASSO\uc2  (R-WY): Thanks, Mike.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Okay. So Senate Leader \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 's trying to have Democrats choose between that bipartisan bill to help the U.S. semiconductor industry versus a Democrat-only climate tax and prescription drug package. Is that because if Democrats all stick together, he has limited options?\par \par \uc2 BARRASSO: Well, the American people are furious right now, and it's all about inflation. That's what this election is going to be about, and the future of our country.\par \par \uc2 Only -- 88 percent of Americans think the country is heading in the wrong direction. So what we've seen now is the Democrats see the handwriting on the wall. The president's own pollster said this is the worst environment he's ever seen in his lifetime for Democrats.\par \par \uc2 So they're trying a Hail Mary pass. \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  said he's once again trying this Build Back Biden and trying to bring it back, rise it from the dead.\par \par \uc2 A trillion dollars in taxes, Mike.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Uh-huh.\par \par \uc2 BARRASSO: More government spending, piling on in the Green New Deal.\par \par \uc2 Look, does anybody actually believe that raising taxes by a trillion dollars is going to help inflation? Or the taxing the companies that produce our oil, our gas to help -- that's going to lower the cost of energy? Not a single Republican is going to support this, Mike.\par \par \uc2 And, Mike, to my friend Joe Manchin from West Virginia, whose vote is going to be necessary for this --\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BARRASSO: -- I would remind him that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's popularity in that state it is as low as it is in Wyoming. Only 17 percent. Joe shouldn't walk the plank for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Part of that partisan Manchin-Schumer proposals expected to include, as you mentioned, bringing in about a trillion dollars in revenue. With what we're told, half expected to go towards deficit reduction, half towards energy and healthcare spending.\par \par \uc2 Is there an economic argument to be made that now is not the time for that kind of spending?\par \par \uc2 BARRASSO: Well, what we've seen is with the last big Democrat-only spending bill, it made inflation a lot worse. Democrats, the economist who worked for \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2 , \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  said, don't do it. It's going to bring out all this inflation. \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  ignored their advice, denied that there was inflation.\par \par \uc2 The American people know what they want. Families are really hurting all across the country. When I talk to people around Wyoming in terms of inflation, they're spending $100 a week more now than they were last year just to -- just to keep up.\par \par \uc2 And, you know, they said, what do you really want? You want enough money to be able to fill your tank with gas. You want enough money that you can buy a week's worth of groceries and you want to have enough money to pay the bills at the end of the month.\par \par \uc2 But \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is not going there. Oh, no. He's going to higher taxes, more government spending and attacking American energy. That is not the solution the American people are looking for, which is why only 1 in 10 Americans support the direction of the country right now.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Senator, let's talk midterms. Republicans believe they have a golden opportunity to win back the majority. But if candidates like \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 , \uc2 Eric Greitens\uc2  and Dr. Oz are not winning over the voters, is there a possibility the GOP could blow this opportunity?\par \par \uc2 BARRASSO: Well, I'm very optimistic about the future, election coming up in November.\par \par \uc2 The number one issue is inflation. And in all the issues that people care about, they look to Republicans as the solution on inflation, on energy, on the economy, on the chaos at the border, on crime in the cities.\par \par \uc2 And all of those nominees that you just mentioned -- and some are already the nominees, others are still in primaries that haven't been decided -- what we know is they understand their states. They understand the pain that people are having in those states.\par \par \uc2 And right now, you have candidates running as Democrats, who just this past week -- in Ohio, the Senate and the governor candidate refused to even show up or be seen when President Biden came to their state. People are distancing themselves as Democrats from this administration.\par \par \uc2 I'm very optimistic about our opportunities in November.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: There are a few reports that former President Trump could announce a third run for the White House before the midterms. Are you concerned that combined with the Roe v. Wade reversal could really galvanized and energized Democratic voters ahead of the midterms?\par \par \uc2 BARRASSO: Well, people will make their own decision as to what they vote on and how they decide to vote.\par \par \uc2 I will tell you, when President Trump was in the White House, gas prices were half what they are today. Inflation was non-existent. The border was secure. We were energy independent.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Uh-huh.\par \par \uc2 BARRASSO: \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's heading to Saudi Arabia hat in hand saying "please give us more energy" because he's killed energy in America. We need to get back to energy dominance, not energy dependence. And President Trump got us there.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Your fellow Wyoming Republican, Congresswoman \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2 , who's been perhaps the most outspoken critic of former President Trump, is in the primary fight of her life.\par \par \uc2 Do you see any path to victory for Congresswoman Cheney?\par \par \uc2 BARRASSO: Well, Wyoming politics is very personal, Mike. It's face to face. It's town to town.\par \par \uc2 And as you know, Liz and I disagree. I voted against impeachment on President Trump. She was for it. I voted against the partisan January 6th Commission. She's all in on that.\par \par \uc2 Now, the election isn't for another month. The travel that I've done around the state, I think she has a lot of work to do if she hopes to win the primary.\par \par \uc2 You say who's leading right now? The people that are doing the best are the people that are selling advertisements, and FOX News is doing very well.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: She has encouraged Wyoming Democrats to cross over and vote for her. Are there enough Wyoming Democrats out there to make a difference in her race?\par \par \uc2 BARRASSO: Well, number one, this wouldn't be the first time that Democrats cross over and flip their registration to vote in Republican -- in Republican primaries. They're really not that many Democrats out there. And even the chairman of the Democratic party of Wyoming, said there are not enough Democrats to do that.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Senator Barrasso, thanks so much. Always good to speak with you, sir.\par \par \uc2 BARRASSO: Thanks, Mike.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Joining us now from Illinois, Dick Durbin, the number two Democrat in the United States.\par \par \uc2 Senator, welcome back to "FOX News Sunday".\par \par \uc2 SEN. DICK DURBIN (D-IL): Good to be with you.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Lawmakers passed the first gun reform package a month ago, first significant gun reform in three decades. And yet, this week, your beautiful state was rocked by a mass shooting in Highland Park.\par \par \uc2 Sir, do you see any appetite for additional legislation, whether it'd be on guns or other root causes?\par \par \uc2 DURBIN: Well, I certainly hope so. I hope that my colleagues will take a look at what happened in Highland Park. It's less than a week ago.\par \par \uc2 And what happened that day, of course, seven people were killed. Scores were injured by a killer who got on top of a building and took a military weapon -- now, Smith and Wesson military and police weapon -- and fired off up to 90 rounds in just a few seconds. People were scattering and running, and sad things happened.\par \par \uc2 We found a 2-year-old who is protected by his father's dying body and he was finally reunited with his grandparents because he lost both his father and his mother to a shooter.\par \par \uc2 This week, we had good news. An 8-year-old who was hit by this killer is starting to speak again. He's coming out of critical condition. Whether he'll walk again is anyone's question at this point.\par \par \uc2 The question we have to ask ourselves and I'll ask my colleagues: Is this what we want in America when people families bring out their kids for Fourth of July parade that some killer can take a military style weapon and fire off 90 rounds into a crowd of innocent people and killing so many others (ph)? I just don't think that that's consistent with America's values or its Constitution.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Senator, the suspect in this week's shooting evaded your state's red flag laws. Is there a loophole there to be closed?\par \par \uc2 DURBIN: Well, certainly. And if you look across the nation, we have to be much more vigilant because there are over 10 million of these military assault weapons that have been sold, some say as many as 20 million.\par \par \uc2 There are 400 million guns in America and they're arguing -- some are arguing, we need more guns in the hands of more people.\par \par \uc2 It certainly hasn't made us a safer nation. We've had over 300 mass shootings this year. I didn't know when we took this Fourth of July break that Illinois and my state would be touched personally as it has been in Highland Park.\par \par \uc2 But ultimately, every senator, sadly, every senator has to face the prospect to have their own Highland Park before this is over.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: To be fair, though, if it were just about gun laws, wouldn't Chicago with some of the toughest gun laws in the country be a safer city?\par \par \uc2 DURBIN: Let me tell you something, quickly, Highland Park is not Chicago. It's a suburb of Chicago, a small town, a great small town. It's the first place you would have chosen to go to a Fourth of July parade with your son or grandson or granddaughter.\par \par \uc2 It's a wonderful community and to say, oh, Chicago is so dangerous. Yes, it's dangerous, but so is St. Louis and so with towns Jackson, Mississippi, and others have a murder rate as well.\par \par \uc2 It's a fact that we proliferated so many guns -- the police that I sat down with privately on a regular basis say, Senator, you just don't have any idea how many guns are out there and they're so easily accessible.\par \par \uc2 The gun industry has made a lot of money out of this, but America is not safer because of it.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Let me be clear. I want to express my heartfelt condolences to the people of Highland Park. And, of course to you, sir.\par \par \uc2 Let's talk policy on Capitol Hill. Senate majority leader \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  is having conversations with your colleague, Senator Joe Manchin, trying to do a slimmed down version of Build Back Better.\par \par \uc2 What are the natures -- what's the nature of those conversations? Take us inside the conversations and what's left to be ironed out.\par \par \uc2 DURBIN: The centerpiece, of course, is high prices -- the prices of prescription drugs.\par \par \uc2 And even before this period of the economy, American families all responded that the price of prescription drugs are just too high. People sometimes can't afford the medicines they need to stay alive. They wonder why the same drugs made in America cost a fraction of what we pay for months in countries like Canada and Europe, and they wonder why we haven't done anything as a nation to bring down the cost of prescription drugs.\par \par \uc2 The Democrats, \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2 , and all of us are working towards bringing down these prices to say to people who are facing insulin dependent diabetes that the cost of their drugs and insulin will be less than not more than $35 a month, dramatic changes, bringing down prices that families struggle with every single day.\par \par \uc2 Now, Senator McConnell has stepped up and saying, I'm going to stop this effort to bring down prescription drug prices. I don't want you to do it and you shouldn't do it. And if you persist in this effort to try and do it, I'm going to stop the bill that gives us better competition with China.\par \par \uc2 The semiconductor industry is central to economic growth and job creation in this country. We've lost ground on it, and we've got to build a change that. And McConnell has said he's going to stop that bill and he's going to do it if we should violate the pharmaceutical companies' plea to us not to lower prescription drug prices.\par \par \uc2 The bottom line is this, American families need help in paying their bills and they need help in lifesaving drugs being affordable. The Democrats are leading that. Senator McConnell opposes it.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: There are Democrats right now pressing for structural changes to the \uc2 United States Supreme Court\uc2 . Here now is Congresswoman \uc2 Alexandria Ocasio-Cortez\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. ALEXANDRIA OCASIO-CORTEZ (D-NY): Congress, we have the - we have the possibility when we are strengthened by the repeal of the filibuster or even the change to a talking filibuster or a standing filibuster. In doing so we can codify Roe, we can codify -- and all of the other cases that the Supreme Court indicated that they would threaten. We can codify same sex marriage. We can codify the right to contraception. We can codify interracial marriage. We can do it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Is she firing up the base that major changes are coming when, in fact, you don't have the votes in the Senate?\par \par \uc2 DURBIN: Well, I can tell you that the bottom line is, she wants to change the filibuster rule when it comes to voting rights and other issues. I support that. I think that we ought to make exceptions to the 60 vote requirement when we get down to fundamental questions like this. And it's really up to the senators, who are currently serving, the 50 Democrats. Two of them decided not to vote with us on one of these rule changes earlier. Whether they've changed their position on that since the Supreme Court handed down this decision, taking away the constitutional rights of Americans, I don't know, but I'll be asking them next week.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: As chairman of the Judiciary Committee, what do you say to progressives calling for impeaching some of the conservative justices?\par \par \uc2 DURBIN: I don't think it's realistic. I can tell you, there is, in my mind, a clear conflict of interest when it comes to Justice Thomas and issues related to the January 6th insurrection. His wife is actively involved politically. Going so far as to give direct advice to the president's chief of staff during this crisis. I would think that Justice Thomas should recuse himself from any decisions that relate to the January 6th episode. Already he was the single vote earlier on a case related to that issue. I think it's a mistake.\par \par \uc2 As to whether he's going to be impeached, that is not realistic. But he should show good judgment.\par \par \uc2 If this court is going to be credible, it has to be as a political as possible. Exactly the opposite has been happening.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Senator, a lot of headlines this week about Democratic governors, including your own \uc2 J.B. Pritzker\uc2  of Illinois. Do you think gridlock here in Washington could potentially create an opening for one of these Democrats to challenge President Biden in 2024?\par \par \uc2 DURBIN: Well, I'm not looking ahead six years, I'm looking ahead to this week and whether we can come together, Democrats and Republicans, and get something done in Washington, bring down the cost of prescription drugs, deal with this issue of semiconductor chips, which has frozen economic expansion in this country. Let's get this agenda underway.\par \par \uc2 And the election, in just a few months, will take care of itself. And the one years off is certainly over the horizon from my point of view.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Senator Durbin, thanks very much for joining us.\par \par \uc2 DURBIN: Good to be with you.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Up next, we'll bring in our Sunday group to discuss the seeming disconnect between voters' impression of the economy and the White House.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: We are in a strong - we are stronger economically than we have been in history.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: White House Press Secretary \uc2 Karine Jean-Pierre\uc2  claiming the U.S. economy's strong despite inflation not seen in four decades and recession fears on the minds of many Americans.\par \par \uc2 And it's time now for our Sunday group.\par \par \uc2 "USA Today" Washington bureau chief Susan Page, Fox News political analyst Juan Williams, Fox News contributor Ben Domenech, and NPR national political correspondent \uc2 Mara Liasson\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Panel, welcome. Great to have you around the table.\par \par \uc2 MARA LIASSON\uc2 , FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Good to be here.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: So, Susan, let's start here.\par \par \uc2 We have a strong jobs report this week. The White House took a bit of the victory lap. But a new Monmouth poll says only about 10 percent of the country thinks the country is heading in the right direction. What about the disconnect here?\par \par \uc2 PAGE: Yes. Well, and it was a good job support. And the news on employment continues to be strong. Perhaps we won't head into a recession. Mixed signals on that.\par \par \uc2 But inflation just overwhelms everything. Inflation affects Americans every time they fill up their tank of their car, it affects them every time they go to the grocery store to buy something. So that is the economic indicator that just defines people's sentiment that the nation is headed in the wrong direction.\par \par \uc2 One other number from that Monmouth poll. Only 10 percent of Americans think the country is headed in the right direction. That is a devastating in historic number.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Ben, less than four months out from midterm elections, and you've got the Democrats trying to get some of the spending across the finish line. The slimed down Build Back Better. Is that putting Republicans in an awkward spot?\par \par \uc2 DOMENECH: I don't think so. It's -- this is something I think now where you have certain things baked in for this election, and they're not going to change. The kinds of uncertainty, the kinds of questions that Americans have about their pocketbook issues, not just energy prices, as Susan said, but the other effects that they're seeing, they know - that run through our economy in lots of different, challenging ways are just things that I don't think this White House or the Democratic Party are going to be able to turn around in time to change things for November.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Juan, you want to jump in?\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Well, I think that, you know, time is short. I think everybody agrees on that. But I do think that if you look back over the last three weeks, Mike, Democrats are in a better position than we would have anticipated, not just because of the good jobs numbers that we saw on Friday. But you think about it for a second. Gas prices actually are headed down. They're still too high, but they're headed in the right direction.\par \par \uc2 In addition to that, you have to stop and think about the January 6th committee, which has had success in terms of penetrating public consciousness and public opinion. In terms of the bringing chaos, Trump's chaos to the forefront of the conversation again.\par \par \uc2 And then you think about the abortion decision. You think about the gun massacres that have taken place. You touched on that with Senator Durbin. A lot of these issues don't play well for Republicans, play well -- better for the Democrats and make the Republicans look like a radical party.\par \par \uc2 DOMENECH: I don't believe, Juan, the January 6th committee has gotten anywhere near close enough to penetrating broader - the public conscious. I think that the numbers that you see, in fact, in response to the decision on Dobbs v. Jackson don't support the idea that there's going to be a dramatic change in terms of Democrats getting a huge vote number out.\par \par \uc2 And the gun issue, while it does play against Republicans time and again, it doesn't actually have this kind of election changing format that I think you would need to see.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Well, I think if you look at the generic numbers, just the generic numbers, do you prefer a Republican or a Democrat in November, those numbers have narrowed. Some polls even have Democrats now in the lead over the last three weeks over these issues.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Let's bring Mara into the mix.\par \par \uc2 So, the president's out with an op-ed this morning basically defending his meeting with Saudi leaders and saying that he's restored stability to the Middle East region.\par \par \uc2 LIASSON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: What do you make of it?\par \par \uc2 LIASSON: Yes, the op-ed didn't mention the word "oil," and that's what he was accused of, you know, downplaying human rights, which the Saudis have violated a lot. To go hat in hand to ask them to pump more oil. That op-ed didn't mention that.\par \par \uc2 But, look, the Middle East is a really important region. The president has to go over there. He's trying to tout his accomplishments. And it's a difficult position to be in because he wanted to make Saudi Arabia a pariah. I think that's the actual word he used. And instead now he's going to be meeting with those leaders.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Susan, we learned early Friday morning about the assassination of former Prime Minister \uc2 Shinzo Abe\uc2  of Japan while he was out campaigning. Talk about his role in terms of his relationships with U.S. leaders over the years.\par \par \uc2 PAGE: Well, he met with four of them I think when they were president. He was - he built strong U.S./Japan ties. He was, I think, especially important in getting Japan to move past its World War II legacy on military fails. He built a stronger Japanese military. He took a particularly interesting stance towards China. Talked to the China -- Chinese leader, recently stood up for the rights of Taiwan, the need for others (INAUDIBLE) definitely a very powerful, the longest serving Japanese prime minister in its history. A big loss and shocking in Japan for there to be gun violence.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Ben, another major shakeup, a political shakeup, in London with \uc2 Boris Johnson\uc2  announcing that he will be leaving office. Here's how Fox Nation host Pirs Morgan explained it.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PIERS MORGAN, HOST OF "PIERS MORGAN UNCENSORED": I think \uc2 Boris Johnson\uc2  has a massive problem with the truth. Just finally come back to home, \uc2 Boris Johnson\uc2 . And then when he was caught, he did what \uc2 Boris Johnson\uc2  always does, and he tried to lie his way out of it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Was \uc2 Boris Johnson\uc2 's departure about policy or politics?\par \par \uc2 DOMENECH: Well. first off, let me - let me say my condolences to a former member -- editor at "The Spectator," where I am also editor at large now. So, pour one out for Boris.\par \par \uc2 He is the third prime minister in a row brought down by \uc2 Boris Johnson\uc2  in certain respects, which I think we should appreciate. He is -- he's an incredible figure and someone who I think certainly changed the dynamic. But part of the problem here is that Boris stopped being Boris. And by that I mean he stopped being the kind of populist that conservatives could have confidence in when it came to forming the kind of coalition that led to getting 14 million votes in 2019.\par \par \uc2 The problem for them now is that forming that coalition again, I think, will prove to be very impossible. And just one thing that I think we should appreciate about politics in the U.K. is that, you know, as much as people may speak in high Dutchen (ph) about honesty or scandal or something like this, this is such a farcical reason for him to be pushed out on so many different respects. It had a lot more to do with the failure of Covid related policies, his break with a lot of the things that he stood for before he ascended to the PM position and not to do to spending too much on parties or drapes. It would be the equivalent of getting rid of \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2  for going to the French Laundry. It doesn't really track as being very serious.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Juan, what do you make of Johnson announcing his departure, but sticking around until conservatives pick a new leader?\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Well, I think, you know, Johnson's a lot -- we were -- Ben's talking about him as a populist leader. I think he's a lot like Trump in that regard. And we see that, you know, Trump is the kind of man of nine lives, always reinventing himself, trying to retain the position of power. Clearly the conservatives in Britain got rid of Johnson. Republicans, conservatives here are still locked into Trump. But I think Johnson wants to influence who might come next. His successor. Maybe he thinks he might be re-elected at some point. And he'll make a lot of money on the speaking circuit, writing, I'm sure, maybe back at "The Spectator."\par \par \uc2 But I think -\par \par \uc2 DOMENECH: We'd love him. He could also come back to America.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: What - what I think he does - he will - he does want to have some influence on the Brexit deal, what comes forward with Brexit.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Sure.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: As well -- and I think this is of more global concern, the Ukraine. He's been a great friend to President Zelenskyy and the Ukrainians. And I think lots of people want to make sure that that remains in place.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: All right, panel, we're off to a great start. We have to take a break here.\par \par \uc2 Up next, a string of mass shootings adding to calls for new gun reforms and change here in Washington. We'll discuss the response from some of the nation's governors leading the charge.\par \par \uc2 Plus, what the fight between \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  and \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2  could foreshadow for 2024.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: You all heard what happened today. But each day we're reminded there's nothing guaranteed about our democracy. Nothing guaranteed about our way of life.\par \par \uc2 GOV. J.B. PRITZKER (D-IL): If you're angry today, I'm here to tell you, be angry. I'm furious. I'm furious that yet more innocent lives were taken by gun violence.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: That's President Biden and Illinois Governor \uc2 J.B. Pritzker\uc2  both reacting to the horrific July 4th parade shooting but in somewhat contrasting tones.\par \par \uc2 And we're back now with the panel.\par \par \uc2 So, Mara, you heard different responses from the president and the Illinois governor. Some people criticizing the way the president responded and elevating Governor Pritzker's response. Your thoughts.\par \par \uc2 LIASSON: Yes, I mean, there are some Democratic activists who wanted the president to be more fiery. Substantively, there's not much difference between Pritzker and the president. They both want gun safety laws, including, you can't buy a gun until you're 21, you have to have a universal background check. Those things are supported by huge majorities of Americans. In some polls over 80 percent or 90 percent are for those measures, and they can't get passed by Congress. And that's, I think, the real root of Democrats frustration.\par \par \uc2 PAGE: I think -- I think it's all about 2024 to.\par \par \uc2 LIASSON: Well, then there's that.\par \par \uc2 PAGE: You know, I think - I think Governor Pritzker is one of several Democrats who want to run -\par \par \uc2 LIASSON: If Biden doesn't, yes.\par \par \uc2 PAGE: If Biden doesn't. Maybe if Biden does. I mean I think some of this - this - the rhetoric we're hearing now is aimed at saying to President Biden, maybe you should reconsider whether you're going to announce that you run for another term.\par \par \uc2 LIASSON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PAGE: You know, you see such an ease among Democrats about what's going to happen this November and then what the prospects are looking two years down the road.\par \par \uc2 LIASSON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: To pick up on that, you've got Governor \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2 , from California, going after Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  in Florida, running ads in Florida. Is this about raising his profile? And is he considering a primary run against President Biden?\par \par \uc2 PAGE: It's hard to imagine why the governor of California is paying campaign funds to run an ad in -- against the Florida governor in Florida unless he's looking down the road. There are both those governors, I think, have ambitions for the White House. We'll see how it goes for each of them.\par \par \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 , definitely annoying president -- former President Trump by being pretty open about his ambitions there.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Ben, do Florida voters give a darn about what \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2 's saying, and are those ads going to make any difference, or do they just make them feel better about their governor being out there in high profile?\par \par \uc2 DOMENECH: In the last couple of years, two years, I believe, the - look -- more than 11,000 people moved from California to Florida, bringing with them about $1.9 billion worth of business, according to the \uc2 Americans for Tax Reform\uc2 . So, I think that there's definitely some movement and it's not in the direction of California.\par \par \uc2 But to pick up on Susan's point, I do think that these are kind of signals to the White House signals to \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and signals to Democratic donors that you don't have to necessarily do this again. And you saw the piece in "The \uc2 New York Times\uc2 " this morning. You see the piece that in - that ran in "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 ." You see other pieces that are coming out now about increasingly open skepticism that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  can run again given the levels of unpopularity and the levels of difficulty that this White House has faced.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Feels like (INAUDIBLE) -\par \par \uc2 : And look at the polls in - about the number of people who don't want Trump to run again, Republicans.\par \par \uc2 DOMENECH: Yes (ph).\par \par \uc2 LIASSON: Pretty similar to the numbers of Democrats that don't want Biden to run again.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: OK, Juan, the more immediate political concern, balance of power up there on Capitol Hill. You've got some Republican candidates who seem to be struggling a bit to connect with voters. I'm thinking of \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 , Dr Oz, \uc2 Eric Greitens\uc2  and perhaps others.\par \par \uc2 What is that doing to \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 's hopes of being the majority leader in the new Congress?\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Well, I think, you know, you just have to look at his behavior. Right now he seems like he's upset about the possibility of the Democrats passing this prescription drug bill that you discussed earlier in the show, and also the competitiveness bill. I think the Democrats clearly want to say, we are doing something. We can get things done. And \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 's tactic over the years has been to stall, to obstruct, and he's employing that tactic now at the risk of looking hypocritical because he supports these bills in the past.\par \par \uc2 So, the question becomes, what does he think? And I think it's about just what you said, Mike, which is that he's worried now that it looks better for the Democrats with weak - with week Republican nominees running for these Senate seats. You know, the numbers in Georgia have shifted recently with suddenly Senator Warnock doing much better against \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 . You look in Pennsylvania, similar shifts. And I think that Senator McConnell is well aware of it.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Ben, what are you hearing in conservative circles? How much alarm is there? Is -- we've seen in the past they thought they could take back the majority in previous election cycles and it didn't work out. So, are the Republicans going to squander this opportunity?\par \par \uc2 DOMENECH: Well, they're great at it, but ideas don't run for office, people do. And that's part of the problem that they're facing here in the sense that you can have ideas of going against the Biden agenda, of emphasizing energy independence, of not, you know, embracing, you know, some of the more radical policies and high spending that has been embraced in the last couple of years. But if your - if your people who were out there on the trail vocalizing this have significant personal challenges, that makes it tougher to do.\par \par \uc2 That being said, every indication we have in terms of the overall perspective, the right track wrong track, the level of shifts that you've seen, and in some of these special elections and the like seem to indicate a Republican wave year that may, as we've seen in wave years before, end up sending a lot of people to Washington who no one ever expected to make it there.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: So perhaps some races that are not getting front and center attention could be surprises. Places like New Hampshire, Colorado, Arizona?\par \par \uc2 DOMENECH: Certainly, I think, and I think Nevada is going to be one of these races that could end up being critical in terms of determining whether McConnell is the majority leader or not.\par \par \uc2 PAGE: But, you know, there are really two models that we see. One is that you have a wave election that really sweeps away their Democratic majority in the House. I think even Democrats are pretty pessimistic. They think that is going to happen.\par \par \uc2 But there have been cases where Senate candidates have been able to shield themselves to -- from a wave to some degree. And so Democrats do hope to hold on to even just a 50/50 split because think of the difference in \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's last two years or second two years of his term as president if he has a divided Congress, or if he has a Congress that is united under Republican control.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: And, Mara, Roe v. Wade. As a female panelist, what's the impact on the midterms?\par \par \uc2 LIASSON: That's - well, that's the big question, how will these issues, like gun violence, Roe v. Wade, will they energized Democratic voters enough to close that enthusiasm gap and make a difference? We don't know that yet. I think the economy is still the number one, two, and three issue driving voters concerns this year.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Folks feeling it in the wallet when they go to the gas station, where they go to the grocery store.\par \par \uc2 LIASSON: Yes. Yes, that's usually - but, look, the reaction to Roe was pretty intense. The reaction to the gun violence is intense. And Democrats are hopeful that those kinds of issues can help in -- energize their base.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Juan, do you think Democrats hold on or do you think we're looking at a Republican Senate?\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: I think that's up in the air. I would -- I would have thought earlier that there was more likelihood that it would lean Republican. I don't think that at the moment. But in terms of the House, this goes back to the fact that Americans have gone through the pandemic. They're upset about gas prices. And they're just angry. And we see historically they vote against the incumbent in these midterms. It happened to Clinton. It happened to Obama. It happened to Trump. And I think it's likely to happen in the House again.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: All right, panelists, thanks so much. We'll see you next Sunday.\par \par \uc2 Up next, a final word on the week ahead.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: And that's it for today. I'm Mike Emanuel. Have a great week and we'll see you back here next FOX NEWS SUNDAY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FOXN000020220710ei7a00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Another Day, Another Batch Of Biden Blunders; Biden Signs Executive Order On Abortion Amid Growing Pressure From Far-Left Voices\b0\par\par\uc2 Gregg Jarrett, Charlie Hurt, Sara Carter, Leo Terrell, David Webb \par \uc2 7,158 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 July 2022\par \uc2 Fox News: Hannity\par \uc2 SHAN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 GREGG JARRETT, FOX NEWS HOST: And welcome everyone to this special edition of HANNITY. I'm Gregg Jarrett, in for Sean.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, top Democrats and their friends in the liberal media all but begging President Biden to do something anything on a variety of key issues. In response, today, the White House signed a rather useless executive order on abortion rights that does little to nothing, just another political stunt from a White House that is truly incompetent in every way. What else would you expect from a president who struggles with basic tasks like -- you know, walking and talking, even with the assistance of a teleprompter? Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: It is noteworthy that the percentage of women who registered to vote and cast a ballot is consistently higher than the percentage of the men who do so, end of quote. Repeat the line. Women are not without electoral and/or political or -- or maybe precisely, not and or -- or political power.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JARRETT: Truly a Ron Burgundy moment. I don't think you're actually supposed to read the speaking clues out loud, Joe.\par \par \uc2 Believe it or not it went downhill from there on out. Take a look.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: And that's has changed -- excuse me, all that's changed is this court, end of quote. Fastest way to restore woe -- Roe.\par \par \uc2 Ten years old, and she was forced to have to travel out of the state to Indiana to seek to terminate the presidency and maybe save her life.\par \par \uc2 When tens of millions of women vote this year, they won't be alone. Millions and millions of men will be taking up the fight alongside them to restore the right to choose. Consider the challenge accepted, court.\par \par \uc2 Right now, in all 50 states and the District of Columbia (ph).\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JARRETT: It's actually "terminate a pregnancy" not "terminate a presidency", although think about it. It's not a bad idea.\par \par \uc2 Joining us with more on Biden's executive order on abortion, Utah Senator \uc2 Mike Lee\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Senator, thanks for being with us.\par \par \uc2 I've read it several times, the executive action, signed by the president. It strikes me as a charade that does almost nothing meaningful. Now, I may be wrong. What's your take?\par \par \uc2 SEN. MIKE LEE (R-UT): I think that's a fair assessment of it. And look, how however badly he bungled the delivery which he clearly did, the message was nonetheless clear, the message was he will stop at nothing to appease the far left base of his party, those who are intent on making sure that the most defenseless and vulnerable members of our society, unborn babies, can have their lives snuffed out right up into the moment of live birth. It's a real problem and, yeah, you're right, the executive order does very little in and of itself.\par \par \uc2 But it's kind of strange that this is how the leader of the free world is devoting his time, to making sure that the far left pro-abortion base gets its way with him.\par \par \uc2 JARRETT: One of the things that jumped out at me as I watched. The president said the Dobbs decision is not driven by the Constitution. My Lord, nearly all 213 pages of that decision and, you know, the other opinions were devoted to the Constitution, weren't they?\par \par \uc2 LEE: Yeah. Look, the whole thing was and I'd encourage all of your viewers who have not yet read Justice Alito's masterful opinion to do so that they will be left with the unmistakable conviction that there is nothing in the Constitution that separates this and makes it somehow a decision that can be made only by justices and judges. This is something that is reserved to the people because it's not taken off the table by the Constitution.\par \par \uc2 JARRETT: You know, the other thing he said is he's waiting for Congress to act to pass legislation to codify abortion rights. Wouldn't that be contrary to the majority opinion in Dobbs which said we are returning the decision making to the states where it belongs, not the federal government?\par \par \uc2 LEE: Yes. It would be contrary to the Dobbs decision, or else, it would also be contrary to common sense. But just as important anything else, it would be contrary to the will of the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Look, President Biden within the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2  still doesn't even have a majority a simple majority to pass the abortion legislation that he wants to enact. So he doesn't have the votes to do that and so, he's just swatting back at the Supreme Court pretending that that wasn't about the Constitution which it plainly was.\par \par \uc2 JARRETT: And, Senator, let's say hypothetically that there are enough votes in Congress, both houses of Congress to pass an abortion rights legislation, it would simply be erased when inevitably the pendulum swings and control goes back to Republicans, as well as the presidency would create chronic whiplash, a sort of ping-pong back and forth, wouldn't it?\par \par \uc2 So from a practical standpoint, it really doesn't work. It's more destructive than constructive.\par \par \uc2 LEE: No, that's exactly right. And this is one of the many reasons why I think they're just playing to their base here.\par \par \uc2 So a little bit like some of their other efforts the efforts they want to nuke the filibuster, to make it easier to pass legislation for a -- for example to expand the Supreme Court, to pack the Supreme Court. As I explained in my book, "Saving Nine", if they did that that too would create a whiplash effect that would prove to be a short-term victory for the liberals because the next time Republicans had control of both houses of Congress and the White House, they would undo it.\par \par \uc2 But as I explained in "Saving Nine", it would also have devastating effects long term for the independence of the federal judiciary and with it, the Constitution of the United States.\par \par \uc2 JARRETT: All right. Senator \uc2 Mike Lee\uc2 , great to see you as always. Thanks for joining us.\par \par \uc2 Here now with more is the author of the new book out next week, "My American Life", Colorado Congresswoman \uc2 Lauren Boebert\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Congresswoman, great to see you. Thanks for being here.\par \par \uc2 I was taking a look at a new national poll, 72 percent of Americans oppose abortion after 50 -- after 15 weeks, including 60 percent of Democrats. So doesn't this show that most Americans are not aligned with the liberal position of, you know, abortion at all times?\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 LAUREN BOEBERT\uc2  (R-CO): Gregg, that's exactly right. The majority of people do not want full term abortions and the left has just taken everything so far. They want to blame the Supreme Court decision on conservatives, on Republicans. But this is absolutely their fault. They have pushed abortions down everyone's throat and they have taken it way too far, even wanting post-term abortions here in the United States.\par \par \uc2 This is something that needs to go back to the states and I think the Supreme Court did a great job on that decision. But everything that they are doing is so radical and driving people to the Republican Party, saying, we are tired of paying the price for bad policy. We want a safe country. We don't want to scream our abortions from the streets. So they are no longer the party of safe legal and rare.\par \par \uc2 But, Gregg, Biden's press conference today really was all bark and no bite, end quote, repeat the line. Biden's press conference today was all bark, no bite.\par \par \uc2 I do like his Freudian slip to terminate the presidency rather than a pregnancy. But seriously, he announced no new legislation, no groundbreaking executive orders. He just stood there and poorly read a teleprompter while Secretary Becerra tried his best not to laugh at this so-called commander-in-chief.\par \par \uc2 And here's the deal, the Supreme Court ruled abortion is not nor was not ever a constitutional right. It's up to the states and the people to decide what protections to give the pre-born. Leftists who now make up the genocide squad want abortion anytime, anywhere, including babies who are already born. But that's not going to happen.\par \par \uc2 JARRETT: Yeah. The other aspect of it today as I was watching, he was engaging in fear tactics, scare tactics, saying, oh, other enumerated rights, same-sex marriage and contraception are now at risk.\par \par \uc2 No, if you read the Alito opinion and I doubt that Joe read it, if he read it, he didn't comprehend it, which is not a surprise. Alito specifically said these other rights are not at risk. They are different rights that that find their protections elsewhere in the Constitution.\par \par \uc2 So this was just in a blatant fear-mongering, wasn't it?\par \par \uc2 BOEBERT: Absolutely. I would say the only right that is under threat by the Biden regime is our Second Amendment rights, with his executive orders and his rhetoric on stripping law-abiding citizens away from being able to have the right to protect themselves and especially protect themselves against a tyrannical government.\par \par \uc2 But, look, Gregg, Biden has -- he said that he doesn't think conservatives have a clue about the power of the American woman. And he's right, we mama bears are powerful and that's exactly why Democrats will lose in November. Moms across America are tired of being called domestic terrorists for caring about their children's education, tired of being called a bigot for wanting the border closed, tired of being called sexist, for saying drag shows are inappropriate for children.\par \par \uc2 So, women across America will be showing up this November to ensure \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  isn't just a sleeping duck, but a lame duck.\par \par \uc2 JARRETT: Yeah. Well, stumbling, fumbling and mumbling through a teleprompter speech really does call into question not only his mental acuity but his competency.\par \par \uc2 We'll leave that for another time.\par \par \uc2 Congresswoman Boebert, thank you so much for being with us.\par \par \uc2 Tonight in the wake of the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2 's decision on abortion, Justice \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2  continues to be stalked and harassed by deranged far-left agitators. Keep in mind just a few weeks ago, a lunatic traveled all the way from California to Kavanaugh's own neighborhood in Maryland with plans to assassinate him.\par \par \uc2 And on Wednesday, the Supreme Court justice was forced to flee from a restaurant through a rear door after a mob of liberals showed up to interrupt his meal. But according to the White House press secretary, \uc2 Karine Jean-Pierre\uc2 , this is what democracy looks like. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: Peaceful protest -- people should be allowed to be -- to be able to do that.\par \par \uc2 PETER DOOCY, FOX NEWS WHITE HOUSE REPORTER: In a restaurant?\par \par \uc2 JEAN-PIERRE: If it's outside of a restaurant, if it's peaceful, for sure.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: Really?\par \par \uc2 JEAN-PIERRE: Peaceful protest, where you were your question to me was intimidation.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: Justices, because protesters do not agree with an opinion that they signed on to have no right to privacy is what you're saying?\par \par \uc2 JEAN-PIERRE: Peter, this is -- this is -- people have the right -- this is what a democracy is. People have the right.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: People don't have a right to privacy?\par \par \uc2 JEAN-PIERRE: Of course, people have a right to privacy but people also have a right to be able to protest peacefully, peacefully. It's the intimidation and the violence that we condemn.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JARRETT: Well, the restaurant where Kavanaugh was dining had a slightly different take on this. Morton's steakhouse condemned the angry mob, calling them unruly, selfish, and devoid of decency.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now with reaction, "The Price of Principle: Why Integrity is Worth the Consequences", Harvard Law professor \uc2 Alan Dershowitz\uc2 , along with FOX News legal -- FOX News contributor Charlie Hurt.\par \par \uc2 Professor, on the left today, they were absolutely cackling at Kavanaugh being forced to flee a restaurant. Their rage, their glee was palpable. That's pretty stunning in civility and I'm reminded of how you were treated at one point in time when you dared to defend \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  during the second impeachment.\par \par \uc2 ALAN DERSHOWITZ\uc2 , HARVARD LAW PROFESSOR: I'm still treated that way on Martha's Vineyard. People threaten to walk out of events or dinners if I'm invited even though it was years ago.\par \par \uc2 The way Justice Kavanaugh was treated was absolutely, absolutely disgraceful. Whether or not there is a right to protest, remember that protest can be restricted by time, manner and location, and it might be constitutional to pass a statute saying that protests that interfere with people's basic rights like the right to eat, of justices, might be unconstitutional.\par \par \uc2 But look I'm -- I oppose the reversal of Roe versus Wade. I think, by the way, what Congresswoman Boebert said, calling people on my side of the issue the, quote, genocide squad and saying that we are prepared for babies already born to be killed is even more disgraceful than anything that happened to the justice. And I just want to condemn those remarks in the strongest terms.\par \par \uc2 I support a woman's right to choose but I do not support anybody protesting justice Kavanaugh eating a meal in peace, being at home in his house, or not being having to fear an assassin walking on his lawn.\par \par \uc2 Americans have the right to protest but they should protest in the right place, the right manner. You can protest in front of the Supreme Court but leave a guy in his family alone to have a decent meal, whether you agree with them or disagree with them. That's the American way.\par \par \uc2 JARRETT: You know, one activist group, Charlie, is now paying $200 for any information about the location of a justice. I mean, this is -- this is not just incivility. It's indecency, isn't it?\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE HURT, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Yeah, no, it really is, and in addition to being absolutely appalling and I would think and in some cases downright illegal. When you listen to the White House defend this kind of behavior and then get into some weird sophomoric idiotic distinction between whether he's dining inside or outside or whatever I don't think she even knew what she was talking about -- but all of that is you know kind of gives you some sense of why uh these protesters feel so emboldened to carry on like this.\par \par \uc2 But there's a whole other thing in addition to the legal aspect of all of this and the intentions of having sort of vigorous debate here on cable television that should be -- that can be monitored uh outside of people's homes or in restaurants or in private places, it's the fact that you have so many people whose entire lives are consumed by politics. And anybody who wants to go into a restaurant to heckle a justice with whom they disagree or anybody who cheers other losers going into a restaurant to heckle somebody a justice that they don't agree with, there's a real problem. People need to get a life. They need to find something.\par \par \uc2 The purpose of our politics is not to sit here and fight and argue. It's -- the purpose of our politics is so that we don't have to do this sort of stuff. We can settle our issues in politics and then practice our lives and go about our lives.\par \par \uc2 And I'm afraid there's so many people that are so caught up in the frenzy of politics that they don't know where to draw the line.\par \par \uc2 JARRETT: Professor, I want to get your reaction to the failure on the part of the Attorney General \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  to take action when after the leak of the draft decision. All of the protesters were allowed to shout and scream obscenities outside the House and demanding that they change their view. That's a violation of federal criminal law, not to mention obstruction of an ongoing federal judicial proceeding.\par \par \uc2 Are you disappointed that \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  did absolutely nothing to enforce the law?\par \par \uc2 DERSHOWITZ: Look, I was a supporter of \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2 's appointment to the Supreme Court. I think he's a terrific person I think he was a great judge. I think he should do more to protect justices. I think you should do more to protect the process.\par \par \uc2 Look I agree with people who devote their lives to trying to undo decisions of the Supreme Court. People feel very strongly about a woman's right to choose just as they feel very strongly about uh the right of a fetus not to be aborted, a vigorous debate is fine, but trying to intimidate justices, the leak, was clearly a violation of practice, tradition and ethics, and I do think the \uc2 FBI\uc2  and the Justice Department have jurisdiction to look into that leak and of course the marshal's office of the Supreme Court, which doesn't have the power to investigate will never get to the bottom of this.\par \par \uc2 The Justice Department should be putting some of its top people on this issue who are technical experts who can get into phone records, who can see if there's probable call, who can get to the law clerks. We have to know who sprung that leak because it violates the integrity of the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 And again this comes from a person who supports a woman's right to have abortion, not at the end of pregnancy --\par \par \uc2 JARRETT: Right.\par \par \uc2 DERSHOWITTZ: -- but in the beginning of pregnancy and I don't support the decision of the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 But I support decency, legality and fairness.\par \par \uc2 JARRETT: All right. Professor \uc2 Alan Dershowitz\uc2 , Charlie Hurt, good to see you both. Have a great weekend. Thanks for being here.\par \par \uc2 Coming up next, breaking news in the Elon Musk Twitter saga. Karol Markowicz and \uc2 Ted Budd\uc2  join us.\par \par \uc2 Plus, the Biden economy -- well, it is gone from bad to worse. Rick Perry reacts when we come back.\par \par \uc2 Stick around.\par \par \uc2 (COMMRECIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 JARRETT: Now, some big breaking news tonight out of Silicon Valley, where \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  is telling \uc2 Twitter\uc2  he is terminating the $44 billion acquisition deal, citing alleged material breaches of the purchase agreement. In an SEC filing, Musk's attorney said he wanted out of the deal because, quote, \uc2 Twitter\uc2  is in material breach of multiple provisions of that agreement and appears to have made false and misleading representations upon which Mr. Musk relied when he entered into the merger agreement.\par \par \uc2 Well, in response, \uc2 Twitter\uc2 's board chairman Brett Taylor said the company will pursue legal action. What a surprise. And that the \uc2 Twitter\uc2  board is committed to closing the transaction on the price in terms agreed upon with Mr. Musk.\par \par \uc2 Here now for reaction, congressman and North Carolina Senate candidate, \uc2 Ted Budd\uc2 , along with "New York Post" columnist Karol Markowicz.\par \par \uc2 Good to see you both.\par \par \uc2 Congressman, since you have the MBA, let me start with you. The only people who are happy tonight are the lawyers, right? As we speak, they're buying Porsches because they know this litigation is going to finance their lavish lifestyle for years to come.\par \par \uc2 But doesn't it all come down to fraud? Were there intentional misrepresentation and false statements that led to a material breach of the contract? What say you?\par \par \uc2 REP. TED BUDD (R-NC): Well, Gregg, this is a billion dollar deal with a billion dollar breakup fee. If there's a not material reason but \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  thinks that there is. He's finding out that \uc2 Twitter\uc2  no surprise to us that are conservatives is just not being transparent about their business practices.\par \par \uc2 This is one of the reasons that I'm running for \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2 . It's one of the reasons that we have to hold big tech accountable. It's one of the reasons. We have to take a good look at DCA section 230 or the Communications Decency Act of Section 230.\par \par \uc2 I've got a plan for this. It's something I've been working on for years and I'd like to do that in the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2  and my plans up on tedbud.com.\par \par \uc2 JARRETT: Yeah. I mean, listen, I totally agree with you, giving liability protection is insane. Maybe it made sense at one point in time, a couple of decades ago, at the outset of the Internet.\par \par \uc2 But, Karol, let me go to you on this. You know, I liken it to buying a car. If the seller knows it's a lemon with a history of problems but claims it's a wonderful car and never had an issue and the buyer is being defrauded here. And anybody who has ever used \uc2 Twitter\uc2  or pays attention to it knows that five percent fake accounts and spam and bots is ludicrous. It's way more than that.\par \par \uc2 What do you think?\par \par \uc2 KAROL MARKOWICZ, NEW YORK POST COLUMNIST: Way more. Yeah, absolutely. I'm an avid \uc2 Twitter\uc2  user. There's no doubt about it, that that number is far higher.\par \par \uc2 You know, a few months ago, some leftist activists tried to buy a pillow company to compete with My Pillow, the conservative, you know, pillow company, and it completely failed, their whole thing fell apart and you know a lot of people on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  got to laugh at them.\par \par \uc2 I think the idea of \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  buying \uc2 Twitter\uc2  is not at all like that. This is a man who knows what he's doing. This is not like he had a shot in the dark, decided to buy some random company and didn't think it through at all.\par \par \uc2 So I think the fact that he's pulling back from this deal right now says that he knows something that can you know potentially lead to maybe a negotiating a better price or something like that that maybe he'll still buy the app. I wouldn't be surprised if that does happen, and but if it doesn't, I wouldn't be surprised if Elon is next to get banned, you know, hot gulag somewhere for conservatives.\par \par \uc2 JARRETT: You know, I checked uh the stock price. It's hovering around 36. The offer was for 54, a share.\par \par \uc2 So I -- what do you think, Congressman Budd? I mean, I think Karol's on to something here. This is a ploy to lower the purchase price?\par \par \uc2 BUDD: Look, I think he's finding real problems in there, about the business practices of \uc2 Twitter\uc2 . They're not being transparent with him. They're obviously not being transparent with their users or with conservatives.\par \par \uc2 I mean, Gregg, how is it that the ayatollah of Iran can be on there but they've banned a former president, when even their oversight board of \uc2 Twitter\uc2  said that the former president shouldn't be permanently banned? I mean, back -- you mentioned the early start of the Internet -- back when we were getting those CD Roms if we can -- if we're