pyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FOXFIV0020220709ei7800001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Left Infects Everything With Their Toxic Politics; Biden Putting SCOTUS Justices' Lives In Jeopardy; Fmr. WH Counsel \uc2 Pat Cipollone\uc2  Testifies To J6 Cmte; Dems' Jan 6 Cmte Getting More And More Desperate; \uc2 FBI\uc2  Director Spells Out The China Threat; U.S. Media Echoes Chinese Propaganda About Abe; Former Secretary Of State \uc2 Mike Pompeo\uc2  Discusses Biden Administration Policy Toward China; Secretary Of State \uc2 Antony Blinken\uc2  Meets With Chinese Foreign Minister; New Yorkers Question Advisability Of President Biden Running For Reelection; Pastor Discusses Ongoing Gun Violence In Chicago\b0\par\par\uc2 Gregg Jarrett, Raymond Arroyo, Ben Domenech, Sean Duffy \par \uc2 7,539 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 July 2022\par \uc2 Fox News: The Ingraham Angle\par \uc2 INGANG\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 GREGG JARRETT, FOX NEWS HOST: Don't forget, you can listen to my podcast, The Brief. You can read my columns at Thegreggjarrett.com. And this fall, I'll be out with a brand new book titled 'Trial of the Century'.\par \par \uc2 Have a great weekend everyone. Sean's back on Monday. Laura is next.\par \par \uc2 RAYMOND ARROYO, FOX NEWS HOST: I'm Raymond Arroyo in for Laura Ingraham. This is a special edition of the 'Ingraham Angle' from New York City.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, Biden's Secretary of State \uc2 Tony Blinken\uc2  is meeting with China's Foreign Minister at the G20 in Indonesia. We will bring you all the details as we get them. But first, the politicization of everything.\par \par \uc2 The political establishment has decided their best bet to stay in power is to keep you in a perpetual state of political outrage. Now, there is virtually no facet of American life immune from politics and partisanship.\par \par \uc2 As a tiny example of what I mean, some friends invited my wife and I to the Essence Festival concert last Saturday night at the Superdome in New Orleans. Should have been a fun time. 50,000 people were there to see Janet Jackson and Patti LaBelle. But just before the headliners took the stage, this well-known warm up act wandered out.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 AL SHARPTON\uc2 , NATIONAL ACTION NETWORK FOUNDER: We are in the middle of critical times. They are trying to take women's right to choose. They're trying to take voting rights. We must vote about this, this year.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Wherever now, may as well have gone to a Trump rally. That room was icy cold. Nobody wanted to see him or hear a political message. So surely at that point, Ms. Jackson hit the stage, right? No.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE OBAMA\uc2 , FORMER FIRST LADY OF THE UNITED STATES: Hello, Essence Festival. This November, we'll be electing the folks who make critical decisions in our communities, decisions about our health care, very personal decisions we make about our bodies.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: With black approval of President Biden below 50 percent, according to a new Quinnipiac poll, the administration is doing all they can to engage African-American voters. So \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  also did an Essence drive- by.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Also, it might be vulnerable that we otherwise thought was settled law, including issues like contraception, including issues like same sex marriage. The statement has been made that the government has a right to come in your home and tell you as a woman and as a family, what you should do with your body.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Now, this is foolishness. Isn't she part of the government? I mean, the VP's party controls all three branches. So who exactly is coming into your home to tell you what to do with your body? Maybe that's why \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  was prowling around the White House this week. They finally found a job he's well trained for, bedroom first responder.\par \par \uc2 But there are real world consequences for political disinformation like that. Stoking anger of people, lying to citizens about the Supreme Court ruling, which merely returned abortion rights to the States to decide. All of this demonization has an impact.\par \par \uc2 The same week that Reverend Al and VP Harris visited New Orleans at a Catholic church a few miles away, this happened. The 'Angle' exclusively obtained this security footage. Masked thugs showed up at 3am, toppled a pro-life statue of the Virgin Mary and then stole it.\par \par \uc2 Since this incident, another statue has been stolen and two others toppled all at the same church. Meanwhile, four churches in Wisconsin's Chippewa County were also vandalized last week. Coincidence? I don't think so.\par \par \uc2 Listen to the rhetoric the president used to describe a co equal branch of government, the Supreme Court today.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT, UNITED STATES OF AMERICA: What we're witnessing wasn't a constitutional judgment. It was an exercise in raw political power. We cannot allow an out of control Supreme Court, working in conjunction with extremist elements of the Republican Party to take away freedoms and our personal autonomy. Consider the challenge accepted, court.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: The dehumanization of the court as extremist or extremist, as he says, and out of control group of justices taking away your rights. This conveys a clear message to fringe elements of American society. Mainly, it's ok to target Justices. It's no wonder the Justices' homes have now become protest magnets.\par \par \uc2 Justice Kavanaugh was threatened with assassination. And just the other night, he was driven from a DC restaurant by protesters. In Ohio, motivated by the Roe ruling, human feces was mailed to 25 Republican members of their statehouse. Thankfully, it was intercepted by the post office.\par \par \uc2 The hyper partisanship, the incitement, and acceptance of violence over any displeasure at all is now an American epidemic. This is footage from a New York City restaurant when patrons were told there was an additional charge for dipping sauce, they rampaged.\par \par \uc2 (VIDEO PLAYING)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: It's pitiable. Any grievance at all is now caused for blocking traffic, attacking law enforcement. Even museums are no longer immune from politics.\par \par \uc2 At British Museums this week, anti-oil (inaudible) glued themselves to the frames of masterpieces. In the U.S., actors have taken to gluing themselves to coffee shop counters as a means of protest. I asked people on the street what they thought of these antics.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: We're also seeing the destruction of sacred art, religious statues as a reaction to the Roe v Wade ruling. Is that appropriate?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: No. I don't think destruction of property is appropriate.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I don't think acts of vandalism, which it is, which are against the law, are the right way to go about things and there should be consequences for your actions as they always are.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Communication of truth, I don't know think so.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: No, I think so. I think there's other ways of showing their feelings and protesting. But actually works of art and national galleries, they shouldn't do that.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: And actors are gluing themselves to great works of art in London here.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I say, let them stay there. Leave them there.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Leave them glued to the frame?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: To the frame. They'll be hungry and then something's got to happen.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: People, no matter their political persuasion, they don't like anarchy. They don't want to see the disruption and destruction of American life in the name of politics. So next time these politicians crash into a cultural event, a sports or entertainment venue and peddle this kind of divisive vitriol, we need to walk out and complain.\par \par \uc2 There must be spaces in American life, free of political combat and division, places where we can enjoy each other as neighbors and friends and citizens. The continued partisan demonization of people will only strain our bonds of unity, and destroy whatever delicate threads of this fragile experiment and liberty together.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is Ben Domenech, he is the editor-at-large at The Spectator; and Sean Duffy, former Wisconsin congressman. Both our Fox News contributors. Gentlemen, thank you for being here.\par \par \uc2 Ben, there are effective ways to protest things you don't like. But is this violence, attacking works of art, the intimidation of good people? Is that a proper tactic?\par \par \uc2 BEN DOMENECH, THE SPECTATOR EDITOR AT LARGE: No, it's not. And look, I think you hit the nail on the head when you said that one of the things that this destroys is the shared space that we want to enjoy as Americans. Where we do not have the ability anymore to sit alongside each other, Republicans and Democrats and independents alike, and cheer on a scene that's in front of us at a sporting event or at a concert, or the like without having this invasion that the cultural left, repeatedly forces in front of us.\par \par \uc2 It's not something that's civilized. It's not something that encourages any kind of actual progress or conversation, or an ability to come together in the ways that we have historically as Americans. And yet it's essential for us, in order to have a representative government where we can have a fight, have a quarrel, go back and forth, determine these policies and move forward together as fellow countrymen without this level of animosity and hatred that we see the left stoking every day.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Yes. The demonization of people without a credible argument, that's got to be a no-go in American politics and in American life.\par \par \uc2 Sean, some great news for election integrity out of Wisconsin today. The Wisconsin Supreme Court barred absentee ballot drop boxes outside election offices. In a 4-3 ruling, the court's majority said, that under state laws and absentee ballot must be returned by mail, or the voter must personally deliver it to the local election office.\par \par \uc2 How do you anticipate this impacting not only Wisconsin's future elections, but maybe national elections as well?\par \par \uc2 SEAN DUFFY (R) FMR WISCONSIN CONGRESSMAN: So just to walk back, Raymond, Wisconsin, conservatives were really concerned because in the last election, we have voter ID in Wisconsin, unless someone is indefinitely confined. Meaning you're shot in a nursing home, you broke both legs and you can't get around and go vote. There's an exception to a rule for voter ID.\par \par \uc2 What liberal counties did was said, Listen, everybody is indefinitely confined in Wisconsin. So no one has to show an ID. Then they set up these drop boxes and it was right for fraud in Wisconsin.\par \par \uc2 So the Supreme Court did a great service to our state and we'll hopefully lead others to say, You know what, this is the right path and we should have voter integrity. People should show an ID, or write down your driver's license number, if you have a ballot that you're going to send in. So we can verify that it's actually you.\par \par \uc2 But what's important to both of your points, if you want to bring the country together, you have to have faith in elections. You can lose elections, not be happy about it. But know that you actually lost. If people don't believe that they actually lost an election, that's a problem. Not just for the loser, it's a problem for the winner as well.\par \par \uc2 You want both sides to go. It was fair. It was - we played by the rules. If you don't feel that way, it's a problem for everybody.\par \par \uc2 And by the way, just one last point. When we talk about elections, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , in his speech on abortion today was talking about vote, vote, vote, vote. This was a campaign speech. He's so concerned that he can't run on the economy, inflation, the border, foreign policy, crime, he has to run on abortion. And most Americans as a top issue, don't look to abortion as the number one issue. It's like a 1 percent issue where the economy is a 40 percent issue.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Yes. Well, Sean, you raise a great point about the integrity of these institutions. And, Ben, here's what a radical leftist group, they're called shutdown DC and they tweeted this today.\par \par \uc2 Now, again, this is a bounty essentially on Justices. "DC service industry workers, the tweet reads. If you see Kavanaugh, Alito, Thomas, Gorsuch, Coney Barrett, or Roberts, DM us with the details, we'll venmo you $50 for confirmed sighting, and $200 if they're still there 30 minutes after your message".\par \par \uc2 I guess it's open season on Justices, Ben. And the Biden Department of Justice doesn't seem to be concerned at all.\par \par \uc2 DOMENECH: They don't seem to be concerned at all. I saw this tweet, and I was absolutely disgusted by it. And I think that any Americans should be disgusted by it, regardless of what you think of the court. Whether the court was inclined to this direction or to the opposite, we shouldn't be treating Supreme Court Justices or really any of our government officials this way.\par \par \uc2 It is declaring open season on them and on their families. And people ask why don't we have better people going into government? Why don't we have more people who have families in balance and careers? Why do we have all these career politicians and people who are compromised by lobbyists and agendas and things like that? Well, guess what, this is what you want. This is what you get when you have a situation like that.\par \par \uc2 Good people get excluded from being in these political roles, because why would they want to put their families in danger? Why would they want to risk this kind of experience for their lives and for the lives of their children.\par \par \uc2 And look, the Biden Department of Justice has done such a poor job in standing up in defense of the security of these Justices and their families to this point. I don't expect it to change. But I do hope that Republicans will start running on and advocating for the impeachment of some of these officials who are making this a reality, including \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 This is something that absolutely is unacceptable and should be rejected as not just opposed to good governments, but as opposed to a representative form of government that we here in America have cherished for so long and has been such a wonderful human achievement for ourselves and for the world.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Sean, before I run out of time. You saw the attacks on those churches and pro life facilities in both of our home states. \uc2 FBI\uc2  director \uc2 Christopher Wray\uc2  was forced to admit this today. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHRISTOPHER WRAY\uc2 , FBI DIRECTOR: Certainly, we have seen an uptick in attacks, threats of different sorts against establishments that are more on the pro life side than we had before.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Thanks for acknowledging what everybody can see. Sean, will the Biden administration start taking this seriously before something deadly happens?\par \par \uc2 DUFFY: No.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: And isn't this an infringement on the free exercise of religion?\par \par \uc2 DUFFY: So, of course, it is, Raymond. And here you have the \uc2 FBI\uc2  in Essence saying, yes, there's an uptick in violence. But yes, we're not going to really do anything about it. Where are the investigative teams? Where's the prosecution's, not just on the attacks on our churches? But where are the prosecutions of the protesters outside Justices' homes, which are violating the law, or the prosecution of individuals that are threatening Justices?\par \par \uc2 The Justice Department, I'm not afraid of this, when they do nothing. It is a (ph) naked awakening, not for violence. Because I think when you talk about extremism, Republicans are extreme, they are domestic terrorists.\par \par \uc2 You get crazies out there who pick up arms and do dangerous things. And especially, when they look at the Department of Justice, who's absolutely absent on these issues in this violence. I think it sends a green light for bad behavior.\par \par \uc2 And again, if they were to stand up and go, we're going to start prosecuting people, you'd see it start to end. But they refuse to do it, because they're using it for political purposes, Raymond. They want the pressure to come on Justices. They want pressure to come on churches. This is how they get things done in America, which is not part of our history, to Ben's point.\par \par \uc2 We have great institutions. We have great debate. We don't resort to violence. We don't call each other domestic terrorists. And when you do that things, devolve. And that's what's happening now in America.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: It's an important conversation. Thank you both for your insights. Ben, Sean, have a wonderful weekend.\par \par \uc2 Former White House Counsel, \uc2 Pat Cipollone\uc2 , today met with the January 6 committee behind closed doors for more than eight hours. Here's how he was greeted at the closed door session. Look at this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Sir, have you corroborated or disputed any of Cassidy Hutchinson's testimony?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Were you able to answer the committee's questions or did you use certain privileges or\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Did you discuss pardons, sir, and members of Congress who asked for them?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Excuse us. Please put it away.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: This is called intimidating the witness with media. The Press spent the majority of the day covering Cipollone's testimony with no details whatsoever. But it's amazing to me, they have a behind closed door testimony. And then they line the holes with all of the media to put pressure on him as he comes in.\par \par \uc2 Cheney and company subpoenaed Cipollone after testimony from former Meadows aide Cassidy Hutchinson about January 6 and the days leading up to it. But it seems like those eight hours with Cipollone might not have been as fruitful and endeavor, as the committee had hoped.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WOLF BLITZER, CNN HOST: I know you can't get into specifics. But can you say if you learned any significant new information, new information from him?\par \par \uc2 REP. ZOE LOFGREN (D-CA): I think a few things, yes. And when we put this all together, I mean, obviously Tuesday is going to be an important day for the committee. And I think the country connecting various dots. Yes, I think this was worthwhile.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: My goodness. In the hangtime on that answer, we could have had another hearing.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is \uc2 Matt Whitaker\uc2 , former acting Attorney General under President Trump. Matt January 6, that committee member, Lofgren, she doesn't sound too competent about what she got from \uc2 Pat Cipollone\uc2  today.\par \par \uc2 MATT WHITAKER\uc2 , FORMER ACTING ATTORNEY GENERAL: She doesn't. And it's good to be with you tonight. Raymond. You know what, you probably would expect this committee really wanted somebody to corroborate their previous witness. They needed his - \uc2 Pat Cipollone\uc2 's testimony to say, did you say these things, did you hear the same things that she heard?\par \par \uc2 And in this case, it sounds like, really all they wanted, Raymond, was to get \uc2 Pat Cipollone\uc2  on video for their made for TV show trial in order to get somebody that was close to the President. And I'm sure they asked every single question every way you can ask it.\par \par \uc2 And \uc2 Pat Cipollone\uc2 , as I know him is a very smart, sophisticated guy. I'm sure he answered their questions, but at the same time protected things like executive privilege, attorney client privilege, and alike.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: That's a great point. Matt, the committee's trying to establish that Trump wanted to join protesters at the Capitol on January 6, which probably proves nothing. But as you mentioned, Pat's attorneys negotiated with this committee for months over concerns about executive privilege. Why is that so important here?\par \par \uc2 WHITAKER: Well, remember, we have a constitutional system that has a separation of power frame. And then so the Congress should not be able to sniff into and look into the advice that the President is getting from his most trusted advisors, especially his lawyer as the White House counsel.\par \par \uc2 So this is a very important area that needs to be protected. And in fact, remember, Pat was able to negotiate through his lawyers, that he was not actually subpoenaed. He appeared voluntarily, that means the threat of being held in contempt, like four other witnesses have, been held in contempt, was taken off the table.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Here's the most pressing question. \uc2 The New York Times\uc2 , once \uc2 Pat Cipollone\uc2  dancer.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KATIE BENNER, NYT JUSTICE DEPARTMENT REPORTER: One of the big questions for Pat is, what did Trump know? And what did he say to you about whether or not what was happening was right or wrong? Again, he can say that as the White House Counsel, there's a lot that you can't discuss in terms of his own conversations with the President. But any behavior that he believed was criminal behavior, potentially criminal behavior, or some kind of wrongdoing. That is really not covered by those privileges.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Matt, is there anything to suggest that Trump's conversations with Cipollone constituted criminal behavior?\par \par \uc2 WHITAKER: No, I have not heard anything, nor have I seen any testimony from the J6 committee. What the \uc2 New York Times\uc2  should actually be wanting to know is, how did these security failures happen? How did these intelligence gaps happen? And how can we fix it? More like the 9/11 committee, instead of this completely partisan, one sided show trial.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: \uc2 Matt Whitaker\uc2 , thank you as always for being with us.\par \par \uc2 Well, before you go, I should play this for you. This is ranking committee member Jamie Raskin. Here's what he had to say, leading up to Tuesday's hearing, taking self righteousness really to another level. Very quickly.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. JAMIE RASKIN (D-MD): I told as I can't wait to go going back to disagreeing with her about everything. But right now, I mean we are in a constitutional emergency, and we are all constitutional first responders.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Constitutional first responders. Have you ever heard that terminology ever used, Matt? No.\par \par \uc2 WHITAKER: And in fact, people like Jamie Raskin and Zoe Lofgren and others on this committee, and \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  are not going to save the Republican. Fortunately, they are what's destroying the Republic through these partisan gamesmanship instead of addressing the important issues that are facing the American people right now.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: \uc2 Matt Whitaker\uc2 , thank you for being with us.\par \par \uc2 Up next, breaking details from \uc2 Tony Blinken\uc2 's meeting with his Chinese counterpart, and how our defense department is quickly eroding our military readiness. \uc2 Mike Pompeo\uc2 , Gordon Chang are here next. Stay there.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: As every branch of the military is reportedly in a recruiting crisis, its top brass is still doing everything it can to deter people from wanting to join. Case in point.\par \par \uc2 Back in November, Defense Secretary \uc2 Lloyd Austin\uc2  made it mandatory for service members to get the COVID vaccine. Many have been fighting it and today we learned that roughly 40,000 National Guardsmen and 22,000 reservists who have refused to the vaccine will be barred from their duties, effectively cutting their pay and benefits.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now, former Secretary of State \uc2 Mike Pompeo\uc2 . He's also a Fox News contributor. Mr. Secretary, what will getting rid of more than 60,000 service members do to our military readiness?\par \par \uc2 MIKE POMPEO\uc2 , FORMER SECRETARY OF STATE: Raymond, thanks for having me on tonight.\par \par \uc2 You mentioned the recruiting problems we're already facing, when you make our military work when you don't do the right things by focusing on its wartime mission. And then you add to that cutting - an amount to between 10 and 15 percent of all National Guardsmen. This is really dangerous.\par \par \uc2 I assure you that \uc2 Xi Jinping\uc2  and \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  aren't worried about the vaccines. They're worried about making their militaries more capable. We should be focused on the same thing. There's a real risk to readiness, a real risk to making sure that we have units that are capable of deploying should something be required of them.\par \par \uc2 This is silly. It's not based on science. It makes no sense. And I hope this President will change his ways. And if there's a president after him, he should reinstate every one of these folks that they denied the capacity to go. Do what they're trying to do to serve voluntarily on behalf of the United States of America.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Particularly, what we're learning now about herd immunity and that so many people who are already having - haven't been vaccinated, have gotten COVID, and therefore they are immune at this point.\par \par \uc2 I want to turn to the assassination of former Japanese Prime Minister \uc2 Shinzo Abe\uc2 . The media reaction was frankly disgusting, Mr. Secretary. NPR tweeted then deleted that "Abe was a divisive arch conservative". And \uc2 CBS\uc2  This Morning, remembered him this way.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: A polarizing figure, he was a right-wing nationalist and conservative, and a fierce supporter of Japan's military.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Mr. Secretary, who exactly was a polarizing? Two, he was the country's longest serving Prime Minister who won by huge margins.\par \par \uc2 POMPEO: Raymond, this is sick. It's sick to see these left-wing media folks go after a man who was just assassinated. He was assassinated, by the way, while he was campaigning for his party, for his ideas for the things that actually have benefited the Japanese people for an awfully long time.\par \par \uc2 There is a reason he was the longest serving Prime Minister. The people of Japan loved him and revered him. He'd become a friend of mine too. He was a great partner for the United States of America. And so to hear NPR and \uc2 CBS\uc2  come after him for being some right-wing crazy. Well, he delivered really good outcomes for the Japanese people. And he was a great partner to the United States.\par \par \uc2 That doesn't surprise me that someone who did that, who worked alongside us in the Trump administration, being attacked by the left. This is the same old tired story. I regret that they would do that at a time where this man was assassinated in his own country.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Terrible. \uc2 FBI\uc2  director \uc2 Christopher Wray\uc2  yesterday talked about one of the greatest threats facing our country, Mr. Secretary. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WRAY: We consistently see that it's the Chinese government that poses the biggest, long-term threat to our economic and national security. And by our, I mean both of our nations along with our allies in Europe, and elsewhere.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Now, I seem to remember President Biden highlighting far greater threats.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: You know, what the Joint Chiefs told us the greatest threat facing America was, global warming.\par \par \uc2 According to the intelligence community, terrorism for white supremacy is the most lethal threat to the homeland today. Not ISIS, not Al Qaeda, white supremacies.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Mr. Secretary, who's right, Wray or Biden?\par \par \uc2 POMPEO: I'm with Wray. Wray nailed it. Wray has got it exactly right. The Chinese Communist Party has been abusing the American people. They stole millions of our jobs. They've taken away intellectual property. They've stolen our secrets and our universities.\par \par \uc2 Director Wray has this right. The Chinese Communist Party, not climate change, not white supremacism, actually present real risk to every American all across this country.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: And they're probably incensed, the media as well as parts of the Biden administration, about the Abe -- with Abe missing, there is a regional balance here that we are losing. Talk to us about what that means and what we may have lost in Abe being assassinated here.\par \par \uc2 POMPEO: Prime minister Abe was really the first and most significant Asian leader to confront the Chinese Communist Party. It's hard when you are in their neck of the woods there in the Pacific. But he was serious about it. He partnered with Australia, with the South Koreans, with our other allies in the region, and of course with us to deliver a central understanding about the Chinese Communist Party, the very one that Director Wray spoke of there. He was fearless in supporting that, and we have lost a great leader who was willing to really change the trajectory of Japan's willingness to defend itself and countries in the region. There's a real risk today, whether that's Taiwan or Japan itself or the south-central parts of the Pacific. Abe was a fierce defender. He's no longer there, and this presents increased risk not only to them but to the United States of America as well.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Mr. Secretary, thank you for your insight tonight.\par \par \uc2 Secretary \uc2 Tony Blinken\uc2  is right now meeting with the Chinese foreign minister Wang Yi. Moments ago the foreign minister spoke. Listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WANG YI, CHINESE FOREIGN MINISTER (through translator): We do need to work together to ensure that this relationship will continue to move forward along the right track. It is important to stay committed to the principles put forward by President \uc2 Xi Jinping\uc2 . We need to respect peaceful consistency and cooperation, because that serves the interests of the two countries, two peoples. It is also the shared aspiration of the international community.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Joining me, Gordon Chang, Gatestone Institute senior fellow, author of "The Great U.S. China Tech War." Gordon, your reaction to what Wang said there. And do you think Blinken was as tough as he needed to be with his Chinese counterpart today?\par \par \uc2 GORDON CHANG, GATESTONE INSTITUTE SENIOR FELLOW: Blinken has never been as tough as he has needed to be with the Chinese. The reality is this week China has had a frigate in Japanese territorial waters in the East China Sea around the islands of Senkaku. They've also had their planes buzzing through Japanese airspace. In the South China Sea this week they've had two of those large Coast Guard vessels blocking the resupply of a Philippine outpost in Second Thomas Shoal. That's the reality. It's military force that the Chinese are using. And when Wang Yi talks about cooperation, he is lying through his teeth.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Talk to me for a moment about these tariffs. We've heard a lot about Biden considering lifting these tariffs. First of all, why were those Trump tariffs put into place? What were they designed to do? And what do we lose by lifting them?\par \par \uc2 CHANG: Those tariffs under section 301 of the trade act of 1974, they were there as a remedy for the theft of U.S. intellectual property, and also President Trump wanted to move supply chains onshore. When Biden even just talks about lifting those tariffs, it says to U.S. companies, stay in China. I'm not going to let you have to worry, and we are going to do business as usual, which means business in China, not business in the U.S.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Unbelievable. No matter the trade record, the human trafficking record, the crushing of religious and human rights, it's scandalous that they would even contemplate lifting those tariffs.\par \par \uc2 Back in February, Biden's DOJ ended the Trump era China initiative, this is a group within the \uc2 FBI\uc2 , after civil rights groups said it racially profiled Asian Americans. But the whole purpose was to prosecute Chinese espionage, Gordon, particularly in our universities. Director Wray was asked about ending it and if it was a mistake. Listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHRISTOPHER WRAY\uc2 , FBI DIRECTOR: We are not going to engage in profiling by ethnicity or national origin, and we haven't. And we are going to work jointly with universities. In many cases that's one of the most effective ways to prevent stealing of research, which is in the hands of our universities. And when law enforcement action is the appropriate tool to be used, we are going to use that tool.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Was it a mistake to end that initiative? It doesn't seem as he is willing to really admit it.\par \par \uc2 CHANG: Oh, absolutely. What China does is it uses Chinese Americans and Chinese nationals to commit espionage against the U.S. The 2017 national intelligence law in China requires every Chinese national to spy if Beijing makes a demand. We need to understand that.\par \par \uc2 What Biden did by ending the China initiative was he is caving into Beijing, which says, look, you target us. This is just terrible. It isn't terrible because we know what Beijing is doing. I am a Chinese American. I understand what they are doing. This is really wrong on the part of the Biden administration.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: They should listen to you and stop the endless dialogue, which as you pointed out, is a stall tactic they've used for way too long. Gordon Chang, thank you for being here.\par \par \uc2 Biden had a tough time with his teleprompter today, and I hit the streets to find out how much confidence people really have in him. Friday Follies with a special celebrity guest is up next. Stay there.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: It's Friday, and that means it's time for Friday Follies. And for that we have a special guest, the legendary Joe Piscopo, host of "The Joe Piscopo Show" on set with me. Joe, what an honor, what a pleasure. Thank you for being here.\par \par \uc2 Now, even members of his own family are now questioning the other Joe's, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's, ability to lead, or his own party, rather. There seemed to be a lot of confusion this week, Joe. And not only from the president. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , (D) VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: My husband and I were staying in Delaware so we could be near the vice president-elect, I mean, president-elect of the time.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , (D) PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I want to thank Vice President Harris on the second gentleman for allowing us to join them. No, for joining us.\par \par \uc2 I'm just stating a basic fundamental notion. The fastest way to restore woe -- Roe, is to pass a national law codifying Roe.\par \par \uc2 Ten years old, and she was forced to have to travel out of the state to Indiana to seek to terminate the presidency and maybe save her life.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: "Terminate the presidency" and restoring woe to the land.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Your thoughts as you watch this?\par \par \uc2 JOE PISCOPO, COMEDIAN: I know you are a Jerry Lewis fan.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: I am.\par \par \uc2 PISCOPO: And Sam and Sandra, Jerry's widow from Jerry Lewis films, very excited that we're together because we are going to do the Jerry Lewis movie. You remember, Jerry, I think it was "The Errand Boy," every time he couldn't pronounce a word --\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 PISCOPO: You're a nice man --\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 PISCOPO: But Jerry wasn't president. Every time I watch the president, he's like --\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: And Jerry brought, joy, not woe.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 PISCOPO: And his I'm not so sure about. And it's not just these confused moments. Fifty-four percent of Americans, Joe, in an ABC-"Washington Post" poll thought Biden lacked the mental sharpness to serve as president. Here's what people on the streets of New York told me.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , some members of his party don't want him to run next time. They say he's not mentally or physically competent. How do you feel?\par \par \uc2 Do you think he is up to the task?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I think so.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It's tough growing old.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: He is hanging in there. But he's probably not going to make it for another session.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Running I think is a different story. We will see there when we get there.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , (D) PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Why the hell when I take a test? Come on, man.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: He could do a little bit better, absolutely.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: So you have confidence in his leadership?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I have confidence in none of our elected leaders. We have seen him physically stumble, unfortunately, create some embarrassing times for him. We need leadership right now, Ray. And I think the whole world, whatever side you're on, would agree with that.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I don't know. I guess it really don't matter at this point.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I wouldn't. If I were him, I wouldn't run.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I know a lot of 80-year-olds that are very competent. I don't think he's competent, but I know that where he's pushing us is not where I want to go.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Joe, people forget Trump is three years younger than \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . So it's not a matter of age. It's really about the fitness at that age.\par \par \uc2 PISCOPO: It's true. And \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  looks better now. He's the only president that didn't age.\par \par \uc2 But I'm older. You are a young man. Please. I'm thinking to myself, we're all going to get there. But we're all not going to be president of the United States.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Hopefully a spouse would stop you.\par \par \uc2 PISCOPO: And that's the thing, too. Did you see the marking? As you look, they had stage markings like they are doing a play.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: A little tape, yes. You see the tape. Look at the bottom of your screen. You see the tape down there, where everybody has to stand. That's every White House event.\par \par \uc2 PISCOPO: I just want ice cream -- this poor guy.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: OK, I've got to get to this before we run out of time. The Biden administration has recruited everyone from BTS to Olivia Rodrigo, as you know, to help advance their agenda. Now in the midst of a gas crisis, while she blocks gas and oil exploration, Biden's energy secretary, \uc2 Jennifer Granholm\uc2 , is turning to a Hollywood heavyweight to recruit her clean energy corps.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ROBERT DOWNEY JR., ACTOR: You might have seen me at one of my many day jobs, billionaire superhero, world's greatest detective, alpaca wrangler. But now, I have got this sweet new office over the Department of Energy. Coffee, Madam Secretary?\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER GRANHOLM\uc2 , ENERGY SECRETARY: Thanks, Robert. Early start today?\par \par \uc2 DOWNEY JR.: Yes, pretty early. That $62 billion isn't going to spend itself.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Robert Down Jr. referencing the $62 billion Congress appropriated for this clean energy corps, Joe Piscopo. Your reaction?\par \par \uc2 PISCOPO: How about the XL pipeline? How about working for the pipeline? How about the supply chain, how about that? I love Robert Downey Jr. How cool is that guy? I was destroyed in "Endgame" when he died, Ironman died, and now he is with this gal, this gal that said I will wave a magic wand for energy. Robert, we love you. Don't do that to us, please.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: This is truly Avengers Endgame. Iron Man couldn't lift off the ground with this group powering his backback.\par \par \uc2 PISCOPO: Don't do it. Don't do it.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Joe Piscopo, what an honor.\par \par \uc2 PISCOPO: Lady!\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Lady.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: \uc2 Lori Lightfoot\uc2 , speaking of ladies, promised the summer of joy in Chicago, but it's anything but. One southside pastor is enraged at how leaders are threatening -- or treating, rather, the bloodshed in his city. He joins us next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: The week began with the horrific mass shooting in Highland Park, Illinois, that resulted in seven deaths, a tragedy that made national headlines, as it should have. But just miles away, residents are seeing this type of bloodshed daily -- 323 people have been murdered in Chicago so far this year. And over the July 4th weekend, eight people were killed, 68 hurt, in 51 separate shootings across the city. The divergent coverage angered southside Chicago Pastor T.J. Grooms, who joins me. Pastor, you say there's no incentive for leaders to take action in Chicago to combat crime. Why not?\par \par \uc2 T.J. GROOMS, CHICAGO PASTOR: Because whenever you are in an area where economically things are disastrous and you are in an area where there isn't as much political engagement as it should be, then it's going to cause people who have power within their grasp not to pay those areas attention. And so you would think that people who have the power would go to the areas where crime and violence happens often so that it can actually turn into a place like Highland where it doesn't happen as often.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: They're not going anywhere near it. In your Chicago, your mayor, \uc2 Lori Lightfoot\uc2 , if off to London and Paris to promote Chicago as a tourist destination while your communities continue to bleed and suffer.\par \par \uc2 Let's turn for a moment to Philadelphia where police are looking for seven teenagers seen in this video beating a 73-year-old man with traffic cones and smiling as they do it. James Lambert died the next day. Pastor, why is it that we keep seeing young kids committing these heinous acts, not only in your city, and my city of New Orleans as well?\par \par \uc2 GROOMS: Because we have to recognize and understand that these kids that are committing violent acts, and even the kids that I deal with and see on a regular basis, this is all they are exposed to. And it's very difficult to expect children and young people to overcome what they are exposed to without giving them something else to be exposed to.\par \par \uc2 And so we have to get to a place to where we start showing our kids and our teenagers and people who are committing acts of violence, or who has the potential to, we have to start giving them another way. And that's why I love organizations like Project Hood because they do that effectively.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: How do we break this? And it really is the cycle of murder, of loss, and you are losing generation particularly of young men in the inner- city. How do we break that cycle, Pastor?\par \par \uc2 GROOMS: There's a couple ways to break that cycle. One, we have to make sure that we have resources for people to have employment. The young men that I deal with on a regular basis that are susceptible to these type of acts, they tell me all the time, Pastor, if I had money, if I had a job then I wouldn't be doing some of the things that I'm doing. And so that is a very important, important tool.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: And mentorship is so important for these young men, the type of mentorship you're providing, Pastor. So I thank you for that. We'll keep our eye on this.\par \par \uc2 When we return, a tribute to a great actor with a story you probably have not heard. Come back for this. I'll make this short and sweet. There's a clue. Stay there.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TONY SIRICO, ACTOR: Tony has given years of his life to that company, and he expects to be duly compensated. Johnny Sack sure as -- ain't going perform.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Who?\par \par \uc2 SIRICO: Mr. Chernelli's (ph) waste management consultant. And since Johnny Sack ain't going to step up, guess what. It's coming out of your --\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: I'm very sad to report that actor, Tony Sirico, who played Paulie "Walnuts" from on "The Sopranos" died this morning. What most people may not know is that Tony was 55 years old and sleeping on a cot in his mother's living room in Brooklyn when he was cast as Pauli "Walnuts" and became a household name. Stick to it, gang. Condolences to the Sirico family. My pal, Father Robert Sirico, Tony's brother, the family is asking for donations to St. Jude's wounded warrior project and the Acton Institute. Tony, rest in peace.\par \par \uc2 I'm Raymond Arroyo. Have a great weekend.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document INGANG0020220709ei7800001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Corona Origins: We Still Have No Concrete Answers; Intra-Agency Abortion Task Force; \uc2 Shinzo Abe\uc2  Was A Well-Liked Prime Minister; AG Garland Failing Duty To Protect Supreme Court Justices; Elon And \uc2 Twitter\uc2  Saga: Musk Says Deal Is Off; The Terminal List\b0\par\par\uc2 Tucker Carlson, Kevin Corke \par \uc2 7,636 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 July 2022\par \uc2 Fox News Channel: Tucker Carlson Tonight\par \uc2 TCARL\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. 