  all saying, we need people to collaborate face to face.\par \par \uc2 I think this would put employers at a serious disadvantage. Your thoughts?\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MARTIN, FOX BUSINESS CONTRIBUTOR: It does, Charles.\par \par \uc2 And you're right about the productivity numbers. They are ugly. And what a coincidence. That kind of goes in line with a lot more folks working from home over that time period, huh? So, just data, just statistics.\par \par \uc2 But I think Jackie pointed out some interesting things. I was shocked to hear that they actually do work in Europe.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 MARTIN: That's a new one for me. But let's say it does spill over to the United States.\par \par \uc2 We hire a lot of folks every year, Charles, and I will tell you, man, a lot of folks that we have the requirement for the job, whether it's the trading and the operations compliance at our firm, we require those folks to be in the office.\par \par \uc2 And a lot of folks -- I mean, look at the JOLTS survey, by the way, the Job Openings and Labor Turnover Survey -- try to say that three times fast -- 11.3 three million jobs are out there for folks to take. But similar to what we have been trying to hiring -- trying to hire this year, Charles, these folks don't want to come into the office.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Right.\par \par \uc2 MARTIN: Or they want to work two days from home, then three, then four. And it keeps accelerating, and get full benefits and full salaries.\par \par \uc2 So, as \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  pointed out, we want to require, at least on our firm, folks to be in the office for a minimum amount of hours or give up some of the benefits or the salary if they want to stay home.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Mercedes, the legalities of this, could the nation even force -- would this even be legal?\par \par \uc2 MERCEDES COLWIN, FOX NEWS LEGAL ANALYST: Well, it could be?\par \par \uc2 I mean, we saw that we have regulations that a lot of corporations were concerned about during the COVID. And, obviously, we're still in the COVID phase.\par \par \uc2 But when you look at some of the regulation that's come down, yes, they can make those types of laws. But, frankly, it's not necessary. I mean, we saw a lot of overreach by some of the regulation that came down during the last couple of years.\par \par \uc2 So there are mandates that can come forward and regulate this type of hiring and firing and whatnot. But, at the end of the day, if the employees want to work from home, guess what? They can choose other places to work. Employers have to make the decision whether that talented candidate who wants to work from home can actually work in their offices without this government oversight and regulation.\par \par \uc2 I think it's kind of ridiculous. It's self-regulating. The corporations can do it on their own without needing government to really have a say in it.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Right. Right.\par \par \uc2 I mean, that right now, that's the way it is. But there's a big push in this country, to sort of follow what Scott was saying, the sort of European model and things like that. And if they did try this, though, I mean, would there be a good challenge?\par \par \uc2 What I worry about, for instance, the \uc2 American Psychiatric Association\uc2 ...\par \par \uc2 COLWIN: Sure.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: ... did a big survey earlier this year. And over half the folks complained about some sort of mental issues associated with having worked from home, loneliness, isolation, those kinds of things.\par \par \uc2 COLWIN: Sure.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: I can see people trying to sue their employer over something -- a decision that they made.\par \par \uc2 COLWIN: And they can. I mean, that's a great point, because, Charles, they can sue under the Americans With Disabilities Act and say, I need to work from home, and for these following reasons.\par \par \uc2 They can sue under the local anti-discrimination laws. There are so many laws already