at agency's findings onto the horseback investigation.\par \par \uc2 And find out what drove this brazen attack at a New York restaurant. You will not believe it.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Forget about road rage. How about restaurant wage -- rage?\par \par \uc2 Find out what took these customers over the edge and why over-the-top incidents like this continue to pop up. It's mind-boggling.\par \par \uc2 And we're back in 60 seconds.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: The chaotic moment as a gunman is taken down after he shot and killed former Japanese Prime Minister \uc2 Shinzo Abe\uc2 . The 41-year-old man in custody, with questions swirling about how and why this happened.\par \par \uc2 To Alex Hogan in London with the latest details -- Alex.\par \par \uc2 ALEX HOGAN, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hi, Charles.\par \par \uc2 The former Prime Minister of Japan \uc2 Shinzo Abe\uc2  has been assassinated. He was the longest-serving prime minister in Japan. And now world leaders around the globe are speaking out, voicing their solidarity with Japan. U.S. President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  said that he was deeply saddened to hear the news and outraged before stopping at the Japanese Embassy in Washington today.\par \par \uc2 The president also highlighted the strong relationship between the U.S. and Japan, which was bolstered under Abe's administration. Former German Chancellor \uc2 Angela Merkel\uc2  called the attack a cowardly and vile assassination. Ukrainian President \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskyy\uc2  tweeted his deepest condolences to the family.\par \par \uc2 Here in London, Queen Elizabeth II saying in part: "I have fond memories of meeting Mr. Abe and his wife during their visit to the United Kingdom in 2016. His love for Japan and desire to forge ever closer bonds with the United Kingdom were clear."\par \par \uc2 Gunfire broke out earlier today in the western part of Japan during Abe's speech at a parliamentary election campaign