ek. Nothing good is going to come out of that.\par \par \uc2 And by the way, goofball \uc2 John Kerry\uc2 , he still thinks China is going to be a key ally in fighting climate change. This is hilarious. Of course, John is just moving full steam ahead with coal and its own refining capacity, building that up huge.\par \par \uc2 So things are so bad. America is so weakened. A State Department official, actually is quoted today as saying that America should essentially abandon Asia to China, that "It's dangerous for Washington to deter a Chinese attack on Taiwan. We need to be investing more in economic engagement, encountering climate change, which will require cooperation with China".\par \par \uc2 Yes. Who cares about Taiwan. That whole Taiwan thing's overrated. Let's just keep pouring billions of dollars into Ukraine forever. Look, we're going to win this debate, we're already winning this debate.\par \par \uc2 And in 2024, the American people will want to know that above all else, the American president this time, as he was in 2016, and his whole cabinet, will put our interests first. Biden and his team clearly don't and won't. And that's the 'Angle'.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now, I'm delighted he's with us, Nigel Farage, former \uc2 European Parliament\uc2  member and founder of the Brexit Party in Britain. Nigel, it's so great to see you tonight. The globalists are - they're on the run? What in your mind is driving this?\par \par \uc2 NIGEL FARAGE, FORMER BREXIT PARTY LEADER: Well, they're losing. I mean, it's quite simple, isn't it, really? The one that you just said that really highlights it to me, is the only benefits of globalization are for the fraction of a percent of giant multinational businesses. Those who work in global is government. And for everybody else, it's been a loss. I just think people have woken up to it. They're seeing it.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Nigel, so I need to get your reaction to some hilarious commentary on \uc2 Boris Johnson\uc2 's