ple I've ever seen assume any type of power in an executive branch ever, and that's saying something.\par \par \uc2 HANSON: Yes. Translated, what \uc2 Pete Buttigieg\uc2  just said, Laura, is that if you're in California, you're paying $6.60 a gallon, it wasn't that bad. You were happy when you were paying $5 when the war started, which was two dollars over three. And he's talking to a public that can't drive very far at almost $7 a gallon gas, and they don't want to fly anymore because they get stranded and there's no flights out of these connections.\par \par \uc2 So he's the transportation secretary. The whole airline industry is in shambles. The transportation industry, trucking, cars, commuters is in shambles. And he's telling you don't worry, gas if sky-high, but only half of the increase was our fault. The other half was \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 . He's just out of touch.\par \par \uc2 And he's, again, symbolic, iconic of this aristocracy, arrogant Democratic elite. And they don't understand what's coming in November. And it's going to be a middle-class push back against their arrogance. And you know what it is? It's also their selfishness. They don't care about the people at the border communities. They have no concern whatsoever for people who can't afford meat. They don't know what it's like to be a long-haul trucker or farmer and pay $7 a gallon for gas.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Victor, we've got a roll.\par \par \uc2 HANSON: So they're very callous people --\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: But I think it's worse than that. I think they want to punish Americans. That's what they want. Victor, great see you.\par \par \uc2 And ahead, the Senate races in Georgia and Pennsylvania are going to be vital in determining which party controls the chamber after this fall. And the two Democratic candidates now find themselves in some really hot water. A full report in moments.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: In tonight's "Retaking America" segment, we bring you a closer look at the Democratic candidates for Senate in Georgia and Pennsylvania. Now, they have largely escaped media scrutiny, but that's about to change. FOX's White House correspondent Kevin Corke is here with new details. Kevin?\par \par \uc2 KEVIN CORKE, FOX NEWS WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Evening, Laura. Great to be with you.\par \par \uc2 There are a couple of races where GOP Senate candidates, frankly, are facing a bit of an uphill battle, but they may be grabbing a bit of popcorn tonight as their opponents deal with some very uncomfortable news.\par \par \uc2 First, we take you to Georgia. That's where it would appear Senator \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  may have illegally used campaign funds to pay for personal legal expenses related to a lawsuit when he was a pastor at Ebenezer Baptist Church. Now, a lot of caveats in that sentence. I'll make it easy for you. If he did used campaign cash to pay for personal legal expenses, yes, it would be a pretty clear violation of federal campaign finance law. Marc Elias, the infamous Democratic Party operative who also represents Warnock's campaign, is calling the suit frivolous in a statement to "Politico."\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, over in the keystone state of Pennsylvania, there are reports that the lieutenant governor there, \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2 , took a taxpayer-funded security detail on a family vacation to the Jersey Shore, all while -- wait for it -- publicly advocating for strict coronavirus lockdowns. Sound familiar? State police spending over $3,500 for lodging and food during that trip to Ocean City. The campaign says, listen, Allegheny County where Fetterman resides was not in a lockdown at the time of the trip.\par \par \uc2 By the way, I should pass this along, too. There's a lot of grumbling in the commonwealth about his health status. As you probably know, Laura, he hasn't been seen or spoken publicly since he suffered a stroke back before the May 17th primary election. His wife keeps saying he's going to be back out on the trail soon. In fact, I did notice that there is an event scheduled for the 21st of July, so we'll see if he shows. In the meantime, obviously, everyone wishes him a very speedy and full recovery. Laura?\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Kevin, thanks for getting us up to speed on all this, we appreciate it. Good to see you tonight.\par \par \uc2 And now on to another battleground, Arizona, where residents there are facing devastating consequences of Biden's policies. The border, of course, that's obvious there, but also inflation. Get this, in May, the Phoenix metro area had the highest inflation rate among big cities in America. So the primaries are less than four weeks away. Republicans hope to unseat Democrat Mark Kelly in the Senate and keep the governorship in GOP hands.\par \par \uc2 Karrin Taylor Robson is a Republican candidate for governor, in the top two, and she joins us now. Karrin, great to see you tonight. You're narrowly trailing Kari Lake in this primary right now. The race has really tightened. How do you convince Republican voters that you are the candidate better poised to keep this governorship in Republican hands?\par \par \uc2 KARRIN TAYLOR ROBESON, (R) CANDIDATE, GOVERNOR OF ARIZONA: Laura, thank you for having me on tonight. And in fact, the most recent polls have me leading, and it is remarkable the amount of momentum that we have in this campaign. And I believe Arizona voters are waking up to the stakes that are at hand in this election so that we can keep the Arizona governor's office in Republican hands. We have to. We know what we're up against --\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Sorry.\par \par \uc2 ROBESON: They know that I am best positioned to defeat \uc2 Katie Hobbs\uc2  in the November election.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Now, you have Governor Ducey came out and endorsed to you, which was -- he's very popular in the state of Arizona. I know some conservatives don't think he's conservative enough, I get it. But that was a big, big deal for you to get that endorsement. How did you convince him? Or did you convince him, he just decided to pick you?\par \par \uc2 ROBESON: Governor Ducey has joined a chorus of leading conservative voices all over this country from Bill Bennett, Steve Moore, Newt Gingrich yesterday endorsed me, Governor \uc2 Mike Huckabee\uc2 . Both the previous Republican governors of Arizona, Governor \uc2 Jan Brewer\uc2 , who is with me tonight, and Governor Fife Symington, both conservative Republicans, were joined today by Governor \uc2 Doug Ducey\uc2 . They all have known me for many, many years. They understand who I am, my conservative credentials. They understand what I have done in business. They know that I'm ready to lead day one. I'm able to tackle the issues that matter to Arizona voters.\par \par \uc2 And as I crisscross this state, I can tell you that voters are hurting. The disastrous policies of \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , we have people that are having to decide whether or not they put gas in their tank or food on their table. We have people having to decide if they can even make it to work. It is so bad, not only economically, but as you mentioned, the border. We've had a tremendous increase in border crossings. In Yuma alone, it has been over 300 percent year over year increase in border crossings. And Arizona is on the front lines of what is really a 50 state border war. And we are disproportionately impacted, and the citizens of Arizona are feeling it.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: That is awful. It's hideous. Is it true that Kari Lake, was she an Obama supporter? I keep thinking that can't be true because President Trump endorsed her. I don't know her. She could be the greatest person ever. I have no idea. She says a lot of very kind of conservative things now, but did she support Obama?\par \par \uc2 ROBESON: She did. She donated to Obama, she knocked doors for Obama.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: What?\par \par \uc2 ROBESON: Her political life and career has been quite interesting from Democrat to independent to Democrat. And quite frankly, she found God, guns, and the GOP about a day-and-a-half before she decided to run for governor. And the voters are of Arizona, they are learning that. The more they learn about her, they are coming to the conclusion that I am the candidate to vote for. That's why we are surging in the polls, and that's why we will keep this momentum going through Election Day.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Karrin, we are going to be watching this really closely. I still can't believe that. That's like the wildest thing ever. But Karrin, we're going to be watching this. Thanks so much for joining us. Say high to the crowd there.\par \par \uc2 Up next, what are they trying to teach your children? We're going to take you inside the \uc2 National Education Association\uc2 's latest assembly. And I'm going to expose the disturbing proposals they are attempting to implement nationwide. So stay there.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BECKY PRINGLE\uc2 , NATIONAL EDUCATION ASSOCIATION PRESIDENT: We will say gay, we will say trans. We will fight tirelessly for the right to choose. We will never stop.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: That was the NEA head \uc2 Becky Pringle\uc2  quickly summing up what she and her group often prioritize over actually teaching what kids need to know. Unless you think that we're exaggerating here on THE ANGLE, we have an inside look at the proposals being considered at their latest national assembly. It just wrapped up, by the way. Among the items adopted as vital for student success, the "NEA will publicly stand in defense of abortion and encourage members to participate in rallies, demonstrations, lobbying, and political campaigns" -- reading, writing, and abortion. The NEA "will support and participate in mass actions for LGBTQ rights and pride." And finally, the "NEA shall create resources and information related to historical reasons that have exacerbated the racial and gender wealth gaps in the U.S.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is someone who says he had his \uc2 Twitter\uc2  account censored for merely pointing all of this out, Dr. Terry Stoops, director of the Center for Effective Education at the \uc2 John Locke Foundation\uc2 . Terry, is this the way these meetings usually end up with these whack-pot declarations?\par \par \uc2 DR. TERRY STOOPS, JOHN LOCKE FOUNDATION: Absolutely. But the difference this year is the NEA decided that they weren't going to publish their agenda and make it available to the public. And so that's where I stepped in and I said I'm going to find this agenda, and I'm going to make sure I let everyone know what the NEA is talking about at the representative assembly.\par \par \uc2 And so I obtained the document and I started publishing their new business items on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  so that the world would know exactly what was being discussed at this meeting. And of course, once \uc2 Twitter\uc2  realized I was revealing this information and individuals started retweeting and commenting on what I had revealed, they shut me down.\par \par \uc2 So fortunately, a lot of my colleagues and friends have been continuing to talk about this insane agenda that the \uc2 National Education Association\uc2  is displaying at their annual meeting. If you took a list of parents most critical concerns about their child's education and compared it to what they were discussing at the NEA meeting, those lists would look nothing alike, because parents aren't talking about things like environmental racism or replacing the words mother and father with birthing parent and non-birthing parent. They are worried about learning laws. They're worried about social emotional skills that their children lack as the schools were locked down and the children were masked for an extended period.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: This event is more like a political rally for the far left. It's kind of a combination of human rights campaign, BLM, earth justice league, whatever it is. But what it's not about is education. And this is why, I think, Terry, a lot of people are saying it's time to defund government education, or at least defund it by giving vouchers to parents so they can say no, we're not doing this anymore. And I think that just has to happen. We have to stop funding this madness.\par \par \uc2 STOOPS: I think if \uc2 Becky Pringle\uc2  is really pro-choice, then the natural extension of that is she needs to be pro-school choice. But of course, they don't want the monopoly to end because they don't want the money to end. I joked with a colleague that the NEA representative assembly is sort of the convention version of a Dixie Chicks concert with better music and more men. But the real concern here is that the Dixie Chicks aren't standing in front of a classroom for 180 days of a school year. And these teachers that were at this NEA conference will be. And so they are taking what they believe and which they talked about at the convention floor in Chicago to the nation's classrooms. And more than anything, we need to parents to be vigilant, and we need ways to get parents options if they find that their teacher is perpetuating indoctrination.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Terry, thank you for bringing this to light and covering it, and we'll stay on it.\par \par \uc2 When we come back, I have a very hopeful message for any of you out there if you think you can't get over a phobia, because earlier today, I got over a phobia, and I have the video to prove it. So stay there.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: You don't know this about me, but I have a terrible fear of heights. Today I made a little progress.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CROWD: Two, one, go!\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It's a baby --\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: The little kid was laughing at me because he had already jumped off. He was like five-years-old. That's the jumping rock at Phelps Lake here in Wyoming. It's about 25 feet high.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document INGANG0020220708ei7700001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Highland Park Shooting Suspect Contemplated Second Attack in Wisconsin; Police Credit Tipster for Preventing Mass Shooting in Richmond, Virginia; President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  Talks Economy as Polling Slump Continues; Uvalde Police Response Under Scrutiny Following Report; Alleged Drug Smugglers Skipped Court Hearing; Probation Department Classified Suspects As Low Risk; Alleged Voicemail of President Biden Shows He Possibly Knew of Son Hunter Biden's Overseas Business Dealings; New Polling Shows Large Majority of Americans Feel Country on Wrong Track; \uc2 FBI\uc2  Director Warns of Chinese Government's Attempts at Stealing Technology from Other Nations; U.K. Prime Minister \uc2 Boris Johnson\uc2  Under Possible Threat of Being Voted Out by Own Party; Shooter in Highland Park, Illinois, Purchased Gun with Sponsorship from His Father; Democratic Supporters Criticize Biden Administration for Handling of Overturning of Roe Versus Wade.\b0\par\par\uc2 Bret Baier, Garrett Tenney, Mike Emanuel, Peter Doocy, Casey  Stegall, William La Jeunesse, Mark Meredith, David Spunt, Greg Palkot \par \uc2 7,671 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 July 2022\par \uc2 Fox News: Special Report with Brett Baier\par \uc2 FNSPBB\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 JEANINE PIRRO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL LEGAL ANALYST: Sandra, can you do it for us?\par \par \uc2 SANDRA SMITH, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: A man dreamed the lotto numbers. He played them and he won.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Yes. All right.\par \par \uc2 S. SMITH: So, don't doubt your dreams people.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: That's right. Do it. And that's it for us, SPECIAL REPORT is up next. Hi Bret, are you there?\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: "THE FIVE" turns 11. I'm just getting my submission ready. Thanks, judge. Happy anniversary.\par \par \uc2 Good evening. Welcome to Washington. I'm Bret Baier.\par \par \uc2 Breaking tonight, haunting revelations from the Illinois parade shooter. The suspect confessing to police that he contemplated shooting up an event in Madison, Wisconsin, while fleeing to the city following his assault, which left seven people dead and dozens others -- dozens of others injured in Highland Park on Independence Day.\par \par \uc2 He's being held without bond, charged with seven counts of first degree murder. Additional charges are coming, we're told from the authorities. Police are still searching for a motive.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, there are new questions about how the suspect was allowed to legally buy several guns despite previous threats of violence, resulting in the confiscation of more than a dozen of his knives.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent Garrett Tenney is outside the Lake County courthouse in Waukegan, Illinois. Good evening, Garrett.\par \par \uc2 GARRETT TENNEY, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Hi, Bret, good evening to you. Today, we learned the alleged gunman waived his Miranda rights when he was taken into custody and shared details with investigators of how he carried out the attack.\par \par \uc2 Prosecutors say the 21-year-old suspect emptied two full clips of ammo on the crowd at Monday's Fourth of July parade, pausing to reload after each time until he fired more than 70 shots in total.\par \par \uc2 We've also learned that a few hours later, the suspect almost carried out a second attack, nearly 150 miles away in Madison, Wisconsin at another Fourth of July celebration.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DEPUTY CHRISTOPHER COVELLI, LAKE COUNTY ILLINOIS, SHERIFF'S OFFICE: It appears when he drove to Madison, he was driving around. However, he did see a celebration that was occurring in Madison, and he seriously contemplated using the firearm he had in his vehicle to commit another shooting.\par \par \uc2 He can't speak to why he decided to come back from Madison. There are indications that he didn't put enough planning forward to commit another attack.\par \par \uc2 TENNEY (voice over): The suspect also allegedly dumped his cell phone while he was near Madison, which the \uc2 FBI\uc2  has recovered and is now processing for more information.\par \par \uc2 We're also learning the gunman passed four separate background checks to buy firearms in 2020 and 2021, despite police flagging him as a clear and present danger in 2019. That came after a family member reported the suspect had threatened to kill his entire family.\par \par \uc2 Illinois State police say no action was taken because none of the family members wanted to move forward with a complaint which could have triggered the state's red flag laws and prevented the suspect from buying a gun.\par \par \uc2 Despite that incident, three months later, the suspect's father sponsored him when he applied for and obtained a firearm owners I.D. card which is needed to buy a gun.\par \par \uc2 His parents have not spoken publicly but in a statement called the shooting a terrible tragedy and said our hearts, thoughts and prayers go out to everybody.\par \par \uc2 Officials have now identified all seven victims whose lives were stolen Monday. Their ages range from 35 to 88. Among them are Kevin and Irina McCarthy, a husband and wife who came to the parade to celebrate with their two-year-old son Aiden. Aiden was found wandering the street alone after his parents were killed. And his grandfather says the young boy only survived because his dad shielded him with his body. A \uc2 GoFundMe\uc2  to support Aiden has raised more than $2.5 million.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TENNEY (on camera): The 21-year-old suspect is facing seven counts of first degree murder. And today, prosecutors again said dozens of additional charges are expected to be filed in the weeks ahead, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Garrett Tenney live outside the courthouse. Garrett, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Also breaking tonight, police in Richmond, Virginia say there may have been another mass shooting on the Fourth of July if it weren't for a hero citizen who saved numerous lives.\par \par \uc2 Chief Washington Correspondent Mike Emanuel joins us with this story and the details. Good evening, Mike.\par \par \uc2 MIKE EMANUEL, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CHIEF CONGRESSIONAL CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Bret, good evening to you. Authority say a concern tipster heard a conversation which led to calling police. The mayor of Richmond is praising the officers for taking that tip seriously, jumping into action.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LEVAR STONEY (D), MAYOR OF RICHMOND, VIRGINIA: Our officers quietly investigated and collaborated with their law enforcement partners and the community to start what could have been a terrible day for the city of Richmond.\par \par \uc2 GERALD SMITH, POLICE CHIEF, RICHMOND, VIRGINIA: We are very grateful to the \uc2 FBI\uc2  for their help and their cooperation with helping us with this investigation.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: The police chief says that tip came in last Friday, July 1st and officers quickly opened an investigation.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Those men and women of the second precinct began an investigation along with Homeland Security and our special investigations team. Went over to the thousand block of Columbia Avenue here in the city of Richmond to begin their investigation.\par \par \uc2 Once inside that residence, they saw evidence in plain view that collaborated the hero witnesses' statement that there was a concern.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: The suspects are identified as Julio Alvarado-Dubon, 52 years old arrested July 1st, and Rolman A. Balacarcel, 38 years old arrested yesterday. Both are charged with being non U.S. citizens in possession of a firearm.\par \par \uc2 Police say the two live together as roommates, two assault rifles, one handgun and 223 rounds of ammunition were taken from their apartment according to investigators.\par \par \uc2 The local chief says he's not sure what country they're from or why they wanted to attack Richmond's Independence Day celebration.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: We know their intent. Their intent was to conduct a mass shooting at our Fourth of July celebration. We do not know what their motive was as to why.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL (on camera): Richmond police say they are working with the ATF to determine the history of those weapons, when and how they were obtained by the two suspects. Both remain behind bars with no bail being offered. The first suspect is due back in court on August 1st, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Mike Emanuel live in Richmond. Mike, thank you. A source tells Fox News that former White House counsel to President Trump \uc2 Pat Cipollone\uc2  is scheduled to testify Friday before the House committee investigating the January 6th attack on the U.S. Capitol.\par \par \uc2 We're told it will be a private transcribed interview or deposition. Cipollone was in the West Wing on the day of the riot, as well as for key meetings in the weeks after the election.\par \par \uc2 Attorneys representing Senator \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  says he intends to challenge a subpoena compelling him to testify from before a special grand jury in Fulton County, Georgia investigating former President Trump and his allies' actions after the 2020 election.\par \par \uc2 Graham was one of a handful of Trump confidants and lawyers subpoenaed to appear on July 12th. The South Carolina senator's attorneys slammed the probe as politically motivated.\par \par \uc2 The campaign to recall Los Angeles County District Attorney George Gascon has collected 717,000 signatures. Campaign spokesman confirming to our own Bill Melugin. They're submitting that petition to the registrar today. The effort needed 567,000 verified signatures by today's deadline.\par \par \uc2 Speaking of California, California Governor \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2  is fending off criticism of hypocrisy tonight for vacationing in Montana.\par \par \uc2 One of the 22 states that California bans state travel too, due to its gay rights policies. Newsom's spokesman defended the governor saying the travel ban only applies to spending state funds and that Governor Newsom did not use those funds for the trip.\par \par \uc2 President Biden is on his way back to the White House tonight after promoting his economic agenda in battleground Ohio. But new polling shows a wide majority of Americans do not like where this country is heading. It's a harsh reality for President Biden as we march toward the November midterm elections.\par \par \uc2 White House Correspondent Peter Doocy shows us tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: We got a long way to go because of inflation because of the -- I call it the Putin tax increase.\par \par \uc2 PETER DOOCY, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): But the president told union workers his economic plan is helping.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: It's good for everyone because when the middle class does well, the poor have a ladder up and the wealthy still do very well.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: Everyone not on the same page about that. 88 percent polled by Monmouth say the country is heading in the wrong direction, that number up from 79 percent last month. And 57 percent say the federal government is hurting recovery, just eight percent say the feds are helping.\par \par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal has analyzed a bright spot though in a story called red states are winning the post-pandemic economy. They calculate that between February 2020 and now, red states have added 341,000 jobs over that time. While blue states were still short 1.3 million jobs as of May.\par \par \uc2 KARL ROVE, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR: Red states tend to be low tax states and pro-business states but also because they tend to be states that came out of the pandemic earlier with fewer restraints, for example, on kids going back to classes and actual classrooms.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: As the Biden administration struggles, other Democrats are hoping to break through, including California Governor \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2  talking abortion access.\par \par \uc2 GOV. GAVIN NEWSOM (D-CA): I'm a little less sorry than I am pissed. A little less sorry than I am resolved and angry to do more.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: And Illinois Governor \uc2 J. B. Pritzker\uc2  talking gun control.\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 J. B. PRITZKER\uc2  (D-IL): If you're angry today. I'm here to tell you be angry. I'm furious.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: Both governors forceful. Aboard Air Force One, the Press Secretary was asked why that kind of enthusiasm is only coming from governors.\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , DEPUTY PRESS SECRETARY: The president has been also very loud and also very focused on those two issues.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: And so, the pressure on the president is also coming from members of his own party which has him remembering a pillar of his primary campaign.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: The third reason I ran was to unify the country, to unify. That's been a harder part of it right now. No, I'm serious, because we've become so divided.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (on camera): You saw it there. It looks like some Democrats may be taking first steps towards 2024. While other Democrats are just trying to protect themselves in 2022. Like Congressman \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2 , he's running for Senate in Ohio where the president was appearing today. Congress here in D.C. out this week. But \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2  did not appear alongside the president in his home state, citing a scheduling conflict, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I got two questions for you. What did the White House say about that? That Ryan didn't show?\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: The White House says that they are in contact with Ryan about a variety of different issues.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: OK. Secondly, you've got the White House Communications Director \uc2 Kate Bedingfield\uc2  announcing she's leaving. Why is she leaving?\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: The official word from people here is that she wants to go spend more time with her husband and kids. But this departure comes just a few weeks after the president said in an interview that he's done a lot of big things and just hasn't been able to communicate them well.\par \par \uc2 So, a few weeks later, they're going to have a new communications director. It is also notable, though, that when \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2  left a few weeks back, the president immediately put out a statement praising her.\par \par \uc2 Now, we have a statement about Bedingfield leaving from the White House. There's praise from the Chief of Staff \uc2 Ron Klain\uc2 , there's praise from the advisor and Anita Dunn. Nothing from the president, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yet. We'll see. All right, Peter, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Stocks were up today, the Dow gained 70. The S&P 500 rose 14, the NASDAQ added 40.\par \par \uc2 Up next, two men caught with enough fentanyl to potentially kill a small town are now missing. We'll explain and bring you there.\par \par \uc2 First, here's what some of our Fox affiliates around the country are covering tonight. Fox 50 in Raleigh, where North Carolina based \uc2 Labcorp\uc2  becomes the first company in the United States to offer a test for monkey pox. A vaccine for the virus already exists but cases are rising with more than 450 confirmed monkey pox cases in the U.S.\par \par \uc2 Fox 2 in Detroit where guitarist Carlos Santana is recovering from heat exhaustion and dehydration after collapsing on stage during a Michigan concert last night. The 74-year-old musician was taken to a nearby medical clinic for observation and is said to be doing well.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at Miami from WSVN, our affiliate there. One of the big stories there tonight, a physician from Florida donates an ambulance to Ukraine after raising more than $37,000 going door to door asking for donations.\par \par \uc2 The Ukrainian American says she plans to deliver additional medical supplies to the people of Western Ukraine in August.\par \par \uc2 That's tonight's live look outside the Beltway from SPECIAL REPORT. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: "BREAKING TONIGHT", a new report examining the shooting at Robb Elementary School in Uvalde on May 24th. And the law enforcement response to that indicates officers missed multiple chances to slow the gunman and possibly save lives more than we thought before.\par \par \uc2 Senior correspondent Casey Stegall has new details tonight from Dallas. Good evening, Casey.\par \par \uc2 CASEY STEGALL, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Bret, good evening. This new report was released by ALERRT, or the Advanced Law Enforcement Rapid Response Training at \uc2 Texas State University\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 STEGALL (voice-over): The 26-page document says that cops missed three chances to slow the shooter. Number one, an officer with a rifle had a clear shot of the suspect before he even went inside the school, but asked for permission to shoot and waited.\par \par \uc2 Number two, involved a cop driving through the school parking lot at a high rate of speed, which apparently diverted a different officers attention away from the suspect as he slipped inside Robb Elementary School.\par \par \uc2 That number three, an exterior door was unlocked, which gave the shooter instant access to the building.\par \par \uc2 The report also questions why officers did not breach classrooms, windows, or walls instead of the door which they said was locked.\par \par \uc2 Today's findings come as the \uc2 U.S. Department of Justice\uc2  investigation and \uc2 Texas Rangers\uc2  inquiry are both still underway. All, while the frustrations have been mounting in that community.\par \par \uc2 Uvalde's mayor, Don McLaughlin, this week suggesting there could be a cover up underway at the state level.\par \par \uc2 McLaughlin claims that he has not been briefed by Texas DPS since the shooting and believes he has been left out of the loop intentionally. He also alleges there is more to the story, which has yet to be released, telling angry constituents at last week's city council meeting that state law enforcement officials are simply not being transparent.\par \par \uc2 DON MCLAUGHLIN, MAYOR OF UVALDE, TEXAS: We're not trying to hide anything from you. We're not trying to do that. We don't have anything. DPS has tied our hands.\par \par \uc2 STEGALL: The mayor has been joined by Texas State Senator Roland Gutierrez and sent a letter to Governor Greg Abbott, calling for the local district attorney to be removed as the head of victim services and replace with the State Department of Emergency Management. Saying that families are not receiving the financial help they apparently need.\par \par \uc2 STEGALL (on camera): Today, Texas DPS told us this is very much an active investigation, but fell short of responding to the mayor's allegations of a cover up. Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Casey Stegall in Dallas. Casey, thanks.\par \par \uc2 The Justice Department says more than 1,500 dangerous criminals were arrested as part of a nationwide sweep dubbed Operation North Star. The 30- day program led by the \uc2 U.S. Marshals\uc2  targeted violent criminals, sex offenders, and self-identified gang members in some of America's biggest cities.\par \par \uc2 Despite increased criticism from local officials in various big cities around the country about rising crime, Attorney General \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  says this operation is an example of the Justice Department keeping communities safe.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, we continue our series on "THE POISONING OF AMERICA", as we examine how what critics call a relaxed criminal justice system helped put a pair of suspects possessing enough fentanyl to potentially kill a small city back on the streets. And that has critics sounding the alarm.\par \par \uc2 National correspondent William La Jeunesse takes a look from Los Angeles.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MIKE BOUDREAUX, SHERIFF, TULARE COUNTY, CALIFORNIA: California system of justice is failing us all.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAM LA JEUNESSE, FOX NEWS CHANNEL NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT (voice-over): And critics say it did.\par \par \uc2 10 days ago, after police stopped this truck carrying 150,000 fentanyl pills and four pounds of cocaine, a judge released the suspects. Jose Zendejas and Benito Madrigal, with zero conditions. Now, they're gone.\par \par \uc2 TIM WARD, DISTRICT ATTORNEY, TULARE COUNTY, CALIFORNIA: We cannot have these decisions being made without at least some semblance of factual information.\par \par \uc2 LA JEUNESSE: Tulare County District Attorney Tim Ward blames the mess on the probation department, because their pretrial risk assessment made no mention of the amount of drugs, their value, or the hidden compartment concealing the cocaine.\par \par \uc2 In fact, probation never even spoke to either suspect, according to the sheriff, but used a computer program as his standard practice to make this public safety assessment, which Judge Mikki Verissimo used to release the pair on their own recognizance, despite facing up to 14 years in prison.\par \par \uc2 WARD: That's certainly the negligent aspect here. How can you make decisions based simply upon ink on a paper and a crime tag charge?\par \par \uc2 LA JEUNESSE: But in a statement, the probation department claims it "does not have the authority to order the release of inmates," only judges do. While Superior Court blames the D.A. for failing to immediately file charges or detainer to keep them in jail.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LA JEUNESSE (voice-over): So, the bottom line is police catch two alleged smugglers with enough fentanyl to potentially kill a small town. One judge lets them go. Now, another judge, same county wants them back, issuing a nationwide warrant, and preemptively setting bail at $2 million each. So, this doesn't happen again. Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: William La Jeunesse in Los Angeles. William, Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Up next, a newly uncovered voicemail appears to strongly contradict President Biden's claim, you never talked business with his son Hunter.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: White House officials say President Biden called the wife of \uc2 WNBA\uc2  player Brittney Griner, who is detained in Russia pledging that he is working to win her release as soon as possible. Griner is currently on trial in Russia, accused of possessing vape cartridges containing cannabis oil. She could face up to 10 years in prison there.\par \par \uc2 Republicans are challenging the Biden administration tonight to provide more details on \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 's overseas business dealings. And a newly uncovered voicemail from \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 's alleged laptop -- it is a laptop by -- of then-candidate Biden, raising even more serious questions that voicemail is.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent Mark Meredith shows us tonight from the White House.\par \par \uc2 MARK MEREDITH, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (voice-over): As the federal probe into \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 's taxes drags on, House Republicans are demanding answers directly from the Biden administration about what it knows of the president's son's foreign business dealings.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 REP. JAMES COMER (R-KY): It's so frustrating that this administration continues to block congressional oversight --\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH: Today, Kentucky Congressman James Comer sent Treasury Secretary \uc2 Janet Yellen\uc2  this letter demanding her department provide copies of any suspicious financial activity reports tied to the Bidens.\par \par \uc2 Comer writes, "The American people deserve to know whether the president's connections to his son's business deals occurred at the expense of the United States' interests."\par \par \uc2 A Treasury official tells Fox, it provides those reports to Congress in, "a manner that enables robust oversight and that is consistent with how other sensitive law enforcement information is often produced."\par \par \uc2 The White House continues to face questions about Hunter's affairs, and whether the president ever discussed business with his son.\par \par \uc2 In 2019, then-candidate Biden told Fox it's never happened.\par \par \uc2 PETER DOOCY, FOX NEWS CHANNEL WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: How many times have you ever spoken to your son about his overseas business dealings?\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I've never spoken my son about his overseas business dealings.\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH: But a voicemail reportedly recorded in 2018 and obtained by "The Daily Mail" appears to contradict those claims. The voicemail, recovered from a laptop once believed to be owned by Hunter, appears to show the president reaching out over a "\uc2 New York Times\uc2 " report which focused on Hunter's ties to a Chinese oil executive.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I thought the article release online, it's going to be printed tomorrow in the "Times," was good. I think you're clear.\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH: This week the White House refused to comment on the situation.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Are you disputing that's the president's voice on the voicemail?\par \par \uc2 JEAN-PIERRE: I'm not going to talk about alleged materials on the laptop.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH: If Republicans become the majority in Congress after this fall's election, it's certainly possible that lawmakers could launch sweeping investigations into \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 , and those Republicans would have power to compel testimony and documents through subpoena power. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Mark Meredith in the White House briefing room. Mark, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in FOX News senior political analyst Brit Hume. Good evening, Brit.\par \par \uc2 BRIT HUME, FOX NEWS SENIOR POLITICAL ANALYST: Hi, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Listening to that story, where do you think this story is and where it goes?\par \par \uc2 HUME: It reminds me of nothing so much as the period during Watergate when the Nixon administration was engaged in stiffing questions about its conduct. It was referred to at the time in a phrase that was associated with Watergate as "stonewalling." That's what the White House is doing on the Hunter Biden laptop. That story, the Watergate story was cracked not by the White House press corps at all but by a couple of reporters working on the city desk at "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " eventually followed up by other reporters, most of them outside the White House press corps.\par \par \uc2 It did not do the reputation of the White House press corps any good that they had seemed to be so gentle during those times. So now this White House press corps, which was certainly anything but gentle during the Trump years, seems by and large to have receded into its former ways and is really not pursuing this story, not insisting on answers from the White House. I don't think that its reputation will age well because of that.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: This story obviously adds to a lot of problems for this administration, for this president. I want to play a soundbite from David Axelrod and then put up a poll. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DAVID AXELROD, CNN SENIOR POLITICAL COMMENTATOR: There is this sense that things are kind of out-of-control, and he is not in command. No one president could control inflation, but it is a -- it's a gale force wind right now. It's affecting politics.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And the comments are getting more and more pointed, Brit. And if you look at this new poll out from Monmouth, "Would you say things in the country going the right direction or have they gotten off on the wrong track?" Right direction is 10 percent, wrong track is 88 percent. And there are not a lot of people who don't know. What do you think about this?\par \par \uc2 HUME: Well, a big piece of that, of course, is inflation, Bret, because everybody experiences it, whether they can afford the higher prices or not. And it's terribly damaging. It's about as corrosive an issue as any political party or president can face.\par \par \uc2 But that's probably a record. And it gives you a sense of how much trouble the Democratic Party could well -- could be in in this upcoming midterm. And there are not very many things that can save the party. It's July now, we're end of July, the election is just a few months away. There is no sign that inflation is going to abate, that gas price will come down in any meaningful way between now and then.\par \par \uc2 There is one thing, however, that's worth keeping in mind. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  may well be