s we were in 2018.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: The comments in London came at the same time two U.S. senators in Washington in a rare show of bipartisan implored the \uc2 Federal Trade Commission\uc2  to formally investigate TikTok, the social media app with parent company ties to China. Senators \uc2 Marco Rubio\uc2  and Mark Warner wrote to the FTC chair, "We write in response to public reports that individuals in the People's Republic of China have been accessing data on U.S. users in contravention of several public representations, including sworn testimony in October 2021."\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 SPUNT (on camera): Bret, in a statement to FOX News, a spokesperson from TikTok said the company has not and will not share U.S. user data with the Chinese government. Meanwhile, in Washington, a new bulletin from the National Counterintelligence and Security Center about China and Chinese threats, Director Wray will head to Scotland Yard tomorrow to meet with more officials. And Bret, I will sit down with him on Friday for an interview one-on-one to talk more about these threats. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We will see that exclusively here on FOX. David Spunt traveling with the \uc2 FBI\uc2  director. David, thanks.\par \par \uc2 British Prime Minister \uc2 Boris Johnson\uc2  is fighting for his political life tonight, staving off calls for him to step down after two top ministers and a slew of junior officials said today they could no longer serve under his scandal-tarred leadership. Senior foreign affairs correspondent Greg Palkot also in London tonight with the latest. Good evening, Greg.\par \par \uc2 GREG PALKOT, FOX NEWS SENIOR FOREIGN AFFAIRS CORRESPONDENT: Bret, embattled conservative British Prime Minister Johnson is trying to hang on to and fighting for his political life just against all odds. You are right, two key members of his cabinet and dozens of other officials have called it quits, citing the lack of integrity by the man some call BoJo. It is triggered by a new controversy linked to a recent Johnson political appointment tainted by sexual misconduct allegations.\par \par \uc2 Johnson has already been hit hard for months about questions concerning his lack of proper conduct during the COVID lockdown, though he did apologize. He has been in power for just under three years. Here is a bit of today's stormy back and forth in the House of Commons.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: At some point we have to conclude that enough is enough. I believe that point is now.\par \par \uc2 BORIS JOHNSON\uc2 , U.K. PRIME MINISTER: The job of a prime minister in difficult circumstances when he has been handed a colossal mandate is to keep going, and that's what I'm going to do.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PALKOT: The U.K. is an essential ally of the U.S. Johnson has been a confidante to former president Trump and current President Biden. Most importantly, lately he has been an aggressive backer of Ukraine along with the U.S. against Russia. But personal and domestic issues seem to be winning out. The U.K. is struggling with the same problem the U.S. has -- high inflation, slow growth, shortages of goods and workers. Contributing to this, according to some analysts, the British exit from the E.U., or Brexit, which Johnson championed.\par \par \uc2 Late into this day and into this evening, more government officials were trying to get Johnson to budge. If Johnson doesn't step down, his party could still vote him out. They almost did last month.\par \par \uc2 And now, according to reports, Johnson says he is not going anywhere. He is even firing another key official late tonight. He says he'll fight on maybe to his end, politically at least. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We'll follow it all. Greg Palkot in London. Greg, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the panel on gun politics, the Biden economy, and the president's plummeting polls.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PAUL CALLAN, FORMER NEW YORK HOMICIDE PROSECUTOR: This is a shocking failure of the Illinois red flag law. It's also a shocking failure, I think, of police officials in Highland Park, Illinois. In most of affluent towns in America, the police chief would know if a kid with a tattoo on his face who has attempted to commit suicide and subsequently threatened to kill every member of his family, the police chief would know if that kid had a permit to get a weapon.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: They didn't stop him before in Highland Park. And if you look at a map of the states with red flag laws, there are a number of them and there are a number of officials who question their effectiveness, obviously in the wake of this latest shooting, and also how they work.\par \par \uc2 "Politico" writes, "Be absolutely furious. Dems want more from Biden after Highland Park. Perhaps no issue better encapsulates the Biden administration's viewpoint and tactics than how it has chosen to tackle the epidemic of gun violence. The president makes no secret of his bolder legislative ambitions, but those efforts have been stymied by Republicans in Congress and he has had to balance dueling demands, righteous indignation of fellow Democrats, and the plodding, incremental progress that comes with bipartisan compromise."\par \par \uc2 With that, let's bring in our panel, former Education Secretary Bill Bennett, Leslie Marshall, Democratic strategist, and FOX News media analyst, host of FOX's MEDIA BUZZ, Howard Kurtz. Bill, let me start with you. In the wake of this Highland Park, what you just heard about red flag laws, where are you in this and how they work?\par \par \uc2 BILL BENNETT, FORMER EDUCATION SECRETARY: Well, I think you could strengthen red flag laws. Certainly, people need to pay attention to them. It's hard when parents aren't doing their job for any red flag to make up for it. Also, the police need to pay more attention. Schools need to pay more attention. Social media, \uc2 Facebook\uc2  will take you off if have a view of COVID that's the same at Dr. \uc2 Marty Makary\uc2 , but you can stay on if you are putting out some of this garbage.\par \par \uc2 But frankly, I have got to tell you, I have been thinking and studying about all of these cases. I'm not sure we are going keep enough. Yes, I think you need police. You need parents for sure. You need schools. You need to clean up social media, you need all that. But, you know, you may need an exorcist, too. Before your audience shakes its head on that, if you look at these boys, these men, these young men, they have deeply spiritual problems, deeply. Few go into the labyrinthine caves of the Internet way down, and I don't recommend it. It's ugly stuff. And these guys dwell there. They live there. They drench themselves in it. And it's as ugly and evil as it gets.\par \par \uc2 Where are the ministers? Where are the rabbis? The priests? Look, I don't want to suggest something that would seem farcical to a lot of your audience, but I don't think that the domain of religion ought to be involved here. It's a deeply spiritual void, I think, that these young men have in their hearts and their souls, and I think it needs to be addressed. And I don't think we get at it, frankly, with these externalities, as important as some of them may be, and they may be. We need to do it all, but we need to do more.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes, Leslie, that is the message also from Democrats, but they are saying the president is not exuding enough emotion about this. He just had a bipartisan win. A lot of critics say it was around the edges on the issue of gun violence and didn't go far enough. But it was an incremental bipartisan win, which with this separation of the majority, was seen as a victory inside. But yet he's getting hammered to the left and the right.\par \par \uc2 LESLIE MARSHALL, DEMOCRATIC STRATEGIST: We saw this after the bipartisan infrastructure bill as well. With the Safer Communities Act, interestingly enough, not just bipartisan, but if this had been passed a while ago, this may have, people are saying, prevented what we saw in Highland Park because of the background check of somebody under 21 years of age, and they would have seen what happened in 2019.\par \par \uc2 Bill, up until exorcism, I actually agreed with what you were saying. It seems there are a lot of failures. We always want to point fingers and want to blame. But sadly we need to do that to look at how do we prevent this from happening again in our communities, whether it's at parades, at schools, at movie theaters, the lists goes on.\par \par \uc2 One of the areas is why on earth, and we have seen at least three in recent mass shootings, parents purchasing or cosigning for permits, as was the case with the father of this individual, for people that are troubled. When police come to your house and take away your weapons, why are you helping them to obtain another weapon? Social media, we need -- in airports people say, or on a plane, if you see something, say something. Well, we need to do that on the Internet. If you see something, say something. We are hearing a lot of people have made, whether violent threats, very disturbing postings, that needs to be brought to someone's attention.\par \par \uc2 And then, of course, the police, red flag laws can only do so much if the families don't help. But this legislation that was just passed should have been passed sooner, and I think would have been helpful.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And that community is going through that now about where the blame is, and it's obviously with this suspect, but there are other things that can change.\par \par \uc2 Howie, I want to turn. "The Atlantic" has a piece, "Is Biden a man out of time? Many Democrats share a sense that on all these issues, abortion included, Biden and his team have been following, not leading. The growing course among the president's internal critics is that even if Biden was the right man for beating Trump, he has become the wrong man for combating Trumpism."\par \par \uc2 And I want to turn to the Monmouth poll. We showed the first one. I want to turn to the second one. What is the biggest concern facing your family right now? Inflation we talk about all the time, if you put up that poll, 33 percent. Gas prices, 15 percent. The economy, nine percent. Abortion, reproductive rights down to five percent. Guns, gun ownership down to three percent. All of these polls collectively, Howie, paint a grim picture for this president and this administration.\par \par \uc2 HOWARD KURTZ, FOX NEWS HOST: Yes, those wrong track numbers are brutal. And the president, and Democrats are openly saying that they want a fighter, that they want to see Biden take more aggressive action. He hasn't delivered, they say, on the Roe decision, the Roe reversal, of course, which everybody knew was coming for weeks. And they want him to speak out more forcefully on the July 4th shooting.\par \par \uc2 And I do want to come back for just a second, Bret, because it's profoundly depressing that this now confessed mass murderer was able to hijack the patriotic July 4th celebrations. And look, I don't want to hear any more mumbo jumbo about rules and so forth, or even red flag laws. It's common sense. The police go to his house, summoned by the family who say they threatened to kill everyone. They could have arrested him on the spot. They could have notified other agencies. They could have put him on a no gun purchase list. Instead, they take 16 knives and a sword, and that's it. So I think there is a two-year-old who would have parents alive today if it had not been for the fact this guy, this raged-filled young man, showed up and was able to do what he did.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes, agreed. And there was a successful stoppage in Richmond that we reported on earlier in the show.\par \par \uc2 Last thing, Bill, and this 