ar \uc2 MCENANY: That she was very scared by, and she actually screamed. Then, she said, hi, Greg, and that she wanted to take it home.\par \par \uc2 So, thank you for letting me teach my daughter about dismembered body parts.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: My pleasure.\par \par \uc2 JEANINE PIRRO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST (on camera): Adorable.\par \par \uc2 JESSE WATTERS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST (on camera): That was the girl you played cornhole with, right?\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: All right. That's it for us. SPECIAL REPORT is up next with Mike.\par \par \uc2 MIKE EMANUEL, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CHIEF WASHINGTON CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Thank you, Jesse. See you in an hour.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: See you in an hour.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Good evening. Welcome to Washington. I'm Mike Emanuel, in for Bret Baier.\par \par \uc2 "BREAKING TONIGHT", a seventh person has died from injuries sustained in the Highland Park mass shooting.\par \par \uc2 Police say the gunman who opened fire on an Independence Day Parade in the northern Chicago suburb pre-planned the shooting for several weeks. We're getting new details tonight about the chaotic scene, the search that led law enforcement to their target and about the victims.\par \par \uc2 And the holiday violence was not isolated to the Chicago area. We have "FOX TEAM COVERAGE" tonight.\par \par \uc2 Molly Line is in Boston covering the surge in Independence Day shootings in major cities around the U.S. But we begin with senior correspondent \uc2 Mike Tobin\uc2  in Highland Park with the latest on the tragic Fourth of July massacre.\par \par \uc2 Good evening, Mike.\par \par \uc2 MIKE TOBIN\uc2 , FOX NEWS CHANNEL SENIOR CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Good evening, Mike. Police did, in fact, say that Bob Eugene Crimo planned this attack for weeks.\par \par \uc2 On the day of the attack, he dressed like a woman to avoid identification.\par \par \uc2 TOBIN (voice-over): He wrapped his rifle in a red blanket, climbed the fire escape to gain access to a rooftop on the parade route.\par \par \uc2 From that rooftop, he fired 70 rounds in rapid fire succession.\par \par \uc2 The weapon is now described as similar to an AR-15, a high-powered rifle, it was discarded on the roof. He then blended into the crowd and fled to his mother's house before escaping all the way to Madison, Wisconsin, and then returning to where he was picked up just six miles from this location.\par \par \uc2 Authorities did have run-ins with him back in 2019. First, a suicide threat, then, a threat to hurt others.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 SGT. CHRISTOPHER COVELLI, SPOKESPERSON, LAKE COUNTY MAJOR CRIME TASK FORCE: The matter was being handled by mental health professionals at that time. There was no law enforcement action to be taken.\par \par \uc2 The second occurred in September of 2019. A family member reported that Crimo said he was going to kill everyone and Crimo had a collection of knives. The police responded to his residence. The police removed 16 knives, a dagger, and a sword from Crimo's home.\par \par \uc2 TOBIN: Police say a total of 45 people were shot and injured. A seventh victim passed away today. Parade goers describe the terror.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: People walking with blood on their shirts and blood on their pants, and listen to a woman wandering the streets screaming her child's name, trying to find her child.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It just didn't stop. You know you want it to stop, stop, stop. And it just kept on going and he reloaded, whoever that person was. And then, he just continued to shoot.\par \par \uc2 And so, we just -- I just knew -- all I knew was I had to keep on moving to get him out of the way and get us to safety.\par \par \uc2 TOBIN: Democrat Illinois Governor \uc2 J.B. Pritzker\uc2  brought the discussion to assault weapons, even though Highland Park has an assault weapons ban.\par \par \uc2 GOV. JAY ROBERT PRITZKER (D-IL): Our founders carried muskets not assault weapons. And I don't think a single one of them would have said that you have a constitutional right to an assault weapon with a high capacity magazine.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TOBIN: Police say Crimo had a total of five guns. One, the rifle he left on the rooftop, another rifle that was found in his car when he was arrested. In addition, he had a shotgun and two handguns. All of them purchased after his run-ins with authorities.\par \par \uc2 Police say he is indeed talking to investigators but they still don't have a motive, and we're waiting on criminal charges. Mike, back to you.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: \uc2 Mike Tobin\uc2 , starting us off in Highland Park, Illinois. Mike, many thanks. What was supposed to be a day of celebration turned into chaos in some of America's biggest cities on the Fourth of July.\par \par \uc2 Dozens are recovering from shootings, including two police officers in Philadelphia. Correspondent Molly Line has the story tonight from Boston.\par \par \uc2 MOLLY LINE, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (voice-over): A celebratory night interrupted by fear and fleeing spectators. As gunfire erupts during a fourth of July fireworks show in Philadelphia, two uniformed police officers working the big event were wounded, but survived.\par \par \uc2 Miraculously, a bullet was recovered from the hat of a highway patrol officer who was grazed in the head.\par \par \uc2 The other officer hurt was a member of the Montgomery County Bomb Squad. Both were treated and released from a local hospital. It's unclear if the officers hit were specifically targeted.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DANIELLE OUTLAW, COMMISSIONER, PHILADELPHIA POLICE DEPARTMENT: It's still too early to tell. We don't know if this was a ricochet from celebratory gunfire. We don't know if this was intentional. We don't know if this was someone taking a shot intentionally at these officers from long range.\par \par \uc2 LINE: Evidence markers littered the street near downtown Minneapolis last night as investigators work together information following a shooting at boom Island Park where people had gathered to celebrate the holiday. Eight people are hospitalized, several in critical condition.\par \par \uc2 And there was no pause and the violence plaguing New York City with dozens of people shot throughout the holiday weekend. From July 1st to July 4th, more than 50 people were wounded and nine killed in various incidents across the boroughs. Including two young men who died after a confrontation inside of Brooklyn bodega.\par \par \uc2 Police releasing video of one day light shooting from Saturday, showing two men climbing out of a grey Honda, one repeatedly firing his gun at a 19- year-old riding a scooter. The victim died.\par \par \uc2 New York City Mayor Eric Adams, whose experience as a former police officer featured prominently in his campaign, says crime is the number one thing on the minds of New Yorkers.\par \par \uc2 ERIC ADAMS (D), MAYOR-ELECT OF NEW YORK CITY: This national problem is played out on our streets. We are making the right adjustments, we're moving in the right direction, and we're going to win this battle.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LINE: The weekend wave of violence has left investigators hunting for suspects as efforts continue to get these guns off the street. Mike.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Molly Line, live in Boston. Molly, thanks a lot.\par \par \uc2 President Biden is again on the offensive against the oil industry. But tonight, the president is also fighting another domestic battle, deadly gun violence in the U.S.\par \par \uc2 The White House has ordered flags lower to honor victims of the deadly Illinois parade shooting.\par \par \uc2 White House correspondent Peter Doocy, reports tonight from the North Lawn.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: To say that this president has not shown urgency is just -- is just false.\par \par \uc2 PETER DOOCY, FOX NEWS CHANNEL WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT (voice-over): The president, himself, may soon take on the role of consoler-in-chief again.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Mr. President, do you plan to go to Highland Park, sir? So, you and the first lady plan a visit to Chicago?\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I'm not sure.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: On Script, he used the shooting to endorse his agenda.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Nothing guaranteed about our way of life. We have to fight for it, defend it, and earn it by voting to refine, evolve, and extend the calling of America to move forward boldly and unafraid.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: Off script, he was clear, he wants more gun control.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: We got a lot more work to do. We got to get this under control. We got to get this under control.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: Gas prices also difficult to get under control. So, the president is trying something new. Tweeting, "My message to the companies running gas stations and setting prices at the pump is simple: this is a time of war and global peril. Bring down the price you are charging at the pump to reflect the cost you're paying for the product and do it now."\par \par \uc2 But, 60 percent of gas stations in this country are operated by small business owners that only own one gas station.\par \par \uc2 TIM STEWART, PRESIDENT, UNITED STATES OIL AND GAS ASSOCIATION: It's not like there is some group chat with 150,000 gas station owners on it, where they send out a message and say, oh, the president told us to lie at lower prices. So, we're going to do it. It just doesn't work that way.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (on camera): \uc2 Jeff Bezos\uc2  says the president's tweet about this. It's either straight ahead misdirection or a deep misunderstanding of basic market dynamics. Which is it?\par \par \uc2 JEAN-PIERRE: As you know, we completely disagree with \uc2 Jeff Bezos\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: I also had a chance to ask her, how it is that the president went from blaming high gas prices on Putin to big oil and now to small businesses, and she doubled down? Officials here think that all gas stations of all sizes can lower prices right away. Mike.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Peter Doocy live in the North Lawn. Peter, thanks very much.\par \par \uc2 Stocks were mixed today. The Dow lost 129. The S&P 500 added six. NASDAQ gained 194.\par \par \uc2 And Atlanta area grand jury investigating former President Trump's alleged attempts to overturn the 2020 election in Georgia has subpoenaed six key Trump allies.\par \par \uc2 Court filing show the biggest names the Fulton County special grand jury wants to talk to. Or Mr. Trump's former attorney \uc2 Rudy Giuliani\uc2 , South Carolina Senator \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2 , and election Attorney John Eastman. All six of those subpoenas are ordered to appear on July 12th.\par \par \uc2 It's been a chaotic few days for millions of Americans traveling to and from their holiday destinations as airlines struggled to keep up with near pre-pandemic demand. Thanks, in part, to bad weather and staffing shortages.\par \par \uc2 Today, FlightAware is tracking more than 3,400 delays and about 520 cancellations domestically.\par \par \uc2 Transportation Secretary \uc2 Pete Buttigieg\uc2  says his department is in constant contact with travel leaders to come up with solutions to get the industry operating more efficiently.\par \par \uc2 The Biden administration touting Operation Fly Formula for bringing more than 39 million baby formula bottles into the United States. But parents are still staring at empty shelves and some Democrats are fed up too.\par \par \uc2 Fox Business correspondent Jeff Flock joins us now from Philadelphia in the international airport where a new shipment has arrived.\par \par \uc2 Good evening, Jeff.\par \par \uc2 JEFF FLOCK, FOX BUSINESS N