tural history auction on July 28th. The dinosaur predates its relative the T. rex by 10 million years.\par \par \uc2 That's tonight's live look "OUTSIDE THE BELTWAY" from SPECIAL REPORT. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Several Texas counties along the border are taking matters into their own hands to stop the unprecedented flow of illegal migrants across the southern border. The officials are invoking the Constitution and declaring the crisis an invasion.\par \par \uc2 Chief Correspondent Jonathan Hunt is here to show us what that means exactly. Good evening, Jonathan.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN HUNT, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CHIEF CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Good evening, Mike. This is essentially an attempt to use the language of the Constitution to hand far greater immigration enforcement powers to local government agencies.\par \par \uc2 In the face of what officials from several Texas counties said today is an unprecedented challenge that the federal government is failing to tackle.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. CHIP ROY (R-TX): An invasion of the state of Texas that is undermining our security, risking the health and well-being of Texans. Allowing fentanyl to pour into our schools and communities for Texans to die. Allow our ranches to get run over. Empowering cartels who are well armed along the border with their tentacles reaching into Texas.\par \par \uc2 HUNT (voice over): The local officials are asking Texas Governor Greg Abbott to follow them in declaring they are being invaded by illegal immigrants invoking Article 4 Section 4 of the Constitution, which says in regard to all states that the United States "shall protect each of them against invasion".\par \par \uc2 And the local officials say that given what they see as the failure of the federal government to protect them, they have the authority under Article One Section 10 of the Constitution to use local law enforcement to repel what they call the