uld say that majority of our seizures come from the cartels in Mexico.\par \par \uc2 LA JEUNESSE: DEA administrator Anne Milgram says that by the numbers, this is unlike anything she's ever seen.\par \par \uc2 ANNE MILGRAM, ADMINISTRATOR, DEA: DEA has interdicted fentanyl counterfeit pills in every single U.S. state and territory. It's everywhere.\par \par \uc2 In Mexico, criminal drug trafficking networks. They're sourcing chemicals from Chinese chemical companies that's then flooding into the United States.\par \par \uc2 JOSH STEIN\uc2 , ATTORNEY GENERAL, NORTH CAROLINA: We are at the deadliest moment in what is the deadliest drug epidemic in American history.\par \par \uc2 Josh Stein\uc2  is the Attorney General of North Carolina where illicit fentanyl caused some 70 percent of all fatal overdoses in 2020. Even worse, says Stein.\par \par \uc2 STEIN: More than three quarters of all teens who are dying of overdoses have fentanyl in their system. It's killing America's youth.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LA JEUNESSE (on camera): Fentanyl is not sold on the street by the pound but rather fentanyl powder is laced into counterfeit pain pills or other antidepressants, then sold on the street.\par \par \uc2 And because it's entirely unregulated, just a few extra specs about the amount that would cover the words in God we trust and a penny can kill you says the DEA. About 300 people a day, 12 every hour says the CDC die of a drug overdose, two thirds because of fentanyl or some other synthetic opioid, Mike.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: William La Jeunesse in L.A. William, thanks a lot.\par \par \uc2 Up next, we'll go live to Ukraine for an update on Russia's ramped up onslaught and with a harrowing story of selflessness from one impactful civilian.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 You have a little chilling going up of your spine, you know, that this is could be it. You know, if they overshoot a little bit, it could be me.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Imprison \uc2 WNBA\uc2  star Brittney Griner is pleading with President Biden to do everything in his power to bring her home. In a letter, the 31- year-old asked the president to not "forget about me and the other American detainees".\par \par \uc2 White House Press Secretary \uc2 Karine Jean-Pierre\uc2  today confirmed the president read the letter and said that getting Griner home is a priority.\par \par \uc2 Griner is accused of bringing cannabis oil into the country while returning to play for a Russian team. She could face up to 10 years in prison. Her trial is ongoing.\par \par \uc2 The governor of the last remaining Eastern Province, partly under Ukraine's control is urging its more than 350,000 residents to flee as Russia escalates its offensive.\par \par \uc2 This comes as after Russian President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  declares victory in Luhansk and tries to take control of the Donbas region.\par \par \uc2 But those setbacks are not stopping one civilian from doing his part to help the military effort.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent Nate Foy shows us tonight from Lviv.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 NATE FOY, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: This was a marketplace in the eastern city of Slovyansk. Local officials say a Russian strike killed at least two and injured seven.\par \par \uc2 46 miles west, Lysychansk is destroyed as Russia takes full control of Luhansk province.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: This is an historic day.\par \par \uc2 FOY: Finland and Sweden are now official \uc2 NATO\uc2  invitees. Their bid to await legislative approvals with the unanimous vote necessary. Russian President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  promises to respond in kind to any military threat. As Russia targets all of Donbas, across the frontline, a Ukrainian American businessman who spent the past 30 years in Cleveland is delivering much need supplies to Ukrainian forces.\par \par \uc2 IHOR KOVAL, UKRAINIAN-AMERICAN BUSINESSMAN: What drives me in general is the fight for our country.\par \par \uc2 FOY: Ihor Koval and his children established an American branch of the charity Evil Cannot Enter Heaven connecting American donations to Ukrainian soldiers.\par \par \uc2 KOVAL: You wish to stay alive, but at that time I don't care, because it's anger as well in your mind.\par \par \uc2 FOY: A level of anger previously unknown to this former businessman.\par \par \uc2 KOVAL: I never thought in my life that I would have that feeling that somebody is going to die and I will be happy. That's war. War turns everything around in people's mind.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 FOY (on camera): Mike, Ihor makes his seventh trip into the Donbas region tomorrow. He's bringing camel bags, LifeStraws, and boots. He tells me in previous six trips he has come within 600 yards of Russian shell. We will send it back to you.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Nate Foy live in Lviv tonight. Nate, many thanks.\par \par \uc2 Let's go to breaking news update on yesterday's mass shooting in Highland Park, Illinois. Let's listen in.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: -- ATF group supervisor Jorge (ph) A. Resindo (ph), U.S. Attorney for the Northern District of Illinois \uc2 John Lausch\uc2 , Highland Park Mayor Nancy Rotering, Highland Park Chief of Police Lou Jogmen. And we'll begin with State's Attorney Eric Rinehart.\par \par \uc2 ERIC RINEHART, LAKE COUNTY, ILLINOIS STATE'S ATTORNEY: Good evening. My name is Eric Rinehart I'm the Lake County state's attorney. As a resident of Highland Park myself, my family and I grieve with our neighbors over this unimaginable tragedy. On behalf of the state's attorneys' office, I want to express my condolences of the families of those who lost their lives yesterday in this premeditated and calculated attack.\par \par \uc2 Separate from those seven lives that were ripped away, over two dozen other mothers, fathers, sons, daughters, brothers, sisters, and neighbors were injured. Hundreds, if not thousands, will be psychologically scarred by yesterday's events. These individuals, our community, and Lake County will never be the same.\par \par \uc2 We are grateful to the unbelievably courageous law enforcement officers who were there when the shots were fired right here in this pathway. They and other law enforcement agencies from throughout the state came to this spot of danger. They came to help. They came to protect. They came to comfort. And they came to investigate.\par \par \uc2 Federal agencies, including the \uc2 FBI\uc2 , ATF, and U.S. Attorney's Office, have also been critical partners in this investigation and you will hear a bit more from them this evening. These officers not only ran towards the danger but worked tirelessly throughout the day and night to investigate and solve this case.\par \par \uc2 I want to also thank all of the community members and business members and leaders who provided us with dozens of videos and photos. We continue our request for those photos and videos. This is still an open and active investigation. Police continue to contact all of the tip lines that we suggested. To those who are physically and psychologically suffering today in hospitals and in homes throughout our community, we offer our most fervent and urgent prayers. We offer prayers for healing even though it feels it may be so far away.\par \par \uc2 I know that Mayor Rotering and Highland Park Police Chief Lou Jogmen, Lake County, and our federal partners have already started the critical work of supporting the individuals who need us so much while they are in the throes of trauma.\par \par \uc2 Today our office deployed victim witness counselors and licensed therapists also to Highland Park. Dozens of private individuals have volunteered to help. This has been one of the most uplifting things I have ever seen in Lake County's history. The community has come together even in the throes of trauma. The loss of life and these devastating injuries and the overwhelming psychological trauma demand we seek justice and that we take broader action to protect life.\par \par \uc2 Today the Lake County State's Attorney's Office has charged Robert Crimo the III with seven counts of first-degree murder.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 RINEHART: For the killing spree that he has unleashed against our community, these are just the first of many charges that will be filed against Mr. Crimo. I want to emphasize that. There will be more charges.\par \par \uc2 We anticipate dozens of more charges centering around each of the victims, psychological victims, physical victims, attempt murder charges, aggravated discharge charges, aggravated battery charges. There will be dozens of more charges against Mr. Crimo. But these seven counts of first degree will lead to mandatory life sentence should he be convicted without the possibility of parole.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 RINEHART: None of these charges would be possible, again, without the unbelievable efforts of law enforcement. I cannot thank them enough.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 RINEHART: I have to thank the men and women of the Lake County State's Attorneys' office who have within working with law enforcement since the moment that we identified a suspect. We continue to work with our federal partners to understand every aspect of this well-orchestrated and carefully planned crime. Tomorrow morning at the Lake County courthouse, we will ask a judge to hold Mr. Crimo without the possibility of bail.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 RINEHART: As I mentioned -- as I mentioned, our support for victims started today, both from the state's attorneys' office, from the mayor's office, from the city of Highland Park, from the police department, from the \uc2 FBI\uc2  victim assistance group. We will guide them through the court process and deal with the trauma that will ripple forever outside of the courtrooms.\par \par \uc2 But how do we make sure that there are no more victims? What should have been a celebration of freedom has ended in despair for our community. All of the people who died steps from here lost their freedom, all of it, every ounce of freedom that they had -- the freedom to love, the freedom to learn, and the freedom to live a full life. Their freedom matters too. We must do more as we think and reflect on their freedom on this July 5th.\par \par \uc2 Illinois has a strong red flag law that keeps communities safe and respects everyone's right. We must vastly increase awareness and education about this red flag law called the Illinois firearm restraining order.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 RINEHART: The goal of this tool is to ensure the safety of the individual and those around them. It allows courts to temporarily remove guns and prevent the purchase of new guns by individuals who pose a significant risk. As I said, this tool will allow temporary removal from the subject and prohibits the new purchase during the duration of the order. It allows individuals to stabilize their treatment -- excuse me, stabilize their behavior, seek treatment, and access other resources that our community must invest in to give those who need help.\par \par \uc2 But separate from these red flag laws, which are very powerful in Illinois, we should also ban assault weapons in Illinois and beyond.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 RINEHART: The assault weapon ban was implemented in 1994 with bipartisan support and with the support of law enforcement. It lasted for 10 years, and studies have shown that mass shootings like what happened yesterday went down during those 10 years. We should have that same ban in Illinois and beyond.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 RINEHART: In the entire country. The Lake County State's Attorneys' Office stands with the people of Highland Park. As I said earlier, this is where I am raising my family, also. Our violent crimes unit will continue to work with all of our law enforcement partners to continue to build this case. As I said, it is an active and ongoing investigation. Our victim services unit will answer every question, every text, every phone call, and every concern. We will stand with the survivors of this awful crime for as long as is necessary. In the courtroom, we will seek the maximum sentence against this offender, not because we seek vengeance, but because justice and the healing process demand it.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 RINEHART: As we go forward in the courtroom and in the community, we must do everything we can to make sure the horror that mark these streets, that echoed from these buildings, never happens again.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 RINEHART: Thank you very much. At this time I would introduce U.S. Attorney and, really, our partner in this investigation so far, U.S. attorney \uc2 John Lausch\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 JOHN LAUSCH\uc2 , U.S. ATTORNEY: Thank you. My name is \uc2 John Lausch\uc2 , I'm the United States Attorney here in the northern district of Illinois. I want echo some of the same words we just heard from State's Attorney Rinehart and commend all of the law enforcement partners and agencies who are represented here today, and all of our first responders for everything they did to bravely put their lives on the line to help others in need.\par \par \uc2 Our office, the U.S. attorney's office, and our other Department of Justice components, most notably the \uc2 FBI\uc2  and the ATF, have been working very closely with our state and local law enforcement partners to investigate this horrific attack. We have been and we will remain in regular communication with these partners and in particular the Lake County state's attorneys' office, and I have been in regular contact with State's Attorney Rinehart since the beginning of this investigation.\par \par \uc2 These state murder charges are appropriate at this time. We will continue to deploy our federal resources in collaboration with these state and local partners to ensure that individuals who commit horrific acts of violence like this using firearms are held accountable.\par \par \uc2 And, with that I would like to turn it over to the chief of the Highland Park Police Department Lou Jogmen.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 LOU JOGMEN, HIGHLAND PARK, ILLINOIS, POLICE CHIEF: The last approximately 36 hours have been a lesson in the duality of humanity. While the actions brought us together exposed the cruelty and callousness that one person could show to people he did not know in taking their lives, the unwavering commitment of our Highland Park team, the drive and determination of those who assisted us in making this arrest, and the outpouring of love and support from the community and from people across the nation and the globe have shown us the best in humanity, the American spirit throughout the response of this horrific and absolutely senseless act of violence.\par \par \uc2 We would not be standing here this afternoon announcing the arrest of this suspect without the assistance of countless law enforcement agencies from multiple counties, from the Illinois state police, \uc2 FBI\uc2 , ATF, NIPAS, Lake County coroner's office, the hospital staff, our medical community and Deputy Chief Chris Covelli of the Lake County Major Crimes Task Force. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 To the Highland community, your strength has buoyed our team through the darkest moments. To our city of Highland Park team, the fire department, public works, our city manager's office, our finance, and our community development departments, your contributions have been invaluable, and we thank you.\par \par \uc2 To Mayor Rotering and the city council, thank you for your sustained engagement and unwavering support. And to the men and women with whom I serve alongside in the Highland Park Police Department, it is an honor and a privilege to serve with you. Your commitment and determination to see justice served for this community inspires me. I'm proud of Illinois law enforcement.\par \par \uc2 At this time, I would like to introduce Lake County Sheriff Deputy Chief Chris Covelli spokesperson for the major crime assistance team.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 DEPUTY CHRISTOPHER COVELLI, LAKE COUNTY, ILLINOIS, SHERIFF'S OFFICE: Thank you, Chief.\par \par \uc2 I'm going to read a brief statement on behalf of the Victims Service Response Team of the \uc2 FBI\uc2 . Beginning tomorrow, the \uc2 FBI\uc2 's Victims Service Response Team will be working in conjunction with local, state, and federal aid groups to staff up family assistance center. At the family assistance center victims will be offered trauma counseling, government aid assistance, and if necessary, financial assistance.\par \par \uc2 Victims are not just those physically injured in yesterday's events, but also those experiencing emotional distress. The center will open tomorrow, July 6th, at noon. Victims of yesterday's incident are invited to access available services at the Highland Park High School, and that's located at 433 Vine Avenue in Highland Park. The family assistance center will be available from noon tomorrow until 9:00 p.m., Thursday and Friday from 9:00 a.m. to 9:00 p.m., and thereafter as needed. Additional information regarding return of personal effects left at the scene will also be released tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 With that, we are going to open it up for some questions. I would just ask for a little bit of patience. We have a lot of stakeholders that are joining us today, so we can filter who is best to answer each of the questions asked.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Chris, could we ask a question of the state's attorney, please?\par \par \uc2 COVELLI: Yes.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Mr. State's Attorney, you went out of your way to note the red flag laws. We now know that this individual had contacts with law enforcement in 2019 where in one of the contacts he said he wanted to kill everyone in his family. A number of knives were taken away from him at that time. Should that have been a qualifying event under the red flag law which would have prevented him buying firearms in 2020 and 2021?\par \par \uc2 RINEHART: We have to look at every case on a case-by-case basis. I don't know the internal process of Illinois state police in terms of after that report was taken. We know there was not an application for a FOID pending at that time. The current red flag laws, you have a process where things are reported to the Illinois state police with respect to FOIDs. And then you have the red flag law which is what I was referring to, which were passed in 2019, or effective in 2019 in terms of getting a court order where family members and other individuals who know the person at issue have to go a court.\par \par \uc2 So my reference to the red flag laws refers to that regime. With respect to the Illinois state police, that has to do with the FOID application process.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: If I'm hearing you right, what you are say something possibly these qualifying events were before the fact, before he actually had applied for a FOID, obtained a FOID, and purchased --\par \par \uc2 RINEHART: I would definitely refer all those questions to Illinois state police in terms of that internal process back in 2019.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Sir, the family members, the timeline, does this mean that he had a legal guardian who sponsored his FOID card. Did he get his FOID card before he was 21? Did he have a legal guardian who sponsored him? And if so, are you looking at charges at all --\par \par \uc2 RINEHART: I am absolutely not going to get into that level of detail in the investigation and our process.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: OK, I have a question, and probably multiple people can take it. For somebody who planned an escape, dressing as a woman -- do you have any theory was somebody would leave a weapon, an easily traceable weapon at the crime scene that could lead to your identity being known easily and swiftly?\par \par \uc2 COVELLI: Investigators are very thoroughly looking at every aspect of this. There are a number of theories on the table as to why he left his weapon there. I don't want to get into and speculate anything at this point. There is still a lot of investigative work to go. But that is something investigators are looking into.\par \par \uc2 (INAUDIBLE)\par \par \uc2 COVELLI: That's very possible. It's very clear to investigators he attempted to blend in with the rest of the victims who were fleeing the scene carrying a rifle. I would imagine wouldn't allow one to blend in very well.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: But are we right that weapon left behind might as well have -- easily leads to the identity of the person who owns it?\par \par \uc2 COVELLI: The weapon led to him directly.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Like leaving your driver's license?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I'm sorry, can I get back to this point, why he went to the Madison area?\par \par \uc2 COVELLI: Investigators are still looking into that. They are still working many different angles. We do know he went on a drive following the event. But as to why, that's not something I can get into right now.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: This is for U.S. Attorney Lausch. You said at this point state charges are appropriate. When do you deem federal charges --\par \par \uc2 LAUSCH: I think the best answer to that is echo the same points that were made. This is ongoing investigation, and it really wouldn't be appropriate for me to comment any further at this time. But the charges right now, these are very appropriate charges right now.\par \par \uc2 (INAUDIBLE)\par \par \uc2 LAUSCH: It should not read anything into it one way or the other. It would not be appropriate for me to comment one way or the other whether or not there would be federal charges.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Can you describe local threats land on federal radar, and what tools you have to identify and prevent these things before they happen.\par \par \uc2 LAUSCH: OK. So, there's a lot packed into that question. Here's what I will say, is the partners that are here, OK, and in particular \uc2 FBI\uc2  and ATF are the ones that are standing with me. They work every single day in order to mitigate threats that they learn of. And this is a particular instance when we had the horrific attack that happened yesterday, how they came to right away in order to help law enforcement ultimately be able to make this case and to bring the appropriate charges in state court.\par \par \uc2 But again, at this point I think it's fair to say from our end this is an ongoing investigation. I'm very proud up here to be with my colleague from the Lake County State's Attorneys' Office. But it really wouldn't be appropriate for me to comment any further about whether or not any federal charges are likely. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Chris, can you give us any further clarification on the weapons, where he obtained them, and what the enumeration was?\par \par \uc2 COVELLI: So, what I know firsthand at this time is that two of the weapons were obtained within the region here. Those were two of the rifles. The rifle used in the attack was purchased by him.\par \par \uc2 And I just want to follow up on the question that you asked a little earlier. It's important to note, Highland Park police when they responded to that 2019 incident, they removed the weapons that Crimo possessed. There were no firearms at that time to be removed. So as the state's attorney, the red flag laws, there were no red flag laws to go after firearms in that home at that time.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: You said the rifles were obtained in the region, but there were two rifles and, was it, four handguns?\par \par \uc2 COVELLI: I can't say four handguns. There were a total of five guns.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I'm sorry, three handguns.\par \par \uc2 COVELLI: Five firearms that he possessed.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: What about -- you said the two rifles in the region. And what about the remainder?\par \par \uc2 COVELLI: They were bought in Illinois. I would have to get specifics as to exactly where.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: He got the FOID card before he was 21, is that true?\par \par \uc2 COVELLI: Yes.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: So that means he was sponsored by a legal guardian?\par \par \uc2 COVELLI: I don't want to speak to the process of the Illinois state police. I don't have enough of their internal information to accurately respond.\par \par \uc2 Yes, sir.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: So Chris, what happened when he went in the house and got the knives, would that appear on a FOID investigation, would they say somebody went into the household and picked up some knives off this guy?\par \par \uc2 COVELLI: Again, Highland Park police notified the Illinois state police. Where it goes from there, I don't want to speak to it.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: The family at all, have they been cooperative with the investigation? Do you have any reason to believe that they may have known things?\par \par \uc2 COVELLI: At this point, we don't have reason to believe that they may have known things. But the investigation is still very active. Investigators have a lot of legwork to do still.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Has the family been cooperative with the investigation?\par \par \uc2 COVELLI: What I'll say is investigators have spoken to the family. I don't want to gauge their level of cooperation one way or the other.\par \par \uc2 (INAUDIBLE)\par \par \uc2 COVELLI: The magazines held approximately 30 rounds of rifle ammunition.\par \par \uc2 Thank you.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: If you guys could just hold here for a minute, I will have more information for you shortly.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: How many magazines were there?\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: An update from authorities there in Highland Park, Illinois, following the July 4th massacre in their community. The Lake County attorney announcing seven counts of first-degree murder will be filed against the suspect. He hinted that dozens more charges are forthcoming but didn't want to specify exactly how many are on the way, just that more charges are coming.\par \par \uc2 A very active investigation. We heard questions there about the suspect after the shooting driving to the Madison, Wisconsin, area. Not entirely clear why he did or why he came back. But we will continue to keep you updated with the latest information on the parade shooting there in Highland Park, Illinois, as we get it.\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in our panel, Juan Williams is a FOX News analyst, Morgan Ortagus, former State Department spokesperson, and former \uc2 Republican National Committee\uc2  communications director Douglas Heye. Panel, welcome.\par \par \uc2 DOUGLAS HEYE, FORMER REPUBLICAN NATIONAL COMMITTEE COMMUNICATIONS DIRECTOR: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: So the breaking news of the moment, the update out of Highland Park, Illinois, another mass shooting. This is a political panel, and so obviously a lot of focus is on what more could be done after Congress passed the first gun reform in 30 years or so. Doug, do you expect calls to pick up here in Washington?\par \par \uc2 HEYE: Without question. This is what about after every shooting, and obviously we've had so many of them that we're used to this. We have become conditioned to it, really. And we passed a good bill that addresses some of the issues in these shootings. It's not the answer, but hopefully it's an answer. And if we see what worked and what didn't, then we can look at in future cases, when we always say somebody fell through the cracks, how to fix and plug those cracks.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, what we are seeing in Highland Park is really fantastic from their government. They are being clear. They're being consistent in the information that they are giving us. It is the mirror opposite of what we saw in Uvalde with their government.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Juan, your thoughts? The president just signed this new bill into law. It was clearly a compromise to get Republican support. What about calls for Congress to do more?\par \par \uc2 JUAN WILLIAMS, FOX NEWS POLITICAL ANALYST: I think there still is a need to do more, Mike. At this point you just stop and think about the effect on us all as Americans. You are afraid to go a Fourth of July parade. You are afraid to go to the grocery store. You are afraid to go to school. You're afraid to go to the concert, the movies. I think that there is a need to do more, but the question is what is possible, because obviously the Congress has resisted doing more.\par \par \uc2 Even with regard to what they recently did, it's giving more time for background checks, mental health checks, juvenile records checks on those under 21. But that wouldn't necessarily have stopped this young man in Highland Park from getting that weapon. And we heard it just in the press conference that it was possible that we had some of his parents helping him.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Morgan, let's get a brief through from you on this topic before we move on to other things.\par \par \uc2 MORGAN ORTAGUS, FORMER STATE DEPARTMENT SPOKESPERSON: I think we're going to be grappling for a long time with the crisis of mental health that we have in this country, and we're only beginning to see -- and there are so many root causes to this, and we can argue politics of it. But I think everybody can agree that since COVID and even before, that mental health crisis started in this country, and we haven't even begun to, I think, wrangle with the ramifications of it having so much of society locked up for so long. We know we have a problem with young men and these lone wolf shootings, and that's something we're going to have to address.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Up next, the panel on Biden versus big oil. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: The headline of the hour, the Lake County, Illinois, state's attorneys' office announced that Robert Crimo III is charged with seven counts of first degree. Authorities say dozens of other charges are coming, a very active investigation. Please stay tuned to FOX News Channel for breaking news updates on this heartbreaking story.\par \par \uc2 Tomorrow on SPECIAL REPORT, President Biden travels to Ohio to promote his economic agenda. We will bring you there.\par \par \uc2 Thanks for inviting us into your home tonight. That's it for this SPECIAL REPORT. I'm Mike Emanuel in Washington. "JESSE WATTERS PRIMETIME" is up next. Hello, Jesse.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FNSPBB0020220706ei7500001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Interview with National Security Council Strategic Communications Coordinator John Kirby; Interview with Georgia governor \uc2 Tate Reeves\uc2 ; Former White House Aide Testifies During Hearing on Capitol Riot; Some Democrats Consider Filibuster Change After Supreme Court Rulings; White House Faces Continued Questions on the President's 2024 Plans; Key Primary Battles Take Center State Ahead of November Midterms\b0\par\par\uc2 Mike Emanuel, Alexandria Hoff, David Spunt, Juan Williams, Mollie  Hemingway \par \uc2 9,552 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 July 2022\par \uc2 Fox News: Fox News Sunday\par \uc2 FOXN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 MIKE EMANUEL, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: I'm Mike Emanuel.\par \par \uc2 President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  returns from foreign travel to the hard reality of inflation and a new reality on abortion.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL (voice-over): Americans facing high food and gasoline prices this holiday weekend as the president points the finger again.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Russia, Russia, Russia.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: And blasts a ruling from the nation's high court while standing on the world stage.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: It is a mistake, in my view, for the Supreme Court to do what it did.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: We'll ask National Security Council strategic communications coordinator John Kirby about the plan to fight global inflation, the prospects for peace in Ukraine and the president's comments on the court.\par \par \uc2 Plus, tensions and confusion as states navigate the post-Roe v. Wade legal landscape.\par \par \uc2 We'll discuss with Governor \uc2 Tate Reeves\uc2  of Mississippi, one of several states with trigger laws now facing legal challenges.\par \par \uc2 Plus, the abortion fight quickly becoming a key issue in midterm races, along with the president's low poll numbers. We'll ask our Sunday panel about Democrats' plans to shift strategy and whether they want Mr. Biden at their side on the trail.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I intend to do the campaigning myself.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: And we'll discuss explosive new testimony from a key witness about former President Trump's actions on January 6th.\par \par \uc2 REP. LIZ CHENEY (R-WY): I just want to confirm that that is when you heard the president say that people with weapons weren't there to hurt him and that he wanted the Secret Service to remove the magnetometers.\par \par \uc2 CASSIDY HUTCHINSON, FORMER WHITE HOUSE AIDE: That's correct.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: All, right now, on "FOX News Sunday".\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: And hello again from FOX News in Washington, and happy Independence Day weekend.\par \par \uc2 The president back on U.S. soil days after meeting with European allies to discuss ongoing support for Ukraine. The president hinting that steep gas prices we are seeing this holiday could be here to stay as Russia's war rages on. And he's promising action to preserve one's access to abortion as state bands go into effect.\par \par \uc2 In a moment, National Security Council strategic communications coordinator John Kirby joins me to discuss the administration's plans to increase the global oil's supply.\par \par \uc2 But, first, let's turned to Alex Hoff live at the White House this morning with how President Biden is balancing foreign and domestic priorities -- Alex.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDRIA HOFF, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Well, Mike, according to the president, they are one and the same, reiterating the stance that Russia is to blame for the current cost-of-living crisis and warning Americans not to grow weary.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOFF (voice-over): President Biden did not arrive home with a remedy for gas prices but left the \uc2 G7\uc2  and \uc2 NATO\uc2  leadership meanings with the plan to cap the price of Russian oil experts and a fast-track invitation for Sweden and Finland to join the alliance Vladimir sought to stifle.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: He's getting exactly what he did not want. He wanted the Finlandalization of \uc2 NATO\uc2 . He got the NATOlization of Finland.\par \par \uc2 HOFF: Additional military resistance was also pledged to allies in a continued fight to preserve Ukraine. Discussions also centered around potential security threats posed by China.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: We rallied our alliances to meet both of direct threats that Russia poses to Europe and the systemic challenges that China poses to a rules- based world order.\par \par \uc2 HOFF: The president also used the world stage to admonish the Supreme Court's overturning of Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 From the White House Friday, President Biden met virtually with nine Democratic governors who vowed to preserve access to abortions.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I share the public outrage at this extremist court, has committed to moving America backwards with fewer rights, less autonomy.\par \par \uc2 HOFF: While the president said he supports a carve-out to the Senate filibuster in order to codify Roe v. Wade, he conceded that Democrats likely do not have enough votes right now to change Senate procedure. After the meeting, President Biden took off to Camp David, joining a pre-pandemic rate of Americans who traveled this holiday weekend. Those who did so by air faced thousands of delays and cancellations.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOFF (on camera): On the legislative side, Senate Minority Leader \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  said this week that Republicans will not support a bipartisan innovation bill if Democrats continue to pursue a slimmed down version their social spending package -- Mike.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Alex Hoff reporting from the White House -- Alex, many thanks.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now, National Security Council strategic communications coordinator, John Kirby.\par \par \uc2 Admiral, welcome back to "FOX News Sunday".\par \par \uc2 JOHN KIRBY, NSC STRATEGIC COMMUNICATIONS COORDINATOR: Good to be with you, Mike. Thanks.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Let's start in Ukraine -- your former specialty being a former Pentagon spokesman. Is the goal to wear out the Russians or to defeat them?\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: The goal is to make sure that Ukraine can continue to defend itself and can defend its sovereignty and its territorial integrity. And you heard the president talk about this at \uc2 NATO\uc2  just a few days ago.\par \par \uc2 We don't need -- we don't want to see Ukraine defeated by Russia. And that's why we are continuing to rush aid and assistance, now more than $7 billion worth of assistance. That's just from the United States alone to Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: There are concerns this war could drag on. Most expect --\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: -- that a negotiated settlement will be required to end it.\par \par \uc2 Is it time for the president of the United States to push the Russians and Ukrainians to the negotiating table?\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: It's time for the United States to continue to support Ukraine, and that's what we're doing.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Zelenskyy, President Zelenskyy, he gets to determine how victory is decided and when and in what terms. And what we're going to do is continue to make sure that he can succeed on the battlefield so that he can succeed at the table.\par \par \uc2 But even President Zelenskyy will tell you the time is not now for those discussions. And certainly, President Putin has shown no indication that he's interested in negotiating talks. In fact, quite the contrary. He continues to not my lash out in the Donbas, but conducts air strikes now in places like Kyiv.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Do you worry that the rules of engagement are too restrictive for the Ukrainians? And do you worry about a stalemate?\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: The Ukrainians are fighting really bravely and skillfully, Mike. And they're not only working to defend territory, and they have done a noble job of that. They're going on the counter offensive.\par \par \uc2 There were press reports this morning that they -- that they struck back in Melitopol. Melitopol, as you might recall, is a town they lost several weeks ago. They're striking back.\par \par \uc2 So they have been willing to go on the counteroffensive.\par \par \uc2 I can't speak to Ukrainian rules of engagement on the battlefield, but I can tell you that they are fighting bravely, skillfully, and quite -- quite creatively.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: OK. President Biden tweeted last night: My message to the companies running gas stations and setting prices at the pump is simple. This is a time of war and global peril. Bring down the price you're charging at the pump to reflect the cost you're paying for the product. And do it now.\par \par \uc2 Jeff Bezos\uc2  tweeted late last night: Ouch. Inflation is far too important a problem for the White House to keep making statements like this. It's either straight ahead misdirection or a deep misunderstanding of basic market dynamics.\par \par \uc2 How do you respond?\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: It's neither. Not in terms of President Biden's view.\par \par \uc2 I mean, the American people are facing pain at the pump, clearly. Now, we are at $5 a gallon.\par \par \uc2 And the president is working very, very hard across many fronts, Mike, to try and bring that price down. At the \uc2 G7\uc2 , working on trying to develop a price cap on Russian oil, asking Congress to support a three month holiday for the gas tax, and, of course, releasing now a million barrels of oil out of the strategic -- a record number barrels of oil out of our strategic reserve to try to bring that price down.\par \par \uc2 He knows that that's not going to solve all the problems but it will help. If everybody cooperates on this, we can bring the price done at least by a dollar a gallon. So he's working very hard to do this because he knows the impact that high gas prices have on the American household.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Misdirection is a pretty serious allegation from a business mogul who happens to be the owner of "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 ."\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: I think that -- anybody that knows President Biden knows he's plainspoken and he tells exactly what he's thinking in terms that everybody can understand. So, I think, we obviously take great exception of the idea that this is somehow misdirection.\par \par \uc2 The president is speaking honestly -- honestly with the American people about what he's trying to do to bring the prices down, but he was honest even before the invasion, Mike, about the fact that it would not be cost- free. Certainly not cost-free to President Putin, but it wouldn't be cost- free to the American people to stand up for democracy, to stand up for Ukraine's ability to defend itself.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: He's expected to travel overseas to meet with leaders in the Middle East to talk about gasoline prices. There's been some criticism about potentially meeting with the Saudis because he's called them a pariah state.\par \par \uc2 Let's play it.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATESE: I guess I will see the king and the crown prince, but that's -- that's not the meeting I'm going to. They'll be part of a much larger meeting.\par \par \uc2 I'm not going to ask -- and going to ask, there's -- all the Gulf States are meeting. I've indicated to them that I thought they should be increasing oil production generically, not to the Saudis particularly.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Does it matter if he discusses oil production with the Saudis individually or in a group meeting?\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: The president is going to the \uc2 Gulf Cooperation Council\uc2 . That's the purpose for this trip, and Saudi is the host of the GCC, and they have led the GC quite well.\par \par \uc2 And the president is looking forward to a range of topics that we have to discuss. Energy is going to be on the agenda, of course. And you heard the president. He'll talk about energy security in the context of the GCC.\par \par \uc2 He'll have a bilateral discussion with the king and the king's leadership team, but there's an awful lot on this agenda, Mike, that we can't forget. And clearly, energy is an important global issue right now, the president understands that, but so is terrorism. So was the war in Yemen.\par \par \uc2 We're now on the second extension of a cease-fire in Yemen that the United States, you know, helped -- with our engagement, helped -- helped get us that through the Saudis.\par \par \uc2 There's -- and then, of course, there's -- you know, there's the issue of climate change. There is a lot going on in the GCC that the president is looking forward to discuss.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: We follow-up. Can you confirm no one-on-one meetings with the king or crown prince or even a pull aside?\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: The president will have a bilateral discussion with King Salman and his leadership team. And, of course, the crown prince is on that leadership team. So as you heard the president say, he certainly expects that he'll be seeing the crown prince in the context of that bilateral discussion.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Okay. Do you worry that countries in the Middle East may -- may not be willing or may not be able to increase production to impact prices here at home?\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: I don't want to get ahead of the agenda and what they're going to decide at the GCC meeting.\par \par \uc2 I would just note, Mike, that -- that already, there has -- \uc2 OPEC\uc2  has increased by 50 percent previously scheduled increases for July and August. So going into the rest of the summer, they've already increased, again, by half, planned increases for oil production. And we'll see what happens, you know, as a result of this meeting and where things go.\par \par \uc2 The president understands that, you know, increased oil production can help, which is another reason why again he's tapped into our strategic reserve a million barrels a day. But there's an awful lot that goes into energy security that isn't just about oil production. And again, the president is looking forward to having that discussion.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: The president spoke this week about inflation. Let's play it.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I can understand why the American people are frustrated because of inflation, but inflation is higher in almost every other country. Prices of the pump are higher at almost every other country. We're better positioned to deal with this than anyone, but we have a way to go.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: But let's take a look at inflation numbers for major economies. There are many with inflation lower than the U.S. And as of May, average inflation in \uc2 G7\uc2  countries is lower than it is in the U.S.\par \par \uc2 Are we in a better position to deal with inflation?\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: The American economy is incredibly strong. I mean, we're still at 3.6 unemployment under President Biden's leadership now. In just the first year, 9 million more jobs were added. That is not an economy that's in a recession.\par \par \uc2 And the president is right. We have the foundational elements here in the American economy to weather through this, and to come through it strongly and good on the outside, on the other end of it.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: The president also made headlines in Spain, criticizing the Supreme Court. Let's play that.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: The one thing that has been destabilizing is the outrageous behavior of the \uc2 Supreme Court of the United States\uc2  on overruling not only Roe v. Wade, but essentially challenging the right to privacy. We've been a leader in the world in terms of personal rights and privacy rights.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Is that appropriate on the world stage?\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: The president was answering an honest and fair question from a reporter, an American reporter, I might add, in a press conference. And so, he said it, answered it in exactly the same way, same tone, same tenor, that he spoke about the decision -- the Dobbs decision when it was announced, when it came out.\par \par \uc2 So, he was very consistent with what he said before. But he was answering a question from a reporter, Mike. He was actually up there at \uc2 NATO\uc2  to talk about the accomplishments of the summit, which were significant.\par \par \uc2 You know, the potential now ascension of Sweden and Finland, which will change the alliance forever. The inclusion of global partnerships at the \uc2 NATO\uc2  alliance, the first time we had a Australian, Japan, South Korea at a \uc2 NATO\uc2  summit. And then, of course, the continued support to Ukraine and a strategic concept, the first one in 12 years, mind you, that not only talks about the threat that Russia poses to European and transatlantic security, but also mentions China and the challenges posed by China first time.\par \par \uc2 So, it was a wide-ranging press conference. He answered a question honestly and fairly, and he used the same exact terms about his disappointment in the Supreme Court's decision as he did right here at home before he left buried\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Let's talk about basketball star Brittney Griner. She's on trial in Russia, could potentially face 10 years in Russian prison.\par \par \uc2 Her wife has expressed some frustration.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ABBY PHILLIP, CNN REPORTER: Do trust that the maximum amount of effort is being put forward to bring BG home?\par \par \uc2 CHERELLE GRINER, WIFE OF BRITTNEY GRINER: No, I don't, and I hate to say that, because I do trust that they are -- that the persons working on this are very genuine people. That I do believe. But I don't think the maximum amount of effort is being done.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Any plans for the president to meet with her and is a possible prisoner swap up for discussion?\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: I don't have anything on the president's schedule to speak to today. I can tell you that he is very much engaged in this and he has been engaged with the team that is not only in direct communication with Ms. Griner, but also working very, very hard to get her released.\par \par \uc2 I mean, she needs to come home. It's past time. The \uc2 United States government\uc2  is very, very focused on her case, as it is on the cases of other unjustly and wrongfully detained Americans around the world.\par \par \uc2 This is something that the president takes very, very seriously.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Remain in Mexico policy, the Supreme Court says you guys can wind it down. I don't want to get into the nitty-gritty of the Supreme Court or the policy. I know that's not your specialty.\par \par \uc2 But broadly, are you concerned about the impact at the border, which is already facing very difficult times?\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: We -- we believe that this "Remain in Mexico" policy was inhumane and that actually forced people that were applying for asylum to then go back to deplorable condition, unsafe conditions, and to face the potential for further violence, being pushed back into Mexico like that to wait.\par \par \uc2 So, look, we're -- we're working our way through e Supreme Court's decision and I won't get ahead of decisions here. But we welcome -- we welcome this decision and we're -- we're going to be working hard here on what the next steps are going to be.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: What about the humanitarian crisis at the border? Just this week, we saw more than 50 migrants killed in a tractor-trailer.\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: Yeah, I mean, obviously, that's deplorable. And I would, you know, make a couple of points here.\par \par \uc2 Number one, under President Biden's leadership now, we have really gone after human traffickers and smugglers. I mean, more than 2,400 arrests just to date since April, since we started this.\par \par \uc2 And then, look at the Summit of the Americas. When the president was out in Los Angeles just a couple weeks ago, for the first time getting more than 20 heads of state from the region to sign up to a migration declaration that for the first time, Mike, actually considers the root causes of a regional -- of a regional focus here, what's causing -- whether it's economically, politically or socially, the root causes -- that causes (ph) of these migrants to flow north into Mexico and put pressure on the Mexican government.\par \par \uc2 So, for the first time now, leaders across the region are going to take a holistic approach to try to get at those root causes to stop these migrants.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Last one, what about your new role at the White House? Some people see you as perhaps a co-White House press secretary. How do you respond?\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: No, no, no. Karine -- Karine is the press secretary. I'm just down at the National Security Council and just trying to help with coordination of some of the comms efforts.\par \par \uc2 And it's a great honor and a privilege to be given this opportunity, and I'm very much excited about it.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Admiral John Kirby, thank you for your service. Thank you for your time this Independence Day weekend.\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Up next, both sides of the debate are preparing for an onslaught of lawsuits over new restrictions on abortions in states across the U.S. Mississippi Governor \uc2 Tate Reeves\uc2  joins us next on the legal challenge over his state's trigger law and what the law means for women and doctors in his state.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: The president continues to blast the Supreme Court ruling that overturned Roe v. Wade. This week, he met with nine Democratic governors to discuss next steps to preserve access. And the marshal of the Supreme Court asked officials in Maryland and Virginia to enforce their jurisdictions' laws against picketing outside of justice's homes.\par \par \uc2 This comes as pro-choice advocates are suing over new laws in multiple states.\par \par \uc2 I sat down with Mississippi's Republican Governor \uc2 Tate Reeves\uc2  on this complicated legal landscape and the questions raised by his state's ban.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Joining us now, Mississippi Governor \uc2 Tate Reeves\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Governor, welcome back to "FOX News Sunday."\par \par \uc2 GOV. TATE REEVES (R), MISSISSIPPI: Well, thank you for having me on today, Mike.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Mississippi's attorney general has initiated steps to certify the state's trigger law and it could go into effect after ten days. But you're facing a legal challenge from the state's only clinic and that case cites a state supreme court ruling from 1998 which reads: We find that the state constitutional right to privacy includes an implied right to choose whether or not to have an abortion.\par \par \uc2 Question, is your trigger law going to survive this legal challenge? And if so, on what grounds?\par \par \uc2 REEVES: Well, yes, we do believe that our trigger law is going to withstand this particular challenge. As you know, the court made their decision last Friday. On Monday of this past week, our attorney general did her duty under the law, which is to certify that Roe v. Wade was, in fact, overturned. It takes ten days for that trigger law then to go into effect.\par \par \uc2 Jackson Women's Health Organization, which is the exact same organization that we were opposite in the Supreme Court case, then filed suit in state court. We are dealing with that. The chief justice of the Supreme Court has appointed a special judge to hear that case because the four chancellors in Hinds County, which is the court of jurisdiction, recuse themselves.\par \par \uc2 And so, we are expecting a hearing sometime early this week on the particular case. But yes, I do expect for the state's law to be withheld -- upheld in the state court, and I do expect our trigger like to go into effect.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Do you plan to enforce the ban even if this challenge is still making its way through the courts?\par \par \uc2 REEVES: Well, obviously, we're going to -- we're going to go through the court system in the manner in which we are required to do. Unless there is an injunction which forces us not to enforce it, then, of course, we're going to enforce the law as it currently exists.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Now that you're preparing for is post-Roe world, is there a plan for their women and the children there going to be delivering? In other words, does the pro-life movement have to shift its efforts now to moms and babies?\par \par \uc2 REEVES: Absolutely, Mike. You're exactly right.\par \par \uc2 And the fact of the matter is that this entire court battle was never about winning a court case. It was always about creating a culture of life, and that's exactly what we're doing here in Mississippi and that's what we as pro-life Mississippians and pro-life Americans have to continue to do. We have to prove that being pro-life is not simply about being antiabortion, and in our state, we are enacting policies to do exactly that.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: The Republican Mississippi state senator who sponsored this law also said he had other lawmakers will be seeking legislation to remove the exception for rape. Are you ready to sign a bill like that?\par \par \uc2 REEVES: I don't believe that an exception for rape will actually make it through the Mississippi legislature and make it to my desk. Again, there's a lot of effort, particularly in Washington and other places, mainly by the Democrats, to try to talk only about the real, small, minor number of exceptions that may exist.\par \par \uc2 As you know, over 90 percent of all abortions that are done in America -- some 63 million babies lives aborted since Roe was wrongly decided in 1973 -- over 90 percent of those are elective abortions.\par \par \uc2 If the far left really believe what they want you to believe, if they really believe that the American people were with them and work for abortion on demand, then they wouldn't be talking about all of these exceptions and minor numbers. They'd be talking about abortion itself.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: When Roe was overturned, the \uc2 American Medical Association\uc2  released a statement calling the decision an egregious allowance of government intrusion into the medical examination room.\par \par \uc2 Are you concerned Mississippi is intruding on medical decisions women make with their doctors?\par \par \uc2 REEVES: Well, the Roe decision was made by the \uc2 United States Supreme Court\uc2  based upon what our United States Constitution says. They've had several opinions over the last couple of weeks and the left has gone crazy on virtually every one of them.\par \par \uc2 And the only problem with the left has is that they just forget this one small document called the United States Constitution. And that is exactly what the court has upheld, primarily on 6-3 votes over the last couple of weeks.\par \par \uc2 And the reality is, if you read the Constitution, there is no guaranteed right to an abortion in the U.S. Constitution. And therefore, I think the court has gotten this one and the other cases that they ruled upon exactly right.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Is there potential for confusion around the exception to save the life of the mother? Researchers in Texas spoke with clinicians who have been navigating new abortion restrictions there in the last year. They quoted one as saying, quote: people have to be on death's door to qualify for maternal exemptions.\par \par \uc2 Are you concerned confusion could impact pregnant women's health?\par \par \uc2 REEVES: Well, there's always the potential for confusion. But when you think about the exception for the life of the mother, in essence, you have a true medical decision that has to be made by the physician because you have two lives at stake. You have the life at stake that is the unborn baby in the mother's womb, and potentially the life at stake of the mother.\par \par \uc2 And so, that is a decision that should not be made, in my opinion, by politicians, but that's a decision that should be made by doctors and physicians, and trying to determine what is the best way to safely protect both lives, hopefully, but certainly a decision has to be made at a moment's notice. And that's only for a physician to make.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Let's talk of the differences between states now that Roe is no longer in effect. What does enforcement look like in your state? Can a woman from Mississippi simply travel to a different state and have a procedure, or would you try to stop her? REEVES: Well, in our state, the enforcement is done by the state Board of Medical Licensure. We have a state Board of Medical Licensure which actually oversees the practice of medicine in our state, and they ensure that any physician that is practicing, whether it's through telemedicine or otherwise, that any position that practices in our state is practicing not only based upon the standards of care that we require in our state, but also based upon state law.\par \par \uc2 And so, if a physician is attempting to practice medicine in the state of Mississippi and they're violating our law, then our State Board of Medical Licensure will pull the license from them.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Should doctors in other states be concerned about prosecution?\par \par \uc2 REEVES: Well, certainly, I think that doctors in other states should be concerned about all kinds of things, but I'm hopeful that in creating a culture of life in our state, by investing in pregnancy resource centers, the things that we are doing now to give the resources to mothers and babies that they need, which I think is incredibly important, that we're going to convince those moms that, yes, those lives are precious and there are people in this world and there are people in the state of Mississippi who love not only the moms, but also the babies.\par \par \uc2 And we're looking to do everything we can to help create a forever home for every single baby in Mississippi, and we believe we'll save lives by doing so.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: What's to stop abortion medication from being sent into Mississippi via the mail?\par \par \uc2 REEVES: Well, what's going to stop that obviously is the Board of Medical Licensure because if a physician is practicing medicine in the state of Mississippi, they have to have a license to do so. And if abortion is illegal in our state, which it is, then those medicines will not be allowed and they will not have a license to practice in our state.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Should women in Mississippi be concerned about surveillance of their mail or their phones?\par \par \uc2 REEVES: I have no reason to believe that there will be any surveillance of mail or phones. But I do think that if physicians are practicing medicine in our state without a license, than those physicians should be very concerned about doing so.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: A political question, sir. You've supported President Trump. In 2024, he's talking about potentially running again.\par \par \uc2 Would you support him in 2024, or is there something that you have heard in the January 6th Special Committee that may give you pause or may make you want somebody who can carry out his policies, but with a fresh voice?\par \par \uc2 REEVES: Well, like most Americans, I haven't spent very much time paying any attention to the January 6th hearings. I did have the occasion of spending some time with President Trump. Just in the last couple of weeks, he was in Mississippi. I was pleased to welcome him to our state.\par \par \uc2 There's no doubt in my mind that were it not for his conservative justices -- and by the way, the campaign in 2016 was largely run on who had the ability to appoint a Supreme Court justices -- justices. And so, therefore, I believe that President Trump's legacy is going to be creating a Supreme Court that focuses on the Constitution.\par \par \uc2 Now, he's going to have to decide whether or not he's going to run for president. And that's a decision that only he can make. And then we'll decide at that point. But he certainly did a fantastic job as president. And the fact of the matter is, when you talk to Americans and people like - - in small towns across Mississippi, the things like inflation are what people are worried about. They're not worried about this trial that's being had by only one side in Washington, D.C., they're focused on the issues that matter to them and their pocketbooks. And \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's inflation is really hurting everyday Mississippians.\par \par \uc2 MIKE EMANUEL, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Governor, thank you for talking with us. Always good to see you, sir.\par \par \uc2 REEVES: Mike, thanks for having me on.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Up next, we'll bring in our Sunday group on stunning comments from a former Trump White House staffer this week about what happened on January 6th and what to expect as the committee investigating the riot drops a new subpoena.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DAVID SPUNT, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voice over): She does not refute. A Secret Service spokesman told Fox News the committee did not reach out to the agency in the days leading up to Hutchinson's testimony, but lawmakers on the committee are defending her credibility.\par \par \uc2 REP. JAMIE RASKIN (D-MD): Her testimony represented the cumulative information that the committee had received. But we were simply of the mind that the public needed to know about all of this.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: Hutchinson's attorney says she stands by her testimony. The committee is in discussions with the Secret Service to allow Ornato and Engel to testify. The committee also subpoenaed former White House Counsel \uc2 Pat Cipollone\uc2 , though it's not clear if he'll play ball.\par \par \uc2 Mike.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: David Spunt reporting from Washington.\par \par \uc2 David, many thanks.\par \par \uc2 And it's time now for our Sunday group.\par \par \uc2 Howie Kurtz, host of "MediaBuzz," Fox News political analyst Juan Willis, "\uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " columnist Marc Thiessen, and Mollie Hemingway, the editor in chief of "The Federalist." Welcome, all of you. Happy Independence Day weekend.\par \par \uc2 JUAN WILLIAMS, FOX NEWS POLITICAL ANALYST: Yes.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: All right, Howie, so the select committee has subpoenaed \uc2 Pat Cipollone\uc2 , former White House Counsel. What are your thoughts on what we heard this week from the White House aide, the former White House aide, Ms. Hutchinson, and what's the impact?\par \par \uc2 HOWARD KURTZ, HOST, "MEDIABUZZ": Well, Cassidy Hutchinson testified under oath about what she'd been told. And some of her ex-colleagues, I think, should also come and testify under oath if they want to challenge that. It could undermine her, rather than us relying on spokesmen and leaks. But, look, here's how we know her testimony broke through. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is calling her a wacko and a leaker and a liar.\par \par \uc2 Secondly, some conservative commentators, who have been dismissing the whole committee as a clown show, attacking her credibility and defending the former president. But there's a split on the right. \uc2 Mick Mulvaney\uc2 , Trump's guy, former acting White House chief of staff, saying he believes Hutchinson.\par \par \uc2 But, finally, new reporting now about whether or not -- we know, based on this reporting, that Trump groups pay for her previous lawyer, which is interesting, but also that she says she got a phone call from an intermediary from her former boss, \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2 , saying \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is thinking about you. He knows you'll be loyal. That has raised questions that could boomerang about whether or not witnesses were being tampered with.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: OK, Mollie, here's how "The Wall Street Journal" editorial board responded to this week's hearing. Quote, the committee is steeped in partisanship. But that doesn't mean Republicans should look away from the considerable evidence it is producing about Mr. Trump's behavior. That would surely be irrelevant to voters if he runs in 2024.\par \par \uc2 Is this moving the needle with GOP voters?\par \par \uc2 MOLLIE HEMINGWAY, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: It seems to be moving the needle in exactly the opposite way than Democrats had hoped for. Polls are coming out showing that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's popularity is increasing, that his -- when he's up against \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  his numbers are even better than they were before. And that's not surprising.\par \par \uc2 I mean this is not a legitimate committee. This is the first time in the history of Congress that you've only had members appointed by one party. That's because \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  kicked off the top Republicans on the committee and only - the only people on that committee are appointed by her. So they're serving her purposes. And so you're not getting a balance of information.\par \par \uc2 There were plenty of people who testified, who contradict what this aide had said. Their testimony is not being shown or released in any way. You're not having cross examinations. And I think Americans generally don't like to see things like star chambers. It feels very Stalinist. And that's what the Democrats are doing and so it's not working out very well for them.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: All right, Juan, let's get you into the mix on January 6th.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Well, it seems to me pretty overwhelming the evidence. This evidence is coming from Republicans, Mike. These are Republicans who are testifying under oath.\par \par \uc2 In the case of Ms. Hutchinson, she was a loyal Trump worker and very much a person who was, you know, not only hired by Mr. Meadows, but, you know, a friend of Mr. Meadows. And so she is simply telling you what took place.\par \par \uc2 And with regard to the January 6th panel, let's not forget, I think President Trump now has indicated he's disappointed that Kevin McCarthy, the Republican leader in the House, did not choose to participate. And it was Kevin McCarthy's choice, once \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  said no to two very provocative, high-energy type Trumpians -\par \par \uc2 KURTZ: It's not her choice.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: It was. She had a choice, and then he had a choice to say, let me put other people on the panel. He, instead, withdrew. That was his choice.\par \par \uc2 MARC THIESSEN, RESIDENT FELLOW AT THE AMERICAN ENTERPRISE INSTITUTE AND "WASHINGTON POST" COLUMNIST: The fact that you had Cassidy Hutchinson giving very compelling testimony and very important testimony, but there was no cross-examination of he from somebody with a different point of view. The -- most Americans look at that and say, that's unfair and that's illegitimate. And so that's the problem they have. It's not the speaker's job to choose who the Republicans are going to be on a committee.\par \par \uc2 The other problem that they have is, this is all captivating to all of us in the Washington bubble. Out in the American heartland, what the Democrats are sending a message to the American people, this is the most important issue facing the country. And a lot of Americans who are having to choose between gas and food are saying, that's not the most important issue to me. What's important - where are the - where are the hearings on the worst - worst inflation in 40 years. The highest gas prices on record. The worst border crisis in American history. The worst crime wave since the 1990s? All the things I'm experiencing every day.\par \par \uc2 So, "\uc2 The New York Times\uc2 " and my newspaper, "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 ," love this because this is - this is feeding their echo chamber. But out in the heartland, this is not what most people are voting on or what they care about.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: But, Marc - but, Marc, I think that that comes across to me as attempting to distract people from the idea that there was a group of people led by the president of the United States -\par \par \uc2 THIESSEN: Not at all.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Who tried to undermine the \uc2 United States government\uc2  -\par \par \uc2 THIESSEN: Not at all.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Led an insurrection.\par \par \uc2 THIESSEN: (INAUDIBLE).\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Nobody is rebutting her testimony.\par \par \uc2 THIESSEN: We had - we had -\par \par \uc2 HEMINGWAY: Well, here's (INAUDIBLE) -\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: That he knew there were people with weapons --\par \par \uc2 THIESSEN: We had bipartisan hearings.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: And he wanted to go up to the Capitol -\par \par \uc2 THIESSEN: We had -\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: And lead those people to stop the certification of an election.\par \par \uc2 THIESSEN: We had bipartisan hearings. We -- I don't disagree with you on that. I'm saying -- what I'm saying is, we had bipartisan hearings and a bipartisan report in the Senate. We had an impeachment trial on this. And now we're having us. And the effort is obviously political. It's to try to re-toxify Trump going into the elections because they're afraid that he's going to run again and that he could win.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: I don't think so. I think Senator McConnell stopped any investigation in the Senate.\par \par \uc2 THIESSEN: And I'm just -- I think it just back - I think it back - back -- there's a backlash against Democrats because it says to the American people, you're not focused on what I care about.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Mollie, you had a comment?\par \par \uc2 HEMINGWAY: Well, just -- people were actually very concerned about political violence throughout 2020 when their cities were being burned down, when the White House was attacked, when federal courthouses were being attacked for months at a time, when police precincts were burned to the ground, and nobody cared. Nobody did investigations when the American people were being harmed. And you have one thing that has been condemned by people across the political spectrum, and it has become the only thing Washington, D.C., cares about, which really emphasizes for the American people that Washington, D.C., doesn't care about them, they just care about themselves.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: OK. So, to the Supreme Court ruling on Roe v. Wade this week. We, again, or previous week, we again see calls for Democrats to end the filibuster in the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2 , Howie, but they don't have the votes.\par \par \uc2 KURTZ: Well, it's obvious they don't have the votes, and yet President Biden went on television and said, I am calling to suspend a filibuster. First time he's ever done that. And then, you know, the press had to say, sorry, Mr. President, you don't have the votes because \uc2 Kyrsten Sinema\uc2  and \uc2 Joe Manchin\uc2  are not in favor of even a one-time suspension for the filibuster. So it makes him look weak and ineffective to do that.\par \par \uc2 Beyond that, you know, there's a lot of growing complaints from Democrats about how Biden needs to take bold action and just stop making speeches about this, and yet he hasn't. And this, I think, is hurting him. It's reviving talk. I know we'll talk in the next segment about the midterms.\par \par \uc2 But at the same time, when you have all these state-by-state battles, you talked to the governor of Mississippi about this, you have some Democratic prosecutors saying they're not going to enforce the law in their red states. I don't see how they can possibly get away with that. You have some pro-life group saying, you know, we're going to go -- try to have legislation for citizens to sue anybody who tries to go to a state where it's legal.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Right.\par \par \uc2 KURTZ: And, finally, you have \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  saying, let's have a nationwide ban, but you have Kevin McCarthy let's -- saying have a ban after 15 weeks. Some Republicans are nervous about the impact of a total ban.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Mollie, the pro-life movement has been going after -- or wanting to go after Roe v. Wade for decades. Now it's actually here in terms of getting the Supreme Court to overturn that ruling.\par \par \uc2 What's the impact for pro-life women of every age?\par \par \uc2 HEMINGWAY: Well, it affects everybody in a beautiful way. The pro-life movement has worked for 50 years to get rid of this really poorly decided assertion from the Supreme Court. And it has shown the success of the conservative judicial movement, of churches, of people working for so long.\par \par \uc2 But I want to also point out, this is -- what it has done is just allowed people to debate this at the state level. And you look at what's happening internationally. Something like protecting unborn human life after 15 weeks is very standard internationally. That's with the Dobbs decision allows states to do. It allows us to be more like the rest of the world in how we protect unborn human life, at least at some point.\par \par \uc2 And I would say that Democrats actually have a very radical position to defend. Their position is, no restriction on abortion for any reason at any time in pregnancy. If you want to end the human life because your child is a girl, they think that should be legal. That is a very difficult, political decision to defend now that it's being returned to the states and people can have a more moderate viewpoint.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Juan, the president talked about changing the filibuster. Doesn't appear to have the votes. Is he too reactive to the most progressive voices in the Democratic Party?\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: No, I think he's reactive to the fact that if you just want to talk politics and not morals, I think that, you know, more than 60 percent of the American people think Roe should have been left in place, and it was in place under various political alignments on the court for almost 50 years. So now it's a question of, wait a second, how do we simply reestablish what was a norm for the American people and for American women so that their daughters don't have less rights than their mothers and their grandmothers.\par \par \uc2 I will say that with regard to the Senate and trying to get a waiver in terms of the filibuster, Sinema and Manchin both say, well, if we do that, what if the Republicans gain the majority, then they could, in fact, put in place a total ban on abortion. But I think they are miscalculating. I think \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  will do that anyway. And now the dynamic has shifted in terms of what Mollie was just talking about, the energy was on the people who were opposed to abortion, working against abortion rights for more than 50 years. Now the energy is taking Democrats and suburban women who want abortion rights to the polls come November.\par \par \uc2 THIESSEN: McConnell didn't do it when \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was president. What makes you say that he would do it under a Republican president?\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: He stopped Merrick garland.\par \par \uc2 THIESSEN: No, but he didn't do it. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  asked him to do - to get rid of the filibuster. McConnell didn't do it because he said, we might be in the minority one day. This - that - that's the -- this is the problem.\par \par \uc2 The Democrats - look, the Democrats are going to lose the -- one house of Congress. We're going to talk about the midterms in a minute. But in 2024, they're defending five times as many seats as Republicans are. So even if we don't take back the Senate, Republicans don't take back the Senate this year, they're going to probably take it back in 2024, then you could have a Republican president, a Republican House and a Republican Senate, you don't want to filibuster-free Senate, Juan.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Well, I -\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: All right, gentlemen, we have to leave it there. These guys are getting into it pretty well.\par \par \uc2 Thanks, panel.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: The road show.\par \par \uc2 THIESSEN: It's good to be in person.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: We'll take a break here.\par \par \uc2 Up next, how reaction to the Supreme Court, the president's poll numbers, and Democrats' enthusiasm or shaping critical midterm races.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DANA BASH, CNN CHIEF POLITICAL CORRESPONDENT: Have you talked to President Biden about re-election?\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is running for re-election, and I will be his ticket mate.\par \par \uc2 BASH: Full stop?\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: Full stop.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  confirming against that she intends to join President Biden on the 2024 ticket.\par \par \uc2 And we're back now with the panel.\par \par \uc2 So, Howie, there's a pretty lengthy "New York Times" piece this week about will he or won't he run. Seems to frustrate President Biden a great deal.\par \par \uc2 Is there anything he can do to tamp down these stories?\par \par \uc2 KURTZ: Probably not. I mean he does want to run. And even if he had private doubts, he wouldn't acknowledge that because then you become a lame duck. But there is growing calls from Democrats, this is not the opposition party, saying he maybe shouldn't run.\par \par \uc2 And this ties into the abortion decision, I think, because, by the left- wing of his party, the president is seen as ineffective, not just on abortion rights, but he always seems to be playing catch up, whether it's baby formula or at the border, hasn't been able to deliver on voting rights or police reform. All of these issues. And so it's a hard position for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . You would think he would at least have the support of his own party. But, you know, the age question and the fitness question are now being openly debated in part thanks to these media reports.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Marc, we've showed the sound byte from the vice president.\par \par \uc2 THIESSEN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Then there was a statement kind of clarifying. Is that for campaign finance reasons or is that to give the president a little wiggle room? How do you see it?\par \par \uc2 THIESSEN: And, generally speaking, the president of the United States announces his re-election, not the vice president doing it for him. So that was a little bit of a slip up.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Right.\par \par \uc2 THIESSEN: But, look, you know, Biden - you pointed out that "\uc2 New York Times\uc2 " story that Biden is irked that people are questioning this. What does he expect? He is the least popular president in the history of presidential polling going back to \uc2 Harry Truman\uc2 . No president has been this unpopular at 500 days into the presidency from Truman to Trump than him. And 17 percent of Americans say they're better off now than they were four years ago. Sixty-four percent of Americans say they don't want him to run for re-election. He's delivered, as I said, the worst inflation in 40 years, highest gas prices, worst crime wave since the 1990s, worst border crisis in American history, baby formula shortage, this summer we're going to have blackouts because of the war on fossil fuel. "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " reported that the Midwest, which has had stable electricity for decades, is going to have blackouts because they've pushed the coal industry out of business and there's no fossil - there's no renewables to replace it. What - what kind of a record is that to run on?\par \par \uc2 So, of course - of course people are talking about it. And, you know, you want a - you want people to stop talking about it, change your policies.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: I see Juan getting ready.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: No, I listen and I think chicken little's in the room. I mean with the - the sky is falling, run from America. I love America. Let me just say this Fourth of July weekend, I'm glad to be here.\par \par \uc2 THIESSEN: I love America too.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: And I think I'm really glad that we had a president who was able to deal with a, you know, once-in-a-lifetime pandemic. I think that's great that we are where we are right now, that we can have Fourth of July celebrations.\par \par \uc2 THIESSEN: (INAUDIBLE) the inflation with that (INAUDIBLE) dealing of the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Let me just say, we put a -- kept a lot of people from going under financially, especially working-class Americans, thanks to Covid relief programs.\par \par \uc2 Secondly, we're stopping Russia from running over Ukraine -\par \par \uc2 THIESSEN: I agree with that.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Which is a clear signal to the Chinese, who are our enemies. And I think, again, it's important. We just heard earlier, Mike ran Biden saying, this is a long-term effort. This is not something you can just quit on.\par \par \uc2 And don't forget, when you have an extreme Supreme Court, when you have these things going on, the American people are discontent. A lot of people say the country is going in the wrong direction, but people understand at Biden's working at it. He may not have success. Howie said it, you know, a lot of people on the Democratic side are frustrated that he's not more aggressive. But this is not just a referendum about him, it's a referendum about a president who was trying to overthrow the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  and Democrats will remind them.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: So - OK.\par \par \uc2 THIESSEN: So the American people are wrong and you're right.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: No, I'm just saying.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Let's get back - let's get this side of the table involve.\par \par \uc2 How about a midterm question for Mollie.\par \par \uc2 Abigail Spanberger\uc2  of Virginia has said that it may be time for new leadership in the Democratic Party. She says President Biden will not be campaigning her -- with her this fall.\par \par \uc2 Do you expect other Democrats in tough races to follow suit?\par \par \uc2 HEMINGWAY: Well, with how toxic \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is, it will be unsurprising to see a lot of people hoping that he does not help with their campaign.\par \par \uc2 But I think that Democrats, and actually the entire political establishment, are misdiagnosing the level of problems here. It is true that Biden is very unpopular, but what's more true is that the entire Democrat policy agenda is unpopular. This is something where the ideas he has are not contested in the House or the Senate. He almost has nobody disagreeing with him. The energy policy he's put forth, his foreign policy that he's put forth, these are things that the entire Democratic Party has supported, and they're very unpopular.\par \par \uc2 So, people knew when they voted for Biden last time that there were rumors of his cognitive decline at that point. The fact that he's doing poorly now is not that surprising. But if the people who were running things actually were more competent and more moderate, you wouldn't be seeing this chatter.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Howie, how are both sides framing the stakes for these midterms right now?\par \par \uc2 KURTZ: Well, you know, President Biden's strongest issue, I think, is his leadership of the western alliance on Ukraine. Unfortunately for him, that is not a big voting issue. Foreign policy was at 1 percent in a recent Fox poll. On inflation, people really do care about, you know, we've gone through several iterations of its transitory, it's going to get better, are we heading for a recession.\par \par \uc2 So, you know, the Democratic president is trying to say, times are tough, part of