oliday and a great week and we'll see you next FOX NEWS SUNDAY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FOXN000020220703ei730002t\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Liberal Media Shamelessly Misleads Americans; Shameless Liberal Media Misreports \uc2 EPA\uc2  Ruling; American People Are Fed Up With The Media's Lies; Dems Plot To Turn Federal Lands Into Abortion Hubs; Biden's Abortion Evolution; Biden Trashes Supreme Court As "Extremist"; Hochul Signs Strick Gun Control Bill; Dems Want Abortion Facilities On Military Bases; Representatives \uc2 Jeff Van Drew\uc2  (R-NJ) And \uc2 Claudia Tenney\uc2  (R-NY) Interviewed On Democratic Policies And Whether Democratic Candidate Want President Biden Campaigning For Them; \uc2 Cornell University\uc2  Reportedly Removes Bust Of \uc2 Abraham Lincoln\uc2  And Plaque Of Gettysburg Address From Library After Complaint From One Person; "Sesame Street" Features Character Getting Vaccinated For COVID-19; FOX News Personality To Judge Nathan's Hotdog Eating Contest\b0\par\par\uc2 Tammy Bruce, Brian Kilmeade, Jason Chaffetz \par \uc2 7,897 \uc2 words\par \uc2 1 July 2022\par \uc2 Fox News: The Ingraham Angle\par \uc2 INGANG\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 TAMMY BRUCE, FOX NEWS HOST: Read my column at amac.us and join me at tammybruce.local.com, and have a fabulous Fourth of July weekend in the greatest country on Earth.\par \par \uc2 Thank you, everyone, and I'll see you next time.\par \par \uc2 BRIAN KILMEADE, FOX NEWS HOST: Hi, everyone. I'm Brian Kilmeade. In for Laura Ingraham and this is a special edition - a Friday edition of THE INGRAHAM ANGLE. Let's get started.\par \par \uc2 You know, trust in our institutions is faltering across the board. Think about it. According to the Morning Consult tracker, trust in Congress is at 33 percent. That's down 10 percent from just a year ago. The Supreme Court was at 46 percent, down from 61 percent, a year ago. The news media, I'm kind of in it, is at 38 percent. And so it says the most polarizing U.S. institution. Great.\par \par \uc2 Frankly, it's not really surprising. Last year, for example, trust in media hit an all-time low, and it just can't rebound, it seems. This last week was - has clarified why. With lie after lie, especially when it comes to the Supreme Court. You wanted to get example, here we go.\par \par \uc2 "\uc2 New York Times\uc2  " Carl Hulse writes this about that big political Roe v. Wade ruling. "Senate Republicans did not have to take the politically risky step of banning abortions. The courts took care of that for them." Really? You would think someone who has been reporting for more than 30 years could easily have got this right. But he didn't.\par \par \uc2 What the Supreme Court actually did, as you know, was kick the decision back to the states. They did not ban abortion at all. Yesterday after the court rejected the case from health care workers, challenging New York's vaccine mandate, the media went after, who else but \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  ,\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ANDREA MITCHELL, MSNBC HOST: As long as you're fact checking now, do you mind factchecking the Supreme Court Justice \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  , who also wrote that the COVID vaccine is made from aborted fetuses.\par \par \uc2 KAVITA PATEL, MSNBC MEDICAL CONTRIBUTOR: Let me just be very clear. There were no aborted fetus cells used in any of our vaccines. His words carry such weight and I think that's the point.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KILMEADE: Let's be clear, that's not what he said. That's not what he said at all. In his dissenting opinion, he said this. Justice Thomas included a description of the healthcare workers' belief that some COVID vaccines were developed using aborted fetal cell lines, somewhat different.\par \par \uc2 Now, I also want to point out this nugget from Axios. "Some vaccines have used fetal cell lines during the early stages of the vaccine development." Got it? So let's move on. There's also the \uc2 EPA\uc2  ruling, which sent the media and the Left into a complete tailspin.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: The emissions from these power plants, we know, are warming the planet, they're causing climate change. But today, the Supreme Court curbs one of \uc2 EPA\uc2  's most effective tools to drastically curbing these emissions.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KILMEADE: All right, I guess it's all - I guess, I'll clear this up for you. The Supreme Court didn't curb \uc2 EPA\uc2  's power on climate. Congress didn't give them the power in the first place. The Supreme Court merely affirmed that fact this has been going on for years, by the way, it's no wonder trust in the media is down, down, down.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now somebody who's up Jason Chaffetz, former House Oversight Committee Chair and Fox News Contributor. He felt the frustration from the media, and now he's a member of it. And also with me, Kurt Schlichter, Senior Columnist at Townhall, and the author of the new book, "We'll Be Back: The Fall and Rise of America."\par \par \uc2 Jason, what do you have to say, where do we start here when you talk about trust in our institutions?\par \par \uc2 JASON CHAFFETZ, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Well, first of all, I trust you, Brian. So, yes, that's - there's one. But the other ones, come on, it's the probably the biggest disappointment. I sat in Congress for eight and a half years, I was chairman of the Oversight Committee, and the way they treated Democrats versus Republicans. I like what my former colleague \uc2 Trey Gowdy\uc2  had to say in this. He said, they never saw an investigation that they thought the Republicans could justify, and they never saw an investigation by the Democrats they ever thought should end.\par \par \uc2 All we want them to do is report the facts. And supposedly the "\uc2 New York Times\uc2  " and "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  " and all these big names that we grew up with, they can't seem to get the basic facts, right. That's the problem.\par \par \uc2 KILMEADE: Kurt, looking where we're at right now, what worries me in the big picture is if you change the rules. If you decide that in football, you don't need 10 yards for first down, you just need eight. And they just start playing with eight yards as a first down. When you start looking at the Supreme Court instead of a coequal arm of justice of the government, you start criticizing it if it's beneath you, and you're doing it overseas. I worry about the big picture because the average person walks around and thinks the country is falling apart.\par \par \uc2 KURT SCHLICHTER, TOWNHALL.COM SENIOR COLUMNIST: Well, it is falling apart. Everything from airplanes, they can't get to where you need to go, to baby formula you can't find in the stores. And we got this alleged President of our staggering around overseas, criticizing our institutions we don't need that.\par \par \uc2 But, look, changing the rules is a bad idea. And you could tell it's a