D fear might have helped you. Being forced to stay home has allowed you to save with a passbook savings account. Wow! Talk about being completely out of touch with the American people. How could the Biden administration think for a second that this messaging could be effective, could work, could resonate?\par \par \uc2 Take a look at these headlines over the last few days. "Stock slump, closing out the worst quarter since 2020." \uc2 Bank of America\uc2  says, "Brace for recession shock after the worst rout in 52 years." Guggenheim warns, "U.S. economy likely entering a recession during the second quarter. And then there's the warning from former Hardee's CEO, \uc2 Andy Puzder\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 He says, "if we were to evaluate today's inflation numbers in the same way that we calculated inflation in the 1980s, we'd already be squarely in \uc2 Jimmy Carter\uc2  territory." Andy joins me right now. He's also a visiting fellow with \uc2 the Heritage Foundation\uc2  . Andy, how many of us remember what happened during the Carter years? And more important, what do you mean we calculate differently now?\par \par \uc2 ANDY PUZDER, HERITAGE FOUNDATION VISITING FELLOW: Well, back in 1983, the \uc2 Bureau of Labor Statistics\uc2  changed the way that he calculated inflation. It took housing costs out of inflation, and substituted an analysis of what rent is to account for the increase in housing.\par \par \uc2 So \uc2 Larry Summers\uc2  , who was the Secretary of the Treasury under Clinton and the Head of the Council of Economic Advisers under President Obama coauthored a report that came out last week that said, Look, if we were if we were computing this the same way we computed it back in 1983, inflation would already be where it was during the Carter Administration. And as you know, that led to one of the most severe recessions that we've had in recent memory and interest rates are up around 20 percent.\par \par \uc2 So we are - the economy in real trouble here. We are almost definitely within a technical recession right now. I think the American people have been intuiting that we've been in a recession for months. The Biden Administration may not have figured it out, but the American people have. And we're soon going to find out that we are not technically in a recession and will be for quite a while.\par \par \uc2 KILMEADE: And that will - which means two consecutive cycles of negative growth. But Andy, let me ask you this. How do we get out of a recession, without - and avoid inflation, and get - bring inflation down without raising interest rates to equal where inflation is. So we're at five point something percent right now on interest rates.\par \par \uc2 If - right now inflation is at 8.3 percent. They say if you want to beat it, meet it. So do we have to look brace ourselves for 8, 10, 12 percent interest rates?\par \par \uc2 PUZDER: Well, number one, we're going to have to brace ourselves for interest rates much higher than that. It took Paul Volcker during the Reagan Administration to beat inflation the last time it was decided to take interest rates up to 20 percent. We're now at 1.5 percent. So even to get halfway there, we've still got a lot of pain to go through.\par \par \uc2 If we want to avoid some of the interest rate damage, the only thing you really can do is increase the supply of goods out there, we can increase the supply of goods and services, because inflation is a mismatch of demand and supply. Right now demand is high, supply is low. And you could increase supply by cutting regulations, by reducing taxes, by focusing on domestic energy production.\par \par \uc2 President Biden could go to the steps of the White House and say we are back in the oil business, we're going to sign leases, we're going to grant permits, we're going to build pipelines, we're going to get banks to finance drilling, and the price of oil would drop. Because oil is based not only on actual supply, but the price of it is also based on anticipated supply. If the world anticipates there's going to be a lot of oil, the price will come down.\par \par \uc2 Biden couldn't do these things in reality. He just can't do them politically, because of the Far Left of his party absolutely would go insane. He's only got 38 percent now in the RealClearPolitics average of the polls. He could lose that 38 percent if he does the things that could genuinely help the American people.\par \par \uc2 Did you hear Brian this today, the National Economic Council - the Head of the National Economic Council--\par \par \uc2 KILMEADE: Yes. We'll get to that--\par \par \uc2 PUZDER: --Biden's - (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 KILMEADE: Yes, we're going to get--\par \par \uc2 PUZDER: --(CROSSTALK) when he was faced with fact that, oil prices are way up, gas prices are up. They asked him, why is that? What do you say? He goes, well, we got to defend the liberal world order. It's ridiculous.\par \par \uc2 KILMEADE: I know. Andy final thought on this. There's a return policy that \uc2 Walmart\uc2  , Target and a few other major companies have. They are so backlogged, so overwhelmed that they say if you want to return something, we'll pay you the money back. Just don't give us the product back. How desperate is that? I mean, it's so easy for abuse. What has to happen - what's happening to our economy, but that's actually the best option.\par \par \uc2 PUZDER: Well, what's happening to our economy is that money is increasingly becoming worth less. So that your people are very concerned about these savings accounts that we we're hearing about in that intro that you gave.\par \par \uc2 Well, people - they're seeing their savings accounts are melting like ice cubes. The money that they're earning, their wages, while they may be getting increases in wages, the wages are worth less, because inflation is exceeding wage increases. So you're in a situation where people are feeling like maybe they do need to return goods, maybe they don't need the things they've been buying. They really need to protect themselves against these Biden policies. They've really - they've wrecked this economy.\par \par \uc2 KILMEADE: Right. And he knows better. He's choosing not to do it. He cares more about his party than he does the country. Andy, thanks.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VICTOR BLACKWELL, CNN ANCHOR: What do you say to those families who say, listen, we can't afford to pay for 85 a gallon for months, if not years. This is just not sustainable?\par \par \uc2 BRIAN DEESE\uc2 , DIRECTOR OF THE NATIONAL ECONOMIC COUNCIL: Well, what you heard from the President today was a clear articulation of the stakes. This is about the future of the liberal world order and we have to stand firm.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KILMEADE: What the hell is he talking about? The White House is practically writing midterm ads for the Republicans at this point. That was President Biden's Economic Adviser. His name is \uc2 Brian Deese\uc2 . Dismissing real world problems like not be able to afford gas, to get to work, as we sacrifice and middle America - and the middle class have to live paycheck to paycheck where the paycheck doesn't pay your bills because of the liberal world order.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now a man that probably understands this better than most, a former Democrat turned Republican New Jersey's \uc2 Jeff Van Drew\uc2  and New York Congresswoman \uc2 Claudia Tenney\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 First to you Jeff, what is he talking about, liberal world order?\par \par \uc2 REP. JEFF VAN DREW (R-NJ): What the hell is he talking about is a good question. And I will tell you that he is telling the truth from his perspective, this is what these people want, this is what the liberal majority wants actually in Congress. It's what the liberal majority wants in every aspect, the billionaires that are around the world, the woke and the crazy celebrities that are out there. What do they want to do, real quick? They want to make sure that we don't have a supply chain. They want to make sure that we don't have law and order. They want to make sure that our borders are open and that we're just letting everybody come in. They want to make sure that anybody, anytime, everywhere can vote if they want to.\par \par \uc2 They are changing America into the part of the global new world and this global order. And I know we had some people on before, Brian, that were very positive and saying things are going to be good. And they are right in a way, but they're only going to be good if we stand up, if we fight, if we believe in what we say. We have a real responsibility as the Republican Party now. We have to move forward, and we have to change so many things that are happening that are so destructive. So when we hear that from them, they are saying what they really believe.\par \par \uc2 KILMEADE: Which is sad. \uc2 Claudia Tenney\uc2  , I want you to hear this, this soundbite. And I think it will tell you a lot, because this is a so-called moderate Democrat, even though she votes with \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  all the time, who is desperate to hold onto her seat. Listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. ABIGAIL SPANBERGER, (D-VA): I intend to do the campaigning myself. I am the candidate. It's my name on the ballot. Certainly when I first ran, I ran in an R plus seven district and I won in that seat by getting out, speaking to voters.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KILMEADE: So she was asked, do you want \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  to come campaign for you? After all, you're in Virginia. It's a short ride. just grab the beast, go in there, and have some fun, raise some money. She says no thanks.\par \par \uc2 REP. CLAUDIA TENNEY, (R-NY): Exactly. No Democrat wants \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  campaign for them. \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2  dodged the question. He is running for Senate in Ohio. Meanwhile, Republicans are clamoring to get President Trump's endorsement, which mostly gets you in the lead or at least puts you ahead.\par \par \uc2 But I love what Dr. Van Drew said. He's talking about this liberal world order. They're talking about elitism and arrogance and their need and obsession with power and control. The Supreme Court last week, in the last couple of weeks, have delivering freedom and individual rights to the American people. And what's the reaction? To double down on taking those rights away again.\par \par \uc2 Look at New York. It is a perfect incubator of exactly what not to do. \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2  is literally the embodiment of power control, obsessive power, and abusive power. And she looks just like \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  in the way that she governs. Today, taking away are gun rights after they were finally given back to us, a civil rights type of case, and she takes them away. Energy, they're trying to shut down our energy, the crime problem, everything that you look to in New York, the Democrats have got power and they are abusing it and they hurting Americans, and they don't care.\par \par \uc2 KILMEADE: Right. And you had so much to do with the Second Amendment actually be returned to states like New York. It must kill you to see these restrictions put in. Maybe the courts will push back.\par \par \uc2 And Jeff, finally with you, \uc2 Henry Cuellar\uc2  came out and fought back a primary challenge from his left. He is a pro-life Democrat who is strong on the border. You are a moderate Democrat, and they basically almost tried to kick you out until you went Republican. When \uc2 Henry Cuellar\uc2  let me be me, what is your reaction? He is saying it to the left.\par \par \uc2 VAN DREW: They should let him be him. They're not going to. They're going to make it harder and harder on him. There's a real difference. The Democratic Party today, the Democratic Party that you saw out there, and the congresswoman said it well. They are arrogant, and they want to change the world order. They don't want us to be the America of strength, the America, the shining city on the hill. We are in a real challenge, and we better be ready for it and we better be ready to stand up and bring our country home.\par \par \uc2 KILMEADE: Right. Right now, it looks like the House is going to be in Republican hands. Let's see about the Senate. But there's a lot more game, there's a lot more time left on the clock. Thanks to both of you, appreciate it. Have a great Fourth of July weekend.\par \par \uc2 VAN DREW: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 KILMEADE: We move ahead. Democrats in Arizona have found a pretty despicable way to celebrate July 4th. We will reveal it but not revel in it. Stay here.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 KILMEADE: Welcome back, happy Friday night. Now it's time for the top cultural stories of the week. Joining me now is Amala Ekpunobi, a Prager University personality and former leftist activist. That's how we describe here. Amala, we have a lot to get to tonight, so let's jump into it. Are you ready?\par \par \uc2 AMALA EKPUNOBI, PRAGERU PERSONALITY: I'm ready.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 KILMEADE: OK, let's go. The Pima County Democrats in Arizona are celebrating Independence Day at a "F the Fourth" event. The group deleted their tweet promoting the event after FOX News Digital requested a statement on it. Amala, it's no surprise Democrats would support an event like this when they hear this kind of stuff in the media. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REV. AL SHARPTON, MSNBC HOST: It is a very challenging Fourth of July, and really many of us are saying rather than use our cookouts to celebrate, we need to use our cookouts to organize. Now this country is moving back toward a rulership where democracy means nothing.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KILMEADE: Amala, your reaction?\par \par \uc2 EKPUNOBI: It's unbelievable to hear, but it's really not when you look at the ideology that is running behind some of these ideas. If you 