're not done yet. But --\par \par \uc2 BRUCE: Good.\par \par \uc2 PAXTON: -- if this stays in place the way it is, it's going to be devastating to my state, it's going to be devastating to the country. It's going to hurt the administration's reputation because effectively, what the Supreme Court has given the Biden administration is a loophole to basically have no immigration policies, just open borders the cartels have every incentive now to keep bringing more and more people.\par \par \uc2 BRUCE: So in other words, obviously, this is -- the Supreme Court mentioned diplomacy with Mexico that can only be done by the executive branch. That really reminds us that the states are limited to some degree when it comes to what we can do to keep individuals out when it comes to the security of our country, being overwhelmed.\par \par \uc2 Obviously, that's another issue that we don't have the facility to handle, you know, vetting these people when it comes to health issues or anything else. I mean, they're supposed to be the COVID geniuses and yet this border beyond COVID, there's Zika, there's tuberculosis, there's -- the unknown viruses and diseases that are coming over.\par \par \uc2 What -- while fighting this, what are the options that you have? What can you tell us?\par \par \uc2 PAXTON: Look, we've got to continue to fight but we still have immigration lawsuits going. We still have business going. We've got to do what we can at the state level but as you mentioned. Some of these cases like U.S. v. Arizona says that -- I mean, we can't step in and stop the feds from violating federal law from not following federal law.\par \par \uc2 I don't think those cases are right and I think we're going to have to continue to challenge them and hope --\par \par \uc2 BRUCE: Right.\par \par \uc2 PAXTON: -- that we can get some of these overturned because otherwise, we're supposedly have to sit on the border and watch crime occur, watch the cartels work basically in conjunction with