E.\par \par \uc2 Not many people feeling patriotic in \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's America this Fourth of July, and it's really no wonder with an out of touch White House telling Americans suffering to just suck it up.\par \par \uc2 According to a new Fox News poll, just 39 percent of Americans are proud of where the country is today. Which is down 12 points from the 2017, and a whopping 30 points since 2011. It marks the first time a majority feels disappointed in this country. And as Americans feel the heat from raging inflation and astronomical gas prices, the White House is doubling down on their deal with that philosophy.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: How long is it fair to expect American drivers and drivers around the world to pay that premium for this war?\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES OF AMERICA: As long as it takes. Russia cannot, in fact, defeat Ukraine and move beyond Ukraine. This is a critical, critical position for the world.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: What do you say to those families who say listen, we can't afford to pay $4.85 a gallon for months if not years, this is not sustainable?\par \par \uc2 BRIAN DEESE, DIRECTOR, NATIONAL ECONOMIC COUNCIL: This is about the future of the liberal world order and we have to stand firm.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: And if people think we are on the wrong track, the White House thinks you just need to be reminded how awesome things really are.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: Eighty-five percent of U.S. adults say the country is on the wrong track, 85 percent. That's up from 68 percent in March. It appears that increasingly the American people do not approve of what this White House is doing.\par \par \uc2 DEESE: But these are uncertain times and when prices are high, people are understandably frustrated.\par \par \uc2 We have and also remind the American people that even as we go through this challenging period. Even as we move through this transition, we also have made historic economic progress.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: You know, Joey, that guy, Deese, the economic council director, I mean, when he points to the fact that, you know, the fact that the majority of Americans feel disappointed in the country right now, he says, you know, they just are not looking at how great the economy is. This guy is in charge of the economic council for the White House.\par \par \uc2 JOHNNY JOEY JONES, FOX NEWS CO-HOST: You know, if one more person on this network says it's the economy, stupid, I am going to lose it. I hear it all day on every - on every network because that is what Americans are. And I believe that's true when it comes to our politicians but when we talk about being disappointed in this country, I think it's cultural.\par \par \uc2 I think the left looks at our country and they see our prosperity as a symbol of oppression, centuries of oppression. They see the reduction of CRT or the Supreme Court decision on Roe versus Wade and they say we are still under the thumb, it's the midwife's tale or whatever that's called. The right looks at woke culture.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 JESSICA TARLOV, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: The midwife's tale. That's a very different country.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Whatever, I'm sorry. It's not --\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JONES: The right looks at CRT, they look at woke culture and they see the decay of what makes this country great to them. And to an extent, they're kind of think right. Not that I believe that something -- something like what the left talks about is oppression, but there was before the societal expectation of just presenting yourself and speaking respectfully and making a point without having to glue yourself to something, and the right sees the decay of that as losing what us great.\par \par \uc2 So, both sides, especially through our political representatives that play into this, see us in a really bad place.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: But the question, Dagen, don't you think the question is how they feel about the country today, and 85 percent of the people think we are headed in the wrong direction, so it's pretty clear how they feel about the country. Is that different from whether or not they love the country?\par \par \uc2 DAGEN MCDOWELL, FOX NEWS BUSINESS CORRESPONDENT: Well, it does play into it because the country has gone in a horrible direction under Biden incorporated by any and every metric. It's worse than when Biden took office. Biden was supposed to be like a palate cleanser, like a champagne sorbet. Instead, he is like an old crab cake that you paid $5,000 for and it leaves a bad taste in your mouth and you're probably going to get food poisoning from it.\par \par \uc2 To Joey's point, though, the liberals default when asked about being a proud American is not being proud. The default for conservatives historically is you are proud. And this bears out in the numbers, where among Republicans, that being American pride has fallen by 28 points since 2017, and then 21 points among independents, and that is because of the direction of the country.\par \par \uc2 Like, when you say for Republicans, yes, we are out at \uc2 Walmart\uc2 , or the wawa buying a koozie, you know, with proud American on it.\par \par \uc2 JONES: With (Inaudible) on it.\par \par \uc2 MCDOWELL: And that's right, and for liberals, that you know, you ask them, are you proud of the United States? And they go yes, it's fine.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Yes. All right.\par \par \uc2 MCDOWELL: And that's -- so it's still even.\par \par \uc2 JONES: That's what Jessica said in the green room.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Yes. But you know, Jessica, when the president says that the Americans should expect to continue to pay these high prices, high gas prices, I expect the food prices and inflation and everything else until Russia is defeated in Ukraine, what if Russia is never defeated in Ukraine?\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Well, that is increasingly what people who used to be in the military are on air commenting or saying that. I went to a talk recently with Admiral McRaven and he said unless we kind of proverbial you know what or get off the pot, we are just going to be stuck in this place for a very long time and the expectation is that yes, Putin thought originally, I can do this in 30 days.\par \par \uc2 Now that he knows that that's not possible, he can wait it out for the rest of time.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: I mean, he can just stand there and yes, we have made bigger moves, and when we sent a new missile system this week, that should change the game a little bit, but Putin has nothing but time and we don't.\par \par \uc2 The messaging on this has been extremely poor. Everybody understands that we are part of a global economy, that's not a hard concept for folks to understand, but when it seems like you don't have a choice in terms of what you are paying for, that's something that rubs you the wrong way.\par \par \uc2 Because I think if you said, we could scale back a little bit or maybe, you know, put a few troops on the ground or build up in Poland, for instance, and get a dollar off your gas bill, I think the people would say, generally speaking, I want a dollar off my gas bill since they have tethered those things so closely together which I actually don't think is the reason that this is happening.\par \par \uc2 On the proud American front, I think Dagen is right, that the way that liberals and conservatives talk about patriotism is just fundamentally different. I don't think that it means that we actually love the country any differently. This trend did start in 2017, that include three years of the Trump era, this is not just Biden walk into office and suddenly everyone decided that we hated it here.\par \par \uc2 But the way that folks talk about this is important to take into account when evaluating our varying degrees of love for America.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: You know, Greg, one of the lines that really concerns me was when Deese, I think it's Deese again, the economic council guy, says, you know, the future of the liberal world order is at stake. Did we elect this guy to change the future of the liberal world order, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 GREG GUTFELD, FOX NEWS CO-HOST: It's actually, this is a really big deal. But first, I am so tired of hearing you people talk about where is the pride in America. We just had a full month. We just had a full month of pride you guys. Wait, that wasn't about being an American. That was about sexual orientation.\par \par \uc2 You can do that for a month. You better do that for a month. Your company better send e-mails on that for a month but if you talk about patriotism, that's so old-school, old-fashioned. What a scary fast trip from America first to America last. That liberal world order phrase used to be something you might hear somebody on infomercials say. Not -- I'm sorry, Infowars say. But it's actually was said. It was actually said out loud.\par \par \uc2 That one sound bite should be in every commercial for every Republican campaign. You should play that liberal world order blurb to truckers, to parents buying food, to delivery workers because this kind of, it's one of the biggest like, reveals in political history. They are saying yes, you know what, the greater good means -- the greater global good means you're suffering doesn't matter.\par \par \uc2 True, it's very Stalinist. People will die. But you've got to break some eggs to make an omelet. Of course, you never see the omelet, all you see is the broken egg. It's a very honest comment and we are learning that the administration just isn't that into us being Americans.\par \par \uc2 So, what is the Democratic Party offering to people? You are either woke or you are broke, right? Woke destroys the internal organs of the count. Everybody is fighting each other. Then you have the new world order which is the external breakdown, we're fighting a war thousands of miles away, right, and that trumps our gas prices.\par \par \uc2 We are willing to accept, perhaps world famine even and our own local misery for a war that won't end because both sides, according to both sides, are winning and we are fueling this war and billions and billions of dollars, and there is a price for everything, including war, which is why Trump had it right which is you drill, drill, drill, you become energy independent so international turmoil has no effect on you. But you can't have it both ways. You can't want cheap oil and go to war against the people that supply the oil, although it's only 8 or 9 percent.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: It's not a problem for us, it's a European problem.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Yes. But it was, well, Biden clearly says it's a problem for us and --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: I know, but I guess --\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: That's his excuse.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: But Biden gave them the waiver for Nord Steam and while he shut down the American oil and gas.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: So, I mean, it's -- the whole thing is a frightening situation.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Yes. Go ask Texas about the liberal world order.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Yes. You have to understand this is -- that is something -- that is a gift from heaven.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: That they said it.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: That they actually came out and said it. Coming up, travel Armageddon is upon us. Thousands of flights getting delayed and canceled ahead of the fourth.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Yay!\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: I do get stressed sometimes especially when the airport looks like this. It's actually pretty packed right now.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: If we could just get there.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: Our flight on American was delayed because of crew so we are going to miss our connection.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MCDOWELL: Americans are sounding off on the insane delays and cancellations rocking airports everywhere as we head into the July 4th weekend. So far today, more than 440 flights have been canceled, more than 4,500 have been delayed, airlines struggling thanks to a perfect storm of problems.\par \par \uc2 Bad weather, staff shortages, pilots threatening to strike, and soaring fuel costs, but if you're stressed out, have no fear, Transportation Secretary Pete Buttigieg he's on the case.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LESTER HOLT, ANCHOR, NBC: Is the July fourth holiday a recipe for disaster given the issues with the system right now?\par \par \uc2 PETE BUTTIGIEG, U.S. SECRETARY OF TRANSPORTATION: There are going to be challenges but we are watching it closely and we are talking to the airlines every day about their responsibility to make sure that they can accommodate any issues the weather or their curveballs might throw at them. A lot of people, including me, are expecting to get to loved ones over this holiday weekend and we need a system that is resilient enough to get them there, plus, good customer service when an issue does come up.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MCDOWELL: Greg, when this administration uses the word "challenge," you know it means hell fire!\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Yes. You know, I just love, I can't get enough of the airport arrival porn, it's repetitious, cliched clips of people giving these exasperated quotes, you know, at the departing gates and stuff. They should just create stock video every year so we don't have to go to the airports and report on it. Just use the same stuff over and over again. Because like, we --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 JONES: Just go to my \uc2 Twitter\uc2  account.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Yes, that's all it is as people (Inaudible