ctually stands up whenever she gets her hair blown and she is as cute as she walks while her owner blows dryer golden locks. Ellie is a multi-pole, and if you want a dog that cooperates when you bathe, that this is one for you. All right, Ellie, that's it. Look at that. She stands up on her own. She stands up.\par \par \uc2 JONES: I bit she kills squirrels.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: I bit she does too.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Can I make it to talk about my --\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: All right.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: NO.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: And don't forget I want you to hop over to shop.com Fox News -- to shop.fox.news.com for great deals on your favorite news merchandise. Fox News merchandise you receive 50 percent off on select items through Tuesday, July 5. We have coffee mugs, hats, visors, tumblers T-shirts, and everything in between come on over here. They're perfect gifts for the fox fan in your life and they're all 50 percent off. And I'm going to wear this home.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Wear it at a Seattle Starbucks.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MCDOWELL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: All right, Dagen, hit it.\par \par \uc2 MCDOWELL: Give me liberty or give me death was Patrick Henry's famous rallying cry to revolution and you can spend the Fourth of July at Patrick Henry's last home and burial place, Red Hill, in my hometown of Brook new Virginia. Massive festivities including a huge fireworks display. And it's $10 a car. It's fantastic. redhill.org, please visit my hometown.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: OK, Jessica.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: OK. So every player on the Stanley Cup-winning team gets a chance to have the cup for the day. But when Avalanche Captain Gabriel Landeskog's turn came up things didn't go quite as planned. We have very little time so basically, it went to his neighbor, walked outside, and said is that the Stanley Cup? And actually with permission, got to take -- didn't get to take a picture with it but got to hold it up which is a missed opportunity.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: That's it for us.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FOXFIV0020220702ei7100001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Holiday Savings; July 4 Forecast; \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 's \uc2 Twitter\uc2  Silence; Interview With Sen. \uc2 Charles Grassley\uc2  (R-IA); Holiday Travel Chaos\b0\par\par\uc2 Rick Reichmuth, Brian Brenberg, Charlie Gasparino, Kelly O'Grady,  Casey Stegall, Madison Alworth, Neil Cavuto \par \uc2 8,419 \uc2 words\par \uc2 1 July 2022\par \uc2 Fox News: Your World w/ Neil Cavuto\par \uc2 CAVU\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NEIL CAVUTO, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: And we are off, Martha.\par \par \uc2 The July 4 travel exodus is under way, as Americans brace for crowded airports and crowded roads. For fliers, the delays and cancellations continue to pile up, close to 4,000 reported so far today. And with fears of fliers getting stranded, more folks are hitting the road, try more than 42 million of them, no matter the cost.\par \par \uc2 And we are on top of it all with Casey Stegall at Dallas/Fort Worth Airport, where flyers are just trying to get out, Madison Alworth at Newark International, where the -- well, at least one airline is reeling flights in, and Kelly O'Grady in Los Angeles, where folks are saying, the hell with it all, and hitting the road instead.\par \par \uc2 Welcome, everybody. I'm Neil Cavuto. And this is, I guess you could say, your very crowded world today.\par \par \uc2 And doesn't Casey Stegall know it in Dallas -- Casey.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CASEY STEGALL, FOX NEWS SENIOR CORRESPONDENT: Yes, Neil, it has been so busy all day long.\par \par \uc2 We got here at 7:00 this morning, local time, steady streams of people, but nothing insane, no lines out the door or anything like that. The folks at AAA say that this Fourth of July is expected to be the second busiest in terms of travel since 2000, so more than 20 years, if you can believe that, with almost 48 million Americans venturing out over the next couple of days.\par \par \uc2 For air travel, it's 3.5 million. That's the projection, which is a little higher than last year, according to AAA. But only time will tell whether those holiday passengers will further bog down the airports and the airlines.\par \par \uc2 As of this hour, I just did a quick check, more than 3,900 delays and 374 cancellations are being reported in the United States alone by FlightAware. So, queue those long lines, the waits and, of course, the frustration.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KAYLEE KOWALSKI, AIR TRAVELER: I do get stressed sometimes, especially when the airport looks like this. It's actually pretty packed right now.\par \par \uc2 AMY, AIR TRAVELER: If we could just get there. Our flight on American was delayed because of crew. So we're going to miss our connection.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 STEGALL: Oh, missing a connection is always fun, isn't it?\par \par \uc2 Major unions representing airline workers say that at least 15 percent of the flights that carriers originally planned for June through August, so the peak travel of summer, have been dropped now, with not enough staff to operate them.\par \par \uc2 So it's a mix of issues, as you have heard, that we're dealing with here. Fewer planes in the air. That means less routes from points A to B. That also means ticket prices are higher, but, most importantly, Neil, the historic delays and cancellations. It's misery if you have been through it. So pack your patience.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Pack your patience is right, Casey. Thank you very, very much.\par \par \uc2 This issue of cutting flights has also been a big issue. Remember, United had said that it was going to curb about 50 of them per day at Newark's Liberty International Airport.\par \par \uc2 That's where Madison Alworth is. And she can give us a good view of whether that's having the impact that they hope -- Madison.\par \par \uc2 MADISON ALWORTH, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hi, Neil.\par \par \uc2 Yes, so some airlines trying to get ahead of the cancellation and delay problems. But as we just heard, it might be a little too late. So announced earlier, but happening today, United has officially cut 50 flights per day from Newark International Airport, where I stand now.\par \par \uc2 This is one of their seven domestic hubs. And while the airline has not said that staffing issues are to blame for the change, all airlines are facing pilot shortages. And with all the training that goes into being a pilot, it's not a problem that can be fixed overnight.\par \par \uc2 Thousands are needed for the next three years to make up a gap created by the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HENRY HARTEVELDT\uc2 , ATMOSPHERE RESEARCH GROUP: We lost far more pilots than normal in 2020 and 2021 because a lot of airlines gave up more senior pilots incentives to leave early in order to bring down their payroll, even after they got money for the government for wage subsidies.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ALWORTH: Yesterday, off-duty Delta pilots protested for better pay and hours, blaming the airlines for overbooking and underutilizing the pilots that they currently have.\par \par \uc2 Now, some airlines are trying to make adjustments. \uc2 American Airlines\uc2 , they're putting out a proposition to increase pilot pay by 17 percent through 2024. That would put them on par with \uc2 United Airlines\uc2  and the proposition that they have with their pilots.\par \par \uc2 It's really kind have a scramble to get the talent that's out there, because developing pilot talent, well, it takes a while -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, and people don't have a while, to your point.\par \par \uc2 Madison, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Madison Alworth at Newark.\par \par \uc2 Let's get the read from industry experts Misty Belles, Michael Boyd, both with us right now.\par \par \uc2 Michael, ending with you, as an aviation consultant, maybe you could give a tip or two here about how you endure this. For a lot of people, they oftentimes find out as they get to the airport that a flight is delayed or canceled. What do they do? Because a lot of them don't like to be put in that position. And it's obviously fraying tempers and patience.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL BOYD, PRESIDENT, THE BOYD GROUP: Well, when I was at La Guardia, you had a lot of people there. Today, it's all electronic.\par \par \uc2 So, when something works well, it works well. But when flights cancel or you have any kind of off-schedule operation, it's very hard to yell at a ticket kiosk.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 BOYD: So that's one of the problems.\par \par \uc2 And to be real blunt, the airline industry is selling product without product support. You have a problem, there's no one to talk to very often. You have a problem, you get on the phone, sit there for two hours with elevator music, then end up talking to somebody in the Philippines that doesn't know anything.\par \par \uc2 That's a problem the airline industry needs to deal with right now. It's not just pilot shortages. It's handling your product after you sell it.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You know, Misty, we gave the airline industry more than $54 billion after COVID, and for good reason. Their business was ground to a halt, pretty much as it was after 9/11.\par \par \uc2 So I think most people are accepting of that and hope they spend the money wisely, protect jobs, and maybe even hire additional workers ahead of what was going to be a busy travel season and the comeback from COVID. It looks like they were just caught off guard, severely caught off guard.\par \par \uc2 BOYD: Yes, they were.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: No, I do -- wanted to get to Misty first, Michael. And I'd like your take as well.\par \par \uc2 But, Misty, you first.\par \par \uc2 MISTY BELLES, TRAVEL EXPERT: Sure.\par \par \uc2 So yes, they were caught off guard. But I also think we have some unprecedented things going on here, with the fact that we have so many numbers of people coming back really rapidly. So you have that, convergence of events where you have got more passengers, limited capacity, on top of the weather delays that the travel industry typically knows how to deal with.\par \par \uc2 But I just don't think they were able to ramp up fast enough to handle all the pent-up demand that came roaring back with travel.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: No, I think you're right about that.\par \par \uc2 One of the other things, Michael, is you have these Delta pilots, I think about 1,200 of them, starting yesterday picketing at seven major airports and disrupting flights all the more. It's like the industry is turning on itself.\par \par \uc2 BOYD: Well, I mean, picketing doesn't really disrupt anything. It just brings some uncertainty.\par \par \uc2 But we got to put things in context here. We're not back to 2019 levels. We are this year going to end up with about 832 million in plane passengers. That compares to 980 prior to this pandemic. So we're not back there. That makes the situation look even worse that, overall, we don't have as many passengers as before, but we can't handle them.\par \par \uc2 That's the real issue.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So, Misty, it comes back to how people can protect themselves. We're going to be getting into this a little bit. A lot of them are saying, the hell with that. I don't want to risk going to an airport, I will just drive, which is kind of hard to do if you're trying to get to London.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But, having said, that they don't want to deal with that. And the more they see these images, the angrier they're getting.\par \par \uc2 And I'm just worried and wondering about all these cancellations wearing, not on the part of the airlines, but of passengers.\par \par \uc2 BELLES: Well, I do think passengers are very frustrated.\par \par \uc2 And they -- understandably so. Nobody wants to be stuck in an airport. There is a little bit of perspective to keep in mind, if you look where we were a year ago, where we had far fewer flights and far more restrictions.\par \par \uc2 So it is easier to fly this year than it has been. But there are ways that you can protect yourself. I would say, have not just your plan A, but have a plan B. Make sure you pad the schedule if you have to be somewhere at a certain day for a special event. Make sure you're accommodating that by flying out earlier than you typically would.\par \par \uc2 There are a number of things that people can do to try to take some precautions, so that they're not caught so off guard and stuck at the airport.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I wouldn't mind the weight, guys, if there was a Cinnabon in the airport. I think I could deal with that wait. But I digress.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Michael, I'm just curious, when do you see things improving?\par \par \uc2 BOYD: Well, I think airlines are trying to hire pilots. We have ALPA saying they're not. They're certainly trying to hire pilots and come back.\par \par \uc2 It's hard to do it. And the dynamics have changed. Those people who were making 15 bucks an hour loading luggage back in 2019, they're not showing up anymore, because it's not a career.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BOYD: You have those kinds of uncertainties. But the issue is this.\par \par \uc2 To fix this, it would be higher wages in the cockpit, higher wages under the wing, and they're going to have higher fares. When you have 8 to 12 percent inflation and $5 a gallon for unleaded, I wonder what the consumer really is going to do it in the next quarter or so.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes, we're wondering so much about the workers in the airline industry. What about the people who make that work possible? That could be in question.\par \par \uc2 Michael, final word. Misty, want to thank you very, very much.\par \par \uc2 There is an alternative, of course. You can drive. Of course, the reality is that prices there at the pump, as you all know, they're rocketing. And so are the taxes associated with them.\par \par \uc2 Kelly O'Grady knows that quite well in L.A. -- Kelly.\par \par \uc2 KELLY O'GRADY, FOX BUSINESS CORRESPONDENT: Yes, with all those flight cancellations, many Americans are finding that plan B that Misty was talking about.\par \par \uc2 After the break, we will tell you just how many Americans are planning to drive this weekend.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Even with the record gas prices, about 42 million Americans are going to be hitting the road this weekend because they just want to get out, they want to have fun.\par \par \uc2 But they will be looking at that sticker shock at the pump., not only with the higher prices, but the higher gas taxes that are going into effect in about a half-a-dozen states, some as early as today. But they don't seem too bothered by that. For now, they just want to get out and have fun.\par \par \uc2 That's oftentimes easier said than done, as our Kelly O'Grady can tell you in Los Angeles.\par \par \uc2 Kelly, how's it look there?\par \par \uc2 O'GRADY: Well, yes, people are doing something very American this weekend for the Fourth of July, right? They're getting out. They're taking a road trip. And can you blame them, Neil?\par \par \uc2 I mean, amidst all this uncertainty with those flight cancellations that we just heard about, people want to make sure that they get to see their family, they get to those barbecues on time. And, as you said, despite those record high gas prices, or near record high, Americans will be hitting the road in record numbers for the holiday weekend.\par \par \uc2 AAA says a record 42 million will take a road trip, and nearly 48 million Americans will travel at least 50 miles from home. And it's not just those traveling for the holiday, by the way. Some business fliers are starting to drive to meetings, conferences to avoid delays and the possibility of flight cancellations amidst this uptick in travel chaos.\par \par \uc2 According to reports, many companies are even allowing employees more time to drive to events, rather than fly, to avoid those no-shows altogether.\par \par \uc2 Now, while it is cheaper to fly than drive, the cost of gas is in no way minimal. We checked in with drivers here in California, where the average price for a gallon of gas is $6.27, about their plans for the holiday weekend. While some plans are too important to miss, not everyone will be hitting the road.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: But I just got to deal with it. That's all I can do.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Have to go to the doctor, go to the grocery store.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I will definitely be at \uc2 Costco\uc2 , so the discount places and using rewards and stuff like that.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Chilling. Exactly. I was going to Vegas, but gas only going up. No, I'm -- just cheaper to stay home.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O'GRADY: And to make matters worse, as you mentioned, a number of states, including California, are hiking the gas tax today.\par \par \uc2 So, even while some folks are choosing to fly, I think it's important to recognize that not everyone has that option. But if you are hitting the road this weekend, I imagine there's going to be a lot of traffic, Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes, I guess just get ready for it.\par \par \uc2 Thank you very, very much, Kelly. Have a safe weekend yourself.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, I do want to bring in Senator \uc2 Chuck Grassley\uc2 , the Republican senator from the beautiful state of Iowa, get his thoughts on all of this.\par \par \uc2 Senator, as you know, almost two weeks ago, the president had a plan. Maybe take a federal gas tax holiday for about three months. That's about 18.4 cents a gallon. He has urged states, California certainly among them, that have some of the highest state gas taxes, if it were to nix that, I mean, that would be more than $1 a gallon that folks would save.\par \par \uc2 Do you support either, both measures?\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 CHARLES GRASSLEY\uc2  (R-IA): No, I do not.\par \par \uc2 And I'm glad that Speaker Pelosi said she hasn't supported it. And, consequently, since tax bills have to start in the House of Representatives, I doubt if it will ever come before a vote in the \uc2 United States Congress\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But, also, I have had some county meetings the last three days in Iowa. And I find opposition even from Iowans about it, at the very same time that they complain about the high cost of gasoline. So I don't believe it has support at the grassroots any more than it does in the \uc2 United States Congress\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So what would you advocate to bring these prices down?\par \par \uc2 GRASSLEY: Well, in a very general way, why do we have high prices? Because we don't build pipelines. We're not drilling. We're not loaning money to energy companies. Were putting additional regulations on fracking.\par \par \uc2 I will bet, except for 20 cents of the $2 increase in gasoline tax over the last year is because of this president's bad energy policies. And just reverse them. I will give you one example that I came to on a couple of weekends ago, a person that's got a contract to deliver fresh fruits and vegetables.\par \par \uc2 He says his price has gone up 7.5 cents. He says, of those 7.5 percent -- percentage points, 6 of it is directly related to the increase in diesel. The trucking, in other words, has gone up because of the high energy prices. So food prices have gone up not because of Putin, like the president said on -- from Europe at a news conference. It's going up because the high energy prices.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Well, the fact of matter is, you're right on the -- certainly the gas and oil prices. They had run up already 50 percent before the first Russian boots were heading toward Ukraine. Then it went up from there.\par \par \uc2 But, having said that, Senator, I'm just curious as to the administration's posture with the oil companies, doing a lot of finger-pointing, refused to meet with the oil chiefs when they were in town.\par \par \uc2 So they met with the energy secretary, the Energy Department, which I'm told is about an eight-minute bike ride for the president, if he were to go to the Energy Department, but given his experience on bikes, maybe -- maybe not an ideal time.\par \par \uc2 But I'm wondering what you made of that, because the industry and the administration have been at loggerheads. Do you see anything that they can work out?\par \par \uc2 GRASSLEY: Isn't it totally inconsistent to have the bad policy that just heard me describe to you, and then, a year later, go to the energy companies and say, you ought to pump more, when you have done everything to hurt their ability to look ahead the years that they must if they're going to invest?\par \par \uc2 And then you go to Saudi Arabians and say -- tell them to send us more oil, when we got it right here under our own two feet. It just -- it's laughable that they would have such an inconsistent policy.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Let me switch gears, if you don't mind, sir, on the president, because what he was also talking when he was abroad, particularly in Madrid at the \uc2 NATO\uc2  summit, where he volunteered his frustration with the Supreme Court on some key decisions, including Roe v. Wade and reining in the \uc2 EPA\uc2 's powers.\par \par \uc2 But he said that he would seriously call for removal of the filibuster rule to protect U.S. abortion rights. What do you think of that?\par \par \uc2 GRASSLEY: Well, first of all, separate from the pro-life abortion issue, all that, the filibuster, the 60-vote requirement in the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2  is what forces cooperation and bipartisanship.\par \par \uc2 And if there's anything I hear at my town meetings around Iowa, they say why you folks can't get along. So I'm able to explain to him that, when you have a 50/50 Senate and a 60-vote requirement, the institution drives cooperation and bipartisanship.\par \par \uc2 And that's what the people want. So, whether it's connected with the abortion issue...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes, but we don't have it, right, Senator? We don't have it.\par \par \uc2 We approve the Supreme Court justices. The filibuster is gone. It's a simple majority. That dates back certainly to Harry Reid doing the same when a lot of Obama administration judgeships were not being approved.\par \par \uc2 GRASSLEY: Well...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But it seems like it's out the window anyway. What do you say to that?\par \par \uc2 GRASSLEY: Well, I think that you brought up Senator -- Senator Reid.\par \par \uc2 When he did that in 2013, we warned him that he's going to regret it. And if Democrats are in the minority after the next election, they're going to regret that they changed the filibuster. So, if it ain't broke, don't fix it.\par \par \uc2 But the other thing is that, if you start doing it just for abortion, that's a slippery slope. And pretty soon, you're not going to have any 60- vote requirement at all. And everything's going to be partisan, whether it's a Republican majority or a Democrat majority. And people want cooperation in their government.\par \par \uc2 The lack of political cooperation is what's hurting the credibility of the \uc2 Congress of the United States\uc2  and the federal government generally.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Senator, while I have you, I don't know how closely you have focused on these January 6 Committee hearings going on. Too soon to tell how they will conclude.\par \par \uc2 We might be getting some evidence that it's putting a dent in some support for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , if he should entertain a run for office again. Do you think that is -- that is hurting him?\par \par \uc2 GRASSLEY: I think it's a little bit too early to tell that yet.\par \par \uc2 But I think some of the evidence that you're speaking to is weak from this standpoint. In other words, the evidence could be entirely accurate, but the fact that there isn't cross-examination and all nine members of the committee think the same way, and there's not an opportunity to, let's say, for instance, with Ms. Hutchinson's testimony about what the Secret Service was doing, and they want to testify.\par \par \uc2 I don't know how you get an answer until you bring the Secret Service in, and hear their sides of the story.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: And they might -- and to your point, they might do that.\par \par \uc2 But isn't a lot of that, sir, Kevin McCarthy's fault? He didn't look for that, and maybe because of frustration with it was going to be a stacked deck. Say what you will.\par \par \uc2 But even President Trump has come back to criticize Kevin McCarthy for doing that. Do you think that's fair? Do you think that, whatever one-sided nature you see of this -- there are two Republicans on this committee -- but that it's not the way he wanted it?\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 GRASSLEY: Well, if I understand your question, right, and it's a little bit crackly, but, anyway, when Pelosi didn't allow the Republicans on the committee that the minority leader wanted to put on...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 GRASSLEY: ... and she didn't want them, that hurts the credibility of the committee.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: OK. So there were other possibilities that could have been mentioned, but Kevin McCarthy stopped it there. But you're right. It's way past that.\par \par \uc2 Let me ask you a little bit. There are some polls also, Senator, that show \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  in Florida leading President Trump in states like Florida and New Hampshire. Now, nationally, the former president enjoys about an eight- or nine-point lead over DeSantis, but he used to enjoy more than a 20-point lead.\par \par \uc2 What do you make of all this?\par \par \uc2 GRASSLEY: Well, I think whoever wants to be president on the Republican nomination in 2024, whether it's President Trump or any of the other nine people that have been to Iowa so far, because we're the first-in-the-nation caucus -- and Senator Cotton is here next week as just another example -- Trump and everybody else is going to have to fight for the nomination.\par \par \uc2 So I think it's too early to draw any conclusions about what's going to happen in '23 and the first half of '24 to get that nomination.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 GRASSLEY: But nobody can count on it just because they're a governor of a famous state like Florida or an ex-president of the United States. You got to fight for it.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Do you have any favorites yourself, Senator?\par \par \uc2 GRASSLEY: Well, we in Iowa don't have favorites, because we don't want to discourage anybody from coming to the first-in-the-nation caucus, Iowa, at least on the Republican ticket.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 GRASSLEY: Now, we don't know whether the Democrats are going to be first- in-the-nation or not, because they're having internal fights.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes. They're debating that.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 GRASSLEY: At least, the Iowa...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: It will still be first for Republicans, right? OK.\par \par \uc2 GRASSLEY: At least, the Iowa Democrats -- the Iowa Democrats want to be first in the nation, but we don't know if the national Democrats will let them do that.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GRASSLEY: But the Iowa Republicans want the Iowa Democrats to keep fighting to be first in the nation.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, in Iowa, you agree on that.\par \par \uc2 All right, Senator, great seeing you. Have a safe July 4 weekend.\par \par \uc2 GRASSLEY: Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, \uc2 Chuck Grassley\uc2  on all of that.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, we have been following other drama building with \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 . You know, it's been 10 days, 10 days since we have heard him on \uc2 Twitter\uc2 . And we haven't, nothing.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Well, at least we started the second half of the trading year on a better note than we ended it, stocks up on optimism that maybe the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  doesn't have to hike so much. Go figure.\par \par \uc2 We'll have more after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, that's about the most noise you're hearing, that music, when it comes to \uc2 Twitter\uc2  and \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  and what he's thinking, because he's not been tweeting that for the better part of, I think, 10 days.\par \par \uc2 Charlie Gasparino, he wakes up every morning, let's see what he says, see what he says. Nothing.\par \par \uc2 What's going on?\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE GASPARINO, FOX NEWS SENIOR CORRESPONDENT: I should be following his lead, let me tell you. I would save a lot of trouble.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: Here's what I -- here's what bankers are telling me.\par \par \uc2 So you can take it for what it's worth. And some of these bankers proclaim to be involved at least tangentially in the discussions, Musk's discussions to take over \uc2 Twitter\uc2 . They say he is -- that he is negotiating right now with \uc2 Twitter\uc2  about a lower price. His lawyers essentially have told him to lay off \uc2 Twitter\uc2  while this negotiation goes down, because they're not sure exactly where it's going to show up.\par \par \uc2 And this thing could also be heading for litigation. For once, he's listening to his lawyers. So that's kind of where we are. And the price tag, a lot of people think that's going to be worked out, and at least they're trying to work out, is a price tag of around $40 a share, somewhere where \uc2 Twitter\uc2 's trading now.\par \par \uc2 It's $39, but between $40 and $43. Dan Ives, the very good tech analyst, says that that's a number that he believes it's going to -- is going to happen. And a lot of people think it's going to happen. And here's the problem that the \uc2 Twitter\uc2  board has, and why they really do have to renegotiate with Musk, even though he backed out of the early deal. He didn't do due diligence. They can sue them. He can pay a billion-dollar breakup fee, and maybe more.\par \par \uc2 If he backs out of this thing, the stock is going to have a $20 -- a 20 in it. Now, it might be 21. It might be 28 or 29. But it's going to go down to 20. And everybody knows it. The board knows it. So the likelihood is right now and what they're saying is he's renegotiating with them. They're looking at a way to creatively finance this, where everybody makes a few bucks and walks away, not giving up too much.\par \par \uc2 They're in a pickle. They can't let him say, look, I'm going to pay $3 a share, because you already -- because you initially came in at $54, whatever it was, $52.40. It was in the 50s.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Got it.\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: But you see what I'm saying here.\par \par \uc2 They are renegotiating. And, for once, he's listening to his lawyers, as I should listen to our P.R. department -- Neil, back to you.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Oh, that, I would love to see.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Have a great weekend, my friend, Charlie Gasparino.\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: You too.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: We have a lot more coming up, including right now this little ditty we got from \uc2 Exxon\uc2  that it is looking at big gains in the second quarter in oil and gas production in a separate securities filing.\par \par \uc2 If true, it would be counter to what the administration would be saying, that it is sort of sitting on oil and not producing any oil. For \uc2 ExxonMobil\uc2 , not so.\par \par \uc2 We will keep you posted -- after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, you're looking at a list of companies that have slowed their hiring, at a minimum, and lately just taking job offers away from college graduates, or planning on that.\par \par \uc2 And now they're nixing that. But it's begun to escalate now to outright removing jobs altogether. \uc2 Novartis\uc2  the latest and the biggest to do so, 8,000. job cuts promised over the next few months. So this trend that many people feared would start with the rise in interest rates and the slowdown in the economy has indeed started. Should we worry about it?\par \par \uc2 Kenny Polcari\uc2  joins us, the Slatestone Wealth chief market strategist, Brian Brenberg, the professor of business economics at the King's College in Manhattan.\par \par \uc2 So, Professor Brian, I end it with you, want to begin with you on what we're seeing here. I think last time you and I had chatted, you said it was just a matter of time. And now it's happening. Where do you see all of this going?\par \par \uc2 BRIAN BRENBERG, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Yes, the time is here.\par \par \uc2 And the reason is because companies are looking ahead. And they're saying, what's the path forward where consumers get excited again, where they want to go out and shop, where advertisers want to advertise? And they're not seeing anything.\par \par \uc2 The Biden administration has failed to paint a picture of how we start growing out of this funk that we're in right now. And until the Biden administration can do that, these companies have to plan for the worst. That's the problem, Neil.\par \par \uc2 They know that consumer pessimism is incredibly high. Business confidence is low. When you're in that situation, you got to get lean, and that's what these companies are doing. And they're doing it, again, because the Biden administration has not given us anything to hang our hat on in terms of when all of this could get better.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I always hate that expression they got to get lean. If they could just go with less fat, that might be good.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But anyway, Kenny, one of the things I wonder about, I go to Broadway shows or movie theaters, they're packed, right? I go to restaurants and all, they're packed.\par \par \uc2 And I hear from some of the proprietors that they're having to devil at the time hiring folks. So there's a disconnect here. So what is it?\par \par \uc2 KENNY POLCARI\uc2 , SLATESTONE WEALTH: Well, listen, I think it's a couple of things, right?\par \par \uc2 Because I think people want to do things like go to the theater, and they do want to go out. But they're going to start to pull back on the other stuff that, while they might like to have it, they don't want it, it's too expensive. They want to spend their -- the money that they do have on the other -- on the experience things, right, things like going to the theater, or things like going to the museum or going out with either relatives or friends.\par \par \uc2 And so that's where it's going to be. I don't necessarily think it's so much a disconnect, because I also think that's going to come to a grinding halt too if this inflation continues to get -- continues to stay elevated the way it is, and people are going to start to get really weary.\par \par \uc2 And, to Brian's point, I think they're looking for a way -- they're looking for a way to see forward. And, right now, it seems that everything doesn't -- everything looks dark. They can't really see through it at the moment.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: And one of the reasons why we were up today, a lot of reasons assigned to it, in the markets, Brian, but this idea that look at the 10- year note, 10-year bond. It came down in yield, not that that's dramatically riveting here, but that maybe it's the markets' way of telling us the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  is getting the impact it wanted from a slowing economy, because that's the way we see it, slowing economy, all the major averages down this week, despite the comeback today.\par \par \uc2 But this notion that it will put a lid on how high the Fed raises rates. I guess they grasp at anything they can. They grasped at that today. What do you think?\par \par \uc2 BRENBERG: Yes, the market wants easy money. They're afraid of having to fight inflation. And they're afraid of the Fed having to fight inflation.\par \par \uc2 But this bothers me, Neil. I mean, we're supposed to be trying to kick inflation right now. That's job number one. And you already have the market saying, I don't think Powell is up to it, I don't think the Fed's up to it. I think when they start to see recession fears, they're going to back off.\par \par \uc2 But where does that lead us? I think that leads us potentially with not getting out of inflation, Neil. I think that's the problem. And remember this. There's only so much the Fed can do on inflation. We need the supply side to grow. And we have got zero policies from Biden on the growth side of this.\par \par \uc2 So, the Fed can do what it can do. But if they start getting nervous about recession, then we could get in a situation, Neil, where the inflation problem doesn't go away. And, to me, that's the biggest problem of all.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So, real fast, Kenny, how does the next half of the year look to you?\par \par \uc2 POLCARI: So, listen, I'm concerned about -- at least I'm concerned for the next couple of months, as we go through this earnings season, which is going to start next week.\par \par \uc2 I think you're already starting to see it. You're starting to see the companies starting to warn. Revisions are going to come down. If the guidance going forward continues to be weak, then you're going to see overall estimates come down and the growth picture come down. And I think that's going to cause some concern.\par \par \uc2 I don't think, though, it's going to -- I don't think the market is going to fall out of bed.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right.\par \par \uc2 POLCARI: I actually think it's just going to trade down to 3600 as a low and then churn for a while, until it figures out where the Fed wants to go.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, about another 10 percent or so from there, if I'm doing my math right.\par \par \uc2 Guys, I want to thank you both very, very much. Have a safe weekend, safe July 4 weekend.\par \par \uc2 It's going to be a hot weekend for a lot of folks. There's also going to be a lot of storms for a lot of folks. We will detail all of that for you -- after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Well, it's all about celebrating the July 4 weekend, the Independence Day weekend.\par \par \uc2 And depending on where you are, you're going to look up in the sky and either going to see clouds and rain and some severe storms or just some heat, but it's a big country, you know, 3,000 miles across.\par \par \uc2 So, anyway, let's get the read from Rick Reichmuth in the FOX Weather Center, what he sees happening.\par \par \uc2 Hey, Rick.\par \par \uc2 RICK REICHMUTH, FOX NEWS CHIEF METEOROLOGIST: There's always something going on somewhere when you got this many -- such a big country. You're absolutely right.\par \par \uc2 It's hot out there across a lot of the country right now. In fact, right now, we're having some triple digits right there across parts of Texas and Oklahoma. That's kind of the bulk of the heat. When you have the heat this time of year, it's also really humid.\par \par \uc2 And so you get these really significant pop up thunderstorms. And when you look at the country right now, almost everybody getting something.Take a look at this out across parts of the Four Corners. That area has been so dry -- in fact, many spots had the driest winter they ever had. So we got a really early start to the monsoon season. That happens every year right around this time.\par \par \uc2 And you see all those storms popping up. It's really good news, but we will see a little bit of flooding from that. And then we have got some other storms we have been watching. One of them right there is off the coast of the Carolinas. Could see a little bit of tropical development for it, not a huge chance of it, but something we're going to watch.\par \par \uc2 But you notice all of that moisture across parts of the Southeast. And then we have been watching another storm that we thought could maybe develop into something tropical here across kind of the Eastern -- excuse me -- the Western Gulf. That did not happen. The center of that area of disturbance has moved in across parts of North Carolina -- excuse me -- across parts of Louisiana.\par \par \uc2 And with that, we're getting some really significant rain. It's another area that's been seeing some drought develop. So we want to get some moisture here, but a little bit of it coming too quickly. And so we will see some flooding there. That will be done over the next couple of days. But we do have some showers here that we're going to watch.\par \par \uc2 Eventually, this all gets by Sunday pulled up into a front that's going to pull off across the Eastern Seaboard. But I tell you what, Neil. Mid- Atlantic and towards the Northeast, take a look at what happens here. We have got some storms that are going to start later today and then tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 And we have got some -- going to be dodging some scattered showers and thunderstorms, maybe some severe weather all throughout the day tomorrow up and down the Mid-Atlantic through the Northeast, so not a great day at the beach. It does get better on Sunday, but tomorrow certainly be watching for this if you are headed out to any of the beaches across parts of the Northeast -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I just want you to back out at those national parks. I love that when you're with Katie.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: We got to get you out there, my friend. So...\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 REICHMUTH: Trust me. I, like, pitch it every day to the producer. Get me out there. Get me out there.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: It was awesome. I loved that.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Thanks, my friend.\par \par \uc2 REICHMUTH: You bet.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Rick Reichmuth at the FOX Weather Center following all of that.\par \par \uc2 Of course, if you are just revving up the grill, you probably are familiar that a lot of the things you're going to put on it about have gotten pricey. But there are ways around that -- after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You know, not everyone is feeling the inflation, or at least resistant to it.\par \par \uc2 Disney\uc2 , for example, launching its first new ship in 10 years, and it's a ship that features, among other things, a $5,000 cocktail.\par \par \uc2 Gabe Saglie\uc2  joins us right now, the Travelzoo senior editor, on this very ship right now.\par \par \uc2 GABE SAGLIE, TRAVELZOO.COM: Indeed.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Gabe, good to have you