ope, while the leaders there hate us for everything we stand for.\par \par \uc2 So this effectively means that we're abandoning the entire rest of the world to China, which of course, is funding the war against Ukraine by buying Russian oil and wheat. And remember, that Biden is on the verge of dropping Trump's tariffs against China, even though we have now a near record trade deficit with them.\par \par \uc2 So stay with me. This means that Biden has us indirectly funding the war in Ukraine that we're trying to help Ukraine win. It's twisted, right. There is nothing patriotic about what Biden is doing in our foreign policy, or with our energy policy, our trade policy, or at the border. All of it is designed to make America less independent, less prosperous, and yes, less happy.\par \par \uc2 So Happy Independence Day. We're going to celebrate this one and the one coming in the midterms and in 2024. And that's the 'Angle'.\par \par \uc2 All right. Joining me now is Senator Mike Lee, \uc2 Senate Judiciary Committee\uc2  member and author of "Saving Nine: The Fight Against the Left's Audacious Plan to Pack the Supreme Court and Destroy American Liberty".\par \par \uc2 And, Senator, speaking of this complete disregard for our institutions, you saw what Biden did today in Madrid. I want to bring it back to something else that the President said overseas.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Most important thing to be clear about is, we have to change, I believe we have to codify Roe v Wade in the law. And the way to do that is to make sure the Congress votes to do that. And if the filibuster gets in the way, it's like voting rights, it should be we provide an exception for this.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: The convulsion over the court continues, and it's now being aided by our president abroad. Your reaction?\par \par \uc2 SEN. MIKE LEE (R-UT): This is appalling, Laura. First of all, the fact that he would go while on foreign soil and attack the \uc2 Supreme Court of the United States\uc2  to undermine it and to de-legitimize it is unpatriotic. I've never seen anything quite like it.\par \par \uc2 Secondly, insofar as he's suggesting that they need to codify Roe, he's lying there. They don't merely want to codify Roe. What they want to do is pass a bill that would, in addition to codifying Roe, it would take the protections of Roe all the way up until the moment a child takes its first breath. So that no state anywhere could impose any kind of legal restrictions, any kind of protections for unborn human beings.\par \par \uc2 So he'd managed to be at once entirely heartless toward the unborn, entirely in conflict with his own prior decisions, his own prior statements about abortion, well, as a U.S. senator and otherwise, and managed to disrespect one of the great institutions of our system.\par \par \uc2 Look, warts and all, the \uc2 Supreme Court of the United States\uc2  isn't a bad court. It is a good court. And the fact that it's run by mortal human beings means they sometimes make mistakes. They did not make a mistake with Dobbs. They corrected a mistake they had made 49 years earlier. This is unacceptable behavior. I don't know whether to chalk it up to incompetence, or avarice or both. But this is not going to fly with the American people.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Well, Senator, \uc2 Ed Markey\uc2  today was talking about why it's so important for the Democrats to finally what they wanted to do is to get rid of the filibuster.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. ED MARKEY (D-MA): The filibuster has to be amended, or it has to be eliminated. Otherwise, we're just going to see not just in this area, but gun rights and climate and in so many other areas, just an evisceration of rights that Americans have come to depend upon.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Senator, I think the truth is that the left now hates the court, because it's the only branch that still respects the original framework of the Constitution, at least as it's currently situated with the justices that are on board.\par \par \uc2 LEE: Yes, that's right. And make no mistake, this idea of nuking the filibuster, in addition to being in conflict with what \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has said in very recent years about the fact that it would be a bad idea.\par \par \uc2 This is not limited to their so-called attempt to codify Roe. No, that's just the first step. They - their real objective there is to expand the Supreme Court, to pack the Supreme Court, which is explaining and "Saving Nine" is one of the most disastrous things they could do to the Constitution. It's not technically unconstitutional, but it's anti- constitutional. It would erode everything about it.\par \par \uc2 And \uc2 Ed Markey\uc2 , as explained in "Saving Nine" is one of the original architects of this modern plan to do it. But every time this has been attempted in modern history, Laura, it has ended badly for the American people, whether it succeeds or fails legislatively.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Senator, this idea of codifying Roe, just because a lot of people have been inquiring about this. I don't know what the legislation would look like, or specifically say, I guess there's a draft of it already written. But I haven't read it.\par \par \uc2 But what were the two or three challenges that the States would lodge against such an effort other than obvious States rights and the 10th amendment?\par \par \uc2 LEE: Yes. So first of all, they would perhaps argue that this is outside of Congress' authority to enact sweeping nationwide legislation like this. You've got to actually have a jurisdictional hook in there somewhere and depends on what ground they rested on. But let's assume just for a minute, that they use the Commerce Clause, which is what Congress typically uses for almost any type of legislation that passes.\par \par \uc2 Abortion is itself not an economic activity. It's certainly not an economic activity that takes place across state lines, typically. And so, those might be some of the challenges that they would lodge against it. But the legislation itself may or may not be upheld at the end of the day.\par \par \uc2 You know, as well as anyone, Laura, the Supreme Court has been fairly permissive and deferential to Congress in defunding the contours of the Commerce Clause. And that's also something I've talked about in "Saving Nine", that is the ugly gift that keeps on giving that came about as a result of FDR's court packing plan 85 years ago. But at the end of the day, what they want to do is make sure that infanticide is legal.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Yes. They don't want the people to have their own ability to determine their own destinies and futures in every state. They hate that. Back to the point that a lot of them just really don't like America, or founding documents. They want to get - if they could, they would - I think they would get rid of it all, and replace it with something much more akin to what governs the EU.\par \par \uc2 Senator, it's great to see you tonight. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RAYMOND ARROYO, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: How are you celebrating Independence Day? Are you celebrating?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: No. I'm black. I celebrate Juneteenth. So that's - I've never, I mean it's never been a big holiday for me and my family. So at the most, we've had the day off or we barbecued. But I mean, Independence Day was for a certain segment of the population and not for everybody.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: We'll play more of Raymond's (ph) van on the street interviews about the meaning of Fourth of July to people when he joins us later in the show.\par \par \uc2 But first, I want to get reaction to that part from Bob Woodson, president and founder of the Woodson Center. Bob, we just discussed with Senator Lee, the attacks on our fundamental institutions. The Democrats claim they care about democracy and process. But in the end, they obviously want to tear most of this stuff down.\par \par \uc2 How worrying is it that more Americans seem to believe less in our founding, the promise of Liberty and Independence Day itself?\par \par \uc2 BOB WOODSON, THE WOODSON CENTER FOUNDER & CEO: Well, it's devastating, I know, for more vulnerable populations, particularly in the black community. Progressives are pushing this anti-American agenda and using America's birth defect of slavery as a bludgeon against the country.\par \par \uc2 One person even saying that she celebrate Juneteenth, but not 1776, or she doesn't understand that if it was not for 1776, there would be no Juneteenth. That black Americans have sacrificed, have demonstrated their commitment to these values and ideals of ours by the shedding of their bloods. Blacks fought in every war that this country has ever had.\par \par \uc2 Every war we fought, and not a single black was ever convicted of treason. We have the Harlem Hellfighters. My dad was a member of that group, the Tuskegee Airmen and all these. So America - people of color are risking their lives to come here and enjoy the freedoms that we enjoy. And so we should celebrate and we will celebrate.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: And a Princeton professor had an interesting take on Independence Day, Bob. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: What are your thoughts? This - going into this Fourth of July weekend, on pride and being an American?\par \par \uc2 EDDIE GLAUDE JR, \uc2 PRINCETON UNIVERSITY\uc2  PROFESSOR: Fourth of July has always been an incredibly vexed holiday for me, given the tradition out of which I come. It's framed by Juneteenth, the way in which we struggle with the legacies of slavery.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: He definitely just doesn't want to teach his students about the wonders of liberty as understood by generations and generations.\par \par \uc2 WOODSON: No. And also, I just think they make this false claim that some of the challenges of disadvantage in these cities around the country, these disparities are the cause by racism and history of slavery and discrimination.\par \par \uc2 In fact, Laura, the first 50 years after slavery, blacks actually in these cities fared much better under segregation than they have over the past 50 years of progressive policies. Children, we have five high schools in urban centers at the turn of the century with 50 percent of the budgets of white schools, used textbooks, crumbling buildings. Yet every one of those high schools scored oust tested all white schools in the cities in 1920.\par \par \uc2 Today, in those same school systems, the blacks are achieving only 10 percent or even efficient in schools today when at the turn of the century on the segregation.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Bob, Happy Independence Day. Happy Fourth of July. So great to see you as always.\par \par \uc2 Now, up next, why is the administration and the Fed lying to you about the real state of the economy? We're going to examine it.\par \par \uc2 Plus, what does a Reagan biographer think about his library, the Reagan Library, hosting \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  last night. Craig Shirley does not hold back, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. LIZ CHENEY (R-WY): I'm a conservative Republican. But I also know that at this moment, we are confronting a domestic threat that we have never faced before. And that is the former president who is attempting to unravel the foundations of our Constitutional Republic.\par \par \uc2 The reality that we face today as Republicans, as we think about the choice in front of us. We have to choose, because Republicans cannot both be loyal to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and loyal to the Constitution.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: That was Democrat, Republican, almost to be soon former Congresswoman \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  speaking at the Reagan Library last night, can you believe that? Why they chose to host one of the most self-aggrandizing politicians in the country, who is kind of the opposite of what I think of conservatives to speak there is beyond me?\par \par \uc2 But it was actually quite revelatory, because between waxing poetic about the January 6 committee and making demands of a party that will soon vote her out of office, she struck a tone resembling, I don't know, she sounded kind of like a dime store \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHENEY: Since January 6, I have been incredibly moved by the young people that I have met, especially the young women. Young women who seem instinctively to understand the peril of this moment.\par \par \uc2 And let me also say this to the little girls and to the young women who are watching tonight. These days, for the most part, men are running the world. And it is really not going that well.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Joining me now Craig Shirley, \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2  biographer, most recent book "April 1945". Craig, why would the Reagan Library even ask \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  to speak. Why?\par \par \uc2 CRAIG SHIRLEY, PRESIDENTIAL HISTORIAN: I have no idea, Laura. It's a mystery to me. When I saw that she was going to speak at the Reagan Library. The first thing that came to my mind was Reagan's famous 11th commandment, Thou shalt not speak ill of a fellow Republican. And yet she's made a career of speaking ill unless recently, of speaking ill of fellow Republicans, not just \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . So why they would have her there, I have no idea.\par \par \uc2 But I think this one is going to leave a mark. I think, the Reagan Library is going to supersede donations trickle down because of this. Then they're going to see attendance trickle down because of this. And then, don't forget the presidential debates are in a couple years, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  certainly is not going to go to the library for a debate and show of unity. Other Republican presidential candidates will not go to the Reagan Library to debate either. So the debates are over for the Reagan Library.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Yes. I guess, that Fred Ryan, who runs the \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  is also on the Reagan Library Board. So maybe the Foundation Board, maybe that's part of the deal. Now, here was the real audience, though, the real audience for that speech last night. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 NICOLE WALLACE, CNN HOST: After testimony for the history books, that was his speech for the history books.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: It's so remarkable to see her talk in such blunt ways about the way that she sees the world, and the courage that she sees in Cassidy Hutchinson. She is part of the courageous women who stepped out and said, If I don't get reelected, oh, well, I'm going to--\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: So you see the line that got the applause.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Holy cow, Craig. That's the commentary we've become accustomed to.\par \par \uc2 SHIRLEY: Yes. Can she pander any more? I heard once - she was the first person pronoun five times in one sentence. This is all about her. This is all about her self aggrandizement. This is nothing about real ideology or anything else like that.\par \par \uc2 And if she's so worried about the commission and what they're investigating, why not investigate the summer of hate, when the left burned 200 cities and caused $2 billion of damage and injured dozens of people, killed dozens of people. Why isn't that being investigated?\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: As a historical matter, she said the country has never faced a domestic threat like this. Never. Historically, Craig.\par \par \uc2 SHIRLEY: Insanity. She doesn't know her history. We've faced wars, depressions. We've faced many, many major issues over 230 years. And this is nothing compared to. We faced a civil war, or very foundings, the American Revolution. All these things make this dwell, make this a tiny matter. In the context of history, this is nothing. She doesn't know history. She doesn't know her history.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Craig, great to see you tonight. Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: America is better positioned to lead the world than we ever have been. We have the strongest economy in the world. Our inflation rates are lower than other nations in the world.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: OK. Quick fact check there for the big guy. The U.S. inflation rate is now 8.3 percent, which is higher than all the G7 nations that you said that in front of. So the question is, why does he keep lying to us about the strong economy? Same thing with \uc2 Jay Powell\uc2 . The \uc2 Atlanta Fed\uc2  just lowered its Q-2 GDP forecast to negative one percent. So if that is right, it means that while Biden and the Fed chair have been declaring how great our economy is and well-positioned, we're in the midst of a recession.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now is Peter Schiff, chief economist, global strategist for Euro Pacific Capital, and the host of "Schiff Radio." Peter, you've waxed poetic about what kind of condition our economy is really in. It's worse than even I just stated, is it not?\par \par \uc2 PETER SCHIFF, EURO PACIFIC CAPITAL: It's been in recession all year. So the whole time they've been bragging about how strong the economy was, in fact, Powell was saying the economy is so strong, it can easily withstand higher interest rates. It was in recession the whole time.\par \par \uc2 But you've got to think about this, Laura. If we're already in a recession, because we had negative 1.6. percent in the first quarter, and if the \uc2 Atlanta Fed\uc2  is right, it's negative one. It may be even worse than that. But we still have near record low unemployment, at least the way the government scores it, which is not very honest. But if we're already in a recession with low unemployment, imagine how much worse this recession is going to get when employers start reacting to the recession by laying off their workers in mass, and as the Fed continues to raise interest rates, and as inflation continues to drive consumer prices higher.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: \uc2 Janet Yellen\uc2  testifying, the treasury secretary, on the Hill, and she was questioned by, I believe it was Senator Rounds, and asked if there's such a thing as too much debt in the country. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANET YELLEN\uc2 , TREASURY SECRETARY: In thinking about what is a reasonable level of debt, there are a number of different metrics that one might look at. Commonly debt to GDP ratios are a measure that's widely used. Ours is a little over 100 percent, which traditionally has been regarded as high. But we are in a very low interest rate environment.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: That was from September. But now that our interest rates have surged, I wonder how she feels.\par \par \uc2 SCHIFF: Yes, and of course, the reason we have so much debt is because we were in such a low interest rate environment for so long thanks to the Fed. And now that the Fed has to raise interest rates, it's basically pulling the rug out from under the economy because now the debt is completely unsustainable, especially if the Fed actually raises interest rates high enough to successful fight inflation, because so far they haven't even come close.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: So peter, how much do interest rates have to go up in order for us to put the brakes on this?\par \par \uc2 SCHIFF: You have got to get interest rates higher than the inflation rate, at a minimum. So if in fact is eight-and-a-half, you're talking about maybe nine, nine-and-a-half at a minimum. Obviously, with all the debt that we have, nobody, including federal government, can afford to service the debt. But we're not going to get ahold of inflation until we cut government spending. We have got to get rid of these deficits that the Fed is monetizing.\par \par \uc2 And nobody in the Biden administration is talking about reducing government spending. In fact, all their plans are to spend even more money when they're supposedly complaining about inflation. Inflation is the tax that we're paying to finance all their spending.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Peter, happy Fourth of July. We love having you on. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 And remember, July 4th is already here, so remember, get your USA made Freedom Matters gear at LauraIngraham.com. All the proceeds for this month, the last day, donated to the Honor Flight Network. Great new women's polos up there. You get a free koozie. You'll love it. All USA made.\par \par \uc2 And up next, we send Raymond on the road with a patriotic pop quiz. He's with us next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: It's for a special, what is it, July 4th edition "Seen and Unseen." For that we turn to FOX News contributor Raymond Arroyo. All right, Raymond, you took to the streets to ask people whether they took pride in our great country.\par \par \uc2 RAYMOND ARROYO, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Yes, and we kind of asked it in a sneaky way, which I like to do. I asked New Yorkers how they're celebrating Independence Day and what it means to them. The answers were startling, and they give us some insight into how people really feel about the country.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: How are you celebrating?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I'm going to celebrate the ideal of America and not the actual country as it is.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: What don't you like about the actual country?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: The whole racism and sexism and everything.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I believe the idea is a lot better than the actual country. The