ield power over Parliament. They will be able to legislate through the Supreme Court just as they have legislated on issues such as homophobia and nepotism, which is, of course, the policy of employing members of your own family as a publicly appointed Member of Congress.\par \par \uc2 We lowered the tax called IPS, the tax on industrialized goods and the Supreme Court said that was not correct. They changed it. Very often, the Supreme Court in Brazil, they make decisions that should otherwise be only decided by the executives, such as, for example, Federal taxes.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: The left in Brazil, Lula, seem to be trying to introduce identity politics, American style race, resentment based politics into Brazil. Do you notice this and is it working?\par \par \uc2 BOLSONARO (through translator): It is a way to divide and conquer. You know, even before Lula took office in 2003, the left wing would already preach that. I mean, the division of people along identity lines such as Blacks and Whites and employers and employees, and people from Northeastern Brazil, vis-a-vis Southern Brazil, they have gained followers as a result of that approach.\par \par \uc2 And the major divisionary split today is between employers and employees. Lots of followers, and they say that, "Hey, you're not doing well today because of your employer." And that divide approach or policy, the workers probably have always gained at least one-fourth of the Brazil population. They've always been supported by at least one-fourth of the Brazilian population.\par \par \uc2 My father in law is known as homem negro, "Black man" and I am blue-eyed White man. Racism is there in Brazil, yes, but not as it is often described. Most of our main football players are, you know, people of African descent. No worries.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: You've liberalized gun laws here, you've made it easier for civilians to own firearms. Why did you do that?\par \par \uc2 BOLSONARO (through translator): I was unable to actually change the Brazilian law, but by means of a Presidential Decree, and also through Ministerial Orders, we interpreted the existing legislation as best as possible.\par \par \uc2 There has been a substantial increase in the number of people who own legally-owned firearms in Brazil and the consequences you see in the Brazilian media is the drop or a drop in the number of people who are killed by firearms in Brazil.\par \par \uc2 We believe, we understand that once people get armed, you know, the crooks, the thieves will think twice before they break into other people's property. If, of course, the number had gone up, I would be blamed, but just the opposite is going on today.\par \par \uc2 And if re-elected, if everything goes well, we will have a substantial support in Congress and we will be able to pass laws on firearms very much along the same lines as in the US.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Chinese companies had been clear cutting huge swathes of the Amazon rainforest for many years. But since Bolsonaro became President, Hollywood has awakened to this and blames him for it. Amazing.\par \par \uc2 We asked him what exactly is happening in the Amazon, that's next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Well, in recent years, the left's moral voices that would be of course Madonna, Jennifer Lopez, Leonardo DiCaprio, people who couldn't find their way unaided to El Segundo have discovered the Amazon Rainforest where turns out fires sometimes breakout. They say the Amazon rainforest is being destroyed, not by clear cutting, committed by China, no, criticizing China is racist, but because of Bolsonaro or something.\par \par \uc2 Anyway, the Earth is in the balance.\par \par \uc2 We asked Bolson are about that.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Is the Amazon being destroyed at the rate that we read that it is?\par \par \uc2 BOLSONARO (through translator): No. No, that is not true. Two-thirds of the Brazilian territory are currently preserved and remain pristine, just as they were back in 1500s when this country was discovered by the Portuguese.\par \par \uc2 The criminal acts that do happen, we will not deny that. Ultimately given too much visibility, because there's a huge interest in making our sovereignty over the Amazonian region a relative fact or perceived in a relative like.\par \par \uc2 I met with President Putin and thanked him because on two occasions when this issue was discussed with other countries, he stated that the Amazon region belongs to the Brazilian people, not to anyone else.\par \par \uc2 The Amazon region is as vast as Eastern Europe. It's huge. It is wonderful. It's just not possible to, you know, take care of every square meter in the Amazon region.\par \par \uc2 There is illegal deforestation, yes. There are fire outbreaks, yes. But not at the rate that is announced out there. California itself always, you know, catches fire and it's not given that much visibility.\par \par \uc2 French Guiana, likewise, I mean, you can't possibly set a rainforest to fire, but there is illegal deforestation. Yes. We are working to tackle that. And how can we solve the issue? Well, it depends on the Brazilian Parliament. It depends on land regularization, we'll get focused and we need the legislation to be passed to that effect.\par \par \uc2 In the future, any heat focus directed by a satellite will point to the ultimate owner of that light, where you seem to have a focus of fire or a fire outbreak beginning, you will be able to know whether it belongs to the Federal administration or to a private owner or to the riverside people.\par \par \uc2 Sometimes the riverside populations set fields of fire for agricultural or cropping purposes, and that technology will certainly help us prevent any kind of deforestation.\par \par \uc2 I mean, the Amazon region is as vast as Eastern Europe. It is not easy to take care of. The Yanomami Indigenous Reserve itself is as big as twice the territory of the State of Rio de Janeiro, which is a big state.\par \par \uc2 So with technology, we will certainly be able to preserve the Amazonian region. We do not depend on international interest in preserving the Amazonian region. It is our own interests, and we of course, want these preservation efforts to be somehow reciprocated by means of carbon credits.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: You are opposed by most actors in Brazil and celebrities, models, TV presenters, and we learned recently that Mark Ruffalo also doesn't like you. You don't seem bothered at all by this. Why?\par \par \uc2 BOLSONARO (through translator): Well, if I do bother or become concerned about all of the criticism I get, I would not be able to work. Of course, no one likes to be criticized, but these are people who do not understand anything about Brazil.\par \par \uc2 I think these people have never been to the Amazon region, and I would actually invite them to come to the Amazon region and overfly the region and they will see an outstanding region. They will be able to overfly it, you will see no fire outbreak. You will not see the devastated areas.\par \par \uc2 I mean, they talk about the Amazon region because it's trendy. It's a fad. I mean, in Europe, if someone says that the Amazon region is not being devastated, they are politically doomed to fiasco. So, it is politically good for them.\par \par \uc2 You know, the criticism yields power, it yields media visibility, it yields votes, and that's what celebrities feel. They're not at all familiar with the Amazon region. They are not at all knowledgeable about the region and the difficulty of investing in it. They just criticize it because it's a fad.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: So you don't seem that bothered by Mark Ruffalo's dislike of you. Have you enjoyed being President of Brazil?\par \par \uc2 BOLSONARO (through translator): I must admit, you know, I first launched myself as a candidate. Of course, I intended to become the President, but I was all too aware of the challenges and difficulties and the experiences we have undergone or I have undergone, I could never, ever have imagined what it would look like.\par \par \uc2 But I am comforted by the fact that the other candidates, the Workers Party candidate, Haddad, if he had won, Brazil would certainly be what Venezuela is today. So, that's a source of comfort to me and that encourages me to say to the population that God is the one who has placed me where I am today.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Mr. President, thank you for having us.\par \par \uc2 BOLSONARO (through translator): You're welcome.\par \par \uc2 A message to the Americans: God, the homeland, family, and freedom. Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: The main reason we came to Brazil was to report on what China is doing, something that is being ignored on purpose in the United States.\par \par \uc2 China is working hard to turn the developing world into a new colony, a colony of China, and that's particularly true in Brazil, a country with richest natural resources in the region.\par \par \uc2 Brazil, a military beachhead for China? It's of concern. We asked Bolsonaro and what he is doing to stop it, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Sonny Barger died yesterday in his home state of California. He was 83 years old.\par \par \uc2 Barger became famous more than 50 years ago as the founder of the Hells Angels Motorcycle Club. Remember that?\par \par \uc2 At the time, a group of working class men on \uc2 Harley-Davidson\uc2 's seemed like one of the biggest problems we had in American society. It was a different country then.\par \par \uc2 But Sonny Barger never changed. At his death, his wife released his final letter. It was written to his friends and family and it contained this advice, "Keep your head up high. Stay loyal. Remain free. And always value honor." The words of an outlaw? Maybe they were, but they are wiser and truer than anything we've heard from our own leaders in a long time.\par \par \uc2 Well, as we told you, we're working on a documentary on China's attempts to colonize the Third World, in particular to turn Brazil into a colony. It'll be airing on "Tucker Carlson Originals" soon.\par \par \uc2 We just took a helicopter around Rio de Janeiro to take a closer look at Chinese energy and infrastructure projects here. China is using its economic and political influence to transform this country and to control it.\par \par \uc2 Our country can learn a lot from what's happening in Brazil. How long before, because they hold our debt, China decides they need to our natural resources, our mineral wealth, our water, our trees?\par \par \uc2 Don't laugh. It's happening around the world, and that's a point that Bolsonaro, the President of Brazil emphasized when we talk to him.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: So given your natural resources, are you worried about the level of Chinese investment in Brazil?\par \par \uc2 BOLSONARO (through translator): I would very much like the Americans to also take part in more investments in Brazil. We, of course, have trade flows with China and they have of course, at least first shown interest in ensuring their food security.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: As we told you, that documentary on China's attempt to turn Brazil into a colony comes out later this summer.\par \par \uc2 We should tell you, we're also working on another documentary for "Tucker Carlson Originals." It's called "Transgressive: The Cult of Confusion" about the left's attempt to castrate and sterilize children and call it liberation. And as we promised you, it's coming out next month.\par \par \uc2 That's about it for us tonight, of course, we'll be back every evening 8:00 PM. The show that's the sworn, but cheerful enemy of lying, pomposity, smugness, and groupthink.\par \par \uc2 Have the best night with the ones you love.\par \par \uc2 We'll see you soon.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document TCARL00020220701ei6u00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  Slams Supreme Court Over "Outrageous Behavior" At NATO Summit; Supreme Court Allows "Remain In Mexico" Policy To End; \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  Effort To Reinforce Domestic And Foreign Policies; Russia Prevents Grain Exports From Shipping; Some In Law Enforcement Criticize Decisions To Release Drug Dealers Soon After Their Arrest; Sen. Rob Portman (R-OH) Interviewed On Steps U.S. Can Take To Reduce Influx Of Fentanyl Into Country; President Biden Says He Supports Exception To Senate Filibuster To Protect Access To Abortion; Threats To Pro-Life Groups Increase In Wake Of Supreme Court Ruling Overturning Roe Versus Wade; Supreme Court Rules On \uc2 EPA\uc2 's Ability To Regulate Industry; Supreme Court Rules On Whether Biden Administration Must Maintain Remain In Mexico Immigration Policy\b0\par\par\uc2 Shannon Bream, Griff Jenkins, Andy McCarthy, Jonathan Turley,  Jacqui Heinrich, General Jack Keane, Hillary Vaughn, Bret Baier, Mike  Emanuel, Harold Ford Jr. \par \uc2 7,939 \uc2 words\par \uc2 30 June 2022\par \uc2 Fox News: Special Report with Brett Baier\par \uc2 FNSPBB\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 DANA PERINO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: You should buy it.\par \par \uc2 JESSICA TARLOV, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR: We usually buy it as a unit.\par \par \uc2 GREG GUTFELD, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Yes, let's buy it.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Put a bid on it now.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Make it happen.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Yes. You could all move there.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Finance.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: You could all move there and do the show from there and get out of the city called New York.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Yes, it's probably safer there.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Speaking of, that's it for us, SPECIAL REPORT is up next with evil Shannon Bream.\par \par \uc2 SHANNON BREAM, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST (voice over): I'm working hard every day to disprove that Greg. But thank you very much. Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 Good evening. Welcome to Washington. I'm Shannon Bream at the Supreme Court in for Bret Baier.\par \par \uc2 Breaking tonight, what could be the most tumultuous and significant \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  term ever is now over. Justices issued their final rulings today. They include a significant defeat for President Biden's climate change agenda and a win, at least for now, in his bid to end the so-called Remain in Mexico immigration rule.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM (voice over): After several days of bruising losses for the left at the High Court, President Biden took a swipe at the justices while in Madrid at the NATO summit.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: The one thing that has been destabilizing is the outrageous behavior of the \uc2 Supreme Court of the United States\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Mr. Biden's remarks aimed at a co-equal branch of government came shortly before the Supreme Court's final two opinions of the term were released.\par \par \uc2 In a firm push back to the administrative state, the justices held six to three that the \uc2 EPA\uc2  went beyond its power in crafting a sweeping plan to curb carbon emissions.\par \par \uc2 Chief Justice Roberts writing for the majority said "It is not plausible that Congress gave \uc2 EPA\uc2  the authority to adopt on its own such a regulatory scheme. A decision of such magnitude and consequence rests with Congress itself or an agency acting pursuant to a clear delegation from that representative body."\par \par \uc2 A position Justice Kagan writing for the dissenters found disturbing. "The court appoints itself instead of Congress or the expert agency, the decision maker on climate policy, I cannot think of many things more frightening."\par \par \uc2 But a win for the White House in a second opinion authored by the chief which found him and justice Kavanaugh aligned with the liberal wing of the court.\par \par \uc2 The five-four decision allows the Biden administration to roll back the so- called Remain in Mexico policy announced under the Trump administration, which permitted authorities to require many asylum seekers to await their proceedings south of the border in Mexico.\par \par \uc2 KETANJI BROWN JACKSON\uc2 , ASSOCIATE JUSTICE OF SUPREME COURT: So help me God.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And history was made at the Supreme Court Thursday when Justice \uc2 Ketanji Brown Jackson\uc2  was sworn in as the first Black female justice in the court's history.\par \par \uc2 Roberts administering her constitutional oath and the man for whom Justice Jackson once clerked, the now retired Justice Breyer giving the judicial oath.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN BREYER\uc2 , FORMER ASSOCIATE JUSTICE OF SUPREME COURT: I'm pleased to welcome Justice Jackson to the court and to our common calling.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM (on camera): We are still awaiting any indication of where the courts internal leak investigation stands. There was no mention of it in the chief's traditional end of term statement today.\par \par \uc2 A Florida judge says he will temporarily block a 15 week abortion ban from taking effect. The action comes following a court challenged by abortion providers who say the state constitution guarantees a right to the procedure.\par \par \uc2 The decision will not go into effect until the judge signs the order which he says will not happen today. The law goes into effect Friday.\par \par \uc2 And a Kentucky judge has temporarily blocked that state's near total ban on abortions. The decision allows the procedures to resume after they were stopped following Friday's Supreme Court ruling.\par \par \uc2 The governor of Texas is warning the Supreme Court ruling on the border policy will lead to more problems there.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent Griff Jenkins has that part of the story from Eagle Pass tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GRIFF JENKINS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): Texas Governor Greg Abbott blasting today's decision as a green light for Biden's "open border policies".\par \par \uc2 Others welcomed the ruling, the \uc2 International Rescue Committee\uc2  who provides legal assistance to migrants says Biden can do even more to further end border externalization policies that put asylum seekers at risk.\par \par \uc2 What's unknown is how this will impact the billion dollar human smuggling industry.\par \par \uc2 Border officials say it benefits the bad guys.\par \par \uc2 BRANDON JUDD, PRESIDENT, NATIONAL BORDER PATROL COUNCIL: It's going to enrich cartels. It's going to allow them to go to countries throughout the world and advertise their services.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the driver of the tractor trailer accused of being responsible for the deaths of 53 migrants. 45-year-old Homero Zamorano, a Texas native making his first court appearance in San Antonio charged with smuggling resulting in death, facing potentially life in prison or the death penalty if convicted. It comes as more human smuggling tragedies occur.\par \par \uc2 Yesterday in La Joya, Texas, two killed, two in critical condition after a high speed chase. In this morning in Texas, seven passengers in a car that collided with an 18 wheeler. Four dead, three hospitalized. The driver, a U.S. citizen was among the injured.\par \par \uc2 SGT. ERICK ESTRADA, \uc2 TEXAS DEPARTMENT OF PUBLIC SAFETY\uc2 : These are the types of services that these criminal organizations are offering. They're offering a safe travel. But as we know, right now, what we're seeing right here, it's not safe at all.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JENKINS (on camera): And Shannon, Customs and Border Protection has yet to publicly announce an official number for border wide migrant deaths so far this fiscal year.\par \par \uc2 But Fox News has exclusively learned from CBP sources, that number is 518, in that it does not include the 53 killed Monday or those in our reporting tonight.\par \par \uc2 Last year's number, also not publicly known is 566. According to those same sources, the last publicly reported number by CBP was for the year 2020. That was 247 deaths, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Griff Jenkins, thank you very much, Griff.\par \par \uc2 And let's bring in our panel now, our legal panel Andrew McCarthy is a former assistant United States attorney and Jonathan Turley is a professor at the \uc2 George Washington University Law School\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Gentlemen, great to have you on this final day of the Supreme Court term. Let's start with what the president had to say overseas in Madrid, just before those two final opinions came out, here's the president.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: We've been a leader in the world in terms of personal rights and privacy rights. And it is a mistake, in my view for the Supreme Court to do what it did. I can understand why the American people are frustrated because of what the Supreme Court did.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And he called it outrageous behavior by the Supreme Court. What do you make of a coequal branch essentially going after another?\par \par \uc2 ANDREW MCCARTHY, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR: I think it's despicable behavior by the president. And you know, Shannon, he was elected to be kind of the anti-Trumpy return to normal, observed norms, you know, things like you don't go overseas and attack other components of the American government.\par \par \uc2 I just think, you know, in a better time in this country, he'd be censured by Congress. Unfortunately, the time in the country that we're in, this is like Thursday, and it's about as unusual as the sun coming up tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: True. OK. Professor, I want to talk to you about this administrative state issue. You've written and spoken about this quite a bit. The Wall Street Journal talking about this decision today that pushes back on the \uc2 EPA\uc2 's power.\par \par \uc2 They say the High Court said federal agencies need explicit authorization from Congress to decide issues of major economic and political significance. Drawing on a principle known as the major questions doctrine. The \uc2 EPA\uc2  powers at issue are central to President Biden's climate agenda with fragile majorities in the Senate and the House, Democrats have limited ability to advance their platform through new legislation.\par \par \uc2 So, Professor, specifically in this case, but more broadly to federal agencies, do you think this ruling will be applicable beyond the \uc2 EPA\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN TURLEY, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR: Yes, it's difficult to overstate how important this opinion is, because of this question of what's sometimes called the fourth branch of government, this new sort of entity of our collection of federal agencies that rivals the authority of Congress, and even the executive branch where it's held.\par \par \uc2 And here, the court really drew a very clear line that these major questions need to be answered by Congress.\par \par \uc2 What was really astonishing is that Majority Leader Reid and Senator Warren and others were apoplectic and saying, how dare you keep the agency from essentially doing our job, it bordered on self-loathing.\par \par \uc2 I mean, the whole point of the opinion is you're taking power away from Congress, and you have these leaders saying, you know, are you really going to force us to do something?\par \par \uc2 Actually, Senator Warren said, you know, who's going to -- who's now going to act here? Well, you act your congress. That's the whole point of the opinion.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes. And to your point, Senate Majority Leader \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  said this, first on gun safety, then on abortion and now on the environment, this MAGA regressive, extremist Supreme Court is intent on setting America back decades, if not centuries.\par \par \uc2 He then goes after the Republican-appointed majority of the MAGA court. He says pushing back to the country back to a time when robbers barons and corporate elites have complete power and average citizens have no say.\par \par \uc2 It's interesting, because today, Andy, there was a decision that was good for this administration. There was one that was not great for this administration. But we now have again the calls for packing the court, for making it over, for doing away with it. What do you think of what we're hearing on Capitol Hill?\par \par \uc2 MCCARTHY: Well, I think it's all based on a lie, Shannon, because what they say again and again, is that the court is usurping power, when what's clear from this term is what it's doing is restoring constitutional order.\par \par \uc2 So, as John just pointed out, they're giving the power to Congress that Congress rightly has under the Constitution.\par \par \uc2 In connection with the case -- the other case that you mentioned, the Remain in Mexico case, they basically made clear that this is a situation where you have the executive branch if to implement this policy has to be able to negotiate with foreign government. So, there's an interplay between the executive and congressional statutes.\par \par \uc2 In the abortion case, they didn't -- they didn't take power to the court. What they did was returned it to the states, the complaint that we've had for half a century is that the states were governing this and were regulating abortion up until 1973 when the court took the issue away from them. All the court has done here is shed power from itself and give it back to the States.\par \par \uc2 So, I think what what's happening here is, this is a court that is restoring order in the sense of the responsibilities being assigned in the places where it was supposed to be assigned as the constitution and the law were understood at the time they were whichever laws or the provisions of the Constitution were adopted.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: It's been a while and we're still waiting for word on the leaker as well. Andy and Jonathan, thank you both.\par \par \uc2 MCCARTHY: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 TURLEY: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Stocks were off. The Dow lost 254, the S&P 500 dropped 33, NASDAQ fell 149.\par \par \uc2 Well, President Biden has just arrived back at the White House following his trip to Europe to meet with leaders there about the economy, energy and \uc2 NATO\uc2 's response to Russia's invasion of Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 The president says the U.S. will bolster its military presence in Europe and that \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  will feel what the president calls the \uc2 NATO\uc2  isolation of Europe.\par \par \uc2 White House correspondent Jacqui Heinrich reports tonight from Madrid, Spain. Good evening, Jacqui.\par \par \uc2 JACQUI HEINRICH, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Good evening, Shannon. The biggest achievement of this summit, a major hurdle clear to Finland and \uc2 NATO\uc2  joining the alliance after Turkey dropped its opposition. But even as the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2  prepares to ratify the new members, another country may now stand in the way.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: This summit was about strengthening our alliance.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice over): Russian President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  promising to put nuclear weapons and missiles on the new member's doorsteps if it happens, not shaking U.S. resolved.\par \par \uc2 SEN. THOM TILLIS (R-NC): And no amount of saber rattling I think is going to change us or change our opinions.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Biden is so excited about \uc2 NATO\uc2 's growth he helped deliver making a flub sure to stir Putin, calling a phone call with the Finnish leader.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: He suggested we call the leader of Switzerland. Switzerland, my goodness, I'm getting really anxious here about expanding \uc2 NATO\uc2 . Oh, Sweden.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: But the process isn't yet finished, parliaments and all 30 \uc2 NATO\uc2  countries have to ratify the applications. And Bulgaria may stand in the way.\par \par \uc2 SEN. JONI ERNST (R-IA): There are a few countries. I'm not going to call them out by name today. But the Secretary of Defense did mention to me offline about one country in particular who draws 90 percent of its energy resources from Russia.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: A short review of which \uc2 NATO\uc2  countries consume the most Russian energy puts Bulgaria at the top.\par \par \uc2 Biden now returns home to the problems he left: high gas prices and inflation. And he's beginning to face questions about whether support for Ukraine should be indefinite.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: How long is it fair to expect American drivers and drivers around the world to pay that premium for this war?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: As long as it takes. Russia cannot in fact defeat Ukraine and move beyond Ukraine. This is a critical critical position for the world.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Republicans and Democrats in Madrid agree that's the right answer. But they're split over how to bring down energy costs at home.\par \par \uc2 ERNST: The object is most certainly to push back against Russia.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Welcome words to Biden.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: The reason why gas prices are up is because of Russia, Russia, Russia, Russia. The reason why the foods crisis exist, is because of Russia.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (on camera): And whether that's true is disputed, including by the Chairman of the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 . Jerome Powell recently testified on Capitol Hill that Russia's war in Ukraine is not the main driver of inflation, and inflation was up before the war broke out, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Indeed. Jacqui Heinrich in Madrid. Thank you, Jacqui.\par \par \uc2 Up next, Gen. Jack Keane with an analysis of the latest developments in the Russian invasion of Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 And later, the effort to make drug dealers pay a price for the fentanyl crisis.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Russian forces have withdrawn from a strategic Black Sea island after relentless Ukrainian attacks. Russia is portraying the pull-out from Snake Island off the port of the city of Odessa as a goodwill gesture.\par \par \uc2 But Russian troops maintain their push to encircle the last stronghold of Ukrainian resistance in an eastern province.\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in Fox News Senior strategic analyst, retired General Jack Keane. General, always good to see you.\par \par \uc2 GEN. JACK KEANE (RET.), FOX NEWS CHANNEL SENIOR STRATEGIC ANALYST (on camera): Yes, it's great to see you, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: I'm going to start off with a little bit of what the president had to say before leaving the \uc2 NATO\uc2  summit in Madrid.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: The reason why gas prices are up is because of Russia. Russia, Russia, Russia. The reason why the food crisis exists is because of Russia. Russia not allowing grain to get out of Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: General, Russia is certainly a major foreign policy problem around the world. But how fair do you think it is for the president to also assign blame for a domestic issues with what's going on with Russia?\par \par \uc2 Well, certainly, they are a contributing factor. But as our experts have said, time and time again, all of that began before the Russian invasion in terms of gas prices and inflation.\par \par \uc2 I mean, it's convenient to use this as sort of an excuse for likely other policy failures, but it's not the real issue here.\par \par \uc2 Certainly, what Russia is doing inside Ukraine truly matters. And I think where we are, frankly, Shannon, is we're at a tipping point here, because Russia is economically devastating Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 The economy has contracted about 35 percent and setting to 50, and a potentially economic failed state, and the reason is Russia was cut off Ukraine's agriculture exports, which the economy is dependent on.\par \par \uc2 The second thing, if we've been witnessing this for weeks now, Shannon, is that Russia has gained military momentum. They're using their artillery which they have more of and then out guns the Ukrainians in terms of range, and they ground down the Ukrainian slowly, methodically, and they have gained more territory, particularly in the eastern Donbas region. But they've suffered significant casualties, Shannon, the Russian military. Ukraine's casualties have gone up as well.\par \par \uc2 So, what we think is going to happen here? Russia, before they continue the attack in the Donbas region to the west and conduct other operations, they're going to have to rearm and refit. This presents an opportunity to president --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: General, quickly -- Yes, quickly, I was going to ask you about Snake Island. What do you make of the Russians giving that up? Saying, it's a goodwill gesture, the Ukrainian said, they have just dug in and fight -- fought to keep it.\par \par \uc2 KEANE: It -- listen, given up territory, and particularly something is symbolic as Snake Island. Certainly, that matters. I don't think the Ukrainian is going to occupy this very quickly, because they got their hands full doing other things.\par \par \uc2 Russia is still going to be able to block the cargo ships from coming into the port of Odessa. It's still going to shut down Ukraine's economy.\par \par \uc2 So, yes, it's important. But it's not incredibly consequential, it would be the best way to express it.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Maybe -- yes maybe --\par \par \uc2 KEANE: What I was trying to say before, is that what is happening here, Ukraine, will have an opportunity here in several weeks to be able to conduct consequential counter attacks. But it's going to be completely dependent in my judgment on what has happened at these two meetings that you referenced: The \uc2 G7\uc2  and also \uc2 NATO\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 We don't know for sure, because they -- what's in the reports that are coming out, they are so general.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes, won't tell us the full picture.\par \par \uc2 KEANE: We don't know what the level of commitment is going to be to provide the arms ammunitions. And we know this problems there. Some of the countries are not all in.\par \par \uc2 France and Germany, not all in. Some other ones are not. The United States is, the U.K. is, and others are.\par \par \uc2 Zelenskyy and his military will not be able to succeed unless they get those munitions.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes, they continue --\par \par \uc2 KEANE: And they have to get them in the right quantity. And they have to get them timely. That's what I'm saying about, it's a tipping point.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes. General, thank you. We always appreciate your insights.\par \par \uc2 KEANE: OK, good talking too, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: You too.\par \par \uc2 Up next, parents fighting for justice after their children die from fentanyl overdoses.\par \par \uc2 Plus, we're going to talk with Senator Rob Portman about what the government should be doing.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Senator \uc2 Patrick Leahy\uc2  of Vermont has undergone surgery to repair a broken hip. His office says the 82-year-old Democrat fell Wednesday night in his Virginia home. He is expected to make a full recovery.\par \par \uc2 Leahy is the longest serving member of the Senate. He announced last November, he would not seek reelection this fall.\par \par \uc2 Latest Fox News poll indicates a growing number of Americans say they're not proud of their country. 56 percent feel that way. Just 39 percent say they are proud. Those numbers are off significantly from just years ago.\par \par \uc2 46 percent of Democrats say they're proud of the U.S. 48 percent say no. 36 percent of Republicans are proud, 60 percent are not. For Independents, just 29 percent are proud, 64 percent say they aren't.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, we continue our series on the poisoning of America, by looking at the fight of parents to get their children's overdose deaths classified as crimes. They want those responsible charged with homicide.\par \par \uc2 Fox Business correspondent Hilary Vaughn has our reports.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MIKE BOUDREAUX, SHERIFF, TULARE COUNTY: Law enforcement is frustrated.\par \par \uc2 HILLARY VAUGHN, FOX BUSINESS NETWORK CORRESPONDENT (voice-over): If you do the crime, you got to do the time. But police say, that's not always the case.\par \par \uc2 BOUDREAUX: We arrest people, and we see them out of custody within hours of making an arrest.\par \par \uc2 VAUGHN: Last week in central California, sheriffs deputies caught two traffickers with a truckload of fentanyl, but a judge signed court order let them go.\par \par \uc2 BOUDREAUX: Let's just go back and make the use of drugs and the sale of drugs a felony where you have to be held accountable and serve time in jail.\par \par \uc2 VAUGHN: Parents whose children are being sold poison pills with deadly doses of fentanyl want answers. Eighteen-year-old Adriana Davies died after taking Percocet she didn't know was laced with fentanyl. The drug dealer accused is an illegal immigrant who was out on bail for another crime.\par \par \uc2 JEFF DAVIES\uc2 , FATHER: This person should have at least been either behind bars or been deported.\par \par \uc2 VAUGHN: Families fighting for justice want drug dealers behind bars for murder if the drugs they sell kill. But in states like California with softer drug laws, federal prosecutors taking their case might be their only hope.\par \par \uc2 DAVIES: It ties the hands of the police. It ties the hands of the district attorneys. And so they are having to find other ways to go about prosecuting these cases.\par \par \uc2 VAUGHN: It took a retired Chicago police officer five months to convince police her daughter's overdose was foul play.\par \par \uc2 TERRY ALMANZA, MOTHER: How are these other parents that don't have this background in law enforcement able to get some form of justice?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 VAUGHN (on camera): And so many parents never get that closure. The conviction rate for fentanyl dealers in some places is zero. San Francisco had 640 overdoses last year, but the now recalled district attorney failed to convict anyone for dealing fentanyl in 2021. Shannon?\par \par \uc2 SHANNON BREAM, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Hillary Vaughn, thank you very much for that look.\par \par \uc2 And for more on the fentanyl crisis, here is my colleague, Bret Baier.\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Shannon, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Continuing our series on the poisoning of America and the flow of fentanyl into the country across the border, a lot of it coming from China, joining us to talk about that, Ohio Republican Senator Rob Portman, a member of Homeland Security Committee. Senator, thanks for being here.\par \par \uc2 SEN. ROB PORTMAN, (R-OH): Bret, always good to be on with you.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We have been doing this series, and all elements of it. It is impossible to overstate how big a deal this is.\par \par \uc2 PORTMAN: We focus a lot on the border because of the people coming across, and last month was the most illegal entries ever again, as it was the previous month. What we don't talk about as much is the fact that a lot of drugs are coming as well, and sometimes with those individuals. So we know last month that there were 1,100 pounds seized of fentanyl, that's a record. By the way, it has quadrupled in the last two years, doubled in the last year. That's enough to kill, they say, 200 million Americans.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We've got these maps showing that a lot of it is coming from China, some going to Canada, Mexico, into the U.S. But the stuff mainly going across the border from Mexico is the biggest problem. You mentioned the crossings and the seizures, 2022, 7,745 pounds. That's down from 2021, 11,102 pounds. And you mentioned the legality. \uc2 The Heritage Foundation\uc2  says it takes only two milligrams to be lethal. That's not even enough to cover the year on the front of a penny in your pocket. I don't think most parents fully get that. You said you were giving a speech at Ohio State, and you had alter it because of deaths they are.\par \par \uc2 PORTMAN: Yes, a few days before the Ohio State graduation, three students overdosed and died. It appears as though it was them thinking they were taking Adderall, which is a study drug that young people take. They apparently bought these on the street. So it was very sad, because my talk was going to be more inspiring about the future, but I had to include in that up including the fact that to all the parents and all the students who were there, you cannot trust any drug, any street drug.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: The cause of death for Americans aged 18 to 45, we have got this stat in 2019, suicide, fentanyl was way up there. But in 2020, fentanyl goes through the roof. I guess what I want to know is, from your perspective, what could this administration be doing differently? Is there a public element to this? Are they doing enough? Where do you think this falls?\par \par \uc2 PORTMAN: I think there are three obvious things to do. One is to tighten the border. This border problem is about contraband. It's not just people. And specifically, it's about this opioid that is synthetic that is deadly that's causing about two-thirds of the overdose deaths in America.\par \par \uc2 Second, we have got to figure out a national message here that make sense to get out to people. I think it's worth spending some money by the administration to do a national media campaign. We did it at one time, and it was somewhat effective. Nancy Reagan was famous for that. But it's a pretty obvious message right now, which is simply any drug you take can kill you.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Any drug.\par \par \uc2 PORTMAN: Any drug.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And even in the smallest amount.\par \par \uc2 PORTMAN: So it is being mixed in everything, cocaine, methamphetamine, heroin, and so on. But even drugs, again, like antidepressants, Xanax, and so on. So that message needs to get out and needs to get out urgently to save lives.\par \par \uc2 Third, more prevention, which is this message we're talking about, but also getting people into treatment and longer return recovery.\par \par \uc2 So it's a combination of things. Obviously the other part of this, which is not so much what we need to do but what these other countries need to do with our strong urging, is China and Mexico need to clean up their act.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We talked to Border Patrol officials. Here is what they said.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BRANDON JUDD, NATIONAL BORDER PATROL COUNCIL: What the cartels do is they flood our resources with illegal immigrants. It pulls those agents out on the field, and it creates gaps in coverage. That's when they are able to cross their higher value products, such as fentanyl, criminal aliens, aliens from special interest countries.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Basically, it's like a water balloon. You hit it one place and they go someplace else. And we have major, major gaps along that border.\par \par \uc2 PORTMAN: You're absolutely right. So they are distracted by a group on purpose. This is deliberate distraction, and then quietly you see guys with backpacks running through the deserts who are carrying something. And the assumption from the Border Patrol that I talk to is that they think most of it is getting through. So I do think tightening up the border makes sense.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Is this an attack by China? Do you think that this is going after our country this way specifically?\par \par \uc2 PORTMAN: I have always felt that China was being irresponsible in how they dealt with the issue. I went to China a few years ago and actually met with a premiere on this and made a strong pitch, saying, again, this is in your interest, too, because there's leakage in China. People are dying from it. They would never acknowledge that, they won't talk about that, but this hurts them, too.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We think it's important, and we're putting some time and effort into covering it.\par \par \uc2 PORTMAN: Sure, thank you.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And Senator, we appreciate your time today.\par \par \uc2 PORTMAN: Thanks, Bret, always good to be on with you.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Shannon, we'll send it back to you.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Bret, thanks so much.\par \par \uc2 Up next, we're going to take a look at the troubling increase of attacks against churches and other pro-life institutions following the decision to overturn Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Breaking night, President Biden says he would support an exception to the Senate filibuster in order to protect access to abortion. The statement comes in the wake of last week's \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  opinion overturning Roe v. Wade. That has also led to an increase in attacks on pro-life institutions and groups, including churches. Chief Washington correspondent Mike Emanuel shows us tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 MIKE EMANUEL, FOX NEWS CHIEF WASHINGTON CORRESPONDENT: FOX News has learned federal law enforcement has told lawmakers the majority of the threats after last week's landmark Supreme Court abortion ruling have been directed toward pro-life groups by anarchists or pro-choice activists.\par \par \uc2 BILLY ATWELL, DIOCESE OF ARLINGTON, VIRGINIA: There are times when it seems like some elected officials are a little bit quiet about acts of religious discrimination, and we would hope that everybody can appreciate that we live in a society where we're going to disagree on key moral issues, key political issues, but that everyone can agree, violence is unacceptable.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Activists from the far left group Jane's Revenge who have been acting in the name of abortion rights claimed responsibility for an arson attack at CompassCare, a pregnancy center near Buffalo, New York, with graffiti left behind that says, "Jane was here." Lynchburg, Virginia, police are investigating vandalism and property damage at the Blue Ridge Pregnancy Center. Officers report the building was sprayed with graffiti and multiple windows were broken out. Security camera footage shows four masked suspects committing the acts.\par \par \uc2 In Fairfax County, Virginia, the St. John Neumann Catholic Church was vandalized with abortion-related graffiti. Investigators found evidence of a fire outside the church saying an accelerant was likely used. The graffiti on the church sign warning this won't stop.\par \par \uc2 MALLORY CARROLL, SUSAN B. ANTHONY PRO-LIFE AMERICA: This is an unprecedented level of violence and vandalism against pro-life Americans.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL (on camera): FOX News has learned federal authorities are warning attacks on churches and pro-life centers could be classified as hate crimes. The Archdiocese of Washington tells FOX it remains in close contact with law enforcement due to, quote, increased risk to Catholic institutions. Shannon?\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Mike, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the final Supreme Court ruling of the term and President Biden's trip to Europe.\par \par \uc2 First, here's what some of our FOX affiliates around the country are covering tonight. FOX 11 in Los Angeles has a memorial service for two suburban policemen killed in an ambush. Sergeant Michael Paredes and Officer Joseph Santana were gunned down June 14th while responding to a reported stabbing at a motel. Both leave wives and young children behind. The gunman died of a self-inflicted wound to the head.\par \par \uc2 FOX 2 in Detroit as former Michigan Governor \uc2 Rick Snyder\uc2  invokes his right against self-incrimination and refuses to answer questions at a civil trial arising from led contamination in Flint's water in 2014 and 15. The testimony comes two days after the Michigan Supreme Court ruled in a separate case that criminal indictments against Snyder and eight other people were invalid.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at Denver. One of the big stories tonight from FOX 31, the parade for the Stanley Cup champion \uc2 Colorado Avalanche\uc2 . The franchise won its third cup Sunday, wrapping up game six with a victory over Tampa Bay.\par \par \uc2 That is tonight's live look outside the beltway from SPECIAL REPORT. We will be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Are you the best messenger to carry this forward when Democrats, many of them, many progressives want you to do more?\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , (D) PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Yes, I am. I'm the president of the United States of America. That makes me the best messenger. And I really think that it's a serious, serious problem that the court has thrust upon the United States.\par \par \uc2 THOMAS DUPREE, FORMER DEPUTY ASSISTANT ATTORNEY GENERAL: It's all fine if the president wants to criticize a particular decision, but when he starts launching these attacks at the integrity of the court, I think that has very harmful long-term ramifications. I don't think it's good for the nation.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Let's bring in our panel, Harold Ford Jr., former Tennessee Congressman and co-host of THE FIVE, Kimberley Strassel, a member of the editorial board at "The Wall Street Journal," and Matthew Continetti, fellow at the \uc2 American Enterprise Institute\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Harold, I'll start with you. You heard what the president said there and the response from Tom Dupree, former DOJ lawyer. What do you make of the president, the leader of this country, the leader of your party going after the Supreme Court?\par \par \uc2 HAROLD FORD JR., FORMER TENNESSEE REPRESENTATIVE: First, thanks for having me. I think that when you consider the court standing, which concerns me more than anything right now when we look at some of these polls that suggests that maybe it's somewhere in the 20s, the mid-20s or the high 20s range, that is concerning.\par \par \uc2 I probably would not have done it the way that the president did overseas. I think that all politicians should refrain, U.S. politicians should from attacking or addressing political domestic issues like this when they are overseas.\par \par \uc2 But I have got to tell you, the court -- as I listened and read this decision today about the \uc2 EPA\uc2 , it struck me in some ways, I was curious if the court actually honestly believes that the \uc2 EPA\uc2  exceeded their authority or they disagreed with the position of the \uc2 EPA\uc2 . And I am not here to say that I think that \uc2 EPA\uc2  may have -- I don't necessarily fully agree with what the \uc2 EPA\uc2  has done in the situation, but I don't quite understand the decision in some ways.\par \par \uc2 And it leads me to believe if you look at the last two weeks of the courts, they have done more legislating than the Congress has done in the last two years. If I were progressives, and I am not a progressive, progressives should be more focused on identifying solutions and paths forward and not criticizing the president. That doesn't do anyone any good, and it certainly doesn't lead us to better answers and outcomes around choice for women and guns in New York where I live.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Let's talk about the \uc2 EPA\uc2  decision. I want to play something from former \uc2 EPA\uc2  director, administrator \uc2 Carol Browner\uc2 . Then we'll dig in.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAROL BROWNER\uc2 , FORMER EPA ADMINISTRATOR: It's devastating. It is yet another example of the court's unwillingness to protect the health and safety of the American people. It has tied \uc2 EPA\uc2 's hands behind its back. It has limited its ability to do what is smart in terms of addressing climate change.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And Matthew, there are plenty of people on the other side in U.S. industry who are celebrating this check on administrative power and the administrative state in the U.S. What do you make of this?\par \par \uc2 MATTHEW CONTINETTI, AMERICAN ENTERPRISE INSTITUTE: The \uc2 EPA\uc2 's job isn't to do what it wants to do. It's to do what Congress tells it to do. And that's precisely the problem, Shannon. For decades now, Congress has delegated authority to these unelected and unaccountable agencies, and they have used it to fob off responsibility for their own political decision-making. What the Supreme Court has done in recent weeks is ay to the elected branch of government you have to decide, you write the laws, not the courts and not the bureaucracies. And I think that's a good thing.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And a win for their Biden administration today, Kimberley, in the other big case, the remain in Mexico, get a little bit of reaction to that from folks seeing this and living it firsthand down at the border.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DAN PATRICK\uc2 , (R) TEXAS LIEUTENANT GOVERNOR: Very disappointed in the decision today. This is devastating for the state of Texas and the United States of America.\par \par \uc2 BRANDON JUDD, NATIONAL BORDER PATROL COUNCIL: If we don't get help, we're going to continue to see this chaos. There's going to be more deaths, and we're going to continue to see more people getting away. That's the problem that we are facing now. And we were hoping that the Supreme Court was going to give us a ruling that was going to allow us to get this under control. And unfortunately, they didn't.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So Kimberley, the real world impact of this is going to make things much tougher on Border Patrol and those down at the border, but as a friend of mine noted today, this also means the border is fully owned by the Biden administration because of this decision.\par \par \uc2 KIMBERLEY STRASSEL, "WALL STREET JOURNAL": Yes, you can definitely understand the frustrations of those down on the border, but I have a harder time laying all of this on the feet of the Supreme Court. This was a very messy decision because there are a lot of statutes in play here. And one of the things that fundamentally came out if you read the totality of the opinions and concurrences and dissent is that this is fundamentally a function of the fact that Congress will not sit down and actually come up with a real immigration policy, one that -- or give the resources necessary to those agencies down at the border to actually do the job that they need to do.\par \par \uc2 And so we keep kicking this to future administrations and asking the court to weigh in. But fundamentally we are going to have to address the root cause, which is a broken legislative system.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And much on the Hill is broken, people would argue, from both sides of the aisle. There have been times when Republicans have fully controlled the White House and both houses of Congress, Democrats have done the same, and yet we don't get immigration reform in any substantive way.\par \par \uc2 Now, the president is talking today about potentially getting rid of the filibuster because of the abortion issue. Here is what he is saying today versus what he said as a senator back in 2005.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , (D) PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I believe we have to codify Roe v. Wade in the law, and the way to do that is to make sure that Congress votes that. And if the filibuster gets in the way, like voting rights, it should be we should provide an exception for this, the required exception of the filibuster.\par \par \uc2 Folks who want to see this change want to eliminate one of the procedural mechanisms designed for the express purpose of guaranteeing individual rights, and they also as a consequence would undermine the protections of a minority point of view in the heat of majority excess.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Kimberley, it sounds like, though, today both Senators Manchin and Sinema are indicating they're not on board with getting rid of the filibuster on this topic or any other.\par \par \uc2 STRASSEL: Yes, and it makes you wonder why as a result the president went there. I think the Democrats feel this enormous obligation to respond to their base which is frustrated and demoralized by this and show that they really are pressing some sort of forward action. But again, it is just doomed to hit up against these two senators that have already made it clear that they weren't willing to do any sort of exception when it came to other things that Democrats say are very important, like voting rights.\par \par \uc2 So I don't see this going anywhere, and I think there is a potential some risk for Democrats, too, of nationalizing this issue. Republicans did that for a lot of years trying to get antiabortion legislation and it didn't necessarily help them out on the ground politically.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Republican, or excuse me, Democratic Representative \uc2 Alexandria Ocasio-Cortez\uc2 , she was tweeting in reaction to the \uc2 EPA\uc2  decision as it was breaking on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  this morning. She said this, "Catastrophic. A filibuster carveout is not enough. We need to do reform or do away with the whole thing for the sake of the planet." Now, I wasn't sure if she first meant the Supreme Court. I think, though, Matthew, she is referring to the filibuster there.\par \par \uc2 CONTINETTI: You never know with AOC, do you, right? I'm sure she would be open to packing the court or any of these major structural changes to government that progressives want because they can't achieve their aims through legislation. And that's the real problem for the left right now. they need to face the voters, and when voters actually see what the left wants to achieve, more often than not, especially on social and cultural or green issues, voters blanche.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Harold, I want to ask you about something "The Wall Street Journal" cautioned about, cautioning Democrats saying, listen, a change to the filibuster rules is going to open the doors to Republicans for things that they may want to pass. We saw this when then Senator Harry Reid changed the rules on judges, and we saw where that has led us. Do you think there should be caution for Democrats against blowing out the filibuster at this point?\par \par \uc2 FORD: I've never been one that has been a big believer in changing the filibuster rules. I understand the impulse on the part of a lot of Democrats, but I think you take that energy, you channel that energy at the ballot box.\par \par \uc2 I think Republicans who believe last week that a sweep of the House and the Senate was something that was certainty or foregone, I think they should relook at this and perhaps a reset will be needed, because I think a majority of the country is against what a lot of the things the court has done over the last two weeks, and we'll see how it plays out. But I am against changing the filibuster rule for these things.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And even more importantly, two current members of the Senate, as we talked about, \uc2 Joe Manchin\uc2  and \uc2 Kyrsten Sinema\uc2 , are against it. They will stand as a block, and we'll see if anything changes as we look at the makeup of Congress after the midterms in just mere weeks from now.\par \par \uc2 Panel, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Finally tonight, a SPECIAL REPORT salute. Florida golfer Michael West calmly lined up his swing despite knowing an alligator, that's right, was stalking him on the green. The man unfazed by the large reptile says he didn't think anything of it and would actually take the same shot again. That is my home state representing where alligators are everywhere.\par \par \uc2 OK, when a Georgia mother ran out of a special type of formula for her six- month-old premature twins, an army of strangers rose up, went into beast mode, quickly shipping her the formula she needed from all over the country to help these precious little girls. The mom is thankful for the support and she says she hopes that everyone who wanted to help her daughter, and they have, will now go out and donate to a local baby supply bank to help others who are in need. Precious.\par \par \uc2 And tomorrow on SPECIAL REPORT, what U.S. tech company are doing to fight Russian disinformation. Thank you for watching SPECIAL REPORT. I'm Shannon Bream. Please join me at "FOX NEWS @ NIGHT", midnight eastern, 9:00 pacific as the bells toll here on Capitol Hill and the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 "JESSE WATTERS PRIMETIME" is up next. Hey, Jesse.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FNSPBB0020220701ei6u00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  Speaking At A NATO Summit In Madrid Blaming Russia For High Gas Prices And Talks About His Made-Up Stories On Inflation While His Approval Sinks 38 Percent; Biden Also Blasting The \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  On Its Decision On Abortion And Wants The Filibuster Gone For Abortion; Horrific Crime: Mother Pushing Baby Stroller Shot Dead In NYC; Woke Liberals Destroying Dems' Midterm Chances; People Want To See Friends Four Times A Month\b0\par\par\uc2 Greg Gutfeld, Jeanine Pirro, Jessica Tarlov, Jesse Watters, Dana  Perino \par \uc2 8,714 \uc2 words\par \uc2 30 June 2022\par \uc2 Fox News: The Five\par \uc2 FOXFIV\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 GIANNO CALDWELL, FOX NEWS POLITICAL ANALYST: You've been very helpful to me. You've been a big brother to me and I want to thank you. You shared the information with the network and you were the first person I reached out to. So, I want to thank you for just being there in my life. You're a great man.\par \par \uc2 NEIL CAVUTO, FOX NEWS: Gianno, words can't say. We're thinking of Christian and hope you find the answers.\par \par \uc2 GREG GUTFELD, FOX NEWS HOST: Hi, I'm Greg Gutfeld along with Judge Jeanine Pirro, Jessica Tarlov, Jesse Watters, and she almost drowned at the beach, but the lifeguard threw her a cheerio, Dana Perino. "The Five."\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES OF AMERICA: All right, guys. No, there's no such thing as a quick one. I'm out of here. Thank you all very much.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JEANINE PIRRO, FOX NEWS HOST: Oh my god.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Inspirational. President Biden cutting his losses after a dumpster fire press conference where he tried to convince the world everything under his leadership is just dandy. The latest RealClear Politics average puts him at 38 percent approval, the lowest of his presidency so far.\par \par \uc2 Biden getting asked why so many Americans think the country is on the wrong track, but all he could muster up was his usual nonsense on Russia and bogus claims about inflation.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I can understand why the American people are frustrated because of inflation, but inflation is higher in almost every other country. Prices at the pump are higher in almost every other country. We're better positioned to deal with this than anyone, but we have a way to go. Ultimately, the reason why gas prices are up is because of Russia. Russia, Russia, Russia.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: How long is it fair to expect American drivers and drivers around the world to pay that premium for this war?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: As long as it takes. Russia cannot, in fact, defeat Ukraine and move beyond Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Jeez Louise. And the guy who helped Obama get elected thinks Joe isn't cut out for this line of work.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DAVID AXELROD, FORMER OBAMA ADVISER: When the president got into those questions, gas prices and inflation and abortion rights, there was a lot less of that certainty, a lot less of that emphatic nature of his initial presentation on \uc2 NATO\uc2  because he doesn't have great answers.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: And as things go south, Biden is being forced to apply for the job he already has. The president has been trying to convince Democrats at fundraisers that he is ready for 2024, arguing that he's the only one who can beat Trump.\par \par \uc2 Apparently, Jessica, he's hoping that Trump runs. You know, do you believe he's running in 2024? Be honest. Let's pretend we're not on T.V. right now. We're just over at a bar having a drink.\par \par \uc2 JESSICA TARLOV, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: This would not be what I would talk about if we were just at a bar having a drink. I actually do think that he is planning on running in 2024. He's in excellent health. I think he does have a strong case based on 2020 that he is the only one who could beat Trump.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: What's your definition of excellent health? There's a cognitive - -\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: I think he could probably run for longer than I could.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Yes, but you know, so could a clock, but you need something with a brain.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: I'm sorry. I'm just going to shift a little bit here. I think what's always interesting, when you see David Axelrod or David Plouffe talking about these things is you see the real fissures between Obama world and Biden world, and they're getting a little bit of those jabs.\par \par \uc2 There are still some resentments obviously about what happened in 2016 and Hillary not winning and carrying on Obama's legacy, and genuine concern that in 2024, if it is anyone but Trump, I think that Trump is the easiest candidate for Democrats to beat in 2024, that we will end up losing.\par \par \uc2 He is correct though about these bad answers and I really don't know why Biden can't just say it's a really tough time, and it's a tough time in a lot of countries, and we don't have to compare them. You don't have to say we have, you know, we're doing better than these places. You're over there presenting as a unit, right, with your \uc2 NATO\uc2  allies.\par \par \uc2 Just talk about that and just say everyone is suffering, this can't be all Russia's fault. We do know about gas production actually increasing in the United States, but just own it a little bit and Americans will smile fondly upon that. They're cool with the honest truth about it.\par \par \uc2 And it doesn't mean necessarily you'll win the election, but you won't have these news cycles about being detached from reality. They're obviously not helpful to the presidency.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: He can't help him, though. That's the problem. I mean, you're asking him to change his stripes and he could barely change his pants. Jesse, so here we just have him admit. Okay, we know that we have hyperinflation. We know that we got a crime wave. We know we have general malaise. He basically just said, yes, we're in it, we're going to pay for a never-ending war. You're going to have to suck it up. That's pretty incredible.\par \par \uc2 JESSE WATTERS, FOX NEWS HOST: That's the policy. His policy is to bog Russia down in Ukraine and bleed them dry --\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: And they're doing great.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: -- and it could take years.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Yes. And Russia is -- the ruble is doing fine.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: So, to the American driver that doesn't sound like that's a plan to reduce gas prices. It looks like that's no plan at all. This thing is going to go on forever. His policy is basically like things are bad, not as bad as Mexico and none of it is my fault and I have no plans to fix it.\par \par \uc2 You can't tell the American people that. You can't tell American the American people inflation is bad but it's worse somewhere else. We don't live somewhere else. We live here in the United States of America. That's like saying, don't worry about crime, you should see Brazil.\par \par \uc2 DANA PERINO, FOX NEWS HOST: Yes.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: We don't live in Brazil. We live here. And then he keeps on saying, oh, well, you know, it's Russia's fault with the gas and inflation. The Fed chair last week came out and said it's not. So, he's just kind of walking right through a blown-up talking point, getting shrapnel all in his calves, and he's like he doesn't even feel it.\par \par \uc2 He's oblivious to the fact that he's making no sense at all. He's been to Europe at least twice. Has he ever been to an oil rig? Has he ever been to the border? Has he ever been to a baby formula factory? Has he ever been anywhere in this country where people say, yes, that's where you should be? No. He loves flying across the Atlantic and talking about \uc2 NATO\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Yes. He hasn't been to our border but he knows the Ukrainian border quite well, judge. So, it looks to me like we're going to be paying for a foreign war for a long time while we suffer. Gas prices aren't going away. They're going up.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: You know what the amazing part of it is, you remember when the president said that he was going to be the adult in the room? He uses every excuse like a child's excuse like, you heard him say when he was asked a question at the press conference, "I'm out of here," You know, that's like a kid picking up his ball and his bat and saying "I'm out of here, I'm not playing with you anymore."\par \par \uc2 Or you know, this his whole thing, you know, he did it. I didn't do it. He did it. It's his fault. I mean, he really forgets the fact that he gave a waiver for Nord Stream to allow Russia to get some oil and start the war. I mean, he doesn't take any responsibility for that. He doesn't take any responsibility for the fact that he is lying. He is openly lying to the American people about everyone's inflation is worse than ours.\par \par \uc2 That is a fact. That is something that can be statistically proven. And, you know, he just says it and he get away with it. He doesn't understand why people aren't out there saying, Joe, go get them. Joe, you're the best. You've got a depressed country right now in the United States. You've got a fearful country. You've got families who are worried about drugs coming through the border.\par \par \uc2 And, you know, the president can't even give us good news. It's like how long are we going to have to pay these prices? Oh, I don't know. It's all about the war. It's Russia, Russia, Russia. Well, that didn't work for the first four years of Trump's term. So, they're going to try it again and it's not going to work.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Yes. But in his defense, we supported the war in Ukraine. So, he's just giving us what we want. Do you want to hear from Hillary?\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Sure.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Alright. Hillary, what do you think?\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HILLARY CLINTON\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: And if he decides to run, he's a sitting president, and you know --\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: Would you endorse him?\par \par \uc2 CLINTON: Look, I would endorse our sitting president. Yes, of course. I mean, you know, this is a silly question. So, let's go to the person most likely to win. \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  beat in a huge landslide victory in the popular vote \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . I think that says a lot.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: What do you think?\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Well, she's out there, but also Governor Pritzker of Illinois, he's out there. Today he said, basically that -- he basically put it out on the table, that he wants to primary him, that he's going to run. So, the president is coming back to the United States with not any -- no good answers. Like there are ways to bring down gas prices while you also support the war in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: You could drill for more here.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Right. That's true.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: But that's never going to be an answer that he provides.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: So, you actually can do that. He just won't do that. Instead, what is the next trip he's taking? It's not to the border. It's to Saudi Arabia.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: And also, you have people in his administration making a little trip down to Venezuela supposedly to talk to one of the Americans being held there, but you know, we also know that they were going to see if Venezuela could start pumping more oil.\par \par \uc2 A big deal amongst Democrats today is this piece by Ryan Grim at "The Intercept" about -- it's called "The Elephant in the Zoom." And it's all about the internal fighting amongst the Democrats. That's where the real political fight is.\par \par \uc2 I mean, they always like to talk about the Republicans, but this is a fracturing party. I absolutely think that you're going to see a primary. Hillary, I don't think she's going to run necessarily, but she loves to be out there.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Yes, that's true. Alright, up next, President Biden bashing the Supreme Court and following the far left on abortion.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  massively caving to the far left over abortion and bashing the Supreme Court while overseas. The president now calling for eliminating the filibuster to legalize abortion nationwide, even though he had previously said it was a very dangerous thing to do.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: The one thing that has been destabilizing is the outrageous behavior of the \uc2 Supreme Court of the United States\uc2  on overruling, not only Roe v. Wade, but essentially challenging the right to privacy.\par \par \uc2 I believe we have to codify Roe v. Wade in the law. And the way to do that is to make sure that Congress votes to do that. And if the filibuster gets in the way, it's like voting rights, it should be we provide an exception for this -- require an exception to the filibuster for this action to deal with the Supreme Court decision.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Okay. Biden's new stance is making AOC quite happy, quote, "Now we're talking! Time for people to see a real forceful push for it. Use the bully pulpit. We need more." And it looks like Biden is more than happy to appease the squad. Listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: Are you the best messenger to carry this forward, when Democrats, many of them, many progressives, want you to do?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Yes, I am. I'm the president of the United States of America. That makes me the best messenger.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Alright. Jessica, it's not -- they're not going to blow up the filibuster. I mean, there is Sinema and Manchin says no way, right? So, what is he talking about?\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Well, Sinema has already said no way. Manchin, we haven't heard from on this particular point. I would doubt that he would go back on it, but he does feel very personally aggrieved that he says that he was lied to by the Supreme Court justices in their private meetings about how they would whether Roe v. Wade would set a precedent.\par \par \uc2 I still don't think that it is going to happen, but I want to push back on the idea that this is only, you know, far left progressives that want to see joe Biden take action on this. It's a very standard Democratic position right now that something needs to be done to get Roe v. Wade codified on some level on a national stage. That there has to be federal protections.\par \par \uc2 I've been talking about the fact that I think that you could probably get even some moderate Republicans to sign on to some production like maybe nearer as what we have in Europe, 12 weeks, 15 weeks as a baseline minimum so the people who are in these states with the most aggressively anti- choice policies like in Arkansas, Ohio, Texas, Louisiana, et cetera, have some protections.\par \par \uc2 So, I hope the conversation goes that way, but I don't see the filibuster going anywhere. He tried this with voting rights as well, and it was no dice.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Do you see that happening, what she just said, about Republican senators joining to codify Roe?\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: I don't. I really don't. I think that what they're going to do is they're going to say, look, the Supreme Court wants us to go back to the states. Let it go back to the states. They're not going to put their reputations on the line. And I think that what's most fascinating about this is that didn't Congress try to pass a law a few weeks ago?\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: They couldn't get the numbers so Congress didn't pass it. When was it, three weeks ago? So, enough of that. I mean, if they want to get the carve-out, it's not going to happen. It's got to go to the states. The Supreme Court said it and that's the way it's got to happen.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: And the squad is pretty pumped up that Biden looks like or sounds like he may be doing something more than he's doing already.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Yes. So, he's going to set himself up again for failure.\par \par \uc2 WATERS: Right.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: And so -- and then they'll be disappointed and then they'll be mad and then they'll kick and scream and they'll try to explain the constitution to us, which has been quite hilarious today actually. The whole issue about it being codified is actually kind of interesting because the Supreme Court said there's no right to abortion in the Constitution. It has to be done at the state level.\par \par \uc2 So, even if they were to pass a law, I don't think that it would actually pass constitutional muster.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: It wouldn't survive constitutional muster.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: So, like, why don't you just go and try to do something in the states. That would seem to make a lot more sense to me. I thought the president not defending our institutions and our system of government overseas was terrible.\par \par \uc2 If President Trump had done that, there would have been an outcry. Now, you can say I disagree with decisions, but our system is the best in the world. Some of these statistics of people who are not proud to be Americans this year, it's terrible. You can say that you disagree with him, but we have a system in which we can try to do something and this is what I'm going to try to do, but he doesn't do any of that.\par \par \uc2 The other thing is, why did he wait a week to do this? How could you be caught flat-footed when you had, one, years of the idea that this could be overturned? You had over 50 days of a leaked opinion out there. And then he goes over -- he waits a week? So now everybody is spun up, they're upset. They come up with ideas like putting abortion clinics on national parkland because they didn't have a plan. They weren't out there in order to do it.\par \par \uc2 The left has to figure out a way to legislate because they a0re no longer going to be able to rely on the judiciary to implement their policies. And it's pretty amazing, Jesse, the Democrats have the White House, the House and the Senate and there's been a string of conservative wins in the last few weeks. So, I think they're on their back feet.\par \par \uc2 WATERS: That's a great point about them needing to legislate because they have been jamming a lot of unpopular things through the judicial system and now it looks like 