LA JEUNESSE, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: Information, making gun owners a target for financial crimes. And of course, potentially burglaries, Sandra. So, at this point in time, the A.G. says, hey, it was just a mistake. Gun owners don't necessarily agree. And they could litigate to keep these things private in the future. Back to you.\par \par \uc2 SANDRA SMITH, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: All right. William LaJeunesse on that for us. And that wraps our to our show. Thanks, everybody, for joining us. I'm Sandra Smith. Great to be with you, Mike.\par \par \uc2 MIKE EMANUEL, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Great to be with you. I'm Mike Emanuel. THE STORY WITH MARTHA MACCALLUM starts right now. Martha.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA MACCALLUM, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Martha. All right, thanks very much, you guys. Good afternoon, everybody. I'm Martha MacCallum. 85 percent of Americans think that the country is going in the wrong track. And you know what, they see it all around them -- signs that the country is just not working the way that it should be. That we are not the America that we want to be, firing on all cylinders, smoking the competition, all of those things that we always took pride in.\par \par \uc2 Now, it seems though, that Americans are kind of getting used to this, used to mediocrity, used to disappointment, faced with excruciating delays and cancellations. Look at these numbers and they're going to get higher as we move through this weekend heading into the Fourth of July. But passengers are, you know what, they're just trying to deal with it and get through it. They say this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: My flight got switch, so I have a five-hour layover.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I got to LaGuardia Airport in New York at 6:00 in the morning to find out that my 8:00 flight is canceled.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Even with the delays, they worked with us and they, we had no problem.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Oh, boy, oh, boy, this people are way more patient than I would be, I have to admit. So, the pilots are out in force, and they are protesting today. They say they are stretched way too thin that even that flights are being put in place that they know they don't have pilots for. And then there's this concern, listen closely to this, to this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: They're trying to fly airplanes without the pilots available. They are pilot pushing, and they are narrowing the margin of safety.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: That's scary. That doesn't sound good. So, you might ask, for instance, where's the Secretary of Transportation in all of this? Well, he did announce a $1 billion dollar pilot program, but it's not for pilots, it's for road equity. His office says they are doing things to make sure that people get paid back if they missed their flight. Bernie Sanders, though, is pushing for answers from Pete Buttigieg, sending him a list of fixes that he believes must be done, and asking him to get back to him as soon as possible.\par \par \uc2 With that, we bring in Anthony Roman, an \uc2 FAA\uc2  Licensed Commercial Pilot and Former Corporate Pilot. Anthony, good to have you with us. You know, first of all, the, the delays the cancellations just keep ratcheting up and I see that Delta has now canceled a ton of flights for over this weekend. What's going on?\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY ROMAN, \uc2 FAA\uc2  LICENSED COMMERCIAL PILOT: Well, it's very, very easy to blame the airlines for, for this. It's a simple answer, which is simply not factual. You know, the average airline pilot, based on \uc2 FAA\uc2  regulations can fly no more than 32 hours in a seven-day work period, and no more than 100 hours in a given month. And so, the pilots are not being overworked, the airlines are faced with an entirely new dilemma, which is not all of their fault.\par \par \uc2 The primary factors are traffic problems at the airports with regard to too many landing aircraft, too many aircraft and crew in queue for taking off, and the airport infrastructure simply isn't supporting the increased airport traffic. In addition to that, Martha, what we have are fewer air traffic controllers. And as a result, it interrupts the real smooth flow of airline traffic and causes delays. It's another factor, global warming -- much more severe weather much more frequently today than in years past.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, but what we're hearing, what we're hearing is that they're scheduling flights that they know they don't have a pilot for. We're also hearing that there's a severe pilot shortage. I've heard this from many pilots, that just not as many people want to be pilots anymore. And so, I'm asking the question, what can be done? A. Why would why would an airline schedule a flight that they know they don't have a pilot to fly?\par \par \uc2 ROMAN: I don't think that that's occurring. I think that's an exaggerated scenario --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: No, but I mean, this isn't because of global warming. I mean, that might be one element. The bottom line is that it used to be that you got to the airport, and your flight said on time. Now you get to the airport and you're thinking what's my plan B, C, and D?\par \par \uc2 ROMAN: But, well, of course there are some crucial shortages, but many of the airlines have accelerated pilot training programs which, which now are adding big numbers to the airlines in terms of flight crews. And a lot of the pilots that were furloughed and offered early retirement packages and happily took them are returning to the cockpit now.\par \par \uc2 So, I think we're seeing the trend that's going to be improving the situation through the end of the year. But as a result of the pandemic, it created highly unusual stresses for all U.S. businesses, but particularly for the airlines with the compounded problems of airport infrastructure and weather. So, it is not as simple an answer, or as simple a problem.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I want to get in one more thing here, because I think when you have all of these shortages, you're going to have people concerned about maintenance, you're going to have people concerned about \uc2 TSA\uc2 . I've been to the airport recently, and there might be only one or two lanes open, because that's all the people they have to work to get people through. And that's another big problem.\par \par \uc2 But this is from the New York Post: "A U.S. pilot's disturbing warning: humans make mistakes under this kind of pressure. So, a lot of times we have to push it even to fly home. The pressure can also make you angry or anxious. And humans make mistakes under that kind of pressure, whether that ultimately results in lives being lost or damage being done to the aircraft." What do you say about that?\par \par \uc2 ROMAN: There are time limitations that the professional flight crews can work. And those time limitations are way below what the average American worker puts in in a given month. The average American worker is working 160 hours or better in a month, flight crews 100 hours a month.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, should that be changed? You said 32 hours in seven days?\par \par \uc2 ROMAN: Yes, that's what the \uc2 FAA\uc2  requires. That is not a lot of time. It's below the average for the average workers work week.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right, well, there's a place we can maybe start, maybe the transportation secretary can start exploring the option for that. Thank you very much, Anthony Roman, good to see you.\par \par \uc2 ROMAN: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, a win for Florida Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 , as he fights to crack down on the issue that is underlying in so much of the immigration problem we talk about and that is human trafficking.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GOV. RON DESANTIS (R-FL): The second thing we are doing is we have today filed a petition with this Florida Supreme court requesting an order to impanel a statewide grand jury to examine international human smuggling networks that bring illegal aliens across the southern border and ultimately to states like Florida.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: \uc2 Ketanji Brown Jackson\uc2  officially sworn in as Supreme Court Justice today, replacing Justice \uc2 Stephen Breyer\uc2 , who is retiring and now has done so. Before that took effect, the High Court closed up their term and they made a lot of huge decisions that really show a lot of the character of this particular Supreme Court. One of the rulings today was that migrants will not have to remain in Mexico while the US considers their asylum claim, saying that the feds can keep phasing out the Trump administration policy that sent thousands of migrants back across the border.\par \par \uc2 So, this is one decision that did not go the way that conservatives had hoped that it would. Let's bring in Arizona Attorney General \uc2 Mark Brnovich\uc2  also running for \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2  in the state of Arizona. Attorney General Good to have you with us today. What was your reaction to this? And what did you read into the opinions about why, in particular, Roberts and Kavanaugh went on the side of allowing, you know, not insisting that these migrants would have to stay in Mexico?\par \par \uc2 MARK BRNOVICH\uc2 , ARIZONA ATTORNEY GENERAL: Well, thank you for having me on. And obviously, it was a very disappointing decision. You know, we were part of the coalition that was trying to force the Biden administration to do whatever it can to secure our southern border. We've talked about the catastrophe and the chaos that's happening and the Remain in Mexico policy was just one of the various rules and procedures the Biden administration has revoked, which has led to the crisis and the catastrophe.\par \par \uc2 So, it's very disappointing. And you know, frankly, I am a bit worried that especially with Justice Roberts, and sometimes they're so worried about political pressure and the blowback that they've seen in some of these other cases, that an effort to look non-political, they're making decisions that I think that are, you know, contrary to the law.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, that's interesting that you say that, you know, there's been a lot of questions about Justice, Chief Justice Roberts. I read a piece today that asked, you know, when he lost control of the court, which I thought was an interesting take, he does seem to be one who likes to stay sort of not rock the boat, which is actually Kavanagh's words. He said, I'm not rock the boat justice.\par \par \uc2 So, I thought it was interesting that they sided together on this. And I wonder, do you think that that is an element to it? Of course, we can't get into their minds, but because there was such blowback for these other decisions. And the other one today also went the conservative way, do you think this was, you know, sort of a bone that was thrown?\par \par \uc2 BRNOVICH: Yes. And obviously, I can't put my mind or myself in the mind of any Supreme Court justice. But the reality is, is that Justice Roberts is an institutionalist. He believes very strongly in the court as an institution. And when he sees protesters, you know, at justices' homes endangering the lives, death threats coming to people like Justice Alito, just for doing their job, I think that causes him a lot of concern.\par \par \uc2 And what I think are what I worry is happening is that in an effort to "de- politicize the court," he's actually making the court more political. And so, I think it's a -- it's an unfortunate irony in his efforts to try to protect the institution. He is -- its times making it more political.\par \par \uc2 Because the reality is, is that whether you're conservative or liberal, what the Biden administration just doing on our southern border, and other instances, we did win the \uc2 EPA\uc2  case today is they're basically trying to systematically unilaterally rewrite U.S. law and undermine our border security.\par \par \uc2 So, the Court needs to step up and make those tough decisions. That's why they have lifetime appointments.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. In most of these decisions, we've seen a very originalist take, hewing to the words of the Constitution and not taking it outside of that. This one was a little bit different.\par \par \uc2 Good to see you today. Thank you for being with us.\par \par \uc2 BRNOVICH: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Attorney General \uc2 Mark Brnovich\uc2 . And Senate candidate in Arizona as well.\par \par \uc2 So, inside every mass border crossing, the cartels are shipping young women, children, into sex slavery. There's no compassion at this point for these women and the way that the cartels are basically turning them into dollars and business in some of the policies that are governing this.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM (voice-over): Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  and others like him are working hard to end these horrific stories. It is a story you will only see right here.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM (on camera): Warning that some of these images and video that you're about to see are graphic. This is the inside of a car in Laredo, Texas. DPS says that there are at least five dead after a migrant smuggler evaded law enforcement and crashed into a big rig this morning.\par \par \uc2 They also just released this dash cam video from a June 10 incident that shows the horrifying moment that a migrant is killed as he runs out of a smugglers car during a traffic stop in Brooks County.\par \par \uc2 All of this, as for people now face charges in the deaths of 53 human beings who lost their lives in this sweltering San Antonio truck earlier this week.\par \par \uc2 In a moment, Florida Lieutenant Governor Jeanette Nunez joins us. And also Tim Ballard, anti-human trafficking activist, who's been in this work for a very long time -- they both have.\par \par \uc2 First, to national correspondent Bill Melugin. Hi, Bill.\par \par \uc2 BILL MELUGIN, FOX NEWS CHANNEL NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Hi, Martha.\par \par \uc2 Well, that brand new dash cam video from Texas DPS really highlights the fact that deadly human smuggling incidents are taking place at our southern border seemingly non-stop.\par \par \uc2 Again, a warning to our viewers. This video you're about to see here is graphic.\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN (voice-over): It shows Texas DPS troopers pulling over a human smuggler and Brooks County, who had about six illegal immigrants in his vehicle.\par \par \uc2 As troopers take him into custody, several of the migrants bail out. You'll see one of them runs right into the highway and is fatally struck by an oncoming vehicle. We are pausing that video at the moment of impact.\par \par \uc2 You'll then see other migrants run off into the brush in an effort to escape the troopers.\par \par \uc2 Texas DPS tells us the driver has now been charged with human smuggling, causing death.\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN (on camera): And some breaking news. We have also learned that several migrants died in Laredo this morning. And yet, another human smuggling-related incident. A warning, again, these images also graphic.\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN (voice-over): Texas DPS telling Fox News, at least four migrants are dead in Laredo after a human smuggler evading law enforcement crashed his vehicle into the back of a big rig truck.\par \par \uc2 We're told the smuggler had six migrants in his vehicle, from Guatemala and Mexico.\par \par \uc2 Texas DPS telling us, the driver -- the smuggler was actually a U.S. citizen who just died at the hospital. Bringing the death total in this human smuggling crash to five people in total.\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN (on camera): And Martha, there have now been three deadly human smuggling incidents in just the last 24 hours at the border. Five dead in this Laredo incident, one dead in a smuggler crash in Brooks County this morning, and two more dead in another human smuggling crash yesterday in the Rio Grande Valley.\par \par \uc2 Those numbers don't even account for the more than 50 who died earlier this week in San Antonio. We'll send it back to you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Bill Melugin, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 The Florida Supreme Court backing Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 's push for a grand jury investigation into surrounding -- an investigation surrounding illegal immigration. It could tackle a range of issues, including drug smuggling, and child trafficking, which is a huge part of this story.\par \par \uc2 Florida Lieutenant Governor Jeanette Nunez joins me now. Lieutenant Governor, thank you very much for being with us. Tell me what you're hoping to target in this legislation and how concerned you are about what's happening to children at the hands of these cartels across this open border.\par \par \uc2 JEANETTE NUNEZ, LIEUTENANT GOVERNOR OF FLORIDA: Indeed, Martha, what we have seen with the Supreme Court, granting the governor's request to impanel, a statewide grand jury is we're going to be able to take the next step investigating the criminal enterprises that are -- that are taking hold on child trafficking, human smuggling, drug smuggling, all the things that are impacting not just a border state like Texas, but states like Florida.\par \par \uc2 And so, Governor DeSantis has been very clear. We are going to do everything we can to keep Floridians safe. And that includes using the tools that we have are just -- at our disposal.\par \par \uc2 The governor has created a strike force to interdict human smuggling that is taking place in Florida. He has got the grand jury that will be investigating and looking at what are the things that we can do to protect Floridians. And he's also made it illegal for common carriers to knowingly work with enterprises that are bringing illegals into Florida.\par \par \uc2 Biden's border disaster has made everyone less safe with a historic numbers -- record numbers of individuals flowing through our border, enough fentanyl to wipe out the entire population of the United States. This is a crisis. It's a humanitarian crisis. It's a national security crisis.\par \par \uc2 And for Governor DeSantis and I, we're going to continue to do everything we can to keep Floridians safe.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, how do you handle the cartel part of this story, when you're in paneling a grand jury in Florida, dealing with investigations, I would assume, into Americans?\par \par \uc2 NUNEZ: Yes, and what we're doing not only working with a grand jury, obviously they will investigate, and they will have all the tools at their disposal to see those criminal trafficking enterprises.\par \par \uc2 Because let's be clear, this is not just well intentioned people trying to get folks across the border. These are criminal cartels. They have an illicit industry that is far surpassing the arms industry and will likely surpass the drug industry.\par \par \uc2 Human smuggling, they do not treat those individuals as we've seen by some of the issues that you brought up in your segment. We've -- what we're seeing is that these individuals have little regard for the safety and well-being and they are using them as commodities.\par \par \uc2 So, in the grand jury, as well as all the other pieces that we discussed, we're going to continue to look at ways to not only interdict the human trafficking that's taking place, but also make sure that we prosecute them to the fullest extent of the law.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Jeanette, thank you. Jeanette Nunez, lieutenant governor of Florida under Governor DeSantis. Very good to have you with us. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 NUNEZ: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, my next guest is the founder and president of Operation Underground Railroad, which is a group that helps to combat child sex trafficking.\par \par \uc2 Timothy, good to have you with us. Timothy Ballard is joining me now.\par \par \uc2 What is it that, that people don't understand about how large and how pervasive these child trafficking rings are? And how much of a part they are of what we see when we see all these groups crossing the border?\par \par \uc2 TIMOTHY BALLARD, FOUNDER AND PRESIDENT, OPERATION UNDERGROUND RAILROAD: Yes. Thanks, Martha.\par \par \uc2 Look, my perspective comes from 10 years as a government agent -- special agent operator on that border. So, I'm glad we're talking about, you know, highlighting the horrible things that have happened to these migrants.\par \par \uc2 But this tragedy has been going on for years, under reported, and it's exactly what you mentioned, children.\par \par \uc2 Our policies right now, incentivize traffickers, they're making $14 million a day, by some estimates. They smuggle people, but then they turn into traffickers when they start abusing children.\par \par \uc2 And the policy that I'm talking about is when children show up at that border, they are -- by law, they have to be released within 72 hours, OK. And then, in 20 days, there -- they have to be released.\par \par \uc2 And it's -- there, what the traffickers and smugglers are doing is they're taking these children, and 1,000s of them are unaccompanied. We find out later, but they pair them up with families and make it look like they're the child of that family. And they're getting them in.\par \par \uc2 So, these children are being used as pawns. And also, they're being sex trafficked.\par \par \uc2 And, by the way, if you knew how easy it was, for a sponsor, right? To come down to the border and say, hey, this kid, that's unaccompanied, he's mine, I'll signed for him.\par \par \uc2 Border Patrol, it's not their fault. They don't have the resources, because the administration is not giving them the resources. And so, these kids, it's just an easy trafficking situation.\par \par \uc2 And so, I'm glad that Governor DeSantis is doing what he's doing. Because these kids end up in Maine, USA, as trafficking victims in what is $150 billion, your business, human trafficking.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: It's disgusting, what's happening. And the cartels are in charge of all of these operations. As the lieutenant governor just pointed out, this business is bigger than their drug business. And we see what the fentanyl business is like. So, they have taken advantage of this opportunity to get people across this border.\par \par \uc2 Tell me what happens to a young -- a young girl, you know, 14, 15-year-old girl who comes across and as a victim of sex trafficking? Once she is brought across by one of these cartels, and whatever kind of, you know, back of a truck or whatever, and gets dropped off, what happens then?\par \par \uc2 BALLARD: Well, it cost about $6,000 or $7,000, OK, to pay a smuggler. But those migrants don't have that money. So, they're abused and sold along the way. OK. They are rapes for money along the way.\par \par \uc2 If they get across the border, especially the kids who are unaccompanied, and the status are shocking, Martha. We're talking -- these are young kids as young as 2 to 12 years old. 14 percent, OK, of the kids who are, who come across unaccompanied are in that age range.\par \par \uc2 And 700 a day are being -- are being identified in May alone. So, this is a lot of people. They -- if they get into the United States, the United States is the number one consumer of child sex material, which makes us a great demand. We're in the top three for destination countries for human traffickers.\par \par \uc2 Once those kids are in the interior, they are sold online. They're sold on every social media platform you can imagine. And it's a major business, and they're faceless, their identity list.\par \par \uc2 They have no one looking out for them. Their parents are probably somewhere in Central America have no idea where they are. And again, it's our policies that are creating the incentive for this billion-dollar a year business to go -- to go on. You can sell a --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, we talk -- we talk about the drug magnet that people here want the drugs, right? People here also want these kids. It is -- it's so appalling that this is not discussed all the time.\par \par \uc2 I've spoken with folks who follow this closely. And you certainly have spent, you know, years and years working on this. And it is outrageous that it isn't a higher priority.\par \par \uc2 The administration says that they are doing things to stop this. Is that -- is that your take?\par \par \uc2 BALLARD: No, not my take. When they're not giving Border Patrol what they need. Border Patrol has to process. They're pulling guys off the line to have in process these hundreds of 1000s of immigrants.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BALLARD: And they're letting in -- they're releasing them into the country. That's what the resources are going. They're not going on the enforcement side. And this Remain in Mexico thing understand that the -- that the solution is quite simple. If you put our asylum courts, and you put our immigration courts attached to our embassies in Honduras, Guatemala, Mexico --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Right.\par \par \uc2 BALLARD: -- then, only legitimate asylum seekers and immigrants will show up. But instead, we now -- we made in Mexico was a step in that direction - -\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: That's a great point.\par \par \uc2 BALLARD: Because it takes --\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Yes. We know that like only 10% of people have an actual credible asylum claim in this country, so all of these kids are just sort of getting over. And then, they're falling into the wrong hands and it's an absolute tragedy. And as you say, nobody's looking for them. In many cases, they don't have any identity on the middle. Timothy Ballard, thank you very much. Anti-human trafficking activist on Operation Underground Railroad, if you want to learn more about it that's the way to do it, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 BALLARD: Thank you, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right. We have a breaking story right now. The anger is boiling over at the city council meeting in Uvalde, Texas where 19 children and two teachers were murdered, waiting to see what just happened in there. We're going to show it to you. Also, President Biden calls out the Supreme Court's "mistake" in overturning Roe v. Wade. He said the court is outrageous. Looking to Congress now to bring it back to make it the law of the land by using a carve out filibuster. We're going to talk to Marc Thiessen and Geraldo Rivera after this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF UNITED STATES: But this decision must not be the final word. My administration will use all of its appropriate lawful powers. But Congress must act.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: OK. We want to bring you this. This is happening right now. Emotions, very high in this room at the City Council meeting in Uvalde, Texas. We've heard that in this town, grief is beginning to turn to anger and that's what we're seeing play out in this room right now about the chaotic unorganized response to 77 minutes that law enforcement stood in the hallway while the killing was underway. And so much of the outrage that is being brought out today in the discussion of this horrific massacre, 19 children, two teachers lost their lives at Robb Elementary School is being directed at the mayor, Don McLaughlin. Watch what just happened moments ago.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Why is it that the little kids are not taking care of? (Inaudible) this man, well he knew where to go because he had gone to the high school