.\par \par \uc2 We want the response to be better. We want to learn lessons from this. So we'll stay on it.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, President Biden says that he supports making an exception to the Senate filibuster rule if that's what it takes to make Roe v. Wade the law of the land, again, going the legislative route. Now that move would let Democrats pass that bill without any Republicans.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I believe we have to codify Roe v. Wade in the law, and the way to do that is to make sure the Congress votes to do that. And if the filibuster gets in the way, it's like voting rights, it should be -- we provide an exception for this. They accept the requiring exception in the filibuster for this action to deal with the Supreme Court decision.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, that got a lot of attention. As you might imagine, that was big news that the President made this morning that he's supposed to carve out for the filibuster to make Roe v. Wade the law of the land. It got this reaction from AOC on \uc2 Twitter\uc2 . She said, "Now we're talking. Time for people to see a real forceful push for it. Use the bully pulpit, we need more," says the congresswoman from New York.\par \par \uc2 But all 50 Senate Democrats would have to agree to change the rules, and \uc2 Joe Manchin\uc2  and \uc2 Kyrsten Sinema\uc2  have said that they don't support sidestepping the filibuster. With that, we bring in Marc Thiessen, former chief speechwriter for President \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2  now and \uc2 American Enterprise Institute\uc2  scholar and Fox News contributor, also Geraldo Rivera, co-host of "The Five." Great to have both of you with us today.\par \par \uc2 MARC THIESSEN, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Hi, Martha.\par \par \uc2 GERALDO RIVERA, FOX NEWS HOST: Good to be with you, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Marc, were you surprise that the President said that this morning? You know, he's had a rough couple of weeks in a lot of ways, what did you think?\par \par \uc2 THIESSEN: I was surprised that he attacked the Supreme Court from foreign soil. I guess, the whole politics ends at the water's edge this is old news. Also surprising that he's talking about Roe v. Wade on foreign soil, he's in Europe, where 39 out of 42 \uc2 European Union\uc2  countries bar abortions at 15 weeks or early -- until 15 -- before 15 weeks earlier. So you can't get an abortion in those countries, elective abortion except for certain exceptions.\par \par \uc2 All those laws would be barred under Roe v. Wade. So apparently their laws are outrageous as well. But look, what's happening here, Martha, is the Democrats are not trying to codify Roe v. Wade. They are trying to impose their legislation, abortion on demand up until the moment of birth.\par \par \uc2 Joe Manchin\uc2  and Susan Collins both oppose the Democrats' bill because they say it goes far beyond Roe v. Wade. Senator Manchin said it would obviate 500 state laws putting restrictions on abortion. It would expand abortion. Susan Collins says she opposes it because it would get rid of bipartisan conscience laws that are in states that protect providers who don't want to provide abortion.\par \par \uc2 So this is not about codifying Roe v. Wade. This is about imposing by a simple majority, a radical position that's opposed by more than 70% of the American people, which is abortion on demand up until the moment of birth.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right. Let's play this moment from the news conference this morning in Madrid for the President.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: The one thing that has been destabilizing is the outrageous behavior of the \uc2 Supreme Court of the United States\uc2  on overruling, not only Roe v. Wade, but essentially challenging the right to privacy. We've been a leader in the world in terms of personal rights and privacy rights, and it is a mistake, in my view, for the Supreme Court do what it did.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Geraldo, what do you think about the President talking about the Supreme Court's decision in that way, calling it outrageous, and also about this idea that you could carve out the filibuster?\par \par \uc2 RIVERA: I think the President of the United States is just blowing smoke, Martha. I think that it is so futile to see him, you know, we're (inaudible) that way. He just has no power to do what he intends or says he intends to do, aside from the fact that he's slandering the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 Putting aside the soundness of their judgment, they are the high court, they have ruled. And now we're stuck with it until we get a new Supreme Court. To think that he can get a simple majority of the Senate to overrule the Supreme Court of United States, he's just wrong. He's flat out wrong.\par \par \uc2 Plus, they tried it just few months ago and failed 49 to 51 to even get a simple majority. I mean, he has more chance, I say only -- it's slightly tongue in cheek of getting the tribal territories, you know, the Indian reservations, the Native American reservations, to establish safe havens for abortions, then he does to get his own majority in the Senate to pass a simple law. It's not going to happen. Roe is being and has been overruled. We have to deal with that reality.\par \par \uc2 If they want to change that, then what they have to do is what Trump did. They have to get like-minded people on the High Court of the United States to change and overrule the decision. But he's not going to be able to do it by just spewing politics.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: But there's another route and it's the route that the court decided. They can do that in the States. They can vote for state legislatures and state representatives who want the rules that apply to abortion that they want. And, you know, this is not a ban on abortion across the country. You said it so many times but I keep hearing people say that it is, and it's just simply not. It just has gone back to the States and that is the other avenue that the President could be talking about and encouraging.\par \par \uc2 RIVERA: Maybe I am not so sure of that. For example, Texas now has the six week snitch law. So what happens if you have, you know, New York is come one come all and Texas snitches out the Texan woman who goes to New York. She comes back and they criminally prosecute (inaudible) sue.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: None of them rules talk about criminally prosecuting the woman. They are focused on the providers.\par \par \uc2 THIESSEN: Nobody is talking about that, Geraldo.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: They're not focused on the women. They're focused on the providers in the state while the --\par \par \uc2 RIVERA: How about the truck driver? How about the truck driver? How about the advisor in Texas? You know, it's very, very -- it's a constitutional catastrophe.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right. Gentlemen, thank you very much. Marc Thiessen, Geraldo Rivera, always good to see you both.\par \par \uc2 THIESSEN: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 RIVERA: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Coming up, I'm going to speak with the former NATO Supreme Allied Commander Philip Breedlove about what the President said today about his intentions with Ukraine and the large expansion of \uc2 NATO\uc2  on Putin's border after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So at the beginning of his comments today, the President spoke in fairly strong terms about the threat from Russia in Ukraine and what it means to Europe. And when asked how long and how much support he would give to Ukraine, President Biden says that he will continue for "as long as it takes."\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: We're going to be providing another, I guess I'll announce shortly, but another $800 billion. $800 million in aid for additional weaponry including, you know, weapons including air defense system as well as offensive weapons.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: OK, that from the President earlier today. With that, we bring in retired General Philip Breedlove, former NATO Supreme Allied Commander and now a distinguished chair at the Middle East Institute. Good to have you with us as always, general. Thank you for being here.\par \par \uc2 You know, one of the things that I noticed in those comments this morning, which were fairly forceful about Russia and about the ramp up of weaponry and troops, that they expect to put on that border. I noticed in that soundbite that the President used the word offensive weapons. And it's a word that they had been quite careful to avoid for quite some time. Here's \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2  in the briefing room not that long ago.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JEN PSAKI\uc2 , FORMER WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: I would note that the equipment that we've provided is defensive, as you know, not offensive and we see that as being a difference.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Does this signal a change in strategy to you?\par \par \uc2 PHILIP BREEDLOVE, FORMER NATO SUPREME ALLIED COMMANDER: Well, if he truly mean it and that is now the policy of America, that is a change in policy, because as you correctly pointed out, the administration has been very careful to tell Mr. Putin over and over, that we're not going to give offensive weapons to the Ukrainians. And we've heard the whole conversation about limiting the range of the MLRS or HIMARS that we gave Ukraine. We gave them the shortest range missile in order to accrued Ukraine firing them into Russia.\par \par \uc2 So, if truly we have now taken that step, that we're going to give Ukraine offensive weapons, that is a policy change. And to my mind, that is a welcome policy change.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: What do you see as the commitment from the other members of \uc2 NATO\uc2 ? There's talk about over time increasing those troops to 300,000. I think that's a seven fold increase. That's a big number along that border. And also putting a permanent base in Poland, what do you think about that and how do you think Putin receives that?\par \par \uc2 BREEDLOVE: Well, first, I think it's a good idea, actually back in '16, when I was the SAC year, and Ukraine was invaded twice by Russia, we called for larger numbers and more forward forces. And \uc2 NATO\uc2  took some great steps, so I'm not belittling what they did, in creating the smaller force, the 40,000 force that you mentioned. And now, what we see is that number increasing to as much as 300,000. The first 100,000 being in one readiness state and the second 200,000 being in an improved but a different readiness state and these are all great steps.\par \par \uc2 And if I could, Martha, just one more remark. If you remember in -- Mr. Putin handed us two letters before this war and said sign them or there'll be other means. We know now what he meant. He meant to invade Ukraine. But in those letters, he asked over and over, no more weapons forward, no more troops forward, no more \uc2 NATO\uc2  in the forward areas. And what we are giving him for his immoral, inhuman war is more,, more and more forward. And so, we're sending a pretty clear message.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. That is for sure. We'll see where it goes. Always good to speak with you, General Breedlove. Thank you for being here. Great insight. We'll see you soon, all right.\par \par \uc2 So we're going to go to London for a little bit of a breaking update from Buckingham Palace's review of \uc2 Meghan Markle\uc2 's alleged, you know, the claim that she made, that she was bullied at the palace. That report is now finished.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: OK. So \uc2 Meghan Markle\uc2  made a lot of claims about bullying. She said that she was bullied when she asked for help, but then there was the other side. And now Buckingham Palace says they won't release the findings of an 