uary 6 committee comes in the form of a subpoena, a former Trump White House Counsel \uc2 Pat Cipollone\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Now, the move is so desperate, even the \uc2 New York Times\uc2  sees the futility in this. Saying, "The move sent a clear signal of the aggressive tactics the panel is willing to use to try to force cooperation of even the White House's former top lawyer, who will most likely be able to invoke attorney- client privilege in response to many questions".\par \par \uc2 So joining me now is \uc2 Matt Whitaker\uc2 , former U.S. Attorney, former acting Attorney General under President Trump. Matt, everybody knows that Pat is one of my close friends. So I'm going to disclose that.\par \par \uc2 But what does this tell you about how the committee is really feeling about the strength of its so-called case?\par \par \uc2 MATT WHITAKER\uc2 , FORMER ACTING ATTORNEY GENERAL: Well, they keep trying to entice everyone with, this is the most important thing you could watch. And then when you see what they have, like this doesn't tell me anything. This - I mean, I'm concerned that this crosses a line of really peeking into the most confidential communications at the White House.\par \par \uc2 I mean, they already obviously tried to go against \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2 , tried to hold him in contempt, tried to prosecute him. DOJ said, We're not going to prosecute our chief of staff. And now we're going after the White House Counsel.\par \par \uc2 This is - the Constitution is designed for accommodation and for this interesting dance between the legislation - legislative branch and the executive branch. This knocks it all down, and I think is really an aggressive and unnecessary step.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Well, in their accompanying letter, they basically said, the need for the information outweighs any concern about the separation of powers here. In this case, I guess, it would be executive privilege.\par \par \uc2 WHITAKER: Yes. But it - that doesn't - that overstates the case. Obviously, the President needs confidential advice from his chief of staff and from his White House counsel at a minimum. And this--\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: How do you operate as president, if you're going to be hauled before a committee at any time when the Democrats get in power? How do you operate? They don't want them to be able to operate. Don't you think that's what this is really all about?\par \par \uc2 WHITAKER: Well, so they don't want Trump to operate as president. I mean, this has been obvious ever since they took control in January of 2019. And I went up and testified and, sort of, put them back in their place. But in all honesty, this is a step that they will rue the day if (inaudible) can knock this down.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: If I were they, I wouldn't want to call \uc2 Pat Cipollone\uc2  up there.\par \par \uc2 All right. Matt, back "In (ph) August of 2020, a federal appeals court ruled that House Democrats could sue to force President Trump's former White House Counsel \uc2 Don McGahn\uc2  to comply with at the time was a congressional subpoena saying that the House has a long-standing right to compel government officials to testify and produce documents".\par \par \uc2 But this is from Trump's impeachment. This is the follow-on to whether he obstructed justice in the impeachment thing. Does that provide any precedent for them calling Cipollone.\par \par \uc2 WHITAKER: I don't think it does. And in fact, if you read that opinion, the court really anchors its opinion in the fact that this was a impeachment. And this was very grave and important. This is - right now, we're in a special oversight at best. I mean, one sided no ability to cross examine witnesses type operation.\par \par \uc2 I just - I don't think that a court is going to hold it, this as important as an impeachment. And so therefore, I think it's going to be less. But if \uc2 Pat Cipollone\uc2  is smart, I mean he's going to hold his confidence as obviously he told the President a lot of things. And there's no, I think, competing interest that it's powerful as the need for confidential attorney-client privilege communications as you would see in this committee trying to find out whether he lunged over a driver and grabbed the steering wheel.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Well, some colorful language perhaps being used by the - in the White House, but has that never been done before, Matt?\par \par \uc2 WHITAKER: If you read the - listen to the Nixon tapes, I think you can.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: All right, Matt. Great to see you as always.\par \par \uc2 WHITAKER: Good to see you, Laura.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: And as you documented last night, the January 6 committee star witness yesterday, former aide Cassidy Hutchinson blew up in a spectacular fashion.\par \par \uc2 We told you last night about how Secret Service agents involved in her tall tale regarding President Trump, as Matt said, just leaning over, trying to grab the wheel of the vehicle to go to Capitol Hill, that they are willing to testify under oath and refute the testimony of Hutchinson. But despite that, the media are still entertaining this clown show.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 NICOLE WALLACE, MSNBC HOST: A stunning denunciation of a president's conduct that is, frankly, unprecedented.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: This is about as historic as it gets. I don't know of anything more shocking than what I heard yesterday, and I think it's going to change history.\par \par \uc2 DON LEMON, CNN HOST: It's like nothing we have ever heard before about an American president.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: In the history of the world. Well, what we learned today was perhaps more damning for the committee. The Secret Service put out on the record a statement saying that, in the 10 days before the Hutchinson hearing, the committee never reached out for a statement.\par \par \uc2 Florida Congressman Byron Donalds joins me now. Congressman, they are so desperate to try to take out Trump into two impeachments. Now, they're doing this. They're so desperate that they don't even do their basic due diligence that frankly, at my old law firm as an associate, you'd be fired for. If you handle the case this way, I would have been fired. OK. That's how bad it is. Your reaction tonight.\par \par \uc2 REP. BYRON DONALDS (R-FL): Listen, any attorney that goes into the court of law and stuff like this, you will be thrown out on your butt. Because you're using third hand hearsay evidence from a staffer, who wasn't even in the room when a lot of these discussions were being hatched. You heard something from somebody else. There's no corroboration. There's no backup evidence.\par \par \uc2 But, Laura, here's the key detail. If they really wanted to know what happened during the time of January 6, they should go back and watch the oversight hearings from last year. Because the reason why there are no Republicans on this committee, I know Liz is about to lose her seat, and Adam's basically gone anyway.\par \par \uc2 But the reason there are no Republicans is because when we were looking at January 6th and the Oversight Committee, the Democrats were flustered. They have nowhere to go. Republican members had the facts. And we were clearly breaking down what happening and what the failures were around January 6th. They don't want opposition. That's why you have silly show hearings like what happened the other day.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: I just love it when it's like, it's a special -- it's like the new coke, the special hearing, new Tide. Every day is like a new gimmick, and the next one is going to be the Cipollone perhaps appearance.\par \par \uc2 Here's how Congressman Jamie Raskin, Congressman, reacted to Hutchinson's testimony unraveling. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. JAMIE RASKIN, (D-MD) JANUARY 6TH SELECT COMMITTEE: She has no motivation or interest in lying in any way, as so what we have on the other hand is some anonymously sourced rumors of feelings of particular agents. Look, anybody who wants to testify can come forward and testify under oath about what happened.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Well, he perhaps didn't see the hugs between the witness and the committee members. There were significant embraces going on there after the testimony. I'm sure if I believe that no one here is trying to gain anything. Look at that. Is that even appropriate? I don't know if that is appropriate. Are they old friends? But Byron, your reaction to that?\par \par \uc2 DONALDS: First of all, Jamie Raskin should be ashamed of himself. There are plenty of witness testimony that they've been taking. They've been at this for about a year now. They won't release it, they won't call key people in.\par \par \uc2 Listen, like I said, in Oversight last year we had Mr. Miller who was the acting head of DOD. He testified under oath that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  authorized National Guard troops on January 4th. They have yet to bring in \uc2 Capitol police\uc2  who are responsible for a lot of this stuff. But why don't they call on some of the people who might've been in the room at the time? They don't want to do that because it unravels their narrative. And that's what Jamie Raskin is after. This is the third impeachment. This is the political impeachment because they couldn't get the job done the previous two times.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: The one person who died that day was Ashli Babbitt, and she was shot and died instantly, I guess for trying to get through that door. But she wasn't armed. No one ever talks about her. I guess she doesn't count at all. Congressman, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Are sex strikes an effective way to advance abortion rights? Raymond Arroyo went to the streets to find out. And Pelosi is back in the Vatican, oh my gosh, and in hot water. "Seen and Unseen" is next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: It's time for our "Seen and Unseen" segment where we explore the cultural stories of the day, and for that we turn to FOX News contributor Raymond Arroyo. All right, Raymond, some female abortion activists have started with they're calling is a sex strike movement.\par \par \uc2 RAYMOND ARROYO, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: That is correct. They're refusing to have sex until abortion rights are enshrined federally in the law. So we took to the streets to get a common man's reaction.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Do you think that's effective at all?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I don't think so because not enough people are going to do it.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I don't think that anybody is going to go on strike against sex, because it feels so good. And it's also a business. Everybody is always going to want to have.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Sex strike?\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: A sex strike.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I guess that's great for women who can choose not to have sex. I'm not sure how, I'm sure if it's going to change much.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: What if we use our most powerful protesting option and withhold sex from penises until this is made right.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I think that's a horrible idea, and that's not an effective way to handle abortion at all.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I don't think that would effective. I think it's going to be more aggressive than necessary. I think it's going to be a little bit backwards.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: The sex strike protest probably won't work?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I would be willing to give it a strike if it would.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Is that a good or effective approach.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I would say I agree with it.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Really? Now, do you agree with it?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I don't really understand what a sex strike is, to be honest.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: A sex strike is where the girl will not have sex with you.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Oh. I think it varies person by person. But yes, that's a tough one.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: I notice he got very sober after that explanation.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Not surprising.\par \par \uc2 JOY BEHAR, CO-HOST, "THE VIEW": Women in the world have conducted sex strikes in history.\par \par \uc2 We have more power than we think we have, and some of it could be right in the bedroom.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I think that women have the right to do with their bodies whatever they want to do. And so I support people in this movement.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Do you think a sex strike is a way to awaken people's attention?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I don't really have a comment on that.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: So you would be prepared to have your girlfriend or significant other go on a strike until abortion is legal.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: See, I don't got no girlfriend, so I can't really say that. But again, if they want to do that, if they want to live their life and I love them, I'm going to support them.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: So you'd be willing to embrace celibacy?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: How long, though? How long? See, see, I don't know.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I don't disagree with the Roe v. Wade thing. You need to make a good decision on who you're going to have a child with, period. Like 60 percent of who is not our race are suffering from the abortion. We need to make better decisions, our women need to make better decisions of who they are having children with.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Your reaction to the sex strike idea, do you think that's effective?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I think it can be. But at the same time, are women really going to do that?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Women have been on strike.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: They've been on strike. Most guys are not with anybody. So what's the difference? What's that going to hurt us?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: OK, I love him.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Laura, I have to tell you, this was insightful for a couple of reasons. First of all, it's a clever protest, because a sex strike removes the controversy entirely because you don't need abortions, right. But it was sad, what that gentlemen pointed out, the loneliness, the estrangement that this recreational has created for this generation and the last couple, it's sad because without happy and loving couples you can't have happy and loving families.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: That guy was awesome. I said to Raymond during the break, I said is there any conservative in New York at all? He's like wait, that's at the end --\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Coming up. And Laura, in the heat of this abortion battle, inexplicably \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  attended a papal mass at the Vatican today. The speaker has been forbidden from presenting herself for communion in her home diocese of San Francisco due to her advocacy for abortion. Still, there she was today gladhanding with the Pope and taking communion. Laura, I've got to tell you, my phone blew up with people saw this. What we do is as important as what we say. And the Pope says the liturgy should be a source of unity, a way to protect our communion. I don't know how this image does that. In fact, I think it estranges people from whatever Catholic doctrine or belief might be.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: And Pelosi was not the only public figure seeking cover this week.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: That's right, the creator of one of most popular TV shows of all time, "Friends," Marta Kauffman, for years brushed off complaints that the show was not diverse enough. But now she has come to a new understanding.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: In an interview Kauffman says it is her internalized, systemic racism the caused the lack of inclusivity on the very popular show. Here she is discussing the problem.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MARTA KAUFFMAN, CREATOR, "FRIENDS": It was to a certain extent a product of the time period and of my own ignorance. There were black shows and there were white shows. There weren't a lot of shows that were interracial. I guess at the time I was thinking this is what I know. This is what I know.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: In expiation for her insensitivity, Laura, over the show she created 28 years ago Kauffman is donating $4 million to \uc2 Brandeis University\uc2  to fund a professorship in African American studies. I know this story has you amped. You were a big "Friends" fan.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Here's what I don't like is going back, and you go back in time and say, no, this doesn't count, this was bad. It was a different, it was a different time. But she feels the need to publicly self-flagellate. And how much did she make on "Friends"? Hundreds of millions of dollars, right?\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: And in syndication.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Remember they got their last contract, Aniston and all --\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: It was $1 million a pop.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: It was $1 million for each episode. So if they're really serious about this, you've got to go more than the $4 million. That is not enough.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Look, she can spend her money any way she wants. My question is, if you want to help the black community, why are you giving $4 million to Brandeis? You want to help the black community, there are tons of great faith-based, Demario Davis's program, that actually mentor young black people.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Raymond, we've got to go. They're yelling at me.\par \par \uc2 All right, he once declared he was the science, but now our good friend Dr. Fauci is back in the news for not following it. We explain with Dr. Peter McCullough who diagnosis it in moments.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DR. \uc2 ANTHONY FAUCI\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE CHIEF MEDICAL ADVISER: What you're seeing as attacks on me, quite frankly, are attacks on science, because all of the things that I have spoken about consistently from the very beginning have been fundamentally based on science.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Really? But remember, Fauci is the science. But after four vaccine shots and double masking, Fauci somehow got COVID, and then this happened.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DR. \uc2 ANTHONY FAUCI\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE CHIEF MEDICAL ADVISER: Given my age, I went on Paxlovid for five days, and I felt really quite well, just a bit of rhinorrhea and fatigue. And after I finished the five days of Paxlovid, I reverted to negative on an antigen test for three days in a row. And then on the fourth day just to be absolutely certain, I tested myself again, and I reverted back to positive by the antigen test. The next day or so I started to feel really poorly, much worse than in the first go around.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: We told him it doesn't work. We said that months ago. And now he's taken another round of Paxlovid. So what does the science say? The CDC and the \uc2 FDA\uc2  both say there is no evidence of benefit to a second round of the drug, let alone benefit to the first round if you've been vaxxed or have had COVID previously. We covered this months ago. But here we are with Dr. Peter McCullough again, epidemiologist, cardiologist, also author of "Courage to Face COVID-19." Dr. McCullough, what in the heck is going on here with Fauci?\par \par \uc2 DR. PETER MCCULLOUGH, MD, MPH, INTERNIST AND CARDIOLOGIST: He should go on to a community standard of care, which has been either prolonged hydroxychloroquine or ivermectin, plus steroids in combinations with other medications. Paxlovid has really struggled to join a community standard of care multidrug regimen, and he's basically a classic case example of what the CDC warned of May 24th of this year. They said, listen, those who are fully vaccinated, based on their papers, Gupta, Charness, and Carlin, that in fact if we rely on Paxlovid alone there can be rebound and get worse about two to eight days after they finish the initial course.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: So he got a Pax rebound, and he is suffering from that, but I think he's going to be OK, thankfully. But Dr. Jay Varma, who is a Cornell epidemiologist in New York is warning that a possible sixth COVID wave, if you lost track, is on the way. Dr. McCullough, are vaccines making the waves more inevitable? Because you've been discussing this for some time antibody dependent enhancement going on.\par \par \uc2 MCCULLOUGH: There's three analyses by Suvermanion (ph), Campf (ph), and Bede (ph), 145 countries now, ecological analysis, Laura, it's fairly clear that mass vaccination, which has been indiscriminate, is actually making things worse. There's more cases, hospitalizations, and deaths the more populations are vaccinated. It's a mistake to indiscriminately vaccinate in the setting of a highly prevalent pandemic.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: In New York where we were, the last several weeks we have been in New York a lot, and they have these COVID-19 tents, have you seen them on the street corner in New York, you can just walk in any time. Is there any reason to even have that? Obviously, it's expensive, but they're everywhere in New York still.\par \par \uc2 MCCULLOUGH: I don't think there's any role for mass testing. Testing has only been \uc2 FDA\uc2  approved for those who are acutely ill with the virus to establish a diagnosis. They do have any role for asymptomatic or just casual testing.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Dr. McCullough, great to see you, as always.\par \par \uc2 All right, I have some great news. Women, you've wanted Freedom Matters gear, and we have it, cool, really cool new polo made in the USA. We have different colors, but I love the pink. I wore this today. And you can get the red or the white for Fourth of July. And if you order before through July 4th, you get a free koozie, one of those things for the drinks to hold the drinks. Anyway, all proceeds for the month of June are going to be donated to the Honor Flight Network, so go to LauraIngraham.com. there's cool stuff there.\par \par \uc2 Up next, we say goodbye to a true American hero. Stay there.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HERSHEL WOODY WILLIAMS, WORLD WAR II VETERAN: I know we're classified as a hero very often, but I keep saying I'm not the hero. The heroes didn't get home.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: that was Hershel Woody Williams, the last surviving World War II Medal of Honor recipient. He died this morning at age 98. He was originally rejected by the Marines for being too short, but Woody persevered, becoming a flame thrower operator during the battle of Iwo Jima in 1945. He's credited with destroying multiple Japanese pill boxes while being covered by only four riflemen. He fought for four hours under enemy fire, wiping out position after position.\par \par \uc2 And months later Woody received the Medal of Honor from President \uc2 Harry Truman\uc2  for his actions. His citation reads, "Woody's unyielding determination and extraordinary heroism in the face of ruthless enemy resistance were directly instrumental in neutralizing one of the of most fanatically defended Japanese strongpoints encountered by his regiment and aided vitally in enabling his company to reach its objective."\par \par \uc2 After retiring from the military, Woody dedicated his life to helping veterans, and of course Gold Star families.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HERSHEL WOODY WILLIAMS, WORLD WAR II VETERAN: It is so full of miracles in my life, I can't count them all. And for those miracles to happen, it could only happen in a country like we have. It wouldn't happen in any other country.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Oh, my gosh. Hershel Woody Williams, he truly loved this country, he loved America, everything it stood for and it stands for to this day, which is why we need to keep fighting to preserve the true mission of our military. It's not about gender identity or diversity equity inclusion or whatever the latest fad is. It's rather about ensuring our nation's security. First principles. So God bless you Woody and every other veteran and current servicemember tonight and always.\par \par \uc2 Remember, it is America now and forever.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document INGANG0020220630ei6t00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 President Biden Hinted That He May Stay Longer Overseas As His Approval Numbers Continue To Sink, And More And More Democrats Are Now Joining The Bandwagon Of Not Giving Their Thumbs Up For Him To Run In 2024; Media's Favoritism Of \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  Seems To Sour And Now They're Looking For Other Options On The Bucket List Like \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2 , \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , Mayor Pete, Or AOC; Former White House Aide Cassidy Hutchinson Gave A So-Called Bombshell Testimony To The January 6th Committee, But Her Credibility Is Now In Question After Secret Service Agents Say They Want To Testify To Counter Her Version Of Events; Pro-Abortion Democrats Still Can't Get Over With The Supreme Court Decision That The Squad Members Are Now Leading The Push On How To Fix The Issue; Roe Rage: Abortion Decision Sparks Liberal Media Meltdown; Defund Disaster: Liberal City To Cut Sheriff Funding Amid Crime Wave; Marijuana Madness: Teens Getting Sent To Hospital Over Highly Potent Weed\b0\par\par\uc2 Jeanine Pirro, Harold Ford, Jr., Jesse Watters, Dana Perino, Brian  Kilmeade \par \uc2 8,842 \uc2 words\par \uc2 29 June 2022\par \uc2 Fox News: The Five\par \uc2 FOXFIV\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NEIL CAVUTO, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: All right. Well --\par \par \uc2 SUSAN LI, FOX BUSINESS CORRESPONDENT: It's going to get worse. I'm going to be staying in my couch for a while.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Now I'm going to get out of my advocates and do some math problems. But guys, thank you both very much. We needed a perspective from young people. I kept pushing for some really, really old. They said, it's only you, Neil. I hear that, it hurt my feelings. Here is THE FIVE.\par \par \uc2 JEANINE PIRRO, FOX NEWS HOST: Hello, everyone. I'm Judge Jeanine Pirro along with Harold Ford, Jr., Jesse Watters, Dana Perino, and Brian Kilmeade. It's 5 o'clock in New York City, and this is THE FIVE.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES OF AMERICA: There's an old expression in a little town in Delaware called Claymont, a little steel town. They say, we are like poor relatives. We show up when we are invited and we stay longer than we should. So be careful. We may not go back.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: President Biden may not be joking about staying overseas given the other disaster he has created here at home. A whopping 85 percent of the country says America is on the wrong track. And that includes 78 percent of Democrats. Apparently, President Biden is irritated by a lack of party enthusiasm when it comes to a potential second term for him.\par \par \uc2 The New York Times\uc2  reporting that the president is, quote, "irked," by Democrats who won't take yes for an answer on 2024. The Times adding, quote, "the president and his top aides have been stung by the questions about his plans, irritated at what they say is a lack of respect from their party and the press." But despite all the odds, \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  is trying to instill confidence in that sinking ship.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES OF AMERICA: \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is running for reelection and I will be his ticket mate.\par \par \uc2 DANA BASH, CHIEF POLITICAL CORRESPONDENT, CNN: Full stop.\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: Full stop. That's it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: And as the Democrats panic over their flailing 2024 prospects, the media thinks \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  will be the one to bail them out. CNN has begun their push for two-time presidential loser to make another run at the White House. And Gayle King is practically begging Hillary to do it.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING, CO-HOST, \uc2 CBS\uc2 : There's no scenario in 2024 that you would even remotely consider?\par \par \uc2 HILLARY CLINTON (D), FORMER U.S. SECRETARY OF STATE: You know, I can't imagine it. I really can't.\par \par \uc2 KING: That's not a no.\par \par \uc2 CLINTON: Well, but what I can imagine is staying as active and outspoken as I can.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: She can't imagine, Jesse.\par \par \uc2 JESSE WATTERS, FOX NEWS CO-HOST: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: She just can't. The two-time loser wants to go for it a third time.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: So, this operation Joe must