\par \uc2 PIRRO: It was.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Again and again, it was just painful. All right, good evening. Welcome to Washington. I'm Bret Baier.\par \par \uc2 Breaking tonight, there are serious allegations from the January 6 committee but there is serious pushback tonight on testimony from a former White House aide about then President Trump's behavior on the day of the Capitol riot.\par \par \uc2 Cassidy Hutchinson's recounting of the day to the House committee investigating the event is being challenged, some of it by people with firsthand knowledge of former President Trump's actions.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent David Spunt is at the Justice Department tonight with the latest. Good evening, David.\par \par \uc2 DAVID SPUNT, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Bret, good evening to you. Discussions are underway this evening over potential testimony involving the Secret Service agents mentioned during yesterday's explosive testimony.\par \par \uc2 Fox News has learned those agents are prepared to testify what the key witness said in part was not true.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The whole truth and nothing but the truth.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT (voice over): Former White House aide Cassidy Hutchinson says she was told by Tony Ornato, a top Secret Service official that President Trump in a fit of rage lunged toward a Secret Service agent and tried to grab the steering wheel of the presidential SUV while demanding to go to the Capitol on January 6th, 2021. Agent Bobby Engel and an unnamed driver were in the SUV with the president.\par \par \uc2 CASSIDY HUTCHINSON, FORMER AIDE TO WHITE HOUSE CHIEF OF STAFF \uc2 MARK MEADOWS\uc2 : Mr. Trump then used his free hand to lunge towards Bobby Engel. And when Mr. Ornato had recounted the story to me, he had motioned towards his clavicles.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: A source close to Ornato tells Fox News he denies her story about the SUV. Ornato was not in the vehicle, meaning Hutchinson's story is secondhand which she does not refute.\par \par \uc2 The source says Ornato, Engel and the SUV driver are ready to testify on camera in public and deny the allegations.\par \par \uc2 KELLYANNE CONWAY, FORMER WHITE HOUSE COUNSELOR: If you really want to know the facts, you're going to get everybody involved as first person witnesses and actors in the event to actually testify.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: In a statement to Fox News, the Secret Service revealed the agency has cooperated with the one-six committee for a year and will continue to do so.\par \par \uc2 An agency spokesman tells Fox News that committee did not reach out in the days leading up to Hutchinson's testimony. Lawmakers are defending her credibility.\par \par \uc2 REP. ADAM SCHIFF (D-CA): And I think she's a very, very smart, very capable, very honest individual. She has no incentive to make up something that isn't true.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: Some Republicans are also supporting Hutchinson, including former White House Chief of Staff \uc2 Mick Mulvaney\uc2  who tweeted I know her, I don't think she is lying.\par \par \uc2 Hutchinson also said that Trump allegedly knew the crowd on the way to the Capitol was armed and he still convinced people to go.\par \par \uc2 HUTCHINSON: I remember Tony mentioning knives, guns, in the form of pistols and rifles, bear spray, body armor, spears and flagpoles.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SPUNT (on camera): And that could potentially open up \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  to criminal exposure. That's why the Department of Justice is involved.\par \par \uc2 Bret, tonight, an attorney for former -- a spokesperson for former Chief of Staff \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2  said he never sought out a pardon and an attorney for Cassidy Hutchinson told us just a little while ago, she completely stands by her testimony from yesterday, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Understanding that David, but is there anything from the committee saying why they would put her forward without the Secret Service? They've talked to them already, why not have that backed up without threatening the rest of her testimony? It seems like it does.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: Well, you know, we have reported that the Secret Service was never reached out to in the days leading up to Hutchinson's testimony which has many people scratching their heads, because you have different stories here.\par \par \uc2 We know that Ornato was interviewed behind the scenes, so was Bobby Engel. But as far as why the Secret Service was not reached out to before, that's what we want to know.\par \par \uc2 We also reached out to the committee multiple times today, still have not heard back from them on that very issue you just bring up.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Exactly. David Spunt at the Justice Department. David, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Amid international confusion and frustration over President Biden's energy policy at major European meetings, there are growing questions tonight about just how Turkey was convinced to drop its resistance to \uc2 NATO\uc2  expansion. Signals from President Biden's team and Turkey's leadership are unclear.\par \par \uc2 White House correspondent Jacqui Heinrich is traveling with the president reports tonight from Madrid, Spain. Good evening, Jacqui.\par \par \uc2 JACQUI HEINRICH, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Good evening to you, Bret. Well, \uc2 NATO\uc2  will soon have two new members and Russia is threatening to put nukes and missiles on its border in response.\par \par \uc2 President Biden's answer sending more U.S. troops to Europe. But all of that is being overshadowed by questions over how Biden is helping Sweden and Finland get into \uc2 NATO\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice over): Turkey wants to buy F-16 fighter jets from the U.S. and after dropping its opposition to Finland and Sweden joining \uc2 NATO\uc2 , President Erdogan suggested his meeting today with President Biden will pay dividends.\par \par \uc2 RECEP TAYYIP ERDOGAN, TURKISH PRESIDENT (through translator): Thanks to our efforts, we believe that we will be able to go back to our countries with our hands full and with full satisfaction.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: It's a pickle for Biden wanting credit for his role in orchestrating the deal but also distance fearing perceived concessions.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I want to particularly thank you for what you did, putting together the situation with regard to Finland and Sweden.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: A senior administration official tells Fox, Turkey's deal with Finland and Sweden happened on its own. Biden just pushed the timeline.\par \par \uc2 But a statement from the Pentagon just hours after the news broke seemed to indicate that wasn't the case. A U.S. Defense official telling the New York Times the U.S. Department of Defense fully supports Turkey's modernization plans for its F-16 fleet.\par \par \uc2 Officials now say that was a reference to support the administration previously expressed in April. No comment on whether F-16s came up today.\par \par \uc2 With Finland and Sweden on the path to \uc2 NATO\uc2  membership, Biden may return to the U.S. with a win. A tough feat to repeat next month with Saudi Arabia and the UAE apparently unable to ramp up oil production much to bring down soaring gas costs.\par \par \uc2 JOHN KIRBY, PRESS SECRETARY, PENTAGON: I won't talk about that particular conversation but I can tell you, first of all, OPEC Plus has already increased by 50 percent scheduled increases of oil production that were set for July and August, so that oil production has already increased.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Now, team Biden turning up the heat on oil at home.\par \par \uc2 AMOS HOCHSTEIN, U.S. ENERGY SECURITY SENIOR ADVISER: There is nothing the industry needs, literally nothing the industry needs to increase production in the United States to have additional oil. They have all the permits they 