uc2 NATO\uc2  officials today, President Zelenskyy says Ukraine needs more artillery and $5 billion per month to fight this war.\par \par \uc2 ZELENSKYY (through translator): I can say what we need. We need security guarantees. And you need to find the place for Ukraine in the common security space. Either urgent help Ukraine that is enough to win or faster delayed war between Russia and yourself.\par \par \uc2 FOY: Russian forces are still pushing for full control of the Luhansk region of Donbas, the city of Lysychans'k, the final piece of land in the region not under Russian control.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 FOY (on camera): Bret, tonight, Ukraine is announcing the largest prisoner exchange of the war so far securing the release of 144 soldiers.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the head of the Donetsk People's Republic in Donbas which is pro-Russian is announcing that it too will receive 144 fighters as part of the deal. We'll send it back to you.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: OK, Nate, thank you. Let's get reaction on today's top stories from Fox News Senior Political Analyst, Brit Hume.\par \par \uc2 Brit, good evening. Let's start with the president's trip overseas and how you see it, the big events and as he's dealing with world leaders on a number of fronts, particularly energy.\par \par \uc2 BRIT HUME, FOX NEWS SENIOR POLITICAL ANALYST: Well, certainly opening the door to Sweden and Finland joining \uc2 NATO\uc2  and getting that obstacle cleared with working with Turkey is important and they'll have long term consequences.\par \par \uc2 Certainly, this is an unintended consequence as far as Russia's concerned when they invaded Ukraine, the last thing they wanted was \uc2 NATO\uc2  expansion to come out of this. So, that's a point certainly in the president's favor.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, of course, all these western countries are struggling to get their sanctions to bite because fuel prices are so high that Russia is making more money than ever selling the same or less oil and other fuels.\par \par \uc2 And that's a problem of western energy policy. And these Western leaders who the president included, would do well to stay, they -- let's get more all kinds of energy, nuclear, you know, fossil fuel and renewables for the time being as much as we can, as fast as we can. That is a step that they've all stopped short of taking. And the consequences are, you know, that Russia is still able to finance the war, which they hoped the sanctions would hamper.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Brit, I want to take you to the January sixth hearing and your thoughts after that yesterday coming out of the testimony, I called it stunning and compelling, because of what was said in the -- in what she was testifying to and the conversations that she said she heard.\par \par \uc2 Then, for the Secret Service, both publicly and privately to tell our reporters that they're pushing back on that one aspect in the piece (PH). It opens the door to a lot of questions about that testimony and that's what's coming today. What are your thoughts?\par \par \uc2 HUME: Well, a couple of things. One is, of course, it was a compelling testimony, I'm not sure how much it advanced the committee's theories about Trump being the leader of, you know, a sophisticated seven point plan that brought us to the brink of losing our democracy. That seems to me that's nowhere near what has been established. And the testimony yesterday, didn't come close to doing that either.\par \par \uc2 However, what it did do now that the testimony is being challenged, is it shows why it's not a good idea to put witnesses on without any cross examination or any viewpoints expressed from the other side of the issue when there is another side and certainly, in this case. This is what the committee has decided it will do. It'll put on a T.V. production, which is what it has done. And it seems to have tripped itself up on that because the testimony is now being disputed openly by the Secret Service.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: First of all, I said the beast, but it was the smaller Secret Service SUV, but either way, how does this happen in a committee that's that scripted and that laying all of this methodically out? They call this surprise hearing and then, they trip on this and it opens the door to critics of the committee from the beginning?\par \par \uc2 HUME: Well, I think, you know, just consistently what the committee has done, when you think about it, they'll call witnesses before the committee and the witness will be sitting there. And then, the committee will proceed to play excerpts from the deposition. You know, the video deposition of the same witness.\par \par \uc2 So very peculiar way to proceed. You know, I've been around Washington a while. I covered the Hill for a long time. I don't remember any committee where all the members were chosen by one party. Even the -- you know, the minority parties, representatives, and they're all on the same page. They all agree.\par \par \uc2 Well, if you don't have any dissenting voices, this is the kind of thing can happen, it's a mistake.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: After two of the congressmen were turned down, then the minority leader walked away, but it still -- it does not have any pushback, and where does it go from --\par \par \uc2 HUME: Oh, you need that to have credibility. You need that.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And so does it have credibility at the end? Do you think?\par \par \uc2 HUME: Well, if this blows up, this sensational testimony, which I don't think advanced the ball on the committee's main thesis, but this testimony, which everybody was, you know, reps and rhapsodies over yesterday, blows up and is found to be, you know, seriously contested and in doubt.\par \par \uc2 I think that will damage the credibility of the committee and its final work product, whatever that may be.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Brit, as always, thank you.\par \par \uc2 The Chairman of the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  says ther