come. Trump-endorsed State Senator David Bailey easily became the GOP gubernatorial nominee thanks in part to his future opponent, Governor \uc2 J.B. Pritzker\uc2 , and the \uc2 Democratic Governors Association\uc2  who had spent close to $35 million attacking GOP establishment candidate Richard Irvin, who potentially could have posed the biggest threat to Pritzker.\par \par \uc2 RICHARD IRVIN, (R) ILLINOIS GUBERNATORIAL CANDIDATE: \uc2 J.B. Pritzker\uc2  won the primary for governor here in Illinois. He spent a historic amount of money to choose his own Republican opponent in the general election.\par \par \uc2 ACUNA: Some analysts point out the risks Democrats take in boosting fringe GOP candidates could backfire come November.\par \par \uc2 JESSICA TAYLOR, "COOK POLITICAL REPORT": Is this is, as it's shaping up to be, a very bad environment for Democrats, it's still putting a chance out there that this candidate could win if the headwinds are bad enough for Democrats.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 ACUNA (on camera): The GOP winners from last night still face uphill climbs in their blue state, but in Colorado, it was the unaffiliated voters that helped bring the candidates across the line, the same voters that Democrats will need in November. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Alicia Acuna in Denver. Alicia, thank you.\par \par \uc2 A 53rd person has died from what is being called this country's worst human smuggling disaster in modern history. Dozens of migrants were found in a sweltering truck tanker in south Texas. The incident is ramping up criticism of the president's border policy. Correspondent Griff Jenkins is in Eagle Pass again tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 GRIFF JENKINS, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Texas Governor Greg Abbott announcing additional measures to secure Texas' border and prevent human smuggling tragedies.\par \par \uc2 GOV. GREG ABBOTT, (R) TEXAS: DPS will create and implement a checkpoint strategy beginning immediately where they will begin targeting trucks like the one that was used where these people perished.\par \par \uc2 JENKINS: And he continues to blame the Biden administration.\par \par \uc2 ABBOTT: It is the deadliest migrant smuggling incident on U.S. soil, and it's on President Biden's watch.\par \par \uc2 JENKINS: Meanwhile, a community grieves.\par \par \uc2 CAROLINA CANIZALES, VIGIL ORGANIZER: Migrants matter, and their lives matter.\par \par \uc2 JENKINS: As the death toll rises to 53, 40 are male, 13 are females, with teenagers among the victims.\par \par \uc2 This as another human smuggling operation goes wrong this morning in the Rio Grande Valley. A high-speed car chase involving local law enforcement in Palmview, Texas. As the smuggler's car crashed, two migrants were killed, two others were hospitalized. The fate of the driver is unknown. \uc2 Texas Department of Public Safety\uc2  Lieutenant Chris Olivarez says these tragedies are on the rise.\par \par \uc2 LT. CHRIS OLIVAREZ, \uc2 TEXAS DEPARTMENT OF PUBLIC SAFETY\uc2 : Forcing a lot a lot more high-speed chases involving human smugglers, especially in the Del Rio sector.\par \par \uc2 JENKINS: All of this comes as the nation awaits the Supreme Court's decision over whether the Biden administration has the authority to end the remain in Mexico policy.\par \par \uc2 Should they rule in favor of Texas, how much confidence do you have in the Biden administration that they will abide by that and reinstate it fully?\par \par \uc2 ABBOTT: If they do not follow the standard, Texas can take legal action and go to court to hold the president, to hold Secretary Mayorkas, and others in the federal government in contempt of court if they fail to live up to that order, and that's exactly what we will be seeking to do.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 JENKINS (on camera): And less than an hour after that press conference, less than a mile away, a group of more than 300 migrants crossed just up the river. We spoke to the property owner. She tells us she sees that now every day, and she was distraught yesterday. She witnessed a migrant women die on her property.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Griff, quickly. the tractor trailers, are they doing more surveillance now, stopping them?\par \par \uc2 JENKINS: Well, what Governor Abbott would like to do is to have a more stringent secondary check of 18-wheelers specifically for this reason, because he believes the manpower with the Border Patrol is not sufficient for the initial stop. So he wants these trucks checked more thoroughly, and that's exactly what he intends to do. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Griff Jenkins live in Eagle Pass. Griff, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Up next, we continue our series about the poisoning of America with a look at what the crime labs are seeing on the streets, an exclusive look behind the scenes.\par \par \uc2 As we go to break, the last remaining Medal of Honor winner from World War II has died. Hershel Woody Williams died at the Veteran Affairs Medical Center bearing his name in Huntington, West Virginia. As a young Marine corporal, Williams went ahead of his unit during the battle of Iwo Jima, February, 1945, and eliminated a series of Japanese machine gun positions. West Virginia Senator \uc2 Joe Manchin\uc2  calls Williams the embodiment of a true American hero. Woody Williams was 98.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Tonight, we continue our series on the poisoning of America. Correspondent Alexis McAdams is in Houston, where she takes us inside a crime lab dealing with the epidemic of fentanyl abuse.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 ALEXIS MCADAMS, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: These may look like prescription pills, but they're not.\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER HATCH, TEXAS PUBLIC SAFETY DEPARTMENT CRIME LAB: These are all illegitimate Xanax tablets, and they look excellent.\par \par \uc2 MCADAMS: Scientists at the \uc2 Texas Department of Public Safety\uc2  tell me each one is laced with one of the deadliest drugs in America, fentanyl.\par \par \uc2 HATCH: It acts in your body a lot like heroin and hydrocodone and oxycodone, which is why you traditionally see it in these types of tablets.\par \par \uc2 This is actually a kilo of fentanyl.\par \par \uc2 MCADAMS: DPS forensic Scientist Jennifer Hatch says nearly every street drug she analyzes is laced with a synthetic opioid, and most Americans have no idea what they really taking.\par \par \uc2 HATCH: They fool us a lot of times here in the laboratory.\par \par \uc2 MCADAMS: Even scientists have to use high-tech instruments like this to find out what's inside.\par \par \uc2 Most of the time you see it in pill form at the lab here in Houston, but it also comes in a pressed brick form. How lethal is this?\par \par \uc2 HATCH: If you consider a lethal dose to be 2 milligrams, it could potentially produce up to 500,000 lethal doses.\par \par \uc2 MCADAMS: And that would kill hundreds of thousands of people.\par \par \uc2 Those doses potentially lethal enough to kill more than the entire population of Cleveland. But before the drug ends up at the lab, law enforcement has to find it. DPS Lieutenant Chris Olivarez says the Mexican cartels hide it in everything from tires to water pumps.\par \par \uc2 LT. CHRIS OLIVAREZ, \uc2 TEXAS DEPARTMENT OF PUBLIC SAFETY\uc2 : They want people to be addicted to his drug because it's about supply and demand.\par \par \uc2 MCADAMS: It's so bad right now, do you think it's going to continue to get worse?\par \par \uc2 OLIVAREZ: It's not slowing down.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 MCADAMS (on camera): Bret, that death toll is expected to climb, and DPS says the drug cartels are making big money by smuggling fentanyl here into the United States, but sadly, it's Americans that are paying the ultimate price. DPS again urging the federal government to do more to step in and stop those exporters in China and also to go after the Mexican drug cartel. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Alexis McAdams in Houston. Alexis, thanks.\par \par \uc2 We will talk about the fentanyl crisis with Senate Homeland Security Committee member, Ohio Senator Rob Portman tomorrow on SPECIAL REPORT.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the panel on the controversy over yesterday's testimony to the January 6th Committee, plus President Biden's trip to Europe.\par \par \uc2 First, Beyond our Borders tonight. House Speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  meets with Pope Francis and receives communion during a papal mass despite her support of abortion rights. Pelosi's home archbishop in San Francisco has he will no longer allow her to receive the sacrament in his archdiocese because of that support.\par \par \uc2 Israeli Prime Minister Naftali Bennett will not run in upcoming elections. Bennett led a broad but fragile coalition government that came unraveled barely a year after taking office. The government announced last week that it would dissolve the Knesset ahead of elections expected this fall.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at Tokyo. One of the big stories there tonight, flights from Tokyo's Haneda airport to Gimpo airport in Seoul resume for the first time in more than two years. The route was suspended due to the spread of COVID-19. Japan began curbing some of its strict border controls earlier this month.\par \par \uc2 Just some of the other stories Beyond our Borders tonight. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CASSIDY HUTCHINSON, FORMER SPECIAL ASSISTANT TO PRESIDENT TRUMP: The president said something to the effect of, I'm the effing president. Take me up to the Capitol now. To which Bobby responded, sir, we have to go back to the West Wing. The president reached up towards the front of the vehicle to grab at the steering wheel.\par \par \uc2 KELLYANNE CONWAY, FORMER WHITE HOUSE COUNSELOR: This is a one-sided committee hearing. I think the committee made a big mistake but not cross examining some these witnesses.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 JAMIE RASKIN\uc2 , (D-MD) JANUARY 6TH SELECT COMMITTEE: Cassidy Hutchinson is an entirely credible witness. She testified under oath in front of the entire country. Everyone was able to judge her demeanor. She has no motivation or interest in lying in any way.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: There's reaction to January 6th committee, and specifically one of the stories about the Secret Service and pushback. We've got reporting that there will be pushback to how it was described, the president in that vehicle going to Capitol Hill.\par \par \uc2 What about this and what it does for the committee and where it stands? Let's bring in our panel, Mollie Hemingway, editor in chief of "The Federalist," Guy Benson, political editor at Townhall.com, host of "The Guy Benson Show" on FOX News Radio, and Jeff Mason, White House correspondent for Reuters. I want to play this soundbite, Virginia Republican Denver Riggleman, reacting to the testimony yesterday.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DENVER RIGGLEMAN, FORMER VIRGINIA REPRESENTATIVE: It was actually stunning what we saw yesterday, and I think we need to applaud the bravery of Cassidy Hutchinson. I think it's time for \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2  to come forward. I think it's time for \uc2 Pat Cipollone\uc2  to come forward. It's time for them to talk to the committee. It's just that simple.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Mollie, what about this and what this does to the committee and its legitimacy going forward?\par \par \uc2 MOLLIE HEMINGWAY, EDITOR IN CHIEF, "THE FEDERALIST": First of all, Denver Riggleman is a never-Trump Republican. He has worked for \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2 , and I think that is a perspective that gets a lot of treatment here. But what we saw yesterday, within hours, every explosive claim that was made by this witness blew apart. She said that Trump lunged for the steering wheel, that he had to be physically restrained, that she had written a note that actually someone else had written. And it just completely blew apart what was supposed to be a very important day for the committee.\par \par \uc2 It's part of the pattern that we've seen for this committee. They falsified a text from Jim Jordan, Adam Schiff did that. \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  gave a false timeline where she claimed that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  didn't do anything for 187 minutes. It was 25 minutes before he called for peace. They keep doctoring the video where Trump says to engage in a peaceful protest. And the entire committee itself is illegitimate in that there are no Republican members.\par \par \uc2 And so they need to convey somehow a sense of legitimacy, and they've done literally nothing to be considered legitimate.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You're saying Republicans. You're discounting Cheney and the Illinois congressman --\par \par \uc2 HEMINGWAY: Because they are appointed by Pelosi.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: By Pelosi. I understand. Jeff, your thoughts?\par \par \uc2 JEFF MASON, WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT, "REUTERS": I'm glad you corrected that, because there are two Republican members. And I also think one thing that you said, Mollie, isn't true. I would not say that all of her claims have fallen apart. Certainly, the ones about what happened in the limousine were hearsay, and there's reporting on FOX and other news networks and news organizations that suggested that may be false. And if it is, then they should come out in testify, and that would be interesting.\par \par \uc2 But there were other things that happened in the testimony yesterday. The video of the man with a gun in a tree near the ellipse, that wasn't made up. That was real. And the fact that the president apparently knew in advance that people were armed before coming to that and then going to the Capitol is real. And those were also important things that she said.\par \par \uc2 HEMINGWAY: Even that, we have evidence that there were people who knew that there were dangerous people that were coming onto the Capitol. Any committee worth its salt should be investigating why the Capitol was so ill-prepared to handle what everybody knew was going to be a very contentious protest. A legitimate committee would in fact look into that. Why the Capitol were so unprepared, why \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  didn't do more, and she's the person who would be in charge of that security. They're not even allowing people to ask those questions, and they have no one on their committee, not a single Republican appointed member. And that is important that those people were appointed --\par \par \uc2 MASON: Those things aren't mutually exclusive.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And it's also important to point out that the two Republicans that they were trying to put on were -- said they couldn't be on, and then the minority leader chose not to put other Republicans on.\par \par \uc2 HEMINGWAY: But never in history has anyone kicked off the ranking member, that's never been done in congressional history. That's completely unprecedented.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And they stepped away. That's right.\par \par \uc2 Here is "The Wall Street Journal" Guy, more testimony and Trump's tirade, as they call it, "Democrats want to use the January 6th investigation to paint the entire Republican Party as a gang of insurrectionist nuts. The committee is steeped in partisanship. But that doesn't mean Republicans should look away from the considerable evidence it is producing about Mr. Trump's behavior that would surely be relevant to voters if he runs in 2024."\par \par \uc2 But Guy, in the wake of what we saw with the Russia investigation, in the wake of the committee hearing we saw with \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2  where we saw that testimony fall apart, now with the questions about this testimony, isn't it legitimate for Mollie's point to say isn't this another job to go after him in some way, shape, or form?\par \par \uc2 GUY BENSON, POLITICAL EDITOR, TOWNHALL.COM: I think that that is definitely part of the agenda of this committee. I think a lot of what was so objectionable about January 6th was readily apparent on January 6th for the entire country to see. And I think what was very bad back then with the fact being bad for president Trump are still very bad facts for President Trump today.\par \par \uc2 I think we learned some new things during this committee. I wouldn't say the whole thing is illegitimate. But clearly it is so one-sided, part of that is the Republicans' own fault, their own decisions that they made in this process, I think it is fair to question how they come at certain issues, whether or not we are getting this public view of some, perhaps countervailing information.\par \par \uc2 Apparently, the agents allegedly involved in this limo situation have spoken to the committee. If they are going to contradict what was young woman said yesterday, let them come and do it in front of the cameras for the whole country to see. If this committee truly wants to get to the truth, the full picture of what happened that day, which I think most Americans would be interested in, they have to not just hear information, but actually present to the contrary information publicly that may come in either direction. good for Trump, bad for Trump, it shouldn't matter. Let's see what actually happened.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We're getting word from the wire services that the committee is subpoenaing \uc2 Pat Cipollone\uc2 , the White House counsel. And obviously he has direct testimony to a number of these events, at least as we heard them. Where do you think this goes?\par \par \uc2 HEMINGWAY: It's unclear because there is nothing about this committee that has been done in a way that lends itself to be taken seriously. And yet these people are very aggressive. I don't think this is just about Trump. I think this is about criminalizing any support for the previous administration, making it so that people really think twice if they ever want to enact policies that are different from what the D.C. consensus says. And I think it's a very serious threat to the republic, and they will not be happy until they are able to get a scalp or something.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I want to turn, Jeff, to the current administration. This is what Vice President Harris said on CNN just the other day.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Your friend, Congressmen \uc2 Jim Clyburn\uc2 , said last week that if President Biden doesn't seek reelection, you would be first on his list in 2024. Have you talked to President Biden about reelection? And what do you say to Congressman Clyburn?\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , (D) VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is running for reelection, and I will be his ticket-mate.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Full stop?\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: Full stop. That's it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: But then there was some sort of clarification, or they talked to a reporter on a trip. What happened?\par \par \uc2 MASON: Yes. She's on her way to California and came back and spoke to the pool, which is this case was just one reporter, and clarified -- I don't have my comments in front of me, but I think it's largely in line with what she said, that she'll be his ticket-mate, which means -- no, I remember. The words were, he intends to run. So maybe that's --\par \par \uc2 BAIER: That's where the clarification.\par \par \uc2 MASON: That's probably the clarification. There's a little bit of a pullback, although everyone else I talk to at the White House also says he's going to run again. There is a little distance there.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Even though there are increasing stories, Guy, that he is upset that people are talking that he's not running, and yet more people are talking that is not running.\par \par \uc2 BENSON: Right, they are saying the out loud part out loud at this point. And I still suspect he won't ultimately run for reelection, but I'm wondering. He is a sort of a cantankerous, ornery guy sometime when he feels like he's being attacked or doubted. I wonder if his fellow Democrats might almost insult him into running, because he wants to say enough with this. I'm doing it.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, we've seen that before. All right, panel, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Finally, it's a special day, especially for Coco, the yorkie. The small pup was relaxing outside her home in Canada when a bald eagle swooped in, snatched her right up. The doorbell camera captures the moment. You can hear Coco. She reappears. She's OK, a few scrapes and scratches. She is now home, well, and recovering. The eagle.\par \par \uc2 Thanks for inviting us into your home tonight. That's it for this SPECIAL REPORT, fair, balanced, and unafraid. That eagle was aggressive, Jesse Watters.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FNSPBB0020220630ei6t00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Update On Migrants Killed In Abandoned Truck In Texas; Texas Governor Speaks After Migrants Found Dead In Truck; 53 Migrants Dead After Truck Abandoned In Texas; R. Kelly Sentenced To 30 Years For Sex Trafficking; Dems Sour On Biden Economy As Midterms Near; Inflation Raising Cost Of Construction Projects; Some Of Former White House Aide's Claims About January 6 Disputed; Kemp And Abrams In Dead Heat In Georgia Gubernatorial Race; Justice Thomas Face Backlash After Roe Ruling; Last World War II Medal Of Honor Recipient Dies At 98\b0\par\par\uc2 Martha MacCallum, Leslie Marshall, David Lee Miller, Lydia Hu,  Aishah Hasnie \par \uc2 8,256 \uc2 words\par \uc2 29 June 2022\par \uc2 Fox News Channel: The Story with Martha MacCallum\par \uc2 HUNDD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LYDIA HU, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: So, all of this means, Sandra, that taxpayers, we're getting less bang for our buck, projects that aren't going to be finished on time, they're going to cost a lot more.\par \par \uc2 SANDRA SMITH, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: OK, appreciate you joining us on that story. We'll be watching it great to be with you all, as we await Governor Abbott in Texas. I'm Sandra Smith.\par \par \uc2 JOHN ROBERTS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: And I'm John Roberts. We will see you again tomorrow. THE STORY with Martha starts right now.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA MACCALLUM, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: All right, guys, thank you very much, John and Sandra. We're going to take you straight to Texas where Governor Abbott is now speaking about the tragic loss of life. 53 people packed in a sweltering truck lost their lives. Let's listen to the governor.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GOV. GREG ABBOTT (R-TX): One is record number of people coming across the border illegally. But no, there is a greater sense of lawlessness that comes from not enforcing the law. When, when whether it'd be migrants or any smugglers or anybody else see the laws are enforced. They will abuse those laws. And we've seen increased brazenness by cartels that feel empowered, because we do not have a federal government that's pushing back against those cartels and their illegal activity.\par \par \uc2 And of course, the worst thing of all, is the catastrophic death that we have witnessed. Now, we saw this horrendous death occur in a truck in San Antonio earlier this week. And it is heartbreaking. The numbers have increased. It is now reported that 53 people in that truck lost their lives. It is the deadliest migrant smuggling incident on U.S. soil, and it's on President Biden's watch. The thing about it is that is not the only way in which people who are trying to migrate here lose their lives in the harsh, high temperature of Texas in the summertime, and the Texas heat, and approve even more deadly.\par \par \uc2 Every year there are many reports about migrants who lose their lives in the heat. I was visiting with the sheriff moments ago. And he was telling me reports about how there have been multiple people in just the past week who had lost their lives, mostly from heat exhaustion, there may have been, in addition to that, one or two who lost their lives in the river. This is harsh terrain and harsh weather. And because of the way that the Biden administration is not enforcing the immigration laws, that's attracting people and enticing people to make this very dangerous trek, causing them to lose their lives.\par \par \uc2 I urge the president, stop the loss of lives. You have the ability to stop people from losing the lives if you make it clear that no one could come across illegally. There's a perfectly legal way that people can immigrate to the United States of America, is that legal pathway, Mr. President, that you must insist upon. And if you do that, you will have a role in reducing the loss of life. Now, going back -- many of these deaths could be prevented if Biden simply fully funded the Border Patrol operation of the United States of America and implemented the policies that the Border Patrol needs in order to do their real job.\par \par \uc2 And the real job is not the paper processing work that they've been assigned to do. Their real job is both to secure the border, as well as to do things like inspect the vehicle that was carrying those people who lost their lives. As you all surely know, the truck has through a border point checkpoint, it was unexpected. It was not inspected because the Border Patrol does not have the resources to be able to inspect all of the trucks. And as a result, the Border Patrol did not have the capability of saving those lives.\par \par \uc2 Mr. President, you can fix that if you provide what is categorized as the minimum number of border patrol officers that, who are needed to do the fundamental job of the United States of America and adequately fund their operations to make sure that they are going to be able to inspect those vehicles. Well, in Texas, we once again, are going to try to step up and play a role by the state to address this catastrophe that President Biden is responsible for.\par \par \uc2 Today, I'm announcing that Texas is going to add additional truck checkpoints. DPS will create and implement a checkpoint strategy beginning immediately. They will begin targeting trucks, like the one that was used when these people perished, to make sure that we will have a better capability of perhaps stopping future trucks like that, as well as stopping the smugglers and the cartel members who are trying to profiteer off of it.\par \par \uc2 Now we're in Eagle Pass today, for a reason. Eagle Pass is part of the Del Rio sector. And Del Rio sector has seen the largest number of illegal crossings in the past week. There was a time when most of the illegal crossings would take place in the Rio Grande Valley. And that's no longer true. Right now, we're at ground zero here and into del Rio, where we have the largest number of border crossings, and we have information indicating that there could be a meaningful rise in the number of border crossings in this region.\par \par \uc2 So, we want to step up and make sure that we will do more, to better secure this area. As the mayor told me earlier, he said the situation in Eagle Pass has gone from bad to worse, with the increased number of people coming through this area. The bottom line is this, despite the increasing numbers, despite these caravans on the Mexico side of the border right now, the Biden administration still is doing nothing whatsoever to stop it. Texas is going to take action, to do our part. To try to reduce the illegal immigration coming into our country, we're going to be adding more resources and stronger strategies to control all the border land that is owned or controlled by state or local governments.\par \par \uc2 Let me make clear what I just said. The property along the border is owned or controlled, typically by three different groups. There is some property that is owned or controlled by state or local governments. This property we're on right now is controlled by the local government. There is other property that's owned or controlled by the state. However, there are some properties on the border that is owned or controlled by the federal government. It was under control by the federal government, Texas does not have the authority or the capability to stop the illegal immigrants from going to that land.\par \par \uc2 There's another area that we're, we're working on and that is private property. There are people whether it be in this community or people up and down the border, who own property on the border. We are getting authorization from as many of them as we can, for the state of Texas, to be able to put up barriers, whether it be concertina wire, or fences, or other types of barriers, so we can prevent people from entering --\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: OK. Governor Abbott, with a lot on his hands. And when you listen to the breakdown of who controls the border, he's talking about there, some areas are local jurisdiction, some are Texas state jurisdiction, some are federal jurisdiction, and absolutely no cohesive policy by the federal government to control the border of the country in terms of who's coming in and out.\par \par \uc2 And we have record numbers this year, record numbers of people crossing that border and interactions with a border patrol that has become increasingly under pressure, and, and less and less supported in the ability to do their jobs. So, let's bring in \uc2 Chad Wolf\uc2 , Former Deputy -- Former Department, I should say, of Homeland Security, Acting Secretary; and Katie Pavlich, Townhall.com News Editor and Fox News Contributor. Welcome to you both.\par \par \uc2 What, what a horrific situation: 53 people lost their lives trying to get into this country in the back of a truck, they were abandoned by someone who according to reports, when they finally found him was "very high on meth." And they believe that the people who were involved in this, in this truck, the people on this side of the border and the other side of the border, all illegal here illegally in this country.\par \par \uc2 And obviously, I don't think it's too much of a stretch to assume that they're dealing with cartels who are basically controlling the border now from everything that we understand. Chad, when that's the situation and you have these people left to die, who is in charge, and why are we not hearing some sense of responsibility and outrage and urgency?\par \par \uc2 CHAD WOLF\uc2 , FORMER DHS ACTING SECRETARY: Well, I think that's a great question. And obviously, it's a devastating incident, what's going on in Texas. But, I look, this stems from not having any deterrence in the immigration and border security system that we have today. individuals feel emboldened, cartels feel emboldened. And we're in the Biden administration at the end of the day is giving them predictability, predictability, and that nothing is changing along that border in the last 15 months. There are no new policies, there's no new procedures, there's no new announcements to try to stop the criminal activity that we're seeing.\par \par \uc2 And so, you have these migrants that are being, being advertised from the cartels saying, look, if we get you into the U.S., the vast percentage of you high likelihood that you will remain in the U.S. because they're not deporting individuals, and they're not removing individuals. So, in some sense of the world, it's a perfect storm that is occurring, you have record number of folks wanting to come to the U.S. And you have an administration that is putting forth a set of policies that are doing nothing, absolutely nothing to deter this illegal behavior. And I think unfortunately, you're going to see more and more incidents like, like we've seen in Texas.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. So, take your life in your hands, make this dangerous trek, try to get across and as the governor points out, people die just in the heat every day crossing that border, Katie. And I couldn't help but think, you know, the, the President's never been to the border to check out this ongoing situation. Vice President Harris was put in charge of the root causes. So, I'm wondering what she thinks are the root causes of what we saw happen in this truck, Katie.\par \par \uc2 KATIE PAVLICH, TOWNHALL.COM NEWS EDITOR: You know, Martha, this administration, through their policies on border security and a lack of interior enforcement have completely emboldened the cartels. They have operational control of the southern border with Mexico, the Biden administration has done very little to pressure the Mexican government to go after these cartel and disease.\par \par \uc2 I was there in April and learned that these cartels are making $100 million per week, per week on smuggling people across the border. A year ago, in June of 2021, Congressman Tony Gonzalez, who represents a border district said that the local authorities were asking them for body bags, because ranchers kept finding so many migrants on their property who were dying as a result of taking this journey.\par \par \uc2 The administration has rolled back a number of Trump administration policies that were in place that prevented this kind of behavior, that prevented the cartels from profiting in the way that they are today making more money on humans as a commodity than they are on drug smuggling. And as a result of their policies, to policy to take back all of those things, regardless of whether they were working, they've implemented an inhumane policy at the border.\par \par \uc2 Open borders are inhumane borders, and you've seen it with this tragedy of the 51 people who were locked into a tractor trailer like animals, and abandoned that happens all the time in the desert, and they do it to little kids as well.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: It's hard to imagine a worst death. And we're hearing that some of them were teenagers. And likely there's such an immense trafficking problem, sex trafficking, that is run by these cartels, which gets little to no attention. We've heard so much this week about women's lives, and women the importance of women's lives, I'm thinking about these girls that were likely part of this.\par \par \uc2 And you know, I don't know whether or not they comprise part of this group that died. But, but just based on everything we're learning, it is likely, it is highly likely that that was some of what we're seeing. Because they you know, a lot of them don't have any identification on them.\par \par \uc2 They're like, they're not connected to they can't find a family that they're connected to. This is starting to hit home though. And I wonder when this starts to make a difference. Take a look at this Quinnipiac poll: Biden approval among Hispanics went from 55 percent in May of 2021 to 26 percent. That's almost half. It's half, more than half of a drop in in one year. Listen to Willie Geist on MSNBC, trying to sound the wake-up call on this for their viewers. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WILLIE GEIST, MSNBC HOST: We've talked about this with progressive pollsters, by the way, who say this idea that Latino voters in America are all in and they're there have this open mind and they'll go with Democrats wherever they go on immigration is just wrong. It's just it's just folly, and it's not true, and it's patronizing in many ways to make those assumptions. So yes, Democrats will have to reassess because some of them may not want to use the word crisis. That's exactly what it is down along the border.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Chad, is there more widespread understanding of what's going on now?\par \par \uc2 WOLF: Well, I think there is. Look at the end of the day, Hispanics, but I would say, you know, other populations across this country, they want a secure border. They believe in the rule of law. And so, when they see this type of lawlessness, and the inability of the federal government to actually enforce the law along that border, I think the vast majority of Americans are sick of it.\par \par \uc2 The vast majority of Americans through other polling have said that this administration's immigration policy and border security policy is widely unpopular. They don't believe in it. They think it needs to have a change in course, and I think you saw that with an election down in the Rio Grande Valley in South Texas, elected a Republican congresswoman for the very first time and hundreds of years.\par \par \uc2 I think the Hispanic community says enough is enough. A lot of them that come here legally do not like to see the illegal behavior that we see at the border.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: It's quite clear that the borders are open and that they are not compassionate. And when you look at this story, we got to leave it there. We've got some breaking news. Katie, thank you very much -- Katie Pavlich.\par \par \uc2 PAVLICH: Thanks, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: \uc2 Chad Wolf\uc2 . Always good to see you both. Thank you. So, we just got this in. Singer R. Kelly has now been sentenced to 30 years in prison for sex trafficking. Speaking of sex trafficking after the court heard emotional statements from his victims. This is the second verdict of this type that we have seen -- a sentencing I should say this week with Ghislaine Maxwell being the other one.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent David Lee Miller with these breaking details. Hi, David Lee.\par \par \uc2 DAVID LEE MILLER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Hi, Martha. You're right. This was a very emotional day in Brooklyn federal court.\par \par \uc2 As you mentioned, R. Kelly now sentenced to 30 years behind bars for sexually abusing children and young women. He faced a possible life sentence.\par \par \uc2 MILLER (voice-over): Seven of his victims spoke before the court. One talked about what she called and I'm quoting now, deplorable acts on both male and female victims. She said, he "used his fame and power for his own sexual gratification."\par \par \uc2 Another victim wept as she described thoughts of suicide. She said, and I quote her again, "I only want R. Kelly put in prison, so he cannot cause any harm to others."\par \par \uc2 As the women spoke, Kelly avoided eye contact, either looking down or at his lawyer's computer screen.\par \par \uc2 An attorney for Kelly, in a memo to the court asked the judge to limit any sentence to no more than 10 years. The memo said Kelly was sexually abused as a child and blamed his molestation for "some of his behaviors".\par \par \uc2 Kelly's lawyer spoke briefly to reporters.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER BONJEAN, ATTORNEY REPRESENTING R. KELLY: Well, we believe that the offences for which he's been convicted do not justify a sentence greater than 10 years.\par \par \uc2 We believe there's significant litigation, and we believe that's the appropriate sentence.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MILLER: In a 31-page sentencing memo to the judge, prosecutors sought more than 25 years in prison for Kelly. A portion of the memo says, "Through his actions, the defendant exhibited a callous disregard for the very real effects that his crimes had on his victims and has shown no remorse for any of his conduct."\par \par \uc2 In September, a federal jury in Brooklyn convicted Kelly of nine criminal charges, including violation of the Mann Act for the coercion and transportation of women and girls to engage in illegal sexual activity.\par \par \uc2 He was also convicted of bribery, racketeering, and forced labor. Kelly's lawyer says the case was overcharged and is optimistic about winning an appeal.\par \par \uc2 MILLER (on camera): Even if successful, Kelly's legal problems, they are not over. He faces federal child pornography charges in Chicago as well as state charges in Illinois, and Minnesota.\par \par \uc2 But the headline today, Martha, R. Kelly, sentenced in federal court in Brooklyn, to 30 years behind bars. Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: David Lee, thank you very much. David Lee Miller with that breaking news.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM (voice-over): And a new poll that has just out moments ago shows President Biden is underwater with his own party on the economy.\par \par \uc2 We investigate how decades high inflation, under the president's watch is now cutting into his own plan to spend $550 billion on things like bridges and roads?\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Some tough numbers here as soaring inflation causes a political headache for the president as this new poll just out about an hour ago.\par \par \uc2 This is an AP/NORC poll. It says that 67 percent of Democrats find the economy they view it as poor. That's a 30 point increase over last year. Obviously an enormous jump, and we see what happened to inflation over that period of time.\par \par \uc2 So, the economy is also dragging down national optimism on the hole with 78 percent of Democrats saying that the country is headed in the wrong direction.\par \par \uc2 That's a huge number, 78 percent from the president's own party, who think that things are not going the right way. That's an increase of 12 points, just among Democrats over the course of the last month.\par \par \uc2 So, these are very consequential numbers that we're seeing.\par \par \uc2 With that, we bring in Reince Priebus, former White House chief of staff for President Trump, now president of the Michael Best law firm.\par \par \uc2 And Leslie Marshall, democratic commentator and Fox News contributor. Welcome to you both.\par \par \uc2 Leslie, let me start with you. What's your reaction to these numbers among Democrats?\par \par \uc2 LESLIE MARSHALL, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR (on camera): I'm not surprised, Martha. And I think this is the reason why the party in power loses since World War II on the average 26 seats in the House, four seats in the Senate. It is the economy stupid like James Carville said back in '92. But it's not just the economy, it's who they blame for.\par \par \uc2 The president himself said that he and the party in power will be blamed, that is typical, whether it's a Democrat or Republican, and whether they have all of the House, the Senate, and of the White House.\par \par \uc2 And also the way that it's presented. If people feel that inflation -- if people feel that gas prices are all the president is doing, the party in power is doing, then they're going to feel that way, rather than if they look at factually, this is a global economic issue, when you have places like Norway with $10 plus a gallon and other countries. That's certainly \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and Democrats are not in charge of.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MARSHALL: But I'm not surprised at these numbers. Certainly, is it worrisome coming up with the midterms? Absolutely, but not surprised.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. I don't know that the international comparisons work with most American voters, because I think that generally as a population, we expect to handle things better here to be more efficient -- to have more efficient, you know, markets that handle these.\par \par \uc2 Reince, you know, when you look at these numbers, and you look at the fact that they have gone south a lot in the past month. And that's during a time -- and Leslie talked about the messaging, which I think is a -- is a good point. That the messaging has been, this is Putin's fault, Putin's war. Putin's war. It sounds like that's not working.\par \par \uc2 REINCE PRIEBUS, FORMER WHITE HOUSE CHIEF OF STAFF: Yes, it's just not going to work, Martha. And I think you're right. People don't say, well, we're going to give President Biden a pass, because things are bad everywhere.\par \par \uc2 The reality is, is that you cannot hang on to power if people aren't happy.\par \par \uc2 And right now, people aren't happy. They're not feeling good about their future. It doesn't matter whether you've saved up $10,000 in the bank in savings or $10 million. You are not feeling better today than you were two or three years ago. And that's just a fact.\par \par \uc2 I think one of the bigger issues here for Democrats going into the midterms, is that when you peel back all these numbers, what you see in that Democrat number is that only 20 percent of all Democrats strongly support \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . And that is a huge problem.\par \par \uc2 If you're running an operation in Wisconsin or Pennsylvania, for a Democrat, and 20 percent of all of your available workers, people putting up the yard signs, the lid dropped, only 20 percent feel good about their own president, you're going to have a hard time get -- filling a phone bank if you're a Democrat.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. All right, we're going to have to leave it there. Thank you very much, Leslie and Reince. Good to see you both.\par \par \uc2 PRIEBUS: You bet.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So inflation is taking a bite out of the president's -- one of his biggest achievements. A trillion dollar package to fix up American roads and bridges.\par \par \uc2 This infrastructure bill was really the crowning achievement of this administration so far. But now, what's happening is that the dollar is, you know, worth less it goes -- it doesn't go as far.\par \par \uc2 So now, you've got these enormous projects all across the country. And the states that are running them are saying, well, we really can't afford the supplies anymore. We're going to have to cut back on all of this to do the construction.\par \par \uc2 Fox Business correspondent Lydia Hu joins us on set. It's always good to have you here, Lydia. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 LYDIA HU, FOX BUSINESS NETWORK CORRESPONDENT (on camera): So good to be here, Martha. But you're exactly right. This means we're going to be able to buy fewer bridges, fewer tunnels, fewer roads, with those federal dollars, now, than just seven months ago, when the president signed that $1 trillion infrastructure law.\par \par \uc2 HU (voice-over): The skyrocketing cost of labor is a particular problem here. Construction companies, they're suffering high turnover as they are competing with other industries for workers. And that's happening even as the average hourly wage for construction jobs is pretty high over $32 an hour. That's up within six percent from last year and higher than the average hourly wage across the entire private sector coming in at around $27 an hour.\par \par \uc2 Hiring is happened -- happening, but it's not what's needed. Contractors hired Around 455,000 people last month. But that's not nearly the amount that it was left open on the table -- still around half a million jobs needed.\par \par \uc2 Now, the prices of materials are soaring too, a trade group called Associated Builders and Contractors says construction input prices are up 21 percent from a year ago, and that's forcing local governments to delay or adjust their construction projects. Watch here.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MIKE BELLAMAN, ASSOCIATED BUILDERS AND CONTRACTORS: The final cost of a project is causing projects to slip, driving up backlogs and driving down the construction spends.\par \par \uc2 So, it is on a macro basis.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HU (on camera): There are examples from coast to coast where projects are now being delayed or adjusted, they're being scaled back. What this means is that, that taxpayer dollar that was allocated just not going to go as far now, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Very interesting story. Lydia, thank you very much. Good to have you here today.\par \par \uc2 HU: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM (voice-over): \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2 , the Democrat candidate for governor in Georgia claims that Republican Governor Brian Kemp, is intentionally misrepresenting where she stands on funding the police.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 STACEY ABRAMS (D), GUBERNATORIAL CANDIDATE, GEORGIA: Brian Kemp is lying. He is taking my words out of context. And he is denying the meaning of what I have said and what I have done.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, we give the governor a chance to respond to that exclusively, right here after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CASSIDY HUTCHINSON, FORMER TRUMP WHITE HOUSE AIDE: The President reached up towards the front of the vehicle to grab at the steering wheel. Mr. Engel grabbed his arm said, sir, you need to take your hand off the steering wheel. We're going back to the West Wing. We're not going to the Capitol. Mr. Trump then used his free hand to lunge towards Bobby Engle. And when Mr. Ornato had recounted the stories, he had a motion towards his clavicles.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So obviously, that riveting testimony got a lot of attention yesterday. But today, people who were in the SUV, with the former president, say that it is not accurately represented. And the Secret Service telling Fox that the January 6 Committee never reached out to the agency before former White House aide Cassidy Hutchinson's hearing.\par \par \uc2 Now, a Secret Service agent and the SUV driver are disputing that account. Hutchinson's lawyer tweeted this, "Ms. Hutchinson testified under oath and recounted what she was told. Those with knowledge of the episode should also testify under oath." So now that actually may happen, we're hearing. Congressional correspondent Aishah Hasnie with the very latest on this on Capitol Hill. Hi, Aishah.\par \par \uc2 AISHAH HASNIE, CONGRESSIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Hi there, Martha. That's right. So Fox has told that the Secret Service agents who were named and who were said to be involved in this incident, do want to testify. They want to tell their side of the story. And apparently discussions are underway right now between the Secret Service and this committee to try to make that happen.\par \par \uc2 Let's go back to this dramatic telling from yesterday. Cassidy Hutchinson testifying that she heard about the former president's physical altercation with Secret Service agents from former Trump Deputy Chief of Staff for Operations, Tony Ornato. But a source is telling fox that Ornato wasn't even inside the vehicle, and apparently is shocked by her testimony because he says he never briefed her. We're also told the agents who were inside including lead agent Bobby Engel, as she mentioned, say that Trump never assaulted them and they want to be able to refute this. Apparently the former president also wants his side known.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KELLYANNE CONWAY, FORMER COUNSELOR TO PRESIDENT TRUMP: I've discussed yesterday's testimony with President Trump. He called me a couple of times yesterday, and I feel like I got his first person account too. And he's -- he want to tell, you know, he would like for that to be known also. But again, this is a one sided committee hearing.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: Now, there are also questions about this handwritten statement. Hutchinson said that she wrote for Trump during the attack on the Capitol, but ABC News is reporting former Trump lawyer Eric Herschmann claims he actually wrote that note. And listen to how committee member Jamie Raskin responded to the lack of corroboration here. I would just, as a journalist and as an American, appreciate more corroboration for the story, since as of now it is hearsay.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JAKE TAPPER, CNN ANCHOR: I mean, it is -- somebody was saying this is what I heard.\par \par \uc2 REP. JAMIE RASKIN (D-MD): Yes. And, of course, we're not in a court of law and we're not charging anybody with any particular offense.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: So we'll have to wait and see if the Secret Service agents do get to testify. We're not expecting another hearing until the House reconvenes as early as July 11, Martha?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, they should certainly be given the opportunity to say what their side of the story is. They have testified in the past but now that there's this discrepancy, it would behoove them to have an opportunity to clear it out.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Thank you, Aishah. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 So Georgia Governor Brian Kemp accusing his Democratic challenger \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2  of putting families at risk by advancing the Defund the Police Movement. More than a hundred Georgia sheriffs have joined Kemp in condemning Abrams for being on the board of a group that they call anti- police, accusing the governor -- Abrams as I said, accusing the governor of lying.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 STACEY ABRAMS (D), CANDIDATE FOR GEORGIA GOVERNOR: I do not believe in defunding the police. I've repeatedly said that. Brian Kemp is lying. And he's taking my words out of context and he is denying the meaning of what I have said and what I have done. He can look at my record. You can look at my record. I spent 11 years in the legislature working with law enforcement, but I do believe in accountability.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Georgia Governor Brian Kemp joins us now. Governor, good to have you with us. Thank you for being here.\par \par \uc2 So, what is your evidence? What do you use to substantiate that she's in favor of defunding the police? And I know you have a clip that actually played for her on Sunday, of her talking to CNN and saying yes to an element of defund the police, but she's saying she wants to reallocate some money to different areas. So, what's your evidence that she truly wants to defund the police?\par \par \uc2 BRIAN KEMP (R), GEORGIA GOVERNOR: Well, look, she said she wants to defund the police. She said she wants to reallocate. That's code word for defunding the police and sending the money elsewhere. You know, if she wants to do something like that, if it's priority, you can find the money elsewhere besides defunding the police. So, you know, she said that in her own words.\par \par \uc2 She's also serving on this board that's been paying her that supports the Defunding the Police Movement. The good thing for Georgian is, despite her telling people that I'm lying, they know that I've been a truthful governor. I mean, people may disagree with certain policies I've done or things that they would have rather had me do, but they know I've been truthful with them, Martha. And they know dang well I have stood with our men and women in law enforcement.\par \par \uc2 I was running on going after street gangs and drug cartels four years ago, talking about this issue, warning of this problem. And for three-and-a-half years that I've been in office, we've been working with state law enforcement, with 