e's proposing, she can't do all that without raising taxes. So the person who's being dishonest in line here is not me, it's \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: How much do you think the Roe v Wade decision will play into the election in Georgia? I asked \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2  about it this weekend and she said that she thinks it's always a medical decision, despite the fact that she used to be pro-life. She's now pro-choice. And she just kept saying it's a medical decision. And when I asked her if she would be in favor of abortion up to nine months, she basically dodged that question.\par \par \uc2 KEMP: Well, she took an extreme view this weekend, Martha, and I saw that clip and I know that she did the same thing on another network. And then today, evidently, she's clarified where she is. But look, that's her MO. I mean, she moves whichever way the winds blow and whichever the way the polls are going. And we saw that when she criticized me over our elections bill, said it was Jim Crow 2.0 and suppressive, you know, pressured big corporations and Major League Baseball to cancel us. And then, when she figured out that the truth was on our side and that Georgians actually support the voter ID requirements, all of a sudden when Joe Manchin introduces a bill she's for that.\par \par \uc2 But, you know, Georgians are seeing through all of that. They want somebody that's going to be transparent with them. I ran as a pro-life governor. I know that people disagree on these issues. But the thing is, you never hear her talk about adoption reform, foster care reform. Our fight in Georgia to end human trafficking, but not only to do that to support the victims and help them be able to become a contributing member to society and give them the support that they need to do that, we've done all of those things. It shows our total value for life, regardless of where you stand on the abortion issue.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right. Governor Kemp, I hope to have you back had a few more questions for you. But we hope we'll get to them next time. We really appreciate your time. Thank you, sir.\par \par \uc2 KEMP: Have a great day.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So coming up, Dr. Ben Carson on the long history of vitriol directed at Supreme Court Justice \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2 . And also coming up, a very special tribute to an American hero, Woody Williams, World War II veteran who was the last surviving World War II Medal of Honor recipient whom we lost today.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HERSHEL "WOODY" WILLIAMS, WORLD WAR II VETERAN: I've been asked many, many times why did I should left the Marine Corps. I knew nothing about the Armed Forces whatsoever. I didn't know the difference between a marine and an army guy. When I finally got to the marine recruiter and gave me my paper, he didn't look at it. He looked at me and just shook his head and said, I can't take you. And he said you're too short. So they lowered the heights requirement to five two or better. And the recruiter came to a forum and as I said (inaudible) yes.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Interesting story. \uc2 George Washington University\uc2  is not caving to the demands to fire Supreme Court Justice \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  from his job as an adjunct professor there. He voted, of course, to overturn Roe v Wade and wrote that the court should also reconsider the rights to contraception and same sex marriage, although the other justices did not hold that opinion.\par \par \uc2 George Washington University\uc2  says, "Like all faculty members at our school, Justice Thomas has academic freedom and freedom of expression and inquiry. It is not the proper role of the university to attempt to shield individuals within or outside the university from ideas or opinions they find unwelcome, disagreeable, or even deeply offensive, but not everybody have the same reaction.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Why this decision has made me a very strong advocate of the pullout method, which is when you pull \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  out of the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 TREVOR NOAH, THE DAILY SHOW HOST: -- so extreme, he's talking about banning rights I didn't even realize could be banned.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Well, you better hope that they don't come for you, Clarence, and say you should not be married to your wife, who happens to be white.\par \par \uc2 HILLARY CLINTON\uc2 , FORMER SECRETARY OF STATE: I went to law school with him. He's been a person of grievance for as long as I've known him. Resentment, grievance, anger.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Resentment and grievance from Hillary Clinton. Let's bring in Dr. Ben Carson, former Housing and Urban Development secretary under President Trump and founder and chairman of the American Cornerstone Institute. Dr. Carson, always good to have you with us. Thank you for joining us today.\par \par \uc2 What's your reaction to the sort of, you know, cultural response to \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  that you just heard there?\par \par \uc2 BEN CARSON, FOUNDER/CHAIRMAN, AMERICAN CORNERSTONE INSTITUTE: Well, certainly nothing particularly surprising. You know, for many on the left, the only thing worse than Satan is a Black Conservative. And, you know, they feel that \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  is right for particular scorn, because he is black and he's supposed to think a certain way. And, you know, in terms of him being embittered person, like \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  said, I've known \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  for decades, I don't find it to be that way at all. He's one of the most beloved members of the Horatio Alger Society, which I'm a member of, who have many people that America admires. And they admire him not because it's Supreme Court justice, but because of his personality.\par \par \uc2 And, you know, he's a perfect example of what my mother used to tell me. She would say, Benjamin, if you walk into an auditorium full of racist, bigoted people, you don't have a problem, they have a problem, because they're all going to cringe and wonder if you're going to sit next to them, whereas you can sit anywhere you want. And that's sort of the philosophy he takes. He's not going to allow other people's grievances, and prejudices, and hatred to affect his life.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So what about the part of his opinion where he talked about the court revisiting whether or not the contraception decision or the same sex marriage decision were rightly decided by the Supreme Court given the broader Due Process Clause in the Constitution and what it does and doesn't apply to?\par \par \uc2 CARSON: Yes. I think he's simply saying that, you know, we have a court now that will perhaps listen, without hysterics, to some of the arguments that maybe they didn't listen to before. And if a compelling case presents itself, I don't think he's advocating let's go out and look for such a case. But if it comes up, they're willing to consider it. And that's a good thing.\par \par \uc2 You know, one of the things that is so bothersome right now, is that we have a court system in which we have certain justices we always know how they're going to vote, because they vote ideologically rather than based on the Constitution. And that's disturbing. And we have people who want to put more of those types of justices on the Supreme Court. That's something that we should all be very concerned about.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Just very quickly, about 20 seconds, you know, speaking out about Loving versus Virginia, the ruling about interracial marriage. What did you think of that comment, given that his wife is a white woman?\par \par \uc2 CARSON: That's an absurd comment. You know, interracial marriage has been something that we've seen a lot of recently because people are getting to know each other. And they recognize that the thing that makes a person who they are is their brain, not their skin. And that's a wonderful kudo for America.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Dr. Ben Carson, thank you, sir. Good to hear from you today.\par \par \uc2 CARSON: Thank you, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So today, we lost the last living World War II Medal of Honor recipient in this country, a man that I was honored to know. Woody Williams, a marine, a hero, recognized for his astonishingly brave acts of valor at Iwo Jima?\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: My life has been so memorable. That gave me some realization of what it meant to survive, leadership (inaudible). The question still comes on me.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Hershel "Woody" Williams like so many young men after the attack on Pearl Harbor in 1941, went to sign up and fight. But he was turned away. At five foot two, he was told he was too short. But as the war continued on, height restrictions were changed and Woody became a marine. He made his way to the Pacific to an eight mile island called Iwo Jima. And there he became a hero.\par \par \uc2 When Woody finally got home, President Truman awarded that young Marine from Quiet Dell, West Virginia the Medal of Honor. When the nation lost him today, we lost our last living World War II Medal of Honor recipient. He had traveled the country talking to young people all over. And there we are, together, I was honored to call him a friend and to serve on his foundation. Here's Woody, whose story is in my book, "Unknown Valor." Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: I was shipped out in early February for the Iwo, not knowing where we were going to go. Most of us couldn't figure out why we were testing that we live in a place and why we needed so many marines to do it. With that tremendous number of our marines in our unit, and my company commander and two other officers, you normally have commanding officer, four or five other officers. And then, (inaudible) so now (inaudible) himself. My commanding officer called for a meeting that those who have left, and the officer ask me, as the only flame thrower operator left, could I do something about some of these toolboxes that stalled with (inaudible).\par \par \uc2 Two of those marines that day sacrificed their lives. I didn't find that out until after the campaign was back to (inaudible). And then, I keep saying over and over why me, why me. I don't have the answer to that. I was just a regular marine doing a regular job with some Marine Corps training. I began realizing this medal has significance I've never dreamed of. But it was still a long time, years before I finally came to the full realization that it doesn't represent me. It represents them.\par \par \uc2 And my life has been so full of miracle. That gave me some realization of what it meant to survive and be the recipient of (inaudible). Yes, the question still comes by me.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Woody Williams single handedly took out seven Japanese enemy positions in a pivotal battle in Iwo Jima. His Medal of Honor citation commends his aggressive fighting spirit and valiant devotion to duty. Woody spent his later life through his foundation erecting monuments to Goldstar families who he revered in nearly every state in this country. He died today surrounded by family and friends at the Veterans Affairs Medical Center in West Virginia that bears his name. He was 98. We thank him for his service and his never ending devotion to this country and his inspiration to everyone who met him.\par \par \uc2 Defense Secretary \uc2 Lloyd Austin\uc2  saying this a short time ago, I hope every American will pause to reflect on his service and that of an entire generation sacrifice so much to defend the cause of freedom and democracy. We will miss you Woody. You can learn more about him and about other American heroes in a series that I'm honored to be a part of called "The Secret History of World War II." It's available now on Fox Nation and in "Unknown Valor" as well.\par \par \uc2 Thank you for being here, everybody. That's THE STORY for today.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document HUNDD00020220630ei6t00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Dems Have Hole In Bottom Of Their Bag Of Tricks; "Star" Witness For Jan 6th Cmte Sees Story Unravel; Media Seems Downright Giddy For Hillary In 2024; Key Primary Results Roll In Across The Country; Businessman Joe O'Dea Wins GOP Senate Nomination; The Left's Casual Racism On Display; Former Marine Discusses Current Military Policy And Reduction In Military Recruitment Among Americans; Rep. \uc2 Matt Gaetz\uc2  (R-FL) Interviewed On Video Leaked Of Department Of Labor Seminar On Inclusion Polices For Trans Employees; Two Suspected Drug Traffickers Of Large Quantities Of Fentanyl Released In California On Their Own Recognizance\b0\par\par\uc2 Laura Ingraham, Mollie Hemingway, Matt Finn \par \uc2 7,486 \uc2 words\par \uc2 28 June 2022\par \uc2 Fox News: The Ingraham Angle\par \uc2 INGANG\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 SEAN HANNITY\uc2 , FOX NEWS HOST: Thank you for making this show possible. For news anytime, foxnews.com, hannity.com.\par \par \uc2 Please set your DVR, so you never miss an episode of "HANNITY". And in the meantime, let not your heart be troubled. Laura Ingraham and THE INGRAHAM ANGLE are next. Have a great night.\par \par \uc2 LAURA INGRAHAM, FOX NEWS HOST: I'm Laura Ingraham. This is THE INGRAHAM ANGLE from New York City tonight.\par \par \uc2 Bad actors, shiny objects. That's the focus of tonight's 'Angle'.\par \par \uc2 I think of many favorite tweets of the day. This was really my favorite tweet of the day. It has to be from Scott Adams of Dilbert fame. "So far, Trump's second term has been a huge success. He has several major SCOTUS victories, looks better than ever compared to Biden, and the Jan 6 theater just went full Jussie. You can't win harder than that."\par \par \uc2 Oh, my God, I was screaming reading that. Well, pretty much sums it up, doesn't it?\par \par \uc2 Now there are a lot of shiny objects being dangled in front of us slightly by the Democrats, and few those diminutive never trumpers. And I'm here tonight to tell you that none of them will have any measurable effect on the midterms or really change the public's realization that under Democrat leadership, America is just going down the tubes.\par \par \uc2 Now, the first attempted distraction or shiny object, a former aide to \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2 , Cassidy Hutchinson was slotted into what was billed as a special super duper special session of the January 6 committee today.\par \par \uc2 And I spoke with some former White House staffers, three or four of them in the afternoon, and they knew her well. And not one person had anything good to say about her performance today because they watched. Especially the second hand claim, supposedly from Tony Ornato, another Trump aide, that was supposedly one of their