uc2 The government says the universities are too ideological. They're too political.\par \par \uc2 Well, the same thing has happened all over the world. It's certainly happened in our country where our universities no longer educate, they seek new ways to destroy the country that made them possible in the first place, and they should be shut down.\par \par \uc2 For a brand new episode of "\uc2 Tucker Carlson\uc2  Today," we sat down with a former Oxford Philosophy professor called Mike Robillard who has seen firsthand the poison that emanates from the universities.\par \par \uc2 Here's his story.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MIKE ROBILLARD, FORMER OXFORD UNIVERSITY PHILOSOPHY PROFESSOR: You trade every conviction and it doesn't even save you in the end, right? Like the monster eats you anyways. Right? But it just eats you as a coward instead of at least leaning into it and dying at your feet.\par \par \uc2 So I think that's the existential position that I was being put in where it's like --\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: What a wonderful description.\par \par \uc2 ROBILLARD: Thanks, thanks. But I'm not -- I'm not going to say two plus two equals five. I'm not going to say men can get pregnant by mere stipulation.\par \par \uc2 But I was silent when other people were saying these things.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Right.\par \par \uc2 ROBILLARD: And that's enough, right? That degree of complicity, I think that is what adds up after a while, where you silently tolerate the other lies and falsehoods and inconsistencies and contradictions that are being said out loud in your workplace, in the pronoun e-mails, in the mandatory, you know, diversity, equity, inclusion, you know -- it's the tacit complicity that goes along with things.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: That episode of "\uc2 Tucker Carlson\uc2  Today" airs tomorrow morning, 7:00 AM on FOX Nation.\par \par \uc2 So here's one of those stories we didn't want to let pass, dozens of US sailors have disclosed an encounter with at least 100 identified flying objects swarming their ship a hundred miles off the San Diego Coast.\par \par \uc2 The Pentagon can't explain it. They're worried about it though.\par \par \uc2 We'll tell you more when we come back from Rio de Janeiro.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: So in Brazil, the government has long but willing to admit what it doesn't know in a way that our government won't, and so people talk about UFOs here much more than they do in the United States. The Brazilian Senate just held its first ever public hearings on the matter.\par \par \uc2 Members of the military explained that they have encountered a lot of UFOs, including a night called the "Night of the UFOs" in which five Brazilian fighter jets were chased by dozens of unidentified flying objects at high speed. Things like that happen in the United States, too, and we're just beginning to hear about them.\par \par \uc2 So for example, dozens of sailors have just told a documentary filmmaker called, Jeremy Corbell that they were swarmed by, "at least a hundred UFOs off the coast of San Diego," all of which had physical capabilities that were beyond anything people have made that challenge the laws of physics. What is this exactly?\par \par \uc2 Nick Pope is a former \uc2 UK Ministry of Defense\uc2  official and we're happy to have him join us tonight.\par \par \uc2 So Nick, tell us what these sailors described and what you think it adds up to, if you would?\par \par \uc2 NICK POPE, FORMER \uc2 UK MINISTRY OF DEFENSE\uc2  OFFICIAL: Well, they described a series of objects literally swarming around multiple \uc2 US Navy\uc2  ships. They said the speeds and maneuvers seemed way ahead of anything they'd seen.\par \par \uc2 Now the DoD and the Navy are saying they're trying to say that some of this is drones, maybe it is, and with China threatening Taiwan, if this is foreign adversary drones, and they can do that to the \uc2 US Navy\uc2 , we're in big trouble.\par \par \uc2 Either way, this is an act of provocation, arguably, an act of war. The American people need some answers. They deserve answers.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: So if these are, in fact, from a foreign military, and as far as I know, the Pentagon has given no evidence whatsoever of that. But if it is, then I mean, it's probably not even worth talking about having a war with China because they've already won.\par \par \uc2 POPE: Well, the first step is to try and bring one of these things down. I know it sounds provocative. But look, if these things are literally in our airspace and over our assets, we do have things that can bring these sorts of objects, whatever they are down.\par \par \uc2 We have lasers. We have the ability to send an electromagnetic pulse. Let's bring one down and let's get those answers.\par \par \uc2 Everyone is asking the question. \uc2 NASA\uc2  is asking. The DoD is asking. The military is asking. The American people are asking, too.\par \par \uc2 And I think we've been having this discussion now for far too long to just put off getting to the bottom of this and giving people the truth, whatever that may be.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: In one sentence, do you have any idea why the Pentagon or the US military and the Navy made no attempt to bring them down or didn't bring them down?\par \par \uc2 POPE: Well, I hope that they did. One answer is maybe they tried and failed. But again, we need more answers on this and Congress needs to hold the DoD and the military to account.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Fair point, Nick Pope, from the UK. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 The Supreme Court overruled Roe v. Wade on Friday and that led to riots all over the country, which you probably haven't read about because they weren't committed by law-abiding middle-aged Trump voters, but they happened.\par \par \uc2 In Phoenix a mob tried to storm the State Capitol as the legislature was in session. So you have to wonder why so many people are so angry when of course ending Roe v. Wade doesn't end legal abortion, it merely lets voters decide what they want, which is after all, democracy. So why is everyone so mad?\par \par \uc2 Well, Eddie Scarry has spent a year thinking about why the left is so unhappy. He's the author of a new book called "Liberal Misery," a columnist with "The Federalist" and our next guest.\par \par \uc2 Eddie, thanks for joining us. So, they seem very unhappy. That's kind of the bottom line when I watch their behavior. Are they?\par \par \uc2 EDDIE SCARRY, AUTHOR "LIBERAL MISERY": Yes, I think what we're looking at is the way liberals and Democrats are going to behave from now on. When they don't get their way, they are going to rage, they're going to threaten, they may even commit actual political violence as we've seen. We saw it all through 2020.\par \par \uc2 And honestly, as I write in my book, "Liberal Misery," this is just a bigger version of the way liberals behave in their everyday life. This is backed up by all sorts of surveys and studies and data that liberals are just really unhappy people and they want to -- now, they want to spread their misery. They want you to feel exactly how unhappy they are when things aren't going their way.\par \par \uc2 They are more likely than conservatives and independents to block people on social media just because they don't like something you said. They will erupt at a social gathering at a perfect stranger. They do this to their family, their friends.\par \par \uc2 They are angry, vindictive, petty, and again, all of this is backed up by data. They just are naturally tend to be unhappy people.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Higher rates of mental illness are much less likely to tip 20 percent, much more likely to send the food back and bark at the waiter. Is there any guesses to where this comes from? It seems like they're mad at dad. But is there another reason?\par \par \uc2 SCARRY: I think that's above my paygrade, but I will say that again, this there is a lot of data that backs this up. The problem is, it would be one thing if they lived this way on their own and they just kind of kept to themselves, and I think that they kind of used to contain some of that rage and anger.\par \par \uc2 But no, nowadays it affects everyone. Everyone has every one of your viewers I would bet money has experienced the extreme discomfort that one of these people, one of these miserable liberals can inflict on everyone at a party that just suddenly everyone gets uncomfortable because there's a disagreement, a political disagreement, but that's all in my book, "Liberal Misery."\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Yes. It's a great point and it raises questions about pharma. If Xanax works, why are they screaming? But that's for another show.\par \par \uc2 Eddie Scarry, congrats on the book. Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 SCARRY: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: More from Brazil.\par \par \uc2 We'll be right back from Rio.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: So we started the show talking about babies and we're going to end the show talking about babies.\par \par \uc2 We are pro-baby and particularly Madeline who is the brand new daughter recently arrived of our producer and friend Casey and her husband, Kenny. We want to say to Madeline, "Welcome to the world. Welcome to America. We can't wait to meet you." Congratulations to Casey and Kenny.\par \par \uc2 That's it for us tonight.\par \par \uc2 We'll be in Brazil for the rest of the week, seeing President Bolsonaro tomorrow in Brasilia. We will bring a report.\par \par \uc2 We will see you then. Have the best night with the ones you love.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission