EWS HOST: Well, I mean, what you do in a situation like that and God bless California. They let these guys out as soon as they arrested them. I mean, these guys who have all the information, a lot of them on fentanyl and these guys in particular. You get information from them. You pull them hard.\par \par \uc2 And they can give you information as to smuggling, whether it's human smuggling or drug smuggling, they know what's going on here. And you just go up the chain. You scare them, you scare them, you scare their families. The feds do it every day.\par \par \uc2 But I got to tell you something. I think in a bizarre way, I think the Biden administration could turn this thing around and say see, the border is closed. We told you the border is secured. See, they have to smuggle them in like this and they're dying to come in.\par \par \uc2 Here's the bottom line that contradicts all of that. The president is in the \uc2 United States Supreme Court\uc2  fighting the "Remain in Mexico" policy. He doesn't want the illegals to remain in Mexico. He is fighting Title 42. He wants to open the border.\par \par \uc2 The president fought with Governor Abbott because Governor Abbott was inspecting the 18 wheelers as they came in. And then everybody brought a lot of criticism and said he was slowing down traffic. Governor Abbott could have found these people before they actually died. But no, we had to stop him.\par \par \uc2 And so, what this administration is doing is they're laying out the welcome mat every day in terms of what they do. So, they can tell me until the cows come home, the border is secure, that the border is closed and they're lying to all of us. They're busy flying them in, bussing them into the interior.\par \par \uc2 They're housing unaccompanied migrant children smuggled in by the cartels that they're accompanying and facilitating, hiring 800 Americans to staff a facility. They're quietly erasing immigration cases giving de facto amnesty to one million illegals.\par \par \uc2 I mean, the goal for them should be to stop the human trafficking and the drug smuggling, but they're doing just the opposite. So, why is he trying so hard to fail and why is it that he let it get this way? Is he that afraid of the progressives? Why?\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Geraldo, take it from there, wherever you want.\par \par \uc2 GERARDO RIVERA, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Well, I totally -- I want to say that it's so easy for Senator Cruz or Governor Abbott to say oh, Biden's fault, Biden's fault, Biden's fault. The fact of the matter is, first of all, let me express sincere condolences. I feel nothing but grief over this horrible mass murder. That's what it was. This is a mass murder.\par \par \uc2 I am outraged at these perpetrators. I want them prosecuted to the full extent of the law. The judge is right, you squeeze them, you walk your way up the chain. You find out at least and maybe you can get the Mexicans involved in tracking down these cartels. This cannot -- we cannot abide 51 murders.\par \par \uc2 I blame President Biden. I do think that his compassion has backfired at the border. I do think it's obnoxious as Trump's moves were. They were more effective in dissuading them from coming and taking this perilous journey. And I think as we approach July 4th, what you have to recognize is the brutality of this evidence of how badly these people want to be in the United States of America. They risk everything. Even their very lives. They voluntarily stuffed themselves in to that semi only to choke to death in sweltering heat.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: It's interesting. Bill Melugin is one of our reporters. He's been there at the border along with Griff Jenkins and many others, Casey Stegall, but I was talking to Bill Melugin not too long ago and he sees no other reporters there and he's been there for months and we report on this every day. And I watch one nightly news broadcast every single night just to see. And I never see any coverage of the border, but this story they would cover.\par \par \uc2 KA TIMPF, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Well, the story you kind of have to cover, but it also sadly, obviously it's devastating. Not that surprising because it's a complete disaster down there. It is a humanitarian crisis. Anyone who is paying attention to immigration at all knows that.\par \par \uc2 It's also such a disaster that when they said see, look, the border is closed. See, this proves it's closed. I would just love to follow that up with being like, okay. Like, does this look good? Does this look like this is going well? Because nothing in terms of the immigration policy is going well including legal immigration.\par \par \uc2 When you talk about people who want to come to this country and get work visas and work and go the legal way, they wasted 66,000 of those visas last year despite the fact that our labor market was so tight that are expired and they can never get them back.\par \par \uc2 So, the whole system is a complete disaster and they're scrambling. And anyone who is paying attention, they can figure that out, even the slightest bit of attention. So, I guess the only strategy that would make sense is they try to get people not to pay attention.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Alright, coming up, a sheriff is furious after a drug trafficking duo, busted with enough fentanyl to kill millions is well, they are now back on the streets.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 RIVERA: Now, to the other deadly pandemic ripping the heart out of our country. It is illegal fentanyl poison flooding our country snuffing out the lives of over 70,000 Americans last year alone. And yet some California authorities as the judge mentioned earlier, say they were forced to release two accused drug traffickers busted with 150,000 fentanyl pills, enough to kill millions of Americans.\par \par \uc2 A judge approving their release following a so-called risk assessment. But the sheriff there who was on with Dana earlier this morning on "America's Newsroom" is understandably furious.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MIKE BOUDREAUX, TULARE COUNTY SHERIFF: Come Monday morning, I learned of this release and I was infuriated. I could not believe that we had 150,000 fentanyl pills, one of the most dangerous epidemics that is facing our nation today with people in custody that we may potentially be able to impact the future of this type of drug trafficking organizations and or cartels in California and my county, and we let them go.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RIVERA: It's absolutely outrageous. It really is, judge. You know, I have nothing against people getting high. I grew up in the 60's, the 70's, you do and you do what you do.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: I did, too. And I had something against them.\par \par \uc2 RIVERA: You do what you do. But fentanyl -- but fentanyl is not dope. Fentanyl is poison.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 RIVERA: These people are not dealers. They are murders or at least manslaughterers. You know, the laws have to reflect that, doesn't it?\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Look, this is -- people are too stupid to understand. Look, these guys who are trafficking that fentanyl, they know not to inhale it. They know now to get near it. I guarantee you they've got Narcan. They've got whatever they need in case for some reason one of those bags rips or the cellophane gets torn off.\par \par \uc2 They know how serious and deadly this stuff is. And for a judge to release those two guys, I can guarantee you as I'm sitting here, Geraldo, if the purpose of bail is to guarantee a person's return to court, where the hell are you from? Where do you live? Do you have a job? How can I be sure you're coming back to court? That's an easy, easy keep in jail.\par \par \uc2 But the court didn't do it because the court is either too stupid, too arrogant or they don't care. Fentanyl right now, the precursor is coming from China through the cartels. The import machines are in Mexico. They're killing a generation of our people, 250 deaths as day. And they're killing them between the ages of 18 and 45.\par \par \uc2 I've said this before. They are killing those who are capable of going to war in this country. It's an undeclared war and we're conspiring with them, between \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and the progressive left.\par \par \uc2 RIVERA: Fentanyl is poison. I remember and I wanted to enormously enhance the penalties, Jesse, for fentanyl as compared to heroin, cocaine and et cetera. The problem is, I remember when they did that with crack cocaine. So, who was impacted was black and Latinos? They get the short end. They used crack. White people use powder cocaine. In other words, it has racial implications if you single out a drug and make it more, you know, the penalties more enhanced.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Well, this is deadlier than crack. And it's killing blacks and whites and Hispanics. So, classify it however you want it. They're selling death. It's like selling cyanide pills. And these kids aren't like college students pushing a few pills to some friends at a party. You scare them straight and they can get back on the wrong -- on the right path.\par \par \uc2 These kids are high up the food chain. They're walking around with 150,000 pills. If you don't hold them, they have cash stashed. They're never going to show up again ever. Judge said it at the top. You put them in prison, maybe you send a little snitch in there, a little rat. Get them to talk. Maybe see who posts their bail. Maybe tail them. How about just calling the DEA and have them followed. It's really pretty simple how to take these people down.\par \par \uc2 RIVERA: Oh, I'm sure they'll do that. I hope -- I hope they do.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: And you know, you can't --\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: That's a given.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: -- you cannot find the judge's name in any of the articles. Now, why is that? Because these judges have these deals with these editors