ng changes in California. They may be outranged for a couple of days, they move on, or maybe the summer of rage continues. We will have to see on that. I do, however, believe that people like AOC and Elizabeth Warren are overreacting to the extent that it will boomerang on them and it's not going to be a good look.\par \par \uc2 Final thing. The Democrats are mad, apparently, at the Biden administration and the White House for being slow to act, not being aggressive enough. And I do wonder, what were they doing the last 55 days when they had the leaked opinion out there and then they have no -- they are so unorganized, not unified. You had \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  and Becerra today saying different things about what is possibly on the table. So, they're on a little bit of a mess.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Maybe their plan was just to attack the justices and raise money?\par \par \uc2 TIMPF: No, I think exactly. I think Kamala said the quiet part out loud when she was asked about the whole national park abortion clinic idea where she said, well, you know, we're going to wait until after the mid-terms. I think that was saying the quiet part out loud because I think that they -- she openly said, we want to see how many people we can get in so then maybe we can do something.\par \par \uc2 I think they want to see how many people they can get in, but I -- if they wanted to do something, I mean there has been discussion about how shaky Roe v. Wade was for a very long time. They could have codified it when they had the super majority under Obama.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Oh, yes.\par \par \uc2 TIMPF: They could have, I'm sure, come up with something better than Planned Parenthood Grand Canyon right now probably if they wanted to. But this is what politics is. I mean, you remember, with DACA, everyone's up in arms about that when Trump wanted to get rid of that and I agreed with that. But the people were saying -- they were campaigning on that.\par \par \uc2 Has that been codified? No, it is not. And I think that there's almost an excitement to say, you know, hey, we're going to point out -- we're going to, you know, point out the most extreme side of this issue, and use that to try and get votes. But people should know that it's about votes. I don't think any of these people really care about you.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Do you see Hillary seeing a lane here with this road decision and doing some shows and maybe jockeying for position, Judge?\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Let me tell you. Hillary has been jacking since she hit the -- since she hit the public screen? Look, I think it's -- I think it's rather interesting that she calls Justice \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  a man with grievances. This woman has got more grievances. I mean, I think she had 116 reasons why she didn't win the election in 2016.\par \par \uc2 And I think it's very interesting the depth of hatred that people have toward \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2 . He didn't even write the majority opinion. He wrote the concurring opinion. Their hatred that they're going to have then should be against Justice Alito who wrote the majority opinion, as opposed to saying, look, the court is spoken, they're giving it now to the states. Now, we have our work to do. We have to go to the legislatures, the legislators. We have our work to do. We are the majority. We are certainly the loudest voice in the room, and we will get a pass in every state.\par \par \uc2 But the racial hatred and the animosity, I mean, the progressives are the one who call everyone a racist. And yet, it's like when you point a finger at one person, you know, what, the three fingers pointing back at you, that that is very offensive to me. Samuel Jackson calling him Uncle Clarence. Lori Lightfoot, F \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2 . \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  a version of grievance. I mean, they are basically the racist. They're seeing racism in this when no one else is.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Geraldo, I know you've kept this bottled up for 