g experience. That company is Iwatani Giken is hoping to bring masked -- man's space travelers to the masses. It was designed to give would-be consumers confidence. If a hamster can do it, you can do it.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: You can do it. I remember when Jasper went to space. That was a big day in our house. All right, I want to tell you about Vera Brown. She's 108 years young, and she deserves a special shout-out. Her son-in-law Al Bell told us about her incredible life story. We're honored to celebrate her. She was born in 1915 in Medford, Oregon, eventually moved to Campbell County, Wyoming. She was married to Dan -- Don Brown, excuse me. They lived on a ranch in Western Wyoming.\par \par \uc2 And she -- that was where the U.S. Post Office was. And she was the postmaster for 37 years. At 108 years old, we want to say hello to you, Vera.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Hey Vera.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Hi, Vera.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Hi, Vera.\par \par \uc2 RIVERA: Vera.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Good luck, Vera. All right, Geraldo.\par \par \uc2 RIVERA: I am headed after the program to Newport, Rhode Island to pick up an old friend, my 24-year-old boat Belle, picnic boat, a Hinckley.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Oh, Hinckley.\par \par \uc2 RIVERA: I have kept --\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Just a Hinckley, Geraldo.\par \par \uc2 TIMPF: Weird. That's exactly what I'm doing.\par \par \uc2 RIVERA: I kept her -- I kept her running 24 years just -- and I've been everywhere, circumnavigated Florida with Erica and California and the Bahamas. It has new engine.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Not bad.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: If that boat can talk.\par \par \uc2 RIVERA: That boat -- that boat has a million tales to tell, a million tales.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Jesse.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: OK, in honor of Greg who has a severe case of the Mondays so bad he took Tuesday off. This is a little "ONE MORE THING" for you, buddy. Animals are great. This fat seal just showing off his rolls. Sometimes you eat a lot. You just have to roll it out on the floor. That's how you do it. There's something sexy about that fat seal. I just can't put my finger on it.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Yes, he's got moves. He's very confident.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: I can't stop watching that.\par \par \uc2 RIVERA: Wait till the shark comes.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: At 7:00, we have a Jesse waters monologue about the truth about \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2 . Everybody needs to tune in at 7:00 sharp.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: I want to watch that.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: 7:00 p.m. sharp. Kat?\par \par \uc2 TIMPF: I just quickly want to say there's a hotel in Switzerland that is apparently bad on purpose. You can look at it so bad it's supposed to make you think about your life and the world around you by spending a night there. And it costs hundreds of dollars though. So, if that's something you're interested in, you want to question your life and the entire world around, a lot cheaper, \uc2 Greyhound\uc2  bus. An overnight trip on a \uc2 Greyhound\uc2  bus will accomplish all that for you.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Wait, what are you supposed to do?\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: What are you talking about?\par \par \uc2 TIMPF: You're supposed to stay there outside the gas station and think about how bad --\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: There's no roof.\par \par \uc2 TIMPF: No.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: That's a hotel?\par \par \uc2 TIMPF: Yes. But it cost hundreds of dollars for the experience. \uc2 Greyhound\uc2  bus, you could do the same thing way cheaper. Trust me.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: When you smell the fuel from the gas station.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: That's crazy.\par \par \uc2 RIVERA: It's gross. It's gross.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: That is -- that is --\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: With all the cigarettes you smoke right next door.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Yes, you blow the place up. Go to the moon.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: All right, that is it for us. "SPECIAL REPORT" is up next. Hey, Bret.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FOXFIV0020220629ei6s00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Former White House Aide Testifies; 51 Migrants Dead From Sweltering Truck; Biden Underscores Need To Stop Criminal Trafficking; President In Spain With NATO Leaders After G7 Summit; Macron Discusses Middle East Oil Production With Biden; Abortion Laws Are Now Up To States; Voters Cast Ballots In Eight States; Groups Supporting Democrats Funding Campaign In Republican Primaries To Promote What Democrats Consider More Beatable Candidates In Midterms And Gubernatorial Elections; At Least Four People Killed And Dozens Injured After \uc2 Amtrak\uc2  Train Crashes Into Dump Truck In Missouri; House January 6th Committee Hears Testimony From Former Trump Aide That President Trump Lunged At Secret Service Member In Attempt To Return To Capitol Hill During Riots\b0\par\par\uc2 Bret Baier, Aishah Hasnie, Bill Melugin, Griff Jenkins; Peter  Doocy, Gillian Turner, Grady Trimble, Alicia Acuna, Rich Edson, Garrett  Tenney \par \uc2 7,623 \uc2 words\par \uc2 28 June 2022\par \uc2 Fox News: Special Report with Brett Baier\par \uc2 FNSPBB\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 KAT TIMPF, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR: Yes, but it costs hundreds of dollars for the experience. \uc2 Greyhound\uc2  bus, you could do the same thing way cheaper. Trust --\par \par \uc2 JESSE WATTERS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: When you smell the fumes from the gas station.\par \par \uc2 JEANINE PIRRO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: That's crazy.\par \par \uc2 GERALDO RIVERA, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT-AT-LARGE: It's gross. It's gross.\par \par \uc2 DANA PERINO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: That is -- that is crazy.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: With all the cigarettes you can smoke right next door.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Yes, you blow the place up.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Had any I had for this (INAUDIBLE), I don't know.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Go to the moon.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: All right, that is it for us.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL ANCHOR (on camera): It is (INAUDIBLE).\par \par \uc2 PERINO: SPECIAL REPORT is up next. Hey, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Smoking with to get them. Thanks, Dana.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Good evening. Welcome to Washington. I'm Bret Baier. "BREAKING TONIGHT", we're following through -- three major stories. The French president raising concerns to President Biden about energy production as the president meets with world leaders who have big questions about U.S. policy.\par \par \uc2 The president's southern border policy under fire once again today after the deaths of dozens of migrants in a human smuggling disaster. We'll take you there live.\par \par \uc2 But we begin with the woman who was a special assistant to then-President Trump's right hand man, detailing the drama inside the White House and as the Capitol riot unfolded.\par \par \uc2 Cassidy Hutchinson was an aide to Chief of Staff \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2 . She says she was in the room when a number of conversations happened. She spoke to a special session of the January 6th committee this afternoon with eye opening testimony.\par \par \uc2 Congressional correspondent Aishah Hasnie is live on Capitol Hill with the latest. Good evening, Aishah.\par \par \uc2 AISHAH HASNIE, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Good evening to you, Bret. The former president has already responded almost immediately during that hearing, calling this witness, bad news, but her testimony was dramatic and may have already caused serious damage.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 BENNIE THOMPSON\uc2  (D-MS): Do you swear --\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: A last minute hearing with a surprise star witness. Cassidy Hutchinson, special assistant to former President Trump in the chief of staff's office, told the House committee investigating the January 6 Riot, she first felt scared when Trump's chief of staff \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2  predicted the day could go downhill.\par \par \uc2 CASSIDY HUTCHINSON, FORMER SPECIAL ASSISTANT TO PRESIDENT TRUMP: Said something to the effect of, there's a lot going on, Cass, but I don't know. Things might get real, real bad.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: When January 6th came, Hutchinson says the president was furious Secret Service wasn't allowing supporters who were armed into the ellipse where he was speaking.\par \par \uc2 HUTCHINSON: Something to the effect of take the effing mags away. They're not here to hurt me. Let them in. Let my people in.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: Hutchinson also said the president was so angry Secret Service wouldn't let him go to the Capitol for security reasons. He allegedly had a physical altercation with the Secret Service agents inside his armored vehicle.\par \par \uc2 HUTCHINSON: The president reached up towards the front of the vehicle to grab at the steering wheel. Mr. Engel grabbed his arm, said, Sir, you need to take your hand off the steering wheel.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Trump then used his free hand to lunge towards Bobby Engel, and Mr. -- when Mr. Ornato had recounted this story to me, he had motioned towards his clavicles.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: She said even White House Counsel \uc2 Pat Cipollone\uc2  had warned them not to go to the Capitol.\par \par \uc2 HUTCHINSON: He said to me, we need to make sure that this doesn't happen. This would be legally a terrible idea for us.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: And after the attack, she says \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2  and former Trump attorney \uc2 Rudy Giuliani\uc2  asked Trump for presidential pardons. Joining a list of Republican lawmakers she is already named.\par \par \uc2 The former president quickly responded to her testimony on his social media platform, writing in part, "I hardly know who this person Cassidy Hutchinson is, other than I've heard very negative things about her, a total phony and a leaker.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HASNIE (on camera): Now, Republicans have long said that this committee is illegitimate because there are no GOP appointed members on this committee, although some legal experts do believe that some of this witness testimony could be used by the DOJ in future prosecutions. But the committee has now been cooperating with the DOJ by providing transcripts or interviews.\par \par \uc2 Chairman \uc2 Bennie Thompson\uc2  says the committee right now is busy conducting their investigation. Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Aishah Hasnie, live on Capitol Hill. Aishah, thanks. More on this with the panel.\par \par \uc2 President Biden is calling the deaths of at least 51 migrants jammed into a sweltering tractor trailer, abandoned near San Antonio horrifying and heartbreaking.\par \par \uc2 But the governor of Texas is blaming the president's border policies for that disaster. Saying, "The deaths are on Biden."\par \par \uc2 This comes as the Biden administration launches a plan to move unaccompanied migrant children to North Carolina after flatly denying it would do so.\par \par \uc2 We have "FOX TEAM COVERAGE". Bill Melugin is in Los Angeles, telling us about that housing controversy. But first, correspondent Griff Jenkins has the latest tonight on this tragedy with migrants from Eagle Pass, Texas.\par \par \uc2 Good evening, Griff.\par \par \uc2 GRIFF JENKINS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Good evening, Bret. There are simply a lot of unanswered questions this evening. As the border officials, local law enforcement and first responders are reeling from what they believe to be one of the worst human smuggling tragedies ever to occur on U.S. soil.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHARLES HOOD, FIRE CHIEF, SAN ANTONIO FIRE DEPARTMENT: We're not supposed to open up a truck and see stacks of bodies in there.\par \par \uc2 JENKINS (voice-over): A distraught fire chief, following the discovery. 51 dead migrants. Teenagers among the victims. 11 still fighting for their lives after being trapped in an abandoned tractor trailer in triple digit temperatures without water without air conditioning. At least three people have been arrested.\par \par \uc2 President Biden issuing a statement, saying, "my administration will continue to do everything possible to stop human smugglers and traffickers from taking advantage of people who are seeking to enter the United States between ports of entry.\par \par \uc2 But Texas Governor Greg Abbott directly blames the administration. Tweeting, "These deaths are on Biden. They are a result of his deadly open border policies. They show the deadly consequences of his refusal to enforce the law."\par \par \uc2 Republican Texas Congressman Tony Gonzales, who represents the area says this could have been avoided.\par \par \uc2 REP. TONY GONZALES (R-TX): This crisis absolutely could have been prevented and it would have been prevented if one, we secure the border. We implement the policies that are in place, you just enforce the laws that are already on the books.\par \par \uc2 JENKINS: This, as White House Press Secretary \uc2 Karine Jean-Pierre\uc2  defends the administration's handling of border issues.\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , SENIOR ADVISER TO \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 : But the fact of the matter is, the border is close.\par \par \uc2 JENKINS: However, the pictures and numbers tell a different story. This large group of 300 to 400 migrants crossing earlier today. The third such group in this many days here in Eagle Pass, Texas.\par \par \uc2 As Fox News witnesses, migrants routinely suffering from heat exhaustion and near drowning.\par \par \uc2 GONZALES: I'm absolutely concerned that the situation has just become too normal, and the manner in which, you know, people are numb to it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JENKINS: And as the DHS continues its investigation into this tragedy, there is no data available for how many border wide deaths have occurred this fiscal year. CBP has not kept that information or made it public since 2020.\par \par \uc2 However, we have just confirmed in the Del Rio sector specifically in the past fiscal year, Bret, there have been 124 migrant deaths. 71 of those from exposure. Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Griff Jenkins live in Eagle Pass. Griff, thanks. The Biden administration is making plans to house minor children smuggled across the southern border at a facility near the East Coast. It's a parent policy -- It's an apparent policy reversal for the administration, and a lot of people are not happy about it, and talking about it.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent Bill Melugin has details tonight from Los Angeles.\par \par \uc2 BILL MELUGIN, FOX NEWS CHANNEL NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT (voice-over): This upscale sprawling former boarding school in Greensboro, North Carolina will imminently house unaccompanied minor children at the direction of the Biden administration.\par \par \uc2 Health and Human Services quietly signing a five-year lease for the vacant American Hebrew Academy campus, where migrant children will live in government custody until they can be reunited with family or sponsors in the U.S.\par \par \uc2 HHS will hire up to 800 workers to staff the facility and children could begin arriving as soon as this week.\par \par \uc2 Some local officials support the move.\par \par \uc2 SKIP ALSTON, COMMISSIONER, GUILFORD COUNTY, NORTH CAROLINA: It's about the children. It's about jobs. It's about doing the right thing for these children. These are innocent children. We should not put politics in the midst of this.\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN: But some North Carolina GOP lawmakers are crying foul, as \uc2 HHS\uc2  Secretary \uc2 Xavier Becerra\uc2  previously denied reports that migrant children would be sent to the state.\par \par \uc2 REP. RICHARD HUDSON (R-NC): Can you confirm if this report is true? Have you already sent or are you planning to send minors to North Carolina?\par \par \uc2 XAVIER BECERRA\uc2 , UNITED STATES SECRETARY OF HEALTH AND HUMAN SERVICES: There is no plan that we have to shelter children in North Carolina.\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN: Several North Carolina Republicans now penning a letter to Becerra, saying in part, "The decision to now resettle unaccompanied children in Greensboro directly contradicts what you said previously, and comes as a complete surprise to us in our constituencies." And some of those constituents aren't happy.\par \par \uc2 GUS KOSTOPANAGIOTIS, RESIDENT, GREENSBORO, NORTH CAROLINA: I don't have nothing against the refugees, but not in the city in that large amount.\par \par \uc2 JOANNE GILBERT, RESIDENT, GREENSBORO, NORTH CAROLINA: How much is this costing the taxpayer? How much is the Biden administration paying to list this facility?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN: And Bret, yesterday, we reached out to \uc2 HHS\uc2  to ask those very questions along with how many children will be housed there? And what changed since Becerra first denied this last year? It has now been two full days, and we still have not gotten any response. We'll send it back to you.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Bill, do you have any update on this major fentanyl bust out of California? What's the deal with that?\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN: Yes, we do. Shockingly, two men who were arrested with 150,000 fentanyl pills in Tulare County, were both released from jail just hours after their arrest. They were let go on their own recognizance as a result of an order from a judge and a risk assessment. The local sheriff is furious.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MIKE BOUDREAUX, SHERIFF, TULARE COUNTY, CALIFORNIA: Allowing people like this to be released from our facilities, we have no control over that. And for law enforcement leaders in California is incredibly frustrating when we are responsible for public safety.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN: And Bret, those alleged fentanyl trafficker had enough fentanyl to kill millions of people. But now they are free.\par \par \uc2 We'll send it back to you.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Amazing. Bill, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Stocks tumbled more than two percent today in a growth led sell off as falling consumer confidence drove recession fears. The Dow lost 491. The S&P 500 fell 79. The NASDAQ plunged 343.\par \par \uc2 President Biden is in Spain tonight with \uc2 NATO\uc2  leaders. The president wrapping up his session with \uc2 G7\uc2  colleagues, still unable to shake criticism of his energy policy.\par \par \uc2 And an unusual encounter with the leader of America's oldest ally may have complicated things a bit further.\par \par \uc2 White House correspondent Peter Doocy reports tonight from Austria. Good evening, Peter.\par \par \uc2 PETER DOOCY, FOX NEWS CHANNEL WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Good evening, Bret.\par \par \uc2 Here in Europe, everybody's heard about President Biden's plan to transition to clean energy. But his plan to find oil elsewhere in the world, in the meantime, is more on the down low, which we saw in this leader-level game of telephone.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 EMMANUEL MACRON\uc2 , PRESI