ar \uc2 DOOCY: Feeling it instead, with high gas prices, inflation, and looming food shortages, \uc2 G7\uc2  and \uc2 NATO\uc2  countries.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: When we agreed we were going to respond, we acknowledged there was going to be some cost to our people.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: The cost has gotten too high for some countries here in Europe. Making all this talk about a green future mostly talk for now, because as Russia continues reducing exports of oil to this continent, some countries are bringing coal power plants back online, including Italy, Germany, and right here in Austria. Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Peter Doocy, traveling with the president in Austria tonight. Peter, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Turkey is ending its opposition to \uc2 NATO\uc2  membership for Sweden and Finland. The senior Biden administration official says no concessions were offered to Turkey to achieve that deal and that Turkey did not ask for any.\par \par \uc2 Russia has voiced significant opposition to that plan, as you can imagine.\par \par \uc2 Back here in the U.S., the former confidant to the late businessman, Jeffrey Epstein, convicted of helping to procure victims, for his sexual abuse of underage girls has been sentenced to 20 years in prison today.\par \par \uc2 Ghislaine Maxwell was found guilty of five counts in December. She apologized to the victims before today's sentencing. Coming up, a preview of tonight's high stakes primaries to run off some special election.\par \par \uc2 But first, Friday's Supreme Court ruling has set off a rush to state courthouses to argue over new abortion laws in the States. We'll show you where the action is.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: The CDC is activating its emergency operations center to deal with the monkey pox outbreak. The agency is saying there's more than 200 cases in the U.S. now. This allows it to further increase operational support. The center has more than 300 CDC staff working in collaboration with local national and international response partners.\par \par \uc2 In Michigan, Michigan Supreme Court saying a county judge had no authority to issue indictments in the Flint water scandal. The extraordinary decision wipes out charges against former Governor \uc2 Rick Snyder\uc2 , his health director and seven other people. The city switched to the Flint River for its drinking water in 2014, led to major health problems because of led contamination from old pipes.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  is turning -- overturning of the Roe vs. Wade abortion decision has set off a wave of legal action in state courts. Abortion bans are being challenged in some places and enacted in others.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent Gillian Turner shows us tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 XAVIER BECERRA\uc2 , SECRETARY, HEALTH AND HUMAN SERVICES: Friday Supreme Court decision was despicable.\par \par \uc2 GILLIAN TURNER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): Health and Human Services Secretary \uc2 Xavier Becerra\uc2  is adding his voice to the Biden cabinet's course of opposition to the Supreme Court ruling in Dobbs vs. Jackson.\par \par \uc2 He insists that despite the ruling which sends the issue back to the states, Americans everywhere in the nation still have a right to access abortion.\par \par \uc2 BECERRA: Under federal law, patients have rights to privacy. They have rights to access to certain types of care. The rights continue for it, whether it's family planning, whether it is in birth control services.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: This issue is at the heart of the legal battle now underway over where Americans can and cannot obtain the procedure. It's estimated that about half the country, 26 states are likely to enact near total abortion bans over the coming months.\par \par \uc2 18 states have so-called trigger laws in place, meant to automatically implement abortion bans should Roe v. Wade be up ended, but only eight of those states have successfully enacted the bans in the wake of the Dobbs ruling.\par \par \uc2 Louisiana was thwarted first, a district judge there issued a temporary restraining order yesterday blocking the ban in response to a lawsuit filed by abortion providers. Experts say the case is creating uncertainty.\par \par \uc2 LISA AVALOS, \uc2 LOUISIANA STATE UNIVERSITY\uc2 : Minute by minute, it's a change in situation. Abortions can resume in Louisiana until the hearing on July 8th. That is of course provided that no higher court, that no status state appellate court or the Louisiana Supreme Court intervenes.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: The other states are now facing legal challenges to enacting their trigger laws. Democratic state leaders say if these legal challenges fail, they'll take the issue to voters.\par \par \uc2 GOV. TIM WALZ (D-MN): Biggest thing to keep in mind is reproductive rights are on the ballot in November.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TURNER (on camera): Legal experts now say they're watching to see whether the Biden administration announces government travel vouchers for women who say they need to cross state lines in order to get access to abortions in person legally, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Gillian, thank you. Up next, we'll dive into a big night of midterm primaries, special elections and run offs. We'll see what the big races are, what's at stake.\par \par \uc2 First, here's what some of our Fox affiliates around the country are covering tonight. Fox 8 in Cleveland with a hearing for Browns quarterback Deshaun Watson to determine possible discipline from the \uc2 NFL\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Watson has agreed to settle 20 of 24 civil lawsuits for sexual misconduct, but he is still facing a significant penalty.\par \par \uc2 Fox 2 in San Francisco as \uc2 Airbnb\uc2  permanently bans all parties and events at its rental properties around the world. The San Francisco based home share co