S. have begun indirect talks in Qatar aimed at finding a way to try to save Tehran's tattered nuclear deal with world powers. Iran's top nuclear negotiator met with a \uc2 European Union\uc2  official after an earlier session with Iran's ambassador to Qatar. The U.S. special representative for Iran met with Qatar's foreign minister. Talks in Vienna about reviving that deal have been on a pause since March.\par \par \uc2 Investigators in Missouri are trying to piece together what happened Monday afternoon that led up to the derailment of an \uc2 Amtrak\uc2  train that we brought you yesterday. At least four people were killed, dozens injured. Correspondent Garrett Tenney is in Mendon, Missouri, tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 GARRETT TENNEY, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: When an \uc2 Amtrak\uc2  train plowed into dump truck Monday, the deadly train derailment wasn't a surprise to some local farmers. For the past three years Mike Spencer has been raising concerns that this very kind of accident could happen, warning that the brush and steep incline leading up to the Mendon train crossing make it hard for cars to see if a train is coming, especially since there aren't any flashing lights or barriers.\par \par \uc2 MIKE SPENCER, MISSOURI FARMER: I tried to prevent this. I'd done everything I knew to do in my power. I have talked to numerous people. And anyway, I'm just really saddened that I wasn't able to do more.\par \par \uc2 TENNEY: The \uc2 Missouri Department of Transportation\uc2  says this particular crossing was on their radar. In a February report the agency identified of the site as needing improvements and suggested adding lights and gates, though MDOT tells us it's still in the process of working out those details and a schedule with the railway.\par \par \uc2 We are also learning more about the heroic actions passengers took in the chaos that traveled the train falling off the rails and onto its side. Among the 300 people on board, more than a dozen boy scouts on their way home from a weeklong backpacking trip in New Mexico who sprang into action, helping pull others from the windows of train cars and using their first aid training to stabilize injured passengers until first responders arrived.\par \par \uc2 ELI SKRYPCZA, BOY SCOUT: When this all happened it all just kind of kicked in, and I just knew what to do, and all of our scouts did. We were just getting people out windows and carrying them down. I know I had to carry a couple kids in my arms two at a time. They were just asking for help, and I was asking them if anything hurts.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 TENNEY (on camera): This past hour the \uc2 NTSB\uc2  said for years it has been recommending upgrades to these kind of train crossings that don't have systems to warn drivers of incoming trains. Those systems are not cheap though and can cost hundreds of thousands of dollars for each location, which is in large part why nationwide only about half of all train crossings have them. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Garrett Tenney live in Mendon, Missouri, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the panel on what we learned from the former Trump White House insider's day on Capitol Hill and what could come next. We'll talk about it with Byron York, \uc2 Mara Liasson\uc2 , and Jonathan Swan.\par \par \uc2 First, Beyond our Borders tonight. A fire at a prison in southwestern Colombia kills at least 49, injuries a dozen others. An official says it's not clear whether all the dead were prisoners. The fire broke out during what appeared to be attempted riot when prisoners set mattresses on fire.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at earth from the International Space Station. One of the big space stories tonight, \uc2 NASA\uc2  sends up a rocket from a site in New Zealand. The agency is testing a new orbit around the moon it hopes to use in the coming years to once again land astronauts on the lunar surface. The initial stages of the launch went according to plan today with the rocket carrying the satellite reaching space.\par \par \uc2 Just some of the other stories Beyond our Borders tonight. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: In that conversation, was he told again in that conversation that people couldn't come through the mags because they had weapons?\par \par \uc2 CASSIDY HUTCHINSON, FORMER SPECIAL ASSISTANT TO PRESIDENT TRUMP: Correct.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: And that people -- and his response was to say they can march to the Capitol from -- from the ellipse.\par \par \uc2 HUTCHINSON: Something to the effect of I think they are not here to hurt me. Let them in. Let my people in. They can march to the Capitol after the rally is over. They can march from -- they can march from the ellipse.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Cassidy Hutchison's testimony on tape there, but obviously she was live at the January 6th hearing, Capitol riot hearing. She also talked about the want of then President Trump to go up to Capitol Hill on January 6th to the point where she testified that he told the Secret Service agent driving the car, the beast, that he wanted to go there. And they said no, you have to go to the White House. Take a listen to that testimony.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CASSIDY HUTCHINSON, FORMER SPECIAL ASSISTANT TO PRESIDENT TRUMP: The president said something to the effect of, I'm the f-ing president, take me up to the Capitol now, to which Bobby responded, sir, we have to go back to the West Wing. The president reached up towards the front of the vehicle to grab at the steering wheel. Mr. Engel grabbed his arm, said, sir, you need to take your hand off the steering wheel. We're going back to the West Wing. We're not going to the Capitol. Mr. Trump then used his free hand to lunge towards Bobby Engel.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: OK, just in the past few minute a tweet from Peter Alexander, NBC White House correspondent, says this, "A source close to the Secret Service tells me both Bobby Engel, the lead agent, and the presidential limousine SUV driver are prepared to testify under oath that neither man was assaulted and that Mr. Trump never lunged for the steering wheel."\par \par \uc2 For the former president on this issue, he said, quote, "Her fake story that I tried to grab the steering wheel of the White House limousine in order to steer to the Capitol building is sick and fraudulent, very much like the unselect committee itself. Wouldn't even have been possible to do such a ridiculous thick. Her story of me throwing food is also false. Why would she have to clean it up? I hardly knew who she was." Answering other testimony there.\par \par \uc2 So, let's just bring in our panel, Byron York, chief political correspondent of "The Washington Examiner," \uc2 Mara Liasson\uc2 , national political correspondent of National Public Radio, Jonathan Swan, national political reporter for "Axios." Jonathan, let me start with you. Obviously that new information in the past few minutes kind of changes the dynamic of at least one of those stories. Your thoughts on the testimony today and what it means big picture?\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN SWAN, NATIONAL POLITICAL REPORTER, "AXIOS": Well, it was the most explosive testimony we have heard so far in the committee. There was story after story, the throwing the food at the wall, the Secret Service car, et cetera, et cetera.\par \par \uc2 But the really important thing to point out about the Secret Service story, which is the most serious of anything she laid out today, that the president would physically lunge at the Secret Service to get them to go to the Capitol, that is secondhand, and she was explicit about that. She wasn't in that car.\par \par \uc2 So, it raises questions about why the committee knowing it was secondhand testimony would not present their firsthand corroboration together with that secondhand testimony, because she gave her testimony, her secondhand testimony, that's fine. She says that's what she was told. It's puzzling to me that the committee did not have that corroboration ready for such an explosive charge against the former president. So I'm very eager to see what comes next on that.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Our own David Spunt just moments ago confirmed Peter Alexander's reporting on this, Mara.\par \par \uc2 MARA LIASSON\uc2 , NATIONAL POLITICAL CORRESPONDENT, NATIONAL PUBLIC RADIO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And it does, to Jonathan's point, raise questions, if you are going to throw that out there -- and it was one of the most dramatic moments in detail. There were other things about the magnetometers that she heard directly.\par \par \uc2 LIASSON: Right.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: But doesn't it raise credibility issues about the whole thing?\par \par \uc2 LIASSON: Yes. Look, Peter Alexander's source I think in that tweet, he says a source close to the Secret Service. We don't know who his source was.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I'm telling you that David Spunt, our own David Spunt has his own source --\par \par \uc2 LIASSON: Oh, he has his own source.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: -- confirming what Peter Alexander just tweeted.\par \par \uc2 LIASSON: A source close to the Secret Service, I guess. OK.\par \par \uc2 But I agree with Jonathan. It would be shocking to me if the January 6th committee which is stocked with former prosecutors didn't have testimony from the Secret Service agents to corroborate what she said. She had so much damning firsthand information, it makes no sense why the January 6th committee would risk undercutting her credibility and their credibility to put secondhand information out there without having any corroboration for it. So we've got to wait and see, do they have the Secret Service agents on tape saying that what she said was true?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: They did talk to the Secret Service agents, Byron. And for all the methodical laying out and running soundbites and it's all scripted, they were not a part of this presentation today.\par \par \uc2 BYRON YORK, CHIEF POLITICAL CORRESPONDENT, "THE WASHINGTON EXAMINER": And that's just part of the problem with this committee. Cassidy Hutchison had spoken, done interviews on video with the committee four times before this, and yet this hearing was presented as kind of a last-minute urgent hot new information sort of thing. So it's -- the committee would benefit so much from having Republican appointed Republicans on there who could ask questions. Have we talked to these witnesses? Have we corroborated this evidence? The questions could be asked in a hearing. They could be actually more spontaneous than this.\par \par \uc2 Now, on the other hand, this hearing presented a lot of really unflattering information about \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , who exercised terrible judgment on January 6th. We know that he was happy to see a big crowd, he has happy to see a big crowd to go to the Capitol, and he had to be pushed to condemn what was going on. So it did confirm that. But of course, we have known that for quite a while.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Jonathan, where do you think this is going? What do you think the committee's ultimate goal is here?\par \par \uc2 SWAN: It's pretty clear they are laying out a roadmap for the Justice Department to indict \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . And it's unclear whether the Attorney General \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  is going to take that step. It would be extraordinary step not just legally but for the nation. And he would have to feel very confident that they could win in court, because these are all the calculations he's having to weigh that come with this uncharted territory of going after a former president. It's not even clear what they would charge him with. Most people I talk to in the legal community don't think anything that he said would reach the bar of incitement, so you have to look at these other things like obstructing Congress, seditious conspiracy, et cetera.\par \par \uc2 But that's where the committee is heading. \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  has been very open about that, her desire to prevent \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  from ever getting near the Oval Office again. Again, there is no empirical evidence so far that this has had that effect with Republican voters. President Trump is still popular despite their best wishes, but that's where the roadmap is headed.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: But if that's the case, just politically speaking, Mara, isn't that antithetical what Democrats would want, that they would want to run against a \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  who was, perhaps, weakened? And if this committee politically achieves their goal, to Jonathan's point, they wouldn't get that.\par \par \uc2 LIASSON: Some Democrats think that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is the most beatable. But other Democrats think that he is such a danger to democracy that it's better to weaken him in any way. I think that's what \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  wants.\par \par \uc2 I said sarcastically earlier, a little flippantly, that this was a good day for \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 . I think this was a damaging day for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . I think it weakens him. He is no longer the 800-pound gorilla. Maybe he is the 700- pound gorilla. He's still polling at the top for Republican voters, but this was a bad day for him. And this makes it just a little bit harder for him to be the nominee.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Ten seconds, Byron.\par \par \uc2 YORK: Trump is losing influence inside the Republican Party but mostly because time is passing, and his time as president is passing, and voters are looking for something new. So, yes, his opponents like \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  are coming up, but not necessarily as a result of these shows on Capitol Hill.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, panel, as always, thank you.\par \par \uc2 It's time for "Tuesday Tweets." Lucas McCain asks, "do your guests know what they are going to be asked about prior to you interviewing them?" No is the answer. The panels get topics, generally, but it's a conversation that evolves, and interviews do not.\par \par \uc2 Up next, NickD wants to know, "Do you think that the January 6th hearings are impacting any Trump supporters, or have they totally closed it off?" Maybe around the edges. So far, I don't have a lot of data that backs that up. There are a lot of people saying they just don't think it's fair that it doesn't have enough side to cross-examination.\par \par \uc2 And Bret Baker asks, "How about the investigation into the \uc2 SCOTUS\uc2  leak? Why haven't we heard anything?" Great question. We keep asking all the time. It is very internal, and they are trying to prevent another leak on that investigation, but we know it's going forward and going forward intensely.\par \par \uc2 Tomorrow on SPECIAL REPORT, we are expecting the final rulings of the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  term.\par \par \uc2 Thanks for inviting us into your home tonight. That's it for this SPECIAL REPORT, fair, balanced, and still unafraid. "JESSE WATTERS PRIMETIME" is next. Hey, Jesse.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FNSPBB0020220629ei6s00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Key Takeaways From January 6th Committee Surprise Hearing; Top Trump White House Aide Testifies At January 6th Committee Hearing; Jeffrey Epstein Pal Maxwell Sentenced To 20 Years; Macron Tells Biden Saudis Can't Pump Much More Oil; Stoltenberg: NATO Summit Pivotal For Future; At least 50 Migrants Dead In Sweltering Abandoned Truck In Texas; Suspects Released Days After Massive Fentanyl Bust; RPT: Biden Left Hunter Voicemail About China Biz; Biden Admin Pledge To Protect Access To Abortions\b0\par\par\uc2 Bret Baier, Sandra Smith, John Roberts, Martha MacCallum, Aishah  Hasnie, Andy McCarthy, Eric Shawn, Mercedes Colwin, Jacqui Heinrich, Jack  Keane, Griff Jenkins, Ben Domenech, William La Jeunesse, David Lee Miller,  Rachel Campos-Duffy \par \uc2 9,063 \uc2 words\par \uc2 28 June 2022\par \uc2 Fox News Channel: The Story with Martha MacCallum\par \uc2 HUNDD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Now, she is with Jody Hunt, who, when he was attorney general, was Jeff Sessions, former Chief of Staff.\par \par \uc2 SANDRA SMITH, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Bret, we'll see you at 6:00 for "SPECIAL REPORT."\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Lot to talk about tonight.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: All right. Thanks so much to everybody for joining us for our special coverage here. I'm Sandra Smith.\par \par \uc2 JOHN ROBERTS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: And I'm John Roberts. Our coverage continues post hearing with Martha MacCallum who starts us off in THE STORY.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA MACCALLUM, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Indeed, it does. Thank you very much, John and Sandra. Our special coverage of the January 6th Committee hearings continues with the surprise testimony today of Cassidy Hutchinson, the former aide of ex-White House Chief of Staff, \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2 . Andy McCarthy is standing by, but first to Congressional Correspondent Aishah Hasnie reporting live on Capitol Hill where this has all been playing out over the past couple of hours. Aishah.\par \par \uc2 AISHAH HASNIE, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONGRESSIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Martha, good afternoon to you. Quite dramatic testimony that we just watched for a few hours here. Cassidy Hutchinson right off the bat told the Select Committee that she was "scared and nervous" about what could happen on January 6th, when her former boss, \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2 , acknowledged that things could get bad on that day. Some key moments here from this hearing. Hutchinson says the president was upset that the ellipse where he was about to speak on January 6th, it wasn't completely full and that he did not want the Secret Service screaming because he said the people, some of whom were armed, are not there to harm him.\par \par \uc2 She also testified that the President desperately wanted to go to the Capitol. And that she said Kevin McCarthy at one point called her up and told her not to come. And when the President was told by the Secret Service that he was not going, she says that she was told he had a very dramatic response inside his armored vehicle called The Beasts. Listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CASSIDY HUTCHINSON, FORMER AIDE TO \uc2 MARK MEADOWS\uc2 : It had very strong, very angry response to that. Tony described him as being irate. The president said something to the effect of, I'm the effing president. Take me up to the Capitol now. To which Bobby responded, "Sir, we have to go back to the West Wing." The President reached up towards the front of the vehicle to grab at the steering wheel. Mr. Ingle grabbed his arm, said: "Sir, you need to take your hand off the steering wheel. We're going back to the West Wing. We're not going to the Capitol." Mr. Trump then used his free hand to lunge towards Bobby Ingle. And when Mr. (INAUDIBLE) had recounted the story to me, he had motion towards his clavicles.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: So, the former president has now responded via his own social media platform. And in that post, he writes this part of it says, "I hardly know who this person Cassidy Hutchinson is, other than I heard very negative things about her, a total phony and leaker." And then he goes on to say that, "she is bad news." And Martha, it's important to point out here that Republicans say this is an illegitimate committee with no GOP appointed members, as the committee tries to build this narrative trying to connect the former president to the January 6th riot. Some experts out there do believe that some of this evidence that we're hearing in these hearings might be used by the DOJ for any future prosecutions. Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Thank you very much, Aishah Hasnie. Let's bring in Former Federal Prosecutor and Fox News Contributor Andy McCarthy. Andy, good to have you with us. I think some of the strongest moments, and I've been listening to your analysis throughout the course of this over the past couple of hours. But since I heard from you, we heard this discussion about cabinet members talking about invoking the 25th amendment. Your thoughts on that?\par \par \uc2 We also saw Matthew Pottinger, who is a Foreign Policy Expert, who said that when he read the tweet that was brought to him about \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  not being able to do what needed to be done, he said he decided in that moment that he was going to quit, as did Secretary Chao and Secretary DeVos at that time. What was your reaction to that discussion about the 25th amendment?\par \par \uc2 ANDY MCCARTHY, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Well, I think the relevance of it, Martha, is that it was the reason that they were able to persuade President Trump to give the speech he didn't want to give on January 7th. As a, as a constitutional matter, I've never thought much of the 25th Amendment, because that's really intended for a situation which we've never had since the amendments been on the books, where the President is medically incapacitated from performing the job.\par \par \uc2 And that was the reason Vice President Pence did not try to invoke it, even though I think what happened was that realizing that you, it would be very difficult to do a snap impeachment. They were trying to figure out some way potentially to wrest authority from President Trump, but in the, in the narrative that we heard today, the main relevance of it was, he was obviously very resistant to giving the speech, like the healing speech on January 7th, he even wanted to include this idea of pardoning people who were involved in the riot, which I think at the time -- I mean, remember how shocked we all were on January 6th, the thought of that on January 7th, would have been, I think, bizarre to people.\par \par \uc2 And I guess, I'd say two things: number one, it's, it's incredibly irresponsible, the way the House did the impeachment back in January of 2021. This investigation should have been done, this evidence should have been in front of the Senate, these charges should have been in the articles of impeachment. And then we wouldn't be talking about criminal prosecution, or if we were we'd be doing it in a very different context. Secondly, that having been said, they're building a very strong case. Now understanding again, that there hasn't been cross examination and all that stuff that we always say. They're building a very strong case, on this idea of corruptly impeding Congress from doing its job.\par \par \uc2 And based on what I heard today, leaving incitement aside, you know, it's, it's a federal crime, to intimidate or assault members of Congress. And I continue to think the important testimony that came out today was just how much information President Trump had about how armed to the teeth this crowd was, and his reaction that well, what's the problem with that they're not coming for me, I'm not the one who's threatened by all this.\par \par \uc2 And then he encourages them, he not only encourages them to go in the Capitol, he wants to lead them. I kept thinking is the thought of him leading a mob down to the Capitol. What would the \uc2 Capitol Police\uc2  have done if the President of the United States was leading the mob? How could they conceivably have contained that?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, it is. It's a stunning image. And, you know, he obviously, according to this testimony, very much wanted to go down there and was stopped. And you have to think about the role of the people who stopped him in preventing us off from having that image in our heads become something that was a reality, Andy. So, just before I let you go, you know, what do you think the DOJ is wanting to or has the ability to, I should say, take action here. You think in the context of Jeff Clark's home being searched the other day, and also John Eastman's phone being taken. So, there are clearly things going on over at justice outside of what we're watching play out here. What's the meaning of that to you?\par \par \uc2 MCCARTHY: Yes, I'm really glad you brought that up, Martha, because I don't think this can be looked at in a vacuum. It's very apparent that the Justice Department's investigation has moved from concentration solely on the, on the riot at the Capitol, to the planning for it and or the planning, at least for January 6th, and the people who are around the president in that time. So, they're clearly looking at the White House, and if you're looking at the White House, there's only, you know, one guy, who's the main guy they're looking at.\par \par \uc2 I think, you know, Attorney General Garland has to understand that it would be devastating to the country to indict the political opposition of the President and someone who was a former president. So, what has to, I think, the threshold that has to be gotten through here is that there's got to be a consensus in the country before it's safe for the Justice Department to do what it can do, but has discretion not to do. The threshold they have to get through is do most people think that's a good thing to do? And that's a reflection of how bad they think this conduct was, which is why I think today's testimony was so important.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Interesting. It's also, it would be reflected in how many people are watching and how much of an impact it's having on, on their thinking as they look back at all of it. We'll see. Andy, thank you very much. Good to have you with us today.\par \par \uc2 MCCARTHY: Thanks, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right, we are going to shift over to the other news of the day because there has been quite a bit going on, actually, while we've been watching this hearing. Breaking just a short time ago, Ghislaine Maxwell has just been sentenced to 20 years in prison for recruiting young girls who say that billionaire, Jeffrey Epstein, sexually abused them. Prosecutors asked for at least 30 years, the defense called for no more than five, saying that Maxwell is a scapegoat for Epstein who died in jail before he could go on trial.\par \par \uc2 It's believed to have been a suicide. There were a lot of questions surrounding that at the time, but that seems to be the consensus at this time. Senior Correspondent Eric Shawn joins me now with more. Eric, what can you tell us about some, no doubt, dramatic moments in the life of Ghislaine Maxwell just a short time ago?\par \par \uc2 ERIC SHAWN, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: Wow! An amazing afternoon in court this afternoon, Martha. As you know, she was Jeffrey Epstein's girlfriend, confidant, and so-called partner in crime, and as you said, socialite, Ghislaine Maxwell, has now learned the price you will for participating in Epstein's infamous debauchery. She is sentenced to 20 years in federal prison, five years supervised release and a fine of $750,000.\par \par \uc2 Maxwell was found guilty in December of sex trafficking, including recruiting and grooming underage girls as young as 14 years old, to be sexually abused by Epstein who notoriously moved through the wealthiest levels of American business, politics and high society by allegedly procuring young teenage girls not only for himself, but also allegedly for others. Maxwell's defense though, blamed Epstein as the mastermind and the abuser, but the prosecutors who caught as you said from 30 to 55 years in prison, say it was Maxwell who was an integral part of Epstein's crimes, and that she abused young women herself.\par \par \uc2 In court, she finally apologized to the victim saying in part: "It is the greatest regret of my life that I met Jeffrey Epstein. I'm sorry for the pain that you've experienced. I hope my incarceration brings you closure." You know, several of her and Epstein's alleged victims did give victim impact statements in court. And earlier some said Maxwell, well, she still hasn't come clean about Epstein's operation.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Ghislaine must die, because I've been in hell and back for the last 17 years.\par \par \uc2 Today's the day I didn't think would ever happen. For me, it's a blessing. It really is.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SHAWN: Well, as you said in 2019, Epstein, prosecutors say killed himself in jail. And other those suspicions have long been raised that he ran a high-level operation to ensnare and blackmail prominent men for having sex within women. He and Maxwell did procure that possibility of that bigger operation so far has not been proven. But Ghislaine Maxwell, spending the next two decades behind bars.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Quite something. Eric, thank you very much. Joining me now on the phone, Fox News Legal Analyst, Mercedes Colwin. Mercedes, good to have you with us. Your reaction to this sentencing that just happened a short time ago?\par \par \uc2 MERCEDES COLWIN, FOX NEWS LEGAL ANALYST (through phone): What's amazing at the very end, at the end of the road, Ghislaine Maxwell still tries to portray herself as a victim of Jeffrey Epstein. She isn't a victim. The jury spoke clearly. She is a predator. She preyed upon these young women. She, she -- there was specific evidence that was expressed during the trial, that she sexually abused these children as well. So, the fact that she stands before Judge Nathan, who was the presiding judge and tries to say, "It is the worst thing that could have happened to me is to have known Jeffrey Epstein."\par \par \uc2 No, the worst thing that you could have done is to actually abuse these young women, these underage girls that could not consent. She did this, she preyed upon them. They were they were, they were from underprivileged backgrounds, they were vulnerable, all of that so that she could then also abuse them alongside Jeffrey Epstein. I'm really curious that Judge Nathan only, only gave her 20 years as you know, and you reported that the prosecutors did after 30 years and with good with good behavior, she could be out in her late 70s, late 70s, which would mean that you'd probably serve somewhere between 15 and 18 years with good time served.\par \par \uc2 But at the end of the day, it was interesting that Judge Nathan said to, to Ms. Maxwell, this is -- "We're not sentencing you because we can't sentence Jeffrey Epstein. We're sentencing you as because of the predatory behavior that you were found guilty of." So, at the end of the day, thankfully there is someone behind bars. Thankfully, there's some of that there's justice for these young girls that were abused by Jeffrey Epstein and Ghislaine Maxwell, and hopefully they will find peace in having justice served.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, I mean, it's astonishing that Jeffrey Epstein is not, did not stick around long enough to get justice from these, for these women. And the Assistant U.S. Attorney Allison Moe said that Maxwell subjected the girls to horrifying nightmares. They were partners in crime together. They molested these kids together, and that is what this jury seems to have concluded as well, Mercedes.\par \par \uc2 COLWIN: Undoubtedly, and I'm glad that at a minimum, but if they know that there were other talks by the defense to try to get significantly less than the 20 years, and that was not, not upheld by the court. And the 20 years will certainly give her plenty of time to pay for the crimes that she's been convicted of.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Mercedes, thank you. Mercedes Colwin with us on this breaking news that happened just a short time ago. Also breaking right now, Texas officials are about to give an update on the 50 migrants who are now dead after they were abandoned in a tractor trailer in sweltering Texas heat.\par \par \uc2 Ben Domenech, Rachel Campos Duffy, General Jack Keane, all here with us on THE STORY, as we move through this hour. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So he's got some attention today. President Biden was caught on camera, apparently getting some bad news or some information on the world's oil supply. With gas prices, of course close to an all-time high.\par \par \uc2 Watch this because French President \uc2 Emmanuel Macron\uc2  kind of pulls him aside as they're walking here in Spain, and tells him about a conversation that he had with the leader of the UAE and the leader is known as MBZ. And then, a White House adviser kind of pulls them aside and says, you know, we should get away from the cameras. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 EMMANUEL MACRON\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF FRANCE: Joe, Mr. President, excuse me. Sorry to interrupt you. With your adviser, I spoke to MBZ. I called for increasing the production. So (INAUDIBLE) told me two things. One, I'm at the maximum, maximum.\par \par \uc2 This is what he claims, and this is my complete commitment. Second, he told me according to us, the Saudis can increase a little bit t