ers to step up as well. They've been doing a lot of this work, but we need to do more of it.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, you know, yesterday, I said that I thought it was somewhat sad that states were sort of advertising themselves, Nomiki, as, you know, you can come here. You can come to our state for your abortion. And really, I was echoing the thoughts of President Biden back in 2006 when he said, you know, every abortion is a tragedy. But now the tone has changed completely on this. You know, people are told to shout their abortion, that it's something to be proud of, and not to be, you know, I never have spoken to anyone who was excited or happy about it.\par \par \uc2 You know, I'm just wondering where we are right now, in terms of compassion? And if you're pro choice, wouldn't you be pro all of these options that Rachel just discussed in terms of ways to turn for help?\par \par \uc2 NOMIKI KONST (D), NEW YORK STATE SENATE CANDIDATE: Well, I love how Rachel talks about family planning clinics when Republicans, the far right, extremists right, very religious groups that are getting tax breaks, has been arguing in state legislatures across the country to shut down family planning clinics. And that's why federal funds should be putting --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 KONST: Rachel, it's my turn. You had a chance --\par \par \uc2 CAMPOS-DUFFY: I think you misunderstood what I said.\par \par \uc2 KONST: Rachel? OK. So if you're listening right now, and you believe that a woman, and you believe in compassion, a woman should have the right to choose what to do with their body and their lives, because how many people in this country right now are at risk of losing their lives because they have an ectopic pregnancy, because there are states that are being controlled by right wing legislatures that are now making ectopic pregnancy termination illegal. That is a medical risk.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, what we think -- I'm just going to jump in for a second because I want to play something. Every state will have the right to vote on and to approve this, through their legislature, because that's what the Supreme Court did. And if you don't believe in democracy and people's ability to make decisions, and believe they want to represent them, then, you know, that's a whole different country that you want to live in.\par \par \uc2 KONST: Well, I want (inaudible) woman should have the right to make a choice about their own body. Rachel, give me a second. You've had your time and you have your control right now. Let me tell you something, as someone who's had an abortion, if I didn't have that I would have died. And there are hundreds of thousands, if not millions of women across this country who are victims of rape, domestic violence, who are --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well there isn't anything that we have heard so far that would prevent those women from finding a place where they could absolutely --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You said you wanted to do your turn so now I'm going to go back to Rachel and let be her turn because I've got about 15 seconds. You've got --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 KONST: It's about punishing women.\par \par \uc2 CAMPOS-DUFFY: Well, I just, today, interviewed Ryan Bomberger, who was conceived through rape, and who will tell you that his life has value. He was amazing. He was great. And I will just say this, I will just say this violence is never the answer all --\par \par \uc2 KONST: Oh you have no comments about gun when children are killed, you don't want to regulate guns. Are you -- it is this ridiculous?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right. Thank you. Thank you, both. I am totally out of time, Nomiki and Rachel Campos-Duffy, thank you for being here.\par \par \uc2 Well, that's THE STORY. We'll be back tomorrow. Thanks for joining us. Have a good evening.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document HUNDD00020220629ei6s00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 California to Issue Inflation Relief Checks; Ghislaine Maxwell Sentenced to 20 Years; President Biden in Spain For NATO Summit; Primary Tuesday; Explosive January 6 Testimony; Interview With Gov. \uc2 Tate Reeves\uc2  (R- MS); Interview With Former U.S. Secretary of Defense \uc2 Leon Panetta\b0\par\par\uc2 Susan Li, Jacqui Heinrich, Eric Shawn, Bret Baier, Bryan Llenas,  Aishah Hasnie, Neil Cavuto \par \uc2 7,674 \uc2 words\par \uc2 28 June 2022\par \uc2 Fox News: Your World w/ Neil Cavuto\par \uc2 CAVU\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CASSIDY HUTCHINSON, FORMER AIDE TO \uc2 MARK MEADOWS\uc2 : I overheard the president say something to the effect of: "I don't effing care that they have weapons. They're not here to hurt me. Take the effing mags away. Let my people in. They can march to the Capitol from here.\par \par \uc2 The president had a very strong, a very angry response to that: "I'm the effing president. Take me up to the Capitol now," to which Bobby responded: "Sir, we have to go back to the West Wing."\par \par \uc2 The president reached up towards the front of the vehicle to grab at the steering wheel. Mr. Engel grabbed his arm, said: "Sir, you need to take your hand off the steering wheel. We're going back to the West Wing. We're not going to the Capitol."\par \par \uc2 Mr. Trump then used his free hand to lunge towards Bobby Engel.\par \par \uc2 Neither Mr. Ornato nor Mr. Engel told me ever that it was untrue.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 NEIL CAVUTO, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Well, we knew we were in for something explosive today, as Cassidy Hutchinson, a former top aide to the White House Chief of Staff \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2  at the time, testifying that the president, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , was warning members of the crowd attending his rally on January 6 were heavily armed, and yet still directed them to march on the Capitol of the United States.\par \par \uc2 She said he insisted on going to the Capitol with them, even as officials, ultimately successful, tried to stop him. It was that close. It was that scary. It was that dangerous.\par \par \uc2 Now, is it all true?\par \par \uc2 Welcome, everybody. I'm Neil Cavuto, and this is "Your World."\par \par \uc2 And growing questions about the ticktock on what now seems like some over- the-top accusations against the former president and what he was doing and saying, even in a ruckus with his Secret Service on that fateful day.\par \par \uc2 Let's get the latest right now from Aishah Hasnie on these explosive developments on Capitol Hill -- Aishah.\par \par \uc2 AISHAH HASNIE, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hi there, Neil.\par \par \uc2 Well, the former president was apparently watching and reacting almost in real time. We will get to that in just a second. But let's first get to this dramatic testimony from Cassidy Hutchinson. She first told the committee that she felt -- quote -- "scared and nervous" about what could happen on January 6 when her former boss \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2  told her that things could get bad, they could go downhill.\par \par \uc2 Some key moments to take away from this hearing now is that she says the president was upset -- and you heard it there -- that the Ellipse, where he was supposed to speak on January 6, it wasn't completely full of his supporters because of Secret Service screening.\par \par \uc2 She says that he wanted that screening removed to let all those people in, some of who were armed. He wanted them inside, because he allegedly said to folks there that they were not there to harm him. Listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. LIZ CHENEY (R-WY): I just want to confirm that is when you heard the president say the people with weapons weren't there to hurt him, and that he wanted the Secret Service to remove the magnetometers.\par \par \uc2 HUTCHINSON: That's correct.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: Now, she's also testified that the president desperately wanted to go to the Capitol.\par \par \uc2 She said Kevin McCarthy at one point called her and told her not to come. And when the president was told by Secret Service he was not going, she says that she was told he got into a physical altercation inside his armored vehicle, the Beast.\par \par \uc2 Now, the president, the former president, rather, has reacted to her testimony via his own personal social media platform. And this is part of what he's written.\par \par \uc2 He writes: "I hardly know who this person Cassidy Hutchinson is, other than I heard very negative things about her, a total phony and leaker." And then he ends with: "She is bad news."\par \par \uc2 Now, Neil, it's important to point out that Republicans have long said this is an illegitimate committee with no GOP-appointed -- appointed members, that the committee is simply trying to build a narrative to tie the former president to the January 6 riot.\par \par \uc2 Some experts out there, some legal experts, do believe that some of this evidence we're hearing in these hearings might be used by the DOJ at a future date for any future prosecutions they might be interested in -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Aishah, do we know the significance of Cassidy Hutchinson saying this now and the allegations that seem over the top right now?\par \par \uc2 When she was giving recorded testimony some months back, do we know whether these exact same comments came to light?\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: Well, it's been interesting, this whole process, because we just found out about this hearing just yesterday.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: It was all shrouded in mystery. We didn't know who the star witness was going to be.\par \par \uc2 And the committee has really been trying to sort of dribble little pieces of information one hearing after another sort of to almost keep people interested. But we also have been hearing reports that Cassidy Hutchinson had a different lawyer earlier, when she was first giving those depositions, I believe four of them, and that she has now changed her lawyer, fired the first one, and has since changed her lawyer.\par \par \uc2 And that may have also contributed to her coming forward and releasing more new information that we heard today -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Aishah, thank you very much for that.\par \par \uc2 I want to go to Bret Baier right now of "Special Report."\par \par \uc2 Bret, the significance of these remarks today, and the former president immediately dismissing a woman who said he barely knew. What do you make of all this?\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Well, Oh, Neil, good afternoon.\par \par \uc2 I think this was as compelling testimony as we have heard on Capitol Hill in many, many years. It was very revealing about that day. This is a witness who had direct contact with the major players, including being right there next to the former president, as he's president, on that day, January 6, on the Ellipse.\par \par \uc2 And she quotes him saying to get rid of the magnetometers and let the people with weapons that the Secret Service was very concerned about through because he wanted to fill -- fill up the area. And he said that they could all march up to Capitol Hill.\par \par \uc2 Now, obviously, there's a lot of focus, and the committee's focused on pointing to what the president said about you need to fight like hell for your country. They don't often and they haven't yet played the sound bite where he says, you're going to march peacefully and patriotically to the Capitol.\par \par \uc2 But the description Neil of what happens in the Beast, the limo, that he demands that he'd been driven up to Capitol Hill, and the Secret Service agent says, no, you can't, we have to go back to the West Wing, and that description as told to her from the agent and the White House counsel, and what she describes inside the White House about his anger in throwing plates in his food up against the wall after the attorney general tells the Associated Press that he does not believe there's enough evidence to overturn any states' votes, is significant, because she's in the room.\par \par \uc2 And that's what is so compelling about today.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I'm sure many of the former president's defenders, though, Bret, said she wasn't in that car, she wasn't in the Beast...\par \par \uc2 BAIER: She wasn't.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: ... the president's car, with the Secret Service agent, which would lead one to think the committee wants to talk to that agent or agents, right?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: One hundred percent.\par \par \uc2 And we saw former White House Chief of Staff \uc2 Mick Mulvaney\uc2  said, before this is over, we will likely see that agent in testimony, as well as \uc2 Pat Cipollone\uc2  and perhaps \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2 , in some sort of a testimony.\par \par \uc2 There was this redirect from \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  saying, you then were in a room with both of them, the agent and \uc2 Pat Cipollone\uc2 , where they talked about this incident, correct? And she then relayed those specifics.\par \par \uc2 My point in saying all of this is, where does this go? The committee is clearly laying this groundwork that the president was trying to force a takeover of that electoral process and perhaps laying the groundwork for seditious conspiracy about the electoral count.\par \par \uc2 And whether they can get there legally or not, it is still damaging testimony to listen to the details of that day.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I'm thinking of the agent, if this is all true, and refusing the president's demands that they go to the Capitol. He's obviously looking at the president's safety and not stirring a hornet's nest.\par \par \uc2 We can only surmise that. But the president was fighting him on that. That's a surreal scene, in and of itself.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But I'm just wondering, the temper part, and we knew all those aspects of the former president and his frustration with the process here.\par \par \uc2 But did anything change with her remarks today, with Hutchinson's remarks today? Now, she had testified, as I was raising with Aishah, some time ago, and then obviously amplified this today best I can tell, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But the significance of this day compared to the five other hearings?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Well, first of all, I think there was another behind-the-scenes deposition with her after she got new lawyers. The first lawyers were part of the Trump crew.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Got it.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And these new lawyers, whatever she decided to do, she decided to do it again. And there was another deposition, which led to the sound bites that were played today.\par \par \uc2 I do think that they are -- some of the compelling testimony was the early days from January 2 to January 6 to, where she is a part of conversations. \uc2 Rudy Giuliani\uc2  says, it's going it'd be a big, big day. \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2  said it could get real, real bad.\par \par \uc2 She remembers about Proud Boys and Oath Keepers being a part of this, and that there were concerns about weapons, and then what she hears that day. I think it does all methodically build a case.\par \par \uc2 But most headline-grabbing is the description of the president's actions. And I think that we hadn't heard that before. We have obviously heard the former Attorney General Bill Barr say what he said about his anger, the president -- the former president's anger.\par \par \uc2 And we should note that, on TRUTH Social, President Trump has said this is not true, none of it is true.\par \par \uc2 And Cassidy Hutchinson is obviously under oath on Capitol Hill. And you have to take that all into consideration.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But she's hearing this secondhand, is she not?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: She is from a Secret Service agents and the White House counsel, but she's in the room as they are relaying to each other what happened inside the Beast.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And then she sees the fallout from whatever was happening in the Dining Room.\par \par \uc2 I think more -- more compelling is her direct knowledge of the conversations that were happening beforehand.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I'm just curious what you make of this. I mean, obviously, it is a president who seems very unhinged, very angry, furious. The whole process he had hoped to stall or stop is moving on.\par \par \uc2 Now we have made, maybe with this as a backdrop, reason to believe maybe this was among the reasons why he waited so long to respond to it. But, clearly, the committee is now going to chase a new avenue here, right?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I think so.\par \par \uc2 I think there are other hearings to come with that lay out more about that day. We saw our colleagues, a couple of them, in text messages that we knew and we have reported on here, saying the president needs to get out there and get this to stop.\par \par \uc2 And there were many other voices saying that that day. And I think what the next hearing is going to be is the ticktock of what exactly happens that day and the hours afterwards, something that Cassidy Hutchinson testified to...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: ... but probably more of that.\par \par \uc2 I think there's a lot of questions about security that haven't obviously come up yet, Neil. They promise that it will come up in this hearing, and what was the president agreed to or not, or \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But today was a big day as far as this hearing. And it did deliver for the surprise witness that they promised.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So, finally, on that point about the need for security and all of that, if we're to take her at face value, Hutchinson at face value, that there were concerns about whether this could get very raucous, if not outright violent, a few days ahead of the approaching January 6 election certification with the electoral votes, and, obviously, Republicans are going to want to hear if these warnings were relayed to \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  and others.\par \par \uc2 That, we just don't know, right?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We don't know. And we don't know if this committee is going to go down that road or go down it significantly. That's why it would have been good to have a Jim Jordan or somebody cross-examining or asking these questions.\par \par \uc2 That's not how this committee is operating. And we have put that out there that that's always a caveat in how this is a scripted play, sort of, of this testimony. But it doesn't discount or somehow diminish the significance of this testimony today.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Bret Baier, thank you very much for that.\par \par \uc2 Bret Baier of "Special Report." No doubt he will have a lot more on this in less than two hours from now.\par \par \uc2 Want to go to Alex Little, the former assistant U.S. attorney, criminal defense and litigation attorney.\par \par \uc2 Alex, you came away from this, and hearing Cassidy Hutchinson's testimony, how much of this has criminal implications for the former president, again, if true?\par \par \uc2 ALEX LITTLE, FORMER ASSISTANT U.S. ATTORNEY: Yes, I think a great deal of it does.\par \par \uc2 The two pieces that are, I think, maybe not new, but certainly she had some hands-on evidence about, was the president's knowledge of weapons in the crowd and his statement essentially not to do -- don't worry about that, let Them through.\par \par \uc2 It suggests, when there's later violence, that he had some awareness of that and was willing to sort of be OK with that risk. The steps he took allegedly in the Beast also show a president who was very, very adamant about getting to the Capitol, even when his advisers advised him not to, when he was told it could increase the violence.\par \par \uc2 That's extremely problematic. And then the discussions in the West Wing when he's back in the White House where she testified about Meadows' conversation about the president really not caring about the fact that there were chants to hang \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 , I mean, that's pretty damning stuff, if you put that in front of a criminal jury in a criminal trial.\par \par \uc2 The other piece that hasn't really been touched on was the potential obstruction of justice here. The text messages that this witness received about, President Trump is going to be reading these transcripts, do the right thing, that's pretty classic obstruction of justice.\par \par \uc2 And I do think there's a whole avenue of potential criminal charges there that haven't really been discussed.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You know, what I discover with each and every hearing -- and again, you have to rely on the people who are making the remarks -- is that a lot of people...\par \par \uc2 LITTLE: Sure.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: ... were seeking a lot of pardons.\par \par \uc2 And the newest additions, \uc2 Rudy Giuliani\uc2 , \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2  himself. What do you make of that? It's a growing list of virtually every congressman...\par \par \uc2 LITTLE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: ... who was for disputing the validity of the election itself and their move to either have a backup slate of electors that could do the president's bidding or just to protest the election itself.\par \par \uc2 LITTLE: You know, if I'm a prosecutor, and I'm building a case that so much is going to be focused on the state of mind of these individuals when they're taking certain actions, did they know what they were doing was illegal, did they think it might be legitimate, the fact that they're openly discussing the president giving them pardon, sometimes blanket pardons over a period of time, certainly suggests they knew, in their own mind, what they were doing was improper.\par \par \uc2 It's going to be incredibly damning evidence if this ever gets to a trial.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So, if you're former President Trump, though, Alex, and you're saying, I know they have weapons and they're not going to harm anybody, you could turn that around and say, you, Secret Service, are worried about my safety, I don't think these people will harm me.\par \par \uc2 LITTLE: Sure.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Maybe there's a way of interpreting that: Well, I don't think they're going to do much harm. But if you're worried about harming me, and the Secret Service agent doesn't want to bring him to the Capitol, for fear that will only endanger the president's safety, to say nothing of those who were marching toward the hill, I mean, is that a possible out for the president?\par \par \uc2 LITTLE: Well, that's certainly going to be the explanation of folks in Trump world.\par \par \uc2 But, realistically, you're the president of the United States. You owe an oath to everyone, including those police officers who are guarding the Capitol, including people on both sides who are representatives inside that Capitol Building. And if you are OK with there being a greater risk of harm because folks have guns and weapons and they're marching towards the Capitol, that shows a real betrayal of your oath.\par \par \uc2 And I think, again, it goes directly in line with charges like seditious conspiracy. You can say, hey, I'm not worried about them hurting me, but you need to be worried about them hurting the democratic process and the folks who are in the Capitol Building.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes, you have to go beyond the -- how this looks for you, right?\par \par \uc2 LITTLE: Sure.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I mean, and you're the expert on law and potential criminality here.\par \par \uc2 At a minimum, this is the leader of the free world, and he is clearly unhinged.\par \par \uc2 LITTLE: And that's why that moment in the limousine, if we hear corroboration from the agents -- I think it's going to be very interesting whether those agents have talked to the Department of Justice already or not.\par \par \uc2 Have their -- has some of their statements been secured outside of this committee process? That's certainly possible. But if you have a president who has been told, don't go to the Capitol, it's only go into inflame things, and he is so desperate to get there that he's physically laying his hands on folks who are meant to protect him, I think that's pretty damning evidence that what was in the president's mind was to get to the Capitol and subvert the will of the voters, at least as we understood it at the time.\par \par \uc2 And that's incredibly problematic for him as you look at a criminal investigation.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, Alex Little, thank you very much, a former assistant U.S. attorney, criminal defense and litigation attorney.\par \par \uc2 Now, again, no doubt this same committee, as we were discussing, is going to try to talk to that agent. Secret Service agents generally, it's very hard to get them to testify. In fact, one could argue, oftentimes, it's impossible to get them to, because they serve at the pleasure and the discretion of the president of the United States. So that might be easier said than done.\par \par \uc2 We will explore that, look at that, also have the impact and look at the potential impact of this going on in voting in some seven states, including in New York, and, of course, the fallout from the big Supreme Court decision on Roe v. Wade and a number of states' efforts to immediately enact anti-abortion laws, including in Mississippi.\par \par \uc2 That state's governor is next.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PROTESTER: Abortion ban!\par \par \uc2 PROTESTERS: Illegitimate!\par \par \uc2 PROTESTER: Abortion ban!\par \par \uc2 PROTESTERS: Illegitimate!\par \par \uc2 PROTESTER: Abortion ban!\par \par \uc2 PROTESTERS: Illegitimate!\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, another primary Tuesday across the country. Seven states involved in pivotal races, including in the Empire State of New York, where nominations for governor are at stake, and the fallout not only from what you just saw at the hearing today on Capitol Hill concerning the January 6 Committee, but that big decision on Roe v. Wade, overturned by the Supreme Court last week.\par \par \uc2 Let's get the latest from Bryan Llenas in Long Island, New York -- Bryan.\par \par \uc2 BRYAN LLENAS, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hi, Neil.\par \par \uc2 Well, Republican Congressman \uc2 Lee Zeldin\uc2  is the front-runner for the Republican primary for New York governor here in New York. Now, behind me is a polling place in Suffolk County, where Zeldin and his family came this morning. Zeldin cast his ballot here.\par \par \uc2 He is touting his experience and electability against Democrats. He's won seven elections in a row. He is the favorite, though, tonight, it could be unpredictable, given turnout is expected to be low for both parties.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 LEE ZELDIN\uc2  (R-NY): Have a primary that's between school letting out last week, July 4 coming up next week. We feel really good about where we are, as long as all of our supporters actually show up today before the polls close at 9:00 p.m.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LLENAS: Now, Zeldin faces three other candidates, including \uc2 Andrew Giuliani\uc2 , the son of the former New York City Mayor \uc2 Rudy Giuliani\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Surging crime, COVID-19 mandates and the economy had been the biggest issues on the campaign trail. The candidates have all promised to fire the Manhattan district attorney, \uc2 Alvin Bragg\uc2 , end cashless bail, end statewide COVID-19 mandates, and enact tax cuts.\par \par \uc2 Now, Giuliani appears to be Zeldin's stiffest competition. He has the name recognition associated with the fighting back crime and former President Trump. Giuliani touts himself, in fact, as the most Trumpian candidate, though Zeldin has been a staunch ally of Trump's who voted to decertify the 2020 election results.\par \par \uc2 Now, the winner will likely face Governor \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2 , who is heavily favored to win today's Democratic primary against her two challengers. The former lieutenant governor replaced Governor \uc2 Andrew Cuomo\uc2  after his resignation.\par \par \uc2 Now, Republicans, they see the best opportunity to get the governorship in New York in some 20 years. They point to underwater approval numbers for Governor Hochul in terms of crime and inflation and the economy.\par \par \uc2 But, Neil, Democrats are pointing to the Supreme Court decision turn on abortion, as well as, well, today's testimony, saying that any Republican candidate that comes out of this primary is going to have to convince a very blue state -- well, explain themselves about their ties to President Trump, who is not as popular here as he is in other parts of the country -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, we will watch it closely, my friend.\par \par \uc2 Thank you, Bryan Llenas.\par \par \uc2 Of course, we told you, after the Supreme Court's decision last week to overturn Roe v. Wade, at least a couple of dozen states so -- were ready to trigger anti-abortion laws of their own to take effect immediately, one of them Mississippi.\par \par \uc2 The governor of that state, \uc2 Tate Reeves\uc2 , with us now.\par \par \uc2 Governor, very good to have you.\par \par \uc2 Is that law now in effect, sir?\par \par \uc2 GOV. TATE REEVES (R-MS): Well, thank you, Neil. It's always a pleasure to be on with you.\par \par \uc2 The way in which the process works here in Mississippi is, our attorney general was given the responsibility to certify that, in fact, Roe v. Wade had been overturned. She did so yesterday. There's a 10-day period for that certification to go into effect.\par \par \uc2 Not surprising, the Jackson Women's Health Organization, the same group that had sued in the Dobbs case, filed law -- a lawsuit yesterday asking our state Supreme Court to overturn that decision. It'll go through the process, but we're hopeful that that litigation will be done quickly.\par \par \uc2 We are hopeful that the Mississippi Supreme Court will rule in our favor and allow our trigger law to go into effect.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: What about the purchase for residents in your state, Governor, who will get these Plan B contraception pills? They're not the Plan C actual abortion-related medications that some women can get.\par \par \uc2 But how do you police either or both, or do you?\par \par \uc2 REEVES: Well, certainly, we don't have any intention in our state of banning any contraceptives. That is -- that is not a conversation that we have had internally, nor do I expect there to be one.\par \par \uc2 With respect to Plan C, we're actually doing the legal research now as to determine, particularly as it relates to telemedicine and physicians and what responsibilities they have within our state to follow the laws. And we're working on that as we speak.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: We should say the Plan C pills require prescription and involve the administration of different pills to terminate a pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 So, technically, those who dispense such pills, doctors who offer prescriptions for such pills and women who take such pills, would all be breaking the law in your state, right?\par \par \uc2 REEVES: Well, under our bill that should go into effect that was passed, by the way, in 2007 -- it was passed with a Democrat speaker of the House and a Democrat chairman of public health -- it just kind of tells you a little bit about how far the Democrat Party has moved on this issue.\par \par \uc2 But our trigger law would not allow for that to occur, because abortion would be illegal, with the exception of the life of the mother and rape.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So, just to understand it, how would you, if it got to the point, Governor, where you had to track these Plan C or abortion pills, for lack of a better term, how would you police their whereabouts? A lot of women order this through the mail, for example.\par \par \uc2 Would you have to check their mail? Would you have to check their purchases? How would you screen that?\par \par \uc2 REEVES: No, we have a Board of Medical Licensure here in the state of Mississippi that ensures that the licensing physicians are doing what they're supposed to do.\par \par \uc2 They have a component wherein they can do whatever research they need to do and ensure that the physicians that are licensed in Mississippi, whether it's through telemedicine or otherwise, are following the laws of our state. And if you're not following the laws of our state, then, obviously, they're going to pull your medical license and you no longer have the ability to practice medicine here.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But, again, and then -- and excuse my repeated questioning in this area. Maybe I'm just slow, Governor.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: If you -- you would have to police commerce, right? You would have to police those pills getting to women in your state, beyond just doctors prescribing them.\par \par \uc2 They -- oftentimes, they would have arrived by mail, right?\par \par \uc2 REEVES: Well, what you would be policing is the practice of medicine in the state of Mississippi.\par \par \uc2 We police that with over 7,000 physicians today. And we actually have been a leader in Mississippi when it comes to telemedicine, because we live in a rural state. And telemedicine plays a vital, critical role in the providing of medical services to our people.\par \par \uc2 But we also have a Board of Medical Licensure, whose sole responsibility is to license physicians and ensure that they are following the laws of our state. And should physicians try to practice in Mississippi and not follow the law, then they will lose their license. And, to the extent they're breaking the law, they will be prosecuted as such.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Got it.\par \par \uc2 All right, Governor Reeves, thank you for taking the time. We appreciate that.\par \par \uc2 REEVES: Thank you, Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the president traveling abroad, this time in Spain, meeting with \uc2 NATO\uc2  country leaders, and committing himself to more troops, more support and more of everything for Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 The latest on whether it could be too little too, late. \uc2 Leon Panetta\uc2  is next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Another big sell-off for the Dow Jones industrials and other major markets, particularly technology stocks, on fears that that recession that they were hoping would lessen the impact of higher rates, well, it's now suddenly a bigger worry than the higher rates. Go figure.\par \par \uc2 More after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, first the \uc2 G7\uc2 , and now the president in Madrid, Spain, for the \uc2 NATO\uc2  summit committing himself and the other major powers to continue to support Ukraine, but some of them are -- well, they're getting a little leery of this and how far it goes.\par \par \uc2 Jacqui Heinrich in Madrid right now with more on that -- Jacqui.\par \par \uc2 JACQUI HEINRICH, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hey there, Neil.\par \par \uc2 A major objective of this summit is to showcase a united front from the West in the face of Russians' invasion of Ukraine. And \uc2 NATO\uc2  has cleared a major hurdle today in keeping that front united, with Turkey dropping its opposition to Finland and Sweden joining the alliance, major news major news happening just about an hour ago.\par \par \uc2 We just wrapped up a call with senior administration officials, who gave us sort of a ticktock of how it all went down. The leaders of Finland and Sweden asked President Biden to call up Turkey's President Erdogan to urge him to seize this moment and get this done in Madrid.\par \par \uc2 Erdogan was about to get on a plane to meet the Finnish president and Swedish prime minister and \uc2 NATO\uc2  secretary-general as well to discuss bridging Turkey's remaining concerns. And President Biden said, according to officials, if Erdogan knew this could get done by the end of the \uc2 NATO\uc2  summit, it would be a good thing, a good opportunity to just hash it out right now ahead of Biden's meeting with Erdogan tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 So Turkey dropped its opposition. We are told that they did not require any concessions from the U.S. Beyond that major headline today, we also learned that \uc2 NATO\uc2  is increasing the size of its rapid-reaction force to 300,000 troops from a level of only 40,000 right now.\par \par \uc2 And President Biden also announced plans to increase the number of U.S. naval destroyers stationed at Spain's naval base, happening, in his words, much sooner than later.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: We're ready to face the threats of Russian aggression, because, quite frankly, there's no choice. It's been the most significant abuse of power since World War II, an invasion of that many people into another country.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: So this summit will produce an agreement, comprehensive assistance package for Ukraine's defense. Awaiting details of that in the coming days.\par \par \uc2 It also is efforting new steps to place a limit on the amount of income that Russia can make from its oil sales that's financing this war. They're trying to arrange a price cap. But it's a very complicated effort, that it requires a lot of voluntary cooperation from multiple countries.\par \par \uc2 So we will see if they end up materializing that, Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Got it. Jacqui, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Jacqui Heinrich in Madrid.\par \par \uc2 They also are talking increasingly about a Marshall-type plan to rebuild a battered Ukraine. It actually could make the Marshall Plan look cheap.\par \par \uc2 Leon Panetta\uc2  on that -- after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, prosecutors wanted her in jail for at least 30 years. Now it's looking like Ghislaine Maxwell is going to be looking at 20 years in prison for her role in sex trafficking minor girls to Jeffrey Epstein.\par \par \uc2 Eric Shawn has been following this, has the very latest.\par \par \uc2 Hey, Eric.\par \par \uc2 ERIC SHAWN, FOX NEWS SENIOR CORRESPONDENT: Hey, Neil.\par \par \uc2 Well, she was Jeffrey Epstein's girlfriend, confidant and so-called fixer. And now Ghislaine Maxwell will serve the next two decades in prison for it. Maxwell this afternoon was sentenced to 20 years in prison and a fine of $750,000 after being convicted of sex trafficking charges last December, including recruiting and grooming underage girls, some as young as 14 years old, to be sexually abused by Epstein.\par \par \uc2 Epstein notoriously moved through the wealthiest levels of American business, politics and high society by allegedly procuring young teenage girls for himself and others. Maxwell's defense blamed Epstein as the mastermind and abuser. But prosecutors said that Maxwell was a key part of Epstein's crimes who abused young women herself, and until now has not shown any remorse.\par \par \uc2 But, in court, she did, apologizing to the victims, saying in part -- quote -- "It is the greatest regret of my life that I met Jeffrey Epstein. I'm sorry for the pain you have experienced. I hope my incarceration brings you closure."\par \par \uc2 Several of Epstein's victims gave impact statements in court, and some said Maxwell has still not come completely clean about Epstein's operation. And they said her apology is just too late.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I wish she was sorry when she was busy forcing me into a room to be raped. That's why I wish she was sorry. She should have been sorry then.\par \par \uc2 BOBBI STERNHEIM, ATTORNEY FOR GHISLAINE MAXWELL: She was being tried and convicted in the court of public opinion. Ghislaine will appeal this case. And we are confident that she will prevail on appeal.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SHAWN: Well, she was actually tried and convicted in U.S. federal court.\par \par \uc2 In 2019, Epstein has killed himself in jail. And although suspicions have long raised -- been raised that he ran a high-level global operation to ensnare and blackmail prominent men for having sex with young women that he had Maxwell both procured, that possibility so far has not been proven in court -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Now, 20 years, and she's had some time served, I mean, but she's looking at most of that 20 years behind bars, right?\par \par \uc2 SHAWN: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 In federal court, her attorneys asked for her to be sent to Danbury federal prison in Connecticut. That's where a lot of the high-profile -- other high-profile figures have been sentenced. And her lawyers say that she has been teaching English and high school GEDs and even taught other inmates yoga, but, clearly, all that falling on pretty deaf ears from the judge, sentencing her to 20 years, though she could have gotten up to 55.\par \par \uc2 Prosecutors wanted 30.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right. And her own lawyers were looking at 5.5 to 6.5.\par \par \uc2 So, wild, just wild stuff.\par \par \uc2 Eric, thank you. Great reporting on this, my friend.\par \par \uc2 SHAWN: Right, Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Eric Shawn on those developments.\par \par \uc2 Also, \uc2 Leon Panetta\uc2 , the former CIA chief, former defense secretary, former chief of staff for \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2 , on the significance of the -- well, all the major \uc2 NATO\uc2  powers coming together to continue to promise more aid for Ukraine and even a Marshall Plan to rebuild Ukraine -- the details after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Think about all the ways that the war in Ukraine has changed the makeup and the world view not only toward Russia, but, well, the state of the world.\par \par \uc2 First, you had Finland and Sweden applying for \uc2 NATO\uc2  membership, and now word today that \uc2 NATO\uc2  plans to boost the number of troops on high alert by more than sevenfold to at least seven -- I'm sorry -- to at least 300,000.\par \par \uc2 Leon Panetta\uc2  on all of this, the former defense secretary, former CIA director, former White House chief of staff under one \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2 , joining us on the phone.\par \par \uc2 Secretary, always good to have you.\par \par \uc2 This boost in troops on high alert, now over 300,000 more, what do you make of this?\par \par \uc2 LEON PANETTA\uc2 , FORMER U.S. SECRETARY OF DEFENSE: I think it's a good move by \uc2 NATO\uc2  to basically reinforce those \uc2 NATO\uc2  countries.\par \par \uc2 The United States is now going to extend our troop presence in Poland. They're going to add about 40,000 more troops as well, along with \uc2 NATO\uc2 . I think it's very important to show that \uc2 NATO\uc2  is prepared not only to reinforce those countries, but to be willing to implement Article 5 if Putin and Russia try to invade any of those \uc2 NATO\uc2  countries.\par \par \uc2 So, I think it's the kind of message that needs to be sent.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Do you think all 40-odd countries are on board with that Article 5 thing?\par \par \uc2 I only mention it because of the kind of volatile responses we get out of Turkey's President Erdogan.\par \par \uc2 PANETTA: Well, look, I think Putin's principal goal here is to try to wear down the allies with this kind of protracted war of attrition, and just kind of continue that war, and hope that \uc2 NATO\uc2  and our allies will ultimately split apart.\par \par \uc2 That -- that's his aim. And the more it's clear that our allies are going to stick together and continue to press sanctions, continue to provide needed arms, and now reinforce \uc2 NATO\uc2 , I think that kind of unity is extremely important to making clear to Putin that his tactics are not going to work.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, and we're showing this Russian strafing of a shopping mall, and maybe two dozen, up to two dozen, and dozens of others could have been killed, at least two dozen killed.\par \par \uc2 I do want to get your thoughts, though, on these efforts that countries take, \uc2 NATO\uc2  countries in particular, to stand behind really hitting \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 , and the Western world responding with serious sanctions, but yet \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 's ruble is at a seven-plus-year high against the dollar. His markets have stabilized.\par \par \uc2 The currency for a lot of his transaction, oil, remains something that's just running into Moscow for any use it needs, and China more than making up for the loss of Western powers. He seems to be holding a lot of cards, Secretary.\par \par \uc2 PANETTA: Well, sanctions are sanctions.\par \par \uc2 I think these are tough sanctions. He defaulted on his debt, payment on his debt...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: That's true.\par \par \uc2 PANETTA: ... which is the first time that's happened since 1918.\par \par \uc2 And I think it is impacting. But let's not kid anybody. The thing that Putin responds to is force. And so the ability to provide medium- and long- range air defense systems, the ability to provide a counterbattery radar which will track these missiles and knock them out of the air, those are very important systems to provide to Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 I think it's important for Ukraine to get back on the offensive, because they have got to show the world that they can push the Russians back. That's -- that is the most meaningful signal you can send to Putin.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You know, I'm just curious, Secretary. And I know you're a defense expert and an overall government expert on what governments can do when pushed.\par \par \uc2 But I'm an economic nerd, or I'm a numbers nerd myself. And I think all of these countries that are trying to provide this help and indeed are forking over help are in a world of financial hurt themselves, at or near or past recessions, ourselves included.\par \par \uc2 And you're already getting smatterings of people saying, enough already. We have helped Ukraine. Let's see what happens, but we're hurting right now. States are moving in this country to provide relief checks for people who are overwhelmed by the cost of higher gas, et cetera.\par \par \uc2 Can you see this fraying because of these economic pressures?\par \par \uc2 PANETTA: Well, Neil, I think you have -- you have just kind of defined probably the vulnerability that we all have to worry about.\par \par \uc2 This is a -- this is a tough time. People -- these countries are facing inflation, high fuel prices. There's a food crisis going on. The longer this war goes on, the tougher it is to maintain the unity of our allies.\par \par \uc2 I think that's Putin's target. That's his goal, is basically to try to split us apart.\par \par \uc2 And our responsibility, very frankly, is to show Putin that we are not going to split apart, that we're going to continue to unify, we're going to continue to provide these heavy weapons systems to the Ukraine, and that they -- right now, the most important thing is, Ukraine has to show that they have the ability to push the Russians back in the Donbas area.\par \par \uc2 That's the -- that's a major signal that we need to see.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Got it. And that could be an uphill battle. But we're watching it closely.\par \par \uc2 Leon Panetta\uc2 , thank you, sir. Very good catching up with you on that.\par \par \uc2 PANETTA: Good to talk to you.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: In the meantime here, you feeling the inflation bite? Well, in California, how does a $1,000 check from the government sound to you?\par \par \uc2 After this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Well, have you had it with paying more at the pump?\par \par \uc2 Well, how about in California a check for $1,000 to pump you up? It's not for everybody. There are limitations. But that's the message the state's governor and the legislature just approved to help people who are down and out because prices are up, up and running away.\par \par \uc2 To Susan Li on the significance of this.\par \par \uc2 Susan, I dare think other states will be thinking of doing similar stuff. What do you think?\par \par \uc2 SUSAN LI, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Well, if there's one state that can afford these $1,000 checks, of course, it's California because they have a record $100 billion state surplus there.\par \par \uc2 But the $1,000 checks, Neil, only go to those families that make $75,000 or less. For those families -- and this is the bottom quintile, by the way, of incomes across America -- you're spending 16 to 20 percent on fuel costs. And that means you're cutting back elsewhere. And some say 25 percent of household budgets are being cut because you have to pay more at the pump. So that means less food on the table.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Got it.\par \par \uc2 I believe, though, Susan, it's $75,000 for individuals, up to $150,000 for couples, but your point is well taken. It won't be everybody there.\par \par \uc2 LI: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: And then I'm wondering what kind of good this will do.\par \par \uc2 LI: And that's poor in California, yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: It could be inflationary itself, right? I mean, where do you see all of this going?\par \par \uc2 LI: Well, you saw the consumer confidence numbers this morning for the Conference Board, lowest in 16 months, and that's on top of the \uc2 University of Michigan\uc2  numbers last week, which was the lowest on record. American households are feeling this.\par \par \uc2 They feel that higher gas, higher food prices means that they are feeling less confident about the economy, which is the number one issue when they go to the polls and the ballots.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: And a big issue for the market sell-off today, right? People are just bummed. And that was one of the big reasons why we fell, right?\par \par \uc2 LI: Yes, and also expectations that in order to tame this 40-year high inflation, you have interest rates need to shoot up pretty quickly, very steeply, very fast.\par \par \uc2 And that is also, by the way, playing out in the red-hot housing market. And you have seen some weakness, signs of it, at least, when it comes to either home prices or even pending home sales, if you dig through those numbers.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right.\par \par \uc2 LI: And real estate accounts for about a fifth of the U.S. economy.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes, not good. Not a good environment right now.\par \par \uc2 Thank you, Susan, very much.\par \par \uc2 Here comes "The Five."\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CAVU000020220628ei6s000b5\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Interview With Gov. \uc2 Tate Reeves\uc2  (R-MS)\b0\par\par\uc2 Neil Cavuto \par \uc2 913 \uc2 words\par \uc2 28 June 2022\par \uc2 Fox News: Your World w/ Neil Cavuto\par \uc2 CAVU\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NEIL CAVUTO, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Of course, we told you, after the Supreme Court`s decision last week to overturn Roe v. Wade, at least a couple of dozen states so -- were ready to trigger anti-abortion laws of their own to take effect immediately, one of them Mississippi.\par \par \uc2 The governor of that state, \uc2 Tate Reeves\uc2 , with us now.\par \par \uc2 Governor, very good to have you.\par \par \uc2 Is that law now in effect, sir?\par \par \uc2 GOV. TATE REEVES (R-MS): Well, thank you, Neil. It`s always a pleasure to be on with you.\par \par \uc2 The way in which the process works here in Mississippi is, our attorney general was given the responsibility to certify that, in fact, Roe v. Wade had been overturned. She did so yesterday. There`s a 10-day period for that certification to go into effect.\par \par \uc2 Not surprising, the Jackson Women`s Health Organization, the same group that had sued in the Dobbs case, filed law -- a lawsuit yesterday asking our state Supreme Court to overturn that decision. It`ll go through the process, but we`re hopeful that that litigation will be done quickly.\par \par \uc2 We are hopeful that the Mississippi Supreme Court will rule in our favor and allow our trigger law to go into effect.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: What about the purchase for residents in your state, Governor, who will get these Plan B contraception pills? They`re not the Plan C actual abortion-related medications that some women can get.\par \par \uc2 But how do you police either or both, or do you?\par \par \uc2 REEVES: Well, certainly, we don`t have any intention in our state of banning any contraceptives. That is -- that is not a conversation that we have had internally, nor do I expect there to be one.\par \par \uc2 With respect to Plan C, we`re actually doing the legal research now as to determine, particularly as it relates to telemedicine and physicians and what responsibilities they have within our state to follow the laws. And we`re working on that as we speak.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: We should say the Plan C pills require prescription and involve the administration of different pills to terminate a pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 So, technically, those who dispense such pills, doctors who offer prescriptions for such pills and women who take such pills, would all be breaking the law in your state, right?\par \par \uc2 REEVES: Well, under our bill that should go into effect that was passed, by the way, in 2007 -- it was passed with a Democrat speaker of the House and a Democrat chairman of public health -- it just kind of tells you a little bit about how far the Democrat Party has moved on this issue.\par \par \uc2 But our trigger law would not allow for that to occur, because abortion would be illegal, with the exception of the life of the mother and rape.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So, just to understand it, how would you, if it got to the point, Governor, where you had to track these Plan C or abortion pills, for lack of a better term, how would you police their whereabouts? A lot of women order this through the mail, for example.\par \par \uc2 Would you have to check their mail? Would you have to check their purchases? How would you screen that?\par \par \uc2 REEVES: No, we have a Board of Medical Licensure here in the state of Mississippi that ensures that the licensing physicians are doing what they`re supposed to do.\par \par \uc2 They have a component wherein they can do whatever research they need to do and ensure that the physicians that are licensed in Mississippi, whether it`s through telemedicine or otherwise, are following the laws of our state. And if you`re not following the laws of our state, then, obviously, they`re going to pull your medical license and you no longer have the ability to practice medicine here.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But, again, and then -- and excuse my repeated questioning in this area. Maybe I`m just slow, Governor.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: If you -- you would have to police commerce, right? You would have to police those pills getting to women in your state, beyond just doctors prescribing them.\par \par \uc2 They -- oftentimes, they would have arrived by mail, right?\par \par \uc2 REEVES: Well, what you would be policing is the practice of medicine in the state of Mississippi.\par \par \uc2 We police that with over 7,000 physicians today. And we actually have been a leader in Mississippi when it comes to telemedicine, because we live in a rural state. And telemedicine plays a vital, critical role in the providing of medical services to our people.\par \par \uc2 But we also have a Board of Medical Licensure, whose sole responsibility is to license physicians and ensure that they are following the laws of our state. And should physicians try to practice in Mississippi and not follow the law, then they will lose their license. And, to the extent they`re breaking the law, they will be prosecuted as such.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Got it.\par \par \uc2 All right, Governor Reeves, thank you for taking the time. We appreciate that.\par \par \uc2 REEVES: Thank you, Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, Inc.  Copyright CQ-2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document CAVU000020220628ei6s0002t\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Loud And Violent Fallout From Roe v. Wade Decision; Roe Decision Sparks Nationwide Liberal Meltdown; Justice Thomas Brings Out Racism Of The Left; Democrats Using January 6 To Crush Political Enemies; Attorney Subpoenaed For Advising Trump And Speaking At January 6 Rally; Three Dead Following \uc2 Amtrak\uc2  Derailment In Missouri; The Last Pro-America Government In South America; Joe Called Hunter About NYT Report On China Dealings\b0\par\par\uc2 Tucker Carlson, Miranda Devine, Kevin Corke \par \uc2 7,336 \uc2 words\par \uc2 27 June 2022\par \uc2 Fox News Channel: Tucker Carlson Tonight\par \uc2 TCARL\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 JESSE WATTERS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: A Kremlin ring. Jesse, from Cleveland: "Jesse, what would you say to Nancy if she elbowed Jesse, Jr. or the twins?" I would wrap my hands -- I wouldn't do anything. I would just sew her.\par \par \uc2 Tucker is up next.\par \par \uc2 Always remember, I'm Watters, this is my world.\par \par \uc2 TUCKER CARLSON\uc2 , FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Good evening and welcome to TUCKER CARLSON TONIGHT.\par \par \uc2 We're coming to you this evening from Rio de Janeiro, which is a huge and beautiful and very complex city on the South American coast.\par \par \uc2 We're going to be here all week. We're filming a documentary for our "Tucker Carlson Originals" series on the rise of Chinese power and influence in Brazil.\par \par \uc2 Brazil is the largest economy in Latin America by far. It is one of the biggest in the world and it is the most important ally of the United States in the Western Hemisphere.\par \par \uc2 Brazil is a big deal and yet, the Biden administration seems determined to hand Brazil over to the sphere of influence of the Chinese Communist Party.\par \par \uc2 Why is that exactly? We hope to find out.\par \par \uc2 We'll also be sitting down for a rare interview with the President of Brazil, \uc2 Jair Bolsonaro\uc2 , soon. We'll bring that to you when we do.\par \par \uc2 But first, I would like to begin with the loud and at times violent response to the end of Roe v. Wade that came down to the Supreme Court on Friday, as you know.\par \par \uc2 If you've been following this, you may have noticed a profound change in the way the Democratic Party talks about the issue of abortion.\par \par \uc2 Gone is the empathy or the room for compromise. So, from the very beginning of this debate 50 years ago, even the staunchest pro-lifer acknowledged the anguish of young women who are pregnant and don't want to be, who are alone and feel trapped by the child growing inside them, and that is the reason that pro-life institutions had built crisis pregnancy centers.\par \par \uc2 And to be fair, for generations, even the most committed pro-choicers acknowledge the inherent sadness of abortion, which, at the very least, is the end of a potential life.\par \par \uc2 "I do not view abortion as a choice," said \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  as recently as 2006. "I think it's always a tragedy." And of course, it always is a tragedy, even if you believe it should be legal.\par \par \uc2 And Democrats once said this out loud, forthrightly. In fact, in 1997, as White House counsel, now Supreme Court Justice Elena Kagan urged her boss, \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2 , to sign a ban on partial-birth abortions.\par \par \uc2 Elena Kagan has always been pro-choice, but ending the life of a child a week before birth was too much, even for her. And of course, for his part, \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2  never spoke about abortion as anything but as a last resort. He famously described it as something that should be, "safe, legal and rare."\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BILL CLINTON\uc2 , THEN PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Our vision should be of an America where abortion is safe and legal, but rare.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: You don't hear that on the left anymore, ever. In fact, many on the left now behave as if abortion is itself a positive good, excited by the fact that a child who would have been born wasn't.\par \par \uc2 Think about that for a moment -- celebrating the failure of a child to come into the world. What does that say about how you feel about children or about people? In fact, some behave as if having an abortion is preferable to having children.\par \par \uc2 How do we know this? Well, no one on the left encourages women to brag about having kids. Having more than a couple of children at most, in fact, it's considered weird and embarrassing, something that Mormons and people who live in the ghetto do.\par \par \uc2 But abortion? That's something to tell your friends about. There's even a shout your abortion campaign, highly well-funded, complete with perky T- shirts bragging about ending your pregnancy. What does that say? Nothing good.\par \par \uc2 Over the weekend, you saw a lot of people proudly shouting about their abortions at pro-choice riots all over the country. Here is what Washington, DC and Los Angeles looked like.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ANTIFA YELLING: Every city, every town. Burn the precinct to the ground.\par \par \uc2 MAN YELLS: We are not scared of you.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: What is that?\par \par \uc2 The hysteria is telling. It's not the assault on bodily autonomy of the end of Roe v. Wade. Those are the same people who demanded vaccine mandates, but this happened all over the country.\par \par \uc2 In Arizona, thousands of enraged rioters tried to storm the State Capitol. On Friday night, they tore down security fencing around the building. They pounded on the Senate's glass doors and windows where the legislature was in session.\par \par \uc2 Police had to fire tear gas from the windows to protect the politicians inside. Here's what the State Capitol of Arizona looked like on Friday.\par \par \uc2 [VIDEO CLIP PLAYS]\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: It looked like insurrection to us, but no arrests were made at the Arizona State Capitol on Friday when that footage was shot. There will be no \uc2 FBI\uc2  investigation.\par \par \uc2 Democrats who call January 6 the racist insurrection said nothing this weekend about the violent siege of the State Capitol of Arizona. Did you expect them to? No, of course not. And in fact, instead, they justified it.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. PRAMILA JAYAPAL (D-WA): Let's be clear. We have so many problems with the Supreme Court and the legitimacy.\par \par \uc2 SEN. ELIZABETH WARREN (D-MA): This Court has lost legitimacy. They have burned whatever legitimacy they may still have had.\par \par \uc2 REP. ALEXANDRIA OCASIO-CORTEZ (D-NY): This is a crisis of legitimacy.\par \par \uc2 JOHN AVLON, CNN SENIOR POLITICAL ANALYST: And that threatens the Court's long-term legitimacy.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS HAYES, MSNBC HOST: The Court is about to face one of the largest threats to perceived legitimacy ever in its history.\par \par \uc2 REP SARA JACOBS (D-CA): The Supreme Court has lost legitimacy with the American people.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE O'DONNELL, MSNBC HOST: The crisis of the very legitimacy of the \uc2 United States Supreme Court\uc2  fills me with sorrow.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Well, you've got to give them credit for all being on the same page as usual. We'd love to get the conference code to the morning call where they set that up.\par \par \uc2 But because the Court did something they don't like, they're now telling us the third branch of government is "illegitimate." The Supreme Court is illegitimate because it's allowing voters to decide what they want to do with abortion.\par \par \uc2 Some would call that democracy, a system in which citizens choose their own form of government. They're telling us it's illegitimate.\par \par \uc2 On Sunday, the unelected Governor of New York, \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2 , explained that without nine members of the Supreme Court dictating the law for hundreds of millions of Americans, women are fundamentally oppressed.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 KATHY HOCHUL\uc2  (D-NY): Because we're going to get a flood of people. I have declared this as our safe harbor. This is where we have the Statue of \uc2 Liberty\uc2  welcoming people who are oppressed, women who cannot receive fundamental right to control their body or receive an abortion, they are oppressed. They're welcome here in the State of New York.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: So, we could go on and on and play you absurd soundbite after absurd soundbite, but what are we seeing here? It's not simply an attack, an explicit attack on the legitimacy of the third branch of government, Court. It's not just an attack on the right of people to govern themselves. It's something bigger than that. What you're seeing is a coordinated attack on the family and on children.\par \par \uc2 People at these protests are angered at the idea that children are being born. Watch what's happening there. That is hardly an overstatement.\par \par \uc2 Here is a photograph on your screen from this weekend. It was taken outside the Supreme Court. It shows a mother humiliating her children in public, implying they are a burden to her because they are still living.\par \par \uc2 We saw things like this everywhere at pro-abortion protests often in full view of children. This was the scene, for example, in Dallas this weekend. Watch.\par \par \uc2 [VIDEO CLIP PLAYS]\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: So, what is that exactly? What about the thought of having children makes these people so angry? Where does an attitude like that come from?\par \par \uc2 Well, as it turns out, that attitude comes from the same place the Democratic Party now gets all of its attitudes, directly from corporate America.\par \par \uc2 Corporate America wants you childless and this is a big change. A hundred years ago, big companies built housing for the families of their employees and then schools and libraries to educate them. It was the humane thing to do, but it also seemed to make good business sense at the time.\par \par \uc2 If you wanted workers you could count on, you had to take care of them and their offspring, but over time, that arrangement got expensive.\par \par \uc2 Employees with families demanded higher wages to support their children, and in many cases, they formed unions to get those raises. So, labor costs soared.\par \par \uc2 So corporate America, in response to this, developed a new model: hire single women. At many big companies, including in the traditionally male banking sector, young women now make up the majority of new employees and you can see why they do. They work hard, they're reliable. They tend to be loyal to the companies they work for.\par \par \uc2 The one downside to hiring young women is they can get pregnant.\par \par \uc2 If you're running the HR Department at Citibank, that is the last thing you want. Children make your healthcare plan more expensive. Worse than that, they tend to compete with an employee's attention.\par \par \uc2 Responding to after work e-mails seems less pressing to most new moms than putting their own kids to bed. That's a huge problem for big companies, so they have every incentive to prevent their workers from having children.\par \par \uc2 You can't say that out loud, of course. It would it be too obvious. Give us the best years of your life and in exchange we'll pay you what's effectively a subsistence wage in whatever overpriced urban hellscape we're based in and then take from you the one thing that might give your existence meaning and joy in middle age, which is having children. That's the deal we're offering.\par \par \uc2 That is the deal they're offering, but they can't say that. It would sound like what it is, which is exploitation, no better than what the cotton mills once did to 14-year-old girls.\par \par \uc2 So, instead of saying that, which is the truth, corporate America uses the language of the social movement it created, "feminism," to spin the entire arrangement as some sort of progressive liberation movement. "Fight the patriarchy. Have an abortion. It's got nothing to do with lowering our labor costs, we promise."\par \par \uc2 But of course, it does have everything to do with lowering their labor costs. Across the country they are making that case: Abortion as liberation.\par \par \uc2 Many of the biggest American companies are now paying female employees to have abortions, to end their pregnancies.\par \par \uc2 That would include \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  and \uc2 Apple\uc2 , \uc2 Facebook\uc2 , \uc2 Yelp\uc2 , \uc2 Netflix\uc2 , \uc2 Comcast\uc2 , \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2 , Citibank, \uc2 JP Morgan\uc2 , \uc2 Nike\uc2 , \uc2 Starbucks\uc2 , et cetera, et cetera.\par \par \uc2 Dick's Sporting Goods\uc2  is offering female employees up to $4,000.00 if they get an abortion. Does the company offer the same amount to female employees who want to have children?\par \par \uc2 Well, the editors at Breitbart wondered that. They asked \uc2 Dick's Sporting Goods\uc2  that question, but the company didn't even respond and that tells you the answer.\par \par \uc2 What's amazing is that in the face of this, so many Americans who ought to know better have fallen for it, so some accountant at a soulless, publicly traded corporation concludes that drones with no personal lives make cheaper workers. That's what happened.\par \par \uc2 But rather than question this or resist it, your average college-educated NPR listener nods in vigorous bovine agreement and then becomes completely hysterical when someone suggests that maybe there is another way to live, that is at least theoretically possible that raising your own children might be more rewarding as a life choice than commuting into a slum on public transportation in order to claw your way up to middle management at \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But the very thought of that, of turning down \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2  to bring new life into the world drives these people into a frenzy of rage.\par \par \uc2 Choosing a family over service to global capitalism? That's disgusting, shut up.\par \par \uc2 Journalist Drew Hernandez ran into people like this over the weekend. Here's how it went.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PROTESTER: A baby is not alive until it takes its first breathe.\par \par \uc2 PROTESTERS: [Bleep] the Supreme Court. [Bleep] the Supreme Court. [Bleep] the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: No, let's not have a [bleep] conversation, you worthless little piece of [bleep].\par \par \uc2 DREW HERNANDEZ, TURNING POINT USA: I guess the pandemic is over? What's wrong, sir? You like killing babies? You love killing babies?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Yes, I [bleep] love killing babies.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Drew Hernandez shot the footage you just saw. He's an investigative reporter and host of Turning Point USA's "Frontlines." We are happy to have him join us now.\par \par \uc2 Drew, thanks so much for coming on.\par \par \uc2 So the rhetoric even a year ago was "My body my choice." It was a bodily autonomy question. It was a libertarian question.\par \par \uc2 The guy in the \uc2 Pete Buttigieg\uc2  T-shirt just told you he loves killing babies and the rest of them were saying the same thing. That seems like a big change.\par \par \uc2 DREW HERNANDEZ, HOST, TURNING POINT USA "FRONTLINES": Well, Tucker, it's the absolute truth.\par \par \uc2 This is a death cult. Whether it's corporate America, whether it's politicians or these so-called activists and protesters and the rioters, at the end of the day, this is a death cult. These people want to murder babies, a human being.\par \par \uc2 And it's interesting because they refuse to call a baby inside of the womb a baby, right? But you see in that clip, he openly says it's a baby. They always say it's a fetus.\par \par \uc2 But because he's telling the truth, that's a demon speaking. These are evil people. They want to be able to murder babies wherever you see it across the United States, and I've been on the ground covering all of this for Turning Point USA about a week and a half ago in DC and immediately after the decision was announced, Tucker, we saw the NPC's on the left to lose their minds and start crying and the pro-life individuals outside of the Supreme Court erupt in cheers.\par \par \uc2 But within minutes, Tucker, Congresswoman AOC spawned out of nowhere in front of the Supreme Court and started calling the Supreme Court illegitimate and calling for people to take to the streets and saying that voting is not enough.\par \par \uc2 And within hours, we saw all of this violent rioting across the United States, and particularly in Los Angeles as well, where a rioter was literally found with a makeshift flame thrower. That's fiery, but mostly peaceful for you. I'll tell you that right now. But this is all about murdering babies at the end of the day.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Killing makes them feel like god, it is why they love war.\par \par \uc2 Drew Hernandez, I appreciate your taking that footage for us. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 HERNANDEZ: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: So the Supreme Court came to this decision. The Supreme Court is of course not elected. They can't really do anything about a Supreme Court decision. So Democrats have new plans.\par \par \uc2 The two most widely disseminated ideas come from the two most unlikable Democrats in Congress. They have joined forces on the issue. Elizabeth Warren and Sandy Cortez, and they are suggesting that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  can issue an executive order authorizing the construction of abortion camps, Federal clinics on Federal lands. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WARREN: There are other places the President could act to dampen the ability of these companies to track women who travel out of state to an abortion clinic, to provide funds for people who are traveling and to look into the use of Federal lands.\par \par \uc2 OCASIO-CORTEZ: We have many ideas. We have some ideas coming from Senator Warren signed a letter along with 25 other Democratic senators asking President Biden to explore opening healthcare clinics on Federal lands in red states.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: We're not going to help you raise your standard of living, afford food or gasoline or housing, but we can help you not to have a family.\par \par \uc2 Abby Johnson is a former Planned Parenthood director. She joins us tonight.\par \par \uc2 Abby, thanks so much for coming on.\par \par \uc2 Abortion camps on Federal lands. Did you ever think you'd hear this?\par \par \uc2 ABBY JOHNSON, FORMER PLANNED PARENTHOOD DIRECTOR: Never. But hey, maybe Elizabeth Warren can get us a discount from her tribal ancestors or something.\par \par \uc2 But listen, this would be extreme executive overreach if this actually happened. And I mean, you're exactly right. Hey, they don't want to spend any money on actually helping women who are pregnant, but hey, let's spend billions of dollars on, you know, building abortion clinics on what Native American land or our Federal parks? I mean, it's absolutely ridiculous.\par \par \uc2 Let's just provide for women who need resources for their babies right now. Women couldn't even get formula a few weeks ago, but yes, let's just build abortion clinics instead.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: What I don't understand is, where is the meaning in American life? I mean, if having children has no meaning, if it's not the main source of joy in a person's life, then what is? I mean, \uc2 Netflix\uc2 ? Like what are they offering up to replace the void of children?\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: Well, I mean, they're really not offering anything right now. I mean, you know, Tucker, when people, you know that saying is when people tell you who they are, you should believe them. These are people who are currently you know, firebombing pregnancy centers. They are calling to kill babies.\par \par \uc2 We need to believe them. These are pro-choice people. These are people who want to kill babies. They are pro-abortion. We need to believe them when they say that.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Yes, yes. I never used to call people pro-abortion. I always felt there was an honest disagreement. I've always been pro-life, but I always respected people who disagreed. I didn't think they were pro-abortion, but they are and it is shocking to me.\par \par \uc2 Abby, I appreciate your coming on tonight. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: Of course. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: A majority of Justices on the Supreme Court has voted to overturn Roe v. Wade. Of course, that's why we're having this conversation. But weirdly, the Democratic Party is focusing on just one of them, the Black one, that would be \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2 . Here is the Lieutenant Governor of California. Watch\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ELENI KOUNALAKIS\uc2 , LIEUTENANT GOVERNOR OF CALIFORNIA: This decision is de- legitimizing the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 Who is \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2 ? Is he my Saudi Arabian father who is going to tell me what I can or cannot do with my body? With my life? That's what this is coming down to.\par \par \uc2 So these people have a misconception about who is going to listen to them.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: There is the vax mandate lady whining about people telling her what to do with their body. Please.\par \par \uc2 Actor, Samuel L. Jackson also went after \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  with a racial attack: "How's Uncle Clarence feeling about overturning Loving versus Virginia?" Loving versus Virginia of course, is a Supreme Court case that overturned precedent. It held that bans on interracial marriage were unconstitutional.\par \par \uc2 So these are just racial attacks. Why against \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  of all the six members who voted to overturn Roe v. Wade?\par \par \uc2 Mark Paoletta is an attorney, a close friend of \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2 . He has a new book called "Created Equal: \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  in His Own Words," and a film. We just interviewed him the other day. He joins us again tonight.\par \par \uc2 Mark, thanks so much for coming on.\par \par \uc2 So it does seem like -- so you had six Supreme Court Justices vote to overturn Roe v. Wade. Again, you could be for legal abortion and still recognize Roe v. Wade, it was an absurd Supreme Court decision that degraded the institution of the Court and be against it. But why Thomas? Why are they going after one out of six?\par \par \uc2 MARK PAOLETTA, ATTORNEY AND CLOSE FRIEND OF JUSTICE \uc2 CLARENCE THOMAS\uc2 : Because the left is racist, Tucker. They expect \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  to think the way a Black man should based on the color of his skin, and \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2 , for 30 years, 40 years has refused to do that and they want to destroy him and that's what they've been doing in coming after him.\par \par \uc2 And the bottom line is, \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  doesn't care at all what they think.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: It is just so funny, though. I mean, if you compare the attitudes of the average Black guy in America to the average White liberal in America on abortion, the Black guy is way more opposed to abortion than the average White liberal.\par \par \uc2 So why does \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  have to have the same views as an NPR listener?\par \par \uc2 PAOLETTA: And that's what he has been saying for 40 years and as the left demands it, and they will -- they will go after him. But it is a great day for the Constitution, you know, with Roe being overturned, with the Second Amendment being strengthened, consistent with the Constitution, with religious liberties today coming down consistent with the Constitution, and it is all aligning with where \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  has been for the past 30 years.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Yes. I think it's a fair point. He has been saying this the whole time, as you point out in your book.\par \par \uc2 Mark, great to see you again. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 PAOLETTA: Thanks, Tucker.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: So this may be news to you, but the January 6 committee isn't really about January 6th. It is about targeting political opponents of \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  using law enforcement.\par \par \uc2 We just obtained the tape of their latest effort to intimidate one of their political enemies, pretty striking.\par \par \uc2 And as you can s