.\par \par \uc2 I just don't think that that was a long-term proposition that would work in any way. I think that the autopsy that was done in 2012 was about a party that was dead. And this new coalition, this one that I think is far more passionate, that has a lot more going for it and is about the American family, above all things, is something that actually represents a long-term and very beneficial development for the country. A move away from the harsh economic perspective of the past, and one that actually meets families where they are and prioritizes the issues as they see them, as opposed to the way that the New York Times Editorial Board believes they ought to see them.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Right. And the GOP establishment will throw them in there as well. Charlie and Ben, so great to see you tonight.\par \par \uc2 And now, allies, what allies. That's the focus of tonight's 'Angle'.\par \par \uc2 Now, whenever Biden meets with world leaders, especially when he's over in Europe, you know, America is going to get taken to the cleaners one way or another. And today was no exception.\par \par \uc2 During his meeting with the G7, ok, this is what went down. First, before Biden even showed up, America got lectured by several of our so-called allies. So it was France, Canada and the UK, they felt the need to respond to the Supreme Court's decision to overturn Roe v Wade with very sharp criticism.\par \par \uc2 Now, for \uc2 Boris Johnson\uc2 , who has single handedly destroyed the Tory Party, joining the dogpile on the U.S. for him. Well, that's really rich, because he didn't tell you that the United Kingdom, in many ways, has tighter restrictions on abortion than many states in the United States, because they require two doctors to sign off on an abortion in the UK.\par \par \uc2 Now, Boris' critique is a transparent effort to bolster Biden and to appear as a unifier in pushing for more Ukraine aid. Good luck in unifying anyone on that.\par \par \uc2 Now, this