 least you can breathe in marijuana fumes all over the city. You get high just walking down the street in New York.\par \par \uc2 ZELDIN: If you want to sell, if you want to be a dispenser, they give you front of the line access if you have a criminal conviction for drugs. It actually gets you to the front of the line to get the license.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: So pro pot, pro porn, anti-Second Amendment, anti-First Amendment. Anything else great about -- pro-criminal. Working well.\par \par \uc2 ZELDIN: Other than that, by the way, it's free. It's a free state, you feel safe, everything else is otherwise good. People are running to the Carolinas, Tennessee, Florida, Texas. They're hitting their breaking point.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: What do they have to do tomorrow? They have to go vote.\par \par \uc2 ZELDIN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: They have to vote for change. Otherwise enjoy things continuing to deteriorate, not just in the city but across this beautiful state. There's no reason this has to happen in New York, no reason whatsoever. Congressman, good luck.\par \par \uc2 ZELDIN: Thank you, Laura.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: We are watching closely tomorrow. Everyone, get out and vote in New York.\par \par \uc2 But up next, Post Roe media and celebrities declare America is over, and they are blaming religion for the end of democracy. Raymond Arroyo has it all in "Seen and Unseen" which is next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: It's time for our "Seen and Unseen" segment where we expose the stories behind the headlines. For that, joining me on set is FOX News contributor Raymond Arroyo. Raymond, do you see an anti-American trend going on?\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Why can't you get through this intro?\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: I'm sorry.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Let me read it. "Ray, do you see an anti-American trend emerging in the media since last week's Roe ruling?" I do, Laura.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Oh, shut up.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: And it's pernicious and dangerous. Look, no matter who is in power or where the ruling goes from the Supreme Court, no American should ever lose hope in their country or its promise. This is Jake Tapper talking to U.K.'s \uc2 Boris Johnson\uc2 , trying to convince him that America is finished.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JAKE TAPPER, CNN ANCHOR: When I talk to friends in Canada, the U.K., Australia, and elsewhere, people express concerns about the United States.\par \par \uc2 BORIS JOHNSON\uc2 , U.K. PRIME MINISTER: The United States?\par \par \uc2 TAPPER: About the United States in terms of our ability and our institutions to thrive and continue after what happened with the election of 2020. They are worried that democracy is on life support. Do you --\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: No. I want to say this to the people of the United States. Reports of the death of democracy in the United States are grossly, grossly exaggerated. I continue to believe that America is the greatest global guarantor of democracy and freedom.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Laura, \uc2 Boris Johnson\uc2  has a better understanding of America than some of our own citizens. And as far as this Roe ruling is concerned, they have given democracy back to the people. It goes to the states. So we're not taking away any rights. You are giving them to the people to decide.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: I like how he played with his hair, and then he went no, no. Best guarantor ever.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: That was kind of funny.\par \par \uc2 I wasn't surprised to see heated reaction to this overturning of Roe v. Wade. What is surprising is the nearly traitorous denunciations of the country abroad. This is Green Day front man Billie Joe Armstrong. During a London concert he yelled "F- America," and said he is renouncing his citizenship over the Supreme Court decision. And then Olivia Rodrigo, also on the English stage, did this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 OLIVIA RODRIGO, SINGER: This song goes out to the justices \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2 , \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2 , \uc2 Neil Gorsuch\uc2 , Amy Coney Barrett --\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 RODRIGO: -- and \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2 . We hate you. We hate you.\par \par \uc2 (MUSIC)\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: They are singing "F you" to Supreme Court abroad and denouncing the country abroad. Protest all you want at home. Doing it abroad, Laura, is a real problem for me.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: That's tacky. Then give back all the royalties from those ugly American that a lot of them probably voted or parents voted for Republicans that put these people on the court. Give it all back. They talk a good game, but they will never give it back because most of these people don't know what they are talking about. They are total frauds, and Billie Joe Armstrong should change the eyeliner.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: He did sing "American Idiot" following that.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: How original. Aren't we tired of that? All right, who wrote "Shut Up and Sing" again? Oh, I did.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: You did.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: "The View" gals today tried to blame religion for this reversal of Roe while in the Bahamas.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: The justices, there are six Catholics on the bench. This is an activist Supreme Court and they should not be deciding the law based on their faith.\par \par \uc2 JOY BEHAR, CO-HOST, "THE VIEW": Don't we have separation of church and state.\par \par \uc2 WHOOPI GOLDBERG, CO-HOST, "THE FIVE": We don't anymore.\par \par \uc2 ANA NAVARRO, CO-HOST, "THE FIVE": Six of the nine justices were born or were raised Catholic. Twenty-two percent of America is Catholic.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Laura, just to stay with our "View" gals we decided we'd also shield our eyes. But you know what the problem with all this is? They are demonizing religion and religious people at a time when we are seeing pro- life centers attacked, churches defaced. This is the worst impulse, particularly from people who claim to be Catholic to denounce Catholicism. These judges didn't rule this way, these justices, because they're Catholic. They voted this way and ruled this way because they're jurists, and that is being lost in this, trying to portray this as some kind of theocracy at play. That's not what happened here.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: I keep thinking \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is on the set when I see you in these glasses. You're making such good points, but I really can't take you seriously.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: All right, we'll take our glasses off.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: But I prefer Joy Behar in the sunglasses. I think it's actually a good look for her.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: She already looks like she is in the home, yes. It worked perfectly.\par \par \uc2 The other thing here, Laura, no one condemned faith when it drove the other great civil rights movement of our time, the African Americans trying to get their right and to be fully free and to get the vote. That was also propelled by religion. So is this pro-life cause. So it's nothing to look down on or certainly to --\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: How long are they in the Bahamas?\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Who knows. They should only stay there for much longer and let's hope the --\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: This is a winning week. We've got Billie Joe Armstrong heading out of the country. We've got Olivia Rodrigo all worked up. This is pretty good so far. We've got all these winning Supreme Court decisions, winning, hashtag winning.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: OK.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: All right, Raymond, thank you. Nice glasses.\par \par \uc2 We warned everyone about how funding the implementation of these red flag laws would put gun owners at risk, and now California is proving us right. Harmeet Dhillon has the concerning details next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Last week's Supreme Court decision on gun rights largely rendered California's restrictive gun laws, much like New York's, unconstitutional. But that is not stopping the state's A.G. from looking for new ways to deny gun permits. In a letter to the state's D.A.s and police chiefs, Attorney General \uc2 Rob Bonta\uc2  said that applicants must provide proof of that good moral character and that the state can go through social media accounts to cut against it.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is Harmeet Dhillon of the Dhillon law group, founder of the Center for American Liberty. Harmeet, we warned about this, giving lessons to political actors to take away constitutional rights on the basis of ideology. Your thoughts tonight?\par \par \uc2 DHILLON: You are absolutely right, Laura, and California is signaling very hard that it is not going to make it easy for law-abiding citizens like myself sitting here in San Francisco to be able to go and get a concealed carry permit, which is now my right under the new Supreme Court precedent. So the only thing that \uc2 Rob Bonta\uc2 , our attorney general, has signaled will change is that we are changing from a may issue to a must issue or shall issue. But he says that it's OK for the sheriff or the police department to still ask you, what is your reason, what is your good cause for having a weapon?\par \par \uc2 They are also requiring that we go through firearms training at our own expense, and that is part of the statute. So that is another barrier to entry for people of limited means. And then finally, of course, they can ask for this good moral character which, as we know, is extremely subjective and can let the government allow us -- prevent us from getting these permits simply because of things we say on social media or our opinions, or let's say we didn't agree with the outcome of the 2020 election.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: I'm going to read something, Harmeet. Yes, let me read something, because this is what the A.G. cited as an example of successful good cause requirements, writing "The Riverside County Sheriff's Department policy, for example, currently provides as follows -- respect for the rights of others, absence of hatred and racism, fiscal stability, profession specific criteria." Harmeet, now, how does that, most of that, relate to your right to keep and bear arms and certainly abide by the new court decision?\par \par \uc2 DHILLON: It doesn't, Laura. It's flatly unconstitutional. I represent the National Association for Gun Rights at my firm. We are currently challenging San Jose's tax on gun ownership, and we are going to be challenging this as well if they stop -- if they stopped citizens from getting their rightful right to the concealed carry in California.\par \par \uc2 And why should I have to jump through hoops and go to an extensive check of my social media and my opinions to exercise my constitutional right? I don't have to do that for the First Amendment or to buy a house. It shouldn't be harder to get your constitutional rights here than it is to, say, get a job or exercise any other normal things that we do in society. So the attorney general is looking for litigation. He knows he is going to get in, and they're probably going to try to fund raise and virtue signal off of it. But this is not going to be the end of the litigation here.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Harmeet, so whether it is a Second Amendment issue or the Dobbs case, we are going to see endless litigation, right, on both sides. But the left on the Second Amendment, there is an enumerated right, right? But they want to deny that. But on the abortion question, there is no right to abortion. There is not right to, quote, privacy in the document of the Constitution. But they want to read a right into the Constitution that doesn't exist. So they have no consistent philosophy, judicial philosophy at all. It's just completely emotive at this point.\par \par \uc2 DHILLON: Absolutely, Laura. Look, even in the wake of prior decisions you have the state of California trying to tax weapon owners. That is unconstitutional and we are fighting that right now. So I think we are not going to see the Dobbs decision be respected by certain states. We are not going to see this New York rifle and pistol decision. We are looking for a lot more litigation here. It is really unfortunate that we have to continue to fight even after the Supreme Court has spoken. But ultimately, Laura, the problem here is the Supreme Court should never be in the position of making these laws in the first place. So it has to be left to the legislature to do this.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Harmeet, great to see you, as always.\par \par \uc2 And up next, it is being called the future of travel, but would you try it? The video, moments away.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: A Yemeni engineer has created a CGI example of what he says what the future of travel looks like. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Introducing Sky Cruise, a nuclear power hotel suspended above the clouds. It features shopping malls, sports centers, swimming pools, restaurants, bars, playgrounds for children, theaters, and cinemas. Sky Cruise's wedding halls give you and your partner, along with your guests, a unique, memorable experience.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: The good thing is you get to meet George Jetson, too. It's really fun. Would you try it? It seems a little creept.\par \par \uc2 That's it for us tonight. Remember, get your USA made Freedom Matters gear at LauraIngraham.com. The proceeds go to, of course, the Honor Flight Network this month. Good stuff.\par \par \uc2 Greg Gutfeld is next.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document INGANG0020220628ei6r00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Left And Right Rampage By Women Are Seen All Across The Country After The Supreme Court Nailing The Decision To Overturn Roe v. Wade; The Associated Press Found That More Than A Million Democrats Have Switched To Republican Party Last Year, And It Could Be A Sign Of A Coming Red Wave This November; Crime Rate In Big Cities Have Shown An Alarming Rise, But L.A. County District Attorney George Gascon Says That He Knows How Things Work Out; Radical D.A. Defends Soft-On-Crime Record; Green Energy Push: Biden Eyes Shift As Solution To Gas Price Pain; Judge Jeanine Hosts THE FIVE Summer Party\b0\par\par\uc2 Jesse Watters, Jeanine Pirro, Harold Ford, Jr., Dana Perino, Greg  Gutfeld \par \uc2 8,398 \uc2 words\par \uc2 27 June 2022\par \uc2 Fox News: The Five\par \uc2 FOXFIV\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 CHARLES PAYNE, FOX BUSINESS HOST: Want to bring you back as soon as possible. Obviously, this is on the nation's mind right now. Thank you both very much.\par \par \uc2 And that will do it for me. Neil will be back tomorrow. Catch him on the Fox Business Network at 2 p.m., Making Money. We're trying hard to do that. Here's THE FIVE.\par \par \uc2 JESSE WATTERS, FOX NEWS HOST: Hello, everybody. I'm Jesse Watters along with Judge Jeanine Pirro, \uc2 Harold Ford\uc2 , Jr., Dana Perino, and Greg Gutfeld. It's 5 o'clock in New York City, and this is THE FIVE.\par \par \uc2 The radical left rampaging across the country over Roe versus Wade, while Democrats attack the Supreme Court's legitimacy. The \uc2 FBI\uc2  is investigating after a pro-abortion extremist torched a pro-life Colorado pregnancy center, leaving behind graffiti that says "if abortions aren't safe, neither are you.\par \par \uc2 In Los Angeles, a man is facing possible attempted charges after attacking a cop with a makeshift flame thrower during a protest over there. And total chaos in Phoenix where Arizona lawmakers say they were held hostage by an angry crowd outside the state capitol. Police eventually had to use tear gas to clear them out. Democrats in Congress echoing the fury by pushing for drastic measures against the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHUCK TODD, HOST, NBC NEWS: Do you think lying at a confirmation hearing is an impeachable offense?\par \par \uc2 REP. ALEXANDRIA OCASIO-CORTEZ (D-NY): I believe so.\par \par \uc2 TODD: OK.\par \par \uc2 OCASIO-CORTEZ: I believe so. I believe lying under oath is an impeachable offense.\par \par \uc2 SEN. ELIZABETH WARREN (D-MA): This court has lost legitimacy. I believe we need to get confidence back in our court. And that means we need more justices on the \uc2 United States Supreme Court\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: And while Democrats pushed the White House to act, the best they're going to get is the Kamala photo opp. The vice president is getting brutally mocked by liberals for this photo she posted of herself just watching the ruling.\par \par \uc2 All right, Dana, you've had a weekend to kind of take it all in.\par \par \uc2 DANA PERINO, FOX NEWS CO-HOST: Yes.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: What do you think here on a Monday?\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Well, it's interesting, I just want to add one other thing to the violence over the weekend. So in Vermont, there was actually attack on the state capitol, vandalism, graffiti. The thing is Vermont, in just 2019, passed a law so that in the state of Vermont, there's a codified right in the state Constitution for a woman to have the right to choose, and the right to have an abortion.\par \par \uc2 So, I don't know what their -- why they're attacking the state capitol in Vermont. That doesn't make any sense. But that also I think over the weekend not only did we think about it, but I think it's hard to tell exactly how this is going to play out in the long run, emotions running super high, especially on the left.\par \par \uc2 I am a little surprised that the pro-choice forces seem a little unclear as to what they're going to do next. They had ample warning. The draft opinion had been out there for about 55 days. There are state court, state legislatures that are going to be taking up this issue. There are lawsuits already happening.\par \par \uc2 But also, as people start to realize that, in your -- as these decisions go back to the states, if you are in x, y, z state, nothing changes for you. Now, is it possible that liberal women in New York are going to be really upset about the women losing the right to choose in a state like Mississippi? It's possible. Will that motivate them to vote? Yes. But they vote in New York.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: So, I don't know practically how that's going to turn out. I do think that this will help the Democrats a least a little with suburban women --\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: And fundraising.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: -- and certainly fundraising. This does not help Republicans in the fundraising front. As I did a little research in that today. That, you know, inflation is starting to take a bite out of the small dollar fundraising on both sides, but really on the right. But this issue for Democrats, they'll raise a ton of money from celebrities.\par \par \uc2 But if you think back in March of 2021, the Democrats -- I'm sorry, that Texas passed their abortion law. And remember, there was all the conundrum, there was a huge fuss. But national polling on abortion didn't change at all.\par \par \uc2 So, again, I'm open-minded in looking at it. And I'll just say one last thing. One of the reasons \uc2 Ruth Bader Ginsburg\uc2  was very frustrated with the Roe v. Wade decision is that up until then, in 1972, the state legislatures were moving forward to try to do what Vermont just did. What Vermont in 2019 said, OK, in the state legislature, in the state Constitution women right to abortion is there.\par \par \uc2 That all stopped. All that activity in the state really stopped when they came up with the idea that there was this right to privacy in the constitution. Now that's over and the fight will continue. Hopefully more peacefully than what we saw over the weekend.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Greg, were this mostly peacefully protest over the weekend?\par \par \uc2 GREG GUTFELD, FOX NEWS CO-HOST: I was talking to Dana about this just a moment ago. Maybe it's me after three years of lawlessness and just seeing crap every day that I thought this was much less than a night of rage. And that could just me on my own subjective opinion comparing -- and I could be definitely wrong, because I'm not there.\par \par \uc2 But I guess the visual spectacle of this was not what I think the media wished it could be. I think that the reason why they were so playing down the stuff going on in front of the Supreme Court justices' homes is hoping that intimidation and the threat of a mob, of an angry vengeful mob would somehow change things and it didn't.\par \par \uc2 I think, too, it's interesting a lot of the protests are in cities that aren't affected by it.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: And so I think what you're going to see is just a lot of kind of like this. There's a lot of ignorance going around. A lot of people don't know what this means. I love hearing, you know, famous liberals renouncing their citizenship. Well, good luck finding a country that has abortion on demand like the United States.\par \par \uc2 If you look at any map of Europe, you're going to find out -- you're going to find out that most countries in western Europe and elsewhere are, like, pretty, pretty much more strict. Then there's this complaint about, you know, Republicans want you to be born but they don't want to -- they don't want to care about you when you're born.\par \par \uc2 This is what the lady from The View said. But the evidence right now is totally in reverse. Who's outspoken on crime, right? Who's outspoken on black-on-black crime? Who's outspoken on the price of gas and the price of baby formula, the economy? Who's talking about school choice? Who's worried about kids being trapped in terrible schools, the lockdown economy. It wasn't Democrats or teachers unions. It was the Republicans.\par \par \uc2 So, if you want to talk about responsibility of people who are born, Republicans have been there all along, it's just you just didn't -- you didn't like the grim truth of what you were protecting.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: When you heard some of the politicians out there on the shows talking about packing the court, and impeaching the justices, do you think that's going anywhere?\par \par \uc2 JEANINE PIRRO, FOX NEWS CO-HOST: No. Well, first of all, there is this -- this miscalculation. People don't understand that what happened with the Dobbs decision was that the court said that there is no constitutional right to an abortion. They never said abortions are illegal. They never said that they are unconstitutional. They just said you don't have a constitutional right to them.\par \par \uc2 So, all you folks that talk about, you know, all of this stuff is tearing down democracy and we've got to protect democracy, well, why don't you learn a little about democracy, get together, and now fight for it in the state legislatures where you will make a difference as to whether or not abortions will be legal or not. That is the essence of democracy.\par \par \uc2 The idea of saying that these justices, as AOC said, you know, lied -- I think one of the complaints had to do with the personal discussion in one of the senators' offices. First of all, this is the same thing that all liberal justices have said, under article III of the Constitution, they are to decide cases and controversies. You were not when you appear before a Senate committee deciding a case or a controversy.\par \par \uc2 You are going to say, I will decide this case based upon the facts and law as presented to me. I will not prejudge any situation, because then you're going to vote for me. And I understand, I did the same thing as a judge. People said to me, how do you feel about this crime or that crime or the -- well, no.\par \par \uc2 It's -- I will do my job as best I can to substantiate those crimes and those laws that have to be protected as a judge. Now, none of these justices said that precedent could not be overturned. None of them said that. There was no lie. And the essence of where we are now is that they're just bad -- they don't like the result, so they want to change the rules of the game.\par \par \uc2 And so, they want to pack the court, which is ridiculous, because then the court is nothing other than a legislative branch that reflects the rule of the mob. The left doesn't know how absurd they look. They are fighting, they are having these hissy fits. Every time they don't like something, they hit the streets they become violent, the summer of 2020. It's just they're losing credibility as opposed to complaining about the Supreme Court losing legitimacy.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Take it home, \uc2 Harold Ford\uc2 , Jr.\par \par \uc2 HAROLD FORD\uc2 , JR., FOX NEWS CO-HOST: So, the violence, I never understood why people engage in violence, too. And it goes on both sides. There are people who blew up abortion clinics, or places over the years, where abortions were performed. Then now you had people are out doing this. I don't know how you're going to make a difference engaging in violence. I think peaceful protesting is fine, but the violence is inexcusable and they should be prosecuted.\par \par \uc2 When it comes to the polling, one of the things that disturbs me in all of this is 20 -- right after this decision, \uc2 SCOTUS\uc2 's approval rating dropped to 25 percent. Now on the legislative and executive branch you expect those numbers are flexible. That's a low number even for president Biden. That's a low number. For congress, that's a low number.\par \par \uc2 But at least you have elections every two years for the Congress, four years for the president, obviously six years for the Senate.\par \par \uc2 Judge, you talked about being a judge and some of the things that -- you talked so many of our judges in this country are elected. So when they say they're going to do certain things, and they don't do them, voters are able to go and un-elect them.\par \par \uc2 I'm with you. I don't think we should pack the court, but I got to tell you, the majority of the country agrees with you as well, it will be interesting to see where that goes.\par \par \uc2 In addition, I think that the larger question for me, and Jesse, I think I've heard you say something on the show about it. I think when we look at 15 to 20 weeks for a woman to be able to get an abortion, I think there's a wide acceptance of that. I think when President Clinton said rare, legal and safe, unfortunately, that middle of the road position on this issue is largely unrepresented in politics.\par \par \uc2 You have those who don't want it at all and you have those who want it on demand. I think the question that we will get back to from an electoral standpoint will be that. Now, what alarms Democrats, I think, an