\uc2 G7\uc2  Leaders Addresses Global Food And Fuel Shortages; Lawmakers Target Justices Who Said Ruling Was Precedent; Attorney General Takes Measures To Allow Interstate Mailing Of Abortion Drug Mifepristone In Wake Of Supreme Court Overturning Roe Versus Wade; Arizona Republican Gubernatorial Candidate Kari Lake Interviewed On Her Views On 2020 Election And Her Criticism Of Drag Queens; Analyst Examine Possible Effects Of Supreme Court Overturning Roe Versus Wade On Midterm Elections\b0\par\par\uc2 Bret Baier, Matt Finn, Shannon Bream, Aishah Hasnie, Peter Doocy,  Brit Hume, Gillian Turner \par \uc2 8,079 \uc2 words\par \uc2 27 June 2022\par \uc2 Fox News: Special Report with Brett Baier\par \uc2 FNSPBB\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 HAROLD FORD J.R., FOX NEWS CHANNEL POLITICAL CONTRIBUTOR (on camera): Saint Jude Children's Research Hospital celebrated its 60 year anniversary in February.\par \par \uc2 FORD (voice-over): But the anniversary was just last week in Memphis on Friday.\par \par \uc2 Congratulations. Thank you, Danny Thomas said no child should die in the dawn of life. They've helped reduce childhood survived -- cancer by between 80 percent. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 JEANINE PIRRO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST (on camera): Danny Thomas is Lebanese.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 JESSE WATTERS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST (on camera): Thank you, wonderful. That's it for us. Go Lebanon. "SPECIAL REPORT" is up next with Bret.\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL ANCHOR (on camera): All right. Jesse, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Good evening. Welcome to Washington. I'm Bret Baier." BREAKING TONIGHT", a passenger train traveling from Los Angeles to Chicago, derailed today in Missouri.\par \par \uc2 Fox News digital is reporting there are fatalities. We're just getting word. Right now about a press conference that may be coming up soon, which will bring you to live. There are multiple injuries. We're getting details this afternoon.\par \par \uc2 This took place in a derailment hitting a dump truck. An \uc2 Amtrak\uc2  train.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent Matt Finn is following the breaking news tonight. Good evening, Matt.\par \par \uc2 MATT FINN, FOX NEWS CHANNEL NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Good evening, Bret. Fox News digital has confirmed there are deaths from that train track -- train crash today, but the number is unknown right now.\par \par \uc2 Amtrak\uc2  says, this afternoon, the train going from Chicago to Los Angeles was derailed when it hit a dump truck that was blocking a public crossing near Mendon, Missouri.\par \par \uc2 FINN (voice-over): \uc2 Amtrak\uc2  says the train was carrying approximately 243 passengers and 12 crew members. In addition to deaths, our Fox affiliate in Kansas City is reporting, at least, 50 people are injured. Video and picture appear to show the horrifying chaos after the crash. Some of the train cars flipped on their side, passengers crawling out of them.\par \par \uc2 Amtrak\uc2  says its incident response team has been activated. A new video also shows ambulances and helicopters arriving to a University of Missouri Hospital in Colombia.\par \par \uc2 That hospital confirming to affiliate \uc2 KMIZ\uc2  that they are expecting at least three patients from this derailment.\par \par \uc2 Mendon, Missouri is in a very rural North-Central part of the state, roughly halfway between Kansas City and St. Louis.\par \par \uc2 FINN (on camera): And the Missouri Highway Patrol has been identified as the lead agency investigating this accident. They've announced they'll give us an update on the latest in this train derailment expected at any moment, around 5:00 p.m. Central, 6:00 p.m. Eastern. Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes. We should point out, The Kansas City Star says there are eight helicopters taking patients from the derailed train. There was a lot of effort to get people out of there. That were stuck inside this afternoon. So, we hope to get more from authorities shortly.\par \par \uc2 Matt Finn, thank you.\par \par \uc2 FINN: Sure.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Also "BREAKING TONIGHT", another major ruling for conservatives from the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  justices decide in favor of a high school football coach who was disciplined for leading a prayer on the field after games.\par \par \uc2 The court says the prayer is protected by the First Amendment. Critics are decrying the decision as another example of an extremist court, they say, out of touch with the country. This comes on the heels of Friday's historic overturning of Roe v. Wade abortion ruling nearly 50 years ago as the Dobbs case.\par \par \uc2 Fox News chief legal correspondent, anchor of "FOX NEWS @ NIGHT", outside the Supreme Court again, Shannon Bream has details. Good evening, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 SHANNON BREAM, FOX NEWS CHANNEL ANCHOR (on camera): Good evening, Bret.\par \par \uc2 Well, this decision brings to an end. It's in favor of the coach after his seven-year battle.\par \par \uc2 He is trying to get the right to be both a public employee and to openly express his faith in that context. It's a big one for him, but the dissenters to say -- today say that it's actually no victory for religious liberty.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOSEPH KENNEDY, FORMER HIGH SCHOOL FOOTBALL COACH: It doesn't matter what your faith is, or if you have no faith. It just proves that, you know, this is America and the First Amendment applies --\par \par \uc2 BREAM (voice-over): By a six to three vote. The Supreme Court landed firmly in favor of a former high school football coach, who clashed with his school district after refusing to stop his postgame prayers on the field.\par \par \uc2 Writing for the majority, Justice Gorsuch summed it up this way, "A government entities sought to punish an individual for engaging in a brief, quiet, personal religious observance doubly protected by the Free Exercise and Free Speech Clauses of the First Amendment. And the only meaningful justification the government offered for its reprisal rested on a mistaken view that it had a duty to ferret out and suppress religious observances even as it allows comparable secular speech. The Constitution neither mandates nor tolerates that kind of discrimination."\par \par \uc2 The descent, written by Justice Sotomayor and joined by justices Breyer and Kagan argues the majority's decision will "Force states to entangle themselves with religion."\par \par \uc2 Writing, "Today's decision is particularly misguided because it elevates the religious rights of a school official, who voluntarily accepted public employment and the limits that public employment entails over those of his students, who are required to attend school that who this Court has long recognized are particularly vulnerable and deserving of protection."\par \par \uc2 The legal team representing the school district, a group that advocates for separation of church and state is now warning about where it fears the majority's opinion leads next.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL LASER, CHURCH-STATE SEPARATION ADVOCATE: It opens the door to coercive prayer in K through 12 classrooms across the country. Because of the Court buys into the myth that when a public official working for a public school, who's praying in a demonstrative matter that pressures kids to join.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: As for what Kennedy is hoping for seven years after this legal fight started --\par \par \uc2 KENNEDY: Soon as a -- the school district says a comeback I am there absolutely. First flight.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM (on camera): And the term is not over just yet. We'll get opinions again on Wednesday morning at 10:00 a.m. Eastern Time. And the court has not indicated whether or not that's going to be the final day of the term. So, we're standing by on that front.\par \par \uc2 But there are still four outstanding cases, including one on immigration and one on just how far the \uc2 EPA\uc2 's power does or doesn't reach in a regulatory scheme. Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: This is the never ending term, Shannon. It's never ending.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: You're correct.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Back to the ruling on Dobbs, overturning Roe on Friday, several states had so-called trigger laws in place. If that happened, then, outlawing abortion.\par \par \uc2 A judge in Louisiana has already blocked some of those. You have a number of challenges in the states. What's the latest?\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So, Bret, literally by the minute, we're getting word about new challenges. Let's start with Louisiana. A judge there has blocked those abortion laws, those trigger laws that kicked in. The abortion providers and their advocates who are challenging those laws say they're constitutionally vague.\par \par \uc2 No, that's referring to the state constitution in these cases. So, that's been put on hold, those trigger bands there until July 8h. There's going to be hearing then, so abortion remains legal in Louisiana for now.\par \par \uc2 There have been filings in Kentucky, Florida, Utah, Idaho, Texas, Mississippi, many other spots. And for now, we got word just coming in that there has been a temporary block on some of the laws, in Utah as well. We'll dig on that. But this is exactly where we expected the fight to go. It's now back at the state level where the Supreme Court sent it.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: This is the battle going forward. Shannon Bream outside the Supreme Court. Shannon, thanks. We'll see you tonight.\par \par \uc2 There were several instances of violence during the protests over the weekend, tied to that ruling, in Colorado, a Christian crisis pregnancy center was vandalized and burned early Saturday morning in Portland.\par \par \uc2 Protesters damaged property, broke windows, scrolled graffiti there on several buildings. Los Angeles police arrested a man accused of attacking an officer with a torch and police in Phoenix had to use tear gas to disperse a crowd of pro-choice supporters protesting outside the state capitol.\par \par \uc2 As you can imagine there is strong reaction to the abortion decision on Capitol Hill, it's continuing now. Democrats are calling for everything from court packing to impeachment of conservative justices.\par \par \uc2 They're hoping to capitalize on the emotion in November, saying everything's on the ballot. Congressional correspondent Aishah Hasnie shows us where things stand tonight.\par \par \uc2 AISHAH HASNIE, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT:\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. ELIZABETH WARREN (D-MA): This Court has lost legitimacy. They just took the last of it and set a torch to it.\par \par \uc2 AISHAH HASNIE, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (voice-over): Emotions are running high as Democrats grapple with last week's Supreme Court ruling on abortion. Democrats are already facing headwinds in November to keep their slim majority. But the plan, according to House leadership is to rally voters in November and gain a larger majority.\par \par \uc2 If they can do that Democrats want to pass laws increasing access to abortions. And change Senate rules that have long allowed the minority to block legislation.\par \par \uc2 REP. PRAMILA JAYAPAL (D-WA): We know that the Senate is broken. You've heard me say multiple times that we need to have get rid of the Jim Crow filibuster.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: Others are targeting the justices, specifically those who told lawmakers Roe was a precedent of the court.\par \par \uc2 CHUCK TODD, HOST, MSNBC 