utright, or allow it all through the entire pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 My guess is we'll get compromises nearly everywhere. And abortion rights will still be widely available in this country, even in some states that now ban it altogether.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes, it's interesting, and we've heard the Arkansas governor said that that's a possibility or he wishes it to be.\par \par \uc2 The Associated Press has more than one million voters across 43 states have switched to the Republican Party over the last year according to voter registration data analyzed by the AP. But nowhere is the shift more pronounced and dangerous for Democrats than the suburbs were well educated swing voters who turned against Trump's Republican Party in recent years appear to be swinging back.\par \par \uc2 This was done before the Roe versus Wade overturn. I'm just wondering how you feel that dynamic adjusts or changes before November?\par \par \uc2 HUME: Well, the political gravity and momentum in this upcoming midterm favors the Republican Party very strongly, but make no mistake about it. This decision on abortion rights is going to animate Democrats and motivate them.\par \par \uc2 Now, whether they will choose to exercise that by voting for Democrats in federal elections House and Senate, instead of state elections where they would probably make more difference remains to be seen.\par \par \uc2 We're also Bret, sitting at the moment and probably the greatest impact from that decision politically. As time goes on and people see that it doesn't ban abortion everywhere, that it's -- that in fact, it doesn't ban -- decision doesn't ban abortion at all. It just leaves the matter of fact -- matter of fact back to the states.\par \par \uc2 My guess is the impact will subside, but in the near term, for sure, it will -- it'll help the Democrats and it may continue to help them through the midterm but I doubt it will. In the end, it will take precedent over such issues as inflation, gasoline prices and all that go with that.\par \par \uc2 Plus, the stain on President Biden that started with withdrawal from Afghanistan and has yet to be erased. His approval rating show that and that's a huge factor in the midterm voting.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Brit Hume, as always, thank you.\par \par \uc2 HUME: Thank you, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Authorities in California say they recovered 150,000 fentanyl pills during a traffic stop Friday night. That could kill tens of millions of people. They say that contained enough lethal doses to potentially kill millions of people as I mentioned.\par \par \uc2 The street value of those drugs is estimated at $750,000. Two men were arrested on charges of possessing, transporting and selling illegal drugs. Jail records indicate they are no longer in custody.\par \par \uc2 Up next, one of the leading Republican senators faces a stiff primary challenge. We'll take you live to Utah.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: The House committee investigating last year's Capitol riot is calling a surprise hearing to present what's called recently obtained evidence. Tomorrow's session comes after Congress left Washington for a two-week recess. Lawmakers on the panel said last week there would be no more hearings until July. The subject of tomorrow's session is so far unclear.\par \par \uc2 Also tonight, we're learning the \uc2 FBI\uc2  seized the phone of former Trump election attorney John Eastman last week. That's according to a new court filing. Eastman is suing the federal government over all of this.\par \par \uc2 Back to the issue of abortion. The Biden administration is responding to Friday's Roe versus Wade overturn by insisting it will do everything it can to preserve access to reproductive care. One of the focal points of that effort will involve birth control medication. Correspondent Gillian Turner has details.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Abortion pills are now widely available online and by mail in all 50 states.\par \par \uc2 GILLIAN TURNER, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: The share of American women seeking abortions via pill is on the rise across the nation. Mifepristone is an \uc2 FDA\uc2  approved hormone drug accounts for 54 percent of all abortions in the U.S. today. Within hours of Friday's Supreme Court ruling, the attorney general announced moves to protect access to this pill which terminates a pregnancy if taken within 70 days of conception. \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  writes in part "States may not ban mifepristone based on disagreement with the \uc2 FDA\uc2 's expert judgment about its safety and efficacy." Today the secretary of Health and Health Services is doubling down.\par \par \uc2 XAVIER BECERRA\uc2 , SECRETARY, HEALTH AND HUMAN SERVICES: What we will do at the federal level is make sure we continue to use our federal authorities to make sure women have access to the healthcare they need. So we're continue to make sure that something like medication abortion is made available to all women regardless of what state they're in.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Last December the \uc2 FDA\uc2  ended a requirement that mifepristone be dispensed in person at clinics in hospital, allowing doctors to prescribe the drug via telemedicine and mail it to patients, including over state lines. That move sparked pushback from state leaders and others who say the practice is wildly unsafe.\par \par \uc2 GOV. KRISTI NOEM, (R) SOUTH DAKOTA: Many times, they are doing it unsupervised. It's a medical procedure. And so I do believe that there should be a physician supervision in place when that is being conducted by any individual.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Pro-choice lawmakers including Senator Elizabeth Warren say the Dobbs ruling means access to abortion pills is more crucial than ever before.\par \par \uc2 SEN. ELIZABETH WARREN, (D-MA): It also means asking the president of the United States to make abortion as available as possible with the tools he has, including medication abortions.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Looking ahead, Bret, this fight over abortion pills moves to the states, a whole slate of them now considering following Louisiana's lead where the governor has clamped down on mail-order mifepristone, making it illegal to send the drug across state lines. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Gillian, thank you.\par \par \uc2 We're still five weeks away from the Arizona primary. It features and intense contest for the Republican gubernatorial nomination. Last week we spoke with candidate Karrin Taylor Robson. Tonight, we talk to GOP hopeful Kari Lake. Kari, thanks for being here.\par \par \uc2 KARI LAKE, (R) ARIZONA GUBERNATORIAL CANDIDATE: I'm so happy to be here. I am the frontrunner here in Arizona and have been since day one, and I'm endorsed by President Trump. And we're doing really well. Our main person that we are looking out for right now is the socialist on the Democrat side United States, \uc2 Katie Hobbs\uc2 . That's who we are focused on.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Let me talk about what we just saw, which was this abortion decision and what it means in the states. Arizona has this trigger law that's similar to Mississippi in that it would criminalize abortions after 15 weeks. But you have a couple of different cities, Phoenix and Tucson, that they say they do not want to move forward with this. They said they are going to push back. And I'm wondering if you're governor, what do you do about that?\par \par \uc2 LAKE: We're going to let the legislature pass the laws to protect the unborn. And we already have these on the books. And it's not surprising we have a socialist mayor running Tucson and one running Phoenix, and they are always trying to push back. We will protect the lives of the unborn in Arizona. And it's going to be very difficult for abortion clinics to survive and be open if abortion is illegal in Arizona.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: What about these abortion pills? Would you outlaw those or try to?\par \par \uc2 LAKE: I think it's really scary that a young, afraid mother could be at home taking a pill without medical supervision. It could be the end of the mother as well. So I would you very much support banning those types of abortion pills.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: What do you think the most important issue for your state is?\par \par \uc2 LAKE: We've got a couple of important issues. We have a wide open border. We have fentanyl pouring in. We have the cartels with operational control. But that wouldn't be happening if we would have fair, honest elections. And we had a fraudulent election, a corrupt election, and we have an illegitimate president sitting in the White House. And because of that, our border is wide open. Five-and-a-half tons of fentanyl was confiscated last year, enough to kill over 2 billion people. It's a weapon of mass destruction.\par \par \uc2 And that's why we are going to secure that border starting on day one. After I take my hand off the Bible, we are going to issue a declaration of invasion. We're going to finish President Trump's wall, and we're going to send our armed National Guard to the border and stop people from coming across. I have been down there so many times, and people just pour in. And they've got a smile on their face, because with \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , they feel they are going to stay here forever. And when I'm governor, they're going back.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Just to circle back, you say that it's an illegitimate president. You say that the election was stolen. You speak a lot about this on the campaign trail, talking about the 2020 election.\par \par \uc2 This is the Arizona House Speaker \uc2 Rusty Bowers\uc2  testifying in front of Congress last week.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUSTY BOWERS\uc2 , (R) ARIZONA STATE HOUSE SPEAKER: Anywhere, anyone, anytime has said that I said the election was rigid, that would not be true. There was no, no evidence being presented of any strength.