nt to uncover the truth about our election. And it makes me wonder if they are afraid they really didn't win in previous elections. But don't worry, because when I'm governor we're going to get to the bottom of it. And we will have honest elections and we will restore honesty.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And you think that Arizona citizens, voters want to spend a lot of time looking at 2020 instead of looking forward from 2022 and on?\par \par \uc2 LAKE: I'm with them every single day. We draw record crowds. Number one issue, because they know every other issue from inflation to the border being wide open, not being able to afford gas and groceries all stems from this corrupt election. We want an Election Day, not an election month. We want paper ballots. We want to get rid of these electronic voting machines, which is why I'm involved in a federal case right now to get them banned here in Arizona.\par \par \uc2 All we want, Bret, is honest elections for our children and grandchildren. We want honest elections for Democrats, independents, and Republicans. We want to know that when we go to bed on election night, when we wake up the next day, we find out who won before we go to bed and we can live with that. But we can't keep having elections that nobody can live with. And we can't have this level of fraud anymore.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Understanding that every Republican leader in Washington says that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is the legitimate president, every one. But I understand what you are working on.\par \par \uc2 LAKE: They are sitting here in Washington. They are sitting there in Washington. They are not here in Arizona.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: This is what you are running on. I get it.\par \par \uc2 LAKE: We just need some decent journalists to come out here and dig through this.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Let me ask you one thing. Obviously, as a candidate you come under scrutiny. "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " has a story today that says Arizona GOP candidate who criticized drag queens was once a fan according to a drag queen. This is the quote, "Arizona GOP gubernatorial candidate Kari Lake, who has attacked drag queens as dangerous to children, attended the shows of drag queen Richard Stevens for more than 20 years and once hired him to perform at her home." Do you care to address that?\par \par \uc2 LAKE: I do care. I actually do care to address that, and I'm really shocked. I'm actually appalled that FOX News would take a defamatory story like that, and we are pursuing legal action against this drag queen. I'm appalled that you would bring that up when you have not talked about our stolen election.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We just spent three questions, Ms. Lake talking about this.\par \par \uc2 LAKE: You haven't.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I just asked you a number of questions about it. I played the Arizona House Speaker.\par \par \uc2 LAKE: This is the first of it.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Let's address this story that's in "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 ." Every candidate takes tough stories.\par \par \uc2 LAKE: Wow.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I'm asking you to respond to, if you'd like to.\par \par \uc2 LAKE: I'm happy to address it. I'm happy to address it, but I'm really disappointed in FOX. I thought you were a little better than CNN.\par \par \uc2 This is a person who I covered for decades, for decades, 20 years. And he has never been in my home. He says he has been in my home for a drag show? That's ludicrous. He has never been in my home. He has lied. We tried serve him defamation papers. And he is so shady that we can't even track him down because he is not even welcome at the places that he worked.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I'm sorry, but this is the last question I'm going to ask. What about these pictures of you with him, Richard Stevens? And what about "The Post," "I performed for Kari's birthday, I performed in her home. I've performed for her in some of the seediest bars in Phoenix."\par \par \uc2 LAKE: That's not true.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I don't want to ask these questions. I asked you to address them. That's it.\par \par \uc2 LAKE: Actually, I think you do want to ask them, but you don't want to ask about 2,000 mules. I think you do want to ask about this. This is absolutely ludicrous. I'm talking about drag shows in schools. This is what triggered this man. Somebody who goes to a drag show with female impersonators is one thing. We don't want our tax money going into drag shows at school.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: OK, I understand you are making a difference there. But you are saying his allegations are wrong is what you are saying?\par \par \uc2 LAKE: They're false. Yes, I am.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: OK, all right.\par \par \uc2 LAKE: And I'm really appalled that you would spend time on a false story like that. It's shocking.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Well, I think it's important to have candidates address things that are coming up that might affect the race. And I thought you would appreciate that.\par \par \uc2 LAKE: True. But you didn't ask my opponent, the establishment opponent who was here last week, you didn't ask her about her votes to allow illegal alien students to get a lower tuition than American students. You didn't ask her about the 70-plus times that she raised tuition on American students. You didn't ask her any tough questions.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: That's not true.\par \par \uc2 LAKE: And here you have me on and you try to bring a defamatory story out. It's really sad. I thought there was --\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Ms. Lake, I really appreciate your time. We thought we would address all issues on the table. That is one of them being covered today. We thank you, and we'll cover the primary --\par \par \uc2 LAKE: Thank you. Please send reporters out to cover this corrupt election. We would appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the panel.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 AYANNA PRESSLEY\uc2 , (D-MA): This is a matter of life or death. The president must declare a public health emergency.\par \par \uc2 GOV. ASA HUTCHINSON, (R) ARKANSAS: This is not about contraception. This is not about same-sex marriage. A very limited decision on this particular issue of abortion.\par \par \uc2 SEN. ELIZABETH WARREN, (D-MA): I believe we need to get some confidence back in our court, and that means we need more justices on the \uc2 United States Supreme Court\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Well, some of the reaction to the Roe versus Wade overturning on Friday.\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in our panel, Guy Benson, political editor at Townhall.com, host of "The Guy Benson Show" on FOX News Radio, Susan Page, Washington Bureau Chief at "USA Today," and former White House press secretary \uc2 Ari Fleischer\uc2 . Guy, what you saw from the response from both sides in the wake of the ruling Friday and what it means politically.\par \par \uc2 GUY BENSON, POLITICAL EDITOR, TOWNHALL.COM: Well, I think it's too soon to tell exactly how this will play politically. I suspect that we might see a similar trajectory to what we saw about two months ago. Early May the leak came out. Everyone had these battles, I sort of called it a dress rehearsal for the real thing. And we saw a significant uptick in chatter on the issue. We saw a little bit of polling come out around that time. And then things subsided because the blackhole of inflation and gas prices is just sucking everything else in in this election cycle.\par \par \uc2 I would guess over the next few months that's probably going to remain the arc of this election. But look, this is a highly emotional issue. People on both sides feel very strongly about it. It's now out in the states, so there are going to be debates all over the country about it. And we are playing out and watching in real time this very aggressive conversation that we previewed a little over a month-and-a-half ago.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Susan?\par \par \uc2 SUSAN PAGE, WASHINGTON BUREAU CHIEF, "\uc2 USA TODAY\uc2 ": I think that this is a seismic decision that we are going to be dealing with for years to come. I think it's something that will really inflame a new culture war in the country.\par \par \uc2 But if you are asking does it affect what will happen in the November midterms, I'm not sure we know, because, as Guy just said, Americans have a lot on their plates. Americans are very concerned about the cost of gas, the cost of food, the availability of infant formula. And those are things that I think tend to be top of mind when people go to a poll. So I'm not sure about November, but we are going to be talking about this for a very long time to come.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: As you look at the map, we have these states that they have these triggered abortion laws after this ruling. Now, Ari, there are a number of them that have challenges, lawsuits in these states, and that's really the next battle that we will see.\par \par \uc2 ARI FLEISCHER\uc2 , FORMER WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: This is the way the process should work. And this should play out in the states, and the stats have different rules, and then there are different restrictions that can be added to this. But the fundamental issue is this was never a constitutional right to begin with. And like so many other issues that are not constitutional, there are multiple sides of them and they get adjudicated by the people's representatives and legislatures. And if there is appeal, if there's an objection, it goes through state courts. So we are going to see this.\par \par \uc2 And I think at the end of the day, Bret, you are not going to see a dramatic reduction of abortions in America because the blue states are going to keep on providing them. The red states have previously made it harder to get an abortion. There just weren't that many abortions in those states to begin with. So the states that are going to be triggered to stop abortion, it's not that great a reduction. So, at the end of the day, it's not going to change a whole lot on the ground.\par \par \uc2 But it's a good kay for the Constitution because you can't just invent a right and stick it in the Constitution because you feel like declaring one, which is what the court did 50 years ago.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Guy, there's another map we have, states where voters are switching to the GOP. This was an analysis by the Associated Press before the ruling on Friday. But for all the optimism the Republicans have, is there are cautiousness after this ruling that effects independents in the suburbs especially?\par \par \uc2 BENSON: Yes. And I think the Democrats' own extremism on the issue of abortion could cut against that in some of these races. But the big ticket issues that we talked about a moment ago in this election remain white hot. I think that's where voters are going to be mostly focused heading into November. And the president has a very poor approval rating. And most Americans believe we're on the wrong track by a large margin. Those are facts of the race right now.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes. And last thing, quickly, Susan. Most candidates are not focusing on 2020 as opposed to our last guest who we questioned about that. And they are not focused on January 6th. They are really focused on how they feel about the economy.\par \par \uc2 PAGE: Yes, and how they feel about the future of the country. And this is landscape of concern about the future of the country that favors Republicans. Does the abortion decision upset that? I think we'll have to wait and see.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: OK. Panel, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Up next, an update on our top story.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Updating our top story, three people killed, several others injured, we don't know how many, in this derailment of an \uc2 Amtrak\uc2  train this afternoon in Missouri. The train was traveling from Los Angeles to Chicago when it hit a dump truck and derailed. More later.\par \par \uc2 Thanks for inviting us into your home tonight. That's it for this SPECIAL REPORT, fair, balanced, and still unafraid. JESSE WATTERS PRIME TIME starts now. Hey, Jesse starts the week right now with five extra seconds. Jesse?\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FNSPBB0020220628ei6r00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Latest Rulings Highlight Impact Of Trump Judges; Ukraine: Russian Missile Hits Crowded Mall; Zelenskyy: Need Powerful Air Defense System; Report: Delta Pilots To Protest At Airports Thursday; Airline Staff Shortages Cause Travel Chaos; AP: GOP Gaining Suburban Voters Ahead Of Midterms; Impact Of SCOTUS Overturning Roe On Midterms; CA Sheriff: 150K Fentanyl Pills Hidden In Vehicle; 5-Month-Old Girl Shot And Killed In Chicago; Fox Analyst's Brother Shot And Killed In Chicago; MS AG Certifies "Trigger Law" After Roe Ruling\b0\par\par\uc2 Martha MacCallum, Marc Thiessen, Nate Foy, Lydia Hu, David Asman,  Juan Williams, Bill Melugin, Mike Tobin \par \uc2 8,998 \uc2 words\par \uc2 27 June 2022\par \uc2 Fox News Channel: The Story with Martha MacCallum\par \uc2 HUNDD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 JOHN ROBERTS\uc2 , FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: -- same people will say, Robert, you're crazy. You're the, you're the reason why the world is as it is, and shouldn't be --\par \par \uc2 SANDRA SMITH, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: I'll make a prediction. I wonder if they change the rules by then.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: I don't know. We'll see. COVID does seem to be sort of turning into something else, though.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Another big day, John. Thanks to everybody for joining us. I'm Sandra Smith.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: And I'm \uc2 John Roberts\uc2 . THE STORY with Martha starts right now.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA MACCALLUM, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Thank you very much, you, guys. Good afternoon, everybody. I'm Martha MacCallum and here's the story. During the Trump administration, Senate Majority Leader \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  vowed that he would leave a lasting conservative impression on the courts. He approved over 200 federal judges across this nation, many of which had been unfilled by the Obama White House before they left, also three Supreme Court justices in one term.\par \par \uc2 As a result, we're now watching the fruits of that inaction as these constitutional originalists do exactly what their past writings and decisions would indicate upholding the Second Amendment. And just a short time ago, in a huge win for religious liberty, the First Amendment. In a six to three court decision, they protected coach \uc2 Joe Kennedy\uc2 's right to take a knee in prayer on a public-school football field, writing that the government essentially cannot pick and choose which secular or religious viewpoints it chooses to promote or suppress.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE KENNEDY\uc2 , FORMER COACH WHO PRAYED ON FIELD: Everybody was keeping their eye on all of this to see what the rights are going to be, are they going to be taken away? And it just shows that, hey, it doesn't matter what your faith is, or if you have no faith. It just proves that, you know, this is America.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: And with the court's ruling on Roe, it has exploded into outrage, that now leading to claims that we continue to see over and over in the world today, that something or someone is simply illegitimate.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. ELIZABETH WARREN (D-MA): This Court has lost legitimacy. We need more justices on the \uc2 United States Supreme Court\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 CHUCK TODD, MSNBC HOST: Thanks to how this court was put together, its credibility has never been more in question.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, if you don't like the outcome, it is illegitimate, as you heard chanted there. Let's bring in Marc Thiessen, AES Scholar and Fox News Contributor; and Democratic Strategist Richard Goodstein. Gentlemen, good to have both of you with us. So, these judges were nominated by duly affected officials, so people vote for the representatives, then those representatives approve the members of the court. That is the way that it works. And if we lose the ability to do that, Richard, where are we as a nation?\par \par \uc2 RICHARD GOODSTEIN, DEMOCRATIC STRATEGIST: Here's what people like Elizabeth Warren mean when they say these justices are illegitimate. Five of the justices on this majority were chosen by presidents who got fewer votes than their opponent --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: That's not how the system works, Richard.\par \par \uc2 GOODSTEIN: And the senators that voted, and the Senators had voted for them represented, represented millions of voters, fewer. OK. Now, the good thing is oddly, for Democrats, it's a horrible outcome for women and people who love them. But for Democrats, we've already seen in the polls, that whereas back in April, and in the generic congressional ballot, Republicans were up three points. Now, today, Democrats are up seven. And every poll shows women independents, and the public at large, wildly -- you can laugh Marc, you always smirk, it's great, I love it. I can bet on it.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: No, Richard. You know what, Richard, here's the thing --\par \par \uc2 GOODSTEIN: But I'm right. And you're way off. Yes.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: No, but what you're saying right now, is that because of what this court has done, that there will be an electoral backlash. So, you're saying the people should be able to have an impact on who makes these decisions, which is precisely what we're witnessing right now. People voted, they leaned towards elected representatives, who, who then put in place conservative originalist, people who believe that the Constitution is not a fluid document, that it was a an actually profound document and that it should be adhered to.\par \par \uc2 So, this is what the American public has put in place by elected officials. Marc, I want to let you get in here and weigh in.\par \par \uc2 MARC THIESSEN, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Yes, sure. So, first of all, Richard seems to think that Americans care more about abortion than they care about $5.00 gas and the worst inflation in 40 years. I don't think that's going to, that's going to work for him.\par \par \uc2 GOODSTEIN: Let's talk about that, Marc. Guess what --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Richard, Richard, let him talk. I'll come back to you, I promise.\par \par \uc2 THIESSEN: Richard, let me speak. Let me speak.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Go ahead. Marc, go ahead.\par \par \uc2 THIESSEN: What, what Richard and the folks on the left don't like as the outcomes is that they, what they don't like is the fact that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is the first Republican President in 60 years, who appointed, has a perfect record when it comes to appointing judicial conservatives on the bench. Every president going back to Richard Nixon, who nominated the, the Justice who wrote the road decision has at least had at least half of their nominees' defect to the liberal side or become swing votes that vote against.\par \par \uc2 Liberal judges never vote against the left wing. They never joined the conservative bloc on a major case. And what happened here is \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  had a perfect record, he appointed three judicial conservative's originalist conservatives who have voted exactly the way they're they their judicial philosophy guides them. And they don't like the fact that he was that successful, and that's why they're upset.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, I just want to play this. This is \uc2 Alexandria Ocasio Cortez\uc2  on "Meet the Press," let's watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. ALEXANDRIA OCASIO-CORTEZ (D-NY): There must be consequences for such a deeply destabilizing action and hostile takeover of our democratic institutions.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Do you think lying in a confirmation hearing is an impeachable offense?\par \par \uc2 CORTEZ: I believe so.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Lying in a nomination process. You know, when I look back, and I know obviously we know what she's referring to. She's talking about \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2  and now Justice Gorsuch, and their adherence during those nomination hearings to the principle of saying, I do accept that this is a precedent. A precedent is something that, you know, we, is in the law has been around a long time. Neither one of them ever said that a precedent could never be overturned, based on the case that comes before them, Richard, and in fact, \uc2 Ruth Bader Ginsburg\uc2  said the exact same thing when she was in the nomination process, watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUTH BADER GINSBURG, FORMER SUPREME COURT JUSTICE: It would be wrong for me to say, or to preview in this legislative chamber, how I would cast my vote on questions the Supreme Court may be called upon to decide.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: And I would add that Justice Sotomayor did the same thing when it, when she was pressed on the Second Amendment. She said, "Yes, it is a precedent, essentially, but I can't really say what I would do based on an individual case that might come before me." So, explain to me again, how these people lied in their nomination process, Richard.\par \par \uc2 GOODSTEIN: Right. Again, just one thing on the illegitimacy. It was withholding Obama from getting a justice in 2016, and ramming through one at the end of 2020. That's what people referring to look. It's that Susan Collins and Joe Manchin feel deceived in the meetings that they had in their personal offices with \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2 . So, you're -- my, your beef is not with me. It's with them. They are the ones who put out the statements that they feel that they were deceived, whether it's impeachable not, I don't think I want to go there.\par \par \uc2 I'm just saying that's the beef, and it's bipartisan, right. Collins and, and Manchin both have said the exact same thing. And the problem is, yes, abortion is not mentioned in the Constitution, either is same sex marriage, either. You know, there are any number of things either is the marriage between a white person of one race and one of another. Those things are not mentioned in the Supreme Court, either. That's what has people upset that those things are at risk to things not specifically mentioned in the Constitution or not accepted --\par \par \uc2 THIESSEN: Those are not.\par \par \uc2 MACALLUM: It's not that these right disappear, because the whole point of this is not a judgement on abortion. The point on this is that it should be the states and the electoral process for people to decide how they want these things to be governed in their state. Marc, but please, you know, address what Richard brings up here about these other issues\par \par \uc2 THIESSEN: Well, first of all, both Judges Kavanaugh and Alito took those off the table in their, in their opinion. So, there's no votes for doing what Richard is saying. But second, a second of all --\par \par \uc2 GOODSTEIN: Not yet.\par \par \uc2 THIESSEN: -- during her confirmation hearings, during her confirmation appearance, Elena Kagan was asked point blank by Senator \uc2 John Cornyn\uc2 , is there a constitutional right to same sex marriage? And she said and I quote, "There is no federal constitutional right to same sex marriage. She then went on to vote in Obergefell to create a federal constitutional right to same sex marriage. So, did she lie?\par \par \uc2 You know, and another thing is that if, if \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2  or Judge Gorsuch told a senator how they would vote in a case before hearing the facts and evidence in exchange for their vote, that would be an impeachable offense, violation of judicial ethics to say that.\par \par \uc2 GOODSTEIN: That's what we're talking about.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Absolutely. And Senator Collins has said that he did not ever say anything, during the course of their meetings, that is not what her -- feeling is not about any sort of promise about how he would, he would, how he would decide in any one of these cases. But I want to ask --\par \par \uc2 THIESSEN: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: -- bring up Marc's point before, you know, as we go here, Richard, so did Justice Kagan lie in her nomination process when, when she said that she would, that the right to same sex marriage is not in the Constitution, and then she decided to have an opinion in favor of it at the federal level that she lie then?\par \par \uc2 GOODSTEIN: Of course not, because as of that moment, there was no such right. But again, what I think you're trying to skirt around is how this is going to be -- Marc, smirk away. This is a disaster for Republicans because the public is wildly against it. People have gotten very energized. The states, we've had people like \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  saying no exceptions whatsoever; \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  is pushing for a total ban and a grand total of 10 percent of the public. Believe them.\par \par \uc2 OK, that's where they 10 percent of the public supports that idea. And two- thirds or more says, no, there should be abortion allowed, in most circumstances, right? So, that's really, that's the problem that I think you're up against.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You know, I would just remind everyone that when you, that when you look back at, at the decision of Roe v. Wade, it talked about the first trimester, then after that it talks about leaving it up to the states in terms of what really happened.\par \par \uc2 GOODSTEIN: Agreed. Right.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, that is where you are now --\par \par \uc2 GOODSTEIN: Tell that to \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: And when you talk about \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 , and you talk about \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  --\par \par \uc2 GOODSTEIN: Tell that to \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: No, but that's what's going on. Those individual states are to have this debate, and the people will vote on them. That is what this process is, is about. It isn't about wanting there to be no abortion in the country. It's about the, the justices saying, you know what, we have jurisdiction over this, it's not in the Constitution.\par \par \uc2 THIESSEN: First of all, 66 percent of American --\par \par \uc2 GOODSTEIN: Women feel like a second-class citizen in these states --\par \par \uc2 THIESSEN: Richard, hold on, hold on. Wait, you just spoke. Let me speak. 66 percent of Americans believe that, that overturning Roe v. Wade would make abortion illegal in the United States, it doesn't. And the vast majority of Americans, 64 percent, support limiting all abortions in the country to 15 weeks. So, the democratic orthodoxy today that embraced by \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is a taxpayer-funded abortion on demand up until the moment of birth. I will put, I will put that up against anything in the Republican policy.\par \par \uc2 GOODSTEIN: Zero -- that's a lie. That is not true.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You know what, here's what I would say --\par \par \uc2 THIESSEN: That is \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's position.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: No, no, I would say that, that, that is the position of certain states, New Jersey included, and that's where --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 THIESSEN: New York. California.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I mean, I spoke to \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2  over the weekend, we're going to show it a little bit, she would not deny that she is against it going up to nine months. But this has become a state issue. That's what everyone needs to understand here. And Richard's 100 percent, right, it may hurt Republicans when it comes to election time. And that's the beauty of the system. We will see what people think when it comes to election time about the fact that this is now a state decision and not a federal decision. Gentlemen, I want to thank you both for being here. Richard Goodstein and Marc Thiessen, thank you.\par \par \uc2 GOODSTEIN: Bye, Martha.\par \par \uc2 THIESSEN: Thanks, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, coming up, we're going to talk to David Asman. We're also going to speak to, to Mercedes Schlapp about exactly that issue. You know, what's the impact on this electorally?\par \par \uc2 Also, we are live in Ukraine after Putin bombs a crowded shopping mall just a short time ago that is expected that several are dead that dozens are wounded. We'll take you there live on the ground when we come back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: A Short time ago, Russian forces reportedly launching long range missiles into a packed shopping mall in the central city of Kremenchuk, Ukraine. You can see the location on this map. We know that there's been a lot of activity in the eastern part of the country.\par \par \uc2 This is a little bit west of that area. Reports say that there is unimaginable carnage on this scene.\par \par \uc2 The strikes, a message of some sort to the \uc2 G7\uc2  leaders as they meet in Germany, and all together say that they are committed to helping Ukraine, "for as long as it takes."\par \par \uc2 Correspondent Nate Foy on the ground in Ukraine for us in Lviv. Hello, Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE FOY, FOX NEWS CHANNEL NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Hey, Martha. The video that's coming out today is one that is sure to give you chills.\par \par \uc2 Just devastating to watch. Ukrainian President \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskyy\uc2 , just mentioned it said over 1,000 people were in the shopping center in Kremenchuk when this missile came down this afternoon.\par \par \uc2 FOY (voice-over): The numbers that we have right now, Ukrainian authorities say at least 11 people have been killed, over 50 people have been hurt. But Ukrainian President \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskyy\uc2  says "it's impossible to even imagine the number of victims. And when you see this video, you're going to understand why.\par \par \uc2 Take a look at this video from Kremenchuk today. You see just a massive fire after those Russian missiles hit. Ukrainian officials say it took 115 firefighters to put this fire out. You may be wondering why Kremenchuk? Its home to Ukraine's largest oil refinery.\par \par \uc2 So, perhaps, that was the intended target of Russia. But it comes after a busy weekend, Martha. We had over 60 missile strikes in Ukraine this weekend. And that's why President Zelenskyy is now pushing for a stronger air defense system. Listen to this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VOLODYMYR ZELENSKYY, PRESIDENT OF UKRAINE (through translator): Some of the missiles were shut down, but only part of them. We need a powerful air defense system. Modern, full effective, the one can provide complete protection against these missiles. We talk about this with our partners on a daily basis.\par \par \uc2 FOY: Martha, Fox News can now confirm the U.S. plans to give Ukraine the same surface to air missile system used to protect Washington D.C.\par \par \uc2 But I mentioned the very busy weekend, unfortunately, here in Ukraine. Just after yesterday's missile strike in Kyiv that killed one person and injured six. Idaho Senator \uc2 Jim Risch\uc2  met with President Zelenskyy in Kyiv.\par \par \uc2 Senator Risch tells Fox News, he wanted to see how American military aid was being delivered, and he learned this. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. JAMES RISCH (R-ID): I am incredibly impressed with the -- with the way stuff is getting to the frontlines. We hear anecdotal stories that oh, the Russians blow up the stuff when it gets here. That is not the case. This stuff is coming through and getting to where it needs to be.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 FOY (on camera): Senator Rich tells Fox News, he is impressed with the competency and the training of Ukrainian troops, and believes that they will take full advantage of all military aid that they get including from the United States.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, President Zelenskyy telling \uc2 G7\uc2  leaders today that he wants the war to end by this winter, Martha, as conditions to fight are set to get worse. We'll send it back to you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Thank you, Nate. Nate Foy in Lviv, Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 So, if you've tried to get anywhere lately, you might be asking this question. What is going on? Why are the airlines suddenly incapable of doing their jobs selling you a plane ticket that can actually get you where you're going when you want to get there?\par \par \uc2 David Asman, here to explain next.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: We were scheduled to leave on a Thursday night. Flight kept getting canceled. Eventually, we were messing our connections, so we decided to book for Friday. There wasn't anything available without an overnight connection. So, we finally got a flight Saturday morning.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par 