IP)\par \par \uc2 HU (voice-over): Now, meanwhile, airlines are now blaming the \uc2 Federal Aviation Administration\uc2 . A letter written by a trade group called \uc2 Airlines for America\uc2  to Transportation Secretary \uc2 Pete Buttigieg\uc2 , says, air traffic control staffing challenges have led to "traffic restrictions under blue sky conditions."\par \par \uc2 The group also says an \uc2 FAA\uc2  facility in Jacksonville, Florida has been understaffed for 27 of the last 30 days. They say, it is crippling to the entire East Coast traffic flows.\par \par \uc2 But, Martha, the \uc2 FAA\uc2  is pushing back here. They are pointing to the pandemic relief, taxpayer dollars, given to the airline, saying, "After receiving $54 billion in pandemic relief to help save the airlines from mass layoffs and bankruptcy, the American people deserve to have their expectations met."\par \par \uc2 Martha, that being a reference to safe, affordable, and efficient travel. And now, the delays and cancellations calm as Americans return to the skies in record numbers, the \uc2 TSA\uc2  screened more than 2.4 million people yesterday. That's the highest volume we've seen since February of 2020.\par \par \uc2 HU (on camera): And now, as you mentioned, the AAA is predicting a record number of people to take to the skies over the July 4th holiday, more than 3-1/2 million people are expected to fly. And that comes as the cost of a ticket, it's absolutely skyrocketing. More than 37 percent over the last year.\par \par \uc2 So, we're paying more, we're travelling more, but it's causing a lot more headaches.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: It's a mess.\par \par \uc2 Lydia, thank you very much. Even that dog is upset. He is like, give me a break.\par \par \uc2 Thank you, Lydia.\par \par \uc2 Fox Business anchor, David Asman is here. My son was in Chicago last night, David, trying to get back to New York. Five hour kept getting, you know, updates on my phone, you know, delayed, delayed another hour. (INAUDIBLE) we'll let you know what's happening.\par \par \uc2 And you know, I think that as Americans, you expect a certain level of efficiency. You know, there's some countries you might be in in the world, you say like, oh my gosh, you know, well, the plane is delayed five hours.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 DAVID ASMAN, FOX BUSINESS NETWORK ANCHOR (on camera): Yes.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Who knows what's going to happen. But you don't expect that in this country. And I fear that we're becoming used to it. We're like, oh, well, that's just the way it is. You know?\par \par \uc2 ASMAN: Well, we are -- we are in many ways becoming third world in the way we operate.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ASMAN: And so much of it had to do with believing that the government could solve the problem by spending our money. First of all, government has to take role.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 FAA\uc2  is easy to spread criticism at the airlines, but they didn't monitor the spending of that money was, by the way, more than 54 billion. We spent 29 billion in grants to Airlines for payroll support, even though right now we have 28,000 fewer workers than we did in December 2019.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Where did the money go?\par \par \uc2 ASMAN: That's a great question. I mean, it went -- it went to keep workers there temporarily. But clearly, many of them left. And just said, the heck with it. I'm leaving this job, I don't need it. Maybe some of it went to pensions, who knows?\par \par \uc2 $25 billion went to passenger carriers directly where they spend money. $4 billion went to cargo operators. And then, there was $25 billion in loan and loan guarantees. And a lot of that was utilized as well. So, you're talking, it could be up to $80 billion that we spent.\par \par \uc2 It's this insane idea that government spending will cure a problem. It doesn't. It usually makes matters -- we spent $200 billion on the schools. Remember K through 12?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: To get them open.\par \par \uc2 ASMAN: In the three COVID Relief bills, we spent $200 billion. Where did that money go? We don't know.\par \par \uc2 The OMB, by the way, is supposed to track the money, they do a terrible job. We've talked to the OMB, we've been unable to get answers in terms of the education the K through 12 spending.\par \par \uc2 There are other things that they spend the money. They want to, by the way, right now the Senate is negotiating with House on whether to spend $52 billion on tech companies for them to compete with China better.\par \par \uc2 You really think spending $52 billion? We're just failing 100s of billions.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, we have a question. Right? So, you say OMB is accountable, because people always say to me, you know, why? Why doesn't someone held accountable? That money, it can't just, you know, go into thin air.\par \par \uc2 ASMAN: Right?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, why not bring the Secretary of Transportation \uc2 Pete Buttigieg\uc2  in front of Congress and say, we want to know where did the money go? Because you're in charge of overseeing transportation. The airlines are an absolute embarrassing mess to the country right now. So, explain to us Secretary Buttigieg, what is going on and how can you fix it?\par \par \uc2 ASMAN: Well, frankly, what is a failed mayor's knowledge of the transportation industry? What is Secretary Granholm's knowledge in the energy industry?\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, you got to get up to speed. Once you accept the job, you better get up to -- you better get up to speed.\par \par \uc2 ASMAN: I mean, all we need, if you're going to spend $50 billion, at least spend $100 million or even $10 million on accountants to track the money.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Right.\par \par \uc2 ASMAN: They haven't been doing that. And it's -- again, the idea that the government ever does anything better than the private sector, they say they know how to fix the problems of the oil industry.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 ASMAN: Yeah, right. Don't believe it. Whenever you hear, I'm from the government and I'm here to save things. Grab your wallet quickly. It's going to be waste of money.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, you know, elected representatives should think about it the next time that they start forcing money through, it's like they say, oh, yes, I voted for the money and then they don't care after that. They just, you know, it's gone. Whatever, I'm not going to follow it.\par \par \uc2 ASMAN: Watch out for the compete with China bills, another $52 billion of your tax dollars up in smoke.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Awesome. David, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 ASMAN: All right.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So we have reports right now that are breaking up an \uc2 Amtrak\uc2  derailment. Speaking of transportation, this is in Missouri, we're working to confirm what happened here and to find out if there are injuries in this situation. We're going to get an update on that shortly.\par \par \uc2 Also Mercedes Schlapp and Juan Williams on the dire new warning for Democrats and the call to bring back \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  to fix what's going on, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: \uc2 Amtrak\uc2  is confirming reports of a train derailment in Missouri. It says 243 passengers were on board. There are reports of injuries. \uc2 Amtrak\uc2  says seven cars went off the tracks after that train hit a dump truck. Obviously a very serious situation we're watching this develop we'll bring you more as we get it.\par \par \uc2 And a red alert today for Democrats as huge numbers of voters switch parties with about four months until the midterms. Republicans according to AP picked up more than a million voters in the past year. Democrats just over 600,000 that according to data from AP, as I mentioned, which writes, "but nowhere is the shift more pronounced and dangerous for Democrats than in the suburbs where well educated swing voters who turned against Trump's Republican Party in recent years appear to be swinging back."\par \par \uc2 So do Democrats need some help from \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2 , Fox News Political Analyst Juan Williams says that she can rescue her party in the midterms, writing in the Hill, "Democrats need a strong voice ready to fight to restore women's rights now that the Supreme Court has struck down Roe v. Wade. So let her -- so let Hillary roar her message to suburban white women who will be key to deciding the outcome in these midterms."\par \par \uc2 Juan joins us now, along with Mercedes Schlapp, former Trump 2020 Campaign Senior Advisor now a Senior Fellow at the American Conservative Union Foundation. Welcome to both of you. You know, my first thought when I was reading your piece, Juan, was, you know, wouldn't it be like, you know, the president who was elected, President Biden and the Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , who would be the people that you would turn to, to be the strong voice of the party. And it would mention there was this image of \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  watching the protests on a plane, which sort of got ripped apart on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  saying, gee, thanks, you know, she's 30,000 feet above, and she's sort of viewing this from, you know, this private aircraft, your thoughts Juan?\par \par \uc2 JUAN WILLIAMS, FOX NEWS POLITICAL ANALYST: Well, I think you're right. You know, you'd normally you would say the President's the leader of the party, but in this case, I think President Biden is afraid that if he speaks out too sharply, Martha, he'll be seen as adding fire to an already polarized political environment. And he wants to be able to appeal to voters as a moderate, not as an extreme as he wants to point to the Republicans, and especially now the court say, this is a radical, what he calls, you know, MAGA extremist party.\par \par \uc2 So that's why I wrote today in the Hill, I think that \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2 , who is very sharp in her rhetoric, and very talented, has clear reach into those suburbs, particularly those suburban women that are likely to determine the outcome of the midterms that she could be a real instrument to reach them and convince them to change the what you pointed out at the very top is, you know, general discontent in the country about, you know, inflation gas prices, and make them focus on abortion, gun rights and the rest of them.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yeah. Well, you know, you -- again, I'll say Mercedes, and let's pull up that picture of \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  on the Vice Presidential aircraft, watching all of this from above, you would think she would be just the obvious person to step into the role that Juan says, now Hillary needs to fill, someone who ran twice for Political -- for the top office and lost.\par \par \uc2 MERCEDES SCHLAPP, AMERICAN CONSERVATIVE UNION FOUNDATION SENIOR FELLOW: Yeah, well, \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  is not the obvious person. Neither is \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2 . So Juan, I just rewrote your whole op-ed, OK. Really, the person who you should be pushing into the suburbs is Michelle Obama, at least she's more likable than Hillary Clinton.\par \par \uc2 The biggest issue with Hillary Clinton is that she's outdated. She no longer connects to the suburban women. There's too much political baggage that comes with \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2 . We might be able to -- the Democrats might be even more successful with a \uc2 Chelsea Clinton\uc2 , or again, like I mentioned, Michelle Obama, they could possibly collect connect a lot more with a suburban women. The reality is, is at the end of the day for these midterm elections, it is going to be about the economy. It is going to be about inflation, high gas prices, food shortages. This is what's impacting the American family today. And I think that that's going to be the driving force of these midterm elections.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You know, let's take a look at a poll because this is changing the dynamic a little bit when you look at these polls, this Roe v. Wade decision, at least for right now. You've got 62% of voters who say that the decision to overturn Roe v. Wade makes them more likely to vote in November. So it's a motivating factor for 62% of voters. And Juan, it seems to me that the issues that Democrats are going to focus on is this, this Roe v. Wade decision guns and January 6. Do you think that those, the focusing on those things if they can find the right messenger? And I think Mercedes has a couple of good ideas to add into the mix there for you? Do you think that those can overcome the economic issues which we see at the very top of people's minds?\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Well, and the Fox polling that we saw last week, Martha, inflation was by far the number one issue, but gun, issue number two, and three were abortion and guns. The Supreme Court -- Supreme Court's popularity, approval rating way down. And so what we're seeing is, I think, now a jump, and that people like \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2 , you know, Mercedes points out, and I appreciate your editing Mercedes, you're so smart. You always been a friend.\par \par \uc2 SCHLAPP: I try.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: I think that -- but I think Hillary is pretty strong, because this is not a 50/50 issue. And she has name I.D. people know her. Remember, she won the popular vote, she lost the election to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . But she won the popular vote, she's pretty popular, and especially --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: She's very popular in New York and California, and maybe Illinois, where there's a lot of people living, especially in that time, now a lot of them have moved to other places. So you know, it, you know, which makes me think about this, I saw a piece earlier, you know, when you look at the fact that you have companies fleeing places like New York, and Chicago, Mercedes, and also Los Angeles, California, and yet now, those cities and states, I know Governor Hochul talked about this. Basically, like, you know, if you want an abortion, please come to our city. I just thought that's a kind of a sad statement, right? I'm sorry that companies are fleeing, but boy, we're going to be your place to come for an abortion.\par \par \uc2 SCHLAPP: Right. And I think this has been the biggest change that we've seen in corporate America, the mere fact that they have gotten involved in so many of these political divisive issues, where they don't take into account that maybe half of their customers are pro-life customers, maybe half of their employees are pro-life employees. And so what you've seen is more and more of corporate America, taking on the woke agenda, taking on the leftist agenda, and in essence, silencing their employees and having customers say, wait a second, do I have any other options for you know, shopping for this product or for my financial services? And so what you're going to start seeing is more and more of what I call the alternative economy where those of red states, those red, you know, state, kind of Republicans and customers will want to find alternatives from these bloated corporations that have decided to take on these very divisive political issues.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Juan?\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Well, I don't think it's -- as I said, I don't think it's a 50/50 issue. I think it's more as the polls indicated, more like two thirds of Americans think that Roe should not have been overturned. And when people hear about things like liberty and individual rights, you know, it's very patriotic this time of year. People say, you know, what, if people in those states want to have abortion rights, they should -- they're entitled to it.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: OK. You know, I think it's worth saying that a lot of people think that the children are also worth the same kind of freedom and entitled to life as well. And that's where the