imes more powerful than morphine. Just two milligrams of fentanyl is considered a lethal dose, that's equivalent to just a few grains of salt. Fentanyl continues pouring into the country through our southern border and according to the \uc2 DEA\uc2  last year, the U.S. suffered more fentanyl related deaths and gun deaths in \uc2 Autodesk\uc2  combined together and this year. The State of Texas says it has already seized enough fentanyl at the border to kill every single American. Go back to you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Right, yeah, I just want to repeat that last thing that you said right, because I think it's really significant. More deaths related to fentanyl in this country than guns or car deaths, Bill, together, which is amazing. And also that it's just takes like a couple grains of salt of this drug to kill you.\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Bill Melugin, thank you so much. Always good to see you. Thanks, Bill.\par \par \uc2 So a five month old girl was among more than two dozen people who were shot and killed in Chicago just over the course of this last weekend alone. The baby and at least four others died. Police say that she was in the car around 6 p.m. Friday, when someone in another car opened fire. The shooter still on the loose. Senior Correspondent \uc2 Mike Tobin\uc2  with the latest on this for us in Chicago. Hi, Mike.\par \par \uc2 MIKE TOBIN\uc2 , FOX SENIOR CORRESPONDENT: And Martha this isn't the first time we have talked about innocent life lost in the crossfire here in Chicago. And unfortunately, there's nothing to indicate that this will be the last. As you mentioned, a five month old baby girl Cecilia Thomas was her name. If you made it another four days, she would have been a half year old.\par \par \uc2 Cecilia was securely in the child's seat of her father's car. He was driving home just before 7 p.m. an unknown vehicle approached and open fire. The little girl was hit in the head. When Cecilia made it to Comer Children's Hospital, she was pronounced dead. Also a 41-year-old man was shot in the eye. He is said to be in good condition.\par \par \uc2 Now on the west side of Chicago, two gunmen boarded a CTA bus and opened fire. A 17-year-old was killed, a 25-year-old was grazed by a bullet and refused treatment on the scene.\par \par \uc2 And the gun violence hits close to home. The brother of Fox News Political Analyst Gianno Caldwell was shot and killed on the south side. Police say the shooting was about 2 a.m., a 25-year-old and Caldwell's 18-year-old brother were both killed. Gunman fled in a black car. Caldwell posted on \uc2 Instagram\uc2 . I never imagined my baby brother's life would be stolen from him. In each of these cases the gunmen are unidentified. And on the run 24 people were shot in Chicago over the weekend, seven people were killed, five of them by gunfire. Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Brutal. Mike, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 TOBIN: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Rob Smith is here, Founder and President of the Douglas Society, Host of the, Can't Cancel Robert Smith Podcast, which is very popular and an Iraq War Veteran. Rob, great to have you with us today.\par \par \uc2 ROB SMITH, THE DOUGLAS SOCIETY FOUNDER: Good to be here.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I know you posted about Gianno Caldwell.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: And his 18-year-old brother who he lost over the weekend. What do you think when you hear these stories? And what can be done? Where's this violence coming from?\par \par \uc2 SMITH: You know, it's heartbreaking as overwhelming. You know, I -- Gianno is a friend of mine. I spoke to him via tears in his voice as he's dealing with what's going on here. And I think that when we see all these stories over and over again, because Martha, this is something that we see every single weekend with the Chicago violence, it's really important to note that there were more homicides in Chicago in 2021, than there were in any other city in the United States. 2021 was the biggest year for homicides in Chicago in a quarter century.\par \par \uc2 And so when we look at Gianno story, when we look at this awful story of this little baby that was shot and killed, you have to realize that there are people that are attached to these statistics. And I think that the criticism here with Chicago is it's all on \uc2 Lori Lightfoot\uc2 , I have to tell you that \uc2 Lori Lightfoot\uc2  instead of bringing the citizens together, instead of sort of governing and managing in a way to where she can support police officers because when you talk to these activists on the streets, they want more police, not less police, right?\par \par \uc2 And so whenever \uc2 Lori Lightfoot\uc2  has the chance to do this, she does not do this. Instead, she hops on the BLM, she hops on to Roe vs. Wade, she moves on to the parental rights and Education Act in Florida. She does everything that she does, but focus on the issue that's going on in Chicago. And I think the answers are more policing for these communities. OK, these are real people that are not being represented by the far left activists on \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , they want accountability. They want more police officer on their streets, not less.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So when you look at the gun laws, because we've talked about gun laws a lot lately, they're very strict in Illinois.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So something's wrong. Something's going very wrong. And what can \uc2 Lori Lightfoot\uc2  do to get these guns off the street to actually use the laws that are on the books to confiscate them, and also isn't a lot of this gang related? And how do you get at the root to that?\par \par \uc2 SMITH: It is gang related. And what \uc2 Lori Lightfoot\uc2  can do is to enforce the laws that are on the books right now. And I think that the reason that a lot of Democrats sort of ignore what's going on in Chicago right now, especially when it comes to this gun violence is because that's very politically inconvenient to the conversation that they want to have in that way with America. And I think that what \uc2 Lori Lightfoot\uc2  needs to do is to listen to these activists on the ground, like this is not a partisan issue. I do not believe that this is left or right.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You know what, what bothers me, you know, you look at the situation that we covered in Texas in the Robb Elementary School, right?\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: And we rightfully so, looked at the pictures of every one of these young people. We listened to their families. You know, we don't do this now with these children in Chicago and more kids die this way than die in school shootings across the country. It's a travesty and it is such an oversight to not give their lives the same value that we give the lives of these poor children who are killed in these school shootings.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: You know, I think we do. And honestly, what we need to do is, like you said, there is a problem, when there's only certain outlets that are talking about this issue. There's a problem, when this becomes an issue that only maybe conservative oriented media talks about. This is something that affects the entire country. And this is something that we all need to come together to kind of figure out. Because if we do not do that, then this will never end.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yeah. I mean, that the lives of these children matter to use a phrase, and they get ignored. And it's a travesty, and we cover a lot.\par \par \uc2 Rob Smith, thank you very much. And thank you for your service to our country.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Thank you, sir.\par \par \uc2 So Mississippi Attorney General Lynn Fitch has been called the architect behind the case that led to the Supreme Court overturning Roe v. Wade. And today she's certified her state's so called trigger law, which would make abortion a felony offense, except in cases of rape or danger to the mother. Lynn Fitch joins me now. Lynn, welcome. It's good to have you with us today. So what are your thoughts as you reflect on the decision that we got on Thursday?\par \par \uc2 LYNN FITCH, MISSISSIPPI ATTORNEY GENERAL: Martha, thank you for having me, as always. You know, it was a great day. And we, you know, we've turned the page now Roe v. Wade. So it is definitely a brand new time and for all of us. It's exciting. Because you know, it's a victory for women. It's a victory for children, and a victory for the court. And I really want to commend the core for restoring constitutional principle, and returning this back to the States. This is such an important issue that states should be making the decision. And now we move on to the next steps about empowering women and promoting life.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Let me ask you about the felony charges and how that will work. I assume those are aimed at providers and not at women. Am I accurate in that?\par \par \uc2 FITCH: You're exactly correct. They are definitely aimed at providers. Today, we did certify the trigger law, it should go into effect July the seventh. I sent official notice to the Jackson Women's Health Care Organization as well, to put them on notice that I had filed the certification on the Trigger Law, and that they should be mindful of abiding by all the rules and regulations of the state of Mississippi.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You know, when you look at Roe v. Wade, which I went back and looked at again today, it basically made it federal law that you could have an abortion in the first trimester. And then after that, when you get into the middle trimester, and beyond it was in the state's jurisdiction. So now, we live in a situation where you have some states where it goes all the way up to nine months, which is just far and beyond what you see in countries, by and large all around the world. We have more liberal laws in some states on this than anywhere else. I just want to play this exchange with \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2 , who's running for governor in Georgia that I had yesterday on Fox News Sunday. Watch us.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Do you think that women should have the right to have an abortion all the way up to nine months?\par \par \uc2 STACEY ABRAMS, (D) GA GUBERNATORIAL CANDIDATE: I believe that abortion is a medical decision. And I believe that that should be a choice made between a doctor and a woman and in consultation with her family. This is not a political issue. It's a medical issue.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: OK. So I'm going to take that as a up to nine months in some cases, depending on the woman, correct?\par \par \uc2 ABRAMS: That is not what I said. I said this is a medical decision.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So you know, hard to determine exactly. You know what she was saying? Because she's saying it's a medical decision. So I'm assuming that means all the way through to the end, because she didn't say that it doesn't mean that. You know, what are your thoughts on that? And what about this idea that it's always a medical decision?\par \par \uc2 FITCH: Well, certainly every state is going to react differently, you're going to have different levels and each state, you're going to have some states that they determined that they're going to have full term abortions and certainly going to have some other states, they're going to have certain more restrictions. And so, you know, the science has been part of our argument too, things have definitely changed. We know that these babies are alive and -- in the womb. And that was part of the argument to that the medical science has changed over 50 years. You only think about it 50 years is a lot to have changed as far as all the number of things that affect what has happened, not only for the women, for the children, and we have to take the professional integrity of the medical profession into context.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right, Lynn Fitch, thank you very much, Attorney General in Mississippi.\par \par \uc2 We want to update you quickly on this \uc2 Amtrak\uc2  derailment that we told you about 243 passengers on board. Train hit a dump truck. Several cars have gone off the tracks. We are getting reports that there were injuries involved in this. So we'll keep you posted as we get more information.\par \par \uc2 And that is THE STORY for this Monday June 27, THE STORY goes on. Thank you for being with us today, we look forward to seeing you back again tomorrow at 3:00. And I will see you on "GUTFELD!" tonight. Have a great evening, everybody. See you later.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document HUNDD00020220628ei6r00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 DeSantis 2024?; Interview With \uc2 Frontier Airlines\uc2  President and CEO \uc2 Barry Biffle\uc2 ; \uc2 Amtrak\uc2  Train Derails in Missouri; President Biden Meets With G7 Leaders; Does Roe v. Wade Decision Change Midterm Outlook?\b0\par\par\uc2 Lydia Hu, Ashley Webster, Mercedes Colwin, William La Jeunesse,  Peter Doocy, David Lee Miller, Charles Payne \par \uc2 8,244 \uc2 words\par \uc2 27 June 2022\par \uc2 Fox News: Your World w/ Neil Cavuto\par \uc2 CAVU\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The issue right now is how we're getting pinched to no end. I mean, the cost of everything is just through the roof.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: The right to choice, the economy, crime.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I think it's going to be huge. I do think there's a lot of other things going on in the country, but, again, I do think this is the biggest issue.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It may have an impact. I don't think it's going to have a big impact. I think that inflation, gas prices, these are issues that people are going to vote on.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Big issues for us are definitely inflation right now. Gas prices are huge. And we also have three boys, two of which are teenagers, and they eat a lot. One is driving already. One actually gets a permit tomorrow. So there's some big concerns there.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHARLES PAYNE, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Voters sounding off as the midterm intensity is taking off.\par \par \uc2 Hello\uc2 , everyone. I'm Charles Payne, in for Neil Cavuto. And this is YOUR WORLD.\par \par \uc2 And it's a political world that keeps turning. With inflation still on fire and the Supreme Court decision firing up both Democrats and Republicans, what issues are going to win the day? Democrats hoping the Supreme Court ruling on Roe v. Wade gives them a supreme edge. But are voters putting pocketbook issues at the forefront instead?\par \par \uc2 Let's ask Francesca Chambers. She's from USA Today. And Democratic strategist Kristal Knight.\par \par \uc2 Francesca, let me go to you first here. Before this ruling, it was overwhelmingly about inflation, food prices, gas prices. Do you think the needle is moving much since?\par \par \uc2 FRANCESCA CHAMBERS, \uc2 USA TODAY\uc2 : Well, it may be a little bit too early to say.\par \par \uc2 But if you look at the protests that were taking place just over the weekend, and how much of a focus issue has been, even as President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is at a huge economic summit right now at the \uc2 G7\uc2 , which is where I'm also at, it's abortion rights and that Supreme Court ruling that we're talking about and that the whole entire nation is talking about.\par \par \uc2 And so, Charles, as you look towards the midterm elections, though, it is expected to be inflation and the economic issues that are at the top of voters' minds. However, as you noted, Democrats are hopeful in this election, as are some conservatives, that they can use this to be something that turns out their bass in the election, as this is expected to be a turnout election for both parties.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: What is interesting, Kristal, was that I didn't see many protests in states where they are banning abortions. I saw a lot of them in New York, California, Illinois.\par \par \uc2 So I think those -- the fate of those states are already decided. Can this actually change would have been really an abysmal looking midterms for the Democrats?\par \par \uc2 KRISTAL KNIGHT, DEMOCRATIC STRATEGIST: Well, I hope that it absolutely can.\par \par \uc2 I mean, what we saw over the weekend is folks protesting all over the country. And there were protests that were happening in states that do have trigger laws. What we hope that this \uc2 SCOTUS\uc2  decision will do is reenergize the base, get more voters out to the polls voting in down-ballot elections, voting in their gubernatorial elections, and paying attention more to who actually represents them.\par \par \uc2 Because now we know that, now that \uc2 SCOTUS\uc2  is out of the federal jurisdiction, it's a states issue. So voters will really have to really zero in on, who are members of their state legislatures, and where do they stand on these issues, and do they support them or hurt them?\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Francesca, you mentioned you're at the \uc2 G7\uc2 . It's all about trying to do something with energy prices over there. It's something that's biting everyone. It's a huge issue over here, along with the inflation.\par \par \uc2 I'm sure you heard the sound bites coming in that. That's probably, though -- it's not going to go away. Certainly, real wages are down, inflation is up, and it doesn't look like there will be any relief on the horizon.\par \par \uc2 CHAMBERS: Well, and the experts that I'm talking to, Charles, say that the dirty secret is that there's very little that world leaders can do at a summit like the \uc2 G7\uc2  to bring down the costs for Americans and other people around the world, particularly in these \uc2 G7\uc2  nations.\par \par \uc2 And when you look at the sanctions that they're putting on Russia, that is something that is contributing to the high cost, is the price of energy. So one thing that they're looking to do is put price caps on Russian oil. And U.S. officials said that that was something they were moving closer to. Why did they want to do that?\par \par \uc2 Because they say it would help bring down inflation by reducing the costs and then making it harder for Russia to sell it to other nations that could be willing to pay more for it, so they say both about punishing Russia for its aggression against Ukraine, but also about trying to stabilize the global economy.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes, I think that's going to backfire. That's probably why they're having trouble with it.\par \par \uc2 But let's switch gears for a moment, because there's a new report from the Associated Press, and it shows that more than a million voters in 43 states have switched the Republican Party just in the past year, Kristal. What do you make of that?\par \par \uc2 KNIGHT: Well, it's challenging, but it's also not -- it's understandable.\par \par \uc2 Voters have been talking about economic issues for the last 18 months. And when you see the price of gas rising, when you see the price of bacon and eggs and milk rising, when there's a baby formula shortage, and you also have to understand that this is a Democratic administration, it's not unusual that we may see some voters shifting from party to party.\par \par \uc2 Now, one million voters in a country as large as America, that's not a lot. It's not enough to affect the outcome of an election. But it is signaling something, that the Democrats really need to hone in on our messaging, and really understand, what are the issues that our voters, our base cares about?\par \par \uc2 And how can we message them to let them know that their vote is safe in our hands?\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Kristal, Francesca, thank you both very much. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 Well, folks, tensions still simmering after that Supreme Court decision to overturn Roe vs. Wade, leading to some nasty scenes across the country.\par \par \uc2 Want to go to FOX's David Lee Miller with the latest -- David.\par \par \uc2 DAVID LEE MILLER, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Charles, it was a weekend of rage.\par \par \uc2 Abortion rights demonstrators took to the streets from coast to coast voicing their opposition to the Supreme Court decision overturning Roe v. Wade. The overwhelming number of the protests were peaceful, but there were exceptions. In Portland on Saturday, a crowd of about 100 black-clad protesters went on a rampage. A pregnancy resource center was vandalized, along with a number of businesses.\par \par \uc2 An aide to the city's mayor called the damage despicable. The executive director of a nonprofit that assists pregnant women and who describes herself as a pro-choice liberal Democrat condemned the violence.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MAURA WHITE, MOTHER AND CHILD EDUCATION CENTER: I believe that people have the right to their free speech. But free speech is not smashing in the very businesses that are helping people, that are helping children.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MILLER: Also this weekend, in Phoenix, protesters tried to tear down fencing outside the state capitol. A small number vandalized state memorials.\par \par \uc2 Police used tear gas to try and control the crowds. And in Longmont, Colorado, about an hour's drive north of Denver, where thousands of demonstrators marched over the weekend, police and the \uc2 FBI\uc2  are investigating the cause of a fire at the Life Choices Pregnancy Center. Graffiti was also found at the scene. There were no injuries.\par \par \uc2 And in Los Angeles, where demonstrators have -- demonstrations have taken place daily since the Supreme Court ruling, one man on Friday was arrested, and he was charged with attempted murder for allegedly attacking an officer with a flamethrower. That officer was treated for burns.\par \par \uc2 And lastly, in Vermont, where state law guarantees a woman's right to an abortion, the Statehouse was vandalized. Numerous windows over the weekend were broken and the building was spray-painted with graffiti, the damage there estimated at more than $25,000.\par \par \uc2 And, Charles, more protests nationwide are taking place today -- back to you.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: David, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 And, folks, we are closely monitoring an \uc2 Amtrak\uc2  train derailed in Mendon, Missouri, \uc2 Amtrak\uc2  saying several cars en route from Los Angeles to Chicago derailed this afternoon, with approximately 243 passengers on board. And there are reports of injuries. We will get you more details as we get them.\par \par \uc2 To the \uc2 G7\uc2 . Is President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's attempt to pass the inflation blend going to be a harder hill to climb than those mountains behind him?\par \par \uc2 Peter Doocy, Steve Moore both coming up.\par \par \uc2 And after another weekend packed with cancellations and delays, how prepared are the nation's airlines for this coming holiday weekend? The CEO of \uc2 Frontier Airlines\uc2  is here.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Gas prices still near record highs here. And, at the \uc2 G7\uc2 , President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's green push is even getting some pushback over there.\par \par \uc2 Peter Doocy following the \uc2 G7\uc2  summit from Telfs, Austria -- Peter.\par \par \uc2 PETER DOOCY, FOX NEWS WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: And, Charles, President Biden is here in the Alps making the case to the rest of the \uc2 G7\uc2  leaders that nobody's ever going to be able to rattle energy markets the way Putin is right now if the developed world just goes green.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. MAXINE WATERS (D-CA): To hell with the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: That sound bite I don't think is exactly what we were looking for.\par \par \uc2 Do we have that sound bite of the president? If not, we can just move on.\par \par \uc2 OK, moving on. The national security adviser, Jake Sullivan, is here in Austria as well. And he is trying to explain the possible benefits of a measure that would cap the price of Russian oil for the countries that are still buying it. Listen here.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN AUDIO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JAKE SULLIVAN, U.S. NATIONAL SECURITY ADVISER: ... describe what the president's been doing here as lobbying, so much as posing the problem.\par \par \uc2 (END AUDIO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: So, big focus on going green, but the Germans here for the summit cannot wait for a greener or cleaner solution or for Putin to relent.\par \par \uc2 So they are planning to fire back up dormant coal plants just completely out of necessity. A big focus has been on Russia, the things that Putin is doing. And Secretary Blinken, who's here with the president, has been saying they think that the things they're doing, pressuring the energy markets in Russia, putting an import ban on gold to the \uc2 G7\uc2 , are working.\par \par \uc2 But there's plenty of evidence that it's not, because, yesterday, we saw Putin bomb a Ukrainian apartment building and kindergarten. And, today, he has apparently also sent missiles into a shopping mall, Charles.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes, Peter, thank you very much, Peter Doocy.\par \par \uc2 All right, President Biden pushing his Putin price hike abroad. But if the White House is hoping this summit will help bring the blame squarely on the Russian leader, Steve Moore says, just not working.\par \par \uc2 Steve joins us now.\par \par \uc2 Steve, Peter was talking about some of the military actions that \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  has taken, but their reserves have gotten higher. The ruble is higher than it was at the start of this thing. Russia is richer than it was with all of these sanctions, and now they want to do some other gimmicks.\par \par \uc2 It just -- it seems all to be backfiring.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN MOORE\uc2 , FORMER DONALD TRUMP CAMPAIGN ADVISER: We have empowered Putin, Charles. We have empowered Putin with this insane, crazy war on fossil fuels, and especially here in the United States.\par \par \uc2 And for the president, United States and the presidents of these other rich European countries to then lecture people from low-income countries in Africa and other parts of the world that they should be using green energy, I mean, how crazy is that? Think about that, Charles.\par \par \uc2 Oh, that's what -- in African villages, they're going to use solar power and wind power? I mean, it is just so frustrating to me. There was a big news story last week that Germany, Germany is moving big time. You know what fuel they're moving towards, Charles? Coal. Coal.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MOORE: So all these countries are continuing to use coal and natural gas and oil.\par \par \uc2 The problem is, we're not producing it in Texas. We're not producing it in North Dakota and Alaska. It's now being produced in places like Russia and Iran. It makes no sense from an environmental standpoint, from a national security standpoint, or from an economic standpoint.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: It's interesting because this \uc2 G7\uc2  communique actually is going to be changed. Initially, they were going to say, we're going to phase out coal. Now they're saying, we're going to phase down coal.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 MOORE: Yes. How hypocritical is that?\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes. The whole thing is blowing up, Steve.\par \par \uc2 Japan -- Japan says we can't go to a zero emissions target by 2030. They say we will try. We're going to try to sell more of these electric vehicles. But we can't guarantee it.\par \par \uc2 MOORE: That's right.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: What about the $600 billion? You referenced this, right, $600 billion. I guess these rich nations are going to try to pay off poor nations to skip their own economic development.\par \par \uc2 I hope no countries go for it. It sounds like a lot of money. But, in the grand scheme of things, it's not if you want to be a self-sufficient nation that controls your own future.\par \par \uc2 MOORE: Yes, these countries should use the same kinds of power sources that we used to become a rich country.\par \par \uc2 And let's not forget, this country was built on coal and oil and gas. But I want to make another point. You mentioned those countries, the European countries and the United States. The one country, obviously, that is not represented there, for good reason, is Russia. And neither is China.\par \par \uc2 And China, by the way, has -- I just looked at these numbers. Over the last 10 years, China has quadrupled, quadrupled their coal output. So, every time we're shutting down a coal plant in West Virginia or Wyoming and Pennsylvania, they're building 10 new coal plants.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MOORE: Somebody's got to explain how that's reducing greenhouse gas emissions. It's not.\par \par \uc2 China has no interest -- they have no interest in climate change. They want to take over the world economy. They're laughing behind our back.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: And they also are building 150 nuclear power plants.\par \par \uc2 MOORE: Right.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: They're just brilliant. They're doing all of the above...\par \par \uc2 MOORE: We should be doing that.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: ... to control the world.\par \par \uc2 MOORE: Yes, absolutely.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Hey, let's go back to President Biden's green agenda for a moment.\par \par \uc2 MOORE: Yes. Yes.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Because he's getting some pushback, we mentioned, from Germany. We talked about the largest coal power plant there going back online.\par \par \uc2 This as President Biden says that clean energy is the way to go. It does seem like these other world leaders, I mean, even these rich countries, right, are understanding that they have boxed themselves in. And it should be a cautionary tale to us, right? Do we want to be like a Germany right now, not even sure where they're going to get their heat from this winter, and having to placate a tyrant like \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 MOORE: You're spot on correct, Charles.\par \par \uc2 And this is the story that a lot of Americans don't know because nobody in the media reports this. The European countries, especially Germany, 10 or 15 years ago, went all in on green energy. They went all in on building solar and wind and they were going to replace all of their -- and they even shut down their nuclear plants.\par \par \uc2 And what happened was Germany almost bankrupted itself there. They had to shut down many of their manufacturing plants because they couldn't produce the power. So what's happening now is Europe is moving back to more conventional fuel. And the bottom line is, Charles, what that means is now the United States is to the left of Europe on energy.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes, nuts.\par \par \uc2 Hey, finally, Steve, watching President Biden with those world leaders kind of reminds me of an inflation claim that he makes quite often. Take a listen to this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: It's no solace that inflation is higher around the rest of the world and gas prices are higher everywhere else in the world.\par \par \uc2 Every country in the world is getting a big bite and piece of this inflation, worse than we are in the vast majority of countries around the world.\par \par \uc2 Put America in a position to tackle a worldwide problem that is worse everywhere but here, inflation.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: All right, I want you to take a look at where we are compared to these other nations. And the bottom line is, it's just -- he's just not being truthful about this, right?\par \par \uc2 MOORE: Well, he's not.\par \par \uc2 I mean, we do have the highest inflation rate in most of the industrial world, as you can see from that chart. But I would make -- make another point. I have said it to you many times on your show on FOX Business and on this show, that there's an old saying that, when the United States sneezes, the rest of the world catches pneumonia.\par \par \uc2 So when we have high inflation, we export that inflation to the rest of the world. We have got to stabilize the value of the dollar, which we haven't done, because that's what -- inflation is just a depreciation of your currency. And then I think you will stabilize inflation in these other countries\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: I know they hope we do sooner rather than later.\par \par \uc2 Steve Moore, thank you very much. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 MOORE: Thanks, Charles. Take care.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: So, growing prices becoming a growing hardship for U.S. households. Now more than eight million Americans are behind in their rent payment because of it. So what are the dangers of that?\par \par \uc2 Personal finance expert Rachel Cruze joins me now.\par \par \uc2 This number is really astonishing, considering how long we have these sorts of rent payments that were put on hold, to find out already that this many Americans and probably a whole lot more are under the gun like this. And, Rachel, what's really worrisome is, you can't buy a house anymore. Inflation, it's taken out of way the interest rates. What are people to do?\par \par \uc2 RACHEL CRUZE, PERSONAL FINANCE EXPERT: Yes, the whole housing market right now, it's really tough for people.\par \par \uc2 And we talk to Americans everyday here on "The Ramsey Show." And you're hearing the struggle. So it's not just the grocery and the gas. It's their housing. And so now that the market is up, it's tougher to buy a home. And then you're seeing rent. And eight million Americans are going to be late on rent.\par \par \uc2 And so one of the biggest pieces of advice I can give people is control what you can control, so not sitting back and waiting on Washington to put a policy in place to rescue you, really looking at your income and say, OK, when my paycheck hits my account, what are my priorities and budgeting more intentionally than ever.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: To that point, a lot has been made of these surveys in the last few weeks. There was one that showed millennials and older demographics making $150,000, $200,000, a quarter-of-a-million dollars, and a percent that said they couldn't make it paycheck to paycheck.\par \par \uc2 CRUZE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: It's like, I did say a funny meme, though. One of the millennials said, listen, I can't make it paycheck to paycheck. My monthly bills include $5,000 for avocado toast, $10,000 for Uber Eats.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Listen, it's tough out there. But, ultimately, people are going to have to find a way to get through this, right?\par \par \uc2 CRUZE: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 And there's an expectation that has to lower, Charles, even when you're going to buy a home. The home you could buy in 2019 is not going to be the home you can buy today. And that's not exciting and fun news for people. But that's the reality. And so looking at your lifestyle, making sure that you're lowering your lifestyle, so that you can be paying for housing, food, transportation, and really putting those priorities in place, your needs vs. your wants, is a conversation that everyone has to have right now.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: You know, Rachel, a lot of people complain about that, all the extra money that went out into this economy, in part because people, Americans, we spend anyway, but it really got us spending money, a lot of folks spending money they never had before.\par \par \uc2 And they, to a degree, got addicted to that. Have you been seeing that?\par \par \uc2 CRUZE: For sure.\par \par \uc2 Well, credit cards -- actually, opening credit cards is the highest it hit this month, more than ever. And so people, yes, are just trying to keep up with their bills. But there's this revenge spending that we're seeing. We were cooped up through 2020. And so now people are out spending money. And so their savings now is dwindled. Credit card debt has risen.\par \par \uc2 And so it really is this kind of level of maturity, Charles, that we have to put on to say, OK, here's the reality of where we're at.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CRUZE: And focusing on being intentional with a budget, getting an emergency fund back in place, getting out of debt, which is something we have been talking about for over three decades, that this is a plan that gets you in good times and bad times.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CRUZE: But when your paycheck comes in and it goes right back out and debt payments, it's really tough.\par \par \uc2 And so, again, you have to make hard choices in the present, but it sets you up for peace and financial prosperness in the future because you're able to say, OK, when my paycheck hits, it's my money that I get to decide, and I don't owe anyone anything.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CRUZE: So getting out of debt is another big, big goal for people. And you may have to bring in extra income to do it. But it's something to work towards.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes, there's no doubt. And, unfortunately, a lot of folks are going to probably have to learn the hard way.\par \par \uc2 Rachel, great stuff. Appreciate it. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 CRUZE: Yes, thanks, Charles.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Hey, folks, we are awaiting an update on reported injuries from that \uc2 Amtrak\uc2  derailment in Mendon, Missouri.\par \par \uc2 William La Jeunesse on what we know right now, though -- William.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAM LA JEUNESSE, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Well, \uc2 Amtrak\uc2  is saying they do confirm that indeed this train traveling from Los Angeles to Chicago, 243 aboard, did crash apparently into a dump truck. This happened around noon local time, or roughly about two, 2.5 hours ago.\par \par \uc2 According to local newspaper, they're quoting there are fatalities and \uc2 Amtrak\uc2  confirms there our injuries. This was the Southwest Chief. It was on a dedicated passenger line traveling in this area. A city official told me that the train did collide with a dump truck. We don't know if the driver of that truck has died as well.\par \par \uc2 Multiple agencies are responding right now. The Sheridan County Sheriff's Office said they are on the scene, are waiting for updates as well, the same thing I received from the Missouri State Troopers. We are told this is a very rural area, as I think you can see right there, surrounded by fields.\par \par \uc2 The nearby town has a population of 2,000, the entire county 7,000. We are now awaiting information on \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , actually, from the Missouri State Troopers. They are handling this. There are eight train cars total.\par \par \uc2 One person on the train said in an interview that those with the worst injuries might be in a dining car, because, of course, they were not secured. And the things that could be flying around there would be different than that seat right in front of you. That's what we know right now, Charles.\par \par \uc2 We're waiting for more information. You can see some people, we're told, were still trapped on the train. Others had to escape via the windows. That's still a developing story. And we will know more later today -- Charles.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: William, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 More right after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Airlines scrambling ahead of the holiday weekend to avoid delays. We have got the CEO of \uc2 Frontier\uc2  right here.\par \par \uc2 And pollster Lee Carter on why she's watching the momentum of a certain Republican governor.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: More turbulence for travelers, flight delays and cancellations soaring over the weekend. And that's before the coming holiday crunch.\par \par \uc2 FOX Business' Lydia Hu has been tracking it all from Newark International Airport -- Lydia.\par \par \uc2 LYDIA HU, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hi there, Charles.\par \par \uc2 Just today here at Newark, we're seeing about one in six flights canceled for the day. Across the country, we're tracking around 4,300 flights that have been canceled or delayed. And we're seeing that number grow as the day wears on.\par \par \uc2 Now, this is on the heels of a very busy travel weekend that saw more than 1,500 flights canceled, more than 12,000 flights delayed. And these cancellations and delays come as Americans are returning to the skies in record numbers. The \uc2 TSA\uc2  screened more than 2.4 million people yesterday. That's the highest volume since February of 2020.\par \par \uc2 And with passenger frustration now comes the finger-pointing. Airlines are blaming the \uc2 FAA\uc2 . A letter written by the trade group \uc2 Airlines For America\uc2  to Transportation Secretary Pete Buttigieg says air traffic control staffing challenges have led to traffic restrictions under blue sky conditions.\par \par \uc2 And the group also says that an \uc2 FAA\uc2  facility in Jacksonville, Florida, has been understaffed for 27 of the last 30 days. And they say that is causing some real severe crippling to the entire East Coast traffic flows.\par \par \uc2 But the \uc2 FAA\uc2  here, Charles, is pushing back on that, pointing out the pandemic relief, for the taxpayer dollars that the airlines have received during the pandemic, saying -- quote -- "After receiving $54 billion in pandemic relief to help save the airlines from mass layoffs and bankruptcy, the American people deserve to have their expectations met."\par \par \uc2 That, Charles, being a reference to safe, affordable and efficient travel. Meanwhile, some pilots are blaming their own airlines for overscheduling amid staffing shortages. One pilots union representing 14,000 \uc2 American Airlines\uc2  pilots is calling for more oversight on airline scheduling practices.\par \par \uc2 Now, finally, Charles you mentioned it. Just ahead of the holiday weekend, the AAA is predicting a record number of people to fly to their destinations, more than 3.5 million people expected to fly more than we saw last year. And that's coming as the price of an airline ticket is skyrocketing, up 37 percent over a year ago, so more Americans flying, paying more to do it, but a lot of headaches that are coming along with it, Charles.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Lydia Hu at Newark, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 So how prepared are airlines for the holiday crunch later this week?\par \par \uc2 Frontier Airlines\uc2  CEO \uc2 Barry Biffle\uc2  joins me now.\par \par \uc2 Barry, you just heard it. I mean, but you probably hear it all the time. I got to tell you, the last few months I have traveled, it's been an unmitigated nightmare. It really has been. And it's been different airlines. What is going on?\par \par \uc2 BARRY BIFFLE\uc2 , PRESIDENT AND CEO, \uc2 FRONTIER AIRLINES\uc2 : Well, there's a lot of challenges. I know some airlines have got staffing challenges.\par \par \uc2 But as you just reported on this the latest segment, the air traffic control center is a challenge. We're fortunate. Team Frontier has done a great job in the last six weeks on the lead-in to the summer. We're actually in third place in completion, meaning least canceled flights.\par \par \uc2 But when we look at the similar period three years ago, we have never had this amount of delays and cancellations related to air traffic control.\par \par \uc2 So it is a challenge. But I know there are other staffing issues around the industry.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: You know, it reminds of a joke my dad used to tell me when I was a kid about the kid who came in third place. That was the good news. The bad news, there are only three people in the race.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: So, I mean, 4,300 planes canceled today.\par \par \uc2 I want to ask about this $54 billion. It was -- it was apparently -- it was ostensibly delivered to the airline industry to keep the payroll up, right, so that, when we came out of the pandemic, there wouldn't be a payroll issue. What happened to that money?\par \par \uc2 BIFFLE: Well, I mean, it was sent directly to employees. I mean, the majority of it went just directly to it.\par \par \uc2 And, in fact, in our case, it didn't even cover but a little over half of our employee payroll. But I think what happened, in some cases, there were a lot of voluntary retirements and so forth. And I think that left some airlines without the staffing.\par \par \uc2 And, in our case, we're fortunate. We're actually growing. We're actually one of the few airlines that is larger than we were in 2019. We're actually up over 12 percent in growth. But some actually let those go and they're having trouble actually finding the employees to fill the gap now.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: People, other than retiring, have they gone on to different industries, different -- I mean, I have been on planes, and they're waiting for a pilot who just landed at another part of the airport.\par \par \uc2 So we're 20 minutes late, an hour late. And, again, it just seems -- it's worrisome, obviously, because we have to worry about how overworked these pilots are, and, apparently, they're concerned about it as well.\par \par \uc2 BIFFLE: Well, look, it's not uncommon to have a crew connect from one flight to another.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Sure. Sure.\par \par \uc2 BIFFLE: I think what's challenging is when you have the delays that you have, it compounds it. There's a ripple effect in the complexities.\par \par \uc2 But I think what's important to note, we're proposing a merger with \uc2 Spirit\uc2 . And not only have we been doing well. Maybe we're third out of nine, but we think we can be even better through a merger. So we can be more reliable and provide a better experience with more scale.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: I'm going to ask you about the merger.\par \par \uc2 Your offer is almost $22 a share, from what I understand.\par \par \uc2 BIFFLE: Today's stock price. But they would benefit from the synergies...\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: \uc2 JetBlue\uc2  is offering $32, $32.50. And yet the board apparently is saying that your offer is superior.\par \par \uc2 I will let folks know your stock symbol is ULCC, ultra low cost carrier? Is that why it's superior? I mean, why would -- why would -- why would $30 -- $22 be superior to $33?\par \par \uc2 BIFFLE: Well, I think what's more important is $50 to $60 vs. $3.\par \par \uc2 And let me explain why. In the case of a Frontier-Spirit merger, we're going to lower fares for consumers at a time when inflation is so important on people's minds. We're going to lower price.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: How can you do that?\par \par \uc2 BIFFLE: Well, because that's what we do.\par \par \uc2 We're in the low price business. And that's what our business model is all predicated on. But the alternative with \uc2 JetBlue\uc2  is actually going to raise prices for consumers. We're talking $400 for a family of four, at a time inflation is hitting him.\par \par \uc2 So when you think about probabilities of success through the antitrust...\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Right.\par \par \uc2 BIFFLE: ... ours rounds to 100 percent. Theirs rounds to zero.\par \par \uc2 So all you're going to get paid is a reverse termination fee\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: I got to go, but real quick, some people are concerned, and I know I'm one of them.\par \par \uc2 Over the years, we have seen this consolidation of the industry. And it feels like these things happen, planes get parked, they go to these boneyards, and they never come back out, and the problem gets worse.\par \par \uc2 Is -- what's the -- what's up with that? Like, I understand, when a plane takes off, and there's an empty seat, that's money you can never make again. So the object is the pack them all. But would that alleviate some of the pressures, if we had more planes in service?\par \par \uc2 BIFFLE: So that's what's great about our proposal, is that we're going to merge and actually add 10,000 more jobs and continue to grow in the 10 to 20 percent range.\par \par \uc2 So, a lot more low fares for a lot more people, it's actually going to save consumers a billion dollars a year. So, we're -- this is a good, good consolidation.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: OK. We will know on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 Again, I have to tell you, I give you props; $22, the board says that's a better offer than $33. Something's going on.\par \par \uc2 Barry, thank you very much. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 BIFFLE: Thanks for having us.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: All right, folks, a first-in-the-nation poll putting Republican, Florida Republican Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  in first place. Should he run in 2024?\par \par \uc2 Why pollster Lee Carter says, that's just the beginning.\par \par \uc2 And don't like what they're hearing out of the Supreme Court? Well, just ignore the court. Why what some are pushing on the left has critics saying, that's just not right.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: To the 2024 race for the GOP presidential nomination.\par \par \uc2 Rising Republican star and Florida Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  is building his national profile and reportedly his war chest, this as a recent university in New Hampshire poll shows him pulling ahead of former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in a potential head-to-head matchup.\par \par \uc2 Republican pollster Lee Carter is here to discuss.\par \par \uc2 Lee, just Piers Morgan -- I want you to know about it -- Piers Morgan actually weighed in. I'm going to -- we will get to that in a moment. Just your thoughts on this poll itself?\par \par \uc2 LEE CARTER, REPUBLICAN POLLSTER: So, my thoughts on this are it is so far away from the election. If you think back to what happened in 2015 and 2016, in June of 2015, there was a \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  article that said, here's the five reasons why Jeb Bush is going to be the next president of the United States.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CARTER: Jeb Bush got nowhere. I think what we're seeing, though, right now is that there's a trend, and Americans are concerned about the age of the president, and they're ready for the next generation to come in.\par \par \uc2 And they're excited by some of the younger candidates who are bringing new ideas and more contemporary ideas. More than half of Americans are concerned about the age of the president. Many are saying that there should be age restrictions on how old you can be to run for office.\par \par \uc2 And so I think we're going to start to see a huge sort of swathe of young candidates coming to the front, and people getting really excited about them.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Right.\par \par \uc2 CARTER: Because what I do know is that people are sick of what's not been working for them up to this point.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 CARTER: Now, the other side of this...\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: I'm sorry, Lee. I was just going to say that what I'm seeing, though, it's -- for the most part, Trump, of course, has that core, strong, solid base. And DeSantis is building up this remarkable base because of his performance in part during a pandemic, the way he has pushed back and stood up for its parents, pushed back against woke corporations.\par \par \uc2 I mean, he is actually living, in real life -- he has a chance as an active governor to do these things. And it certainly is not hurting him. What about this war chest?\par \par \uc2 CARTER: Well, I think it's pretty amazing that he has been able to raise as much money as he has.\par \par \uc2 But he has a real knack for grabbing the attention of the nation. We don't know what many governors are up to, right? It's just not, nationwide, all the focus is on one state in the same way, unless there's a scandal or something really controversial.\par \par \uc2 What he's doing is shifting the conversation and changing the debate from everything from COVID, to the economy, to tax codes, and even to his very, very controversial don't say gay bill. He knows how to engage the conversation, set the stage and make sure he's part of every conversation that we're having about all of the most important issues.\par \par \uc2 He's very, very astute. He's very, very savvy, but, again, a long time until 2024.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Right.\par \par \uc2 CARTER: And so I'm not so sure that the momentum will hold. I think we're going to see some other people start to capture the hearts and minds of Americans.\par \par \uc2 But he certainly is fascinating to watch. And he definitely is grabbing a lot of money at this point, because he is sort of -- I think that the hope of people are saying, you know what, I like Trump's policies, but I'm ready for someone who's got a little bit something new.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Right.\par \par \uc2 Let me ask you quickly about inflation. Of course, we had the Roe decision Friday, but so far inflation has been far and away the number one topic, the number one issue. Is it still that case right now?\par \par \uc2 CARTER: In this moment, it still is.\par \par \uc2 But we have seen a lot of shifts in the polls in the last weeks. So, if you think about gun control, for example, 28 percent put that as a top issue. Now 40 percent say that's a top issue. So we have got a lot of conversations about gun control.\par \par \uc2 The abortion debate, we're just starting to see the tip of the iceberg as far as what people are going to view. And so what I really want to do is unpack, what does this mean for the suburban women voters who really made the decision that Biden was going to be the president?\par \par \uc2 Because he -- for he -- and people really swung that direction. Now, a lot of the conversations that you're starting to see about women and Roe vs. Wade isn't just about abortion itself. It's also about IVF. There's a lot of people saying it's going to be harder and harder to get IVF, or fertility treatments, because of the restrictions around that.\par \par \uc2 Lots of conversation, lots of fear. And when fear gets involved, that can become really an energizing factor for people to vote on a certain issue. So I think this is going to be interesting to watch in the coming weeks. But up to this point, inflation, our pocketbooks is still the number one concern.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 Lee, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 CARTER: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: The list of companies, by the way, jumping into the post-Roe continues to grow. But what are those employers risking? And are new attacks on the High Court from the left hitting a new low?\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PROTESTER: We're not sorry!\par \par \uc2 PROTESTER: We're not scared!\par \par \uc2 PROTESTERS: We're not scared!\par \par \uc2 PROTESTER: We're not sorry!\par \par \uc2 PROTESTERS: We're not sorry!\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: With Roe v. Wade overturning the list of companies offering abortion-related benefits is growing.\par \par \uc2 Ashley Webster has the latest from Palm Coast, Florida -- Ashley.\par \par \uc2 ASHLEY WEBSTER, FOX BUSINESS CORRESPONDENT: Yes, Charles, the reaction from corporate America has been swift and on different levels as well.\par \par \uc2 For instance, Patagonia says it will provide bail for employees who are arrested while protesting the ruling and pledging to pay for travel, lodging and food for staff who need to seek abortion procedures.\par \par \uc2 Hollywood has been very quick to jump in, not surprisingly. Disney Sony, \uc2 Paramount Global\uc2 , \uc2 Netflix\uc2  and \uc2 Comcast\uc2  all immediately announced new policies offering travel reimbursements for female employees seeking abortion care.\par \par \uc2 And they have been joined by a broad range of companies, including \uc2 Apple\uc2 , \uc2 Amazon\uc2 , \uc2 J.P. Morgan\uc2 , health insurer \uc2 Cigna\uc2 , \uc2 PayPal\uc2 , \uc2 Alaska Airlines\uc2 , and just today \uc2 Wells Fargo\uc2  also jumping in.\par \par \uc2 But it should be noted that dozens of other big businesses have yet to speak out, like \uc2 McDonald\uc2 's, \uc2 Coca-Cola\uc2 , \uc2 General Motors\uc2  and the nation's largest employer, \uc2 Walmart\uc2 . But some employees are speaking out, including Jose Castillo, who works at \uc2 Disney\uc2 . Take a listen to this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOSE CASTILLO (R), \uc2 DISNEY\uc2  WORKER RUNNING FOR CONGRESS: I'm sure there are -- there's going to be guests who are going to be saying, should I really pay for a ticket to go watch a movie, to go to the theme parks that will eventually end up paying for someone's abortion?\par \par \uc2 Like I said, this is already their policy. There was really no need for them to come out and send this memo only to make a political statement.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WEBSTER: Well, \uc2 Disney\uc2  certainly wasn't alone.\par \par \uc2 But as states consider their options, Charles, lawmakers in Texas, we should note, have already threatened \uc2 Citigroup\uc2  and \uc2 Lyft\uc2  with legal repercussions if they go ahead with plans to help pay for out-of-state abortions. It's going to turn into a legal quagmire, according to many legal analysts, Charles.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes, no doubt about it, Ashley. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 It's one thing to disagree with the Supreme Court, but delegitimize the court? Why the push from some on the left has someone here worrying.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WATERS: To hell with the Supreme Court. We will defy them.\par \par \uc2 NEIL CAVUTO, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: So she wasn't saying just ignore whatever the Supreme Court says? Because that, speaking from someone who serves in the \uc2 United States Congress\uc2 , would be scary, right?\par \par \uc2 REP. AL GREEN (D-TX): She's not saying ignore, but I think we can all differ with the opinions of the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 You can't ignore an opinion. Practically speaking, that's impossible. But I think you can differ.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Democratic Congressman Al Green defending his Democratic colleague, \uc2 Maxine Waters\uc2 , who cried, "To hell with the Supreme Court" following its ruling on Roe vs. Wade.\par \par \uc2 Do those calls to defy the court set a dangerous precedent?\par \par \uc2 Joining me now to discuss, Judicial Crisis Network president Carrie Severino, along with FOX News legal analyst Mercedes Colwin.\par \par \uc2 Mercedes, some serious vitriolic commentary from some pretty high and powerful people in our government. Your thoughts?\par \par \uc2 MERCEDES COLWIN, FOX NEWS LEGAL ANALYST: Right.\par \par \uc2 Well, it's obviously very understandable to have such a reaction. Think about this precedent been in existence for 50 years and the deep reliance that women across the nation have held -- have held that right. Of course, it would be that type of reaction.\par \par \uc2 That's why, in your last thought, you talked about all of the corporate America stepping forward to give the opportunity for their employees, to do something to do an end-around what the Supreme Court has done in reversing Roe vs. Wade. So, yes, it's completely understandable.\par \par \uc2 And, frankly, we will have to see the flow that comes from overturning Roe vs. Wade. We're talking about enumerated -- non-enumerated privacy rights that flow from the 14th Amendment that we enjoyed for many, many decades and we have enjoyed in other types of cases that have ruled.\par \par \uc2 Now there are other cases that are going to be threatened by overturning Roe vs. Wade. We're talking about the contraception in Griswold.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 COLWIN: We're talking about same-sex marriages in Obergefell. We're talking about intimate relations between same-sex couples in Lawrence vs. Texas.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 Carrie...\par \par \uc2 COLWIN: All of these cases are now going to fall prey to it.\par \par \uc2 And, Charles, it's understandable that there will be this type of reaction.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: So, Carrie, number one, I mean, listen, everyone's emotional about it.\par \par \uc2 But there's three branches of government. When one branch openly calls for another one not to matter anymore. I think that's disturbing. But I will let you talk about the constitutionality of the ruling and your thoughts, because it feels like you clerked for \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2 , and now the left is taking his lone opinion and extrapolating that to represent all the justices.\par \par \uc2 CARRIE SEVERINO, PRESIDENT, JUDICIAL CRISIS NETWORK: Yes, as you said his opinion, which is nothing new.\par \par \uc2 He -- this is a position he has about a particular mode of interpreting the Constitution that goes way back. But he was the one vote for that.\par \par \uc2 Look, this is an issue that obviously has a lot of opinions, and strongly held opinions on both sides. Think of it. For 50 years, pro-lifers were concerned not just that the Supreme Court reached out to invent a new right -- yes, they tried to hook it on to the 14th Amendment, the Fifth Amendment, whatever they could find, but those aren't blank slates to just bring whatever rights you want to invent into the Constitution.\par \par \uc2 But it's not just any old constitutional right they invented. It was a right for the mother to end the life of her unborn child. This is something that for pro-lifers is tantamount to murder. And yet they weren't saying to hell with the Supreme Court, let's just ignore it, let's tear down the institution.\par \par \uc2 They worked, they fought, they prayed, they marched for 50 years to get to this day. This is not something -- the idea that any time our government institutions don't do exactly what we want, we're going to then try to burn them down is not a positive mode in our development.\par \par \uc2 Yes, there are strongly held opinions.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SEVERINO: Bring them to your statehouses. Now there's a lot of opportunities to make those heard at the legislature, but don't try to destroy our institutions simply because you disagree with the Supreme Court's conclusions.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 Ladies, I want to apologize, because I really -- I love both of you, strong opinions on this. But we have run out of time. Want to bring you back as soon as possible. Obviously, this is on the nation's mind right now. Thank you both very much.\par \par \uc2 And that will do it for me. Neil will be back tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 And you can catch me on the \uc2 FOX Business Network\uc2  at 2:00 p.m., "Making Money." We're trying hard to do that.\par \par \uc2 Here's "THE FIVE."\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CAVU000020220628ei6r00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Interview with Sen. \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  (R-SC); Interview with Georgia Democratic Gubernatorial Candidate \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2 ; ; Supreme Court Strikes Down Roe; \uc2 G7\uc2  Summit Talks about Inflation and the Ukraine War\b0\par\par\uc2 Martha MacCallum, Alexandria Hoff, Shannon Bream, Juan Williams,  Aishah Hasnie \par \uc2 9,448 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 June 2022\par \uc2 Fox News: Fox News Sunday\par \uc2 FOXN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 MARTHA MACCALLUM, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: I'm Martha MacCallum.\par \par \uc2 In the biggest week in Supreme Court history in decades, the court overturns Roe versus Wade, setting decisions on life back to the states where voters can decide. With it, an already divided nation has a new firestorm and a new issue likely to shift the political landscape.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 (CHEERS)\par \par \uc2 (CHANTING)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM (voice-over): Fifty years of precedent undone. Demonstrators take to the streets, some elated.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I think it's a celebration not only for children in the womb, but also for women.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Others infuriated.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: We stand up and fight back.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 ELISE STEFANIK\uc2  (R-NY): Victory for the sanctity of life. It will save countless innocent children.\par \par \uc2 REP. JAMIE RASKIN (D-MD): One incursion into the liberties and rights of the people will lead to the next and will lead to the next.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Now, President Biden is vowing swift action and calling for calm.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Keep all protests peaceful.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: We'll discuss how the ruling will affect state law across the country and what it means for the Roberts court going forward.\par \par \uc2 And talk with Republican Senator \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  about the court's moves on abortion, as well as Second Amendment rights and about his long fight to put conservative judges on the bench.\par \par \uc2 Plus, how it all plays in the coming election. We will ask Georgia Democrat \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2  how the ruling could impact her rematch against Republican Governor Brian Kemp.\par \par \uc2 Then, we'll ask our Sunday panel how a series of high stakes court rulings could shake up the 2022 election.\par \par \uc2 All, right now, on "FOX News Sunday".\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM (on camera): And hello again from FOX News in Washington.\par \par \uc2 As the Supreme Court's monumental decision to overturn Roe v. Wade sinks in across the country, there's jubilation from those who never thought they would see this day and outrage from those who thought they would never witness this reversal. And some clinics close their doors and emotions spill into destruction and tear gas.\par \par \uc2 (VIDEO CLIP PLAYS)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Around 25 people arrested.\par \par \uc2 In Washington, thoughts turn, though, to the midterms. The political impact and calls now for Congress to act on their own.\par \par \uc2 But decision is just one of the huge signs that the three justices nominated by then President Trump are having an enormous effect on places like guns and school choice as well. This as Congress pulls off a bipartisan gun bill. President Biden signing that into law yesterday.\par \par \uc2 In a moment, we will break down the Supreme Court ruling with FOX News legal correspondent Shannon Bream. But, first -- and we'll also get reaction, I should say, from Republican Senator \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  of the Judiciary Committee and from Democrat \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2  who's running for governor in Georgia.\par \par \uc2 But, first, we turn to Alexandria Hoff who is live at the Supreme Court this morning, where protesters are likely to be gathering later this morning.\par \par \uc2 Good morning, Alex.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDRIA HOFF, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Good morning, Martha.\par \par \uc2 Yeah. All is quiet but we do expect crowds to return at some point this afternoon for what will be the third day in a row.\par \par \uc2 (BVEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 (CHANTING)\par \par \uc2 HOFF (voice-over): The reaction from pro-life groups was instantaneous and joyous. Moments after the wait of what s lost for abortion advocates hit. Forty-nine years, five months and two days after the Roe v. Wade decision was handed down, it was undone, along with Planned Parenthood v. Casey, removing the federal constitutional right to an abortion.\par \par \uc2 The majority opinion authored by Justice \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2  reads: We now overrule those decisions and return that authority to the people and their elected representatives.\par \par \uc2 In a dissenting opinion, Justices Breyer, Kagan and Sotomayor wrote: In overruling Roe and Casey, this court betrays its guarding principles. The ruling transfers power over abortion policy to the states, 22 of which have either already or likely to ban abortion. Four more have pending legislation.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR KEVIN STITT (R), OKLAHOMA: It is my hope that the rest of America will follow Oklahoma's lead.\par \par \uc2 HOFF: Virginia Governor Glenn Youngkin also announced Friday that he will pursue a 15-week ban. But in Washington, D.C., plus at least 16 other states, there will be no reduction in abortion action.\par \par \uc2 According to the pro-abortion rights research group, the \uc2 Guttmacher Institute\uc2 , 11 Democratic-led legislatures moved to expand access in anticipation of the ruling and invitations have been extended.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR KATHY HOCHUL (D), NEW YORK: You come to New York so our providers will take care of you.\par \par \uc2 HOFF: President Biden vowed to stand by the Department of Justice and guaranteeing a woman's right to travel for an abortion and made a plea Friday for the issue to become the cornerstone of the midterm elections.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: We need to restore the protections of Roe as law of the land. We need to elect officials who will do that. This fall, Roe is on the ballot.\par \par \uc2 REP. MAYRA FLORES (R-TX): I woke up this morning praying for this and I never thought that it would happen.\par \par \uc2 HOFF: While Republican lawmakers celebrated the ruling, many Democrats took on a tone of mourning.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 CHUCK SCHUMER\uc2  (D-NY): Today is one of the darkest days our country has ever seen.\par \par \uc2 HOFF: Some taking to the streets to join protesters.\par \par \uc2 REP. ALEXANDRIA OCASIO-CORTEZ (D-NY): We heave to fill the streets. Right now, elections are not enough.\par \par \uc2 HOFF: On Friday, protesters gathered in front of the home of conservative Justice \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Earlier this month, Congress passed a bill expanding 24-hour protection of justices to include their families.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOFF (on camera): Now, protests out here have remained peaceful. Yesterday two people were taken into custody though after they appeared to pour red paint beyond the security gate behind me. They have so far cleaned it up -- Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Alex, thank you very much. Alex Hoff, reporting from the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now, FOX News legal correspondent Shannon Bream.\par \par \uc2 Shannon, great to have you with us this morning.\par \par \uc2 Obviously, we're continuing to get -- she's our senior legal correspondent, I should point out, as you well know and watched her throughout the week.\par \par \uc2 So, tell us a little bit about the latest that the impact of this across the states, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 SHANNON BREAM, FOX NEWS SENIOR LEGAL CORRESPONDENT: So, Alex did such a good job in touching on this. There were trigger laws that actually go in both directions to states across the country. There are those that are tightening down on restrictions, making it nearly impossible to get an abortion in their particular state.\par \par \uc2 But there are others that did expand in anticipation of what may happen in Roe being overturned. And we had Governor Polis from Colorado on "FOX News Sunday" a few weeks ago and I really pressed him on how far the law goes. And he couldn't give me any limitation up to and through the ninth month of pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 There are number of places where that will be true across the country. So, it will be about people choosing to go those states, if that's what they choose.\par \par \uc2 But keep in mind that -- with these trigger laws that go in the other direction that say there is no ability to get an abortion or after 6 weeks, 12, 15, whatever it is. The penalties in those laws, nearly every one I reviewed, the penalties are never for the woman. It is always for the doctor or the medical personnel who help her to try to obtain an abortion, or actually obtained an abortion. A number of states have made it clear they don't want the women themselves to be subject to any criminal penalties, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: But, Shannon, one of the things that you hear is that it will fall on -- the burden will fall on poor women who cannot travel, who cannot get access, who can't leave their state. What is the remedy for that? Is there an accurate remedy for that?\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yeah. We've heard a number of states step up and say, we're going to make sure we provide options so that our state, which is very liberal with their policy, will be an open door for women who need to travel. You heard Governor Hochul in New York say we're putting $35 million towards this, $25 million of that is to beef up our clinics, our personnel to make sure they are able to ramp up their ability to see patients. Also interestingly enough, $10 million of that money in New York is going to go to clinic safety for abortion providers.\par \par \uc2 There's nothing in that conversation about security provisions for crisis pregnancy centers who don't offer abortions but offer other options. There are private groups raising money saying, you know, if you are angry about this decision, donate here and we'll make sure that we share this money with women who need to tap into our funds.\par \par \uc2 And, you know, a number of private employers as well have a -- we've got a growing list that we're keeping track of there at FOX have said we will pay as an employee benefit for people to travel. For example, Dick's sporting goods had said we will pay up to $4,000 per employee to reimburse them for travel expenses if they have to go out of state.\par \par \uc2 So, Martha, a lot of public and private money stepping up to address that particular question.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yeah. You know, I'm just thinking of something that I heard a lot of over the course of this. And I don't know if there's an answer to it yet, Shannon. But, you know, with regard to mailing prescription abortion pills across state lines. Do we know whether or not there will be restrictions on that?\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yeah, some states have gotten very specific. They've gone that far. They've talked about not only the in-person physical abortions that you would under go as a medical procedure, but all the pharmaceuticals that you could order.\par \par \uc2 And this is something that the Biden administration has talked about, too, that they want to make sure it can cross state lines. That's going to be the next phase I think of weeding through this, to see what the different states have done and how that will work across state lines.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, with regard to the other laws out there -- same sex marriage, the use of contraception. I'm going to talk a little more about this with Senator Graham in a moment.\par \par \uc2 But what is your take when you read the opinion on whether or not overturning those is possible?\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Not on the basis of the reasoning in this case. And Justice Alito was very clear. He said, I don't know how I could be more clear. We are distinguishing away the issue of same sex marriage, of contraception, of other issues of sexuality this court has weighed in on.\par \par \uc2 Nothing, he said, nothing in this opinion could be used, none of this reasoning for anything but abortion. He says, we differentiated as a different kind of right, just as Roe and Casey did, because they will look at it as involving, quote, potential life. So, he said, none of this reasoning could apply to those.\par \par \uc2 Now, what's getting attention is Justice Thomas' concurrence where he says, that's true. I agree with the majority in this. He signed on to the majority. Nothing in this opinion goes to those topics. But then he has an added line because he says, when it comes to issues of substantive due process, other cases like the contraceptive, the same sex marriage cases, we may need to re-examine those at the future -- in the future at some point.\par \par \uc2 But he's saying nothing in this case gets us to that conversation.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Okay. More on that to come.\par \par \uc2 Shannon, thank you very much. Shannon Bream joining us this morning.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now is Republican Senator \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2 , member, of course, of the \uc2 Senate Judiciary Committee\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Senator, welcome back to "FOX News Sunday". It's good to have you here.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 LINDSEY GRAHAM\uc2  (R-SC): Thanks for having me.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I want to start with this. This is the language from Justice Alito, who wrote this opinion.\par \par \uc2 He said: We therefore hold that the Constitution does not confer a right to abortion. Roe and Casey must be overruled, and authority to regulate abortion must be returned to the people and their elected representatives.\par \par \uc2 That was called by one columnist the greatest victory in the history of the conservative movement. It's certainly something that you have worked for, for a very long time, Senator.\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: Yeah. Well, what it does, it takes us back to before 1973.\par \par \uc2 Before 1973, the law of the land was that each state can decide the issue of life when life begins, whether or not to have an abortion, what circumstances around having an abortion. All of us in the conservative world have believed that there's nothing in the Constitution giving the federal government the right to regulate abortion. There's nothing in the Constitution that creates a right to abortion as a constitutional principle.\par \par \uc2 This was judged (ph), made out of cloth law. Now, what this court has done is taken us back to pre-1973 where each state can decide through their elected officials when life begins and how to treat -- treat life. And this is a huge victory for the pro-life movement.\par \par \uc2 President Trump deserves a lion share of credit here. He fought like a tiger to put three constitutional conservative judges on the court. He stood behind Kavanaugh.\par \par \uc2 And all of us who've been working for the last 50 years to get this right, to have a constitutional reset, Friday was a glorious day.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yeah, I think of you fighting like a tiger, as you say, over \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2 's nomination process, which you thought had gone to, you know, I think you used the word disgusting --\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: -- places in terms of what happened there.\par \par \uc2 So, what's it like for you personally, Senator, as you look at this? And we -- I think we have a video of you giving that speech. You sort of grabbed victory from the jaws of defeat.\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: Well, it's satisfying to know that through the constitutional process, you can make a difference.\par \par \uc2 You know, when Roe came out, we didn't burn down the Capitol, as conservatives. We didn't go to liberal justices' homes and try to intimidate them.\par \par \uc2 The radical left are constitutional anarchists. They are literally trying to change the country from top to bottom. They want to pack the court because they don't like this decision. They want to abolish the Electoral College so California and New York can pick the president in perpetuity.\par \par \uc2 At the end of the day, they want to federalize elections, take it away from the states, to make sure you have ballot harvesting and do away voter IDs.\par \par \uc2 So, these constitutional anarchist -- here's my advice to you. Quite trying to burn down America and work like we did in the fields. Elect people who agree with you at the ballot box.\par \par \uc2 Your next guest, one of your next guests is from Georgia. I'm dying to say what she will say about how she would handle as governor any effort to regulate abortion in her state.\par \par \uc2 This was won through the ballot box by conservatives and we're not going to liberals intimidate the rule of law system to take it away from us.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, it certainly has lit a fire under politicians on Capitol Hill, and they're going to use this as an opportunity in the midterms.\par \par \uc2 Here's \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  talking about how she viewed that decision on Friday. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 NANCY PELOSI\uc2  (D-CA), SPEAKER OF THE HOUSE: A woman's right to choose, reproductive freedom, is on the ballot in November. We cannot allow them to take charge so that they can institute their goal, which is to criminalize reproductive freedom, to criminalize it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: To criminalize reproductive freedom.\par \par \uc2 You've got very tight Senate races in Arizona, Nevada, Georgia, New Hampshire, Senator, and this will be, no doubt, a powerful line of debate.\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: Yeah. This will be one of the issues we talk about when we elect senators and House members and members of the state houses and governors. But it's not going to change the 2022 outcome.\par \par \uc2 Most Americans believe that -- I really do believe most Americans are comfortable with elected officials making decision about life. Let every state do it the way they would like.\par \par \uc2 What's going to be on the ballot box is $5 gas. You can't walk down the street without being attacked. Crime is rampant. We have broken borders.\par \par \uc2 But, yeah, this is a -- this is a big day for the pro-life movement. For all of you -- I wish Henry Hyde were alive so he could see this. For all of you who have been working for 50 years to elect members of the House and Senate and presidents that would put constitutional conservatives on the court, your day finally arrived.\par \par \uc2 And to the left, the way you do this, is to do what we did. You take to it the ballot box. You don't try to destroy America.\par \par \uc2 These constitutional anarchists like AOC have to be dealt with. And there will be a backlash against this effort to intimidate our judges.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I want to read from Justice Thomas' concurrence. Shannon just mentioned this. And this is getting a lot of attention in terms of whether or not this decision leads to other decisions as well.\par \par \uc2 He said: In future cases, we should reconsider all of the court's substantive due process precedents, including Griswold, Lawrence and Obergefell --\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: Right.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: -- which are same sex marriage and contraception decisions. He said, we have a duty to correct the error established in those precedents.\par \par \uc2 That's going to send a chill through many moderate voters' minds across the country, Senator.\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: Well, I really respect \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2 . They tried to destroy him. Remember his confirmation process?\par \par \uc2 They tried to destroy Kavanaugh. You know, Bush 41 stood by \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2 . President Trump stood by Kavanaugh and here we are today.\par \par \uc2 But Alito I think set the right tone. He said nothing in this decision puts those cases at risk. The reason he decided that Roe v. Wade was wrongly decided is because it deals with the potential for life. These other privacy issues like contraception. Do not deal with the potential for life.\par \par \uc2 He made a distinction between same marriage and contraception which I think will win the day over time.\par \par \uc2 One thing we're not talking about is Harry Reid. We wouldn't be here if Harry Reid had not changed the rules of the Senate, allowing a simple majority.\par \par \uc2 Harry Reid believed they're going to have Democratic presidents forever. He took the rule the Senate, he changed them. And those simple majority rules led to Trump getting three great conservatives on the court.\par \par \uc2 And one thing we're not talking about is Justice Ginsburg, who was a mighty voice for the liberal cause. She chose not to retire.\par \par \uc2 So, it was amazing series of events that allowed us to get here today. And at the state level, to my friends on the left, you're going to have a hell of a fight on your hands at the state level, at the ballot box, and that's what they should have been all along.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yeah. I think you're going to see a lot of attention paid to state legislature, elections in places where people might not have paid attention in the past.\par \par \uc2 But your colleagues in the Senate, Joe Manchin, he's a pro-life Catholic. He's calling for legislation to codify Roe v. Wade. Murkowski, Collins also doing the same. \uc2 Bernie Sanders\uc2  says we got to end the filibuster and we must codify as the law Roe and Casey.\par \par \uc2 What do you say to them?\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: I don't think Joe Manchin is going to turn the Senate into the House. He may be disappointed in this decision.\par \par \uc2 If you're pro-life, you should embrace this decision. It allows states to determine life, not five unelected judges.\par \par \uc2 I don't see -- \uc2 Susan Collins\uc2  or Murkowski are pro-choice -- changing the rules of the Senate.\par \par \uc2 The rules of the Senate would require 60 votes to enshrine Roe v. Wade. Unless you blow up the Senate, that's not going to happen any time soon. As conservatives, you'd need 62 votes.\par \par \uc2 So, here's what you have. You have a full assault on the Constitution by the left. They want to do away with Electoral College. They want to expand the number of judges on the Supreme Court to pack it. And now, they're pushing \uc2 Bernie Sanders\uc2  to change the rules of the Senate.\par \par \uc2 The Senate will hold here. The Senate will not change. The 60-vote requirement for legislation will hold.\par \par \uc2 But if you left it up to \uc2 Bernie Sanders\uc2  and Elizabeth Warren, they would blow the Senate up to get their way.\par \par \uc2 All these people just want an outcome. The constitutional provisions restricting their ability to win, those be damned. We want to change America to get outcomes.\par \par \uc2 And conservatives are going to fight and we're going to uphold the Constitution.\par \par \uc2 And I don't think Joe Manchin or Sinema or Murkowski or Collins are going to blow up the Senate because they're disappointed with this court ruling. I don't believe that. I pray they will not.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I want to end on this note because I know that this is something that you feel personally very passionate about, with regard to life. You have fought for heart beat laws and pain laws throughout the course of your career. You spent a lot of time in the Senate, and so did now President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 He's evolved dramatically. When I think about the things he said on Friday about what a somber day Friday was, and then I want to hear -- I want to play for everyone what he said in 2006 about this issue. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (D-DE), THEN-U.S. SENATOR: I do not view abortion as a choice and a right. I think it's always a tragedy.\par \par \uc2 And I think that it should be rare and safe, and I think we should be focusing on how to limit the number of abortions. And you ought to be able to have a common ground and consensus to do that.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Where did that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  go, Senator?\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: The radicalization of \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , the guy that ran and the guy we actually have as president are two different people. He ran as a centrist. He's governing in the most radical way in any president maybe in modern history.\par \par \uc2 What happened is the radical left has taken over the Democratic Party. And he is going where they're taking him.\par \par \uc2 What do they want to do in light of this opinion? They want to change the makeup of the court, to pack it with additional judges so it will become liberal, not conservative.\par \par \uc2 What do they want to do when it comes to elections? They want to federalize elections, take the power away from the states, institute ballot harvesting, and doing away with voter IDs. They want to make D.C. and Puerto Rico a state.\par \par \uc2 At the end of the day, their constitutional anarchists are in charge of the Democratic Party. And I appreciate President Biden saying to the left, don't use violence.\par \par \uc2 What I worry about is this protest going unchecked at judges' homes is giving a green light to there are no rules anymore. If we don't watch it, somebody's going to get killed out here.\par \par \uc2 And I'm urging \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  to start putting people in jail who show up at justices' homes to try and intimidate them and their family.\par \par \uc2 If we don't reset here pretty soon, somebody's going to get killed.\par \par \uc2 Do what we did. Go to the ballot box. Try to win elections. Don't burn down the country.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, the attorney general certainly has the law on the books to do exactly that, at judges' homes, at justices' homes.\par \par \uc2 Senator, always good to see you. Thank you very much, Senator \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  --\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: -- for joining us today from Columbia, South Carolina.\par \par \uc2 Coming up next, we will get reaction from Democrat \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2 , who's running for governor in Georgia, one of the states that could soon see major changes in the wake of this ruling from the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Now that the high court has overturned federally guaranteed access to abortion, states begin the process of governing the issue according to their own laws.\par \par \uc2 In the state of Georgia, in 2019, the legislature passed the Georgia Life Law, which puts in place a ban after six weeks or when a heart beat is detected. But it was held up in court until now. But now, Governor Kemp says he believes it will go into effect soon.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now, Georgia Democrat \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2  who is running against Governor Kemp this November.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Abrams, welcome to "FOX News Sunday". It's good to have you with us.\par \par \uc2 STACEY ABRAMS (D), GEORGIA GUBERNATORIAL CANDIDATE: Thank you for having me.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So with regard to that law, I know that you said you were appalled by the outcome of the court's decision. But given the law that was voted in by the legislature in Georgia, do you support the fact that they will carry out the will of the people in Georgia and put that law at a six- week ban into effect?\par \par \uc2 ABRAMS: I do not. And I would reject the notion that this is the will of the people. This is a political decision made by the narrowest of margins and done to satisfy an even narrower constituency.\par \par \uc2 A majority of Georgians rejected the notion of overturning Roe v. Wade. They reject this bill. And this is not the law that will be safest for Georgia women.\par \par \uc2 What is more concerning to me though is the notion that our constitutional rights and bodily autonomy that women for 50 years have come to rely on will now be subject to state by state imprimatur rather than being governed by a federal notion that no matter who you are, no matter where you live, we live in the United States and our ability to control our bodies should be sacrosanct regardless of state lines.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, we are seeing that there will be different limitations based on when people would be able to get an abortion. Do you support any limitation on abortion or does that -- do you think that women should have the right to have an abortion all the way up to nine months?\par \par \uc2 ABRAMS: I believe an abortion is a medical decision. I believe that should be a choice made between a doctor and a woman and in consultation with her family. But I think the challenge that we have is that we keep putting this in a political space.\par \par \uc2 This is a medical decision. And the medical choices that should be made should be governed by what is best for that woman and what is best at the suggestion of and advice of their doctor.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: But -- so you used to be pro-life. And you say that you then converted to being pro-choice.\par \par \uc2 You know, you say it's a medical decision. A lot of people would disagree with that. A lot of people would say it's a decision about life and respecting life.\par \par \uc2 I want to play this sound bite for you from another woman in leadership. This is Virginia lieutenant governor, Winsome Sears. And she's talking in this sound bite about the situation in Virginia. And I want to get your thoughts on it on the other side.\par \par \uc2 ABRAMS: Uh-huh.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WINSOME SEARS (R), VIRGINIA LIEUTENATN GOVERNOR: 2019, 15,000 plus abortions were done, 46 percent of them were done by Black women.\par \par \uc2 Now, if the KKK had said, we will pay for every Black woman to have an abortion who wants one, we would say to ourselves, wait a minute, something's up. Why don't they want us to have our babies?\par \par \uc2 But you see that we -- we don't understand what's going on. And so, we're losing so many Black lives that matter.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Do you think that she has a point in terms of losing so many Black lives that matter? I have heard that sentiment from other people as well.\par \par \uc2 ABRAMS: I think that's a very specious agreement that's used to cloak what is I think a deeply disturbing approach to this conversation. In fact, during my tenure in the legislature, I beat back a bill design to deny access to this medical decision using race as a divisive tactic. And I reject that.\par \par \uc2 Black women in Georgia face the highest maternal mortality rate. They are three times more likely to die. We know that Black women are the most likely to be underserved by their medical care. And we know that sometimes the choice that they need to make medically, economically and personally is to have an abortion.\par \par \uc2 If she -- if Lieutenant Governor Sears or others want to address the racial dimensions, then we have to address all of the racial dimensions. The likelihood women -- black women being underpaid, the likelihood of Black women living in broken public health infrastructures, the likelihood of them not having access to Medicaid and not having access to insurance which tends to lead to worse pregnancies and worse outcomes.\par \par \uc2 We cannot cherry-pick when we pay attention to the lives and safety of women. And that is the deepest challenge that we face here in Georgia.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: But what about the lives and safety -- what about the lives and safety of the child?\par \par \uc2 ABRAMS: Brian Kemp has refused to expand Medicaid in the state of Georgia. He's refused to support women at every stage of their lives when they are trying to make the best choices for themselves and their families.\par \par \uc2 And the reality is, if we care about the medical - if we care about the medical needs of women, especially black women, then we have to care the entire time. The medical decision about an abortion -\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I understand, but I'm just asking you -\par \par \uc2 ABRAMS: I know.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I asked you about, when you see -- you know, I asked you if you believe in it all the way up to nine months. So, I'm just asking you, ,you're talking about caring about the lives of women. I'm just asking, at what point do you start to care about the life of that baby, that baby girl, in this - in some cases?\par \par \uc2 ABRAMS: What I would say is that it is a medical decision. I don't know of a single woman who reaches the stage where this decision is easy. That is not the case. And so this is a medical conversation. And while we are absolutely compelled to have these difficult conversations, they should not be political ones. I should not be making decisions without having true understanding of the facts, nor should any other political leader. This is not a political issue. This is a medical issue.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: OK. So I'm going to - I'm going to take that as up to nine months in some cases depending on the woman, correct?\par \par \uc2 ABRAMS: That is not what I - that is not what I said. I said this is a medical decision. And the medical decisions that have to be made have to be made in context. This is a specious approach to try to denude the entire argument. And the reality is, abortion and reproductive care is personal and it is singular and it should be made between a woman and her doctor. I do not have either the experience or the capacity to know what is happening with others.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: OK. I want to move on. We could talk about this a lot longer, I'm sure. But I want to ask you a little bit about this recent headline in an interview that you did with "Axios." And it's -- the title is "\uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2  wants to fund the police."\par \par \uc2 So, what would you say to those who look at that headline and hear you calling for an increase for pay to certain law enforcement, that this is election politics and that this is a change of tune given this sound byte from an interview that you did with CNN that is now part of an ad that your opponent has put out there, I should point out.\par \par \uc2 Let's watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Listen to \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2  on defunding the police.\par \par \uc2 ALISYN CAMEROTA, CNN ANCHOR: So, yes, to some defunding?\par \par \uc2 STACEY ABRAMS (D), GEORGIA GOVERNOR CANDIDATE: We have to reallocate resources. So, yes.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, what's your reaction to that? And do you believe that you've changed your tune on defunding the police?\par \par \uc2 ABRAMS: No, because if you listen to the whole clip, which Brian Kemp conveniently leaves out, I said, if the choice is between the murder of black people and serving black people, then certainly. But I don't think that's where we are and I don't think that's where we have to be.\par \par \uc2 My intent is to balance public safety and justice. Because doing otherwise has never worked. We cannot punish our way into public safety. But we also have to recognize that there are deep challenges in how law enforcement engages our communities. And that is why I'm pushing both for public safety measures, accountability measures, and criminal justice reform measures.\par \par \uc2 What I believe most fundamentally, and this is true if you look at my entire record, is that living wages have to be paid especially to our public worker, especially to law enforcement. And in the state of Georgia, if you're a correctional officer, you made $39,000. If you're a community supervisor, that is someone who deals with probation and parole, and Georgia has the highest rate in the nation, you make $39,000. If you're a trooper cadet, you make a little under - you make a little over $40,000.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Understood.\par \par \uc2 ABRAMS: We need to pay a living wage in Georgia. And that is what my proposal says.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: And I know -\par \par \uc2 ABRAMS: Play a living wage to our public safety workers, just as I want to pay to our teachers, $50,000, because that's the right thing to do.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: OK. Your opponent has also called and implemented for pay raises for members of law enforcement. But that aside, in terms of the defund the police movement, would you say looking back at it that it caused damage? That it -- what we saw was a mass leaving of the - of office of police officers across the country? We saw an increase in homicide rates, 19 percent in Atlanta, 55 percent last year.\par \par \uc2 Do you think it was a mistake to defund the police, to have this movement across the country?\par \par \uc2 ABRAMS: The intention that we had in 2020 was to call attention to the harm that was being done to black people by the police. We have a litany of murders that shocked the consciences of every American. But let's be clear, in the state of Georgia, violent crime went up 55 percent in 2019 to 2020 under Brian Kemp. It had nothing to do with the reaction to George Floyd's murder, to Rayshard Brooks' murder, to Ahmaud Arbery's murder, to Breonna Taylor's murder. It happened under Brian Kemp's leadership. It happened under a president and a governor who believed that punishment rather than a balanced approach was the answer.\par \par \uc2 We know that has never worked. That we have to have both public safety and justice. And that's simply the intention that I have. Let us invest in our public safety workers. Let us invest in making certain that our communities are served, that we center the well-being of our communities, that we think about their health care, we think about their safety and we think about those who are providing that safety.\par \par \uc2 I want us to have the best law enforcement possible to protect our communities, but they also have to be held accountable. And we can do both and. It is a false choice to say that we have to do one or the other.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: But many look at that movement and say that it backfired, that it led to an exodus of police, as I mentioned, that you have seen an increase in homicides, specifically in many cities across the country, that a lot of people who get arrested are back out on the street the next day. This is a dangerous movement that has led to a lot of violence and unrest in cities across the country.\par \par \uc2 Do you disagree?\par \par \uc2 ABRAMS: I am pointing to the data that is in Georgia. And we saw the largest increase in violent crime preceding that conversation that happened after George Floyd was murdered, after Ahmaud Arbery was murdered. Let' be clear, Georgia was facing a rise in assaults, a rise in violent crime. We're going to face a rise in gun violence because we have a governor who has weakened gun laws and allowed dangerous people to carry weapons without a permit, without a background check. And so I think it is very convenient to try to attach this to one moment that is insufficient in terms of its data and in terms of its outcome.\par \par \uc2 But we know in Georgia, the number of people leaving law enforcement often cite pay. They cite pay, they cite morale and that's because they are not feeling completely supported by our governor and by our leadership.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Morale. Yes.\par \par \uc2 ABRAMS: They need the ability to take care of themselves and their families. And we have law enforcement officers, especially at the local level, who are on food stamps because we are not doing our job to provide a living wage for those we ask to do a difficult job.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, we certainly have seen a dip in morale across the country in police and I think a lot of them would attribute it to movements to try to diminish their job and to try to put them in a light that many of them felt they didn't deserve.\par \par \uc2 Before I let you go, I want to ask you one more question, and it's about President Biden.\par \par \uc2 His approval numbers are at 39 percent. Obviously, we have soaring inflation. We've got gas prices that are putting a real dent in people's wallets across the country.\par \par \uc2 Will you be asking the president to campaign with you in Georgia in the governor's race against Governor Kemp?\par \par \uc2 ABRAMS: I welcome anyone who wants to come to Georgia to help improve the lives of Georgians. And that includes the president of the United States.\par \par \uc2 But, more importantly, I invite people to go to my website, it's staceyabrams.com, to learn about my plans for Georgia. This is a state race. This is a race about the future of Georgia, one Georgia, where we treat our public workers - public safety workers, our teachers, anyone who should be doing their best for our communities deserve the best from our government.\par \par \uc2 And please go to my website, it's staceyabrams.com, to learn more about my plan for one (ph) Georgia.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right, we look forward to the conversation. We appreciate you being here today. \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2 , we hope we'll have a chance to talk to you in the future more about this important race in the governor's -- for the governor's office in Georgia.\par \par \uc2 Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 ABRAMS: Thanks for having me.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Up next, we will bring in our Sunday group and get a look at what the real impact is likely to be on the upcoming races as Democrats push to end the filibuster in reaction to what happened in the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Do you think the Supreme Court is broken, in your view?\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I think the Supreme Court has made some terrible decisions.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So that was President Biden expressing his disappointment over the court's recent decisions.\par \par \uc2 Time now for our Sunday group.\par \par \uc2 Fox News congressional correspondent Aishah Hasnie, Fox News political analyst Juan Williams, former \uc2 RNC\uc2  communications director Doug Heye, and "Axios" national political reporter Jonathan Swan.\par \par \uc2 Welcome to all of you. It's great to be with you in Washington.\par \par \uc2 You know what, I actually want to take a look at another sound byte because we went out and asked people on the street what their reaction is because ultimately it's going to be how voters feel about this as we head into the midterms that will matter.\par \par \uc2 Watch this\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Elections are not enough. We need - we really need all kinds of state legislators and courts to act rationally and judge fairly.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I think the country's already pretty polarized on this issue. I think people who are pro-life are already Republican and who are, quote/unquote, pro-choice are already democrat. I also think there's a lot of other issues that are going to be in play, like inflation. I think it's certainly an issue that people are concerned about, and gas prices. So it may have an impact, but I don't think it's going to have a big impact.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You know what, Jonathan, let me start with you and let's start with President Biden and his response. I played a sound byte a moment ago where he said back in 2006 that any abortion was a tragedy. He's definitely changed his tune on that. How does this play for the president going forward?\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN SWAN, "AXIOS" NATIONAL POLITICAL REPORTER: So, inside the White House they've been preparing for this since the leak happened, since the leak came out at the start of May of the decision. They immediately saw, in terms of the political dimension's oven it, they saw this as their best opportunity to energize Democratic voters this year.\par \par \uc2 The guns issue was another one, but that's sort of been neutered (ph) a little bit because of the bipartisan deal on The Hill. So, to the extent that they have an issue that can rev up their base, their base has been very depressed, they've got probably the most horrible political environment for Democrats in a decade. And this issue has been, to some extent, a life raft for them.\par \par \uc2 So, they're going to do a full court press on this. The vice president has a really prominent role in this. She's been meeting with pro-choice groups almost every week since that leak happened and they're going to mobilize their political and outside infrastructure to get as much political bang for their buck as they can out of this decision.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 Doug, I just spoke with \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2 . When you look at the Kemp/Abrams race, right, what's the impact of a decision like this in a place like Georgia?\par \par \uc2 DOUG HEYE, FORMER RNC COMMUNICATIONS DIRECTOR AND FOUNDER OF DOUGLAS MEDIA: You know, the short answer is, in that race, also Pennsylvania, governor and Senate race, we don't know yet. And there are a lot of reasons we don't know. We don't know if it's, as Jonathan mentioned, how much that's going to motivate Democrats versus all the other things that everyday Americans are dealing with every day, inflation being the most prominent example. And one thing that Republicans need to be concerned about, looking back at 2010, 2012 and 2014 Senate races is, what kind of Republican rhetoric are we going to hear from this?\par \par \uc2 So, Mary Miller, last night in Illinois, talked about white life, whatever that means. This is a shade of Todd Akin, \uc2 Richard Mourdock\uc2 , Sharron Angle, Christine O'Donnell, four Senate races that we left on the table. And it's one reason in Missouri that Republicans are so concerned about Erick Wright and this potentially being the nominee, as opposed to the other candidates who have -- don't have the controversial rhetoric and also private life that he has had.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, I mean, it's a great point. You know, how do Republicans sort of frame this discussion and how do they talk about it given the fact, Juan, that you're going to have a lot of moderate voters in places like Pennsylvania or Michigan who may be swayed by this issue.\par \par \uc2 It's really difficult to tell how much though given the things that Doug points out, given inflation, given gas prices. How much is it going to matter?\par \par \uc2 JUAN WILLIAMS, FOX NEWS POLITICAL ANALYST: Well, I think it does matter. And it matters if you look back at exactly the segment of the electorate that decided some past elections, you point directly at suburban white women and you'd say, are they motivated to turn out to vote? Is this a big issue for them? What we know historically is that abortion has really been a trigger for people who are opposed to abortion rights. They've made it their number one issue, their litmus test that takes them to the polls. It's the issue they vote on.\par \par \uc2 This now shifts the dynamic. The dynamic now becomes, people who say, hey, I didn't think my abortion rights were going to go away. They've been here for 50 years. My mom, my grand mom had abortion rights and now that precedent has been erased and now I'm outraged and so I am now incentivized to go and vote. And I think for Democrats there is a big hope there. I think you're looking, just picking up on what Doug was talking about, you're looking not only at those states, you're looking at Arizona, with the Mark Kelly race, you're looking at Pennsylvania in a big way in terms of that -- the governor's race and the Senate race, where all of a sudden the suburbs outside of Pittsburgh, outside of Philadelphia, they are going to make a big difference.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Maybe. You know, I mean I -- I think it's an open question, Aishah, when you look at this issue because it has been a very long time that this has been in place and yet a lot has changed in the world since then. I look at these young womens pro-life groups who are outside the Capitol. They have grown in size. You look at the Hispanic vote, which is now becoming more conservative slowly over time.\par \par \uc2 What about the political impact of this?\par \par \uc2 AISHAH HASNIE, FOX NEWS CONGRESSIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Yes, I think, obviously, Democrats are going to - congressional Democrats are going to try to capitalize on this as much as they can. I think if they can try to bottle this emotion up that we're seeing out in the streets, a call for protests, to try to pour that into the midterms, it could work. I could see some sort of ads out there playing like - playing on that unknown fear of, you know, something like, you know, if you saw what they took away from you with reproductive rights, think about what else they could take away. Kind of that -- playing up that unknown fear.\par \par \uc2 So, I think they will absolutely try that. \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  talked about what the plan is going to be for the midterms. I don't know if it works. In -- four months is a very long time, ahead of November, especially in Washington. Anything could happen.\par \par \uc2 Think about what happened last year in August. I mean we're approaching that one year anniversary. If something like that were to happen again, things could definitely go a different direction. So I don't know that it helps Democrats the way I think they're hoping that it might help, especially with inflation and gas prices. That is what people are talking about mostly, especially independents. In states like Nevada, that I just went to a week ago, talking with independents, people who used to be Democrats, people who used to be Republicans who are now unaffiliated voters, so those - that's now the majority in that state. They are mostly worried about gas prices, inflation, not as much about social issues.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: You know, I think, though, for those states that you just visited, I think about the Latino population Martha mentioned earlier. We see some inclination now more towards voting for Republicans. But this decision really is a tremendous burden on working class to middle class women, and specifically those who are Latino, black women who, you know, may have a child. Most people who have abortions have a child. Half of them are in poverty. So, someone who is like trying to get their nursing certificate suddenly, you know, an unintended pregnancy, suddenly their life choices are limited because their rights have been taken away. The court talks about individual rights and individual liberties. They've taken away individual liberty from these people.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, they've just returned them to the states.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: No, but -\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: They're now allowing the states and the people who vote for their representatives in the state to govern based on what the people desire.\par \par \uc2 But we're going to hold it there. I've got to -\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: That's not what - wait a second, but let me make - respond to your point.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Go ahead. Well, that's a fact.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Because this very week they said the states cannot decide with regard to guns and what people - regulation of guns in the state but they said, oh, we're going to decide about abortion. We're going to take away abortion and make it -\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, they said the Second Amendment is no less a right than any other.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Yes. Well, all of a sudden -\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: It's a very specific right in the Constitution.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Well, it seems to me they trust the states -\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: And their argument is that abortion is not a specified right in the Constitution.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: They're - they're going to put that out so the states can send women to jail. They can send doctors to jail. But they can't say anything about who gets to walk around with a gun?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right, we're going to be back with more conversation with our panel.\par \par \uc2 Take a quick break here.\par \par \uc2 Up next, President Biden heads to Europe to meet with world leaders. We're going to discuss their new plan and keeping up the pressure on Russia.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: And we are back with the panel.\par \par \uc2 Today, President Biden beginning talks with the \uc2 G-7\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Jonathan, let me start with you. We're short on time.\par \par \uc2 So, there's discussion about banning Russian gold imports. That's happening now between the United States and the U.K. What's your take on how seriously this crackdown on Russia is actually having an impact, given what we're seeing on the ground there?\par \par \uc2 SWAN: Well, the Russian economy has proven to be quite resilient. And, actually, despite all of the early kind of heady optimism, Russia, it's digging -- they're digging in and they're making slow gains. And this is looking like it's developing into a war of attrition. So, the big challenge for Biden right now is keeping unity in the west. That's easier said than done. You're already seeing here in America how strong the argument is particularly on the right in the base, in the right, about not sending any more money to Ukraine. And we've got all these problems here domestically, why are we sending $40 billion to Ukraine? They're going to have a lot more trouble when they come back to The Hill again to get something else for Ukraine. This is becoming a more and more difficult issue.\par \par \uc2 So, they're trying to hold it together and it's really fragile. It's very, very fragile, this coalition that they've got together.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You've been in the region, Aishah. You know, we are seeing a situation where you've got a blockade of grain exports out of Ukraine right now and a question about whether or not the United States should be doing more to break up that blockade and get those shipments through.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: I think not only that, but think about, you know, the \uc2 G-7\uc2 , the \uc2 NATO\uc2  alliance is - it's incredibly influential. But then you've got the rest of the world too. You've got countries like India that are now importing a ton of Russian oil. So, you have to think about why we're not hearing more of that from congressional leaders. Why we're not talking about secondary sanctions when other countries around the world are seemingly looking the other way and not, you know, siding with us on this.\par \par \uc2 So, I do wonder why they're not questioning more about that and -- when the front door is open - when the front door is closed, but the back door is open, does it really matter? Are we really gaining any significant outcome out of this?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Just about 15 seconds each. A quick thought on that, Doug?\par \par \uc2 HEYE: Look, \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 's best asset right now is time. He not only knows that, he's depending on it, which is why what Jonathan was referencing causes him great benefit and real concern here in the west.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 Juan.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Well, I think that the Russian combat capabilities in the morning papers it was reported, the U.S. intelligence thinks they're limited. So, if the United States can keep the allies together, time may actually reverse and we may see more power with the Ukrainian capacity being supplemented by the allies.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Thank you all. A big week ahead. Great to have you with us this morning.\par \par \uc2 HEYE: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Thanks, panel. We'll see you next Sunday.\par \par \uc2 Up next, a final word on the week ahead.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Not enough time. That went fast.\par \par \uc2 Thank, everybody. That is it for today. I'm Martha MacCallum. I'll see you on "THE STORY" tomorrow at 3:00 on Fox News Channel.\par \par \uc2 Have a great week and we'll see you next FOX NEWS SUNDAY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FOXN000020220626ei6q00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Supreme Court Overturns Roe v. Wade; Protests In Major Cities Following Abortion Ruling\b0\par\par\uc2 Sean Hannity, Kevin Corke, Mike Tobin, Lawrence Jones, Jonathan  Turley, Kayleigh McEnany, Tammy Bruce, Gregg Jarrett \par \uc2 10,423 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 June 2022\par \uc2 Fox News: Hannity\par \uc2 SHAN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 SEAN HANNITY\uc2  , FOX NEWS HOST: And welcome to HANNITY.\par \par \uc2 We begin this Friday night with a FOX News alert. It is official: Roe v. Wade is no more.\par \par \uc2 And coming up, we'll have my full monologue and we're going to cut through all the clutter, hysteria. We will separate for you fact from fiction. There's a lot of lying, propaganda misinformation, fear-mongering going on. We'll tell you the truth that the media mob purposely ignores.\par \par \uc2 But first let's take you to the ground where protests now are breaking out all across the country at this hour, including some calls for a night of rage and violence. Here outside the Supreme Court, our own Kevin Corke is with us.\par \par \uc2 Kevin, what's going on?\par \par \uc2 KEVIN CORKE, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: It's interesting. If you had been here earlier in the day, you would have seen a very, very different crowd. We're talking about primarily female.\par \par \uc2 I'm going to step out of the way so you can see it. It was mostly female, mostly young, very peaceful. But as is often the case in circumstances like this, Sean, as the day goes on, the crowd begins to change just a bit. More young men show up, more people in tactical gear and masks and glasses, dark glasses, shades and that sort of thing.\par \par \uc2 And somehow it changes the tone of what you're seeing. But thus far, all day long, it has been -- it has been peaceful, and we expect it to stay that way hopefully because there's a rather large law enforcement presence along the fence line at least about that we've seen uniformed officers.\par \par \uc2 Obviously, there are more officers that are plain clothes, within the crowd. We're hoping for the best. But thus far, we've seen a lot of people, passion, some prayer and mostly peaceful protesting. We'll keep an eye on it for you, but for now, back to you.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Kevin, any word on whether or not President Biden authorized calling up National Guard troops if needed?\par \par \uc2 CORKE: I have actually heard that. I have not confirmed that. It's a good question. As soon as I can nail that down, I'll let you know.\par \par \uc2 I will tell you this, just over there which you can't quite see because of the tree line probably, Sean, there are at least a dozen maybe two dozen uniformed officers with shields and helmets and riot gear.\par \par \uc2 When I drove up here, I noticed there were several military personnel carriers as well. So that is possible that that's the case. I haven't been able to lock that down. Of course, as we see any, I promise to let you know.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: All right. Kevin, thank you as always. Great report.\par \par \uc2 We turn now to Chicago where our own Mike Tobin is standing by there tonight.\par \par \uc2 Mike, what's the latest on the ground in Chicago?\par \par \uc2 MIKE TOBIN, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Well, they certainly brought out a lot of numbers and a lot of passion to downtown Chicago. This is federal plaza where the demonstrations started tonight.\par \par \uc2 And essentially what they've done is taken a big march all through downtown not only with the blessing of police but Governor JB Pritzker got out with the women and march. And I say women because what makes this demonstration remarkable is it's almost entirely women. I would ballpark the male population in this crowd somewhere around five percent.\par \par \uc2 With the women I've spoken with out here, they say they felt very disempowered when the decision came down. So their choice to come out here and demonstrate was to gather up in numbers and feel empowered again. They're pretty straightforward. They say they want to send a message to the elected officials that they are indeed a powerful voting bloc and the elected officials are going to have to keep that in mind.\par \par \uc2 As far as the way the march has gone, nothing along the lines of threatening behavior anything that would make you think that uh things would get violent. The worst we've seen there's a couple of vulgar signs and some vulgar chants -- Sean.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: All right. Mike Tobin from Chicago tonight, thank you.\par \par \uc2 We'll bring you updates uh from those protests all night tonight.\par \par \uc2 Now, despite the hysteria on the left, the Supreme Court ruling was a momentous decision that returns abortion law back to the states where it belongs as it is not enumerated in the Constitution like the right to keep and to bear arms which we discussed at length last night. Now some states with the most liberal abortion laws that even allow third trimester abortions -- take a look at your map -- they will continue to allow them. That will include Alaska, Colorado, New Jersey, New Mexico, Oregon, Vermont, Washington, D.C.\par \par \uc2 So while Democrats in the media mob predictably are demagoguing and lying about this very ruling, we'll give you the facts straight up, let you decide.\par \par \uc2 Now, first let us be very clear, this decision does not make abortion in America illegal. It does not. I know they're implying that, they're saying it even, but it does not. Instead, individual voters, you will now decide abortion law at the state level per the 10th Amendment of our Constitution.\par \par \uc2 Now, remember powers that are not enumerated in the Constitution like the right to keep and bear arms are reserved for the states. So, again, in liberal states, there will be few if any restrictions on abortion, in some cases up until the very moment of birth. In fact, most states will continue to allow abortion at some level. There might be some restrictions in one state, different ones in another state.\par \par \uc2 Here's the second fact about this ruling: abortion was never enshrined in the U.S. Constitution as a right and it was never codified in law. Instead, it became the status quo for 50 years because of activist judges pretending that they were the legislative branch of government, supposed to be separate co-equal branches. They're not sometimes.\par \par \uc2 As Greg Jarrett rightly pointed out, a great column on FOXNews.com, Roe v. Wade is just one of more than 230 times that the court has overruled this precious precedent that we often hear Democrats talking about.\par \par \uc2 That would include Plessy versus Ferguson. That's what legalized segregation. Yeah, that precedent was done away with, rightly so.\par \par \uc2 And that awful Dred Scott decision that upheld slavery, another example. In other words, overturning a precedent is not uncommon and in many cases as we just described the right thing to do.\par \par \uc2 Now, naturally, Roe v. Wade was always at the mercy of the courts. But don't take my word for it, listen to far left hero of the left, socialist herself, Justice \uc2 Ruth Bader Ginsburg\uc2  . Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDOE CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JUSTICE RUTH BADER GINSBURG, U.S. SUPREME COURT: Roe became a symbol I think for the right to life movement. They had a single target to hit that. This decision is the most undemocratic decision by nine justices who nobody elected to make policy for the country. My criticism of Roe was that it seemed to have stopped the momentum which was the momentum wasn't on the side of a change.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Now, Ginsberg's prediction has come true. She was right. Roe has now been overturned.\par \par \uc2 And now, this brings us to our third fact about today's ruling. The court's decision today is about abortion and abortion alone. It's not about contraception. It's not about gay rights. It's not about interracial marriage, as many have been suggesting and lying about.\par \par \uc2 In fact, the majority opinion makes this quite clear. Quote: To ensure that our decision is not misunderstood or mischaracterized, we emphasize our decision concerns the constitutional right to an abortion and no other right. Nothing in this opinion should be understood to cast doubt on precedents that do not concern abortion.\par \par \uc2 Now, naturally the media mob, Democrats, they're kind of conveniently ignoring the truth, the facts, the text and the ruling. Now, why let truth get in the way of a great political narrative, sort of like January 6. They want to use abortion, just like yesterday's Supreme Court ruling on the Second Amendment as a wedge issue to fire up their base in the hopes that they may mitigate the pending losses that many are predicting.\par \par \uc2 So, according to them, America is in peril all because of evil Republicans and, of course, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  . Take a look.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDNTIFIED MALE: This is as devastating ruling as can be imagined. T\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: This is a court that is just upended a half century of law, of a constitutionally protected right to abortion for it affects nearly every family across America in one way or another.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Listen, it's a heartbreaking betrayal of half of the country, sorry. I'm getting -- you know, watching the women there. It's emotional.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: New debates over other rights that we thought were settled, the right to contraception, the right to same-sex marriage, all of those are thrown into question today.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: This is going to be a legal civil war between the states.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: This is monumental because it's the first time we've seen the court take away a right.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: There really isn't a lot of people who believe this is a rigged court.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: All right. Top Democrats were just as dramatic and even more shameless with a ploy to turn this ruling into votes in November. Take a look for yourself.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: This fall, we must elect more senators and representatives who will codify the women's right to choose in the federal law once again. Elect more state leaders to protect this right at the local level. We need to restore the protections of Roe as law of the land. We need to elect officials who will do that.\par \par \uc2 This fall, Roe is on the ballot. Personal freedoms are on the ballot.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 NANCY PELOSI\uc2  (D-CA), SPEAKER OF THE HOUSE: Be aware of this. The Republicans are plotting a nationwide abortion ban. They cannot be allowed to have a majority in the Congress.\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2  , VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: You have the power to elect leaders who will defend and protect your rights. And as the president said earlier today with your vote, you can act, and you have the final word.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Now, remember they can't run on the great state of the economy because we have a 41-year high of inflation. They can't run on gas prices because we set record after record after record and it's still going higher. They can't run on their foreign policy. They can't run on the damage they've done to our borders, but that's -- this is what they're going to run on.\par \par \uc2 They're telegraphing it. They're saying it. They're being quite clear where they're headed with this.\par \par \uc2 And it gets worse, \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  tweeted today that today's opinion will live in infamy. Meanwhile, \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  followed up her rant about the election with a dramatic reading of a poem. Take a look.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PELOSI: From time to time, I quote this poem by Ehud Manor.\par \par \uc2 Here is my home. I will not be silent for my country has changed her face. My country has changed her face.\par \par \uc2 I shall not give up on her. I shall remind her and sing into her ears until she opens her eyes.\par \par \uc2 Clearly, we hope that the Supreme Court would open its eyes.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Now, Pelosi, of course, is speaker in name only. But the real speaker of the house, Congresswoman \uc2 Alexandria Ocasio-Cortez\uc2  , was even more distraught today. Take a look for yourself.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. ALEXANDRIA OCASIO-CORTEZ (D-NY): What the Supreme Court just did was that they chose to endanger the lives of all women and all birthing people in this country. But not only that, they've chosen to strip rights from men, too, because frankly, the right to our own bodies and the right to form our families, this is -- this is something that belongs to all of us.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: AOC also then went on and predicted that people will die because of the court's decision and even joined the protests outside of the Supreme Court calling for people to take to the streets.\par \par \uc2 At the same demonstration, her comrade \uc2 Maxine Waters\uc2  called for an insurrection against the court. Take a look.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. MAXINE WATERS (D-CA): You ain't seen nothing yet. Women are going to control their bodies no matter how they try and stop us. To hell with the Supreme Court, we will defy them. Women will be in control of their bodies.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Now, thankfully, there are fences, heavily armed guards, in riot gear around the court they're there for a reason.\par \par \uc2 Ever since the majority opinion was leaked, the justices have been targeted with harassment, threats of violence, and by the way, no condemnation from the Democrats, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  , his press secretary, other Democrats. Their addresses have been published online. Protesters showing up at their home - - at their homes. A man traveling from California to the front door of Justice Kavanaugh in what was an attempt that it's an assassination a plot to assassinate him. We have in the case of \uc2 Amy Coney Barrett\uc2 , you got the name of her kids school and location, the church she goes to, the time she goes to church. Wow.\par \par \uc2 Radicals have vandalized dozens of anti-abortion pregnancy centers all across the U.S., breaking windows, lighting fires, spray painting the message "if abortions aren't safe, then neither are you".\par \par \uc2 Now, a militant group named Jane's Revenge that took credit for many of these attacks is calling for tonight to be a night and a summer of rage. The group declared, quote, open season on pro-life Americans, claiming that it was fun and easy to attack conservatives, even hinted at future attacks that might be much worse than fire and graffiti.\par \par \uc2 And yet, \uc2 Maxine Waters\uc2  , other Democrats, the media mob, they're not calling for calm. Instead, they're whipping up their radical supporters into a frenzy tonight. It's almost as if they don't actually respect our institutions.\par \par \uc2 And let's not forget, it wasn't that long ago, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  himself was very much against abortion. Take a look.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Tell me about abortion. Where do you stand and how will you face that issue?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: It's going to be very difficult. I do not view abortion as a as a choice and a right. I think it's always a tragedy.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Here now with more, he's on the ground in New York for us tonight, our own Lawrence Jones.\par \par \uc2 LJ, what's going on in New York City?\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE JONES, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hey, good evening, Sean. You picked up where \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  was attacking Kavanaugh, saying that you were rue the day. Well, the protesters are here, about 5,000. I haven't seen that many protesters since the summer riots. They're on their way right now to \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  's home to protest in front of his home.\par \par \uc2 I don't know if this is the stand in solidarity of \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  . We still haven't heard any calls from peace or the condemnation of his past comments after that sound bite that he said led to one person to actually make their way to Justice Kavanaugh's house and with what we know to be an attempted assassination. Of course, he ended up turning himself into authorities.\par \par \uc2 But these protests right now have been mostly peaceful. We saw some vandalism on some statues earlier today, but they're marching about of them on their way to \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  's home.\par \par \uc2 And again, Sean, we still don't know who is the person that leaked those documents. We do have the decision now, but of course, the person that lit the match, \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  , as well as that leaker that put that leaked document out there for the public, I think they're getting what they want tonight. Sean, I'll send it back to you in New York.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: So you're saying that people are marching in \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  's home, I would say anybody that calls themselves or identifies themselves as a conservative, turn around and go home. Stay away from a politician's home. That is an extraordinarily dangerous thing to do and a dumb thing to do, just like what he did on the steps of the Supreme Court was done.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Well, we -- you're exactly right. Sean, the people, I just want to know, that the people that are on their way to \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  's home, they are liberal activists. They are shouting "no justice, no peace," "my body, my choice". These are progressives.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: So I guess he's going to go there to cheer him on. LJ, thank you, we'll be checking in with you throughout the night.\par \par \uc2 Here now with reaction, law professor, FOX News contributor, Jonathan Turley is with us.\par \par \uc2 I want to make sure from the legal perspective that the analysis that we're giving is accurate. Now, there is an enumerated right to keep and bear arms. That would justify yesterday's decision on the Second Amendment. There is no enumerated right when it comes to the Constitution, but our framers and their wisdom anticipated such conflicts or moments, and hence we have the Tenth Amendment, to a certain extent I would argue the Ninth Amendment that sends issues like this back to the state, which seems appropriate in this case, which is why I've always felt that that Roe is bad law.\par \par \uc2 Your thoughts?\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN TURLEY, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Well, you know, this is part of what the court was grappling with. You know, people talk about Roe v. Wade as if it's an inviolate decision. In fact, Roe v Wade itself was gutted. That privacy-based analysis that came out of the 1973 opinion was later removed in Casey, and there was a substitute which gave us the undue burden standard.\par \par \uc2 But Roe was criticized by many liberal academics. I mean people like Laurence Tribe called it a smokescreen. As you saw, Ginsburg herself said it went too far.\par \par \uc2 So this wasn't an inviolate decision. It wasn't a decision that was widely accepted in terms of its analysis.\par \par \uc2 Now, there are arguments that can be made under the Constitution for this privacy or equal protection or due process right, but the court clearly didn't see that. And their argument was not that abortion should be unlawful. They're not saying anything about what should happen to abortion. They're saying more who should make that decision.\par \par \uc2 And they said in the opinion that in 1973, they essentially stopped a political debate that was going on in the country. And what they said today was, have at it. Have that debate. Have 3,300 or 30,000 million Americans make the decision rather than nine justices.\par \par \uc2 And you're going to see a variation among the states. Most are likely to protect the right to abortion.\par \par \uc2 I'll remind you, by the way, Sean, that in 1973, 30 states when Roe was handed down prohibited abortion and all by the most limited exceptions. So the country has moved on the issue, but this issue is now going to move back to the country, and people will make this decision state by state.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Is it a fair statement to say that the odds are zero that abortion is going to be illegal in the United States knowing the current makeup of New York and New Jersey and Illinois and California, and even knowing for example Mississippi which brought this before the Supreme Court? They just had a regulation, state regulation of 15 weeks. Is that a fair statement?\par \par \uc2 TURLEY: Yes. In fact, the majority of people in this country are virtually guaranteed to live in a state where abortion is not going to be unlawful. You're going to have 