ed in my lifetime in terms of the Supreme Court and how it is being treated. To have the now Senate majority leader making threats, calling out two justices by name really shocked all of us. And this is at an age of rage, you know, that people are addicted to rage.\par \par \uc2 And certainly, Senator Schumer knows that. I don't think he was calling for violence, but what he was doing was reckless. That there are people that hear those types of words and they take a much different meaning, a very violent meaning. We saw that in front of Justice Kavanaugh's house.\par \par \uc2 And today, we're seeing the same failure of leadership as these politicians go out and say things that are not true. You know, the Vice President Harris and Speaker Pelosi talking about how this opinion endangers things like multiracial or interracial marriages and contraceptive protections.\par \par \uc2 The court went to an extraordinary degree to swat down those arguments. I don't think I've ever seen an opinion where the court repeatedly returns to this issue and says this is not going to endanger those rights. It keeps on coming back and saying, that saying that we are going to extend this opinion to things like marriage or contraception is simply untrue.\par \par \uc2 There was only one justice who said that there might be grounds to re- examine these cases, one. The five other justices in the majority expressly said that is not going to be the case. And yet that's being ignored.\par \par \uc2 I had one law professor respond to me today and said, well, we just don't believe anything they say anyway. Well, then, what's the point of legal analysis if you're just going to say that everything's a lie. That certainly makes our task rather easy.\par \par \uc2 But it's not a lie. These justices made -- drew a very clear line. Abortion in their view is unique and this does not extend to those rights.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Jonathan Turley, great analysis. Thank you as always. Appreciate you bein