ney, and that are, as you pointed out, just corrupt politicians that don't care about the women that are suffering at the end of these abortions.\par \par \uc2 I'm proud that -- I do want to say this -- that mob justice did not ruin this circumstance. You know, I'm proud that despite the Supreme Court Justices having their lives threatened, they actually stood up and they took a stance, and they said, "You know what, this is the Constitution and we're going to stand by the Constitution," and that needs to happen more often.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Yes, and babies are the greatest source of joy. Children are beautiful. They're a gift. I don't know why anyone would take pleasure in destroying them. Clearly, many do.\par \par \uc2 I think you've gone off track if you take pleasure in that in my view.\par \par \uc2 Candace Owens, I appreciate your coming on tonight, reminding us of everything you said. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 OWENS: Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: So tonight is supposed to be a night of rage, and I remember this is a Supreme Court decision. No one voted for anybody who sits on the Supreme Court, therefore public pressure on the Court should play no role because they can't be unelected. Right? They're not standing for election in the next cycle.\par \par \uc2 So when you protest against the Supreme Court, what are you really protesting against? We are attempting to intimidate them.\par \par \uc2 Now, thousands people as we said are marching in New York City; 4,500 people are heading to \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2 's office.\par \par \uc2 Jeremy Alfredo works for Rebel News. He is there tonight and we thought we'd check in with him. Jeremy, are you there?\par \par \uc2 JEREMY ALFREDO, REBEL NEWS: I'm here. Thanks for having me, Tucker.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Of course. What do you see? What's happening there?\par \par \uc2 ALFREDO: There are thousands of people here. I've seen chants and signs calling for the abolition of the Supreme Court, calling f