rned by Brown versus the Board of Education.\par \par \uc2 So, I just want to point that out. That's what each of those individuals said. They said, I accept that it's settled law. They did not go so far as to say how they would determine if they were confronted with a new case. Long-winded but important. Leo, go ahead. Your reaction today?\par \par \uc2 TERRELL: Yes, well, first of all, you're absolutely right. Plessy versus Ferguson, look, 30 years of practicing civil rights law and constitutional law and everything that Jessica said, respectfully, wrong. Plessy versus Ferguson 1896. No, you can laugh all you want.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: I will.\par \par \uc2 TERRELL: 1896. Brown versus Board of Education, 1954 -- 58 years, they got it right. And for you to inject the race card and say about black. 20 million black babies aborted since 1973. But let me just focus on the law, because that's what I think I know. These justices looked at the Constitution and said, we don't see anything about abortion. The 14th amendment was created after the civil right. It was about racial equality. Period. End of story. I'll challenge Jessica to find any section where there is any legislative constitutional history supporting abortion. What these five justices did, Martha, and Fox viewers where they interpreted the Constitution, and they refused to make a political compromise. They follow the law.\par \par \uc2 Now, you may not like the law, we -- but we are a nation of laws, and that's what they did. And what they basically said was, if you don't like the ruling, you go to states because the 50 states are the 50 laboratories have a federal system, and that's exactly what it is. And for you to make reference to the inability to people to travel. This is not 1950. Interstate travel is, is legal for every American in this country. That's the law. That is the absolute law.\par \par \uc2 And also, in the citing by Alito, he listed numerous cases, numerous cases where the Supreme Court made a correction in the early decision. Final point, Martha, to accuse these justices of line where you have \uc2 Susan Collins\uc2 , Pelosi, and all these individuals trying to make political points, trying to do fundraising is insulting to the American intelligence. And President Trump got it right; he got three justices who interpreted the Constitution. Thank you for allowing me to say that, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Absolutely. You know, with regard to the politicalization of this, it happened within minutes, basically. This is Chuck Todd earlier, here's what he said, we heard \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  before saying that these were basically you know, conservative, political activist, jurists. Here's what Chuck Todd said earlier today.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHUCK TODD, MSNBC HOST: Well, I think the Supreme Court is, this is -- I think there are no more black robes. I think the robes are red and blue. I think that is you know, this is the -- we've been watching this happen, particularly in an aggressive way over the last 15 years.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right, what do you think?\par \par \uc2 FLEISCHER: Every time somebody does something conservative, the media say, it's not based on facts, it's not based on truth, it's not based on analysis or history or law. But when a Democrat does the same thing, I didn't hear Chuck Todd criticizing the three liberals on the court or the previous liberal majority on the court. This is what's wrong with the press. This is their hypocrisy, but you know, I want to talk about, touch base on one thing Jessica said and many ways on the left are making the case now about contraception.\par \par \uc2 You know what, if any legislature is foolish enough to want to ban contraception, let them try, they will all get voted out of office. This is not the 1920s or 1930s. But the principal point is that people's representatives and legislatures have the right to make these decisions. But the Constitution and the Declaration grant us, that any alienable right to life, liberty, and the pursuit of happiness. A lot of the rest of it is up to legislatures. So, if they wanted to try to ban contraception, which is the big fear now on the left, not a single one would do it because the American people left, right, and center would rise against them. But it is the prerogative of legislatures to make these decisions under our system. That's how it should work.\par \par \uc2 MACCALUM: Jessica, I'll let you respond to that.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: I know how the system works. I'm not a constitutional lawyer, but I'm pretty well read about it. And the point that I was making wasn't that these justices did anything wrong vis-a-vis what they could actually do, but that the implications of what they've done and what they have done are so dire for millions of women across this country.\par \par \uc2 And I would like to see if these conservative legislatures that Ari is speaking about are going to suddenly grant maternity leave for everybody, if there are going to be entitlements put into help mothers who are now going to be taking care of babies that they did not want. If every man that takes part in conceiving a child, one night stand at a nightclub, if suddenly he's on the hook for child support, will we have universal pre-K everywhere? Well, the conservative part --\par \par \uc2 TERRELL: Oh my gosh!\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Stop saying, oh God, this is what it looks like out there in the other world.\par \par \uc2 TERRELL: I just can't believe you're using this quality time to, to, to talk, to push a Democratic agenda. Go ahead, I'll be quiet. Go ahead. Keep talking. It's ridiculous.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: It's not ridiculous, Leo. I am a Democrat. But most of all, I'm a woman who just went through a pregnancy. And I know --\par \par \uc2 TERRELL: Oh --\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: I'm sorry. Were you pregnant? Can you answer me?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Hold on, 