exactly the reason that Roe v. Wade should stay in place. There was a spike in the late 80s or early 90s. And as you point out yourself, Martha, it has been declining since then, what will happen now that, that is, that the justices have made this decision is that there will be a spike again, but they will be unsafe abortions, they will be back-alley abortions in many cases.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: It doesn't seem like there's a need for that, because there's going to be transportation, there's going to be places where you can get an abortion, if you want to have one.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: What because -- if you work at \uc2 Amazon\uc2 , they're going to pay for it for you. How many women work two jobs, right and support a family already and get pregnant and then you tell them you just no big deal. Like, Leo says, just hop on the highway. No big deal. What about the money for that?\par \par \uc2 TERRELL: I did not say that. I did not say that. I did not say that.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Final thought to Leo here, Because I know he has something else he really wants to say. Leo, go ahead.\par \par \uc2 TERRELL: Yes. Thank you very much. I did not say what you just said -- those are classic democratic talking points. Martha, if you, if you scan all these other left-wing programs, you know what you say like how this subject is about Roe v. Wade, but they expanded to everything on the Democratic platform. For example, they're talking about this affecting interracial marriages -- a total disconnect. I can sit here and tell you right now the 14th Amendment equal protection prevents that, but that's not stopping them from misrepresenting the truth about the limitation of this ruling.\par \par \uc2 One final point, you have \uc2 Maxine Waters\uc2 , you have people basically arguing (INAUDIBLE). Maxine just said a few minutes ago, ignore what they said. That is a total disregard to our branches of government, the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, everyone benefits from having an understanding of why and how this decision was made.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 TERRELL: It's amazing. This is unbelievable.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: It's about whether or not the original decision was actually constitutional. And it's -- it is separate from the other sort of broader interpretation that a lot of people want to ascribe to it.\par \par \uc2 But we're going to continue to go through this throughout the course of today.\par \par \uc2 Great group. \uc2 Ari Fleischer\uc2 , thank you so much. Always great to have you with us. We haven't seen you in a while. So, I'm so thrilled to have you back.\par \par \uc2 And Leo Terrell and Jessica Tarlov.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: See you later at 5:00.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Yes. (INAUDIBLE)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Thank you, guy. You bet.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM (voice-over): So, THE STORY investigates this group called Jane's Revenge. Who are these people who are promising a night of rage in the streets? Who supports them? Who finances them? Where did they come from? We have investigated this and we'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Women's rights are being turned back decades. That is a right that we had for over 50 years. And that now it's time to do everything we can to make sure that we have a pro-choice majority in the House and the Senate, who's going to pass these laws, who's going to codify these rights. So, we don't have to rely on five radical justices because to acknowledge the rights that we have in the Constitution.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Jane's Revenge, a purported anonymous online collective is calling for a night of rage this evening, because the decision came today, in reaction to the Supreme Court's overturning of Roe vs. Wade.\par \par \uc2 A web site tied to the group claims credit for a string of recent attacks including arson on this pro-life facility in Wisconsin.\par \par \uc2 Last month, you can see the damage that was done to these various places with red paint splattered and the vandalism. Here you're seeing is on a faith-based pregnancy center in North Carolina.\par \par \uc2 Two weeks ago, the executive director of that center joins us in moments with her reaction how it felt to come to work that morning and see what had happened to the place where she works and helps women.\par \par \uc2 First, our correspondent David Lee Miller with the backstory on Jane's Revenge. Hi David Lee.\par \par \uc2 DAVID LEE MILLER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Hi, Martha.\par \par \uc2 Jane's Revenge. Now, that's the name of a group or as you point out, really possibly a loose collective of groups that has vandalized offices associated with pro-life organizations and individuals and is now threatening violence.\par \par \uc2 MILLER (voice-over): An Internet post signed by the group says it is comprised of various cells. The moniker is evocative of a group of women in the 1960s called the Jane Collective, who helped women obtain illegal abortions.\par \par \uc2 A fire scene recently around Washington, with the group's name said and I quote, "D.C. call to action. Night of rage." "The night SCOTUS overturns Roe v. Wade." "\uc2 HIT\uc2  THE STREETS." "You said you'd riot." "To our oppressors, if abortions aren't safe, you're not either," signed Jane's Revenge.\par \par \uc2 Now, the first crime associated with Jane's Revenge occurred on May 8th in Madison, Wisconsin. The offices of the anti-abortion organization Wisconsin Family Action was firebombed. A statement to an online journalist reportedly from Jane's Revenge took responsibility for the attack.\par \par \uc2 And since then, there have been dozens of similar incidents targeting pro- life affiliated organizations. Often, the perpetrators are anonymous, but in some instances, such as in Minneapolis, Buffalo, and near Baltimore, graffiti associated with Jane's Revenge was seen at the site.\par \par \uc2 A June 15th web site posting that was attributed to Jane's Revenge said, in part, and I quote, "We have demonstrated in the past month how easy and fun it is to attack."\par \par \uc2 We reached out to the \uc2 FBI\uc2  and we've been told the agency does not have a comment about a specific group, but according again, is investigating a series of attacks and threats targeting pregnancy resource centers and faith-based organizations across the country.\par \par \uc2 MILLER (on camera): Jane's Revenge, meanwhile, has an ominous warning, saying, damage from future actions might not be so easily cleaned up such as fire and graffiti. Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, we know the president called for demonstrations to be peaceful today. And we certainly agree with that. I'm sure most Americans agree with that. And we hope that that's what we see. But no doubt there are going to be people who are very nervous who work in these facilities in the days and weeks to come.\par \par \uc2 David Lee, thank you very much. Always good to have you with us.\par \par \uc2 Kristi Brown is executive director of the Mountain Area Pregnancy center in Asheville, North Carolina. The Jane's Revenge web site is claiming responsibility for vandalism at her facility.\par \par \uc2 Thank you very much, Kristi, for being with us today. Explain to us what happened at your facility. And what you do there? What service do you provide?\par \par \uc2 KRISTI BROWN, EXECUTIVE DIRECTOR, MOUNTAIN AREA PREGNANCY SERVICES: Well, thank you for having us on. We were contacted by our local police the morning of Tuesday, June the 7th, to say our building had been vandalized.\par \par \uc2 So, we rushed here to find the pictures that you have on screen, which is the red paint, the broken glass, the threats. And, fortunately, the inside of our building was mostly spared. So, we were able to continue the services which include services to women and families going through an unplanned pregnancy or loss of the baby.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, you know, this is what strikes me when I think about these places or like the service that you provide. People who are pro-choice should be in favor of the ability to choose, right?\par \par \uc2 So, you are providing women who choose to have their babies a place to go and provide them support. Correct? Are you trying to persuade people to choose life?\par \par \uc2 BROWN: We desire that they choose life. That our primary goal is to educate them. When women call us needing services, they are panicked many times. They just need help.\par \par \uc2 They are pregnant and often don't want to be. A part of them making a decision as they need to be educated on the consequences of their three choices, which are you can carry, you can adopt, you can go through with abortion.\par \par \uc2 And our goal is to educate them and give them the truth and the facts so that they can then make an educated decision. We work with women who go through abortions, we see the devastation it causes. We never want to see a woman go through that.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You know, give us a sense of the people that you work with and what you've learned over the years from working with them.\par \par \uc2 Well, we work with people from all walks of life. Obviously, we're mostly women, a childbearing age. But they call us, in many times, they're in tears when they call us.\par \par \uc2 They come in here and they start sharing their life story, and how they got pregnant and what's going on in their world. And the story sometimes will break your heart.\par \par \uc2 Many times they don't know where to turn for help, and they don't know where to turn for a listening ear. And that is why pregnancy centers are so incredible, because we spend time with them, and we listen and we care. And we don't judge them for any decision that they have made.\par \par \uc2 So, the fact that we are being targeted for we are just trying to give them unconditional love is absolutely crazy.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Are you getting security from your town or the police can spend some more time in front of your place in the coming days?\par \par \uc2 BROWN: Yes. And we have been working with authorities, quite honestly, at all levels of government since the attack. We feel really good about our connections with authorities. We have been working for weeks on increased protection here at our facility. We had security in place when the attack occurred. But we are looking at additional steps to our current system.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. Yes. Yes. So, if you're in favor of choice, you have to be truly in favor of choice. Allowing people who decide that they want to choose to give up their baby for adoption or to have the baby. Also, deserve the same kind of peace and security and safety as those who make the other choice if that other choice of abortion is legal in their state, and that there are places for them to make that choice as well.\par \par \uc2 Kristi Brown, thank you very much. Good to have you here today. Thanks for the work that you do.\par \par \uc2 BROWN: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, the response from Florida Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  to today's SCOTUS ruling is straight ahead. And an exclusive with Texas Attorney General \uc2 Ken Paxton\uc2 , who just made today a holiday in his state in honor "of unborn babies killed in the womb."\par \par \uc2 We're going to talk to the attorney general of Texas about the changes that are coming there next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Now that the Supreme Court has overturned Roe v. Wade, Virginia Governor Glenn Youngkin says that he will try to ban most abortions after 15 weeks of pregnancy. Remember that, it was what -- was issue with this Mississippi law. Mississippi wanted a 15-week ban, almost four months of pregnancy, to say after that