ve also guaranteed the same kind of coverage, insurance coverage as part of their health plan. What's your reaction to that? Do you support that on the part of independent companies?\par \par \uc2 PAXTON: Yes. So look, and the Supreme Court went back to what they should. This is (INAUDIBLE) based on elected representatives. That's a (INAUDIBLE). They took away from this back in the early 70s and they should have. This is (INAUDIBLE) to make different choices.\par \par \uc2 Companies (INAUDIBLE) whether we like that in Texas or not, it's really up to them. So if California has -- once they have completed abortion, they can do it and companies are free to make their own decisions.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: OK, I'm sorry that your audio is breaking up a little bit, but I'm glad you were able to stop by today. Obviously, it's a very big day. \uc2 Ken Paxton\uc2 , thank you for joining us. Good to see you today.\par \par \uc2 PAXTON: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Some moments ago, we've been keeping one eye on the first comments on this from Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  to the Supreme Court's ruling overturning Roe v. Wade and here's what she had to say.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: This is a healthcare crisis. Because I understand, millions of women in America will go to bed tonight without access to the health care and reproductive care that they had this morning, without access to the same health care or reproductive health care that their mothers and grandmothers had for 50 years.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: That from \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , the Vice Presidential, just a short time ago. And another \uc2 SCOTUS\uc2  ruling that has come out this week. It's been a very big week for the Supreme Court delivered a big win for school choice advocates. And we don't want this one to get lost in the shuffle here. It's a very significant decision. We're going to talk to the Former Education Secretary, \uc2 Betsy DeVos\uc2  next.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: We're fighting for school choice, which really is the civil rights of all time in this country, frankly, smoke choices, the Civil Rights sacred of the year, of the decade and probably beyond, because all children have to have access to quality education.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So another major Supreme Court decision this week was a victory for school choice. The justice is ruling that the state of Maine cannot exclude religious schools from a program that helps families pay for private education, which they were trying to do there.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now, \uc2 Betsy DeVos\uc2 , Former Education Secretary under President Trump and author of the brand new book this week, "Hostages No More: The Fight for Education Freedom and the Future of the American Child." And there it is. It's a great read, highly recommended. Betsy, great to have you with us.\par \par \uc2 So let's just jump right in. What does this mean, decision mean to schools across the country and what a Justice Roberts say about why they ruled this way?\par \par \uc2 BETSY DEVOS, FORMER TRUMP EDUCATION SECRETARY: Well, this is a huge victory for parents and families and students because it really does strike another huge blow at the bigoted Blaine amendments that have been the excuse in many states for not permitting -- or education freedom for families. So this is going to reaffirm the drive toward freeing families to find their education fit, including private religious schools, if that's what the families choose.\par \par \uc2 And the justices were very clear that in their step to restating and reasserting that there may not be religious animus toward education providers, and that in the situation where families and parents are making decisions about where their children go to school, it is not a government- related interest. In other words, it is not an issue that sends up the church-state separation flag, because families are making this decision.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. And what they ruled is that you can't forbid someone to go to a religious school, if that's their choice, and they're entitled to the money that they are designated as a child in that public school system. They should be free to use it in the way that they want to.\par \par \uc2 There's, you know, a lot obviously going on in this arena. And I was curious about something that you wrote about with regard to Senator Booker, because I know that in the past, he was a very strong proponent of charter schools. And at one point, you worked together on some of these initiatives, or you were on the same side on some of these initiatives. What changed in that relationship between him and charter school expansion?\par \par \uc2 DEVOS: Well, sadly, in his goal to run for president, he had to comport with the school union and status quo and all their allies demands that he really be opposed to anything that freed families to make these choices. And it was really, really a disappointment, a huge disappointment. And especially -- for me personally -- but especially thinking of all the students whose lives are impacted by his reversal. And I'm hopeful that he's eventually going to come around to this principled stand he held for many years again.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. I remember years ago, hearing him speak in Newark, New Jersey, about the benefits of charter schools. And there are thousands of kids on waiting lists in New Jersey --\par \par \uc2 DEVOS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: -- whose families and parents want them to have access to some of these very good schools. And there's now this push against expanding these schools to provide spots for all of these kids. So I would, you know, love to have Senator Booker come on, and talk about, you know, why the change of heart on this, especially at this point.\par \par \uc2 So San Francisco, we covered this story, when they basically had a big changeover in their school board in San Francisco. They put in a number -- or they took a number of people out, and they put in some people who lean more conservative onto that board. And since then, you have this San Francisco elite high school that has returned to merit-based admissions, and you also have a mural of George Washington that now they are going to keep up on those walls. And that was really driven by the parents in the community, wasn't it?\par \par \uc2 DEVOS: Absolutely. I think a very clear message was sent to the system in San Francisco, that parents want to be heard. And this school that was formerly and now again, based on merit admissions is a good signal. It's a signal that we should be sending across the contrary, and that is a pursuit of excellence and a pursuit of achievement and merit. And I think that is a -- it bodes well for the future of opportunity in San Francisco. But it's good to see that parents have spoken and their voices have been heard there.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, we've seen the same thing in New York with the gifted program. There's really a push against anything that sort of allows kids who have worked really hard and who have achieved a lot to be recognized for that and to have that be an entry point into some of these places where they can really get, you know, promoted even more in what they're able to achieve.\par \par \uc2 Betsy, thank you very much. \uc2 Betsy DeVos\uc2 . "Hostages No More" is the new book out this week from \uc2 Betsy DeVos\uc2 . It's her first book. That's a good read. Thanks, Betsy.\par \par \uc2 DEVOS: Thanks, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So more with the Former Education Secretary \uc2 Betsy DeVos\uc2  on the latest episode of The Untold Story podcast. It is available for download now. We had a really interesting conversation. In fact, this school choice decision from -- wrote with regard to Maine, came down in the middle of our conversation.\par \par \uc2 So I got her firsthand, take on reaction to that, and it digs into what she's talking about in the book. I think you'll find it really interesting. That's the podcast which drops today, or dropped yesterday, actually.\par \par \uc2 I also want to shed some light on this. And this is the question that has been talked about a lot today with regard to Senator \uc2 Susan Collins\uc2  who cast the key vote to confirm \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2  on the Supreme Court. And there's a lot of discussion about precedent. And I can share with you what her office is now saying about some of the reaction that they think that is inaccurate with regard to how she's being portrayed in this today.\par \par \uc2 We'll talk about that coming up next.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. SUSAN COLLINS (R-ME): To my knowledge, Judge Kavanaugh is the first Supreme Court nominee to express the view, that precedent is not merely a practice and tradition, but rooted in Article III of our Constitution itself.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BRETT KAVANAUGH\uc2 , ASSOCIATE JUSTICE OF THE SUPREME COURT OF THE UNITED STATES: Said that it's settled as a precedent of the Supreme Court entitled, "The respect under principles of Stare Decisis." And one of the important things to keep in mind about Roe v. Wade, is that it has been reaffirmed many times over the past 45 years, as you know. And most prominently, most importantly, reaffirmed in Planned Parenthood versus Casey in 1992.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Supreme Court Justice \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2  at his confirmation hearing almost four years ago, he said that precedent is critically important. But, quote, I listened to all arguments. And after hearing the arguments in this particular case, which is new, and was not before him on that day or prior to that day, he did decide that he would side with the opinion of Justice Alito, that overturned Roe v. Wade, which had been in place for 50 years. I would point out Plessy versus Ferguson was in place I think for about 57 or eight years at the time that it was overturned.\par \par \uc2 Let's go first to Congressional Correspondent Aishah Hasnie who has the backstory here on Capitol Hill. Hi, Aisha.\par \par \uc2 AISHAH HASNIE, FOX NEWS CONGRESSIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Hi there. Well one of the biggest critics of Justice Kavanaugh, and this ruling today has been Senator \uc2 Susan Collins\uc2  of Maine. And apparently, Martha, she or at least her staff have been watching your show because they have just now responded and sent a statement to your show about some claims, or at least some criticism claims that she doesn't understand what precedent means. We'll get to that in just a second.\par \par \uc2 But first, let me just lay out what's been happening today. So \uc2 Susan Collins\uc2  came out today and basically blamed Justices Kavanaugh and Gorsuch for what she says, will throw this country into chaos. And here is the original statement from today. She writes, "This ill-considered action will further divide the country at a moment when, more than ever in modern times, we need the court to show both consistency and restraint."\par \par \uc2 She goes on to say, "It is a sudden and radical jolt to the country that will lead to political chaos, anger, and a further loss of confidence in our government. Now, Collins is also accusing Justice Kavanaugh of -- going back on his word in a meeting that happened nearly four years ago back in 2018, Collins claims then Judge Kavanaugh told her he saw Roe v. Wade as settled law, as a precedence. But today Kavanaugh basically a pine that Roe v. Wade was the wrong decision.\par \par \uc2 And as I mentioned, Collins office just sent a statement to your show, Martha, to respond to those claims, that she doesn't understand how precedents works. And I'm going to read this to you slowly here. They say this. "We never said you can't overturn precedent. Of course, we know that." That's one. And they say, "And, of course, Kavanaugh did not promise to uphold Roe v. Wade. We never said that he did and never would have asked any nominee to do so."\par \par \uc2 So there is the reaction and response now from \uc2 Susan Collins\uc2 ' office. Meantime, Collins also writes that she and Senator Lisa Murkowski have actually introduced a separate bill to codify Roe v. Wade. And this is important, Martha, too, because their bill is very different from what the House passed earlier. It's different because apparently the House bill was too expansive for even Collins and Murkowski and Senator Joe Manchin.\par \par \uc2 And an interesting note, just to point out here, we have yet to hear from Senator Murkowski of Alaska. We have not seen anything on her \uc2 Twitter\uc2  account yet. I've reached out for a statement, she is facing a very tough re-election in Alaska. She just voted for gun reform. So this one could also be kind of dicey for her.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. Well, we remember that Murkowski and Collins were sort of the final two decision makers --\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: -- when it came to the Kavanaugh nomination approval.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: You got it.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Murkowski did not support and Collins did support and she gave a very impassioned speech on the floor of the Senate when she decided to support the nomination of \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Thank you very much, Aishah. Good to have you with us.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: Sure.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Let's bring in \uc2 Mike Davis\uc2 , a Former Law Clerk for Justice \uc2 Neil Gorsuch\uc2 , former \uc2 Senate Judiciary Committee\uc2  Counsel and President of the Article III Project. Mike, good to have you with us today.\par \par \uc2 MIKE DAVIS\uc2 , FORMER LAW CLERK TO JUSTICE GORSUCH: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You know, I think it's very clear and I know it's very clear to \uc2 Susan Collins\uc2  as well, she's very smart woman, a smart Senator. She understands what precedent is. But I think a lot of people in the country misinterpret when they hear someone in a hearing say that something is settled law. And here's some examples of those moments from the nomination process for now Justice Gorsuch, Justice Coney Barrett, and Justice Kavanaugh. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 NEIL GORSUCH\uc2 , ASSOCIATE JUSTICE OF THE SUPREME COURT OF THE UNITED STATES: I would tell you that Roe versus Wade decided in 1973 is a precedent \uc2 United States Supreme Court\uc2 . It has been reaffirmed.\par \par \uc2 KAVANAUGH: That it's settled as a precedent of the Supreme Court entitled, "The respect under principles of Stare Decisis."\par \par \uc2 AMY CONEY BARRETT, ASSOCIATE JUSTICE OF THE SUPREME COURT OF THE UNITED STATES: Well, it's not a super precedent because calls for its overruling have never ceased, but that doesn't mean that Roe should be overruled. It just means that it doesn't fall on the small handful of cases like Marbury vs. Madison and Brown versus the Board that no one questions anymore.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So none of these individuals, Mike, sat at that table and said if a case comes before me, and I am a justice, that they wouldn't evaluate it and make a decision on whether or not Roe v. Wade would hold in light of their decision on that case. Correct?\par \par \uc2 DAVIS: Correct. So I ran Justice Gorsuch's confirmation effort from the outside. I ran Justice Kavanaugh's confirmation effort from the \uc2 Senate Judiciary Committee\uc2 , and I was very involved on Justice Barrett's confirmation effort at the Article III Projects and I worked on the Chief Justice and Justice Alito's confirmation in the Bush 43 White House.\par \par \uc2 This is how Supreme Court nominees always answer these questions on precedent --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Right.\par \par \uc2 DAVIS: -- because they're bound by judicial canons that say that they could not exchange their future vote in a case for a confirmation vote by a senator.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. And as \uc2 Susan Collins\uc2  office says that she'd never asked for, nor did she receive any promise from \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2  that she wouldn't do that, obviously, that is unethical. And it's actually illegal for them to make that kind of commitment. That's not the kind of world that we live in here.\par \par \uc2 So, you know, but she is saying that she thinks that this is a decisive decision, a divisive decision, I should say, and that she believed that it was incumbent on the court to lean towards consistency and restraint given the division in the country. And I think that what's interesting about that is that it isn't too far away from what we heard injustice Roberts says concurrence on this opinion. He wanted to simply support the Mississippi case individually and not have it have the broader ramifications that this decision has. I think for somewhat similar reasoning, I think that's fair to say. Your thoughts.\par \par \uc2 DAVIS: So yes, so they uphold the 15-week ban in Mississippi, and then the state passes a six-week ban. And then we're back to where we are now. I think what the five justices on the Supreme Court did correctly was follow the Constitution. They ripped off the band aid and they got rid of nearly 50 years of bad precedent, precedent that has unnecessarily divided our country.\par \par \uc2 Now abortion regulations go back to the 50 states and our elected state officials can decide --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 DAVIS: -- abortion instead of unelected judges on the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Right. Mike, thank you very much. We got to get in some breaking news here. Thank you. \uc2 Mike Davis\uc2 , so experienced in all of this, as you point out. Good to have you.\par \par \uc2 Also break in today, the first major gun safety bill in almost 30 years heading to President Biden's just the House joining the Senate and passing the measure, with support from both parties. Congressional Correspondent Chad Pergram covering it all for us, of course, on Capitol Hill. Hi, Chad.\par \par \uc2 CHAD PERGRAM, FOX NEWS CONGRESSIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Hey there, the vote 234 to 193. The plan expands background checks for those between the ages of 18 and 21. It also increases security for schools but the bill addresses few things wanted by liberals. There's no assault weapons ban and no limit on high capacity magazines that bothered the left.\par \par \uc2 But the legislation is the art of the possible. That's why this is the first major gun bill to pass since the Brady bill on background checks in 1993. Still, conservatives believe the bill goes too far.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Today, they're coming after our Second Amendment liberties and who knows what it'll be tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: Civil libertarians believe red flag provisions in the bill undercut due process for some gun owners. Martha?\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. RAND PAUL (R-KY): But we've only heard from their spouse, and we didn't hear from them. We only heard from their estranged spouse, or we only heard from the person that's angry with them at work, or we only heard from the person from the opposite political persuasion that read their writings on the internet. We can see, we can all see the mischief for this.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right, that was \uc2 Rand Paul\uc2 , Senator \uc2 Rand Paul\uc2 , obviously, at the tail end of Chad's report there. Thank you to Chad Pergram on the Hill.\par \par \uc2 That's the story for today, Friday, June 24th. Very, very consequential day. This weekend, I will be anchoring Fox News Sunday. And I will speak with Senator \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  who has been on the \uc2 Senate Judiciary Committee\uc2  for a long time and who was a big part of the processes that nominated these three justices who are so consequential in today's decision. I look forward to speaking with him on Sunday. But before then, I will see you on "THE FIVE" just about an hour away. See you then.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document HUNDD00020220625ei6o00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Supreme Court Overturns Roe V. Wade; Roe Was A Flawed Creation From Start; This Win Was 50 Years In The Making; Protesters Threaten "Night Of Rage" Over Roe; Justice Alito Reveals Truth About \uc2 SCOTUS\uc2  Precedent; Biden's Shift Left On The Abortion Issue; Protests Across Country After SCOTUS Overturns Roe; Supreme Court Ruling Overturns Roe Versus Wade Decision Providing Constitutional Protection For Women To Obtain Abortions; Protests Take Place Across U.S. Over Supreme Court Overturning Roe Versus Wade; House Speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  Reads Poem In Protest Against Supreme Court Decision On Roe Versus Wade; Rep. Kat Cammack Interviewed On Supreme Court Decision\b0\par\par\uc2 Laura Ingraham, Kevin Corke \par \uc2 9,038 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 June 2022\par \uc2 Fox News: The Ingraham Angle\par \uc2 INGANG\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 SEAN HANNITY\uc2 , FOX NEWS HOST: They decided who gets to the side and the constitution is clear, it's the States.\par \par \uc2 It's all the time we have left this evening. Thanks for being with us. Thank you for making the show possible. Stay with the Fox News channel for continuing coverage. Let not your heart be troubled, Laura's next. We'll see you on Monday.\par \par \uc2 LAURA INGRAHAM, FOX NEWS HOST: I'm Laura Ingraham, and this is THE INGRAHAM ANGLE on a triumphant Friday night from New York.\par \par \uc2 Answered prayers. That's the focus of tonight's 'Angle'.\par \par \uc2 On January 22, 1973, almost 50 years ago, seven members of the Supreme Court held that under the 14th Amendment of our Constitution, the same constitution that Americans had fought and died to protect. Elected officials could not protect the unborn from abortion. Now, ever since that time, abortion has been a constitutional right.\par \par \uc2 Now, this was a painful and humiliating experience for tens of millions of Americans who love this country, and who were ashamed to think that our Constitution would be used to justify abortion. But the pro-life community never gave up on this country. We always believe that someday Roe v. Wade would be overturned, and this blot on our history would be purged. And today was that day.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (CROWD CHEERING IN UNISON)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: This is amazing. This is probably the best day of American history in my lifetime. God bless America. It overturned Roe.\par \par \uc2 (CROWD CHEERING IN UNISON)\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Now, look at all those young women and men celebrating life. That is the future of America. The pro-life movement.\par \par \uc2 But there's still some who consider abortion a holy sacrament. And they're furious. They're trying to intimidate the justices at their homes, threatening to defy the court. They're desperate to inflame the nation as it was in the summer of 2020.\par \par \uc2 (VIDEO PLAYING)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: The 5-4 majority that overturn Roe and Dobbs simply returns the issue of abortion to the States where it properly belongs. It will be legal in many states, but illegal or highly restricted in others, until the people decide differently. It's called the democratic process. Ever heard of it?\par \par \uc2 But people who should know better, of course, they're engaged in what can only be described as pathetic and lame efforts to mistake and twist the ruling. Chief among them, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  himself.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT, UNITED STATES OF AMERICA: It was three justices named by one President, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , where the core of today's decision to upend the scales of justice and eliminate a fundamental right for women in this country.\par \par \uc2 State laws banning abortion are automatically taking effect today, jeopardizing the health of millions of women, some without exceptions. So extreme that the women can be punished for protecting her health. Core principles of equality, liberty, dignity, and stability of the rule of law demand that Roe should not have been overturned.\par \par \uc2 With this decision, the conservative majority of the Supreme Court shows how extreme it is. How far removed they are from the majority of this country. This decision must not be the final word.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Well, wait, whoa, whoa, whoa. Wait a second.\par \par \uc2 I thought court rulings were the law of the land, Joe. Oh, is that out the window now? OK. I have to keep up. I'd have to take notes here.\par \par \uc2 Now this is just typical of people who know what they're doing is wrong. And \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , as a Catholic, should be ashamed of himself, to use the word dignity to justify the old Roe v. Wade ruling.\par \par \uc2 Perhaps the worst, though, was Biden's attempt to conjure up phony emotion. Did you catch this moment? He was invoking outlier cases in order to whip up political support for their lifeless party?\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: It's a realization of an extreme ideology. So extreme that women and girls were forced to bear their rapists' child, with a child of consequence. It just stuns me.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: What a phony. I'm sorry. If Democrats think that this is going to help them in November, they're going to be sorely mistaken. It's usually not good for any political movement to be losing. And Americans are seeing that the populist movement is winning, and it's succeeding.\par \par \uc2 Hispanics who are more pro-life than most Democrats, they're not going to turn away from the GOP because they suddenly want more abortions. And no one's going to tolerate a terrible economy, wasteful war spending, and an incompetent gerontocracy, just so more people can get more abortions. That is ludicrous.\par \par \uc2 And notice a difference, by the way, between the way we react to losing at the court, and the way they do.\par \par \uc2 Now, pro-life forces lost 50 years ago. It was devastating to them. But they never stopped believing in America. Never. They still believed in the system, and they worked within it to arrive at the ruling that happened today.\par \par \uc2 Now, they elected pro-life Republicans. They held countless prayer vigils, they pushed for pro-life language in the \uc2 RNC\uc2  platform. They wrote op-eds, they held debates, they did campus outreach. They started crisis pregnancy centers, many of which are under attack now.\par \par \uc2 Remember, they conducted sidewalk ministries and were ridiculed for it. They hosted and attended the March for Life for decades in rain, in ice, in snow, in shine.\par \par \uc2 And, of course, they elected \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , who would put not one, but three justices on the court and the majority today. And let's not forget, he was the very first sitting President to speak live, let alone attend the March for Life.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT OF UNITED STATES OF AMERICA: Unborn children have never had a stronger defender in the White House. Every life brings love into this world. Every child brings joy to a family, every person is worth protecting. And above all, we know that every human soul is divine and every human life born and unborn is made in the holy image of Almighty God.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Now, this isn't in the prompter. I'm just going to say this. OK. That was the best speech I think he ever gave. And he's given a lot of great speeches. That was really special.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the left, they lose once at the court and they're threatening to tear down the whole system. They want to take down the Senate, they want to blow up the Electoral College, and now the Supreme Court itself. They hate our constitutional democracy.\par \par \uc2 But this decision, like the gun decision yesterday, simply follows the plain language of the Constitution. Remember, the Second Amendment clearly provides for the right to keep and bear arms. And the left, they're like, No, it doesn't. Right?\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the Constitution says nothing about abortion. But the left claims, it is a constitutional right. Constitutional rights can't be made up out of whole cloth. It's ludicrous. But that's what the courts stupidly did in Roe. The left cites the need for a living Constitution. Don't you love that?\par \par \uc2 Now, talking about twisted logic, where a document must live and be protected for living, while a preborn human being must have no right to life at all. Very logical.\par \par \uc2 Now, as much as the establishment of big business, the media, they work to silence and demoralize the pro-life movement all these years. It didn't work. The pro-life movement was not left behind. It didn't fade away. Its members didn't grow old and die out. Instead, it kept renewing itself. And it grew stronger and younger.\par \par \uc2 Year after year, decade after decade, it continued. Of course, we still had a problem. Because too often Republican presidents and you know who you are promised to appoint pro-life justices, but didn't keep their promises.\par \par \uc2 Justices O'Connor, Souter and Kennedy, all voted with the judicial activists on these and related cases on abortion. Now, had conservatives including yours truly, not forced \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2  to pull the nomination of Harriet Miers back in 2005, she would have been another vote to uphold Roe. Thank goodness, we have \uc2 Sam Alito\uc2  on the court.\par \par \uc2 Now, we always seem to be one or two votes short, didn't we, over all these years? But in 2016, pro-life voters saw a chance to change the Republican Party and the court. So \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  wasn't part of the GOP establishment, was he? He wasn't one of the politicians who had led us on for decades, all lip service, only to disappoint us at the end.\par \par \uc2 He promised to be different. He was a threat to the old guard. He promised to appoint pro-life justices, who would hold firm no matter how many threats they received from the outside.\par \par \uc2 So Gorsuch, Kavanaugh, Amy Coney Barrett, all voted the right way. Many Republicans doubted those promises by President Trump. But most of us decided to take our chance. And in the end, President Trump kept this promise.\par \par \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  and the Republicans in the Senate, they kept their promises as well. And today, for the first time since 1973, our constitution is free of the taint of abortion.\par \par \uc2 Now, this court, this decision, solidifies President Trump as one of the giants of American history. For many Americans, this is like the Berlin Wall coming down. Something they hoped and prayed would happen, but in their gut they never thought it would really happen.\par \par \uc2 All those who claim that Trump would be bad for conservatism, they owe him an apology tonight. If you're honest with yourselves, you will apologize. He got more done in four years than the Bushes did in a combined 12.\par \par \uc2 There are times when we all wonder whether it's worthwhile to spend so much effort as we do in politics, gets tiring, gets depressing, gets demoralizing. We March, we pray, we donate, we speak, we go on TV, we write, we organize, we vote. And many times, you can't tell really if you're making much of a difference. Are things really getting any better? I don't know.\par \par \uc2 But some days, it's different. Some days, you win. And some days you get to celebrate and give thanks that you were alive when our prayers were finally answered. And the United States of America finally did the right thing. Today is one of those days. And that's the 'Angle'.\par \par \uc2 All right. Joining me now, Carrie Severino, Judicial Crisis Network President; Robert Dunn, partner at Amer Stahl, both are former Supreme Court clerks for my old boss Justice \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2 . And also with me is Megan Wold, former clerk to Justice Alito and partner at Cooper & Kirk law firm.\par \par \uc2 Carrie, I want to start with you. Now the court just handed an issue unmentioned in the Constitution back to the electorate. And the left wants America to believe that we're less democratic, because the people of all 50 states will be able to determine this for themselves. How does that make sense?\par \par \uc2 CARRIE SEVERINO, FORMER JUSTICE THOMAS CLERK: Yes. there's a whole lot of bizarre things that have been said about today's decision. But that's one of the top ones.\par \par \uc2 And people who are saying, well, how could the court make a decision? They have polls, they cite, they say, Well, Americans don't want to be pro-life. Americans want to have access to abortion. I say, Well, great. The court just gave you a decision that says you can all make your own abortion laws.\par \par \uc2 And so the idea that this is somehow going against the American people. I heard \uc2 Alexandria Ocasio-Cortez\uc2  say, this is a victory for the minority being able to govern. I'm like, woman, no, it goes back to the elected - democratically elected branches.\par \par \uc2 So that isn't handing the pro-life movement a win really. It's not the beginning of the end, this is the end of the beginning. This just says, pro lifers now have the opportunity to make their case and in those branches, and we will see what the results are, and they're going to look different all across the country. But it will be democratic.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Now, Robert, we have heard a lot about precedent today, and how the court disrespected precedent. But Justice Alito wrote in his opinion that this court has overruled important constitutional decisions. And without these decisions, American constitutional law, as we know, it would be unrecognizable, and this would be a different country.\par \par \uc2 Overruling a precedent is a serious matter. It's not a step that should be taken lightly. Robert, your response to this assertion that justices, when they were nominated, the nominees lied during their confirmation hearings about precedent?\par \par \uc2 ROBERT DUNN, FORMER JUSTICE CLARENCE THOMAS: Thanks, Laura. Yes. All of the justices in their confirmation hearings made it clear that Roe was a precedent, it had been followed. But that it was on shaky ground. They made no bones about that.\par \par \uc2 And I think Justice Alito does an excellent job in his opinion of explaining that, A, every single member, sitting member of the court has voted to overrule precedent. And in fact, every member of the court for the last 50 years has voted to overrule precedent. He has this phenomenal footnote that goes on for multiple pages, where he lists all of the Supreme Court decisions in the last 50, 60 years that have overruled constitutional precedent.\par \par \uc2 And I think he makes a compelling case that if there was ever a decision that satisfied all the conditions for being overruled, this one is it, and he makes the point. It was egregiously wrong and it was decided under as an originalist matter. It has been totally unworkable, and there are no reliance interests that essentially dictate that it must be cemented in stone forever.\par \par \uc2 And as Carrie put it very well, I think this is going to go back to the states. My home state of California is probably going to join North Korea in being one of the most liberal places on earth to get abortions, places like Texas. It's going to be much more restrictive. But the point is, the people are now going to be able to have the debate and have the argument that we've just have not been able to have as a polis for the last 50 years.\par \par \uc2 And I think that's a positive development. And Biden was - he was correct on one thing, this isn't the last word on abortion. There's going to be a lot more words. It's just that they're not going to be spoken by the justices, they're going to be spoken by the people.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Well, the main thing, Robert, is that it's not a fundamental right enshrined in our Constitution. That's the victory. That was a lie in 1973. And as a result, the millions and millions and millions of babies who never were born. It was a lie, and the lie has been corrected. Now, the states are going to have to decide this for themselves. But that lie has been corrected.\par \par \uc2 And, Megan, Justice Alito also wrote, your old boss, "We don't pretend to know how our political system or society is going to respond to today's decision overruling Roe and Casey. And even if we could foresee what would happen, we would have no authority to let that knowledge influence our decision. We can only do our job."\par \par \uc2 Well, Megan, that is the role of a Supreme Court justice. But today, over and over again, we heard commentators on TV who should know better, want justices to be super legislators, not Supreme Court Justices. they blur the line every time.\par \par \uc2 MEGAN WOLD, FORMER JUSTICE ALITO CLERK: That's absolutely right. And I don't think enough can be said about the integrity of the Justices who reached this decision today, including against some of the most vociferous and open campaigning for judges to act as legislators that we have ever seen in this country.\par \par \uc2 And the reality is that abortion has never been a right protected in the Constitution. And Justice Alito recognized that today. His decision explained just how flimsy and illegitimate the reasoning of Roe was. It was premised on bad legal reasoning. It was premised on faulty history. And it was premised on bad science, that has been completely disproven by developments since Roe was decided.\par \par \uc2 And here's the thing. Abortion advocates have never actually liked Roe anyway. What they liked about Roe was that it delivered them a policy victory that they wanted and that they couldn't get for the entire country at the ballot box. And they've been open about the (inaudible)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Yes. and now, they're in - even \uc2 Ruth Bader Ginsburg\uc2 , she wasn't wild about the reasoning either.\par \par \uc2 And, Carrie, after hearing \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  and so many of the Democrats over the last 18 months really slam the Republicans for tearing down our institutions, not caring about process, obviously focused on January 6 and the election results. I was struck by something that Speaker Pelosi said today. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Even though you don't like process to get these Justices onto the court, which resulted in this decision. Do you respect the process from what they've come out with?\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 NANCY PELOSI\uc2  (D-CA): Some of these justices who came before the Senate said, they respected precedent and privacy. No, I don't respect a process that criminalizes a woman's right to her reproductive freedom.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: So, Carrie, it's not the law of the land. When they don't get the result they want, the whole system, the process is broken. Is that good for our institution, or institutions, or the court itself?\par \par \uc2 SEVERINO: Oh, no. And it's so funny, because the radicals out there are not even pretending anymore to keep out the institution. They're saying, blow up the court as an institution, pack the court, destroy the Justices. They're people advocating for the government to refused and they're already - our governors are saying, I won't enforce the laws in my own state. Attorneys general very state saying this, this is really outrageous that there's just simply saying, Well, I refuse to even be involved in this system.\par \par \uc2 And it is so funny them declaring this decision is illegitimate, and we can't follow it. When they just kind of gave a lecture about stare decisis. It's stare decisis for thee, but not for me. They want everyone else to be locked into their bad, erroneous constitutional precedent. But then when they get a decision they don't like, then they'll throw it out immediately and say, tear down the whole city to start over.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Now, Robert, the \uc2 New York Times\uc2  in their piece about the ruling summed up what Justice Kavanaugh said in his separate decision. Saying, "In addition to declaring that he thinks states can't bar residents from traveling to another state to obtain an abortion, Kavanaugh strongly suggested that he thinks the Constitution requires abortion bans to include an exception when necessary to save the life of the mother."\par \par \uc2 Robert, whatever people think about that as a policy matter, was that unusual for the Supreme Court Justice to get into that level of detail, or did they think that was necessary, given the heat on this particular issue?\par \par \uc2 DUNN: Yes. I think with Justice Kavanaugh, you have to appreciate the fact that given the leak of this opinion that happened several months ago, he has personally been under an immense amount of pressure. He's had protesters out in front of his house. For months, he had a lunatic who tried to assassinate him just last week.\par \par \uc2 So I think he feels, everything that he does is going to be highly scrutinized. And he's trying to do everything he can to assuage the public that the decision doesn't ban abortions nationwide. You go on \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , and you'll see a bunch of people that now think abortion is illegal nationwide, which is obviously not true. And to let them know that the court isn't poised to do more than it has done, which is essentially withdraw itself from the process.\par \par \uc2 So I read Justice Kavanaugh as this is the man who was just - he's sort of been faced with extreme pressure, and he's trying to do everything he can to be clear about what the court is and isn't doing. And really all it's doing right now is withdrawing itself from the process. And that is what he said.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Now, Megan, on this - Yes, well, Megan, the Justice Department is weighing in on Supreme Court decisions, which is kind of unusual, right? I mean, they decided to weigh in on the gun case. Now they decided to weigh in on the abortion case. And basically throw sand at the whole thing and say, in a way they didn't think it was a legitimate or correct ruling. Is that unusual?\par \par \uc2 WOLD: Oh, I think it's highly unusual. And it's another example of what you've said tonight, which is that, they make all these appeals to process and everything's great as long as they're winning. And as soon as they're losing, they're ready to burn it all down.\par \par \uc2 And the reality is that this decision today was fundamentally democratic. It returns the ability to set abortion policy in this country to the American people. And I would just ask those officials at the Department of Justice, what is it that they're afraid of?\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Oh, they're afraid of the people. That's what they're afraid of. They've been afraid of the people for some time.\par \par \uc2 Panel, great to see all of you tonight. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Now, as abortion decisions moved to individual states, many wasted no time with existing trigger laws. Now, one of those states is South Dakota, and its governor \uc2 Kristi Noem\uc2  joins me now.\par \par \uc2 Governor, great to see you tonight. Now, yours is one of the few states making abortion illegal immediately with few exceptions. Your response to those, including the President today, who said it puts women's lives in jeopardy.\par \par \uc2 GOV. KRISTI NOEM (R-SD): Well, Laura, let's remember that today is a wonderful day. Today is a day of good news, something that many people have waited many, many years to have happen that lives would be saved.\par \par \uc2 In South Dakota, we had a trigger law already in statute. And what it said was that abortions would be illegal, except to save the life of a mother at the moment that Roe v. Wade was overturned. So as of today, that trigger law goes into effect. And we are focusing a lot on supporting mothers, on supporting individuals who find themselves in a unplanned pregnancy or crisis situation and making sure that we're connecting them to the resources that we have.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: What is the approach that your state will take in the event that a woman seeks to have a chemical abortion, or abortion through medication, maybe getting medication in the mail? Because you've heard a lot of Democrats today saying that, they will not allow that to stop or be stopped by the states. In other words, I guess, interstate mail and so forth. What will your response to that be?\par \par \uc2 NOEM: Well, the statute and the language that we use will be specific to being utilized for an abortion that is going to be referenced to that does not save the life of a mother. So that's one of the things that I and legislative leaders have been working on.\par \par \uc2 We believe that we'll call into a special session, because of this decision that came out today. And there may be some language that we want to address during that special session. And also, I launched a website today called life.sd.gov, which puts resources out there and information like this to those individuals that are in these situations and connects them to medical help. It connects them to adoptive services, to make sure that people really do have the information that they need at their fingertips.\par \par \uc2 But, Laura, this year, in legislative session, I brought a bill that dealt with chemical abortions to make sure that people were going to be protected from this dangerous situation of getting drugs through the mail, and being unsupervised for that medical procedure.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Will there be potential criminal penalties for doctors and/or women who obtain an abortion in that manner? Or who conducted abortion contrary to state law?\par \par \uc2 NOEM: Yes. Our penalties are focused on the doctor that performs the abortion. I don't think our message here today should ever be on punishing mothers. I would like to see that we focus on making sure that those doctors who knowingly perform abortions when they know it's against the law that they are punished. And that's some of the statute that we'll be looking at as we go into a special session.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Governor Noem, we appreciate it. Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 NOEM: Thank you, Laura.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: And in a meeting of equal minds, press secretary \uc2 Karine Jean- Pierre\uc2  and MSNBC's Nicole Wallace decided to have a chat today. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It's affecting people that cannot afford to go from state-to-state or to another country.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Absolutely. And this decision is now - will ensure that people will be forced into pregnancy. No question about that.\par \par \uc2 NICOLE WALLACE, MSNBC HOST: What actions can protect women in the 19 states with trigger laws?\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE DEPUTY PRESS SECRETARY: Being able to, for the DOJ, to be able to defend women who crossed the state. Also making sure that we're protecting and making available medications so that women have - could be able to make that choice, or the way that we're going to restore Roe is to make sure that Congress acts.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: And then mad Maxine said this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. MAXINE WATERS (D-CA): You see this turnout here, you ain't seen nothing yet. Women are going to control their bodies, no matter how they try to stop this thing. The hell with the Supreme Court. We will defy them.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: And what she said there, that defiance, that anger could be translated into chaos tonight.\par \par \uc2 Let's go now to independent reporter Savannah Hernandez from outside the Supreme Court. Savannah, I understand, you were following a contingent of Antifa tonight. What can you tell us?\par \par \uc2 SAVANNAH HERNANDEZ, INDEPENDENT JOURNALIST: Laura, what I'm seeing here on the ground is basically the biggest tantrum I've ever seen in my life. I was following Antifa for a while, and they're basically just hijacking this movement. And they're trying to make it about abortion, but also hating police officers, but also burning the American flag and crapping on our country.\par \par \uc2 The people in the group don't even know what they're fighting for. So, again, just a mass tantrum. And another example of how Antifa is trying to utilize this event and hijack this outrage. I went in, I talked to several women in the group. And I was like, why are you upset? And they were like, Well, because pregnancy is equivalent to slavery, because women don't control their own body, similar to what \uc2 Maxine Waters\uc2  is saying.\par \par \uc2 And I would just counter that by saying, women do control their bodies. And it's so easy to dispute these people, which is why they're out in mass just having a tantrum, because they know that they've been fighting to defend democracy. And democracy is now in action, and they cannot stand it.\par \par \uc2 So Antifa is marching around the streets of DC. They're trying to shut things down. But there's a big police presence. And to be quite honest, they're fairly low energy for tonight. So that's what we're seeing as of now, but it's still very early.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Yes. Savannah, I'm looking at them with their goofy umbrellas, they just look like clowns. I mean, they lost. OK, 50 years ago, the pro- life movement lost. And they worked and worked and worked. And why doesn't Antifa just go to the states where it's going to be restrictive, like South Dakota? And they can petition the government and elect pro-choice state legislators?\par \par \uc2 But they don't - they're not interested in the process. They want to tear the process down. And that's what you're seeing on the streets tonight. It's nothing to do with - OK, we're going to fight in the trenches like democratically. They want to rip things down and terrorize people, including Supreme Court justices.\par \par \uc2 Did you hear anything about any of those individuals going over to some of the Justices' homes tonight?\par \par \uc2 HERNANDEZ: Oh, 100 percent.\par \par \uc2 Number one, the Justices are being doxxed all over \uc2 Twitter\uc2  right now, unfortunately. And, of course, big tech doesn't care about that. They're going to go ahead and leave that up. It's already been up for hours.\par \par \uc2 Second off, many people in the crowd chanting about Kavanaugh, Alito, \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2 , of course, saying that they're going to go to their homes that they're going to target them. Abort the court is another chant that they have been saying all night.\par \par \uc2 Again, they're calling for violence. They've taken to the streets. They're saying, if we don't get it, burn it down. Regarding, I guess, reversing this decision to overturn Roe, so they're calling for violence. Of course, if the right was doing this, it would be an insurrection and MSNBC and CNN would be up in arms. But when the left does it, it's ok. It's just people fighting for democracy.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Now, these heroes like to do things like target volunteers at crisis pregnancy centers and terrorize them. They're such good people. Savannah, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Now, so which party has really shifted on the issue of abortion? Well, one particular native of Scranton, PA, tells the story for us.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Today, the Supreme Court the United States expressly took away the constitutional right from the American people. I do not view abortion as a choice and a right.\par \par \uc2 They didn't limit it. They simply took it away. I think we should be focusing on how to limit the number of abortions.\par \par \uc2 I will do all in my power to protect a woman's right in states where they will face the consequences of today's decision.\par \par \uc2 I think it's always a tragedy, and I think that it should be rare.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Joining us now, Monica Crowley, former assistant secretary of the Treasury and host "The Monica Crowley Podcast," and Dr. Ben Carson, former HUD secretary, presidential candidate, doctor, neurosurgeon, founder and chairman of the American Cornerstone Institute. Monica, let's start with you. The left's unhinged reaction in the streets today, today's ruling, I think kind of tells the story.\par \par \uc2 MONICA CROWLEY, FORMER ASSISTANT SECRETARY OF TREASURY: Yes, and not a surprise at all, Laura, given what we know about the left. They will always look for a pretext to try to tear the country down, whether it's the George Floyd murder, or this Roe v. Wade decision. So again, no surprise, and I think we can anticipate more of this because they are literally at war with all of our institutions, and so they will look for any chance to tear them down.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Dr. Carson, just your reaction tonight after 50 years of toiling away, being ridiculed, written off, social conservatives where kind of laughed at by Hollywood and ridiculed by all the elites. To have this day that a lot of people never thought would, how impactful is this, how important is this, how significant?\par \par \uc2 DR. BEN CARSON, AMERICAN CORNERSTONE INSTITUTE: It's a monumental day. I'm very happy to see it come. Realizing, of course, that this is really just the beginning. And we must continue to emphasize the fact that what the Supreme Court did today was reestablish the way things are supposed to be done in our democratic republic. Life and death decisions, monumental decisions, are to be in the hands of the people and the people who represent them, not in the hands of unelected justices. And that was the way it was supposed to be. That has been reestablished. Nobody's rights have been taken away. We've just recalibrate in the way that it should be done.\par \par \uc2 And that's so vitally important. It's also important for people to realize that we have to take into consideration the women who are being affected now. And we need to make it easier for adoptions to occur. It's incredibly expensive and cumbersome at this stage of the game, and other types of support mechanisms so that these women can become self-sufficient and teach that to their children and break some of these terrible cycles.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Exactly, support crisis pregnancy centers, don't bomb them or deface them or defame them. And Monica, Chuck Todd neatly summed up the media mindset today. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHUCK TODD, NBC HOST: How \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  appointed the Supreme Court, how we went about it, particularly in the whole Scalia thing with McConnell and all that. There really is a lot of people who believe this is a rigged court.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Monica, it seems that you're only able to claim things are rigged if it doesn't suit the left. When other people say things are rigged, they just castigate them endlessly. But I guess they get to say things are rigged.\par \par \uc2 CROWLEY: Well, the left, Laura, is a revolutionary movement, and it's also a movement of bullies and it's also a death cult. So we should not be surprised by these kinds of statements. Whether it's elections, or whether it's Supreme Court decisions or anything else, when things don't go their way, because remember, they are solely focused on a fundamental transformation of the nation. So when there is an obstacle placed in their path like a Roe v. Wade reversal, they go absolutely bananas, and they do it publicly, right, because the whole purpose is to tear down the Constitution and all of our institutions.\par \par \uc2 So any kind of opposition has to be destroyed, whether it's in the person of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , whether it's in the institution of the Supreme Court, they focus on the absolute destruction. They've got to clear the path in order to reengineer and remake the country.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Dr. Carson, I have to get your reaction to something that Congresswoman Barbara Lee said today.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. BARBARA LEE, (D-CA): We don't have the money because were primarily low-wage workers, so how in the world are we even going to travel even without travel ban? And so yes, this is impacting rural women, younger woman, women of color. It's impacting black and brown people in disproportionate rates, and so you understand the racist nature also of this decision.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Dr. Carson, it's racist too.\par \par \uc2 CARSON: Isn't it interesting how everything comes back to race. We always circle around and use that as a cudgel to beat people down. And this is not a racial issue other than the fact that Margaret Sanger was a eugenicist and put these clinics primarily in black and Hispanic neighborhoods in order to eliminate the people that she didn't think should be around. If there is anything that's racist, that's what it is. That's what we should be looking at.\par \par \uc2 But let's come back to how important life is. Let's once again become a culture of life, not a culture of death. All you have to do is see what an abortion is in the first trimester, sucking up babies in the second trimester, putting instruments in, tearing their limbs apart, pulling them out. This is barbaric. And this is not what we should be doing. This is not America.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: And Monica, just coming back to President Trump for a moment, he was just roundly accused of being a fake conservative by many so-called stalwart conservatives in the 2016 campaign. They said he was going to be bad for the pro-life movement, he wasn't going to be true to it, it was all an act. Where are the apologies from those people, given what was accomplished just on the issue of taking this out of the constitutional context and putting it back to the states?\par \par \uc2 CROWLEY: President Trump has been completely vindicated. Remember 2015, 2016 when so-called conservatives, Laura, were all up in arms about how appalling \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was and how he wasn't a true conservative. He is only Republican president from \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2 , who also was one, but \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  actually put his money where his mouth was and appointed pro-life justices. You nominate a justice, you have no idea how they are going to behave on the court. He actually chose three that stood up for the Constitution, the rule of law, and life. And he will go down in history as the greatest president -- this is, he's got some great legacies Laura, but this legacy of life, reversing Roe v. Wade, has him down in history books for all time.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: You know who I think of tonight as well, Dr. Carson, is I think of Justice Scalia and all that he did in the course of his career for originalism, textualism, his faith to the actual language of the Constitution and constitutional principles. And you have to think that after the Casey decision in the early 1990s and losing the Webster decision, I'd like to think he is smiling and looking down on this day, the Dobbs case.\par \par \uc2 CARSON: Absolutely. He was a true patriot.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: I'm thinking of him tonight.\par \par \uc2 CARSON: He was a true patriot, and so are some of the others who are being brave. You have to be incredibly courageous. And we must remember we cannot be the land of the free if we're not the home of the brave. And I want, as Monica did, express thanks to President Trump who was very diligent in the way that these people were appointed. And we got people who were courageous and would stand firm for the principles of life.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: And also \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  for keeping \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  off the court. My God, that would've been a disastrous Supreme Court justice pick. So I can criticize McConnell on a lot of things, but he was fantastic on the court stuff, really, really important.\par \par \uc2 Monica, Dr. Ben Carson, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Now let's head back to the court where our own Kevin Corke is standing by. Kevin what can you tell us?\par \par \uc2 KEVIN CORKE, FOX NEWS WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Laura, a very interesting night to be out here at the court. Very different vibe then what we saw earlier today. Everything was nice and calm. And I can report tonight it still remains calm, although the crowd is a little bit different, and that happens once the sun goes. We covered protests not just here at the court but all over the country, and that's usually the case.\par \par \uc2 I can also tell you that despite the fact that we've had at points over 1,000 people here, it has been a relatively cool, calm evening, people chanting, holding up signs, even some people praying. Earlier you saw many pro-life demonstrators here. But for the most part here tonight it has been pro-abortion protesters here tonight. And I can only say this whenever I come out here to the court, there's a magnitude to being out here when there's a big case, and you certainly feel that here tonight.\par \par \uc2 But again, so far peaceful, so for spirited, and we'll keep an eye on things. Laura, back to you.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Kevin, thank you.\par \par \uc2 We're getting continue to monitor events across the country tonight, but for his take on the media reaction to today's Supreme Court ruling and more, we're going to go to FOX News contributor Raymond Arroyo tonight. Ray, this is an issue you've covered for so many years on television and written about. The media, they have been in complete meltdown mode from the moment Roe v. Wade was overturned this morning, and it has been nonstop all day.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We do just have breaking news into CNN.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: A conservative majority on the Supreme Court has completely transformed for the worse the lives of all American women.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: This is a court not of judges but of politicians in judges' robes.\par \par \uc2 CHUCK TODD, NBC HOST: I think there is no more black robes. I think the red and blue.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The originalists are winning.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I'm getting -- watching the women there, it's emotional.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: We have a whole different crop of Republican appointed justices compared to back in the 90s, 70s and 80s.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RAYMOND ARROYO, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Now, Laura, everybody has to be fair here.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Everybody -- yes, go ahead.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: I don't recall this kind of analysis claiming that judges were politicians or partisans when Obamacare was affirmed, or the gay marriage decision was handed down. Americans accepted it. But what do you make of that comment that these Republican justices are different from those that went before? I'm interested in your take on that one.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Yes, OK, you know why they're different? For the same reason Democrats loved \uc2 John McCain\uc2  and they love \uc2 Mitt Romney\uc2 , love \uc2 Adam Kinzinger\uc2  and they love \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2 , OK? Because they trash conservative principles on key and fundamental issues. So as long as you're not acting as a constitutional conservative when it comes the court, they are going to love you. They thin, you're enlightened, you're mainstream, you're compassionate. But if you actually just follow the Constitution, which most of these people don't much like, then you're a horrible, awful, rotten, racist person. So that's just a rule of thumb for you.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Trump nominated three of the Supreme Court justices in the majority today. And as you previewed in the Angle, Laura, \uc2 Sam Alito\uc2  might not have had the opportunity to write this Dobbs opinion were it not for the fact that a certain commentator named Laura Ingraham took on Supreme Court nominee Harriet Miers, challenged her record and President Bush back in 2005. Remember this?\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: The burden really is on him to show us why Harriet Miers is a judicial conservative.\par \par \uc2 Given what he said in 1993 about abortion, given what she said about the rule of the courts, how could she square those comments with what she said in 1989 in that questionnaire where she said I'm for a pro-life amendment to the Texas constitution?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: And as you know, President Bush rescinded her nomination and nominated \uc2 Sam Alito\uc2 , which wouldn't have happened had it not been for your activism. But judicial philosophy matters --\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: I can't believe you found that.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: -- and that was your gripe with her.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: It was a tabula rasa. We didn't know what she thought. And we didn't know what Justice Souter was going to, and he ended up going with the liberals. And you can't have a tabula rasa. You have to have a judicial philosophy. She had none.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: And Laura, \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  was not having a good day today.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 NANCY PELOSI\uc2 , (D-CA) HOUSE SPEAKER: There's no point in saying good morning because it certainly is not one.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Pelosi continued her cheery mood by reading a poem from an Israeli poet. For a speaker with familiarity changing faces, this seemed on brand.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 NANCY PELOSI\uc2 , (D-CA) HOUSE SPEAKER: I will not be silent, for my country has changed her face. My country has changed her face. I shall not give up on her.\par \par \uc2 A woman's fundamental health decisions are her own to make in consultation with her doctor, her faith, her family, not some rightwing politicians that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  packed the court with.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: What the speaker fails to tell everyone here is women still have that option. Through the state legislatures, abortions will either be available or not available in those states. It's allowing the people to regulate it.\par \par \uc2 But look, here's the good news, Laura. Today we did find common ground. Roe v. Wade was aborted, and \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  finally found an abortion that she objected to. That may be progress.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Oh, my gosh. Pelosi's plan, Raymond, to get up there and read and like all sour-faced about it, I don't think that helps Democrats one bit. They think they're going to get some big bump out of this. I don't think the American people are going to want to put up with a lousy economy, and $6.00 gas prices, and a gerontocracy that just can't govern so they can get more abortions. I don't see people going to the polls for that choice.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: You can see it in the crowds tonight, Laura. When you see the footage of the crowd, it's a lackluster shuffle. There is no energy in these protests happening across the country, and no real dynamism. The pro- life movement had been in spades for 50 years, and it's through their prayer and activism that this moment has arrived in large part.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: The Antifa crowd, Raymond, even their masks were beginning to slip off their faces, tonight, those tedious black masks.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: They were a little sleepy tonight.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Aren't you tired of those costumes? Keep in the circus. It's absolutely ridiculous. Raymond, thank you, great to see you.\par \par \uc2 And CNN's Ana Navarro with one of the most ghoulish comments we've ever heard. Congresswoman Kat Cammack, who has a personal stake in this abortion debate, she's here to respond, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ANA NAVARRO, CNN POLITICAL COMMENTATOR: I have a brother who is 57 and has the mental and motor skills of a one-year-old. I have a step granddaughter who was born with Down syndrome. And I've got another step grandson who is very autistic. Their mothers and people who are in that society and that community will tell you that they considered suicide because that's how difficult it is to get help.\par \par \uc2 How can I be Catholic and still think this is a wrong decision? Because I'm American.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: How disgusting, using her own family members as examples of what? Those who should have been aborted and weren't? But this is personal and not merely political to so many people, including our next gas. When Congresswoman Kat Cammack's mother was pregnant with her, she was advised by doctors to have an abortion. But her mother declined, chose life, and now her daughter is not only the youngest female Republican in Congress but the co-chair of the pro-life caucus. Congresswoman, it's great to see you tonight. I'm sure you can appreciate a day like this better than most. Talk about that.\par \par \uc2 REP. KAT CAMMACK, (R-FL) MOTHER WAS ADVISED TO ABORT HER: Absolutely. This has been an incredible day, one that we've talked about for so long. And I say to so many people that the pro-life movement is so important to me not only for my first personal story and that of my family, but because pro- life movement, the pro-life generation that I represent, we're here today, and we believe in this movement in large part because of science. The law has finally caught up to the science.\par \par \uc2 And today was a momentous day, an incredible victory for the sanctity of life but also the sanctity of our Constitution. And I know so many people, so many advocates across this country are sleeping soundly knowing that their years of hard work and prayer and every effort that they have given to this cause will be continued this save lives and provide a better voice, a louder voice for Americans all across this country and communities as this issue returns back to the states.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Now, listen to some of your fellow Congresswomen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. JACKIE SPEIER, (D-CA): There is a war out there, and we need to recognize that we've got to armor up.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 AYANNA PRESSLEY\uc2 , (D-MA): That's right. This is not a drill. This sets a dangerous precedent that would allow the court to undermine other fundamental human rights. There's no low that these people won't go.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: This is war time, it's time to armor up. What, what is that?\par \par \uc2 CAMMACK: It was a pretty wild experience being on Capitol Hill today, Laura. I was walking past camera crews, where some of my colleagues were doing interviews, saying comments like that, saying ridiculous statements like we heard Ms. Navarro commenting about the need for abortion so that families could abort a potentially special needs child, which is incredibly beyond the pale and disgusting beyond unbelief.\par \par \uc2 I heard other comments like this is restricting health care to women. That's not true. We all know that abortion is not health care. And I look back on the story for my own life, how my mom had had a stroke at the age of 27 and was told then she would never be able to have kids again. And for sure she would not survive nor would I. And so years later she finds herself in a very precarious situation, and she chooses life. She makes that brave decision against all odds, against the advice of doctors.\par \par \uc2 And so I look at this situation, these awful, horrific comments where my colleagues are saying this is health care. No, that's not health care. We are a country that is based on life, liberty, and the pursuit of happiness, and it starts with life. So today has been an incredible day. It returns this issue to the states where it should have always been, and I couldn't be more proud of the efforts of the pro-life community.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Congresswoman, so great to see you tonight, we really, really appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 And up next, six years ago \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  made a prediction. We're to share it with you. The Last Bite is next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS WALLACE, CNN ANCHOR: What I'm asking you, sir, is, do you want to see the Court overturn -- you just said you want to see the Court protect the Second Amendment. Do you want to see the Court overturn Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER U.S. PRESIDENT: Well, if we put another two or perhaps three justices on, that's really what's going to be -- that will happen. And that will happen automatically in my opinion, because I am putting pro-life justices on the court. I will say this. It will go back to the states, and the states will then make a determination.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Just another thing that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  predicted correctly. A final thank you to President Trump for everything he did to make this night possible. Remember, the Constitution matters, the words matter, democracy works, and the people get to decide.\par \par \uc2 Remember, it's America now and forever. "GUTFELD!" next.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document INGANG0020220625ei6o00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Supreme Court Overturns Roe Vs. Wade; President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  Condemns Supreme Court Decision On Abortion Ruling; Protection For Justices After Roe Ruling; Bill Awaits Biden Signature After Passing In Congress; House Passes Bipartisan Gun Safety Legislation; Senators Criticize Supreme Court Justices Who Overturned Roe Versus Wade As Providing Misleading Testimony During Nomination Hearings; Some States Look To Ban Abortion In Wake Of Supreme Court Decision While Other States Vow To Protect Abortion Rights; President Biden Urges Americans To Vote In Midterms As Possible Political Remedy For Supreme Court Decision Overturning Roe Versus Wade\b0\par\par\uc2 Bret Baier, Aishah Hasnie, Alexandria Hoff, Shannon Bream, Chad  Pergram, David Spunt, Mike Tobin \par \uc2 7,719 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 June 2022\par \uc2 Fox News: Special Report with Brett Baier\par \uc2 FNSPBB\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 MARTHA MACCALLUM, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: We're going to dig into that.\par \par \uc2 DANA PERINO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: And also, you could go back to the Bush administration and Alito and that one was also a transformative you could say.\par \par \uc2 I'll be on \uc2 Tucker Carlson\uc2  at 8:00 p.m. as well, so you're going to just keep seeing us all night long.\par \par \uc2 That's it for us though. Now, we will see you back here on Monday. Bye.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: This is a sad day for the country in my view.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: And now, it's just a great day for the country. Great day for life.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I'm grateful I'm grateful to the Lord the Roe vs. Wade has been overturned.\par \par \uc2 REP. MAXINE WATERS (D-CA): They hell with the Supreme Court, we will defy them.\par \par \uc2 REP. KEVIN MCCARTHY (R-CA): Threats of violence against the justices did not win.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Good evening, I'm Bret Baier.\par \par \uc2 Breaking tonight, history from the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2 . Justices voted to overturn the landmark Roe vs. Wade decision. Today's ruling ends nearly 50 years of federal legal protection for abortion. The ruling finds there never was a right to abortion in the U.S. Constitution, and they were returned to the authority to regulate abortion to the states. Many of those already have restrictive laws. Others have passed laws that were triggered by today's ruling and still other states are taking steps to protect abortion rights following the decision.\par \par \uc2 Security around the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  is tight tonight as crowds had swelled throughout the day. Precautions are being taken at abortion counseling facilities across the nation. Some abortion clinics are shutting down.\par \par \uc2 Political reaction as you heard some of it, forming along predictable lines. A visibly angry President Biden calls it the realization of an extreme ideology and a tragic error. Pro-life advocates say the decision will save countless lives and is the result of five decades of effort.\par \par \uc2 We have extensive coverage tonight with reporting and analysis from a variety of journalists and experts. We begin though with Fox News Chief Legal Correspondent anchor of "FOX NEWS @ NIGHT", Shannon Bream at the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2 . Good evening, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 SHANNON BREAM, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CHIEF LEGAL CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Hello, Bret. Well, in contrast to what normally happens here after a big opinion, the crowds have not dissipated. If anything, they've only grown as have their chance about what they say is the loss of a constitutional right.\par \par \uc2 But what the majority of the court held today is that the constitutional right to an abortion never actually existed.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM (voice over): Nearly half a century after Roe v. Wade, the Supreme Court overturned the precedent critics and some supporters, including the late Justice \uc2 Ruth Bader Ginsburg\uc2  had warned was long on shaky legal ground.\par \par \uc2 Writing for the majority, Justice Alito called Roe's reasoning "exceptionally weak". Justices Thomas, Gorsuch, Kavanaugh and Barrett joined him and ultimately holding: "The Constitution does not prohibit the citizens of each state from regulating or prohibiting abortion. Roe and Casey arrogated that authority. We now overrule those decisions."\par \par \uc2 Justices Breyer, Kagan and Sotomayor jointly signed their dissent, warning about the practical implications.\par \par \uc2 Today, the court says that from the very moment of fertilization, a woman has no rights to speak up, as they can force her to bring a pregnancy to term even at the steepest personal and familial costs.\par \par \uc2 Across the country, numerous states have already acted in anticipation of today's decision. The trio of dissenters warned not only about state actions, but also the damage they believe the majority has done to the court itself.\par \par \uc2 One of us once said that it is not often in the law that so few have so quickly changed so much. For all of us in our time on this court, that has never been more true than today. In overruling Roe and Casey, this court betrays its guiding principles.\par \par \uc2 Chief Justice Roberts tried to find the middle ground. Both the court's opinion and the dissent display a relentless freedom from doubts on the legal issue that I cannot share. I would decide the question we granted review to answer and there is no need to go further to decide this case.\par \par \uc2 Democrats, including the president are calling for a political response at the polls, suggesting this opinion means that rights like contraception and same sex marriage are now in danger.\par \par \uc2 Likely in reference to Justice Thomas's solo concurrence. "I agree that nothing in the court's opinion should be understood to cast doubt on precedents that do not concern abortion. For that reason, in future cases, we should reconsider all of this court's substantive due process precedents, including Griswold, Lawrence and Obergefell."\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: This extreme and dangerous path to corner is now taking us on.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: But that does not align with the majority opinion in which Justice Alito writes: rights regarding contraception and same sex relationships are inherently different from the right to abortion because the latter, as we have stressed uniquely involves what Roe and Casey termed "potential life". It is hard to see how we could be clearer.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM (on camera): It is interesting to note that despite big decisions yesterday on guns, today on abortion, the courtroom is actually not over. There are seven more opinions to come. We will get the next batch 10:00 a.m. Eastern on Monday morning. They include issues on abortion -- excuse me, immigration.\par \par \uc2 The reach of the (INAUDIBLE) also that case involving the high school football coach out in Washington state who refuse to quit praying on the 50 yard line and no longer has that job, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Shannon Bream live outside the Supreme Court. Shannon, thank you.\par \par \uc2 President Biden unloaded on the justices during an emotional response at the White House to the decision.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent Alexandria Hoff shows us tonight from the North Lawn of the White House. Good evening, Alex.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDRIA HOFF, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Good evening, Bret. Yes, the president did indeed call this decision today the realization of an extreme ideology and one that he says he has no power to undo.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: It is not hyperbole to suggest, a very solemn moment.\par \par \uc2 HOFF (voice over): President Biden said this afternoon that the Supreme Court's decision expressly took away a constitutional right and threatens the health and lives of women.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: This decision is a culmination of a deliberate effort over decades to upset balance of our law.\par \par \uc2 HOFF: President Biden called out former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  saying that the three justices named by Trump: Kavanaugh, Gorsuch and Barrett, were at the core of the decision to "up end the scales of justice".\par \par \uc2 When the former president was asked if he takes any credit, he told Fox News: God made the decision.\par \par \uc2 President Biden's own views on abortion have shifted throughout the years as recently as 2006, he said this.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I do not view abortion as a choice and a right, I think it's always a tragedy.\par \par \uc2 HOFF: But the president's stance became more progressive and in light of Roe's reversal today, Biden cast abortion rights as a central issue of November's midterm elections.\par \par \uc2 MCCARTHY: The right to life has been vindicated. The voiceless will finally have a voice.\par \par \uc2 HOFF: Reaction among Republicans has been overwhelmingly celebratory.\par \par \uc2 SEN. TED CRUZ (R-TX): This is a momentous moment.\par \par \uc2 REP. MAYRA FLORES (R-TX): I woke up this morning praying for this. And I never thought that it would happen.\par \par \uc2 HOFF: But Republican Senator \uc2 Susan Collins\uc2  released this statement reading in part, this decision is inconsistent with what Justices Gorsuch and Kavanaugh said in their testimony and their meetings with me.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 NANCY PELOSI\uc2  (D-CA): There's no point in saying good morning, because it certainly is not one.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 CHUCK SCHUMER\uc2  (D-NY): Today is one of the darkest days our country has ever seen.\par \par \uc2 HOFF: On the left, lawmakers mourned nearly five decades of precedent and vow to use the decision to get out the vote in November.\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: With your vote, you can act and you have the final word. So, this is not over.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HOFF (on camera): President Biden also vowed today to continue on access to abortion related medication as well as contraception and a woman's right to travel to terminate a pregnancy, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Alexandria Hoff live on the North Lawn. Alexandria, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Let's get some expert analysis right now. Carrie Severino is president of the Judicial Crisis Network, Elizabeth Rodriguez, president of the Constitutional Accountability Center. Ladies, thanks for being here.\par \par \uc2 Elizabeth, first to you. You know, this does track with the leaked draft opinion we had weeks ago. However, it wasn't five-four, it was six-three, anything surprise you in this opinion?\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH RODRIGUEZ, PRESIDENT, CONSTITUTIONAL ACCOUNTABILITY CENTER: So, nothing really surprised me in the majority opinion. It did track closely with that leaked Alito draft. I was surprised, frankly, that Justice Thomas and his concurring opinion was so open in wanting to reconsider precedent that protects the right to choose contraception for married couples and others, marriage equality for LGBTQ, couples and other fundamental rights that, you know, people feared would be an issue if abortion rights fell, but I was surprised he was so open about that.\par \par \uc2 I just simply think that the majority got the Constitution wrong. The Constitution protects a fundamental irreducible minimum of liberty that was intended to include the right to decide for oneself, whether with whom and when to form a family. And the right to choose abortion is inherent in that constellation of rights.\par \par \uc2 When the 14th Amendment was passed, it was passed after the Civil War, when the question of what it meant to be free was urgent and pressing as a matter of law, and there's sweeping protections for equal protection of the law and liberty contained in the 14th Amendment, protect rights including bodily autonomy and the decision of whether when and with whom to form a family\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Carrie, I want to get your overall opinion. But I want to specifically address what Elizabeth mentioned there about Justice Thomas.\par \par \uc2 You know, Justice Alito, in the majority opinion, writes we have stated unequivocally that nothing in this opinion should be understood to cast doubt on precedents that do not concern abortion.\par \par \uc2 Justice Kavanaugh also writing: I emphasize what the court today states: overruling Roe does not mean their overruling of those precedents, and does not threaten or cast doubt on those precedents.\par \par \uc2 Seeming to say despite what Justice Thomas said, we're not going down that other road.\par \par \uc2 CARRIE SEVERINO, PRESIDENT, JUDICIAL CRISIS NETWORK: Well, Justice Thomas himself says we're not going down that road as Shannon quoted earlier. He said this case doesn't address those issues. It's no surprise that he thinks we should reconsider them because the idea of subsidy to process is something he's long, a bit concerned about, not because of the policy results of those cases, but just simply because of the legal underpinnings.\par \par \uc2 But, you know, I think Elizabeth is right, what we should be looking at is what the ratifiers, the 14th Amendment thought they were doing here and clearly because it was -- abortion was illegal throughout the country at that time.\par \par \uc2 They didn't -- they thought that this meant the equal protection of the laws, they wanted to free slaves, absolutely, all of those things had nothing to do with abortion.\par \par \uc2 The Constitution does not create this right. And I'm so glad and relieved that we have a court that understands its job isn't to try to make decisions on these controversial issues for the entire country. Their job isn't to try to make the call between what's the right and wrong way to address the issue. Their job is to simply say, does the Constitution speak to it? If not, it goes to the elected branches and Congress and the states are going to have a right to do this.\par \par \uc2 In the face of all of the hostility, the threats, the violence, even assassination attempts that they were willing to stand by what they knew was constitutionally required. This is a huge victory for the rule of law.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Quickly, Elizabeth, Speaker Pelosi said in her news conference today that it's a contradiction in her mind that the Supreme Court said you can't make laws governing the right to bear arms, the states can't. And they're saying -- she said the reverse the states can overturn a constitutional right for 50 years that she saw an abortion.\par \par \uc2 But the right to bear arms is in the Constitution. The right these justices found the right to abortion is not, is that the difference?\par \par \uc2 RODRIGUEZ: You know, I think honestly, the question is what does the Constitution mean? And obviously, there's an interpretation about what the Second Amendment means which the Second Amendment is not about an individual right, that's not what the text says. That's the interpretation.\par \par \uc2 Similarly, you look to the 14th amendment, and you look at the equal protection of the laws. And what does that mean?\par \par \uc2 And the point about states banning -- in some states banning abortion at the time of the 14th Amendment, the 14th amendment was intended to disrupt unjust and unequal state practices. That was when we were coming out of a civil war, when half the states were slave and half the states were free.\par \par \uc2 The 14th Amendment was intended to move forward along the arc of progress. And that's what we've done when we applied the 14th Amendment in the case of interracial marriage, in the case of marriage equality, in the case of other rights of heartland home, that have protected individual's rights to make these decisions for themselves.\par \par \uc2 And Americans know that one cannot be a free and equal citizen in society if they can't make those decisions for themselves. And that's why the majority of Americans support abortion being legal.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Well, now it's up to those Americans in those states to make those decisions. Carrie, Elizabeth, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Let's look at what is being done now to maintain security for the justices on the High Court. Senators have been rebuffed so far in asking for emergency funding and for existing laws to be enforced.\par \par \uc2 Congressional Correspondent Aishah Hasnie has that part of the story from Capitol Hill.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I call on everyone to keep all protests peaceful, peaceful, peaceful, peaceful.\par \par \uc2 AISHAH HASNIE, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONGRESSIONAL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): Tonight, the President is calling on Americans to refrain from violence as calls for pro-choice protests spread nationwide, some of those calls coming from members of Congress.\par \par \uc2 REP. ALEXANDRIA OCASIO-CORTEZ (D-NY): We have to fill the street. Right now elections are not enough.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: Protests outside the justice's homes which are illegal under a federal statute have been ongoing since the draft court opinion leaked in May.\par \par \uc2 And just this month, police arrested an armed man outside the home of Justice \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2  for threatening to assassinate him. He has pleaded not guilty.\par \par \uc2 And yet, Congress is heading home soon for July break without passing emergency funding to help protect the justices.\par \par \uc2 A bipartisan bill sponsored by Senators Bill Hagerty and Mark Warner passed unanimously in the Senate this week, but it has not been scheduled for a vote in the House yet.\par \par \uc2 The bill would have delivered $10.3 million to the U.S. Marshal Service and 9.1 million to the Supreme Court to address unprecedented threats.\par \par \uc2 SE. BILL HAGERTY (R-TN): If Congress doesn't immediately provide this funding, the court and marshal services may be forced to transfer funds from other critical functions that's an unacceptable outcome.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: GOP aides tell Fox, the Court and the U.S. Marshals Service are currently spending money on security needs that were not planned, including overtime pay.\par \par \uc2 Leader \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  warned ahead of the Supreme Court ruling that time is running out, pointing to the pro-choice group Jane's Revenge and its upcoming plans.\par \par \uc2 SEN. MITCH MCCONNELL (R-KY): One activist group is promising that our cities will be submerged and quote, listen to this. "A night of rage."\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HASNIE (on camera): And Bret, the concerns about what might happen next are so great that Democratic Senator Mark Warner even used January 6 to essentially warn other colleagues in the House of what might happen if they do not prepare for threats. Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Aishah Hasnie live on the Hill. Aishah, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, we'll take a look at what is ahead for the state's following today's landmark Roe v. Wade reversal. Plus, up next, big news in Washington about gun reform legislation.\par \par \uc2 We'll tell you why history is being made there too.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Quickly, there are other stories. Here is some other headlines we're following. \uc2 The Centers for Disease Control and Prevention\uc2 , recommending \uc2 Moderna\uc2  shots as an option for school-aged kids and teens.\par \par \uc2 This group has been able to get shots made by \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  since last year. Last week, the \uc2 FDA\uc2  authorized full strength doses for children ages 12 to 17. Half strength for those 6 to 11.\par \par \uc2 A federal court has temporarily blocked the government's order for \uc2 Juul\uc2  to stop selling its electronic cigarettes. \uc2 Juul\uc2  had filed an emergency motion earlier today so it can appeal the sales ban.\par \par \uc2 Yesterday, we told you the \uc2 Food and Drug Administration\uc2  ordered \uc2 Juul\uc2  to pull its products from shelves over accusations it's fueled a surge in youth vaping with its marketing.\par \par \uc2 And Ukrainian forces will retreat from a besieged city in the country's east to move to stronger positions. The plan withdrawal from that city comes after relentless Russian bombardment that has reduced most of that sitting in the Donbas to rubble as the war entered its fifth month.\par \par \uc2 BAIER (on camera): On Wall Street, a big day today. The Dow rallied to 823 points. The S&P 500 gained 116. The NASDAQ jumped 375 today. For the week, the Dow gained 5-4/10 of a percentage point. The S&P 500 surged almost 6- 1/2. The NASDAQ was up 7-1/2.\par \par \uc2 Congress has approved a wide ranging gun control bill following a series of deadly mass shootings across the nation. But the bipartisan measure has plenty of critics on both sides of the gun issue.\par \par \uc2 Congressional Correspondent Chad Pergram takes a look tonight from Capitol Hill. Good evening, Chad.\par \par \uc2 CHAD PERGRAM, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONGRESSIONAL CORRESPONDENT (voice-over): Good evening, Bret.\par \par \uc2 It's now destined for law. The House synched up with the Senate on its bipartisan plan to curb gun violence. The vote 234 to 193, only 15 Republicans voted yes. All Democrats voted yes. But progressives were skeptical.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. NORMA TORRES (D-CA): We are finally here this morning to take up this miserable legislation sent over to us from the Senate last night. This bill before us today is the bare minimum and we should be embarrassed.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: The bill ignored liberal demands. There is no assault weapons ban and no limit on high capacity magazines.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 NANCY PELOSI\uc2  (D-CA): I have to say that this bill doesn't do everything we would like to do. We need to do more on background checks. Let us not judge this legislation for what it's not in it. But respect it for what it does.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM (on camera): The package expands background checks for those between the ages of 18 and 21. It also increases school security. But most Republicans warn that the bill was a great first step despite its narrow scope.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. JIM JORDAN (R-OH): Today they're coming after our Second Amendment liberties and who knows what it will be tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM (voice-over): GOP Representative Tony Gonzales voted yes. He represents Uvalde. Gonzales says he voted yes, because his district wanted him to deliver solutions after the shooting.\par \par \uc2 15 GOP senators voted yay last night, staunch advocates of the Second Amendment are fuming at senators who supported the bill.\par \par \uc2 REP. THOMAS MASSIE (R-KY): The American people shouldn't be subject to kangaroo court proceedings because a handful of senators use an artificial deadline in rushing to pass a law for political reasons.\par \par \uc2 The senators threw some words on paper, hoping nobody would notice what a terrible and ambiguous scheme they were creating.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM (on camera): This is the first major gun bill to pass Congress since the Brady Bill in late 1993. It imposed a waiting period and background checks. Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Chad Pergram live on the Hill. Chad, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Up next, our panel of legal experts explains what is ahead for the state's following today's landmark Roe v. Wade reversal.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Today's \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  decision striking down the Roe v. Wade abortion ruling sets in motion a variety of moves at the state level.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent David Spunt is outside the High Court tonight to tell us what's on the horizon.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I'm really excited.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I've devastated.\par \par \uc2 DAVID SPUNT, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Within minutes of the justices' decision, the question, what's next?\par \par \uc2 CROWD: Legal abortion on demand!\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: Democratic governors tried to comfort anxious Americans.\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 KATHY HOCHUL\uc2 , (D) NEW YORK: We are fighting back. We are protecting our providers. We're making sure insurance companies are covering abortion services. We are giving money to support our providers.\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 GAVIN NEWSOM\uc2 , (D) CALIFORNIA: I'm sorry as well. I'm a little less sorry than I am pissed. A little less sorry that I am resolved and angry to do more.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: California Governor \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2  signed legislation earlier this year to expand abortion access. He joined other west coast Democrats with a message to the court and the nation.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: We will not stand on the sideline.\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 JAY INSLEE\uc2 , (D) WASHINGTON: The west coast of the United States is going to stand strong.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: Thirteen states are expected to trigger abortion bans following the hours old Supreme Court decision. They join a larger group of states planning to end the procedure. Attorneys general from Wisconsin and Michigan say that they won't enforce abortion bands. Missouri Governor \uc2 Mike Parson\uc2  tweeted, "Breaking, Missouri has been the first state in the nation to effectively end abortions." Louisiana is also ready to act.\par \par \uc2 JEFF LANDRY\uc2 , (R) LOUISIANA ATTORNEY GENERAL: There's no qualms about it. In Louisiana, it's over.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: U.S. Attorney General \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2 , once a Supreme Court nominee himself, expressed disappointment with the court's ruling, writing "Today's decision does not eliminate the ability of states to keep abortion legal within their borders, and the Constitution continues to restrict states' authority to ban reproductive services provided outside their borders."\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: The attorney general, Bret, went on to say violence will not be tolerated when it comes to this Supreme Court decision. We also know that federal and local authorities are on standby here in D.C. and across the country if need be in the coming days and weeks. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: David Spunt outside the court. David, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Let's talk about the next steps in this emotional issue. Joining is now, former assistant United States attorney Andrew McCarthy, George Washington University Law professor Jonathan Turley, and FOX News chief legal correspondent, anchor of FOX NEWS AT NIGHT, Shannon Bream at the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 Shannon, we heard a lot today about lawmakers saying we are going to fight this, we're going to try to codify Roe. This ruling essentially moves it to the states. Is anything in Washington going to change that dynamic?\par \par \uc2 SHANNON BREAM, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: You heard the president even say that this is not going to be the end of the story, and he called on people to get out there and vote in their Senate and House races. He said there would be a better chance to codify Roe here on the Hill. You know, everyone on this panel knows that what they actually tried to vote through earlier this year was much more expansive, and both sides could probably agree, more radical than Roe was. They didn't have to vote then, and now that the Supreme Court has made this decision, we'll have to see what happens the midterm elections.\par \par \uc2 It's doubtful that the Democrats get the numbers that they would need in both the Senate and house to make that happen. We have seen this happen over the years when the Supreme Court make a decision that Capitol Hill doesn't like, they pass something a response. So there is sometimes some ping-ponging back and forth between the two branches, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: But Andy, the battle is now in the states, and if any other legal challenges, they go to the states, right?\par \par \uc2 ANDREW MCCARTHY, FORMER ASSISTANT U.S. ATTORNEY: That's right, Bret. But as I was listening to David Spunt's report, I just marvel at the fact that this is now such a dynamic regulatory environment. For the last half- century, and certainly since Casey was decided where everything was litigated, and it was litigated in the courts, but it was under the rubric of this idea that there was a right to abortion and the only issue was the regulation and undue burden or not.\par \par \uc2 Now it's an open field, and I think it is going to be a wonderful thing to have people actually feel like they can impact the regulatory environment that they will live in in their community, and that will be able to actually compare the way that abortion regulations are handled in different places and people can get a real sense of what they think is right about it, what they think is wrong about it. It's a much better discussion about abortion, and it should always have been in the political realm, not the legal realm.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Jonathan, I want to put up what a couple of senators said about being disappointed. Joe Manchin, Democrat from West Virginia, said "I trusted Justice Gorsuch and Justice Kavanaugh when they testified under oath that they believe Roe v. Wade was settled legal precedent, and I am alarmed that they chose to reject the stability the ruling has provided for two generations of Americans."\par \par \uc2 Senator \uc2 Susan Collins\uc2 , Republican from Maine, "Throwing out a precedent overnight that the country has relied upon for half-century is not conservative. It is a sudden and radical jolt to the country that will lead to political chaos, anger, and further loss of confidence in our government." She too questioned whether those justices lied in the questioning. Here is what they said in part. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JUSTICE \uc2 NEIL GORSUCH\uc2 , SUPREME COURT: I would say that Roe versus Wade, decided in 1973, is a precedent in the \uc2 United States Supreme Court\uc2 . It has been reaffirmed.\par \par \uc2 JUSTICE \uc2 BRETT KAVANAUGH\uc2 , SUPREME COURT: It's settled as a precedent of the Supreme Court, entitled the respect under principles of stare decisis.\par \par \uc2 JUSTICE AMY CONEY BARRETT, SUPREME COURT: Roe is not a super precedent because calls for its overruling had never ceased. But that doesn't mean that Roe should be overturned. It just means that it doesn't fall on the small handful of cases like Marbury versus Madison and Brown versus the Board that no one questions anymore.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So Jonathan, address that accusation of hiding the ball in this testimony in their hearings.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN TURLEY, GEORGE WASHINGTON UNIVERSITY LAW PROFESSOR: The suggestion that these justices committed perjury is outrageous and unfair, and completely unfounded. I testified at the Gorsuch hearing in favor of his confirmation. I'm very familiar with what he said. I'm also familiar with what Kavanaugh said. What they said was virtually identical to what every one of their colleagues said. They can't commit to vote in a particular way in a future case. Many of us objected during those hearings that the Democratic senators seemed to be suggesting that they wanted an assurance of how they would vote as a condition for their own votes of confirmation. And many of us called them out on that.\par \par \uc2 But those assurances were not given. What they said was obviously true, that this is settled law. It's entitled to protection under stare decisis, this doctrine favoring precedent. But stare decisis is not an absolute doctorate. Obviously, the court overturns precedent, otherwise we wouldn't have Brown v Board of Education, we wouldn't have Obergefell. We wouldn't have any of these cases that are celebrated.\par \par \uc2 And the majority noted that with a long footnote in its opinion and said don't tell us that you can't reverse a case. Many of the members, some of the members in dissent, favored all of those cases. And they're celebrated by many. So I think it's very unfair attack.\par \par \uc2 But what is really happening here for the court is not that they're telling how the public should treat abortion. They said that when Roe was handed down in 1973 it interrupted a political debate over abortion. And it said it's time for that debate to occur, and for 330 million citizens to make this decision and not nine justices about the \uc2 United States Supreme Court\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Shannon, last thing. For lawmakers on the left, maybe pundits on the left, they're hanging their hat on this expanded attack on what Justice Thomas said in a supporting opinion about possibly looking at other cases. But as I pointed to earlier, the majority opinion written by Justice Alito and supported by Justice Kavanaugh said, no, this is strictly abortion, and we are definitely not going down those other roads, right?\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes, and Thomas sign onto that majority opinion as well, which made it very clear. He actually says, if people will read his concurrence, he says it's true that nothing in this opinion today goes to any of the case is about abortion. He brought up a future scenario in which they could revisit other cases that have been found right under a substantive due process examination, and he did talk about Obergefell and the other cases that would touch on things like same-sex marriage and contraception and those kinds of thing. But he said this case itself will have nothing to do with overturning any of those decision. He was positing something that may or could happen in future.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Shannon, gentlemen, thank you very much. A big day at the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the reaction to the Roe versus Wade overturn, what does it mean politically? We'll got live to Chicago, one of the protests there.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: There are several demonstrations not just outside the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2 , really throughout the country. Senior correspondent Mike Tobin is at one of them tonight in Chicago. Good evening, Mike.\par \par \uc2 MIKE TOBIN, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: And that's it, Bret. Abortion rights advocates are taking it to the streets from the east coast all the way to Hawaii. Here in the center of the country in Chicago, this is Federal Plaza downtown in Chicago's Loop. It is standing room only. And as I look to the north and the south I see a constant stream of people flowing to this location.\par \par \uc2 This particular demonstration is organized by a group called Equity Illinois, with particular emphasis on a state bill that would protect abortion providers. New York City, thousands are taking to the streets with three different demonstrations planned there.\par \par \uc2 The interim director, president of Planned Parenthood, said the Supreme Court has robbed millions of people of the right to control their own bodies and personal health care decisions. Atlanta, Austin, Texas, Boston, Cleveland, and Detroit are all expecting street demonstrations. The west coast as well where the governors of California, Washington, and Oregon have vowed to create a west coast offensive to protect access to reproductive care.\par \par \uc2 One reason to fear violence tracks back to the Midwest here. The Wisconsin Family Action Center was the first known pro-life organization to be firebombed and vandalized with the saying "If abortion isn't safe, then you aren't either." That is the slogan of that shadowy group that goes by the name Jane's Revenge. The website for that group, which uses a lot of symbolism from Antifa, is calling for a night of rage.\par \par \uc2 Wisconsin is one state that has abortion ban that dated all the back to 1849. Planned Parenthood in Wisconsin in light of the Supreme Court decision has stopped performing abortions. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Mike Tobin live in Chicago. Mike, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in our panel, Leslie Marshall, Democratic strategist, Morgan Ortagus, former State Department spokesperson, and Kimberley Strassel, a member of the editorial board at "The Wall Street Journal." Kimberly, what do you make of today, it's impact and big picture? It's obviously historic, but how much do you think it's going to change the fabric of America in what has come down in this ruling?\par \par \uc2 KIMBERLEY STRASSEL, "WALL STREET JOURNAL": Hopefully for the better, Bret. Pretty much my entire life we've been living with this fight, and a lot of us have long believed that the Supreme Court decision to essentially invent this right out of whole cloth really kicked off the culture wars in the United States. And this decision that the court seem to hope would settle the issue in fact just inflamed, especially because so many people felt as though they were being cut out of their ability to have a political debate and make decisions in their own state that best fit their people. Let's hope that this actually, by returning it to the states allows us to begin forging a new path.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: There is a lot to talk, Leslie, about the political vocations of this from many Democratic lawmakers today as well as the president. It was all about this is on the ballot. How do you see it?\par \par \uc2 MARSHALL: I do see it as on the ballot. There is rumblings in states, how to prevent the abortion pill from coming, even talking about how can we override a federal travel ban to prevent women from traveling from the state that doesn't allow abortions to a state like I'm in, California, that does and will continue to do so.\par \par \uc2 Other things that are an issue, approximately 10 states that will not allow exceptions with abortion bans for rape and incest. Also rumblings in Republican Party that if they get the House and Senate, very key to the midterm and the voters in November election, that they may have a federal ban on abortion as early as two weeks after a missed period. So this is on the ballot.\par \par \uc2 And I have to say, I wish Democrats would take a page of the Republican handbook. One of the things that Republicans do very well is they get one issue, you know, Bret, and they go really hard with that one issue. This is that issue, and if you want to add a second for Democrats, it's guns and gun control. But this is a big issue, and I certainly hope my party will come out in numbers like we've never seen because certainly Republicans usually outnumber us, especially in the midterms.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: \uc2 Morgan\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 MORGAN ORTAGUS, FORMER STATE DEPARTMENT SPOKESPERSON: I think that there is a lot to unpack here. First, this is the most animating decision for the Republican Party, and it's probably the single most important conservative policy outcome that I think any of us have seen in our lifetimes. As Leslie pointed out, you've had people working around the clock for 50 years to try and see this overturned.\par \par \uc2 So if you take a step back and look at it, what does it mean for the midterms, as Leslie was just talking about, all the Republicans operative I said today just don't believe that people around this country are going to be voting based on abortion. You'll remember in 2018 during the Kavanaugh hearing when Republicans got incredibly animated by how he was treated by Democratic senators, during those hearings everyone thought, well, maybe we will actually keep the House. A month or so later we had the November elections, and of course the Democrats took back the House.\par \par \uc2 So what I'm seeing in nationwide polls after this was just leaked several weeks ago, voters are still going to be voting on inflation and the economy. The Democrats might get a bump in key purple states, but I think ultimately, it's not the motivating factor that they think it will be.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, final thoughts with the panel, maybe even winners and losers. We'll see on the other side.\par \par \uc2 First, here's what some of our FOX affiliates around the country are covering tonight. FOX 10 in Phoenix as a driver is arrested after authorities say they found thousands of fentanyl pills hidden inside fire extinguishers during a traffic stop. We've been doing that special all week long. Authorities say a deputy found three fire extinguishers in the car, 56,000 fentanyl pills were allegedly inside them, could kill millions.\par \par \uc2 And this the live look out of Cleveland. One of the big stories there from FOX 8, the USFL playoffs come to Canton, Ohio. The New Jersey General take on the Philadelphia Stars in the opener. You can see on the FOX Broadcast Network 3:00 p.m. eastern Saturday. The second game features the Birmingham Stallions against the New Orleans Breakers.\par \par \uc2 There you go. That's tonight's live look outside the beltway. We will be right back with some final thoughts on a big day in Washington.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 ELISE STEFANIK\uc2 , (R-NY) REPUBLICAN CONFERENCE CHAIR: Today's historic Supreme Court decision is a victory for the sanctity of life.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , (D) PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Voters need to make their voices heard. This fall we must elect more senators and representatives who will codify a woman's right to choose.\par \par \uc2 This fall, Roe is on the ballot. Personal freedoms are on the ballot.\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , (D) VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: With your vote you can act, and you have the final word. So this is not over.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Bring back our panel, Leslie, \uc2 Morgan\uc2 , and Kimberley. Kimberley, there are 26 states that either have laws, 13 of them have triggered law based on this ruling today that have a restriction or some different bans about abortion. What about that and the argument that people in those states are going to be unjustly treated if they, for example, want to have an abortion?\par \par \uc2 STRASSEL: Watch this space, Bret. I would like to see how this actually plays out, because a lot of those trigger laws you're talking about were put in place a long time ago, and views on this have changed over the years. Most Americans are kind of in the middle on this question. A lot of them were, for instance, in favor, there's large majority for like the Mississippi 15-week ban, which was what was in front of the court. So even some of those trigger laws may not stick. We've a long way to go to see where states shake out on this.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Leslie, it's also interesting that the president said today that the U.S. would be an outlier in the world. But in Europe, if you look at different nations, a lot of them have bans or some restrictions on abortion already, and the current position was kind of an outlier.\par \par \uc2 MARSHALL: The majority of people that I know that are pro-choice, and I include myself in that, Bret, they are not people that want, and the majority don't want late-term abortions. They do want limits. They have problems with no exceptions for when the mother life is in danger or like we have in 10 of those 41 bans in 10 states currently, of those 26 states, there is no exception for rape or for incest victims. Those are the things that are most problematic to those of us that call ourselves pro-choice.\par \par \uc2 Morgan\uc2 , \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  tweeted out, "Most Americans believe the decision to have a child is one the most sacred decisions there is, and that such decisions should remain between patients and their doctors. Today's Supreme Court opinion will live in infamy as a step backward for women's rights and human rights."\par \par \uc2 And I saw someone tweet out, what will live in infamy is that you should have campaigned in Wisconsin, because elections in 2016 had consequences for the Supreme Court and this decision today.\par \par \uc2 ORTAGUS: Three Supreme Court justices because she didn't campaign in Wisconsin, you're right. I was just thinking that. And that is why to people who are not very friendly to each other, former president Trump and \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 , deserve a lot of credit in Republican and conservative legal circles for getting these justices through today. And I can tell you there's a lot of people that think that other Republican presidents may not have stood by their nominees the way that President Trump did. And so you're going to see I think a little bit of bump in support for him for this ruling today.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Could there be, Kimberley, a pro-life benefit for Republicans in the midterm election?\par \par \uc2 STRASSEL: Yes, I think that that's possible. This goes both ways, and this is one of the reasons Trump was elected was because of his Supreme Court list that said that he was going to put those kind of people on the court.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, ladies, thank you very much, a historic day.\par \par \uc2 It is Friday. You know what that means -- time for "Notable Quotables."\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CROWD: Abolish abortion.\par \par \uc2 CROWD: Legal abortion across this country.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: What a great day. This is historic.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: There's no point in saying good morning, because it certainly is not one.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We cannot allow New York to become the wild, wild west.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , (D) PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I'm calling on Congress to suspend the federal gas tax for the next 90 days.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: People need a holiday from \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's bad policies. We don't need a gimmick.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Inflation is hitting my people so hard they're coughing up bones.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Are you saying that we would rather have lower gas prices in America than Putin's iron fist in Europe?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Would you say that the war in Ukraine is the primary driver of inflation in America?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: No. Inflation was high before.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We're going to see a majority of parents ending up getting their children protected with these vaccinations.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: We need more money to plan for the second pandemic.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 LINDSEY GRAHAM\uc2 , (R-SC): There is enough fentanyl coming across the border to kill virtually everyone in America.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Based on actual investigations, actual witness interviews, these allegations simply had no merit.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We are not introducing a national red flag law.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: This bill will strengthen our red flag laws.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 MITCH MCCONNELL\uc2 , (R-KY) SENATE MINORITY LEADER: The result is a product I'm proud to support.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I was getting off the bike and got stuck.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: This has been a newsy week. This weekend, join Martha MacCallum on FOX NEWS SUNDAY. Her guests, Senator \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  of the Judiciary Committee Georgia Democratic Gubernatorial nomination \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2 . Don't want to miss that.\par \par \uc2 Thanks for inviting us into your home tonight and every night. That is it for this SPECIAL REPORT, fair, balanced, and unafraid, covering all the news, and there was a lot of it this week. We've got you covered on all angles. JESSE WATTERS PRIMETIME" is up next, maybe even early, a couple seconds.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FNSPBB0020220625ei6o00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 All Hell Broke Loose In The Streets After The Supreme Court Gave Its Decision To Overturn Roe Versus Wade; Democrats Like \uc2 Maxine Waters\uc2  Encourage More People To Go To The Streets And Express Their Outrage Over The Decision On Abortion Rights; President Biden Say That He Is Disappointed With The High Court's Decision But It's Not Going To Be Over; President Biden Now Calling On Protesters To Exercise Peaceful Protest Because Violence Is Not An Expression Of Speech; Nationwide Protest Breaking Out After Roe Ruling; It's All On The Ballot: Dems Promise To Make Abortion A Major Midterm Issue; Dems, Media Blast Supreme Court Gun Ruling\b0\par\par\uc2 Neil Cavuto, Dana Perino, Shannon Bream, Katie Pavlich, Jessica  Tarlov, Martha MacCallum, Greg Gutfeld \par \uc2 8,508 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 June 2022\par \uc2 Fox News: The Five\par \uc2 FOXFIV\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NEIL CAVUTO, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: How far does this go and what will be the impact in a split nation.\par \par \uc2 DANA PERINO, FOX NEWS HOST: Fox News alert. A monumental day in America with the Supreme Court overturning Roe versus Wade. I'm Dana Perino along with Katie Pavlich, Jessica Tarlov, Martha MacCallum, and one lucky guy, Greg Gutfeld. It's 5 o'clock in New York City, and this is THE FIVE.\par \par \uc2 The Supreme Court sending the issue of abortion back to the state. The historic decision overturning the 50-year-old ruling on Roe v. Wade which guaranteed women the right to an abortion, the federal right to an abortion.\par \par \uc2 The move already having significant political, social, and cultural implications. Both sides of the debate gathering all day outside the high court as America absorbs the news. President Biden coming out today attacking the decision.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES OF AMERICA: It's a sad day for the court and for the country. Now with Roe gone, must be very clear, the health and life of women in this nation are now at risk. With three justices named by one president, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , with a core of today's decision to upend the scales of justice and eliminate a fundamental right for women in this country.\par \par \uc2 It's a realization of an extreme ideology and a tragic error by the Supreme Court in my view. But this decision must not be the final word.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PERINO: For more, let's go to our chief legal correspondent Shannon Bream. And Shannon, we were on television this morning together on America's Newsroom as the news broke. And you were so able to quickly read the decision, discern it. But I'm wondering now after several hours later how it's settled in for you.\par \par \uc2 SHANNON BREAM, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT & ANCHOR: Yes, a couple of things interesting that this morning, Dana, normally what happens with the Supreme Court opinion, we have all that sound and fury in the morning and then after the decision kind of settles in, the groups will go away. But we got the opposite here today.\par \par \uc2 More and more people are showing up here at the court. They are chanting, they are vowing this is not going to be the end. It's interesting to hear President of the United States say this can't be the final word saying this to the judicial branch, to the independent branch from him.\par \par \uc2 But for now, the Supreme Court is the final word on this. The left has been putting this in terms of taking away a constitutional right. But what the right has argued and what the court said today is, there was never a constitutional right to an abortion under Roe. People across the political spectrum had worried that it had weak underpinnings.\par \par \uc2 We talked about this. Justice \uc2 Ruth Bader Ginsburg\uc2  was among those who worry that it was susceptible and vulnerable to challenge, exactly like what we've seen happened play out here today. Now the president and the Speaker of the House have also made comments to the effect that you've got to get to the polls.\par \par \uc2 Of course, it's smart for them to harness this politically. But they're saying things like contraception and same sex marriage are now going to be a threat. And they're pointing to a line in a concurrence from Justice Thomas. But what he said if you go and read that is he says, there is nothing in this opinion today, this abortion opinion that has anything to do with those rights. It will not impact them. It should not.\par \par \uc2 But it does go on to say that he thinks the courts should engage in a different kind of process for other cases moving forward. And he does mention reexamining the substantive due process rights in cases like (Inaudible) which is the one that made same sex marriage legal in all 50 states.\par \par \uc2 So, everybody that signed on to this opinion today agrees there is no threat. There should be no threat. Let me read something from Justice Alito to those other key opinions. He says we have stated unequivocally that nothing in this opinion should be understood to cast doubt on precedents that do not concern abortion. We have also explained what is -- why that is so. Rights regarding contraception and same sex relationships are inherently different from the right to abortion because the latter as we have stressed, uniquely involves what Roe and Casey termed potential life. It is hard to see how we could be clearer.\par \par \uc2 So, while the left will continue to make hay out of that, the court has been very clear those rights will not be under attack because of this particular decision today. Dana?\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Shannon Bream, thank you. I know you've done incredible work today and have a lot cut out for you the rest of the weekend. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Martha, one of the things that going into this is about 65 percent of Americans mistakenly believe that Roe being overturned means that it was just banned all across the country. And what we're trying to explain to people, is that this sends the decisions back to the states.\par \par \uc2 Now in some states like in New York you won't have any changes. But in other states like perhaps Missouri, for example, or even Texas, you will have significant changes.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA MACCALLUM, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: That is exactly right. And I'm amazed by how many people, you know, a lot of us and certainly Shannon spent a lot of time focused on the way that the justices approach this sort of decisions. But it just -- it doesn't just -- they don't wake up one morning and say, you know what, we have a majority in the court right now. We are sort of like minded on this, why don't we overturn Roe v. Wade?\par \par \uc2 It's not like that. They had a case that came before them. Mississippi had implemented a 15-week ban, which is a shorter ban than what's really enshrined in Roe v. Wade so that it bubbled all the way up to the court. And then they have to make this decision on it.\par \par \uc2 And more than half the states look like they will maintain their abortion rights and it looks like about half of them will either ban or will have six week or 15-week bans, you know, which is a considerable period of time.\par \par \uc2 It's interesting in our polling, 54 percent of those say that they think it's right to ban after 15 weeks, which is close to four months of pregnancy. They say that seems like a reasonable measure, a reasonable point. So, I think when you really get behind this, you realize that it is going to be nuanced. There will certainly be places in the country where it's more difficult to get an abortion.\par \par \uc2 But I would also point out one thing, and that is that we have seen really a complete change in abortion in this country. More than half or as many as 60 percent of abortions in the country are done by prescription pill that comes in the mail. They're not done at clinics across the country.\par \par \uc2 That's one of the reasons that you've seen clinics shutting down. It's not just opposition to abortion. It's also that they are -- their business is shrinking. And I think a lot of people would say that that's a good thing, a good development in all of this. We remember when \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2  said he wanted to see abortion that was safe and legal and rare. That's not the kind of voices that we're hearing on the other side of this debate right now.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: This decision has long been derided, Jessica, as one that was not a good decision -- not a decision well-made and an argument well-made. And even the late Justice \uc2 Ruth Bader Ginsberg\uc2  said that. And yet, even though this decision had leaked in a memo about 55 days ago, it still can be shocking for some that it turned out the way that it did.\par \par \uc2 JESSICA TARLOV, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Absolutely. I was definitely one of those people who knew it was coming. I was on with you guys talking about it the day that the opinion got leaked. I felt the pain of it, you know, not just for myself but for millions of women across the country who depend on safe and legal access to abortion and to women's healthcare more generally.\par \par \uc2 And yes, \uc2 Ruth Bader Ginsburg\uc2  had a problem with the opinion. She's not had a problem with the right to abortion. The original case was a privacy case. It was decided seven to two. And the chief justice was appointed by a Republican. And I think that that is something that has gotten lost in the conversation today. The people are not using the privacy word enough here.\par \par \uc2 And I do firmly believe that if this was a case, if men were the people that were carrying the children, that this would never have happened. I think that women are being put in a terrible position. The statistics are very clear about who this is going to affect the most, it's women who earn less, it's predominantly women of color.\par \par \uc2 There is a laxness to the idea that it's just easy to cross state lines to just go get an abortion. It's no big deal. I mean, today in West Virginia and Arkansas, immediately all abortions that were scheduled for today moving forward were cancelled.\par \par \uc2 And abortions are not just for women that got pregnant by accident because they slept with the wrong guy. It's for medical reasons, protecting the life of the mother. Some of these states are going to have no exceptions for rape or incest. And I find it very hard to believe for the conservatives on TV.\par \par \uc2 I saw Ted Cruz on earlier, you know, touting the ruling. That if one of Ted Cruz's daughters got pregnant because she was raped, or God forbid, you know, an uncle or got her pregnant that he wouldn't do everything in his power to make sure that his daughter didn't have to carry that baby to term.\par \par \uc2 The hypocrisy is beyond comprehension here. Republican women get pregnant, too. And I'm not saying we can't have a conversation about 15 weeks, or 20 weeks. I know a lot of people who are pro-choice have numbers like that. But when this goes back to states, 13 of which have trigger laws that are going to go into effect right away, that means that at six weeks when they don't even have you come in for a heartbeat appointment yet at that point, women are putting in an incredibly unfair position.\par \par \uc2 And I was on with Martha earlier for a discussion about this and we were talking about what Gorsuch and Kavanaugh had said. And Susan Collins and Joe Manchin have both release statements saying that they were misled, quote from Justice Gorsuch. "I accept the law of the land." Roe v. Wade was the law of the land. He deceived the senators and he deceived the American people, Kavanaugh as well. And there's some language from language from Amy Coney Barrett that I would add to that.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: OK.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: You know --\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Jonathan Turley did push back pretty hard on that.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: So did Martha.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: The justices haven't. But Katie, I'll go to you. So, there are a lot of women that actually support this decision --\par \par \uc2 KATIE PAVLICH, FOX NEWS CO-HOST: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: -- as well. A lot of women who have been pro-life and they have fought for this ruling to be overturned and for the decisions to be sent back to the states.\par \par \uc2 PAVLICH: Right.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: As it is now.\par \par \uc2 PAVLICH: Yes. A number of pro-life organizations, in fact, I think almost all of them are run by women. The Susan B. Anthony List, the March for Life all run by women. And the narrative is that women disagree with this decision. That's not true.\par \par \uc2 This for pro-life activists today is a huge victory they've been working on for five decades, this is something they believe is far overdue that an injustice against millions of innocent lives inside the womb has been corrected at a federal level.\par \par \uc2 But the fact is that this is now being sent back to the states, it's actually expanded democracy by allowing 50 state legislatures to debate this to be accountable to the people in their states about what they want to do. And the fight for the pro-life activists and the pro- abortion activists isn't over.\par \par \uc2 They are going to have to convince the people in their state that an abortion ban with no exceptions, with exemptions, maybe 15 weeks like we're about to see in Virginia under Republican Governor Glenn Youngkin that that is politically feasible and something that the people who live in that state actually want.\par \par \uc2 I mean, the tenth amendment says anything that is not in the Constitution specifically is sent back to the states and the people who live in those states. And that is what has happened today. And also, when it comes to how we got to this moment, they actually blame Democrats for this in a way.\par \par \uc2 Ruth Bader Ginsburg\uc2  didn't want to retire. She could have retired earlier. That could have been a Democratic seat put there. \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  lost to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  after taking advantage and taking for granted the campaign, not working as hard as she could have. And Harry Reid who is the Senate majority leader changed the rules on the filibuster. So, it's easier to confirm Supreme Court justices.\par \par \uc2 So, for pro-life activists this is a huge moment in American history, but it's not over. You know, this will continue at the state level and pro-life groups led by women will continue to fight it in their principle there.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: There was one other woman who mentioned it -- we just want to play this for you, this is \uc2 Alexandria Ocasio-Cortez\uc2 . This is her take today. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. ALEXANDRIA OCASIO-CORTEZ (D-NY): What the Supreme Court justices is they chose to endanger the lives of all women and all birthing people in this country. But not only that, they have chosen to strip rights from men, too.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PERINO: All birthing people.\par \par \uc2 GREG GUTFELD, FOX NEWS CO-HOST: Yes. It must not forget the birthing people. They're now the most important people on the planet because they're being included everything, in everything. But I think the real injustice today is that I am the only male on this panel. And I'm still not in the center seat.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Poor Greg.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: You think about that. What is that? What is that? It's -- in all seriousness, this is as bad as the \uc2 Knicks\uc2  trades last night. And if you guys saw that it was sports humor because I'm the dude here, one lucky guy.\par \par \uc2 I think here's -- I'll be short because I'm the only guy here even though my opinion matters more than all of you combined. There's an obvious one sentence explanation for both rulings. The guns -- the guns are in the Constitution. Abortion isn't. Let the states figure it out. I know that sounds harsh but that's actually inarguable.\par \par \uc2 It's not in the constitution. I do feel bad for liberals and the media. I think someone should check in on The View between the gun ruling and this one. We should remove all sharp objects from their set, perhaps their shoe laces.\par \par \uc2 But I think you can have three or four different opinions in your head. For example, you can admit that Roe v. Wade was a deeply flawed ruling, and not only is it overturned, it's ending. That's the important thing. But you can also say, hey, it's been around for 50 years.\par \par \uc2 When something has been around for 50 years, why don't you just let it be. All right? We're already used to it. We don't want to upset the apple cart. But the fact is we have upset a lot of apple carts that we don't like. There have been things that have been around for hundreds of years that we changed. We fought a war over something. OK?\par \par \uc2 You can be -- I don't know. You can be confident even if you're pro- choice, you can be confident that this will settle state by sate. And it might even -- life will even go on for some unborn children which will really, really nice. If you don't like COVID regulations you can vote in new politician or you can move to Florida.\par \par \uc2 If you don't like your abortion regulation you can elect new leaders or you can move to California or New York. So that is how stuff works here. Right? And I also think that the decision was a great rebuke to the leakers. I believe it was leaked by the Dems because it went to Politico. It didn't go to like National Review or Fox News. And that was with the expressed purpose of foreshadowing liberal outrage that would intimidate and influence the court. And it just didn't happen.\par \par \uc2 The court gave them -- the ruling showed that you can't scare the court. And I think that's great. It's just too bad that Biden doesn't seem interested in dissuading any of the hysteria that's going on right now. And just that last, I've heard it a couple of times. What if men could get pregnant? You can't prove it. I mean, aren't men getting pregnant?\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Birthing people.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Yes, birthing people. So, we should defer to them, Jessica. We should defer to them. Birthing people get the final say in everything.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: I think you do know what I meant about that --\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: But it's that kind of hypothetical cliche that I always hear. And it's like, you know, well, we've been fighting wars forever. That was our abortion.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: But we had a case --\par \par \uc2 PAVLICH: I would argue that it actually makes men more accountable for their behavior.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Amen. Amen.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Well, let's hope so.\par \par \uc2 PAVLICH: And that they actually have to be accountable for what they do --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 PERINO: And also, they --\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: I look forward to (Inaudible) policy.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: And also, men are allowed to care about life as well.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Exactly. It's kind of arrogant to think that we don't care even if we don't.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: All right. Up next, Democrats telling people to get into the streets as America braces for potential violence over this Supreme Court abortion ruling.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (CROWD CHANTING)\par \par \uc2 REP. JACKIE SPEIER (D-CA): There is a war out there. And we need to recognize that we've got to arm a lot.\par \par \uc2 REP. CORI BUSH (D-MO): This far right racist sexist Supreme Court that makes this decision based on politics.\par \par \uc2 REP. MAXINE WATERS (D-CA): You see this out here? You have not seen nothing yet, the hell with the Supreme Court. We will defy them.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PAVLICH: AOC and \uc2 Maxine Waters\uc2  calling for people to get into the streets as left-wing radicals vow violence in the wake of today's Supreme Court ruling overturning Roe versus Wade, security being beefed up around the Supreme Court and justices' homes. That wild group like Jane's Revenge are planning a night of rage and said it was open season on the pro-life movement.\par \par \uc2 At least 40 pregnancy centers have been vandalized and activists have been allowed to legally protest outside justices home. With one deranged man trying to assassinate Justice \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2  and after weeks of silence President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  finally calling for peaceful protests.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I call on everyone no matter how deeply they care about this decision to keep all protests peaceful, peaceful, peaceful, peaceful. No intimidation. Violence is never acceptable. Threats and intimidation are not speech. We must stand against violence in any form regardless of your rationale.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PAVLICH: So, Dana, for six weeks, we've seen the White House encourage intimidation outside of the justices home. And now that homeland security saying there may be real violence, he wants to step in and try and get ahead of it.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Well, the attorney general could have arrested any of those protesters at any time. Because it is against the law, the federal law to protest outside a justice's home. They didn't do it. And now actually the attorney general went to Kyiv and I'm all for it. Like go help support the Ukrainians. Great. But on your way out, could you issue a statement or some guidance to the Department of Justice to actually do their job and enforce the law? They didn't do that. So now there is -- there is no deterrence.\par \par \uc2 So, now Biden six weeks later, he's like, please be peaceful. That nobody is listening to him at this point.\par \par \uc2 PAVLICH: Right.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: So, if there's violence, immediately they've got to put -- put that out or else it just will continue to simmer. And the other thing is like, when \uc2 Maxine Waters\uc2  says screw the Supreme Court, we'll defy them. I mean, the way to do that would be to argue persuasively in the states and to be organized.\par \par \uc2 And I think that they do have a fundamental problem though, that it seems, the left has long had this disconnect with getting a lot of money from a small group of people on this issue. And then the voters aren't necessarily there for them because we are -- if you look at any of the issues and any of the elections we've had recently the primaries, what's your number one issue. Inflation, gas prices, crime, immigration, even edu -- I said education, missing a couple others. And then it's like climate change and then abortion.\par \par \uc2 PAVLICH: Right.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: So, this is really -- this is obviously seismic. Who knows? But in November on election night, Martha and I could be sitting there saying, wow, abortion is the number one issue? I just kind of find that hard to believe.\par \par \uc2 PAVLICH: So, Jessica, --\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Hard to imagine.\par \par \uc2 PAVLICH: You know, Dana makes a point that these lawmakers on Capitol Hill could say the states need to basically figure this out. Congress also could pass laws to do this.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PAVLICH: However, the last law they tried to pass was so outrageous and expanded Roe to the point of partial birth abortion that Democrats in Washington who have a complete majority on everything can't get it passed. So, is their message here to get out in the streets and do what since legislation seems to be off the table for \uc2 Maxine Waters\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: You need to be very specific when you tell anyone to go anywhere because they're in that cases amongst all of us. Right?\par \par \uc2 PAVLICH: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: We have mental health problems and political extremism are a terrible mix and the left and the right both have them. So, I don't like any of this talk. I was very happy to see President Biden be so declarative and so clear about this at that particular moment.\par \par \uc2 But I've gotten this question a lot today specifically about doing this in '08 when Obama had the super majority. Why was there not a codification of Roe on a national level? And I think unfortunately, that we take too many things for granted and that people have only so much political capital. He knew that the ACA was going to be his crowning achievement. And so, he moved on this thing that's gotten heath care for over 20 million people.\par \par \uc2 It's really big deal and it was hugely important, but now we're looking back at so many decisions like \uc2 Ruth Bader Ginsburg\uc2  deciding not to retire, nuking the filibuster and saying to ourselves, we have something that over 60 percent of Americans agree with to some level. I'm not saying up until the point of birth but in the first trimester.\par \par \uc2 And now we're staring down the barrel of potentially half of the states restricting this at the six weeks mark.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: And we have to be smarter about it. It will not be the number one issue but it already has jumped 17 points since the leak came out. And this will matter.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PAVLICH: So, Martha, you know, the debate about this issue is always very heated and the left just seems like they don't want debate on this. I'm on the board of the Network of enlightened Women. And they had their annual conference on Capitol Hill today. It's a group of pro-life conservative women. And the conference had to be cancelled because of the threats that are happening on Capitol Hill today.\par \par \uc2 So, you know, it doesn't seem like they're open to much debate in the wake of this decision.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, you know, I would say if you're in favor of choice, then you truly should be in favor of choice. So, if someone wants to go to a pregnancy center, if they want to carry their baby to term and adapt it, you should be in favor of protecting that right for them as well.\par \par \uc2 So, it really makes you question, you know, are you just pro-abortion or are you truly pro-choice? The other thing that I would just mention is that, you know, I look at President Biden, I look at \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  who are both Catholics, who t claim to be practicing Catholics.\par \par \uc2 I didn't hear once from either one of them today, look, my faith is on the side of ending abortion in this country. But I respect that this is the decision that has been made. But from the courts and I respect people's rights to protest, why don't they say that? Why don't they ever mention their own faith?\par \par \uc2 I looked up. It's been a long time since President Biden has brought this part of this story up. And it just seems disingenuous to not say that at this point and especially say that in the context of, you know, we believe in peaceful protests and make sure that no one is hurt in the carrying out of your protest over this weekend. It would be nice to see some respect for their faith in terms of the way they're talking about this today.\par \par \uc2 PAVLICH: Well, Greg, here we are today, you know, businesses in downtown D.A. are boarding up as we speak and they're preparing. The question is like why would they be doing that? What's the purpose of the fence outside the Supreme Court today?\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Well, you know, the thing is, you know, for viewers of this show, I think we should tell them that violence is never acceptable even though there will be no fan mail Friday. But you are going to have to just deal with that.\par \par \uc2 Now, if they choose, if they choose to bump my show, which we taped with Dana at 11 p.m., if they bump it because something is going on in D.C., then all bets are off. You can burn the whole place down. OK? Burn it all down. I'm kidding actually.\par \par \uc2 That's the funny thing. We never have to tell our fans or Republicans don't riot.\par \par \uc2 PAVLICH: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Have we ever -- I mean, it's like --\par \par \uc2 PAVLICH: No.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: It's like Dems rage. It's like, it's like, it's one word. Right? Is there any other kind of rage? We learned that violence is actually an effective tool in influencing justice. You witness the Ferguson effect. You witness the rioters and the arsonists. The Floyd -- after the Floyd -- during the Floyd riots, they were effectively shielded from exposure because they were the right kind of protests. And they're always the right kind of protest on that side.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, you know, some guy walks around in an unguided tour in January 6 and he's in jail for life for however long he's been in hail. But we've seen this on the left, campuses shutting down, speakers targeted. Trump supporters attacked early on. Remember that? When Trump got elected, do you remember that? None of that stuff seem to matters.\par \par \uc2 Violence became their own language because they felt above and beyond debate. And that's the problem these days in woke culture. Debate is no longer necessary. You just -- you just get violent because those are words. Violence is words. Words are violence.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Can i just add quickly? There's been a lot of violence at Planned Parenthood clinics. There was a federal judge --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 PAVLICH: And that's unacceptable.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: But it exists and those are right wingers. Those were not --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: You want to go back even further to the 70s when there were thousands of domestic acts of violence during the 70s by the left, not the right. So, we can keep going backwards and backwards and backwards. But we only have 30 minutes. I'm not doing fan mail.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Thirty-two minutes.\par \par \uc2 PAVLICH: All right. Coming up, how Democrats in the media distorted the reality of today's historic Supreme Court abortion decision.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, the Supreme Court's historic ruling to overturn Roe v. Wade now returning the abortion decision back to the States. But none of that is stopping Democrats from claiming that today's ruling is a nationwide ban on abortion.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 NANCY PELOSI\uc2  (D-CA): The Republicans are plotting a nationwide abortion ban. They cannot be allowed to have a majority in the Congress to do that. But that's their goal. It's such a contradiction. Yesterday the states cannot make laws governing