nteresting. So, the Republicans have done fairly well over the last 12 years in state legislatures, and actually the most popular politicians in America today are Republican governors, most of them in the northeast. And so, that's also where you start to see new talent arise and the leadership, and you don't know who it will be. I'm curious about the Hispanic vote on lots of different levels as you've seen some changes especially in Texas. And it's like, they're not just going from blue to maybe purple and then to red. It's like straight to red. Do not pass -- do not pass $200. They are going to vote Republican. And I'm not sure how this issue will play into that. But watch the media misrepresentation of this. It's just very frustrating that I feel like, why do we have to be the ones that read the bill and nobody else has to? And it's not fair. It's really not fair because -- and it's really unnecessary -- it's unnecessarily hurting the ability to have civil discussion. And I think that we've proven that you can do that here. And I encourage everyone to think about this with grace and dignity and recognize that people have very strong feelings, or whatever their feelings may be, and to try to then work this out.\par \par \uc2 My last point is now that the court has made this decision, no one can just say, well, it's in the -- it's in the Supreme Court's hands. Now, it's not. Now it's back in your hands, the people's hands. And so, make your voice heard.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Katie.\par \par \uc2 PAVLICH: Midterm elections are usually based elections, the problem which would be helpful for Democrats in this instance, because abortion is a big issue for Democratic base voters. The problem is that 90 percent of people are being affected by gas prices. 85 percent of people say that inflation is destroying their family budget and causing major problems for them when it comes to their future planning and their retirement. So, I think those issues certainly dwarf it.\par \par \uc2 But on the issue of abortion that Democrats will use as a political talking point, as \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  did today, and as \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  did. Democrats have gone from saying abortion should be safe, legal, and rare in the 1990s to today, not a single presidential candidate who's running as a Democrat will say that they support any kind of restrictions at all.\par \par \uc2 So, if you look at the Senate races where Democrats are, you know, maybe Pennsylvania for example, that question will come up. And the majority of people in this country do believe in abortion restrictions, most of them at 15 weeks, and Democrats believe in no restrictions. So, if Republicans can make that clear argument because the media makes it so that their abortion is just banned, and they don't talk about the restriction argument, then they can probably push it through, but this is going to be an issue.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Greg?\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Well, I mean, it's a legitimate political question, will the ruling energize Dems the way the January 6 hearings won't energize anybody. But it's kind of a strategy to get you to root against your own interests. You know, I sure hope I don't get what I want because then that will really upset people and they'll come back and just be angrier. The left never does that. We're always expected to be the ones that -- we better not get what we want, because if we get what we want, oh boy, the pendulum is going to swing.\par \par \uc2 But the fact is, you know, the Democrats and the media have never thought once about the effects of their mission creep on abortion, or their mission creep on guns, or their mission creep on biology. Like, I mean, think about, like, you know, again, the pregnant men, right? They're like -- now, feminism is no longer feminism unless you accept the hierarchy, you know, where the pregnant male is up on the top, you know. So, I think -- you know, we don't have to, like, so what if it has an effect, all right? Is that going to change anything that you want? It shouldn't, but they want that -- the media wants you to believe that but they never asked that of the left, right? They never say, do you think this stuff is going to hurt you, because that's what happened with Youngkin, right? Anyway, that's -- those are my thoughts.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right, straight ahead, more on what the Supreme Court's abortion ruling means for the Midterms and what top Democrats are saying about it today.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Welcome back, Democrats now trying to make the Midterms all about the Supreme Court ruling on abortion.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: This fall, Roe is on the ballot, personal freedoms are on the ballot.\par \par \uc2 SEN. AMY KLOBUCHAR (D-MN): To me, the answer is simple. It's November. It's the ballot box.\par \par \uc2 SEN. ELIZABETH WARREN (D-MA): Making Roe vs. Wade the law of the land. That's what November will be all about.\par \par \uc2 PELOSI: Make no mistake, it's all on the ballot in November.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Well, you know, I don't blame them, Katie. I mean, the economy is in the crapper. They got a president who can't complete a sentence. You got criminals who don't complete their sentences. So, why not, you know, bet all your money on killing unborn children?\par \par \uc2 PAVLICH: Yes, that's what they're trying to hold on to. I think this is going to fall flat because they're making the argument that this is on the ballot and you need to elect more Democrats. Democrats are in charge right now. They voted on a pro-abortion bill earlier this year that failed because it was so extreme and was -- would allow partial-birth abortion, late-term abortion across the country and they dishonestly said it would codify Roe when it really expanded Roe.\par \par \uc2 So, I'm not really sure of the argument of we're in charge, haven't done this yet, but give us more seats? I don't know.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Yes, you know, Jessica, we saw this year the Democrats going after parents, calling them domestic terrorists. We saw these horribly, almost nearly violent school board meetings which are terrible. Now, that you're going to go after unborn children, Jessica, is the Democratic Party no longer the party for families?\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: I don't think that we were ever called that?\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: A party for families.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: No, we were, but that's you, guys.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: You have -- you have --\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: It's conservative.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Yes or no, Jessica.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: I object. I want to something else. So, there's something that's going on. So, when the leak happened, conservatives only focused on the leak. They didn't actually want to talk about the implications of this. And I think that there will be a lot of Republicans out there especially in purplish districts, people who had -- people that support them in 2016 but then voted for Biden out there where this ruling will matter. So, there is a way to capitalize on it that way, and that's where I would prefer to say.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: What do you make of this, Martha? We haven't talked about this yet.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You know, I -- but I do have a new thought on this. And it is -- it is that I think that Katie is right. One of the things that happened with the leak, it came out early. It sort of diffused the acceptance and understanding over a longer period of time. And I think that the people who are fired up on cable news networks about this arm do not actually represent the rest of the country.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Like I said before, abortion has been declining. It's down 20 percent in the last 15 to 20 years. And also with these over-the-counter medications that can end a pregnancy quickly, it is not