s gun.\par \par \uc2 ELIE MYSTAL, JUSTICE CORRESPONDENT, THE NATION: We suffer this violence because of six conservatives on the Supreme Court. It is their fault. And until we start acting like it, we will continue to suffer the violence.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Katie, I'm going to come to you first on this. What did you see as the main takeaways from yesterday's ruling?\par \par \uc2 PAVLICH: Well, first I want to address the conflation between law-abiding concealed carry holders of which there are millions and most lawful group of people in this country and they conflate it always with the criminals who get their guns illegally and then shoot people with them, which is also illegal.\par \par \uc2 The main takeaway is this. What \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  said about the Second Amendment is crucial. It's a constitutional right. That is not a second class right. It's a first-class right and it shouldn't be treated differently than all of the other amendments. And so, I think that's the key takeaway. And local governments don't have the right to restrict you from carrying a firearm outside of your home for lawful purposes.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Well, Greg, Governor Hochul thinks that the answer to this is to increase the number of sensitive areas which is what's gone on in D.C. as well. Do you think that this will be the tack that liberal governors are taking all over the country now?\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: There's an error in your question. Hochul doesn't think. There's nothing in that head. My -- I will make a prediction. Crime unrelated to this ruling will be blamed on the ruling, right? All of a sudden there'll be like an explosion of gun crime and it won't be from permit to carry, trust me, but they will blame it -- you know that this was a great ruling and the reason why, it caused Keith Olbermann to call for the Supreme Court to be destroyed, right? He said it's a solution.\par \par \uc2 An insane response from an insane person theoretically means it's a good thing. If he had liked it, I would have really been worried.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I think it's the first time people have talked about Keith Olbermann in a really long time. He's probably really thrilled that calling the end of the Supreme Court finally got people talking about him again.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: We should throw a picture up of him because no one knows who he is.\par \par \uc2 PAVLICH: No, don't do that.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: All right, there was something else that happened in the gun world. We have a bipartisan gun bill that passed today. Did you think that this was possible? What do you think the implications are?\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Well, I think it's interesting because the Democrats really did not get what they wanted in that bill. The Republicans were able to advance something that actually deals with things that they've been talking about, which is people who should not have a gun should not be allowed to get a gun and dealing with mental health concerns and background checks.\par \par \uc2 So, yes, I thought that -- I think it's good that they could get it done. And I don't think that it's everything -- it's certainly not no parade of horribles that the Conservatives thought it might be, although I know that there are some that really hate it, but the Democrats certainly didn't get what they needed here now or wanted.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: No. Well, they got something and anything helps in this area. So, up next, more on the breaking news with the Supreme Court overturning Roe vs. Wade.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Protests happening across the country right now over the Supreme Court overturning Roe vs. Wade. And sending the issue of abortion back to the States, we've talked about it all hour. We'll get final thoughts start with you.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Well, I think that the real protests are going to start if my show gets bumped at 11:00 for this noise. We already see these people. We know what they do. Why do we have to keep showing it? Watch my show. You got Dana Perino, you got Joey Jones, you got Kat Timpf, you got -- oh, boy.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Liz MacDonald.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Liz MacDonald. It's a great show. You're not going to want to miss it. That's my final thought.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Final thought, Katie.\par \par \uc2 PAVLICH: I'm going to protest as well if I don't get to watch "GUTFELD!" tonight when I get home from New York City. No, but you know, there's a huge --\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Will there be counter-protest, Katie?\par \par \uc2 PAVLICH: Yes, there have been counter-protests. There are lots of pro-life groups at the Supreme Court today. They had a different attitude towards this. They're very celebratory. So, congratulations to all of them and the people who've worked for 50 years to protect the most vulnerable human beings in our society.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: And Jessica, final thoughts?\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: No violence, but peaceful protest all that you want. It's an incredibly important issue for people on both sides of this. Call your legislators, your local legislators now. Call your governors. Make sure you turn out to vote. State and local elections matter often a lot more than what happens on the federal level. And mobilize safely and responsibly to ensure that at some point, women get this very important right back.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: And Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, I'm an anchor "FOX NEWS SUNDAY" this weekend. We'll go to \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2 . And one of the things I find really fascinating about this whole thing is the big picture. That you look at one -- a one-term president so far who had three picks in one term and the transformative nature of that along with \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  is an extraordinary story and we're going to dig into that.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Shoot the box.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: And also, you can go back to the Bush administration and Alito.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: That's right.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: And that one was also transformative you could say. I'll be on \uc2 Tucker Carlson\uc2  at 8:00 p.m. as well. So, you're going to just keep seeing us night long.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Right.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: That's it for us tonight though now. We will see you back here on Monday. Bye-bye.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FOXFIV0020220625ei6o00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 How Will Abortion Decision Impact Midterms?; Interview With Sen. \uc2 Charles Grassley\uc2  (R-IA); Interview With Fmr. Sen. Joe Lieberman (I-CT); Interview With Sen. \uc2 Mike Lee\uc2  (R-UT); Supreme Court Overturns Roe v. Wade.\b0\par\par\uc2 Lauren Simonetti, Charles Payne, Ted Williams, Alexandria Hoff,  Shannon Bream, David Spunt, Neil Cavuto \par \uc2 8,395 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 June 2022\par \uc2 Fox News: Your World w/ Neil Cavuto\par \uc2 CAVU\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Today, the \uc2 Supreme Court of the United States\uc2  expressly took away a constitutional right from the American people.\par \par \uc2 PROTESTER: Legal abortion on demand!\par \par \uc2 PROTESTERS: Legal abortion on demand!\par \par \uc2 PROTESTER: Today is the first step in America, a united nation that values people's lives, that has equality for women that we know they have to kill other people to be equal to men, and I'm so freaking excited!\par \par \uc2 PROTESTER: There is one word for this decision. And that word is....\par \par \uc2 (SHOUTING)\par \par \uc2 PROTESTER: And now it's only the beginning. Roe v. Wade is in the ash heap of history. And now we start to state to state, And we fight that battle.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 NANCY PELOSI\uc2  (D-CA): This morning, the radical Supreme Court is eviscerating Americans' rights and endangering their health and safety.\par \par \uc2 REP. KEVIN MCCARTHY (R-CA): The right to life has been vindicated. The voiceless finally have a voice.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 CHUCK SCHUMER\uc2  (D-NY): Today is one of the darkest days our country has ever seen.\par \par \uc2 REP. MAYRA FLORES (R-TX): I woke up this morning praying for this. And I never thought that it would happen.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 NEIL CAVUTO, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: And this may be just the beginning.\par \par \uc2 Roe v. Wade is dead, but the fury over the nation's highest court effectively killing it is alive and for some not going so well. Today, FOX on top of what comes next, with Shannon Bream at the Supreme Court, where the crowds are still building, David Spunt in Washington the violence that some police are still fearing, Utah Senator \uc2 Mike Lee\uc2  on what a whole bunch of states could now be planning, and Joe Lieberman on a president who might have missed his chance at calming.\par \par \uc2 Welcome, everybody. I'm Neil Cavuto on this historic day.\par \par \uc2 Let's go right to Shannon, at the Supreme Court on how things are going there now -- Shannon Bream.\par \par \uc2 SHANNON BREAM, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hey, Neil.\par \par \uc2 So, yes, it's loud here today. What normally happens with these Supreme Court days is that they may be loud, both sides out and waiting for an opinion. But once it comes, after a couple of hours, it dissipates.\par \par \uc2 But here what we're hearing and what we're seeing is the crowd is continuing to grow. The pro-lifers who were celebrating this morning have left. But those who are now vowing to fight back against this decision, the pro-choice groups, they are here, and there are a lot of folks joining them.\par \par \uc2 We know that this is a day where they have called for getting to -- taking to the streets. That's something that Congresswoman \uc2 Alexandria Ocasio- Cortez\uc2  said to this crowd, that voting isn't enough, waiting until November is not enough. She wants people out there making their voices heard today. That continues to grow here at the court.\par \par \uc2 We also heard from the president. He is warning, saying it's not just about abortion. This could go even further. Here's what he said a short time ago:\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Justice Thomas said as much today. He explicitly called to reconsider the right of marriage equality, the right of couples to make their choices on contraception.\par \par \uc2 This is an extreme and dangerous path the court is now taking us on.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So what Justice Thomas said in that concurrence -- it was solo -- he was alone.\par \par \uc2 But he said, listen, if we're going to follow the reasoning we have in this case, we might be able to revisit and maybe it should be appropriate for us to revisit some of the other decisions.\par \par \uc2 But the majority absolutely slaps that down. Justice Alito, writing said, maybe you didn't hear us. He says this: "We have stated unequivocally that nothing in this opinion should be understood to cast doubt on precedents that do not concern abortion. We have also explained why that is so. Rights regarding contraception and same-sex relationships are inherently different from the right to abortion, because the latter, as we have stressed, uniquely involves what Roe and Casey termed potential life."\par \par \uc2 He goes on to add: "It is hard to see how we could be clearer."\par \par \uc2 So the majority saying none of those rights are up for debate, none of them will be touched or revisited. But you can believe that the right is going to -- or the left is going to continue to say we need you to get to the polls because of this. We need you to show up because there are other rights in play, but the majority says, Neil, they're not.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Shannon Bream, thank you. See you tonight no doubt covering this from every single angle.\par \par \uc2 To David Spunt right now in Washington, where they're promising a night of rage. And that has police very, very worried -- David.\par \par \uc2 DAVID SPUNT, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hi, Neil.\par \par \uc2 Authorities are on high alert this hour, both federal and local, in case there are any problems relating to violence or destruction as a result of this historic decision from the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2 . We have a live look outside the court this hour.\par \par \uc2 Several thousand people have been in front of the court since the decision came down at 10:10 this morning. A pro-choice group is calling for -- quote -- "a night of rage." And that has authorities beefing up numbers.\par \par \uc2 "The night SCOTUS overturns Roe v. Wade hit the streets, you said you would riot." This coming from a group called Jane's Revenge. The poster says: "To our oppressors, if abortions aren't safe, you're not either," again from Jane's Revenge.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MCCARTHY: The Supreme Court was not bullied by the far left mob.\par \par \uc2 Threats of violence against the justices did not win. And the truth is, pro-life Americans are not planning a night of rage.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: Attorney General \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2 , the nation's chief law enforcement officer, once a Supreme Court nominee himself, released a lengthy statement today, Neil, disappointed with the High Court, however, calling for no violence.\par \par \uc2 He said in part: "Advocates with different views on this issue have the right to and will voice their opinions. Peacefully expressing a view is protected by the First Amendment. But we must be clear that violence and threats of violence are not. The Justice Department will not tolerate such acts."\par \par \uc2 The National Guard is on standby at this hour, but has not been mobilized. The East Side of the U.S. Capitol has been closed off for security reasons -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: David Spunt, thank you for that.\par \par \uc2 One of the things you have been hearing about the waiting for this decision from the Supreme Court was that at least two dozen states were ready to trigger their own anti-abortion laws and put them in effect virtually immediately.\par \par \uc2 Senator \uc2 Mike Lee\uc2 , he sits on the Judiciary Committee. He joins us now, the senator, of course, from the beautiful state of Utah.\par \par \uc2 Senator, good to have you.\par \par \uc2 I don't know if whether Utah is one of those states, sir, but what do you think of states that are going to implement these triggers?\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 MIKE LEE\uc2  (R-UT): Yes, Utah has a trigger law. It is written by one of our great state legislators, Karianne Lisonbee.\par \par \uc2 And Utah, like so many other laws, really does have an interest in making sure that it legislates. What we see is the democratic process unfolding now. That process has been taken off the table for 49 years. And this is what the Republican form of government looks like.\par \par \uc2 It allows people through their elected lawmakers to make laws, including laws regarding how best to protect unborn human life.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So, Senator, do you think it just adds to the confusion, though? I understand that, bringing it back to the states, maybe 24 will have this position that they know abortions are allowed.\par \par \uc2 I don't know whether they're as strict as Texas' abortion law that from fertilization, almost to conception, that it would be illegal. What we do now separately, 16 states and the District of Columbia that are protecting access to abortion.\par \par \uc2 What do you think of the kind of confusion that's been wrought here?\par \par \uc2 LEE: Well, that's not really confusion. That's what we call federalism. That's not confusion. That's the way our constitutional system works.\par \par \uc2 And when something isn't taken off the table by the Constitution, it's reserved to the people. And unless it's made federal, then it's reserved to the people to be exercised at the state level. There's -- it is not unusual to have differences between one state and another on laws governing all sorts of things.\par \par \uc2 It's not a horrific event to have different laws in one state than in another. And abortion is no exception to that.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So, Senator, if there's a doctor performing abortions now in your state or any other of these roughly two dozen states that have this so- called trigger provision, are they breaking the law? Can they be arrested?\par \par \uc2 LEE: It depends on the circumstances.\par \par \uc2 This trigger law that Utah has enacted, Representative Lisonbee's bill, does allow for abortions to be conducted under certain limited, narrow circumstances, and it would depend on those circumstances.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So, if you have a business, let's say -- I spoke to the \uc2 Rite Aid\uc2  CEO earlier today, sir, and she was reminding me that, of course, \uc2 Rite Aid\uc2  is everywhere.\par \par \uc2 But if you're filling out prescriptions for abortion-related drugs or treatments, it's possible in those states that have these trigger laws \uc2 Rite Aid\uc2  could be violating the law, right?\par \par \uc2 LEE: Well, yes, that's possible. They will have to seek counsel in their respective states from people who are familiar with the laws in question.\par \par \uc2 But here, again, it's important to remember this is the nature of the system in which we operate. Different states are going to have different laws. And it's not surprising that a state like Utah might have a different set of policy preferences when it comes to how we're going to protect unborn human life than you might see in New York or Massachusetts.\par \par \uc2 It's neither surprising nor unusual.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So, in Utah, if you could educate me, Senator, what is the -- is it similar to Texas, no abortions beyond fertilization, beyond conception?\par \par \uc2 LEE: In Utah, we have exceptions under our law to protect a woman's right to access to an abortion in circumstances where the pregnancy resulted from rape or incest or certain rare medical conditions can be found either in the mother or in the baby. In the case of the baby, if it's a certainly fatal, diagnosable ailment that won't allow the child to live past birth, that's one of them. Certain brain defects will also qualify.\par \par \uc2 And if the mother faces substantial impairment to major bodily functions, in some circumstances, she might be able to get one.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Senator, there are now pills for abortion. You can take pills, and they are across all state lines.\par \par \uc2 LEE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I'm just wondering, do you think, given what Utah has committed to do in this trigger provision, they should be outlawed in your state?\par \par \uc2 LEE: Well, I'd have to look at the law and figure -- again, the Utah law doesn't allow -- doesn't outlaw abortions in every circumstance.\par \par \uc2 And so, no, I wouldn't say that the -- that that pill would be outlined in every circumstance, given that the law doesn't outlaw abortion in every circumstance.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Got it.\par \par \uc2 LEE: It will depend on the specifics of the abortion in question.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, I just wanted to get some clarification in my thick skull to understand where this goes from here.\par \par \uc2 Senator \uc2 Mike Lee\uc2 , very good seeing you. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 LEE: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, so now the big question is, what is next? What is legally next?\par \par \uc2 Let's go to Jim Trusty, the former Justice Department prosecutor.\par \par \uc2 Jim, if you don't mind, I might repeat a couple of those same questions to you. What is next for these states, a couple of dozen that are enacting the so-called trigger laws? I don't know how far each goes. I know, in the case of Texas, it sounds like Utah as well, it would ban abortions out right from the moment of conception.\par \par \uc2 Now, I don't know, again, if that applies to the other 21, 22. But where and how far does this go? And if you're in a state that has those provisions, is anyone who has an abortion or helps with that abortion or sells drugs or medications like at \uc2 Rite Aid\uc2  or CVS or \uc2 Walgreens\uc2  culpable, legally culpable for those actions?\par \par \uc2 JAMES TRUSTY, FORMER FEDERAL PROSECUTOR: Yes, I will give you a big I don't know, because it's really going to be state to state.\par \par \uc2 And at the end of the day, this is kind of what the founding fathers had in mind a lot of times when it came to a restricted, limited federal government, and that is 50 little laboratories, or at least now it's 50.\par \par \uc2 So we're going to see variants between the states. We're going to see different degrees of punitive components to the statutes. We're going to see some that are much more forgiving. And, of course, we're going to see stuff lining up on the other side of the aisle, kind of the blue side of the aisle, in terms of accommodating or enshrining the ability to get an abortion, maybe even support for interstate travel to have abortion.\par \par \uc2 So we're going to see an awful lot of, I think, variances between the states about what's going to be considered lawful and what's not.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Earlier today, Jim, I was talking to some folks, and it just likened it to me at least like which states are during and before the Civil War slave states vs. those that are free states. Now, I'm not comparing the two at all, my friend.\par \par \uc2 But I do want to get your sense of the confusion as a result. I understand putting this back in the states. But it does add to the confusion, because, depending on how you look at the country, we're split in half.\par \par \uc2 TRUSTY: Yes, it does.\par \par \uc2 I mean, I think maybe the better analogy is gaming, gambling. We have had - - we had this kind of universal bar on states having gambling, except for Nevada and New Jersey. And this is something my law firm deals with all the time. Well, the gates were opened by a Supreme Court ruling to allow for states to validate or not gambling. And you had federal laws that in some way seem to conflict with states.\par \par \uc2 So you're right. There's a lot of chaos that comes with that. I don't think the Supreme Court justices should make decisions based entirely on whether there's going to be chaotic results in real life. And I don't think they did. But, yes, we're going to have an awful lot of open questions, an awful lot of kind of corollary litigation in lower courts about what exactly can happen state to state.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Now, we don't know what will happen tonight, Jim, outside either the Supreme Court itself or at some of these justices' homes, particularly of the six who voted for this.\par \par \uc2 It's probably a safe bet that they're not in town right now. But there was a lot of criticism of \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2 , as the attorney general, that he wasn't doing more to enforce safety for the justices and limit these protests.\par \par \uc2 There are laws on the book that prevent that, but now a lot of protesters have been telling me, since this is already decided, and the law was meant to avoid protests at justices' homes where a case is about to be decided, it's free and fair game to go to these justices homes and do whatever the heck they want. What do you think of that?\par \par \uc2 TRUSTY: Yes, I hope that's not the right reading of the statute.\par \par \uc2 I think intimidation could still include aftermath, hopefully. But, look, there was a couple of flash points today in terms of the attorney general and President Biden talking about nonviolence. I was glad to hear the nonviolence piece, although I was not happy that there was such general disrespect for the institution, including talking about it being cruel, un- American, referring to the justices as essentially picking on women.\par \par \uc2 That's not really what they do. That's not what they really did. And so the institutional safety, kind of the reputation of the Supreme Court over the long run is concerning. What concerns me about the short run, like this weekend, is what -- not just what was said, which was generally OK about nonviolence, but what's been done.\par \par \uc2 I mean, we had an attempted assassination of Justice Kavanaugh and an attorney general who still refused to enforce the obstruction law at that time. So they're saying the right things now, but we have to wait and see as to whether there is violence and how, in fact, this Department of Justice treats it.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You know, sometimes, you didn't get that signal out of the president today. I'm going to go into this a little bit more depth a little later in the show, Jim.\par \par \uc2 But he was more or less criticizing the process and the former president, his predecessor, that laid the groundwork for this, even though it was \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's right to appoint the justices he did. Many went back to the \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  situation with \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  and all, but back and forth over the process, and how wrong the process was, because you didn't agree with the decision.\par \par \uc2 No such comments when you do agree. And I'm wondering if that is a slippery slope right there.\par \par \uc2 TRUSTY: Yes, I mean, that's -- and it is the type of stuff that creates a climate of disrespect and of possible harm.\par \par \uc2 I mean, Neil, I just have to tell you, when I was a prosecutor, shockingly, I may have lost one or two cases. And when I would lose those cases, I was trained to know that, if somebody asked you about it, even if it burnt in your mouth, you would say, look, we felt strongly about the case, but we respect the verdict.\par \par \uc2 That was a very low-grade version of respecting the institution of either the courthouse or the jury system. When we have a president and an attorney general who can't bring themselves to show that same kind of deference or respect for the institution, but instead use words like un-American and cruel, well, that sets a tone for the rest of the public that I don't like here.\par \par \uc2 And with the recent track record of lack of safety for Justice Kavanaugh in particular, it's a very worrisome weekend and something that really they should have rose to the occasion and been the big boys and said the right things about not just saying violence is bad, but that the institution is a valid and important institution in American life.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: That's well put, Jim. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 I can't imagine you lost a couple of cases, but I guess it happens.\par \par \uc2 Jim Trusty, always good seeing you.\par \par \uc2 TRUSTY: Well, the darn jury.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes, that's right. OK.\par \par \uc2 TRUSTY: Yes, I hear you, Neil. Thanks, man.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So there's a lot -- thank you.\par \par \uc2 There's a lot of give and take on this and what we could be in store for tonight. A lot of people have concern about this so-called night of violence. So we hope cooler, calmer heads prevail.\par \par \uc2 But Joe Lieberman did get a chance to catch the president's comments on this as to whether the president calmed tensions or maybe created them, we will ask.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I call on everyone, no matter how deeply they care about this decision, to keep all protests peaceful, peaceful, peaceful, peaceful, no intimidation. Violence is never acceptable.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, that's the hope from the president of the United States.\par \par \uc2 We will be talking to Joe Lieberman about whether that message got through.\par \par \uc2 To Alexandria Hoff first at the White House on the message the president is sending as a result.\par \par \uc2 Alexandria, what are you hearing?\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDRIA HOFF, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Well, Neil, it's good to be with you.\par \par \uc2 Yes, today, the president said that this is a solemn moment for America. He said that there is no executive action that he can take to undo this decision. Instead, he seeks to make abortion rights a key issue in the midterm elections. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: We need to restore the protections of Roe as law of the land. We need to elect officials who will do that. This fall, Roe is on the ballot.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HOFF: The president seeks for Congress to restore sweeping abortion rights by codifying the federal protections that Roe v. Wade provided.\par \par \uc2 President Biden also vowed that his administration will protect access to abortion-related medications that are approved by the \uc2 FDA\uc2  and vows to defend a woman's right to travel to a state where abortion services are available.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: They have made the United States an outlier among developed nations in the world. But this decision must not be the final word.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HOFF: So, right there, you have heard the president say that the U.S. is now an outlier, according to this decision.\par \par \uc2 But according to a statement released by Senate Minority Leader \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 , the United States was already an outlier prior to Roe v. Wade being struck down. He wrote this -- quote -- "More than 90 percent of Europe restricts abortion on demand after 15 weeks, but every state in America has been forced to allow it more than a month past that after a baby can feel pain, yawn, stretch and suck his or her thumb."\par \par \uc2 Now, Vice President Harris spoken on the decision a short time ago from Chicago, echoing what the president said earlier, that this warrants a call to action for midterm voters.\par \par \uc2 And, Neil often, on a big day like this, you might see a crowd in front of the White House protesting. That's not the case today with a far more relevant location located not far from here. But, once again, the president he did close his remarks by saying today that you have to keep those protests peaceful, even if you're very passionate about this issue, that violence and intimidation are not free speech -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Alexandria, thank you very, very much for that.\par \par \uc2 HOFF: Sure.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Alexandria at the White House.\par \par \uc2 To Joe Lieberman right now.\par \par \uc2 You hope cooler, calmer heads prevail, Senator, but that's the big worry. This is a very polarizing issue. And emotions run extreme on both sides.\par \par \uc2 What do you tell people?\par \par \uc2 FMR. SEN. JOE LIEBERMAN (I-CT): Well the first thing is that it is -- this is an enormously significant decision.\par \par \uc2 I mean, it's one of the big days in Supreme Court history. Justice Alito himself compared it in defending the overturning of a previous decision to the Brown vs. Board of Education decision, which overturned Plessy vs. Ferguson on the basic idea that our principle that separate but equal is not equal.\par \par \uc2 So there's a lot of emotions out there. But I appreciate what President Biden said today. Obviously, he's against the decision. He's disappointed by it. But he's the president, and he sets the tone. And he's saying to people who are -- agree with him the decision was wrong, you have got to keep it peaceful. That's the way we are in America.\par \par \uc2 We try to -- we accept the judgment of courts, even if we disagree with them. And I would add this note. If you disagree with this decision, but you are also horrified by what happened at the U.S. Capitol on January 6 because of this attempt to violently interrupt the constitutional border of counting electoral votes, then you can't tolerate violence in response to a Supreme Court decision.\par \par \uc2 You have got to go back to court. You have got to go back to the legislatures. As the president said, you got to get involved in the elections this fall and in 2024.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But he didn't say all of that. And I do appreciate what you're saying, Senator, about his expressing his disappointment with this decision, and urging everyone to keep calm. I'm just paraphrasing.\par \par \uc2 But he went beyond just expressing his disappointment, right? He questioned the motives behind it and the way and the approval of the three conservative justices in their arrival on the court, more or less impinging the entire court and the system we all have to rally around, whether we will always agree with the decision that court makes or not.\par \par \uc2 Doesn't that further incite anger, that someone hears that, says, the president's right about this, this is a kangaroo court, and it was Trump that put all these guys in there, and that's a mistake, and it was rigged from the beginning?\par \par \uc2 It feeds a beastly kind of behavior, doesn't it?\par \par \uc2 LIEBERMAN: I don't think so, Neil.\par \par \uc2 Look, the president really is disappointed, obviously, by this decision. He's got a right of free speech, if you will, to say so.\par \par \uc2 If you read the decisions of Justice Alito, and the -- and Justice Thomas, and the three dissenting justices, they say some pretty tough things about each other and the opinions each side articulated. So that's just part of our system.\par \par \uc2 But, again, when the Supreme Court decides, that's the law of the land. And I thought you have to consider not only President Biden's call for peaceful, peaceful, peaceful protests, no intimidation, but also the fact that he redirected people who are upset with this decision to the election process, to the campaigns, and, ultimately, I think probably back to the court.\par \par \uc2 There's a lot of room. This isn't over. I mean, the court today obviously removed the constitutional right to an abortion. But it did not, I don't think, say that Congress has no role here. And I think in the days ahead, you're going to find people in Congress trying to figure out how to, again, establish a national standard for abortion.\par \par \uc2 Whether they can do it or not depends on the election in November. If Congress is as closely divided as now, or if Republicans control both houses or one, it's not going to happen.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 LIEBERMAN: So the activity will go back to the states. And that's not such a bad thing overall.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right.\par \par \uc2 Well, and, again, commenting on that decision and the implications of it are one thing. More or less questioning the institution that created it, I don't know, Senator. On this one, we might not be seeing eye to eye.\par \par \uc2 But it's always good seeing you, my friend. Thank you very, very much.\par \par \uc2 LIEBERMAN: You too.\par \par \uc2 Take care, Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: It happens, right? It happens.\par \par \uc2 Joseph Lieberman, the former U.S. senator.\par \par \uc2 LIEBERMAN: Every now and then, barely.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Now and then. Now and then, it does happen.\par \par \uc2 Of course, the former vice presidential candidate in 2000 for the Democratic Party.\par \par \uc2 Before I leave here, I do want to bring your attention. We haven't forgot. We had a huge day in the stock market today, not moved by any of these events on Capitol Hill. It was about the -- at least the hope, and just today, the hope.\par \par \uc2 This was the first up week in four, by the way, one of the best days we have seen in a few months, but ending a very, very strong week for the markets, on the hope, again, I stress, the hope that interest rate hikes won't be that severe.\par \par \uc2 Now, it's born of the possibility here that we're cutting back on our spending, and, and we're also cutting back on our mood. A lot of us are not as optimistic as we were, consumer sentiment that came out earlier today hitting an all-time low, worries growing from an economy where inflation is growing.\par \par \uc2 But, from investors today, a belief, a hope, a prayer maybe that that doesn't go on forever.\par \par \uc2 We'll have more after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, a night of protests that has some wondering just how bleak or violent a night this could be.\par \par \uc2 They hope everything stays contained, but, right now, given the crowds that are building in Washington and in other cities, worries abound -- more after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, a lot of concerns for what could go down in Washington tonight, to say nothing of other American cities, to protest the Roe v. Wade decision today that historically just killed it off.\par \par \uc2 And now a lot of folks are saying the process, the institution itself is the problem, and they're angry. Just how angry, how anger-filled will get?\par \par \uc2 Let's go to Ted Williams, the former D.C. homicide detective, also a Supreme Court bar member, I must point out. So we get the best of kind of both worlds here.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So, Ted, I always enjoy seeing you, my friend. We always meet under these kind of scary situations, though.\par \par \uc2 How bad do you think it could get? Do you think, ultimately, as I have been hoping, cooler heads prevail?\par \par \uc2 TED WILLIAMS, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Well, I'm also, Neil, hoping that cooler heads will prevail.\par \par \uc2 I can tell you I have just personally came from the Supreme Court, where I have seen the groups out there. They're very passionate, but these groups at this stage seem to be very peaceful.\par \par \uc2 But what is happening at this time at the Supreme Court is that the law enforcement officers there, you have the \uc2 Capitol Police\uc2 . You have the Metropolitan Police Department, and you have police are from other jurisdictions that are there at the Supreme Court separating these groups.\par \par \uc2 I'm happy that this decision, if it was going to come out, that it came out this morning, hoping that it will give some of these individuals time to cool off. But I'm also concerned, Neil, if I may say, about the justices' homes.\par \par \uc2 As you know, you and I just did a piece on the fact that these are demonstrators who are demonstrating that some of these Supreme Court justices' homes. And I would suspect that will continue perhaps into the night and into the coming days.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You know, Ted, the law that was in place, if I understood it correctly -- you're the lawyer -- I'm not -- but that you couldn't protest outside a justice's home while a decision could be in the making or to influence said decision.\par \par \uc2 Now protesters, can't they come back and say, well, the decision has been made, and we don't like it, and we're going to protest?\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Well, yes.\par \par \uc2 Yes, you know, there's that definition between the decision being made and influencing the decision, or I don't think it's crystallized enough in the law, and the demonstrators should get out there, and if they do anything out of the ordinary, you're going to have not only the \uc2 U.S. Marshals\uc2  and other federal law enforcement officers there, but you're going to have the local police officers there to try to attempt to protect these justices' home.\par \par \uc2 But the law itself is just not that crystallized, unfortunately.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: We live in very vitriolic times, strained times, extremely divisive times. I get all of that.\par \par \uc2 But I get a sense with this that people are upset or they're delighted. There's no middle ground here. But we get past this sort of stuff. But I wonder if there's something here that has some more -- that ignites sort of a summer of protests, you know, not akin to the Floyd protests and that kind of thing, but a different kind of summer-long protests.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Well, Neil, you have touched on it.\par \par \uc2 And this is one of my greatest concerns of other groups other than groups who are fervent about this decision are coming in and using this incident or this decision as a means to create havoc in this country.\par \par \uc2 We have quite a few groups out there, Neil, that want to do that. And so, as nightfall comes up on the Washington, D.C., area, that is certainly a concern. But I got to tell you -- and you mentioned it a little earlier -- I'm concerned about all over this country individuals or groups taking advantage of what has taken place here in this decision at the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 And I'm not talking about the groups who are for or against abortion. I'm talking about other individuals who will use this as a vehicle for their own personal isms.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes, and we have seen that happen once or twice, my friend, haven't we?\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Ted Williams, thank you very much. I appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 All right, we will be following all of those developments, as will Republican Senator \uc2 Chuck Grassley\uc2  on what he's urging right now from both sides of the aisle.\par \par \uc2 Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: We don't have the systems in place to support the children of children that weren't ready to be parents.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We are very stoked that this is going back to the states. And it's only the beginning.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, the reaction coming fast and furious, not only outside the Supreme Court, but across the country right now, to the justices, six of them, essentially junking Roe v. Wade, which was sort of the precedent, if you will, on life issues dating back to 1973.\par \par \uc2 Of course, it was shot down because these justices, the six that held this count and this view, thought it should not be in the court's place to decide these anyway, and back to states, it goes.\par \par \uc2 Chuck Grassley\uc2  with us right now, the esteemed senior senator from the state of Iowa.\par \par \uc2 Senator, what do you make of this decision and those who say that it's just going to lead to confusion now, that you're going to have 16 or 17 states allowing abortions, and maybe that many or more not allowing them?\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 CHARLES GRASSLEY\uc2  (R-IA): We are a federal government of very limited power, only the 18 powers that are mentioned in Article 1 of the Constitution.\par \par \uc2 And the Supreme Court interprets the Constitution. So, where we are is, all powers not specifically given to the federal government are reserved to the states under the 10th Amendment or the people. So this is the way our Constitution is supposed to work.\par \par \uc2 And the decision that was made 50 years ago was a bad decision. Even Ginsburg said, even she agreed women ought to have the right to control their body, the decision, Roe was decided wrong from a Constitution standpoint.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: As you know, Senator, Democrats have seized on this all the way up to the president himself as sort of galvanizing their party, the base of their party, I assume, to vote Democrat in the November elections.\par \par \uc2 Do you worry about that?\par \par \uc2 GRASSLEY: I don't worry about it from the standpoint of the politics, because I keep in touch with my constituents through my 99 county meetings every year.\par \par \uc2 And Iowans are concerned about inflation, about gas prices, and about the border and the Rio Grande not being controlled. That's what I have heard all year. And those are still the top items that we hear. I think this is going to elevate abortion a little bit for a while, but people are -- pocketbook in November 8.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right.\par \par \uc2 Senator, very quickly on other news that shows the two parties can work together on something, this bipartisan gun measure that had 15 Republican votes in the Senate got passage today in the House.\par \par \uc2 So it does show, I guess regardless of what your personal views are on that legislation, that the two parties can still work together. Are you confident they can continue to after this?\par \par \uc2 GRASSLEY: I can give you three bills right now, Klobuchar-Grassley to take on big tech's anti-discrimination thing, Grassley-Tester on cattle bill, and Grassley-Wyden on prescription drug prices, just to name three that I have been working on for two years.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, got it. I see that Grassley name in all of them, so they must be a good thing.\par \par \uc2 So, Senator, thank you very, very much. We appreciate your time on this crazy day.\par \par \uc2 A lot of companies have already made up their minds what they're going to do in light of this Supreme Court decision today, help out workers who still want an abortion, give them money to travel elsewhere if they're in states where it's forbidden and to stand by them. But is that their role?\par \par \uc2 After this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Where \uc2 Rite Aid\uc2  stands on this and your reaction to what went down today?\par \par \uc2 HEYWARD DONIGAN\uc2 , CEO, \uc2 RITE AID\uc2 : This decision obviously has a big impact.\par \par \uc2 We are an employer of 53,000 associates nationwide. And our number one concern has been and will continue to be their health and their wellness. We have already been working with our health plan administrator on contingencies. We aren't able to address the specifics. But I think this just shows the important role of health care in people's lives.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, the \uc2 Rite Aid\uc2  CEO with me a little earlier on FOX Business, essentially saying that's the kind of the box they're in right now, especially at \uc2 Rite Aid\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Forget about what you do for workers, many of whom might want abortions, and some of the companies you see listed here, including \uc2 Amazon\uc2  and \uc2 Netflix\uc2  and a host of others, that are providing support for workers who will need that help or they say should get that help.\par \par \uc2 But \uc2 Rite Aid\uc2  is a different beast, if you think about it, too, because it provides some of the medications and the treatments that might not be legal in some of the states in which it operates, about half of them in this country if some of these triggered states exercise that option to make abortion illegal.\par \par \uc2 So again, a bit of a corporate mess here.\par \par \uc2 Let's get the read from Lauren Simonetti at FOX Business, Charles Payne, the host of "Making Money" on that fine network.\par \par \uc2 Charles end it with you, begin with you.\par \par \uc2 And the position it puts a lot of companies in, some that -- \uc2 Rite Aid\uc2 , were really trying to figure out the legality of what they provide in some of these states as the abortion crackdown maybe extends to them, but whether and they can or what they do for their workers.\par \par \uc2 A lot of companies are trying to thread that needle. What do you think?\par \par \uc2 CHARLES PAYNE, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Yes, you're right, Neil.\par \par \uc2 And it's -- the CEO, she said they already had a plan in place or they were working on a plan. I have got the list in front of me, \uc2 Disney\uc2 , \uc2 Meta\uc2 , \uc2 Microsoft\uc2 , CVS, \uc2 Netflix\uc2 , Square, or Block, \uc2 Amazon\uc2 . These are all the names I would suspect would have been on it. They have been working on contingency plans.\par \par \uc2 It's their right to want to do this for the employees if they want to. I do worry, though, some of the some of the comments from some CEOs, mostly smaller companies like \uc2 Yelp\uc2 , Reddit, and Box, it could draw a -- it could cross a line where they begin to alienate certain customers.\par \par \uc2 And also, you just have to worry if some companies start -- go even further and say, hey, I'm going to snatch my business out of any of these potential 26 states that go down this abortion route. I think that would be a big mistake.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You know, you always hear this push for companies that want to be woke. It's night and day difference between talking about a bill in Florida and a Supreme Court precedent established today, Lauren. I get that.\par \par \uc2 But sometimes you can't win with the position you're taking. You're going to get just as many workers at some of these companies who, let's say, are against abortion who won't like the company standing by those who want an abortion. It's a slippery slope, right?\par \par \uc2 LAUREN SIMONETTI, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: It's a very slippery slope.\par \par \uc2 Neil, if I could pick up on where Charles left off, if you look at Texas, which has very restrictive abortion laws, right, been in place for some time now, companies still deciding that they're going to move their headquarters there, despite those laws, right?\par \par \uc2 And then you look at Georgia, the six-week ban. Back in -- it was 2019 that the CEO of \uc2 Disney\uc2  at the time, \uc2 Bob Iger\uc2 , said, well, we might have to rethink producing some of our content in the state of Georgia because of these laws. I reached out to \uc2 Disney\uc2  today. I haven't heard back. I don't think they moved any production despite those laws, although, yes, they have a new CEO now.\par \par \uc2 So, for companies, tech companies, right? Let's say -- let's say some of these states will choose to criminalize abortions, meaning they might ask the big tech companies for some digital communication records, text messages, location data. What do the tech companies do in that situation?\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: That's a very good point.\par \par \uc2 There is a way that's going to get a little out of hand, Charles. I was even thinking -- and you kind of touched on it as well -- this idea of boycotting companies that are doing this or not like boycotting if they don't. I'm just wondering how far you see this going?\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: I don't -- I don't think it's going to go that far.\par \par \uc2 But, again, I think a lot of these companies have had the ability, and you can certainly saber-rattle, to Lauren's point, but a lot of these companies have seen what's happened just with the Major League Baseball debacle, the Disney-Florida debacle.\par \par \uc2 I think they're smarter right now. If they were the push the envelope, they would be making a big mistake.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes, it's always that middle ground you got to find.\par \par \uc2 Lauren Simonetti, Charles Payne, thank you guys both very, very much.\par \par \uc2 All right, in the meantime here, you already heard \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  today, kind of touched on by no less than the president of the United States today, that this will be a galvanizing force, uniting Democrats and maybe getting those who weren't too intrigued or excited about going to the polls anyway to do just that, and that it could be a midterm election turning event.\par \par \uc2 Could it be?\par \par \uc2 Let's ask Bob Cusack, The Hill editor in chief.\par \par \uc2 Do you think it galvanizes the Democratic base, and, as a result, Bob, it changes the complexion of this midterm election?\par \par \uc2 BOB CUSACK, THE HILL: Oh, well, number one, I do think it galvanizes the base, and it helps them get more voters out, especially in Senate races, where obviously that's very important, because the House has no role in confirming judges.\par \par \uc2 But will it change everything? Is it a game-changer? I don't think it is. It's not going to shift the political winds. Most people are going to be voting this year on the economy and gas prices and inflation. And those are all bad things for Democrats. Does it help them a little bit? It does.\par \par \uc2 But at the same time, I think the left is also deflated. So this -- and, remember, you think back to the 2016 election, this decision really was born out of that, because a lot of Democrats stayed home and didn't vote for \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2 . And \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  got three justices on the High Court.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes, and to get people galvanized, you have to keep them galvanized for a while, right? We still have, what, four or five months to go here.\par \par \uc2 CUSACK: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: That's kind of hard to do.\par \par \uc2 CUSACK: I think it is.\par \par \uc2 I think it's very hard to do right now. Democrats raised expectations. That was a mistake in the beginning of the Congress. They didn't really reach what they promised to do, certainly on Build Back Better. That's nowhere right now. So, they haven't passed a big health care bill, other than COVID. And that's not going so great.\par \par \uc2 So, overall, Democrats, I think there's a little bit of wishful thinking here. Certainly, people will be talking about abortion. But I don't think it's going to be that much of a game-changer.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 The only thing I would imagine that could complicate it for those same Democrats is if it gets violent or this night of protest turns into something worse or triggers a summer of protests, violent or not. That might tick off the general electorate. There's no way of knowing, but your thoughts?\par \par \uc2 CUSACK: Yes, I think that's right. That's one to watch out.\par \par \uc2 Now, the battle for the Senate is really a jump ball right now, because Republicans have to defend a lot more seats. So that's going to play a big role going forward next year, because let's say there's another Supreme Court opening next year. Well, if \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  is the majority leader, he doesn't have to hold a vote. There could be another sequel to \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2 , where McConnell didn't hold the vote on him.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Wow.\par \par \uc2 CUSACK: So the battle for the Senate is very, very important.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You know, speaking of that -- and they all hearken back to the \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  thing and the president himself questioning even the Supreme Court, the propriety of the Supreme Court and these justices chosen by his predecessor, in other words, questioned not only the decision, but the way it came about to the decision.\par \par \uc2 I don't know if that carries the -- maybe among the base, it carries some weight. But, to average Americans, aren't they going to think that's a little troubling?\par \par \uc2 CUSACK: Yes, I think average Americans, it doesn't really -- I think it does help the base. I agree with you, Neil.\par \par \uc2 But average Americans, independents who helped swing whether it's control of Congress or control of the White House, I don't think that's going to be a very convincing argument and get people to the polls. There will be other issues that get people to the polls. This is one of them. But there are a lot of others.\par \par \uc2 And we will see. So, you were talking to Senator Grassley. And I agree with him that this issue is going to move up in the polls, abortion, but is it going to eclipse inflation and the economy? I doubt it.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes, it's always about the economy, stupid, as you tell me, when you call me stupid, but I don't think that was your intent.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But I know what you're saying.\par \par \uc2 Bob Cusack, always seeing you, my friend. Thank you very, very much.\par \par \uc2 CUSACK: Thanks, Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: We're going to be exploring this a lot more, because we are expecting this night of protests, might turn out to be nothing, certainly nothing violent.\par \par \uc2 We're going to watch it. There are planned protests this weekend around the country. We will be monitoring beginning at 10:00 a.m. Eastern time.\par \par \uc2 We're going to talk to Al Green, the Democratic Texas congressman, what he makes it, \uc2 Bill Cassidy\uc2 , the Republican Louisiana senator. We have got the lieutenant governor, Winsome Sears, of the beautiful state of Virginia.\par \par \uc2 Fair and balanced look at an issue now that has galvanized the country. How far does this go, and what will be the impact in a split nation?\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or 