E UNITED STATES: You guys ride bikes? Well, you - - they have, some of them have this thing you put your toe you raise your foot so you cab (INAUDIBLE) off the pedal. I was getting off the bike. It got stuck on the right side.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Economists are saying a recession is even more likely than ever.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Not the majority of (INAUDIBLE) are saying that. Come on, don't make things up, OK? Now, you sound like Republican politician. I'm joking. That was a joke. But all kidding aside, no, I don't think it is. I was talking to \uc2 Larry Summers\uc2  this morning. And there's nothing inevitable about a recession. I think, I believe, I think we're going to be able to, I think we're going to be able to get a change in Medicare and a reduction in the cost of ensign.\par \par \uc2 We also can move in a direction that we can provide for the tax increase, the tax taxes on those in the, in the corporate area, as well as individuals as it relates to Trump's tax cuts. We have a chance here to make a fundamental turn toward renewable energy, electric vehicles, and not just at the river but across the board. And that's something we should be - - my team is going to be sitting down with the CEOs of major oil companies this week, cited an explanation how they justify making $35 billion on first quarter.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right, so if you listen closely there, the President's had quite a bit on the beach this afternoon. The top story panel, Fox Business Anchor, David Asman, and Fox Business Senior Correspondent Charlie Gasparino joining me now. Gentlemen, great to have you with us today.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE GASPARINO, FOX BUSINESS SENIOR CORRESPONDENT: Good to be here.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: The, this is the, the phrase, the catchphrase of the moment: "recessions not inevitable." And what I'm wondering is, what are people supposed to do with that? What is actionable? What does that, what does that even mean? Just go, go, Dory, honey, not, not inevitable.\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: Nothing is inevitable. I mean, may happen, it's probably going to happen. I mean, I think what was really interesting about that conversation is how little backup he had for recession is not inevitable. I mean, what did he give us? What data point did he give us to believe that that we're not going to have a recession, and there is no data points right now. All the data points between the Fed having to raise interest rates, because we have 8.5 percent unemployment, higher mortgage rates, consumers starting to back out, back out of spending. All the data points, the real data points point to a recession. So, I don't know what the hell he's talking about.\par \par \uc2 DAVID ASMAN, FOX BUSINESS ANCHOR: You know, one of his, one of Charlie's best subjects is \uc2 Janet Yellen\uc2 , the Treasury Secretary. Charlie's been writing a lot about her. She was on the Sunday talk shows, yes, and made a statement that simply wasn't true. I don't mean to call her a liar. But, but I think we have the soundbite of that, and I just love to play that.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, let's watch that.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANET YELLEN\uc2 , TREASURY SECRETARY: Most consumers, even lower income households, continue to have buffer stocks of savings that will enable them to maintain spending. So, I don't see a drop off in consumer spending is the likely cause of the recession in the months ahead.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ASMAN: A buffer?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Tons of, tons of cash on --\par \par \uc2 ASMAN: A buffer stock of savings. Two-thirds of Americans are living paycheck to paycheck. Two-thirds, last I checked was a majority, right, Charlie? You checked me on that. But the point is, is that she's saying there's a buffer of savings that will protect us from any kind of recession, because people can get into that, that buffer of savings and spend money and that'll keep us out of recession.\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: Isn't that scary?\par \par \uc2 ASMAN: It is very scary. The frightening thing is, is that it's just not true. She knows that two-thirds of Americans are living paycheck to paycheck, and more important than that, Americans know that. And I'm just wondering, it was interesting that the President mentioned \uc2 Larry Summers\uc2  during that that brief on the beach conversation.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I'm glad you brough that up.\par \par \uc2 ASMAN: I'm just wondering if maybe \uc2 Janet Yellen\uc2  leaves after the election, after the drubbing they get in the election, and \uc2 Larry Summers\uc2  comes in to save the day. That would be an extraordinary -- it would it would take off the left side of the party tremendously, but he might decide to do something about that.\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: We, we have reported that she's probably out. I mean, she's the fall person for this. I don't know what a buffer stock is. And what's so cool crass --\par \par \uc2 ASMAN: It's just not true.\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: -- about that, and (INAUDIBLE) about that, if she's telling Americans to dip into their savings to save the economy, how sick is that?\par \par \uc2 ASMAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: One other thing I want to avoid, they have this insane fetish with, with electric vehicles. Like it's going to happen overnight. I mean, they, there is some weird attraction that they have to green energy and electric vehicles that even \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  laughs at them when they talk about and he's the CEO of \uc2 Tesla\uc2 , the largest electric car company, that they're going to take average Americans are going to start buying $60,000 EVs. I mean, really guys?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: And even \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  said, you know, look, I am all for electric vehicles, but we need to have more, more, more regular energy and more coal fired energy. And I also thought that was very interesting. I think there's a chance that the President was floating that little bit because every, every other day, we get a comment from \uc2 Larry Summers\uc2 , which is contrary to what's going on in this administration.\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: And he, you know, worked for Obama, he worked for Clinton in the economic realm. So, so what he said was, I was talking to \uc2 Larry Summers\uc2  this morning, and there's nothing inevitable about a recession, and I think it's going to be very interesting if he goes down that road. I also --\par \par \uc2 ASMAN: He could just be, he could just be using \uc2 Larry Summers\uc2  to say --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Maybe.\par \par \uc2 ASMAN: -- I listen to all sides. But at the same time, if he, if he really wanted to shock the people, remember, he's, he claimed at one point a couple of weeks ago to be ticked off that people were correcting him all the time in his administration.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ASMAN: And he may want to assert himself and say, he knows he can trust \uc2 Larry Summers\uc2 , he worked with \uc2 Larry Summers\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: You're, you're scribing --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: You describes some -- no, I'm serious. How do we know that he really spoke to \uc2 Larry Summers\uc2 ? How do we know that \uc2 Larry Summers\uc2  said that? We don't know anything.\par \par \uc2 ASMAN: I think we could probably check on that one.\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: Yes, it's worth checking, it's worth checking. But I will say this, remember, \uc2 Larry Summers\uc2  was the guy that said, you're going to get us inflation and recession.\par \par \uc2 ASMAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: He's the first guy to say that after all the spending.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I want to pull this up. Because, you know, I mean, I don't want to make more out of the falling off the bike, I could fall on my bike, anybody can do -- and if they do, you know, your foot can catch on that thing, right. But here's what the Wall Street Journal wrote this morning: "Breaking the Biden age taboo." And suddenly, you've seen over the past couple of weeks since the \uc2 New York Times\uc2  story came out where you've got avenues that would not normally be pointing at the President's age, pointing quite clearly at the President's age.\par \par \uc2 And the Wall Street Journal basically says, look, you know, the rest of the world just woke up and learn how old this President is, and that he would be 82 at the end of the first term. So, you mentioned to me in a break, that is interesting that they did a big profile piece on \uc2 Pete Buttigieg\uc2 . What do you see happening here?\par \par \uc2 ASMAN: It was "60 Minutes" that that did the piece, I think the media, it wants a replacement, the left wing leaning media wants a replacement for Biden, and \uc2 Pete Buttigieg\uc2  was, was profiled by Cooper on "60 Minutes" as being the solution -- and when you, when you think of him specifically as, as a member of the Cabinet who in the middle of the worst supply chain transportation crisis, we have, as Secretary of Transportation took a two month maternity, paternity leave. I mean, for goodness sakes, I don't know if he's got all the credit, but they're looking for anybody to replace --\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: But you know, it's so ridiculous, because no one would care about his age if his policies weren't so bad. I mean, he ran as a moderate, he adopted Bernie Sanders policies.\par \par \uc2 ASMAN: He did.\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: And now, we got, we got massive inflation and all this other stuff. And now they're saying there's a problem with his age. I mean, it's, it's so --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: No, but you're absolutely right, because you can be you know, you can be in your 90s.\par \par \uc2 ASMAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: And be sharp as a tack and have great policies and nobody would care.\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: Reagan wasn't exactly sharp --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: The age in and of itself is not the issue, right?\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: Reagan, Reagan wasn't sharp at the end, but --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: But it's individual.\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: But he was, but by the way, his policies were pretty sharp.\par \par \uc2 ASMAN: Reagan was just 69 when he took office, by the way. No, no, I'm talking when he took, he was 69. But the, the Wall Street Journal editorial mentioned ends by saying the President may not want to leave town as easily as some Democrats want him to. So, he's, he's going to push. I mean, he's getting a lot of pushing in that direction. They say, it's like taking away the car keys from your parents when they control.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Not easy.\par \par \uc2 ASMAN: It isn't an easy thing to do.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I mean, that's a very strongly worded piece from the Wall Street Journal going after him and his age. And I think you're right, Charlie, it's important to point out, it's, you know, what kind of 79 are you? You know, what I mean, some 79-year-olds are supper sharp and surprised, some are not.\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: Are you 79 years old and with it enough to basically, you know, give the smart people in the room the chance to do policy. I mean, he is literally, I think he overrules half his staff. They've got to know better.\par \par \uc2 ASMAN: Is the \uc2 Janet Yellen\uc2 , the smartest person who could serve --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: To be continued. We'll see what happens with \uc2 Janet Yellen\uc2 . We'll see what happens if they bring \uc2 Larry Summers\uc2  back into the room. Thanks, guys. Great to see you both today. Lots of talk about so in moments.\par \par \uc2 ASMAN: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, in moments, Joe Concha, on the security breach at the Capitol that kind of puts Democrats in a bit of a tricky spot today. And J.D. Vance joins me on the set as he gears up for a closer than expected Senate race in which his Democrat opponent is drawing distance between himself and President Biden, next.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Will you invite President Biden to come campaign for you?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Look, we welcome everybody support, but I will be the face of this campaign.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, this getting some attention. Seven members of Stephen Colbert Late Show production staff face unlawful entry charges. They were arrested. They have a possibility of further criminal punishment after they were allegedly found roaming unattended around a congressional office building, the Longworth House Building.\par \par \uc2 They were told according to the reports that they needed to leave earlier in the day, the arrests, and they were also I think in the trying to get into the hearing room earlier for the Jan. 06 hearings, and got booted out of there as well.\par \par \uc2 So, these arrests prove a bit tricky for Democrats to downplay in this moment. In moments, we're going to get some analysis on this from Joe Concha. But first Congressional Correspondent Chad pilgrim lays it out for us from Capitol Hill. Hi Chad.\par \par \uc2 CHAD PERGRAM, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONGRESSIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Martha, the Colbert seven were banging on the doors of GOP members and recording skits in a House office building after hours. They weren't allowed to be there without an escort after the building closed, especially since they were asked to leave earlier in the day trying to cover the one six committee hearings without proper credentials.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM (voice-over): Police cited them for unlawful entry.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. CLAUDIA TENNEY (R-NY): Were they doing surveillance as they claim that Barry Loudermilk and his guests were doing. I mean, this is the absurdity, and this is why the American people aren't buying any of this. They know that it's a charade.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: That's a reference to so-called surveillance tours, the committee accused Barry Loudermilk of leading the day before the riot. If convicted, the Colbert seven could face a year in prison and a $5,000 fine.\par \par \uc2 The Feds charged 132 people with unlawful entry after last year's riot, but many also faced more serious charges.\par \par \uc2 The one six committee has two more hearings this week.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM (on camera): The hearing tomorrow will focus on efforts to persuade state election officials to help President Trump win the election. Among those testifying, Georgia election officials \uc2 Brad Raffensperger\uc2  and Gabe Sherman (PH). Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Chad, thank you very much. Let's bring in Joe Concha, media and politics columnist for The Hill, and a Fox News contributor. Joe, Great to have you here.\par \par \uc2 Basically, the \uc2 CBS\uc2  spokesperson said that the Capitol interviews were authorized and prearranged after leaving the members office. On their last interview, the protection team stayed to film stand ups and other final comedy elements in the halls while they were -- when they were detained by \uc2 Capitol police\uc2 . What do you think?\par \par \uc2 JOE CONCHA, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR (on camera): They broke the law. Right? That's unlawful entry. If it was so innocent, why do you then put them in jail overnight? Right?\par \par \uc2 And I can totally picture by the way, Martha, the staffers from the "Johnny Carson Show", or the Jay Leno Show doing something like this, actually, no, I couldn't, because they're not political activists, like these people are supposed to be working in comedy, by the way, or in this situation.\par \par \uc2 And meanwhile, we see the escapism gone from late night, right? Overall, just last week, the president doesn't do an interview for nearly 120 days, a broadcast interview.\par \par \uc2 So, does he go to "60 Minutes"? Or does he go to Bret Baier, and Martha MacCallum to be asked serious questions? No, he goes to \uc2 Jimmy Kimmel\uc2  for a rhetorical foot rub over on ABC. Right?\par \par \uc2 And this has been ignored, by the way. You know, NBC is ignoring the story, ABC's Good Morning America, 25 seconds. That's according to the Media Research Center.\par \par \uc2 And I'm trying to picture what would the coverage be like if seven Fox News staffers unlawfully broke into the Capitol and overstayed their welcome?\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Oh, can you imagine?\par \par \uc2 CONCHA: Actually, I don't want to picture that.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: No, that could -- but you have to in order to understand, you know, the shoe on the other foot in these situations.\par \par \uc2 Now, Jonathan Turley is always a good person to turn to for sort of a scene, look at something, because there's obviously a lot of compares, you know, is it an insurrection? Is it this? No, they went into the building, and they overstayed their passes, what it sounds like to me.\par \par \uc2 Now, we all know any of us have gotten out of those buildings, you need credentials.\par \par \uc2 CONCHA: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You go through magnetometers. When you're done with your interview, it's time to leave. They don't let you just wander around the halls and go over to Kevin McCarthy's office or (INAUDIBLE) office.\par \par \uc2 We'll see -- we'll see what comes out about all of this. They didn't injure anybody. They weren't trying to, you know, change an election outcome. But nonetheless, as Turley says, "The arrest could put Speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  in an awkward position. Speaker Pelosi has repeatedly attacked Republicans for not taking the new security precaution seriously and imposing hefty fines for failures to comply, including evading metal detectors. It may be hard to spin these arrests. Or seek the dropping of the charges in light of statements of the Speaker, though she was also accused of evading those new protections." Joe.\par \par \uc2 CONCHA: And we don't hear the House Speaker speaking about this very much.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: No.\par \par \uc2 CONCHA: It's almost like she's, you know, avoiding this, like she avoids the Paul Pelosi arrest a couple of weeks ago when he crashed his \uc2 Porsche\uc2  in an intersection.\par \par \uc2 And the thing is, they can get away with not speaking about it, because the media now is just hoping the story just washes away.\par \par \uc2 Stephen Colbert, for his own part has not tweeted about this and has not spoken about in any way, shape, or form. He's usually pretty unapologetic when it comes to everything. Haven't heard much from him.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right. We will see when he weighs in on it.\par \par \uc2 Thank you very much, Joe. Great to see you.\par \par \uc2 CONCHA: Great to see you as always.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Joe Concha media and politics analyst for us.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM (voice-over): So, will President Biden confront the Saudi Prince \uc2 Mohammed bin Salman\uc2 , about the killing of Jamal Khashoggi when he heads to Saudi Arabia?\par \par \uc2 Well, you know what? It kind of depends on whom you ask.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I'm not going to be with MBS, I'm going to an international meetings.\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER GRANHOLM\uc2 , UNITED STATES ENERGY SECRETARY: He is going for the purpose of this international conference, but I know -- well --\par \par \uc2 DANA BASH, CHIEF POLITICAL CORRESPONDENT, CNN: While they -- will they meet?\par \par \uc2 GRANHOLM: I think he will meet with the Saudi crown prince.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: OK, Congressman Michael Waltz on the Biden administration's mixed message on this. Next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM (on camera): So, this caught my eye this morning. British troops, "must prepare to fight in Europe once again. That coming from the new U.K. head of the Army.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM (voice-over): General Sir Patrick Sanders sent in a message to his soldiers that Russia's war on Ukraine "Underlines our core purpose to protect the U.K. by being ready to fight and win wars on land."\par \par \uc2 Prime Minister \uc2 Boris Johnson\uc2  has said that British forces will not fight the Russians in Ukraine. However, Russian bombings continue to fall all over Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 The country confirms now that Russia has now also gained control of a village near Sievierodonetsk, tightening Russia's hold on the region.\par \par \uc2 And meantime, Germany says that they will have to start again to fire up their coal plants as Russia continues to tighten the flow of natural gas to Europe. Germany calling the move a bitter but necessary step in this environment.\par \par \uc2 And is President Biden going to meet with the Saudi Crown Prince \uc2 Mohammed bin Salman\uc2  next month? It's a question that the White House has fielded multiple times this past week, and the answers are conflicting.\par \par \uc2 Remember on the campaign trail, candidate Biden said that he would make Saudi Arabia a pariah after the death of journalist Jamal Khashoggi.\par \par \uc2 Congressman Michael Waltz is standing by. But first, to the White House correspondent -- to our White House correspondent Jacqui Heinrich. Hi, Jacqui.\par \par \uc2 JACQUI HEINRICH, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Hey there, Martha. Well, President Biden again today framed his upcoming visit to Saudi Arabia as an international trip that the Crown Prince \uc2 Mohammed bin Salman\uc2  just happens to be a part of.\par \par \uc2 But team Biden, many members, including his energy secretary have repeatedly admitted that Biden will meet face to face with MBS, and he'll be pushing for the Saudis to ramp up oil production.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GRANHOLM: I think he will meet with the Saudi crown prince. He has asked for all suppliers around the globe to increase production. That includes \uc2 OPEC\uc2 , that includes our domestic oil and gas producers.\par \par \uc2 He is asking for an increase like other leaders around the globe are increased -- are asking --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 BASH: So, they will have a one on one meeting?\par \par \uc2 GRANHOLM: That's my understanding that he will be meeting --\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Now, this is an about face after Biden, on the campaign trail, called the crown prince and the Saudi government a pariah, and more recently released a CIA report saying that the crown prince ordered the killing of \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  journalist Jamal Khashoggi.\par \par \uc2 The White House has been dodging questions over whether Biden accepts that report as his own view.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: But I'm still not hearing you say yes, he agrees with that assessment that MBS authorized the killing of Jamal Khashoggi.\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , SENIOR ADVISER TO \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 : I'm telling you, this is something that was -- the president took very, very seriously and he released that report.\par \par \uc2 So, clearly, he would not release that report. If he did, this is not something that he believed in.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: But with gas prices where they are, there is evidently reason to try a new approach. The White House is reconsidering an idea they initially tabled sending gas rebate cards out to Americans.\par \par \uc2 And the president said today, they hope to have a decision by the end of this week on whether to extend a gas tax holiday. Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, we'll see what happens with that. Jacqui, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 With that, we bring in Florida Congressman Michael Waltz. He serves on the \uc2 House Armed Services Committee\uc2  and is a former Green Beret commander.\par \par \uc2 Congressman, great to have you with us. The White House has said that this trip --\par \par \uc2 REP. MICHAEL WALTZ (R-FL): Yes, thanks.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: -- to Saudi Arabia is not one that's being made out of desperation over the oil situation. What's your take?\par \par \uc2 WALTZ: Yes, Martha. Isn't it incredible that, once again, the president has boxed himself in with his asinine energy policy, that he is having to go over to the Middle East after insulting the Saudis throughout his campaign and go over as a supplicant?\par \par \uc2 Basically, I mean, it's transparent to everyone that he's over there to Mother May I, beg and plead for the Saudis to pump more, when all he would have to do is take on the progressives here back home, and pump more which, oh, by the way, is cleaner American oil and gas.\par \par \uc2 And if he would just do that, if he would just take them on politically, he would not only be lowering prices for Americans and helping himself politically, but he would also be starving oil-rich dictatorships around the world, from Iran to Venezuela to Putin himself, of their cache.\par \par \uc2 And, by the way, just very quickly, Martha, Putin right now is back at pre- war export levels. For every European country that has turned away or the United States that turn away, China has stepped in two-fold. And China's now seeing a 50 percent increase in imports of Russian gas.\par \par \uc2 So, this is just broken across the board. And there is one fix, that's unleash American energy, and he refuses to do it.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, it's a great point. India is also taking a lot of Russian oil. So, that's --\par \par \uc2 WALTZ: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: -- why Russia can afford to say, well, Germany, we're going to cut you off, because they are getting --\par \par \uc2 WALTZ: That's right.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: -- you know, an enormous amount of demand coming from China, and India, and others in the course of this whole thing.\par \par \uc2 You know, with regard to this is Olivier Knox's analysis. He wrote about this today. He said, "That pariah," meaning MBS, "has extensive access to the White House, new U.S. arms sales, and now a presidential visit -- including a face-to-face with MBS, who wasn't even welcome on the phone a year ago. This, over fierce opposition from Saudi human rights activists, who see the crown prince as the architect of repression at home."\par \par \uc2 And a U.S. intelligence report clearly laid out in February of 2021, Congressman, "We assess that Saudi Arabia's Crown Prince \uc2 Mohammed bin Salman\uc2  approved an operation in Istanbul Turkey to capture or kill Saudi journalist," Jamal Khashoggi.\par \par \uc2 So, what do you suggest we do about that?\par \par \uc2 WALTZ: Well, that look, that the killing of a journalist is despicable. It should be called out. It's unacceptable.\par \par \uc2 But again, the president now put himself in the position, he did that, of having to then, still, go to the Saudis and beg them, as a leader of \uc2 OPEC\uc2 , to pump more. What we should do about it? We should pump ourselves.\par \par \uc2 I do have to say though, it is a bit ironic from the left who are just having a meltdown over this trip, and his engagement with MBS over the killing of a journalist. They say absolutely nothing when it comes to the president's engagement, and I would say even pandering, to the Chinese that are arresting and murdering journalists, human rights activists, doctors who call out COVID --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: That's a great point.\par \par \uc2 WALTZ: -- and a million Muslims. So I mean, it is a little, kind of, all over the place from their perspective and a bit hypocritical.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, quick question with regard to Nicaragua. There are reports that Russian troops are training in Nicaragua at this point, how much of a concern is that to you?\par \par \uc2 WALTZ: Oh, it's a huge concern. I mean, I feel like we're going back to the '70s with the Biden administration and we're going back to that as well, the Russians in Central America. They were already in Venezuela.\par \par \uc2 This administration, as we saw with the disaster that was an \uc2 Organization of American States\uc2 , has completely turned their back on the Western Hemisphere, on our own backyard.\par \par \uc2 Well, you know who's there big time? The Chinese, who control both sides of the Panama Canal now, and now the Russians. So, I mean, it's just -- once again, just a disaster after disaster on the foreign policy front for this administration.\par \par \uc2 Final thing, I hope, back to the Saudi trip, the President can sit aside his disdain of President Trump, his ego, and actually talk about the Abraham Accords.\par \par \uc2 He's going to Israel after, wouldn't it be great if we've revived those amazing -- that amazing dialogue and had some kind of engagement between the United States, the Saudis, and the Israelis to start moving peace in the Middle East forward again?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. That's --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 WALTZ: That's what I hope to see.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: It would be a very powerful alliance, and that was a huge breakthrough agreement reported on all sides.\par \par \uc2 Thank you very much, Congressman. Always good to see you. Congressman Michael Waltz, thank you sir.\par \par \uc2 WALTZ: Thanks, Martha. All right.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You bet.\par \par \uc2 So we have been digging into this story. It is a tough one but it is important. Were the doors to the classrooms inside Robb Elementary School open all along, as police waited 77 minutes in that hallway before confronting the shooter? It's just the latest question that is coming out of Uvalde, three investigations are going on, and we have just learned when they may be able to get some answers next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So we have been on this story and they're brand new developments in this investigation into what happened at Robb Elementary in Uvalde, Texas. As the chairman of the committee tells our producer, we could get a preliminary report in mid July, just a few weeks away. And as reports claim, police waited 77 minutes now, the longer time period that is now this now surfacing in the hallway outside the classroom while a gunman killed 19 students and two teachers. And the surveillance video suggests that they never tried the door to see whether or not the door was locked. Correspondent Garrett Tenney fills us in on the reporting and the investigation. Hi, Garrett.\par \par \uc2 GARRETT TENNEY, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Yes, Martha. You mentioned that police officers were reportedly outside that classroom for more than an hour waiting for a key to be able to get inside. Now we are learning that door may not have even been locked. You'll remember the doors, those metal frame doors are designed to lock automatically when they are closed. But as the San Antonio Express was the first to report citing a source close to the investigation, investigators now believe the gunman couldn't have locked the door from the inside.\par \par \uc2 Surveillance video from inside the school reportedly shows the gunman was able to open the door and walk in without a key, suggesting there may have been some kind of a malfunction in the door locks at the school, especially since the exterior door that he used to get inside the building had also failed to lock automatically after a teacher closed it. The officers outside the classroom had no way of knowing that. And surveillance video reportedly shows they never checked to see if the door was in fact locked.\par \par \uc2 This comes as the \uc2 New York Times\uc2  reports one of the first Uvalde officers to arrive at the school had a split chance to shoot the gunman outside the school, but hesitated because there were children in the background that could have been hit if he missed. We are also learning more about the extent to which Uvalde City officials are fighting the public release of information related to the shooting. According to Texas Public Radio, the city of Uvalde has hired a private law firm to argue that to the state's attorney general that records from the investigation should not be released. Among the reasons why, the city is being sued, some individual's criminal history could be highly embarrassing, and it's not of legitimate concern to the public. The state's attorney general will ultimately decide what records get released, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: OK. Garrett, thank you so much. Garrett Tenney reporting.\par \par \uc2 So with that we bring in Chris Swecker, former Assistant Director of the FBI as Criminal Investigative Division. Sir, very good to have you here. Obviously, these are tough things to hear. But I think it's really important that we learn lessons from what happened here. Because it seems like there were a lot of mistakes and malfunctions. What jumps out at you as you hear this new reporting?\par \par \uc2 CHRIS SWECKER, FORMER ASSISTANT DIRECTOR, \uc2 FBI\uc2 : Yes. Martha, we learned lessons from Columbine, which basically set the scene that law enforcement officers must go into the sound of shooting. You don't establish a perimeter and get all your resources there and go. Parkland reinforced that, that officers that show up when the shooting is going on, while people are getting shot and killed and wounded. They have to go.\par \par \uc2 So this is reinforces that notion or that, you know, that concept which is you must go in if people are being injured and wounded, and killed, and the firing is going on no matter what you -- whatever lack of resources you have, it doesn't matter. You form the best plan you can and you go in. So I think going forward, this is going to be an interesting report that comes out of the investigation of the congressional or the State House investigation, but I think there are other investigations that are going to be far more revealing and far more impactful in terms of liability and, you know, all the way down the line.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Why do you think that the other investigations will be deeper?\par \par \uc2 SWECKER: Well, this is just an informational report. DPS is doing an investigation of the shooting. \uc2 Department of Homeland Security\uc2  is doing a shooting review. I think the Department the US Department of Justice in the form of the \uc2 FBI\uc2  is hovering over all of that, and perhaps doing their own investigation because there's a potential -- this is a shooting, there's always a civil rights aspect to a shooting because it is a police shooting, by definition, DOJ has the right to investigate.\par \par \uc2 So, there are -- there's potential criminal liability in a couple of areas here. And then they're actually -- what also comes into play is the guarantee rule, which means that you can -- court case states that you can't be compelled (inaudible) or part of the incident is for the law enforcement perspective, you can't be compelled to give a statement that violates your fifth amendment rights.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right. Well, stay tuned. But the word that there's video that may show that they never even tried to open the door is obviously very difficult. And you also heard that, that some of what might come out could be highly embarrassing. And I think that's maybe, you know, I don't know if that's the right word but we'll see what comes out of this. Chris Swecker, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 SWECKER: Long list of failures, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, long list of failures. And we have to learn from them, and that's why we continue to cover this story because we want children to be safer the next time this happens. And we want the people who are trained to act in these situations to be able to act. Thank you, Chris. Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 SWECKER: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, JD Vance is squaring off with Tim Ryan for Ohio's open currently Republican Senate seat this November, in a race that may be a bit tighter than first expected. We're going to hear directly from JD Vance. We'll get his take on this ad from another Republican Senate candidate out there aimed at members of his own party next.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ERIC GREITENS, FORMER MISSOURI GOVERNOR: I'm \uc2 Eric Greitens\uc2 , Navy Seal, and today we're going Rhino hunting.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right, folks. The midterms are coming at us just a few months away now, the latest polling from Ohio in the Senate race there. This is for Rob Portman seat that he is vacating. So this would be a place that Republicans would want to hold obviously. That seat is tighter than expected with Democratic candidate Tim Ryan in a virtual dead heat when you look at the margin of error in this poll against GOP candidate JD Vance.\par \par \uc2 Fox News Power Rankings has this race has likely are, in a state where the majority of Buckeye voters turned out for President Trump and Republicans in the last few election cycles. Ohio has been increasingly solid red over the course of those last elections. So with that, we bring in Republican Senate candidate JD Vance of Ohio. And we should note that we've also asked Tim Ryan, to appear we look forward to having him on as well. JD Vance, welcome, good to see you.\par \par \uc2 JD VANCE, REPUBLICAN SENATE CANDIDATE: Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Marine, business -- successful businessman, author of "Hillbilly Elegy," which was, you know, one of the biggest books of the last several years. So you run for Senate, decide to run in your home state of Ohio. And now it looks like this race, which as I said, would be a Republican hold is getting pretty tight. What's going on out there?\par \par \uc2 VANCE: Well, first of all, you have to remember the polls in 2020 said Trump would lose in Ohio when he won by eight points, and the polls have us ahead. But I try not to judge by the polls too much. I think at the end of the day, the race is about \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's inflationary policies that have made it harder for people to live a good life, and Tim Ryan has unfortunately gone along with those policies 100%. We don't need a rubber stamp for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . We need somebody to actually push back against what's going on in Washington.\par \par \uc2 And I think when that -- the terms of that debate are set, we're going to win. We're going to win convincingly.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You know, I mean, obviously, there's been some focus on this Jan 6 hearings. President Trump very loudly endorsed you. It said to have made a big difference in your breakout in that primary. Do you think that there are some moderate voters in Ohio who might be looking at that and wondering about whether or not they should support you?\par \par \uc2 VANCE: Sure. I think there are some people who were looking at the January 6 hearings and are very worried about it. But at the end of the day, most voters are worried about the fact that they can't afford gas, that they can't afford the basic necessities that life has gotten worse under \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . And I think that most of the people who are paying attention to the January 6 hearings are not really paying attention to the ground in Ohio, where life has gotten worse for most people, I think because of that.\par \par \uc2 You know, the issues are going to be about kitchen table issues, right? Is your life getting better? Is it easier to put basic necessities on the table? That's a debate we're going to win.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, it's interesting. We showed a clip before about Tim Ryan being asked, you know, do you want \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  to campaign with you? And he goes, you know, completely deer in the headlights at this question.\par \par \uc2 VANCE: Sure\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, you know, that raises -- and it's interesting because he's clearly distancing himself --\par \par \uc2 VANCE: Sure.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: -- from the President right now. He's also talking about tax cuts for the middle class. You know, he's moving into your territory when it comes to how to handle this inflation.\par \par \uc2 VANCE: Well, he's trying to, right? He plays a moderate on his TV commercials, but he plays a radical when he's in Washington, DC representing the people of Youngstown. So that that really is the debate in this race, is whether Tim Ryan can pretend to be somebody that his record shows he's clearly not.\par \par \uc2 I think it's funny that he's running away from \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . Of course, if I was a Democrat, I'd be running away from \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  right now too.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So when you look at the most important issues to Ohio voters, economy --\par \par \uc2 VANCE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: -- gas prices which we've talked about. But number three is abortion rights, and I think that's another area where you're going to have to, you know, show people where you stand against Tim Ryan, who is saying that he believes in choice. Now, that's going to get some moderate voters, maybe even more so moderate women voters who might be looking at this \uc2 SCOTUS\uc2  decision, and not be so sure about where you stand, which is where exactly when it comes to race and incest.\par \par \uc2 VANCE: Sure, yes. So look, I'm 100% pro life. I campaign that way. That's how I actually feel in my heart and that's important. I will say as much as -- look, people may disagree with me, there are people who are more moderate, let's say, on the abortion issue. We can have an argument, we can have a debate, we can try to identify ways to make it easier for young women to bring successful and happy children into the world, where Tim Ryan is so different from where even 90% of Ohio voters are. He's defended abortion up to 40 weeks.\par \par \uc2 So yes, let's have a debate. Like I said, I'm 100% pro-life, but it's one thing to be pro-choice and another thing to defend abortion up to 40 weeks. That's so radical and so out of touch. I don't think that the voters of Ohio are going to stand for it.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So where, you know, where do you think that line should be? I would imagine, you know, that that's going to come up in a debate. Where do you think that line should be in Ohio? Because really what's going to happen with the Supreme Court decision if it goes the way it leaked, is not that it's going to ban abortion across the country. It's going to move it back to the states and allow them to decide. So you're from Ohio, where do you think that line should be?\par \par \uc2 VANCE: Yes. You're exactly right. So, what Roe versus Wade would do would not ban abortion nationwide, it would make it a state's issue where we figure this out on a state by state level. Look, I'm in Ohio. I'm running to represent the federal legislator. But if I'm in the state of Ohio, what I'm saying is, look, we need to make it easier for young women to bring children into the world. We need to protect the rights of the unborn, yes, even from the moment of conception.\par \par \uc2 But at the end of the day, so much of what the abortion debate has done to this country, I think is it's taught us as both moms and dads, you know, on the father of three young kids, to see babies as inconveniences to be discarded. And I think we have an opportunity, whatever the Supreme Court does on Roe to really redefine the terms of debate and to think of children as blessings to be cherished, and to be nurtured, and to be welcomed. That's what we have to do as conservatives when this abortion fight takes its next step.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. I know you're going to be asked this in a debate and I don't mean to belabor this, because there's a obviously the inflation issue and economies bear. But in terms of rape and incest, do you have an exception for that or no?\par \par \uc2 VANCE: Look, like I said, I'm 100% pro-life. And I think that if you look at where most abortions are happening, they're not happening in the edge cases. They're incredibly tragic, they're incredibly terrible, but most of the abortion debate in this country is abortions of choice abortions of convenience. And I think that's where we should focus, because that's where the policy is actually happening.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: OK. This is not your race, this is Missouri, but this ad is getting a bit of attention. It's a web ad, I believe, I don't think it's on TV, from \uc2 Eric Greitens\uc2  who's running in Missouri. And \uc2 Josh Hawley\uc2  came out on the backside of this ad and has put his support. He's endorsing Vicky Hartzler in this race. But let's take a look at it. I'm just curious because it speaks to the tenor of politics in this country right now. And I want to see what you think, let's watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GREITENS: Join the MAGA crew, get a rhino hunting permit. There's no bagging limit, no tagging limit, and it doesn't expire until we save our country.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: What do you think?\par \par \uc2 VANCE: Well, \uc2 Josh Hawley\uc2  is a very good friend of mine. I think that so much of political advertisement. Look, I've run my own political advertisements in this campaign. You're trying to grab headlines a little bit. You're trying to do something that's a little bit unorthodox. So at the end of the day, I think that what Greitens is trying to go after is, look, a lot of our voters, a lot of our base, very frustrated with sending Republicans to Washington who don't do anything. I'm not going to comment on the Missouri race because it's not my race, and it's not my state. But I'm not surprised to ads like that --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: (Inaudible) to a house with a gun and saying they want a bag as many rhinos as they possibly can. Do you think that's a good place to go, given all the violence that we are seeing in the country right now?\par \par \uc2 VANCE: Look, like I said, good friend, good friend -- a friend of \uc2 Josh Hawley\uc2 , I don't think \uc2 Eric Greitens\uc2  is saying that he wants to murder a bunch of people. I think it's clearly a television advertisement that perfect, we should take it like that.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: OK. Last thought in terms of Tim Ryan and in terms of the economy, are you going to also be backing middle class tax cuts? I mean, this is where -- this is also going to be on this debate stage. And do you believe that he's behind that idea in a sincere way?\par \par \uc2 VANCE: No, of course.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: President talked about corporate tax hike stay and taxing the rich more.\par \par \uc2 VANCE: Exactly. Look, I'm pro middle class tax cuts. I'm pro tax cuts. I've supported tax cuts when they were being debated. He has opposed every single effort to cut the taxes of the middle class. So he's running away from his record. Again, at the end of the day, we have to fix the fundamentals of this \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  economy. That is where we really have to do the work. Not with gimmicks from a guy who's never supported a real tax cut in his life.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right. As I said, we've invited Tim Ryan to come as well. JD Vance, very good to have you. Thanks for stopping by.\par \par \uc2 VANCE: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Hope to see you again soon, going to be an interesting race to watch. Thank you, sir.\par \par \uc2 So, we have a breaking news story in New York right now. There has been a taxi crash that has sent several people to the hospital. We got an aerial view of this. It's a bad situation. The (inaudible) right on the sidewalk in Manhattan, six people are hurt, including the driver. They're all in the hospital. Three are in critical condition. Police say right now that it looks like this was an accident. We're going to have more on that for you as we get it.\par \par \uc2 And a California mom is now warning other parents about the extreme dangers of fentanyl, after her 17-year-old son Zachary Didier was killed by a fake pill, a counterfeit pill, thought to be Percocet but made up of fentanyl. A devastating trend among American teenagers as we learn that fentanyl contributed to 77% of adolescent deaths. Think about that, 77% of the kids who died in 2021 in this country between the ages of 14 and 18 can be traced back to fentanyl.\par \par \uc2 With that we bring in Dr. Marc Siegel, a Fox News Medical contributor and professor of medicine at \uc2 NYU Langone Medical Center\uc2 . And Laura, who is the mother of Zachary, who says that we all need to talk about what is happening. Welcome to both of you.\par \par \uc2 Laura, let me start with you. Tell us what happened to Zach.\par \par \uc2 LAURA DIDIER, MOM OF FENTANYL POISONING VICTIM: So this was nothing we were prepared for. Zach was a 17-year-old high school senior. He had no history with abusing drugs or alcohol, so it was a complete shock to us when he was found at his desk, passed away two days after Christmas of 2020 came to find out he had gotten what he thought was a Percocet pill and he had had taken that pill after everyone had gone to bed and never woke up.\par \par \uc2 So, when the authorities came, there were no drugs newsroom, there was no injury to his body. So it was a real mystery why he would be dead. But tragically in our county, \uc2 Placer County\uc2 , California, there was a large uptick in fentanyl deaths. So the coroner's office was the first one to suggest it was fentanyl.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: He's a beautiful boy. And I am so sorry for your loss, and I give you so much credit for having the strength to speak out about this because it's something that everybody needs to know more about and be more aware of.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Siegel, tell us what we need to understand here and what should parents say to their teenage kids.\par \par \uc2 MARC SIEGEL, FOX NEWS MEDICAL CONTRIBUTOR: Well, it starts with transnational criminal organizations in Mexico, that's cobbling together supplies from China and now India, fentanyl, and putting them in pills as Laura just said, that look like something else, look like Percocet, look like Xanax, look like Adderall. And that makes it almost impossible for physicians and parents to warn kids properly because this is a new situation than what we've had before you. They think they're getting one thing and getting something else.\par \par \uc2 So, the idea that fentanyl is 50 to 100 times more potent than morphine is an enormous part of this because it stops breathing, and we can have Narcan but we don't have enough Narcan around which is a great antidote, and it doesn't last long enough. So I tell parents out there to be on the lookout for what your teens are doing with social media to have a conversation with them about it, to talk to them about -- and look for changes in behavior also, sudden changes in behavior. Are they becoming more isolated because of the pandemic, is there more depression going on, more anxiety. The mental health crisis right now, because of the pandemic is directly tied into the substance abuse problem.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. Laura, does any of that ring true for you? Was any of that true in your situation?\par \par \uc2 DIDIER: Well, Zach was very happy. I mean, his concerns, it was obviously locked down and it was his senior year of high school. So he's certainly, you know, had some struggles with that. But like every child was having, it wasn't out of the norm for this extreme circumstance. And all of his friends we've spoken to afterwards, they were like Zach was happy. Zach was doing great. And just they're being so blindsided by it as well. Why is our best friend gone? It just showed me what an information gap there was, and I just really want to try to fill it.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: And our heart breaks for you. And it can happen to any child, they cannot take any pill that anyone gives them because they don't know what's in it. 20 seconds, Dr. Siegel, how much of this is due to an open border,\par \par \uc2 SIEGEL: A lot of it is due to an open border. And I want to emphasize what Laura just said if the only one pill can kill, you may not have any warning. And now (inaudible) was coming on the block through Mexico as well, which is 20 times more powerful than fentanyl. So we have an enormous problem and physicians have to bear in on this and get educated. And we have antidotes to this called Suboxone. They have to get out there as well.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: It's a big issue, we're going to stay on it. Thank you both so much for speaking out. Laura, thank you. And Dr. Siegel, it's always great to have you with us. Thank you, sir.\par \par \uc2 So that is "The Story" for this Monday but as always "The Story" goes on. So we will see you back here at 3:00. Have a great afternoon, everybody. Thanks for being here.\par \par \uc2 1530\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document HUNDD00020220621ei6k00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Americans Losing Confidence In Biden Presidency\b0\par\par\uc2 Pete Hegseth, Chad Pergram, Joe Concha, Trace Gallagher, Jason  Chaffetz, Gregg Jarrett, Sara Carter, Tom Homan, Rachel Campos-Duffy \par \uc2 8,034 \uc2 words\par \uc2 17 June 2022\par \uc2 Fox News: Hannity\par \uc2 SHAN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 PETE HEGSETH, FOX NEWS HOST: Welcome to this special edition of HANNITY. I'm Pete Hegseth, in tonight for Sean.\par \par \uc2 And we begin with that FOX News alert because multiple people associated with Stephen Colbert's "The Late Show" were arrested yesterday at the U.S. Capitol and charged with illegal entry.\par \par \uc2 Here with the latest is our own Chad Pergram -- Chad.\par \par \uc2 CHAD PERGRAM, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Good evening, Pete.\par \par \uc2 Well, seven people according to U.S. \uc2 Capitol police\uc2  have been arrested with unlawful entry. They were all associated with "The Late Show with Stephen Colbert" on \uc2 CBS\uc2 . They came yesterday for the 1/6 committee hearing in the Cannon House office building.\par \par \uc2 Now, FOX has confirmed that the group came to the house office buildings to interview two members of the 1/6 committee, Adam Schiff and \uc2 Stephanie Murphy\uc2 . They also interviewed Massachusetts Democrat Jake Auchincloss. Auchincloss is not on the 1/6 committee.\par \par \uc2 Capitol police\uc2  shooed the group away from the 1/6 committee hearing area because they did not have proper press credentials. FOX is told the House radio and TV gallery denied them credentials because they are not news. However, FOX has confirmed that the group was let back into the Longworth house office building by an aide to Auchincloss after 4:00 p.m. FOX is told the aide thought they had more interviews to do. They remained in the House office buildings unattended for hours.\par \par \uc2 The group was arrested after the building closed. The arrest came around 8:30 p.m. Eastern Time. They were near the office of GOP Colorado Representative \uc2 Lauren Boebert\uc2 . They had no escort at that point.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. RODNEY DAVIS (R-IL): We've got to get more information. That's why we need an investigation. We need to make sure that we understand who in their offices brought these individuals in. It's my -- it's my understanding that these individuals are brought in by someone associated with Adam Schiff's office.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: Now, \uc2 Capitol police\uc2  say they arrested seven people charged them with unlawful entry. In a statement, \uc2 Capitol police\uc2  say, quote, the building was closed to visitors and these individuals were determined to be a part of a group that had been directed by the \uc2 Capitol police\uc2  to leave the building earlier in the day. Among those arrested, Robert Smigel. He is known for producing TV funhouse on "Saturday Night Live". He also does the voice of "Triumph, the Insult Comic Dog".\par \par \uc2 Now, we have reached out to \uc2 CBS\uc2 , we have nothing yet here, and this came, Pete just after the 1/6 committee released a video earlier in the week of GOP Georgia Representative Barry Loudermilk giving tours in the house office buildings on January 5th of last year, one day before the Capitol riot.\par \par \uc2 The Democrats on the committee, they have released these tapes and they have said that GOP members gave what they termed surveillance tours of the U.S. Capitol ahead of the riot -- Pete.\par \par \uc2 HEGSETH: Bizarre. Comic the Insult Dog roaming the Capitol. Chad Pergram, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 All right. Also tonight, the Biden presidency is going from well bad to worse. As inflation surges, gas prices hit one record after another, stocks are sinking and Americans are suffering under the cost of \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's failing far left agenda. And \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  himself, he couldn't be more checked out. Just look at your screen.\par \par \uc2 Earlier today, Biden was spotted literally, you know, jogging, running to Marine One. He couldn't get there quick enough as he scurried out of Washington for yet another relaxing weekend at the beaches of Delaware.\par \par \uc2 And even his wife Jill had to grab him by the arm and tell him, quote, we've got to go. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Number three, I think we're going to be in a position where we're going --\par \par \uc2 JILL BIDEN, FIRST LADY: We've got to go.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 PEOPLE: The 1.2 billion people in India are waiting for you to visit India.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I've been to India twice and I'll be back in India and my relationship is very good.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HEGSETH: Jill Biden playing the role of the Easter Bunny.\par \par \uc2 Now, Biden's blunders come as his approval continues to hit one low after another because according to a new poll from \uc2 YouGov\uc2 , Biden's disapproval it's at yet another new high. A whopping 56 percent of Americans disapprove and get this more Americans now say they would support \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in a potential rematch by a margin of 44-42.\par \par \uc2 But if all that wasn't bad enough for Biden and the far-left Democrats a new report from "The Hill" is exposing more White House turmoil, with several aides reportedly tapped out the article says and looking for the exits. I mean, can you blame them?\par \par \uc2 Having to constantly clean up and walk back Joe's remarks having to defend one failed policy and blunder after another, and having to work for a president who frankly is in steep decline and seems to be trying to bring down the country with him.\par \par \uc2 Costs surging because of \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . Recession fears growing because of \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . And confidence in American leadership is dwindling because of \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Here for reaction former White House chief of staff Reince Priebus, along with FOX News contributor Joe Concha.\par \par \uc2 Thank you both for being here.\par \par \uc2 Reince, let me start with you.\par \par \uc2 You had a boss who was famously unorthodox but everyone knew where the buck stopped, who was making the calls. What do you make of what's happening for staffers, for the organization inside the current Biden White House?\par \par \uc2 REINCE PRIEBUS, FORMER WHITE HOUSE CHIEF OF STAFF: Well, I -- thanks for having me, Pete. First of all, you're right. I mean \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was the chief of staff. He was the comms director, the press secretary, you know that and we all know that.\par \par \uc2 But the one thing that he did do was that there were plenty of inputs. He listened to everyone's opinion and he listened to people who believed the opposite things about a particular issue and he had -- he had a lot of inputs.\par \par \uc2 And so, I think one of the things that you're seeing from \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and this decision making blunder after blunder is that he doesn't have a lot of inputs which creates a scenario for a lot of bad decision making and then what happens there is that nothing's going well.\par \par \uc2 I mean what \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has done is he directly attacked the policies of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and those direct attacks whether it be gas, whether it be inflation or you name it, haven't worked. He didn't listen to the warnings from his own economists about pumping the economy full of cash.\par \par \uc2 And so, at the end of the day, you've got a staff that is getting nowhere. They're not winning on any particular issues. The Democrat base is not happy with the president. He's at 20 percent approval -- of strong approval among Democrats.\par \par \uc2 Whereas if you look at \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , in spite of a very vitriolic political environment he was always at strong approval within his own party.\par \par \uc2 Last thing, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  didn't do what he promised. He said he was going to look out for the American worker and the American worker is getting cremated every single day out there across America.\par \par \uc2 HEGSETH: No doubt.\par \par \uc2 Joe, what is the communication strategy? So policy is one thing and that's the most important thing, then you don't have to spin it if it's actually working. But if you're having to spin it and you're staring at a binder and you're giving late night interviews and you're being pulled away from the press pool by your wife, what is -- what is their approach?\par \par \uc2 JOE CONCHA, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Well, the communication strategy p is craptastically, patently horrible, right? Because for instance, the president said in an interview this week that inflation is high all around the world. That we have a global problem and we're just a part of that global problem.\par \par \uc2 And then you look at all the countries that have lower inflation than we do. China, for example, has 2.1 percent inflation. We are at 8.6 percent. We are four times more than China right now, and I could go through all the countries, from Saudi Arabia to India to Indonesia to Australia to Italy that have lower inflation than we do. Yet this president thinks that we're stupid enough to think that, okay, well, everybody's having bad inflation, it's just something that's happening globally and I really have nothing to do with it.\par \par \uc2 So, look, if \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  runs and it's almost virtually guaranteed that he will because you don't hold rally after rally in the past year if that isn't the plan, he has some pretty easy contrast to make if he stays on message. On inflation, I'm fairly certain that a businessman like Trump wouldn't say that the solution like Biden has is for lowering inflation is spending trillions more.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 CONCHA: You know, just a hunch on that one that Trump's not going to go to that route, right? Exactly, on gas prices, it's absolutely certain that a President Trump 2.0 or \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  if he's a nominee or basically any Republican not named, you know, Kinzinger or Cheney would restart the expansion of the Keystone pipeline, the XL pipeline, would open up drilling in ANWR, and wouldn't reject these oil leases in the Gulf of Mexico and the Alaska coast.\par \par \uc2 And on the U.S. border, if we could even call that anymore, wall construction would be restarted, remain in Mexico policy would be reinstated. Look, the bottom line is that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is currently polling at the lowest numbers of any first term president in polling history. You got to go all the way back to Truman to Trump and he's lower than that.\par \par \uc2 And David Axelrod, and he's a former chief adviser to Obama Biden in that administration, said that the prospect of a \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  being closer to his 90s than his 80s in the second term is one that almost all Americans -- Democrat, Republican -- will not embrace in any capacity. That's where we are right now, Pete.\par \par \uc2 HEGSETH: Reince, real quick. Joe just laid it all out. If you're \uc2 Ron Klain\uc2  and the chief of staff, you've been in that seat, what do you do right now?\par \par \uc2 PRIEBUS: You walk into the Oval Office and say, Mr. President, it's not working out. You're not happy. I'm not happy. Let's find a replacement to start this thing over because they need a reset.\par \par \uc2 You know, if you -- if you took the SAT today and guess C every single time, you'd be have a better score than what \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and his team has done.\par \par \uc2 HEGSETH: That is an indictment.\par \par \uc2 Reince and Joe, we got to leave it right there. Thank you both for breaking it down this morning -- we've heard this morning. I'm a "FOX and Friends" guy it's the evening. Sorry, guys.\par \par \uc2 All right. Now, beyond all of Biden's blunders and beyond all his baffling and bizarre moments, failed energy policy is still actually at the center of \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's struggling presidency.\par \par \uc2 Remember, it's Biden who surrendered his agenda to the fringe far left climate alarmist from day one. Those folks who want to ban fossil fuels. And it's \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  who canceled the Keystone XL pipeline. It's \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  who's refused to unleash the power of America's energy sector and refused to restore Trump-era energy independence.\par \par \uc2 Sadly the consequences are getting more devastating by the day. For example, farmers are now warning that record diesel prices could actually lead to food shortages because farmers say they rely heavily as they do on diesel to fuel their tractors and heavy machinery. So just 18 months, we've gone from builds back better that was a promise to surging inflation, surging gas prices, along with a border catastrophe, a baby formula shortage, a possible food shortage now, surrendering to the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  in Afghanistan, a massive crime surge, and I could go on and on.\par \par \uc2 Ask yourself: has \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  done one singular thing to improve the lives of common sense tax paying law abiding Americans? Has he done one thing to make your day-to-day life better?\par \par \uc2 For many, Americans the answer appears to be a resounding no.\par \par \uc2 Here for reaction co-host of the "Buck and Clay Show," Buck Sexton, along with former Massachusetts Senator Scott Brown.\par \par \uc2 Buck, let me start with you. Food shortages adding that to the list. I mean, it's inflation. It's gas. We didn't no one had baby formula under bingo card.\par \par \uc2 Now, food shortages? That is incredibly serious.\par \par \uc2 BUCK SEXTON, CO-HOST OF "THE BUCK SEXTON AND CLAY TRAVIS SHOW": Oh, it's going to get worse