k out into the universe. We need it now more than ever.\par \par \uc2 Look, this misgendering policy um is very subversive. It's about forcing your kids to submit, to say things they know are not true and to do so because the government says they should. And if you talk to anyone who has escaped communism whether it was the Soviet Union or Cuba, China, Venezuela, they will tell you not to cooperate with these regime lies and our children in the school system Pete as you know are captive victims and they need, they require us to be courageous and stand up for them whether it's mass policy or misgendering policies, this is very un-American.\par \par \uc2 HEGSETH: Absolutely right.\par \par \uc2 Carol, the idea of kids, high school kids, middle school kids having to identify themselves as oppressor or oppressed, what effect does that have, in New Jersey?\par \par \uc2 DR. CAROL SWAIN, AUTHOR AND FORMER POLITICAL SCIENCE PROFESSOR: Well, I mean, it shames children, and it violates their rights. And I think it's important for parents to get all the knowledge and information they can and so they need to read books like your new book. I have a book on critical race theory. There's tons of materials out there.\par \par \uc2 And American parents and grandparents are equipping themselves, they are fighting back and they need to because our children's lives are being destroyed, that innocence is being stolen. And the people that are doing it I think they have evil motives and we need to fight back.\par \par \uc2 HEGSETH: So true.\par \par \uc2 Rachel, you're a parent. We all know that. What are parents and grandparents to do? We see this. It's seemingly everywhere.\par \par \uc2 CAMPOS-DUFFY: I think both of you would agree with me that the answer is to -- if you're -- if you don't think your school can change and so many of the public schools can't, the answer is to pull them out, to home school and to find a great classical great books academy that you and I have both found for our own children at great sacrifice and for me having to drive a long distance, but we're willing to do it because we know it's the right thing to do.\par \par \uc2 I'll say this, Pete, a lot of this craziness came out of the Ivy Leagues that you came from, frankly, and I've known you a long time. People don't realize that I've known you -- you know, our friendship predates Fox and -- I would say that you've said many times that you would -- you would like to return your degree back to Harvard. Maybe it's time that you do that finally. Maybe Harvard wants you to, Pete.\par \par \uc2 HEGSETH: Leave it to Rachel Campos-Duffy. I have said that, Rachel. Maybe, maybe now is the time. I have. Maybe tomorrow morning on FOX and maybe you put me in the spotlight and maybe now is the time to give it back. They don't deserve --\par \par \uc2 SWAIN: Maybe we should boycott the Ivy League.\par \par \uc2 HEGSETH: Absolutely -- maybe we should -- that's an even better point. So it's a two for now, the pressure's on, because two ladies I respect immensely have put me on the spot.\par \par \uc2 CAMPOS-DUFFY: I think there's another reason to tune in to "FOX and Friends" on the weekend now. Let's see if Pete actually returns or tears that degree.\par \par \uc2 HEGSETH: My Harvard diploma is right across the room here. I'm bringing it to the set tomorrow. I'll see you, Rachel.\par \par \uc2 Thank you both very much. More HANNITY after the break.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HEGSETH: Well, welcome back to this special edition of HANNITY. Unfortunately, that is all the time we have left for tonight. Be sure to tune in tomorrow morning for "FOX and Friends Weekend". Rachel Campos-Duffy just ensured it might be a little bit more interesting on the couch tomorrow with her challenge.\par \par \uc2 Don't worry. \uc2 Sean Hannity\uc2  is back on Monday. Have a great night, everybody.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document SHAN000020220618ei6h00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Increase In Arrests Of People On Terror Watch List In Southern Border; \uc2 FBI\uc2  Probing Attacks On Pregnancy Resource Centers; President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  Talks Renewable Energy As Prices Surge; Stock Market Sell-Off: Volatility Fueled By High Inflation And Recession Fears; Trump Headlines Confidence Of Faith-Based Voters; Capitol Riot Committee Hearings Loom Over Midterms; Parents And Schools Boards Across U.S. Continue To Clash Over Teaching Of Sexuality And Gender Identity Issues; China Launches New Naval Aircraft Carrier; Emma Soames, Granddaughter Of Winston Churchill, Interviewed On Her New Book About World War II; Biden Administration Officials Address Concerns Over U.S. Economy Including Inflation And Possible Recession; President Trump Gives Speech To Faith-Based Voters In Nashville, Tennessee\b0\par\par\uc2 Shannon Bream, Bill Melugin, Lucas Tomlinson, Jeanine Pirro, Peter  Doocy, Jackie DeAngelis, Rich Edson, Phil Keating, Bret Baier, Mark  Meredith, Jennifer Griffin \par \uc2 7,784 \uc2 words\par \uc2 17 June 2022\par \uc2 Fox News: Special Report with Brett Baier\par \uc2 FNSPBB\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 JOEY JONES, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR: The Crucible which is how you earn it, the gas chamber, rifle qualification, swim qualification. It's an amazing thing. Sam, congratulations. Welcome to the corps, buddy.\par \par \uc2 JEANINE PIRRO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL LEGAL ANALYST: Wow, that's fantastic. And that's it for us. We'll see you back here on Monday. Have a terrific weekend. Thanks so much, bye.\par \par \uc2 SHANNON BREAM, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Good evening and welcome to Washington. I'm Shannon Bream in for Bret Baier.\par \par \uc2 Breaking tonight, as the illegal immigration crisis along the southern border continues to break records, we take a look at just who is trying to come into this country.\par \par \uc2 We have exclusive new information tonight illustrating just how dangerous may potential migrants may be. Correspondent Bill Melugin reports from South Texas tonight. Hello, Bill.\par \par \uc2 BILL MELUGIN, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Shannon, good evening to you. That's exactly right. Brand new data in from CBP reveals that so far this fiscal year, Border Patrol has already arrested 50 people on the terror watch list right here at our southern border. Those numbers are higher than the last five years all combined together.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: They're running out to -- heading to that flat top area.\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN (voice over): An average of 1,800 illegal immigrant runners have slipped past Border Patrol every day since October, CBP sources tell Fox News.\par \par \uc2 And new CBP data on the number of potential terrorists encountered at southern border highlights why that is a major concern.\par \par \uc2 According to CBP in fiscal year 2017, there were only two people on the terror watch list arrested at the southern border. Fiscal Year 2018 had six, fscal year 2019 had zero, fiscal year 2020 had three, fiscal year 2021 had 15. And so far, in fiscal year 2022, Border Patrol has already arrested 50 people on the terror watch list.\par \par \uc2 The trend is clear, the numbers of known or suspected terrorists arrested at the southern border have exploded under President Biden.\par \par \uc2 GOV. GREG ABBOTT (R-TX): We're here once again dealing with the ongoing failure of the Biden administration to do his fundamental job.\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN: Texas Governor Greg Abbott was in the Rio Grande Valley this afternoon. He reacted to those terror watch list numbers.\par \par \uc2 ABBOTT: What you did not mention is all of those on the terrorist watch list who got away. So, what that means is there's a high probability that there are a lot of people on the terror watch list who have been -- who have come into the United States.\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN: Fox News was embedded with Texas DPS as they track down illegal immigrant runners in mission, some of them scaling offense in an effort to evade capture.\par \par \uc2 At night, more of the same as troopers found migrant runners hiding in the brush hoping to sneak into the U.S.\par \par \uc2 And in Maverick County, a human smuggler being pursued by Texas DPS bails out and runs along with his human cargo, illegal immigrants who flee into the brush in all directions.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN (on camera): And Shannon, earlier this morning, I did reach out to CBP to ask them about these elevated terror watch list arrest numbers. Specifically I asked them why do they believe the numbers are so much higher this year, in fiscal year 2022 compared to prior years?\par \par \uc2 Keep in mind, we still have four months left in fiscal year 2022. I have not yet gotten a response. We'll send it back to you.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: We know you will let us know if you do, Bill. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 President Biden has signed legislation to increase security around \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  justices and their families. It comes as left wing protesters step up threats and violence against a number of targets.\par \par \uc2 Lucas Tomlinson has details tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LUCAS TOMLINSON, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): The \uc2 FBI\uc2  now calls the attacks on pro-life centers and churches potential acts of domestic violent extremism and wants the public to report any suspicious activity, adding it as investigating a series of attacks and threats targeting pregnancy resource centers and faith based organizations across the country. The \uc2 FBI\uc2  takes all threats seriously, and we continue to work closely with our law enforcement partners.\par \par \uc2 A group calling itself Jane's revenge is taking credit for many of the attacks following the leak of the draft Supreme Court opinion which would overturn Roe versus Wade.\par \par \uc2 House Speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  did not appear to share the \uc2 FBI\uc2 's concern about the attacks on pro-life centers.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: They're saying that your rhetoric is contributing to these attacks on these crisis pregnancy centers.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 NANCY PELOSI\uc2  (D-CA): Any other questions on another subject because I'm not going to be talking about that anymore.\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON: Law enforcement remain vigilant after the presence of armed \uc2 U.S. Marshals\uc2  outside Justice \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2 's home help thwart a would be murder.\par \par \uc2 Republicans also calling on Attorney General \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  to shut down and prosecute protesters outside Supreme Court justices homes which have stayed peaceful.\par \par \uc2 SEN. MARSHA BLACKBURN (R-TN): They are breaking the law. This is a federal offense, and they need to be taken down to City Hall and booked.\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON: A majority of registered voters appear to agree, including 62 percent of independents who say it's inappropriate to protest outside the justices' homes.\par \par \uc2 President Biden signed a bill late Thursday adding round the clock protection for that justices' families, two days after the House passed the measure.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON (on camera): The outcome of the much anticipated abortion case could come as soon as Tuesday when the Supreme Court is expected to issue new opinions. Security around the court is expected to be ramped up through months end or until the future Roe versus Wade decided by those nine justices, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And we will bring it to folks when it happens. Lucas, thank you very much. Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 Well, there's more disturbing news tonight about the gunman who killed two suburban Los Angeles police officers earlier this week.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent Jeff Paul has been following the story and has the very latest now from L.A. Hello, Jeff.\par \par \uc2 JEFF PAUL, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Yes, Shannon as the shooting investigation continues, criticism over L.A. County D.A. George Gascon and his policies is only getting louder. And it is coming from not only folks within the community who are mourning the loss of both officers Corporal Michael Paredes and Officer Joseph Santana.\par \par \uc2 But now, the mother of one of the fallen officers is airing her own frustrations over Gascon. She's struggling with why the suspect who had previously been arrested on felony charges was given probation under Gascon's direction.\par \par \uc2 OLGA GARCIA, MOTHER: Gascon is letting all these criminals out. And they just keep doing one crime after the other. That guy should have been in jail, he wouldn't have been out. My son and the other officer would still be here.\par \par \uc2 PAUL: The suspect Justin Flores had already served time roughly 10 years ago for car theft and burglary but was arrested and charged with felony drug and gun charges in 2020.\par \par \uc2 He was facing almost three years in prison but after Gascon took over as D.A., a special directive push prosecutors to offer probation and plea deals.\par \par \uc2 So, we asked Gascon's office about that who told us in part, experienced managers in the office have reviewed the facts of the case, and the criminal history of Mr. Flores and determined that this offer was on par with offers in previous administrations.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PAUL (on camera): Now that statement went on to say that prosecutors were able to challenge that policy under special circumstances. But they say in this case, no requests were made, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Jeff Paul tracking it from the West Coast. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 President Biden is trying to mitigate some of the political damage he's sustaining over skyrocketing inflation and record high gas prices.\par \par \uc2 He's doing this by emphasizing his much criticized green agenda, but international issues are getting in the way.\par \par \uc2 White House Correspondent Peter Doocy shows us tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I'm using every lever available to me to bring down prices for the American people.\par \par \uc2 PETER DOOCY, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): One lever, a trip to the Middle East to speak with oil producers. Although the president won't have a private meeting with Saudi Arabia's Crown Prince.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I'm not going to be with MBS. I'm going to an international meeting and he's going to be part of it.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: That as another international crisis may be brewing for the first time the president confirms three Americans are missing in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I have been brief, we don't know where they are.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: Most talk this week has been dollars and cents. President Biden tells an A.P. reporter, you talked about a recession. First of all, it's not