ot going to start now.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 EDSON (on camera): Missing from this year's conference, former Vice President \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 . Now, source close to Pence says he was invited, but was attending an energy forum in Ohio instead.\par \par \uc2 The source also notes that Pence was at a Faith and Freedom event in North Carolina a few weeks ago. Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right. Rich Edson in Nashville. Thank you. We'll talk about this with the panel coming up.\par \par \uc2 And up next, as the economy struggles, President Biden wants to spend $10 million of your money to figure out how to ask people about their sexuality.\par \par \uc2 First, beyond our borders tonight, Palestinians blame Israel's military for three deaths during the gun battle in the West Bank. Troops were conducting a weapons raid. Israeli forces say they shot back after militants opened fire and threw explosive devices.\par \par \uc2 At least one person killed in India during protests over the country's military recruitment policy. Demonstrators' burn train coaches, blocked highways, and attacked police with rocks.\par \par \uc2 The new program forces 75 percent of military recruits to retire after four years with no pension benefits.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at Chiba, Japan. One of the big stories there tonight. The head of the \uc2 U.S. Marine Corps\uc2  vows to bolster defense in southern Japan. The area stretches from Japan's East China Sea islands through the Philippines.\par \par \uc2 A promise comes amid increased Chinese and Russian naval activity in that region.\par \par \uc2 Just some of the stories beyond our borders tonight. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: The Michigan Supreme Court is rejecting a final appeal from sports Dr. Larry Nasser, who was sentenced to decades in prison for sexually assaulting gymnasts, including Olympic medalist. Nasser's attorney said he was treated unfairly in his trial and deserves a new one.\par \par \uc2 They pointed to statements by the lower court judge who said of Nasser's 40 year sentence at the time, "I just signed your death warrant."\par \par \uc2 There's a new move by the Biden administration to spend $10 million of your taxpayer dollars to study the best ways to ask people about their sexual orientation.\par \par \uc2 This effort comes amid controversies over the role of the government, schools, and parents in this issue.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent Phil Keating reports tonight from Miami.\par \par \uc2 PHIL KEATING, FOX NEWS CHANNEL NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT (voice-over): The festivities are well underway for Pride Month. That means parties and gay pride parades, which today include LGBTQ Americans.\par \par \uc2 Count President Biden among those celebrating the month.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: And the message is simple: Pride is back at the White House.\par \par \uc2 KEATING: Counting is what the \uc2 U.S. Census Bureau\uc2  has been doing since 1902. It aims for the most exact population data it can get, like numbers, race, gender, and now, sexual orientation and gender identity.\par \par \uc2 To get that, the bureau wants to ask the question as best as possible. So, wants to spend $10 million to find out how to ask the question and get the most honest and accurate numbers.\par \par \uc2 Previous census head counts did not ask whether someone was gay, straight, lesbian, transgender or binary. The president of Miami Beach's Gay and Lesbian Chamber of Commerce, the second largest in the country, approves of what the Census Bureau is doing with some cautious hesitation.\par \par \uc2 STEVE ADKINS, PRESIDENT, GAY AND LESBIAN CHAMBER OF COMMERCE: If it is really to make our lives better to create policies within government to help us distribute goods and services more appropriately amongst all of the population bases in the country, great.\par \par 