tack from unknown hackers. Shannon?\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Jen, thank you so much from the Pentagon tonight.\par \par \uc2 Up next, Winston Churchill's granddaughter on her mother's experiences during World War II, and an update on \uc2 WikiLeaks\uc2  founder Julian Assange's legal status.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: The \uc2 British government\uc2  is ordering the extradition of \uc2 WikiLeaks\uc2  founder Julian Assange to the U.S. to face spying charges. It is the latest move in a decade-long legal saga sparked by his website's publication of classified U.S. documents. \uc2 Wikileaks\uc2  says it will challenge the order. American prosecutors say Assange helped \uc2 U.S. Army\uc2  intelligence analyst \uc2 Chelsea Manning\uc2  steal classified diplomatic cables and military files that \uc2 WikiLeaks\uc2  later published, and they say that put lives at risk.\par \par \uc2 Japan's supreme court says the government is not liable for the 2011 Fukushima nuclear crisis. It is dismissing thousands of evacuees demands that the state, not just the utility, pay compensation for damage to their lives. The ruling is the first by the top court on government responsibility for the disaster in four compensation lawsuits filed by about 3,700 Fukushima residents. Three reactors at the nuclear plant melted after a magnitude nine earthquake and tsunami hit in March of 2011, releasing massive amounts of radiation in the area and displacing more than 160,000 people.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, we bring you a unique perspective on history. The granddaughter of British Prime Minister Winston Churchill recounts her mother's experiences touring the Second World War. Emma Soames talks with my colleague, Bret Baier.\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Thanks, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is Emma Soames, granddaughter of Winston Churchill, author of the new book "Mary Churchill's War, the Wartime Diaries of Churchill's Youngest Daughter." Ms. Soames, thanks so much for being with us, glad to have you here, and fascinated about the book. What made you put your mom's diaries down and reflect on your grandfather's legacy?\par \par \uc2 EMMA SOAMES, WINSTON CHURCHILL GRANDDAUGHTER: I and my siblings, we had another look at the diaries and decided they were so interesting and compelling that they should be published in their entirety. Just to give you an idea, on page three, she quotes her father as saying, and this is 1939 at the beginning of the year, before God almighty, I say I do not want a war. And she says to this I am a witness.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: It's an amazing time as World War II is really bubbling up, and what Great Britain is getting ready to do as Winston Churchill becomes prime minister in that tumultuous time. What was your relationship with your grandfather?\par \par \uc2 SOAMES: I have very powerful childhood memories of his goldfish, his swimming pool, of him, indeed, sitting on the lawn and his Stetson with a large cigar, looking at his favorite view in the world, which was across the valley of Kent.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We have a lot of Winston Churchill lovers in this country, as you well know. One of them was our late colleague Dr. \uc2 Charles Krauthammer\uc2 , who wrote once, "Without Churchill, the world today would be unrecognizable, dark, impoverished, and tortured." This book takes a real look into a family perspective and your mother's view of the beginning of World War II, and just gives an insight that people just don't usually see.\par \par \uc2 SOAMES: Indeed, it is historical, but it was not written by a historian. When war breaks out, she is a 17-year-old girl who is literally just left school. But what it does give is a very intimate view of Churchill during the war and the stresses and strains he was under, and of her growing devotion to him that lasted all her life.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: It's a fascinating read. Congratulations. Ms. Emma Soames, granddaughter to Winston Churchill, thanks for being here.\par \par \uc2 SOAMES: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Shannon, we'll send it back to you.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Thank you, Bret.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the panel on President Biden's sinking polls, former president Trump's speech to faith-based voters in Nashville, plus their winners and losers.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JARED BERNSTEIN, WHITE HOUSE ECONOMIC ADVISER: -- frame is off, if I can be a little poetic. This country has gone from being net importer to a very significant net exporter.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , (D) PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: And the good news is climate security and energy security go hand in hand.\par \par \uc2 HEATHER BOUSHEY\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE ECONOMIC ADVISER: And family household balance sheets remain strong. So all of this puts us in a good position.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, let's see what the panel thinks, Harold Ford Jr., former Tennessee Congressman and co-host of THE FIVE, Byron York, chief political correspondent of "The Washington Examiner," and syndicated radio host \uc2 Hugh Hewitt\uc2 . Gentlemen, good to see all of you.\par \par \uc2 You heard what the administration had to say, and that final line, family household balance sheets remain strong. But let's look at our brand-new FOX News polling, what's a problem for your family? Gas prices, 90 percent said yes. Grocery prices, 88 percent. Utility costs, 81 percent. Healthcare costs -- the list goes on.\par \par \uc2 So Hugh, there is some disconnect between what the house is saying about the health of our economy and how real Americans actually feel in their own lives about it.\par \par \uc2 HUGH HEWITT, SYNDICATED RADIO HOST: Shannon, "disconnect" is charitable. I remember in March of the first year of the Biden presidency, he brought in all the presidential historians to talk about FDR, Michael Beschloss, Doris Kearns Goodwin, Eddie Glaude Jr. And they spent two hours talking about how to be FDR. They ought to have talked about how to avoid being Hoover, because these are Hoover-like numbers, and we are headed towards 1930 and 1932 style election for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . It's been that rough of a week.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And he in talking to the Associated Press, admits that and acknowledges that. Now, he starts out by saying this, that Americans are really, really down. He says -- they say he bristled at claims by Republican lawmakers that last year's COVID-19 aid plan was fully to blame for inflation reaching this 40-year high, calling that argument bizarre. But as for the overall American mindset, Biden said, quote, people are really, really down. Harold, that's not a good place to be going into a very contentious midterm.\par \par \uc2 HAROLD FORD JR., FORMER TENNESSEE REPRESENTATIVE: Certainly not on a Friday afternoon, and happy Friday. I think the White House has to understand that blaming or trying to assign responsibility outside of the White House for some of these challenges is not what the American people looking for, even if it's the truth. And who is it for me to say whether they know better than I, but what I do know is that the American people don't believe that gas prices are falling, that fuel prices are falling, and that their lives economically are looking better.\par \par \uc2 There is no doubt that extremism on both parties over the last year is affecting this, from public safety to how we think about the economy. And for that matter, how we even think about protecting our Supreme Court justices.\par \par \uc2 I was pleased they seemed to be getting something done around guns. It's not what everybody wants. I say this because maybe it means they can begin to address some of the challenges facing everyday families as well. But for the White House to believe everything is OK and that things are only bizarre in the minds of those who believe that the economy and inflation is high, I think they need to