nce that he's going to run before the midterms.\par \par \uc2 But the real big question here, the big question is, what are the other Republican candidates going to do if President Trump does enter the race? Is it going to clear the field, or are they all going to stay in and have a robust multicandidate primary? That's really the big question in the Republican race right now.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: In the meantime, several pieces recently talking about the power of the evangelical voting bloc, that it's still very much active in what happens within the GOP, and certainly this event is aimed at that. "National Review," though, asking these questions, "Why are Republicans still defending him? And more so, why are Christian conservatives catering to him, even adoring him? Please, reasonable people on the right, it is long past time to move on from \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  whose very presence divides." Hugh, what's your take on that debate?\par \par \uc2 HUGH HEWITT, SYNDICATED RADIO HOST: Very simply, the men in the presidency who were most faith-based, \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2  and his father, made Supreme Court appointments who disappointed on issues of free exercise of religion and the right to life. President Trump delivered three Supreme Court justices who have been very reliable on the issues most important to faith- based voters. And so faith-based voters are very grateful to him. It's that simple.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Harold, I will give you the final word on that, quickly, if you will then kick us off with your winners and losers.\par \par \uc2 FORD: I think Republicans have to come to grips with one question. Do they want to win primaries, or do they want to win generals? I'm a Democrat. I hope they keep wanting to win primaries and perhaps we have a chance in the general. So President Trump, his popularity, his standing, we'll see how it continues to play out. His record has been mixed in these primaries so far. We'll see how it goes.\par \par \uc2 My winner is Steph Curry, the \uc2 Golden State Warriors\uc2  last night. He gets in rarefied air, the team does as well. Congratulations to them.\par \par \uc2 And my loser is due process in Russia. Staying with basketball. Brittney Griner is there. Our prayers are with her. We hope that the \uc2 NBA\uc2 , WNBA, and our government are able to secure her release.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Pithy winner and loser to you, Byron, and then Hugh.\par \par \uc2 YORK: Winner is Nancy Mace. She's a South Carolina Republican representative, ran in a Republican primary in which \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  endorsed her opponent, and she still won. She had a back-and-forth relationship with Trump, that's true. But she won.\par \par \uc2 Other loser is American homebuyer. The average interest rate for a 30-year mortgage has more than doubled in the last few months, adding hundreds of dollars to monthly payments. That's a big, big loss.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Even pithier to you, Hugh.\par \par \uc2 HEWITT: My winner is Mayra Flores who upset Democrats in Texas 34th Congressional district, taking a district that went for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  by four points and winning as a Republican by eight, and it's an 86 percent Latino district. That means the loser is every Democrat on every ballot, especially people like Michael Bennet in Colorado facing Joseph O'Dea, people who thought they were safe. They're not.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right, panelists, thank you very much. Happy Father's Day and a great weekend to all of you.\par \par \uc2 It's Friday, time for "Notable Quotables."\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Have you decided, sir, whether to go Saudi Arabia?\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , (D) PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: No, not yet.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I fully expect that the president will see the crown prince.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We shouldn't be in a position where we have to go to any country for our energy security.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 MITCH MCCONNELL\uc2 , (R-KY) SENATE MINORITY LEADER: The American people are bracing for a very pricy summer.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The labor market is extremely tight and inflation is much too high.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I don't want to hear any more of these lies about reckless spending. We're changing people's lives.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: We want to get an overwhelming majority in November.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It's my understanding that the president intends to seek a second term.\par \par \uc2 REP. ALEXANDRIA OCASIO-CORTEZ, (D-NY): You know, if the president chooses to run again in 2024, we'll cross that bridge when we get to it.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Not only was there the big lie, there was the big rip-off.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I thought, if he believes this stuff he has become detached from reality.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 CHUCK SCHUMER\uc2 , (D-NY) SENATE MAJORITY LEADER: To many senators on both sides, this debate certainly feels different.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: What we need to do is protect Americans' Second Amendment rights, and Republicans in the Senate aren't doing that.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 LINDSEY GRAHAM\uc2 , (R-SC): One thing Bernie and I agree is that Putin sucks. So --\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I assume you concede that point.\par \par \uc2 SEN. BERNIE SANDERS, (I-VT): I'm not favor of the vulgarity, but the intent is correct.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: All right.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: See, they can agree on some things.\par \par \uc2 Monday on FOX News and SPECIAL REPORT, we begin a series on fentanyl we call "The Poisoning of America." Please join me also on "FOX News Sunday." My guest will be national economic council director Brian Deese, and Utah Republican Senator \uc2 Mike Lee\uc2  of the Judiciary Committee.\par \par \uc2 Thank you for watching SPECIAL REPORT. I'm Shannon Bream in Washington. I will see you on FOX NEWS AT NIGHT this evening at midnight eastern, 9:00 Pacific digging into all of the day's top stories before you kick off your weekend.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, JESSE WATTERS PRIME TIME is up next on this Friday night. Have a great show, Jesse.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FNSPBB0020220618ei6h00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Biden Hits Big Oil For Making "Exorbitant Profits"; Texas Lawmakers Investigate Uvalde Shooting Response; \uc2 FBI\uc2  Investigating Attacks On Pregnancy Centers; \uc2 FBI\uc2  Investigating Attacks On Pregnancy Centers; California Governor \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2  Joins Trump's Truth Social; General Keane: On Collision With Iran; Biden Hits Big Oil For Making Exorbitant Profits; Alleged Hazing Leaves Student Unable To See, Walk; Great White Shark Spotted Off Cape Cod\b0\par\par\uc2 Martha MacCallum, Lawrence Jones, Katie Pavlich, Tomi Lahren,  General Jack Keane, Edward Lawrence, Garrett Tenney \par \uc2 8,659 \uc2 words\par \uc2 17 June 2022\par \uc2 Fox News Channel: The Story with Martha MacCallum\par \uc2 HUNDD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 SANDRA SMITH, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: OK, you too. And thanks to all of you for joining us, John. Great to be with you. I hope you have a great weekend. I'm Sandra Smith.\par \par \uc2 JOHN ROBERTS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Yes, we we've got lacrosse and Father's Day.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Happy Father's Day.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: What can be better than that?\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Thanks for reminding me.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: Good to see you, Sandra. See you again next week. I'm John Roberts, thanks for joining us today. Have a great weekend yourself. THE STORY starts right now.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA MACCALLUM, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Thank you very much, John and Sandra, breaking right now on THE STORY. President Biden took a moment out from his schedule to shame and blame big oil for the prices at the pump. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: So, if you just base it on what a barrel of oil costs then actually it should not be this high, they're making exorbitant profits, number one.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, in moments, we will be joined and we will play you the whole thing there by a member of the Biden White House team, Economic Adviser Jared Bernstein. But first, we want to take you to Texas, where there are two developing stories at this hour. One is this ongoing, new secretive investigation into what happened in the hallway while 19 officers stood outside during the killing at Robb Elementary. A Texas State Congressman explains this morning, his reasoning why this is all so secretive.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: One person's truth may be different than another person's truth and another person. I think that we know this from all sorts of investigative processes and judicial processes. Before this committee is willing to announce what we believe is to be factual information, we want to hear from all sides.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: But we know ultimately, there's only one truth about what happened in that hallway. And remember this moment.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BETO O'ROURKE, FORMER DEMOCRATIC CONGRESSIONAL CANDIDATE: The time to stop the next shooting is right now. And you're doing nothing.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Did that moment make the Texas governor's race tighter than it was before? In other words, did it help \uc2 Beto O'Rourke\uc2 ? We're going to break that down. Lawrence Jones, Host of "Lawrence Jones Cross Country" on Saturdays at 10:00 p.m. Eastern -- great program. He reported from the very beginning and Uvalde, Texas. It's his home state. Following that shooting Lawrence, good to have you with us today.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE JONES, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Hey there, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: First of all, tell me what you think about this investigation. There are two separate arms that are investigating this. And I want to actually lead into you by playing a sound bite with Representative Roland Gutierrez, who is a Texas State Senator who was left off that committee and it's his district, he wanted to be in there. Here's what he says.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. ROLAND GUTIERREZ (D-TX): There are so many problems in this investigation. This is the worst law enforcement fiasco since George Floyd. We need to know what happened here so that this never happens again, in Highland Park, in River Oaks, and Houston or any other neighborhood throughout this country, throughout the state. And as long as law enforcement wants to hide behind some loophole, it's unfortunate.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Lawrence, what do you make of this investigation?\par \par \uc2 JONES: Well, when you have the person on the scene that's in charge that is not cooperating with the investigation, I think that tells you everything that you need to know. You know, I was frustrated as well as the governor, because a lot of my sources that I, we're talking to at the time, got misinformation. These are people that were conducting the, the investigation, they were told one thing.\par \par \uc2 And when the actual interview started to happen, the statements started to change because they were then under oath. I have several questions. For example, I hear from my sources that the commander on the same, the person that is the chief of police for the school district did not only have his rifle, but didn't have a radio on the scene. He also didn't have any keys on the scene.\par \par \uc2 I mean, we can speculate back and forth about the investigative agency. But what I've called for, Martha, is just release all the tapes. I mean, the school had tape, I know some of them will be graphic. And we don't want to see what happened in that classroom. But I think the public deserves the full truth. All the radio calls, all the phone calls that were made to the 9-1-1 dispatcher, I think that's the only way that we're going to get to the truth.\par \par \uc2 Look, I love the \uc2 Texas Rangers\uc2 , I grew up wanting to be a Texas Ranger. A lot of my sources are within that department, and so, I believe they can conduct a fair investigation. But when you had a police chief who was now on the City Council, not willing to cooperate with that investigation, I think that's problematic. And those kids deserve answers. Did he tell officers to stand down? Why did we have to wait until the BORTAC unit got there? Why didn't he have keys to the classroom? I mean, these are legitimate questions.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: They are. You know, and that's why we're not trying to point fingers of blame at anyone. But we know that there were parents out there because we've seen them on video who were yelling at these individuals to go inside. And for someone to do something. We also know that after they arrived on the scene that more people lost their lives and we don't want this to happen at the next situation. We want people to be better trained. We want to know exactly what the procedure and format is going to be. And if there's anything that can come from this, it's to learn some of these lessons and not shove them under the carpet so that no one can see what happened. It's really, really important.\par \par \uc2 JONES: And the last thing I'll say, Martha, is that you've already as a law enforcement town. That means there's \uc2 FBI\uc2 , ICE Agent, and all their kids were in the school, why don't I have to conduct their own plan to get inside of the building? Why wasn't the commander on the scene trying to do that?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, we had people who showed up on the scene who were married to a teacher inside, a man, as well. So, I want to ask you a political question here because it's interesting to me. We broke in this happened live on, on our show, when \uc2 Beto O'Rourke\uc2  stormed into that news conference and started yelling at Governor Abbott. And since that happened, look, what's happened to the polls in the governor's race there: in December, Abbott was ahead 52 to 37 against O'Rourke. This race has tightened considerably, it's now 48 to 43, Lawrence.\par \par \uc2 JONES: You know, I think if you're a parent, and you saw what happened, especially when you didn't trust those to serve and protect us to do something about it, I think a lot of people were upset and they want someone to stop the pain, the bleeding, what's happening in our schools today. But I don't think this poll, is an indicative of what's going to happen in that race? I mean, just for example, a lot of Uvalde parents and a lot of community members were very -- I was there on the ground when this happened. They were very upset when Beto decided to do that. And it wasn't just Republicans.\par \par \uc2 I mean, it's a Democrat town to be clear on it. So, they weren't excited about this as well. I think Beto is taking a moment and trying to make this more of a, a national story for him that he's the one who was rallying the troops for the kids. But I think when you look at all the issues facing America, especially Texans, I don't think this is going to be the issue that gains him support. He's, he's a media guy. And so, he knows how to sway the media, but I think when people go in there to cast their vote, there's a lot of issues that are going to be impacted, and Texans that is going to play in their vote.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: We will see. Lawrence, thank you so much. Great to see you this afternoon. We'll watch you this weekend.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Thank you, my friend.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, on the latest episode of "The Untold Story" podcast, I spoke with Texas State Senator Roland Gutierrez, we went into depth on this investigation. He calls it the most infamous day in law enforcement since George Floyd and he'll explain why he says that. Obviously, two very different situations, but we'll talk about what has to happen in this investigation. Here's a little a little clip from it.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ: I think it's clear that what happened here was that you had law enforcement just kind of freeze and I get it, I get fear, I understand that completely. But if you take on the job, you're not supposed to be in that situation. You're supposed to have an active shooter protocol that says you go in, every expert has said it, and that didn't happen here.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: "The Untold Story" is available for the download of that podcast now at FoxNewsPodcast.com. And a string of attacks and threats against pregnancy centers and faith-based organizations across the country. Now, the \uc2 FBI\uc2  says these could be acts of domestic terrorist, terrorist extremism -- domestic extremism. Katie Pavlich and Tomi Lahren, join me in just a moment on this rise of political violence and also what just came out of the White House with regard to \uc2 SCOTUS\uc2  and protection of their families. And this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Smokes!\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Oh, there he is.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: That thing is huge, dude. That's like 15 feet. That thing is enormous.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Happy Summer, everybody. We are going to talk to those folks later this hour.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: As we await the Supreme Court's decision that impacts Roe v. Wade, a series of really disturbing attacks and threats on centers that help pregnant moms has now received the attention of the \uc2 FBI\uc2  who considers these potential acts of domestic violent extremism. The pro-abortion group known as Jane's Revenge posted this ominous statement, "From here forward, any anti-choice group who closes their doors and stops operating will no longer be a target. But until you do, it's open season, and we know where your operations are." With that, we bring in Katie Pavlich, Townhall.com News Editor, and Tomi Lahren, Host of "Tomi Lahren is Fearless" on Outkick, both are Fox News Contributors. Great to have both of you with us today. Katie, tell us, tell our viewers at home, what is Jane's revenge.\par \par \uc2 KATIE PAVLICH, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: So, Jane's Revenge is a pro-abortion group that has ties to Antifa, that, as you mentioned, in your opening has put out the statement this week, threatening to become even more violent. They took credit for a number of these violent attacks against crisis pregnancy centers around the country. And they said, they're the allies who are with them now continue to do what you're doing, and they're welcoming other people to get involved in activity that is not just about graffiti, or breaking glass.\par \par \uc2 They said, they're going to carry out attacks that are harder to "clean up." And so, this is a group that has you know, serious violent ties. They're open about that. They are a domestic terrorist group by the definition of carrying out attacks for political means. They are doing it in the name of abortion, and trying to intimidate this crisis pregnancy centers where women go for help. They're being very violent.\par \par \uc2 And the \uc2 Susan B Anthony\uc2  list put out a 22-page report this week, detailing the dozens of attacks that have happened. Firebombing, broken windows. And the question for the \uc2 FBI\uc2  is: A. Why did it take so long for them to get involved? And B. What is the standard for the left when it comes to condemning this act? Because as you saw this week, \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  did not do so when given the opportunity.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, certainly, everybody should be able to condemn this kind of potential violence. And certainly, a destruction of this property that should be pretty easy to condemn, because clearly, they're intimidating people who want to come in and go out of these places.\par \par \uc2 Let's look at some video of some of the protests that have taken place in front of the justice's homes, Tomi. Because the president did sign into law, the additional protection for Supreme Court justices and their families. But they did it with zero fanfare, and with no mention whatsoever of what had happened to \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2 , Tomi.\par \par \uc2 TOMI LAHREN, FOX NATION HOST: Yes, I think the reason that you're not seeing a whole lot of leftists, or a whole lot of elected Democrats, including our president and vice president come out about this is because they cannot afford to lose this extremist base.\par \par \uc2 This is what they are banking, not only the midterms, but future elections on and we don't even have a decision in this Roe v. Wade case yet. This is just the warm up of what we're going to see when we actually do get a decision. And if there's a decision that the left doesn't like, I fear, we're going to see a lot more violence, intimidation, and destruction.\par \par \uc2 But I think the left has to hold on to this, because it's what firing up their base might be the only thing that is firing up their base that would get them to go to the polls. That's why you're not seeing them condemn this as loudly as they should.\par \par \uc2 But I find it a bit ironic, because as we know, President Biden just tapped \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  to head up an online bullying and harassment task force. Well, this would be a perfect place to start something that's actually happening in the real world. Violence, intimidation, and threats.\par \par \uc2 I wish you would get a handle on this, maybe tackle one of the jobs she's been given a perfect opportunity.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right, very quick response to this one, if you would. Truth Social is the social media arm that is run by President Trump, and Devin Nunez is running it.\par \par \uc2 This is the governor in California, \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2 . He is joined Truth Social. And here he is telling why.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GOV. GAVIN NEWSOM (D-CA): Hey, everybody, its Governor \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2 . And I know we're all on this platform in search for the truth. But the truth is, I've not been able to find a simple explanation for the fact that we have a red state murder problem.\par \par \uc2 Eight of the top 10 states with the highest murder rates happen to be red states. So, the question is simple. What are the laws and policies in those states that are leading to such carnage?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I got to get a quick response from both of you, Katie. Truth Social says they will accept anyone. It's a non-censored social media site.\par \par \uc2 PAVLICH: I think it's pretty obvious that \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2  plans on running for president in 2024, which is why he's going to President Trump's social media platform to try and take him on early. It should he decide to run as well.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Tomi?\par \par \uc2 LAHREN: And everything he's doing here is gaslighting the American people into believing what are actually his lies and the problems with his state. You know, if the state was so great, people wouldn't be leaving it to flee to every other red state out there.\par \par \uc2 I think he does exactly right. He wants to run, he wants to be the anti- Trump candidates. I don't think it's going to play so well. Look at how badly his state is doing.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Tomi and Katie, thank you. Great to see both of you.\par \par \uc2 So, coming up, I'll be joined by General Jack Keane about this dangerous new territory that has been reached by Iran's nuclear program as the Biden administration looks to renew the Obama-era Iran nuclear deal.\par \par \uc2 We'll tell you what Iran is up to now. Next.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: It's very likely that Iran will have nuclear weapons before the end of President Biden's term.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM (voice-over): Now, a new report today that Iran is building a massive new tunnel network near one of their nuclear facilities, apparently, to guard against bunker busting bombs and cyber-attacks.\par \par \uc2 That's according to \uc2 The New York Times\uc2 , quoting American and Israeli intelligence officials.\par \par \uc2 It comes as the Biden administration is trying to revive the Iran nuclear deal that President Trump pulled out of and after the \uc2 U.N\uc2 .'s nuclear watchdog warned that Tehran, "maybe weeks away from being able to build the bomb."\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM (on camera): Let's bring in Fox News Senior strategic analyst General Jack Keane, former vice chief of staff for the army and now chairman of the Institute for the Study of War.\par \par \uc2 General, good to have you with us today. First of all, do you believe that, that timeline is accurate that they might be only weeks away from acquiring what they need for nuclear weapon? And what do you know about these tunnels and their meaning?\par \par \uc2 GEN. JACK KEANE (RET.), FOX NEWS CHANNEL SENIOR STRATEGIC ANALYST: Yes, I do believe that. But here is a couple of added factors.\par \par \uc2 Yes, weeks away from having the ingredients to build a nuclear bomb, but then being able to weaponize that bomb and deliver it is something else again. And that's probably a year or more away from that, according to Pentagon intelligence sources that have reported that publicly.\par \par \uc2 As to the underground sites themselves, I think what they're doing, the Israelis blew up a centrifuge site that was near Natanz and 2020. And I believe this is a replacement for that site.\par \par \uc2 It's not deep enough that it would be beyond the range of our bombs that are definitely penetrators. The site that challenged us was the Fordo's site. We found out about that through Iranian informants a number -- a number of years ago, and we do have the penetrators to be able to deal with that.\par \par \uc2 But listen, when we draw back from these issues, and what's really happening, we're on a collision course with the Iranians. And there's just no doubt about that.\par \par \uc2 The Biden administration trying to repeat the nuclear deal as flawed as it was in 2015, still flawed today. The fact is, Iran is heading towards this bomb. And the world has got to make up its mind. What is it going to do about it? I think the Biden administration should abandon in negotiations, double down on negotiations, strengthen our relationships with the Arabs.\par \par \uc2 When they came in, they stiffed the UAE. They stiffed Saudi Arabia. They held back the weapons that we would then deliver to them. These are weapons that are needed to counter the Iranians.\par \par \uc2 The Arabs and Israelis came together. As you know, Martha, in the Abraham Accords --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KEANE: The primary reason is to counter the Iranians. Let's help strengthen that and stand up against the Iranians. And look at, here is the path we're on. If they get the nuclear weapon, if they're able to weaponize it, the Arabs are not going to sit on their hands while that happens, they are going to get weapons themselves. They are going to buy those weapons from people that have it, like Pakistan.\par \par \uc2 And then, we'll be in the most dangerous situation the world has ever faced, where we have two opposing partners, who feel there's an existential threat and they're armed with nuclear weapons.\par \par \uc2 Henry Kissinger\uc2  said many years ago, the most dangerous and critical situation in my lifetime is not World War II, it's the Arabs and the Iranians both having nuclear weapons, leading to the possible first nuclear exchange.\par \par \uc2 So, we have got the sort of men up here and recognize what's happening. And let's work with the Arabs and the Israelis to double down on the Iranians here and prevent this from occurring.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, I would imagine that that's the message that the president is going to hear from the Saudis and the Israelis, and he will hear it loud and clear whether or not he will listen to it, General, is another question.\par \par \uc2 KEANE: No, it is -- you're absolutely right about that. I mean, the real reason he's going in here to have this visit has to do with oil prices. It's not because of the Iranians, but he is going to meet with the \uc2 Gulf Cooperation Council\uc2 . And those are all the Gulf States.\par \par \uc2 And you're absolutely right, you put your finger on. He is going to get an earful about where Iran is, and what a threat they are to them. And they need the United States involved in all of this.\par \par \uc2 And certainly, the Israelis are going to give him an earful as well, as they have been doing. I mean, the Israelis haven't been silenced since President Biden came in. They disagree with a nuclear deal, just like the Arabs do.\par \par \uc2 And they have been -- they have been warning them about the pending threat of where the Iranians are in moving towards this nuclear weapon. Yes.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. Meanwhile, the Iranians are building tunnels and trying to make it more difficult to get the work that they've obviously been continuing throughout this period.\par \par \uc2 General, thank you very much. Always good to see you, sir.\par \par \uc2 KEANE: Yes, great talking to you, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: General Jack Keane.\par \par \uc2 So, president -- what President Biden needs to do in order to bring down gas prices? I'm going to speak with one of his top White House economic advisers, Jared Bernstein, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: President Biden just a short while ago taking aim at Big Oil for the prices at the pump. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF UNITED STATES: So, if you just base it on what a barrel of oil costs then actually it should not be this high, they're making exorbitant profits, number one. Number two, I've contacted them with, well my team has, to ask what their plans are and to give any suggestions they have. They have over 9,000 leases, 9,000 they could drill the -- but they're not doing it, number two. Number three, I think we're going to be in a position where we're going to -- oh.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right. And then, it was time to go. So in moments, I'll be joined by a member of the Biden Team, White House economic adviser Jared Bernstein, but first to Fox Business correspondent Edward Lawrence at the White House. Hi, Edward.\par \par \uc2 EDWARD LAWRENCE, FOX BUSINESS CORRESPONDENT: Hi, Martha. Yes, he got the hook from his wife there. She grabbed him by the hand and pulled him away in the middle of that answer there. So President Biden has said that he wants to bring down inflation and gas prices, but he refuses to change any of the administration policies, or restrictions and regulations that were added on the energy industry. And instead, as you heard there, the President attacking oil companies and calling on them to draw more now. I think it's important to hear the first part of this again. Listen to this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: PRESIDENT OF UNITED STATES: So, if you just base it on what a barrel of oil costs then actually it should not be this high, they're making exorbitant profits, number one.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Exorbitant profits. So \uc2 ExxonMobil\uc2  responded to the attack saying long term, the President needs to signal a clear policy to support the industry, including streamlining the process for infrastructure investments like pipelines. Now another bank is warning that we're likely see a recession sometime next year. We're talking about \uc2 Wells Fargo\uc2 . This adds up to 47% of Americans feel like they're falling behind financially, with only 10% who say they're actually getting ahead, and 82% of the people that were polled, registered voters say the condition of the economy is only fair or poor. 18% feel as good or excellent. So, Republicans say that this President is out of touch with happening and is making things worse.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Now on gas prices to bring that down, the White House floating the idea again of gas rebate cards, that's on the table, White House official telling me that no final decision has been made but they're circling that as a way to help Americans bring the cost of gas down, not inflation. Back to you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Thank you very much Edward Lawrence at the White House. Here now, White House economic adviser Jared Bernstein. Jared, thank you. It's good to have you with us today.\par \par \uc2 You know, it seems like that these two sides are at odds, right? That the President keeps going after the oil companies. The oil companies are now sending him letters saying things like, look, we have invested twice what we have earned in terms of increasing capacity in this country. We're doing what we can. We would really like to see the relaxing of regulations and ability for us to have less structure in terms of the way that we do our job if you really want us to increase oil.\par \par \uc2 But, you know, the thing that \uc2 Chevron\uc2  said was look since 2021, we've gotten a clear message that the administration aims to impose obstacles for our industry, to deliver the energy needs that the world needs. And I think they think that because of this. Let's look at the soundbite from the debate back during the presidential race.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Number one, no more subsidies for fossil fuel industry, no more drilling on federal lands, no more drilling including offshore, no ability for the oil industry to continue to drill, period.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, that message was pretty clear and it seems like they have done what they can, Jared.\par \par \uc2 JARED BERNSTEIN, HOUSE ECONOMIC ADVISER: So, if you go back to the clip that you played from the President speaking today, not during the campaign but today, he said two things. He said, yes, elevated profits, no question. And look, this is a time we are a nation who's supporting a war, that's a bipartisan support, that's putting great pressure on energy prices. Republicans and Democrats alike have to recognize that part of supporting Ukraine is recognizing that that's going to lead to some elevated gas prices.\par \par \uc2 This is a global price. The President is doing more than any president I've ever worked for. The other thing he said is that, he wants to work with these companies. And that's where I think the blame game frame is off, if I can be a little poetic. And the reason for that is that not only are our oil companies pumping more oil than they ever have before, and that's under this president, but this country has gone from being a net importer to a very significant net exporter.\par \par \uc2 Our oil producers are being extremely productive and we were very happy to see that. What the President is also saying, though, is this is the time to step up the patriotism and do something for the American people to help them with what they're struggling with --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I mean, that's the question. So first of all, there's, there's no less oil on the market now than there was before the war. And you've got 50% of Americans saying that they think that, you know, it's mostly the policies of the government that is increasing their gas prices, not what's going on with the war. So what we're seeing is that Russia is selling oil, they're selling it to China, they're selling it to India. The oil supply has not decreased. So the war is a difficult person, is a difficult entity to blame in this case. We also know that oil prices were already going up considerably over $1.65 before the war even happened. And it's really because of the war on fossil fuel companies and that entire industry.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, the price of oil has gone up about $1.70 a barrel since, sorry, $1.70 a gallon since the war started. And I don't think you can find any credible energy economist who will tell you that the war in Ukraine has had no impact on the gas price. So I think we probably have a factual disagreement there. We also have a factual disagreement about barrels on the market.\par \par \uc2 And by the way, I very much appreciate this kind of analysis getting right down to the supply and demand. It's exactly where we need to go. Something like between 800,000 per day and a million per day Russian barrels have in fact come off the market. Many others have, of course, been shuffled to different places. So there's no question that the oil market has been very much upset by the conflict. And by the way, it's not just oil, it's also refineries.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: And here, the President -- he reached out to our refineries this week, many of whom are operating at or close to capacity to say, let's work together to see what we can do to help the American people. He is fighting for the middle class at the pump and that's exactly what he should be.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: But the policies that have led to that, we have not built a new refinery in this country since 1970. It goes back a long way on the war against this industry. So now, the President saying do more, refined more, get more products to the market. And there's -- we, you know, we're doing everything we can possibly do within the rules and regulations that you have put on us. So we're going to turn this out.\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: So I think that war against the industry, it certainly doesn't reflect the fact that these companies continue to benefit from all kinds of preferential subsidies. Now you heard the President criticize those subsidies, and that's very much in his wheelhouse, but they continue to get them. We also, by the way, helped our petroleum companies, both oil and refineries, to the tune of something like $5 billion during the fiscal support during the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 So we've given them very deep support, and now we're asking them to step up and give a little something back. Not to be unprofitable, we recognize that they had some tough years there, but go ahead and maybe thin those profit, those really quite high profit margins out of it so you can give Americans a break during a period where we are trying to help in a bipartisan manner. Republicans and Democrats together helped support the Ukraine effort.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Understood. You know, and I think the best thing for Americans for your side and their side to get together but they've made it very clear what they need. They need a waiver of the Jones Act provisions. They need some fuel specifications to increase their supplies to be cut back. They're asking for more infrastructure, more pipelines, not banning the Keystone Pipeline, allowing things like that. Are you in a position now where you're willing to change the tone about some of those things?\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: I think the tone, I don't know if the tone is changed or not --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: The policy, it is more accurate? Sorry, the policy.\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: Well, first, let me just say on the tone, the President himself, in a clip you just played, said I want to sit down and talk to these folks. He's actually done that before he's going to do it again. I think when it comes to policy, we've got to walk and chew gum at the same time. We have to make sure that these companies have the support they need right now. And frankly, the fact that they're pumping more than they ever have before, they have so many leases available to them suggests that they have the ability to pump more. The reason they say they're not pumping more is because of concerns about capital discipline and that the price might come back down. And so, they'll have made investments that they won't be able to recoup.\par \par \uc2 I personally understand that. We as our administration understand that. They should come and sit down with us and we'll talk about ways in which we might be able to help. But at the same time, not only do they have to understand, they understand very well, and they've actually made some very smart investments in this space that we have to transition. This is walk and chew gum. We have to do -- we have to make the investments are talking about now in production today as we transition to a clean energy economy.\par \par \uc2 Anyone who doesn't believe that that is the path, the pushing back on Petro states and their toxic impact on a global politics isn't paying attention.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right. So, why does it make sense to ask Saudi Arabia to drill more and to produce more? Why would it make sense for them to do more and not for our people to do more?\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: So first of all, the President is very clearly and unequivocally asking our producers to do more. When it comes to other countries, yes, he is certainly doing all he can to get countries to meet or exceed their production quota at a period when supply and demand for energy are clearly misaligned. Also --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: But they don't operate with the same kind of environmental restrictions that our producers do. So why would it be OK for them to be negative to the economy but -- I mean, to the environment but not ours?\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: So, when we're trying to realign supply and demand, we have to try to get oil producers to at least hit their quotas, if not do better than that, and the announcement from \uc2 OPEC\uc2  a couple of weeks ago that they're going to do, that was actually welcomed. At the same time, of course, the President released 180 million barrels from the strategic reserves.\par \par \uc2 So yes, this is the walk and chew gum moment. This is about making sure that we can realign near term supply and demand by kicking up supply in particular, and asking some of our trading partners to do so as well. But at the same time, plotting a clear path to clean energy.\par \par \uc2 Now, we're asking Congress to help us with that. Pass our clean energy tax credits and cut the utility bill, the average American household by $500 a year. Congress could do that tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: OK, all right.\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: So everyone needs to get out of the cart and push.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right. Jared, I ran out of time but there are a lot of other things I want to ask you about the gas rebate card. Are you in favor of that just quickly? Yes or no?\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: That's not a yes or no question. It's a longer conversation having to do with the various things we have on the table. I personally think that a better play as I've tried to express to you in our conversation is to try to do what we can on the supply side. The demand side is already quite strong.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right. We'll dig in more next time. I do appreciate you being here. Thank you very much, Jared Bernstein.\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: Great conversation, thank you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Good to have you with us. Thanks.\par \par \uc2 So, the new push for charges to be brought in an alleged hazing case that left one student unable to speak, see or walk. This student's father and sister join me next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: \uc2 University of Missouri\uc2  students demanding justice for this classmate, 19-year-old Daniel Santulli can no longer walk, talk or see after a night of alleged hazing that left him in cardiac arrest with severe brain damage while he was pledging Phi Gamma Delta fraternity. In a moment, the Santulli family and their attorney joins us but first to correspondent Garrett Tenney with the backstory. Hi, Garrett.\par \par \uc2 GARRETT TENNEY, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Martha, this one night forever changed the lives of this young man and his entire family. And after eight months, they are still waiting for those who are responsible to be brought to justice. This surveillance video from the Phi Gamma Delta or Fiji fraternity house back in October shows Danny Santulli and other pledges shirtless and blindfolded, being led downstairs as part of a ritual where each pledge was expected to finish an entire bottle of vodka.\par \par \uc2 After several hours of heavy drinking, you can see Danny lose his balance and fall backwards. The 18-year-old freshman is left on a couch unresponsive. And by the time his frat brothers take him to the hospital, it's too late. He is in cardiac arrest. His blood alcohol level is more than five times the legal driving limit and he has suffered severe brain damage. The family recently settled a civil lawsuit against the fraternity and 22 others, and the \uc2 University of Missouri\uc2  which was not named in the lawsuit banned Fiji from campus.\par \par \uc2 However, eight months later, there are growing concerns and frustrations with the criminal prosecution. Earlier this month, the Boone County prosecutor's office disregarded investigators suggestion that there was enough evidence to charge at least one of the frats leaders with hazing, a felony under Missouri's anti-hazing statute that's punishable by up to a year in prison. Instead, that individual was charged with a pair of misdemeanors.\par \par \uc2 The prosecutor's office has not responded to any of our messages this week, but in a statement last Friday said it is still reviewing the evidence and will file more charges if necessary. Adding, this office takes allegations of hazing very seriously and we are terribly saddened by the injury sustained by Denny Santulli. This office will seek justice in this case based on the evidence and Missouri law.\par \par \uc2 That explanation prompted friends of Danny to launch a petition on change.org calling for the state's hazing laws to be enforced. At last check, more than 10,700 people have signed that petition. Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Garrett, thank you very much. With that we bring in Daniel's father and sister, Tom Santulli and Meredith Santulli, and their attorney David Bianchi, a trial lawyer who has obtained the largest fraternity hazing verdict in the country winning tens of millions of dollars for hazing victims and their families. Good to have all of you with us. And I want to thank the members of the family for joining us.\par \par \uc2 I know this is, I'm sure, very hard for you every time to relive what happened to Danny. Tom, you know, why is it that you feel justice has not been served here? And I know there are two specific individuals who you feel bear responsibility for what happened to your son?\par \par \uc2 TOM SANTULLI, DANNY SANTULLI'S FATHER: Well, I feel like within Missouri, there's an anti-hazing law which is pretty strict. And after speaking with David, my lawyer, he agrees it's well-written. And after reviewing all the evidence, the surveillance, the videos, the text messages, the chat sessions, it's all right there. It's all right there for you. So it was actually handed to the prosecutor on a silver platter. So that's my whole point is that, they need to enforce the anti-hazing law in Missouri.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: David, why wouldn't they enforce this anti-hazing law? It could potentially save future lives or save others from being injured to the extent that Danny was which is which is horrible.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BIANCHI, LAWYER FOR FAMILY OF DANNY SANTULLI: Martha, there is no explanation for why this is not being done, none. It's a complete mystery. This is a slam dunk hazing case under Missouri law. There are countless hours of real time surveillance video, which you almost never have in a hazing case but we have it here. It was 144 page police report. Everything the prosecutors need are there. Read the hazing statute, every fraternity officer and everybody who participated in this is guilty of hazing under the Missouri statute. But after eight months, they're refusing to bring the charges and nobody can understand.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You know, these are -- you make a great point. The video that you're looking at is surveillance video, which I believe this fraternity knew these cameras were active in this building. Right, David?\par \par \uc2 BIANCHI: That's correct. I mean --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Unbelievable.\par \par \uc2 BIANCHI: You do this to somebody with surveillance cameras --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Unreal. Well, they obviously have no fear of being caught. They know that some of the supporters of the fraternity and other entities, and apparently the prosecutor's office, are not willing to pursue the laws that actually are on the books here.\par \par \uc2 Meredith, I know that you spoke with Danny that week and he was concerned about what was happening during this hazing process. What was he telling you?\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH SANTULLI, DANNY SANTULLI'S SISTER: I saw him that Saturday night, a couple days before, and he broke down to me at the house and was just saying, I can't take this anymore. I'm failing my classes. I have no money. And I just kept telling him, you know, it's OK if you want to draw up. I mean, you still have friends. No one will give you any type of criticism. And he just, you know, decided to stick with it.\par \par \uc2 And after that I called my parents and I was just really concerned for his well-being. And then a couple days later, I saw him that Tuesday, hours before the accident happened and he seemed OK. I dropped him off at the house. And then later that night, his life changed forever.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You know, I mean, it is so barbaric to ask people to drink an entire bottle of vodka as is alleged in this case. This is such a beautiful, handsome young man and he's still is, and I'm glad you still have your son, Tom. How is Danny doing?\par \par \uc2 T. SANTULLI: Danny is Danny, right? So, we're always going to remember him as Danny. We still have him. That first dreadful weekend down in Columbia, in Missouri, one that was really, really bad. We didn't know if he was going to live. We all got together, the four of us, and we said we want Danny alive one way or another. You know, we're parents, you know, we're siblings, and that was our goal and we got him. We got him alive.\par \par \uc2 He has a long road, Martha, a very long road. You know, a couple of things here and there, in and out of the hospital with some infections but we're going to manage it. So, it's tough.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I'm so sorry. You're a wonderful family and we wish Danny and all of you the best. There's not a parent of -- I have two young, two boys, you know, roughly college age as well. There's not a parent who puts his son goes through this process, who doesn't worry every single day and it needs to be stopped. This behavior cannot go on. It is absolutely barbaric. And we see what is the result of it.\par \par \uc2 We've seen deaths, many deaths across the country, in fact, and we see what happened to Danny. So, David, we hope you can get somewhere with this prosecution. We hope you'll keep in touch with us on this case. And Tom and Meredith, all the best to you and, Tom, happy Father's Day this weekend to you.\par \par \uc2 T. SANTULLI: Thank you so much. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 M. SANTULLI: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You bet. Keep in touch. Thank you both. Thank you all.\par \par \uc2 OK, switching gears here. Texas Governor Greg Abbott is on the border right now. He's meeting with some members of the National Guard in McAllen, Texas along that southern border with Mexico, also giving an update on the state's border security efforts. As Fox News learns these shocking latest numbers from DHS, so far this fiscal year agents report encountering 50 people who were on the terror watch list who came across the border. So that's great in terms of what they might be up to or what their plans are now that they're here in the country.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Department of Homeland Security\uc2  says that immigration arrests along the southern border rose in May to the highest ever recorded levels, more than 239,000 people crossed that border and encountered our border agents in the course of May, putting the agency on pace to exceed 2 million detentions during the fiscal year. And remember that so much trafficking, so many drugs come across the border with all of this. So this is not just about individuals seeking a better life. People understand that, but the open border has enormous ramifications for the health of young people in trafficking and drug use. So we'll stay on it.\par \par \uc2 All right, switching gears again here. It is Friday, it is summertime, and so we always like to keep a watch on the shark activity, especially up in the Cape Cod Area. So check this out.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Holy smokes.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Oh, there he is.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: That thing is huge, dude. That's like 15 feet. That thing is enormous.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: That is a great white shark, folks, showing up near a boat off the coast of Cape Cod, Massachusetts, giving a family from Iowa what the captain calls a once in a lifetime look at the undersea world. Captain David Stamatis is the owner of Billingsgate Charters and he joins me now. David, good to see you, great to have you with us today. So you were about four miles off the coast, is that right?\par \par \uc2 DAVID STAMATIS, OWNER, BILLINGSGATE CHARTERS: Thank you, Martha. Yes, about four miles off the coast, just east of Barnstable and sound of (inaudible).\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So how long would you say this shark was?\par \par \uc2 STAMATIS: My best guess estimate, it was at least 15 feet.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Wow. You know, so obviously, they tag sharks off the coast of Cape Cod all the time. Do you have any idea if this was a tagged shark or an untagged shark?\par \par \uc2 STAMATIS: Well, we did check with Sharktivity on that when we recorded the shark initially. And they said that they did not have that shark recorded. So what we described to him and what we gave for a location when we call to them.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Wow. So that's not one of the ones that they have dibs on. But you can really see these from spotter planes up above because there's so enormous. So, how often do you see sharks out there when you're out on your boat?\par \par \uc2 STAMATIS: Not so often, I'd say. Like last year we saw three sharks. The year before was a crazy year, we saw 17. But this year is the earliest that we've ever seen them.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, did the family pay extra for the shark signing?\par \par \uc2 STAMATIS: No, no. They're very excited about it.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Were they frightened?\par \par \uc2 STAMATIS: No, not at all. They're really curious. They all get over to the side of the boat with the cameras and do the best they could. The angle that that video was taken from is actually at the bottom of my boat. My mate, Eric Stone, jumped up on the (inaudible) and took the video while I was maneuvering the boat. We saw the fin first while we were about to pull off one of my (inaudible) supply. And (inaudible) investigating. We don't want to get too close to it but we wanted to make sure that if there was an opportunity for a family who wants to check it out (inaudible) shark.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, it's a great amazing video that you got and fortunately I didn't end up like "Jaws," so that's all good. David, thank you very much, great to have you with us. Happy Friday.\par \par \uc2 STAMATIS: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Happy Father's Day, happy Summer on Cape Cod.\par \par \uc2 That is THE STORY of this Friday but THE STORY goes on. We'll see you back here on Monday. To all the dads, we wish you a happy Father's Day and stay tuned for the close of the market as "YOUR WORLD" starts right now.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document HUNDD00020220618ei6h00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Los Angeles District Attorney George Gascon Is Being Blamed For The Killing Of Two LAPD Cops When They W