c2 The National Weather Service says the system is stretching from Central Nebraska to West Virginia, North into Wisconsin and south into Mississippi.\par \par \uc2 The high temperatures are also expected in southwest parts of Texas, experts are advising people to stay indoors and keep hydrated.\par \par \uc2 Meantime, major flooding swept away at least one bridge, washed out roads and set off mudslides in Yellowstone National Park today. Authorities have closed entrances, evacuated visitors from that area.\par \par \uc2 Park officials said the flooding hit after recent unprecedented rains, power is out in some areas of the park.\par \par \uc2 Now, to Capitol Hill and the Supreme Court, we are still waiting for a decision from the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  on the challenge to the Roe versus Wade abortion ruling. The case was not among those announced today. A draft decision overturning Roe was leaked several weeks ago.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, Correspondent David Spunt looks at some of the possible scenarios of what could happen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DAVID SPUNT, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): Anticipation mixed with anxiety outside the tollgates surrounding the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  this morning, ahead of what could be the most significant ruling in more than 50 years.\par \par \uc2 The justices in the coming days are set to decide whether to uphold Mississippi's ban on abortion after 15 weeks, the justices could move to overturn Roe's nationwide guarantee of abortion access. If Roe is overturned, where a woman lives may determine if she can legally get an abortion.\par \par \uc2 ABBY JOHNSON, PRO-LIFE ACTIVIST: I do not believe that abortion should have ever been a federal issue. This should be something that is -- that is fought at the state level.\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 KATHY HOCHUL\uc2  (D-NY): We'll be there for the women of other states. We will stand up, we will fight back. Look to New York for a nation leading response to this crisis.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: Some court watchers believe Chief Justice \uc2 John Roberts\uc2  may be forging a compromise.\par \par \uc2 THOMAS DUPREE, FORMER DEPUTY ASSISTANT ATTORNEY GENERAL: It would not surprise me at all if there's lobbying going on behind the scenes. That said, I would be surprised if the final outcome is dramatically different from what we saw in the draft opinion.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: The impending decision comes as the safety of justices is also front and center. A 26-year-old man sits behind bars, charged with attempting to kill Justice \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2 , almost one week later, still radio silence from President Biden.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SPUNT (on camera): More protests are expected outside the court over the next few days. Last month, the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2  passed a bill to enhance protection for justices and members of their family on a bipartisan basis which is unusual in Washington, Bret.\par \par \uc2 But we do know now, this has been held up in the House, it's been collecting dust. However Bret, we are told as soon as tomorrow, House members may take a vote on this bill to protect Supreme Court justices and members of their family, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: David Spunt live outside the Supreme Court. David, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Up next, Senators \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  and \uc2 Bernie Sanders\uc2  debate the hot issues here at the Kennedy Institute in Boston. We will have some of the lively exchange.\par \par \uc2 First, here's what some of our Fox affiliates around the country are covering tonight. Fox 8 in Cleveland as country singer Toby Keith reveals he was recently diagnosed with stomach cancer and is privately receiving treatment.\par \par \uc2 The 60-year-old Keith has canceled a scheduled performance next month at The Ohio State Fair.\par \par \uc2 Fox 9 in Boise, Idaho as authorities arrest 31 members of the Patriot front group considered to be white supremacist by some near in Idaho Pride event Saturday.\par \par \uc2 Authorities say the men were packed into the back of a You-haul truck with riot gear. A local police chief says the man intended to stage a riot there.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at Salt Lake City from Fox 13. One of the big stories there tonight, students and chaperones with the Utah schools for the deaf and blind complete a 36-hour race from Tacoma Washington to Port Townsend, Washington.\par \par \uc2 The 70 mile journey filled with challenges. Last year, a team from the school had to leave the competition 20 miles short of the finish because of bad weather. This time, they made it.\par \par \uc2 That's the night's live look outside the Beltway from SPECIAL REPORT. We're outside the Beltway too. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Welcome back to the \uc2 Edward M. Kennedy Institute for the U.S. Senate\uc2  in Boston. It feels like you're in the Senate.\par \par \uc2 Earlier today, I moderated a debate here between South Carolina Republican Senator \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  and Independent Vermont Senator \uc2 Bernie Sanders\uc2 . It was part of what's called the Senate Project. It was streamed live today on Fox Nation. You can also get it on Fox Nation now.\par \par \uc2 The Oxford style debate is designed to have substantive discussion of policy in the hopes of finding some common ground like the Senate of old with late senators \uc2 Ted Kennedy\uc2  and \uc2 Orrin Hatch\uc2 , for example, men with much different views of the world who came together to get significant legislation passed, and in the meantime, established a true friendship.\par \par \uc2 Senator Sanders today won the coin toss to start the debate. And we begin there.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 BERNIE SANDERS\uc2  (I-VT): The working class and the middle class of this country are in serious trouble. We are moving toward an oligarchic form of society, where a small number of billionaires not only have extraordinary wealth, they have extraordinary political power.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 LINDSEY GRAHAM\uc2  (R-SC): I have a different take on things than Bernie. So, I just want to remind you a little bit about where we're at. The Democrats have the House, the Senate and the White House. Their agenda is not working, crime is rampant. This defund the police effort that you've been leading and others has taken a toll.\par \par \uc2 The bottom line is it's a tough time to be a cop now. Who wants to go into law enforcement? It's time to let the cops know you're not the problem, the criminals are the problem.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Senator Sanders, your response.\par \par \uc2 SANDERS: I never called for the defunding of the police.\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: I stand corrected.\par \par \uc2 SANDERS: What I do believe in is, former mayor, as a matter of fact that worked very well with our police department, is we need community based policing that reflects the people in those communities that we have to fight racism in every way that we can. But I've never called for defunding the police.\par \par \uc2 Now, it's interesting to hear Senator Graham speak, because other than the partisan rhetoric, what you don't hear him talking about are in fact the most important issues facing this country. I didn't hear Lindsey talk about the crisis of climate change.\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: At the end of the day, I believe climate change is real, but that's no reason to destroy the fossil fuel industry in this country. We're moving to electric cars, but that's going to be 2034, 2035.\par \par \uc2 Your party has made us miserable when it comes to filling up our tank.\par \par \uc2 SANDERS: Well, let's talk about gas prices, because you are right, they are outrageously high.\par \par \uc2 I think the president should bring the major oil companies in and tell them we are going to have a windfall profits tax on what they are doing in order to stop them from ripping off the American people.\par \par \uc2 What we need is a government that represents all of us and not just wealthy, powerful, special interest.\par \par \uc2 And do you know what the American people want? They do want Medicare for all. You talk about the joys and beauties of private insurance. Talk to the millions of workers who lost their private insurance during COVID.\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: In terms of your proposal for Medicare for all, I am challenging you and your party who run the Senate to bring it to the floor for a vote, and we'll see where the votes are.\par \par \uc2 Now, let's go back to gas prices. We should be producing more oil and gas here. And when you say it's the oil company's fault -- bull. They are in a business. The reason they are not producing more is because it's impossible to produce more under this administration. And if Bernie was in charge and people like him, it would get worse, not better.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Senators, thank you. That is the end of segment one.\par \par \uc2 If you will, come join us at these desks. We will make a transition to the seated, moderated section, and we'll go deeper into some of the discussions you have talked about.\par \par \uc2 Senator Sanders, President Biden has said recently that this is, and Senator Graham mentioned it, part of a giant transition for the U.S. economy. Is pain at the pump part of that transition to a greener economy?\par \par \uc2 SANDERS: Let me say this, that in terms of inflation, as I'm sure you know, we're looking at an international phenomenon. We can create millions of jobs transforming our energy system away from fossil fuel to energy efficiency and sustainable energy. And I think at the same time, I think the president has got to call those oil companies, those executives into the Oval Office and say stop ripping off the American people.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Do you think there is any part of that to Senator Sanders' point?\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: You don't have to be a rocket scientist to figure this out. Their campaign agenda was to destroy fossil fuel production. It's not gas companies not wanting to produce more oil and gas. It's the policies have made it almost impossible here at home. Russia, which is invading Ukraine, and one thing Bernie and I agree is that Putin sucks. So --\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I assume you concede that point?\par \par \uc2 SANDERS: I'm not in favor of the vulgarity, but the intent is correct.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: Putin is a bad guy. There you go. I will use the other word. The bottom line is people know when they go to the pump it doesn't have to be this way. I don't see anything changing, until you change leadership.\par \par \uc2 SANDERS: Right now we're looking for the first time in my lifetime, and yours, at a real threat to the existence of democracy in America. And do you know why? Because we have a former president, whose name is \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , who goes around the country telling people, hey, I won the election. In fact, I probably won it by a landslide, but they stole it. They took it away from me. All right, now, that happens to be what we call a big lie.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Can you address that, Senator? Can you say definitively the election was not stolen?\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: Yes. I voted to certify the election. There were some mail-in balloting chicanery out there, but no, no, I voted to certify the election. President Biden is the president.\par \par \uc2 SANDERS: He won the election?\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SANDERS: OK. Then what about all of the candidates out there who are trying to say that he didn't, your Republican candidates that you want people to vote for.\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: What about the people saying defund the police? You talk to them, and I will talk to that crowd.\par \par \uc2 SANDERS: Your crowd is a lot larger than my crowd.\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: So here is the point. Why is he talking about Trump? Because he can't talk about anything else.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Senator Sanders, you chuckled before when Senator Graham mentioned socialism.\par \par \uc2 SANDERS: Yes. That's exactly what I was going to say.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Right, and so I want to let you deal with that, about --\par \par \uc2 SANDERS: I understand we can do redbaiting. I guess it works among a certain.\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: Well, you're a socialist, aren't you?\par \par \uc2 SANDERS: I am a democratic socialist.\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: OK, I'm sorry.\par \par \uc2 SANDERS: And the policies that I advocate are taking place all over the world.\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: You describe problems, but your answer is always the government. It's always socialism.\par \par \uc2 SANDERS: Let's ask the American people whether they think it makes sense that somebody who makes a million dollars a year or $10 million a year or $100 million a year pays the same amount into Social Security as somebody who makes --\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: Let's vote on it.\par \par \uc2 SANDERS: Let's ask them. You know what, the polling has already given us the answer. They think it is nonsensical.\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: Why aren't we voting on it?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Let me ask it a different way, Senator Sanders.\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: This is fun.\par \par \uc2 SANDERS: It is fun.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Let me ask, hold on --\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: Because his plan, the reason he doesn't want to vote on it, nobody is going to --\par \par \uc2 SANDERS: I want to vote it.\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: You are in charge. Let's vote.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Can I get the Senate sergeant at arms here? No, I'm sorry. Let me ask the question this way. The national debt, Senator Sanders, stands at more than $30 trillion. It's 133 percent of GDP, gross domestic product. Should Americans care about that?\par \par \uc2 SANDERS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Is that a problem long term?\par \par \uc2 SANDERS: Yes, it is. It is. And it's something we want to deal with.\par \par \uc2 Lindsey apparently thinks that people just love paying money to the insurance companies. If I go to the American people and I say hey, Bret, guess what? You are going to pay more, you are going to pay $5,000 more in taxes but you are going to pay $8,000 less in premiums, deductibles, and co-payments -- I know he works for FOX, maybe he can't say it -- but most people would say Bernie, that's a pretty good deal. You saved me $3,000 bucks, and you are guaranteeing comprehensive healthcare.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Senator, I was waiting for the FOX, I was waiting for it.\par \par \uc2 SANDERS: It could have been worse. It could have been worse.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: OK. OK.\par \par \uc2 Let's see if we can get to something you all agree on. Is there a common ground on any issue?\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: Sure.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: That the two of you can see eye to eye on, and what is that?\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: I don't know. You know, I'm on the Budget Committee with Bernie, and it's been a blast. Look, I thoroughly enjoyed it. There are things we can do.\par \par \uc2 Here's where I think upper income Americans, like both of us, I am willing to take less from Social Security. I'm willing to have a different COLA and reshape my benefits. I'm willing to pay more into Part A and B.\par \par \uc2 SANDERS: So I think you should lift of the cap completely. You will make Social Security solvent for 75 years, solve the legitimate issue you raised. And, yes, \uc2 Jeff Bezos\uc2  and \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  will pay a lot more in taxes. So what? Now on Medicare --\par \par \uc2 BAIER: On the common ground question, I didn't get it out of that answer.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 SANDERS: Well, what you got is that he sees it as a problem. I see it as a problem. All right? Medicare, I see Medicare solvency as a problem. That is why I want us to stop spending twice as much per person on healthcare as any other country on earth.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: OK, there is a bipartisan agreement currently on gun control legislation. Senator Sanders, you are not as of yet. Is that a common ground issue around the edges that don't deal with everything you want that you all can get to?\par \par \uc2 SANDERS: Look, if that bill -- you're right, it hasn't been written yet. We have an outline of it. If that bill came to the floor, would I vote for it? I'm quite sure I would. It is a step forward. I think clearly it does not go far enough.\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: So the fact that Bernie is willing, Bret, maybe to vote for this is very encouraging to me. We will see how it shakes out.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Senator Sanders, Senator Graham, we will have to leave it at that. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Thanks to Senators Graham and Sanders. You can get all of that debate on FOX Nation right now on demand. We're also going to repackage the hour and it will run on FOX News Channel this Saturday coming up, 7:00 p.m. eastern time.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the battle to win the Republican nomination in a Senate race the GOP considers crucial to its takeover plans in November.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Life-sized replica of the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2  here at the \uc2 Edward M. Kennedy Institute\uc2 . Tonight, we're getting a look at the mugshot of Paul Pelosi, the husband of House Speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2 , from his arrest last month for driving under the influence. Pelosi was detained in \uc2 Napa County\uc2 , California. The picture was taken in the early morning hours of May 29th. It was released today. The district attorney's office is refusing to distribute any dash or body camera video from that arrest, saying it could jeopardize the ongoing investigation.\par \par \uc2 In tonight's midterm focus, the Nevada Senate primary for Republicans, voters will choose a nominee to square off against incumbent Democrat \uc2 Catherine Cortez Masto\uc2 , who is considered vulnerable this year, as the GOP tries to gain control of the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2 . FOX Business correspondent Hillary Vaughn reports tonight from Reno.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 HILLARY VAUGHN, FOX BUSINESS CORRESPONDENT: In Nevada's closed Republican primary on Tuesday voters will pick who will go head-to head with sitting Democratic Senator \uc2 Catherine Cortez Masto\uc2 . Former Attorney General \uc2 Adam Laxalt\uc2  is a household name in Nevada politics. His grandfather served as governor.\par \par \uc2 ADAM LAXALT\uc2 , (R) NEVADA SENATE CANDIDATE: I have been beating Masto in every head-to-head national poll that has come out on this race, because we already are known in this state as someone that has a record.\par \par \uc2 VAUGHN: And Sam Brown, a war veteran and newcomer to politics and Nevada after living in Texas where he deployed to Afghanistan.\par \par \uc2 SAW BROWN, (R) NEVADA SENATE CANDIDATE: I will outwork \uc2 Catherine Cortez Masto\uc2 . I put in the sweat equity, and that's what we're going to need to win that middle victor of the vote and ultimately flip this state.\par \par \uc2 VAUGHN: Laxalt has been the favored frontrunner. He's criticized Brown for being newbie in Nevada and losing a race in Texas before he moved here.\par \par \uc2 LAXALT: He just moved from Texas just a few years ago.\par \par \uc2 BROWN: And when I was wounded, I came back. I was medevaced and recovered in San Antonio. I was serving all of America including Nevadans when I was there.\par \par \uc2 VAUGHN: Laxalt as a leg up when it comes to star power, hitting the trail with GOP heavyweights and an endorsement from former president \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , which so far has been a big boost for candidates -- 96 percent of people Trump endorsed, or 91 out of 95, have won their primaries or advanced in their races. Brown is brushing off Laxalt's long list of glitzy endorsements.\par \par \uc2 BROWN: We are not relying on political celebrities and surrogates from D.C. to come out and rescue us in a primary.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 VAUGHN (on camera): "The Cook Political Report" calls Masto the most endangered Democrat in the midterms, which means this could be the best bet for Republicans to win back the Senate. But Republican voters here do not want to bet on the wrong horse. So some voters are making up their mind Vegas style. One voter said it was too tough to choose between the two, so he flipped a coin. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Hillary Vaughn live in Reno. Hillary, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the price of a gallon of gas soars past $5 a gallon. We will talk about the U.S. economy and today's Capitol riot hearing session as well.\par \par \uc2 First, Beyond our Borders tonight. Actor Kevin Spacey is being formally charged with sexual offenses against three men in Britain. The 62-year-old is due to appear in court Thursday. Spacey has been accused of similar actions in the U.S. He has denied all allegations.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at Tokyo. One of the big stories there tonight, a Japanese foundation says it's launching a fundraising drive to provide more than 1,200 Ukrainian evacuees in that country with additional financial support for language studies and other needs. Acceptance of the Ukrainian evacuees is unusual for Japan which has extremely strict refugee and immigration policies despite its own shrinking labor force.\par \par \uc2 Just some of the other stories Beyond our Borders tonight. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WILLIAM BARR, FORMER U.S. ATTORNEY GENERAL: Before the election it was possible to talk sense to the president. And while you sometimes had to engage in a wrestling match with him, then it was possible to keep things on track. But I felt that after the election he didn't seem to be listening.\par \par \uc2 REP. ZOE LOFGREN, (D-CA) HOUSE SELECT JANUARY 6TH COMMITTEE: The Trump campaign and surrogates misled donors as to where their funds would go and what they would be used for. So not only was there the big lie, there was the big rip-off.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: The January 6th committee continuing today, second committee hearing talking again about former president Trump and his efforts to say that the election was stolen.\par \par \uc2 Let's start there with our panel, Guy Benson, political editor at Townhall.com, host of "The Guy Benson Show" on FOX News Radio, Kimberley Strassel, a member of the editorial board at "The Wall Street Journal," and Harold Ford Jr., former Tennessee Congressman, co-host of THE FIVE. Harold, where do you think this is going as you look at the last two committee hearings?\par \par \uc2 HAROLD FORD JR., FORMER TENNESSEE REPRESENTATIVE: Well, I think -- first of all, thanks for having me, and I am glad you are there at the Kennedy School. I think the way Bill Barr and the former campaign manager, Mr. Stepien, their words to describe this -- Stepien said he was on team normal, and for Bill Barr to say that it was idiotic, it was completely nonsensical, detached from reality. I think the former president has to be wondering if this is leads to more than just what is occurring before this committee.\par \par \uc2 I think it's important that it be aired to the public, to the country those who supported President Trump and may even adhere to the belief that he is the winner, and even Democrats, those who are glad he is out of office but some of those who may not be loud enough about protesting and demanding action for those protesting violently outside the houses of the Supreme Court justices.\par \par \uc2 Our politics have gone to a bad place. That's why I started with I'm glad you are where you are. That debate was civil, robust, and serious between Mr. Graham and Mr. Sanders. And it aimed for outcome, not an incitement. That's what politics is when it's at its best. And that's why the world looks up to American politics. I hope this hearing, if it does nothing more, it reminds Americans of whom we are and why are politics is admired and revered around the globe when we are at our best.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Kimberley, your thoughts?\par \par \uc2 KIMBERLEY STRASSEL, "WALL STREET JOURNAL": I agree with Harold that there is a lot of interesting information and that Americans definitely deserve to be clued into that. A lot of people still have a lot of questions about that day.\par \par \uc2 I think the more concerning thing for some people as they watch this, especially if you see comments like that from Congresswoman Lofgren and others, is the committee using this as a basis to try to build a case for some sort of criminal referral to the Department of Justice, some sort of action afterward against \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  or others for their actions leading up to or on the day of January 6th?\par \par \uc2 And then I think we move beyond the realm of educating, having a discussion, talking about how we make sure this doesn't happen again, and again, throwing this into partisan politics and a lot more upheaval going forward.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Guy, I want to turn now to all these stories popping up whether President Biden is running in 2024 or not. The White House saying again that, yes, he has said that he is running. However, \uc2 Alexandria Ocasio- Cortez\uc2  over the weekend saying this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. ALEXANDRIA OCASIO-CORTEZ, (D-NY): If the president chooses to run again in 2024 -- first of all, I'm focused on winning this majority right now and preserving a majority this year in 2022. So we'll cross that bridge when we get to it.\par \par \uc2 DANA BASH, CNN CHIEF POLITICAL CORRESPONDENT: That's not a yes.\par \par \uc2 OCASIO-CORTEZ: Yes, I think we should endorse when we get to it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: When we get to it, Guy. And "The \uc2 New York Times\uc2 " this weekend that Democratic whispers of no start to rise. Really, the whispers are loud.\par \par \uc2 GUY BENSON, POLITICAL EDITOR, TOWNHALL.COM: Yes, and they are getting louder. First of all, Bret, your backdrop is just amazing. It's so cool, I have to say it. But this story you are referencing in the "New York times" is long, and it quotes a lot of people on the record and quite a few people on background with these rumors starting to swirl and grow louder, to your point, that there are people starting to actually articulate, not just at dinner party conversations but within the halls and corridors of power, that they are very concerned about \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's ability to lead that party into the next presidential election. And AOC was almost squirming a little bit over those questions. It's all hypothetical. Maybe we will cross the bridge if we come to it.\par \par \uc2 I'm still of the belief that he is not going to run again. I have no special knowledge of that. I just don't think he can. I don't know if he can do it physically. I don't know if he can do it mentally. And I say that with respect. I just think two years from now it's a very taxing process, and I'm not sure there are many Democrats that you would talk to anywhere in the country, including in the White House, in a true moment of quiet honesty who would say, yes, this is going well, and we are confident about this man's ability to be the standard-bearer again in a rough and tumble yearlong presidential campaign. And I think those noises are starting to gain more momentum, if you will, even though we are not five months out from the midterm election. People are looking ahead.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes. Harold, Brit Hume earlier said what's interesting about the January 6th Committee is if they are successful -- let's not say that they go after a conviction, but they're going after denigrating somehow former president Trump so that he doesn't run again or is incapable of running again, politically, Democrats would be hurt by that potentially because \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's case would be I'm the guy who beat \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . If there is another Republican or an up and comer, it becomes a different scenario.\par \par \uc2 FORD: What I hope it does, and it could happen that way. Brit is smart. I hope it reinvigorates our sense and adherence to the rule of law. I remember when \uc2 Al Gore\uc2  lost the presidency to \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2 . The court decided to stop counting votes in Florida. I accepted that. I was as big a supporter of him as perhaps anyone, other than him. He even accepted it.\par \par \uc2 Can you imagine President Trump if the state of Pennsylvania, Arizona, Georgia, where the governor of that state was \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's brother, as Jeb Bush was in Florida, him, how he would be behaving today? The rule of law should be venerated. I would remind Guy, also, that last week I thought the \uc2 Rangers\uc2  were going to be in the finals of the \uc2 NHL\uc2  for the Stanley Cup, and they went up two-nothing, and they lost. Six months is a long time and two- and-a-half years is a long time. But if Biden doesn't fit it, he will lose.\par \par \uc2 BENSON: The \uc2 Rangers\uc2  aren't 79 years old.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Last word Kimberley.\par \par \uc2 STRASSEL: ON what a part of that? The rangers, or?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Maybe Biden.\par \par \uc2 STRASSEL: Yes, Biden. Look, the age, as Guy just pointed out, is a real problem. This is something that isn't just being said among the Democratic Party. Look at the polls. Americans are increasingly concerned about his ability to continue leading.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, panel, thanks so much.\par \par \uc2 Here at the \uc2 Edward M. Kennedy\uc2  Center, we got a tour behind-the-scenes from the chair here, Bruce Percelay. And we started just down the hall in Senator Kennedy's mock-up of the D.C. office. Take a look.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 BRUCE PERCELAY, CHAIR, \uc2 EDWARD M. KENNEDY\uc2  INSTITUTE: Well, that is the goal of the Senate Project is to try to bring the Senate back to the days of Kennedy and Hatch.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So from the office it's only a short jaunt to the Senate chamber.\par \par \uc2 PERCELAY: Correct.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: This is the Senate chamber, and it's almost exactly a replica.\par \par \uc2 PERCELAY: This is virtually the same building, the same space, the same dimensions. This was known as the candy desk. And the senator that sat here had to keep this stocked with candy. And today when a senator walks out and wants some candy, they get it right here.\par \par \uc2 There is so much meaning to everything here, but it is the only replica of the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2  in the world.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, fair, balance, and unafraid, from the \uc2 Edward M. Kennedy Institute\uc2 . We ran over a little bit. "JESSE WATTERS PRIMETIME" is right now. Hey, Jesse.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FNSPBB0020220614ei6d00001\par }\par \b \uc2 Search Summary\b0\par \par  \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx2000\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx9360\pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \uc2 Text\cell \pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \li50\cell \pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\